t lack of electricity was a huge part of the elevated mortality. This time around, the grid failed almost immediately plunging 1.5 million households and businesses into darkness.\par \par \uc2 These repeated power failures along with the repeated failures of the body that governs the grid, which is called \uc2 PREPA\uc2 , have led to years of sustained public outrage, political mobilization, and investigations. Puerto Rico`s government has hired a private company to manage electrical distribution. That deal has also sparked protests.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, in the era of climate change on a tropical island that is part of the United States, the number one priority is a resilient grid, which Puerto Rico demonstrably still does not have. Federal government has allocated $9.5 billion to repair the grid. That project is moving at an extremely slow pace. Now, hurricane Fiona is calling attention again to this crucial need. And the only hope, I suppose, amidst these awful scenes is that this latest disaster will put the necessary pressure on government officials and the grid operator to ensure the Puerto Rico`s more than three million residents finally get power they can depend on.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We`re living in one of the most precarious times in American history as a nation as a democracy. You don`t have to take my word for it. Last month, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  met with a group of historians in the White House where they told him the same, warned him about the dire condition of democracy. And now, that same warning is coming from award-winning filmmaker who spent decades documenting America`s past. A new docu-series from Ken Burns analyzes the flawed U.S. response to the atrocities of the Holocaust, and the parallels to the current moment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANIEL MENDELSOHN, WRITER: The fragility of civilized behavior is the one thing you really learn because these people who we now see in these photographs, the sepia photographs and they`re receding into time, they`re no different, no different from us. You look at your neighbors, the people at the dry cleaners, the waiters in the restaurant, that`s who these people were. Don`t kid yourself.\par \par \uc2 GUY STERN, VETERAN, WORLD WAR II: We have seen as a media of human behavior and we have no guarantee that it won`t recur. If we can make that clear and graphic and understandable not is something to imitate but as a warning of what can happen to human beings, then perhaps we have one shield against its recurrence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Two of the Emmy Award-winning co-directors and producers of that new PBS three-part docu-series, The U.S. and the Holocaust, Ken Burns and Lynn Novick join me now. Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 LYNN NOVICK, CO-DIRECTOR AND PRODUCER, THE U.S. AND THE HOLOCAUST: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KEN BURNS, CO-DIRECTOR AND PRODUCER, THE U.S. AND THE HOLOCAUST: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I think when I saw it, when I saw the title of this coming out, I thought to myself, OK, what did I learn -- what do you learn about the U.S. and Holocaust? And I think what you learned generally is we went to war against the Nazis, and then liberated the camps, more or less. Why did you want to make a movie on this topic?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Because I think we`ve conveniently sort of settled ourselves in that nice, comfortable suburban decided that`s where we`re going to live and raise our kids and our grandkids and it`s just not the case. We knew what was going on from the very beginning 3000 articles in 1933 alone about what Hitler was doing in `33, the first year of his of his reign. The real question is what we did and what we could have done in what we didn`t do. And I think all of that is points to the fact that though we let in more people than any other sovereign nation, 225,000 refugees, we could have let in five or 10 times that amount, and really made a difference when it comes to the people who lost their lives in the Holocaust.\par \par \uc2 But because we have such -- had such extraordinary anti-Semitism, prevalent anti-Semitism, because we were a country that had enslaved Africans that we had isolated our native populations, that we were xenophobic, and nativist, and all that sort of stuff in the middle of the Depression, and subscribing to eugenics, which saw a hierarchy of race, when of course, there`s only one race, the human race, we were in the perfect position to have everything get clogged up.\par \par \uc2 It`s not Roosevelt`s fault. It`s not one part of the executives fault. It`s all of our faults, and we have to own it and reckon with it, because in this story are the seeds of what`s going on there.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That the point that stuck out to me in the documentary, the 3000 articles in 1933. And it reminded me of when I read -- I think it was Meacham`s Andrew Jackson biography. There`s a -- there`s a part in there about the Trail of Tears. And I remember reading that and realizing that at the time --\par \par \uc2 Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: -- there are people at the time during those years being like, this is abhorrent, horrible, you can`t do this. This was enormous point of debate. And what I had learned later on was like, oh, they didn`t realize. And I think this comes through really well in documentary that people realized people were ringing the alarm bell about the fate of European Jewry quite early on here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:28]\par \par \uc2 NOVICK: Yes, because, you know, as many people say, when people tell you who they are, believe them. I think it was Maya Angelou who said that, and certainly true for Adolf Hitler. He said, when he felt. He was quite direct, you know, he evolved the plan to actually commit the Holocaust over time. It wasn`t from day one. But nonetheless, there`s no secret that there was just direct persecution, violence, concentration camps, deportations, taking people`s citizenship rights away little by little.\par \par \uc2 As Deborah Lipstadt, the Holocaust historian says in the film, you know, it`s drip, drip, drip, little by little, little by little. There was tremendous coverage as Ken was saying. The American people have access to all this information. And, you know, why were we not more willing to open our doors as the crisis intensified? And it became clear that people were in mortal peril and desperate to get here. And there were waiting lists at consulates. There were, you know, piles and piles and piles of applications, hundreds of thousands of people trying to get into a very small number of slots that were available. And the United States did not see fit to change our policies, and make it easier for people to escape and to get here.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I want to play this bit -- a little bit about that, because you guys do a great job of setting up the context of U.S. immigration politics, particularly at that moment. We`ve seen, of course, the enormous influx of immigrants in the late 19th century and early 20th century and there had been this real restrictionist move by the --\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Right, the backlash.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Real backlash. And the backlash hits right at the sort of moment, when we should be opening the doors to take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: By 1910, New York would be home to more than a million Jews, more than a quarter of the city`s population, far more than any other city on Earth.\par \par \uc2 PETER HAYES, HISTORIAN: The anxieties about urbanization, about unlettered, untutored, relatively uneducated peoples coming in in large numbers, the sense that disease was a problem. All of these worries were amalgamated into a belief that immigrants caused these problems. And thus immigration should be held down.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Many white Protestant Americans came to fear they were about to be outnumbered and outbred by the newcomers and their offspring, that they were being replaced.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NOVICK: Sounds familiar?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and the continuities is quite strong.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: So many things, conservatives in Germany who install Hitler are certain that they can control him. Hitler plays down anti-Semitism, steps up street violence in order to convince the country they`re on the verge of a civil war. There`s -- you know, after a while, the rhymes were getting too much for us. And we actually accelerated our production to come out now this year, September, and not comfortably next year, because there were so -- there`s a moment in the film when Deborah Lipstadt again says, the time to stop the genocide is before it happens to which I would humbly add, the time to save a democracy is before it`s lost.\par \par \uc2 And that`s, you know, an important reason why, as we set the table of all the conditions that set up the perfect storm that is The United States and the Holocaust, it seemed important also not to just conveniently say goodbye in 1965 when the pernicious Johnson read the Immigration Act of 1924 is changed by LBJ at the Statue of Liberty, this symbol, the MacGuffin throughout the film, but to really take us through all sorts of things like Charlottesville and the Tree of Life synagogue in Pittsburgh, and Dylan Ruffin, and the things that are sort of around us. The \uc2 ADL\uc2  says they`ve never seen the number of anti-Semitic incidents as are taking place now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This point about what a nation is, what its identity is, which is obviously the question that Hitler is posing to Germans, right, but also the Americans are asking themselves about how many people they want to take, right? It`s so striking that the Johnson -- the 1924-65, right? 24 is the big sort of like immigration restrictionist. A bill that really for the first time like creates this sort of --\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Quota system.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Quota system. 65 we get rid of it. That -- well, you look at other networks that will -- they will pinpoint as the pivot point that America starts to go to hell.\par \par \uc2 NOVICK: Well, that does speak to the question of, you know, from the clip we just played, who is a genuine American, who is a real American. At one point, \uc2 Calvin Coolidge\uc2  says we have to keep America for Americans. He doesn`t qualify what does that mean, but there`s an implication there that certain people are worthy of being Americans and other people aren`t and we`re still struggling with that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All three episodes of The U.S. and the Holocaust are now available via \uc2 PBS\uc2 . Ken Burns, Lynn Novick, it`s fantastic work. Thank you so much. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 NOVICK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Monday night. Hey, it`s an "ALEX WAGNER" bonus show tonight. It starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220920ei9j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for September 18, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui \par \uc2 6,410 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:00:00]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening, and welcome to politics nation. Tonight`s lead, rallying cry.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow marks 50 days until most of the country votes in midterm elections. Democrats are fighting to protect razor thin majorities in both the House and Senate. Republicans are scheming on how to take at least one of those chambers, taking advantage of historic trends that tend to favor the opposition party in the midterms. But brand new polling from NBC finds Democrats tied with Republicans in congressional preference driven in large part by voter disapproval over the reversal of Roe versus Wade, while President Biden`s approval rating is at its highest mark in nearly a year and Donald Trump`s favorability rating is at its lowest mark in more than a year.\par \par \uc2 Still a red flag for Democrats on the issues, the polling showing that Americans favor the party on abortion and health care by giving Republicans all-time high marks on the economy, crime and immigration. The latter issue having been ghoulishly exploited this week by the GOP governor of Florida, Texas and Arizona, they`re forced relocation of Hispanic migrants from red states to liberal cities horrifying critics on the left while right wing media is cheering the cruelty and claiming, of course, it is the president`s fault. How do Democrats sling mud on their own and how did they sling mud of their own while staying out of the muck themselves with just weeks left before the Election Day? That is the big question tonight on POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 And later, I will dig into the firestorm surrounding the owner of the NBA`s \uc2 Phoenix Suns\uc2 , as the league weighs how to react to Robert Sarver`s allegedly racist and sexist conduct over the past year. Player, advocates and corporate sponsors are calling for Sarver to give the team up or else. You won`t want to miss my take on the controversy, coming up shortly.\par \par \uc2 But, first, joining me now is Congresswoman \uc2 Joyce Beatty\uc2 , the Democrat of Ohio, is the chair of the congressional black caucus.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, today -- let`s go right to it. Today marks 50 days out from Election Day. As I mentioned at the top, new polling from NBC News finds Democrats in a stronger position than many expected only a few months ago. President Biden`s approval rating currently at 45 percent, a three point bump in the last month while his disapproval rating has dropped by three points to 42 percent. Americans are evenly split over who they want to see in control of Congress, 46 percent for Democrats, 46 percent for Republicans, and the country trusts Democrats more on health care, education and, of course, abortion rights after the reversal of Roe.\par \par \uc2 Still, on the economy, crime and immigration, Republicans appear to have massive leads going into November. Do Democrats need to expand their message or stick with what appears to be working with just seven weeks left, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 REP. JOYCE BEATTY (D-OH): Well, thank you, first. I think our message is working because we put people over politics. And you were dead on. When we look at the numbers, when we look at the number of women, the increased number of women who have registered to vote, and who will be voting for Democrats because of Roe v. Wade, when you look at what we have done in the course of this past year, when we talk about everything, from bipartisan infrastructure, the American rescue plan, when we look at the new Inflation Reduction Act, what we are seeing is Americans realizing that it is Democrats that`s making their lives better, whether that is health care, whether that is education.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:20]\par \par \uc2 And I will also say that we are doing a better job, when you talk about keeping our communities safer.\par \par \uc2 So, I think the American citizens understand that there is a lot of theatrics with the Republicans. And what they`ve been doing in the past weeks and months speaks to their being nervous but the real reality that Democrats are going to hold the majority in the House and we are looking great in the Senate as well. Yes, we have a razor thin margin, but that`s why the Congressional Black Caucus has been continuing to make a difference, fighting hard, writing legislation and going back home in our communities, standing with the people and getting people registered to vote.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: They are talking about going back home into our community. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in your state last night, Congresswoman, stomping for his pick in the Ohio Senate race, Republican J.D. Vance. In addition to repeatedly suggesting that Hispanic migrants are essentially invading the country, he had this to say about what it`s like to be persecuted in America. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: For six straight years, I have been harassed, investigated, defamed, slandered and persecuted like no other president and probably like no one in American history.\par \par \uc2 The radical Democrats and the deep state is a form of political repression unlike anything our nation has ever seen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I mean, I have to raise that to you, Representative. As a woman, as chair of the Congressional Black Caucus, how do you respond to Trump`s contention that no one has been persecuted and repressed like him in American history?\par \par \uc2 BEATTY: I would say that we have never had an administrator or a president who has behaved and acted and set us back in American history. So, anything that he has been persecuted, prosecuted for, he deserves it. He was the worst that we`ve ever seen and he set this country back. And he is getting everything that he deserves.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And when you think of -- and I don`t have to tell you, but when you think of all of those in the civil rights movement and protest movement, many harassed and all kinds of things done, to say he is the worst in American history, I mean, many of us have gone through things that -- well, that`s another time, another show. But it`s amazing, the statement in itself, and he`s acting like a victim, like he`s a martyr.\par \par \uc2 Let me also bring this to you, Congresswoman. Last week, an Ohio judge temporarily halted the state`s six-week abortion ban. Clinics in Ohio able to return, providing abortions up to 20 weeks while the two-week restraining order is in place. Your state`s Democratic Senate candidate, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , has made abortion a central issue in this very close race against Republican J.D. Vance, who said this on a podcast early this year. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R), OHIO SENATE CANDIDATE: Let`s say Roe versus Wade is overruled, Ohio bans abortion in 2022 -- let`s say 2024, and then every day, \uc2 George Soros\uc2  sends a 747 to Columbus to load up disproportionately black women to get them to go have abortions in California. Of course, the left will celebrate this as a victory for diversity. If that happens, do you need some federal response to prevent it from happening because it`s really creepy? And I am pretty sympathetic to that, actually.\par \par \uc2 So, hopefully, we get to a point where Ohio bans abortion in the California and Soroses of the world respect it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Soros sends a plane of black women. But apart from the casual racism and anti-Semitism in Vance`s message, how do you respond to what you are state`s Republican Senate candidate is saying as the lives of Ohio women, black women specifically, hang in the balance?\par \par \uc2 BEATTY: Well, I think that what we have to do is make sure that we get women out to vote because we know how it will affect black women. And that`s why the Congressional Black Caucus has been out front on this issue, and also that`s why we have more black female candidates than we`ve had in history.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:04]\par \par \uc2 I think when we listen to J.D. Vance, he speaks for all of his Republican colleagues who agree with him. And we have a candidate that is standing with black women because he understands what will happen if we don`t turn around what is happening not in Ohio. We looked at Kansas, and what they have been able to do and having a more women registered. We also look to Michigan and Wisconsin and how their numbers have increased for more women getting registered. And I can tell you, we also have some female candidates in Ohio who are doing quite well. We are hoping that we pick up an open seat in Ohio, someone who has been out in the forefront, standing up with me for women and other women across the state.\par \par \uc2 We understand what is at risk for us as black women. We know our history. We know what happened when we have Republicans and others like the candidate for Senate talking about reversing Roe v. Wade, taking abortions away no matter what. Remember, I`m from a state the where the ten-year-old had to go to Indiana to have a safe procedure to abort a child because of her young age.\par \par \uc2 So, we are all in on this, Reverend Al. We are going to be out there in the fight. And we were in there before the Dobbs decision, we understand the risk, and another thing, that`s why we have to make sure that we look at these judicial candidates and that we vote for judicial candidates who stand with us.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You have to vote the whole ticket, no doubt about it. Whatever your preference, vote all the way down to the bottom.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Madam Chairman \uc2 Joyce Beatty\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Minnesota Attorney General Keith Ellison. Thank you for coming on today.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, I went to the White House, Attorney General Ellison, in my capacity as president of National Action Network to attend a summit about the rise of hate-based violence. And President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said America can`t remain silent when it comes to combating white supremacy and hate. According to police data compiled by the Center for the Study of Hate and Extremism, hate crimes in major U.S. cities rose again during the first half of 2022 after posting double digit percentage increases over the past two years.\par \par \uc2 You`ve dealt with your fair of hate and discrimination, as you are the first Muslim to serve in Congress and also the first African-American elected to Congress for Minnesota. Do you believe the White House is taking effective measures to combat this crisis?\par \par \uc2 ATTORNEY GEN. KEITH ELLISON (D-MN): I`m impressed with the response of the White House. I saw you and our friend, Marc Morial, and \uc2 Jonathan Greenblatt\uc2  and others at the White House taking it really seriously, raising the alarm on the threat of these terrorist acts that are based on people`s race and religion, their ethnic identity, their sexual orientation. I mean, they are busy, they are organizing and we`ve got to stand against them.\par \par \uc2 In Minnesota, I remember when Trump was talking about using anti-Asian sort of language. We saw a rise in not just hate crimes but also harassment, not just criminal conduct and attacks but also threatening just leaving threatening notes on people`s cars based on their Asian background. And, of course, in Minnesota, we had a terrorist attack on a mosque that took place during the Trump administration and he never said a single word about it. Three people drove all the way up from Illinois to bomb a mosque, and we got nothing from the president.\par \par \uc2 Those people were convicted and that`s a good thing. But the bottom line is, we are in the middle of a real, serious challenge. If I may say so well, I mean, I think prosecutors like myself and police and \uc2 FBI\uc2  folks, they can investigate these things and prosecute them. But at the end of the day, we are going to suppress hate in our congregations, in our neighborhoods, in our communities, and by leaders like yourself, speaking out against it.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Ahead of the midterm election throughout the country, we are seeing candidates who perpetrate former President Donald Trump`s big lie. They are being nominated by Republican voters for such key positions as governor, attorney general, secretary of state. These are positions that may have some say on the outcome of the next presidential race in 2024. From your experience of holding the responsibility of an attorney general, can you speak to the importance of these offices that are up for grabs this November?\par \par \uc2 [17:15:09]\par \par \uc2 ELLISON: Let me just say, I spent 12 years in \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 , and I can assure everybody that the action is in the state. I mean, I`m not saying Congress isn`t important. I spent time there with \uc2 Joyce Beatty\uc2 , your previous guest. But the state is where the voting qualifications are determined. If you could -- the state is where the lines, which congressional leaders and state leader -- congressional districts will be drawn. The state is where the action is. You`ve got to focus in on who your secretary of state is, your attorney general, and your governor. It is as important as any federal office and anybody in federal office will tell you that. The focus needs to be right there at the state level.\par \par \uc2 As a matter of fact, I will just remind everybody that in the last election, when Trump was trying to cut down and throttle people`s voting by mail, it was the Democratic attorneys general who sued \uc2 United States Postal Service\uc2  and stopped them from undermining people`s ability to vote by mail.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And I`m talking about attorney generals. You are running for reelection in November yourself, and you successfully prosecuted Derek Chauvin in the murder of George Floyd. You have long been an important voice on criminal justice issues in Minneapolis and very few prosecutors have been able to convict a police officer of murder for the killing of -- for killing someone on the job. You are one of those instances. You support a measure to overhaul the Minneapolis Police Department while your Republican opponent, Jim Schultz, seems to be eager to talk about local crime.\par \par \uc2 Now, if reelected as attorney general for a second term, how are you going to continue to address crime, police misconduct and hate-fueled violence in your state?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON: Well, people have a right to be safe. As attorney general, I take that responsibility seriously. I`ve prosecuted many criminals as attorney general, including people who were government officials who abused the rights of others, like Derek Chauvin, and, of course, hate criminals, as well. But there is more to the job than even that. We`ve done the job, which my opponent has never, ever, ever done but we`ve also gone after the opioid manufacturers and marketers because we know that fentanyl and other drugs are fueling so much of the hardship people are facing.\par \par \uc2 We also have done the job of convicting and going after people who are manufacturing and producing all these guns. We`ve had 200 mess shootings in the last 300 days. And myself, along with other attorneys general, have lobbied the Biden administration to get a hold on ghost guns. And we`ve gone to work to defend the rules that make those guns subject to regulation and one in court on that front.\par \par \uc2 So, I am going to continue to doing the job that we do, defending people, protecting their rights both in the criminal justice field but also in the field of housing, chemical dependency and, of course, the proliferation of guns, which is a real problem in every community in America.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, also, as you`ve been campaigning for reelection, you recently took a strong stance for abortion rights after touring a newly relocated abortion clinic that moved from a red state, North Dakota, to across the river to Minnesota. Last month, you joined attorney generals from 19 other states to file a brief in federal court challenging Texas` assertion that states shouldn`t have to comply with federal law that protects doctors who, in pregnancy, to save the patient`s life. How would you protect abortion rights in Minnesota and also of the residents in neighboring red states if you are reelected?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON: Well, every attorney general has an important role to play in terms of protecting the right to choose. We file amicus briefs in federal court, including \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 . And then also if anyone comes to Minnesota, which is now an island in the Midwest, along with Illinois, if they come here to seek a safe, legal abortion, we will protect their right to do so even if they go back to their own state. It is legal to travel to Minnesota and it is legal to get a safe, legal abortion in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:00]\par \par \uc2 And if you find that you are suffering repercussions because of that lawful, constitutional activity in Minnesota, the Minnesota Attorney General`s Office will defend you wherever you might be from. The fact is that we have stood with people who are pursuing their rights and we will continue to do so and we will stand up wherever we can to make sure that abortion rights are safe, legal and protected in the state of Minnesota and we will advocate for them to be expanded in other places, which I think is very critical, because this really should be a federal right. Your right to exercise your right to privacy should not be based on what state you live in. It is a national right. And that is why I am running for reelection, because I want to protect people`s rights and make sure that they are safe.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Attorney General Keith Ellison, I thank you, as always, for coming on the show tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, it is time to rise up and put a stop to racism and misogyny in the \uc2 NBA\uc2  from the basketball court to the owner`s suite.\par \par \uc2 And later, the January 6 committee might not be done yet. We`ve got the details on the possibility of an October surprise from the lawmakers investigating the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 But, first, my colleague, Richard Lui, with today`s other top news stories. Richard?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC HOST: Rev, a good Sunday to you. Some of the stories that we are watching for you today, Hurricane Fiona is hitting the southwestern coast of Puerto Rico. Its governor is saying the electrical system is down. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  approved an emergency declaration today.\par \par \uc2 British intelligence said that Kremlin widened its strikes on Ukrainian civilian infrastructure in the past week. Russian attacks in the eastern region killed five civilians over the past day. Several dozen residential buildings, gas pipelines and power lines were hit in the west, say regional governors.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden and first lady paid their respects to Queen Elizabeth II at Westminster Hall today. The U.S. ambassador to the United Kingdom joined them. The final ceremonies are tomorrow at Westminster Abbey.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:25:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: This week, I`ve been gratified to see fans, community leaders and corporations rise up and take a stand against bigotry in the \uc2 NBA\uc2 . After reports surfaced about racist and misogynist from \uc2 Phoenix Suns\uc2  owner Robert Sarver, in my capacity as the head of the National Action Network, I sent a letter a couple of months ago expressing concern over Sarver`s behavior.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NBA\uc2  fined Sarver $10 million for his action and suspended him from the league for one year, which is progress, but I don`t think it goes nearly far enough. Neither do two of the most vocal leaders in the game. LeBron James and Chris Paul, who plays on Sarver`s team, both expressed disappointment in Silver`s decision, saying it was too lax considering Sarver`s use of the N-word, and his sexual comments to employees.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, \uc2 PayPal\uc2  announced it would no longer sponsor the Suns if Sarver remained in charge. However, the Suns other corporate sponsors, including Footprint, \uc2 Verizon\uc2 , \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2  and Gatorade among others have not followed suit. The \uc2 Phoenix Suns\uc2  have not commented about this situation to NBC. I have had, however, pretty decent relationship with \uc2 NBA\uc2  Commissioner \uc2 Adam Silver\uc2  but I was surprised that he would come with such a soft and, in my opinion, less than adequate decision. The Board of governors should vote for Sarver`s removal from the league in recognition that his behavior does not represent the values of the \uc2 NBA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And while I`m on the subject of professional basketball, I would be remiss if I didn`t mention Britney Griner, who remains unjustly imprisoned in Russia, even as the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  holds its finals tournament this weekend. On Friday, President Biden met with Griner`s wife and the sister of Paul Whelan, who also is jailed in Russia. They went then to discuss the continuing commitment to bringing them home safely. And three weeks ago, when civil rights leaders and I met with the president, I brought up Brittney and he was firmly saying then he was going to continue to do all he could.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NBA\uc2  and \uc2 WNBA\uc2 , both have been vocal in their support of Brittney Griner as well. But even as the season is coming to an end, we must not forget this promising and inspiring woman who is right now being used as a political pawn. Both leagues must continue to use their global platform to do everything in their power to rectify this injustice.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:33:29]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. To better understand today`s top headlines, let`s turn to my political panel for their analysis. Joining me now is Charles Blow, Political Analyst and Columnist for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . And Reed Galen, Co-Founder of the Lincoln Project.\par \par \uc2 Charles, let`s begin with the new NBC poll that shows President Biden`s approval rating is up three points from last month to 46%. Also, the poll offers a deeper look into what is motivating voters this midterm cycle. Republicans have favored for the handling of border security, economy, immigration and crime.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, voters have favored the Democratic Party when it comes to handling issues of protecting democracy, education, abortion and health care. Are you concerned Republican governor`s recent efforts to transport migrants from the border to other states is a tactic to change the subject heading into the election?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BLOW, THE NEW YORK TIMES COLUMNIST: Well, I think it`s more a tactic to try to keep the subject kind of focused on the issues on which they are winning. I think I want to remind viewers that a few months ago, there weren`t very many issues that Democrats were leading on at all before the overturning of Roe and before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  reemerged as a kind of prominent figure primarily following the rate at Mar-a-Lago, Democrats were losing really badly because Republicans had basically run this campaign of anti-wokeness. And they were talking about, you know, banning books and school choice and parental rights.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:16]\par \par \uc2 And that was a very potent and effective messaging had done well, for Republicans in Virginia. And they were going to nationalize that, that strategy. And what happened with Roe and with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  kind of took the wind out of the sails on that.\par \par \uc2 So, the fact that Democrats are leading on any issue at this point is, I think, a victory for Democrats. They need to hold that ground and play that up as much as possible. And that`s exactly what they have been doing. It`s why you see a more vigorous kind of combative, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who are taking on these particular issues head on. And Republicans are basically now trying to figure out of the things they have left. Can we really raise the profile of those things and crime and immigration are some of those things that they`re trying to press on the things that they have left?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Reed, on that note, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  held a rally in support of the Ohio senatorial candidate J.D. Vance last night in Youngstown, Ohio. He spoke for nearly three hours, but made no mention not at all of the migrant flights controversy that has been dominating right wing media. Do you think the former president is feeling upstaged by Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who has found a new way to fire up the conservative base that has nothing to do with Trump?\par \par \uc2 REED GALEN, THE LINCOLN PROJECT PODCAST HOST: Well, if he`s not worried about being upstaged, I think he`s certainly making sure that he`s not giving DeSantis more attention and praise for something that Trump would have loved to have done. But certainly, I think that the rest of that (technical difficulty) should scare the heck out of every last American who believes the law and constitution and, you know, the equality of all Americans before, you know, before the courts and before Justice. And so, I would say it makes \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  better, but it was an opportunity (technical difficulty) typically.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You`re a little choppy there. Let me see if we get your sound fix. But Charles, let me go to you on this one. The President this week is expected to give a speech to the United Nations surge support for the Ukrainian fighters, before turning to the midterms. In the meantime, Biden issued a stern warning to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  this weekend, in case he`s thinking of using chemical or nuclear weapons in Ukraine. Here`s a clip of his interview on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: Don`t, don`t, don`t, if would change the face of war, unlike anything since World War II.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And the consequences of that would be, what? What the U.S. response be?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You think I would tell you if I knew exactly what it would be? Of course, I`m not going to tell you. It`ll be consequential. There`ll become more of a pariah in the world than they ever have been. And depending on the extent of what they do, will determine what response was there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: How much does Biden`s leadership abroad and the U.S. involvement in Russia`s war affect voters this November?\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Well, you know, I think Biden has gotten a boost a little bit from see -- American seeing Russia kind of back on its heels a little bit retreating in certain parts of Ukraine as the Ukrainians advanced. But, you know, this was a tricky thing all along. There were certain people, kind of hawks also, people who believe that this posed a real threat to democracy in all parts of the world who said, yes, we must do everything we can.\par \par \uc2 There are also people in the electorate who say, you know, this is a whole lot of money. We keep sending to Ukraine and their needs here in America, and those people are getting, you know, more and more skittish as the war was dragging on, and they kept having new, newer kind of expenditures for the war. So, to turn that corner, and to start to see that -- those expenditures pay off, I think is really good for voters. It is reassuring, but we, you know, Biden will continue to keep coming back to this either the Ukrainians will continue to make progress, and he`ll want to brag about it. Or, you know, the danger that still lurks out there is that people do not believe that Putin is going to take a loss well. And that he may become more desperate and do something really rash. And then then it puts Biden in another precarious situation, because what does the U.S. do in that circumstance?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yeah. Reed, the stakes of growing for those House January 6 Committee members to finish his investigation. Axios reports, the committee plans to hold at least one more hearing late this month and release early findings and recommendations before the midterm elections. The panel claims the scheduling plans are not political. Are they trying to engineer in October surprise to influence the midterms, Reed?\par \par \uc2 [17:40:14]\par \par \uc2 GALEN: In October (technical difficulty).\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Your sound is very choppy. I cannot hear, Reed. Let me pose the same question to you the, Charles.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Well, listen, there`s no way to take politics out of the January 6 investigation and there`s a political body that is investigating. The Republicans chose not to put, you know, a lot of members on it. There`s two Republicans on the committee. Most of these are Democrats. No way to take that out, right? So, and if Republicans retake the House, they got to scrap the Committee immediately. So yes, the Democrats on the committee are trying to rush to get their work done. And they want to make, you know, their work matter. And the way to make that work matter most is to underscore how much of a threat to democracy to Republicans had been. And I think that that is going to be what they`re going to be focused on.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, Charles Blow, Reed Galen, sorry about the sound of you, Reed, but thank you both for being on.\par \par \uc2 After the break, college enrollment is down overall, but it`s a whole different story when it comes to HBCU. I`ll tell you about it, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:45:04]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The effects of the pandemic on higher education are still being felt, as many universities have reported historically low enrollment numbers. Overall, college enrollment down nearly 10% in the last two years, but there is one exception to the drop off. Historically Black Colleges and Universities seeing record applications boost as high as 60% for some schools, still the enrollment surge has not necessarily translated into enough federal and state resources to keep pace with needs. Just one area, Black Colleges continue to struggle compared to their predominantly white counterparts.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is \uc2 Lodriguez Murray\uc2 , Senior Vice President of Government and Public Policy for the \uc2 United Negro College Fund\uc2 . Mr. Murray, we appreciate you joining us tonight. Let me start off with some numbers. According to the National Center for Education Statistics, the percentage of black students enrolled at HBCUs fell from 18% in 1976, to 8% in 2014, increasing to 9% and 2020. Then largely in the last two years, many black colleges have reported record applications and dominance and enrollment numbers. What are driving -- what are the driving factors that you`d attribute to the surge while enrollment is down overall nationwide, is surging at HBCUs?\par \par \uc2 LODRIGUEZ MURRAY\uc2 , SENIOR VICE PRESIDENT, PUBLIC POLICY AND GOVERNMENT AFFAIRS, UNITED NEGRO COLLEGE FUND: First of all, Reverend Al, thanks for having me on this evening. I think we you see is a continuum of issues. You can start back in the summer of racial reckoning, which was happening during a pandemic, which was really a twin demic for African Americans. It was not just the health issues that were going on with Coronavirus. But in addition to that, you also saw African Americans moved in a way and shown to the country in a way that has never been shown because of the killings, the murders of George Floyd as well as Breonna Taylor, and so many other names that we know.\par \par \uc2 And you find that black students want to find their home, they won`t be (technical difficulty), just be number but they will be a real person. And also, it`s an environment that is set just for them. And so HBCU enrollment is on the move, but there`s so many other issues that still have to be solved to make sure that the students that are choosing historically black colleges and universities will find the home that best suits them.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Mr. Murray, a black kid starting college today, or would have been a toddler when \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  became our first black president. They would have been teenagers watching the protests moment that followed the murder of George Floyd, what, if any impact would you say those events have had on enrollment this summer?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Huge. The presidency of \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , and then you add to it the Vice Presidency of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , you add to it sitting U.S. senators, like we have in the state of Georgia with \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . And you see African Americans seeing themselves in these very public figures. And they`re not just seeing themselves but they`re seeing themselves and people who went to HBCUs that are completely tearing down the overarching stereotype that you have to go to an Ivy to get to where you want to go.\par \par \uc2 You can go to the institution that`s best suited for you getting education that allows you to be comfortable in those formative years, and then allows you to be a world changer. I tell people all the time, I don`t know a lot of people with national holidays named after them. But I happen to have gone to a college where one of my fellow alumnus hits. He`s got one of those.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I assume you`re talking about Morehouse College and Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Yes, indeed.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yeah, I`ll be with you guys, next month. You know, HBCUs have been historically underfunded compared to predominantly white institutions. But along with the surge in enrollment over the last few years, there has been a surge in charitable giving, the likes of which many black colleges have never seen. According to WeAreHBCUs, a nonprofit group that tracks donations to black colleges, about a third of the nation`s 107 historically black colleges received their largest gifts ever between July 2020 and December 2021. The trend that schools are of course trying to continue as best they can 2022.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:12]\par \par \uc2 But that, of course, is private funding, Mr. Murray. In terms of federal funding, how are black colleges doing under the Biden administration, in your view? We`re out of time, but I want you to respond to that briefly.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: I`ll tell you that President Biden is probably the best president ever for HBCUs. Most presidents and their budgets that they send to Congress, usually flat line the amount of money that HBCUs got the previous year, and they recommend that same number the Congress the next year. This is the first president in my lifetime, that recommends increases every year for HBCUs. He`s put HBCUs in every single one of his must pass pieces of legislation.\par \par \uc2 Congress may have had other difficulties with passing it. But the fact that the President put HBCUs right in the middle of the American Rescue Plan, he put HBCUs right in the middle of his infrastructure plan. When he introduced it, he put it right in the middle of everything that he`s been doing, including Build Back Better, which turned into the Inflation Reduction Act. This shows a president that views these institutions not as a fringe issue, but it`s something central to the American ideal.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you \uc2 Lodriguez Murray\uc2  for being with us this evening. More POLITICSNATION ahead. But first, I want to remind you about a brand-new op-ed I`ve written on Msnbc.com. It`s on Phillip Pannell, a New Jersey teenager killed by police in 1990. And how his death helped begin the movement against police violence. And tonight, Watch Model America by MSNBC films. This documentary goes deeper into Pannell`s case and discusses race relations in New Jersey at that time.\par \par \uc2 The four-part series begins at 10 p.m. Eastern tonight on MSNBC and is streaming on Peacock. I`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:55:47]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As we had the first all day sessions summit on hate crimes and violence would firm commitments from President Biden and members of his administration on Thursday we must continue. We must continue to follow up and make sure that promises made are promises kept. And voters come out to stand with that.\par \par \uc2 I was also heartened to see on the very next day President Biden met with the wife of Brittney Griner and the sister of Paul Whelan -- of Paul Whelan, who are both being unjustly held in Russian jails. And we`ve been raising that since earlier this year. And I heard it today as I marched and walked through Harlem for the historic African American Day Parade. It is back in the streets, 1000s came out and all the way up Adam Craig Powell Boulevard, people were saying stay on umbrella now. And let`s bring Brittany home as well. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 5 p.m. Eastern. "AMERICAN VOICES" with Alicia Menendez starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220919ei9i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for September 17, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Gigi Stone-Woods \par \uc2 7,066 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:18]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening, and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: Divided we stand united.\par \par \uc2 Right now, I`m still reflecting on our President admitting that the greatest terror threat we face is from within, and it stems from our enduring division around race.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We need to say clearly, unfortunately, White supremacy, all forms of hate, fueled by violence have no place in America.\par \par \uc2 Fair to call it out as complicity.\par \par \uc2 There are those who say we bring this up, we just divide the country. Bring it up, we silence it, instead of remaining silent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I left this week`s White House Summit on hate crimes as head of National Action Network, thinking about the spike in hate-driven violence from the far-right over the last six years.\par \par \uc2 I appreciated that President Biden was candid about that threat, but I couldn`t help but think, this week, especially about how comfortable even mainstream Republicans have become with cruelty as a political tactic.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the two prominent GOP Governors who thought it decent to leave migrants and asylum seekers stranded to score political points, there was a MAGA Republican, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , announcing his bill for a national abortion ban, just three months after the reversal of Roe and its effects on millions of women.\par \par \uc2 And then there was the party`s leader \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , again, suggesting an indictment for his handling of classified documents in the Mar-a-Lago investigation would result in violence, as the January 6th probe continues to unpack the violence of that day, and the former President`s role in it.\par \par \uc2 Still, the reality is none of this matters if Republican voters aren`t turned off in November, and Democrats must still turn out.\par \par \uc2 And so the President and his party have seven weeks left to convince Americans to choose their platform of health, while the other side continues to choose harm.\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight on POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Congresswoman \uc2 Bonnie Watson Coleman\uc2 , Democrat of New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, first of all, thank you for joining us. And before we get into a packed news week, I have to start with the White House Summit on extremism that I and other four other Civil Rights leaders pushed for, after several hate-based attacks in El Paso, at various synagogues, across the nation, and of course, the racist massacre in Buffalo this summer. That`s where I was when I called them and said we need to push for this Summit. And some of those families joined me at the Summit.\par \par \uc2 I want to play a bit more of what President Biden had to say about the current domestic terror threat.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Our own Intelligence agencies in the United States of America have determined that domestic terrorism rooted in White supremacy is the greatest terrorist threat to our homeland today.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been around a while, I never thought I`d hear that or say that.\par \par \uc2 Enough.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congresswoman, we both know that seven of the participants in the insurrection were active and retired military, we know from federal law enforcement that White supremacy and extremist groups have been actively recruiting from the military.\par \par \uc2 Now, you as a member of House Homeland Security, how worried about -- how worried are you about this threat? And do you anticipate Republicans taking this particular threat seriously, should they regain the House or the Senate or both chambers of Congress? Will they take this seriously?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BONNIE WATSON COLEMAN\uc2  (D-NJ): Thank you for having me, Reverend.\par \par \uc2 You and I both know that we`ve been concerned about hate in this country emanating from those within our country -- White supremacists, antiracist and all those kinds of folks for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:10]\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: So I want to thank God that our President is giving voice to the seriousness of this moment, and the urgency with which we need to address it, put resources behind it, and try to heal our communities.\par \par \uc2 You ask a very significant question about Republicans. No, I do not think that they care one iota about this. I think that they are very much engaged in fomenting this divisiveness and this hatefulness that`s taking place in our country right now, and should they gain control of either House or both Houses, I think that we would see that there would be even greater encouragement of the kind of this disarray and hateful actions against gay people, black people, Latinos, immigrants, Asian-Americans, everybody that isn`t White and principally White and male.\par \par \uc2 I think that our democracy is in peril here, right now, and that Republicans are okay with that and that is a very dangerous place for us to be as individuals and as a country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Congresswoman, I`m still struck, but not surprised about the cruelty in the name of politics that we saw this week. Two Republican governors seeking reelection -- Florida`s \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Greg Abbott of Texas -- transported dozens of migrants from their respective states to Washington and Massachusetts, Martha`s Vineyard in the case of DeSantis, who says he intends to escalate the relocations and may ultimately collaborate with Abbott.\par \par \uc2 You sit on the House Homeland Security, where does the agency come down on this?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Well, I think the agency recognizes the threat of what`s happening with both our -- with immigration and what these two Governors are doing. You call it transporting, some people refer to it as trafficking.\par \par \uc2 We need to see our agencies, our Federal agencies to look at it for what it is and to use the resources to ensure that these two Governors who have so little regard for human life, who has so little regard for the rule of law to be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 Thank God, these immigrants were greeted warmly by those in at least Martha`s Vineyard, who want to do something on their behalf and to help protect them.\par \par \uc2 They didn`t know where they were going, Reverend, and they were misled as to where they were going and what was going to happen. What is that? That`s trafficking of a human being.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And it is the same thing segregationists did to Blacks in the South, there was once a trip brought to Massachusetts telling them they were going to meet with President John Kennedy, supposedly dumping on Massachusetts about integration, and it has not been lost on me that DeSantis and Abbott, send the migrants to cities that have Black mayors -- Washington, Chicago and New York, and Martha`s Vineyard, which is known for where a lot of successful Blacks including \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , have homes.\par \par \uc2 But let me go to another subject, though Congresswoman. Senator Lindsey Graham`s bill introduced this week for a national abortion ban is reported to be a non-starter even amongst Senate Republicans. But still it has been cast as both a midterm tactic, and for Graham, a Republican effort to rally his base around the abortion issue ahead of November.\par \par \uc2 Now, as Democrats have since the reversal of Roe had to deal with this, with barely seven weeks left before the midterms, whose base is more energized because of that decision of Roe by the Supreme Court and its aftermath?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: I think Reverend, what we`re seeing is that more women have registered to vote than men. I think we`ve seen more activism for those who want to protect and preserve the rights of women to be able to access the health care they need, with regard to reproduction or anything.\par \par \uc2 I think that it has to energized Democrats, Independents and reasonable Republicans to recognize that this is a hateful, hurtful, dangerous precedent that Republicans are supporting. Even though this may be an nonstarter, it doesn`t -- you wonder what is \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  thinking? Who are they listening to? Because I don`t believe that they are paying attention to the polling and the surveying and the outreach that is taking place right now, even just on this issue of a woman`s right, and on agency over her own body.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:22]\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: So, I used to think he had a modicum of decency and rationale about him, but something is really happening with \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  because he has taken a nosedive into the swamp that the President left behind, when he moved on to Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 But you know what, here`s the deal? We`ve got seven weeks, we talked about that. We have a responsibility to connect with as many people as we possibly can because the contrast between Democrats trying to protect people, Democrats doing policy and protection over politics and Democrats who pass legislation, the President who has signed legislation, that`s all about lowering costs, making life better for individual working families, creating jobs, from the Rescue Plan to the Infrastructure Plan to the recently signed Inflation Reduction Plan.\par \par \uc2 The contrast between Democrats and Republican is just so monumental that I cannot believe that the majority in this country would want to support going back to where we would be if Republicans were elected and placed in the majority.\par \par \uc2 Our, I think, challenge is to get that message out. I think that the January 6 Committee, the way it handled its investigation, and its hearings has helped tremendously in having people understand that democracy is in peril here, that we have an assault upon the rule of law here and that individual rights, collective rights, and just autonomy, and the responsibility of the government is supposed to be of by, and for the people. All of that is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes. That is what is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: And so our challenge is to get that word out to the young, to the old, and the in between.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Thank you for being with us tonight, Congresswoman \uc2 Bonnie Watson Coleman\uc2  of the Homeland Security Committee in the \uc2 US Congress\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the first African-American elected statewide -- in a statewide office in the State of Nevada. He serves as the Attorney General since 2019. He is now up for reelection this November.\par \par \uc2 Aaron Ford, thank you for joining me today.\par \par \uc2 Standby for one moment, before we continue. We want to show you live pitches out of England, where Air Force One has just landed with President Biden and the First Lady. They are in the United Kingdom to attend Queen Elizabeth II`s funeral on Monday. The BBC is reporting about 500 Heads of States will be present at the Queen`s funeral.\par \par \uc2 All right, Attorney General \uc2 Ford\uc2 , let me come back to you. This November, there will be a staggering amount of 2020 election deniers on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-seven different states will have someone running for statewide office who believes the 2020 election was stolen, according to a States United Action Report.\par \par \uc2 Because of this, the role of a State Attorney General and Secretaries of State feel more significant than ever before. What does this wave of election deniers running for office say about the current state of our democracy?\par \par \uc2 AARON FORD (D), NEVADA ATTORNEY GENERAL: Reverend Sharpton, thank you so much for having me on. It is a pleasure and delight, but you know, you`ve raised a very important issue, and as the Congresswoman talked about, the fact is democracy is at stake. I have spoken to several folks around our State, and frankly, as the co-chair of the Democratic Attorneys General Association, I`ve spoken to colleagues around the country.\par \par \uc2 And at the top of the mind of a lot of folks is the democracy that we -- that we so love and we cherish. And so when you have election deniers running for and not only running for, but winning in primaries in some important states in all of our States, many of our States, it is important to note that we`re going to have to have people who are willing to stand up against those individuals when they bring forth what are sure to be unfounded allegations of voter fraud, and widespread voter fraud that undermines the integrity of our elections.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen it before, they have had a test run in 2020. They are gearing up for `24 and we can`t let them it. We can`t let them have it.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now throughout your career, you`ve made ending gun violence a top priority. You`ve advocated for banning ghost guns, expanding background checks and preventing domestic abusers from owning firearms.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:11]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You also supported a Red Flag Gun Law in Nevada that passed, but it hasn`t been used as frequently as similar Red Flag Laws in other States.\par \par \uc2 And while crime has decreased under your tenure, the murder rate in Nevada rose 27 percent from 2019 to 2020. Why aren`t Red Flag Laws working at the scale they were intended to in Nevada? And what specific actions do you plan to take against gun violence if reelected?\par \par \uc2 FORD: Well, thank you so much for bringing up that issue. It`s a very important issue. You`re right, in our State, I have been at the forefront of trying to ensure that we enhance public safety by cracking down on gun violence.\par \par \uc2 You`re right that crime has decreased under my tenure, but the particular type of violent crimes that relates to guns has in fact increased and we have a gun problem in our state, and not many will disagree with that fact.\par \par \uc2 And so what we`ve endeavored to do is to pass things like Red Flag Laws, which to your point, have not been used as frequently as possible. And so what we intend to do about that is to actually coordinate and cooperate with our legislative branch and with the Governor, we`re already talking about a public information campaign of sorts to remind people of the existence of this Red Flag Law, which by the way, those on the other side of the aisle have sued to stop the Red Flag Law from being utilized.\par \par \uc2 My office has had to defend that lawsuit, it is still in effect, and we will continue to educate the public around when it is appropriate to be used, and let them know that it is a tool that can help cut down on gun violence.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, when it comes to Criminal Justice Reform, your Republican opponent has accused you of being soft on crime social justice reforms, referencing the 2019 Justice Reform Bill you supported that aims to reduce the prison population in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 But with the passage of this comprehensive reform bill, your State has seen a decrease in overall crime, and an astounding 40 percent decrease in robberies.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to critics like your political opponent who refute your approach to Criminal Justice Reform?\par \par \uc2 FORD: I say what you just said, the proof is in the pudding. The truth of the matter is, when I went to Law School, and hopefully when she went to Law School, we learned that there are several purposes to the Criminal Justice System.\par \par \uc2 They include to be sure, punishment and isolation, but they also include rehabilitation and restoration.\par \par \uc2 And as Attorney General, my job is to focus on all four of those things, and you know, we`re really good at the first two, not so good at the last two and when you try to focus on the last two, they want to use pejorative terms, like soft on crime. But the truth is, when you employ a holistic approach to the criminal justice system, you have positive results as you`ve been able to rattle off here in our State.\par \par \uc2 I will continue to do my job, as the top enforcement officer in the State irrespective of have ridiculous connotations of being soft on crime, because again, the proof is in the pudding and those numbers demonstrate that our approach is working.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: We`re out of time, but I must ask you this, get a brief answer from you.\par \par \uc2 Across the country, we are seeing an impressive number of Black candidates run for office in 2022, even states without a long history of electing African-Americans as the first African-American elected to the State that they`re running in. You, being the first elected -- Black elected officials statewide in Nevada, how does your perspective and background inform your priorities and decisions as the Attorney General for the State of Nevada?\par \par \uc2 FORD: Listen, Reverend, right after George Floyd was murdered, I went to the television air and I said, listen, before I became Attorney General, I was a Black man. I`m a black man, married to a Black woman raising three Black sons and a Black nephew. And when I`m done being Attorney General, I will be a Black man.\par \par \uc2 And so that absolutely informs the approach that I take, recognizing that I have some experiences that hadn`t otherwise been represented in this particular office.\par \par \uc2 And so for example, when you have an opponent running against me, who has said she wants to see me hanging in front of an effing crane, that too plays a huge part in the way that I approach this, recognizing that I represent a struggle that has not yet been fulfilled. We will continue to work toward ensuring that we have great representation and all that we do.\par \par \uc2 I`m proud and honored to be the Attorney General for Nevada, and I`m proud to represent a background that has not been represented before.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Nevada Attorney General, Aaron Ford, thank you very much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, an attack that Republicans are now using to slow down democracy up next, in this week`s Got-cha.\par \par \uc2 And later, the Governor of Mississippi takes jabs at Jackson`s water crisis as other GOP leaders play political games with migrants seeking safety.\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague Gigi Stone-Woods with today`s top news story -- Gigi.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:10]\par \par \uc2 GIGI STONE-WOODS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Thanks, Rev.\par \par \uc2 Here are some of the stories we are watching at this hour: Queen Elizabeth eight grandchildren stood guard beside her coffin in Westminster Hall today. Brothers William and Harry were among them dressed in uniform.\par \par \uc2 Earlier \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  and Prince William`s surprised mourners waiting in line to see the Queen lying in state. Civilians have been waiting up to 16 hours to pay their respects.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian officials said on Friday that they had found hundreds of bodies buried in territory recaptured from Russian forces in what President Zelenskyy called proof of war crimes by the Kremlin.\par \par \uc2 The mass burial site was found in Izium, a city between the border of Kharkiv and the Donetsk regions. It became an important hub for the invading military during five months of Russian occupation.\par \par \uc2 Puerto Rico is bracing for a possible hurricane as Tropical Storm Fiona approaches. It is expected to dump as much as a foot of rain on the island tonight, which could result in severe flooding and landslides.\par \par \uc2 Fiona is expected to cross over to the Dominican Republic on Monday.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  after the break.\par \par \uc2 I am Gigi Stone-Woods. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:25:25]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: On Monday, the midterms election will be just 50 days away, and the stakes could not be higher. Nearly 60 percent of Americans will have an election denier on their ballot, according to the polling website FiveThirtyEight.com.\par \par \uc2 In 27 States, election deniers will be running for Governor, Attorney General, or Secretary of State, all positions that are responsible for ensuring the integrity of future elections.\par \par \uc2 We must turn out big to defeat these candidates, but that may not be enough. Already, there are signs far-right Republicans aren`t just questioning past elections, they are already disputing this year`s races as well.\par \par \uc2 Former Alaska Governor \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  narrowly lost a Special Election bid for Congress in a ranked choice race, all the candidates agreed upon ahead of time. Now, she is calling that system potentially fraught with fraud.\par \par \uc2 Arkansas Republican Senator Tom Cotton went even further claiming without evidence rank choice is "scam" to rig elections.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reports Trump backers are flooding election offices with requests for 2020 election data. According to Elections Administrators, the requests are so numerous, frivolous, and repetitive that one Republican clerk told "The Post" they were designed to deliberately break the system.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen these tactics before and we know exactly where they lead. Trump`s refusal to concede the election in 2020 led to the violent insurrection on January 6th, as well as to the wave of voter suppression laws passed by Republican lawmakers in States across the nation.\par \par \uc2 These election deniers have had two years to present real evidence of widespread voter fraud, and have failed to do so.\par \par \uc2 Many call themselves patriots, and yet show little concern for the political turmoil they`ve needlessly created in the country they claim to love.\par \par \uc2 Our democracy has never been perfect, and yet it has grown stronger over time, weaving together a nation of unparalleled diversity and prosperity.\par \par \uc2 Those of us who love this country seek to preserve, protect, and perfect the nation will never give in to those who deny it and seek to burn it to the ground.\par \par \uc2 I got-cha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:32:14]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to my political panel for their insights on today`s biggest topic.\par \par \uc2 Joining me is Adrienne Elrod, Democratic strategist and former senior aide on the Biden Harris campaign; and former Republican Representative, Joe Walsh of Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Let me go to you first, Joe. Let`s start with the crisis Republican Governors are actively trying to make worse.\par \par \uc2 Texas Governor Greg Abbott sent a busload of 50 Venezuelan migrants from Texas to DC today, dumping them outside the Vice President`s residence. Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has been sending two dozen migrants to Martha`s Vineyard, where they`re being housed at a military base in Cape Cod, where they are being fed and cared for.\par \par \uc2 This tactic of human beings as political props is nothing new. As the JFK Library Foundation pointed out in a tweet recently, Southern White leaders in the 60s organized so-called Reverse Freedom Rides, giving Black people one way tickets to the north with false promises of jobs, housing, and better lives that was done to an effort to humiliate Black people and embarrass northern liberals.\par \par \uc2 Beyond politics, these are real people, many of them children. How do we put an end to what`s going on right now and start treating these migrants with the dignity that all of us deserve?\par \par \uc2 JOE WALSH, FORMER US REPRESENTATIVE: First off, Rev, these are cruel political stunts. And let`s be clear, especially what DeSantis did. I mean, he lied to 50 people to get them on a plane, he sabotaged their asylum seeking process by flying them off to another jurisdiction.\par \par \uc2 Look, the truth is our immigration system is messed up. Our asylum system is messed up. Our border states have borne the brunt of this broken immigration system, but to do what DeSantis did and what Abbott is doing, Rev, that`s not contributing to any sort of solution.\par \par \uc2 Look, the Republicans are trying to change the conversation. They believe this issue helps them if they can get people talking about immigration, they think it will benefit them for the midterms.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Adrienne, in about an hour \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be speaking at a rally in Youngstown, Ohio, where he is stumping for Republicans Senate candidate, JD Vance.\par \par \uc2 The race is surprisingly close between Vance and Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [17:35:08]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: A new Emerson College and Hill poll has the two candidates virtually tied among Ohio voters.\par \par \uc2 Now this week, comments made by Vance on a podcast earlier this year, earlier this year resurfaced. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JD VANCE (R), OHIO SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Let`s say Roe versus Wade is overruled. Ohio bans abortion, you know, in 2022, let`s say in 2024. And then, you know, every day \uc2 George Soros\uc2  sends a 747 to Columbus to load up disproportionately Black women to get them to go have abortions in California. And of course, the left will celebrate this as a victory for diversity.\par \par \uc2 If that happens, do you need some Federal response to prevent it from happening? Because it`s really creepy. And you know, I`m pretty sympathetic to that, actually.\par \par \uc2 So hopefully, we get to a point where Ohio bans abortion in California and the Soroses of the world respect it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, hearing that, aside from the casual racism and antisemitism, how do you respond in this week, where we`ve seen Republican leaders actually fly migrants to liberal cities?\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE ELROD, POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: Yes. I mean, Rev, you cannot square it because they simply don`t make any sense. I mean, the party of "personal freedom" is only the party of personal freedom when it applies to men, not when it applies to women`s reproductive health.\par \par \uc2 And seeing what they`re doing to these migrants, I completely agree with Joe. They are trying to deflect from the fact that they are not performing as well as they thought they would in the midterms. They are trying to get the topic off of abortion access, and away from that. A lot of candidates don`t want to talk about it. They`re erasing their previous positions and being pro-life, pro-life, pro-life on their websites.\par \par \uc2 But look, I mean, this is why \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , one of the many reasons frankly, why Tim Bryan, who is a good personal friend of mine is doing so well in this race. This is a race that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- a State that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won handedly in 2022, but \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  is pulling ahead of JD Vance in most polls right now.\par \par \uc2 I think the race is going to continue to be tight. I think this is going to be a very close race. But JD Vance is so out of touch with voters in Ohio and especially those key suburban women who will ultimately decide who wins that Senate race.\par \par \uc2 And they don`t want to talk about abortion. I doubt JD Vance is out there stumping on that topic talking about it. I would not be surprised if it doesn`t come up during the rally, because they know that this is not a winning issue for them. It is backfiring on Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And I also think what DeSantis and Abbott are doing by flying, you know migrants who are trying to escape communism, who are trying to do what`s best for their families, flying them to other States, it just screams a nasty gross political stunt, and I think those key Independent voters and moderate voters who will decide the midterm see it as that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Joe, for weeks, Jackson, Mississippi residents have been dealing with a water crisis after catastrophic flooding knocked out the city`s aging water treatment facility. A day after the State`s Republican Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  declared victory in helping to restore the water supply, he was cracking jokes about the situation. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES (R-MS): I`ve got to tell you, it is a great day to be in Harrisburg. It`s also as always a great day to not be in Jackson.\par \par \uc2 So, like, I should take off my emergency management director hat and leave it in the car and take off my public works director hat and leave it in the car.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, this situation was no laughing matter for tens of thousands of residents who have had to deal with the water that smells like raw sewage coming out of their faucets and showers.\par \par \uc2 Health officials are still telling pregnant women and children to stick to bottled water. What do you make of the Governor`s heartless comments?\par \par \uc2 WALSH: Well, what a cruel thing to say. The Governor of a State saying that about his capital city. Look, here`s the deal. They`ve all learned, every member of my former political party has learned from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that cruelty works and divisiveness works and ugliness works.\par \par \uc2 It worked for Trump et al, sadly, all these Republican candidates up and down the line, they are emulating him. Cruelty is the thing, and look, it is just one more reason why I believe my former party is such a danger right now to our Republic.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Adrienne, new developments in the legal showdown over the classified documents seized from Trump`s Mar-a-Lago residence. Last night, the Department of Justice filed a motion with the 11th Circuit Court of Appeals for a partial stay pending appeal, asking for investigators to be granted access to the classified records, which a Judge blocked their access pending an examination of the documents by a Special Master.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:22]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Meanwhile, new reporting from "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reveals months before National Archives officials retrieved classified documents and 15 boxes from Mar-a-Lago, they were told none of the material were sensitive or classified, and that Trump had only 12 boxes of news clippings.\par \par \uc2 The former President`s delaying tactics are all too familiar, but are they also a real threat to national security when we`re talking about classified materials?\par \par \uc2 ELROD: Yes, of course, Reverend. Again, I mean, as you know, I worked on Hillary Clinton`s campaign both in 2008 and 2016. The hypocrisy coming from the Republicans, the hypocrisy coming from Trump`s team in terms of the very minor circumstance that had to do with Hillary Clinton`s e-mails versus \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  having boxes and boxes and boxes of classified information at his residence in Mar-a-Lago illegally is stark.\par \par \uc2 But look, I think Trump`s attorneys have lost almost all of their credibility. There was also a really good piece in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " today from Maggie Haberman talking about how the attorneys are also going through their own legal jeopardies, because of situations and things that they have said to try to defend \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  under oath. So, they are getting themselves in trouble.\par \par \uc2 It is just a big giant mess. I do think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is trying to run out the clock. You know, he is trying to do whatever he can to avoid indictment, but this just screams a hot mess.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I think this time he is in really, really deep trouble, and it is just a matter of time until we see that play out.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Adrienne Elrod and Joe Walsh, thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, more than 30 years after his death at the hands of law enforcement, Phillip Pannell`s life story is being told in a brand new documentary and his family will join me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:45:53]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Thirty-two years ago, the supposedly progressive community of Teaneck, New Jersey was rocked by the death of Phillip Pannell, a Black teen shot and killed by a White Teaneck Police officer who claimed Pannell was reaching for gun after a foot chase.\par \par \uc2 Witnesses and forensics later suggested otherwise, finding that Pannell had been shot in the back, his hands raised at the time of the shooting. Regardless, the officer who fired the fatal shot was found not guilty of manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 It was in the wake of Pannell`s killing that I would go on to found the National Action Network organization. Three decades later, I`m proud to say that MSNBC Films has revisited the incident and the movement it sparked in its four-part series, "Model America" premiering tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 And joining me now the family of Phillip Pannell, his mother Thelma Pannell-Dantzler and his sister, Natacha Parnell, Vice President of the Phillip Pannell Foundation.\par \par \uc2 Thelma and Natacha, thank you for being with us tonight. I might add in the building, I didn`t know he was coming, Bishop Herbert Daughtry, one of the leaders 31 years ago and still remains that.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you this, as there is one thing I`d like to drive home before we begin, and that is that Teaneck was supposed to be this progressive beacon in New Jersey, the first American town to voluntarily desegregate its schools through busing in 1966. It was known for its efforts during integration to incorporate its growing Black community into local government and School Board. Yet, the town exploded along racial lines after your son`s killing and your brother`s killing.\par \par \uc2 So, I`d like to start with a clip from the series, one that I think sets up just how different Teaneck was supposed to be, and then come back to both of you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: This was middle class model city America.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A great place to raise a family in a very diverse environment.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The fact of the matter is, Phillip Pannell was executed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We live in Teaneck. This doesn`t happen in Teaneck.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There was no reason for that cop to shoot him in his back.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting chanting "He had a gun.")\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And he was going to shoot me. That`s the truth.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My 16-year-old brother was slain 30 years ago in Teaneck, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My shooting will never go away. It`s with me every day of my life.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That was my child. We endured this pain for 30 years and we`re back here again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Thelma, let me ask you first. This was your son, and I remember 32 years ago, one of the most difficult things to do was even get your talk in the media. You were in so much pain.\par \par \uc2 But now that this story is coming out, and there has been so many police shootings, I think the difference here is this was in suburban America. This is where Blacks and Whites had congratulated each other, coming together.\par \par \uc2 But when your son was shot in the bank with his hands up, they couldn`t deal with it. They didn`t even want to prosecute the police.\par \par \uc2 How do you feel watching this and preparing for the premiere of this documentary?\par \par \uc2 THELMA PANNELL-DANTZLER, MOTHER OF PHILLIP PANNELL: Well, I never thought that this will come to life. So now, since this is out and the truth how came out, my soul is at my heart. It doesn`t ache like it used to ache because we always knew that my son had his hands up, but to prove it to the way the world is now, that is huge to me.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:06]\par \par \uc2 PANNELL-DANTZLER: So, God bless you all these years, and I`m one of the mothers to see the truth come out about my son. There`s a lot of mothers that didn`t even get justice for their son, the weeping mothers. I feel for them, I really feel for those mothers,\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Natacha, you have been a driving force for justice for your brother, and stood by your mother all these years, founded the foundation trying to help others. How do you feel now that the nation is going to see what really happened 32 years ago?\par \par \uc2 NATACHA PANNELL, PHILLIP PANNELL`S SISTER: I feel grateful that like my mother said that I`m here to live it. You know, there have been many other mothers and fathers and sisters and brothers who did not see justice, whereas they died of broken hearts, you know.\par \par \uc2 So, I`m really thankful that I was able to keep up the good fight, and continue to, you know, fight for the truth. You know, because it`s about my -- it`s not just about my brother at this moment, it is about our future, you know.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You know, Natacha, you watched both the murder of George Floyd in the last two years, national conversations around policing reform. Are you heartened by it all, despite the lack of tangible reform on a Federal level?\par \par \uc2 PANNELL: I definitely am. I mean, I was surprised that they did not pass the George Floyd Bill on a State level, you know, and it had to go to a Federal level.\par \par \uc2 You know, I stand by like the end of qualified immunity. Also the fact that it should be independent prosecutors appointed for these type of killings that police are doing, and then also for the Community Civilian Review Boards that have subpoena power. Those are important to me.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You know, Thelma, two years ago, on what would have been Phillip`s 47th birthday, a memorial tree was planted in a local park that Phillip played in, contained with the printed program was an ad from the Teaneck Police Department.\par \par \uc2 I bring that up, because I wonder whether the family gets the sense that the department and the city have learned anything from that day in 1990. Are your thoughts, if any about that, you or Natacha?\par \par \uc2 PANNELL-DANTZLER: I think some of them have gone -- have -- how can I say it -- that they have learned that there was racial tension in Teaneck all the time. We were separated.\par \par \uc2 But me as a mother, I worked a lot. So, my children used to come home and tell me different things, but they love Teaneck. They never had this problem until that day. I never thought that Teaneck was like this. I never dreamed of it because I love Teaneck.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, let me say again, the courage you`ve shown, and the consistency to fight over three decades to finally get this out. And the courage that both of you have shown, and I know when myself and other activists came out there, Bishop Daughtry who I want to have on, we were ridiculed, but you stood strong, and your father stood strong.\par \par \uc2 PANNELL: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And I want to give him credit. He was tenacious, and I`m glad that it is going to come to light. Now, people can watch it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Thelma Pannell-Dantzler and Natacha Pannell.\par \par \uc2 You can read more about Phillip Pannell`s case and its impact on the movement in my op-ed on msnbc.com right now, and then Sunday, watch the "Model America" four-part series that begins at 10:00 PM Eastern on MSNBC and is streaming on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 I will be right back with more POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:57:35]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: This week as President of National Action Network, I participated in a series of things in Washington, DC, fighting for Civil Rights and women`s right to choose.\par \par \uc2 First we had a meeting with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  with reproductive rights leaders and Civil Rights leaders joining together. I joined virtually with others to talk about fighting against these abortion bans in States across the country that rob women of their civil right to choose over their own body.\par \par \uc2 And then the next day, we had the White House Summit. I was heartened when I walked in the White House to be able to see the new portrait of President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and of First Lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  hanging in the White House. My seeing it for the first time.\par \par \uc2 And then the Summit, the first All-Day Summit, President Biden greeting me as I was on the front row as one of the five leaders they called for and Ambassador Susan Rice and her staff really working in having a program with some of the victims and some of the unifiers working together.\par \par \uc2 But more important or as important, the Attorney General of the United States announced new efforts and funding them around dealing with hate crimes, the Secretary of Homeland Security, Mayorkas announcing new drives and funding it.\par \par \uc2 And of course, the President saying in his budget he wants the Congress to pass, they are going after these groups that thrive on violence based on hate.\par \par \uc2 We came up with this when I was about to do the eulogy in Buffalo, of a grandmother, a Black grandmother who was one of 10 people killed because she was Black, but we wanted to show it was not only Blacks in buffalo, but it was Asians, it was Latinos in El Paso and Atlanta and LGBTQ. It was Jews killed in synagogues in Pittsburgh and Southern California.\par \par \uc2 We must all stand together. We cannot act as though hate is only for your silo. We must fight against hate everywhere and for everyone.\par \par \uc2 That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 "American Voices" with Alicia Menendez starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:04 ]\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ: Thank you so much, as always, Reverend Sharpton.\par \par \uc2 Hello everyone, I`m Alicia Menendez. We`ll kick things off this Saturday with the GOP`s political theater using migrants as pawns in their hope to own the libs.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY 