ieve Barr forced you out of that job at SDNY because he no doubt believed that by removing me he could eliminate a threat to Trump`s reelection.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Just when you thought it couldn`t be any worse than it looked.\par \par \uc2 GEOFFREY BERMAN\uc2 , FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY, SDNY: We were very close to inviting that case around the time I got fired. And Barr knew about the case.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, Andrew Weissmann on the stunning revelations from the SDNY prosecutor fired by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then, what we`re learning from newly unsealed portions of the Mar-a-Lago affidavit, and the Republicans unveiled their plan to ban abortion nationwide.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I thought it`d be nice to introduce a bill to define who we are.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Senator \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2  on the instant overwhelming backlash to Lindsey Graham`s plan.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Give me a chance to vote on this bill.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Well, it was as bad as it looked. In fact, it was even worse. I`ve had a lot of occasions to say that in the Trump years of American life, but this new example of Donald Trump`s misconduct in office is about as clear as it gets. It comes directly from the former United States Attorney for the Southern District of New York, arguably the most powerful and active district in the entire country.\par \par \uc2 For two and a half years, that man you see there, \uc2 Geoffrey Berman\uc2 , served in that role as the head of that district during the Trump administration. Berman has written a new book out today in which he details several instances when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  abused the power of the Department of Justice. As Berman told Rachel Maddow last night, Trump and his DOJ repeatedly tried to use the Southern District to protect his allies and punish his enemies.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: I wanted people to understand the full scope of the outrageous and improper political interference by Trump`s Justice Department in the cases of the Southern District of New York that demonstrates what Trump is capable of and what he`s likely to do. And it also provides a frontline view of just how vulnerable our justice system is.\par \par \uc2 Trump turned the department into his own personal law firm. He put in people who would do his bidding. And they would, you know, target Trump`s political enemies and assist Trump`s friends. And it was a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We should note, as Rachel did last night, Berman did raise some alarms at the time. It wasn`t all just kept tight-lipped until the book came out. And of course, this kind of behavior is no surprise from the foreign president. In fact, targeting political enemies was one of the central promises, the central theme, arguably, of Donald Trump`s 2016 campaign.\par \par \uc2 Remember how often he called for his opponent, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , to be locked up. Even replaced his first Attorney General Jeff Sessions after Sessions failed to lock the right people up and Trump routinely berated him for doing so on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Jeffrey Berman stories from his time in the Southern District make it clear that behind the scenes, it was even worse than that.\par \par \uc2 The ex-president`s second attorney general, Bill Barr, and other senior officials in the Department of Justice would actually call up Berman`s office and explicitly tell them who they did or did not want the Southern District to prosecute. Berman recalls one instance in September 2018, two months before the Midterm Elections, when a DOJ official called his deputy. As a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  describes it, "After citing the recent prosecution of two Trump -- prominent Trump loyalists, the official said the office which had been investigating Gregory Craig, a powerful Democratic lawyer, should charge him and do so by election day."\par \par \uc2 According to Berman, the official told his deputy, "It`s time for you guys to even things out." And Berman`s office declined to prosecute Craig. Get this. The department sent the investigation to federal prosecutors in Washington where Craig was indicted, tried on a single count in making false statements, was acquitted by a jury in less than five hours.\par \par \uc2 Berman also details what happened when Bill Barr tried to push him out. Earlier today he told my colleague Nicolle Wallace about his strategy to get Barr to back down.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: At the very end of my tenure, there was a situation where, you know, Barr was going to impose an outsider who He trusted in charge of the Southern District of New York. And at that point, I went public. I was noisy. You know, I don`t think there`s precedent.\par \par \uc2 NICOLLE WALLACE, MSNBC HOST: You played some killer, killer off his politics.\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: I don`t think he`s a precedent in the Department of Justice for what I did. I sent out a press release. And I told the entire country what Barr was trying to do, how he had crossed the line. And I use the language from the obstruction of justice statute. And it was because of that --\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Intentionally.\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: Very intentionally. And it was because of that very noisy exit that Barr back down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, in the end, Barr did fire Berman, though Berman was able to put his trusted deputy in charge. And as he told Rachel Maddow last night, the reason for what was exact -- that was exactly what it looked like. Berman was a threat to Trump and his agenda.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: How was your work as U.S. Attorney a threat to Trump`s reelection?\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: Well, at the time I was fired, the Southern District of New York was working on a couple of politically sensitive cases. One of those cases is the \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  We Build The Wall case. And we were very close to indicting that case around the time I got fired, and Barr knew about the case. And as you know, that case was indicted by the Southern District of New York by \uc2 Audrey Strauss\uc2  who took over as Acting U.S. Attorney after I was fired, and she brought that prosecution.\par \par \uc2 And then, you know, President Trump pardoned \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , which was an outrageous pardon. But that`s one of the cases that we were investigating and we were very close to indicting. And the other case was a Ukraine investigations arising out of the \uc2 Lev Parnas\uc2  and \uc2 Igor Fruman\uc2  indictments. And that was something that we had been investigating for quite a while and then we continued to investigate for quite a while. And both of them were very sensitive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, as Berman mentioned, the case against \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  has become notorious. After Berman was fired, federal investigation against Bannon continued. In August of 2020, U.S. Postal Inspectors arrested Bannon onboard a Chinese billionaires yacht in the Long Island Sound. He was charged with fraud and then subsequently pardoned by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in Trump`s final hours in office. That`s after January 6 and after all Bannon did to promote the coup.\par \par \uc2 That pardoned whoever does not preclude Bannon from facing state charges. And just last week, he was charged by the Manhattan district attorney with essentially the same crimes related to his alleged scheme to defraud donors to his We Build The Wall Organization. And it looks like the prosecutors in New York have much of the same evidence against Bannon, the Department of Justice had accrued.\par \par \uc2 Now, the other investigation Berman cites as being particularly threatening to Donald Trump`s reelection is the one around the ex-president`s attempt to pressure Ukraine into investigating his then-opponent \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . You might remember those two names Berman mentioned, \uc2 Lev Parnas\uc2  and \uc2 Igor Fruman\uc2 . They were associates of Trump`s personal lawyer \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  who kind of ran errands for Giuliani. They helped Giuliani and his pressure campaign with Ukrainian officials.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 Lev Parnas\uc2 , they took their orders from Giuliani. In a 2020 interview, Parnas told Rachel Maddow he did not do anything without the consent of Giuliani or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Now, Lev and Igor have both been sentenced to prison time for their roles in that scheme, but Giuliani is walking around free. We know he was also under investigation by the Southern District of New York as of 2019.\par \par \uc2 In April of last year, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  searched his home and office, seized his phones and computers. Two months later, a federal judge appointed a special watchdog to review that seized material and recommended where prosecutors can view. Still, nothing has come of this investigation. Just last month, the Times reporting Giuliani is unlikely to face charges. But Giuliani, like \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , seems incapable of keeping himself out of legal trouble.\par \par \uc2 While he may have avoided charges in that Ukraine scheme, he is now a target in the criminal investigation to election interference in Georgia where he actively attempted to overturn the election. There`s every reason to believe the current January 6 federal investigation which is growing larger and larger by the day may end up on Giuliani`s doorstep as well given how intimately involved he was with helping \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  plan and execute the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, here`s the thing. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  were to be reelected, none of this would matter. That`s the explicit promise of another Trump term. There will be more Bill Barrs at the Department of Justice, probably even more craven figures than Barr, and more Rudy Giuliani`s and more \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , and there will be fewer Geoffrey Bermans standing up to do the right thing. They will be replaced by MAGA authoritarian lackeys willing to convert the government into a ruthless weapon for Trump`s personal use and power. And there will be no one standing in his way. That is also the explicit warning for people like Berman who has now joined the legions of those who have left the circle of Trump`s influence to warn in the loudest, clearest voice possible that it is exactly as bad as it looks.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:32]\par \par \uc2 Andrew Weissmann spent years working in the Department of Justice, most recently served as a lead prosecutor for Robert Mueller`s Special Counsel Investigation. And he joins me now. Andrew, first let`s just start with some level setting. For folks that don`t know the way the Department of Justice works, the way that a given district works, in the U.S. Attorney`s Office works. The idea of a senior Justice Department official calling up the office of a district and saying prosecute this person, don`t prosecute that person, do it by Election Day, have you ever heard of such a thing?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, FORMER LEAD PROSECUTOR, \uc2 ROBERT MUELLER\uc2  SPECIAL COUNSEL INVESTIGATION: Well, the do it by Election Day, that`s outrageous. That is, of course, a no. There are some investigations that are so sensitive or so important that Main Justice does get involved. I`ve been a prosecutor in the field in New York, and also at Main Justice, and of course, you`re in the field, you`d never want to hear from Main Justice.\par \par \uc2 But you know, having been in Main Justice, there is a reason for them to be there. And sometimes you really do want to make sure that the field office is complying with appropriate policies. And you know, it can -- you can play it out in a different setting where they`re acting -- where the field is acting outrageously and you want Main Justice to be saying, you know what, that`s not consistent with our policy. It`s not consistent with what we`ve done with other people facing similar crimes.\par \par \uc2 So, there is a give and take. There is a natural friction between Main Justice and the field. What Jeff Berman describes, however, is not that, just to be clear, saying that you`re going to open investigation to \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  based on the Logan Act, which is not prosecuted. And by the way, the Republicans were the ones who went -- were flipping out when they thought that the Obama administration was looking at the Logan Act for \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2 , which they weren`t.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, he`s describing something that is really not a question of Main Justice getting involved, it`s that Main Justice getting involved in a political way.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, what struck me was the \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  and Greg Craig examples, right. So, we had -- we had pretty good examples of -- and here, we now have tangible evidence, right, of using the Department of Justice to put pressure to drop increase into people that are allies. But the more -- the even more dangerous power, right? Like, we see this in Turkey. We see it in Russia, obviously. Like, the prosecutorial power is one of the ultimate powers the state wields. And if it`s wielded essentially to go after dissidents or people that you don`t like, politically, then you`re not really living in a free society anymore. And so, to call up and be like, go after Greg Craig, puts you on the edge of something that`s pretty dark.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: That is the fundamental truth here and the main point of books like Geoff Berman`s and many other people talking about how this is an unwritten rule at the Department of Justice because the Department of Justice is part of the executive branch. So, a president has the power to dictate what they can and cannot do. And at times, if it`s a general policy, like we should prosecute drugs more, we should prosecute drugs less, that`s fine.\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to saying we`re going to prosecute Democrats, but not Republicans, that actually is antithetical to living in a country where there`s a rule of law. And that is a norm. It is not written into the Constitution. And as you pointed out, Jeff Sessions, of all people who was so loyal to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he was asked precisely because he adhered to that norm when Bill Barr was willing to throw it to the wind.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s an example here, which I thought was a small one but illustrative about the \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  charging document. And it was -- it was changed to remove references the idea that Trump acted in concert with and coordinate with Cohen to make illegal campaign contributions, even though it was very, very clear that there was pressure from above to do precisely that.\par \par \uc2 What do you think about -- when you think about a just -- Department of Justice in a Trump second term, how do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: I think I`ve said this before. I think of that, that I don`t have enough alcohol in my apartment to deal with that because there clearly would not be a rule of law. I mean, the -- you know, basically, he`s going to pardon whoever he wants to pardon, he can ask them to commit crimes for him, and you could have people who as you said are like Bill Barr but even worse. I mean, he was thinking of installing \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2  as a special counsel.\par \par \uc2 So, the people he surrounded himself, he emulates, are an absolute nightmare to a working fundamental democracy. So, it`s really unfathomable to people in the department and outside of the department, and those are Republicans and Democrats who I think all share that view if you believe in a rule of law.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:44]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. \uc2 Geoffrey Berman\uc2 , we should say, is a lifelong Republican. He calls himself that in his book. Andrew Weissmann, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: You`re welcome. Coming up, a flurry of activity on multiple investigative fronts as it relates to the ex-president. The January 6 Committee is now teasing a new hearing and we`ll share more evidence with the DOJ. Also, what we`re learning tonight from a newly unsealed portion of that Mar-a-Lago affidavit. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We are seeing a flurry of activity on two fronts surrounding the ex- President. First, the investigation into classified documents stolen from the White House. On that front today, a federal judge unredacted additional portions in the Mar-a-Lago search affidavit. From that document, we learned a Trump lawyer was told that all the White House records were located in the storage room and he "was not advised that there were any records in any private office space or other location at Mar-a-Lago. That suggested someone, quite possibly \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , lied to said lawyer.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, on the January 6 investigation, yesterday, we learned the DOJ has escalated its investigation into the coup plotters issuing 40 subpoenas to Trump aides, seizing phones belonged to top advisors. Today, the chairman of the January 6 Committee, Congressman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  of Mississippi, told reporters that the panel is looking at possibly resuming hearings on September 28, that they are looking into cooperating more with the DOJ.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BENNIE THOMPSON\uc2  (D-MS): I think now that the Department of Justice is being proactive in issuing subpoenas and other things, I think it`s time for the committee to determine whether or not the information we`ve gathered can be beneficial to the investigation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, what does all this mean for these two investigations? Joining me now is Asha Rangappa, former FBI Special Agent, senior lecturer at \uc2 Yale University\uc2 . And Renato Mariotti, former federal prosecutor and legal affairs columnist with Politico.\par \par \uc2 Asha, let me start with you. There`s been some back-and-forth filings about the special master, about asking a Judge Cannon to review or change order that essentially stopped federal law enforcement from investigating pending the outcome of the special master. And then there was that redaction, that unredaction today of a portion of the affidavit Do you think there`s any significant we learned in that -- in that unredaction?\par \par \uc2 ASHA RANGAPPA, FORMER FBI SPECIAL AGENT: Oh, yes. There were several little tidbits that we learned from the additional redactions. We learned, for example, that the certification that was made to the Department of Justice by the lawyers, that everything had been returned in response to the subpoena, that the lawyers had been -- had "been advised" in passive voice, not clear by whom, that everything had been returned. And this starts to put -- to point the finger at Trump as being the one making these representations to his lawyers.\par \par \uc2 We learned that the Department of Justice obtained surveillance video going back as far as January of 2022 after they had served the subpoena and gone and come back, so they were able to see what was happening in that area in the six months leading up to going to Mar-a-Lago to retrieve these documents.\par \par \uc2 And finally, there`s one little bit were the classified documents that were returned in response to the subpoena, that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent who signs the affidavit says these were represented as having been kept in the storage room. And this is in the section where there is redacted probable cause that classified documents were found elsewhere. So this suggests to me that they were lied to even on that, and that the classified documents that they recovered on that trip were actually kept somewhere else. I suspect that they learned that from watching the surveillance video.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right, right. And that`s part of what obviously ends up leading to the search warrant itself. And we know that from both the sort of application and some of the subsequent reporting. There`s going to be a decision, Renato, on whether Judge Cannon will reconsider her initial order, which people found really surprising, some said unprecedented, some even called it, you know, lawless.\par \par \uc2 Basically, DOJ asking her to back down from some of that, said there were five of the special master but unfreeze our investigative abilities allow us to retain these 100 classified documents. What are the stakes here for her decision depending on which way she goes?\par \par \uc2 RENATO MARIOTTI, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Well, I will say, you know, her -- I think questionable is a good way of putting it or kind way of putting it. I think that if she reverses that, it`ll allow the Justice Department to move forward with the investigation, not impede the review of those records to determine the threats to our national security.\par \par \uc2 And I have to say, DOJ has really put her in a tough position because if she doesn`t do that, they really zeroed in on the parts of her opinion that are the least defensible, the easiest to overturn on an appeal. I think they`ve set themselves up well for an appeal. Som I think she is definitely going to be hard-pressed not to reverse herself or walk this back particularly because Trump`s team in this filing all but, you know, refuse to make any assertion regarding his supposed unredaction of these documents.\par \par \uc2 You know, they dance around that but the DOJ I think in the filing today make crystal clear what wasn`t said there. And I think that what wasn`t said in Trump`s filings speaks volumes.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:49]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let`s talk about the January 6, investigation committee hearing. You know, Asha, there`s been obvious tension between the Department of Justice and the investigation of the Committee. This has been publicly stated, sometimes. It`s been kind of implied and tacit. I`ve never quite understood what the tension was, but I did feel like we got a big clue from Benny Thompson today.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Thompson comes out and says, now that they seem like they`re doing their job, maybe we`ll now share some information with them. It was a pretty striking thing for him to say. And it connects to the fact that we`ve actually learned that in some cases, the DOJ has subpoenaed information that witnesses have already provided to the committee as opposed to getting it directly from the committee. How important is it for them to get on the same page?\par \par \uc2 RANGAPPA: I think this is a huge development. We`ve talked before, Chris, of this show that the Congress -- the Congressional Committee and DOJ have different purposes, and goals and timelines with regard to investigations. And that if they`re not really in coordination, they can even work at cross purposes. We`ve seen this before, for example, with Iran Contra.\par \par \uc2 But I think if they work in tandem, they can actually sort of become the legal wonder twins. You know, they can really leverage some of the benefits of both institutions. And I think one thing here is that Department of Justice is coming late to the game, quite frankly.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, the January 6 Committee has done a ton of investigation, and they have covered a lot of ground, especially at a time when the Department of Justice may be having a manpower issue just in terms of addressing all of the aspects of the January 6 investigation from the thousands of, you know, ground level minions that they`ve had to prosecute to all of these different threads, fundraising, the violence, the false electoral scheme, having so much of this groundwork done by the House Committee and having that shared, and working in a way that they won`t compromise each other and using Department of Justice is powerful tools. Because remember, people can`t stonewall a grand jury subpoena or get very far with things like executive privilege with Department of Justice like they can with Congress. So, I think this is a huge turning point.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And finally, to you, Renato, on the sort of flurry of activity that we`ve had in the last week. You know, there`s this DOJ policy around politically sensitive investigations and the traditional 60 days. I think there`s a widespread expectation that things will kind of similar down and go dark to the election, and then maybe something will pop back up after then. Is that is that sort of your anticipation of what we`ll see and what the timeline is now?\par \par \uc2 MARIOTTI: I think that`s right, Chris. I don`t think we`re going to see any big, overt investigative steps until after the election. But boy, as you alluded to a moment ago, we saw a very big step recently in the reporting of not only that flurry of subpoenas, but I think the seizure of two phones is really important, because that means a judge signed a warrant indicating there was probable cause that evidence of a crime existed on those phones.\par \par \uc2 So, really, it suggests to me that the investigation of, for example, the fake electoral scheme is in high gear. And, you know, kudos to Congressman Thompson and others regarding getting that prompting DOJ to move that forward.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it does look like that has been the causal mechanism here, at least in the timeline that`s how it looks. Asha Rangappa, Renato Mariotti, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 MARIOTTI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, more January 6 rioters are convicted for the roles and the attack and the ongoing threat from the far right, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: One of the most violent days in the United States for service members after 9/11 was January 6. We know that about 140 officers, 73 from the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2 , 65 from the Metropolitan Police Department in Washington were injured. One of those individuals was D.C. Metropolitan Police Officer \uc2 Michael Fanone\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On January 6, he was stationed in the Capitol tunnel where some of the most violent attacks occurred. He was then kidnapped by the rioters, dragged to the ground, and beaten and even tased. Body camera footage from the incident documented the entire disturbing attack.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Come on, MPD. Push.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Your guys -- fight the right people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Push.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`ve got you, Mike. That`s my hand. You`re good.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Push them back.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL FANON, FORMER POLICE OFFICER: Guys, don`t do this. I have kids.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:35:10]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Officer Fanone suffered a heart attack and barely made it out of the riot alive. He sustained significant injuries including taser burns at the back of his neck, as you can see in this court filing. The man who used the taser on Fanone, a guy named Danny Rodriguez, was in court this afternoon for procedural hearing. He was indicted last year on eight counts, including inflicting bodily injury on an officer with a dangerous weapon. He actually confessed to police last year in a video that has since been made public.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANNY RODRIGUEZ, CAPITOL RIOTER: What do you want me to tell you, that I tased him? Yes. Explain. Explain. Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Explain what were your thoughts at the Capitol when you tasered Officer Fanone or when he went into the Capitol building?\par \par \uc2 RODRIGUEZ: I thought that we were going to save this. I thought we were going to do something. I thought that it was not going to end -- happen like that. I thought that Trump was the state president, and they`re going to find all this crooked stuff. And we`re going to get -- I mean, we found out that -- we thought that we did something good.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Also in court today, three additional insurrectionists who attacked officers at the same tunnel where Fanone was assaulted. It include a man named David Mehaffie, who was known to internet sleuth as the tunnel commander. Today, he was convicted for his role in the attack on the Capitol during -- directing armed insurrectionists into the tunnel where much of the worst violence occurred. We`ve seen a bunch of it. There were two other men who were convicted on their most serious charges as well.\par \par \uc2 There are hundreds of other stories like these, violence and bloodshed from Donald Trump`s failed coup attempt. Coming up next, a war correspondent, someone who was there in war zones, one of the people who was also there on the ground on January 6 capturing it all.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Some of the most harrowing frontline footage we got from the January 6 insurrection came from a reporter named Luke Mogelson. Mogelson is a correspondent who made his name covering the war on terror. He`s covered conflicts in places like Afghanistan, Syria, Iraq, and Liberia. But on January 6, he was at the Capitol, a kind of warzone of its own.\par \par \uc2 He embedded with the Trump mob, following them into the capitol where he captured images like the ones you are seeing, providing one of the most comprehensive views of the scene as the violence broke out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Defend your country. Defend your constitution.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: 1776.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You`re afraid of Antifa? Well, guess what, America showed up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What Mogelson chronicle that day fed into a larger thesis about the rise of extremism and the fraying of America`s social fabric. Luke Mogelson has documented that growing threat of extremism in a new book titled The Storm Is Here: An American Crucible. And he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Luke, this is a great book and you`re a heck of a reporter. Some of that footage you captured on January 6 is some of the most indelible. What`s sort of prompted you to make the move from covering war zones to covering the far right and political turmoil here in the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 LUKE MOGELSON, CONTRIBUTING WRITER, THE NEW YORKER: Well, it was early in the pandemic in April when right-wing militias in Michigan occupied the State House in Lansing with rifles and equipped in body armor and helmets and accosted lawmakers. When I saw that footage and images from that day, I bought a plane ticket for Michigan.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You write in the book about there was a kind of similarity, familiarity a little bit to those images you saw from some of the places you had covered, places in which essentially civil peace is broken down, in which trust and social norms have been replaced with essentially like the use of force. How much did you see on what you chronicled connect to some of the things you did see in warzones?\par \par \uc2 MOGELSON: Well, with January 6, specifically, it was a bit different because it was hand-to-hand combat, which is something else from shooting war. But it was mob violence, and that`s a very particular kind of violence, which I have witnessed overseas in Iraq, I saw a mob drag somebody out of a house and kill him once. And I was actually struck on January 6 by how similar the energy was to that day in Iraq in the sense that there was a -- there was a palpable performative quality to the violence, which is to say that the participants seem to be perpetrating the violence as much for each other and for themselves.\par \par \uc2 As for their victims, it was a kind of -- it was a way of demonstrating their dominance and assuring one another that they were capable of pulling such a thing off, and that they weren`t victims because that`s another similarity. I think that you can`t doubt the sincerity of their experience of victimhood. Even if it`s based on lies and propaganda, they truly feel that they`re oppressed and persecuted. And so, this kind of lashing out, even if it accomplishes no practical objectives, I think is -- was for them immensely just psychologically and emotionally gratifying.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:51]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it`s really -- that`s very well said. I want to play actually that footage. You`re showing it as B roll but I want to actually play it which brings some of this to bear which is the -- there`s a moment in which the crowd that you`re recording and sort of embedded with starts attacking basically a bunch of camera equipment, AP camera equipment, and sort of celebrating their own strength and virility as they do so. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There you go. You won`t report the media. Now, you can`t.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You met a guy with some alcohol.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hey, everybody. CNN is hiding out behind Union Station.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Their headquarter is over there.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don`t know where that is though. I`m not from here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Start making a list. Put all those names down and we start hunting them down one by one.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Traitors get the guillotine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You`re right about this sort of this sincerely felt victim -- victimization, even if it`s based on lies, but the actual sort of visceral, emotional, subjective experience of these folks is actual -- they feel humiliated, right? They feel -- they feel powerless, they feel victimized. How do you understand diffusing that? Like what is the tool that draws that that toxin out or that takes away that sense, which in other contexts often does lead people to violence or to all sorts of extreme action?\par \par \uc2 MOGELSON: Well, I think it`s very much tied up with fear. They`ve been provided all of these fantastical antagonists and villains and convinced of their reality. And so, I think a lot of the rage actually is symptomatic of fear. And honestly, I don`t know how you address that. You know, the purveyors of those conspiracy theories and nativist aetiologies have been incredibly successful at inculcating that fear in their followers and in trading the situation in which, you know, half the country is terrified of the other.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Luke Mogelson whose new book about this movement and moment in American life is out today. He`s an incredibly exceptional, exceptional reporter. It`s a fantastic book, The Storm Is Here: And American Crucible. Check it out. Luke Mogelson, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 MOGELSON: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  rolls out a plan for a national abortion ban, but people on his own side are not that happy. Headaches all around for Republican candidates next\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today, South Carolina Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  proposed what would be the first-ever nationwide abortion ban. It would drive a stake for the heart of the last vestiges of Roe v. Wade and federalize the anti-abortion right-wing politics of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: We should have a law at the federal level that would say after 15 weeks, no abortion on demand, except in cases of rape, incest, to save the life of the mother. And that should be where America`s at. I look forward to the debate. I look forward to the vote. If we take back the House in the Senate, I can assure you we`ll have a vote on our bill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This bill, if passed, would make the same type of abortion ban that was actually at issue in Dobbs v. Jackson women`s health. That`s a Mississippi law that was at issue in that -- in that case. That`s the case that the court used to get rid of federal abortion rights. It would make that Mississippi law effectively the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 Keep in mind, up until now, Republicans including \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  had been insisting they wanted Roe overturned because the right to an abortion or abortion restrictions should be left up to the individual states.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: The point I`m trying to make is I`ve been consistent. I think state should decide the issue of marriage and state should decide the issue of abortion. I have respect for South Carolina. South Carolina voters here I trust to define marriage and to deal with the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:55:02]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: But apparently doesn`t have trust for I don`t know the voters of California or Kansas or Minnesota or New York because now Graham is reversing course. It`s easy to see why. Republicans are facing some pretty bad polling on abortion. According to a recent survey from the Wall Street Journal, 60 percent of voters think abortion should be legal in all or most cases. That`s up from 55 percent in March.\par \par \uc2 That same survey showed the ruling in Dobbs made more than half of voters more motivated to vote in Midterm Elections. And you see that`s mostly weighted towards Democrats and Independents. That`s why \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is trying to energize his own party`s anti-abortion grassroots while attempting to tip the debate back towards what he thinks is more favorable terrain.\par \par \uc2 But make no mistake. He is introducing what would be an unprecedented federal abortion ban. If you`re living in Michigan or Pennsylvania states that currently have access to abortion, this ban would come for you, same for California, New York, New Jersey, Connecticut. It means no matter what state you live in, abortion is absolutely on the ballot for November.\par \par \uc2 For more on the Republican plan to ban abortion, I`m joined by Senator Amy Klobuchar Democrat from Minnesota. She serves on the Judiciary Committee, and she joins me now. I suspect I know where you are on this kind of legislation, but I will ask you if you think this kind of thing is a good idea and whether it would get a single Democratic vote.\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-MN): It would not. And I want to make clear for your viewers here. It is actually abortion ban immediately in many of the states because it is a 15-week ban in states that haven`t put anything in place except to follow the Roe v Wade precedent. In the other states, 12 of them don`t even have exceptions for rape or incest. That would remain, Chris.\par \par \uc2 So, basically, he`s allowing the states that have already banned abortion right away to have that stay in place. And then all the other states like Minnesota, which is an island along with Illinois in the Midwest, they would have to follow his federal ban which is at 15 weeks. And I mean, it is -- so, it is much worse than it sounds just when you say 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and it wouldn`t -- obviously, yes. So, it wouldn`t pre-empt -- it wouldn`t preempt those state laws that are far more restrictive. What do you think about the political reaction that we`ve seen? I mean, it was really fascinating to me today. Graham comes out. He wants to make a bigger show of this. \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  says, I`m really thinking about inflation in the railroad strike and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  says I don`t really want to talk about it. And everyone comes running away from it almost as soon as it comes out and gets mad for anyone covering it. What does that say to you?\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Well, a lot of people want to distance themselves from it. That`s for sure. But I think it`s really important to know that difference even for those candidates out there that say, oh, I wouldn`t support what \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is saying on the Republican side. The question of them is, would they put Roe v Wade into law? Would they correct the Supreme Court reversal of 50 years of precedent to simply say, women should be able to have the freedom to make their own decision about their health care and not have Ted Cruz in the waiting room, and not have politicians make the decision?\par \par \uc2 That`s what the candidates have to answer because no matter what they think of Senator Graham`s proposal, none of them that`s far as I know, on the Republican side and running for Senate here, they are all extreme when it comes to abortion. And they`ve been very clear in order to get the endorsement of their party that they actually would not allow for the Roe v. Wade protections for women`s health.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I know, in your state, obviously, abortion is at the center -- abortion rights at the center of the gubernatorial race in that -- in that state, your state, which I think is fair to say, a pro-choice state in its sort of public opinion and its laws at the moment. I mean, one of the things I think that Graham illustrates is that when you combine this with the recent petition to have the 14th Amendment find a fetal personhood, right, constitutional right to life for, you know, a fetus that obviously the end goal here is total nationwide abortion ban of the movement that brought us to this point. It seems crazy for anyone to think otherwise, unless you think I`m wrong.\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: No, you`re right. That is exactly -- \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  himself has said that, that that`s where they`re headed. That`s what they would do if they took over the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  and the House. And people should make no mistake about that. And that is why you saw the voters of Kansas turn out in droves, 500,000 of them, more than the total it even voted at all in the last Midterm Election, to be able to uphold women`s right to make their own health care decisions.\par \par \uc2 It`s why you see those races in Alaska and in Hudson Valley in New York. You saw the results there, Chris. And that is that people are voting that we didn`t expect to vote actually before. And then you add to that the celebration we had at the White House today the fact that Democrats have the backs of the American people. And that means bringing down the cost of prescription drugs, something I`ve been working on since I got to the Senate leading the bill for negotiation.\par \par \uc2 We finally took on the pharmaceutical big pharma and said you`ve got to be able to allow for a negotiation or to an investment in climate change or the name of the bill, the Inflation Reduction Act, brought down the deficit by $305 billion. Those are all things where Democrats are actually making very clear whether it`s for the burn pits for our veterans, we have peoples back.\par \par \uc2 So, you contrast that with their extreme, and you contrast that with our awesome. I was just with Mandela Barnes, our candidate in Wisconsin. We have some incredible candidates all across the country.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Senator, thank you so much. Sen. \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2 , we appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Tuesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220914ei9d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for September 10, 2022 MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Matt Bradley, Richard Lui, Susan del Percio, Dana  Milbank \par \uc2 7,824 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:00]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Right now, while England is mourning the late Queen Elizabeth, much of the rest of the world reflecting on what her passing represents.\par \par \uc2 Here in America, another potential sea change is looming, a midterm election taking place, amid the growing concern that our democracy is legitimately at stake, because one segment of a political party is wedded to the idea that one man should remain above the law. And that man \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appears to have done as much as possible while President to ensure he would remain legally untouchable even once out of office, and his actions as President are now impacting the federal investigation into classified materials seized from Mar-a-Lago last month.\par \par \uc2 A Trump-appointed Florida Judge, ruling in favor this week of his legal team`s request for an independent Special Master to review just what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  retrieved. Now, that relationship between the Judge and MAGA is under a spotlight while the Justice Department prepares to appeal the decision.\par \par \uc2 Of course, we`ll be looking at all of that, along with the investigation`s impact on midterms less than eight weeks away. That`s tonight on POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Plus, as memorials for Queen Elizabeth continue in England this weekend, I will talk to a Black British civil rights activists about what the Queen`s death means for Black countries that gained independence from Britain while she was on the throne.\par \par \uc2 What is her legacy to the Black diaspora? And what does the future of Britain literally look like? I`ll ask that shortly.\par \par \uc2 But first, let`s go to London, England for a quick update on the latest funeral arrangements following the death of the Queen. NBC`s Matt Bradley is standing by. Matt, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY, MSNBC FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Oh, good evening, Reverend.\par \par \uc2 I mean, what we heard just in the past couple of hours, the kind of the sketches of this funeral, kind of coming into a little bit of a clearer perspective. We understand now that it has been confirmed that the funeral for the Queen won`t be this Monday, but will be the following Monday.\par \par \uc2 So, we`ve got well more than a week of pomp and circumstance that is going to be stretching from the very north of the country from Scotland in Balmoral, where the Queen died, all the way down to where I am here in London and it will include including with \uc2 King Charles III\uc2 , the newly minted King, Charles, all of the four constituent countries of the United Kingdom.\par \par \uc2 So, while we see, we watch as Britain`s mourn the Queen, and this, again, all of this pageantry has been called by the \uc2 British government\uc2 , has been planned for years, it has been called Operation London Bridge.\par \par \uc2 In parallel, there`s Operation Spring Tide, and that is to welcome the new King and he is going to be traveling again to all of those constituent countries -- Scotland, Wales, England, and Northern Ireland -- to try to essentially meet all of his new subjects and to draw all of these countries in a little bit closer.\par \par \uc2 It is a really interesting moment, and I hope that you`re going to cover with your next guest, talking about the Queen`s colonial legacy or the monarch`s colonial legacy.\par \par \uc2 We also have these countries that are closer to home, and there are worries among many in the political class here in Britain that the death of the Queen will ignite the spirit of republicanism that`s been dormant for the last couple of years, really the last couple of decades, both in Scotland, and in Wales, and Northern Ireland, where we`re seeing a lot of issues, and also in places like Australia and Canada. We just saw just in the past couple of months, Barbados, shedding their allegiance to the Queen.\par \par \uc2 So, this is something that really could ripple out from here in Britain, from London, where I am, throughout the entire world, across all of the Commonwealth.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, a lot of this pomp and circumstance, a lot of the pageantry that we`re seeing in the next week or two, a lot of it is going to be aimed at making sure that those Commonwealth countries and those countries within the United Kingdom that they remember, they recall why they need the Queen.\par \par \uc2 Now, whether or not they actually do need the Queen, that`s going to be up to voters and people who are, you know, a lot of these things are going to be coming to a referendum particularly in Scotland, where there is a lot of talk about staging yet another referendum on whether Scots want to remain within the United Kingdom.\par \par \uc2 All of this is going to be coming to a head as \uc2 King Charles III\uc2 , who is popular, but in the past has gone through some very, very serious periods where he was not popular with the public. This is going to be a major reckoning for the monarchy after 70 years, the longest serving monarch ever in the history of Britain has finally died -- Reverend.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:16]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Thank you to NBC`s Matt Bradley in London.\par \par \uc2 Back to domestic politics, joining me now is Congressman \uc2 Gregory Meeks\uc2 , Democrat of New York and Chair of the Foreign Affairs Committee.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thank you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 I want to start with the week`s developing -- the developments surrounding the classified materials removed by \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents from Donald Trump`s Mar-a- Lago estate last month.\par \par \uc2 The week began with the Florida Federal Judge appointed by President Trump issuing an order approving an independent Special Master to review the materials retrieved, effectively halting the investigation by both the Justice Department, Appeals, and the DOJ, and Trump camps have made their request for who that Special Master should be.\par \par \uc2 Then, of course, you have the Judge`s appointment by Trump two years ago. Critics of this week`s ruling scrutinizing that along her relative inexperience and conservative affiliations has played a role in her decision.\par \par \uc2 Still, for all the drama, it feels like a stalling tactic by the Trump camp is an indictment coming regardless of how long this Special Master review takes, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 GREGORY MEEKS\uc2  (D-NY): Well, I think that as the investigation continues that clearly that this grand jury should look at this case, an impartial grand jury. And if the facts come as I think that they will, then there should be an indictment.\par \par \uc2 I also think that no one is above the law. We talk about that all the time. And for the first time, we have a President of the United States, who has done things even worse than \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  has done, apparently, and has been one that has decided not to cooperate in the manner that one should.\par \par \uc2 It seems to me now that it is before the Judge, and I know that any other citizen, if there was any contact between that Judge and that defendant or potential defendant, that Judge would have to recuse themselves.\par \par \uc2 So it would seem to me that it would maybe be reasonable for a Judge who was appointed by the former President should recuse themselves from being on offense so that you can avoid even the impression of impropriety for the purity of justice in that regards.\par \par \uc2 So, not talking about a Special Master, which I think that then goes against what we`re looking for, but the very appointment of the President of the United States of a Judge, if you really want to avoid the appearance of any improprieties, then that Judge should sit down and an individual who was not appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should be the one that hears the case.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman, on Tuesday, I`ll be in Washington as head of the National Action Network for the White House United We Stand Summit on Hate. The 21st anniversary of the 9/11 terror attacks is tomorrow, and I`ll be thinking about that day on Tuesday, specifically, the war on terror that emerged and how Republican lawmakers justified surveillance of even peaceful Islamic groups because of a presumed terror threat.\par \par \uc2 Then, you fast forward to this past week, Congressman, and you have this report from the \uc2 Anti-Defamation League\uc2 , which found the names of hundreds of law enforcement, public officials, and military personnel in the leaked membership rolls of the far-right Oath Keepers group, which was of course at the center of the insurrection, and they of course, weren`t the only extremist group or hate group out there.\par \par \uc2 Still, you have the sitting Members of Congress defending the events of that day and their participants. Some of those lawmakers will turn around tomorrow and tweet about Homeland Security and protecting us from foreign threats.\par \par \uc2 Your thoughts, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: The biggest threat to us in America is internal. Clearly, when you look at what took place on January 6th, the biggest threat to our democracy is internal.\par \par \uc2 They did more damage to the United States Capitol, killing police officers, violating all types of rules and laws. So the biggest threat is from within and that is becoming very clear when you look at the Oath Keepers and what the non-defamation group has looked at and we`re looking at people who are being recruited, who are police officers or firefighters or, you know, former elected officials.\par \par \uc2 That is something that shows you the deep threat internally that has to be looked at and it is something that I thank you, Reverend Sharpton, and all the civil rights groups you have rung that alarm for a long period of time, and it is very significant and important for the President of the United States to be the one that is bringing people together, so that we can say that we who are living in America, you know, all of us are concerned about that internal threat that could tear this nation apart if it goes unchecked and unlooked at.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:30]\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: So, I think that it is very important and very significant that you be there, along with the President of the United States and other civil rights groups, and of people from across the board and police departments across the board, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and everyone looking at what`s taking place so that we can preserve our democracy right here in America.\par \par \uc2 When I travel, you know, and I do often now as Chair of the House Foreign Affairs Committee, you know, people are looking at what`s taking place here and they ask me, you know, "How can the United States who is supposed to be the leader of the free world allow the kind of thing that took place on January 6th, and the kind of recruitment that the Oath Keepers have been doing of law enforcement and others continue without being a strict investigation, arrest, and removal of those type of individuals from our society?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Finally, Congressman, is it your belief that Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  will be able to get Republican voters the Republican votes in the Senate he needs to pass legislation making same sex marriage Federal law, a vote he announced was pending this week.\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: But I think he surely should try. We need to call the roll. He should call the vote.\par \par \uc2 We passed the Protection of Marriage Act in the House. We had some 47 Republicans that did, a whole lot of other Republicans that did not, but they all should be on the record. Let`s see where they stand.\par \par \uc2 We heard what Justice Thomas said where he wants to rollback and for the first time, we`ve seen the Supreme Court talking about rolling back rights.\par \par \uc2 So, we need to reaffirm the fact that we want to protect marriage and protect, you know, for LGBTQ folks, as well as interracial marriage. So call the rolls.\par \par \uc2 I think it is a good thing that Senator Schumer will be bringing that vote to the floor of the Senate. We passed it in the House, let`s see what takes place in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Thank you, Congressman \uc2 Greg Meeks\uc2 , as always, for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is \uc2 Earl Butch Graves\uc2 , Jr., President and CEO of "Black Enterprise."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Graves, thank you for joining POLITICSNATION tonight, and I wanted to have you on because we`re just eight weeks to the midterms. What role can Black publications like "Black Enterprise" and independent Black newspapers play in driving turnout in key races like Georgia, like Pennsylvania, like Florida, and others?\par \par \uc2 EARL BUTCH GRAVES\uc2 , JR., PRESIDENT AND CEO "BLACK ENTERPRISE": Reverend Sharpton, thank you for inviting me to be a part of the show today. You know, Black media, I`m not going to call it Black newspapers or Black magazines, because really, it`s really about media as a whole, but Black media has to be out in putting articles and informing our people about the importance of voting and why it`s so important that because the lack of voting or what is taking place in places like Georgia, places like Texas, whether getting in the way and standing in the way potentially of people getting out to vote will have an impact for dozens of years, if in fact, we don`t come out in strong numbers.\par \par \uc2 And so we`ve seen what`s happened since 2020. Hopefully, there are people that are out that are getting behind \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  and others who are running for key positions across this country. But all of Black media, whether or not it`s "Black Enterprise," "Essence," it`s BET, anything that is talking to our respective communities, including the Latinx community has got to come out and provide impact and exposure to the importance of getting out to vote.\par \par \uc2 It`s not just about voting every four years, it is about voting every time we have an opportunity.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And Black media has a particular credibility with people that need that urging because they are coming from a position where they deal with the issues all the time.\par \par \uc2 Like for example, Mr. Graves watching what is happening in Jackson, Mississippi, about to go into its third week of a water crisis affecting more than 150,000 mostly Black residents.\par \par \uc2 Our National Action Network just joined Reverend Jamal Bryant and others in sending trailers, trucks for have bottled water down there. The crisis has obviously galvanized the civil rights community, but it is also an environmental issue, some say worsened by climate change, which is already disproportionately affecting Black communities.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:15]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I can`t help but think that the Black media of the future will have to deal with both of these issues, both racial and environmental justice, because the Black communities they serve will be forced to.\par \par \uc2 Your thoughts on that, Mr. Graves.\par \par \uc2 GRAVES: Yes. The environmental issues disproportionately impact Black and Brown communities, and so we can`t just focus on things having to do with police violence or other things that we deem to be important, not that they`re not important, but we have to understand that all of these things when they vote against having \uc2 EPA\uc2  segments and restraints on certain things having to do with pollution and others, the aftermath of that is what you see happening in Jackson, Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 This would not happen in a well-to-do White community, it just would not happen. They would have -- these things would have been dealt with some time ago, and this has been a problem. This problem didn`t just happen, Reverend, this problem has been in place and has been warned about for at least 10 years.\par \par \uc2 And now that has come out is, it is too late in the process and we`ve got to make it at the top of the list of what is important to us.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, finally, Mr. Graves, your father gave me permission to call you Butch years ago, we`ve talked about the crucial need for Black turnout in the midterm cycle and the impact Black voters can have on these high- profile statewide races.\par \par \uc2 But I know you`re a big proponent of Black political participation in local elections specifically to speed the recovery of Black business after the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 How are the two linked in your view?\par \par \uc2 GRAVES: Well, at the end of the day, we have to vote -- we have to vote for those people who are going to take care of us, and the reality is, is that that has not been oftentimes the case.\par \par \uc2 If in fact, we don`t have Black politicians in place, then we will not have anyone speaking up on behalf of us. It is like the need to have Black-owned media stay in place. Right? Black-owned media is the only ones who are going to lead with our issues, and having Black politicians to represent us in local races, in midterm races, we need to see a population of politicians that is representative of the population in general, and that`s not what we see.\par \par \uc2 We represent thirteen, fourteen percent of the population, Latinos represent thirteen, fourteen percent of the population, but yet we don`t have anywhere close to 27 or 28 percent of politicians that are voted upon positions.\par \par \uc2 So, it is imperative that we get out, that we vote for folks that look like us, that represent us, and Black-owned media and Black media in general will play a substantial and important role in making sure that happens.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: \uc2 Earl "Butch" Graves\uc2 , thank you for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`ll look at what the death of Queen Elizabeth II means to her Black subjects and former subjects, Lord Simon Woolley will join us to offer his unique perspective.\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague Richard Lui, with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, a very good Saturday to you. In fact, more on that story of the loss of the Queen.\par \par \uc2 The United Kingdom remaining focused on the passing of Queen Elizabeth II. \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  was officially announced as Britain`s monarch today. Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  and other party leaders also took an oath of allegiance to the new King. The Parliament met for a rare Saturday session.\par \par \uc2 And Prince William and Harry along with their wives Kate and Meghan, they met with mourners outside of Windsor Castle today. They also spent time as you see here to view tributes laid on the ground to the Queen.\par \par \uc2 The funeral, that`s Monday in Westminster Abbey, US President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will be there.\par \par \uc2 Also today, Russia`s Defense Ministry announced it will pull back troops from Kharkiv in Ukraine, its second largest city. Ukrainian soldiers have now recaptured nearly a thousand square miles of that city. This adds to other cities Ukraine`s military has regained control of in recent days.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:23:39]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to my political panel for their takes on today`s biggest stories. I am joined by Susan del Percio, Republican strategist and Dana Milbank, author and political columnist for "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for joining us.\par \par \uc2 I want to start by getting your thoughts about the death of Queen Elizabeth II. She was not explicitly a political figure, but met personally with 14 US Presidents going all the way back to \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2  and served as Head of State to America`s closest global ally.\par \par \uc2 What will her legacy be, especially from the perspective of American politics?\par \par \uc2 Briefly, Susan, you first and then you, Dana.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DEL PERCIO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: I think that she will be remembered in the United States as someone who was above it all, and when we look at our current political system, it shows that we really need someone who can guide our country and be a moral compass and be able to provide some stability and I think that that is what she represented.\par \par \uc2 The other thing was, looking at her meeting with those 14 Presidents. It was like a timeline. It was a look back at history, and I`m just happy that the last President she met with was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Your response, Dana?\par \par \uc2 [17:25:05]\par \par \uc2 DANA MILBANK, AUTHOR AND POLITICAL COLUMNIST FOR "THE WASHINGTON POST": Well, I agree very much with Susan. You see, in our system of government, our head of government is our Head of State, and we don`t have somebody who can just stand for the country as opposed to being mixed up in the day-to- day politics.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is a very good reason our founders did not want to establish a monarch, and I`m not a monarchist, but I absolutely agree that she shows that there can be somebody who can be identified with the State who is above politics, and that is something we really could use in this moment.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me go to another subject.\par \par \uc2 Susan, the DOJ and the Trump legal team are clashing over who should serve as the Special Master in the investigation of classified materials held by the former President, with both sides offering two names each for the role.\par \par \uc2 What qualities would you like to see, in this Special Master? What qualities would you feel the Special Master should possess?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: Although, it is likely that anyone who is appointed will, to as Special Master will have served as a Judge and be appointed by a previous President or State, I think that it`s very important that that person show that they have been independent in the past, that they`ve been nonpartisan in the past, that they`re above reproach, and also that they have worked with classified information, and that they are aware of the severity of what`s happening right now. So, it definitely has to be someone who`s had that experience.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Dana, the Judge who issued the order to appoint the Special Master also has effectively halted much of the Justice Department`s work on the case of declassified documents.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  weighs in, in a "Meet the Press" interview with Chuck Todd that airs tomorrow. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC NEWS HOST: What do you say to the argument that it would be too divisive for the country to prosecute a former President?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think that our country is a country that has gone through different periods of time where the unthinkable has happened, and where there has been a call for justice, and justice has been served.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: What`s your reaction to the Vice President`s comments, Dana?\par \par \uc2 MILBANK: Well, I think that`s about as far as she can go. It would be really poor form and dangerous indeed, for the Vice President to be weighing in one way or the other on this potential prosecution because the White House cannot be seen as influencing whatever happens in this prosecution. Now, of course, Trump supporters will believe that no matter what.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, to the extent anybody in the administration should be weighing in, it should be the Justice Department and their message right now is a very, very clear one, and that is, the longer this is being delayed by the Trump side and the demands for the special prosecutor and not being allowed to continue the intelligence review, it is endangering our national security. That should be in every American`s interest to have that going on right now.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Dana, President Biden gave a forceful message at a \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  reception in suburban Maryland on Thursday. Biden outlined his administration`s accomplishments and escalated his attack on Republicans who are loyalists of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With less than two months before the midterm elections, Biden is calling for unity and putting MAGA Republicans on notice. What do you make of the President`s strategy to brand Trump loyalists as a threat to democracy and invoking harsh political rhetoric that includes accusations of their embrace of semi-fascism?\par \par \uc2 MILBANK: Well, look, Reverend, it`s certainly an accurate description. I think what the President needs to do and I think what he has been doing is say, we`re talking about the former President, we`re talking about Republican leaders in Congress. We`re talking about election deniers on the ballot. They`re not talking about the tens of millions of people out there who may have supported \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . A lot of them had been lied to and a lot of them have been manipulated.\par \par \uc2 Nobody is calling them semi-fascists. What they`re saying is the techniques being used by a certain Members of Congress, by the former President and his allies are tools of fascism and that is a very clear argument and they should focus on that.\par \par \uc2 [17:30:03]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Susan, major developments this week in the fight for reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the Michigan State Supreme Court ruled voters this November will determine whether to place abortion rights in the Michigan Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the South Carolina Senate failed to pass a ban on abortions earlier this earlier than six weeks into pregnancy. Democrats are hoping ballot measures on abortion rights will drive voter turnout.\par \par \uc2 Could this backfire by also energizing Republicans who are abortion opponents?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: Well, first, I think I just want to follow up on what Dana said about the type of Republicans, those are the extremists. They`re also the extremists that had been fighting, especially in the primaries on these abortion laws. For example, you know, one is Pro-Life, but it makes an exemption and then the other comes out and says nothing. It`s got to be no exceptions whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 This is not going to be an issue that really brings out Republicans in my opinion, because frankly, they have what they want. It`s very hard to turn out votes when you have what you want. So, I think it will serve the Democrats. Well, I think they`re using it as a very good tool, because they are setting it up that it`s a fight about not being pro-choice or pro-life, but being an extremist and going too far, and that is a winning message for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you, Susan and Dana.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a Black British perspective on the death of Queen Elizabeth. Stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:35:43]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: It is 10:35 PM in London where mourners continue to mark the passing of Queen Elizabeth after 70 years on the British throne, a reign that saw the British Empire lose almost all of its African and West Indian colonies as those countries became independent, leaving a complex relationship between the late Queen`s legacy and the Black diaspora.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Lord Simon Woolley, Director of Operation Black Vote, and former Chair of the \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  Race Disparity Unit Advisory Group. Lord Woolley, my thanks for you joining us at this hour.\par \par \uc2 I want to have you on tonight, I wanted to have you on because while the world marks the passing of Queen Elizabeth, her reign included anti- colonial movements in Black countries colonized by the then, British Empire, most of which were successful in gaining independence. And of course, you have various Black communities in England descended from immigrants from those colonies.\par \par \uc2 So while the country mourns overall, how would you describe the relationship between Black Britons and the Queen?\par \par \uc2 LORD SIMON WOOLLEY, DIRECTOR OF OPERATION BLACK VOTE: Well, you know, we are more the passing of the Queen having 70 years in the reign. But as you suggested, the relationship is complicated.\par \par \uc2 The history of the Royal family, its roots very much in the enslavement of African people, descendants like myself, but I think that comes forward in a big way to the 21st Century. There is a long way to go.\par \par \uc2 The uncomfortable truths of the past are not for the King, \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  will begin a journey. I was with him, Reverend Al, last year in Barbados where Prime Minister Mia Motley severed the ties as the Queen being the Head of State, and \uc2 Prince Charles\uc2  declared that that was good for our country, any country that decided to do that was fine.\par \par \uc2 And I think what occurred there, Reverend Al, was the beginning of compensation that colonialism is gone. The Head of State needs to be decided by the people and not by the Head of State, and so I think that journey must continue.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, the racial dynamics in England, many should know has had its tensions. I remember first meeting you before you were Lord Woolley, when you and others had me come over in 1991, and there is a picture of it. I helped to lead a march on the racial killing of Rolan Adams, who was killed as a Black there, I am leading a march with his parents, hundreds of people poured in the streets with you and I and we were met with racial epithets.\par \par \uc2 But since then, I`ve seen Rolan`s aunt go to the House of Lords and I`ve seen you become a Sir and you`ve had me back over and over again and including speaking at Oxford twice, and the racial dynamics within the Royal family after the marriage of \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  and Duchess \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2  have revisited in the last two days since the Queen`s death.\par \par \uc2 The question of whether the monarchy is adaptable to a changing racial world is there? A year ago, the Royal family reportedly went so far as to consider hiring a diversity czar amid fallout over Markle`s accusation of racist treatment from her in-laws.\par \par \uc2 Can you have a monarchy historically based on elite White British bloodlines and still have a modern culturally inclusive country?\par \par \uc2 WOOLLEY: I think that you know, the monarchy has to have a reset. The first element in that reset, Reverend Al, is the acknowledgement of what occurred in the past. As I said, the King, then \uc2 Prince Charles\uc2  apologized for the atrocities that happened in the name of the United Kingdom.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:10]\par \par \uc2 WOOLLEY: Have the king then \uc2 Prince Charles\uc2  apologized, apologized for the atrocities that happened in the name of the United Kingdom. Much of that or some of that was in the house of the Royal -- at the Royal family.\par \par \uc2 Look, my view is that the Royal family is revered in this country and in many domains. I don`t think it is going away anytime soon. So, I think that if it is to remain, it needs to adapt, and the young Royals that need to go with that today, for the first time in many months Reverend Al, we saw \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2  and Harry and William and Kate walk in as a close family.\par \par \uc2 And so even the schism that has happened within the family that often happens, the death of Her Majesty, the Queen, the longest reigning monarch in British history, has brought the family together, has brought the nation together. But these uncomfortable conversations about the legacy and the infrastructure that has built Great Britain as it is today, it is still not fully impact.\par \par \uc2 As you know, Reverend Al, I am now head at \uc2 Cambridge University\uc2 , Homerton College, I hope that you will come and visit anytime soon, but in a couple of weeks` time, we are we are laying bare a report on the history of the enslavement of Africa from it.\par \par \uc2 It is deeply uncomfortable, but it is one that I think the modern society, Reverend Al nice to have.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, we will be watching and we`re glad that you graced us, Lord Simon Woolley of the \uc2 University of Cambridge\uc2  in England, and certainly an activist in his own right. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 WOOLLEY: I hope that you will come and visit. I hope that you will come and visit my college sometime soon next year. That we are hoping, we are praying that you will and you`ll be warmly welcomed by the first Black -- the first Black head of any college, either in Oxford or Cambridge. The first Black male head is your friend, Reverend Al, Brother Lord Simon Woolley.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, Lord Simon Woolley, I will definitely be coming. Thank you. And congratulations.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a new documentary explores the United States` role before, during, and after one of the greatest humanitarian crisis in history, the Holocaust. Why it matters today, in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:46:20]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 A new three-part series documentary explores the United States role before, during, and after one of the greatest humanitarian crisis in history, the Holocaust. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The Holocaust was beyond belief. People just disappeared.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The primary goal was to get to the United States.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But the golden door was not wide open.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We are challenged as Americans to think about what we would have done, what we could have done, what we should have done.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: In our better moments, we are very good people, but that`s not all there is to this story.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The documentary is set to premiere on \uc2 PBS\uc2  next Sunday, September 18th, and I`m glad to welcome two of the creators of the series, award- winning filmmaker, Ken Burns; and co-director and producer Lynn Novick.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for joining me today.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Burns, let me go to you first. As one of our great historical filmmakers, I feel I must ask you very briefly first about the death of Queen Elizabeth II. So much of your work focuses on the way extraordinary personalities have shaped the course of history, and the Queen was undoubtedly a larger than life figure to many.\par \par \uc2 What are your quick thoughts about her legacy and the future of the British monarchy?\par \par \uc2 KEN BURNS, CO-DIRECTOR, "THE US AND THE HOLOCAUST": Well, I`m not a great fan of the monarchy. I`m with the Americans who separated. In fact, I`m working on a big history of the American Revolution. And of course, her Great, Great, Great grandfather was George III, the King that we were able to gain independence from.\par \par \uc2 I had an opportunity to meet her at a State Dinner. I think she provides a kind of continuity for Great Britain that very few of us have. The people in the celebrity world, people in arts that sometimes do that in America, but no one stands in except the figure of Uncle Sam in the way the Queen did, and she was a lovely person to meet.\par \par \uc2 But again, I think the monarchy is a distraction -- and the crimes of that Empire that she didn`t really preside over, she presided moreover, it`s just illusion, still have to be reckoned with.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Okay. Now, let`s go to your documentary. It notes America`s role in helping normalize fascism in Germany, an anti-Jewish hate through its own racism during the Jim Crow-era. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: To uphold the supposedly racial purity of the German people, these laws banned marriage or sexual relations between Jews and persons of what they called German or kindred blood.\par \par \uc2 The German jurists who wrote these laws had closely studied statutes in the United States that had for decades reduced African-Americans to second- class citizens and barred interracial marriage in 30 States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Mr. Burns, you`ve been quoted as saying that: "American institutions and policies like segregation and the brutal treatment of indigenous populations and African-Americans were influential in Hitler`s Germany." Can you elaborate on that point on how American institutionalism influenced the Holocaust?\par \par \uc2 [17:50:10]\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Well, let`s be absolutely clear, Reverend Sharpton, and we`re so happy to be with you again that the United States in no way bears responsibility for it, but the fact of racism, the fact of the treatment of indigenous peoples and virulent antisemitism in this country were also prevalent in in Germany. Hitler admired the fact that we were able to exterminate or to isolate into essentially concentration camps, our Native population. He was happy that we had passed a restrictive immigration law that limited -- that placed really pernicious quotas on nations except for the Northern Nordic, he would say, Aryan Nations.\par \par \uc2 And as you heard the German jurists studied our Jim Crow statutes to pass the first of the discriminatory laws against the Jews, and whenever we were officially in protest, their response to us was Mississippi. You know, you say that these people are inferior to you, well, we consider the Jews inferior to us. And how can you -- how dare you say this?\par \par \uc2 So, what happens is that the combination of that racism, eugenics, the antisemitism that`s prevalent, the Great Depression, the virulent rise of sort of authoritarian sentiment, helps to create a situation where Americans are unwilling, and I mean, all of us, not just the administration, not just the Congress, but Americans -- us.\par \par \uc2 It could also be not US and the Holocaust, but us and the Holocaust, are unwilling to let in a fraction of the people that we could have, and more importantly, should have let in. We get an F on this grade.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Miss Novick, the number of antisemitic incidents around the world dramatically increased last year. A study from \uc2 Tel Aviv University\uc2  has found naming the United States, Canada, the United Kingdom, Germany, and Australia as countries where there was a sharp rise.\par \par \uc2 In America alone, the \uc2 Anti-Defamation League\uc2  found over 2,700 incidents of antisemitism in 2021. That`s a 30 percent rise from the year before.\par \par \uc2 As co-director, you have said that this documentary is a story with enormous relevance today. Please explain.\par \par \uc2 LYNN NOVICK, CO-DIRECTOR AND PRODUCER, "THE US AND THE HOLOCAUST": Yes, thank you for that question.\par \par \uc2 We are seeing this very disturbing rise in antisemitism, not just in the US, but around the world, and there are many reasons for it, and I`m not the expert to explain all of them, but I think one of the main things we can point to is kind of a hate speech and racist, antisemitic ideology that was sort of previously relegated to the far-right into the dark corners of the internet is now really in our mainstream political discourse, public discourse.\par \par \uc2 And we`re seeing people saying in public, prominent people saying things that 10 years ago they would have been castigated for and they just keep on saying them -- antisemitic tropes, racist tropes, antiimmigrant, demonizing people who they say are not like us, meaning who they consider to be "real Americans."\par \par \uc2 And words have power as we`re seeing, and people hear those things and decide to do something horrible about it, and that`s a lot of what we saw in Hitler`s rise. And some of that same sentiment, as Ken was saying was also prevalent here in the US during the time that this uncovers.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You know, next Thursday, I`ll be heading to the White House as President of National Action Network to participate in a Summit the Biden administration is hosting, called United We Stand and I`ve joined other civil rights leaders of various communities, and pressing the White House to do more in response to hate-motivated attacks, like the one we saw earlier this year in Buffalo, New York, and synagogues and other places.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, on Wednesday, the Anti-Defamation League Center released this analysis of more than 38,000 names of leaked Oath Keepers membership list, a far-right extremist group with known racist, antisemitic beliefs.\par \par \uc2 The report identified more than 370 people it believes currently works in law enforcement agencies, including Police Chiefs and Sheriffs, and more than a hundred people who are currently members of the military.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Burns based on what you`ve learned about America`s response to right- wing hate in the past, how do you see this country dealing with these modern day threats from within?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Well, the fact is, is that we have to deal with it. We have to address it. We have to be speaking about it a lot more forcefully. I`m not saying that the conditions that created the Holocaust per se are happening, but I can borrow from the great Holocaust scholar and now an ambassador-at- large at the State Department, Deborah Lipstadt who says in our film, "The time to stop a genocide is before it happens." The time to save a democracy, Reverend Al, is before it is lost.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:18]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Miss Novick, one of the surprising things people can learn from this documentary is that American Jews were divided about how to respond to what was happening in Nazi, Germany. Some were concerned that advocating too forcefully for German Jews could lead to a backlash here in the US. Talk a little more about that.\par \par \uc2 NOVICK: Yes, that was very surprising to us to understand that antisemitism was so strong in the United States, that the Jewish community was divided, and there were big arguments about this, whether they should speak out, and they were afraid of sort of aggravating antisemitism here.\par \par \uc2 There were all kinds of conspiracy theories that President Roosevelt was, you know, the puppet of some kind of Jewish conspiracy, which was obviously not true. And also that the Germans would retaliate against the Jews in Germany, if the Jewish community here was seen as instigating our government to protest what they were doing over there.\par \par \uc2 So, American-Jews got themselves in a very difficult situation. Over time, as the news came out more and more of the atrocities, the mass killings, and Hitler`s horrendous evil plan in action, I think the Jewish community became more unified. That, you know, we have to stand up to this.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I am out of time, but let me ask you, Miss Novick, what is the message you hope people will come out after -- come away with after watching this series?\par \par \uc2 NOVICK: Well, I think as Ken was saying, just recognizing the dangers to our own democracy right now, through all the things we`ve been talking about, this film is a really sort of sobering look at events in the past that are so relevant to today.\par \par \uc2 And you know, being able to take a tough look at our own past and ask the hard questions of ourselves: Why didn`t we do more? In the hope that we can learn from the past, as Deborah Lipstadt says in our film, in the hopes of doing better next time.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, Ken Burns and Lynn Novick, thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 For my final thoughts tonight: I agree with Attorney Sunny Hostin who said you can mourn the Queen and should, but also that does not mean you embrace all of the history of the British Empire, the colonialism, and the developing situations around racial disparities in the United Kingdom, and throughout other areas where the Queen and the Royal family still held a symbolic lead.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen progress, but we`ve not seen it go all where it should go. And as Simon Woolley said -- Lord Simon Woolley said tonight from England on this show, we have a long way to go, but we`ve come a long way from the day, 31 years ago, I helped lead that march in England, where you would have thought we were in one of those same kind of hostile situations in the United States.\par \par \uc2 That`s why as we gather this week, with the Hate Crime Summit at the White House with leaders of the Jewish and Asian and Latino and LGBTQ and Indigenous communities, meeting and talking about dealing with the rise of hate crime, we must not just talk about it as commentary, we must talk about it as commitment to come across all lines.\par \par \uc2 We do not have to agree with each other`s political views or even each other`s past, but we are at a place now that you cannot protect Blacks in Buffalo being mowed down at a supermarket, or Jews being mowed down in Pittsburgh, or San Diego in synagogues, or gays, or Latinos or indigenous people or Asians.\par \par \uc2 We cannot sit silently by without joining each other in saying hate must stop. If we have differences, debate them, vote about them. But we must criminalize and enforce the criminalization of those that will take violence against people not because of who they are, but because of what they are. And even then, we must treat the crime as what it is -- a crime.\par \par \uc2 Hate is a criminal act when it is used as a tool for violence.\par \par \uc2 That does it for me. Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 We`ll have a no POLITICSNATION tomorrow. There`s special coverage of 9/11 anniversary events. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 5:00 PM Eastern.\par \par \uc2 "American Voices" with Alicia Menendez starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [17:59:56]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: Hello, everyone. I`m Alicia Menendez. We begin this Saturday in Great Britain, a day of mourning, but also celebration.\par \par \uc2 Charles III officially ascending the throne. The monarchy officially recognizing the end of 70 years of Queen Elizabeth`s reign with "Long Live the King."\par \par \uc2 [18:00:14]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220911ei9a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for September 9, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,134 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA, FORMER FIRST LADY OF THE UNITED STATES: Walls of the most famous address in the world. Then again it is so important for every young kid who is doubting themselves to believe that they can too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Braids, dress, a compliment from your man, portraits. \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 , boom. She won the week. Maria Teresa Kumar, and former congressman \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , thank you both my friends. That is tonight`s "REIDOUT." She won the week you all, big time. ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight on "ALL IN."\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN, (D-CA): So not only was there the big lie, there was the big rip-off.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: DOJ picks up where the January 6 committee left off.\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: We found evidence that the Trump campaign and its surrogates misled donors as to where their funds would go and what they would be used for.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, Congressman Jamie Raskin on the criminal grand jury investigating fundraising fraud, and late-breaking reports that \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2  has been subpoenaed, then new signals that Donald Trump`s favorite judge may be backing down to the Justice Department. Plus a victory for democracy and reproductive rights as the state Supreme Court smacks down Michigan Republicans, and as King Charles greets his subjects for the first time, Melissa Murray, on coming to terms with the Imperial past of the British Empire, when "ALL IN" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Good evening from New York, I`m Chris Hayes. The Department of Justice`s investigation into January 6, the attempted coup, is once again expanding its scope quite a bit, in fact. The DOJ sent out a raft of new subpoenas this week, we`re just learning about, subpoenas of former Trump White House and campaign officials, reportedly in relation to Trump`s Save America PAC as well as the so-called fake electoral scheme. Top Trump policy adviser and speechwriter \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 , and Trump`s White House political director Brian Jack were among those subpoenaed. That`s according to a brand new report that broke just about an hour ago from \uc2 New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Trump`s PAC is an issue that the January 6 committee has been focused on for months. In fact, they`ve argued that Trump solicited hundreds of millions of dollars in donations for what he claimed was a legal defense fund to help pay the legal expenses of his efforts to overturn the election when in reality, such a fund simply never existed. Instead, Trump allegedly just directed the money to his PAC where it was then funneled to a number of pro-Trump groups run by his allies. Listen to how the January 6 committee described it this spring.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 AMANDA WICK, SENIOR INVESTIGATIVE COUNSEL, JANUARY 6 COMMITTEE: Between Election Day and January 6, a Trump campaign sent millions of fundraising e-mails to Trump supporters sometimes as many as 25 of the day. The e-mails claimed the "left-wing mob" was undermining the election and implored supporters to "step up to protect" the integrity of the election and encourage them to "fight back." But as the select committee has demonstrated, the Trump campaign knew these claims of voter fraud were false, yet they continue to barrage small-dollar donors with e-mails encouraging them to donate to something called the Official Election Defense Fund. The Select Committee discovered no such fund existed.\par \par \uc2 HANNA ALLRED, FORMER TRUMP CAMPAIGN STAFFER: I don`t believe there is actually a fund called the Election Defense Fund.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is it fair to say that the election Defense Fund was another, I think we`ve called it a marketing tactic.\par \par \uc2 GARY COBY, FORMER TRUMP CAMPAIGN DIGITAL DIRECTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And tell us about these funds as marketing tactics.\par \par \uc2 COBY: Just topic matter. Where money could potentially go to be -- how money can potentially be used.\par \par \uc2 WICK: The claims that the election was stolen were so successful. President Trump and his allies raised $250 million, nearly $100 million in the first week after the election. On November 9, 2020, President Trump created a separate entity called the Save America PAC. Most of the money raised went to this newly created PAC, not to election-related litigation. The Select Committee discovered that the Save America PAC made millions of dollars of contributions to pro-Trump organizations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, so you`re tracking that right? They raise all this money saying the election was stolen, donate to Legal Defense Fund, they get $250 million, a quarter of a billion dollars from these small donor donors. Then they create this new save America super PAC. The money goes there and then goes to all other kinds of stuff.\par \par \uc2 Now, the fact that the Department of Justice has subpoenaed people associated with this is really, really interesting for a number of reasons. First, because fundraising has basically been the only thing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has ever in his life been really truly remarkably good at. Almost all of his other life`s enterprises save for playing a business mogul in a reality show have been middling to failures. But Trump has been wildly successful at hoovering up donations from the MAGA faithful. It`s what he does best, so much so that his fundraising enterprise is one of the largest in the country. In fact, so large it`s actually gotten him into hot water with his fellow Republicans.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Because he has such a hold on the party`s base, these donors are sending him everything all the time instead to other Republicans. In typical Trump fashion, he has been reluctant to share any of his money, even as the NRSC, the entity in charge of getting Republican candidates elected to the Senate is basically broke. Republican Senator \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  of Utah even called Trump out publicly for this, telling the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 . "I would sure hope President Trump would use some of that money he`s got to get Republicans elected to the Senate. It`ll be good for the country and good for the people he`s endorsed."\par \par \uc2 Of course, there is absolutely no reason to believe Trump is actually going to do that. Why would he? He doesn`t even really know where the money raises goes or at least we don`t. You might remember there was even a whole other investigation at Trump`s inaugural committee, which also raise huge amounts of money over the alleged misuse of funds. Now, misusing funds or raising money under false pretenses can be something that government takes very seriously. Because while our modern campaign finance laws allow for lots of shady behavior, and I mean, lots, there are still some clear lines. Explicitly lying to people about where the money they`re donating to you will go for instance, to an entity that does not exist, may cross one of them. Not clear if it does, but it may. And that is, of course, what the January 6 committee alleges Trump did with truly enormous amounts of money.\par \par \uc2 Now, take a step back for a second because there are so many concurrent investigations into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it can be difficult to keep them straight, so just to reset the table, right? There`s also the grand jury in Fulton County, Georgia, investigating potentially criminal election interference by Trump and his allies. We know his close associate and lawyer \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , who is integral in plotting Trump`s coup attempt has been formally notified by the DHS office there that he is a target of that criminal inquiry. So that`s one.\par \par \uc2 Two, there`s the federal inquiry into roughly 100 stolen classified documents which received from Mar-a-Lago after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  executed a search warrant. Apparently, in response to Trump`s own lawyers, lying about what he still had in his possession, including it appears, according to one report, documents related to a foreign country`s nuclear weapons, so sensitive only cabinet-level officials could OK being read into it.\par \par \uc2 There`s also, of course, the House Select Committee investigation in the January 6 insurrection, which should pick up steam this fall. But the third criminal inquiry, right, there`s Georgia, there`s the documents, the third one, this investigation partly into Trump`s super PAC that we`re learning more about today is part of the Department of Justice`s overarching investigation into January sixth and the attempted coup and now potentially fraud as well. That`s being run out of DC where there`s a grand jury taking a lot of testimony.\par \par \uc2 Now, here to me is the other really striking thing about this development, right? Trump`s crime in a colloquial layman`s sense is obvious. I don`t mean crime, like the technical sense. I mean, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tried to overturn the results of a free and fair election and disrupted the peaceful transfer of power for the first time arguably in modern American history at least since the Civil War by inciting a violent armed mob distorting the Capitol`s behalf. He wanted to join the mob and be at the front of them while they storming the Capitol to overturn American democracy. That`s obviously wrong, bad, and criminal even to the average person.\par \par \uc2 From the legal perspective, prosecuting someone like that is not as cut and dried as maybe it feels like it should be. There`s the question of what laws he broke what he can be charged for criminally, and what kind of case you could make against him and the people around him personally, for those actions, which are obviously offensive to the very core of our democratic being. That question, what can you technically prosecute is kind of adjacent to the raw facts of the situation. But fraud, one of those areas of law seems much more cut and dried. There are paper trails, documents, and wire transfers, which can lay out a criminal case much more clearly.\par \par \uc2 And this new line of inquiry should inspire some worry in Trump`s already overstretched legal team. Because look no further than \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  in New York yesterday in handcuffs. \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  is currently awaiting trial for his alleged role in another scheme to defraud Trump supporters, probably the very same donors giving money to the super PAC that Trump was raiding before. In Bannon`s case, it was a bogus campaign to crowdfund Trump`s border wall, which despite his promises to the contrary, Trump was unable to get Mexico to pay for.\par \par \uc2 The organizer of that campaign called, We Build The Wall, promise not to take a cut of the money raised, but he was secretly skimming hundreds of thousands of dollars off the top, which Bannon allegedly helped him launder. So that organizer again raised money from the MAGA fell faithful skimming money off the top. That organizer and another associate have already pleaded guilty to federal charges and potentially face years in prison when they are sentenced later this year.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:00]\par \par \uc2 And things aren`t looking great for Bannon, seen here, in handcuffs in the New York courthouse. It`s early. Of course, he`s innocent until proven guilty under the U.S. Constitution. The case against him seems pretty strong. And the broad strokes of what he`s accused of doing, taking money from Trump supporters under misleading pretenses. Looks awfully similar to what the DOJ now appears to be investigating Trump`s Pac for. Based on what we`ve seen so far, I think Trump should be worried.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Jamie Raskin is a Democrat from Maryland. He sits on the bipartisan January 6 committee, and he joins me now. First, I guess, Congressman, just your reaction to these developments which I imagine you`re learning from the news, as we all are. The subpoenas that have gone out to some of these individuals, including \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 , the focus of these grand jury subpoenas on the super PAC created in the wake of the election. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2 , (D-MD): Well, nobody should be surprised by it because financial crime follows in Donald Trump`s wake. And as you know, I`ve argued for a long time that Trump really should have been impeached the first time for his rampant violations of both the foreign and domestic emoluments clauses as he was raking in millions of dollars from foreign governments and also taking money from domestic federal departments and agencies at the hotels and the golf courses and so on.\par \par \uc2 It`s always been a scheme for self-enrichment for him and he converted the government into an instrument of capital gain for him and his family and his friends. And that is really what explains the motive behind his determination to stay in offense, a determination that was so ferocious that he was willing to try to overturn a presidential election for the first time in American history and to unleash a violent insurrection against his own vice president and against the Congress.\par \par \uc2 But -- so I`m not surprised by all of it. And I`m glad that people are finally starting to bear down on the financial details because they decided to make money off of the election. And remember, Trump was telling people as several witnesses have told us that he was planning to announce that he won the election, whether or not he won it. And they were very clear that he was going to be even more forceful about having won it if he had lost it. Well, why is that? They then proceeded to unleash this huge fundraising campaign which netted them a quarter-billion dollars by telling people that they had to go and fight in order to you know vindicate their stolen victory.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. I just want to play this clip again, from your committee`s inquiry here about once you get that money in the super PAC -- and again, federal regulation of this is incredibly loose, thanks to the conservatives on the Supreme Court. But once you get the money to the super PAC, then it can go in all kinds of places. Here`s just a little rundown of some of the places that money again raised from donors saying donate legal funds so we can fight because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won. Here`s just a little rundown from your committee about where that went. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WICK: $1 million to Trump Chief of Staff Mark Meadows`s charitable foundation, $1 million to the America first Policy Institute, a conservative organization, which employs several former Trump administration officials, $204,857 to the \uc2 Trump Hotel Collection\uc2 , and over $5 million to event strategies Inc., the company that ran President Trump`s January 6 rally on the Ellipse.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, a million dollars to \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  foundation is -- that`s a big donation. I mean the kind of thing that might even plausibly change an individual`s mind about whether they want to cooperate with congressional subpoenas.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: He was definitely purchasing a lot of goodwill from people who were prospective witnesses against him in subsequent criminal or civilian -- or civil prosecutions or a congressional investigation. And so -- you know that does demonstrate the slipperiness of our campaign finance laws. But, of course, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is somebody who pushes everything to the max. And they turn this into a money-making enterprise and he, of course, is now the king of the Hill in the Republican Party as he`s got all the money and all of the members of Congress are terrified of him that he would, you know, endorse somebody against them or spend their -- spend money against them. They`re completely beholden to the guy. And you know, the -- Abraham Lincoln`s party has become the authoritarian cult of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Final question for you. You were a constitutional law professor before you were a United States Congressman. I would like to hear you talk about these different aspects of an inquiry or making the case here. I mean, your committee is not -- doesn`t have criminal prosecutorial power, nor should it. It`s a-- it`s a -- it`s a congressional committee. There`s a difference between what I would call the great crime of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which was a frontal assault on the core of American democracy, and whatever statutory violation of the U.S. Code he may have committed. And sometimes it can feel like a little bit of a mismatch there. And I wonder how you think about those sort of two lanes?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, obviously, we`re going to need some legislative changes to the Criminal Code to account for, now the possibility of presidents just taking a headlong rush at seizing the presidency. But there are federal statutes out there that will do the job. We, of course, have a seditious conspiracy, and we have the conspiracy to interfere with a Federal proceeding, which I think everybody can agree --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: -- Took place here. After all, the whole chant was Stop the Steal and they bum-rushed the Capitol, they assaulted Federal officers all in order to interfere with the Federal proceeding, everybody agrees that`s exactly what took place. And it didn`t happen by accident. And it wasn`t spontaneous. Even if it were spontaneous, of course, there were people who agreed to go in together and do it, but this thing was planned.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: So, yes, I think that we`re going to have to look at some new federal statutory changes. And we have to look at the way that the electoral system itself is vulnerable to strategic bad faith actors like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: The Electoral College is an accident waiting to happen, and we have to deal with that at some point in American history, and why not now?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Congressman Jamie Raskin, thank you so much for your time.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Thank you so much, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, could Donald Trump`s handpicked judge be backing away from her protection of the corrupt ex-president? We`ll break down her latest order next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Last night, the Department of Justice filed a response to Donald Trump`s handpicked Mar-a-Lago judge, Aileen Cannon. A 38-year-old judge has been getting a lot of justified public scrutiny this week after issuing that questionable special master order and holding the DoD`s ability to use these documents for further investigation, a move that most legal experts said had no actual precedent. In its filing, the DOJ asked the judge for a partial stay of that order and warn "if the court does not grant a stay by Thursday, September 15, the government intends to seek relief from the 11th circuit." That threat of appeals, the 11th circuit appears to have gotten the judge`s attention. Now maybe she`s looking for Trump and the government to work this out. Cannon issued a paperless order saying "the parties are instructed to consider the DO`sJ request to continue investigating the classified documents.\par \par \uc2 Renato Mariotti is a former federal prosecutor, and legal affairs columnist for Politico where his latest piece is titled Trump`s Lawyers Might Think They Just Won. They still botched the case. And he joins me now. So walk me through where we are procedurally here. The order was issued, and DOJ responded to the order and said this is crazy, we`re begging you to only stop this one portion where you said we can`t use the 100 classified documents we have which we`ve already segregated for our investigative purposes, please reconsider that. So she can now reconsider right before it gets appealed above her.\par \par \uc2 RENATO MARIOTTI, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Yes, that`s right. They basically gave her an out, Chris. They said look, we`re going to -- putting all the rest of this aside. We`re going to try to resolve the issues regarding attorney-client privilege. We`ll allow a special master for that. We`re going to return a lot of his personal property.\par \par \uc2 But as the classified documents, obviously, the government owns those, obviously, they`re not an attorney-client privilege. There is a very serious potential harm here to the public. And so if you don`t give us an accommodation here to let us continue to investigate, to continue to determine whether or not those documents, for example, you know, are a potential threat to national security, we`re going to have to appeal. And you know, essentially, what I think that order that you just mentioned, is her sending an overcharge to Trump`s team, you know, please give me a way out of this by backing off of the classified documents.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We should know that the DOJ made this argument in its filing, which to me, again, is the whole case. And again, I always say on the show, I`m not a lawyer, I`m married to an extremely good lawyer. But Nixon V. Administrator of General Services, which is the -- you know, the kind of mean precedent for a lot of this stuff indicates a former president may not successfully assert executive privilege against review by the very executive branch in whose name the privilege is invoked, meaning the current Executive Branch who`s on the other side from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and asserting executive privilege that does seem to be continued, particularly when we`re talking about the classified documents. The nub of the issue and one would think should prevail on appeal, although the 11th circuit is stacked with Trump judges, so who knows?\par \par \uc2 MARIOTTI: Yes. I have to say, Chris. That was the most bizarre portion of the opinion, the most bizarre -- you know, frankly, argument that was being made by Trump`s team.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:00]\par \par \uc2 It`s really as if one of my clients wanted their documents back and I asserted attorney-client privilege against my own client. I mean, it makes no sense. So the point of attorney-client privilege is to protect the client, and it`s their documents. So very bizarre, I really have trouble seeing that hold-up on appeal.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s a -- there`s a deadline now. I mean, can you imagine -- I guess he -- the judge now has it in her ability to issue some order, right, where she amends what she had said before, right. And that`s going to happen either between now and Thursday, or they`re going to appeal. Is that where we are right now?\par \par \uc2 MARIOTTI: That`s right. She could say, look, I`m going to -- I`m going -- I`m going to walk this back as the declassified documents, or perhaps Trump`s team could give it to her. Trump`s team could say, you know, we`re changing our position. We`re going to back off as to declassify documents, thereby mooting this issue and essentially making it so the state is not necessary, because we`re going to back off. One of those two things could happen and if not, there`s going to be an appeal.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And we`ll see how long the appeal takes. All the delay, delay, delay has always been the name of the game for the Trump legal team. So the speed of all this will be something to keep our eyes on. Renato Mariotti, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARIOTTI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, the Michigan State Supreme Court smacks down a Republican attempt to keep abortion off the ballot. Michigan Secretary of State \uc2 Jocelyn Benson\uc2  joins me on the persistent threat from state-level Republicans. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: One thing we`ve learned in the past few months is that overturning Roe v. Wade has been a huge energizer for the voting public. We`ve seen clear evidence of that in state after state. And the latest is in Michigan where more than 750,000 people signed a petition to get a ballot initiative to enshrine abortion rights into that state`s constitution.\par \par \uc2 When that petition went to the Michigan elections board to be certified and placed on the ballot in November, Republican members managed to block it on a technicality, a typo involving spaces between words. Well, yesterday, the Michigan Supreme Court destroyed that ploy, ordering the proposal to appear on the ballot with a scathing rebuke to the elections board of Republicans from the chief justice of that court.\par \par \uc2 And I quote, "753,759 Michiganders signed this proposal, more than have ever signed any proposal in Michigan`s history. The challengers have not produced a single-signer who claims to have been confused by the limited spacing sections in the full-text proportion portion of the proposal. Yet two members of the Board of State canvassers would but prevent the people of Michigan from voting on the proposal because they believe the decreased spacing makes the text no longer the full text, even though there is no dispute that every word appears and appears legibly and in the correct order.\par \par \uc2 And there is no evidence that anyone was confused about the text. Two members of the Board of State Canvassers with the power to do so would keep the petition from the voters for what they purport to be a technical violation of the statute. They would disenfranchise millions of Michiganders not because they believe the many thousands of Michiganders who signed the proposal were confused by it, but because they think they have identified a technicality that allows them to do so. A game of gotcha gone very bad. What a sad marker of the times."\par \par \uc2 Now, that crucial measure will appear on the November ballot, and one of the people who helped in the legal effort was Michigan`s Democratic Secretary of State \uc2 Jocelyn Benson\uc2 . And she joins me now. Secretary Benson, first just your reaction to the decision by the Michigan Supreme Court to override those dissenting voices on the board of canvassers and put this on the ballot for Michiganders.\par \par \uc2 JOCELYN BENSON\uc2 , SECRETARY OF STATE, MICHIGAN: Oh it`s a vindication for the law and for the people of Michigan and the hundreds of thousands of people who signed this petition wanting it to go on to the ballot. And it really also showed and sent a strong message to the Republican members of the Board of State canvassers stay in your lane. You know, you have a responsibility under the law to certify elections and petitions based on the will of the people not your own opinions.\par \par \uc2 And my hope is that this will send a strong message and ensure that the Board of State canvassers resumes their role, their long-standing practice of supporting the will of the voters in a nonpartisan manner. But we have to remember, they will certify the elections this fall. And this could be a sign as to perhaps additional partisan shenanigans we may expect to see in the future.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, you have moved on to my next question. So, this state board of canvassers is a little bit of a strange institution for us outside of Michigan, although there are similar sorts of such institutions in other places. In your state, this is a state board and these -- and it has an equal number of Democrats and Republicans. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Exactly. Now, the part -- each party appoints to members of the board and essentially gives the governor a selection of three people that she can then choose from to appoint individuals to the board. So, these are party-appointed people. And it`s notable that those who were on the board in 2020, Republican appointees who did not follow the mandate of their party but instead follow the law have since been replaced by individuals who, in this case, voted not to follow the will of the people and allow these petitions on the ballot originally.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:17]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I just want to trace this for people because you might remember back, there was some controversy. It was a Wayne County canvassing board in which Republican members at first said they would not certify that county and then change their mind, but they were lobbied directly by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at that time, the present United States. Then the state canvassing board had to certify it. And one of the Republicans on that board-certified it as he should have, because there was no problem. That individual has been replaced, right?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Exactly. Correct.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, I guess the obvious thing is it just seems like this attempting this gotcha game, as the Supreme Court Justice said it, indicates a level of bad faith that`s profoundly disquieting for the administration of elections in your state if there is an election result that these two canvassers, members the board don`t like this fall. Can they just say, we`re not certifying that election?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Well, what played out this week shows you exactly what could happen if and when they were to do something like that, which is they won`t succeed. The law is clear. What was created this week was not a blocking of the will of the people. The court stepped in and did the right thing and upheld the law. That will happen again if there`s a failure to certify.\par \par \uc2 But what also happened is several days of confusion. Will this be on the ballot? What does this mean? Why didn`t they vote to put it on the ballot? And that state of confusion is really harmful to democracy. It`s harmful to our work to ensure voters are informed and have confidence in our democracy. So, harming the confidence people have in our democracy is the real detrimental effect of these shenanigans.\par \par \uc2 But you know, that said, we will stand -- the Attorney General and I will stand to make sure if the Board of State canvassers or any county canvassers failed to do their legal duty in certifying election results, we will go to court and ensure the law is upheld and the will of the people rules the day.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Michigan has a 1931 law that was going to go into effect with the overruling of Roe v. Wade. That has been enjoined by a judge pursuant to a lawsuit by the governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 . This is the polling in your state right now. What issue would most motivate you to vote this fall? Abortion rights 34 percent, inflation 26 percent, Education 10 percent, economy eight percent. Just a matter -- a matter of Democratic accountability. How important is it to have this before the voters this fall?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: It`s critical because not just because hundreds of thousands of voters have signed a petition asking it and putting it on the ballot, which is their duty to do under the law and responsibility and ability to do, but also really underscores that the fundamental rights and freedoms, indeed democracy itself in our state are literally on the ballot this fall.\par \par \uc2 And it gives voters, Democrats, Republicans, and independents an opportunity to weigh in substantively on the issue of reproductive freedom as well as voting rights, which is the second petition that is now on the ballot. So, it ensures that people can act on these issues that are it`s so poignant right now for so many citizens on both sides of the aisle. And I think we`ll see a high level of participation and engagement on both sides as a result.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s really a remarkable position for people to say we want the cryogenically frozen 1931 abortion law to control women`s bodies in the state of Michigan rather than the elected public of 2022 to make that decision. \uc2 Jocelyn Benson\uc2 , thank you very much. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, a certain Texas senator slash podcaster attempts to troll another state during a climate crisis and it doesn`t go well, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Republicans and Fox News hosts have a weird obsession with the great state of California. They`d love to rag on it. They claim it as an example of liberal governance gone wrong the disaster of a blue state.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, HOST, FOX NEWS: California in decline.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , HOST, FOX NEWS: The State of California is collapsing and many Californians are fleeing.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It`s not affordable, it`s not livable, and it`s locked down for most of the time.\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , HOST, FOX NEWS: Totally unlivable.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Take a look at the dumpster fire that is California.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The failed state of California.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: California is dying a slow and painful death.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: California has gone off the cliff.\par \par \uc2 ANDY KESSLER, COLUMNIST, WALL STREET JOURNAL: We have the highest taxes, the highest energy cost, they take it away our toilets that actually flush.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, California is a state that`s got problems like all of them, but it`s really weird and super insecure to see this on Fox News over and over. But politicians, especially those from the second most populous state of the union, Texas, love to compare themselves with California as a kind of competing model of governance.\par \par \uc2 Just look at this tweet from the Republican Governor of Texas, Greg Abbott, "Remember those high taxes, burdensome regulations, and socialistic agenda advanced in California, we don`t believe in that. We believe in less government more individual freedom. If you agree with that, you`ll fit right in." They try to lure Californians away especially businesses inviting them to leave, the supposed tyranny of California for the land of the free in Texas. It`s actually worked with \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , luring them from \uc2 Palo Alto\uc2  to Austin, Texas, much to the delight of podcaster and Senator Ted Cruz.\par \par \uc2 Now, it is true that California right now in this moment currently is in crisis. They are in the midst of a record-breaking heat wave, the most severe ever in the month of September. Sacramento hit a 116 degrees on Tuesday, an all-time high. A friend of mine driving his car there had just had it stopped, just overheated. Of course climate change is making heat waves like this more intense, more common, longer lasting, more frequent, but California also has some of the most forward-leaning decarbonisation efforts in the entire country.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:19]\par \par \uc2 Just a couple of weeks ago, they announced a ban on the sale of new gasoline cars taking effect in 2035. Earlier this year, renewables power just under 100 percent of California`s daily energy needs for the first time. So, as California meets this latest energy crisis, we`re seeing the results of their model, the alternative to the Texas plan, which is much more focused on fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 Check this out. This graph, a little hard to read, but check it out. It shows different sources of energy California uses to power its grid and how the supplies fluctuate over the course of the day on Wednesday in the thick of the heat wave. Each colored line here represents a different types of energy. Bright green is renewables, mostly solar power. As you can see, during the hottest daylight hours, renewables picked way up.\par \par \uc2 At noon right in the center of this graph, renewable sources were providing half of the energy California needed. Another interesting thing you can see in this graph is how batteries are helping power California`s grid. Take a look at the light orange line in the lower right section. You can see the battery power kicking in there in the evening when the sun starts to go down when solar panels start making less energy. This all works so well this week during this intense heat that California was actually able to avoid widespread blackouts are rolling brownouts, something that honestly used to be just a fact of life in the state.\par \par \uc2 The government has also been asking residents to reduce demand on the grid during the early evening hours when it`s most stressed. Requesting people keep their air conditioning at 78 degrees and not run major appliances before four and 10:00 p.m. But that did not sit well with the fossil fuel loving podcaster from Texas who took the opportunity to deliver some almost inspiringly audacious dunking.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Today`s Democrat Party is all about California environmentalist billionaires. I love that \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  was wearing his fleece, obviously in cool air conditioning saying let them eat cake, let them sweat, you don`t get air conditioning.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, I don`t know about the air conditioning. I don`t know what the fleece. All I know is that when Texas faced an extremely rare and devastating weather event last winter, their grid heavily dependent on natural gas, didn`t hold up. No, the infrastructure basically froze, and millions of people lost power some for days on end.\par \par \uc2 While California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  may or may not be spending time in air conditioning, at least his state has electricity. And we all remember that when Texas is grid failed, Ted Cruz tried to quietly skip off to the Ritz Carlton in Cancun to soak up the sun with his family while people in his state that he represents literally froze to death. And he only came back after people found out and took pictures and he was shamed into returning to the crisis. So in that respect, score one for the California model.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES III, KING, UNITED KINGDOM: With Catherine beside him, our new Prince and Princess of Wales will I know continue to inspire and lead our national conversations, helping to bring the marginal to the center ground where vital help can be given. I want also to express my love for Harry and Meghan as they continue to build their lives overseas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The new King Charles III announcing the new org structure where his first son William with a nod to young Harry and Megan. Family drama is just one of the dynamics that has millions of people in the U.K. and, of course, around the world glued to the story as Britain enters a period of mourning for its late monarch. But of course, the Queen, particularly the Queen of the British Empire, which was, was not a universally beloved figure. She represented an empire that at one point controlled roughly a quarter of the world`s population and landmass.\par \par \uc2 Many of the people whose countries were colonized by that British Empire left with righteous anger at that Imperial Project. And we`ve seen in the wake of the Queen`s death all of these different views mixing together. I was struck by this detail in the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reporting that when he first traveled as a senator to meet the monarch, now-President Joe Biden`s proud Irish mother told him "Don`t you bow down to her." And he didn`t, because it is of course a point of natural pride for the Irish who had to win their independence from the British Crown at the cost of much bloodshed. That feeling not to bow down share by much of the global south that the British colonized.\par \par \uc2 Melissa Murray is a frequent guest on this program is a professor at New York University School of Law and also an obsessive Royals watcher. And she wrote this thread that I thought was fascinating on her conflicted feelings and the crown as the daughter of Jamaican immigrants. "There will be an outpouring of respect, maybe even love and admiration for life devoted to duty, but also desire to move beyond an imperial past to a more independent future. Maybe that is inevitable, the legacy of Britain`s first post-modern monarch."\par \par \uc2 And Melissa Murray, who also co-hosts podcast Strict Scrutiny with Leah Litman and my wife, Kate Shaw, joins me now. It`s good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA MURRAY, PROFESSOR, \uc2 NEW YORK UNIVERSITY\uc2  SCHOOL OF LAW: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I love the thread. And I already knew because we are friends. I should say, full disclosure, that you are a real, real Royals obsessive. Tell me a little bit about your perspective on it as the daughter of Jamaican immigrants, obviously also colonized by the British, your relationship with the crown and how you`ve been feeling and thinking through these kinds of contradictions.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: So, I think it`s really complicated. So, as I said in the tweet, my entire childhood is sort of bound up and visits back to my relatives in Jamaica. And there you know, 20 years since Jamaican independence. The Commonwealth and the monarchy were very much visible parts of life. If you went to a government building, you would not only see photographs of the Prime Minister, you might also see a photograph or a painting of the Queen. If you walked down a road, it might be named for a British aristocrat. So, the residue of colonialism was everywhere. It was sort of the backdrop against which we lived our lives.\par \par \uc2 And you know, I think many people very much admired indeed, even loved the Queen, because of her steadfastness, her attention to duty, and the fact that she was an accidental monarch in a lot of ways, assume this role in the wake of the abdication of her uncle Edward VIII. And she`s just in the background the whole time. She`s the only monarch many people have ever known.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:36]\par \par \uc2 But even as you love and revere her, you can also be clear-eyed about the institution that she represents and how it is inextricably intertwined with the history of colonialism, oppression, and even violence against the indigenous people of those particular colonized lands. And I think that`s part of the complication for me. I can be mournful for someone who`s always been in the backdrop of my life to some degree, but also recognizing the way in which the institution that she headed has really shaped the lives of a land that I love where people I love live in very material ways.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What is the -- why do you think you like to follow the Royals? What is it?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, I`m an only child. This is a sprawling family. I think only children are always sort of idealistic about families are like.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s fascinating, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: And you know, I remember being interested -- and my -- one of my earliest memories is watching the wedding of Charles and Diana and just sort of, you know, for an American kid raised on \uc2 Disney\uc2 , like watching someone transform into a princess before your eyes is actually, you know, heady stuff. But thinking about the legacy and the way in which this family sort of infiltrated all of these other families and like sort of penetrated every aspect of the geopolitical landscape in Europe is actually quite fascinating.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I`m a family law professor, as well as a constitutional law professor, and the idea of marriage as political alliance is completely intertwined with the Royal Family.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I want to talk a little bit about it. Let me just say this. I mean, this is -- this I think the first reaction -- of course, morning and people really grief-stricken which I understand and -- but then there`s been sort of a second round. And the piece just being like, look, there is a reason that there are some people in the world we`re not exactly weeping at this. This The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  piece today about Africa, right, where the Queen`s death renews a debate about the legacy of the British Empire. It was while a young Elizabeth was on official tour of Kenya in 1952. That`s when she finds word of -- she is found and given word of her father`s death.\par \par \uc2 She learned to her father`s death. She would become queen. The clampdown on Kenyans was began just months after the Queen ascended the throne and led to the establishment of vast system of detention camps that torture, rape, castration, and killing of tens of thousands of people. Of course, similar kinds of things happened in other -- in other countries.\par \par \uc2 Race also feels like a huge part of this and particularly race with respect to the global south, to Africa, which is colonized to India, of course, and other places and the Rudyard Kipling White Man`s Burden. That seems to suffuse a lot of the understandable resentment people have towards the crown that we`ve seen kind of bubbled up in the wake of this.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, I think we`ve seen over the course of the last three years not just in formerly British colonies, but around the world a reckoning, a desire perhaps to reckon with the past and the racial animus that has sometimes punctuated our past whether it`s in the United States or elsewhere throughout the world. And I think this is part of that. I mean, it is indelibly about race and racism and I think you can`t avoid that.\par \par \uc2 And even in the U.K., in modern times, there`s some real stuff there that needs to be unpacked. Like, you know, I remember the Windrush generation. These are guest workers who were brought to the U.K. in the wake of a post war labor shortage, and were unceremoniously deported back to their countries because they didn`t have the necessary paperwork in the 1970 to be allowed to stay in the U.K.\par \par \uc2 And people in the Caribbean really felt the sting of that to go over, to help, to feel like you are part of this family of nations that the Commonwealth purported to be, and then to find out you weren`t really a British citizen and you had to leave.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s also, of course, the fact that the Royal Family is now a multiracial family. And that itself is remarkably fraught because of the status of Harry and Meghan who the new king, of course, shout it out there. But my lord, I mean, I feel like that`s a lot of the subtext as well, of course, obviously.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, again, I don`t think all of it is about Harry and Meghan, but the fact that you have Windrush, you have this history of colonialism, and then you have this failure to really embrace the biracial member of your family and their multiracial children and to the extent that they actually exiled themselves to the United States, I mean, that`s really significant.\par \par \uc2 And I thought it was interesting that the new King Charles III -- that`s going to take some getting used to -- he mentioned them but also noted that they don`t seem to be coming back anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I thought that was a real -- there was a lot going on in that one sentence, but the "My love for Harry and Meghan" seem very intentional. And I was glad he said it actually.\par \par \uc2 Melissa Murray, you can come -- we can talk Royals anytime -- anything you want to talk about. Come on. It`s always great to have you.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN for this week. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220910ei9900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for September 7, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,533 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Drinking water should be an issue for everyone in Mississippi. And for God`s sake, they should care about their own capital. \uc2 EPA\uc2  Administrator Michael Regan, I really do appreciate you being here. Thank you for all you`re doing and I appreciate you. And please come back.\par \par \uc2 All right, everyone, that`s a wrap. I hope you guys enjoyed the Obama and Biden coming together today. I know I sure did. That is tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts right now.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. The full scope of Donald Trump`s purloined stash of classified documents keeps growing day by day. We`ve now of course learned that among the documents seized by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  from Mar-a-Lago was "A document describing a foreign government`s military defenses, including its nuclear capabilities." That`s according to \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  citing sources familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 The Post also notes "Some of the seized documents detailed Top Secret U.S. operations so closely guarded that many senior national security officials are kept in the dark about them. Only the president and some members of his cabinet or a near cabinet-level official could authorize other government officials to know details of these Special Access Programs.\par \par \uc2 Now, Trump`s fellow Republicans would already stuck their neck out for him without knowing all the facts, always dangerous, were left exposed, struggling to explain why it was actually fine that the ex-President kept nuclear documents in the basement of his Florida Country Club.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE TURNER\uc2  (R-OH): It depends on what the nuclear information is. You can say nuclear weapons, but there are things that are highly, highly classified. There`s things that are not extremely classified, but are nonetheless classified. The other aspect with respect to access is we don`t know what those documents are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , right, had previously reported last month that investigators were looking for information related to nuclear programs and the search for Mar-a-Lago. That`s what Congressman Turner was responding to there. But again, at the time, it was unclear if the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had actually found what they`re looking for.\par \par \uc2 The initial report was a pretty massive deal, hence the response from Turner there. But then things quieted down for a little while. And again, all the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  previously reported was they were looking for nuclear docs. Trump, of course, did what he does best, he used an information vacuum to try and shift the discussion, in this case, appearing to outright lie.\par \par \uc2 Back on August 12, the ex-president took to the MAGA \uc2 Twitter\uc2  knockoffs to declare "Nuclear weapons issue was a hoax." Incidentally, that`s the same post where he suggested the \uc2 FBI\uc2  planted documents at Mar-a-Lago, an excuse which he seems to have dropped in recent weeks. Now, at least according to \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , we know it was not a hoax. And even with a month to repair, Republicans are still struggling to respond to these latest allegations.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you think it`s appropriate the way that the former president was storing those top secret and classified documents at his private estate at Mar-a-Lago?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Yes, I don`t really have any comments on this. This whole investigation has been dominating the news for the last month. I think we`re following it like all of you are.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: These are classified documents, Mr. Leader. I mean, that`s important. You don`t have any comment on that?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Just sitting here following it all like you. I mean, I`m \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . I`m basically just a dude. Now, the revelations comedy pivotal moment for the investigation into those stolen documents. This week, in a widely criticized and ridiculed ruling, a Trump-appointed federal judge in Florida bent over backwards to accommodate Trump`s late request for so- called Special Master to review the documents seized by the DOJ weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 Now, she even went further. She did something that almost no one had ever heard of which is she enjoined the \uc2 FBI\uc2  from investigating. She ordered them, you have to stop investigating. Many legal observers point out that decision which seems detached from reality and law, could also harm investigators attempts to do a damage assessment even though she said OK, yes, you can do that part of it.\par \par \uc2 And on the point of the damage assessment, it should not be forgotten that Trump taking these documents is a massive breach of federal secrets. It has to be, at least as far as we know, probably the biggest since the Edward Snowden case, the former NSA contractor leaked tens of thousands of documents, and he did it to blow the whistle on what he viewed as immoral, illegal surveillance campaign by the federal government.\par \par \uc2 Now, Snowden was ideologically motivated. Whatever you think of him, his motive was pretty clear. He also did it kind of out in the open. I mean, we know he was doing it. We have no idea why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took these documents, or why he apparently refused to give them back, or why he apparently lied to investigators about getting it back, or why you had his lawyer sign a document saying you have all of them but didn`t actually give them all.\par \par \uc2 We don`t even know what these documents are. Just that they are reportedly among some of the most closely held secrets our country has. We also don`t yet know what the damage to the nation`s security might be as a result of Trump`s actions.\par \par \uc2 Olivia Troye\uc2  served as Homeland Security Adviser to former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . Glenn Gerstell is -- served as the General Counsel at the \uc2 National Security Agency\uc2  from 2015 to 2020. They both join me now. Glenn, let me start with you on this kind of damage assessment aspect.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:19]\par \par \uc2 GLENN GERSTELL, FORMER GENERAL COUNSEL, NATIONAL SECURITY AGENCY: Sure.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What`s your reaction, given your experience to the reporting about this particular document, but also, what must be going on right now as people inside the Intelligence Community try to figure out what got out where, it is, and who might have seen it?\par \par \uc2 GERSTELL: Thanks, Chris. You`ve addressed absolutely the key issue that the Intelligence Community must deal with first. We need to find out what was in the classified documents, both in terms of their substance so we can see what secrets might possibly have been compromised, as well as the sources and methods. How did we find out about that particular information?\par \par \uc2 So, these comments about -- that you alluded to earlier in the program about, well, maybe the documents aren`t all that important, that the secrets aren`t all that big a deal, in some cases, that might even be true. But what may be really secret is how we found out that information, what networks were penetrated, what telephone systems were accessed. Maybe that`s the real part of the secret.\par \par \uc2 So, the first test -- the first task, rather, is for our Intelligence Committee to find out what was in the documents, and then figure out if there`s any way of reaching a conclusion that we`re -- we feel comfortable that no one had access to them. And that sounds like a really difficult problem. That`s made even worse by the fact that in some cases, we`re not even sure what documents may be missing from these files.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I want to follow up on that in a second. Olivia, I want to get to you, though, next about Congressman \uc2 Mike Turner\uc2  who`s on the Intel Committee as you saw. And, you know, I got to imagine Republican politicians and officials who work in national security who have clearance who take it seriously and there are many of them, privately are pretty aghast at this because everyone that I`ve spoken to in that world is pretty freaked out, pretty aghast.\par \par \uc2 And they`re not going to come out and say it. But the McConnell non common today seemed to me to indicate he at least understands it`s not very smart to go out on the limb defending what happened here.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA TROYE, FORMER HOMELAND SECURITY ADVISER TO FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Absolutely. Anyone who has worked near, anywhere near the national security apparatus or understand the Intelligence Community at any length understand that this is a horrifying situation that puts potentially lives at risk and puts, honestly, the lives of all Americans at risk. We have no idea what was in those documents.\par \par \uc2 And it also puts or intelligence sharing with partners, foreign nation partners at risk because why would they ever trust us again when they`re watching this? I mean, it`s a complete embarrassment for our country on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Glenn, you talked about getting a handle on what there is. Now, clearly, there was some indication -- it seemed clear to me that some folks inside the intelligence community had an idea of what might be out there when they kept trying to get some back, right? So, Trump gives them finally in January a bunch of boxes, they`re kind of freaked out by that. They refer to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . They send the Chief of Counterintelligence down there who gets another 38 documents. Then they serve the warrant, right? This is all -- you know, they keep ratcheting up.\par \par \uc2 My question to you is, is there some list somewhere the way that like, you know, a company knows how many laptops they`ve got to their employees with the serial numbers of like, what`s out there floating around?\par \par \uc2 GERSTELL: It would certainly be nice if there was such a list, but there isn`t. And that`s one of the scariest aspects of this entire affair, which is, in the case of the documents that we know that have been retrieved in the various times, we can take a look at them and make an assessment, make a judgment of just how bad is this, and make a decision as to whether we need to change codes, whether we need to pull agents out of a foreign country, or what we need to do in response to that.\par \par \uc2 But in the case of documents that are missing, we really don`t have a handle on that. It`s unknowable. We don`t know what`s missing. And that`s the most serious part of this problem here because it`s just completely unknowable. And, you know, if I lose my wallet, and I`m not really sure exactly what`s in my wallet, well, OK, I could cancel all my credit cards and just assume that maybe they`re all at risk. But when I don`t even know what`s missing, it`s a different problem.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. That strikes me as a very different problem. I mean, there`s also the question now, Olivia, I have avoided pretty assiduously the question of motive because it doesn`t strike me as particularly relevant for the legal ramifications or even really the national security ramifications, though, I think it starts to become irrelevant, but at a certain point, it is the elephant in the room.\par \par \uc2 This wasn`t haphazard. This wasn`t just like, I was packing fast. This is hundreds of documents, some of the most closely held secrets that was -- he was deceptive about it, he lied about it he refused a subpoena about it, they got himself -- like, why, why, why? And I -- you know, I don`t know -- you don`t necessarily have any insight of this, but it does seem like we got to get to the motive at some point.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:22]\par \par \uc2 TROYE: Right. And it`s hard to know. Is it -- are these just souvenirs to say, oh, show and tell? That`s bad enough, right, as he`s sitting at dinner talking about it, as he showing them off? Is he -- I mean, just think about that scenario, right, when he`s sitting there, because that`s who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is. Secondly, like, where did these documents go? And were they sold to someone? Were they being used as leverage?\par \par \uc2 These are all the questions that goes through my mind, especially when it comes to the fact that we now know that there was nuclear information related to a foreign country. And I`m sure that those countries right now, let`s think about Israel. Let`s think about them sitting there right now wondering where that went, who knows about it? Is it theirs? Did they share it? Did they not share it? Did we acquire it through subdomains? I mean, there are so many questions here.\par \par \uc2 And let`s think about the Saudis. \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  is very close to the Saudis, the Trump administration. There were a lot of ties there that happened during -- that I heard, that I was there for internally, and saw. You know, I covered counterterrorism and Middle East politics at times because they were related to counterterrorism issues. And I certainly know the people that covered that topic.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, when I think about this, I think what was the point of this? Why? Why would you take these documents. And this group of people, they do this in a very calculated matter. We`ve all seen this. We`ve seen how they behave. So, they`re certainly -- I just hope that we get to the bottom of this and that there is -- the damage is minimal and not as grave as what many of us probably believe it to be.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. I have this image of, you know, Donald Trump`s sliding into the ballroom at Mar-a-Lago where a young couple has booked it for a wedding, which he would often do, and just sort of like pulling out the folded piece of paper to the bride and being like you want to see with the Israelis launched -- have their nukes? I mean, completely possible, you know what I mean? If that`s the case, 100 percent would believe it.\par \par \uc2 Olivia Troy, Glenn Gerstell, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 OK, this is the part of the show where I give you a quick glimpse into the story we`re going to cover next. It`s called a tease. And here`s tonight`s tease. Are you ready? Up next, new details on Steve Bannon`s surrender to authorities. I think a mistake for that one. See on the flip side.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Among the 100 plus people that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  granted clemency to during his final hours in office, a fact that I had a sort of memory hold a little bit, to be honest, was his one-time campaign chairman, later White House adviser \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 . Now, Bannon was arrested by federal agents back in the summer of 2020 accused of defrauding Trump supporters. These are -- these are diehard Trump-loving donors, defrauded them out of millions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 Donors believed they were giving money towards the construction of a wall along the Mexican border to fulfill the promise that Trump had not. And the money didn`t do that. But then Trump came along, wipe Bannon`s charges away with the stroke of a pen as he is empowered to do by the U.S. Constitution, clearing the way for Bannon to take on a new role, which is -- was key sponsor of Trump`s attempted coup, or at least he was doing that while Trump was preparing to wipe it away.\par \par \uc2 Now, Bannon may be back in legal jeopardy reportedly for the same crime this time under a state indictment, New York State, by the Manhattan district attorney. According to \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , "The precise details of the state case could not be confirmed. The people familiar with the situation suggests the prosecution will likely mirror aspects of the federal case in which he was pardoned.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Bannon said the district attorney was pushed suing "phony charges," adding "I have not yet begun to fight." But just a short time ago, CNBC got word from Benton`s lawyer that he was, "in route to surrender." Lots of questions about what we`re expecting to play out tomorrow. I`m glad to have two experts with me at the table to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Roiphe\uc2  is a former Manhattan district attorney. And Charles Coleman is a former Brooklyn Assistant District Attorney. And they both join me now. It`s good to have you both here. Rebecca, let me start with you as someone who worked as assistant district attorney in Manhattan, which I think is going to be prosecuting this case.\par \par \uc2 The big question here is double jeopardy, right? The Constitution bars you being tried twice for the same crime. Obviously, there`s different jurisdictions. What is the constitutional lay of the land about the feasibility of this prosecution?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA ROIPHE, FORMER MANHATTAN DISTRICT ATTORNEY: Right. So I think the legal issues are not as complicated as they were. For instance, when \uc2 Paul Manafort\uc2  faced charges in federal court and state court. First of all, because \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  wasn`t actually convicted, he was pardoned before he was convicted in federal court. And second of all, because the law in New York has subsequently changed basically closing up a loophole that New York had previously created for somebody who had been pardoned and using that for double jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 So, normally, a state and a federal charge wouldn`t be considered double jeopardy, but New York had fairly strong laws suggested that it would. Now, they don`t have those same laws.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In terms of the strength of the case, there were two other people that were arrested and tried by the federal government, Department of Justice, for their role in this scheme. Both of them have pleaded guilty, which, you know, if I were Bannon, that would not augur well for me, I would imagine.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES COLEMAN, FORMER BROOKLYN ASSISTANT DISTRICT ATTORNEY: Absolutely, Chris. You know, prosecutors are among the most underappreciated and maligned group to public servants. And \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  was in the public eye if you recall a while back when the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  investigation cooled. and he was taking a lot of heat, some of them from his own ADAs about not continuing with the investigation.\par \par \uc2 What we`re seeing here is the fact that D.A. Bragg understands there are multiple routes to justice. And what he`s doing, in this case, is going after, in a very strong way, people who are associated with Trump for things that they did, and he`s holding them accountable. The fact that there were two other people connected to this case, who were convicted is clear evidence, if you will, that there`s a strong case and that there`s something there. Where there`s smoke, there`s fire.\par \par \uc2 And so, regardless of the public persona and the posturing that \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  is actually doing in public, he has to understand that he`s facing a very serious uphill legal battle with respect to these potential charges.\par \par \uc2 [20:20:45]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s very weird to me his posture. I mean, obviously, he -- I think he thinks it`s good for his bottom line. I don`t know if anyone`s told him that you can`t podcast in prison and they only give you one shirt not two. But you know, he`s already facing like the possibility of time in prison for his conviction in criminal contempt where he refused. I mean, he could have gotten it and just said, fifth, fifth, fifth, fifth, fifth like \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2  did. He could have tried to, like, litigate something.\par \par \uc2 He could basically just said, like, go take a long walk off a short pier to Congress, and he was convicted by a jury of his peers. He`s now facing sentencing for that, right?\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: Yes, completely. And I mean, I think part of what`s happening here is that there`s a political battle to be won, and there`s a legal battle to be won. And these people are fighting the political battle and ignoring the legal battle, potentially, because the legal battle is one that they are not going to win that he is just, you know, dead to -- dead, to words there.\par \par \uc2 And so, he`s essentially trying to play this in the public, but ultimately, he`s going to have to face a judge and a jury, and you know, whatever he`s doing in public isn`t going to work for him anymore.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Well, Chris, if I may, on that point, a big part of the Trump playbook and anyone associated with him, particularly \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  is narrative control.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, right.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Because they know that with respect to mainstream media, if you will, their supporters, their followers, they`re not following that. They`re getting information straight from them. And so, the idea of being able to control this public narrative and say, look, this is the weaponized justice system that has been politicized ---\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Totally.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: -- 60 days before election, it`s right out of their playbook.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. And I think -- I mean, what`s weird about this is -- and again, I don`t I don`t really care one way or the other about what Steve Bannon`s fate is, but you know, yes, I mean, I think he`s probably, you know, he issued this preposterous statement that it looks like it was like written by a Bond villain or something. It just seems to me that, right, you can probably raise a bunch of money off your, you know, your devoted podcast fan base, but at a certain point, like, that`s not going to work. Like, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , like, I don`t think you really want to go to jail in New York State.\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Am I wrong? I don`t know. Maybe he really does. But I don`t think you do.\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: Right. I mean, I think that there`s just a complete denial of what this is actually doing. And I also think that narrative about the, you know, weaponization of the -- of prosecution is working less and less. And this is the bread and butter of local DA`s office. This is a straightforward fraud case. And so, it becomes harder and harder to claim that this is politically motivated when what you`re accused of doing is just theft.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Also, we should note, the victims here are ardent, diehard Trump supporters who gave money to an organization called We Build The Wall. Another legal development today that I wanted to get you guys -- just your thoughts on. This involves a federal investigation, of course, federal criminal investigation.\par \par \uc2 We know that there`s a grand jury in Washington, D.C. that`s investigating January 6. We know some of the people that have come before to testify, including Marc Short, chief staff of \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . We know that \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  came last week, of course, the president`s -- today, we got this interesting kind of incremental development at the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  that \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents in Florida tried to interview William S. Russell who served as a special assistant and the deputy director of advance in the White House and continue to work for Mr. Trump as a personal aide after he left office.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is someone who doesn`t -- isn`t as high on the org chart as the White House Counsel, but presumably as someone who`s like, very close and around \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  all the time. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: I think that for prosecutors, for investigators, for the committee, it is a win-win. And it`s a win-win because you`re not going to have the barriers and the obstacles of executive privilege or attorney- client privilege that have held up some of the questioning with respect to the committee in the investigation in the past. This is someone who doesn`t have a privilege but has all the information because they were just as close to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as anyone else.\par \par \uc2 So, they can talk about what he knew. They can talk about what he said. They can talk about what he did. And there is no barrier to prevent them from giving that information.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I hadn`t thought about that. Interesting.\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: I think that`s right. And I also think that, you know, the people who have the most information are not always the people who are the highest step. I mean, there`s a lot of ways to --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. Cassidy Hutchinson who was not super high in the org chart.\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: And you know, when you`re a prosecutor, you can get value -- really valuable pieces of information. And often it`s connecting dots, but sometimes it`s vital information for a prosecution that comes from somebody like a secretary.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, I just don`t -- look, I`ve said this before on the show. You know, probability, you know, the odds of independent things happening, right, to get two things to happen independently, right? Like, it`s greater odds, you multiply them. It`s like the odds are just skating out of all the various traps. Whether your Bannon who now is like staring down the barrel of sentencing on a criminal contempt and is now turning himself into a prosecution New York state where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was facing, like, stolen documents he was in possession of. And he`s also got this January 6. Like, both of them, I would not be in -- I would not be feeling particularly confident.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:37]\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: You know, as a lawyer, we are taught very, very seldom do we make predictions or strong predictions. But we were talking during the break, and I feel very comfortable saying this. \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  is going to jail one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You really think that?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Yes, I do believe so.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, I mean, I think he`s going to get sentenced in the -- in the contempt case Either way, so yes.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Exactly. He`s going to jail for something. Either it is going to be because of this contempt case, as well as the case in Manhattan, or a combination of both. The options and the walls are just becoming so slim and closing in so fast that he really does not have any recourse from a legal place.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What do you think?\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: Yes, I agree with that. I mean, you know, I don`t know where the fraud case is going to go. And with the fraud prosecution, you don`t -- you never want to take odds because there`s always a question of intent. We don`t know what they have. But if it was a strong federal case, then it`s a strong state case. These are basically the same conduct. They`re overlapping laws. And so, you know, I think that since the federal government felt confident about bringing these charges, I think it`s probably likely that they have a pretty good case in Manhattan as well.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. You also wonder if Brian Kolfage who was the disabled vet who is implicated in this and who pleaded guilty and is going to be set to be sentenced in December might be talking. It would be interesting to see that. \uc2 Rebecca Roiphe\uc2 , Charles Coleman, thank you. That was great.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, he is the most important Republican you`ve never heard of, or at least until we`ve started talking about him maybe. How one man is threatening American democracy as we know it, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA, FORMER FIRST LADY, UNITED STATES: Traditions like this matter, not just for those of us who hold these positions, but for everyone participating in and watching our democracy. You see the people, they make their voices heard with their vote, we hold an inauguration to ensure a peaceful transition of power. Those of us lucky enough to serve work, as Barack said, as hard as we can for as long as we can as long as the people choose to keep us here. And once our time is up, we move on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Former First Lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  and her husband, the Former President, were at the White House today for the unveiling of their official portraits. Now, that ceremony probably should have happened a few years ago. It was delayed because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just chose not to host the Obamas despite the long-standing bipartisan tradition of doing so.\par \par \uc2 What the former First Lady said about democracy is particularly important as a notably, virulently anti-democratic movement takes root on the political right. And a particular threat of that movement larger than even \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- and I know that`s crazy thing to say -- is a bizarre fringe idea that conservatives started warming up to after they lost the 2020 election, called the Independent State Legislature Theory. `\par \par \uc2 It was an idea infamously pushed by coup memo author John Eastman and his allies in Congress which basically says state legislatures, according to U.S. Constitution, are the ultimate final arbiters of how elections are running the state. They have, as Eastman like to say, plenary power, absolute power. The government doesn`t matter. The Secretary of State doesn`t matter. The State Supreme Court that interprets the election law and the state constitution doesn`t matter. None of it matters. Only the state legislature regulates the rules of federal elections, which in its most extreme form, would mean the Wisconsin legislature, which we discussed last night, wildly gerrymandered, could just decide it wanted to give its electors to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , even though state law and the state Constitution required to give those electors to the winner of the popular vote in the state, but they can just say, no, we don`t want to do that.\par \par \uc2 Now, this was quite famously one of the crank theories coup lawyer John Eastman was proposing to help Trump pull off its coup. And it is a loaded gun aimed directly at the heart of American`s self-governance. It`s now set to be heard by the Supreme Court in the upcoming turn in a case from North Carolina called Moore V. Harper.\par \par \uc2 Essentially, North Carolina Republicans are upset that the State Supreme Court threw out their wildly gerrymandered congressional districts. And they want the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  to reverse the decision based on the independent state legislature theory which is that the State Supreme Court doesn`t have any power over them.\par \par \uc2 The theory is being boosted by a conservative group called the, sirens, Honest Elections Project. And when you hear that, you know what you`re getting. It is filed what is known as an amicus brief trying to sway the court. And the group is backed by none other than this man you see here, the infamous \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2 . You may remember him as the guy who had a role in stacking the court with each and every conservative Supreme Court Justice seat on the bench. He is the same guy who, as we featured on the show, was just handed $1.6 billion in tax-free, untraceable dark money, a transaction that was of course itself made legal impossible by the right-wing court he helped bring about.\par \par \uc2 And now, the organization linked to \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2 , will help argue to justices \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2  helped install about why the legislature of North Carolina should just be able to do whatever it wants to the state`s elections, regardless of democracy.\par \par \uc2 Rick Hasen is Professor of Law at UCLA School of Law, director of the Safeguarding Democracy Project. And he joins me now. Rick, first, did I get anything wrong there since this is -- it`s a fairly technical uncomplicated case? The theory is kind of bizarre, so please correct me if I misstated anything.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:30]\par \par \uc2 RICK HASEN, LAW PROFESSOR, UCLA SCHOOL OF LAW: No, I think you basically stated the right thing. I would just say that even some of the more extreme versions of the independent state legislature theory would not support what John Eastman was trying to do, which was to say, even after the legislature had chosen, we`re going to have a popular vote for president, yes, you could take that back and start it over. So, that`s more extreme. And I didn`t see any of the amicus briefs going quite that far, even John Eastman`s brief.\par \par \uc2 John Eastman, himself filed a brief on behalf of Claremont Institute`s Constitutional Center in this case as well.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, that`s important, right? The Eastman theory was so -- was out past even the realm of this sort of radical position here. But it would -- if enough of the Court were to side with the North Carolina Republicans here and with Leonard Leo`s group, what would the implications be for American democracy?\par \par \uc2 HASEN: Well, first, they`d be pretty dire, even putting aside the potential for election subversion, right? It means that state Supreme Courts couldn`t apply the state constitution to limit the state legislature from violating people`s voting rights. It would empower two bodies. One, it was empowering -- it would empower state legislatures. they could run roughshod over electro administrators over you know, all state actors. So they would be empowered.\par \par \uc2 And the other one it would empower is the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  because it would turn every dispute about what an election law means in a state as applied to a federal election into a federal constitutional question. And the ultimate arbiter of that is, of course, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . So, it`s this kind of feedback loop where the court is not only giving more power to state legislators, it would be giving more power to itself.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And it`s -- yes. And we should note here, like, you know, often there`s sort of arguments about states` rights or deference to the states, and there`s, you know, conservative attacks on the overuse of the Commerce Clause in the U.S. Constitution for the federal government to overly regulate the states. Here, they`re doing something very scalpeled, right, very fine edge. It`s like, well, the institutions we think have the power here are the state legislators, but not the State Supreme Courts. We like the legislators, because we -- there are people and not the Supreme Courts. But then also the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , because we like that one too.\par \par \uc2 Like, it does look like a theory reverse engineered around the institutional strength of conservatives and the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 HASEN: That`s absolutely true. And you know, one of the things the elections clause in the Constitution, Article One, Section Four, if you want to look it up, says that state legislatures get to set the manner for conducting congressional elections subject to congressional override.\par \par \uc2 Now, nobody thinks it`s an independent Congress theory where Congress can just go out and act without the president, you know, signing a bill or that the Congress is not constrained by the federal constitution. But it does recognize a role for Congress and Missouri. \uc2 Jay Ashcroft\uc2 , the secretary of state of Missouri filed a brief that went even further than the Honest Elections Project brief. He says that not even Congress can act.\par \par \uc2 And he`s got this theory that only state legislators can act. They`re the only ones. And he said, could you imagine if they could turn a seven Republican, one Democratic district into a 4-4 district, four Democrats, four Republicans? That was the parade of horribles he was offering, which kind of tells you what the mindset is right there.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, as historical matter, it`s ludicrous that this is what the original public meaning of the 14th Amendment at least as it -- you know, as it pertains here or anything having to do with the Reconstruction Amendments were when they talked about voting, right? Like, they understood that they were giving the federal government power to do a lot of this stuff.\par \par \uc2 There`s also -- there`s also a fascinating amicus brief that I want to feature as well which is a very strange and rare one, which is all of the Chief Justices of each state in the union, all 50 states -- so this includes like Mississippi, and Alabama, and New York, and California, the full gamut of the politics of this great nation, all unanimously filing an amicus brief saying this is nonsense, absolutely don`t do this, right?\par \par \uc2 HASEN: Right. So, this is a conference of chief justices. They don`t find a lot of briefs. This one was approved by a committee of Chief Justices from I think all from red states. And they see this as just completely unworkable. Because if what the courts going to say is, well, you can apply, you know, a clear part of the state constitution but not an unclear part, there`s just going to be, you know, a ton of litigation in federal courts and state courts that`s going to put the federal courts and state courts on a collision path.\par \par \uc2 They see this as unnecessary and also just not the way we normally think about state legislatures that they are creatures of the state constitution. The state constitution creates them and limits them. This is this idea that they`ve kind of this free-floating entity that exists on its own that have limitless power. And the Chief Justices, although they filed the brief on behalf of neither party, it`s very clear that they are not supporting the theory that the North Carolina legislators have put forward here.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:40]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Finally, you know, that there was an earlier case in which this was raised in some of the post-election litigation in which three justices, I believe it was Alito, Thomas, and Gorsuch indicated some interest in this. How do you see the receptivity of this court to this?\par \par \uc2 HASEN: So, the three of them expresses great interest in it, I would be surprised if they didn`t take on a maximalist view. Justice Kavanaugh is what I would call independent state legislature theory curious. And so, the real question is, where`s the Chief Justice and where is Justice Kavanaugh? And there, you know, it`s not just about the text and original public meaning, it`s also about the burden this will put on the court and how it would undermine both confidence in elections and confidence in the judiciary.\par \par \uc2 Remember, the judiciary -- the judiciary took a big hit after the Dobbs case came out. So, there`s a lot of institutional legitimacy arriving on the line here in terms of how the court decides this case as well.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Rick Hasen, thank you. That was really, really, really clarifying. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HASEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, after spending half a century working to overturn Roe v Wade to criminalize abortion, make it illegal and unlawful, suddenly, some Republicans can`t wait to talk about anything but. The latest candidate attempting a political U-turn next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When American Palestinian journalist Shireen Abu Akleh was shot and killed in May of this year, people across the U.S. and the Middle East erupted in grief. Shireen was a beloved icon, a household name in the Arab world. She was a veteran journalist who covered the Israeli-Palestinian conflict on the frontlines for 25 years.\par \par \uc2 And on May 11, she was doing that again, she was in the West Bank covering an \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  raid on the Jenin Refugee Camp. Abu Akleh was wearing a helmet and a protective vest labeling her as a member of the press when shots rang out and she was struck in the head. At the time, eyewitnesses including fellow journalists who are with her in Jenin said that they believe she was shot by an Israeli soldier. They also said that there were no Palestinian fighters present in the area contrary to Israeli claims that both sides were under fire before Abu Akleh was shot.\par \par \uc2 Now, Israeli officials said that it was not immediately clear who fired the bullet but it was possible that a Palestinian gunman had. U.S. officials were deferential to the Israeli, supporting an investigation by their military. Others, including Shireen Abu Akleh`s family called for an independent probe.\par \par \uc2 Well now, almost four months later, the Israeli military has finally announced its findings. They admit that the account given by the eyewitnesses at the time and then subsequently by multiple news outlets that investigated the shooting was likely correct. According to the Israeli military, there is a "high probability an Israeli soldier shot and killed Shireen Abu Akleh.\par \par \uc2 They say it was a mistake with an official telling the Associated Press the soldier "misidentified her." The military has turned over its findings to Israel`s chief prosecutor who "was satisfied with them and decided against a criminal investigation." That means no one will face charges for Shireen Abu Akleh`s death.\par \par \uc2 Here in the U.S., the State Department has responded with suggestion that Israel review its rules of engagement.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VEDANT PATEL, PRINCIPAL DEPUTY SPOKESPERSON, U.S. DEPARTMENT OF STATE: We continue to raise directly with Israel that closely review its policies and practices on the rules of engagement to take additional steps to mitigate risks to protect journalists, to protect civilian harm, and to ensure that similar tragedies don`t happen in the future.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 Shireen Abu Akleh`s family who have been calling for independent investigation and accountability for their slain family member from the very beginning are angry about Israel`s recent statements and about this new finding, which they say are an attempt to "obscure the truth and avoid responsibility." They go on to renew their call for action saying apart, "since Shireen was killed, our family has called for a thorough, independent, and credible U.S. investigation that leads to accountability which is the bare minimum the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  should do for one of their own citizens.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Since the Supreme Court overturn Roe v. Wade and got rid of federal abortion protections, anti-abortion Republicans have faced intense backlash. Some have gone to great lengths to hide their previously stated positions. Here`s the latest example, Republican Scott Jensen. Jensen is running for governor of Minnesota, also currently running as fast as he can away from his previous extreme positions on abortion. Now, here`s what he told Minnesota Public Radio just in March.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE MULCAHY, RADIO HOST: If you were governor, would you try to impose new restrictions on abortion or would you try to ban it outright?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT JENSEN (R-MN), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: I would try to ban abortion. I think that we`re basically in a situation where we should be governed by -- there is no reason for us to be having abortions going on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I would try to ban abortion. There is no reason for us to have abortions going on. Weird syntax but very clear, fairly straightforward, black and white. That guy right now trails his opponent, incumbent Democratic Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  by 18 points according to recent polling. And while we should take polling with an enormous grain of salt, it could be why Jensen released this new ad possibly trying to make up some ground.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:04]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENSEN: I`ve delivered 500 babies in my career. Abortion is divisive. And \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  is weaponizing the issue. In Minnesota, it`s a protected constitutional right and no governor can change that. And I`m not running to do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, first of all, it`s weird to be holding a baby in your ad. I mean, I get -- you know, he delivers babies. I get that. But like, it`s weird. Like, it makes me uncomfortable. Like, just focus on -- focus on one thing. Focus on the ad or focus on the baby. But also, I don`t think that`s going to cut it. In March, you wanted to ban abortion. You said it very clearly, there`s no reason we should have abortions. He says he`s not running do that now? Will Minnesota voters buy it?\par \par \uc2 Shefali Luthra is a health care reporter for The 19th, an independent news organization, where she`s been covering the political backlash in the wake of the Dobbs decision. And she joins me now. Shefali, you know, Minnesota is interesting case. Obviously, it is not a swing state in the same way that like Wisconsin is, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  came very close in 2016, quite famously. And it`s an interesting test case of how the politics of this are playing there. And it seems like Jensen is aware that he`s not on the right side of this right now.\par \par \uc2 SHEFALI LUTHRA, HEALTH CARE REPORTER, THE 19TH: I think you`re correct. And what we see in Minnesota, right, is the latest example in a trend. We have seen candidates, Republican candidates in Arizona, in North Carolina, in Michigan try to backtrack from their professor desire to ban abortion, often all circumstances, often with no exception for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Now, many things have changed right now. This is a general election, not a primary. The overturn of Roe v. Wade is no longer theoretical. It`s real. And there`s all sorts of reasons that you`d want if you are a Republican candidate to avoid adopting such an unpopular position. But what I want to emphasize is that this isn`t new for the Republican Party. Since the day that Roe was overturned, we saw the Republican Senatorial campaign arm advising candidates to say that they would not criminalize doctors for performing abortion.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: And that simply isn`t true. Republicans know that, in many ways, they are not on the popular side of this issue. And that`s why they are hesitant to talk about it. And when they are, they are trying to change what their positions have been.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, one of the most striking examples of this is in -- there`s two states in the Midwest that have -- that have different situations. So, Wisconsin has an old law that has now the law of the land, so you can`t access abortion in Wisconsin right now. And \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2  is -- here he is. He`s the Republican who`s very ardently anti-abortion, has given millions to anti-abortion cases. Like, there`s no question this guy can`t run away from it. Here he is saying he support the ninth -- 1849 Law, which is the law that has been triggered as his position. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Abortion could be headed back to states for control in Wisconsin. It would be the 1849 Law. Would you sign any legislation? Some Republicans -- Speaker Vos has said he would support adding exceptions for rape and incest. Would you sign that into law?\par \par \uc2 TIM MICHELS (R-WI), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: So, I`m pro-life. It comes from my faith. And I believe that we have to protect the unborn. The 1849 Law is an exact mirror of my position. And my position is an exact mirror of the 1849 Law as well, which has an exception for life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But you wouldn`t support exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 MICHELS: That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, it`s pretty striking. I mean, this is going to be a test case in a very closely divided state whether being on the side of the 1849 Law, the 1849 Law with no exceptions for rape or incest is a winning issue for him.\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: Just today in Michigan, right, we saw a court block enforcement of their pre-Roe abortion ban which dates back to the 1930s. We are seeing courts have really immense influence in determining whether abortion is available in the state or not. One thing that I do want to emphasize further is we know that banning abortion with no exception for rape and incest is incredibly unpopular. Very, very, very few Americans support this even in states with abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 But I do want to note that even in states that have banned abortion and include a nominal exception for cases of rape or incest, these are very difficult to enforce. Rape is very rarely reported. You often require verification from a doctor or law enforcement. And in practice, if you are enforcing an abortion ban with or without rape or incest exceptions, the effect is largely the same. And it goes against what many voters support.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s a -- it`s a really important point. We`ve seen someone like Mehmet Oz come out and say yes, I support these exemptions -- you know these exemptions, but in practice, like you have just survived sexual assaults, you are pregnant, you are going to some law enforcement person to get some piece of paper signing off.\par \par \uc2 I mean, just the actual reality on the ground of that is also pretty horrifying actually to contemplate when you think about what it actually looks like when implemented. Shefali Luthra who`s been doing great reporting on this topic, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Wednesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220908ei9700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for September 5, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Andrea Mitchell, Ryan Reilly, Kelly O`Donnell, Mike Memoli, James  Stavridis \par \uc2 9,152 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: And thank you for staying with us for this hour of "CHRIS JANSING REPORTS," I`m Andrea Mitchell in for Chris today.\par \par \uc2 And following the breaking news from Florida where a Trump-appointed federal judge has granted the former president`s legal team its request for a special master to review the items seized by \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents during their search of Mr. Trump`s Mar-a-Lago home in August. At this hour, an individual has not been named by Judge Cannon as the Special Master that will be submitted to the judge. There will be a meeting, I believe, by September 9.\par \par \uc2 This hour, President Biden is expected to speak in Milwaukee. So when he arrived there, he will then later today be heading to Pittsburgh this afternoon. All part of his Labor Day campaign blitz. It was just a few months left to help make his party`s pitch to voters ahead of the November midterms.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now for more on the breaking news, NBC justice reporter Ryan Reilly, NBC senior White House correspondent Kelly O`Donnell, and joining us will be former prosecutor David Henderson as well.\par \par \uc2 Ryan, first to you. You`ve now had an hour -- we`ve given you an hour to look through this. I don`t know why you wouldn`t (INAUDIBLE). Let`s talk about what you`ve learned as we evolve in speed reading this report, which came -- this judge`s order which came out just about an hour ago, Ryan.\par \par \uc2 RYAN REILLY, MSNBC JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: That`s right. So I think you know, one of the citations here is they mentioned Rudy Giuliani`s special master and the idea of having someone look over the questions of executive privilege in that, and that was back in April of 2021. But that was a much more sort of limited circumstance because that was all -- because first of all, A., \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  was an attorney. So looking over his materials was much more complex because he had a talk -- he had to protect all the materials of talking with his clients after the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s searched Rudy Giuliani`s home and seized his cell phones.\par \par \uc2 But this is -- this would -- it could be a particularly cumbersome process when they open this up, this idea of looking at whether or not executive privilege claims should set some of these investigators in so -- some of these records they seized from Mar-a-Lago aside because this isn`t just Rudy Giuliani`s communications with the president, this is pretty much all of the material from the White House that had been seized and had been kept in Mar-a-Lago. So they`re going to have to make these calls about executive privilege on a boatload of documents that were never supposed to be in Trump`s possession in the first place.\par \par \uc2 So it really just -- I can`t imagine this is going to be an expedited process because you know, it`s one thing when you`re just talking about questions of executive -- of attorney-client privilege, but if you really opened this up to a special master looking through what we know is, you know, 11,000 pages of government documents and deciding on each one the questions of executive privilege, this can get complicated really, really quick, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 I just think it really could get really messy if that`s the path we go down. So I`m really curious to find out what DOJ ultimately decides to do here, whether or not they`re going to go for an appeal or whether they live with this because, you know, that`s sort of the whole investigation right here is whether or not he held on to these records that he was not supposed to have in the first place.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, Kelly, we haven`t seen or heard a direct response on this from former President Trump but this is a clear win for him and his legal team. They were criticized by a lot of people saying that they had opened up a Pandora`s Box, even asking for a master because that precipitated the filing by the Justice Department, and then the judge`s order telling them to be even more specific. So a lot was released that ordinarily would have been kept secret.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, it was a very fulsome response by the Department of Justice with a lot more detail that to everyday citizens able to read that and certainly people with more knowledge about how investigations work. So a lot of what appeared to be very damaging material against the former president and perhaps even some of his counsel because of what was put through that filing.\par \par \uc2 That gave us the evidence photo that allowed us to actually see evidence of classification markings on documents inside the Trump 45 Office as it`s known. Also a lot more detail about the numbers of documents and so forth. And that was all because the former president made this request and the response it drew from the Department of Justice gave us a lot more detail.\par \par \uc2 One of the things so far in this is the former president has not formally exerted -- asserted executive privilege on any of these documents yet. So that is something that would have to be litigated down the line. What the judge here, Judge Cannon, who was appointed by former President Trump when he was in office, she is saying that a special master needs to be included here in part because of the potential damage to reputation, to the potential of a material that could be given to investigators. That is personal material.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 And that the former president -- she really specifically writes about the fact that the nature of him being a former president, the unprecedented circumstance here, the media involvement, the scope of public interest is all so great that having an extra set of eyes, extra care, someone not involved in the investigative process, were not employed by the Department of Justice to review these documents is warranted. She says in her writing here that, yes, it is not ideal to have an investigation slow down, she is in joining the government to stop investigating using these documents, but allowing the assessment of risk to national security to go forward.\par \par \uc2 But she`s really calling out the fact that this is a search that was carried out at the home of a former president. And with that comes additional burdens of trying to bring about fairness and the perception of fairness. And we certainly know that that is something, Andrea, where the former president has amped that up to an extreme degree, where he has claimed he has been so unfairly treated even though the Department of Justice has gone through this. And the judge here finds that his constitutional rights have been protected thus far. So there`s so much to read in this. We have many more steps to come. But this is undeniably a victory for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  today.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, David Henderson, I want you to jump in here. This judge was appointed. We should point out she was a lame duck appointment and confirmation very late in the game after he had lost the election. I want to read an excerpt from the judge`s ruling. This came from a footnote that we spotted. In addition to being deprived of potentially significant personal documents, which alone creates a real harm Plaintiffs faces, and unquantifiable potential harm by way of improper disclosure of sensitive information to the public.\par \par \uc2 To have this investigation proceed without the master, in other words, but, David, there`s already been a lot of disclosure to the public precisely because the former president applied for the master, which led to the unusual justice woman affidavit, rather the -- not in the affidavit the brief to this judge opposing the master, which included a lot of more information about why they had justified -- this is supposed a justified search and what they had gotten.\par \par \uc2 And then the judge herself said last week that she wanted more details so they released even more, which is when we found out about the folders that said -- you know, the classified folders that were empty. So in a way, he brought a lot of disclosure upon himself just by going after this master already.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HENDERSON, FORMER PROSECUTOR: Andrea, that`s exactly right. I`m going to give you a quote from one of my favorite legal movies, which is A Few Good Men. There`s this great line that says there`s a difference between paper law and trial law. And part of the reason I`m saying that is because I`m a trial lawyer, and especially when we start talking about President Trump, you`ve always got to think about this in terms of the fact that he is going to push the legal process as far as he possibly can.\par \par \uc2 The special master was something that was always going to happen. I could have told you from the beginning that one was asked for, he was going to get it, it doesn`t matter who the judge is. Basically, he`s saying, hey, they showed up, they took a bunch of boxes and they`re arguing about whether or not the information in that box belongs to them, but some of that is mine and at the very least, somebody needs to sort through and see whether or not I have a -- I have a legitimate claim that some of it need to be returned back to me. He was always going to get that.\par \par \uc2 But the bigger problem here is that it pays DOJ, the Justice Department, further into a corner because enough hype has been built around this to where you are going to have to do something at the end of the day. And as reprehensible as much of this conduct is, speaking as a trial lawyer, you do not yet have a story that ultimately will lead everyday people to convict basic information you`ve got so far. The longer this gets discussed in the public, the further off their game. Prosecutors are going to be thrown because this is not their arena. This arena is much better suited for former reality TV star and that`s exactly what President Trump is.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And you don`t need to point out that the standard that the Justice Department goes by is that they don`t indict if they don`t think they can convict, to your point, David. Let me just play one prominent lawyer, none other than Bill Barr, the former Attorney General, who, of course, was with the president all through the Mueller probe and helped to -- help to sort of you know solve the public perception of the Mueller investigation with that premature release of his findings -- of his conclusions. But this is what he had to say about the search and about the president`s claim that he had the right to these classified documents.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL BARR, FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL: No, I can`t think of legitimate reasons why they should have been -- could be taken out of the government -- away from the government if they`re classified. How long is the government going to try to get that back? You know, they draw bones for a year.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 They were deceived on the voluntary actions taken. They then went and got a subpoena. They were deceived on that. They feel. And the record -- the facts are starting to show that they were being jerked around.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: So, David, how frustrated do you think the Justice Department officials are that not the -- you know, not the damage assessment by the Director of National Intelligence, but the investigation itself is put on hold?\par \par \uc2 HENDERSON: I mean, 100 percent, Andrea. And this is what it all comes down to. If you ever want to start a fistfight with a prosecutor, you accuse them of being dishonest or not acting with integrity, even when they actually are being dishonest. And so part of what we forget is when these administrations change over, maybe the upper echelons changes, but you`ve got people in the trenches who`ve been there for 10, 20, 30 years doing the same work. And every time their integrity gets questioned, their banging on the AG`s door, saying, hey, we need to respond to this, we need to reply.\par \par \uc2 They`re frustrated, they`re also going to be frustrated that this investigation is going to stop, and this is where it`s something you and I have discussed before it comes at play and that is, the law is a game of nerves and President Trump knows that. The more attention gets mounted, the more you run the risk that somebody is going to get so nervous, so frustrated, they make a mistake. And given where he stands, that`s what he is hoping for at this point.\par \par \uc2 Barr`s comments are directly on point. There was nothing wrong with this investigation. But we`re far outside the scope of a specific search warrant at this point. What he`s doing is forcing a greater reply, which they`re not necessarily in a position to provide and that`s not going to change even after this delay.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: So, Ryan Reilly, what do we expect from the Justice Department? They -- they`re looking at this over, do you think we`ll hear from them today?\par \par \uc2 REILLY: Potentially. I mean, it`s a -- it`s a delicate issue because typically they like to speak through the -- these court proceedings, they like to go through, you know, the four corners of the court, you know, not the indictment in this case, but of the court filing. But there is one line I wanted to point out from this judge`s ruling because you know, she`s a 41-year-old Trump-appointed judge, a pretty young judge, who was obviously appointed by the individual in question here. So a lot of complicating factors here.\par \par \uc2 But she does agree, actually, with the government on one point, which is that there`s not enough evidence right now that the government showed callous disregard for the former president`s -- the former president`s constitutional rights. She writes "the Court agrees with the government that at least based on the record to date, there has not been a compelling showing of callous disregard for plaintiff`s constitutional rights, this factor of cuts against the exercise of equitable jurisdiction."\par \par \uc2 So essentially saying here that they`re -- you know, based on the really lengthy record that the Justice Department has put out, that this was a good showing by DOJ why they -- why they took these steps. But basically, I think she is coming to the conclusion not necessarily based on just a strict reading of the law, but based upon these outside factors and the sort of precedent in nature of this that there`s a need for more disclosure and more oversight in this issue that could have really profound effects on this ongoing criminal investigation, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Ryan Reilly, David Henderson, Kelly O`Donnell, thanks to all of you.\par \par \uc2 And Biden on the midterm trail, the president in Milwaukee for his Labor Day fall campaign kickoff. It`s a live picture of the event. We expect to hear from him momentarily and we will bring it to you live. And a Democrat of Michigan, Congresswoman \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2  will be here to watch along with us. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: We`re waiting for remarks from President Biden in Milwaukee. First up in his pack Labor Day campaign scheduled with the midterms just months away. Joining us now, NBC White House correspondent Mike Memoli outside of Pittsburgh where the president will be next, Former Obama White House Press Secretary Robert Gibbs, and Michigan Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2 . Welcome, all.\par \par \uc2 Mike, first to you, your history of covering \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on Labor Day is replete with plenty of stops. We`ve got you in Pittsburgh, including this from Labor Day just -- I guess for you four years ago?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Four years ago, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Talk about what this means in terms of your political future here. Is this an audition for 2020?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My political future. I`ve been these guys my whole life and my grandfather, Finnegan strand, you say, Joe, your labor from belt buckle to shoot the old man. I go anywhere with these guys. These are guys that run me an advantage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: So the question, Mike, is are the labor guys still with him given just how red-ish Pennsylvania is going to all the polling, and according to the way, the vote went last time around?\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Yes, Andrea. This is obviously kind of a political home base for the president. This is not just where he had that audition for 202o, 4 years ago, it was where he held his first rally of his 2020 campaign, and his final rally with Lady Gaga of the 2020 campaign, as well. And so this is really an important day, an important moment for the president to start the Democrats off on a, you know, optimistic foot. He`s going to be talking about him being as he sees himself the most pro-union president in American history, but he`s also going to be talking about those economic successes of late including the inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 And it`s an important balancing act, Andrea. There are a lot of reasons why Democrats feel like maybe not that they no longer face headwinds. They wouldn`t say the wins that they`re back, but they certainly feel their political fortunes have improved. And that`s a large part because of the abortion decision. But also they -- there is an important opportunity and real need for Democrats to talk about bread and butter issues that are important to union workers, like the economy. Listen to part of my conversation this morning with Josh Shapiro, of course, the Democratic nominee for governor who talked about he`s sort of walking and chewing gum at the same time. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Abortion really did seem to change the race for a lot of Democratic candidates across the country. How are you balancing these issues? How are you -- what are you finding voters are most interested in as you`re talking to voters across the state?\par \par \uc2 JOSH SHAPIRO\uc2 , PENNSYLVANIA DEMOCRATIC CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: You know, look, Pennsylvanians know how to walk and chew gum, right? They can think about multiple things at the same time. I`ll be a governor that protects a woman`s right to choose. I`ll be a governor that protects people`s right to vote. And I think people all across Pennsylvania understand we`ve got to do both. Create opportunities and defend our freedoms.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: So, Andrea -- that`s quite a statement, Andrea, from a potential future Pennsylvania Governor when you know, the history, as you know, well, in Pennsylvania, of a former Pennsylvania Governor, Bob Casey, who was anti-abortion. Even \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , of course, has had his position on abortion all over the years, but that`s one of the main reasons why Democrats do feel like they do have a fighting chance here. But it is important to note. I also spoke this morning with Congressman \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He, of course, ran in that Senate primary, but he had run in three tough House races over the years in a Trump-leaning district. He said, yes, abortion has given Democrats a real uphill opportunity that is less so now. But he said we need to focus on those bread and butter issues as well. And that was exactly, Andrea, as well the same message from the local union leader as well, saying Biden is a pro-union president. We`re getting out. We`re getting back to basics and talking about the economy.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Yet, Congresswoman Dingell, you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in Wilkes- Barre following on the heels of the president there, just -- he was there Saturday night. The president was there just a couple of days earlier. And he`s got real appeal in your state of Michigan, real appeal in the -- in Wisconsin outside of Milwaukee. You know Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, and Michigan, three states that are really important to the Democrats but they`re going to be a lot tougher in this midterm, aren`t they?\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL, (D-MI): So you can`t take Michigan for granted. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is obsessed with this state. And my hair looks frizzy because I have been at eight different union picnics already and about five more to go. So -- and I met the teachers where choice is very much an issue that on their minds, but I had been with the IPW, I`ve been with the laborers, and pipefitters, the painters, and they are worried about the economy. \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  is right. We got to talk about both.\par \par \uc2 They like with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doing. The younger workers feel like he`s fighting hard to give them an environment it`s supporting them in their ability to unionize at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and other local stores. Some of the older established unions are worried. You know, the IPW is worried about what do electric vehicles mean to them. But \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wins with those workers. Connect, talk to them, understands their insecurity, and he knows he`s got to talk to them. And I know that`s why we`re going to win in November because we got to be focus. We have to do -- choice really matters as well as the economy.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Robert Gibbs, you had Fetterman there today in Pennsylvania with the president but not there in his last appearance. And you`ve got Mandela Barnes, the Senate candidate in, you know, MIT -- in the Wisconsin, not even showing up in Milwaukee, even though he is in Milwaukee at other events today. What kind of a snub is that to a president whose pulling numbers have been inching up and they have been producing legislative victories for Democrats?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GIBBS, FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY, OBAMA ADMINISTRATION: Yes, Andrea, I think each candidate is going to make decisions really by the week and by the event on whether or not they appear with the president or really any surrogate. I think that`s not really all that terribly uncommon. I would say to most candidates if you look at what`s happened over the last six weeks that you mentioned, the president`s approval rating has gone from about upper 37 percent to more than 42 percent.\par \par \uc2 So over the last six weeks, the president`s political health has gotten stronger. I think if you look at excitement among the Democratic base, that`s clearly ramped up. So I think with this nine-week sprint going into Election Day from today and Labor Day, I think you`re going to see more and more people that do appear with them. But again, each of these candidates is going to make their own decisions.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Congresswoman Dingell, what about the abortion issue and how big a motivator that is after the Kansas referendum and what we`ve seen in those two special elections as well?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: I can tell you that in Michigan, it`s the number one issue. I just got my falling back for my election in the general election and choice was actually the number one issue, the economy was only number three. But if you`re out and about, there are a lot of Republican women that are very uncomfortable with the reversal of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 For many, like myself, I`m a Catholic woman, I couldn`t but I do believe it`s a woman`s personal health care. It`s an individual`s right to choose. And there are Republican men that you were talking to sort of making it very clear that they`re uncomfortable about the Republican gubernatorial candidate because of that issue. So it is front and center on this ballot and it`s going to drive people to the polls in November without question.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Mike Memoli, I also want to ask you about the president`s speech last week. The criticism has been from some that it was too hard-edge that by going after the MAGA Republicans and, you know, calling them names, and, of course, going after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by name, that he also may have turned off some independents and some other Republicans, even those who don`t fully support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . What do you say?\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Yes, Andrea, I think it was important. When you looked at the president`s remarks, he did try to make an explicit distinction between what he called mainstream Republicans and that extreme MAGA swing. But I`ve been talking to some Democrats and others close to the White House, who felt like the president could have been even clearer in making that distinction.\par \par \uc2 For instance, one person told me that perhaps the president could have invoked the name of \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  to try to make it clear, as the president did himself say the next day, he wasn`t talking about anybody who voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  being part of who that message was directed toward. He understands that people vote for a presidential candidate for a number of reasons. But he was trying to talk specifically about those people who, as he says and he sees it, embrace political violence, who don`t accept the results of free and fair elections.\par \par \uc2 And so it was interesting, as part of my conversation this morning with Josh Shapiro, that he did sort of distanced himself as well, from the president and his speech there. He said, you know, it`s -- I`m not sure whether that helps me or hurts me here in my race, but that he`s focused on, as he put it, Washington County versus what`s taking place in Washington, DC.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, Robert Gibbs, how do you weigh and balance -- weigh the -- weigh the balance between going after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who is now you know, looming so large, especially because of all of the events on the legal front and dominating the conversation, and drawing that contrast without turning off Republicans and others who might, you know, vote for Democratic candidates might cross over?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Yes, it`s a great question too, Andrea. I think -- look, I think the most important thing is that this president is trying very hard to bring \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  into parts of the discussion in this campaign that I think are good contrast for him and for other Democrats, particularly on democratic - - on issues of democracy. We`ve seen those issues in the NBC poll and other polls become more important for voters. And I think each time that this president can make the election a choice rather than a referendum on him, he`ll be well served and candidates will be well served.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Well, Mike Memoli, Robert Gibbs, and Congresswoman Dingell, and we love you, and love the hair. You know, you`ve got natural on us.\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: It`s great.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Thanks for being with us on Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 And stabbing attacks in Canada. 10 dead after knife attacks in Saskatchewan that -- and the suspect still on the loose. That`s coming up, plus the latest on Ukraine. You`re watching MSNBC -- CHRIS JANSING REPORTS. I`m Andrea Mitchell.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:13]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Fears of a nuclear meltdown are ripping parts of southeastern Ukraine, where a nuclear plant, the largest in Europe, is caught in the middle of renewed fighting. Russia claims Ukraine targeted the territory on Sunday. Both sides accusing the other of endangering the plant. International inspectors are set to report back to the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . tomorrow on efforts to avert a potential catastrophe, although they said last week they were very worried about the plant`s physical integrity.\par \par \uc2 Former NATO Supreme Allied commander \uc2 James Stavridis\uc2 , joins us.\par \par \uc2 So, Admiral, let`s talk about this. This is the first time I can recall a nuclear plant of this size in the middle of a war zone basically and there`s no cease-fire agreement, there`s no any kind of negotiation between the two sides to try to protect it.\par \par \uc2 ADM\uc2 . \uc2 JAMES STAVRIDIS\uc2 , FORMER NATO SUPREME ALLIED COMMANDER: It`s a very dangerous situation, Andrea. And the closest we could come to it I think would be the Fukushima, Japan scenario where you have both an earthquake and a tsunami at the same time, creating almost combat-like effects. That put hundreds of thousands of people at risk with radiation. And it caused hundreds of billions of dollars in damages.\par \par \uc2 The potential here is equally high. The other obvious parallel is Chernobyl. But, again, no war zone there. What`s deplorable is that this is preventable at this point. And the international community needs to put additional pressure on \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to create a no-fire zone, a free-fire zone around this vital nuclear power plant.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And you told me nearly a week ago, Admiral, that this plant was a, quote, "ticking time bomb." The fact that the inspectors have been able at least to get in and assess the situation, has that ease your mind at all?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: It does a tiny bit, and the inspection team, which came from the \uc2 IAEA\uc2 , the \uc2 International Atomic Energy Agency\uc2 , is going to leave a couple of observers there. Those are some brave men and women who are willing to stay and stand and deliver in this combat zone.\par \par \uc2 But to be perfectly honest, Andrea, we ought to continue to be very concerned about this. That clock is ticking closer and closer to midnight.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, Admiral, the other issue here is a possible no-fly zone? Do you think that`s something that could be negotiated?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: I think that by threatening to nut a no-fly zone over this, enforced by \uc2 NATO\uc2  jets, U.S. combat aircraft, I think that would create the kind of pressure on \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  that might, and I underline the word might, get him to back down, get his troops out of this nuclear power plant zone.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:09]\par \par \uc2 There are six reactors here, Andrea. And the comparison here is, we put pressure on him, about the blockade of the Black Sea that he was effecting in order to keep grain from coming out. When the West threatened effectively to break that blockade with escorts, something you and I talked about some weeks ago, Putin caved in. Now that grain is flowing out.\par \par \uc2 I think by telling Putin very directly the consequence of your continued placement of all of Europe at risk from radiation, including your own country, by the way, the threat of putting jets in the air to enforce this might have a, shall we say, clarifying effect on the mind of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: I was going to point out exactly that breakthrough on the blockade that you were pushing so hard for. We`re talking about reflagging ships in the ways that they could come up with the corridor. They did it. Erdogan probably was involved, and did some good here as an intermediary.\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . of course significantly, the visit from the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . secretary-general. And that made a huge difference. That grain is arriving in Africa now. There have been, you know, maybe 10 or more shiploads that have come through.\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: They have. And I think the more the international community shines a light on those maritime operations, and by the way kudos to President Erdogan, who pursued this very doggedly, and the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . secretary- general who stepped in and brought that \uc2 U.N\uc2 . imprimatur over the operation. Now we need to keep the klieg lights on to make sure that it continues to flow and yes, Andrea, I think the same model could be applied here.\par \par \uc2 Put the pressure on Putin, show him there`s no way out of this, that the international community is going to continue to sink its teeth into this particular issue, I think would have a chance of making him step back from the edge of the abyss, which is where this is parked at the moment.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: One of the other things that just happened today. We understand that President Zelenskyy is now calling for a war crimes tribunal. There have been various conversations about war crimes. The U.S. was kind of late to respond until the president in Warsaw said that we should. And now they`re asking for it in Europe. I don`t know if it`s the \uc2 International Criminal Court\uc2  or what venue. But do you think that that can be convened while the war is still underway?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: I do. And the question is, can it create a (INAUDIBLE) to step out and enforce whatever comes out of such a tribunal? I think at this point probably not. But simply the act of bringing these horrific war crimes in front of the public through a tribunal or the \uc2 International Criminal Court\uc2 , take your pick, gets the light shining on it, and that is good.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And I am going to have to break away because we`re going now to Milwaukee and President Biden at a labor audience.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Folks, if you have a seat, don`t hesitate to take them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We love you, Uncle Joe.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Thank you, man.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, you know, I think this is getting to be a pretty good year for a Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Eduardo, thank you for the introduction. It`s great to be with the Governor Evers, Representative Glenn Moore. Where`s Glenn? There you go, Glenn. I love you, kid. You`ve been there every step of the way when I`ve needed help as president. Thank you, thank you, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And Mayor Johnson. Where`s the mayor? There you go. You`ve got those two beautiful girls and a son. I`d tell you what. I have to tell you, girls, you`ve got to be patient with we dads. We`re hard to raise. We`re hard to raise. And where`s the county executive? Stand up, big guy. I think these guys spent -- I think these two guys spend all their time in the gymnasium. They look like they`re in too good a shape.\par \par \uc2 And I want to thank Tammy, she couldn`t be here today, who`s a champion of working people and always there. You know, and the same goes to Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes, who couldn`t be here, but --\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: He`s going be your next United States senator.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Oh, he is.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:02]\par \par \uc2 And I want to thank \uc2 Liz Shuler\uc2  for her fearless leadership there at the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 . And thanks to Pam, for your leadership of the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  here in Milwaukee. Thank you, thank you, thank you. Where is she? Back there? OK, there you are, Pam. I was -- OK. I was wondering where everybody is sitting.\par \par \uc2 And how about actually having a union guy as the secretary of Labor?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I`d tell you what, if you`re going to be in a foxhole, you want Marty in there with you. Now you may have trouble understanding when he starts talking about car barns and (INAUDIBLE) garages and talks -- he talks kind of funny, but he knows what he`s talking about.\par \par \uc2 I promised to be the most pro-union president in American history, and Marty is keeping me to make that promise.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And by the way, the reason for that, I`ll -- and I`ve had this conversation with the Business Roundtable and all the major Chambers of Commerce, it`s not just that I think that it`s important that people get a shot. But, look the reason why, the reason why businesses should be hiring labor folks is simple. You`re the single-greatest technicians in the world. You`re the best laborers in the world. You build the best product.\par \par \uc2 No, no, I`m not just joking. I`m not just saying that. But people forget, a lot of the trades, they forget, you go to four, five years of school, your apprenticeship. It`s like going back to college. It`s not like you all of a sudden just step in. You build a better product, it lasts longer, and it`s cheaper for the business, and it`s better for the country.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Look, folks, Labor Day is a special day in the country for -- and here in Milwaukee. This is one of the biggest Labor Day events in America you all have here.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It`s a special day to me as well because the fact of the matter is, I wouldn`t be here without unions. Unions. Electricians, iron workers, letter carriers, teamsters, laborers, bricklayers, transit workers, plumbers, and pipe fitters, steel workers. I wouldn`t be here without cops, firefighters, teachers, nurses. I wouldn`t be here without painters, pilots, autoworkers, custodians, carpenters, grocery store workers, steel metal workers and so many others.\par \par \uc2 That`s not a joke. That`s not a joke. I got elected when I was 29 years old to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  and won a state that had recently been right to work. You know why? Because union labor endorsed me and fought for me.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: So the bad news for you all I`m here because of you. Look, the middle class, the middle class built America. Everybody knows that. But unions built the middle class. That`s a fact. That`s a fact. Look, folks, I believe we`re at an inflection point in American history. I really mean that. It comes around every five or six generations, where one of those moments where everything is changing.\par \par \uc2 Everything is changing. And we`re going to have to ask whether we want to be a country that moves forward or backwards. We`re going to build a future or we`re going to obsess about the past. I`ve said many times, we`re the only country in the world that`s come out of every crisis we ever faced stronger than we went in it. No other nation has done that.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And we do it because we`ve been a nation of unity, of hope, of optimism. Not as a nation of division and violence and hatred being preached by some others. I want to be very clear up front, not every Republican is a MAGA Republican. Not every Republican embraces that extreme ideology. I know because I`ve been able to work with mainstream Republicans my whole career.\par \par \uc2 But the extreme MAGA Republicans in Congress have chosen to go backwards, full of anger, violence, hate and division. But together, we can and we must choose a different path. Forward.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I really mean it. We have to. The future of unity, of hope, of optimism. We`re going to choose to build a better America. A better America. There`s no question it`s been a hard few years but we have come a long way. Today, COVID no longer controls our lives. More Americans are working at any time than all of American history. Last year, more people applied to start small businesses than any year in history.\par \par \uc2 But it didn`t just happen. We never gave up. We never gave in. And we`re delivering for working and middle class Americans now. Now.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 [13:45:07]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Nearly 10 million, more jobs than have ever been created at this point in any presidency in history. Wages are up. Unemployment remains near a 50-year low. And the big reason for all this is the American Rescue Plan that I signed into law shortly after taking office.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: With the help of your Democratic members of Congress, that`s how we got it done. Not one single Republican vote. The plan took America from an economic crisis to economic recovery. And here in Wisconsin, your governor and your mayor of Milwaukee used that money like it`s supposed to be used, keeping city workers on the job in the face of rising costs. Keeping streets safe by fixing street lights. Training 130 new firefighters, helping to hire close to 200 more police officers.\par \par \uc2 Not a single Republican voted for that. Not one single one. Every single Republican in Congress in this state voted against that funding. So let me remind you.\par \par \uc2 (AUDIENCE BOOING)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, I really mean it. Let me remind you, what else was in that American Rescue Plan? A thing called the Butch Lewis Act. And look, for too long most Americans don`t know what that means, though you`ve been driving trucks from factories to stores, bagging our groceries, building our buildings, bridges, roads, so much more. And with each paycheck you earn, employees put money into that pension plan.\par \par \uc2 But some companies didn`t set aside enough money to pay for those plans. And as a result, hundreds of pensions, two million to three million workers and retirees were going insolvent. Millions of retirees are at risk of losing their retirement security through no fault of your own. So with the American Rescue Plan we passed the Butch Lewis Act to protect pensions all of you worked so damn hard for.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Pensions you sacrificed for. In retirement, it`d be a little like losing your Social Security and your Medicare. It`s one of the most significant achievements union workers and retirees -- for union workers and retirees in over 50 years. And I`ll say it again, not a single Republican congressman voted to protect your pensions. Not one.\par \par \uc2 (AUDIENCE BOOING)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And by the way, I know a lot of them wanted to vote for it but they`re afraid of losing primaries in this new Republican Party. They also passed once in a generation investment in our nation`s roads, highways, bridges, railroads, ports, water systems, high speed internet.\par \par \uc2 Folks, your governor has already repaired over 5,000 miles 1500 bridges in Wisconsin alone, just to start.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We will, when this is over, have the best infrastructure, not a joke, in the entire world. The single best in the world. And that means jobs. No, that means jobs. That means we continue to lead the world. You can`t do it without the best ports, you can`t do it without the best airports, you can`t do it. I`m not going to go on, but the point is, you can`t do it.\par \par \uc2 Right here in Milwaukee, it`s estimated 40 percent of the homes still have lead service water, lead pipes for the water. Because of the Infrastructure Law, the city is receiving millions of dollars to replace those lead pipes, to make sure families have safe drinking water. Your child shouldn`t have to worry about turning on the faucet or going to school, going to the water fountain and worry about any kind of lead in the water.\par \par \uc2 Look, it`s the biggest investment in America since President Eisenhower`s Interstate Highway Act. And we made sure the Infrastructure Law included significant labor protections. For example, majority of the funds in the law are subject to Davis-Bacon. And it`s not just because I wanted to help unions. It`s simple. You`re the best in the world. That`s not hyperbole. No, you really -- it`s not a joke. You really, genuinely are.\par \par \uc2 You just don`t decide you want to be a pipefitter or an electrician. It takes you four to five years of hard work. The apprenticeships. It`s like going to college. You`re the best trained workers in the world, and you deserved to be treated that way.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And by the way, those of you who know me, no one ever doubts I mean what I say. The problem is I sometimes say all that mean.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I`ve made the same comments to the Business Roundtable, to the Chamber of Commerce. They understand it. They don`t want to pay it, they understand it, though, because it`s true.\par \par \uc2 I also signed the CHIPS and Science Act, a groundbreaking law. We`ll once again manufacture semiconductor chips that power everyday -- everything, our smartphones, dishwashers, automobiles, national security stuff right here in America.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:07]\par \par \uc2 Guess what? We invented it here. We invented it here. In America.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: By the way, the reason why last time out and last year inflation was so high, you know the biggest reason? Cost of automobiles. You know why they cost so much? They didn`t have the computer chips to make the automobiles.\par \par \uc2 Folks, here in Wisconsin, Senator Baldwin made clear that you`re ready to take advantage of this law with world-class universities and a workforce that knows how to manufacture projects here in Wisconsin. This law is going to create tens of thousands of jobs in America, bringing billions of dollars from investments here in America, well over $100 billion in investments in America, revitalizing American manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Where is it written, where is it written that says America can`t lead the world in manufacturing? Where does it say that?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We`ve exported too damn much. In fact, next week I`m going to Ohio for the groundbreaking of a multi-billion dollar semiconductor manufacturing plant.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The company is Intel. It`s one of the largest investments of its kind ever. They`re putting $20 billion in as a start. It`s going to create thousands of jobs, thousands. 7,000 construction jobs just building the facilities. 5,000 permanent jobs for blue-collar workers who will make $125,000 a year in those jobs.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And, by the way, if you take a look, manufacturers all over the world are coming to the United States from Korea, from Japan, from all over the world. Why? You know why the head of the outfit out of Korea told me they`re coming here? Because we have the safest environment and the best workers in the world. That`s a fact.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We do. I`m not just saying this. This is real. Look, folks, it means we`re going to build the future in America with American workers in American factories, using American-made products. Look, something most people don`t know but you guys out there in this audience know, 15 percent of our military veterans become union workers when they leave the military. 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 How many veterans out there? Holler up. You`ve got it.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: In my State of the Union address, I put forward what I called a unity agenda, including taking care of our veterans. Look, we have a lot of responsibilities and only one sacred responsibility, not a joke. And I mean this from the bottom of my heart and I`ve said it from the first day I entered politics. We have an obligation to equip those we send into harm`s way and care for them and their families when they come home. Both our families.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Especially our veterans who`ve been exposed to toxic burn pits like my son. Those pits the size of football fields. Eight, nine, 10-feet deep in Afghanistan and Iraq. I was in and out of those countries over 35 times in the middle of these wars as vice president. That incinerate waste of war, tires, poisons, chemicals, jet fuel, so much more. A lot of people like my son had their hooch just probably 300, 500 yards from those pits. You could smell it, you could inhale it.\par \par \uc2 Well, guess what? These poisonous fumes just came. People came home with headaches, numbness, dizziness, cancer. I just signed what we call the PACT Act to take care of these veterans who need medical assistance.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And to provide for their families when they are gone.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: For God`s sake, it`s the least we can do. It`s the least --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We love you, Joe.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, I love you, too, kiddo, I`d tell you. Look, like I said, I believe we have a lot of obligations. We have a lot of obligations but only one sacred obligation, and that`s to take care of those vets we send overseas and when they come home.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:03]\par \par \uc2 No, I -- but I really mean it. And, as I said, I just signed into law historic Inflation Reduction Act. It wasn`t easy to take on big drug companies, but we did. I`ve been fighting them since I got in Congress 180 years ago.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You know, we pay more for prescription drugs in the United States of America than any major country in the world. Here in the United States, OK? There`s no reason for it. For the last several decades many of us have been trying to fix the problem, but for decades big pharma tried to block giving lower drug prices for those on Medicare or anywhere else. For decades big pharma won year in, year out because they own chunks of the Congress, because they had help like your senior senator, Ron Johnson, who said --\par \par \uc2 (AUDIENCE BOOING)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I want to say what he said. He said he opposed lowering drug costs because it would result in punishing the pharmaceutical history. Bless me, Father, for I have sinned. I mean, come on, man. Not this year. We beat pharma this year. We beat pharma this year, and it mattered.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We`re going to change people`s lives. We finally beat pharma. Now Medicare will have the power to negotiate lower prescription drug prices. We can guarantee no senior, no senior will have to pay more than $2,000 out of pocket for their drugs for the entire year, no matter whether the bill is 50,000, no more than $2,000. Period. And if you`re on Medicare and you have diabetes, your cost of insulin will be capped at $35.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And, by the way, there`s a reason for that. You know how much it costs them to make and package insulin for diabetes? Ten bucks. Ten. And if you need that insulin or your children need it, you know what it costs. It costs you somewhere between $650 and a thousand bucks a month. It`s outrageous.\par \par \uc2 Well, guess what? We also had in this bill of mine. We also had a provision that affected people who weren`t on Medicare. But because of the leadership of your senior senator and others, it got defeated. But I`m coming back and getting it.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Imagine -- think about this. Imagine being a mom or a dad. I mean this from the bottom of my heart. Imagine being a mom or dad with a kid with type 2 diabetes, knowing you need that insulin and you don`t have the insurance, you can`t pay for it. I`m not joking. Think about it. Think about how it would rip your heart out. It`s wrong. It`s simply wrong. And we`re going to end it. It costs 10 bucks and you can make 35 if you want. That`s it.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: For decades the biggest corporations, and by the way, I know corporations. I come from the corporate state of the world. More corporations are incorporated in Delaware than every other nation, every other state in the country combined, OK? So I can write a doctorate on corporations. So it`s not like I`m anti-corporation generically, but I do think everybody should pay their fair share.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And, by the way, and by the way, look, for decades the biggest corporations and the wealthiest Americans have fought to block a fair tax code. Republicans passed a $2 trillion tax cut mainly benefiting the wealthiest corporations under the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 (AUDIENCE BOOING)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: By the way, increased the debt by $2 trillion. Well, guess what? In 2000, 55 of those corporations earned $40 billion in profit. They didn`t pay a single solitary penny in taxes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And wealthiest Americans, the 700 plus billionaires in America, you know what their average tax rate is in federal tax? Eight percent. They pay fewer taxes than any one of you pay.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s not right.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It`s not right. No, no. I mean, this is -- it`s just outrageous. Well, guess what? Guess what? I wasn`t able to take care -- anyway.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220906ei9500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for September 2, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Chuck Rosenberg \par \uc2 8,168 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Because my pick as well happens to be Serena M and Effing Williams because she is the baddest in the game. Game set match, Serena Williams. You won the week. Thank you Gyasi Ross. Thank you Atima Omara. That`s never happened before on Who Won the Week. That`s actually kind of amazing. She literally won the week.\par \par \uc2 We should also make a runner-up of Dark Brandon because Dark Brandon is the runner-up for Who Won the Week because he secondarily once a week. Because Dark Brandon went off this week.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the MAGA Republicans Representative extremism that threatens the very foundations of our Republic.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The president confront of fascist threat as his predecessor vows to free of violent mob.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I will be looking very, very strongly at that pardon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Amen.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Full pardon.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, the extreme and divisive truth of our American reality. Then from potential national security concerns to possible tchotchkes at Trump grill. Chuck Rosenberg on what`s missing from classified folders found at Mar-a-Lago. And he was among the very few people in the room where it happened.\par \par \uc2 PAT CIPOLLONE, FORMER WHITE HOUSE SPECIAL COUNSEL: I walked in. I saw General Flynn. I saw \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2 . The Overstock person, I`ve never met -- I never knew.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What we know about what \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  told the grand jury investigating January 6 today when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. As you may have seen, President Biden deliver speech in Philadelphia last night. We covered it live. The speech outlining the threats to our democracy and what he called the battle for the soul of our nation. Now, it was, of course a political speech as all addresses by a sitting president inherently are.\par \par \uc2 But in the aftermath, one of the two major parties dominated by the MAGA authoritarian faction the President was talking about has been whining and moaning about how politically divisive it all was. Listen to the Republican Leader of the House, Kevin McCarthy, who actually started whining about the speech before Biden had even delivered it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): President Biden has chosen to divide, demean, and disparage his fellow Americans. Why? Simply because they disagree with his policies. That is not leadership.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Say what your well about Kevin McCarthy, one of the finest orders of his generation. Senator and podcaster Ted Cruz of Texas echoed McCarthy tweeting about how the President "vilified millions of Americans in a divisive and angry speech, and there was just a lot more where that came from. Now, to be fair, they are actually not wrong about that core critique in a certain way. It was a divisive speech because some level of division is necessary when there is a conflict of this magnitude between two parties.\par \par \uc2 One party wants to preserve the democratic constitutional order of our American democracy, and the other seeks to upend it. That may sound dramatic or hyperbolic or even extreme, but it is extreme because the truth is extreme. That is exactly what we`re facing as President Biden laid out last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Democracy cannot survive when one side believes there are only two outcomes to election. Either they win or they were cheated. And that`s where the MAGA Republicans are today. They don`t understand what every patriotic American knows. You can`t love your country only when you win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That line stuck with me. It`s the dangerous distortion that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  helped create, and that his followers believe. Elections are only legitimate if he and Republicans win, and more deeply, that power of the state, the power of the state is only legitimately wielded by Republicans even when they are in a minority. That view that only we shall rule is toxic, insidious, and completely at odds with living in a democracy. President Biden spoke about the dangers of this lie in his speech.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And they see their MAGA failure to stop a peaceful transfer of power after the 2020 election as preparation for the 2022 and 2024 elections. They`ve tried everything last time to nullify the votes of 81 million people. This time, they`re determined to succeed in thwarting the will of the people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Again, this is just plainly true, a factual statement of where things are. They are trying again to destroy the American experiment. January 6, was the first-ever violent insurrection in this country that launched by a sitting president against his own government to stop the peaceful transfer of power. It only happened once. It was the first time the peaceful transfer of power was interrupted since, of course, the great crisis of the republic in 1861. Those are just the plain facts of the matter.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:19]\par \par \uc2 And after that attempt failed on January 6, a majority of Republicans in the House Representatives -- let us not lose sight of this -- 147 of them voted to overturn the results of the election. They voted on behalf of the mob and the coup and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and against the voters. They voted for minority rule for the President to stay in power against the will of us, we the people. And over the past two years, they have defended and facilitated the efforts of their leader as he plots quite openly another coup.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , more than half of the Republican winners so far in primary races for positions that hold power over elections have embraced Donald Trump`s false claims about his defeat two years ago. That`s about 250 people who deny the legitimacy of Joe Biden`s presidency and could soon be in position to help Trump overturn the next election.\par \par \uc2 But these minor Republicans, as the President repeatedly call them last night, are nowhere near the majority of this country. President Biden spoke for the majority of Americans last night and we see from the hysterical whining about his speech the MAGA Republicans who are indeed a minority are flailing. They know they no longer have a lock on the Midterm elections. They`re leader is in increasingly dire legal peril. We saw Trump himself wildly lashing out yesterday, just hours before President Biden`s speech, when he dangled pardons for the insurrectionists.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I will tell you, I will look very, very favorably about full pardons. If I decide to run, and if I win, I will be looking very, very strongly about pardons.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Amen.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Full pardons.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That`s well-deserved.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I think that`s probably going to be the best because even if they go for two months or six months, and you know, they have sentences that go longer than that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Years and years.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : But we`ll be looking very, very seriously at full pardons because we can`t let that happen. What`s happened here, and I mean full pardons with an apology just too many an apology.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Amen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Not only just \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not apologize for leading an attempted coup on January 6, he wants everyone to apologize to him, to his accomplices. He wants to pardon the criminals who savagely beat officers, infiltrate and overrun the Capitol, desecrated the people`s house, threatened members of Congress and their staff, and chanted Hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The people who have gotten the longest sentences that he mentioned there were the most violent. Those are the ones he`s talking about when he wants to pardon. And we know from the investigations and charges filed against hundreds of them, they would have done far more damage if given the chance.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, the President spoke about the threat posed by those people, the MAGA Republicans, and on the same day, their leader announced he wants to pardon the most dangerous elements of that seditious faction. So, yes, President Biden`s words may have sounded extreme and divisive. And that`s because we live in extreme and divisive times.\par \par \uc2 Adam Serwer is a staff writer for The Atlantic. He recently wrote about Trump keeping classified documents at Mar-a-Lago, in a piece entitled, If It Were Anyone Else, They`d Be Prosecuted. And he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Adam, I thought that the routing things in the idea of there being a pro- democracy majority in the country was an important aspect of last night. It`s something that you`ve written about a lot that that is the fundamental issue here that this attempted minority rule, this attempt to subvert elections because you don`t win them is fundamentally continues to be a minority faction in American life that is intent on ruling from that position.\par \par \uc2 ADAM SERWER, STAFF WRITER, THE ATLANTIC: Look, there are always going to be people who have conservative economic beliefs, who are pro-life, who believe, you know, we should have less immigration, things like that. This fight is really about, do you believe that we should live in a country where only one group of people get to govern because they make up the true soul of the country. They`re the only real Americans and therefore they are the only ones who have a mandate to govern?\par \par \uc2 And what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  believes -- and this is a belief that, you know, he more or less adopted as a result of seeing it on Fox News.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SERWER: This is a belief that the minority of Americans have that they believe they`re entitled to roll no matter the circumstances, no matter whether they have a majority, no matter no matter whether they win an election. They believe because they are the only true Americans, they`re entitled to govern no matter what. And that is a very serious problem. It`s a very complicated one. And it`s not one -- you know, it`s not one you can solve easily no matter what path you take.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:09]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And I thought about -- thought about -- you wrote this piece about the Alaska election. And the title was Republicans have only themselves to blame for the Alaskan defeat. That, of course, was the fairly shocking victory of the Democratic Congresswoman -- now-Congressman Peltola over \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  and Nick Begich. And you say the whining after that. Right now, they lost this election, and it`s -- the system is rigged.\par \par \uc2 The running -- you know, rank choice voting election system is not being evaluated on the basis of whether or not results in a fair election, but whether it produces a GOP victory. If it does not, the system is corrupt by definition. That conservatives are embracing majoritarian arguments to argue against rank choice voting is nevertheless illuminating because it shows they believe in majoritarianism only where Republicans can expect a majority. I thought that was a really good point.\par \par \uc2 SERWER: Yes, I mean, look, you know, people who think that the Electoral College is good because it overweights conservative votes in such a way that makes it easier for Republicans to win presidential elections suddenly saying that rank choice voting is bad because, you know, most people voted for a Republican. But this is -- you know, this is a red herring. And rank -- and rank choice voting, the majority -- the person who wins still has to have a majority.\par \par \uc2 The fact was that Republican -- people in Alaska, while they might have preferred a Republican, did not prefer the Republican on the ballot. When it was between Palin and the Democratic candidate, they preferred the Democrat because they didn`t want to see Palin in office. That was the majority of them. The attack on the system, as I said -- as I wrote in that piece, the attack on the rank choice system which was adopted by the very conservative voters of Alaska, you know, it was an attack based on the fact that Republicans simply lost the seat that they believe they were entitled to.\par \par \uc2 And that`s exactly the same attitude that led to what you were talking about in the assault on the Capitol. It did not matter that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the election. The fact that he drew from constituencies that they consider illegitimate, meant that his election was illegitimate. It didn`t matter how many million more votes he won. It didn`t matter that he won in the Electoral College, a system that very strongly favors Republican candidates. The fact that he was not representing true American constituencies meant that the peaceful transfer of power didn`t need to take place, and they could simply overthrow the government and install \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in power.\par \par \uc2 And Trump has continued to encourage this by, as you noted, say that, you know, if these people engage on political violence in his behalf, you will pardon them. He said, do whatever you need to do even if you get in trouble, I will protect you later. And there`s only one reason you do that, because you want these people to act violently.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And there`s the -- there`s also this question, right? So, you know, when you -- when you point to Alaska, you say, yes, they chose -- they didn`t want that individual. You know, Palin ended up being too toxic. When you look at the polling right now, for instance, on things like, you know, should \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continue to be investigated, it`s 57-40, right? If you look at the polling on Roe, right, you get like 60-30 -- 65-35, in that neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 If Republicans -- I have come to the belief, and I`m curious what you think. If Republicans really truly huddled together and viewed their mission as a party, to win a popular majority in the country, 51 percent, not use the Senate and the Electoral College to try to like, get in there or use threats and intimidation, and it would be better for everyone. That they would force to abandon some of this because it genuinely is unpopular, and they could compete under normal terms. And yet, they will not do that right now.\par \par \uc2 SERWER: Yes, I mean, I think that is definitely the case. I don`t think it`s impossible for Republicans to win majorities. There are a lot of people with conservative views in this country, and not all of them are white, by the way.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SERWER: The reality is that Republicans have chosen to exploit the counter- majoritarian levers of the system in order to stay in power so that they do not have to be accountable the people who do not hold only those views that are represented on Fox News. That`s just the way that they want it. They would prefer not to be accountable to constituencies that might compel them to moderate their positions.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at like the Democratic Party, the Democratic Party, it`s not that Democrats are inherently more virtuous than Republicans. It`s that they are beholden to a constituency that is diverse, ideologically, racially, religiously. And as a result of that, they cannot engage. And as a result of that, in the nature of our system, they cannot engage in the kind of counter-majoritarian anti-democratic behavior that Republicans are engaging in right now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I think that`s exactly the nub of it. Adam Serwer, always great to get a chance to talk to you. Thanks for joining us tonight. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:01]\par \par \uc2 Coming up, new filing sheds more light on the hundreds of classified documents \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  stash in his re4tirement home, but also raises new concerns some of those documents are still unaccounted for. Chuck Rosenberg is here to talk about it all next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, so there`s a bar on the ground floor of the fifth avenue on the Trump Tower in Manhattan that was recently retrofitted. It`s called 45 Wine and Whiskey and it`s basically a \uc2 Hard Rock Cafe\uc2  knockoff where visitors can enjoy walls plastered with Trump presidential memorabilia that ex-president must have snuck out of the White house in suitcases.\par \par \uc2 If you go there, you can enjoy $25.00 cocktails and $45.00 cheese plates while sitting underneath a giant framed picture of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and North Korean dictator Kim Jong-un. That`ll get you hungry, right? Or beside a photo of Trump holding a newspaper touting his acquittal in one of his impeachment trials.\par \par \uc2 Now, the centerpiece of the bar is this display case. It`s full of random knickknacks from Trump`s time in the White House, including a copy of the trade deal he signed, a space for seal, a Trump football, and this folder marked classified. Now, we cannot say for certain the reason Trump was hoarding documents in his Florida retirement home, but we do know that today Florida judge unsealed more information about the document seized from Mar-a-Lago, and according to a DOJ filing, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  retrieved more than 100 documents with classification markings ranging from Classified to Top Secret in the search of Trump`s property.\par \par \uc2 The document also lists hundreds of press clippings retained by the ex- president, as well as dozens of "empty folders with classified banners. That`s a weird one to say the least. Many people have been scratching their heads as to why Trump took so many empty folders marked classified. The truth is we don`t really know. We may never know. One theory that seems at least plausible, if only because it`s the dumbest possible reason, maybe he was hoarding documents at Mar-a-Lago to use them as decorations at his new hotel bar.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Rosenberg is a former U.S. attorney and senior \uc2 FBI\uc2  official. He`s been following Donald Trump`s potential legal trouble as closely as anyone. And he joins me now. That detail was the big detail that jumped out from the list that was unsealed today. What do you make of the piles of empty classified folder?\par \par \uc2 CHUCK ROSENBERG, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Well, like you, Chris, I think it`s a bit of a head-scratcher. We don`t know if those folders were empty when they were taken out of the White House. We don`t know if they were emptied while at Mar-a-Lago. We don`t know if the classified documents strewn around Mar-a-Lago came from those empty folders.\par \par \uc2 But like the other stuff that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  found, it`s concerning because we know Mar-a-Lago is a huge counterintelligence target. And we know lots of people are roaming around. I understand that some of this stuff was under lock and key, but it doesn`t seem to have been -- well, I`m confident it wasn`t properly secured. So, yes, maybe it`s a decoration for his bar. It`s a head-scratcher. I agree with you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The last time I had you on, we were talking about, you know, the -- you know, the unsealing of that of the affidavit which was redacted and what evidence was presented in there. And we were talking about the fact that, you know, you say there`s probable cause of a crime. We searched, we find the evidence we were looking for, some form of it.\par \par \uc2 And you made the important point, right, that were you to prosecute and indict, you`d have to prove something more than just that, right? You`d have to prove that this -- that the individual who you`re prosecuting in this case were to be \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was the one who had control of those documents. He took them, he had them in his possession, he was the one doing the willful act here.\par \par \uc2 Since then, we`ve gotten more evidence, right, in the form of that brief filed by the DOJ that says some of the documents were in his desk. They were mixed in with his personal things and effects like his passports. And I wanted to return to you in terms of where you see that and that evidence in terms of building a case possibly against the ex-president?\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: Sure. It`s a great question, Chris. So, if the stuff is just in the basement, perhaps he can credibly claim that he didn`t pack the boxes or move the boxes or look in the boxes. When you put the stuff in his desk drawer, right, that helps you show more clearly, I think, as you said, the intent, right, willful possession, willful retention, whatever the statute may require.\par \par \uc2 And we do that all the time as prosecutors, right? The gun under the seat of your car is more likely your gun, more likely than the gun under the seat of your friend`s car. And so, the closer you are to the thing that we care about, the more likely it`s yours and the more likely -- or the more easily we can prove intent.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s also the -- when you zoom back out, right? So, we`ve gotten all these details and when we got inside of filings. But it`s been interesting to watch a few Republicans, people that are supportive to the president, but who have been around classified documents and presidential records, state the obvious that whatever happened here, he never should have had these and they weren`t his.\par \par \uc2 Karl Rove said this the other day. He said look, whatever this is about, they`re not his to take. Then we also have now Bill Barr saying this quite clearly. Here`s what he had to say. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:06]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL BARR, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: What people are missing is that all the other documents taken even if they claim to be executive privilege either belong to the government because their government records, even if they`re classified, even if they`re subject to executive privilege, they still belong to the government and go to the Archives.\par \par \uc2 And any other documents that were seized, like news clippings and other things that were in the boxes containing the classified information, those were siezable under the warrant because they show the conditions under which the classified information was being held.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, it`s been striking to watch people that do support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and work for him, you know, but who know a thing or two about how they should usually go -- state the obvious, which is this is obviously a violation at the most basic level.\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: Yes, I mean, here`s my ridiculous answer, Chris. The law is the law, is the law. I mean, Bill Barr stated the law. For those of us who worked in classified environments, and maybe this will be helpful to your viewers -- I hope so -- we really take this stuff very seriously. And so folks might be looking at and thinking, oh, it`s not a big deal. And you know, at least they found it, and it doesn`t seem to have gotten out and he was the president after all.\par \par \uc2 But the value of some of these highly classified documents are not always apparent from the content of these highly classified documents. And that may sound weird, but I can explain that, Chris. There might be something in one of these documents that you read, and you think, oh, my, that`s sort of benign or I wonder why that`s classified. That doesn`t seem all that compelling or all that important.\par \par \uc2 And the information itself may not be, but the way we got it, maybe from a highly placed confidential source, or from an incredible technical penetration is. And so treating this stuff in a cavalier fashion without understanding the equities at stake, the sources and methods that might be exposed is a very big deal. I remember reading this stuff for the first time and having that exact question. This doesn`t seem all that sort of mind-bending. It was interesting, but it wasn`t, you know, extraordinary, until someone explained to me, what`s extraordinary is how we got it.\par \par \uc2 And so, the notion that this stuff is strewn about the house in different boxes, and there are empty folders, and some of it is in his desk drawer, and people are coming and going all the time from Mar-a-Lago is really bad stuff. It doesn`t mean it got out. We don`t know that yet. And the intelligence community needs to do a damage assessment. That`s really important to protect those equities I was just describing.\par \par \uc2 But Bill Barr has it right. It`s not his. And moreover, the stuff that`s not his, if treated in a cavalier fashion, can do great damage to the national security of the United States. That`s just a fact.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: To your point about the cavalier nature here, there`s some reporting at the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  about the security concerns at Mar-a-Lago and these, you know, being in the basement. A person familiar with the club`s working who spoke on condition of anonymity describe regular movement from club facilities, the basement, and back. This is an operating property, this person said. There`s a kitchen and a guy who does pastries and a liquor cabinet. There`s a restaurant here. You see activity. A guy getting vodka to bring to the bar. A person going to get cupcakes to bring upstairs. So, I think safe to say not exactly a skiff in there. Chuck Rosenberg, as always great pleasure to talk to you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, we all heard the damning testimony Trump White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  gave before the January 6 Committee. Today, he testified before a D.C. grand jury. Why his story is so important for the case against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:33:18]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAT CIPOLLONE, FORMER WHITE HOUSE COUNSEL: I opened the door, and I walked in. I saw General Flynn. I saw \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2  sitting there. I was not happy to see the people in the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Explain why.\par \par \uc2 CIPOLLONE: Well, again, I don`t think they are providing -- well, first of all, the Overstock person, I`ve never met, I never knew who this guy was.\par \par \uc2 Actually, the first thing I did. I walked in, I looked at him. And I said, who are you? And he told me, I don`t think -- I don`t think any of these people were providing the president with good advice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, NBC NEWS HOST: Some of the most explosive testimony from the January 6 committee`s hearings this summer, Donald Trump`s White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  giving a sliver of his version of the coup-plotting before the insurrection, and it was big news, we found out that Cipollone and his deputy White House Counsel Pat Philbin, we`re going to testify before a criminal grand jury investigating the attempted coup.\par \par \uc2 Well, today was the day that Cipollone and Philbin showed up to court walking in the door with cameras watching.\par \par \uc2 Cipollone spent nearly 2-1/2 hours before the grand jury while Philbin spent about two hours in there. So, what does all this mean for the trajectory of the investigation?\par \par \uc2 Renato Mariotti is a former federal prosecutor, legal affairs columnist for Politico Magazine, host of the news and legal analyst podcast On Topic and he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 So, Renato, the first thing I think just to note here is, after the January 6 hearings, we saw increased activity in this grand jury that clearly was investigating high levels of the coup-plotting because they were talking to Marc Short and Greg Jacob, what`s the significance of them finally getting Cipollone and Philbin in to testify before an actual grand jury?\par \par \uc2 [20:35:01]\par \par \uc2 RENATO MARIOTTI, LEGAL AFFAIRS COLUMNIST, POLITICO MAGAZINE: Well, I`ve got to say, Chris, this is a witness that we have a very good idea of the value of because as you mentioned a moment ago, we are at a sliver of his testimony. And I think the key word there is sliver, there were so many times during the January 6 hearings, where they would ask him a question and he would say privilege, or there`ll be an objection he wouldn`t answer. He would talk about his own beliefs but wouldn`t talk about what he told \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that`s going to all change in front of the grand jury.\par \par \uc2 The case is very clear on the D.C. circuit that the grand juries need for that testimony, Trump`s any executive privilege, or certainly any attorney client privilege for a lawyer in the government.\par \par \uc2 So, he`s going to be able to provide that and I think it`s going to be really important testimony because I really think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  saw him as his own attorney, and is somebody who would be giving, you know, his own views for -- you know, directly to get frank advice.\par \par \uc2 So, I expect some really important and potentially explosive testimony coming from the former White House Counsel.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let`s talk about that grand jury, because right now we have a situation, there are two different -- well, there are three different legal cases \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  faces. There is a grand jury that`s been impaneled in Fulton County, Georgia, in which \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  has been calling lots of people, clearly investigating efforts to overturn the election in that state there in that county. \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , you know, Newt Gingrich on January 6 Committee, I lose track of who.\par \par 