e candidate, of course, running for the Senate seat against John - - well, running for the Senate seat against \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , who, of course, has his own, you know, health issues, and has been unable to campaign as actively as some might have hoped on the Democratic side.\par \par \uc2 JULIA TERRUSO, POLITICAL REPORTER, THE PHILADELPHIA INQUIRER: Yes, I think it`s clear that we`re seeing Biden recognizing both the symbolic importance of Philadelphia giving this -- his speech about the soul of democracy and also just the huge political importance of the state, as you said, we have a Senate race that could determine control of the Senate. Mehmet Oz is up - - is down between five and 10 points against \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . And the governor`s race could determine, you know, abortion rights in the state, how elections are run in the state. So, two hugely important elections, you have Biden here three times, you have Trump trying to rally his endorsed candidates Mastriano and Oz on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Those are two Republicans, by the way, who were very different. They have not done any campaigning together at all. And they`ll be sharing a stage with Trump and hoping that you know, that can boost their campaigns and we`ll have to see if you know, they wind up running a little more in line with one another. And if they`re different bases might help each other`s campaigns out, so a lot of interesting stuff going on in both races in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, Sahil, nationally how important have you`ve seen abortion as an issue that is galvanizing both new registrations for the Democratic Party and also more turnout in some of the races we`ve seen so far and, of course, importantly, the referendum in Kansas?\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, MSNBC SENIOR NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: It`s been a major galvanizing force around the country, particularly for Democrats, Andrea. A lot of voters who support abortion rights seemed to have taken this for granted. Of course, Roe vs. Wade was on the books for about half a century and it was overturned by the Supreme Court enabling states and Congress as well to outlaw or ban abortion. We`ve seen evidence of that around the country.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 Most recently in that New York 19, that special election in that Bellwether House District in Hudson Valley where the Democrat Pat Ryan scored a rather unexpected upset victory against the Republican \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2  and what was basically a test of the two party`s strongest candidates for that region, as well as their strongest messages. Pat Ryan branded heavily on abortion rights. He used that issue to galvanize voters in that election and connected it to this larger struggle for freedom. And across the country, we are seeing more and more signs of that red wave receding.\par \par \uc2 Let`s put up on the screen some of the examples we have of that. There`s the New York 19 special election. Our recent NBC News poll found that persuadable voters are trending toward Democrats. They lean Republican by six points earlier this year, now they`re leaning Democrats by three points, unusual for the party in power in the midterm election year. The Republican enthusiasm gap has shrunk from 17 points to two points.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned McConnell downplaying Senate expectations, although he`s trying to walk that back, he does know that Mehmet Oz and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in Georgia have to win in order for him to become Majority Leader the fact that Republicans are cutting ad spending in Arizona and spending more money in Ohio, it should have been a cakewalk, a layup for Republicans and a year like this. It`s essentially a red state nowadays, Ohio is. So the signs are all pointing to a receding red wave.\par \par \uc2 But having said all that, Andrea, it`s important to know Republicans don`t need a red wave to capture control of Congress this fall, even a series of red ripples will do given how narrow the democratic margins are. But given all this, all the evidence, we`re seeing Democrats, I think, for the first time have reason to hope that they can potentially hang on to not just the Senate, but maybe even the House.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, Eugene, just very briefly, this is the first time that President Biden is going to be weighing in, of course, on the fallout from Mar-a-Lago as he`s out on the campaign trail. How aggressively do you think that he should pursue that issue?\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: I think one of the things is it`s -- it is almost a perfect issue for President Biden to go out and create a contrast between himself and the Republicans. Republicans have been worried for years about -- the last year and a half about how much \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was going to factor into the November midterm elections. And this gives the president an opportunity to do so.\par \par \uc2 I will say I wouldn`t expect the president to weigh in too aggressively. And if he does, he`ll do it, you know, in a more nuanced way, possibly, and especially because the DOJ is a part of that. He doesn`t want to be seen as putting his thumb on the scale. That has been something very important to this president and this White House as they walked into the door because of the way \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  treated the DOJ while he was president. So I think Democrats want to see that. I think we`ll get a little taste of that, but probably not as much as progressives, for example, would want.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Eugene Daniels, Mike Memoli, Sahil Kapur, and Julia Terruso, thanks to all. And between the lines, what the Justice Department has found in the documents taken from Mar-a-Lago and what could it mean for the investigations around the former president? This is "CHRIS JANSING REPORTS" only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: We`re entering a new phase in the investigation into former President Trump`s handling of 