e nuggets every time there`s a new one. And then Trump making these attacks over social media, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: So you`re basically hitting refresh every few seconds.\par \par \uc2 REILLY: Correct.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Last couple of weeks, as a matter of fact. Melissa Murray, thanks for being with us. So given that the initial review of the material is now complete, Justice Department arguing that Trump`s request for a special master came too late. Do you agree that they`ve already had a filter team looking at it? When does he still have standing to -- well, he`s standing, I guess, but does he still have a legal argument to make?\par \par \uc2 MELISSA MURRAY, FORMER CLERK TO THEN-JUDGE SONIA SOTOMAYOR: Well, it`s true that it`s unorthodox to request a review by a special master so late in the game almost two weeks after those documents were first recovered and returned. But we can still obviously file that request and obviously, the judge is inclined to entertain it. But it really does come down to whether have we already done what you`re requesting the special master do. And so the DOJ has said it`s conducted a review of all of the material that could be subject to the attorney-client privilege and set those aside. The president -- the former president rather, has said that he`s actually more concerned about issues of executive privilege.\par \par \uc2 Those arguments, I think, are still really complicated for him to make at this juncture, given that they are turning over these documents to the Justice Department, which is a branch of the executive. So and again, the whole idea that executive privilege can be wielded by a former president has already been firmly refuted both by the National Archives and by a number of lower courts that say that privilege resides with the incumbent president. So there`s a lot here that could happen. We`re eagerly looking forward to Thursday.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, Melissa, amid reports that the president -- the former president had a lot of trouble assembling a top legal team, he has reportedly hired Chris Kise. He`s Florida`s former solicitor general to represent him. Kise has close ties to Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , has a winning record in the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , reputation as a political night fighter. So how could this affect the defense strategy? There was a lot of questions raised, shall we say about the lawyering up to now.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: That is very generously put, Andrea. I imagine that it will be a more buttoned-up effort going forward. Chris Kise is a good lawyer. A number of the lawyers who were already on Trump`s team, Evan Corcoran, have good reputations. But this is really unprecedented -- an unprecedented situation of a former president perhaps being the subject of a criminal inquiry, also fighting on a number of different fronts in a number of jurisdictions. So you really need someone who`s not only skilled in the criminal justice system but also someone who can fight a multi-front war. There are a number of different episodes that could lead to possible legal exposure, and they all relate to each other. So it`s important that someone can be on top of all of them.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And to Ryan, Tony Ornato, the Secret Service Officer, then-Deputy Chief of Staff where -- if unprecedented, rise from the service to a political role or transfer, I should say. He`s now retiring. He`s had his 25 years. But there were questions as to whether he`s going to be testifying to a homeland inquiry about the delay in reporting some of those e-mails that were lost from January 6 texts and e-mails.\par \par \uc2 REILLY: That`s right. And then Cassie Hutchinson had that dramatic testimony that we saw before the January 6 committee relaying a story that she heard reportedly from Tony about this idea that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was trying to steer essentially and force his secret service members to go back to 