t his public postings.\par \par \uc2 It came up in the congressional -- recent congressional testimony of \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Wray, he was asked about it and he said that he had some concerns about those tweets was familiar with them, and really stressed the importance of \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents to not only be unbiased and open-minded when it comes to their investigations, but also in their -- in their public statements and tweets, and outward messaging. So I think that`s something that really concerned the director.\par \par \uc2 We have no idea why he retired. We are told through our colleague, Mike Cosner, that he was eligible for retirement. It is possible that he just decided to leave on his own. It`s possible that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director`s remarks had some impact on this.\par \par \uc2 The bigger thing and the reason why Hunter Biden`s name has come up in all this is back in late July, Grassley sent another letter saying he`d been made aware by two whistleblowers that there were some concerns that they had that this same a sack, Thibault was involved in an effort to close up an inquiry involving Hunter Biden`s laptop that he says -- the whistleblowers say, was based on public information that was verifiable through the results of a search warrant, this seizing of Biden`s laptop. That`s something that I think needs to be put into a little bit of context so we understand it, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 First off, at that time, it`s highly unlikely any investigation would have been open in the fall of 2020. You know, that`s kind of that window where the Department of Justice prefers not to take any overt investigative steps that may impact an election, hard to think of something more overt than the presidential candidate`s son being having an investigation opened on him.\par \par \uc2 And then as we know, in two separate investigations, the \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  investigation for which we recently just reported charges are unlikely, and in