e. New satellite photos out today show the damage on the roof of that power plant after being caught in the crosshairs of shelling between Ukraine and Russia. Escalating fears of the stability of the Zaporizhzhia plant occupied by Russia have led to local authorities giving iodine tablets to residents in order to prevent some of the worst possible consequences if there were a radiation leak.\par \par \uc2 NBC's Josh Lederman is in Poltava, Ukraine. So, Josh, where this area is located had seen some of the most intense fightings in recent weeks, you know, you've been there. What can the inspectors do to make the situation any safer?\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, part of this, Andrea, is going to be a fact-finding mission with the \uc2 IAEA\uc2  saying they want to go and assess exactly how much damage has been done to the Zaporizhzhia nuclear power plant and which parts of this sprawling facility, the largest in Europe, have been affected.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:01]\par \par \uc2 Beyond that, they also want to assess whether the safety and backup power systems that are traditionally kept intact at power plants like this are actually working right now. They want to know if something were to go wrong, whether those fail-safes are still in place or whether they have been knocked out by the shelling and other fighting that's been done around this plant.\par \par \uc2 But they also, Andrea, want to speak with the Ukrainian nuclear workers who have been working there for months now under duress, according to many accounts. They have a claim that they have been tortured there, perhaps, you know, being held against their will, in some cases by the Russians who are now in charge of that plant. But the key question is, given that Russia does control that site, whether they are going to actually get a fair and transparent look at what's happening there or whether the Russians may try to hide some of what's actually been going on there.\par \par \uc2 And 