nto as many homes. For the residents that are impacted, that's one or two homes too many, or whatever the handful of homes there are.\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS: Now I understand, I spoke with someone at the National Weather Service that you guys have a reservoir. That's actually not there to protect the city. It's for recreational purposes, but it's been holding that water. That reservoir is still putting out I believe 40,000 cubic feet of water, which is a lot more than usually we have. So there's still a lot of challenges to deal with.\par \par \uc2 LUMUMBA: Well, the reservoir, you are correct that it is not a flood mitigation reservoir.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:02]\par \par \uc2 It is actually a drinking water reservoir. So that is the body of water contain for it to provide drinking water to the city of Jackson. But right now based on what is being released, it is creating complications there as well. There have been efforts and discussions about mitigating the flooding in this area for some time. More than 30 years. And so prayerfully, we can get past the point to really find solutions to our residents.\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS: OK. Well, Mayor, thank you for taking the time to talk to us. I know you have a press conference coming up to inform the media of the latest. Good luck. Hopefully it doesn't rain anymore and things continue to improve here in Jackson, Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 Back to you, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Our thanks -- if you can convey our thanks to the mayor for taking time to give us all that briefing. That was really helpful.\par \par \uc2 Thanks you so much, Guad Venegas, in Jackson, Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 And school daze. Parents and students working to get a handle on the school year while navigating classroom COVID regulations there a little bit confusing.\par \par \uc2 This is CHRIS JANSING REPORTS on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:08]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Children in Philadelphia are heading back to 