om some teachers and the new superintendent here in Philadelphia about some of the challenges and some of the questions that some parents have with regard to the 10-day masking policy. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONNA, PHILADELPHIA TEACHER: Our plan is to start the school year running as strategically as possible the first day. We have an after-school program, we have incentives already lined up. We're going to have mentors assigned to kids.\par \par \uc2 TONY WATLINGTON, PHILADELPHIA SCHOOL DISTRICT SUPERINTENDENT: The pandemic has had a lingering effect on us. They may continue to present some challenges. Today marks an opportunity to steer our students toward a brand new beginning.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SOLIS: And the superintendent making it very clear he's not really interested in what other school districts are doing, saying this is what's going to work best for the students and staff of Philadelphia. And right now, they are monitoring the CDC levels of transmission. It's currently medium here in Philadelphia. So right now, again, letting and allowing for that 10-day policy. They say if things transition to the high, well, then they may have to reevaluate the mask mandate and that could create a lot more controversy. Andrea?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Thank you so much, George. And I saw Mayor Kenny was out there, too, with the superintendent. They were really welcoming those kids back to school. So there's a lot of excitement about the first day of school everywhere. Masks or no masks.\par \par \uc2 Doctor Blackstock, what concerns do you have about the return to school and what prevention methods do you suggest?\par \par \uc2 DR. UCHE BLACKSTOCK, FOUNDER AND CEO, ADVANCING HEALTH EQUITY: Yes, Andrea, thank you so much for having me. I just preface this with, you know, I have two school-aged children. I have a 5- and 7-year-old, who will be returning to New York City public schools next week. And I would say, you know, I'm concerned because most areas of the country have substantial to high transmission levels right now. And so we are basically returning teachers, staff and students back to school with many of the COVID protections lifted.\par \par \uc2 So no more surveillance testing. There's no test to exit. So if a child is infected and comes out of isolation, they don't have to repeat a test to say that they're negative and we know that people are usually infectious for more than five days. And then also many districts do not even have mask policies. So this policy that even Philadelphia has of bringing a mask for the first 10 days, to be honest, with me, I don't know what the evidence is behind that.\par \par \uc2 I would say that if most areas of the country are substantial to high transmission, that, you know, students and staff and teachers should be masking. We know that schools are not inherently safe, but they are safe when a layer of mitigation strategy is used. And so that is masking. That is testing. But also paying attention to air and clean air, and that is something that, although schools receive congressional funding, I have not seen it being invested into ensuring that there's an infrastructure in place to make sure that there's clean air in our schools for children.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Yes, and because Republicans in Congress have blocked COVID funding, the government announced, the administration announced that they are no longer going to provide those free tests that they need to save them in case there's another surge.\par \par \uc2 BLACKSTOCK: Right.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: So that's another area where there's going to be a lot less surveillance.\par \par \uc2 BLACKSTOCK: Absolutely, Andrea. So we have a more contagious variant. We're heading into cold weather where people will be indoors more, and all of this, and we have relaxed, you know, COVID guidance.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:03]\par \par \uc2 So the guidelines have been relaxed. And so I'm concerned that we are going to see a spike in cases and people are not going to have the, quote- unquote, "tools" that the administration has discussed available to their use. Those rapid tests are incredibly helpful. It can tell you whether or a person is infectious, meaning whether they are able to transmit the virus and infect somebody else. And I would say that, you know, heading into the winter, that is a tool that every American should have readily available and accessible to them.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Especially with the kids back in school and bringing things home as well.\par \par \uc2 BLACKSTOCK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Doctor Uche Blackstock, thank you so much. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BLACKSTOCK: Thank you, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And rinse and repeat. An old idea for how to reform Social Security gets revived by Republicans ahead of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 This is CHRIS JANSING REPORTS. Stay with us on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: The Social Security program in the U.S. is so important to most voters. Some have called it the third rail of American politics actually for decades. And now some Republican candidates from Blake Masters in Arizona to Senator Ron Johnson in Wisconsin are floating ideas for changing the program and Democrats are hoping they will pay a big political price.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:04]\par \par \uc2 This whole idea of privatization goes all the way back in fact to the \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  years. Joining me now is NBC News senior national political reporter Sahil Kapur and White House editor for "Politico," Sam Stein.\par \par \uc2 So, Sahil, you wrote about how Democrats see this as a big opportunity. Why are some Republicans taking this on now with the midterms approaching?\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Andrea, there's no master plan coming from the top of the Republican Party here. These ideas have come about organically as many of these Republican candidates campaign on tackling inflation by cutting government spending. And when asked to get specific, many of them invariably mentioned the need to cut spending on these retirement programs like Social Security and sometimes Medicare, just given how big a part of the federal budget they are.\par \par \uc2 Let's put up a list of some of the prominent Republicans floating this. There's \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 , a Senate nominee in Arizona who recently made waves by calling for privatization of Social Security. There's Ron Johnson, the senator from Wisconsin facing reelection, who's called for shifting Social Security from a mandatory program to a discretionary program. What does that mean? It means Congress would have to reauthorize it every year or the funding would go away.\par \par \uc2 There's \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , who several months ago proposed a plan that would put all federal laws on the chopping block every five years, unless Congress acts, passes a low to continue them. Those programs would include Social Security. And then there's the Republican Study Committee led by the man you see there, Jim Banks. It's a group of about a hundred influential House Republicans.\par \par \uc2 They proposed a budget in June that would include long-term changes like raising the retirement age in accordance with life span and reducing benefits over the long haul by indexing them to a lower rate or rather a slower rate of growth.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is a split within the party because many of the old guard Republicans such as Senate minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , who were around for the 2005 debacle when the Bush administration tried partial privatization of Social Security, they're not touching this. They know that it could be politically lethal with voters and Democrats are trying to exploit this issue, drive that wedge between the Republican coalition, you know, with fiscal conservatives on one side who support these changes, with the base of older voters in the party who like Social Security, get spooked by the prospect of any cuts to that program.\par \par \uc2 I put these questions to the \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  campaign because he's been back and forth on this issue. I want to put up what he said or rather what they said on the screen. His campaign spokesman told me that \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  is not backing off his support for private accounts but he wants them to be -- in the future he wants them to be optional and only for younger generations who are not currently getting Social Security, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: The whole theory behind Social Security and these other entitlement programs, that they have to be mandatory because they'll never get renewed, especially not now in a 50-50 Senate. I remember all the way back, former White House chief of staff James Baker, James A. Baker III, you know, said that this is, you know, this is the third rail and we're not going to touch it. And there were Republicans then trying to do it. So it just seems as though this is a really tough issue to be taking on.\par \par \uc2 But if you want to deal with the deficit, Sam Stein, the only way to deal with the big money in the deficit is to go after entitlements. That's why Democrats have always said it should be off the table.\par \par \uc2 SAM STEIN, POLITICO WHITE HOUSE EDITOR: Yes, I mean, you can solidify Social Security by actually raising the threshold of payroll taxes so more people pay into it, it gets elongated as a program. There's other ways to stabilize these entitlements other than cutting them. And I think, you know, what's interesting here to me at least is the new politics on the Republican side of things.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is unconventional in a lot of ways. One of the ways he was unconventional is that he was the first Republican presidential candidate in modern times who overtly was aligning himself with these traditional bedrock entitlement programs. He did not want to touch Social Security. He did not want to touch Medicare. He made it very clear that he thought it was a political loser for the Republican Party. In fact he was hyper critical of \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2 's budget at the time.\par \par \uc2 You know, with him out of the scene, a new generation of Republicans come up, they are maybe less chastened by that possibility. But I will say the fact that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  for instance is not speaking out on this, the fact that Kevin McCarthy has not endorsed any of this stuff, does signal to me that, you know, there's enough Republicans out there who know that this would rebound on them politically and don't want to touch it at all.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: That doesn't mean the Democrats can't message on it, though, Sam.\par \par \uc2 STEIN: No, it doesn't.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Of course.\par \par \uc2 Sam, switching gears, let's talk about the president's popularity. It's been a drag on midterm candidates, but a new \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll now showing his approval rating is up from 42 percent to 45 percent. So what do you think? That's the abortion decision? What do you think is the biggest factor?\par \par \uc2 STEIN: I think it's a mix of things. So one obviously is Dobbs and the decision has obviously generated a ton of interest in the election among progressives and even independents, I should say. You see that in the polling. I think, though, you know, it might be even more crude than that. It could just simply be that the price of gasoline has fallen for something like 50 to 60 straight days now and people -- that is like the quintessential pocketbook issue.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:05]\par \par \uc2 People literally are reminded of the price of gasoline as they drive down the road. And so when you see that price falling, you start feeling a little bit better by your own situation, that's coincided neatly with Biden's own approval rating coming up. I think some of this probably is Democrats coming home after a long time of feeling a little bit down about the presidency but you see the numbers on independent rising, too.\par \par \uc2 And I'll just note this. It doesn't guarantee like fall success. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is roughly in the same spot that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in 2018 and \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  in 2010. But there is one other element that's different than those two situations, which is unlike those two situations, you do have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the ex-president lingering over the political discourse, very firmly still in the political landscape, both with respect to the Mar-a-Lago seizing of records and the possibility that the January 6th Committee comes back in September with new hearings and new information.\par \par \uc2 We've never really had a situation like this and it is making the selection less of a referendum and more of a contrast, which is exactly what Democrats want.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Sam Stein and Sahil Kapur, thank you both so much.\par \par \uc2 And that does it for this edition of CHRIS JANSING REPORTS. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday at 1:00 p.m. right here on MSNBC. And to join me tomorrow at noon for AMR, "ANDREA MITCHELL REPORTS."\par \par \uc2 "KATY TUR REPORTS" starts right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: There is a lot to catch you up with, and good to be with you. I'm Katy Tur.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220830ei8t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for August 27, 2022 MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui, Susan del Percio \par \uc2 6,541 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:26]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: Biden Resurgent.\par \par \uc2 Right now, while all eyes are following the dramatic news about the bombshell Mar-a-Lago investigation, and after a year of forecasts, presuming a red tide at November midterms, Democrats appear to have turned a corner. At the center of it, the President whose approval ratings have surged to their highest level in a year.\par \par \uc2 The latest Gallup poll of American voters showing a six-point jump for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  since May, driving that rebound, a sustained string of good economic news and legislative wins for the White House.\par \par \uc2 The President`s actions on student debt this week, capping a busy summer, but some of that began with the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  decision to overturn Roe, putting Democrats on the defensive as primary season was opening up and now closing out with the midterm picture largely in place and a national anger over the Roe decision driving much of the narrative from Democrats ahead of November.\par \par \uc2 So, with pieces in place and headwinds behind them, the President is going on the attack on the campaign trail for Democrats with a sharp message for a Republican Party deep in thrall to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The MAGA Republicans don`t just threaten our personal rights and economic security. They`re a threat to our very democracy. They refuse to accept the will of the people. They embrace -- embrace political violence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Biden seems to be bouncing back just in time, but will it sealed the deal for midterm voters with 10 weeks left to go.\par \par \uc2 That`s POLITICSNATION tonight.\par \par \uc2 And shortly, the son and daughter-in-law of Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , Jr. join me ahead of the 59th anniversary of the march for jobs and freedom.\par \par \uc2 Has the country made good on the Civil Rights movement`s demands more than half a century later. You`ll want to hear their answer.\par \par \uc2 All of that, plus the latest on the news out of the Mar-a-Lago investigation.\par \par \uc2 All of that tonight on POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 But first, joining me now Congresswoman Shantel Brown, Democrat of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, great to see you as always, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 REP. SHANTEL BROWN (D-OH): Hi.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I have to start of course, with your reaction to yesterday`s bombshell development in the Mar-a-Lago investigation, the release of a redacted copy of the affidavit used by Federal investigators to obtain the warrant used to search Donald Trump`s estate three weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 I have to be clear that this affidavit is not evidence of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  having committed a crime, only that the DOJ believes it has probable cause a crime has been committed.\par \par \uc2 But the affidavit and its related memo bears out more of what we`ve already learned that President Trump held on to dozens of classified documents after leaving office, some of which according to the affidavit, were highly classified and could have compromised our Intelligence operations and operatives if they had fallen into the wrong hands.\par \par \uc2 Regardless, the narrative from many Republican lawmakers, including your colleagues in the House, has been to downplay the seriousness of these implications as they have all done this month.\par \par \uc2 I mean, are you surprised that House Republicans have the so-called law and order party continue to defend Trump?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Sadly, I am not, but first and foremost, thanks for having me, Reverend Al. It`s always a pleasure to be on your show.\par \par \uc2 I have to take the words of \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  and just say that their shamelessness is their superpower, the hypocrisy of the Republican Party is boundless. It is not surprising to me that they continue to defend this man who, whose campaign started out as a big lie, whose election into office continued on as a big lie as he debated the numbers of people that were in attendance for his inauguration.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:09]\par \par \uc2 BROWN: I mean, this is just a repetitive cycle. And sadly, it is very disappointing that the folks on the other side of the aisle do not have the courage to be able to stand up to this man.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know what else it is going to take, but I am proud to say, as a member of the Oversight Committee, we are going to do everything that we can to get to the bottom of this, Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Congresswoman, after a bruising year, President Biden appears to be rallying. Again, there is that Gallup poll I mentioned at the top showing six-point bounce in his approval rating since May, kind of a capstone after a summer of wins for the White House, including, of course, his actions this week on student debt and which we`ll get to.\par \par \uc2 The President on the campaign trail for Democrats, and as you heard in that clip, at the top of the show, his language criticizing MAGA Republicans is about as forceful, as we`ve heard from him ahead of November midterms.\par \par \uc2 What`s your take on the Biden`s brand-new bag, let me put it that way?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: I don`t know that it`s a brand-new bag, but we`re happy to see this Joe-mentum coming back again, once and for all for us. I think that President Biden has shown people when they count him out, the people can`t count, and that is a quote from my good friend and partner, Antjuan Seawright.\par \par \uc2 Listen, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , as his numbers go up, prices of gas is going down. He has been able to do some tremendous things in these two years. And so as Ohio`s 11th Congressional Representative, these things are going to have a tremendous impact on the people that I was elected to represent.\par \par \uc2 We are going to be doing things that are going to be putting money back in the pocket books of people, helping to lower prescription drug prices. We are going to be making and creating jobs to the tune of 10,000, at least in Ohio, and these jobs will be good paying union jobs, many that do not require a college degree.\par \par \uc2 So, I cannot express how excited I am, that the rest of the country is finally starting to recognize the great work of this administration and this Congress because we have had a smorgasbord of accomplishments Reverend Al, who are people`s political palate, and now they`re finally getting a taste.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, but for the record, Antjuan Seawright, does my radio show every Tuesday, co-hosts with me. So, he is a different kind of partner for me, too.\par \par \uc2 But Congresswoman on Thursday, as the President was unveiling his actions to reduce student debt, you tweeted: "It is imperative that we acknowledge how race plays a role. Black college graduates, typically owe nearly double would White college graduate typically owes. This ultimately contributes to the existing racial wealth gap."\par \par \uc2 I don`t have to tell that some critics of the President`s actions contend that the relief often is not enough, especially for Black borrowers, Black women, specifically, who hold a disproportionate share of student debt, according to census data. Of course, others have said the actions are good stuff on a pending crisis.\par \par \uc2 Do you expect congressional action on student debt if Democrats hold on to the House, or at least blunt GOP gains in November?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: I certainly do, and why I say that is because you know, my political godfather is Representative and Whip Clyburn, and he has been fighting this fight for a long -- a very long time. So, this continues to be high on his priority list, and I want to give credit where credit is due.\par \par \uc2 He was one of the champions behind this legislation moving forward and pushing President Biden to make the move that he did to go ahead and forgive this $10,000.00 student debt relief.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think that it is the first step and that is what we have to acknowledge, Reverend Al. We have to continue to look at the glass as half full, not half empty. We don`t eat an elephant in one bite. So, this is a first step of what I expect to be many more to come.\par \par \uc2 As a proud member of the \uc2 CBC\uc2 , the Congressional Black Caucus, Chairwoman Beatty, who also continues to champion this effort, and so I think we just have to really take pride in the things that we have been able to successfully accomplish, but also recognize the fight is not over.\par \par \uc2 Yes, there is still more work to do, but this is going to help at least one in four Black borrowers be able to eliminate their debt. This is going to reduce the debt of over 40 million Americans and these Americans aren`t all Democrats. There are some Republicans and Independents as well.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that this is definitely a good first step and I will tell the people that there is definitely more to come, but we have to do exactly what you said. This is something that is going to be critical for the midterm election because if we do not hold the House and if we do not pick up seats in the Senate then we can only expect for us to go backwards from here.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:08]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Finally, Congresswoman abortion rights have emerged as one of the driving forces of our upcoming midterms. You had a Democrat win a special election here in New York State this week. His campaign centering on abortion rights protections, after Roe`s reversals.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are defining their Republican opposition with this issue. We`ve seen where it is driving turnout and fundraising for the party. Was this rollback of abortion rights across so many states a political mistake for the conservative movement? Republican politicians who promoted it for so long as it appears to now be driving their opposition ahead of November?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Absolutely, Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 I think that the Republicans grossly underestimated the power and the vote of women. When you tell us that we can`t do something, you can see in Kansas, a State that tends to be a swing state and maybe even leans a little bit more Republican, you could see in the outcome of their primary, how critical and how motivating this issue of abortion is.\par \par \uc2 And let`s be clear, it is not just about abortion. What I think you are recognizing is that women are realizing and their partners that the government is trying to take control of their bodies, take control of their healthcare.\par \par \uc2 So, this is about us being able to decide our own healthcare, as well as a privacy issue. And so yes, I think it is going to be a motivating factor for these midterm elections, and I guess if there was a silver lining with the decision that this right-wing radical Justice Supreme Court did make, it was definitely a victory for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you, Congresswoman Shantel Brown.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is global human rights activist, \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , III and Arndrea Waters King, not only his wife, but the President of the Drum Major Institute.\par \par \uc2 Brother Martin, Sister Arndrea, thank you both for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 I started talking to you, as parents, who watched President Biden take steps to rein in student debt this week that $10,000.00 in Federal debt relief for borrowers, making less than $125,000.00; a twenty thousand relief for Pell Grant borrowers.\par \par \uc2 Your daughter, Yolanda, who I call my niece. She is a teenager now. But I wonder what the President`s actions on student debt will mean for kids her age? I also think about the impact on Black borrowers, which I just discussed with Congresswoman Shantel Brown.\par \par \uc2 Brother Martin, I`ll start with you. What are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN LUTHER KING III, GLOBAL HUMAN RIGHTS ACTIVIST: Well, I think that it propels yet generations unborn in a sense, because it is hard to imagine going to college for four years and ending up with $100,000.00 of debt, which could be attributed to so many other things.\par \par \uc2 When I went to school many, many years ago, you know, you didn`t have this kind of albatross around your neck. Sometimes people ending up with $300,000.00 of debt, just going to college. That is unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 I`m so thankful to see that the President has done this. I think it is certainly something that my father and mother would have honored and as it relates to Yolanda, we`re going to be on another zone, I think people will be in another zone, even though it`s a very short period of time.\par \par \uc2 You know, when I think about it, it is only now four years, which is frightening, but that is going to help propel her generation as well.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Arndrea, next year, we mark the 60th anniversary of the March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom, where your late father-in-law did the historic "I Have a Dream" speech.\par \par \uc2 It is nearly a lifetime since the impact of that day, the actions that followed that shaped our entire nation. What can we share that you and Drum Major Institute, National Action Network and I know among others are following your leader, working with you. What are you planning for the 60th anniversary?\par \par \uc2 ARNDREA WATERS KING, PRESIDENT, DRUM MAJOR INSTITUTE: Well, I think it`s fair to say that our nation is on her knees. Democracy is on her knees, and everything that \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , Jr. and Coretta Scott King and so many others fought and literally died for is being challenged, the gains that were made, and so we can`t think of any better time to call the nation together.\par \par \uc2 The Drum Major Institute along with National Action Network, next year for the 60th anniversary, it is time for us to stop talking about the dream, it is time for us to stop talking and using \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , Jr. as an idol and it`s time for all of us to live up to those ideals.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:07]\par \par \uc2 A. KING: And with that in mind, we are all coming together. We all will be in Washington and we are all, for the 60th anniversary next year, looking forward to having an opportunity in this nation to realize the dream.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Martin, I have that as much as I don`t want to, you`ve seen where Republican politicians, certainly in those first days after Mar-a- Lago searching the former President`s house, where they were comparing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to your father, because both were supposedly subject to \uc2 FBI\uc2  harassment.\par \par \uc2 Now speaking to those same politicians, explain to the nation what is the difference between what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is dealing with, and what your father and his co-workers and team endured from the Federal government?\par \par \uc2 M. KING: Well, it is a simple word "morality." What \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , Jr. represented was a moral force for change in our nation. It is -- it should be clear to everyone, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  represents a force, but it certainly is not moral.\par \par \uc2 In fact, most of what he is, I would say, some of what he has done, maybe even a significant amount is immoral, basically, really just how you treat people.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that alone ought to be enough for people to say, "You know what? There is a better way, even if you disagree." What Dad showed us was how to disagree without being disagreeable.\par \par \uc2 What \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  shows us is civility is out of the window, "I`m going to denigrate a person. I`m going to on talk about them. They may be disabled; I`m going to talk about them."\par \par \uc2 So you know, but all of the things that we`re looking at the fact of the matter is, all of these documents that exist were documents that it has shown now that the government has been asking for, for a long time and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  refused to give them, so they had to go and get them, which are classified, that should be not seen by everyone.\par \par \uc2 So you know, this is -- these are two -- this doesn`t even -- it is like comparing an \uc2 Apple\uc2  to a peanut.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Finally, I know you`re on the cusp of a major initiative to reshape our politics on a national level, incorporating the progressive ideas, you both share, and your parents and parents-in-law share, basically taking the kind of statewide organizing we`ve seen succeed in your home of Georgia, coast to coast.\par \par \uc2 Arndrea, what can you tell us about tomorrow, and what is going to be announced without taking away tomorrow`s announcement? I know it`s going to be major and going to be a game changer.\par \par \uc2 A. KING: What we are announcing is the Drum Majors Coalition, where we are raising $100 million that is going directly into 40 groups that we have identified that are Black and Brown led groups in key swing states, and also states that have the possibility of turning from red to at least purple with the right infrastructure and the right connections.\par \par \uc2 So, we are raising $100 million. All of the money is going directly to groups on the ground. We quickly realized after the Federal Voting Rights not being passed thus far, that this has to be a state-by-state on the ground battle, and we are wanting to invest in the people that are every single day out working for all of us.\par \par \uc2 We want to build political power in the Black and Brown community and we really feel that this is the best way to do so.\par \par \uc2 I happen to be mentored by Reverend C.T. Vivian, so I know what happens when you are people that are on the ground, how they can literally change the world.\par \par \uc2 This is not theoretical for me this and for us. This is something that we know will work and can work and at this point has to work.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, there is a major announcement coming tomorrow and then we start the countdown to the 60th anniversary, where \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , III and Arndrea Waters, Yolanda, and National Action Network will be there to support.\par \par \uc2 But all of us are clear, Martin that Arndrea is in charge.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 President Biden`s recent win could help propel America forward. So, why are Republicans so focused on going back to the past?\par \par \uc2 That`s this week`s Got-cha.\par \par \uc2 And a Trump-backed candidate`s love of history goes too far. My panel joins me later in the show.\par \par \uc2 But first my colleague Richard Lui, with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, a very good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 Russia and Ukraine are accusing each other of firing weapons near Ukraine`s largest nuclear plant. Just another week of those accusations. Experts warn the shelling could trigger a catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the facility was temporarily disconnected from the national power grid for the first time in its 40-year history.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:12]\par \par \uc2 LUI: Families of Uvalde school shooting victims marching at the Texas Capitol today. They want the Governor to raise the minimum age to purchase semiautomatic rifles.\par \par \uc2 This week, a Federal Court in Fort Worth ruled a Texas law that bans 18-to- 20-year-olds from carrying handguns that that is unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 And the countdown is on for the launch of NASA`s Artemis rocket Monday. The unmanned mission is expected to travel 40,000 miles beyond the moon. It will set up the stage for a manned lunar mission which will land the first person of color and woman on the moon.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:24:55]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The midterms are just over two months away and if you listen to Republicans you might, think the most important issue in this election is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOP lawmakers have been lining up on cable news to defend the former President after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  searched his Mar-a-Lago home for classified documents.\par \par \uc2 Senator Ted Cruz called the investigation an abuse of power, even though the search warrant was legally obtained.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  wants to defund the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and her pal, Congressman \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  calls the search a form of fascism.\par \par \uc2 Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy is promising a massive investigation should Republicans take back the House.\par \par \uc2 The reason Republicans are fixated on the legal problems of the former guy is obvious, it is because Democrats are on a winning streak.\par \par \uc2 Biden and the Democrats in Congress are passing significant legislation with the slimmest of majorities, problems are being solved, and issues are being addressed that Americans actually care about.\par \par \uc2 In the past few weeks, Democrats passed the Inflation Reduction Act to tackle climate change, lower health costs, reduce the deficit, and impose a minimum tax on profits of the largest corporations.\par \par \uc2 They also passed the Public Safety Officers Support Act of 2022, which extends disability and death benefits to families of officers who suffer from posttraumatic stress disorder, or die by suicide.\par \par \uc2 Also, President Biden issued a second executive order to protect access to reproductive healthcare services, including abortion and contraception.\par \par \uc2 And this week, Biden announced student loan borrowers can have up to $20,000.00 forgiven if they qualify; and delayed repayment on loans until 2023.\par \par \uc2 All of this was accomplished with the backdrop of a strong job market and record low unemployment, which has resulted in Biden`s highest job approval rating in a year according to a new Gallup poll at 44 percent.\par \par \uc2 Voters know that the tide is turning and what once looked like an impending red wave is now turning bluer by the day.\par \par \uc2 You saw evidence of this earlier this week, when Democrat Pat Ryan won his congressional race in New York`s bellwether 19th District.\par \par \uc2 Republicans fell in line behind Trump when he got the presidential nomination in 2016. Now, half a decade later, that partnership has done the party more harm than good.\par \par \uc2 We can only hope that after the midterms, Republicans can come up with some fresh ideas.\par \par \uc2 I got-cha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:32:02]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 We have some breaking news. A Federal Judge in Florida has just issued a preliminary ruling on former President Donald Trump`s request for the appointment of a Special Master to review the documents seized by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  when they executed their search warrant on his Mar-a-Lago home. A hearing in the case has been scheduled for Thursday, September 1st.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get more insight into the legal problems swirling around \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and other big news stories from my political panel.\par \par \uc2 Joining me is Michael Hardaway, founder at Hardaway Wire; and Susan del Percio, Republican strategist.\par \par \uc2 Quickly both of you, give me your reaction to the breaking news of a hearing this coming Thursday. Susan, you go first.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DEL PERCIO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: It`s not surprising, it`s also not a big deal.\par \par \uc2 Let`s not forget, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tends to make his legal problems, PR problems and his PR problems, legal problems. So, this is just part of the narrative.\par \par \uc2 I find it interesting that he didn`t ask for a Special Master from the documents that were seized back in January, or I shouldn`t say seized, that were turned over. But now he`s heard a lot of mention of a Special Master on the news, on FOX News and other conservative places, so he wants it.\par \par \uc2 It`s not going to make a difference. A Special Master does not mean it`s Donald Trump`s person, it means it`s an independent person who will be reviewing and separating documents of a personal nature or those that may fall under a confidential nature between attorney-client privilege, and then everything else.\par \par \uc2 This isn`t going to make a difference.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Michael?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL HARDAWAY, FOUNDER, HARDAWAY WIRE: I agree, this makes no difference. You`re just delaying the inevitable. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had no legal standing to steal these classified documents from the American people. And so this decision that was just put forward today may delay this in the short term, but in long term, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has no standing and it has no impact on the long-term decisions here.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Susan, staying on this topic. In addition to the Special Master, Trump is also requesting a more detailed receipt of the items taken from his home arguing that the affidavit released yesterday was just about half of the 38 pages redacted does not provide any information that would allow Trump to understand why the search took place.\par \par \uc2 The affidavit is revealing that classified documents found at the residence include some of the country`s most sensitive secrets. It is clear Trump is in the DOJ`s crosshairs.\par \par \uc2 How damaging do you think this affidavit is for the President -- former President?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: I think it is extremely damaging to the former president because it is outlines that there are people who are -- I shouldn`t say it doesn`t exactly say this, but it is widely believed that because of the memo that introduced the affidavit that the DOJ was seeking to protect witnesses.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:13]\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: Let`s not forget, in this January 6 Select Committee hearings, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  said there are witnesses that have been threatened by the former President. Now, they are concerned about that with DOJ.\par \par \uc2 They are also worried about the safety of \uc2 FBI\uc2 , members of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . That`s a former President that is threatening witnesses and people are afraid will threaten the security of our law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 So, I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a lot to worry about. I think he doesn`t stand a chance of getting things further redacted. But you know, those national defense documents, Reverend, we`ve discussed this before, they talk about sources and methods, those aren`t just two words. Those are human lives. Those are people protecting our country overseas that he can flippantly throw around those documents and expose them and the secrets of our country is so dangerous.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately, now there is talk that some of those documents probably won`t even become public because they are so dangerous to be released for our national security\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Michael, the Judge unsealed the redacted affidavit in the interest of transparency. It reveals in the initial 15 boxes turned over to the National Archives in January, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  found 184 unique documents, bearing classified markings, including 67 marked as confidential, 92 marked secret, and 25 marked top-secret.\par \par \uc2 Did the released documents counteract Trump`s claims of a political witch hunt?\par \par \uc2 HARDAWAY: No, of course not. I think the first thing we should remember is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has no credibility in terms of his explanation for any of these things, and the one main question we should all be asking ourselves is, what did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  plan to do with these documents?\par \par \uc2 That`s a real concern for all of us, for the Intelligence Community, for those in government who have provided their specialties and their lives to serve in this regard, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has all of this classified information, and none of us know what he wanted to do with them, but clearly someone who has spent their entire lives grifting and committing illegal activities should be someone that we should be concerned with if he has access to this information.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Susan, switching gears now to Trump-backed Pennsylvania gubernatorial candidate, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Reuters has obtained a photo of Mastriano posing in Confederate uniform from a 2013-2014 faculty photo at Army War College.\par \par \uc2 The Keystone State was the site of some of the bloodiest fighting during the Civil War, where tens of thousands from the state died defending the Union. Now you have Mastriano, supported by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who was at the Capitol on January 6th to try to overturn a free and fair election running for Governor.\par \par \uc2 Is this photo a bad look for someone connected to a modern-day insurrection?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: I think it`s a bad look for anybody at any given time connected to anything at all, Rev. This is unacceptable. This is 2014. You can`t even say it was like when he was 16 years old going to a Halloween party like some elected officials have said before.\par \par \uc2 This was not too long ago. This was less than a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 You know, and let`s just add to that, not only is he an insurrectionist, not only was he wearing a Confederate uniform, he is losing in the polls right now and he has ties to an antisemitic social media site that he even paid to do some advertising for him.\par \par \uc2 So, it doesn`t surprise me at all, but I think that given where things are in this country right now and where Pennsylvania is, we are going to be looking at a Governor Shapiro.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Michael Hardaway and Susan del Percio, thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Just ahead, my next guest joins me to talk choice, prison reform, and the possibility of making history in the Deep South.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Yolanda Flowers has made history in Alabama. This year, she became the first Black major party nominee in the race for Governor. She says she is running a campaign based on God and has focused her efforts on reconstruction of the justice system, healthcare, and education.\par \par \uc2 She now faces off against incumbent Republican governor \uc2 Kay Ivey\uc2  in the General Election this November and she joins me now, Democratic nominee for Governor of Alabama, Yolanda flowers.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 YOLANDA FLOWERS (D), ALABAMA GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE: Thank you, Reverend Sharpton. I`m glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Miss Flowers, first of all, congratulations on your historic primary win and I know a lot about Alabama. I`m a New Yorker born in Brooklyn, but my mother was from Dothan, Alabama and spent her last years there. So, I know a little about Alabama politics.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve seen several governors across the country use their positions of power to elevate claims of election fraud and change the ballot process for voters. Your opponent, Governor \uc2 Kay Ivey\uc2  still falsely declares that the 2020 election was stolen from President -- former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 What is the significance right now of electing someone who will stand up for justice and against false election claims?\par \par \uc2 FLOWERS: The significance -- thank you for that question -- the significance of this election is ensuring that someone is placed in office who will care for the people, who will want to stand up for truth.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, the current abortion ban in Alabama is arguably one of the toughest antiabortion laws in the country prohibiting all abortions, with the single exception being the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:14]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You`ve been endorsed by Democrats for Life of America, an antiabortion rights organization and you told Alabama political reporter in May, you would have to, "Do research and get a panel together to decide whether to repeal Alabama`s abortion law." But you have also gone on record saying we don`t need to ban abortions and said that it`s up to God`s judgment. Can you please clarify for voters what is your stance on abortion?\par \par \uc2 FLOWERS: My stance on abortion, as I`ve stated several times via the media that I myself being a woman, I don`t like abortion, but -- and I want everyone to hear me clearly -- if it was me, it would take the act of God for me not to abort that child.\par \par \uc2 But on the same level, everyone has the right to choose what they want to do to their bodies -- every woman, every man, whatever they desire -- and even God doesn`t use his authority on us to choose, He just lays there. He lays it before us to choose death or life, you know.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what we should do as legislators. Leaders, we should not use our authority to place it on anybody. So, my stance as it is right now, it is prochoice.\par \par \uc2 I try to say, a whole life, no one could fathom what I was, you know, trying to say on that regards. So, you know, being a mother, a grandmother, and a great grandmother, and I have great granddaughters, I cannot see my grandbabies being or my granddaughters being raped and cannot have an abortion. That`s just wrong.\par \par \uc2 So my stance, I had to choose one of them and I chose pro-life -- prochoice, excuse me.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, that is interesting, and thank you for that clarity. You`ve spoken also a lot during your campaign about criminal justice reform.\par \par \uc2 Now, your opponent Governor Ivey signed off on $400 million of COVID relief money, which is nearly 20 percent of the State`s Federal relief allocation to go towards building state prisons. You condemned this behavior and said that private prisons are corrupting Alabama`s justice system.\par \par \uc2 How do you plan to reform the justice system without being labeled as soft on crime?\par \par \uc2 FLOWERS: The first thing I want to introduce to everyone as I`ve been trying very hard, is to reduce recidivism. And the way I was going to do that I plan to do that is implementing what I call a Restoration Rehabilitation Plan that will allow individuals who are up for parole to be able to enter into this program, whether it is, you know, for six months or a year that they can obtain the job readiness training, any training that they may need once they leave the system that they will be productive citizens.\par \par \uc2 So we`re looking at vocational training such as welding and carpentry, auto mechanics, things such as that, horticulture, anything that will give them money, a stable income once they leave.\par \par \uc2 And on this Board, which will be implemented as what I will call a Justice League, the Justice League will ensure that our Parole Board will adhere to the decisions or to the policies that are at hand and we will make sure that they will -- and we will allow the inmates to be able to leave, you know, and we don`t want them to come back, but we want to educate them and prepare them, get them where they can have their own place. We want them to be productive citizens.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, let me say this. We`re out of time, but I want to ask you this briefly. Across the country, we`re seeing an impressive number of Black candidates run for office in 2022 even as states without a long history of electing African-Americans.\par \par \uc2 You are the first Black major party nominee for Governor in Alabama and if you win the General Election, would be Alabama`s first Black Governor.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:04]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: What is the significance of breaking such a longstanding barrier and how will your lived experiences influence your work as Governor?\par \par \uc2 FLOWERS: Well, as being the first and I am so humbled by it, I am so grateful that it has been placed in my heart to do so, what I will give our children, the little Black girls and Black boys are, some, you know, hope. Give them hope that, you know if one day, then they can as well.\par \par \uc2 Being an educator, I want to educate all children that it doesn`t matter based upon your ethnic group, but it is more than just the predominant Caucasian group that there are many people here of all ethnic groups in Alabama, and that we should be more inclusive and more involving other than just one race, and that gives our children hope and then expect to live to the end.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Thank you, Yolanda Flowers, candidate for Governor -- Democratic candidate for Governor in Alabama. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 After 30 years behind bars, an innocent man is back home tonight. The lack of evidence that ultimately set him free.\par \par \uc2 Plus, my final thoughts on this week`s biggest stories, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:55:09]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Lastly, tonight, I want to highlight a few more stories that caught my attention. The State of Maryland has launched a system to track racist incidents and hate crimes. It`s called the Emmett Till Notification System, named in honor of the boy whose lynching galvanized the Civil Rights movement in the 1950s.\par \par \uc2 Maryland has experienced a growing number of racist incidents this past year, including bomb threats aimed at HBCUs and vandalism of Black churches.\par \par \uc2 In Mississippi, a Federal Appeals Court upheld the Jim Crow-era voting law that bars people convicted of certain felonies from voting. In their majority opinion, the Court stated that although the law was created with racist intent, it no longer operated in a racist manner.\par \par \uc2 And in Louisiana, a Black man has been exonerated after spending more than three decades in prison after being falsely convicted of rape. Sullivan Walter, who is now 53 years old, was only 17 when he was arrested. The Court has found numerous red flags over the years, including lack of evidence to match the DNA of Walter to the victim.\par \par \uc2 As we are on the eve of the 59th anniversary of the March on Washington and Martin Luther King`s historic "I Have a Dream" speech, we must continue to fight for equality and fairness in criminal justice from Jelani Day`s case in Illinois to cases all over the country, the struggle continues.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 "American Voices" with Alicia Menendez, my friend, starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:39]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220828ei8r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for August 26, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Alicia Menendez, Peter Alexander, Lester Holt \par \uc2 7,569 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:00:59]\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC HOST: And that`s tonight`s "LAST WORD". THE 11TH HOUR starts right now.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, revelations from the highly anticipated affidavit that led to the surge in Mar-a-Lago. Confirmation of classified documents discovered in Florida and the possible threats to national security. Then, be heavily redacted document leaves a lot of unanswered questions, especially about potential legal implications for the already twice impeached former president. And as we try to navigate this uncharted territory, one of our favorite presidential historians tries to put it all into perspective as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this very busy Friday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, I`m Alicia Menendez in for Stephanie Ruhle. It was nearly three weeks ago that former President Trump first told the nation the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was executing a search warrant at Mar a Lago. Today, Americans had a chance to see some of the Justice Department`s reasoning for that move. The heavily redacted affidavit that underpins the warrant has been unsealed and made public. The documents supports much of what was already known about the criminal investigation into whether Trump and his aides took secret government papers and failed to return all of them despite repeated demands from senior government officials. NBC`s Peter Alexander has more on today`s bombshell developments.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: The 38-page affidavit focuses on what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says it found in 15 boxes Mr. Trump returned to the National Archives in January, mixed in with newspapers, magazines and presidential correspondence were 184 classified documents 67 of them marked confidential, 92 marked secret and 25 documents marked top secret where the government says their unauthorized disclosure could result in exceptionally grave damage to the national security.\par \par \uc2 Also discovered documents that contain information about clandestine human sources and Mr. Trump`s handwritten notes. The Affidavit reveals more about the timeline in May of last year, the National Archives first requested the missing records from the former president. In January Mr. Trump turned over those 15 boxes containing that classified information. A month later, the archive sent a criminal referral to the Justice Department. President Biden authorized the archive to reject any executive privilege claims by Mr. Trump over the documents allowing the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to examine them.\par \par \uc2 In June Mr. Trump turned over additional documents. Two months later, the Justice Department submitted that affidavit that a federal judge approved granting the Mar-a-Lago search. In its filing, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  argued there`s probable cause that more classified documents with National Defense Information were at Mr. Trump`s residence, and there was probable cause to believe that evidence of obstruction will be found.\par \par \uc2 But the affidavit with entire pages blacked out leaves many unanswered questions about the extraordinary search of a former president`s home that sparked fierce blowback from Republicans and Mr. Trump, who today said the judge should never have allowed the breaking of my home. And slammed the redacted affidavit is a public relations subterfuge by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and DOJ after arguing he`d been turning over documents and that the investigation is politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Our thanks to Peter Alexander for that report.\par \par \uc2 Justice Department filed a memo along with the unsealed affidavit explaining the many redactions, the DOJ says some edits are there to protect people involved in the criminal investigation. Prosecutors refer to "the safety and privacy of a significant number of civilian witnesses, in addition to law enforcement personnel." Many legal analysts say that may refer to people close to the former president who might now be cooperating with the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . The affidavit also offers more hints about that investigation, including that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has not yet identified all potential criminal confederates nor located all evidence related to its investigation.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:03]\par \par \uc2 This afternoon the current president described how he handles highly sensitive government documents.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: Depending on the circumstance. For example, I have in my home a cabined-off space that is completely secure. I`m taking home with me today today`s PDB. It`s locked, a person with me -- military with me I read it. I lock it back up and give it to the military.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: And just about an hour ago, the former president`s legal team refiled his request that the government stopped reviewing the material seized at Mar-a-Lago. Trump is asking for a neutral special master to sift through the documents from the original request was filed on Monday, but a judge asked for clarifications to his motion be submitted by midnight.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s bring in our leadoff panel, Peter Baker, Chief White House Correspondent for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , Harry Litman, former U.S. attorney and former Deputy Assistant Attorney General, Frank Figliuzzi, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Assistant Director for Counterintelligence, Tracy Walder, former CIA Officer and \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent. She is the author of the book, "The Unexpected Spy."\par \par \uc2 Harry, a lot here. What do you consider the critical revelation in this document? And where does this leave Trump now? So you have tonight, the Wall Street Journal editorial, implying, this isn`t really a big deal?\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Yeah, you know, I just don`t see how anybody can say that. So you look at the affidavit, overall, I guess two things stand out, Alicia, first, look at paragraph 47 when they finally find out what`s involved here, we`re talking about documents, dozens and dozens of documents that can result in people being killed that can result in the revelation of sources and methods that are just being hanging around Mar-a-Lago where nobody has a security clearance, nobody knows what to do.\par \par \uc2 And I just want to be clear about this. The reason you have to go, as the president was just talking about those skiff, you have to surrender your phone. If you even have your phone on you sophisticated adversaries can read everything here, everything. So it`s a remarkably cavalier and dangerous aspect for the national security.\par \par \uc2 The second thing that really comes out is just this prolong negotiation that completely puts the lie repeated, by the way in the motion that you just mentioned tonight, to Trump`s idea that he was somehow cooperating on the contrary. For a year he holds them at bay, they find out it`s classified, four more months they forbear and forbear and forbear with this radioactive stuff, with his own handwriting, by the way, what the heck is that, down there on and until finally, really, nobody but nobody with any national security experience could say other than they had to go and get it, especially after a final attestation that said they had no more classified documents was determined to be a lie with fresh probable cause. That`s part of the stuff that`s redacted from it. But at that point, forget about it, you must, must go in and retrieve all the documents.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Frank, I want to dive back into the first part of Harry`s answer, would is should we make -- the number of highly sensitive documents that Trump had, and then the conditions under which they were stored?\par \par \uc2 FRANK FIGLIUZZI, FORMER \uc2 FBI\uc2  ASSISTANT DIRECTOR FOR COUNTERINTELLIGENCE: It`s a worst case scenario in terms of unlawful retention of documents, we`ve got an environment where clearly, it`s not authorized for the storage of the sensitive information. People coming in and out even today reports that this woman was able to penetrate Mar-a-Lago several times, even though she seems to be a scam artist that at best, and then then we`ve got this situation where they`re told repeatedly, you can`t have this stuff. It`s too sensitive, and they`re not securing it and the negotiations go on.\par \par \uc2 Look, I spent 25 years in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  eventually heading up all counterintelligence and espionage investigations. These classification markings that I saw today really caused the hair on the back of my neck to stand up. HCS, human lives are at stake, that`s for -- you know, there`s been a lot it`s kind of close, but not their accurate descriptions or inaccurate descriptions of what this means. These are human beings who said, I`m going to betray my country. And I`m going to work for Team America at the risk of death, or imprisonment. And myself or my family are at risk. They`re reporting is in HCS marked documents. SIGINT, SI, those are electronic intercepts the most sensitive things that our intelligence community can do and the techniques used. They could be singular in nature. A conversation could be one of these documents that only one person overheard. If someone saw the document they would say there`s only one person I told that to I know who the source is for working for the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [23:10:05]\par \par \uc2 The same goes for NOF (ph), no foreign, not allowed for any foreign national to see. ORCAN (ph), Original Agency Controlled, don`t give this to anyone without asking us first. This goes on and on and on. It`s the most sensitive information we can have in one of the worst places it can be from a security standpoint.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Tracy, I want to bring you in on this too, the affidavit describe several types of security classifications, as Frank said, one human intelligence, the clandestine sources. Tell us what would that mean, when you saw that that was one of the classifications? What went through your mind?\par \par \uc2 TRACY WALDER, FORMER CIA OFFICER: Yeah, so it was really interesting. You know, there was a lot of talk about the affidavit, the legal implications, and all of that. But really, to Frank`s point, what I zeroed in on really was the levels of security clearance that that were in there. That to me was, I guess, sort of the smoking gun. And really to further that more, and to really hit home, the larger implication that has that would make it really difficult for someone like myself, who recruited human assets and worked with human assets, to do my job, and to also work with other foreign countries.\par \par \uc2 You know, when you see and the other countries see that this information was out there. And in such an unsecured fashion, it makes it really difficult for them to want to work with the United States. And there have been countless terrorist operations, intelligence operations that have been incredibly successful. And that`s only because of the work that we have done with foreign countries and their intelligence services. And so I am extremely concerned about the implications that this is going to have for the future and for the future of our work with other intelligence services.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Peter, there`s the sheer number of documents, there`s the condition, they were held in there, the real world stakes that we have now laid out. There`s also the timeline here tonight, your colleagues at the Times they have a detailed report on the 20 month effort, 20 month effort to get Trump to return these government records. And it describes this was interesting how some conservatives were telling Trump to resist. What was going on?\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, THE NEW YORK TIMES CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, look exactly right. I think that`s an important point, because a lot of focus has been on the final chaotic days of the Trump administration when he was leaving the White House and didn`t want to leave the White House. And a lot of people say, well, look, you know, they were, you know, scrambling at the last minute, a lot of things got stuck in boxes where that`s almost irrelevant if you look at this timeline. Because it`s not about what he took with him, it`s what about it`s -- about what he refused to give up when he was confronted with it. And he refused to give it up time and time again, right? All the way up until a subpoena that was issued just a few months ago, in which they basically said, OK, you know, we`re not -- we`re done asking nicely, this is a legal order to return these documents that was once again defy.\par \par \uc2 So I think that timeline is important to remember here. There`s something going on here, which we haven`t fully grasp yet and is unfortunately answered by his affidavit. What was he thinking? What was he doing? Why was he doing this? There was this idea of defying the government that sense that, you know, the deep state doesn`t get to tell me what to do. I`m in charge as my papers because I was the president and I`m in charge here, but obviously, as Frank and Harry and we`re talking about here, these papers do not belong to him. They never belong to him, they belong to the public. They belong to the \uc2 United States government\uc2 , to the taxpayers, who funded the government.\par \par \uc2 And even aside from whether they are secret or not, he is not entitled to take them with him, and to hold on to them in defiance of Justice Department or the archives request. So this security part of it is an important aspect of it. But even if that wasn`t the issue, he is not entitled these documents under the law, and he cannot simply take them with him and defy the request to return them.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Right. They`re ours. They are not his. Frank, you mentioned this a few moments ago, the \uc2 Pittsburgh Post-Gazette\uc2  reporting about a Russian speaking woman who impersonated an heiress bypass security at Mar-a-Lago with ease, a year before the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search. Here`s the reporter on that story earlier on this network.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SALLAH, PITTSBURGH POST-GAZETTE INVESTIGATIONS EDITOR: There is an active investigation, and I think they`re just trying to determine, you know, a lot of her background and how she found her way in there. She went back to Mar-a-Lago in and out in her, she was driving a brand new \uc2 Mercedes Benz\uc2  SUV, and she at least five to six trips in and out of Mar-a-Lago with really nobody checking your ID, nobody really finding out like who really is she. All the guests in there thought she was a Rothschild.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: MSNBC has not verified this report yet. Frank, if true, it would seem to underscore just how potentially dangerous the mishandling of these documents was when we talk about this through a security lens?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: Yeah, look, this is a real time teachable moment. We were all looking at the issues security around Mar-a-Lago and here comes this report that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  separately is investigating at best a scam artists at worst potentially, depending on where this case goes, some kind of intelligence operative. We simply don`t know, but the message is clear. It`s not the first time the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has had investigations regarding persons unauthorized lurking around, penetrating Mar-a-Lago, a couple of Chinese nationals at a minimum in the past had done so.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:29]\par \par \uc2 And so the question is, what`s going on with the security posture around Mar-a-Lago, around this President, the tone for security is always set from the top. If he doesn`t want vetting of people, and guess, it`s not happening and apparently isn`t happening. And so it proves the point that this is one of the worst places you can store information. And that his approach to DOJ saying, yep, double lock the doors. Don`t worry about it, I got this covered, just simply doesn`t fly when you`ve got at best scam artists who are coming in under false ID, false name, taking their photos with the president, with Senator Graham, playing golf. We`ve got a problem here.\par \par \uc2 And Tracy alluded to this longer term consequences. Allies are looking at this potential sources of information looking at this and they`re saying how can we possibly do business in good faith with the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  when this kind of thing can happen?\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Right. I mean, Tracy, you both have America`s world, America`s role on the world stage, and you have the reality that the Intel community has been absolutely trashed by the former presidents. So they`re also then as a morale question, how frustrating is all of this to people in the Intel community who put their own lives at risk to keep this nation safe?\par \par \uc2 WALDER: It`s incredibly frustrating. You know, I wrote an article in 2017, when I said that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was really the largest national security threat that our country was facing, because in reality, he`s usually using the national security apparatus really, for his own personal gain. And that`s highly problematic. You know, I worked under two, three different presidents, and none of them, were using the national security apparatus like that. And that becomes a problem when you`re utilizing it for really your own personal gain, own personal needs, or your own personal vendettas to settle.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Harry, over these past 20 months, two things now seem clear Trump`s talk of cooperating, not true. And yet there continued to be a level of deference to the former president that other Americans, of course, would never have been given. Do you agree, knowing what you know now about how all of this has been handled?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Well, you mean, should they have come down on him harder, sooner? Yes. But it`s astonishing. Just in broad strokes, Alicia, it takes a year for them to -- they just know he`s carded stuff away, but they think it`s the -- just the Kim Jong-un love letter and \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , they don`t find out for a year what`s contained. And when they do, that`s when their alarm rings, they still forbear for four months, because Trump says I want to look at national executive privilege materials, letting the DOJ and \uc2 FBI\uc2  know finally they say forget about it and send this letter that says you have no executive privilege claim we`re turning it over tomorrow. By the way, that`s the claim and the only claim he`s still trying to sell.\par \par \uc2 And it`s then that it rolls in quick motion to subpoena personal visit. And finally, this just tell us, just tell us there`s nothing more classified, they sign a piece of paper that they find out is wrong. That is, as you say, 20 months and 20 months that they halfway through find out there that he`s been juggling radioactive material, the entire time with the with the stomach wrenching consequences that Frank and others have brought out. So as you say, incredible forbearance and complete intransigence and sort of contempt. And, you know, thumbing his nose by Trump from the start over 20 months. And that`s what`s really laid out broadly in the affidavit, even in the redacted parts. You can kind of look through the black lines and see that basic architecture.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: All right, all of you are staying with me.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, well, the affidavit confirms so much. It also leaves out a lot of critical questions remain unanswered, perhaps number one, can an indictment be next and for who?\par \par \uc2 And later there has been a subdued, almost silent response to the GOP? What could that mean for the midterms and for 2024? THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on Friday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:23:48]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, FORMER FBI GENERAL COUNSEL: I have to say my -- when I read this today, my big overarching takeaway is -- and I notice this is just a prediction and speculation. But I`d say it`s an educated one is that the former president is going to be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: For sounds like the former guy is in serious legal jeopardy over his possession of classified material and it was a law he signed back in 2018 made the mishandling of classified documents a felony lengthening the potential prison time. Peter Baker, Harry Litman, Tracy Walder and Frank Figliuzzi are still with us.\par \par \uc2 Peter, what are you hearing about how Trump`s inner circle is responding to all of these revelations?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Well, I think there`s a mix here. There`s obviously those who believe this is a good issue for him politically, that he`s trying to actually make this about grievance and persecution, a continuation of the witch hunt all that kind of thing and believe that He has profited politically from it and generating support his base. Clearly his money raising efforts have been benefited by this. They themselves have said this. But they`re also some people in the Trump orbit particularly the larger Republican, you know, sphere who were very nervous about this. Did not think this is necessarily a good thing even politically much less legally who see the president being exposed to potential prosecution, that affidavit uses the phrase probable cause for a crime. Well, it doesn`t say the president Trump committed one. But it`s hard to imagine who else they could have had in mind, given that he`s the one who took the documents and had possession of them in his residence.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:17]\par \par \uc2 That`s a big, big red flag for a lot of Republicans who are concerned that this leads someplace dangerous, particularly Republicans who spent a career talking about the need for guarding the nation`s secrets, who made an awfully strong argument against \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in 2016. Because they said that she was being careless, by having a private email server that used -- that sent out emails that were later deemed to be classified in a handful of occasions.\par \par \uc2 So it`s, I think, a real danger spot. And a lot of people in the Trump orbit, even those who were not saying it publicly, are quite concerned.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Tracy, I want you to take a listen to what former homeland security chief of staff Miles Taylor said this afternoon. Here we go.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MILES TAYLOR, FORMER CHIEF OF STAFF, DEPARTMENT OF HOMELAND SECURITY: In Trump world, they are in a full panic, they are in a mole hunt in Trump world to try to find who these people are, who are clearly around him who clearly ratted \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  out. And the investigators want to protect the security of those individuals, because they know from Donald Trump`s history that he tries to root out and punish whistleblowers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Tracy, there`s been a lot of reporting about the growing paranoia within Trump world, given that environment, how do these law enforcement agencies then protect their sources?\par \par \uc2 WALDER: Yeah, that`s going to be incredibly difficult, because really, that`s really when he`s made sort of his sole mission is, is finding out who those moles are, who gave the \uc2 FBI\uc2  or, you know, Department of Justice, that information to then go in and apply for that search warrant. And so I think, really making sure that all of the names are redacted are going to be incredibly important here, and also making sure that everything is absolutely sealed in regards to this case. I also think, too, you`re going to have to be very, very sure that doxxing doesn`t become a problem with some of these former law enforcement officials, as well as attorneys and things like that, because I can see folks coming after them as well.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Frank, you pointed out to us that if Trump applied for a barista job at the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , inside \uc2 FBI\uc2  headquarters, he couldn`t get it. I`m assuming you make that as a security commentary and not a commentary on his coffee making skills. Are these documents so sensitive that prosecutors might actually have trouble than using them in court?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: So the answer is clearly based on my experience, with investigating and attempting to have prosecuted espionage cases, that there are scenarios where the material you have in front of you is so incredibly sensitive that the agency from which it originated tells you they won`t support a prosecution. It can`t see the light of day, it can`t go to a defense attorney, it can`t get to a jury. Even if you clear everybody, they still don`t want this getting out into the public record.\par \par \uc2 So in this case, however, there`s good news, which is -- it`s good news, bad news, we have 184 classified documents from which prosecutors can choose and say -- and this is the irony, Alicia, right, you`ve got this highly sensitive stuff that many agencies will say there`s no way you could use this for your prosecution. So they have to go to the lesser sensitive and the lesser qualified documents to actually use in a prosecution.\par \par \uc2 Don`t be surprised if they actually decide, you know what, this is tough, and maybe not worth the risk. We`re going to go with the obstruction charge, which still fascinates me, by the way, right? And charge for obstructing the case, and try not to get even into the highly sensitive information, which would be the espionage charge.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: It`s always interesting to me, Harry, because you and Frank often will look at the same issue from different vantage points. So I want to sort of hear your legal take on the same question. I also want to know where you think \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  goes from here.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Right. So this is actually one point. I mean, we`re on two tracks here, right? And we`ve been from the start and this counterintelligence, but also now criminal prosecution. And this is one where they potentially divert, you can have real Donnie Brooks within the Department of Justice, where they want to bring prosecution and that and the security folks are saying you can use that stuff.\par \par \uc2 Frank is right. But even if they go on obstruction, you can expect Trump to say here`s how it`ll play out, Alicia, he`ll say I cannot defend myself unless I have access to this ultra-classified material. And therefore, it`s kind of a gray male.\par \par \uc2 I think, you know, he`s got a pretty limited playbook. I have no doubt they`ve thought it through and there are ways to charge and ways to prove to get around it. Where does it go now? I mean, this is the big revelation. We could have thought on the eighth. This was just a retrieval mission. We know now it`s much more serious, including what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has been doing since.\par \par \uc2 We won`t be hearing from \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  for a while. I think we`ll be hearing a lot from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but that`s because they`ll be diligently pursuing the investigation and, you know, people have been wrong before including me thinking that he`s really turned -- that a corner has really been turned.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:11]\par \par \uc2 But I understand where Andrew Weissmann is coming from. This does feel like the kind of free standing charge and the kind of charge that really gets at what is vicious about the Trump presidency, his thinking that everything is his, not the peoples that really could make \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  say, this is a fair and righteous prosecution. And I don`t think there`s any doubt but that they`ll have the goods in terms of the law and the evidence. So this is something that really could be put together within a few months. What I talked about unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Unprecedented indeed. Peter Baker, Harry Litman, Frank Figliuzzi, Tracy Walder, thank you all.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, well, the Republican response to today`s affidavit has been subdued. Trump allies insists the investigation is all politics, we`re going to get into that side of today`s big revelations when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:35:39]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, I just want you to know I`ve declassified everything in the world. I`m president. I can do it. Come on. Declassified everything. I`m not going to comment because I don`t know the detail. I only want to know let the Justice Department take that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: President Biden nodding to his predecessors legal troubles before boarding Air Force One this afternoon. Meanwhile, many Trump allies either dismissed the release affidavit as politics or ignored it altogether.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account for Republican House members did tweet this before the release, fake redacted affidavit with just the lyrics to Rick Astley`s Never Gonna Give You Up visible.\par \par \uc2 Joining us, Julian Castro, former HUD Secretary and 2020 Democratic candidate for president and Democratic Strategist, Adrienne Elrod, she was also a senior aide on the Biden-Harris campaign.\par \par \uc2 It is good to see you both. Secretary what is the Republican silence tell you about the level of concern they are experiencing within their party?\par \par \uc2 JULIAN CASTRO, FORMER HUD SECRETARY: They`re having this oh, you know what moment of, Alicia, where they`re realizing every single day more and more that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is such a political albatross in the 2022 cycle. It`s so fascinating that at the beginning of this right after the raid, you had \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , you had a number of other Republicans, \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 , others just go gangbusters and attack \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  and the DOJ, be very vocal about it. And then with each passing day and new information that`s come out that has incriminated \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  more as well as his team down there in Mar-a-Lago they`ve gotten quieter and quieter.\par \par \uc2 And I think politically, what it means is they want this to go away. They know that every single day that Americans are watching this, they`re digesting it, understanding the gravity of it, that`s a day that Republicans are losing, that`s a day that it`s less likely that they`re going to recapture the House or the Senate.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I think a lot, Adrienne, about the fact that you have President Biden out on the stump now talking about MAGA Republicans, right? Clearly making that a choice about the way they`re going to message of this going into midterms. You got \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sitting on $100 million in his Save America PAC. Well, you got Senate Republicans pulling ads in Arizona, you you`re thinking on how Democrats should be responding to all of this?\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE ELROD, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Yeah, I look, I think I`m glad that President Biden went there today and made it clear that this is not the way that he or most presidents, and, you know, frankly, like most presidents that we hadn`t had in our lifetime, would not treat classified documents this way, and drew that contrast and kind of, you know, laughed about the fact that, you know, Trump was like, oh, I declassified these documents, and made it clear that this is not only how you conduct yourself, not only how you don`t conduct yourself as president, but you also, of course, have a very methodical procedure in terms of how you handle classified information. And President Biden really went there.\par \par \uc2 But look, Alicia, here I think is the bigger picture is that Republicans are silent just as Julian said, because they are scared. They know that this is not working with those moderate independent swing voters that will dictate not only what happens in 2022, but of course, most importantly, for the Republicans in terms of the presidency, what happens in 2024. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has about 32% to 33% of the electorate who was with him, you know that the guy who can see it on Fifth Avenue, no matter what he does, those guys are going to be with him.\par \par \uc2 But the people who are going to get him elected or potentially elected are the moderate independent voters. And we know from recent polling, of course, then pulling the NBC News is done, as that those moderate independents believe that his illegal possession of classified documents is of concern, and is going to further detract them from potentially supporting him in 2024. So I think that`s where the bigger picture is. That`s why Republicans are worried. And that`s why you`re not hearing frankly, a lot coming out of their mouths over that -- after the affidavit came out today.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Secretary Castro, I would layer on to Adrienne`s analysis that you`re also experiencing some of the policy real world consequences, the hangover of the Trump years, Texas` abortion trigger law went into effect this week, making it a felony to provide an abortion in your state, punishable by life in prison. I want to know sort of how that is playing out in Texas. And when you look at sort of step back and look at the composite, right? A former president who continues to have myriad legal troubles and then the reality of what Republicans have started to put into place. How voters are going to feel about all of it?\par \par \uc2 [23:40:15]\par \par \uc2 CASTRO: Yeah, it`s interesting that down here in Texas, the Democratic statewide candidates are making choice, one of their front and center issues, just a couple of days ago, for instance, Rochelle Garza who`s running for Attorney General against the incumbent indicted \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , put out an ad along with the Lieutenant Governor candidate, it was this interesting joint ad that she did with him, but one of the focus issues was choice and that she would fight for a woman`s right to choose.\par \par \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  and others have said that even in rural communities in Texas, they`re breaking up abortion access. So this is turning into the kind of powerful issue that is making these Texas races much closer than people thought they would be. We`ve also seen just recently a few days ago in the special election for New York`s 19th Congressional District Health, Pat Ryan was able to pull off an upset victory by making abortion access the primary issue that he focused on. So I think it`s going to have legs not just in places like New York, but also from what we can tell in places where you wouldn`t expect it as much like in Texas.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Julian Castro, Adrienne Elrod, thank you both so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was once roasted over her handling of sensitive government information. Little did we know her competitor would later be investigated the same thing it has one of our favorite presidential historians questioning how dare he. THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:46:25]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) FORMER UNITED STATES PRESIDENT: Some bad things came out today, you know those classified, you know the word classified. She sent vast amounts of classified information, including information classified as top secret. They said that she was extremely careless. And frankly, I say grossly incompetent. We can have someone in the Oval Office who doesn`t understand the meaning of the word confidential or classified and may be --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Six years after Trump repeatedly ridiculed \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  over her private email server. Today`s affidavit revealed the disgraced former president took nearly 200 classified documents with him when he left office, dozens were labeled top secret and contained highly sensitive information and were kept in various rooms at the Palm Beach residence.\par \par \uc2 You`re happy to welcome back celebrated author and presidential historian Michael Beschloss. His latest work in a bookshelf full of works is, "Presidents of War."\par \par \uc2 Michael, typically, when you join us, we try to find historical comparisons to our current political moment. But as you tweeted today, and we all took notice, there is nothing in our history that quite compares to this.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BESCHLOSS, NBC NEWS PRESIDENTIAL HISTORIAN: Yeah, that`s exactly right. Lovely to be with you, as always, Alicia. You know, here`s a case where an ex-President of the United States was told numerous times by the National Archives, and shouldn`t have needed to be told, you`re in danger of being prosecuted for breaking the Presidential Records Act of 1978 and other laws against an ex-President taking and storing classified documents. And yet, why did he do this? You know, we still don`t know. Did he share the information from these classified documents with someone who shouldn`t have it, foreign governments, terrorists, someone else?\par \par \uc2 And you know, and the other thing is, we have to ask the question, did he get cash? Did he get favors for sharing that information? We do not know. You know, you were talking about history, Alicia, think of \uc2 Dwight Eisenhower\uc2  as a general, 1944 planning D Day, whose success depended on hitting the Germans by surprise, where the allies would land and what the plan of attack was? If someone had left out one piece of paper that could have doomed 10s of 1000s of allied soldiers and sailors to their deaths.\par \par \uc2 The next year, \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2  was president. He had an atomic bomb, which Americans did not know more than one. And he was thinking about using it against Japan, that dependent on surprise. What if Truman or someone in his entourage had sloppily left a piece of paper out that got to the Japanese or some other country that wished us ill and undercut the ability that Truman had to end World War Two? This is what is at stake? Why did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  do this?\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: But as you all know, that`s not just what`s at stake, because that is as it relates to our national security. But there`s also this piece about what it means for America`s role on the world stage, I mean, as other countries are watching this unfold, is the damage that has been done irrevocable?\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: Nothing is ever irrevocable, I think, but what friendly intelligence service would cooperate with us today, in the same way that they might have 10 or 20 years ago. Though when they didn`t have to worry about an ex-President of the United States of all things jeopardizing national secrets.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:08]\par \par \uc2 As you know well, Alicia, you know, we have great intelligence services in the United States. But part of the reason that we are so prepared against surprise attacks, terrorist attacks, other dangers in this modern world is that we have friendly governments with great intelligence services with whom we share information. If they close up their briefcases, we`re going to be living in a much more insecure state, the children and our families are going to be jeopardized. And I hold that at the feet of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Michael, you asked if Americans are ever going to get a confession, an apology for any of this, I`m going to go out on a limb and imagine that you`re probably not holding your breath for that from Trump. But what about other Republicans? I mean, those who have carried his water, those people who might still have a political career or career of service ahead of them, do you think we could hear the same from them?\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: I think that we`re -- there`s something that is really reassuring. You know, Alicia, here we are, what is, about 11 hours since that affidavit was released today. And usually you expect, you know, hundreds of Trump supporting leaders, Republican leaders and others through the states saying bum rap. He`s being treated unfairly. He didn`t do anything wrong. As of 11 hours in, there was a resounding silence. And it may well be that the Republicans who have styled themselves as the party of national security for decades, maybe this was too much for them to take and they will say, you know, we`ve swallowed enough to go along with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but putting the lives of young Americans in danger after centuries of young Americans giving their lives and risking their lives for our freedom. That`s one step too far.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: As you said, maybe, maybe. Michael Beschloss, as always thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: We will see.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Staying up, spending some time with us, be sure to catch Michael`s latest episode of Fireside History that is available right now on MSNBC on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, half a century ago, Neil Armstrong took the first step on the moon. \uc2 NASA\uc2  wants to increase that foot traffic details on its new mission when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:57:03]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That`s one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.\par \par \uc2 Oh, gee, that`s great! Is the lighting half way decent?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, indeed. They`ve got the flag up now and you can see the stars and stripes on the lunar surface.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: The last thing before we go tonight, return to the moon. Back on July 20, 1969 Neil Armstrong became the first man to ever walk on the lunar surface. Some 53 years later, we are still waiting on the first woman to take those momentous steps. But we may be getting closer and the start of the nation`s long awaited next trip to the moon could be just a few days away.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, \uc2 NASA\uc2  is hoping to kick off its first moon mission in 50 years. The launch of an uncrewed test spacecraft called Artemis I as Lester Holt explains.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC HOST: Sitting on Launch Pad 39B, the Artemis rocket NASA`s most powerful yet taller than the Statue of Liberty. 55 engines and motors will lift the nearly 600 million pound rocket sky word on a historic mission, a first step to eventually return Americans to the lunar surface.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): So how real is this going back to the moon?\par \par \uc2 VANESSA WYCHE, NASA JOHNSON SPACE CENTER DIRECTOR: Oh, it`s very real. We`re definitely going. We`re going to actually live there. We will have landers go to the moon, we`ll have rovers on the moon and eventually we`ll have a base camp on the moon.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Once beyond the pole of Earth`s gravity, the Orion capsule perched the top of the rocket will orbit the moon for 42 days, uncrewed but with male and female astronaut dummies loaded with sensors to study the potential effects of the mission on future real astronauts.\par \par \uc2 Jessica Meir is one of 18 veteran astronauts part of the Artemis team who could fly on the first mission to the moon since 1972. The goal learn more about the moon and use it as a jumping off point to Mars.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Learn more about the moon. Artemis I is scheduled for liftoff from NASA`s Kennedy Space Center between 8:33 a.m. and 10:33 a.m. Eastern on Monday morning, we will be watching. We hope you will too.\par \par \uc2 And on that note I want to wish you a good night. It was so fun to be with you this entire week. Make sure to tune in this weekend for my show, American voices, 6 to 8 p.m. every Saturday, every Sunday. This Saturday, I`m going to talk with Congresswoman \uc2 Ayanna Pressley\uc2  about with President Biden`s big announcement on forgiving student loans means for you, for your kids, for the midterms and where this big fight goes next. I want you to check it out, again. It starts at 6 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thank you for staying up late. Stephanie Ruhle will be back on Monday. And I will see you this weekend.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  