ers themselves. Do you want your rights under the Kansas constitution taken away? They said, heck, no by a huge majority.\par \par \uc2 So, the reality is because the Supreme Court took this extreme, extreme decision to overturn 50 years of constitutional law, the American public doesn`t want it, and you`re seeing the consequences of that of it not being embraced.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: One of the things -- I just want to zoom in on here, because I think it speaks to the before and after of the politics of this. The extremism sort of knew -- knows no bounds, right? The idea that not -- it`s not just enough to overturn Roe v. Wade, that we need a federal personhood law. This is on Blake Masters` website. Ideally, a constitutional amendment that recognizes unborn babies are human beings that may not be killed. That would confer full rights 14th amendment rights on fertilized eggs. That`s now gone. But that doesn`t mean that there aren`t people in this country working to try to make that happen still.\par \par \uc2 NORTHUP: There absolutely is a movement working to try to make personhood the standard of law in the United States which would outlaw abortion across the country, which would threaten things like in vitro fertilization, and which would continue the crisis that we already have. I mean, today was not a good day, but we saw more states have their trigger laws go into effect, extremism. Texas` trigger law, which went to effect today, it has a consequence for abortion providers of five to 99 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is what`s happening right now. It is extreme. And it is just frightening that we have the country right now, one in three women in the United States have lost the ability to access abortion in their home states. This is just 60 days after the Supreme Court`s decision.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Texans who perform abortions will now face up to life in prison, $100,000 fine as of -- as of today. That`s just one of the laws that have been triggered and or passed in the country right now. What do you see as the means of opposing this kind of thing in those states like Texas or Ohio that have either had trigger laws or have passed new legislation with these just absolutely draconian criminal penalties and limitations on women`s bodily autonomy?\par \par \uc2 NORTHUP: Well, as you know, we have been filing court challenges to different outcomes, good outcome today in North Dakota, but we were able to get a preliminary injunction against the trigger ban, the abortion ban, that would have gone into effect today in North Dakota. The \uc2 United States government\uc2  has been challenging states who are not implementing Idaho good ruling yesterday on behalf of the Department of Justice lawsuit saying that you have to be able to give emergency medical care and you can`t have your abortion law preempt that.\par \par \uc2 But in states like Texas, it`s really tough. The challenges in court have not been able to prevail. And so, we now have in Texas the trigger law in effect. This is the up to 99 years in prison. The pre-Roe 1925 abortion ban in effect, still the vigilante SB.8 law in effect. And people in those states need to make sure their elected officials know how they feel about it.\par \par \uc2 Regardless of what their elected officials positions are, they need to let them know they don`t support these extreme laws and what they`re doing to reproductive health care. I mean, the fact that women in emergency medical situations cannot get hospital treatment in the state of Texas is absolutely an outrage.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, we should know that that that the federal -- the federal government under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  issued an executive order about emergency medical care, which basically required certain standards of care regardless of state abortion laws. That was challenged by Texas. A district judge in Texas upholding the challenge so that the Texas law remains in place, an opposite finding by a federal judge in Idaho which has blocked part of their laws. That is going to continue to work its way up the appellate ladder, I imagine, before the Supreme Court rather quickly.\par \par \uc2 We will continue to report and monitor that. \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NORTHUP: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Thursday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220826ei8p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for August 25, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Yasmin Vossoughian, Ken Dilanian, Glenn Kirschner, Ryan Reilly,  Blayne Alexander, Ali Vitali, Gabe Gutierrez, Morgan Radford \par \uc2 8,079 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN, MSNBC HOST: Hey, everybody, GOOD afternoon, I'm Yasmin Vossoughian in for Chris Jansing at MSNBC headquarters here in New York.\par \par \uc2 As we're coming on the air, we are following some major developments in two different cases of special interest to the former president. In Atlanta, a Fulton County Judge just finished hearing arguments on whether Georgia Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  should in fact be forced to testify before Grand Jury investigating allegations of criminal interference by the former president and his allies in the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 And then over to West Palm Beach, Florida, the DOJ met their noon deadline, just an hour ago, to submit proposed redactions to the Mar-a-Lago search warrant affidavit. The man who approved that warrant, U.S. Magistrate Judge Bruce Reinhart, will now begin considering the Justice Department's suggested redactions of that affidavit, which basically lays out the government's reason for requesting the search in the first place of the former president's compound. An expert is saying it is likely at containing evidence of a possible crime that they expected to find on the property.\par \par \uc2 Today, the former president once again said he "did nothing wrong." The government vehemently opposed the affidavit, even being made public arguing that the release could actually "cause significant and irreparable damage to an ongoing criminal investigation that they note is still in its very early stages." The judge's deliberations are taking place in private, and how much, if any, of the affidavit? We will see is still unclear. The judge said earlier this week, that he might, he might ultimately conclude that it would be best not to release any of that because redactions would effectively make it meaningless.\par \par \uc2 We're going to have much more on that Georgia investigation we talked about earlier into election interference. But first, we want to dig into the Mar- a-Lago search warrant with NBC News justice and intelligence correspondent Ken Dilanian, NBC News justice reporter Ryan Riley, Glenn Kirschner, a former federal prosecutor, and an MSNBC News legal analyst, and Javed Ali, former senior director for counterterrorism at the National Security Council.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Ken, let me just start with you on the nuts and bolts on this thing. I guess the major question is now this thing has been met, right knew and they handed this over to the judge, the judge now considering these redactions. What are we expecting was actually redacted in this warrant, A., and how much do you think the judge will be accepting of it?\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, MSMNBC JUSTICE AND INTELLIGENCE CORRESPONDENT: So that's the big question, Yasmin. I mean, we can intuit that a lot, a lot was redacted because both the Justice Department and the judge agreed that there were many, many sensitive things in this affidavit that could not be exposed, the names of witnesses, the identities of \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, investigative techniques, and as they put it, a roadmap to the investigation. All of that is extremely sensitive and will not be exposed.\par \par \uc2 But we can imagine that there are other things that possibly -- that have already been reported that they could be unredacted. It might shed some light on stuff. For example, the back and forth between the Trump side and the Archives and the Justice Department over getting these documents back and the extent to which, if at all, the Justice Department felt misled about the existence of classified documents at Mar-a-Lago. Right now, we just simply have to wait. The Justice Department has submitted its redactions. We see a sealed entry in the docket that the DOJ has confirmed is theirs. And now we wait to see what the judge does, Yasmin.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Glenn, what are we expected to get a decision from this judge? How long do you think it's going to take?\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: You know, it looks like the judge and the courts more generally, are really rising to the moment. They appreciate -- they seem to appreciate the urgency of moving through these legal issues quickly. So I have a feeling given the expedited schedule this has been on, we could see something within a matter of days. I mean, it could be as early as this afternoon or tomorrow. But I don't think we're going to see much.\par \par \uc2 I agree with Ken that there will likely be so many redactions. It will leave us all frustrated. But I also agree that there are some things that are publicly known that it seems like the Department of Justice could disclose without compromising the ongoing investigation.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So here's an interesting development that we're just getting in from our own Tom Winter, news organizations have now filed a motion, I'm reading this from my e-mail that's why I'm looking down guys, to unseal summer all of the Justice Department filing addressing their proposed redactions, the Mar-a-Lago search warrant affidavit, so we're going to have to wait and see how that develops, Glenn. But I kind of want to follow up on the position that the attorney general is in, right? How do you strike this balance in proving the validity of this search and yet maintaining the integrity of an ongoing investigation with these redactions in mind?\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: The reality is you don't have to prove the validity of the search to anybody except the judge who issued the search warrant in the first place. That's the person who made the decision that based on the sworn affidavit that was presented to him there was probable cause to believe there was evidence of a crime on the property at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 Beyond that, you know, we don't draft these affidavits in investigations with a view toward it ever being disclosed to the public unless and until our investigation is complete, somebody has been indicted, and we are now in the run-up to the trial. So it really is not DOJ's job now to prove the legitimacy of the search to the public satisfaction.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Javed, talk to me about this highly classified information we now know was in the hands of the former president, right, at Mar-a-Lago. And the national security concerns this judge has in deciding whether or not to release this affidavit.\par \par \uc2 JAVED ALI, FORMER SENIOR DIRECTOR FOE COUNTERTERRORISM, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL: Hey, Yasmin, thanks for having me on the panel. So this was another one of these really interesting questions. Yes, it seems like from the search and the warrant that was filed, that there is a large pool of classified documents that were in President Trump's possession after he left office. And it seems within that pool, there is a smaller batch, I would have to assume, of the most sensitive classified information, top secret SCI information, and what's been reported in the media, perhaps even what is called Special Access Program, information or intelligence within that smaller batch.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALI: So these are really sensitive documents that only need to be or only should have been kept or stored or reviewed, and what is known as a skiff. And I lived in that world, my whole government career. So the fact that these were in the possession of former president Trump in an unsecured location, even if they were in a safe, they weren't an accredited skiff, and the potential compromise, a release of that information or viewing of that information with people who did not have a need to know or didn't have security clearances. I mean, these are serious, serious national security issues that have to be examined if true or false.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: How does the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , Javed, how did -- how do they prepare for a possible release of some of this information?\par \par \uc2 ALI: I don't think that any of that will be disclosed and even redacted information because again, you can't compromise sources, methods, or classified information.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALI: So at best, there might just be a suggestion of the type of material or like a general theme or topic, but they can't get into the actual sort of information itself or the more particular details around it because that in and of itself would be a security violation too unless it is somehow declassified.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Glenn, just kind of to wrap up this conversation, where are you predicting the judge is going to land on this thing?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: I think the judge will decide to redact -- to disclose almost nothing.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Got it.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: I wouldn't be surprised, Yasmin, if he said, listen, everything in here is too sensitive. If I just piecemealed out a word here and a line there, it would be meaningless and it would be more frustrating than it would be enlightening. So I don't think we're going to see much of anything, though I do predict, we then may see an appeal of the judge's decision.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, so I want to talk about a related matter here, Glenn, and we'll pick up a little bit on where you left off and that is, of course, with the former president -- Judge Aileen Cannon, I should say ordering the former president essentially to file this supplement to his request for this special master by tomorrow, including, by the way, asking essentially, why it is her court is even overseeing this. What are you expecting from this?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: You know, it was kind of embarrassing when the judge issued that minute order, which is just an informal order that's dropped on the public docket for all to see, basically saying, listen, she was communicating -- why don't you just take this one back and submit a competent legal pleading, answering the following questions. And then she posed, I think, five questions. But here's the thing not to mix stories here. But even though we might not get anything from the affidavit being unsealed --\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: -- What we may get as a response filed by the Department of Justice to this motion for judicial oversight, the appointment of a special master. We know DOJ typically says they only speak in their court filings, indictments, and motions, we may get to see some speaking by the Department of Justice in responding to this kind of nonsensical request by the Trump team.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Ryan Reilly, thank you for being patient throughout this conversation because there are so many different tentacles to what we're talking about today. And, of course, when we talk about these affidavits, now we're also talking about this unredacted affidavit, right, that has now been released --\par \par \uc2 RYAN REILLY, MSNBC JUSTICE REPORTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: -- With regards to former Attorney General Bill Barr from that 2019 memo, essentially saying the former president will in fact not be charged with obstruction of justice. What are we learning about this?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 REILLY: You know, it reads in many ways, like a defensive memo. You know, the big headline here was that this decision had already been made. Bill Barr had already decided that he was not going to be prosecuting President Trump, his boss, the very person who put him in office. So I think that was essentially what this memo was written for. It was essential to justify that predetermined decision.\par \par \uc2 But you know, if you read into the details of it there is -- it's -- you can turn to realize there's a reason why they didn't want this to come out sooner because some of the arguments they make just aren't extremely strong. It seems to be making essentially an argument for the best possible way to interpret a lot of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's moves rather than interpreting them in a way that were interfering in an ongoing investigation. All this had to do with, essentially, obstruction of justice and potential charges that could have been brought against the former president for interfering in that ongoing Mueller investigation.\par \par \uc2 And essentially, what they chalked all that up to in this memo was that, OK, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was doing all this because it was interfering with his political abilities and his ability to get done, what he wanted to get done through Congress. So they basically sort of whitewash in a way. I think a lot of those decision-making processes that he was interfering with an ongoing investigation that for anyone else would have been certainly something that they would have looked for to charge someone with obstruction of justice, but he sort of got a clean slate from two officials that were in the Trump Justice Department at that -- at that moment, who decided to make their case to former Attorney General Barr.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Hey, Glenn, what did you make of this unredacted memo? What stood out to you?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: You know, this reads like a public relations piece for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  rather than a legitimate legal analysis. One line, which I think highlights that, Yasmin.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: -- Is they actually say that when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was urging Flynn, Manafort, Stone, and Cohen not to flip, that wasn't obstruction of justice, that was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  believing that if they cooperated, the prosecutors would make them lie about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . This memo reads throughout like the author's saying, look, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had the best intentions and everything he said and did. And I'll tell you, it makes experienced prosecutors' heads hurt when they read that kind of so-called legal analysis.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Any new insight, Glenn, into former Attorney General Bill Barr?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: A little bit. And I would say because this memo was published on the very day Bill Barr stepped to the camera. And I'm going to use the words of Judge Reggie Walton when he ruled that Bill Barr mischaracterized the Mueller report, spun the Mueller report, and "Bill Barr lacks candor." The fact that they issued this memo as cover for him to do precisely what he did on the same day he did. It sort of tells you the whole thing feels like more public relations and less legal analysis.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, we're going to be watching for developments on all these fronts as we've covered a lot in these first 30 minutes of the show. Thank you guys, for now, of course, tapped me on the virtual shoulder if anything develops in the time between now and the end of the show. Ken, Javed, Ryan, thank you. Glenn, I'm going to ask you to stick around for our next segment as well.\par \par \uc2 And the other investigation involving the former president this time in Atlanta where a judge is deciding whether Georgia Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  must testify in the state's election interference probe, Plus, student loan debt forgiveness, who qualifies, what groups will be most affected, and what borrowers are saying? You're watching MSNBC. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, welcome back. A judge in Fulton County, Georgia now weighing Republican Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 's request to quash a subpoena asking him to testify before a special Grand Jury after arguments from his lawyers concluded in a public hearing in the last hour. That special Grand Jury investigating whether the former president and his allies broke any laws in their attempts to influence the 2020 election. NBC's Blayne Alexander is outside the courthouse for us in Atlanta. Also, I want to bring back former federal prosecutor Glenn Kirschner.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Blayne, let me -- let me just start with you on this one, give us the nuts and bolts of kind of what took place inside that courtroom. Where are we?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Sure. Well, Yasmin, that hearing lasted about two hours or so, the judge didn't make a decision today, but essentially, it came down to this. The attorneys for Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  really laid for three central arguments. One, sovereign immunity, two, executive privilege, but third, the thing that they really focused on the most and notably, the thing that the judge didn't want to hear about, per se, were political implications, what the attorneys are calling political implications of his testimony.\par \par \uc2 Essentially, they're arguing that because we are on what they call the eve of one of the most closely watched gubernatorial races in the country, right here in Georgia, that it's -- there's no way that the testimony or just any sort of news around this could not seep into the election itself, would not seep into this gubernatorial race. You know, they basically said that it's not proper for politics and legal proceedings to intersect this way when we're about two and a half months away from this election.\par \par \uc2 Now, the DA's office says that's not the case at all. They are doing everything they can to avoid any sort of political, you know, meddling or the appearance they're up. They say simply that the governor's testimony is so important that they can't push this, they can't delay this any further, that it is important that the grand jury hears from him. Now, to all of that point, while all of this is going on, what is interesting is as this race has been progressing, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , of course, the Democratic nominee, has seized upon this rather contentious back and forth that we've seen between the DA's office and the governor's office.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 And essentially is kind of using this to paint some sort of a closer allegiance between Governor Kemp and former President Trump. Even kind of tweeting out a sort of campaign-style ad to that effect, essentially saying that what we're seeing this back and forth shows, in \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 ' words, that Governor Kemp does not want to "testify against former President Trump."\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, Glenn, let's tick through some of the points that Blayne just beautifully made, and kind of the overarching question all this, right? Will the governor actually be able to quash the subpoena, A., and or push it down the road until after his election, right? First and foremost, Blayne talked about sovereign immunity. What do you make of that case that he's trying to make?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: You know, there's a tell here, Yasmin, when you have to offer so many different potential privileges, in essence, throw a whole bunch of stuff against the wall and see which sticks. Typically, none of it sticks. Because you're playing against yourself, right, when you offer these alternative theories. I don't believe the judge will buy that he has sovereign immunity because he's not being sued. Sovereign Immunity means somebody's trying to sue a government official for performing some duty that is within the scope of their official duties. So that dog likely won't hunt.\par \par \uc2 I don't think the executive privilege claim will fare much better. There was also some reporting that he was going to argue attorney-client privilege. I don't know if he made that argument in the courtroom. But the other tell here, Yasmin --\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: -- Is that at the end of it all? He said, well, at a minimum, maybe you can just delay it until after the election. That is almost a concession that, look, Judge, I know, these aren't all that persuasive with respect to these legal theories, but can you at least push it until after the elections? I have a feeling he's probably going to lose on all fronts. This is not going to be viewed as the -- by the judge through a political lens. And the judge is likely going to order Governor Kemp to testify.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Why do you -- why do you suspect it's critical for the governor to testify now, Glenn?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Well, I think, you know, DA Fani Willis has made it clear that she is going to investigate this case without fear, without favor, and she sure seems to be doing that, casting a wide net with respect to anybody who has any information about not only the election -- the suspected election fraud, the potential solicitation of election crimes, but also with respect to the way the Georgia state elections are run. That is something that Governor Kemp has lots of information on and I think he'd be a valuable source of information for the grand jury that is investigating all of these potential election crimes in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Hey, Blayne, you brought it up, right? This idea that \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is essentially the kind of capitalizing on this situation, this back and forth that we're hearing from the current governor there. How are folks there reacting to what's happening?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: You know, what's interesting, Yasmin. And I do want to point this out, too before I get into that Kemp's office, the governor's office did tell us in a statement, look, he's willing to testify, he's willing to cooperate, he's willing -- he's been willing all along. They're just asking that this happened after the election. So I do think that's an important point to make.\par \par \uc2 As for what voters are making of this, you know, we spent time talking to voters. And this really has not been something that's come up when -- the people have talked about as for being a driving force or something that they think about when they make their decision on who they're supporting. Really, among voters -- among people, this hasn't necessarily been at the forefront of folks' minds. They talk about the economy, they talk about abortion, they talk about any number of things, Georgia being among the first -- the first state to reopen during the COVID pandemic. This has not been something that I've heard from voters that they're watching very closely, at least when it comes to being a factor in their decision this November.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Blayne, thank you. Glenn Kirschner, thank you as well. All right, starting next week, everybody, teachers in Ohio's largest school district expected to head back to class after striking when the school year started. The union representing the teachers in Columbus reached a preliminary agreement with the school board this morning. The details of that agreement have not yet been released. Students expecting to continue online learning until Monday, the strike is ending after three days of teachers protesting for air conditioning in classrooms, smaller class sizes, and better resources as well.\par \par \uc2 All right, today, new restrictive abortion law is going into effect in a handful of states. One could mean a life sentence for an abortion provider, the changes that start today, and how they affect millions of American women. You're watching MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, welcome back, everybody. So today, new laws banning abortion going into effect across this country. In Oklahoma, abortion now entirely illegal with the only exception being if it is necessary to save the life of the mother, Tennessee, shifting from a heartbeat ban to an almost complete ban on the procedure, and then in Texas, the new law making providing an abortion of first-degree felony, the penalty of up to life in prison and a civil penalty fine of $100,000.\par \par \uc2 But in Idaho, a little win for the federal government. A federal judge, they are temporarily blocking part of the state's strict abortion ban that was scheduled to take effect today, delivering the administration a narrow courtroom win in its first lawsuit to protect reproductive rights after the fall of Roe.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  national health reporter Rachel Roubein, and NBC's Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vitali.\par \par \uc2 Rachel, let me just start with you on this one. And I kind of want to -- want you to talk us through some of the trigger laws that are now coming into effect today. In addition, as we look ahead to tomorrow as well, you're looking at North Dakota also they're almost complete ban going into effect, as well. Where are we with these? How are they going to affect the women in this -- in these areas?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL ROUBEIN, NATIONAL HEALTH REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON POST: Hi, thank you so much for having me, first off. And so you mentioned Idaho, that's one of the states where a trigger law is now in effect. And as you mentioned, there was a narrow legal victory for the Biden administration. Their first legal victory since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 But the crux of the trigger a lot there is that it has a near-total ban on abortion. What the federal judge ruled yesterday was that providers, doctors can't be penalized if they perform an abortion in the case of a medical emergency. Now Tennessee will also have a near complete ban on abortion as well as pending penalties for providers in Texas.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: What does this kind of narrow win, Rachel, right, in the state of Idaho, what does it mean for the bigger picture and kind of the strategy really, the federal government, the Department of Justice can take in maintaining reproductive rights for women across the country right now?\par \par \uc2 ROUBIEN: That's a great question. So we saw the administration last month put out guidance for doctors in emergency medical situations. And in general, we've seen the administration look to what they can do kind of within the scope, now this relied on an existing law, so kind of in the scope of some existing regulations and laws.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Talk to me about the last two months, Rachel. I know you've been kind of monitoring how states, how women have been adjusting, readjusting kind of to this new normal of what's happening, right. You have the scattered laws across this country and people not necessarily knowing what is legal, where it's legal, when it's legal, these trigger laws coming into effect. Women in those states not necessarily knowing those trigger laws are now in effect. How are health care providers dealing with this change specifically?\par \par \uc2 ROUBIEN: Yes, so I think starting with the big picture, right now roughly 20.9 million women have lost access to elective abortions in their home state. And so looking broadly, that's roughly 14 states where abortions are banned. And we've seen, you know, with the legal landscape here, there have been providers and groups that have sued over these restrictions. And so what you're seeing is sometimes the laws change, can change very quickly where one day abortion might be legal in a state and in another day it might not be legal.\par \par \uc2 So the landscape is evolving and shifting very quickly and that's something providers, you know, on the ground are making sure to watch and be cognizant of.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Ali Vitali, let's get into this a little bit because obviously your book out, "Electable," right, much of which is about getting women into higher office, why is it that women haven't necessarily made it to be president of the United States as of yet. It's incredible, by the way, if you haven't had the opportunity to sit down and read it, you should. Congratulations, my friend.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: But with all of that being said, right, you see what took place in upstate New York, right, this idea of abortion rights, it was a rally cry for voters in this swing district. It was huge for Pat Ryan to take that win home. I'm wondering now, as we think about abortion rights in this country, seeing these trigger laws take effect, right, if this will be something that will help more women get elected?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: How could it not be. And at least how could it not galvanize more women into public office. And I think this is not just something that is impactful for women, it's something that is clearly galvanizing both men and women, voters across the country. You watch the way that it's playing out in polls in terms of the generic ballot, closing the enthusiasm gap for Democrats, all of that is critically important.\par \par \uc2 And if I think about it through the lens of the book, we are here now 30 years from the first year of the women, 1992, when four women entered the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  in part because you saw more women run after the \uc2 Anita Hill\uc2  hearings. And we saw the same thing then happened after 2016 in the loss of Hillary Clinton to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the presidential election. These are pivot points and critical inflection points for women and women candidates, and not just candidates but voters as well.\par \par \uc2 Things that might take people off of the political sidelines and put them central into the system and so certainly in the same way that we look back at the Me Too Movement over the course of the last few years, this is a moment where gender is front and center because millions of women across the country now are grappling with the loss of one health care decision because of this Supreme Court decision. Clearly this is having big ripple effects for November.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So here's the thing, right. There is the idea, Ali, right, to decide to run, right, to put your hat in the race for more women to do that. The more women that do it, the more women will get elected ultimately because that's how math works, right. But then there is the discussion surrounding women in leadership, how they are talked about, right. And that discussion is still very much gendered, no matter where we are in this country.\par \par \uc2 You look at highest office in the land, right, the vice president of the United States is now a woman, yay for the United States, but even that being said you think about the way in which we talk about her political future and oftentimes that conversation is gendered, not unlike the conversation surrounding former Secretary of State \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [13:35:08]\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes. And Yasmin, there are so many things baked into this. One of the things I talk about in the book is the idea that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is elevated but not elected in her own right. Certainly she ran for president herself. She was one of the options that Democratic voters had in the most female field ever in 2020 but at the end of the day her presidential ambitions fell short, although the fact that she was elevated to the ticket with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was a sign of progress in and of itself, because as I talk about in the book, when you had \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  in 2008 running with \uc2 John McCain\uc2 , when you had Geraldine Ferraro in 1984 alongside \uc2 Walter Mondale\uc2 , they were tut there as sort of a throw spaghetti at the wall, see something that could energize the electorate kind of way.\par \par \uc2 But in the elevation of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , this was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his team recognizing the fact that having a woman on the ticket was an asset, it was a power play, it was something that they politically had to do because it was advantageous to them, and because after having the most diverse field of candidates ever in 2020 they needed a ticket that was reflective of the Democratic coalition and the coalition that they were hoping to build nationally.\par \par \uc2 So what that means for us going forward both in this very female moment but also charting the path ahead, past 2022 into 2024 and well past that, too, I hope, is I do think that gone are the days when we will see primary fields of only men.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Enough women now are in the pipeline from both parties with enough experience to run viable presidential campaigns themselves whether that's \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  challenging Trump in 2024, I know that's something that she's actively toying with right now. But think we will never see a primary again where women are not at least actively considered and part of the conversation in the primary and then ultimately when you get to a general election.\par \par \uc2 I think gone are the days of only white men on the ticket. And I think that's a very good thing for our country where reflective governance means better governing for every American.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: You think about the two biggest issues, right, the Democratic Party is fighting right now. And there are women at the forefront of them.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: It's women's right and it's fighting for democracy really, and the maintenance of democracy really in this country.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Rachel Roubien, thank you. Ali Vitali, my friend, thank you as well. By the way, be sure to check out Ali's new book "Electable." It's out available in stores now.\par \par \uc2 All right, New York's highest court has granted Hollywood mogul \uc2 Harvey Weinstein\uc2  an appeal more than two years after being convicted on rape and criminal sexual act charges. The appeal will challenge Weinstein's 2020 conviction and has cleared the way for oral arguments to begin as soon as next year. Weinstein was sentenced to 23 years in a New York state prison. He is now back in Los Angeles awaiting a separate trial on similar charges coming up in October.\par \par \uc2 And a key decision in Uvalde. What the city school board has decided what to do with the police chief there over the botched highly criticized response to the mass shooting at Robb Elementary three months later.\par \par \uc2 You're watching MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:42:06]\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right. Moments ago, Ukrainian President Zelenskyy said he spoke to President Biden, that news coming in the wake of Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ordering a significant increase in armed forces in Ukraine. Putin upping troops by 137,000. That's about a 10 percent jump in Russia's combat personnel. The move coming as the war in Ukraine passes its six- month mark and a day after Putin's defense chief admitted Russia's military efforts in Ukraine they've stalled.\par \par \uc2 Also three months after the tragic school shooting in Uvalde, leaving 19 children and two teachers dead, the school's police chief is now out of a job. In a heated meeting held last night, the school board voted unanimously to fire Chief Pete Arredondo. Arredondo did not attend the meeting but sent a lengthy 17-page statement calling his treatment a, quote, "unconstitutional public lynching."\par \par \uc2 NBC national correspondent Gabe Gutierrez is joining me now with more on this.\par \par \uc2 It's taken three months for this decision to happen after two delays by Arredondo's own attorneys. Here we are, though, and this community is still reeling of course understandably so from this unspeakable tragedy. How are they reacting today?\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, this has been a long time coming, Yasmin. And as you said, the community has been to several of these meetings, has been calling for his ouster for quite some time. And it happened yesterday. There was a 21-second moment of silence at the beginning of the meeting for the victims here. But Arredondo was unanimously fired.\par \par \uc2 As you said, say 17-page statement, though, from Arredondo. These are his lengthiest public comments even in a written form that we have seen from him so far. A public lynching he called it and his attorneys also added that none of his decisions or actions demonstrate a failure to meet the accepted standards of conduct for law enforcement officers in similarly situated school districts in Texas, basically saying, listen, he did what was required of him to do.\par \par \uc2 He has said before that he did not believe he was the incident commander on the scene but law enforcement experts around the country have essentially said that that argument is ridiculous. Many community members also feel the same way. We heard from several of those parents yesterday as well. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You don't care splat (PH) about these families. If it was one of your children, heads would be rolling right now. But because it's not, you don't care.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: 21 lives lost. And Mr. Pete Arredondo is asking for justice. We ask for justice for our grandkids, our kids.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: And there is no word on whether Arredondo will appeal this decision. School starts there in just a few weeks, Yasmin.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Unbelievable. All right. As always, Gabe Gutierrez, good to see you. Thank you, my friend. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:00]\par \par \uc2 All right. High school students in Oxford, Michigan, returning today for the first time since a deadly shooting at the school last November. This morning students lined up on campus all wearing the now required clear backpacks in order to enter, the school underwent renovations last year following the tragic shooting that killed four students and injured seven others. The accused shooter also appeared in court today to confirm his placement at the county jail. The former student being charged as an adult and facing murder charges.\par \par \uc2 It's what nearly everybody is talking about today around the nation. Student loan forgiveness. So who qualifies, who benefits the most, and does it actually solve the real problem in this country's education system? We're going to try and get those questions answered coming up next. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right. Welcome back. So millions of Americans across the country woke up this morning with less debt after the president announced plans for student loan forgiveness for low and middle income borrowers. For perspective, you've got 75 percent of students with debt currently owing less than $40,000 and more than half owing less than $20,000.\par \par \uc2 The cancellations expected to impact three distinct groups. Younger people who were just starting to pay off their loans, those with lower credit scores, and black and Latino American students.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:02]\par \par \uc2 Here to discuss those impacted groups and what the cancellation means for higher education is NBC's Morgan Radford.\par \par \uc2 So I talked about -- by the way, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN RADFORD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Thank you. Good to see you, too. I love being on set with you.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: I know. It's so good to see you. So I talked about those three kind of impacted groups, right.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: How soon could they be seeing this debt relief?\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Yes. It's sort of TBD at this point, especially to see when they're going to see those checks sort of hit. What it means, how to apply. Like the site has almost crashed with just the volume of information people are looking for.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: So when it comes to actually having that student loan debt cancelled, there's a little bit of a waiting period ultimately. First borrowers will have to fill out a form with the Education Department to make sure they qualify based on their income. And the White House says Education officials will be rolling out more information about where to find that application in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 But that does not mean that many borrowers aren't feeling the impact right away. That's because that pause on federal student loan payments was set to expire at the end of the month. Well, now it's extended to the end of the year. So a little bit of time, a little bit of relief.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: And also the White House estimating this is going to affect 43 million Americans. That number is astounding. Everyone who has federal student loans but it will mean more for other groups, right?\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Yes, it does. I mean, when you look at that, sort of as you mentioned, this relief will have a huge impact on black and Latino borrowers especially. In fact if you break down those numbers, 90 percent of black students and 72 percent of Latino students take out loan compared to 66 percent of white students. And then if you actually sort of fast forward to four years after graduation, the average black college graduate owes more than $52,000, compared to around $30,000 for Latino graduates, $28,000 for white graduates.\par \par \uc2 So at the end of the day, you know, a lot of these loan holders that we spoke to, they look like Juan Ramiro Sarmiento, he's a young activist who said that he actually had to drop out of school and get a job after his mother had to self-deport back to Mexico during the Trump administration. There are also borrowers like Sheranda Pender, a mother of two in her 40s who still to this day has a loan balance hanging over her head, even though she had to stop going to school to get a job. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHERANDA PENDER, STUDENT LOAN DEBT HOLDER: I'm 46 years old and still have this, you know, over my head. So this announcement definitely, you know, it gives a little hope, you know, for starters, some type of relief for us.\par \par \uc2 JUAN RAMIRO SARMIENTO, YOUNG INVINCIBLES MEDIA MANAGER: I voted for the president specifically for this issue. $20,000 for Pell recipients would be a major victory. And I would have -- I voted for the president for those reasons and I would feel like the president is delivering to an extent, even if it's not everything that we advocated for.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: And what's interesting, Yasmin, you talked about those 43 million student loan borrowers.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes. '\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: 39 percent of them, which is something we don't often talk about, actually didn't get a degree in the six years since the first day they enrolled. So that's a lot of people, right? A lot of people who have loans who didn't actually get the value of that degree.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: But they're still carrying the debt.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Exactly. Not even the short term. So, you know, when we talk to these different students who were loan borrowers, they were all happy to see this happen, but they all said that they hoped that this is just the first step in many.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Right. Because it's part of kind of the bigger problem when it comes to the education system in this country. That's so much of what we're hearing from so many of these students.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Access.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Access. Right. Morgan Radford, as always, great to see you, my friend. By the way, first time on camera with you. Congratulations since I've seen you, and hearing about your pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Thank you, thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Hope you're doing well. You look beautiful as always.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Wow. Wish me luck. Life comes at you fast.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: It comes at you fast. I know it a little better than anyone.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Comes a little fast. Whoa. Adulting real hard over here. Who would have thought?\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Adulting -- yes. No, you can still be a kid when you have kids. That's fine.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Yes. All right. Wish me luck.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right. Thanks, hon.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right. I want to bring in now "Atlantic's" Adam Harris, he's out with new reporting headline, "Biden's Cancellation of Billions in Debt Won't Solve the Larger Problem."\par \par \uc2 Adam, I'm so glad to have you on with us as we were just kind of having this conversation, Morgan and I, right, about what this student debt cancelation is going to mean for the bigger picture, right. What it means for the bigger problem when it comes to the education system in this country and access to education. Talk to us a little bit about that, how this might just be the first step but what more needs to happen.\par \par \uc2 ADAM HARRIS, "ATLANTIC" STAFF WRITER: Yes, when you think about the sort of higher education, there are a couple of issues that we had -- that we have had for the last several decades. One of them is the sort of student loan debt crisis, right. What do you do with the people who have already taken on student loan debt? But the second part of it is how do you ensure that Americans don't end up in the same situation again, right?\par \par \uc2 I think that the Biden administration, one of the things that they did in this most recent announcement, the headlines of course were all about the debt cancellation but they also helped reform the income-driven repayment programs, right, to 5 percent of your discretionary income over a shorter period of time. But the last piece of that was always this sort of how do we improve the affordability of college.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: That's why it was during the campaign you saw that free college programs coupled with student debt cancellation.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Do you think that this decision will impact that answer to the question you just posed, i.e., access, right?\par \par \uc2 [13:55:09]\par \par \uc2 How higher education, how colleges, how universities go about helping students with access?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I think so. Right. I think that we are in an interesting moment when you do have real conversation at state levels about what have been known as college promise programs, right, which are also, can be tuition- free college, debt-free college.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: But you've also seen it at a national level. And of course debt cancellation started as an idea during campaigns, something that grew rapidly as the student loan portfolio grew. But it started as an idea. And I think that now that we've seen one of these things through to fruition, even if it wasn't the $50,000 that some people wanted, even if it wasn't the full elimination that others might have wanted, it's a sort of stepping stone in order to sort of more fully address issues of affordability in colleges.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes. And certainly as I mentioned yesterday, and I think it's important to mention again, it's something that I hear over and over again from young people when I'm reporting on the streets, whether it be about what's happening with \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 , or something else, and that they talk about the promises from the Biden administration, forgiving college debts. And so it seems as though this could be a win for the Biden administration amidst all of it.\par \par \uc2 For now, Adam Harris, thank you for this conversation. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us, everybody. I'm going to be back in the chair for Chris tomorrow at 1:00 p.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC. You can also catch me also on weekends, "YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN REPORTS," Saturdays and Sundays 2:00 to 4:00 p.m. Eastern also on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 Katy Tur is picking things up after a very quick break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220826ei8p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle for August 24, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Alicia Menendez, Gabe Gutierrez \par \uc2 7,551 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: And that is today`s "LAST WORD". THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:23]\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC ANCHOR: Tonight, a secret memo to former Attorney General \uc2 Bill Barr\uc2  is unsealed today. The middle of supports Bill Barr`s questionable decision not to charge \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with obstructing the Russia investigation.\par \par \uc2 And the former president says he was surprised the \uc2 FBI\uc2  came to get top secret documents at his club. But it turns out the government had been trying since before he left office.\par \par \uc2 Then, President Biden`s game changer announcement on student loan forgiveness, a lifeline for millions of Americans. Plus, the big lie turned big grift, how some are profiting off of disproven election fraud claims goes beyond the former president of THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Once again, I`m Alicia Menendez in for Stephanie Ruhle. As if the current day controversies of the former president were not enough. We begin with new insight tonight into how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may have avoided accountability in the investigation that consumed much of his presidency.\par \par \uc2 Late today after a lengthy court fight, the Justice Department released a document related to the Russia investigation. The memo was top secret and now we know why former Attorney General \uc2 Bill Barr\uc2  wanted to keep it a secret.\par \par \uc2 In March 2019, \uc2 Bill Barr\uc2  told us before the Mueller report was released that the report had all been exonerated the former president and therefore, there was no reason to charge him. The memo essentially lays out Barr`s defense of that decision.\par \par \uc2 The memo contends Mueller`s report did not establish a case to charge Trump with obstruction and failed to establish any underlying crime related to Russian election interference. But as NBC has reported, Mueller`s report actually did identify 10 episodes that could be considered potential obstruction of justice. There`s one former Mueller prosecutor points out that`s just what a couple of high level Justice Department officials are worried about.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, FMR. SENIOR MEMBER OF MUELLER PROBE: Two senior staff say to \uc2 Bill Barr\uc2 , that the reason he should make the decision is because if the memo comes out, it might be read to imply that the President committed obstruction. This memo makes it clear that the reason that \uc2 Bill Barr\uc2  did not ask \uc2 Robert Mueller\uc2  to simply come to a conclusion is because it as it says here, the report can be read to find that indeed, he did commit obstruction.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: As for the ongoing investigations into the former president. Tonight the Justice Department is facing a deadline related to the search warrant for Trump`s Florida home. Prosecutors have until noon tomorrow to give a judge suggested redactions to the warrants affidavit. Today, at the White House, President Biden was asked about search.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, how much advance notice did you have of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s plan to search Mar-a-Lago?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: I didn`t have any advance notice, none, zero, not one single bit. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Turns out efforts to retrieve presidential records from Trump had been well underway even before he left office. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  as the National Archives was asking for presidential documents during the final days of his term after one of Trump`s own lawyers agreed they should be returned. One of the reporters on that story is going to join us in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, NBC News reports top intelligence officials do not appear to have launched a formal damage assessment relating to classified documents found at Mar-a-Lago. There were also signs the former president is increasingly upset over the \uc2 FBI\uc2  seizure of those records.\par \par \uc2 Rolling Stone\uc2  reports he`s been pushing his lawyers to quote, get my, my top secret documents back. One prominent constitutional lawyer had a no holds barred response to that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE CONWAY, CONSERVATIVE ATTORNEY: These documents belong to the executive branch they belong to the United States of America. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can`t assert an executive branch privilege as a former president against the current executive branch. Trump took them he stole them in fact.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: One Washington Post columnist says Trump`s determination to cling to classified documents could create his biggest legal headache yet. An op- ed titled behind Trump`s campaign of delay and defiance at Mar-a-Lago, David Ignatius writes that Trump is now like King Lear an angry exile. But he adds Trump`s notion of supreme personal power is document narcissism might have caught up with him in the Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:04]\par \par \uc2 Ignatius ends with a suggested solution to this controversy he writes stop denigrating the rule of law, respect it.\par \par \uc2 And with that, let`s bring in our leadoff panel Jackie Alemany, Congressional investigations reporter for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , and MSNBC contributor, Eugene Daniels, White House correspondent for POLITICO, and Barbara McQuade, a veteran federal prosecutor and former U.S. Attorney for the Eastern District of Michigan. She worked with the DOJ during the Biden transition, and she`s a professor at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 `s law school. Good to see you all.\par \par \uc2 Jackie, you have new reporting on just how long the National Archives has been trying to get these documents back from Trump, even before he left the White House, but the former president acts like he`s surprised by this. Tell us what you learned.\par \par \uc2 JACKIE ALEMNAY, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, thanks so much for having me on tonight. We reviewed an email of correspondence between the former president`s legal team and officials from the National Archives in the May time period that showed that there was a determination weeks before former President Trump left office that certain items around roughly two dozen boxes of original presidential records that were being kept in the residence of the White House needed to be returned to NARA under the Presidential Records Act, and that Trump`s top lawyers at the White House including \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , along with Scott Gast had made a determination that these boxes would be returned to the National Archives and to the White House Office of Records Management.\par \par \uc2 It`s unclear in via this email, if NARA knew exactly what was in those boxes in the residence at the time, but where we are now we know that multiple boxes 15 initially, and now multiple sets of classified information and subsequent seizures by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  have been recovered in -- from Mar-a-Lago and into the possession of the National Archives.\par \par \uc2 And now the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , showing that this all unfolded far earlier than was previously known, and that even in the final days of the administration, there were officials already concerned about the former President`s record keeping practices and trying to recover materials, again, as early as January of 2020.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: And Barbara, this timeline is critical. The more we learn, the longer it seems the dispute with the archives over documents has been going on and the harder it would seem for Trump for his allies to defend all this. What would you be telling him if you were his lawyer right now?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE, \uc2 FMR\uc2 . U.S. ATTORNEY: Well, I think the best advice I think you can give someone is that when you find yourself in a hole, stop digging. And it seems that every time he turns around, he`s saying something that makes his case a little bit worse, you know, the evolving defenses. For example, at first he didn`t do it, and then that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  planted these documents, and then that he declassified them, all of those statements can ultimately be used against him. And the changing narrative, I think just tends to show a consciousness of guilt.\par \par \uc2 But the significance I hear from Jackie`s story is that this lengthy duration of the battle back and forth, really establishes this element of willfulness. And unlike other crimes, where ignorance of the law is no excuse, you do have to prove that a person knew that what they were doing was illegal when it comes to mishandling classified documents. And so this length of duration of this battle with the archives, you know, tends to show that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew very well that what he was doing was against the law, and yet continued to hang on to these documents. So, every time he opens his mouth, he`s making the case worse for himself.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: So tomorrow in Florida, DOJ is going to submit it suggested affidavit redactions. Barbara, what should we be looking for? What`s the process here?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Well, I the judge has ordered them to produce some redactions. I can`t imagine that there`s an awful lot that they are going to want to expose to the world because it is going to potentially involve the disclosure of some of the witnesses here, even if not by name, to identify, you know, maybe their job descriptions or when and where they observe certain things. And it wouldn`t take much for someone to figure out who these people are.\par \par \uc2 And so the judge will review these documents. So there will be a proposed redaction. It seems likely that there will be so much redacted as to really render this exercise futile. But nonetheless, maybe the judge will feel like, you know, just putting in a few random words here and there is all they can do.\par \par \uc2 So I think the judge is going to review the Justice Department`s proposed redactions tomorrow. You may push back and see if they could disclose a little bit more, but I don`t imagine we`re going to see a whole lot.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: All right. The President and his allies have been criticizing law enforcement. The former president and his allies, excuse me, have been criticizing law enforcement ever since the Mar-a-Lago search and not has federal agencies on alert.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:02]\par \par \uc2 One man died in a police standoff after firing a nail gun at an \uc2 FBI\uc2  bureau in Cincinnati. New Video has just been released from that incident as NBC`s Gabe Gutierrez reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The barrage of gunfire alongside in Ohio cornfield came during a tense standoff after police say a man trying to breach this \uc2 FBI\uc2  field office in Cincinnati this month.\par \par \uc2 A high speed chase follow the attempted break in. This video provided to NBC affiliate WLWT by the Warren County Sheriff`s Office just one of the agencies involved.\par \par \uc2 The standoff lasts nearly six hours. Eventually law enforcement closes in the suspect shot and killed.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Authorities have not confirmed the motive. But the suspect posted on social media about his desire to kill \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents after former President Trump`s Mar-a-Lago residence was searched.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Eugene, the attacks from the former president, the attacks from his allies, they have real world consequences. How worried is the White House about this investigation and the continued threats to federal law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 EUGENE DANIELS, POLITICO WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: This current White House is very concerned about it so much so that on September 15, they`re kind of bringing people together in a day of unity. We`re talking to lawmakers, clergy, people, you know, activists and advocates to figure out how to tone down the rhetoric in this country. It`s really hard to do when you have a former president who is still the leader of his party, and a party following him, you know, blindly as he kind of says whatever he wants, it was kind of shocking, I think to all of us, as soon as president -- former President Trump started talking about the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the way that he was, which he`s done for years after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  went to his home.\par \par \uc2 He started to see Republicans say something I never thought it was able to defund the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , right, defund law enforcement, something they have attacked Democrats for a very long time. And so the fact that you have a Republican Party that`s willing to follow \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , as he attacks, you know, whether that is election workers right, before and before January 6, whether that is law enforcement officials currently, and it`s not going to stop this is something that he`s done for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 And so when you talk to people in this White House, then it`s not very clear how much they can do. Right? They can try to get the American people to tone down the rhetoric. But when it comes to stopping the former president from doing that, there`s almost nothing they can do. They`re hoping to September 15. Meeting and conversation helps to kind of jumpstart that conversation.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Well, Jackie, you`re also covering the House January 6 committee, they`ve been working through August, what`s going to be their focus going into September? And when might we see them resume hearings?\par \par \uc2 ALEMANY: Yes, well, so far what we know about that is the committee plans on holding a series of hearings in September, and wants to continue their work until the very last moment, until possible in the case that the GOP ultimately takes back the majority in November after midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 If that`s the case, what we have heard again, but this is all in flux, and not quite nailed down, as investigators are still knee deep in some of the investigations that they have been conducting this summer with regards to various topics. But that means that writing the actual report could also go until later than previously anticipated with a sort of interim half-baked report coming out ahead of the midterms and then the final report coming out at the end of the very, very end of December.\par \par \uc2 But we know that there were several topics that investigators have been pursuing, including investigators who traveled abroad to obtain new materials. They`ve also been pursuing conversations that took place amongst Trump`s top cabinet officials about invoking the 25th Amendment questioning the former president`s mental fitness at the time post insurrection. And we also know that they`re still digging into the issue with the \uc2 U.S. Secret Service\uc2  trying to recover text messages that were deleted in the aftermath of January 6, despite multiple preservation requests from various entities, unclear whether or not they`re going to be able to recover those in time, though for again, the fall set of hearing season two, so to speak,\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Barbara, there`s the investigation into the documents. There is the work of the 1/6 committee and then there`s today`s development in the Russia investigation. The group that sued to get the DOJ memo released issued this statement today saying quote, It significantly twist the facts and the law to benefit \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The memo supports the chilling conclusion that any president can interfere with any investigation. They believe it could damage them politically. Is this group right? Did this memo give \uc2 Bill Barr\uc2  the cover he needed?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Yes, I read the memo and it really strikes me as you know reverse engineering the result that they want to get.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:03]\par \par \uc2 It has, you know, just legally inaccurate analysis. You know, for example it says that because there was no sufficient evidence to show a conspiracy, that they should not charge obstruction. That`s just not the law. It`s thwarting an investigation is bad in its own right. It`s a crime in its own right. It doesn`t matter that you don`t make the ultimate case. It says because aids didn`t follow through on his orders that they shouldn`t charge but the statute makes it a crime to attempt to obstruct justice.\par \par \uc2 And the point I think that crew is making there is this one, which is it may not have been that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  really wanted to thwart a criminal investigation. It may be that he simply was concerned that investigation would harm him politically, therefore, we shouldn`t charge him but that conflates motive with intent, it`s still corrupt, it`s still wrongful to try to thwart an investigation. It doesn`t matter what your motive is.\par \par \uc2 So, it completely ignores what I thought was the most egregious example, which was directing \uc2 Don McGahn\uc2 , the White House counsel, to fabricate a memo to say that he had not -- Trump had not directed Mueller, or directed the firing of Muller. So for all of those reasons, it really was just cover for \uc2 William Barr\uc2  to reach the results he wanted, in my view.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Eugene, I got to ask you before we go, it has been six months since Russia launched its unprovoked invasion of Ukraine. Ukraine says a Russian missile hit a train station today killing at least 22 people, the White House pledged billions more in aid, nearly 13 billion has been promised since the invasion. What are you hearing from the White House about their plan moving forward?\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Yes, something that this White House has felt from the beginning. And every single room every time they talk about this is that this is a fight about democracy era autocracy, and that they want to see this to the end. And so that means, you know, keeping Ukraine with the weapons that they need to continue to fend off Russia for as long as they possibly can, making sure that the American boots never hit the ground in Ukraine, and definitely not in Russia to participate, and giving billions of dollars and different types of aid to make it possible for Ukraine to make it through this.\par \par \uc2 Well, the issue with that is that has continued to be holding together the coalition internationally to do this as a world market has been has struggled, right? Economies in different countries, not just ours, have hit a bumpy road. And so this White House is going to continue to focus on that.\par \par \uc2 And you talk to aids, you know, this is I think people did not expect this to go on as long as it has. But now that it has gone on, you know, at the six-month mark, you talked to folks, I think that it could go through the winter and in the Ukraine when it will be a lot harder to do some of these things. And I think that is concerning for the world writ large.\par \par \uc2 And for White House that did not want to spend a lot of time focusing on Europe. They wanted to spend a lot of time in their foreign policy arm focusing on China and the kinds of things that China can do and we should be in they feel that we should be stopping them from doing and I will say this are helping Ukraine is also a part of that, right, and make giving everything to Ukraine so that China realizes that if they were to invade Taiwan, if they wanted to invade Taiwan, America would, you know, possibly give support to Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: To say nothing of the Ukrainians that have been displaced and lost so much. Jackie Alemany, Eugene Daniels, Barbara McQuade, thank you all so much for getting us started.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the president keeps a campaign promise forgiveness of a portion of federal student loan debt. One more thing Democrats hope to campaign on. We`ve got a member of Congress who was also a student borrower himself, next.\par \par \uc2 And later, a closer look at just who`s been making big money from the big lie deep in the heart of Texas. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:23:28]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The news today will give millions of Americans a significant financial boost while fulfilling a Biden campaign pledge.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Provide more breathing room for people so they less burdened by student debt.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: President Biden says the government will forgive federal student loan debt of up to $10,000. For those earning less than $125,000 a year, Pell Grant recipients will get $20,000 in loan forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Today`s announcement on student debt cancellation was welcome news to a great many borrowers and not just graduates. As NBC News reports the debt forgiveness applies to undergraduate, graduate and Parent Plus loans.\par \par \uc2 With us tonight California Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 . He personally took out more than $100,000 in student loans is the same struggles to make those monthly payments. Congressman, you carried student debt yourself. How big is this for borrowers?\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA) OVERSIGHT AND REFORM COMMITTEE: It`s a big significant step. It`s long overdue. You know I took out over $100,000 of loans. There was a year I couldn`t pay it back. I had to take forbearance. Now I was fortunate I was able to pay my student loans back.\par \par \uc2 But you know what? I don`t have any resentment that other people other Americans shouldn`t have to go through the same hardships that I did. I mean, it`s crazy in this country. No other Western democracy makes young people take out 3040 $50,000 of debt to finish their education. I`m glad the President did this.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: All right. The big headline on this plan is the lump sum cancellation $10,000 or $20,000 depending on if you have Pell grants but it also extends the payback pause through the end of the year it caps monthly payments at 5 percent of income, some of that fine print, how important is that?\par \par \uc2 [23:25:15]\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Well, it`s very important, especially right now where you have rents having increased or you have gas prices up food prices, young people, people who are trying to make ends meet, they, as it is, are struggling. And so to ask them to be paying back their student loans right now with a difficult economy is just wrong. And I`m glad that the President is extending that time so that they can make their living expenses and not have the burden of paying back the student loans.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: You alluded to this earlier, but the President was asked about the fairness of this policy after today`s announcement, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, is it unfair to people who pay their student loans, or chose not to take out loans?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Is it fair to people who, in fact, do not own multi-billion-dollar businesses if they see one of these guys getting all the tax breaks. Is that fair? What you think?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Congressman, you know, I know you`re going to hear this fairness argument over and over again, between now and November, your thoughts on the best way to push back?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Well, I understand that their parents in this country sacrifices who struggle, so that their kids could go to college. I understand that they`re young people who took two, three jobs to pay a college and I have so much admiration for them. I respect their resilience. And that`s something that they will always be proud of.\par \par \uc2 But just because they had to go through hardships, just because they had to do something that was unfair taking out $40,000, $50,000 doesn`t mean that`s the way it should always be. America`s a nation where we make progress for the hardships of previous generations don`t have to be the hardships of future generations.\par \par \uc2 So I think we have to respect their sacrifice, but say that we can do better, and then no person should have to go into that kind of debt ever again.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Well, I think that`s part of the fairness argument that you`re going to hear. You`re also going to hear a lot about how people who chose not to go to college and have the opportunity to go to college will benefit from the program. So feel free to speak to that piece of things. There are also critics warning about the impact of debt forgiveness on inflation. What is the pushback there?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: On vocational education are people who don`t go to college, of course, their loans should be forgiven as well. So let`s make sure that this is clear. This applies to trade school, applies to vocational school, and let`s double down on investing in people who don`t go to college. 