played a role in the whole events leading up to January 6.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:12]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: They did a lot of video recording of themselves it seems. I mean, just about everywhere all throughout all the cases in January 6, the bigwigs as well. No shortage of that. Congresswoman \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2 , thank you so much for coming on tonight.\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: Anytime.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, a big, big move for the Biden administration that will help millions of people struggling with student loan debt. That`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Some more election news. After last night`s primary election, we are now almost certainly going to have a new youngest member of the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 . The U.S. Constitution requires members of the House to be a minimum of 25 years old. And the last 25-year-old elected to Congress sworn in right before January 6 was Republican Congressman \uc2 Madison Cawthorn\uc2  of North Carolina. You remember him.\par \par \uc2 Cawthorn was initially embraced by Trump, bunch of other Republicans, until a series of scandals tarnished his appeal. Most notably his claim that Republican colleagues invited him to cocaine-fueled orgies.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MADISON CAWTHORN (R-NC): I look at all these people, a lot of them. you know, I`ve looked up to through my life, I`ve always paid attention to politics, guys that you know -- then all of a sudden, you get invited to like, hey, we`re going to have kind of a sexual get together at one of our homes. You should come. And I`m like, what did you just ask me to come to? And then you realize they`re asking you to come to an orgy.\par \par \uc2 Or the fact that, you know, there`s some of the people that are leading on the movement to try and remove, you know, addiction in our country, and then you watch them doing a key bump of cocaine right in front of you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Just a keep bump of cocaine right in front of you. That earned him a rebuke from the Republican House leader. He was not liked about that. Criticism from fellow North Colorado Republicans, a whole controversy that he should name names. So, after alienating just about everyone in his own party, Cawthorn lost his primary election three months ago. He will leave Congress after his term ends in January.\par \par \uc2 His replacement as the new youngest member of Congress will almost certainly be Maxwell Frost. Now, Frost is a 25-year-old who won his Democratic primary race by double digits in Florida`s comfortably blue 10th congressional district last night. Maxwell Frost is in every way, essentially the opposite of \uc2 Madison Cawthorn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While Cawthorn was busted twice for trying to board an airplane with a loaded gun, Frost is a gun violence prevention advocate, a former national organizing director for the pro-gun safety group March of Our Lives founded after the shooting in Parkland, Florida. While Cawthorn, a who`s who of Republican lawmakers who wanted to get rid of him including the senators from his own state of North Carolina, Maxwell Frost had a who`s who of progressive voices and politicians behind him including Senators Bernie Sanders and Elizabeth Warren.\par \par \uc2 And while \uc2 Madison Cawthorn\uc2  tried to support the overthrow of American democracy by helping to incite a violent mob to stop certification of Biden`s win and subvert the will the voters, Frost was trying to expand democracy by leading a voter turnout program which helped drive record numbers of young people to the polls.\par \par \uc2 From where I stand, it seems like a pretty good trade. And on his first day as the Democratic nominee in what will almost certainly be a Democratic seat, Frost can tout one of the main issues he campaigned on, student debt cancellation. The President just made a big announcement on that really impressive details are hidden in the fine print, but they`re there. And that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Have you ever wondered what at the federal level unified Democratic governance gets you? Well, you`re seeing the answer right now and it`s quite a bit. I mean, just look at the list of major legislation passed since the beginning of Joe Biden`s administration with the Democratic House and Senate behind him, although with very thin majorities.\par \par \uc2 The American Rescue Plan invested $1.9 trillion in the pandemic recovery, expanded the Child Tax Credit only for a year, unfortunately, but it put $1400 checks in millions of American mailboxes. It also helped state and local budgets. They didn`t have to cut and fire a whole bunch of people. Then, the $1.2 trillion infrastructure bill signed into law last November, funds everything on roads and bridges, to broadband access and power systems, utilities.\par \par \uc2 There`s the new Inflation Reduction Act, the largest investment in fighting climate change in this country`s history, will spend over $300 billion over 10 years. Also lower the cost of prescription drugs, creates a minimum tax rate for the wealthiest corporations. That`s legislatively.\par \par \uc2 Today, President Biden announced a sweeping plan for student loan relief. The administration will forgive up to $10,000 of debt for people making less than $125,000 a year, up to $20,000 for Pell Grant recipients.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There`s 20 million people who can start getting on with their lives. All this means people can start -- finally crawl out from under that mountain of debt, to get on top of their rent and their utilities, to finally think about buying a home or starting a family or starting a business. And by the way, when this happens, the whole economy is better off.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As the President said, this one program is going to help millions and millions of people just in material terms. The Democratic agenda so far have moved trillions of dollars focused primarily on middle working class and poor people. Tressie McMillan Cottom is a professor at the \uc2 University of North Carolina Chapel Hill\uc2 , an opinion columnist for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . She`s also the author of Lower Ed: The Troubling Rise of For Profit Colleges in the New Economy. She joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Tressie, you`re one of my go-to people on this issue on things about higher ed. It`s your area of academic expertise, though you write about a wide variety of topics. What`s your sort of top-line takeaway to folks who I`m sure are texting you all day about what this means? What do you think of it?\par \par \uc2 [20:55:00]\par \par \uc2 TRESSIE MCMILLAN COTTOM, PROFESSOR, UNIVERSITY OF NORTH CAROLINA CHAPEL HILL: It has been a busy day. Good to see you, Chris. You know, there`s a saying where I`m from in the American South this isn`t nothing to sneeze at. And so, that is my top line takeaway. You know, it can be difficult to accept a win when there`s so much that we need to win and still need to fight for.\par \par \uc2 But this is a pretty good day, a day that we owe a lot of credit to student activists for, for people who kept this issue in the limelight that kept pushing Democrats to the left on this issue. That is why we`re looking at something that actually targets and addresses negative amortization, which is when your loan balance increases over time even though you`re making payments.\par \par \uc2 This is something that takes into account that some people go into student loan indebtedness with a negative wealth position. So, targeting those people who have Pell Grants, those are -- tend to be the lowest income college students. So, all of these things are actually really good things. And we should probably enjoy this day. That is my top line takeaway.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. Your point there about negative amortization, which is one of the sort of sickest and most perverse aspects of this problem, right, people ending up paying much more than they initially borrowed. I mean, much, much, much more orders of magnitude, sometimes three or four times more because of ballooning interest payments. There`s some provisions here on that right to cap that.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s also -- this, I think, is an important thing that slipped through, the maximum percentage of your income paid. So, over and above the cancellation of some of this debt, they`re going to reduce that from 10% to five percent. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 COTTOM: That is correct, which takes into account something that activists and scholars who study this issue have been concerned about for some time, which is a lot of student loan indebtedness was taken on with the assumption that wages would rise over time, and that they would keep up with the cost of living inflation and what have you.\par \par \uc2 And I think this is an acknowledgment that the percentage of income that people were paying to this debt could not keep pace with wages. And so, I think this is an acknowledgment that the labor market hasn`t done its fair share to and rewarding people`s educational attainment. So, yes, this means more money in people`s pockets month over month, it also means paying less over the term of the loan.\par \par \uc2 And listen, this is money by the way that is going back into the federal government. The idea that there should have been so much profit extraction from the student loan system was always one of the more perverse and negative consequences of some policy missteps over the last few years. And this begins to address that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, one of the thing -- one of the reasons I wanted to have you on particularly because I -- your book -- really learned a lot from your book, Lower Ed. You`re seeing -- this has provoked a lot of debate and discourse, right? And I think there`s some good faith criticisms of it and some folks across the spectrum. But one thing I find really pernicious is this kind of class war framing that you`re seeing often from like extremely wealthy, credentialed and degreed conservatives that this is -- you know, this is a handout to like the upper class, the expense of like the folk like the welders and the -- and the real working class.\par \par \uc2 And as someone who like really studied this issue, I want to hear what you say to that line of argument which has been everywhere today.\par \par \uc2 COTTOM: Well, it is -- first and foremost, it is disingenuous. It isn`t supported by evidence or fact, but that isn`t the point, right? When we start talking about class warfare and we start, you know, using sweeping terms like, you know, the wealthy, the working class, the real American, what we`re then talking about is political sportsmanship, right? And these are actually two different issues.\par \par \uc2 What was released today was actually really substantive engagement with understanding what has created the student loan crisis. The politics of it does not pay attention to those really real material conditions at all. The idea that this is a giveaway to wealthy people ignores the fact that wealthy families don`t need to use the student loan system.\par \par \uc2 To the extent that any wealthy family does use the student loan system, you know why they use it? They use it for tax planning purposes, right? To get low interest, cheap money that -- so they can use their money for investment vehicles in the interim. They are not using student loan money the way that most Americans use it, which is to survive, to pay their bills, to pay their tuition, right, and to get through school.\par \par \uc2 So, the idea that there`s this substantive group of student loan debt holders that have an extreme amount of wealth and this is a giveaway to them is the kind of political gamesmanship that happens when, I would point out, a party is losing the narrative. You know, despite what we have argued about in the discourse over the last few months, the idea of getting people out from under student loan debt is actually popular, right? This is the party who has lost the popular line with people that they need to turn out to vote.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I can say, I was fairly agnostic on this but -- you know, whether this would be popular or not, but I think the polling has been consistent that it is quite popular. And I think that you`re right that some of the caterwauling today is because of that. Tressie McMillan Cottom, thank you so much. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN on this Wednesday night. the Alex Wagner Show starts right now -- "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" I should say. And I`m coming to you 15 seconds late. I apologize.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220825ei8o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for August 24, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Yasmin Vossoughian, Dasha Burns, Shaquille Brewster, Sahil Kapur,  Ken Dilanian, Melissa Murray, Josh Lederman, Ben Hodges, Kelly O`Donnell,  John Della Volpe \par \uc2 8,125 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN, MSNBC ANCHOR: Hey, everybody, good afternoon. I`m Yasmin Vossoughian, in for Chris Jansing here at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 So right now, we`re just about 75 days away from the midterm elections, and one special House race may signal a major tide shift for November. In New York, Democrat Pat Ryan defeated Republican \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2  in a race experts are saying could be a national bellwether with major questions remaining of what messaging actually moves the needle with voters.\par \par \uc2 Ryan made abortion rights the centerpiece of his campaign in a swing district, Molinaro tried to tie Democrats in the White House to high prices in the economy as well. Ryan`s win appears to show once again that abortion rights are a powerful motivator in helping Democrats get people to the polls. As "MEET THE PRESS FIRST READ" put out this morning, this red wave is looking much more like a ripple.\par \par \uc2 And roughly a thousand miles south of here in Florida, we now know who will take on Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in November. It is a familiar name in that state, \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 . He`s been the governor there before and he`s running again. He told her colleagues on "MORNING JOE," his plan to defeat DeSantis relies on not playing dirty.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 CHARLIE CRIST\uc2 , (D-FL): I`m going to beat him because I`m running on love. And love always wins. And if he wants to run on hate and culture wars and dividing people and making people hate each other, that`s his turf. It`s not mine. He`s trying to tear my state apart that`s why I`m running against him. I don`t care about the money he`s got.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, a lot to unpack here. Let`s get right to it. NBC`s Shaq Brewster is with us in Fort Lauderdale covering the Florida primary election results. We got Dasha Burns, who`s in Kingston, New York, covering that New York special election. Also with me, former Biden campaign Senior Advisor Cristobal Alex, and NBC senior national political reporter Sahil Kapur. Welcome to you all, guys. Thanks for joining us on this.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Dasha, let me just start with you on this because I know that you had a one-on-one with Pat Ryan after his win yesterday. What did he tell you?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, Yasmin, look, this is significant, right? The first competitive congressional race since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, and potentially the last real Bellwether that we`re going to have ahead of the midterms. And the signal that we got out of this race is a hopeful one for Democrats that this abortion issue really does motivate and galvanize voters.\par \par \uc2 Look, this was far from a guaranteed win for Pat Ryan, most polls had him trailing behind his Republican opponent, \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2 . And this district, it`s a swinging one, and it`s reflected the national mood for the last several years. It went from Obama to Trump to Biden by a narrow margin. So we knew that this was going to be a close one.\par \par \uc2 In my conversation with Ryan this morning, I asked him if his campaign was ready for the flurry of calls from Democratic strategists to sort of find out the secret sauce of this campaign. But the reality is, this is not a one-off. We saw a similar mood, similar shifts in Kansas, you know. When that abortion -- anti-abortion amendment was on the ballot, nobody expected the overwhelming victory that Democrats had in that campaign. And this -- we`re starting to see those sorts of trends in this primary season.\par \par \uc2 Talk -- I want you to take a listen to my conversation with Pat Ryan. I asked him about what message his victory says to the country. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAT RYAN, DEMOCRATIC CONGRESSMAN-ELECT, NEW YORK: I think the message is when fundamental rights and freedoms are under attack, we have to stand up, we have to fight, we have to be strong and clear. And when you do that, people rally. I mean, the issues at stake, reproductive rights, abortion access, are our fundamental rights that transcend partisanship. And we saw that in Kansas, we saw it last night here in New York, I think we`re going to continue to see it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And the framing that Ryan has been using is it`s important to listen to, he`s repeatedly connected the abortion issue with the issue of freedom.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: We heard that also in Kansas, they really use the platform of government overreach, trying to reach across the aisle with that kind of rhetoric too, that this is not a partisan issue. This is an issue of constitutional rights and freedoms. And that seems to be resonating with folks. I expect we`re going to hear more similar rhetoric in the months to come here.\par \par \uc2 We did get a statement from his opponent, \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2 , who told us that while this is not the outcome we hoped for, we knew this was an uphill battle.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 When Democrats scheduled the special election on the same day as two Democratic primaries with five candidates pushing their turnout, he said they`ll continue to look for solutions for upstate families. This is -- this is big, yes. And I -- and I think that Democrats are going to be talking about this race up until November 8.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So I think one of the major questions coming out of this all, Cristobal, I`m going to go to you on this one, is how do you kind of harness right, this special sauce as Dasha just put it when we`re seeing what took place in Kansas, you`re seeing what took place in this district in upstate New York? You`re seeing the polling nationally across the country, if you are running a midterm election, how do you harness this and go forward?\par \par \uc2 CRISTOBAL ALEX, FORMER BIDEN CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: Yes, that`s a great question. And I think the analysis we just heard was spot on. The message you heard from Pat Ryan is a very powerful, very compelling message. There`s a lot of outrage in this country and for good reason. A right- leaning Supreme Court just stripped away a fundamental constitutional right. Never before has that happened. And what you saw in Kansas were hundreds of people -- thousands and thousands of people turning out in record numbers in a special election that we hadn`t seen before that kind of turnout. It`s actually presidential-level turnout.\par \par \uc2 The New York race we saw, I think we can read a lot of positive into that. As a Democrat, I`m very excited about it. I think that singles where we`re going for this -- going forward in this country, in terms of the midterms. We`re just approaching the fourth quarter. As I like to think about it, politics is a lot like a football game, right?\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEX: Or any kind of sport where you have momentum going into the final stretch, and that`s critical. You could be down by a touchdown or two. But if you got that momentum going into the fourth quarter, which to me is post-Labor Day, you`re going into this fourth quarter, and you`ve got all of this -- these wins under your belt. If you look at what the White House and Democrats in Congress have done is one win after another whether it`s the Inflation Reduction Act. Today, President Biden announcing student loan forgiveness that is very targeted, and will lift low-income folks, middle- class folks out of poverty, and help folks like me and others who have student debt.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s a very major win. And I think if you look at the messaging, and what`s coming out of the Supreme Court decision in Dobbs, it`s going to fuel all of this interest and the huge spike in outreach by Democratic campaigns and others to really tap into that, that new interest from voters that may have been sitting on the sideline before.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Sahil, I liked the point that you wrote. And I want to read it for folks to kind of understand where you`re at with this, saying it was a battle between these two competent contenders showcasing their party`s preferred messages, saying Democrats on abortion guns, democracy, Republicans on Biden referendum, inflation, crime. And you said there`s no candidate quality at the problem to pin this defeat on. What did you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, MSNBC SENIOR POLITICAL REPORTER: Right. Well, this means that -- what I meant was that Republicans put up their strongest candidate against you know, what Democrats thought was their strongest candidate and Republicans fell short in this district. \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2  does not look or sound like an extremist. He is not an election denier. He has tried in the past to downplay and moderate on the issue of abortion. It didn`t work. It seems like voters are mad enough to show up.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: And, you know, take out their anger on anything and anyone with an R by its name because it was Republicans who assembled the Supreme Court that overturned Roe v. Wade. I think Pat Ryan`s attempt, as Dasha was just pointing out, to reclaim freedom is something that Democrats are doing a lot more of. We`ll see how that plays out in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 But beyond that, \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2  also tried to run on the messages that Republicans believe are their strongest messages for the fall. He tried to make this a referendum on President Biden, who is on profit.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: It didn`t work.\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: It didn`t work. He tried to make it a referendum on inflation, tried to, you know, highlight crime as well, I think in various parts of the race, and that didn`t work. So I think Democrats can look at the fact that this district, which has made or broken majorities in the past for the Republicans before 2018, and since 2018, for the Democrats as a good sign.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So what`s the reality on the ground in Washington, Sahil, as you`re talking to some of your sources there in that how they`re feeling about specifically this race is kind of Bellwether race. What they`re going to do going forward? Are they as worried about control or losing control of the House as they were before? Is this something that they can harness -- is everybody in the back room basically a campaign headquarters saying, all right, guys, this is what we got to do to move forward?\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: Well, Democrats are undoubtedly much more optimistic today than they were yesterday, around this time about holding the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: It is still an uphill climb. It`s not going to take a red wave for Republicans to claim control of the House of Representatives. Even a series of ripples will do given how narrow these margins are. But, you know, the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee pointed out yesterday that there were 222 House districts that were more pro-Biden than this one. That is the majority, if they can do -- if they can replicate this performance in the fall then they would be in a pretty good position to hold control.\par \par \uc2 Now, the big caveat. And this isn`t Marc Molinaro`s statement. I was mentioning it yesterday because Republican strategists were always -- were already telling me and others, you know, not to read too much into the result whatever it was.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: Because it`s scheduled on the same day as Democrats` primaries in New York.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: This is a blue state. Their argument is voters in primaries will turn out in bigger numbers and that the Republican turnout will be comparably better this fall. Let`s see if that pans out. If so, then the Republicans still have a decent shot at claiming control of the House, but not by a red wave margin.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Of course, we know you`re going to be watching it incredibly closely. With that, we talked a lot about New York, I want to head South also to Florida where Shaq is. Let`s talk about former Governor \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , Shaq, right? We just heard from him and the sound that I played coming into you guys saying essentially, he`s going to talk about more love, right? Love wins, he says. He`s not going to make this kind of a hateful campaign as he says the current governor is making it. He`s not necessarily worried about that $130 million, I believe, war chest the current governor has, as well. Can this work?\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, that will certainly be tested. And let`s be clear, \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , even during the primary battle with \uc2 Nikki Fried\uc2 , he went after DeSantis on both personality and policy. And look, he`s going to have to go after him with everything he`s got. DeSantis is someone who has an approval rating above 50 percent. He has that national profile that he turned into. That war chest that you described, that`s a historic amount of money that we`re talking about before the general election even started. So, already this morning in the hours after the primary ended, we`re getting a sense of where these battle lines are going to be drawn. Listen to what we heard from DeSantis last night and Crist today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R-FL): We will fight the woke and the businesses. We will fight the woke in government agencies. We will fight the woke in our schools. We will never ever surrender to the woke agenda. Florida is the state where woke goes to die.\par \par \uc2 CRIST: He is for the hard bright red meat vote of Republican primary voters in Iowa and New Hampshire. And his blind ambition for the presidency of the United States of America has taken his eye off the ball of this most beautiful state in the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Now, yesterday`s primary also finalized the Senate battle here in the state of Florida. Val Demings winning that primary race easily. She`s going up against Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 . That is a candidate that is a popular, as -- I was to say also popular identification on a nationwide level.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: She`s been able to raise a significant amount of money for a House race or for her Senate race. But again, many people are seeing the Democrats in this state as the underdogs coming into the general election. No matter what, this is going to be a fight an expensive fight. They`ll get a lot of national attention, Yasmin.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Cristobal, let`s pick up a little bit on where Shaq left off and specifically talking about the governor`s race there. And I want to read for you what NPR pointed out saying this. Crist is going to have to sway some of the nearly 4 million unaffiliated voters -- Florida voters I should say. Registered Republicans now outnumber registered Democrats in this state, but statewide races have often been close, DeSantis, himself, won by about a single percentage point back in 2018.\par \par \uc2 This idea -- there is this idea as we see in the polls as well, current governor is pretty popular in that state right about now especially amongst obviously Republicans. Does Crist even have a shot here?\par \par \uc2 ALEX: He certainly has a shot. As you pointed out, these races are always extremely tight on the margins, usually just a couple of percentage points. And so Florida can go either way. I`ll tell you, what worries me as a Democrat about Florida though is DeSantis has that gigantic war chest, $140 million.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEX: I can`t think of another incumbent statewide who has ever had that kind of money. I will also say a challenge for Democrats is going to be getting outside investors into the state whether it`s major national organizations, major donors, and others because Democrats had been burned the last few election cycles there. They`re a little more gun shy about going into Florida and spending that kind of money.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, I`ve campaigned with Crist before. In fact, the very first time I traveled with then-Vice President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on a campaign swing was in 2014. We left California, on to Vegas, and then into Miami to campaign with \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  and then his lieutenant governor nominee, Annette Taddeo. And that was an uphill battle, but he knows the state extremely well.\par \par \uc2 And I think his messages solid, give -- is a little bit of tightening up there. But there`s definitely a huge juxtaposition between what you heard from DeSantis really almost throwing red meat into the base and what Crist is doing. So he definitely got a shot, going to take a lot of money, but there`s hope there. Val Demings, by the way --\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEX: Extremely exciting candidate there and a lot of folks very excited about where she is in her polling right now. We didn`t expect her to be ahead at this point in the game when she is now.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Do you see her having a shot up against \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ALEX: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: You do?\par \par \uc2 ALEX: I really do.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Shaq Brewster, Dasha Burns, Sahil Kapur, and Cristobal Alex, thank you, guys. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Also in New York, everybody, these aren`t the only races voters are going to decide this fall. They`re also going to choose a new governor. We are counting down 100 key races across the country now that we`re less than 100 days from the midterms. Here`s where things stand right about now. Incumbent Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  will run against Republican opponent Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . The latest polls give Hochul a double-digit lead less than 11 weeks to Election Day.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 And then a reminder, everybody, our "PLAN YOUR VOTE" tool is here to help you successfully cast your ballot in the midterm primaries, no matter where you live, get key information on the voting rules in your area, head to NBCnews.com/PlanYourVote now.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, new details surrounding the investigation into the former president and the highly sensitive material he was storing at Mar-a- Lago, what does that mean for national security? And then, later on, a long-anticipated announcement on student loan debt forgiveness, that could be a game-changer for millions of Americans, what we expect to hear from the President and how it could reshape the midterms. That`s ahead. You`re watching MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, some breaking news this hour, everybody. First Lady Jill Biden just tested positive for rebound COVID after testing negative yesterday. She`s not had a reemergence of symptoms and will remain in Delaware where she has restarted isolation procedures there.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 We`re also learning more today about this highly sensitive material \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was storing in his Mar-a-Lago basement. The National Archives has confirmed that more than 700 pages of classified documents were taken in January. Some include the highest levels of classification used to identify only the government`s most sensitive secrets. That is, by the way, in addition to the documents seized in the search earlier this month. But despite this potential threat to national security, four U.S. officials telling NBC News that the country`s top intelligence officials have not yet ordered a former -- formal, I should say, damage assessment.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to talk more about this, NBC`s justice and intelligence correspondent Ken Dilanian, and Melissa Murray, a former law clerk to then- judge \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , and an MSNBC legal analyst, welcome to you both, guys. Thanks for joining us on this.\par \par \uc2 Ken, let me just start with you on this one. And just kind of understand if they really know -- if there is a sense of how damaging this may be to national security.\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, MSNBC JUSTICE & INTELLIGENCE CORRESPONDENT: Well, the fundamental question that goes to that issue, Yasmin, is whether there has been an unauthorized disclosure. I mean, we know this stuff shouldn`t have been stored in Mar-a-Lago in an unsecured location. But the further question that intelligence officials don`t know the answer to and neither does the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , it appears is whether this got into the wrong hands somehow.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Whether any of this information leaked to an adversary. So that`s the first question they have to answer. But even if they don`t know the answer to that, generally, the intelligence community wants to go through some process protectively to determine whether any -- for example, any human sources are at risk, any technical intercepts are at risk.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Because let`s remember, this document shows that some of the most highly classified documents in the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  were included in this -- in this hall, TS/SCI, Special Access Programs that only a handful of the highest cleared people would be read in on. So this is serious stuff, Yasmin.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So I guess my follow-up to that, Ken, is why are they waiting or what are they waiting for in order to launch this formal damage assessment? And I want to read for you from what the DNI says about formal -- about these formal damage assessments. I`m saying this. Damage assessment shall be conducted when there is an actual or suspected unauthorized disclosure or compromise of classified national intelligence that may cause damage to U.S. national security. Going on to say, damage assessments may also be conducted when there was an actual or suspected loss, misuse, or unauthorized access to or modification of classified national intelligence that could adversely affect national security.\par \par \uc2 What are your sources telling you, Ken, as to why this has not yet been launched?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: So, it sure seems like those rules would call for an assessment, in this case, right? But what our sources are saying is a number of things. One, there was a delay between the time that the government first learned there were highly classified documents in Mar-a-Lago in the January batch. There was a four-month delay before the \uc2 FBI\uc2  actually got to look at the documents. That happened in May. So there hasn`t been all that much time.\par \par \uc2 But secondly, there seems to be some concern in the Biden administration of taking any action that would appear to be political, of any agency other than the independent Justice Department have its hands on this case. We`re sensing that reluctance. And then also there`s a reluctance to put anything in writing that could be later subpoenaed by the president or anyone else who becomes a defendant. It`s called graymail. Sometimes defendants in cases involving classified information will subpoena this stuff and threaten to make it public. And so there`s a great reluctance essentially write any secrets down as part of this process.\par \par \uc2 But look, there -- what we are also being told, Yasmin, is there definitely have been informal damage assessments. They`re looking at these documents. They`re trying to figure out whether there are any possible vulnerabilities here in the chance that there was an unauthorized disclosure.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Before I go to Melissa, Ken, can you just answer as to whether or not a damage assessment -- the results of a damage assessment would become public? Would we see them?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Generally, not. There -- but the congressional committees have asked that it be done. And so the Gang of Eight may see it, and the intelligence committees are likely to see it. And we could see some very heavily censored form of it, Yasmin.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So, Melissa, if in fact, this damage assessment is launched, how could that play into the legal cases that are mounting against the former president?\par \par \uc2 MELISSA MURRAY, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Well, regardless of whether or not classified information was unsecured or disclosed, it doesn`t necessarily matter for any of the statutes that were implicated in that search warrant. None of those statutes requires classified information to be an issue. It`s just simply information that could adversely affect the national security. And I think we`ve already established based on the National Archives letter that indeed, this was information that had national security implications. So I don`t know that that necessarily sheds light on that part of the case.\par \par \uc2 I think what the letter does shed light on is the president`s state of mind. And we`ve been speculating about what he could have been doing during this period of months where there is apparently negotiations between the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the National Archives and the former president about the return of these documents.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 And some had speculated that maybe this was merely negligence, you know, like -- but this letter and the fact that there`s this back and forth that has been documented, suggests that there`s something that seems more like willfulness or purposefulness in keeping these documents and not returning them to the National Archives as requested. And so I`m actually quite surprised that the former president wanted this information to be public because it seems quite damning.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Melissa, while I have you, I just want to ask you one more question and that`s about this request from the former president`s team for this appointment of a special master. What stood out to you about the judge`s order in that?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, I mean, the judge`s order was sort of the jurisprudential version of what even is this?\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: I mean, the complaint that was filed in the Southern District of Florida with this new judge was so shoddy and devoid of any of the markers of an ordinary pleading that even the judge was forced to ask for a do- over. And so she`s basically asked them to do it again, to do it right to provide a jurisdiction statement -- a jurisdictional statement that shows why she should be the one to hear this as opposed to Judge Reinhart. He`s already handling an ancillary matter that is related to this case already.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: And, by the way, this judge, a Trump-appointed judge, it`s important to note there with that development. Ken Dilanian, thank you. Melissa Murray, thank you as well.\par \par \uc2 New warnings, everybody, from Ukrainian President Zelenskyy on his country`s Independence Day as the United States announces another massive aid package six months into this war, what impact is it going to have? You`re watching MSNBC. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right. Today Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  had a clear message for his people, for Russia and for the world, really. Six months into this war, he said, quote, "On February 24th, we were told you have no chance. On August 24th, we say, Happy Independence Day, Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 Today marks 31 years of Ukraine`s independence from Soviet rule. He warned his people to be ready, saying that Russia may unleash brutal strikes.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden just announced this morning a new aid package for Ukraine today, worth as much as $3 billion. The largest batch of assistance as of yet.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Josh Lederman is in Kyiv for us. Also with retired Lieutenant General \uc2 Ben Hodges\uc2 . He`s the former commanding general of the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  in Europe. He`s now a senior adviser to the group \uc2 Human Rights First\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hey, Josh, let me just start with you on this one, and as I mentioned a little bit earlier, the Ukrainian president warning of possible brutal strikes on this Independence Day, six months into that war. Did any of that bear out?\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, so far, Yasmin, it hasn`t been nearly as bad as what a lot were fearing here in Ukraine. We did hear from President Zelenskyy today telling the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . that there was a Russian strike on the railway station in the Dnipro region that injured 50 people. Unclear how many people if any might have died in that attack.\par \par \uc2 Here in Kyiv we`ve also heard the air raid sirens go off more than half a dozen times today and the day is not over. In the past, many of the strikes that had been particularly damaging have taken place after nightfall so people are not letting their guard down quite yet. But in the meantime, there`s been a real vibe of pride and defiance here in the capital and in other places in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Today we saw \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 , the prime minister of U.K. here, making an announcement about more than 50 million pounds of aid. Obviously the president of the United States announced that the $3 billion package you mentioned which includes new surface-to-air missiles for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And in the streets of Kyiv today we saw lots of people out and about in traditional clothing, some of them having draped themselves in the blue and yellow of the Ukrainian flag, trying to signify that they are, you know, still here, standing strong six months after a war started that President Putin anticipated was going to be over very shortly. Yasmin?\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: A country surviving amidst this utter destruction that they`ve had to experience over the last six months.\par \par \uc2 Josh, I know you`ve got to go. So thank you for your reporting on that.\par \par \uc2 Lieutenant General, let`s dig into some of this and some of the reporting that we heard from Josh there and talk specifically about where Russia is right now. Early on in this war you actually predicted that Russia would fail to take Kyiv. That, in fact, is true. It seems as if they have now kind of stationed themselves in the eastern part of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Do you believe that is where they will remain or they will continue to make gains west in Ukraine or try to, at least?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. BEN HODGES (RET.), FORMER COMMANDING GENERAL, U.S. ARMY EUROPE 2014-2017: Yasmin, thank you. Russia has culminated, which means they don`t have the ability to continue a large-scale offensive praying. Their logistics system is exhausted, they have terrible manpower issues, they have leadership issues, and we know that war is a test of will and it`s a test of logistics.\par \par \uc2 Russia`s logistics gets worse every week, Ukraine`s gets a little better every week. And obviously from the video you just showed, there`s no doubt about who has the superior will here led by President Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: You`ve commanded U.S. troops throughout Europe, Lieutenant General. Talk to me about what $3 billion in aid in addition to the billions of dollars that they have gotten so far throughout these last six months, what it will do for the fight against Russia.\par \par \uc2 HODGES: I`m proud of what the administration continues to do to show support for Ukraine, but because this is about more than just Ukraine, this is about tyranny versus freedom. And so why it`s so important the United States continues to rally support from all of our allies as well.\par \par \uc2 The kind of things that are included in this package are important in the near term, air and missile defense, ammunition for long-range precision fires. But also, and this is important, the administration is doing things that will help Ukraine in the long term, because even though I expect Ukraine will push Russia back to the 23 February line by the end of this year, there`s still going to be a massive requirement for security against Russia in the coming years. So this package does some of that as well.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:00]\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: I`m wondering, though, what it would take for Russia to bail, right, to stop, because it doesn`t necessarily seem as if \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has that intention, no matter what Ukraine does here, despite the fact that his initial goals that he set out to accomplish when he invaded Ukraine were not achieved.\par \par \uc2 HODGES: Well, you`re right. I don`t think President Putin has any intention of himself deciding, OK, this was a mistake, we`re losing, let`s get out of there. But what we are seeing, the symptoms of a collapse, the exhaustion of the Russian logistics system, which I just mentioned, the fact that nobody wants to join the Russian army and get into the fight. So a country like Russia having trouble getting the soldiers that they need.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, when you think about the Russian Black Sea Fleet, I mean, once the pride of the Russian Navy, it`s hiding on the other side of Crimea. Ukraine does not even have a navy. But yet Russia`s fleet there will not come out and engage. The aircraft will not fly over Ukraine. They choose to fly from inside Russia and Belarus and launch missiles against civilian targets. So -- and then even today Defense Minister Shoigu in response to a question I think said, hey, we`re slowing down operations because we need to worry about civilian casualties.\par \par \uc2 What a line. I mean, nobody believes him, but even there, they are acknowledging that they are failing. And I think we`re going to see a crack here in the next couple of months.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Right. A huge admission, by the way, of Russians when it comes to something like this.\par \par \uc2 Lieutenant General \uc2 Ben Hodges\uc2 , thanks so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HODGES: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, U.S. F-15, F-16 fighters bombed nine bunkers being used by Iran backed forces in eastern Syria early on this morning. The bunkers are believed to have housed ammunition and military hardware. U.S. officials said there are not believed to be any casualties and that Russia`s military was warned minutes before the attack using a deconfliction line. The strike comes in the wake of rocket and drone attacks on American forces in the country earlier on this month. A military official telling NBC News that the U.S. is certain Iranian aligned groups were the source of those attacks.\par \par \uc2 All right. Three months after the shooting at Robb Elementary School and two postponements later, we could finally learn whether the Uvalde school police chief will lose his job. We`re going to look at what parents are demanding ahead of a critical meeting there tonight.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:42]\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right. Hours from now the Uvalde School Board is expected to discuss whether to fire Police Chief Pete Arredondo three months after that deadly shooting at Robb Elementary School that killed 19 kids and two teachers. The board was originally set to meet back in July but was postponed twice. Arredondo has come under intense public scrutiny over the police response to the May 24th massacre.\par \par \uc2 "Texas Tribune" public education reporter Brian Lopez has covered the story from the (INAUDIBLE) and joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Brian, thanks for joining us on this. I mean, two postponements later, right, folks were calling for the firing of Arredondo weeks after the shooting, after I was there on the ground reporting on this. And yet here we are and it still hasn`t happened. How is the community reacting to this meeting that may take place this evening, that is taking place this evening, and the possible firing of the police chief?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN LOPEZ, THE TEXAS TRIBUNE PUBLIC EDUCATION REPORTER: Well, the Uvalde community is hopeful that they`ll get some sense of closure tonight. They`ve been, like you`ve mentioned, pushing for Arredondo to be fired, especially after the Texas House Committee report, he was one of the first to be on the scene with almost 400 officers. And they still took more than an hour to engage. So that`s the hope for tonight for these residents who have been waiting for, like you mentioned, two months now for a resolution and some closure.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: What is the likelihood a decision will be made?\par \par \uc2 LOPEZ: That`s one of the, you know, things that we`ll be looking for. We really don`t know if this is going to be the end today or if there is going to be first of many meetings. You know, one of the reasons this was postponed, you know, twice is because his lawyer was calling for it to be done the legal way. At the end of the day, this is a hearing, so he`s going to present his side and he`ll present his arguments, whatever those may be, and we`ll see what the school board, you know, does. You know, I will say if they do not do anything tonight, fire him tonight, there will be much -- a lot more, you know, outrage within the Uvalde community.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: How is the community doing, Brian, now that kids, especially, are back to school?\par \par \uc2 LOPEZ: It really depends on who you ask. You know, I`ve been talking a lot to parents about the decisions they`re making. You know, some are going back to the district, mainly because they have no other choice, right. It`s a working-class community, some parents are working, you know, got to wake up at 7:00 a.m., get home at 6:00, deal with their families. Others have the capability to, you know, go out of the district, go to private schools.\par \par \uc2 The Uvalde CISD is offering an online option as well. So it really depends who you ask but the main key thing is, you know, people aren`t just thinking about, you know, what`s the best education like, how this is going to get people are thinking about, you know, where are they going to be the safest. You know, you have people (INAUDIBLE) expensive, you know, bulletproof backpacks because of this. And things, you know, people in this community will never in a million thought they would be doing.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Just a very difficult and sad state that we are in right now after such an incredibly tragic moment in that community.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:05]\par \par \uc2 Brian Lopez, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We are also following, everybody, some breaking news out of the nation`s capital. Police confirming that five people have been shot in northwest Washington, D.C. No word on their conditions. These are live pictures from the scene that you`re looking at right now. It is right at North Capital Street, northwest, and O Street, that`s right off New York Avenue. We`re going to continue to monitor the situation and bring you the very latest when we have it.\par \par \uc2 All right. Pretty soon we expect a major announcement from the White House on student loan debt forgiveness. The amount the White House is considering canceling and what it could mean for the midterms.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MSNBC. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right. Welcome back. Millions of Americans saddled with student loan debt could be getting some relief today. President Biden expected to make this formal announcement at the White House moments from now. The administration is going to cancel, it seems, up to $10,000 in student debt for those making less than $125,000 a year, and up to $20,000 for recipients of Pell Grants. The pause on student loan payments will also be extended until January.\par \par \uc2 For more on this, we want to bring in NBC`s Kelly O`Donnell at the White House for us and John Della Volpe, the polling director at the Harvard Kennedy School`s Institute of Politics and MSNBC political contributor.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:10]\par \par \uc2 Welcome to you both. Hey, Kelly, let me just start with you on this one, of course, and take us behind the scenes of the thinking of the timing of this announcement and what more we`re going to learn from the president when we do hear from him.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, there have been a lot of pressure building for an answer on what President Biden might do concerning student loans. There was a deadline coming up for the beginning of repaying loans, which have been on hold during the COVID period where there`s been a pause on repayment. So part of what you just mentioned is the package he will announce.\par \par \uc2 Well, that would be extended through the end of this year. They`re calling it a final extension to give people time to make plans to put that back in their budgets, to repay what they owe for January of the new year. So that extension will be part of what people can get accustomed to. And now this new piece based on income threshold and the type of loan that you may have in your student debt, this piece of loan forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 Now some advocates had wanted more. The president was under pressure from his own party on the liberal side of his party to do as much as $50,000. He did not go that far. But many are saying this is a positive step in the right direction and of course Republicans are viewing this quite differently and saying that this is giving a benefit that skews to more affluent Americans with higher levels of education, often graduate degrees and higher income earners, and is regressive to blue collar workers who will end up ultimately paying this as members of the tax-paying community.\par \par \uc2 So there is a lot of emotion when it comes to this, some who believe this is a very positive thing, which helps to relieve the debt burden on something that is so critical in our society to have education, to have economic advancement, and others who see this as unfair. So the timing is also playing into the midterm election of course where they want to try to have as many Democrats motivated, not only by issues like abortion, which has certainly been a game changer in the landscape of politics at a time when Democrats had been concerned about how they would energize their own base.\par \par \uc2 And this is one of those issues that has been a campaign promise of the president, saying he`s delivering on a part of that today. Yasmin?\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So, John, let`s talk about how both sides are reacting here, right. We got this tweet out from the president around 11:30 or so kind of teasing this announcement he`s going to be making about this student loan debt forgiveness. And then you have already this tweet out from Kevin McCarthy essentially calling it a, quote, "debt transfer scam." As a polling expert, how are you expecting voters to react to this?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DELLA VOLPE, POLLING DIRECTOR, \uc2 HARVARD KENNEDY SCHOOL\uc2  INSTITUTE OF POLITICS: Thanks for having me, Yasmin. I think the way voters are reacting is it`s less about policy and, in fact, I think it`s transcending policy and it`s a matter of young people, all voters remember specifically President Biden promising to relieve $10,000 of student debt. The promise was made, promise will soon be kept. And young people the last several months have been questioning whether government can actually work and do big things, whether President Biden was actually on their side.\par \par \uc2 They know the incredible impact they made in electing him. If not for young people in Arizona, Michigan, Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, Georgia, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is president today. We`re having this conversation because young people showed up and now they`re receiving I think a show of respect from the White House but also something that connects to what they`re looking for, which is just some sense of financial independence.\par \par \uc2 Millions of young people, I think 90 percent of this relief is going to young people who make less than $75,000 a year. This is going to be something that will affect this generation and potentially generations to come.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: And it really matters to young voters. I remember being outside of the Supreme Court after the decision was leaked with regard to Roe v. Wade and in the same sentence some women were talking to me about abortion rights were also talking about student loan forgiveness and how the president needs to live up to the promises he made in his campaign.\par \par \uc2 I want to play for you, John, something that my colleague Ellison Barber got outside of \uc2 NYU\uc2  I believe here in New York City, a reaction to this announcement that`s coming and then we`ll talk on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALISSA BIDWELL, GRADUATE STUDENT: Looking at a world where students might not have to live with that burden would open a lot of doors and possibilities for them in general. And so I definitely vote with that in mind and I definitely -- I know like looking at what Biden has promised to, you know, knock down some of our student debt is something that really encouraged me to vote for him because I know the impact that that can have on my life personally and of course hundreds of thousands of other students out there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So there`s an idea of living up to the promises that you made in the campaign, A, but, B, there`s also the importance of harnessing the power of a young voter and it seems as if this may help that.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:11]\par \par \uc2 DELLA VOLPE: Without question. Again, president Biden is not the president today without younger people. Democrats don`t hold the House or the Senate without young people in 2018. Gen Z has turned the page in terms of how we think about youth participation. 2018 and 2020 they voted in record numbers, far larger numbers than millennials, Gen Xers and baby boomers did at a similar period. And by understanding the tangible difference their participation can make, makes it much more likely they will continue to participate in politics and in civic, in civil society more generally.\par \par \uc2 I just think this is just a very, very good day both for Democrats but also for the country generally to see young people so willing to participate. And what they`re asking for is just what their parents and their grandparents had, an opportunity to pursue, you know, their dreams, pursue higher education without being in debt for decades. That`s what they`re asking for and now with some help that`s what they`re able to get.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Especially when you see the lack of wage increases as well in this country right now with inflation where it`s at. But that`s a whole other conversation.\par \par \uc2 John Della Volpe, thanks so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 That does it for this hour, everybody. I`m going to be back in the chair for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS tomorrow at 1:00 p.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC. You can also catch me on weekends, "YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN REPORTS" Saturdays and Sundays 2:00 to 4:00 p.m. Eastern also here on MSNBC. Katy Tur is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast with