be go have some black beans and rice in that restaurant because that is one of my favorite places to go in Florida. I`m a little bit jealous not of the heat, but of the food you`re about to have. Thank you for that, Shaq. So, Cornell, food aside and just looking at that sign I`m hungry, this is I think the critical question. If Democrats have some momentum going into the midterms, you still have to have the candidates to meet the moment, right? And there are skeptics who have said they don`t know whether they have a Democrat who can do that in Florida. How do you shake -- how do you think that Florida race is going to shape up?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Well, I think it`s going to shape about -- it`s going to shape about turnout. I mean, if you look at the last governor`s race in Florida, it was actually a really close race. But you also had in that race -- and Florida is an interesting place because all of the dynamics are changes -- demographic changes that you see happening all across the country, is really ground zero for that in Florida. We have such a diverse population, African Americans, Hispanics, Latino voters, Asian American voters.\par \par \uc2 But you also have, Chris, are older Americans who love to go to Florida and retire. So you also have an electorate that is also growing older sort of - - the sort of battleground between this older, less diverse electorate, and a younger more diverse electorate is all front and center there in Florida. And I think is ultimately it is, you know, what kind of electorate will you have in Florida? Will you have an electorate where Democrats can compete strongly with Hispanic Latino voters, as well as turnout younger voters and African American voters? JANSING: So, Steve, I want to go back to those protest signs. I`m looking at them right here. And three of the top responses suggest abortion restrictions remain a major factor for voters, right?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes. I mean, you could take a look here, this is the 