th Georgia Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 . Remind us how these could fit into the big picture.\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEONNIG, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Well, there are two big ways, Chris, that this is significant. One is \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , politically, is very much still aligned with former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and has viewed him as almost a shield for getting reelected and also for helping other Republicans and continuing Republican control of other Houses of Congress or getting control of them. He`s viewed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as a -- as an important part of that effort and campaign.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  has his own exposure here. He made calls to Republican officials and state officials in Georgia to ask about the same thing that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was seeking to press at the end of 2020. And that was, hey, isn`t the election result in Georgia in question? Isn`t it possible that there`s fraud here, as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said in one call to the Secretary of State, you know, just fine the X number of votes, which was the difference between him winning and losing?\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  made some calls on Donald Trump`s behalf. And he has to answer for what he was doing, what was his motive, what led him to make those calls. And ultimately, once he answered some of those questions where he has his own exposure, I no doubt believe he`ll be asked as well. What about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? What did you and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  discuss? What did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ask you to do? What was his motive? What was his goal? What was his hope? And what did he convey to you? And that`s really important for the Georgia investigation, determining motive, intent, goal, what was said behind the scenes, and \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  can unlock some of that secret.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Without a doubt. And Glenn, meantime, you`ve got a kind of an evolving series of explanations for why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would take classified documents to Mar-a-Lago. Let me play a few.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 DAN CRENSHAW\uc2 , (R-TX): I still haven`t seen any evidence that he was even asked -- that Trump was even asked to get these documents back. Do any of us really believe that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is like reading his nuclear secrets on his bedside at night?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE, CORRESPONDENT, CBS NEWS: What use could a former president have for classified or top secret information once he has left office? Why bring it home with him to Florida?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE TURNER\uc2 , (R-OH): Well, I don`t know. I mean, you have to ask him. But certainly, we all know that every former president has access to their document. That`s how they write their memoirs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Glenn, from a legal standpoint, does any of that ring true?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: No. And you know the time to make those arguments is when there`s a prosecution up and running, and the defense has its opportunity to argue to a jury, all of those I think it`s fair to characterize them as really weak arguments. The fact of the matter is, these records should not have been removed from the White House and whisked away to Donald Trump`s you know, Florida home. Even just taking them frankly, whether they were classified or not, violates any number of federal statutes so you know what, the time to make these arguments will be to a jury, and I suspect the jury will find them no more compelling than we find them when we listen to them.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Carol Leonnig, always great to have you on the program. Glenn, you`ll be back a little later on in the hour.\par \par \uc2 Political and legal battles across the country over abortion rights, one state fighting the DOJ in a lawsuit as another takes on its own biggest city over enforcement, we`ve got all that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BRE