 on all of this is Dr. Ebony Hilton, associate professor of anesthesiology and critical care medicine at the \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2 , and she is an MSNBC medical contributor.\par \par \uc2 Always good to see you, Doctor. Let`s talk about the kind of dent that 1.8 million doses could make in the effort to protect people against monkeypox.\par \par \uc2 DR. EBONY HILTON, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Right. You know, it`s unfortunate that we are in a situation where we have a great demand and a limited supply. We know over the last two weeks we`ve literally doubled our cases in the United States of America up to 14,000 at this point. And the question is, are those vaccines going to the most vulnerable persons? When you talk about L.A. County, we know that we now unfortunately have outbreaks within jail systems and within homeless shelters. Those populations are high risk.\par \par \uc2 But we also see across our nation, in New York City and in Georgia, for instance, we`re seeing that there is a lack of equity. We`re not seeing the most vulnerable communities getting the access to the vaccines first and when the most vulnerable are not targeted first for interventions, all of us will suffer. We have to take care of those that are most at risk.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, look, we`ve got this release coming, as New Yorkers are reaching that four-week time frame for their second dose with absolutely no word from the city on how soon they`ll be able to get the vaccine so they can make those appointments. What do you tell the patients who frankly are nervous because they`re waiting for their second dose and it is time?\par \par \uc2 HILTON: Right. You know, it`s going to be one of those moves where we have to make real life decisions in real time. Unfortunately we do have that shortage and we tried to make some adjustments by doing dose (INAUDIBLE), meaning they said that intramuscular injections or subcutaneous injection. We`re doing it right under the skin that can allow us to do a lower dose and have still that same efficacy. That only gets us so far, right.\par \par \uc2 We know that it takes weeks before we could have bottling of the vaccines made available for distribution within the United States. And so the majority of Americans don`t expect to be vaccinated before 2023. So what does that mean? That means we have to target first the most vulnerable communities, make sure those individuals get access to that vaccine now, and especially if they`ve had exposure to monkeypox at this point and that way we could mitigate the spread. It`s the same as that concept that we`re talking about with COVID-19 back in 2020.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There`s also news on the COVID front this morning, Pfizer-BioNTech asked the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to authorize its new COVID booster shot designed especially for the BA.5 Omicron subvariant for those who are eligible for another booster. Should they get it now? Should they wait for that new formula to be approved? What`s your best advice?\par \par \uc2 HILTON: Right. I think we need to follow the science and I would love to see the data on what are the outcomes now with the current population that we have, with COVID-19 exposures.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:02]\par \par \uc2 You know, the question is, not necessarily the technology that is going to be able to keep up. My question is will the population be welcoming of that. At this point only 33 -- 37 percent of Americans have received a booster shot. We`ve been stagnant at two doses at 67 percent in America for months now. What will we do moving forward? Will people buy into this idea that we are following science, and understand it is those interventions that keep people alive because we`re continuing to bury people from COVID- 19 today and that doesn`t have to be the case. We can get vaccinated, we can get boosted, and we can wear those masks to help prevent infection in the first place.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I know that there are people who have at least said to anecdotally that they`ve been waiting for this. They wanted to make sure that they were protected against the BA.5. Once that is available, would you recommend to them if they`ve been waiting for the booster go get it?\par \par \uc2 HILTON: I most certainly would. You know, we have to understand that until we stop this telephone game of viruses going from person to person to person, every time it goes to another person, it wants to make sure that it could stay alive. So what does it do? It slightly changes itself which is - - that`s where we get the variants. The more people we get vaccinated to interrupt that telephone game, the more likely we will be to getting closer to not having another variant that makes BA.4 and 5 looks like child`s play.\par \par \uc2 We know we get a surge every July. We know we get a higher surge every November. So getting those vaccines, getting those boosters before we head into fall will be what keeps us from burying 4,000 people a day like we have in the previous years before us. Right? So that`s what we need to start doing. And like I said, especially with schools opening back up, getting those children vaccinated, getting them boosted if they have not been boosted at this point, and really try to mitigate the spread again in 2022.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Dr. Ebony Hilton, thank you so much. Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Mounting fears of possible escalation from Russia in its war in Ukraine after a car bomb just outside of Moscow killed a top Putin ally`s daughter, Darya Dugina, Saturday. She was the daughter of Russian ultranationalist Alexander Dugin who is frequently referred to as Putin`s brain. Now Russia`s \uc2 FSB security agency\uc2  is pinning her death on Ukraine`s Secret Service. Kyiv already dismissing that claim calling it propaganda.\par \par \uc2 A viral video shows three officers violently beating a suspect under arrest. The new information about the investigation now underway in Arkansas. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:22]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Three officers are off the job after a brutal video appears to show those officers repeatedly punching and kicking a man during an arrest in Arkansas on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 A warning, some viewers may find this video disturbing. Take a look. It`s video that went viral and the state is now investigating.\par \par \uc2 Crawford County Sheriff Jimmy Demonte told reporters just moments ago the incident is being investigated as a criminal act and has been referred to the Arkansas state police. He also added none of the officers were wearing body cameras. Governor Asa Hutchinson also has a press conference to talk about this incident within the next hour.\par \par \uc2 Here`s more from NBC News correspondent Blayne Alexander.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): It is the shocking video that has been viewed millions of times. This morning Arkansas state police are investigating a violence arrest that was caught on camera.\par \par \uc2 According to state police, in the video are two Crawford County sheriff`s deputies and a Mulberry police officer. The video appears to show them pinning a man down during an altercation and repeatedly punching and kneeing him to the head and body. And at one point one of the officers appears to slam the man`s head into the ground multiple times. The incident took place Sunday morning outside of a convenience store. At the end of the video one of the officers appears to point at the camera.\par \par \uc2 Throughout the day police had been on the lookout for the suspect, identified as 27-year-old Randal Worcester of South Carolina after he allegedly spit on a store clerk and made what officers called terroristic threats in a nearby town. While it is unclear what happened the start of the video, Worcester was quickly transported to an area hospital and was later released and taken into custody. His condition is unclear.\par \par \uc2 Worcester is now facing a slew of charges, including battery, resisting arrest and refusal to submit. All three officers involved have been suspended. In a statement the Crawford County sheriff says, in part, "I hold all my employees accountable for their actions and will take appropriate measures in this matter." A statement from Mulberry Police says the department will take the appropriate actions at the conclusion of the investigation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That was NBC`s Blayne Alexander. With me now is NBC News-MSNBC legal analyst Glenn Kirschner.\par \par \uc2 Glenn, look, we don`t know, as Blayne said, we don`t know what happened leading up to this but there were three officers, one man on the ground. What goes through your mind in a legal sense as you`re watching that video?\par \par \uc2 GLANN KIRSCHNER, NBC NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: What goes through my mind, Chris, is this appears to be horrific, excessive force. And yes, it would be wonderful if we had sort of, you know, wall-to-wall video footage, coverage, courtesy of body cameras, surveillance cameras or concerned citizens pulling out their cell phone and recording what was going on there, but you know what, there are some things that speak for themselves. And this video sure seems to speak for itself on the excessive force front.\par \par \uc2 The next question that I have is, you know, not only what will the local authorities do, the Arkansas state police reportedly will be investigating, but will the Department of Justice continue to sort of reach out on the civil rights front and engage in the kind of aggressive investigations of civil right abuses that we saw them engage in when police officers murdered George Floyd, when civilians murdered Ahmaud Arbery?\par \par \uc2 You know, this is another incidence where I think the Department of Justice will have to decide whether there is something here that they need to weigh in on.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:05]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, at least there`s a start, right, because we did as I just said, we just heard from the Crawford County Sheriff Jimmy Demonte, who said just moments ago this is being investigated as a criminal act and, as you pointed out, has been referred to the state police.\par \par \uc2 No body cam video. I don`t know what the law is in that state. I don`t know whether or not they were supposed to have body cameras on, but again, you have somebody there who clearly showed what was going on. You saw that one of the officers was pointing to the camera. What are the first things that the state police will do in terms of an investigation?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Well, the first thing they have to do, Chris, is make sure that whatever team they put in charge of this investigation is completely conflict-free because a police department when they are investigating their own regarding potential excessive force incidents, you know, they generally can`t -- I don`t want to say they can`t be trusted, but that is not the kind of investigation that instills public confidence.\par \par \uc2 And, again, I don`t think the federal government or the Department of Justice is necessarily the cure-all all the time, but I have been heartened that Merrick Garland`s DOJ has opened multiple pattern or practice investigations of police departments in Phoenix, in Louisville, in Minneapolis when there seem to be a systemic problem. And maybe that is also true of Crawford County, Arkansas, maybe not, but hopefully there will be a full, fair, robust conflict-free investigation of this incident.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Glenn Kirschner, thank you so much. Appreciate you sticking around to talk about this.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. Don`t go anywhere. "KATY TUR REPORTS" starts next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS