of the Democratic side attributed at least in part to anger over the Supreme Court`s reversal of Roe v. Wade, also factoring in to the midterm picture, the web of investigation that appears to be tightening around former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Nearly three and five Americans pulled by NBC News saying that those investigations into Trump`s alleged wrongdoing should continue after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid of his Florida estate. And the revelation that it was related to possible espionage violations. Of course, all of this new data is about to be field tested in a pair of state primaries Tuesday. One in dark blue New York, one in deep red Florida, whose base comes out more energized ahead of November. We will be watching. I`ll ask not one but two statewide candidates in Trump racist tonight.\par \par \uc2 But, first, as always, the perfect guest, joining me now is \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 , Chair of the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 . Mr. Harrison, thank you for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 And I want to start off with the new NBC poll data showing that Democrats and Republicans are essentially tied over voter enthusiasm. Now, as the DNC chair, I would imagine you are pleased to see that, yet despite the near match on enthusiasm, President Biden`s approval ratings remain low at 47 percent of registered voters want Republicans in control of Congress, versus 45 percent who won Democrats in charge, according to the same NBC poll.\par \par \uc2 Now, that`s despite congressional Republicans going against lower cost for prescription drugs. It gets the federal right to birth control and continuing to back former sitting president who`s proximate to several state and federal investigations. Your response, Mr. Chairman?\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, DEMOCRATIC NATIONAL COMMITTEE: Rev, thank you for having me. Listen, I have said this once, I have said this twice, I`m going to say it a thousand times more between now and November. Never bet against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Democrats when it comes to delivering for the American people.\par \par \uc2 Now, regardless of with the polls say, when you take a look at with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and Democrats have been able to do in these last two years, and you put in the historic perspective, it`s nothing short of amazing. It`s about delivering for the American people.\par \par \uc2 Think about what they have accomplished so far, and this will be the message that we put out there across the country. He started off with the American Rescue Plan, which really saved so many businesses, historically black colleges, so many institutions and cities.\par \par \uc2 It started with the bipartisan infrastructure law. Even with that, we had 200 Republicans in the House who voted no. We just recently got passed this Inflation Reduction Act. Not one Republican voted for it in either in the House and Senate, and this bill is a game-changer in terms of the impact that it will have on the climate, the impact that it will have on prescription drugs, allowing Medicare for the first time to negotiate the price of prescription drugs. That is such an important thing for so many seniors across the country.\par \par \uc2 When you think about the violence against women act, you think about putting the first black woman on the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 , the number of black judges he has appointed.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:07]\par \par \uc2 I can go on and on and on again, but \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has delivered for the American people, while the Republicans have been extreme.\par \par \uc2 These Republicans, Rev, they want power so desperately that they would vote against their mommas on Mother`s Day if that meant that they could get power. We cannot allow them to have that. We can`t allow that for the good of the American people to let these extreme people be in power.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: What do you think, Mr. Chairman, is the reason that there is not even more enthusiasm or even a change in the polling? Do you think it`s a problem of messaging? Because I get it on my syndicated radio show out daily, a lot of blacks, why should we go, what are we getting from the Democrats? Is it a messaging problem, since you`ve been able to run a litany of accomplishments that has happened in less than two years?\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Well, I also think people are still dealing with the lingering impact of COVID as well, Rev. In my household, we still have one of our kids who is still not fully vaccinated. And so that has prevented us from doing certain things. We are waiting for September, because he will get his last shot. And that would open up a whole door for us to do more things and to get back out there. And I think many families are still dealing with that.\par \par \uc2 But what they`ve seen, Rev, is that they have a president who is fighting for them. And that`s why you`re starting to see these numbers. I`ve heard all of the pundits say for the past year or so, you know in midterms Democrats don`t -- the party in the White House will lose an election on this and that. And we are now starting to see big people big -- American people are starting to wake up.\par \par \uc2 And they see when you have over 200 Republicans vote against the Pact Act for veterans. Think about that. a bill to help veterans who were impacted by these chemicals in some of these pits. And yet you`ve got a Republican Party voting against that, you`ve got a Republican Party voting against gun safety bills, Violence Against Women Act. They see the extremism and they`re starting to come back home.\par \par \uc2 And so the momentum is on the Democrats` side. We`re seeing the president numbers creep up as well. And that is what we want to see at this point as people start to wake up and realize what`s going on. And then the Democratic Party, the DNC, just this week, Rev, put $7 million -- over millions of dollars, let me you put it that, on T.V., on radio, in print, to make sure that the message is getting out of the American people about how \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats have delivered for them in the Republican tent.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Now, we still have a handful of nominating contests before November, dueling primaries in two states on Tuesday, New York and Florida. And what are your expectations and how will you gauge these two that are left and moving forward beyond Tuesday?\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Well, we will continue to look at the trends. We saw what happened in Kansas. That is not a traditional Democratic state. But, overwhelmingly, women and men, Republicans and Democrats stood up and said, we are tired of the extremism of the Republican Party as it relates to the issue of abortion and women`s right to control their own bodies. This is about taking away the freedom of women in this country. For the first time in 50 years, we are retracting our freedoms in America. That`s unheard of. But it`s led by the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re going to continue to look at those trends, learn from that, learn from what the right language is, to make sure that we put that language in our messages and our outreach efforts. But I feel really, really good about where we are right now, Rev. We`ve got some amazing Senate candidates, while \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  was talking about the duds that he has in his party right now. And we see the same type of duds on the Republican side in their House candidates.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to continue to put our message out for the great candidates that we have, and in the end, I think we`re going to buck history just like we did in 2020.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, let me ask you this finally. We`re out of time, Mr. Chairman, but President Biden and the congressional Democrats have strung together some big wins in recent weeks, big wins. Perhaps the most visible being the passage and signing of the Inflation Reduction Act, but some strategists have cast -- has given the party something to run on as the primary season gives way to the general election campaign, that and, of course, Donald Trump`s escalating legal woes.\par \par \uc2 But Trump aside, what are the Democrats` biggest selling points with less than three months to go? You`ve always shot straight with me, even before you were chairman. What are the selling points? Because, frankly, as one who has our always held everybody accountable, think that Biden/Harris is doing a lot more than I expected.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:02]\par \par \uc2 What is the selling point that you`re going to give to the public?\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: The selling point is this, Rev. With a 50/50 Senate and sometimes less than a five-seat majority in the House, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats have delivered one of the strongest economies we have seen in American history. They have delivered real climate change, historic, no other country in the world has done what we just did in the Inflation Reduction Act to bring down emissions. We have seen record unemployment. We have seen record job creation. We have helped make sure that American jobs are still there.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line is this. Democrats have delivered for the American people, and there`s so much more that we can do, and that`s why we need a few more votes in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  and we need to keep the House and we need to make sure that we pick up statehouses and governors` mansions and send back some of these folks who don`t believe in one person, one vote, on the Republican side. Let`s win these attorney generals and secretaries of state as well. If we do that, America will continue to progress. Otherwise, we give Jim Jordan and Kevin McCarthy and a bunch of the clown car on the Republican side an opportunity to really put this country in a ditch. We cannot allow that to happen.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, there`s a lot at stake. Thank you, Chairman \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2  of the DNC.\par \par \uc2 Now, from a national point of view to a local one, joining me now is Charles Booker, Democratic candidate for Senator of Kentucky. Thank you for coming on today, Mr. Booker, and for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Now, you`ve recently made history as the first black nominee in a federal election in the state of Kentucky. As you know, the state`s Democratic candidate for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , running against two-term incumbent Republican Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 , of course, you sought the challenge, Kentucky`s other senator, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , for his seat in 2020, but could not secure the nomination. Now you have the nomination. And you have been a state legislator. You`ve been one of the main advocates of some cases, like Breonna Taylor. Talk about the effort to break the stranglehold Republicans seem to have on Kentucky`s Senate seats.\par \par \uc2 FMR. STATE REP. CHARLES BOOKER (D-KY): Shoo, Rand, there is a fly in here. But, Reverend, it`s good to be with you today. And you`re exactly right. We are defying the odds right now. We`re showing that change is possible, even in a place where we are often counted out. And the stranglehold has really been ignoring regular people. I`m building a coalition of Kentucky`s from the hoods to the holler, every walk of life, because we`re speaking about issues. We are going beyond the political division and talking about the challenges we face and show the audacity to call \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  out. And because of that, we are making history, and we are bending the arc, and we`re going to win this race.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Kentucky`s Democratic governor, \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , announced last months that he plans to back your run for the Senate. You have been endorsed by prominent Senate progressives, like \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , Elizabeth Warren, \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2 . But even as you make and pursue history, you`ve said that racist experiences inspired your Senate run. Can you elaborate on that? What would it mean to you and for the state of Kentucky to become its first black Senate?\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Well, first of all, I stand in this moment understanding the importance of shining the light and breaking barriers, and making it clear that as a Kentuckian, my family in goes back several generations, some of whom were enslaved in the commonwealth, some of whom were lynched in the commonwealth of Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 And so to be here now talking about how we end poverty, how we make sure everyone has quality health care, how we support our veterans, how we take care of our loved ones and make sure we have clean air and clean water and repair our infrastructure, this is a huge moment. And it`s a reminder to so many who have been crying out for justice that change is still possible, and we have to meet this moment together.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Mr. Booker, earlier this month, the Department of Justice charged four former and current Louisville Metro Police Department members with violating Breonna Taylor`s civil rights in the 2020 police raid that resulted in her killing. I`ve heard you say that Taylor was a good friend of your family, and that she was actually at the funeral for a cousin of yours who was tragically murdered in 2016. And you described attending Taylor`s funeral back in 2020. You`ve even visited me at National Action Network and talked about issues of civil rights. So, you have been involved. Can you talk to me about with that meant to you, especially during a time where you are also running for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 [17:15:03]\par \par \uc2 And what is your reaction to these latest charges against the officers involved in the killing?\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Reverend, so many people across Kentucky and across this country are tired. To call out for justice, to cry out for our humanity, it`s exhausting. And there are times where you feel like change is not going to come. But we are reminded that it`s still possible, the announcement of the arrests of officers finally at the federal level, because our attorney general failed us. It was a reminder that justice and accountability could still be met big.\par \par \uc2 And even today, I`m heartbroken just thinking about all the pain that so many -- not just Kentuckians but people across the country have gone through in this cry for justice. I learned that a good friend, an organizer in the movement every way, Chris Wells lost his life. And in this moment of time, I`m hoping that this campaign can be a testament to the love and hope and potential of us not just meeting this moment but continuing to fight for the justice and healing.\par \par \uc2 And so I`m encouraged by the progress and accountability but there is so much more work to be done here in Kentucky and across the country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Briefly, since we are out of time, Eastern Kentucky has been ravaged by the severe flooding in recent weeks with more than 1,000 people rescued after torrential rains tore through the state last month. Rescue teams in boats searching through creeks clogged with rubble, work crews trying to restore power and water to homes, residents desperate to repair those homes and lives after the flooding. And, of course, you have the president and first lady visiting the state after the disaster.\par \par \uc2 Can you speak to the devastation that many residents in Eastern Kentucky are still experiencing a month later?\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Reverend, the devastation is really hard to put to words. Places that I`ve worked through the years, places that I`ve seen thriving have been completely washed away, churches gone, businesses gone. And in this moment of pain, just like when the tornado hit and Kentucky, we`ve shown up as family. We are organizing together, not about party but about humanity. And the one person who has not been there is \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  and the people of Kentucky see it.\par \par \uc2 Look, our infrastructure is crumbling. So, we understand why President Biden is showing leadership on the infrastructure act is critical. We need investments in our home and in our land so that we can have a thriving economy, but we have a lot of work ahead. And I`m sending on my prayers to Eastern Kentucky, everyone join us and support, go to charlesbooker.org/crisisresponse. Help us organize and build a way forward.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Charles Booker, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 After the break, basketball steps his game up in the name of democracy.\par \par \uc2 And later, how Texas` impending trigger law on abortion access could have consequences beyond its own borders.\par \par \uc2 But, first, my colleague, Richard Lui, with today`s other top news stories. Richard?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC HOST: Rev, a good Sunday to you. Some stories we are following for you, car bombing killing the daughter of a prominent Putin ally in Moscow. Russia`s investigative committee has said they are looking into the incident and have opened a criminal murder case. An adviser of Ukraine`s president denied any Ukrainian involvement.\par \par \uc2 First Lady Jill Biden no longer in quarantine as of today. She just received two negative COVID test results. A spokesperson says she will join President Biden in Delaware where he is vacationing.\par \par \uc2 And Wendy`s says they are pulling lettuce from sandwiches amid an E. Coli outbreak. Friday, the CDC was trying to see if romaine lettuce was the cause. They believe at least 37 people across four states are affected with ten hospitalized.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:20:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: There are two and a half more months until the November 8th midterm election, and it is time for all of us to rise up and plan our votes. This year, the \uc2 National Basketball Association\uc2  is doing its part. They say they`ve decided not to play any games on Election Day, so their employees and fans can spend the day focused on democracy rather than basketball.\par \par \uc2 I applaud the \uc2 NBA\uc2  for making this move, though I am seriously holding them accountable on other moves I want to see them make, and I hope that other corporations will follow suit. But it should not be up to private enterprises to defend our sacred right to vote. It`s time for the government to make Election Day a holiday.\par \par \uc2 There shouldn`t be anything partisan about making it easier for anyone to go to vote. And right now, many Americans have to make difficult decisions about work and childcare just to get to the polls. And the situation becomes even more complicated in areas where the number of polling places have been reduced, resulting in long lines and wait times.\par \par \uc2 Making Election Day a national holiday should be something both sides can agree on. I personally see no problem with mail-in and absentee voting. Many Trump supporters have claimed they are more comfortable with the idea of in-person voting, and a national holiday would make that easier. So, why not support it?\par \par \uc2 Perhaps if more of us had free time on our hands when we went to the polls, we might take a few minutes to linger after we cast our votes in, get to know some of our fellow Americans who are also rising up to participate in our democracy. We might find out that even if we don`t all fill out our ballots the same way, we all share similar values of faith and family and hope for more peaceful and prosperous future.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:08]\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. Let`s discuss all of today`s big topics with my political panel.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Adrienne Elrod, former senior aide for the Biden/Harris campaign, and \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , former Republican representative of Florida.\par \par \uc2 Now, Carlos a new poll, a new NBC poll out today, showing the Democrats have been closing the enthusiasm gap with Republicans since earlier this year, 66 percent to 68 percent respectively. That two point GOP is down from a whopping 17 points in March and 8 points in May.\par \par \uc2 [17:30:02]\par \par \uc2 Now, this comes as threats to democracy tops the list of the most important issues heading into the midterms. Carlos, what is be on the decline in enthusiasm among Republican voters?\par \par \uc2 FORMER REP. CARLOS CURBELO, (R) FLORIDA: Rev., I think there`s two big factors. Number one, Democrats are on a winning streak. They`ve gotten some big things passed through Congress. The Biden administration has had some wins overseas. So there`s a renewed energy in the Democratic Party. I think the Democratic base has a renewed faith in their leaders.\par \par \uc2 On the Republican side, who has come to the forefront? \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  again. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sure motivates some Republican voters. But we also know that he motivates the Democratic base. So the more headlines we get having to do with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , with everything he did in 2020, with a big lie, the more energized these Democrats get and the harder it`s going to be for Republicans to have those big wins in Congress and a lot of people have been prognosticating for some time now.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Adrienne, on Tuesday, voters in New York and Florida will cast their ballots in critical primary elections. One of the most closely watched races is in New York`s 12 district, hitting too long serving Congress members of nearly 30 years respectively, against each other due to their districts being redrawn and partially combined. According to a new poll from Helen Emerson College, Congressman \uc2 Jerry Nadler\uc2  is leading his closest challenger, Congresswoman \uc2 Carolyn Maloney\uc2  by 22 points ahead of the Democratic primary. What makes this race so significant?\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE ELROD, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, Rev., it`s significant because you have two longtime entrenched Democratic incumbents running against each other, both of whom serve as current committee chairs in Congress. This is something that New York does not see very often. In fact, you don`t see this happening across the country very often. And, you know, these are two people who have progressive records, who had been part of the Progressive Caucus who have been longtime allies. So it really is challenging and, you know, there`s been, Rev, I know you`ve covered this some too. There`s been some controversy about the maps in New York and sort of how redistricting played out.\par \par \uc2 And it is unfortunate that the Democrats will lose one of these two, because, again, Congressman Nadler and Congresswoman Maloney have both been longtime, huge Democrats, both allies and Speaker Pelosi, you know, it does look like Nadler has the edge. But you also know it`s August in New York, primaries are -- the turnout can be very strange. So it`s going to be upon on both of them to figure out how they can get their base to turnout. And I think that latch will likely be closer than it looks in the polling.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yeah, it`s definitely about who gets people out. And you saying there`s been a lot of conversation about the maps being drawn might be an understatement.\par \par \uc2 But let me stay with you a minute, Adrienne, next week, President Biden will be in Pennsylvania to give remarks on his Safe for America Plan to help reduce gun violence and save lives. Then in the coming weeks, the Biden administration will host a summit, dubbed, United We Stand countering, "the corrosive effects of hate-fueled violence on our democracy and public safety." According to the White House on Friday this was their quote. This comes after the heinous attack in Buffalo where 10 black people were killed in mass shooting at a supermarket, as well as hate motivated crimes in Pittsburgh, El Paso, Atlanta, and a number of U.S. cities that got some of us in the Civil Rights communities of all communities to come together and acts for this summit. Right now we`re seeing a rise and fall right violence threats. What responsibility, Adrienne, does Republicans have to tamp down their rhetoric to lower the temperature?\par \par \uc2 ELROD: Oh, Rev., they have all the responsibility in the world. And I want to applaud you for all the work that you`ve done and continue to do in this space. And I`m glad that the Biden administration is focusing on this, you know, they put a lot of their muscle behind getting the Inflation Reduction Act passed. Of course, they passed four major economic bills in just a year and a half in office. But now they`re pivoting. They`re not wasting any time with a full Democratic House and Senate to try to get some of this other legislation passed and to also focus on the issues that have maybe taken a backseat to some of these major economic policies that just passed.\par \par \uc2 So I think it`s smart that the administration is doing this. I think it`s incumbent upon President Biden and his allies in the White House, its colleagues, that they draw the contrast with the Republicans. So the point that you just made, it is not the Democrats that are using divisive rhetoric to try to divide our country. It is coming from the MAGA wing of the Republican Party. So not only does the President need to go out there and, you know, show leadership which he`s done before but really in this huge magnanimous way in September when he holds this summit, but it`s also important that they draw the contrast with the lack of leadership from the Republican side. And of course, the fact that the Republican Party MAGA wing in particular has been using this divisive rhetoric to try to divide our country because they know, Rev., it`s the only way they`re going to try to get any more votes by, you know, rubbing up that -- no pun intended by rubbing up that right wing base of the Republican Party, which unfortunately is oftentimes energized by this kind of divisive, hateful rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:38]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Carlos, Texas trigger law, banning nearly all abortions in Texas after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 `s reversal of Roe takes effect in less than four days, the law enhances criminal penalties for medical professionals attempting to perform abortions, making the attempt itself a second degree felony, that`s up to 20 years in prison for successful abortion. That becomes a first degree felony with the possibility of life in prison. Texas already has one of the most restrictive abortion laws in the country, banning the procedure up to six weeks. What are you hearing from people in the state you`re in, Florida, about the future of abortion rights there?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Well, Rev., there`s certainly some concern that we can see some of these policies here because, you know, all these Republican governors and legislatures are always trying to outdo one another. But I do think that Kansas has become a cautionary tale for a lot of Republican leaders throughout the country. They`re starting to understand that most Americans just don`t support these policies. And it`s not just women. You look at the polling. It`s not just Democrats, either. There are a lot of independents and a more moderate Republicans that reject these policies. And we certainly saw that in Kansas. So Republicans, I think will be more cautious. There is no talk here in Florida, of making the laws more restrictive, because I think Republicans understand that there could be some serious political peril. And Republicans at the national level are already seeing that in polling and in different election results. So we`ll see. I mean, this I think in Texas, there`s going to be a backlash, and I think a lot of Republican governors and legislatures are going to start taking a couple steps backwards on this issue.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Adrienne Elrod and \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, with the attacks on the McCain family and calls to decertify presidential election, Arizona`s race for governor is looking a lot like 2020 but my next guest has a plan to move forward. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:41:49]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to Politics Nation. Arizona is shaping up to be one of the most contested and watched election in the midterms and at the top of the governor`s race, the Republican nominee Kari Lake is a former Phoenix news reporter and former Democrat who now denies the 2020 presidential election results and has extremely close ties with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And on the Democratic side, Arizona Secretary of State \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , who oversaw the presidential election voting in 2020, and adamantly defends the fair election process that took place. This governor`s race has come to signify more than just Arizona politics, holding a light to Trump stronghold or lack thereof on swing states across the country.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Arizona Secretary of State and Democratic nominee for Governor \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 . Secretary Hobbs, welcome to Politics Nation. And I want to start with a statement, your campaign came on Thursday saying you want changes to the format of the upcoming governor`s debate featuring you and your opponent Kari Lake, and that you will, "not engage in a circus that insults and embarrasses Arizonans." You also refused to take part in the Democratic primary debate this past June. Moments ago, Kari Lake tweeted out a video saying if you agreed to debate her, you could pick the debate moderator, time, and place. What are the specific format changes you ever requested for the scheduled of October debate? And if you don`t get them, do you plan to skip out?\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC CANDIDATE FOR GOVERNOR OF ARIZONA: Well, we`re in the middle of negotiating this situation with the debate host and so I`d rather not kind of litigate that in public. But I will say that the Republican primary debates made Arizona a national laughingstock because Kari Lake is unable to be in front of a reporter and a microphone without making herself a spectacle. And that is embarrassing to the voters of Arizona and it doesn`t allow them to see a substantive discussion about the issues and where we stand on them and how we would govern. And that is not doing a service at all to Arizona voters.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Republican Kari Lake has been accused of hypocrisy and a drastic shift in a political ideology since she decided to run for Arizona governor. The family of late Arizona Senator \uc2 John McCain\uc2  once considered her close friend has now said they are confused where this version of Lake has come from. Lake says if she was governor during the 2020 election, she would have refused to certify the presidential election results. She says if elected governor she will establish a "declaration of invasion" at the Arizona border. I mean, what do you think is behind this seismic shift in Lakes` ideology? Is it all to get in the good graces of former President Trump? Or is there more to it than that?\par \par \uc2 [17:45:17]\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Well, I can`t comment on her mentality because I don`t understand that either. But what I can say is this, that since she`s gotten into this race for governor, she`s done nothing but divide Arizonans. Now, that she`s the nominee, she`s done nothing to try to unite Arizonans behind her, running away, as soon as she got the nomination to the CPAC conference where she mimicked stabbing McCain in the hardest hit about driving a stake through the McCain machine. And she`s offering extreme positions on every single issue that she thinks matters to her Republican base as a distraction from the fact that she`s not offering any real solutions. She`s not talking about issues in a serious way, and how she`ll tackle them as governor.\par \par \uc2 I`ve spent my campaign across the state talking to Arizonans about their real challenges we`re facing from skyrocketing costs to an affordability to our water crisis, fixing education and protecting reproductive health care. And we`ve offered real plans and solutions for all of these things. We`re talking about how we can bring Arizonans together to solve these problems. And she`s not offering any of that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, as the Arizona Secretary of State, you were the chief election officer during the 2020 presidential race. After defending the Arizona election results against false claims by Trump and his supporters, you received multiple death threats, even needing to receive protection from the State Department of Public Safety. And it wasn`t just you also receiving threats were all the five supervisors of Maricopa County, where Trump filed an election fraud lawsuit that was later dismissed, and four of those supervisors receiving threats were Republicans. I mean, what does this conduct say about this current political climate and the threat of violence toward those that stand up to a false election, in false election claims?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Well, it is honestly one of the most daunting things about being in politics today. And I think is, having a chilling effect, not just people who might want to run for office and lead but also just people stepping into the line of election work. There`s a lot of non-elected election officials that make sure the wheels keep turning and that elections work the way they`re supposed to. And we`re losing a cadre of qualified workers in that field.\par \par \uc2 This is dangerous, it is dangerous to our democracy. We have elected leaders and other politicians who are continuing to incite the false allegation or cite the false allegations, inciting these threats against people like myself and other election workers. It is not OK, and they need to be held accountable. And it`s what we`re up against in this election. We need to elect leaders who are going to stand up for the truth and tackle the real issues we`re facing, that continue to litigate an election that happened two years ago, and spread these false allegations. And I need folks to join me in that at Katiehobbs.org.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Time for one last question. The Phoenix area has been a 35% rise in homelessness according to a March report by the Maricopa Association of Governments, you said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that your opponent Kari Lake is clueless when it comes to tackling issues like this. She has proposed a ban on urban camping and has alluded to penalizing homeless people saying her plan will get people off the streets one way or another, I`m quoting. How do you plan to tackle the complex and systemic issue of homelessness in Arizona if you`re elected governor?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: I`m a social worker. And my first job out of college was working with homeless youth. I`m -- I`ve been intimately involved with this issue. And what I can say is that it is very complex. And you can`t just arrest people out of homelessness. There are many, many factors that lead to people being in these situations. And we have to tackle them from a systemic perspective. Her plans show -- and I hate to even call it a plan, just show that she has no real understanding of how to tackle these kinds of complex problems. And first of all, we don`t have enough services to provide for these people. So you can say you have to take services that`s part of what she`s proposing, but there aren`t enough services available and there`s not enough housing. And the state needs to be at better partner in providing resources for our local jurisdictions to tackle these problems and provide support and services for these people who are out on the street right now every day and plus 100 degree temperatures. It is devastating to drive down near the campus and see all these people out there on the street.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:24]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Secretary \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , thank you for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my final thoughts. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: This Tuesday`s primaries in New York and Florida, will just about bring the end of the primary season and we go into the real drive for the general election in November after Labor Day. But I want to be clear, this general election will not be Republican versus Democrat in my opinion, in my opinion it will be autocracy versus democracy. Because if some people went around this country, they have decided that they will put into a process that will become law, that they can decide elections that have nothing to do what happens at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:24]\par \par \uc2 People need to vote this year, like they never voted before. Because we are at risk to mobs controlling how we determine the elections. We cannot in the light of what happened January 6, turn this country process over to mob control. Because don`t forget, they were there to stop the certification of an election. Many of us that never had the right to vote too recently. Don`t forget, blacks just started getting the protection of voting rights in 65, women just got the right to vote. People 18 just got the right to vote. How dare you sit back talking about you didn`t get everything you want. We`d have the right to vote, use it. We have a lot to protect. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 5 p.m. Eastern. "AMERICAN VOICES" with Alicia Menendez, my friend starts right now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copy