oining us tonight. Thanks for your reporting, Allison Donahue.\par \par \uc2 DONAHUE: Yes. Thanks so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right. Coming up -- how Democrats plan to talk to voters about the economy this fall. Jared Bernstein, a member of the \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2 , joins me next.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:56]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Here is a truly shocking headline. Inflation over 10 percent in the United Kingdom. It`s a sort of headline that messes things up for Joe Biden`s critics who feel like he is kind of to blame for all the ills, but it`s hard to blame Biden for the record breaking inflation in the U.K. or in Turkey where it`s over 75 percent, or Argentina at about 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 Are \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his policies to blame for that to? Of course not. Inflation in the U.S. is still high. It`s over 8 percent, but it`s trending down in large part due to global factors.\par \par \uc2 Supply chain issues caused by the pandemic, Putin`s invasion of Ukraine and its impact on world oil and gas prices. Gas prices now averaging $3.91 a gallon according to AAA with many states in the south significantly lower than that.\par \par \uc2 And remember, during the presidential campaign, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  used to taunt voters to check their 401(k)s before they voted.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:58]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Well, the Dow is currently 33000, a number far higher than it ever was during the Trump administration, the unemployment rate is as low as it`s been in recent history, 3.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 A recent Gallup poll shows voters understand all of this. 72 percent of people say it`s a good time to find a quality job. That`s the highest that number`s been in 20 years.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Jared Bernstein, a member of the \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2 . Jared, good to see you. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 I`m kind of -- you know, this act, this big bill that`s passed, the Inflation Reduction Act. I know that there is going to be a big effort by you folks to go out there and tell people about how it reduces inflation, or fixes things in the economy. But you`re dealing with a broken start.\par \par \uc2 You`re dealing with a whole bunch of critics blaming \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for things that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  didn`t do.\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, WHITE HOUSE COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Yes, it`s very important to bring in the global perspective, as you just did there. Inflation is a global phenomenon and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not president of the globe. If you ask yourself well what did happen in every country over the past couple of years, and of course, the answer is COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 And as you`ve mentioned, it wrecked havoc on supply chains across the globe. Especially during the period where we were importing a lot of manufactured goods, that helped to contribute to the inflation problem. And it is still elevated today.\par \par \uc2 But as you suggested, the month of July, inflation posted a zero, 8.5 percent year over year, down from north of 9 in June. So that`s a movement in the right direction, but it is still unacceptably high inflation.\par \par \uc2 So I think the issue there is what is our administration doing under the president`s leadership to try to ease prices? And there you have the results at the pump which he talked about, very much related to his release of oil from the strategic reserves.\par \par \uc2 But even that unsnarling of the supply chain relates to many of our efforts in the ports trying to get goods from ship to shelf more smoothly. We will certainly be trying to help people understand the efforts we have been undergoing to help reduce price pressures.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: In all the stuff that is happened and all the enumeration of the bills that have been passed, and even some bipartisan work that has been done, the one that`s almost most interesting to me and probably the least newsworthy to a whole lot of people is semiconductors.\par \par \uc2 We have a serious semiconductor problem in this country. We have a bill that is now going to try and alleviate that. Do you even bother trying to tell that to people? Like is that the kind of stuff that you guys are going to go out and talk about?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Well, definitely. Every accomplishment that this president has overseen is fodder for trying to explain how he`s made and kept the promises that brought him here. I think that one way to think about this, Ali, which I think might resonate with you. We`ve been talking about this kind of economics for a long time, you and I. Is if you think about the infrastructure plan, it`s investments in public goods. If you think about the Inflation Reduction Act, it`s investments in clean energy. And the Chips Act, it standing up domestic semiconductor industry here on our shores.\par \par \uc2 What you really have there is a triumvirate to reverse the disinvestment in America that has occurred for decades on end. Disinvestment in public goods, disinvestment in resilient supply chains, certainly disinvestment or not enough investment in clean energy.\par \par \uc2 So not only will we boost the general public goods -- the roads, the bridges, the ports, the broadband systems. That`s the infrastructure bill that you like so much, as do I.\par \par \uc2 But also, we will crowd in private investment by signaling that in the area of clean energy, in the area of semiconductors, this is a great time to invest in America for a strong return on investment. And especially with semiconductors, we are already seeing that. Numerous companies are telling us they`re going to take advantage.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And that`s where people used cars, and new cars are going to become cheaper. I have one last question for you. What do you say to people that say you all caused some of this inflation stuff by giving everybody money when you got into office?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yes I think when you look at some of the supply concerns we mentioned earlier, that`s very much related to the inflation. Look, I think that fiscal policy and monetary policy, what the fiscal authorities and the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  were doing was very much trying to fight off the deepest recession that we`ve seen since the Great Depression.\par \par \uc2 And this was a real success under the American Rescue Plan. You know, we haven`t had a chance to talk about this today, but we learned today that there are now 22 states with record low unemployment rates below 3 percent. That is a record, going back to when those data started in the mid 70s.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:48]\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: That relates directly to shots in arms and checks in pockets that this president did when we got there. So did we help to create more demand? Absolutely. Did that demand help to build the strongest labor market we`ve had in generations? No question that it has.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Jared, good to see you, my friend. Thank you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Jared Bernstein joining us from the White House. Tonight is -- that`s tonight`s LAST WORD, by the way.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to see you tomorrow morning, special edition of "THE VELSHI BANNED BOOK CLUB".\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Ruhle joins us now -- I`m going to talk about Salman Rushdie, by the way.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Ruhle joins you for her show starting right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020220820ei8j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for August 19, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 7,731 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [23:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, WHITE HOUSE ECONOMIST: The strongest labor market we`ve had in generations, no question that it has.\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: Jared, good to see you my friend. Thank you for joining us tonight. Jared Bernstein joining us from the White House. Tonight is -- that`s tonight`s Last Word, by the way. I`m going to see you tomorrow morning, Special Edition of the Velshi Banned Book Club. Stephanie Ruhle joins us now. I`m going to talk about Salman Rushdie, by the way. Stephanie Ruhle joins you for her show, starting right now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, the former president unleashed an angry emotional rant infuriated over the many investigations. Trump attacked law enforcement which is already on high alert from threats of violence, some from Trump supporters. The question is, is he making it worse? And as Trump stokes extremist anger the planning of disinformation is spreading. Unfortunately, tonight we have another report on the danger of extremist violence in America.\par \par \uc2 Plus, women across the country motivated by the Roe reversal are out there registering to vote, as a judge in Michigan blocks a 90 year abortion ban law written almost entirely by men. All that and more when the 11th Hour gets underway on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. Here we go after yet another week of growing pressure, the former president is taking his attacks on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to an even more alarming and potentially dangerous level. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his allies have been escalating their rhetoric against law enforcement ever since the search at his Florida home, which was almost two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 America`s law enforcement officials are now on high alert, and they have been asking to tamp down the heated rhetoric. But tonight here we are again, on Trump`s social media site, I want to hear how he referred to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and DOJ, "atrocities being perpetrated by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and DOJ having to do with the raid and break in of my home, Mar-a-Lago." He goes on to write, "They have no shame. They are destroying our country." Of course, there was no break in. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  search not raid was carried out legally with a signed warrant by a federal judge. That very judge has also been threatened repeatedly by Trump supporters.\par \par \uc2 And one prominent defense attorney says that is just one of the many reasons he turned down an offer to represent Trump in the classified documents investigation. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JON SALE, DEFENSE ATTORNEY: I had made it clear I was not going to participate in the political agenda. I would have spoken out as strongly as I can against the rhetoric and the threats and the anti-Semitism and the defunding the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the credit threat to the judge.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Imagine that what an honor it would be to represent a President of the United States and he can`t get an experienced lawyer. He`s also facing a bunch of legal problems, not just one. And this week, we saw key developments and three of the separate cases that he`s linked to. There was yesterday`s hearing to unseal the affidavit for that Mar-a-Lago search. Trump`s former CFO also pleaded guilty to tax fraud. And Trump`s one time lawyer and former New York City Mayor appear before the Georgia Grand jury looking into election interference.\par \par \uc2 Plus, today a federal judge ordered loyal Trump allies Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  to testify on Tuesday before that very same Georgia panel. Graham has made several attempts to get out of testifying. None of them have worked. He is waiting on one more appeal.\par \par \uc2 To other lawyers who played key roles in Trump`s efforts to overturn the 2020 election, Jenna Ellis and John Eastman, they have also been ordered to testify in that Georgia case later this month.\par \par \uc2 There is also -- it`s a big word tonight, also a chance Trump`s Vice President, this is a really big news could end up talking to the January 6 committee. Vice Chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  told ABC News that talks with \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  himself are underway.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): We`ve been in discussions with his counsel, he played a critical role on January 6, if he had succumbed to the pressure that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was putting on him, we would have had a much worse constitutional crisis. And I think that he has clearly as he`s expressed concerns about executive privilege, which, you know, I have tremendous respect. I think it`s, you know, hugely important constitutional issue in terms of separation of powers. I believe in executive privilege. I think it matters. But I also think that when the country has been through something, as grave as this was, everyone who has information has an obligation to step forward. So I would hope that that he will do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: For somebody who just lost her primary, she does not look rattled. She also did not rule out the possibility of subpoenaing the former president himself. The committee is expected to hold at least two more hearings this fall. There`s a lot to talk about tonight.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:03]\par \par \uc2 So let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel. Luke Broadwater joins us, Pulitzer Prize Winning Congressional Reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , Amna Nawaz, Chief Correspondent for PBS NewsHour, and an MSNBC Contributor, and Harry Litman is here, former U.S. attorney and former Deputy Assistant Attorney General.\par \par \uc2 Amna, let`s turn to you first, what should we make of Trump`s latest rant about law enforcement? It`s almost as this he`s calling on the public to back him up here. That`s scary.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ, \uc2 PBS\uc2  NEWHOUR CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: It is scary, Stephanie. And there`s concerns as exactly what we`re seeing. I mean, look, part of this is a pattern that we`ve all seen by now, when Mr. Trump is under attack, there`s bluster and an escalation of rhetoric and threats of retaliation in some way. That`s what we`ve been seeing tonight, as well. He`s not been accusing law enforcement though of "destroying our country."\par \par \uc2 And we`ve seen that his attacks on the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , on law enforcement since that August 8 raid, they`ve been followed by people who follow him acting upon it. I mean, you just look at what`s unfolded since then, the days after that, there was a group of heavily armed men, including with assault rifles, who stood outside and \uc2 FBI\uc2  office in Phoenix on August 11, there was a man who tried to attack this \uc2 FBI\uc2  office in Cincinnati ended up in a deadly shootout with law enforcement outside. On August 12, the Pennsylvania man was arrested after posting a series of violent threats against \uc2 FBI\uc2  and law enforcement saying that people who deserve there -- people who work there, pardon me, they all deserve to die.\par \par \uc2 And there`s also as you mentioned, those really violent, dangerous threats against the judge who signed that search warrant in the first place. So we`re seeing in many ways when you talk to Homeland Security experts, national security experts, this same kind of mix of grievance and violence among his supporters, people saying we were wronged, we`re under siege, these are the people behind it. These are the people we need to go after in many cases, they need to die. It`s the same kind of mix that we saw leading up to January 6, and that is what has a lot of sources I`m talking to very, very worried because this is all unfolding at the same time that Homeland Security experts have been warning for months about an escalation in political violence, and this rhetoric is contributing to it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Given all of that, Luke, are there any Republicans and I mean, Republicans in power, in office that can make a difference, who are watching what is happening out there, and finally getting worried enough about this rhetoric to do something about it?\par \par \uc2 LUKE BROADWATER, CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER, "THE NEW YORK TIMES": Well, you have seen some calls from the less crazy wing of the party to tamp down the rhetoric, you know, you heard former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , to say stop attacking the \uc2 FBI\uc2  guys. And you heard, you know, the only \uc2 FBI\uc2 , former \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent in Congress, call on his fellow Republicans to calm the rhetoric and stop attacking law enforcement. And it has reached a dangerous level. I mean, there was just a bulletin recently that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  put out to law enforcement across the country that said there -- they have picked up chatter about a dirty bomb to be placed outside the \uc2 FBI\uc2  headquarters. There was talk of civil war and armed rebellion against the government.\par \par \uc2 And so every time \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  puts out one of these inflammatory statements, you know, today he talks about atrocities being committed by the DOJ and \uc2 FBI\uc2 , that -- there`s inevitably an uptick in the violent rhetoric online. And so yes, there are some members of the Republican Party who are saying cool it, knock it off, but it does seem like they`re being drowned out by those more violent voices.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yeah, and all of these Trump voices and Trump himself or blah, blah, blah lying about the atrocities and the illegal search, but they ain`t doing nothing when it comes to filing a motion against it. All they`re doing is going on TV, I want to share what Christina Bobb, Trump lawyer did, on TV, again, talking about the classified records investigation. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA BOBB, TRUMP ATTORNEY: I don`t see an indictment coming down. I also think the nation`s reaction to this rate, I think the department was surprised at how angry America got and how frustrated we all are that we feel like we`re losing our rights. And this is so overly political. So I think it`s causing them to go oh, you know, maybe we don`t have what we think we have, though, I hope that they take a second look and are cautious about doing something as stupid as trying to go after President Trump because they`ve tried this before.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Harry, that kind of sounds like a not so thinly veiled threat to the Department of Justice. How are they going to take that?\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: They`ll ignore it. It`s an empty threat and you know he`s got this two track strategy and it`s evident to anyone who knows the law. On the one hand he comes out with political rhetoric, like I want full accountability. But of course, he doesn`t, for a moment want to really release the affidavit. And that`s just one example.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:19]\par \par \uc2 So the same thing he`s, on the other hand, there`s the actual pro now law enforcement investigation where he seems in hotter and hotter water. He`s go, you know, you just have to sort of ignore and I think the DOJ is ignoring his rhetoric except to one point. It`s not simply that he`s letting it happen. It`s fairly despicable. For example, when they the DOJ released the war and moved to release, they redacted the names of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, he included them and they`re now under special federal surveillance. So he really is kind of akin to January 6, fanning the flames in a way that is posing a personal threat. And they stress this a lot yesterday in the hearing to \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents. It`s kind of loathsome.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But hairy while he`s not which is quite telling, could \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  fight the DOJ in court? He was back on social media talking about this fourth amendment that is the one that protects people from unreasonable searches and seizures by the government. Could he take him to court? Because thus far, and I just said it, it`s been 10 days and the only thing he`s done his complaint in public and taking no real action.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Anyone can take anyone to court. But of course, this is, on the contrary. And not only is it not a somewhat derogation, this is the constitutional process laid out in the Fourth Amendment. So it`s kind of venerable, he`s saying I`m going to bring up Fourth Amendment motion, it shouldn`t surprise us. He doesn`t understand how it works. If he`s charged, he can bring one and try to suppress the evidence. Before then all he could try to do is sue \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents who would have qualified immunity. So he`s really whistling in the wind again, unless and until he`s charged and of course, then all bets are off huge game changer and let him try to bring.\par \par \uc2 I think anyone who knows the DOJ knows this has been bulletproof. Careful, prudent and any his attempt on his part, for example, to show the videotape will just underscore that there`s no Fourth Amendment weakness here. I think you can be pretty confident.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s take another turn here to another Trump story we`re looking at tonight, talk of Trump and his allies or his allies, releasing their own surveillance video from the search. NBC`s Kelly O`Donnell is tracking that story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, NBC NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Mr. Trump`s allies have repeatedly dangled a possible video release to show what happened inside the estate during the search. A Trump attorney says that should be made public.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would love for the country to see what I saw from those cameras.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: But today, a Department of Justice official told me they urge those with access to video taken during the Mar-a-Lago search to use caution. If video is provided that it obscures the faces and identifying information of law enforcement personnel.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We`re going to see if we ever see any of that surveillance video release. But meanwhile, we are now in a holding pattern on that affidavit. If we ended up getting it, and the whole thing is basically blacked out, Luke, what was the point of doing it to begin with?\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: Yeah, that`s a fair point. I mean, you know, I`ve seen these affidavits released in the past and other public corruption cases. And they almost never looked good for the person whose home got searched. I mean, there`s usually pretty damning allegations in that affidavit. So, yes, there could be lots of things redacted. But it`s also public. It`s also possible the public will learn more about exactly what the justice department wanted from Mar-a-Lago and why they were doing it, was it simply just to get those documents back? And now that they have them that sort of the end of the road? Maybe there`s some loose ends to tie up? But that was the primary thing? Or did they suspect something else more nefarious, possibly was going on with those documents? And they had that information from other people that there were investigative steps to take beyond just retrieving the documents?\par \par \uc2 I mean, we could learn more about the investigation, which is why the media obviously we, you know, our job is to get as much information as possible. So we want to see that affidavit. And yes, I mean, it would be, you know, obviously there are concerns about the security of witnesses. And I understand the media attorneys in court said that they understand some things would need to be redacted, but we definitely want to see as much as legally possible\par \par \uc2 [23:15:00]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Amna, we keep talking about what are the consequences to Trump for having these confidential classified documents? But what about the consequences for the country? How concerned is this White House about the documents he had and what he could have done with them?\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: I think this is the other thing to point out remind folks here, is that Mr. Trump is not denying that he had these documents. He`s not denying that the National Archives wanted them back. He`s saying basically, that he as president has ultimate authority to declassify documents. And that`s what he did. He used the phrase a standing order to declassify these documents. And while it`s true that authority does rest with the President, anyone, you talk to national security folks, people who`ve been involved in declassifying procedures before say this is just not how it`s done.\par \par \uc2 And what you`ve got some folks coming out and saying, and Kash Patel, for example, the former Chief of Staff to then acting defense secretary who said, he absolutely has authority to do that. I was there when he said I`m declassifying these documents. You have other people from within the Trump administration, including the former national security adviser \uc2 John Bolton\uc2 , the former general counsel for their national security also coming out and saying, this just isn`t how it`s done.\par \par \uc2 And so while a lot of the focus has been around whether or not these are classified documents he has. The other thing to underscore here is that it may not matter at all. I mean, it is important to point out, I think that none of the laws that were cited as a predicate for the search warrant require that those documents have to be classified for any kind of violation that might have occurred.\par \par \uc2 And so I think there`s a lot we don`t know in this. We don`t know what`s in those documents. We do know how they were labeled. But this conversation has now the best defense that Mr. Trump has had is that as President I declassified them so there shouldn`t be a problem here. And that is what you`re seeing his followers and his supporters echoing as well.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Harry, next week should be a very, very busy week in that Georgia investigation. Again, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  was denied by the judge what are you looking for?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: He`s going to take an emergency appeal to the 11th circuit, he`s already taken. I think he loses, he`s saying it`s speech and debate clause that just doesn`t fly at all. But I`m also very interested, you brought it up at top, Jenna Ellis, we`ve got you know, more people coming. She was involved, you know, thick as could be. And you know, she`s got a very -- Ellis have a very broad investigation going.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what I`m looking for. Will they pull the trigger on indicting Giuliani? Because if they were to indict Trump, and everyone sees her as being earnest and it will immediately trigger a kind of legal, law school hypothetical going to federal court, lots of delays not so if they get Giuliani and he could also cooperate if they do. So the number one thing I want to see as well she sort of jumped the gun, rather than wait till the special jury packages is all up and move on Giuliani as normally one would do after issuing the sort of target letter that she did.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I agree with you. I want to see what happens in Georgia. I really want to see what happens in the next January 6 committee hearing. Why? It is going to be one of the last things \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  does before she leaves office. We will see if she lets it rip.\par \par \uc2 Luke Broadwater, Amna Nawaz and Harry Litman, thanks for joining us on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, as we keep saying, there can be real world consequences when disinformation fuels distrust and anger, like accusing the nation`s law enforcement of destroying the country. But is it a lesson right wing politicians and others refuse to learn? We`re going to ask a former \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agent if anything can stop this.\par \par \uc2 And later, with just 80 days to go into the midterm elections, how much of a female factor will there be at the ballot box? A lot may depend on how red the status. The 11th Hour just getting underway on a Friday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:23:43]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRACY WALDER, FORMER CIA OFFICER: In my personal opinion, I do believe that they are domestic terrorist. If we look up what domestic terrorism is, it`s violent criminal acts committed by individuals and groups to really further ideological goals and that`s what`s going on here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Domestic terrorism is what is going on here. And it is only getting worse, whether it`s online threats to Boston Children`s Hospital we told you about last night or conspiracy theories warning that \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents are about to start breaking down your door, they`re not. The anger is often rooted in one thing, misinformation. And as we told you at the top of the hour just today, the former guy is accusing the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Justice Department of atrocities, warning the people will not stand for it, sounds an awful lot like the things he said before January 6. And so much for him suggesting that the temperature needs to come down.\par \par \uc2 Let`s dive into all of this and bring in \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2 , West Point graduate, Army Veteran, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agent and a distinguished research fellow at the Foreign Policy Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 Clint, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  a week ago issued a bulletin about the threats they`re facing. And now you`ve got this guy saying law enforcement is destroying the country. This feels pretty out of control?\par \par \uc2 [23:25:00]\par \par \uc2 CLINT WATTS\uc2 , FORMER COUNTER TERRORISM DIVISION FORMER CONSULTANT: It does, Stephanie. And it`s just consistent with the rhetoric we`ve seen against all institutions over the last five to six years. I think the biggest factor that I worry about the most is it`s harming an institution, a federal institution that every American citizen ultimately needs over time, meaning that in a lot of the very far right districts that support President Trump, there are going to be federal crimes, cyber in particular, that only the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the federal government can handle. And what happens when these baseless claims are made. What happens when these conspiracies are advanced, you have Americans losing trust in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents that they will need one day and separately, it puts a big wedge between state and local law enforcement and federal law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 Imagine going and doing a search warrant in certain parts of the country, you need state local support, you need to work with state and locals, our state and locals need the support of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . And that unity is not there. When that unity breaks down, it becomes very hard to protect every citizen, all based on conspiracies that have no merit and truth that are all for political or personal survival of one person.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, they have one thing in common, they all come from Trump, and you`ve got some Trump supporters already talking Civil War, who or what can ever convince them to just calm down, is the only hope that there`s not enough of them to make a significant difference?\par \par \uc2 WATTS: Yeah, I think the hope is that it`s just rhetoric that is around one person. The thing that we consistently see in what is known as stochastic terrorism, that is terrorism where the target is known, but the perpetrator is random. They are receiving or believing they`re receiving some sort of signal, in this case from the former president, that they should point their angst at certain individuals, which are institutions, federal law enforcement or what I`m most worried about heading into November is election officials. This is a consistent theme that we`ve seen where we have people running for office that deny the outcome of the 2020 election, yet they still want to be in charge of the 2022 or 2024 election, and they are using threats of violence or trying to target their energy towards election officials in a way that is both intimidating, and if not intimidating, downright violence. So I think where this starts to break apart is at the state local level as we head into the congressional election this fall.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But Clint, you would think this would start to break apart after January 6, Trump got them worked up and worked up that the election was stolen, it wasn`t stolen, they stormed the Capitol, they got arrested, he did not pardon them, many of them are going to jail. Now, he is in trouble again, not the country. And he`s got those very same people threatening acts of violence again, why do they continue to get played by this guy?\par \par \uc2 WATTS: I think that`s -- the disturbing part is there is a certain demand for the misinformation that is out there on the internet, and they look to the former president to direct them to where they`re anxious be. What`s troubling about is there`s really no breaking that wall down facts do not penetrate this illusion, this, you know, alternative perception of what`s going on in this country. They want that to be true.\par \par \uc2 The other consistent problem that I think we sometimes forget about is it`s episodic. Every two years, we have a big election in this country with domestic extremism, domestic politics become the driving force every two years, because everyone builds their hopes up around an election, or candidates or the belief that a former president might run again. So that sort of energy is still there. I think it`s dramatically less than what we saw on January 6, by the way, but it`s still significant enough, that anytime that there`s some sort of threat of violence word of federal official, there`ll be a small percentage of Americans around the country that were already thinking of being violent. That`s the \uc2 IRS\uc2  is one that I think is particularly troubling right now. There`s a long history of threats of violence against tax collection, the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . What happens is you will have one, two or three and if one, two or three do it, that it creates a cascading sort of effect where other people then feel empowered that they should do it as well.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But then Clint, if domestic politics is stirring up domestic terrorism and it`s not going away, do we need to get a lot more serious about fixing the problem?\par \par \uc2 WATTS: We treat domestic terrorism wildly different than we do international terrorism, meaning there is no terrorist organization designation. There`s no law around terrorism, which means we are more reactive to each of these individual incidents, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  leading those efforts. But it`s hard to connect the dots and it`s hard to be preemptive hints that comes to social media. Some of those posts that you saw right there, you will see a long string of claims of we need to start a civil war or we need to act. You saw this with the case in Cincinnati where an individual went and attacked an \uc2 FBI\uc2  office there. He was very clear and explicit about what he intended to do. There`s been other arrests over the last two weeks of individuals making direct threats against the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . So this sort of stirring things up I think will get only more intense over the next 90 days and I do worry about some of these targets being designated that if there`s one successful attack, you will see not one but a string of attacks. This is a very common phenomenon that we`ve seen over the last 5 to 10 years where one attack inspires another. And the targets in all cases most recently had been federal targets that come up in the context of former President Trump and his social media posts or public statements.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:30]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2 , I hope our government officials are watching and listening to. Thank you for joining us tonight, I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, hear them roar arguing policies from the right or making them second class citizens, I`m talking about women. They`re angry, they`re speaking out and guess what, they may about -- they may be about to vote in very big numbers. We`re going to get into that when the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:35:32]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN GROSSI, MICHIGAN CHIEF DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL: Women are upset because in June of 2022, they woke up one day and to find that with a stroke of the pen, they had been relegated to second class citizens in their own homes. And women are upset and angry because in the greatest country in the world, we are being denied access to critical health care.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Michigan Deputy Attorney General Christian Grossi, did not hold back during her closing statements in this week`s hearing over the state`s abortion ban. And today a Michigan judge blocked enforcement of the ban for months until voters or the state Supreme Court can weigh in. in reading his decision, Judge Jacob Cunningham noted something very interesting about the 1931 law.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUDGE JACOB CUNNINGHAM, OAKLAND COUNTY, MICHIGAN CIRCUIT COURT: This statute is the product of the 57th Michigan legislative session notably comprised almost entirely of men.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Judge Cunningham also addressed the impact this fan would have had.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CUNNINGHAM: Subjecting providers to criminal penalty for medical care that was available not to mention safe as the testimony suggested safer than pregnancy itself, without any justification from the state for doing so simply does not pass constitutional muster under our state`s constitution. Women and all persons capable of carrying children, not having the ability and freedom to consult with their medical professionals without fear of prosecution puts the government into the patient examining room. A person carrying a child has the right to bodily autonomy and integrity as well as a safe doctor patient relationship free from government interference as they have been able to do so for nearly 50 years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: With us tonight to discuss, two of my absolute faves, Political Strategist, Rachel Bitecofer, the author of the forthcoming book, "Hit Em` Where It Hurts: How to Save Democracy by Beating Republicans at Their Own Game." And John Della Volpe joins us, Director of Polling at the Harvard Kennedy School of Politics and author of, "Fight: How Gen Z is Channeling Their Fear and Passion to Save America."\par \par \uc2 Rachel, you have called women mobilizing after the Roe reversal, a pink wave, can decisions like this really build momentum that`s going to make a difference come November?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BITECOFER, POLITICAL STRATEGIST: Well, I remember very early on in the cycle, giving an interview to a publication and they asked me about Roe and whether it would have an effect on the midterms coming up. And you know, at that time, I was thinking, you know, they probably restrict some, hold up some of these bans and the prohibitions that they`ve been putting into place. But I was so dubious that they would take the step of evisceration of Roe that I told -- I remember I told the journalist that I just didn`t really think that that was very likely to happen. And the reason why guys is because that is Titanic as the video that we were watching as leading for the interview, as the attorney was citing for women, the personal is now political, again, with a stroke of the pen loss, that freedom of bodily autonomy. And it really does change the moral imperative, which has favored the opposition to abortion over the last 30 to 50 years. Now, it`s putting that moral imperative on women and girls and the reality of what these restrictive, really draconian abortion bans will do in public policy terms.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: John, anecdotally, we are hearing that scores of women across the country are registering to vote, what are you seeing in the data?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DELLA VOLPE, \uc2 HARVARD KENNEDY SCHOOL\uc2  DIRECTOR OF POLLING: I think it`s far beyond anecdotal evidence. Stephanie, we see in most every state in the nation starting with Kansas, significant increases of women registered to vote and those women who are registering overwhelmingly Democratic women, specifically younger Democratic women, number one.\par \par \uc2 As Rachel said, I think this is a second midterm cycle in a Roe where there`s a series of public events that are transforming the shape of the electorate. In 2018, we saw this with the youth movement following the Parkland shooting and what those young people did. We`re seeing the same thing now post, Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, before Dobbs, Democrats were already doing better than most people expected by drawing close to even or maybe down a couple of points, Republicans, post Dobbs. We see Democrat leading in most national surveys in several of the key states there has been about a four or five point shift premium post Dobbs, most of that shift is coming from supportive women moving from undecided Republicans to Democrats now.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:06]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Rachel, these bans or restrictive abortion laws are not an accident. They`re what Republicans have been pushing for, for years. Now that they`re happening, and they`re seeing the impact, it`s having the backlash with women. Are they realizing the one thing this is going to do a shrink their party, they were never going to lose the white evangelical vote. But now they`ve just made them extra happy. And they risk losing scores of women.\par \par \uc2 BITECOFER: And extra happy people don`t vote, right? I mean, I think we`re four years past the negative partisanship theory being deployed in elections. And we`re seeing again, and again, it`s fear and threat that make people motivated to vote. So the losers of the fight are going to be more motivated. The people who, you know, last beating Brett Kavanaugh`s nomination are more motivated them the winters and here we really see Republicans like the shrewd ones like \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , they never wanted to catch this car. They understood exactly what would happen when they did. And that`s why when the memo leaked, I think it was that night or the next day, I tweeted about, nobody other than women. You know, in the political sense, though, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  had a very bad day finding out that Roe was going to be completely gutted, because he understood it would be a titanic shift. And now we`re seeing Democrats capitalize on it, too. It`s not enough that you get the shift. You`re really seeing strategic effort to exploit these advantages.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And that`s how twisted politics is. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  had a very bad day realizing Roe would be gutted. But of course, it`s what he and his party have been promising voters for years. John, what I want to know is, are we seeing more women get more motivated registered to vote activate in red are states where these abortion bans are being put in place, then let`s say on New York or California where women feel safer about their access to reproductive rights?\par \par \uc2 DELLA VOLPE: Yes, Stephanie. There is a correlation between, essentially the redder the state, the more angry and the more likely women are to vote. In fact, the states that were women are most vulnerable and most likely to lose their reproductive rights also, other ones are overlaid with key Senate officer, Wisconsin, Michigan, Pennsylvania, North Carolina, Florida, Arizona, in these states, women`s reproductive rights are in question more than love of traditional blue states. And that`s where we see specifically independent women`s, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 Before Dobbs, the Little League, Republicans were winning among independent women by a couple of points. Today, Democrats are winning by 13 points, with plenty more undecided on the fence, ready to tune in, I think post Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Be careful what you wish for. Rachel Bitecofer, John Della Volpe, thank you both for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the stolen year, has the pandemic set kids back in school and added stress for moms at home, when the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:47:40]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: From abortion bans to stress over transitioning back to school from remote learning, American women have been feeling the pressure. Many have been feeling stress like this for years and years. Anya Kamenetz writes this in The Atlantic. "In one survey, nearly half of mothers with children home for remote learning reported that their mental health had worsened, compared with just 30% of fathers who said the same."\par \par \uc2 Let`s discuss and bring in Anya Kamenetz, author of the new book, "The Stolen Year: How COVID Changed Children`s Lives and Where We Go Now." It comes out next week. It is a must read. She was also a longtime Education Correspondent for the NPR. So let`s talk to our expert.\par \par \uc2 And let`s start right there, how do we begin to address the impact of the stolen year?\par \par \uc2 ANYA KAMENETZ, "THE STOLEN YEAR" AUTHOR: I think the beginning is to talk about it. Because we kind of tacitly agreed as a society that we were going to allow women generally to bear the brunt of what happened. And when we talked about what happened to children, we talked about resilience, but we`re not spending enough time on what kids actually went through and what the caregivers who were with them on those days and nights actually went through. That`s what I`m trying to do with this book.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We`ve talked a lot over the last two years about loss of learning, what it did in terms of social interactions, isolations, kids even moving their bodies, but we forget that many, many children missed out on meals, heat, a safe place to go outside their home, how damaging was that?\par \par \uc2 KAMENETZ: You know, something like being hungry and child hunger in the very first month after the pandemic spike to what researchers say are unprecedented levels because nothing could replace the way the school lunch program gives hot meals to kids in school every day. That has lasting effects because of the insecurity that comes when people don`t know where their next meal is coming from. And that`s just one example. I mean, we`re worried about kids that were unsafe in their own homes. And then sadly, as the pandemic wore on, we know that now over 200,000 children in America have lost a parent to COVID itself. So what they call adverse childhood experiences. What they call toxic stress these have the impact on the developing brain of a child that we`re going to see 10 years down the road when that child is an adult.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:09]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We talked so much about the stress this put on children, the stress this put on parents, there`s also the enormous stress it puts on teachers, many of whom are parents themselves. And now we`re facing a teacher shortage. That was a problem before COVID. How do we start to address that?\par \par \uc2 KAMENETZ: You know, it`s so hard because there are so many people that come together to take care of children and teachers. And I would add childcare workers to that, they`re so important in all of our lives. And yet, after a brief period of feeling appreciation, when everyone had to take care of their own kids, and teach them and realize how hard these jobs actually are, teachers started to be the scapegoats for, you know, why don`t I have school five days a week? Or why isn`t my child catching up? And/or now, more often, that there are controversies out masks about, you know, race in the classroom, gender in the classroom, and teachers are just taking it from all angles. And a lot of them have expressed to me that they can`t do this anymore. They`re tired, you know, they`re feeling these unprecedented levels of burnout. And how do we come together as a society and re- establish the overall here for is to make sure that the kids are OK.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Are there are other issues that we`re not hearing about, that are a really big problem, because all the noise was around school re-openings and mask mandates, what are we missing?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The childcare situation is an absolute disaster. OK. There are almost 90,000 caregivers that have left the childcare workforce, many ran out of money, ran out of aid during the pandemic, and we`re not able to reopen and when you have a labor shortage, and you can make $25 an hour at \uc2 Costco\uc2 , and $12 an hour at a daycare center taking care of developing infants. It`s very, very hard to fill those roles, and so without subsidies that were in the build back better plan but did not get passed in the Inflation Reduction Act, I don`t know what happens to our childcare system. It`s a really dire situation.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, it`s important that we talk about it because people are certainly dealing with it at home. Anya, thank you for writing this book. And thank you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 KAMENETZ: Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When we come back, the Mar-a-Lago omelet station with a side of classified documents, the late night rose from a legendary comedian, when the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:57:14]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FALLON, TELEVISION HOST: Please welcome the one, the only Martin Short.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, A Short Roasting. We`re going to end this very busy workweek with a Martin Short appreciation segment. The comedy legend and only murders in the building star has been making us laugh for decades. And last night was no different. In a few of Short minutes on The Tonight Show, he managed to roast Jimmy Fallon the entire Trump family and himself. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARTIN SHORT, ACTOR, COMEDIAN, SINGER: It`s hard for me to express how excited I am to be here tonight. Because the Botox is fresh.\par \par \uc2 FALLON: You could barely see it.\par \par \uc2 SHORT: You know I haven`t seen you since the big Saudi golf tournament that was you were who knew that you and the Crown Prince was such buddy. The giggling, in the giggling and he`s knitting and you`re holding the wool. It`s so beautiful. That tell everyone what you`re telling me about why the Proud Boys is so much more than a club? What do you mean by that? What does that mean? What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 No, no, Jimmy -- here`s the thing about Jimmy, we do hang out because we`re not just fake show business friends. We`re fake friends in real life.\par \par \uc2 FALLON: We really are. That is correct. How`s your summer going, though?\par \par \uc2 SHORT: Well, it`s so beautiful. I`ve been up -- I`ve been up with the looms. At my cottage.\par \par \uc2 FALLON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHORT: But -- and do I regret my Kim and Pete forever tattoo? Sure. Absolutely. Jimmy had a few.\par \par \uc2 FALLON: Do I? Yes, I do.\par \par \uc2 SHORT: You oh -- and how about this, you know I was at the -- I was at Mar- a-Lago during the big \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid.\par \par \uc2 FALLON: Wait, what?\par \par \uc2 SHORT: Oh, I`m going to lie, you know. No, no, no, I`m a member there.\par \par \uc2 FALLON: I didn`t know you`re member of Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 SHORT: God, like I don`t like Trump`s politics, but I love his Omelets Station.\par \par \uc2 FALLON: No, that makes sense.\par \par \uc2 SHORT: It`s beautiful. And you know, it`s so great because you can just -- if you`re a member, you can just read as many classified documents as you want, all over the site.\par \par \uc2 FALLON: They`re gathered everywhere. I didn`t even know, you knew the Trumps?\par \par \uc2 SHORT: Oh, I know the Trumps. I used to babysit the kids for God`s sake. Eric and Don Jr. We would play monopoly but their version is, the winner is the first person to yell bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I must say, we are not worthy of Martin Shorts. And on that pretty wonderful note I wish you all a very good night, thank you for staying up late with us. I will see you all next week. I am very lucky to be in the seat. That was pretty spectacular. See Monday.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220820ei8j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for August 19, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Chuck Rosenberg, Tiffany Cross \par \uc2 8,377 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CROSS, MSNBC HOST: Plus the other Republican candidates following his footsteps. Trumpism is on the ballot this November even if Trump isn`t. We`re going to talk about that. Plus, if you`re a parent, you absolutely know we`re heading into the new school year with a teacher shortage. We`ll take a look at some of the reasons why.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll talk to the director of an Emmy-nominated documentary on Kanye West. It`s called genius. It`s a really amazing film. We`ll have the filmmakers on with me. It`s truly like Ye like you`ve never seen it before. It`s really good. So, I`ll see you guys tomorrow at 10:00 a.m. on "THE CROSS CONNECTION." Thanks so much for bearing with me this week while I fill in for Joy.\par \par \uc2 And "ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voice-over): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 RUDY GIULIANI\uc2 , TRUMP ATTORNEY: Bill to the Espionage Act, it`s not really about taking the documents, it`s about destroying.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: New admissions, new excuses, and threatening new language from Trump lawyers.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA BOBB, TRUMP ATTORNEY: I think the department was surprised at how angry America got. I hope that they take a second look and are cautious about doing something as stupid as trying to go after President Trump.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, Chuck Rosenberg on the former president`s attempts to menace investigators.\par \par \uc2 Then, Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  on \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , the next January 6 hearings, and the Committee`s discussions with \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): Everyone who has information has an obligation to step forward. So, I would hope that people do that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Plus, new signs of hope in the post Roe backlash in Michigan. And what you really need to understand about this week`s depraved election fraud stunt in Florida when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Nearly two weeks out from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of the ex-president`s Florida home, Donald Trump`s excuses keep getting worse. They keep changing and they keep getting worse. He`s now threatening to take steps that could further inflame tensions and outright endanger \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents.\par \par \uc2 Now, at first, Trump tried to push nonsense conspiracy theories about the \uc2 FBI\uc2  planting the classified documents in Mar-a-Lago, which was frankly absurd. And when it didn`t work, he landed on the consistent excuse. Yes, the documents are mine, yes, I had them with me, but it`s fine because I declassified them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN SOLOMON, CONSERVATIVE COMMENTATOR: This is from President Trump`s office. It just came in a few minutes ago. As we can all relate to everyone -- as we can all relate to, everyone ends up having to bring home their work from time to time. American presidents are no different. President Trump, in order to prepare the work the next day, often took documents including classified documents to the residence. He had a standing order. There`s the word I`ve been looking for. That documents removed from the Oval Office and taken to the residence were deemed to be declassified the moment he removed them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, that`s an almost comical monologue there from John Solomon who`s essentially a mouthpiece for Trump. That excuse was pretty obviously a lie, but we`ve got new reporting to actually confirm it, nail it down. 18 former White House officials, including two of Trump`s White House Chiefs of Staff, \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  and \uc2 Mick Mulvaney\uc2  say there was no standing order to declassify documents, which certainly appears that Trump is just lying to try to save his hide.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  who appears to be in quite a bit of legal trouble as the target, again, of that criminal investigation in Georgia, went on the far- right outlet \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  to try and clear things up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI: Now, they want to make them responsible for having taken classified documents and preserve them. Really, if you look at the Espionage Act, it`s not really about taking the documents, it`s about destroying them or hiding them, or giving them to the enemy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI: It`s not about taking them and putting them in a place that`s roughly as safe as they were in in the first place.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, the Espionage Act is weird and in some ways, bad piece of legislation, but that`s not true what Giuliani is saying. According to Trump`s own former head of counterintelligence at the Department of Justice, "The Espionage Act explicitly applies to the willful retention of classified and non-classified defensively to documents in an unauthorized place. That is obviously what we`re talking about. In fact, it`s all but been admitted to by the Trump people.\par \par \uc2 Setting that aside, it sure sounds like Giuliani is openly admitting Trump took the documents that he apparently knew were classified in order to preserve them. Of course, we know that Trump was storing at least some of the documents in the basement of his golf resort, which no matter how you slice it, I don`t think is safer than with the National Archives.\par \par \uc2 We even know the Department of Justice investigators again and this long back and forth, right, where they`re trying to get the papers back and secured, told Trump he needed to put a lock on the door where the documents were kept, meaning his attempts at preservation certainly left something to be desired.\par \par \uc2 Now, just watch Trump`s current lawyer flounder when Fox News lobbed her a softball question about how secure the documents were.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA BOBB, TRUMP ATTORNEY: They asked for one more lock and another lock, which we did. And then for whatever reason, they decided that they still needed to raid the place.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:03]\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, HOST, FOX NEWS: And only one or two people had access to that room to your knowledge?\par \par \uc2 BOBB: That`s my understanding. I mean I would have to check with, you know, the maintenance of that area, but my understanding is there`s very small number of people that could get in there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Laura is like, please work with me here. I`m trying to help you out. Not a very reassuring answer. We`re talking about top secret documents. And in case you`ve forgotten, of course, Trump doesn`t really deserve the benefit of the doubt when it comes to preserving any kind of national security secrets. I mean, just for one example, and there are truly countless, listen to the musician, Kid Rock, described how Trump was apparently discussing sensitive issues of foreign policy and national security with a nuclear power with him when he visited the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KID ROCK, SINGER: We`re looking at maps (BLEEP) and I`m like, you know, I`m like, am I supposed to be like (BLEEP)? I make dirty records sometimes. What the (BLEEP) am I doing here?\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , HOST, FOX NEWS: You don`t think you`d have a hand --\par \par \uc2 ROCK: What do you think we should do about North Korea? I`m like, what? I don`t think I`m qualified to answer this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s the most relatable I`ve ever found Kid Rock and he`s right. He`s not really qualified to answer it. But all this stuff aside, what is less funny than the kind of haphazard and somewhat ridiculous attempts by Trump to wriggle out of this, what he`s doing now with his attacks on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  I think is legitimately dangerous.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s been reported that Trump made this overture to the Department of Justice. \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reported this through an emissary, and he sent this message to \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . He wanted to get this to \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . And the question was, the country is on fire, what can I do to reduce the heat? And by country on fire, what he meant was he talked to all these people who were enraged by the search on Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 And there`s a way to read that which I think is a plain reading of it, which is ominous, right, a threat. Trump telling the DOJ, be careful, that his supporters are agitating for violence. Be careful what you do, because obviously, it`s very obvious that Trump is not interested in cooling tensions at all.\par \par \uc2 Now, we don`t know for sure who initially leaked the actual Mar-a-Lago search warrant to pro-Trump news outlets, including Breitbart, although I`ll leave it to you to guess. But a copy of the document that circulated in some far-right circles did not redact the names of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  officers involved, putting their lives in danger.\par \par \uc2 And again, I`m not being hyperbolic here. This is not just empty threat. Just last week, right after the search, a man took a gun to an \uc2 FBI\uc2  building in Ohio and later fired directly at officers in the standoff after posting online about he wanted to kill the \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents. Earlier this week, a Pennsylvania man was arrested for allegedly using the far right social media platform Gab to share explicit threats against the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Statements like "Every single piece of blank who works the \uc2 FBI\uc2  in any capacity from the director down to the janitor who cleans their effing toilets deserves to die. You`ve declared war on us and now it`s open season on you."\par \par \uc2 The Florida judge who approved the search warrant on Mar-a-Lago is now receiving death threats which are apparently so serious. His synagogue canceled tonight`s scheduled service, OK. That`s what it means when Trump sends a message to \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , the country`s on fire. And now the ex- president is reportedly mulling the possibility of releasing the surveillance camera video of the Mar-a-Lago search which would, it seems, further endanger those agents by making their faces public to Trump`s enraged supporters.\par \par \uc2 In a new statement this evening, the Department of Justice told NBC News, "We would hope the video would only be provided in a format that obscures the faces and identifying information of law enforcement personnel." But that doesn`t seem to be a concern. Just listen to how another one of Trump`s lawyers put it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So you can`t see the affidavit and of course, you can`t see what they were doing in the raid. We love transparency in this government, don`t you?\par \par \uc2 ALINA HABBA, TRUMP ATTORNEY: We don`t want you to see their identities either, because we don`t want you to know who did that. Well, if you`re doing it as an agent, I`m not sure that you should have that right. Listen, \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, undercover agents, that`s one thing. But when you go into a president`s home, an ex-president`s home, what do you expect is going to happen? What do you expect?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Think about that. What do you expect is going to happen? What`s that mean? Trump could care less, right, about protecting law enforcement. In fact, just today, he used his weird off-brand platform to further inflame the country, to really insight. He referred to the execution of a search warrant as "atrocities being perpetrated by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and DOJ, that law enforcement in our country has become that of a third world nation and I don`t believe the people will stand for it. How much are we all expected to take?"\par \par \uc2 "Never in our country`s history has there been a time where law enforcement has been sufficiently viciously and violently involved in the life and times of politics in our nation. They have no shame. They are destroying our country." Again, someone shot up an \uc2 FBI\uc2  headquarters after the raid. He knows that.\par \par \uc2 This morning, Trump`s lawyer again took to right-wing media to issue what again sure seems like a threat to the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:10:02]\par \par \uc2 BOBB: I don`t see an indictment coming down. I also think the nation`s reaction to this raid, I think the department was surprised at how angry America got and how frustrated we all are that we feel like we`re losing our rights and this is so overly political. So, I think it`s causing them to go, oh, you know, maybe we don`t have what we think -- what we think we have. So, I hope that they take a second look and are cautious about doing something as stupid as trying to go after President Trump because they`ve tried this before.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, yes, especially --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Again, listen to what they said there, right? They basically admitted to the crime, right? They have the documents. They willfully retain them, right? Now, they`re not making legal arguments. They`re just saying, you will destroy civil peace in this country because our followers will what if you do this. They`re threatening law enforcement and the DOJ and claiming the country will erupt into violence if they don`t do what Trump wants.\par \par \uc2 I got to say, the last time that Trump`s rhetoric was this openly inciting, his platform was much higher, it`s not the same, but the last time it was this inciting, a mob stormed the Capitol and people ended up dead.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Rosenberg is a former senior \uc2 FBI\uc2  official as well as a U.S. attorney, and he joins me now. Chuck, what do you -- what do you make of this not-so- subtle attempt to bully and menace and intimidate and threaten the Department of Justice in the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , both through what the former president said and what his lawyers are saying?\par \par \uc2 CHUCK ROSENBERG, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, I think the threats, and putting aside, Chris, how sickening they are, are not really designed just to intimidate the Department of Justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , because frankly, I don`t think those people can be intimidated. The only effect that may have is causing them to work 20 hours a day, rather than 18 hours a day on the investigation.\par \par \uc2 I think it sends a broader message, which is equally disturbing. It`s to everyone else, to all the lay witnesses, to people who may have worked for him, to people who may have been in and around Mar-a-Lago, to anybody who the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is talking to, if we can threaten agents, we can threaten you, if we can get them we can get you. If they can shoot up an \uc2 FBI\uc2  building somewhere in the country, they can come to your house.\par \par \uc2 And so, I know the men and women of law enforcement have more fortitude, and each of their little fingers than that man has in -- has in his entire body. And I don`t worry about them as much as I worry about all of the other people out there who might be cooperating in this investigation or some other investigation down the road. That`s why the Justice Department wants to keep this affidavit under wraps until charges are brought or the case is closed. And that makes sense to me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, there`s -- so there`s a non-bad faith, non-threatening version of this argument that I`ve seen people that I like and respect make, which is it`s adjacent to it, right? I mean, this is -- you know, Trump`s lawyer saying maybe, you know, our people will riot in the streets for this. But Mona Charen who we have in the program who I like quite a bit despite the fact that we don`t share much in common politically, you know, her making the argument that like it or not, a significant constituency will have its faith in the rule of law further eroded by a Trump trial with consequences we cannot tolerate. The Trump minions are openly discussing civil war. And so, though it means surrendering to intimidation, the wiser course is to refrain.\par \par \uc2 I`ve seen other people making this argument that you have to consider legitimacy and civil peace when considering whether to push forward on what the Department of Justice is doing, whether on the document question or the January 6 though.\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: Yes, look, I think these are all really interesting questions and much harder than we might imagine them to be. I read her article. But you know, think of it this way, Chris, not to get too philosophical at this hour. But what`s the goal? Is the goal to punish misconduct -- is the goal to punish criminal behavior? Because if it is, and you take one path, is the goal to make Mr. Trump as irrelevant as possible as quickly as possible? If it is, then you take another path?\par \par \uc2 So, the answer you get depends on the question you ask. I mean, that`s always true. But I think there are a lot of questions we need to ask ourselves as a nation, including where do we want to go and how do we want to get there, and how much of a platform do we want to give to this gentleman for another five years? My answer would be not very much. I`m tired of talking about him now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. And that is, of course, going to be somewhat in tension with the possibility of a criminal case and accountability under the law, right? Because to your point, right, those are somewhat inimical just because it would necessarily be an enormous story, an ex-President being charged with crime. Where do you see where we are in this investigation given what`s been made public, given the comments that DOJ lawyers have made in their arguments vis-a-vis the affidavit about what we`re looking at here in terms of this part of the investigation around these classified documents?\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: Yes, it`s a great question, Chris. And it`s hard to answer, which is what makes it a great question, frankly. I mean, we know that they had enough evidence, a quantum of proof to meet a probable cause standard to get a search warrant. That`s clear. And by the way, all this nonsense from Mr. Trump`s lawyers about rights being trampled, actually his rights were strictly adhered to, right? The Fourth Amendment standard was applied and the Federal judge from a different branch of government reviewed and authorized the affidavit. Let`s be clear about that.\par \par \uc2 But where are we? It`s really hard to say because we don`t know what they took, not precisely. And we don`t know what`s in the affidavit at all. And so, without those two really big questions, we don`t know. But I will say this. The Department of Justice has said we have a sensitive, ongoing criminal investigation about this and other matters. We`re following all leads, right, everybody -- everything is on the table.\par \par \uc2 And here`s what concerns me. There are two legitimate competing interests, one by the media and the public to see the affidavit as quickly as possible, and one by law enforcement to keep you from seeing the affidavit for as long as possible. And they`re both legitimate interests. They`re both completely valid. But if what you care about, if the goal if the question you ask is, you know, do we punish criminal behavior and the answer is yes, that`s what we do, that`s what we should do here. If that`s your answer, then the resolution of this question about the affidavit has to be we don`t make it public and everyone just has to be patient.\par \par \uc2 I think the judge is making a bit of a mistake by trying to find a middle ground which in the end might leave both sides completely unhappy and us no better informed.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it is interesting that this -- the judge`s direction is go redact, give it to me, I`ll then make the final decision, right? And one of the arguments the government have made is look, this is going to be so redacted, it`s going to be useless. So, we`ll see where it ended up. On the Fourth Amendment, I got to note, professor of law, Professor Steve Vladeck who`s very sharp down at the University of Texas made the point the way Trump finds out what the Supreme Court has done to Fourth Amendment jurisprudence about the remedies that had been whittled away by the current \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  for people who have had their Fourth Amendment rights violated.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Rosenberg, always great to have you on, always illuminating, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, a federal judge ordered Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  to testify in front of that Georgia grand jury. And I`m going to talk to -- talk about the Fulton County District Attorney`s aggressive strategy next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Can we expect to possibly see additional subpoenas from people in former President Trump`s inner circle or Trump associates?\par \par \uc2 FANI WILLIS\uc2 , DISTRICT ATTORNEY, FULTON COUNTY, GEORGIA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Are we talking about family members? Are we talking about former White House officials?\par \par \uc2 WILLIS: I mean, we`ll just have to see where the investigation leads us. But I think that people thought that we came into this as some kind of game. This is not a game at all. What I am doing is very serious. It`s very important work. And we`re going to do our due diligence in making sure that we look at all aspects of the case.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: True to her word, Fulton County, Georgia District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  has been relentless in our pursuit of the investigation into Donald Trump`s efforts to overturn the election in her state. It`s ultimately the reason that we got to see Trump lawyer \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  make the trip to Fulton County on Wednesday and testify before a grand jury in the case. I should add, of course, that we now know that Giuliani is a target in that very investigation.\par \par \uc2 Now, Willis has her sights set on sitting U.S. Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina. After the 2020 election, fairly early we should say in the timeline after the election, Sen. Graham called up the Georgia Secretary of State twice reportedly pressuring him about rejecting absentee ballots, and making references to voter fraud.\par \par \uc2 Graham has been trying very hard to get out of testifying until today when a federal judge ordered him to appear, setting the stage for him to make that same walk as Giuliani into the Fulton County Courthouse next Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Gwen Keyes Fleming served as District Attorney for the Stone Mountain Judicial Circuit in DeKalb County, Georgia which, of course, is a neighbor of Fulton County. She`s also co-author of \uc2 The Brookings Institution\uc2 `s analysis, The Fulton County investigation, and she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Gwen, there`s obviously interesting constitutional arguments being raised by \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  vis-a-vis the immunity, the broad immunity for speech and debate that`s conferred by the first article of the constitution. His lawyers arguing that protects him. He shouldn`t have to show up. But we now have a federal judge ordering him. There`s an emergency appeal they filed. But what do you think about the likelihood that Graham is going to have to go through and testify?\par \par \uc2 GWEN KEYES FLEMING, FORMER DISTRICT ATTORNEY, DEKALB COUNTY: Well, I think that`s what we`re waiting to see. And barring any action by the 11th circuit court of appeals between now and Tuesday when his subpoena -- today in his subpoena, I think both the DEA and the grand jurors will expect him to speak in court and see him answer questions.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We`ve -- he`s just one of a number of high profile people that this grand jury has issued -- that the prosecutors issued subpoenas to. There`s Graham, of course, Giuliani who again tried to fight it was ultimately ordered to comply with it. Now there`s a governor of Georgia \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, Kemp`s counsel and Willis are in a back and forth about whether he`s compelled. He says a subpoena is barred by executive privilege, and he wants to not. Willis issued a fairly blistering letter to Kemp`s office. How do you think -- see that resolving?\par \par \uc2 [20:25:08]\par \par \uc2 FLEMING: So, I think much like what we`ve seen in some of the other venues, courts are recognizing that the D.A. has broad powers for investigation. And while there may be some areas that she has to stay away from, generally these witnesses are being called to appear and answer questions in the areas that she is fully within her right and her role to investigate and explore.\par \par \uc2 And as you heard her say in the opening, this is a very important matter. And so, she`s taking her job very seriously and she`s a tough prosecutor. She is used to these types of challenges, and she`ll continue to fight them to ensure that the special grand jury gets all of the evidence that it needs to make its recommendation.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. we should note, of course, that Kemp was contacted I think twice if I`m recalling this correctly by the -- by the ex-President, right, in seeking to overturn the election similar to the lobbying that he`s doing with Raffensperger. So, he clearly has direct -- he`s a -- he`s a witness to what happened. It`s not like this is a fishing expedition. In fact, I think much of -- much of this investigation happened so publicly. To your point, do you think it helps \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  to have such an extensive public record of what was happening in Georgia at the time when she`s attempting to get these people to come before and comply to subpoenas?\par \par \uc2 FLEMING: Well, generally, prosecutors love when they have contemporaneous videotapes or audio tapes of things that have happened. It certainly makes the case much easier than having to rely on witnesses who are recalling from memory or multiple witnesses that view things differently.\par \par \uc2 Now, every juror will still view those tapes and videos differently if it gets that far, but the fact that so much has happened in the public record, I think her team is seeing that as a benefit.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, you were -- you were the district attorney for a neighboring county and I`m curious how you view this because I think there was -- when I go back and watch that interview where \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  says like, people thought this game, it`s not a game. There`s a little bit of this sense I think people have that like, is it really going to be a district attorney from a county in Georgia that`s the one that brings the criminal charge against the ex-president? And the way that she`s conducted herself is like this is -- I`m doing my job here. And I wonder what you think about that institutional dynamic at play?\par \par \uc2 FLEMING: Well, as a former state prosecutor, I`m going to say don`t block my colleagues. We always step up to the challenge when it`s presented to us. And again, very often we don`t get to choose our cases. Things happen. And as we evaluate the facts about those things, if there is a violation of the law, it`s our sworn duty to investigate. And if there`s sufficient evidence for the charges or indictment, it`s our obligation to do that.\par \par \uc2 That is what it means to have integrity and prosecution. And again, all of us at the time, swore an oath not to be influenced by fear or favor or affection. So, we look at the law, we look at the facts, apply the two and make a decision as to what charges are appropriate based on that merger.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Gwen Keyes Fleming, thank you so much for your time tonight.\par \par \uc2 FLEMING: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, we haven`t heard from the January 6 Committee in a few weeks, but their work is definitely not finished. Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  tells me what the committee has been up to next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I know you`re interviewed by the January 6th Commission, what can you share about your lovely time with them?\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE OF \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : You know, they were -- they were chasing witches, right? This was -- this was a far field wandering interview. I spoke the truth to them. I can`t tell you the details, Laura. They were looking for discussions about the 25th Amendment discussions that I never seriously undertook with any of my counterparts. They were -- they were looking to build a storyline, the conclusion of which was already written.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Did you catch that? I hope you caught that. That was Trump`s Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  discussing his interview with the January 6th committee, in which he says he never seriously discussed the 25th Amendment. Seriously doing a lot of work there because it seems like Pompeo sure is admitting to have at least having some discussions with colleagues about removing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from office.\par \par \uc2 The January 6 committee has been making its rounds speaking with Trump cabinet officials, including most recently, the ex-president`s Transportation Secretary \uc2 Elaine Chao\uc2  is also the wife of Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . There`s also Trump`s Treasury Secretary \uc2 Steve Mnuchin\uc2 . The committee has said that new hearings are coming next month when their work starts back up.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  of Maryland serves on the January 6th committee, he was the lead impeachment manager during Donald Trump`s second impeachment trial, and he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Raskin, I know, your committee spoke with \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , as he confirmed just there. I`m just wondering if the word seriously jumped out to you as well?\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): Yes, that definitely jumped out to me. I suppose in hindsight, everything looks like it was trivial or frivolous. But there were definitely serious discussions going on about the 25th Amendment.\par \par \uc2 And certainly, there were in the legislative branch, we don`t forget the concurrent context of this was that the House of Representatives voted in a strong majority vote to call upon the vice president and the cabinet to meet in order to consider transferring the powers of the president to Vice President Pence for the last several weeks of the administration, precisely because we felt he could not successfully conduct the powers and duties of office, which is the standard under Section 4, 25th Amendment.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:22]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I haven`t spoken to you obviously since your colleague on the committee \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  lost her primary in Wyoming in resounding and definitive fashion. Obviously, she`s given a number of interviews.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of that, I know that you`ve developed a strong and close relationship with Congresswoman Cheney. I`m curious your reaction to seeing it in black and white that this is what the Republican voters of Wyoming want?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, in our business, they say that defeat is a far profounder experience than victory. And I`m certain that Liz has done a lot of soul searching. And it`s tough to go from a situation where you`re, you know, winning by three-quarters of the vote in your primary, and then, you know, you`re struggling to get, you know, a quarter of the vote. I don`t know quite exactly where it ended up. But I`m sure she had a lot to think about.\par \par \uc2 But on the other hand, Liz is a constitutional patriot, even with all of our other very substantial differences on a lot of policy questions. And I think that she views that race as one battle in what is going to be a sustained and protracted political engagement within the Republican Party to fight for -- to fight for the Republican Party being a party that respects the rule of law and the Constitution. And I think she`s taken in for the long haul.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Speaking of that, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  was at an event earlier this week in New Hampshire, one of those like I`m going to run for president events that happened in New Hampshire. He was asked about in treaties from the committee and how he received in treaties from the committee to testify. He said he would consider them but also mentioned his "constitutional duty".\par \par \uc2 Here`s what your colleague \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  had to say in a recent interview about speaking with Pence, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): He has clearly as he`s expressed, concerns about executive privilege, but I also think that when the country has been through something, as grave as this was, everyone who has information has an obligation to step forward. So, I would hope that he will do that.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2 , ABC NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: So, you think we`ll see him here in September in this room?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: I would hope that -- well, I would hope that he will understand how important it is for the American people to know every aspect of the truth about what happened that day.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Does the committee want to hear from Pence, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, I don`t know that the committee has, you know, pronounced definitively on this question. In general, our philosophy has been we want to hear from everybody who`s got anything to contribute.\par \par \uc2 And as you know, we`ve done more than a thousand interviews. And, you know, everybody`s coming in from lots of different directions. And we have to weigh the credibility of evidence, of course, and we have to weigh the meaning of evidence.\par \par \uc2 But I think, you know, speaking for myself, I would like to hear what -- you know what Vice President Pence has to say on a whole range of questions. And I understand that certainly today, he`s very much a political actor. He`s looking at running for president.\par \par \uc2 And so, we would have to read his testimony through that prism. But, you know, he would be sworn under oath if he came to testify to us. And I would be very interested to hear what he`s got to say on a whole number of things that have come up over the course of our investigation.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Just quickly, do you think that the concerns about privilege or that there`s some constitutional bar to him talking to the committee are legitimate?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: No, I`ve not heard any. I mean, there`s no -- I mean, the president -- the president certainly has no power to keep the vice president from testifying about truthfully about things that have happened. And so, I`m not aware of any constitutional privilege that would either keep him from testifying or entitle him not to testify.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, the basic -- the basic principle that everybody`s got to start off with under the American rule of law is that everybody owes the sovereign government his or her truthful testimony, when subpoenaed to come forward when asked to come testify.\par \par \uc2 So, there are certainly certain discreet exceptions to that, but it`s really an extreme cases. And all of that is based on some countervailing compelling necessity.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:02]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 , thank you so much for making some time with us, I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Thanks for having me, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, Florida`s wannabe Trump Governor cracks down on voting. How \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is rolling back the clock on voting rights in the Sunshine State, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This morning, a judge in Michigan blocked the enforcement of a 1931 abortion ban statewide, that near century-old law put abortion access for Michigan residents in question of course after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in June.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:08]\par \par \uc2 Now, the reason the court blocked it was partly due to Democratic governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , who filed suit and has been working to prevent the law from going into effect.\par \par \uc2 Michigan is one of those states and there are a few like Pennsylvania, Wisconsin where there`s a gubernatorial election this fall and where the outcome of that gubernatorial election will almost certainly determine straight up whether abortion care is legal in that state or not.\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin Democratic Governor \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  is fighting his state`s 1849 abortion ban. Evers Republican opponent \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2  who donated a substantial amount from his personal wealth to the anti-abortion rights groups in the state, told local media the 173-year-old law is just fine with him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TIM MICHELS (R), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE OF WISCONSIN: The 1849 Law is an exact mirror of my position, and my position is an exact mirror of the 1849 law as well, which has an exception for life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But you wouldn`t support exceptions for rape or incest?\par \par \uc2 MICHELS: That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In Pennsylvania, the Republican Trump candidate for governor \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  has been a rabid opponent of abortion rights. He`s been fighting access since he first entered the state Senate back in 2019.\par \par \uc2 Since Roe was overturned, Mastriano has been a bit muted on the subject which could be because of the numbers. A poll conducted just before Roe was overturned found that an overwhelming majority of Pennsylvanians favorite keeping abortion legal in at least some circumstances, just 14 percent saying it should be fully illegal.\par \par \uc2 After the pro-abortion rights upset in Kansas earlier this month, a lot of attention now being paid to an increase in voter registrations in the wake of the Supreme Court`s decision.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 Tom Bonier\uc2 , who`s a Democratic strategist and elections data analyst: "In Pennsylvania women accounted for more than 56 percent of new registrants with a wide majority of them registering as Democrats. Most are also under the age of 25."\par \par \uc2 There are still 80 days until the midterm election, but the data shows us anything, it`s that voters really do grasp exactly what is at stake.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This week, Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida called a press conference to announce with great fanfare, the first arrest by his new Office of Election Crimes and Security.\par \par \uc2 They are charging 20 Floridians in a state of 21 million people with the crime of voted while be -- voting while being a convicted felon.\par \par \uc2 And I got to say, the entire display was pretty disgusting. And even more so when you consider the context here. OK?\par \par \uc2 Back in 2018, a bipartisan multiracial coalition came together to support changing Florida`s constitution, which permanently barred citizens convicted of a felony from voting, it was the strictest felonies restriction in the nation.\par \par \uc2 Their only recourse was an appeal to the state clemency board and what in - - basically, it was worse than almost any other state in the nation. It affected 1.7 million people in the state, one in five black Floridians of voting age.\par \par \uc2 And we covered the campaign for what became known as Amendment 4. The month before the election, I went down to Orlando, talk to some of the advocates including musician John Legend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEGEND, MUSICIAN: It`s really a bipartisan issue. And it`s polling in a bipartisan way. All kinds of people around the state agree that folks shouldn`t be punished forever for a crime they commit.\par \par \uc2 This is not fair that people are being punished for the rest of their lives for something they did when they were young. And something they`ve already paid whatever debt society has deemed they should pay.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, in November, the voters of Florida, the same voters who elected \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  as governor overwhelmingly approved the amendment to get rid of the voting restrictions for most felons.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A large contingent here to support Amendment 4, an amendment that tonight passed was more than 60 percent of the vote, and will change the face of the Florida electorate for years to come.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, my God, we changed Florida forever. What a statement for democracy. This is one of the largest expansions of the right to vote in the history of the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Amazing story, good for democracy, good for Florida, should have been the end of it. But no, no, no, no, because almost immediately, Republicans got to work. They didn`t like what the people had to say. So they wanted to get it.\par \par \uc2 In June of 2019, Governor DeSantis signed a bill banning citizens with past felony convictions for voting unless they paid off so-called legal financial obligations, which can include court fines, fees and restitutions. And if they couldn`t pay, guess what? They couldn`t vote.\par \par \uc2 Now, a contingent of civil rights and voting rights groups filed suit challenging the law. But the state of Florida won on appeal, leaving them free to essentially gut the amendment that more than 60 percent of voters clearly wanted.\par \par \uc2 And now, what remains in the wake of this is a ridiculous overly complicated system. As \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  points out, Florida has no centralized database to allow people to figure out what legal financial obligations they owe to the state.\par \par \uc2 The state also does not track information for federal out-of-state convictions, which people are also required to pay off before voting.\par \par \uc2 And then, now on top of this mess that they have created, Governor DeSantis has written in to establish a new special police force to hunt down former felons who made the mistake of casting a ballot.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): The state of Florida has charged and is in the process of arresting 20 individuals across the state for voter fraud.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:02]\par \par \uc2 They did not get their rights restored. And yet, they went ahead and voted anyways, that is against the law and now they`re going to pay the price for it. This is a third-degree felony in the state of Florida. They could face a $5,000 fine and up to five years in prison for illegally voting in our elections.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, look at that tough guy. Look at tough guy \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  with all the cops behind him, he`s going to hunt down those felons that voted and throw them in jail for five years.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis pointed out these 20 people were convicted of murder or felony sexual events, crimes that were excluded from Amendment 4 to be clear.\par \par \uc2 But also, you can pretty easily imagine how it may not be clear who is and is not eligible to have their rights restored after the governor Republican of the state created this convoluted and callous process totally against the will of voters.\par \par \uc2 And of course, what he`s doing here is making an example these 20 citizens, and it is meant obviously to dissuade other former felons even if they think they are eligible from voting, because they get it wrong. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  wants them to know that they will be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Desmond Meade. He`s the president of the Florida Rights Restoration Coalition, who had his right to vote restored in 2018 after he led the effort to pass Amendment 4. It`s great to see you again, Desmond.\par \par \uc2 Is your interpretation of this announcement by \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is the same as mine? Do you see this as an effort to essentially scare people off of voting?\par \par \uc2 DESMOND MEADE, PRESIDENT, FLORIDA RIGHTS RESTORATION COALITION: Well, thank you so much for having me, Chris. You know, when I look -- when I look at this announcement, what I see more than anything else is the state`s failure to do its job. Right?\par \par \uc2 What I see is a direct contrast to what we`ve seen when we pass Amendment 4, you know, and as you rightfully said earlier, you know, people were excited, you know, and it was people from across the aisle from all walks of life and all political persuasions that was happy that we were now -- people like me, were able to have a second chance to be able to participate in democracy.\par \par \uc2 The other piece to that was that when we did have an opportunity to register for the first time in January of 2019, supervisors of elections across the entire state was celebrating along with us, because there is this concept that the state should encourage voter participation, a state should encourage to participate in their democracy, because that`s good for everyone, right.\par \par \uc2 But that announcement yesterday really highlighted the fact that the state have not held up its end of the bargain, because those individuals that the governor is talking about were people that should have never been given a voter identification card.\par \par \uc2 And if the state would have had it system fix, and they`ve had many years to fix it, we can go as far back as the Bush-Gore election, where this was highlighted when people were erroneously removed from the voting rolls, that there was a problem there, right and the state have yet to fix it.\par \par \uc2 And it`s disheartening to see that the state can come up with money for election integrity police, but cannot come up with funding and the necessary systems to fix it on the front end. States should make it easier for its citizens to be able to participate in elections, and its citizens should be assured, so they have some guarantee.\par \par \uc2 If you can`t rely on the state to determine your voter eligibility, then who can you rely on?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s a really great point, which I hadn`t considered, right. If you go to register to vote, and they let you register to vote, I don`t think it`s crazy for you to assume that you`re allowed to register to vote, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you -- if you show up the DMV, and you`re 13, right? Like, they`d be like, no, sorry, come back, you`re too young.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s not crazy that there`s an anticipation that the system of the state would do the screening about who and who isn`t eligible?\par \par \uc2 MEADE: Chris, you know, and the main reason why I agreed to come on tonight was to make sure in the face of this political back and forth, that the voices of the people are not lost.\par \par \uc2 Right before I came on with you, I received an e-mail from a wife of a 50- year-old who voted for the first time ever. This person now is arrested, right.\par \par \uc2 But at the time that they registered the vote, they were led to believe that everything was OK. And this woman talked about how excited her husband was to be able to vote for the first time in his life.\par \par \uc2 I know about that excitement, because I experienced that myself. And so, when we have people who just generally want to be of some -- a part of something greater, and people who rely on state officials to make that determination.\par \par \uc2 Because let`s be clear, Chris, the burden is on the state of Florida to determine whether or not someone is eligible before they issue a voter identification card.\par \par \uc2 And so, if people are given the impression by state officials that it`s OK for them to vote, and they go ahead and vote because they feel it`s their civic duty. They go ahead and vote because they feel that it`s a great feeling to be a part of something greater.\par \par \uc2 How dare the state respond by arresting them, right? How dare the state respond by scaring people from wanting to be a part of this democracy?\par \par \uc2 [21:00:08]\par \par \uc2 The responsibility is on the state to fix the system on the front end, and they need to do it now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There are millions of dollars allocated to this election a unit that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has announced. It would be good to get some of that money on the front end as Desmond Meade points out. Desmond, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 T