day -- I appreciate the generosity.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Alex.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Well, Donald Trump`s life is a disaster zone now, a legal disaster zone. The toxic waste of his life in business and his life in the White House is drowning Donald Trump`s hope for happier days in the final chapter of his life.\par \par \uc2 Every day gets worse for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Every day is worse than the day before.\par \par \uc2 What happened to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in a courtroom in Manhattan today would have been the worst thing that has happened in Donald Trump`s life if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had never been president. Donald Trump`s accountant, Allen Weisselberg, the man who handled the money going in and going out of Donald Trump`s accounts pled guilty today for 15 felonies involving that money, 15 financial crimes. All of those crimes were committed to benefit \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in some way. Some indirect ways.\par \par \uc2 Nothing as bad as that has happened in Donald Trump`s business life, including Donald Trump`s multiple business bankruptcies. It obviously brings \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  much closer to facing criminal charges for many of the same financial crimes. But Donald Trump`s legal life is so bad now that that is not our lead story tonight.\par \par \uc2 We will cover the Weisselberg guilty plea and what it means for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in our next segment.\par \par \uc2 But we begin tonight with an even more important story of the federal criminal charges that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could face under the Espionage Act. Today, a Florida federal judge magistrate held a hearing on motions by some of these organizations, including, according to "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " and NBC News, to unseal the \uc2 FBI\uc2  affidavit that was presented to that same judge to convince him to authorize a search warrant of Donald Trump`s Florida home. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " asked the judge to release the entire \uc2 FBI\uc2  affidavit.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has publicly pretended to be in favor of releasing the entire affidavit but his lawyers did not file a motion with the judge asking the judge to release the affidavit. That is the truth, that is the truth of the Trump position, and that is the proof that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows that releasing any part of the affidavit will be very damaging to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is playing a game here, he is pretending publicly that he wants every word of the affidavit released so that he can complain when it is not released or when there are redactions in the released affidavit. When that happens, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will say it is a cover-up, about what is really going on here. These reductions are a cover-up. That the reductions are hiding what`s really involved in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  searching his home.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has proved that he does not want anything about the search warrant or the affidavit released because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been able to release some of that information and he did not. He was in position of the search warrant for a full week until the court released last week and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  released that -- refused the entire week to make the search warrant public.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was also in possession of the inventory of material that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  took from his home. And he refused to make that public. He held it for one week. The inventory showed that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had classified information removed from his home, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  refused to make that public until the Justice Department asked the judge to make a public, along with the search warrant. That`s how it became public.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  now claims to have surveillance video of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of his home. And he is refusing to release that video. Now, remember, it`s entirely possible that no video exists. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is, and always has, been a pathological liar. So he could just be lying, and there is no video.\par \par \uc2 But, if, for just this moment, we take Donald Trump`s word that he has surveillance video of everything the \uc2 FBI\uc2  did in his home, the only reason \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has not released that video already is that that video will be very harmful to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows. It this program will not waste one second of your time playing the Trump game of pretending that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants the affidavit made public.\par \par \uc2 After considering the Department of Justice argument today, to keep all the affidavits sealed, the judge said that he is inclined, that was his word, inclined, to release some of it and order the Justice Department to spend next week redacting information from the affidavit to make sure that public release of the affidavit will not harm the ongoing investigations where the Justice Department said today the investigation in Florida was in its early stages.\par \par \uc2 If the judge approves the redactions submitted by the Justice Department next Thursday, the judge could release the redacted version of the affidavit that.\par \par \uc2 Today, the judge released two more pages of documents concerning the search warrant including the cover page of the application for a search ward which indicated that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  believed that the execution of the search warrant would find evidence of a crime and find contraband, fruits of crime, or other items illegally possessed. The application said, quote, the search is related to a violation of willful retention of national defense information, concealment or removal of government records, and obstruction of federal investigation.\par \par \uc2 Leading off hour discussion tonight, Andrew Weissmann, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  general counsel and former chief of the criminal division in the Eastern District of New York. He is professor of practice at \uc2 NYU\uc2  law school and an MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 Also with us, Ben Rhodes, former deputy national security to President Obama, and MSNBC political analyst. He is the author of "After the Fall: Being American and the World We`ve Made".\par \par \uc2 And, Andrew, your assessment of what we have learned in this hearing today about the possible release of the affidavit and what you expect a week from now.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: I think the judge made the right call here. I think the weakness in the government`s position was that they only addressed the footnote, the issue of redaction. And they simply told the judge that their opinion, a redacted version of the affidavit would-be not helpful to the public, we would be virtually meaningless.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, this judge has actually seen the fully unredacted affidavit. So he was able to sort of take a look at bat and presumably came to the conclusion that the pieces of it that wouldn`t harm national security, wouldn`t harm the ongoing criminal investigation, and wouldn`t identify witnesses. I think those are categories that I think he`s still going to be very protective of, and I`m sure the government is going to propose redactions in those areas.\par \par \uc2 But having said, that that does leave other information that we still could learn such as, the back and forth between the Department of Justice and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his lawyers and representatives in trying to get the documents back. And I think that`s the kind of thing that the judge might be looking at saying, why should we get that? That certain something that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and a lot of people know about, it doesn`t seem that sensitive.\par \par \uc2 So, it would be interesting to see, what exactly the government`s position is going to be. Are they going to sort of get their backup and think they should try to appeal this and not sort of accommodate the judge. I don`t think that`s what they are going to do, I`m not positive, but I think they are going to try to propose redactions that are strictly in those three areas.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:04]\par \par \uc2 And then I think we are going to see an unredacted portion -- unredacted portions of the affidavit.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Ben Rhodes, as we sit here tonight, since we discovered that there was an \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of Donald Trump`s home, and that it was for classified material, and that, indeed, classified material was obtained in that search, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his representatives have not said a single word about why that material was in his home.\par \par \uc2 BEN RHODES, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yeah, Lawrence, I mean, that`s really the glaring question here. We have confirmed that he had highly sensitive, highly classified information that the top secret and top secret compartmented information level. This is the highest level of hesitation in the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We know, despite what Trump has said, that this information is still classified, or else we would not be talking about redaction and the government not wanting to release that information. What we don`t know is why on earth he felt necessary to pack up and take with him boxes of classified information, to keep it at Mar-a-Lago, which is not a secure location, and to not return it to the government, even after they ordered him to do so.\par \par \uc2 And so, we are left to wonder here, the why. Why keep this information? Just taking it is illegal. Just taking it is violating every protocol as it comes to the classified custody of classified information.\par \par \uc2 Was he seeking to have this for nostalgic purposes? Was he seeking to have this to show people who are coming over to Mar-a-Lago? Was he seeking to have it so he could profit off of it personally in some fashion? Was he seeking it so he could have some leverage over someone else?\par \par \uc2 Every one of those explanations doesn`t justify the illegality and the compromising of national security for him taking that information. So, this is the piece that has yet to be filled in, the motive that he had for taking and keeping this information even after he was instructed to return it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Andrew, some of the crimes being investigated here do not seem to require or contain a motive component. There are certain things that are simply illegal to possess. And it doesn`t matter what your motivation is for possessing them. These documents, some of them, seemed to fall in that category.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Absolutely. Motive is not something that the Justice Department would be required to prove. And very often, you don`t know the motive, whether, as Ben said, it is to monetize, for power, or some other reason. And it could be a whole range of reasons, because there are a whole range of documents. It is also not require that the documents be classified.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it is highly suspect, the claim that these were -- there was a standing order. I am sure that the government is going to get to the bottom of it. And I should say, the unredacted piece that we might get, something that I am curious about is, if Donald Trump`s defense is, oh, this was material that I declassified, you think he would have told that to the government when they said, please return the classified information. He would have said, wait a second, there was a standing order.\par \par \uc2 I am confident, I`m sure as we are all sitting here talking, that that did not happen. Because his defense at the time was, I don`t have classified information, I returned everything. That was the problem, that he completely lied about what he had. And said he returned it all.\par \par \uc2 So, he has got a very difficult case. And I personally think, the more he sort of trot out these sorts of defenses, the better it is for the government trial.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Ben, with your experience national security, what sort of things in material documents -- including, by the way, one document referred to as, information about the president of France. That is one thing we saw on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  inventory. What are your biggest concerns about what you have learned about what was found at Donald Trump`s home?\par \par \uc2 RHODES: The biggest concerns I have is the level of classification being top secret and top secret SCI because this means that that is information that is derived from sensitive intelligence collection or national defense information of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 . So what do I mean by that? It means it is not just state report, that you are describing with the intelligence community is happening somewhere. It is a report that makes reference to or was derived from very sensitive methods of intelligence collection.\par \par \uc2 So, sources -- human sources -- could be involved in the gathering of information, in those documents. Technical sources of collection could be involved in the gathering of information for those documents. Movements of the United States military intelligence assets could be in those documents.\par \par \uc2 This is also part of the reason why these declassification argument is so laughable on its face. First of all, it`s contradictory. He said famously that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  might have planted some information, but if they did, I declassified it. It doesn`t make any sense. Typical Trump gaslighting -- but he clearly did not notify, or any process to notify the director of national intelligence, or the CIA director. There is no record of him declassifying this information.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not as simple as him, I am declassifying boxes, because the documents in those boxes, make reference, presumably, the sensitive national intelligence collection or sensitive defense information of the \uc2 United States government\uc2 . So, he`s not just the classifying talking about declassify boxes, he`s talking about, are we going to notify the people running those sources of information, or human sources, or people involved in military movements?\par \par \uc2 The declassification argument doesn`t really hold any water, except for Trump to have some talking point to make. And what I am talking concerned about is, again, not even motive, just having that degree of lack of security around information that could make reference to how the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  conducts national events or intelligence operations. There is a reason why you don`t let this information leave secure government facilities. And there is a very strict protocol amount around managing this information. There is a reason, Lawrence, all of those documents are stamped top secret on every single page. This is not something you could make a mistake and not know that the document is sensitive.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Andrew Weissmann, we didn`t learn much in this hearing, it wasn`t designed for us to learn much. But one thing we did learn is this phrase that the federal prosecutors used, saying that the investigation involving that evidence that Donald Trump`s home is in its early stages. That was the phrase they used, in its early stages.\par \par \uc2 What did that mean to you?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: So, it means two things. I think, from a national securities perspective, that is, the part of the investigation that is going to be checking to see, how they recovered everything and tried to determine, and has it been disseminated in any way to anyone outside of Mar-a-Lago -- that that is something that really is starting, now that they have the documents back, from a criminal perspective, I also think that they are going to trace all of these ludicrous defenses, because if you are going to bring a case, you want to make sure you can track that down -- high on my list would be Kash Patel, who is claiming that he was there for this.\par \par \uc2 To me, that is like raising your hand for a grand jury subpoena, so that you can now testify under oath. And they can hear that story and assess it. And they can hear so, I think there are a lot of witnesses that are going to be talking to in light of the documents they have seen, and a lot to do. I am not sure infancy might be -- I was a little surprised by that. I think it is probably a bit further than that. But I think that just is right.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Ben Rhodes, thank you for sharing your national security perspective on this story tonight, thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 And, Andrew Weissmann, we are going to need you to stick around for the next legal story about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And that is coming up.\par \par \uc2 And that is, what is next for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  now that the chief financial officer of Donald Trump`s company, who has been with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  throughout Donald Trump`s entire business career, he`s now, as of today, a convicted felon who is going to testify -- has vowed to testify truthfully -- against Donald Trump`s business. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:23:24]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And so today, Donald Trump`s accountant`s life of crime ended in a courtroom in Manhattan when he said one word, yes.\par \par \uc2 Allen Weisselberg said yes to the question of changing his plea from not guilty to guilty. After the judge in the case had guided Allen Weisselberg through each count of the 15 fell needs that Allen Weisselberg had agreed to plead guilty to in exchange for leniency sentence of less than six months.\par \par \uc2 After the judge made sure that Allen Weisselberg understood all of the legal implications of a guilty plea, the clerk of the court spoke to directly to the defendant saying, Allen Weisselberg, do you now withdraw your previously entered plea of not guilty, and now plead guilty to count one, scheme to defraud, count two, conspiracy, count, three grand larceny, count four, five, and six, criminal tax fraud, count seven, criminal tax fraud, count nine -- eight, nine, ten, and 11 offering of false -- count 15, falsifying business records. To which Allen Westbrook said, yes.\par \par \uc2 The prosecution agreed to accept Allen Weisselberg`s guilty plea with the condition, quote, the defendant must agree to testify truthfully at the trial of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The judge went over this point repeatedly with Alan Weisselberg saying, you have agreed to testify truthfully at the trial of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , to which Weisselberg said, "That is correct."\par \par \uc2 [22:25:07]\par \par \uc2 Before accepting Allen Weisselberg`s guilty plea, the judge came back to that point, more emphatically, saying this time, if you violate a condition of this plea agreement, I will not permit you to withdraw your plea and I will be at liberty to impose any lawful sentence, which in this case would include a period of imprisonment up to 5 to 15 years. For example, if you fail to testify truthfully at the upcoming trial of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , I would then not be bound by my sentence promise, I would not permit you to withdraw your plea of guilty, and I would be at liberty to impose any lawful sentence, which again, in your case, would include imprisonment up to 5 to 15 years. Do you understand?\par \par \uc2 The defendant, I do.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Weissmann is back with, us and joining us is Tim O`Brien. He is senior executive editor for "\uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  Opinion" and author of Trump nation. He is an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 Andrew, let me begin with you, and one of the mistakes that has quickly floated out there about, this and the fact that "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " fell for it in their reporting, and that is the notion that Andrew -- that Weisselberg has reached an agreement with prosecutors, that he will tell the truth about the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , and that is, it nothing, more he will not testify one word against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . As if that is within Weisselberg`s power, to not honestly answer questions about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Clarify this for us, please?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Yes, I think the times got that a little bit wrong. I think what is true is that Allen Weisselberg is not required to give the government information about facts unrelated to the current indictment against him and against the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . But he is required to testify truthfully, if called as a witness, of the Trump organization trial, scheduled for October 24th. And that date is highly unlikely to move.\par \par \uc2 At that trial, the judge, as you noted, repeatedly said, you have to testify truthfully. So it is going to be difficult, not impossible, but difficult for Allen Weisselberg to come up with a story that doesn`t mention the T word. This is a small organization. And, we are talking about a 15-year scheme.\par \par \uc2 He would have to say, under oath, that this payroll scheme happened but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  never knew anything about it. That all the people involved deliberately kept it from him. Why would even do that? Because he`s the beneficiary of it. I mean, this is something you actually -- he might actually like and be proud of the fact that you figured out scheme to save him money.\par \par \uc2 But it seems like that`s the conundrum if he is going to go down the root of trying to not implicate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The final point I would like to make his, the lawyers here for Allen Weisselberg, they are real lawyers. These are people who are representing Allen Weisselberg and just him.\par \par \uc2 So I am confident that before he testifies, there are going to be stressing to him the need to just tell the truth and the ramifications if he doesn`t.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Tim O`Brien, I think I first heard the words Allen Weisselberg from you because you know more about the Trump business than other reporters out there. You know more than most. So you`ve been reporting on this for years. You know these players, you know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you know the Weisselberg relationship to the whole entity.\par \par \uc2 What are the chances that Allen Weisselberg never once, never once, had a conversation with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  about any one of these 15 felonies?\par \par \uc2 TIM O`BRIEN, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: I think zero, Lawrence, absolutely zero. There were no financial decisions of substance made in the Trump organization whether they pertain to deals or compensation or anything else that involved money going out the door that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did not sign off of, in word if not on paper.\par \par \uc2 And there were only two people in that organizations outside of the children who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would say make sure both of them have signed off on this before I do it. One was Allen Weisselberg, the other was \uc2 Jason Greenblatt\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [22:29:40]\par \par \uc2 And it`s still strange to me that Jason Greenblatt`s name hasn`t surfaced in all this. He`s a general counsel at the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . Donald would always turn to Allen and Jason, or to -- get a stamp of approval on anything.\par \par \uc2 I think Jeff McConney (ph) and Matthew Calamari`s names will also come up in the future. Having said all of this, I think that, you know, as Andrew pointed out, the idea that Allen Weisselberg is not cooperating is true only in the narrowest sense.\par \par \uc2 He now has to go to trial with this sort of sword hanging over his head that if he is found to be lying he`d get 15 -- as much as 15 years in prison. So he`s over the coals now. And there might as well be a cooperation agreement in place because he is now going to, I think, be a material witness against the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The other thing to remember is that the Trump organization has been in financial trouble over the last two years. It has an enormous amount of debt hanging over the company. It`s been battered by the COVID lockdowns. It`s in the worst businesses you could imagine being in during COVID. Urban real estate and essentially tourism and entertainment.\par \par \uc2 And Trump has had every reason to try to dodge corners financially. I think there`s a real possibility that the Manhattan D.A.`s investigation combined with the New York state attorney general`s civil investigation could put the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  out of business.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: So, Tim, the new risk that Weisselberg faces, if he wants to somehow protect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , would be the risk of perjury. Is Weisselberg, at age 75, prepared to risk perjury, which would then cost him a few years in prison, at minimum?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, predicating this with, you know, I cannot get into his head, so I don`t know for sure. But anyone who has spent any time around \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows that loyalty is a one-way street in his universe. He demands extreme loyalty from everyone around him. But it never goes in the other direction.\par \par \uc2 And I can`t imagine that Allen Weisselberg, at this stage in his life, is going to want to wind up in an orange jumpsuit to show that he is loyal to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I just don`t see that happening.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Tim O`Brien and Andrew Weissmann, thank you both very much for joining us tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up, the title of Charles Blow`s new column for the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is, "Republicans are America`s problem". Charles Blow joins us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:32:35]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Our next guest, Charles Blow`s new column for the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ", says that Liz Cheney`s loss in the Wyoming Republican primary this week shows, as the title of his piece says, Republicans are America`s problem.\par \par \uc2 Charles Blow writes, "Her loss does crystalize something for us that many had already known. That the bar to clear in the modern Republican Party isn`t being sufficiently conservative, but rather being sufficiently obedient to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his quest to deny and destroy democracy. We must stop thinking it hyperbolic to say that the Republican Party itself is now a threat to our democracy.\par \par \uc2 I understand the queasiness about labeling many of our fellow Americans in that way. I understand that it sounds extreme and overreaching. But how else are we to describe what we are seeing?"\par \par \uc2 Joining our discussion now is Charles Blow, columnist for the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ", and an MSNBC political analyst. Charles, thank you very much for joining us again tonight.\par \par \uc2 You have said what has been clear for a very long time, the problem is the voters. They are the people who delivered \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the electoral college victory. They are the people who believe his lies. They are the people who have gone beyond his lies, into wilder beliefs in the QAnon space that even \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t advocate.\par \par \uc2 But there is a news convention -- and I think it is beyond the convention, I think it is built into the very mindset of so many people in the news business -- that you must never even think that the voters are a problem. It has to be something else. It cannot be them.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BLOW, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Absolutely. And you know, I am the first to say that propaganda is big and misinformation is big. They are real things. And they do lead some people a stray.\par \par \uc2 But this is not what is happening here with conservatism in this country, and particularly in this moment. Conservatism in this country has always been against democracy. They have never wanted full democracy and always pushed against it.\par \par \uc2 We never even had anything approaching real democracy until 1870, with the passage of the 15th Amendment, which finally gave black men the right to vote -- but women in general still didn`t have the right to vote. That didn`t happen until 1920, with the passage of the 19th Amendment, right?\par \par \uc2 [22:39:50]\par \par \uc2 BLOW: But by that time, Jim Crow is already raging and black people were basically disenfranchised in the American South where most of them live. So, you didn`t get back to something that approached democracy until 1965, with the passage of the Voting Rights Act. 48 years later the Supreme Court goes in and guts that, right.\par \par \uc2 So all along conservatism always wanted Americans` electoral system to be less than democratic. What we have now, though, in the last, you know, 20 - - 30 years is people casting about, looking for another vehicle that says that I can latch onto and give that my anti-democratic fervor.\par \par \uc2 And for a long time, there were (INAUDIBLE) people who said you have to be more moderate. They have latched on to the Reagans and the Bushes.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  comes along and says, you can actually do your anti- democratic thing without having to moderate, that you can go headfirst into it and be your full self. And unapologetically say that you do not want to be a democracy, and that is ok.\par \par \uc2 And that is what we are seeing now. And right now, the Conservative Party is the Republican Party. And that is what they have become, the threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 I don`t really care if people are squeamish about it. I don`t care if they think it is hyperbolic to say that. I don`t care if people say, well, you are pushing us toward civil war if you say half the country is problematic.\par \par \uc2 Well, they are. If you were answering on a poll that you don`t believe that someone who won the election who actually did, and you don`t believe that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  created a problem with the insurrection and what have you, you are a problem. If you are electing people who want to overturn a legitimate election, and are actually saying -- basically saying that they are not into democracy anymore, then you are the problem.\par \par \uc2 You can`t put that off on somebody else. You can`t say, oh, I listen to Fox News. I can`t listen to QAnon. It`s you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes, and registered Republicans are not half the country. They are a small minority of the country.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 O5; And the Republican Party and Republican presidential candidates from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  forward now, have no plan, no hope, no strategy of winning the most votes for the presidency. That isn`t the plan.\par \par \uc2 That was what \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  was trying to do. He was trying to win the most votes and the Electoral College. Now the plan is nothing other than how do we win the Electoral College, because we know -- we know -- we can never get more votes than the Democratic candidate. And one way to win the Electoral College is to rig it. It`s to put our new secretaries of state in the key states that will give us the Electoral College.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Right. First of all, isn`t it quaint that people use the (INAUDIBLE) with the majority of the votes.\par \par \uc2 But also, they are not just attacking it on the secretary of state level. They are attacking on every single level possible. They are attacking the people who work at the election -- the ballot offices, and work elections. And now they don`t want to do the job, right.\par \par \uc2 So, that is all the way from the bottom, all the way up to the Supreme Court gutting the civil rights act, on every single level. And now you have the Supreme Court about to take up a case about whether or not you can shift the responsibility for calling an election away from secretaries of state altogether, and give it to state legislatures, which are the epitome of political bodies and are the ones who are instituting, in many cases, the restrictive and oppressive voting laws.\par \par \uc2 So, on every single level, Republicans are trying to set up a system where they can have minority rule.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Charles Blow, thank you for saying it so clearly once again, as you always do. Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, writer Nicole Walker`s new op-ed piece in the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is titled, "My Abortion At 11 Wasn`t A Choice; It Was My Life". Nicole Walker will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:24]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: In today`s "New York Times", Nicole Walker describes what it felt like on the day the Republican judges on the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 She writes, "The world changed on June 24th, 2022. On that day, I understood the extent of what we were losing. On June 24th, I felt the prison gates fall around me, around my daughter, around everyone with a uterus."\par \par \uc2 The title of Nicole Walker`s essay in the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is, "My Abortion At 11 Wasn`t A Choice. It Was My Life". She describes being impregnated by a 14-year-old boy who was her babysitter when she was 11 years old.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:43]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Nicole Walker writes, "I did not feel lucky to get an abortion. I felt like garbage. The babysitter did not have to go to the clinic. The babysitter was not shunned and censured by our community. Most people didn`t even know what he had done, though they seemed to know something bad had happened to me, or perhaps that I had done something wrong.\par \par \uc2 Only my mom and I were subject to the shame of entering that special building for that special procedure. Although no one in the neighborhood or at school talked to me about it, I could feel the electric gossip surge around me.\par \par \uc2 The freedom to choose wasn`t what I experienced in 1983. My abortion wasn`t a choice. It was my life. If I had been forced to give birth, I would not be texting my mom from my home in the beautiful mountain town. I wouldn`t teach at the nearby university. I wouldn`t be working on a book about climate change and how to shatter predetermined destinies. I wouldn`t be married to my husband or have my two children.\par \par \uc2 My life would not have been my own. I would be a prisoner, subject to a body`s whims, and not my body`s whims but the whims of a teenage boy who, as best I can tell, experienced no consequences for inflicting what his body wanted upon my own."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is author Nicole Walker. She teaches creative writing at \uc2 Northern Arizona University\uc2 . Thank you very much for joining us tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE WALKER, AUTHOR: Thank you so much for having me, Lawrence, I am grateful to be here.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You begin your essay in "The Times" asking people to look at this, acknowledging that it is difficult. It is -- there are graphic passages in your piece that you want people to look at and consider because you believe that this subject needs to be discussed in this kind of detail.\par \par \uc2 What have you found in your own approach to talking about it is the most effective way of making people understand this?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: You know, I mean I feel like what is coming clear and coming more available, since the overturning of Roe v. Wade, is using the word "abortion".\par \par \uc2 I have friends on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  who are like, Sarah just -- is proud to have had on abortion, last name, you know. People are using this word and using being able to describe the experiences they have had, because I think there was a lot of, well, we are going to get by with the abortion system as we have it, and keep it really hush hush.\par \par \uc2 But it was not until that day, June 24th -- to me, my eyes were just wide open. I was suddenly -- it was suddenly clear to me what the difference between having control over your body and not having control over your body was.\par \par \uc2 And it was automatic and immediate. And that sense of being able to then talk about it, or that the idea of having to talk about it became abundantly clear.\par \par \uc2 I have written about what happened to me when I was a kid, obliquely, in the books I have written. I have written about it in different magazines articles. But it`s always sort of under the cover, under the guise of something else.\par \par \uc2 And I finally realized this is the time where we need to speak up and tell our stories. I want to encourage everyone to tell their stories.\par \par \uc2 You know, mine is an extreme example. What happens to me is one of those special cases that people are like, oh, yes, if we can we should make exceptions for rape or incest. But my argument is, I think, a little bit more complex, which is, you do not have your own body autonomy if you cannot decide what happens to your body. What does it even mean to be a person? It strips us, I think, of our humanity. And I feel that really deeply.\par \par \uc2 O5; You are the second woman who`s written an op-ed piece like this for the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ", about having an abortion at that age. The other has joined us on this program. And I know it is a difficult subject to write about and to discuss here.\par \par \uc2 And so I want to do this with the utmost respect for how difficult it is. You make the point in your piece -- and this is something that I have seen, I`ve seen some commentary, some comment to this effect. That some abortion rights supporters worry that devoting too much energy to the stories of young children who need abortions narrows the cause. What is your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Right. I mean to some degree, I agree that it does narrow the cause. Again, it says that, oh, well, there are these exceptions.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:50]\par \par \uc2 WALKER: The fact is that, if you look at the comments on my "Times" article today, you will see so many stories of so many young people becoming impregnated at such a young age. And that inability to imagine not accessing -- to be able to access abortion -- I don`t want to use, necessarily, this story to say that it is only for those particular cases.\par \par \uc2 But to me, the point of the article is really to say that pregnancy, for anybody, changes your life. And you are right, I cannot predict exactly what would have happened if I had been forced to give birth at age 11. But I cannot imagine that life.\par \par \uc2 And there is a stripping of the imagination that I think happens when you are forced down paths that you have no control over. And even if you are a 25-year-old woman who is in the middle of college and are on your way to graduate, it`s to me your body and your ability to decide what path you get to go on.\par \par \uc2 And you know, as a kid, it is different, right? Obviously, I did not have control over the situation. I did not have control over even just making my -- I could not explain why what happened to me has happened to me. I still have a hard time explaining what had happened to me.\par \par \uc2 But to imagine that our lives are supposed to be purely set based on what our bodies do naturally, and what our choices are limited by what our government says, it has become very striking to me and very different world that we are living in.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Nicole Walker, I am very sorry for what you suffered that brought you to this point into the show tonight. But I thank you very much for sharing your feelings about all of this with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Thank you so much, Lawrence. I`m really grateful to be here and I just, you know, in the book I`m writing, I really am encouraging more people to tell their stories. So, I hope this opportunity comes for other people as well.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 [22:57:14]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Nicole Walker gets tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020220819ei8i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for August 18, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Lester Holt \par \uc2 7,770 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Nicole Walker gets tonight`s "LAST WORD". THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, legal walls a little closer to the farmer guy, a judge considers whether there`s more he should make public about the search at Mar-a-Lago. And Trump`s former right hand financial man likely heading to notorious Rikers Island jail. Then the dangerous threat of right wing extremism, a children`s hospital in danger because of hate filled disinformation, poll workers law enforcement and now a hospital. The threat is real.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the Senate is a toss-up diminished hope for Republicans taking control. And \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  now blaming candidate quality that`s code for blame it on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , with the clock ticking towards the midterms. THE 11TH HOUR getting underway on this Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. New revelations about the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of Mar-a-Lago may soon see the light of day. Several media organizations, including NBC were in a South Florida court today asking the judge to unseal the affidavit behind last week`s search and seizure that Donald Trump`s Florida home.\par \par \uc2 Judge Bruce Reinhart told the Justice Department he is inclined to release a redacted or edited version of the affidavit. That document details what prosecutors call probable cause for evidence that brought the \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents to Trump`s home in the first place. Prosecutors want to keep the entire thing under wraps, but the judge today ordered them to list their proposed redactions within a week.\par \par \uc2 Well, the former guy and many of his allies have said they support the release of an unredacted version. And this afternoon Trump supporters who were angered by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search showed up outside the courthouse. NBC`s Cal Perry was there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: We have seen Trump supporters circling sort of the courthouse in jets with the flags, American flags, don`t tread on me flags and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  flags. The DOJ for their part talked about this, talked about the atmosphere, talked about the safety when it comes to witnesses and when it comes to what happened after the search. The political atmosphere as they put it in the court as for a reason why some of these things should stay sealed.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what was said in court quote, and this is a DOJ official quote, "This is a volatile situation with respect to this particular search address on the political spectrum," especially "with one side in particular."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Trump lawyer, Christina Bobb, was actually inside the courtroom today. However, she was just a spectator. She made no arguments, she filed no motions. She did not lift a single finger that would advance getting this affidavit unsealed. However, tonight, she was talking on television, and she was asked about that very thing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA BOBB, TRUMP ATTORNEY: We believed that judicial watch and some of the other parties actually had argued it quite well. And we believe it was very interesting and somewhat encouraging that you had parties from both sides of the aisle, of course, liberal media outlets coupled with a conservative watchdog group, really together on the same side. And we really just chose to see how it will play out. We need to wait and see. I can`t be certain at this point because we haven`t seen the affidavit, and we certainly haven`t seen the redactions and how it`s going to play out. But, you know, we`ll be making that decision as it comes out. We, you know, we got to see it. We haven`t seen it. It has been under sealed. So I don`t know we can`t say.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Trump who has been in 1000s of lawsuits, who is known to fight and fight back is going to sit back and see how this thing goes. The judge did release additional documents that were part of the search warrant that was made public last week. These noted that the search was related to quote willful retention of National Defense Information, concealment of government records and obstruction.\par \par \uc2 The Mar-a-Lago search turned up 11 sets of classified documents and sources tell NBC News that more than one week later, investigators are still sifting through everything. And that is just one of the many legal issues the former guy is dealing with right now.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there is a guilty plea in another case. The former chief financial officer for the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , 75-year-old Allen Weisselberg stood in a New York courtroom this morning and pleaded guilty to 15 felony counts. Prosecutors say he failed to pay millions in taxes and help top \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  executives do the same for more than a dozen years. He`s now facing five months in jail, millions of dollars in penalties and has to testify at the trial of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  this fall if he`s asked.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:11]\par \par \uc2 Weisselberg has been a key part of the Trump team for nearly half a century. Donald Trump`s father Fred hired Weisselberg more than 40 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Later, today, the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  issued a statement that said this in part, Mr. Weisselberg, in an effort to put this matter behind him and get on with his life, decided that the best course of action for himself and his family was to plead guilty. Jury selection and the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  trial is expected to start at the end of October.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel tonight, NBC Justice and Intelligence Correspondent Ken Dilanian, and David Fahrenthold, a New York Times Investigative Reporter covering the Trump family and its business interests, and Chuck Rosenberg, former U.S. attorney and former Senior \uc2 FBI\uc2  official.\par \par \uc2 Ken, start us off, the judge saying he`s inclined to release part of this affidavit. Is that what you`re expecting?\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, NBC NEWS JUSTICE & INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: (Technical difficulties) says we have a pending criminal investigation with grand jury information and sensitive names of witnesses, names of federal agents, investigative techniques, we need to protect that. Generally, the judge goes along. In this case, he said they didn`t meet their burden.\par \par \uc2 Let`s remember what this is. This is an affidavit that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents wore out to the judge to request a search warrant and to make the case that there was probable cause that a crime had been committed. And then evidence of that crime was present at Mar-a-Lago. So it could contain almost everything the \uc2 FBI\uc2  knows about this case. And the Justice Department said they were very concerned in particular about witnesses, and even if they -- even if witnesses aren`t named people being able to identify witnesses by their description, and that future witnesses in this case might be intimidated. And for that reason, I strongly suspect that many, many critical details if not all of them, will end up being redacted in this thing, Stephanie, because the judge is now giving the Justice Department a week to propose redactions, blacking out portions of it. And the judge actually said whether the final product makes sense or means anything to the public is not really my concern. This could end up being, he said, meaningless gibberish.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s very unlikely that we`re going to see much that`s revelatory, but I do think that because he`s taking this, Steph, he must think the judge that there are some things in that affidavit that would inform the public that can be released.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Chuck, inclined to release is not a done deal, but it is certainly not what the DOJ wanted. How did this happen?\par \par \uc2 CHUCK ROSENBERG, FORMER SENIOR \uc2 FBI\uc2  OFFICIAL: Well, that you`re right about that, Stephanie. Look, it`s a little bit surprising. I think Ken has described it perfectly. Normally when you have an ongoing investigation, and the Department of Justice said they did these affidavits remain sealed for the reasons can articulate it to protect witnesses, to protect sensitive techniques, to make sure you don`t give a roadmap to the people who are under investigation.\par \par \uc2 How did it happen? So you have two competing interests in the courtroom. Both of them were illegitimate, right? Press organizations know that there`s a great deal of public curiosity and want the stuff now, and the Department of Justice doesn`t want it out because it will undermine their investigation. But I think one of those is far more legitimate and far more compelling than the other. And that is the need to keep this affidavit under seal for now, while the investigation plays out.\par \par \uc2 I think the judge made a mistake. I think he was trying to split the baby in some way and made a mistake. I wouldn`t be shocked if the Department of Justice came back to the judge with one of two things, Stephanie, either a heavily redacted proposed release, which as Ken described would render it virtually meaningless. Or perhaps underseal. Another pleading another document filed with the judge explaining why releasing any of it would be a mistake.\par \par \uc2 And let me add one more thing to that, if I may. If the judge does something that the government simply can`t live with it, live with, they have the right to appeal it. So this process of deciding what will be unsealed if anything, could take a lot longer than you might imagine.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right. So that`s what the DOJ is doing. That`s what the judge is doing. Let`s talk about Trump`s team, Chuck. Because Trump`s lawyer was in the courtroom, though she said absolutely nothing. But she`s got lots of things to say tonight on conservative television, mocking the Department of Justice and those expected redactions, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALINA HABBA, ATTORNEY FOR FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I think that there won`t be very much transparency I would expect it will get a lot of black ink all over that piece of paper. If I was the DOJ, Biden DOJ, I`d probably be redacting everything other than the word "and" and "the."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yeah, yeah, but here`s the thing, Chuck, this is all performance on his team`s part, a big, big show she`s talking big but she sat on the sidelines today, filed no motions asked for absolutely nothing.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:11]\par \par \uc2 Why is the Trump World not formally demanding the affidavits released or doing anything about the search that they believe was unlawful? All they`re doing is talking?\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: You`re right. But they do a lot of that, Stephanie. And often it doesn`t make much sense. You played a clip from the other attorney first, Ms. Bobb. And what struck me about that, and it seemed rather disingenuous, is that she said on one hand, they wanted to see how the other side did at the argument before deciding what to do themselves. However, this, I guess, is the other hand, Stephanie. At that point, they can`t if the other side had done terribly, they`re not able then to stand up and ask to be heard, not realistically, because they hadn`t filed anything in the case in front of the judge. And so that made no sense at all, it seems to me to be, you know, sort of filling the space with noise, but not with actual legal arguments, and not putting anything at risk. In other words, if the judge rules against them, well, they didn`t participate. So they`re not at fault. And if the judge rules for them, it`s because they articulated that point of view, on net on various networks.\par \par \uc2 So seems to be a little bit of wanting to have your cake and eat it too, not taking a real position in court, understanding that the department will likely redact all of the -- as much of the document as it possibly can, and then complain loudly that, you know, they have been treated unjustly. It seems to me to be completely disingenuous.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Ken, let`s talk about the documents, the boxes and boxes of documents that were seized last week that investigators are still going through. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew he would be in trouble if he didn`t return them. Apparently he defied a subpoena that was demanding them. Does that not tell you that the contents inside those boxes were so damning that he couldn`t return them?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: I`m not sure what it tells me, Stephanie. It`s such an interesting mystery. I mean, one possibility is he was just stubborn. He`s quoted as saying in one account, these are not their documents. They`re my documents. And that`s, of course, a fundamental misunderstanding of how presidential records work. These documents are the property of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 . By the way, whether they`re classified or not, because the statutes at issue in the warrant, as Chuck and others have been saying, all week, do not require that the documents be classified in the Espionage Act, most cases involve classified information.\par \par \uc2 There`s a whole range of possibilities here, Stephanie, you have a president who we know from our own reporting had a pension for grabbing intelligence documents from his briefings and taking them away from the briefers and they didn`t know whatever happened to them. So that`s one possibility that these boxes include some of those documents. Then you have this effort by people like Kash Patel and others who were helping Trump gather up documents about the Russia investigation, and they tried to declassify them at the end of his presidency. It`s not clear that they were successful. They thought they were going to expose wrongdoing by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . And people inside the government were saying, wait a second, these are sources and methods. You can`t put this stuff out. It`s quite possible, some of those documents found their way to Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 We`ll just have to wait and see. But one thing is clear, there is a pending criminal investigation that the Justice Department is taking very seriously, investigating whether anyone mishandled these documents or otherwise broke the law.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: He was just stubborn and like to grab stuff, and that`s how documents containing nuclear information should end up in his private home in Florida. We`ll find out.\par \par \uc2 There`s another big story for us all about the former guy tonight, the guilty plea of his financial right hand man Allen Weisselberg. NBC`s Kristen Welker break that -- breaks that down now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Allen Weisselberg, the former Chief Financial Officer of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  pleaded guilty to tax fraud charges, admitting he took $1.7 million in untaxed perks, including free rent, school tuition and multiple Mercedes automobiles. Weisselberg agreed to pay nearly $2 million in taxes and fines and serve five months in jail. As a part of the plea deal, Weisselberg is expected to provide information about those business dealings, but not about his former boss.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And if he does not hold up his end of the plea deal Weisselberg could face 15 years in prison. That`s a lot longer than five months. David, you have been reporting on Trump`s finances for years. How big of a deal is this guilty plea for Weisselberg, for Trump and for the company?\par \par \uc2 DAVID FAHRENTHOLD, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Well, it`s certainly the -- I didn`t think I`d ever see it. Allen Weisselberg is perhaps more important to the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  when Trump was. He was the man who ran all sorts of financial and organizational aspects of that company for at least the last 20 years. So to take a guy like that out of the organization, to have a guy like that plead guilty he`s a huge blow just to the operations of a company.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:03]\par \par \uc2 To me, the striking thing is just how small, I mean the numbers are big but this is a big company Weisselberg got paid a lot of money, and he put himself on the line, he got himself into this kind of trouble for a relatively small amount of money. To me, it just shows how common lying and dishonesty how normal that was within the company, that he would do it for such small stakes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But here`s what`s not normal, whenever somebody linked to Trump gets in trouble. He likes to say they`re a coffee boy, low level, barely knew them. He cannot say that this time. Allen Weisselberg was part of the fabric of the Trump family.\par \par \uc2 FAHRENTHOLD: That`s right. I mean, like I said, you could take out that company, you could take out one of the Trump children, you could take out even Donald himself, as long as you left Weisselberg could keep going for a long time. He was the indispensable man. And I think in many ways still is, we wrote today that they threw him a birthday party after he agreed to plead guilty. So he still remains very, very essential to the operations of that company. So yes, this is not somebody that can be disregarded as distant or and barely knew him. I mean, he`s the heart of that operation.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But if he`s going to be forced to testify next month, or in October when the trial starts, is the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  going to be throwing parties for him? The fact that he was doing things for top executives, there are no top executives outside the Trump family. This is not some fortune 500 company. This is a rinky-dink family operation.\par \par \uc2 FAHRENTHOLD: That`s what`s so interesting to me. So our story today says that the Trump organization had a chance to plead guilty here and chose not to. It could -- if they did pleaded guilty and accepted some financial penalties, it could have reduced Weisselberg`s penalties as well. But the Trump board decided to stick it out, go to trial, a trial that certainly the deck is stacked against them because of Allen Weisselberg. Being a witness.\par \par \uc2 The key here is that you`re right, they have not established a criminal case against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself. And to do that you`d need to show Trump understanding what was going on. And, you know, understanding why he was breaking the law and doing it anyway, in a world where he doesn`t use email at all. So there`s no paper trail in that way. It`s all done verbally, and the guy will be done verbally with is Weisselberg. And if Weisselberg isn`t going to make that case, it`s going to be very, very hard for the Manhattan DA to make that case against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the person even if it can make it against the company.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the person is the company. So Chuck, when and if Weisselberg testifies next month, can you actually separate the two? Like the company is Trump there is nobody else.\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: Well, you`re right. But Trump won`t be on trial, only the company will be on trial. But Stephanie, you and David are getting at something that`s really interesting to me. So as a former federal prosecutor, if someone pled guilty and agreed to cooperate, they do not get to pick and choose against whom they cooperate. They have to cooperate fully against anyone and any buddy that we ask questions about. And so that`s the weird thing here to me. Admittedly, I was never a New York state prosecutor. They may do business differently. But he is agreeing to plead guilty and testify against the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , but not against individuals. And I`m not quite sure how people get to make that deal. You`re either in or you`re out. You`re either cooperating or you`re not. And so this does strike me as a bit unusual.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And where he`s headed, at least for a few months, potentially Rikers Island. That is not a country club jail. David Fahrenthold, Chuck Rosenberg, Ken Dilanian. And thank you all for starting us off tonight.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the same sort of lies and vitriol that as people threatening poll workers and law enforcement now have a children`s hospital in Boston at serious risk. The former CIA insider is up next and the real world dangers of disinformation.\par \par \uc2 And later, as the top Senate Republicans questioned the, "quality" of his own party`s candidates, we`ll ask one of our favorite historians whether anything surprises in these days. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on an important Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:23:37]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Boston Children`s Hospital is facing a barrage of threats tonight over a false social media post. A far-right social media account attack the hospitals gender affirming health care, falsely claiming it performs hysterectomys on minors. Well, for facts sake, the \uc2 American Academy of Pediatrics\uc2  describes gender affirming care for children as the integration of medical, mental health and social services. But the Boston hospital denies performing gender affirming surgery on any patient younger than 18. That still has not stopped the dangerous threats and disinformation. Here`s what one doctor at another Boston hospital had to say about it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT HADLAND, MASS GENERAL HOSPITAL: It`s been very hard for me over the years to care for LGBTQ youth and engage in, you know, what is really the standard of care, evidence-based treatment, and then to have people, you know, throw terms around like pedophile or child abuser. And now I see this happening to my colleagues and it`s really heartbreaking to watch happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Heartbreaking and dangerous. Let`s bring in Tracy Walder, former CIA Officer and \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent. She`s the author of the book, The Unexpected Spy." Tracy, this is not an isolated incident. What on earth is working these people up to such a frenzy that they`d want to threaten a children`s hospital over a social post?\par \par \uc2 TRACY WALDER, FORMER FBI SPECIAL AGENT: Well, thank you so much for having me. You know, I think it is sort of the intersection of a bunch of different things. I think you have COVID exacerbated people`s access to online and the time that people spent online, and through those online channels, you know, far-right, far-left, whatever, the kind of went further down the rabbit hole.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:19]\par \par \uc2 But I think what`s also really important we talk about the influence of social media and these folks becoming radicalized, but we really don`t talk about the factors that really get them to this point that get them to become radicalized. And really, the biggest factor is this quest for significance. So a lot of these folks felt like their lives don`t matter, they didn`t have an impact in what they were doing. And they found a sense of purpose in these actions.\par \par \uc2 And then I think the second kind of factors, the permission to use violence, you know, we`re hearing this whole discussion about this pending civil war and to take up arms. And they`re feeling like they have the permission to do that. And then, you know, finally we`re looking at community or people that kind of support that use of violence. And, you know, we saw Senator Grassley talking about the hiring of the 87,000 IRS agents, and to, you know, take up arms, they`re going to come at you with AR-15. And I think this is really causing this this pervasive amount of threats by all different venues.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But no one gave this movement permission to be violent. Just ask the insurrectionists, would you then call this what we`re seeing domestic terrorism, a threat of domestic terrorism?\par \par \uc2 WALDER: So in my personal opinion, I do believe that they are domestic terrorists, really, you know, I worked at the CIA, I worked at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . And, you know, I spent a lot of time, my background was in working on counterterrorism. And the reality is everyone is radicalized, for the most part in the same way, it doesn`t matter what you believe, who you are, what your gender is, and what your religion is. And really, if we look up what domestic terrorism is, it`s violent criminal acts committed by individuals, and groups to really further ideological goals. And that`s what`s going on here. They have ideological goals, whether it`s the children`s hospitals, whether it`s abortion clinics, whether it`s 1/6, these are ideological goals that stem from politics, religion, and social beliefs.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: They do stem from politics. And when you go back down the path, they stem from right wing media, and the GOP. Former CIA Director, Michael Hayden just yesterday agreed online, that today`s Republican Party is the most dangerous political force he has ever seen. Do you agree with that? I mean, that`s a heavy statement from the former head of the CIA.\par \par \uc2 WALDER: So I actually do agree with that. I`ve had a lot of time to really kind of reflect on that I don`t want to, you know, use that lightly in terms of how I feel. But the thing is, is when we see senators, so, you know, sitting members that were elected to these offices, really encourage these conspiracy theories. I think that`s where we`ve seen the shift. The reality is the deep state has existed since the formation, really, of our country. And these ideas have existed, but they`ve always been on the fringes. But now we have this, I guess, mainstream validation of them really solely by the Republican Party. And I have to agree with General Hayden here.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So if you`re law enforcement right now, how difficult is it to monitor and track these threats? They`re coming from everywhere, they`re based on lies, and they could pop up in any given place, I mean, a children`s hospital based on a social media post?\par \par \uc2 WALDER: So you know, you`re absolutely right. I think it`s incredibly difficult. And now that sounds really disconcerting, but it`s almost impossible to monitor all of these, it`s almost like playing a game of whack a mole, you know, you shut down one side, I saw that lives of TikTok was with their account was suspended on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  today, but you shut that down, and they just find another platform to be on. And so the reality is, is we can only track what we know. And so there`s so many websites out there that we just don`t know exists. And, you know, unless it`s really brought to the attention of law enforcement becomes extremely difficult to track that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It is a difficult time to be in law enforcement. That is for sure. Tracy Walder, thank you for joining us tonight. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 WALDER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When we come back, it wasn`t all that long ago when Republicans are feeling pretty darn good about the midterms. But tonight, maybe not so much. And some Republicans really important ones, they`re blaming the former guy`s favorite candidates. We`re going to dig into that when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:34:32]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R) KENTUCKY MINORITY LEADER: I think there`s probably a greater likelihood of the House flips than the Senate. Senate races are just different, they`re statewide. Candidate quality has a lot to do with the outcome.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Candidate quality that is a new one, a sign of trouble for Republicans as Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  casts doubt on his party`s ability to flip the Senate. McConnell blaming candidate equality which is his code for Donald Trump`s favorite candidates and candidate equality is part of the reason the Cook Political Report says the Senate is now a toss- up with less than three months to go until the midterms. Of course, things can change any minute any day. But a toss-up is certainly a new chapter.\par \par \uc2 [23:35:19]\par \par \uc2 With us tonight, Juanita Tolliver, a veteran Political Strategist to progressive candidates and clauses, and Tim Miller, a contributor to the Bulwark and former Communications Director for Jeb Bush. He wrote the book, "Why We Did It."\par \par \uc2 Tim, you know, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  better than anyone in this conversation, so I start with you. How big of a problem is candidate quality for Republicans, they pick their own people?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, THE BULWARK CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah, it`s a huge problem. And this is a beaten down \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  that interview, let`s be honest. Look, Mitch was riding high for about 2014 through 2018. Because he felt at the peak of his powers and being at the biggest powers meant he could help influence and pick who would come out of Republican primaries, and he would choose the candidates that he felt that was the strongest and could win. This wasn`t always the case back in the 2010 cycle. People might remember the witch candidate Christine O`Donnell and Sharron Angle and some of the others who cost Republican seats.\par \par \uc2 Well McConnell put a stop to that, but he has lost control of his party. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , despite the fact that he`s not elected and he`s hanging out with Cougars at Mar-a-Lago, controls the party, he controls the candidates. And a lot of the candidates that he`s chosen are pretty weak. Let`s be honest, and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  knows that and this is a guy that`s frustrated, he`s beaten down and he realizes that they`re going to leave seats on the board. Obviously, we will mention \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and Mehmet Oz as being two particularly weak candidates, but people don`t talk as much about in Arizona and New Hampshire. McConnell had his hand-picked candidates, the sitting governors, Sununu, and Ducey popular center right governors, both of them took a pass on the race because they didn`t want to deal with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And instead, there`s going to be insane pro-insurrection candidates that run instead. Those are two additional seats that Republicans are probably going to leave on the table that they might have won with better candidates. So that`s why we got to sad \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  today.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right. Well, you said a whole lot there but perhaps the most important -- what`s wrong with cougars?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: I love cougars. I`m going to say it, if you`re hanging out with them, you`re hanging out with them by the pool. Why are you picking Senate candidates? You`d think that the Senate majority leader would have a better, you know, would have more influence than somebody, you know, kind of hanging out at a cougar club in South Beach or, you know, South Beach adjacent.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You`re flailing on that answer. Juanita, you`re advising Democrats, right? You`re Democrat, if you`re looking at a race, and there`s a far- right, super crazy. If you`re the Democratic Party, what do you do? Dive to the middle and hungry, hungry Hippo style, pick up every swing voter, and undecided and centrist?\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, PROGRESSIVE POLITICAL STRATEGIST: I think it`s a delicate balance, Stephanie, because you absolutely need to call up the extremism absolutely call out the hate filled agenda that Republicans have been pushing, because we know that mobilizes voters, any Republicans who are pro-abortion bans and anti-LGBTQ individuals that`s going to stir up and mobilize voters and opposition to that energy. We saw that play out in Kansas earlier this month with the ballot initiative on abortion. And that`s absolutely something that Democrats should keep calling out in these unfit candidates.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats also need to shout every one of their accomplishments from the rooftop every single day between now and November 8. And they need to do it in a way that emphasizes that they achieved these legislative wins on their own, while Republicans were voting against capping insulin at $35, voting against making corporations pay their fair share, voting against climate mitigation measures when we`re experiencing historic floods and storms and fires and make sure that they`re drawing the contrast with these extremist candidates. Because what Democrats have done right now as of late, is they`ve delivered on key components of President Biden`s 2020 agenda that he ran on that is wildly popular.\par \par \uc2 And so they have a massive opportunity here to grab the attention of voters. And what I appreciate is that it looks like progressive organizations are already lining up to do that in a coordinated way. $10 million dedicated to advise to talk about the Inflation Reduction Act, $50 million from Planned Parenthood going to strategic states to emphasize the abortion attacks and bans coming from the right and that is a critical pathway for Democrats to occupy that absolutely will be at play that Hungry, Hungry Hippo roll and pick up some independent voters who are all supportive of these provisions.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Juanita, this week`s Wyoming race focuses on, reminds us of a really big problem with the Senate. The State of Wyoming has less than 600,000 residents. California has almost 40 million. Both states get two senators, how do you approach this imbalance? It sounds like you`re going to change the rules overnight?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: Not going to change the rules overnight, but Democrats could change the rules a little bit if they pick up a few more Senate seats, right? Eliminating the filibuster will be a first major step for Democrats to even start to consider different provisions that could start to address this imbalance, whether that`s considering the votes on D.C. statehood, considering opportunities for Puerto Rico to be considered for statehood and have senators added and modifying the body that way.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:21]\par \par \uc2 Because you`re absolutely right, Stephanie, the imbalance is critical. And I think the imbalance is only going to continue to worsen, as you have Republicans continuously wanting these more homogenous, predominantly white, rural, rural, smaller population states. We`re seeing that in Wyoming, we`re seeing that across the country, and I see it every time come to a head that Republicans obstruct highly popular legislation, again, whether it`s related to abortion bans or voting rights or anything that requires 10 Republican votes, they put a stop to it because they have that imbalance of power and it`s a massive problem.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Tim, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a huge amount of power. Did he miss an opportunity here? Would there not better or more qualified candidates for him to back \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and Mehmet Oz were the best they could do? Aren`t there a lot of people who want to be senators?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yeah, firstly, Juanita is much more on message to me tonight. Just kind of hit all the points over there. Yeah, of course, there were better candidates. Look, there were not perfect candidates in any situation if in order to be a good Republican in good standing in 2020, you need to believe the fantasies and conspiracies about the -- or excuse me in 2022 about the 2020 election, and you need to be an extremist on abortion. I think that`s going to limit candidate quality, no matter what.\par \par \uc2 But yes, I obviously David McCormick was not a perfect candidate. He`s on flaws. We`ve talked about him before on here, but he certainly would have been a better candidate than Mehmet Oz. And I was in Georgia, you have a popular sitting Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 . You know, in a normal time, somebody like \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , who showed courage and 2020 standing up to Trump might have won and been able to be promoted run for Senate, he would have been very tough for Democrats to beat because I think he would have been able to get crossover votes, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a man baby. And he just wanted -- wants people who are going to suck up to him. And so those are the candidates that he picked, and that has caused Republicans in candidate quality across the board.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, let`s be clear, David McCormick, very clearly sucked up to Trump, but it still didn`t work.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Still didn`t work, true.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Juanita, Tim, thank you both for joining us.\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: Right.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I appreciate it. Coming up, Presidential Historian and dear friend of this show, Michael Beschloss is here to discuss an already eventful week a very significant headlines, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:47:14]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: As we mentioned and covered all day long here, ex-\uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  CFO Allen Weisselberg has pleaded guilty to tax fraud. This is only one of several stunning developments involving the former president this week.\par \par \uc2 Back with us tonight to discuss, celebrated author and presidential historian Michael Beschloss. His latest work in a bookshelf full of brilliant works his, "Presidency of War." Michael, welcome.\par \par \uc2 So much to put in perspective, but I want to go with just one. How significant is it that someone this close to the former president is pleading guilty to tax fraud a month before the former guy`s family business has their moment in court?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BESCHLOSS, NBC NEWS PRESIDENTIAL HISTORIAN: It does not look right. And from everything I`ve understood, and what I understand from watching you earlier in the hour, you know, Allen Weisselberg could be the key to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  conceivably losing a case and going to jail. And so here you`ve got this odd situation of Weisselberg now going to Rikers Island with the potential promise that he will testify at the end of his imprisonment against the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . And I hate to say this, but Rikers Island, there have been almost a half dozen people killed in Rikers Island just this year. I hope he`s guarded very carefully.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Michael, the news, gods do you not believe in summer vacation.\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: No, I don`t.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last time you were here we talked about how history can be made in August. So much has happened in the last two weeks. What sticks out to you the most?\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: Our democracy sad to say is wobbling. Begin with rule of law, just one example of that. When in history have we ever seen a House Republican leader like Kevin McCarthy, making a threat to Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  as if he`s some kind of Don Fanucci in The Godfather, saying I would advise you if we Republicans take over the House in November as we might he said, preserve your documents and clear your calendar that really is more from the Godfather than from, you know, political movies that are a little bit more mainstream. This is not normal. This is not what you see in a functioning democracy. We also saw on Tuesday night, more election 2020 deniers, people who if they`re elected to state office or federal office are going to be on the side of if they don`t like the person who gets the most votes in 2024 or even 2022. They`ll say take the person I like and put that person in office anyway, corruptly.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing is public safety is something that we all should expect in a democracy. Look at the number of candidates and their allies in this midterm campaign for advocating violence beginning with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who is suggesting at least in a subtle way as subtle as he can be, that if he were to be acted against by the Justice Department, if he were to be indicted, there were reports that he said this could make this country blow up in flame. An ex-President making another threat like this. All I`m saying, Stephanie, is that, you know, for our younger viewers, these are things we have not seen before in American history.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:32]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Before the Mar-a-Lago search, you and several other historians met with President Biden to warn him of what you just told me, that democracy is teetering. Since then, we`ve seen people threaten the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , go after an \uc2 FBI\uc2  office, go after a children`s hospital.\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Is there anything you wish you told him then that you would tell him now, things have gotten worse in the last two weeks?\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: They have. I think, I would say basically the same thing, although sadly, I would have new incidents, just as you`re saying, new examples of our democracy being in jeopardy. But when in history have we seen, you know, if you don`t like one thing the \uc2 FBI\uc2  does, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  searched Mar-a-Lago with a legal warrant. Now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his many knees who are running for office all over the United States say, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  must therefore be corrupt. But this the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , that is so opposite to conservatism, I can tell you. It`s very important for this country to have a rule of law. If we lose that, if we lose our free and fair elections, we will have lost our democracy. And that could conceivably happen in the next couple of months.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Michael Beschloss, thank you. And a reminder for our audience, yes, that \uc2 FBI\uc2  search on Mar-a-Lago was legal.\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: Totally.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And before that search, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  defied a subpoena, and ignored request after request from the National Archives begging the question, why not give the government back their stuff? You certainly have.\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: Yeah, perfect, perfect comment. I agree. I agree. Thank you, Stephanie. Be well.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Michael, thank you for joining us tonight. You make us smarter every time you are here.\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: You too, thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Be well. We`ll be back with more in just a few, THE 11TH HOUR is not over yet.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:56:59]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Some heartbreaking news to report in our NBC family tonight. Henry Engel, the six-year-old son of our dear friend and colleague Richard Engel has passed away. Henry was diagnosed with an incurable genetic brain disorder called Rett syndrome. Richard and his courageous wife Mary have spent years bringing awareness to this rare condition. Lester Holt has more on their story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC HOST: He`s faced tragedy and conflict across the world, but the news that reached Richard Engel while on foreign assignment one day in 2017 was like nothing he had ever faced.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: I got back in this convoy like shaking was the worst day of my life.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Richard and his wife, Mary Forrest, learning their then two-year-old son had an extremely rare and incurable genetic condition.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: I called you and said, we have a result. And I said, it`s not good. It`s not just delay. It means lifelong, permanent, untreatable physical and intellectual impairment.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: As he physically grew, Henry was unable to walk, speak or efficiently feed himself. A tough journey was ahead. Richard and Mary chose to share it publicly.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: We wanted to raise awareness.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The worst days.\par \par \uc2 MARY FORREST: I think it`s the hardest that it`s ever felt, as a caregiver, physically, mentally, worrying the anxiety about what`s going to happen with him regression. What does the future look like?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And they shared the best days.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Swimming in the sea.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Like the celebration when Richard first heard those two unforgettable words.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Henry looked at me and he called me dada. The first time just a few days ago, and it was something I`ve been waiting for, for years.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Physical therapy helped briefly sitting up unsupported a major milestone. Richard and Mary dedicated themselves to creating awareness of Rett syndrome. Researchers even learning things from Henry studying his cells to one day help others. But in May, Richard tweeted, Henry had taken a turn for the worse, but it was home getting love from brother, Theo. Today, Richard and Mary tweeting the crushing news that Henry had passed away, writing he had the softest blue eyes, an easy smile, and a contagious giggle. We always surrounded him with love, and he returned it and so much more. Richard and Mary`s beloved Henry was six years old.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Our heavy hearts are with the Engel family tonight. If you would like to learn more, and support the Rett syndrome research donations are being made in Henry`s name, to Texas Children`s Hospital. And from all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News we`re thinking of the Engel family and thanking you for staying up late with us tonight. I will see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220819ei8i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for August 18, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Symone Sanders-Townsend, Joy Reid \par \uc2 8,159 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CROSS, MSNBC HOST: And firing Gableman, Vox told reporters, that the costly venture was "worthwhile." Was it though? He also said that the Wisconsin Republican caucus would now turn their focus to banning abortions, stripping voting rights, and strengthening their legislative power. What a mess. It sounds like all the GOP. But this is what the big lie gets you.\par \par \uc2 I would like to note that this fall, Wisconsin voters will get to decide if they want to elect more election deniers like Republican \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2  who`s looking to unseat the current Democratic Governor \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 , or Senate Senator Ron Johnson who was being challenged by Democrat Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes. The choice is yours.\par \par \uc2 All right, that does it for me. That`s tonight`s "REIDOUT". But don`t go anywhere because ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If they do indict him, that doesn`t render him an eligible to run for even be president?\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH DIGENOVA, FORMER TRUMP ATTORNEY: He can -- listen, Eugene Debs ran for president from the federal prison.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As the Mar-a-Lago hearing convenes in Florida, \uc2 Trump\uc2  Org`s chief financial officer pleads guilty in New York.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL COHEN\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP ATTORNEY: Allen will ultimately be providing testimony that is against Donald.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, the rank criminality surrounding the 45th President, and what we know we learned about the search warrant for Trump`s home.\par \par \uc2 Plus, new reporting the Secret Service held onto violent threats to \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  until after the insurrection. And why Republican leaders are raising red flags 82 days away from the Midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Candidate quality has a lot to do with the outcome.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. There`s a bunch of legal news surrounding \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  today as there is seemingly every day these days. None of it particularly good and for the ex-President. First up today, a federal judge in West Palm Beach said he will consider releasing some of the affidavit central to last week search of Donald Trump`s Florida property.\par \par \uc2 Judge Bruce Reinhart ordered the Department of Justice to submit a redacted version of the document that will not undermine its ongoing investigation. He will then decide in one week whether to make that version of the document public. That`s a little surprising. We`ll have more on that.\par \par \uc2 While we are a long way from potential criminal charges against the ex- president, let alone a conviction, we`ve already got Trump`s former lawyer publicly speculating about him running for a second term while indicted.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DIGENOVA: \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is now crazy. He is going to indict \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They are going to figure out some charge because they want to harm him, to ruin him, to basically cut him off at the knees so he can`t run for president. Eugene Debs ran for president from the federal prison. There`s no reason that that`s going to happen to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He`s not going to be in a federal prison. But if he`s indicted, he will run for president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Again, no indication Trump is going to be indicted anytime soon. But Trump`s close confidant Allen Weisselberg, the former chief financial officer of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , that`s the guy that you know, keeps all the books, he will be going to prison. Today, Weisselberg pleaded guilty to 15 counts of criminal conduct related to a scheme by the organization to commit tax fraud, among other charges.\par \par \uc2 While the terms of Weisselberg`s plea deal to not require him to testify against Trump directly, Donald Trump`s former fixer, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , who also did some prison time for his work for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and who knows Weisselberg and the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  well, says either directly or indirectly Weisselberg testimony will implicate the ex-President.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Well, he had no other choice but to plead guilty as I -- as you know, Kara just stated. He was looking at 15 years based upon crimes that I had provided them documentation on that proved without a doubt that he would have been found guilty. Allen will ultimately be providing testimony that is against Donald. No matter how you want to parse it in words, it doesn`t matter. He will be providing testimony.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, I`ve been covering politics for about two decades. And generally, the rule of thumb, it`s not a good thing when a politician or their associates are charged with a crime or convicted of a crime, let alone. But it happens a lot to people in Donald Trump`s orbit. Let`s just take a step back. \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  who you just heard, of course, pleaded guilty to eight counts of tax evasion, bank fraud, and then also campaign finance violations back in 2018. Those campaign finance violations stemming from a scheme in Trump`s first campaign to pay hush money payments to multiple women who alleged they had extramarital affairs with the then- candidate. The checks of course signed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Cohen also negotiated hush money payments for another Trump ally, Republican megadonor and former RNC Finance Chairman during Trump`s time, \uc2 Elliott Broidy\uc2 . Remember him? Now, Broidy stepped down from his position at the head of the RNC Finance Committee after it was revealed that he paid a former Playboy model more than $1.6 million to cover up their affair, also a transaction put together by \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  who carved out it seems a bit of a niche in that.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:09]\par \par \uc2 Now, that of course in and of itself is not illegal. But of course, you might have forgotten this one. Broidy also actually pleaded guilty to one count of conspiracy to violate the Foreign Agents Registration Act back in 2020 for his work as an unregistered lobbyist on behalf of a Malaysian development company. Broidy was also allegedly embroiled in a scheme along with a man named \uc2 George Nader\uc2 . Remember him?\par \par \uc2 He had met with the Trump campaign, had some liaisons with them. The scheme was to lobby the Trump White House on behalf of the Saudis and the United Arab Emirates. Now, Nader was charged with illegally funding UAE money into the 2016 election. He`s now serving 10 years in prison for child sex trafficking and child pornography offenses.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of the UAE, \uc2 Tom Barrack\uc2 , longtime friend of Trump, associate, spoke at the \uc2 RNC\uc2  in 2016, that campaign, he was the chair of the 2017 Inaugural Committee. He was indicted last year for his unregistered lobbying on behalf of a Gulf Nation. Barrack was the one who told the Trump campaign to hire \uc2 Paul Manafort\uc2  as campaign manager back in 2016. And Manafort ultimately pleaded guilty to conspiracy charges relating to money he earned working as an unregistered foreign agent for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Are you sensing a theme here on the America First campaign? Manafort was also pardoned by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Manafort`s close ally, \uc2 Rick Gates\uc2 , who is Trump`s deputy campaign manager pleaded guilty for assisting Manafort in laundering that Ukrainian money as well as lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And the unregistered lobbying still does not end there. Trump`s first National Security Adviser \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2 , he was also accused of acting as an unregistered agent on behalf of the Turkish Government, though he never charged for that misconduct. He did of course plead guilty to lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  about his communications with Russian officials. But he later tried to change his story. The Trump`s Department of Justice tried to drop the case against him even though he already pleaded guilty before he was again, ultimately pardoned by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There was a man named George Papadopoulos also on that Trump campaign team back in 2016, the foreign policy team. He also pleaded guilty to lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  about his contacts with Russians like Flynn. He was also pardoned by Trump in 2020 as was \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 , a longtime friend, associate, Trump advisor, the man who takes credit for convincing Trump to run for president in the first place. Before Stone`s pardon, he was convicted of lying to Congress and witness intimidation back in 2019 and sentenced to three years in prison.\par \par \uc2 Now, Trump campaign CEO and Senior White House adviser \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , he`s an interesting case. Bannon was charged with conspiracy to commit mail fraud and money laundering. Remember that Postal Service cops picked him up on a Chinese billionaire`s yacht? And this -- those charges stemmed from an alleged grift that allegedly bilked millions of dollars from Trump supporters for a bogus plan to crowdfund Trump`s border wall creatively titled We Build The Wall.\par \par \uc2 Trump pardoned Bannon before he could even go to trial but then Bannon just intent on getting into prison last month was convicted of two counts of contempt of Congress for his refusal to cooperate with the January 6 Committee subpoena. Speaking of contempt for Congress, let us not forget the one and only \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2 , another Trump White House adviser. He is also been indicted for Contempt of Congress for his refusal to cooperate with Committee. He has pleaded not guilty and is awaiting trial.\par \par \uc2 Navarro and Bannon are not the only Trump allies facing current potential legal consequences for their role in the attempted coup. Trump lawyer \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  last seen in Atlanta where he was testifying before a grand jury, he is now the target, it has been confirmed, of a local criminal investigation in Georgia from the Fulton County DA`s office for his role trying to overturn the election in that state. He testified before a grand jury there yesterday.\par \par \uc2 That`s a lot. I mean, it`s more than any politician I`ve ever covered in my life. It`s more than any politician that would be willing to bet that anyone has ever covered in this country in the last few decades. The cult of criminality surrounding \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been present since he first ran for president. So, Trump executive Allen Weisselberg is really just the latest in a very long list of people charged with crimes in Trump`s orbit. And given where we are now with the amount of investigations, criminal investigations going on, it shows no sign of slowing down.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Ilya Marritz. He covers Trump legal matters for NPR. He`s the co-host of the Will Be Wild podcast and a great reporter on this beat. It`s great to have you here, Ilya.\par \par \uc2 ILYA MARRITZ, CONTRIBUTOR, NPR: Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You were in court today. Is that correct?\par \par \uc2 MARRITZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What was the scene like?\par \par \uc2 MARRITZ: Well, you know, it`s the downtown criminal court building familiar to anybody who watches, you know, Law And Order and any of those types of shows, dimly lit, echoey marble hallways. And here`s Allen Weisselberg coming in with his team of lawyers wearing a face mask, hard to read his expression. And the proceeding took a little under an hour.\par \par \uc2 And what we saw was the judge run Allen Weisselberg through his rights, make sure that he is doing this of his free will. And then there was this sort of remarkable passage, the allocution where the judge ran Alan Weisselberg through all of the facts that he is admitting to by attending to by -- these 15 counts. And, you know, yes, your honor, yes, your honor, yes, your honor.\par \par \uc2 This is somebody whose voice we have hardly ever heard before. And now, he has been in two crimes. And for the first time, we have the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  sort of directly implicated in crimes. These are crimes so far right now that only Allen Weisselberg has committed, but the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  which is hard to separate from Trump, the man, is -- will have to defend itself and show that it didn`t also participate.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:55]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, I want to talk about it because there`s so much focus, right? There`s been so much coverage of the Manhattan district attorney offices investigation, right? We knew there`s grand jury, there was Cy Vance. It was handed over to \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  who`s the individual now heads up that office.\par \par \uc2 Then there was the news that they won`t be pursuing charges against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . There was a resignation from one of the lawyers who worked on that. And a little bit of this like, oh, well, that`s yesterday`s news. But I have literally forgotten they actually criminally charged the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . What does it mean to criminally charge an entity, a company?\par \par \uc2 MARRITZ: Right. Well, you know, if prosecutors are successful, and they`re more likely to be successful now that they have this guilty plea from Allen Weisselberg because we know now that there were crimes, the only question is whether the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  participated in those crimes. If they are found guilty by a jury, the Trump organization becomes a felon just as Alan Weisselberg has become a felon. So, in a concrete way, that may scare off banks and counterparties and, you know, business interests.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t seem to have taken a lot of interest in his hotels and golf courses as an active businessman lately, but nevertheless, that`s going to hurt. This is his family business. But in terms of hitting Trump the man, no, it does seem that that part of this investigation is over.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What does it mean -- I mean, he` s going to cooperate in testifying against the org and not the individual. It does seem to me and the other legal analyst I`ve heard say this, that if he`s a CFO and he`s testifying against the org, it`s going to be a tough rap for them to be. I mean, anything`s possible, but --\par \par \uc2 MARRITZ: Yes, I mean, I don`t see -- I mean, there was a crime here. There`s a mountain of documentary evidence. Some of it is very interesting. You know, we`re talking about a ledger where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was, personally out of his personal bank account, paying to private school tuition for Allen Weisselberg`s grandchildren, writing those checks and signing those checks. This was in the allocution today.\par \par \uc2 And Allen Weisselberg admitted that he directed another employee on the accounting staff to remove information from there so that that would be less -- presumably, that would be less obvious to investigators. So, that`s the one place where Donald Trump`s name came up twice in this proceeding. So, you do see him tangentially touching the case in just these little but quite significant ways.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What are the possible -- I mean, I guess the last big -- one of these I can think of is \uc2 Enron\uc2 . Of course, that case ends up being very complicated and some of those are actually vacated on appeal like 10 years later before the Supreme Court. What does it mean -- what are the penalties of it? You know, you can`t put a corporation in jail, right? So, what are the penalties that could possibly face?\par \par \uc2 MARRITZ: Yes, well, so there are financial penalties, of course. But actually, this is where I kind of want to pivot over and look at the New York attorney general case which is, I should say, not yet a case but an investigation. It could become a civil case. Tish James, the New York Attorney General, has been investigating, you know, inflating and deflating assets, another thing that we kind of learned about from \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And if we think about that office, the New York Attorney General, that`s the same office that shut down \uc2 the Trump Foundation\uc2 . They did not merely institute penalties. They shut it down and banned Trump from serving on charitable boards. So, it is thinkable that when that gets going, if it gets going as we think that it may well do, that you know, the one-two punch of penalties coming from the Manhattan District Attorney, plus a civil case could really harm this branding golf, hotel, or casino empire.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it does seem like it`s getting pretty squeezed in an unprecedented fashion. Ilya Marritz, it`s great to have you.\par \par \uc2 MARRITZ: Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, federal judge indicated we might learn more about the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s raid at Mar-a-Lago. Our next guest was supervisor in the office that`s currently leading the Mar-a-Lago investigation. He`s here to explain what it means for the case against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Downright spectacle today outside the West Palm Beach, Florida federal courthouse, as you might expect. 10 days after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of Donald Trump`s Mar-a-Lago residence, Trump supporters, police, news media all surrounded the building where a federal judge heard arguments on whether or not to unseal the affidavit that led to that search warrant.\par \par \uc2 In the roughly hour-long hearing, the chief of counterintelligence for the Department of Justice, Jay Bratt, "Argued the detailed and reasonably lengthy document needed to be kept completely under wraps because it contains substantial grand jury information in a unique case with national security overtones." The judge however ruled that "There are portions of it that can be unsealed, saying he would give the government a full and fair opportunity to make redactions and order them to turn in the redacted version by next Thursday."\par \par \uc2 [20:20:11]\par \par \uc2 With this big development, surprising one, I think, I want to turn to Brandon Van Grack. He`s a DOJ veterans who`s overseen investigations since the disclosure of classified documents, prosecuted dozens of cases involving the Espionage Act which is at issue here. He led the Russia investigation before Robert Mueller`s appointment of Special Counsel and served as the lead prosecutor afterwards. He`s also the guy who signed the Trump National Security Adviser Michael Flynn`s plea agreement. He was even a supervisor in the office that`s currently leading the Mar-a-Lago investigation, which is the counterintelligence and export control section of DOJ`s \uc2 National Security Division\uc2 . Brandon, it`s great to have you on the program.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON VAN GRACK, FORMER DOJ OFFICIAL: It`s nice to be here, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today was a bit of a surprise, I think. I thought most people I`d spoken to seem to think this was sort of a slam dunk. The affidavit is going to say sealed. Were you surprised?\par \par \uc2 VAN GRACK: If this was an ordinary criminal case, then I think there would be a surprise. In most cases that are just that the investigative stage, the government arguing that the unsealing of an affidavit would cause harm to the investigation is almost a given. Here you have investigation where one of the most important investigations of the -- for the Justice Department that raises national security issues, political issues. And so, it`s not surprising that you have a judge that is going to push the department as far and as hard as he can to disclose as much information as possible.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Did you feel like you`ve learned anything new from the proceedings today or the new documents that were unsealed today? We have a criminal cover sheet the search warrant application, we`ve also got the basis or for the search under -- cover sheet was evidence of a crime, there`s the codes, which are the different descriptions of the defenses, which are willful retention of National Defense Information, concealment or removal of government records and obstruction or federal investigation. Anything new or anything you gleaned from what we learned today?\par \par \uc2 VAN GRACK: There are a few things. The first is we really got unmistakable confirmation that this is a detailed and thorough criminal investigation. There`s references to substantial Grand Jury information to specific investigative techniques to multiple witnesses. So, we`ve seen it in filings that the government was pretty clear about that point.\par \par \uc2 Second piece is once again confirming that the national security interests here are paramount not just because, as you explained in your lead in, that you had the chief of the counterintelligence and export control section making the arguments. But the arguments made at the hearing really continued to reinforce the national security issues here.\par \par \uc2 And then finally, and maybe most importantly, we learned that this investigation is going to take a while. The government for the first time said that the investigation is in its early stages. And so, we really need to temper our expectations in terms of when this investigation is going to end.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I should note that some of the folks in court pushing for this to be released, media organizations, I believe NBC is part of that group that that was pushing for this. Obviously, we want maximal transparency as our position in the press. What -- as someone who was in that division of DOJ, how are you understanding the conceptualization of what we are seeing here?\par \par \uc2 Because I think there`s a few ways to think about this. One is the guy just wouldn`t give the stuff back and it`s not his. And the \uc2 FBI\uc2  wanted it back for every very understandable reasons. And essentially, we`re forced to escalate to this point because he was non-compliant. And then that`s it. They got the documents.\par \par \uc2 The other is that there`s something more here. There`s something more being developed, there`s something more being investigated. There`s another part of this story. Which are you?\par \par \uc2 VAN GRACK: So, it`s no longer just about the documents. I think this investigation probably begin that way. And if this is a situation where ultimately all of the documents that are marked classified were returned to the archives, or returned -- or provided in June when the Department of Justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  were at Mar-a-Lago, I don`t think -- I really don`t think we`d be talking about it, or at least not in the same way.\par \par \uc2 But something did happen. There was clearly conduct that triggered an escalation in the investigation. And so, that doesn`t change the fact that the national security interests here are still paramount and the focus. But it`s no longer just about the documents. And that`s, again, I think, one of the interesting takeaways from the hearing today, which is, ultimately, the fact that there was this unprecedented search conducted really, I think, needed to be justified by the fact that there is a criminal investigation. It was difficult that was -- if it was just about the documents.\par \par \uc2 But the statements in court today especially it being in the early stages I think is an indication that they have the documents and there`s more to it than that.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:03]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So there`s been a lot back and forth about classification, right. And classification is in some ways, sort of strange because it`s -- you know, it`s not in the Constitution. It adheres the article on the powers of the President, the President is the ultimate authority of the executive branch, which is the part of our government that classifies and declassifies, all sorts of talk about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  having this sort of magical power where he just says some words over some papers. People are disputing that. How central is classification to your mind to the story here?\par \par \uc2 VAN GRACK: So, it`s central but I think you need to unpack it, which is, it is important to ultimately the criminal charges. I think there are some statutes here or one, in particular, involving the retention of National defense information where, if in fact, these documents were unclassified, however informally, it would impact the nature -- you know, the ability to bring those charges. But it doesn`t impact the national security implications here.\par \par \uc2 And this is where I think it`s important to take a step back, because an argument that`s been -- that`s been made is that there was this standing order for declassification. And so, you`re left with the two scenarios. Either that wasn`t the case, in which case this information was classified from the moment it left, and that does have come out. Or that there was a standing order that just simply based on convenience and the proximity to the president United States, information regardless of his classification became declassified without coordination with the intelligence community.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that`s a remarkable order if that`s true, almost certainly unprecedented. And it leads to the scenarios that we have now, which is where you have all sorts, you know, untold numbers of classified documents of varying levels that were mishandled and inappropriately retained.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, we should know there`s some reporting today, I think with 18 sources from previous administrations, including former Chief of Staff Kelly saying, it`s -- that`s hogwash. There was, of course, no standing order. So, I think I think it`s unlikely that such a standing order existed. It seems much more likely to be sort of an ad hoc justification after the fact.\par \par \uc2 Final question for you is just how are you -- how do you think your colleagues in that branch of DOJ are thinking about what their task is right now?\par \par \uc2 VAN GRACK: This is -- this is a section that counterintelligence and export control section that has handled not only these types of cases since its beginning in terms of the retention of mishandling of classified information, but they`ve also handled some of the most politically sensitive and charged investigations. So, this is -- this -- you know, for better or worse, this is what they`re used to dealing with, these types of issues. And so, even though it`s -- it is somewhat unusual for the chief of that section to be making these arguments, it`s reflective of the fact that this is exactly where the Department of Justice wants these investigations being led.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Brandon Van Grack, thanks for sharing your expertise. I learned a lot. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 VAN GRACK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, a new report the Secret Service knew about a threat against Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and didn`t do anything, apparently. The latest scandal that plagued the already beleaguered agency, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We`re coming in like white on rice for Pelosi, Nadler, Schumer, even you AOC. We`re coming to take you out and pull you out by your hairs. How about that, Pelosi? Go muscle-make yourself another appointment. I get down with you. You`re going to need to shine up on top of that bald head.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: After the January 6 attack on the U.S. Capitol, we knew that Trump mob had targeted Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . That video you saw was entered into evidence by the January 6 Committee. They of course derisively chanted her name as they ascended the steps of the Capitol, scour the halls looking for her, creepy voices until they found her office and started ransacking it.\par \par \uc2 One rioter even posed for pictures with his feet on the desk in Pelosi`s office, left her note which he described in interview later that day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How did you get it?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I didn`t steal it. I planned on it to the (BLEEP) and I couldn`t (BLEEP) see. And so, I figure, well, I`m in her office, I got a ledger in her office. I put a quarter on her desk even though she (BLEEP) and I left her note on her desk. It says, Nancy, Big O was here, you (BLEEP).\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What we did not know is that in the days before the insurrection, the days before all that footage we played you was recorded, the Secret Service apparently knew that Pelosi was a target, that she was in danger and did not share that information with the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  until well, after the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 According to emails obtained by the nonpartisan watchdog group Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington, Secret Service discovered a parlor account making threats against various lawmakers including Pelosi on January 4. One of the posts reads "January 6 starts. #1776 all over again. Fight for everything." It then list -- names Pelosi in a list of enemies.\par \par \uc2 Donald K. Sherman is the Chief Counsel of CREW, was formerly a senior counsel in the Senate Homeland Security Committee where he handled the Secret Service portfolio, and he joins me now. Donald first, just give me a little context about this email and how we know that secret service, you know, had had sort of found this account and was sending messages about it.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:12]\par \par \uc2 DONALD K. SHERMAN, SENIOR VICE PRESIDENT, CREW: Well, so, right now we have more questions and answers. But we have an email dated on January 6 sent at 5:55 in the evening, obviously after Trump insurrectionist laid seized the Capitol, which said that Secret Service discovered these messages on January 4, discovered this account on January 4th, unleashed patriots on Parler which had made threats against President-Elect Biden. Included in in additional research about that account was a threat made on December 31, 2020 which included that enemies list and the references 1776 and listed a number of politicians and public figures including Speaker Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We should also note this from January 3 from the same user, we`re on a mission to save America, Lone wolf attacks are the way to go. Stay anonymous, stay alive, guns up, patriots. And then you know, hashtag -- cult conspiracy hashtag after that. What is -- what is the protocol of someone who had a secret service portfolio when you were on the Senate Homeland Security Committee for Secret Service to process and share threats if they`re not directed against their direct protectees?\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: To be honest with you, I don`t know. There`s not a lot of good information about this. And frankly, when I covered the Secret Service portfolio, we didn`t have threats like this that came to our attention because they were either dealt with quickly or they didn`t result in action -- actually realized attacks.\par \par \uc2 So, this is the first -- this is the first example of this that I have seen. But what we have learned through our FOIA requests is that there have been significant communication failures with respect to how agencies including Secret Service and \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  communicated at a time where clearly on January 6, there was meant to be hand-in-glove communication, because the Vice President was -- who is a secret service protectee was going to be at the Capitol. And obviously, we saw -- we`ve gotten trickles of information about how that communication has failed.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And it seems to me there`s a broader issue here just about a little bit of the impenetrability and lack of transparency around the Secret Service, which the secrecy is in the name of course, and I understand why they don`t want to be, you know, super explicit about their methods and tools and things like that. But are central players in this entire thing -- and it does seem a bit like a black box as of now -- what is your hope at CREW in terms of penetrating that?\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: Well, my hope -- frankly, my hope is that Congress already has more information about this than the public has, and that the January 6 Committee will make recommendations and has made recommendations to Secret Service for reforms. But my hope is that there is some public airing of how communication can be improved between these law enforcement agencies.\par \par \uc2 I mean, frankly, these posts were made on December 31. They weren`t discovered until five days later, and then weren`t communicated until after the Secret Service -- or excuse me, after the Capitol was sieged. So, you don`t need to know anything about the Secret Service and how it communicates to understand that there was a failure there.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, Donald Sherman of CREW, thank you so much for your time.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, 82 days out for the Midterms, it`s not a great sign when Republican leaders start complaining about the quality of their candidates. The Republican concerns about a shifting playing field next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , HOST, FOX NEWS: Let`s be clear, this is not a guaranteed wave election. Anyone that says Republicans have a lock on the House and the Senate, they`re lying to you. They don`t know. The races can be extremely competitive in every Bellwether state. This is really for the country an all-hands-on-deck situation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I love it when he says, they`re lying to you. That was Fox News personality \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  last night admitting that the outcome of the Midterm Elections is very much in the air. And on that, I agree with him. Now, it`s one thing for talking head to say that, but you really have to stand up and take notice when someone like the Republican Leader of the Senate comes out and says essentially the same thing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: I think there`s probably a greater likelihood the House flips than the Senate. Senate races are just different. They`re statewide. Candidate quality has a lot to do with the outcome. Right now, we have a 50-50 Senate and a 50-50 country. But I think when all is said and done this fall, we`re lucky to have an extremely close Senate, either our side up slightly or their side up slightly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Again, as analysis goes, I largely agree with a fair amount of that. With less than three months to go, the top Republican in the Senate does not sound that confident about his party`s chances. Notice the candidate quality line there too. And that`s in line with the current forecast. FiveThirtyEight currently projects the Democrats are actually slightly favored to win the Senate which is surprising, while Republicans are favored to take control of the House.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:09]\par \par \uc2 Now, I`ve said this a million times and we`ve been through this a bunch of times in my almost 10 years here at this desk. Take it all with a grain of salt. The future is uncertain. Polling has been wildly off, right? It`s early. Recent election cycles have taught us to be cautious about trusting particularly statewide individual polls, which have been like wildly off. There`s another key indicator that we can trust, though, or at least an indicator of what the people who are professionally obsessing over this are thinking, and that`s where money`s going.\par \par \uc2 The Senate Leadership Fund, which is the political action committee with close ties to Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  announced today they`re launching a massive new ad campaign in Ohio, reserving $28 million worth of TV and radio ads as cleveland.com points out, a huge escalation of the roughly $5 million national Republicans have spent in Ohio so far in the race between Republican J.D. Vance and Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, that my friends is a huge tell. Ohio Senate race was not supposed to be a top tier battleground fight. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won Ohio fairly easily, solid eight points in 2016, again in 2020. The fact that Republicans are having to make such a huge investment in that state shows that things are at this point, not going as planned.\par \par \uc2 This follows several other signs of which way the wind is blowing, including the reaction of the Supreme Court decision overturning Roe vs. Wade and the recent surprise victory for abortion rights in Kansas by a dramatic margin.\par \par \uc2 There`s another key center race where things are going so badly for the Republican candidate the Democrat is now, according to prognosticators, favored to win, which again, surprising. We`re going to talk about that with two Democratic campaign veterans next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: When you run for political office, one of your basic responsibilities to voters is debate your opponent. I take this responsibility very seriously. That`s why I`ve agreed to five -- that`s right, five debates, all based in Pennsylvania. But \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , he hasn`t agreed to a single one. Pennsylvanians deserve debates and the senator who`s willing to show up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I love the fact he says all based in Pennsylvania, in case you thought I wanted them in New Jersey. TV Doctor Mehmet Oz, the Republican nominee for Senate in Pennsylvania is increasingly turning to desperate measures in his race against Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . There`s a rule of thumb that the guy that wants to debate more, the candidate that wants to be more is the one who thinks they`re down.\par \par \uc2 His latest line of attack, basically calling Fetterman a coward for not yet agreeing to scheduled debates. But again, it`s clear why Oz is upset. Fetterman has pulled ahead in the polls, opening up a nearly 12-point lead according to FiveThirtyEight. Again, grain of salt, we`re before Labor Day. They just think -- consider the dead heat.\par \par \uc2 But today, The Cook Political Report moved its rating of the Pennsylvania Senate race from toss up to lean Democrat. Now, Oz is pretty clearly not a great candidate. But Pennsylvania is just one example of how things are going for Republicans more broadly, with less than three months to go until the Midterms.\par \par \uc2 David Plouffe is the former campaign manager for the 2008 Obama Presidential campaign as well as the former senior advisor to President Obama. Symone Sanders Townsend worked on both Senator Bernie Sanders in 2016 presidential campaign and President Joe Biden`s 2020 campaign. She`s, of course, the host of "SYMONE" on Peacock and MSNBC. It`s great to have you both.\par \par \uc2 Symone, where do you see the field right now mid-August coming up on the sort of like official opening of campaign season Labor Day and a lot of news around seeming to position Democrats in a better place than they were just say, three, four, or five months ago?\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS-TOWNSEND, MSNBC HOST: Well, look, I think Democrats are in fact in a better place than they were three to four or five months ago. And oh, I hope I`m not frozen, Chris, but I`m going to keep talking and telling you that look, the reality is that I think people saw Kansas and they got very excited. And I think that Kansas does demonstrate that voters are paying attention, and that they do not like extremism, and they`re willing to come out and vote against that extremism and vote to defeat it.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is that it`s not a straight or up or down question on the ballot in November about abortion or about guns. People are voting for a candidate. And I think that a number of the candidates that the Republicans have put up, they`re just bad candidates to be very clear. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , we just talked about Dr. Oz, J.D. Vance in Ohio. I mean, when we talk about governor`s races, Tudor Dixon in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Some of this -- these are just not good candidates. And I think because of the bad candidates, because of the extreme climate, if you will, on top of the fact that Democrats led by President Biden and Vice President Harris have been able to get a number of things done, I think it`s put Democrats on the ballot in November in a way better position than before.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that point --\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: I still think the House has a very, very high fine.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I would agree with that. And that`s of course, that`s similar to what \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  I thought said fairly honestly about how he assesses this. And to Symone`s point, David, I mean, McConnell says it`s right candidate quality, which clearly was a very interesting thing for him to say, and to me a dig at Trump because Trump, of course, has endorsed some of the candidates who I think -- whose quality I think he`s a little - - a little underwhelmed by. Do you share that assessment that there`s some space to be made up by Democrats against candidates that are not the strongest necessarily?\par \par \uc2 DAVID PLOUFFE, FORMER CAMPAIGN MANAGER, 2008 OBAMA PRESIDENTIAL CAMPAIGN: Oh, no question, Chris. I mean, first of all, the national political environment is probably as good as you could hope for if you`re a Democrat, you know, particularly to look back where we were six months ago. You know, abortion, gun control, fight for democracy.\par \par \uc2 Then you add in -- I mean, this is really a rogues gallery. And you know, McConnell seen it before. I mean, they had, you know, not great Senate candidates back in 2010 and 2012, so here you are again. There`s no question about that. So -- and listen, I would just double down on your caution. So, you showed the Pennsylvania race and clearly Fetterman has a lead. And you know, Oz is really just kind of a world class tool. I mean, it`s hard for me to see him turning his campaign around.\par \par \uc2 But you know, that -- those polls, you know, I think it was like 50 to 37. So, for -- you know, that means 13 percent of the votes unallocated. I think Democrats, if not at their high watermark right now, were getting close to it. And so, I would expect that a lot of these places where you see polls showing Democrats up, a lot of the unallocated votes are going to come home to the Republicans. So, these races are closer than they appear.\par \par \uc2 That being said, you know, Democrats right now have the potential to add Senate seats. I mean, first things first, you got to hold the majority because, you know, Wisconsin looks super competitive. Obviously, Mark Kelly looks very strong. And obviously, you showed that rogue`s gallery of the three hall of shame Senate candidate.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, I think there`s a bunch of things you put it together, national political environment, bad Republican candidates. And McConnell is right about this. I mean, he knows this. Senate races are like many presidential candidates. They take on, yes, the national political environment. It`s super important. But who these candidates are, they become household names over a period of months. And so, quality matter is a great deal.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And I think, you know, there`s also the -- when you talk about the national political environment, I mean, Symone, six months ago, things look so dire in so many ways, or even four or five months ago. I mean, particularly, you know, we`re talking about right after the war starts in February or March and gas prices are through the roof, you now have this like, comet that hits, right? This exogenous, you know, entrance into the - - into the race and folks are not happy with gas prices, understandably.\par \par \uc2 It is interesting to me to see how closely inflation and gas prices track with the political environment because Republicans have hung so much on that, but like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , they too can`t control it, right? So, that`s out of everyone`s control.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: Surely it`s out of -- look, I think that hanging your hat on the outside forces, if you will, and I`m talking about the war in Ukraine, and I`m talking about even inflation, because inflation is a -- is a global issue as we all know, Chris, really affecting folks. Obviously, people in America are concerned about inflation in America, but people are dealing with it in Europe. It`s happening in Canada.\par \par \uc2 I think hanging your hat on those things that you cannot control is dangerous for Democrats, right? So, what they`re saying is, look, given what is going on, we have done what we can. The President has done everything he can to get gas prices down. He released oil from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve. Democrats in Congress specifically have tried to put forth votes on legislation. The House passed legislation, every Republican representative voted against it, right? The Senate didn`t even take it up.\par \par \uc2 The Inflation Reduction Act, the name of the bill would Inflation Reduction Act. Every single Republican Congress voted against reducing inflation and lowering prescription drugs for not just seniors, but at least 30 million Americans, by the way. So, I think that, again, it`s what can we control and what have we done about what we can control. And things we can control, we need to make it better for the American people.\par \par \uc2 And I think now and November, it is the job Democratic candidate up and down the ticket (AUDIO GAP) Democrats that want to see Democrats in control, go out there and communicate that message very succinctly and forcefully.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: How much, David, do you think money matters these days? You know, I think in small races, they matter a ton. I think the bigger the race is, the less money tends to matter at the margins. So, you know, if you don`t put a ton of money into a state senator race, like you can really affect the outcome. By the time you get into presidential, it gets a lot harder because the structural factors are bigger.\par \par \uc2 When you see a $28 million ad buy in a state like Ohio, like, what is your assessment of in the year 2022 how much that money matter?\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Well, first of all, it`s breathtaking they`re spending that much in Ohio, you know. And so, I still think -- I mean, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  -- by the way, can the quality -- Democrats also have some excellent, excellent candidates. You know, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  being a great example of that. But still -- I mean, he`s leading there, but you know, he`s not at 50, so it`s a long way.\par \par \uc2 But clearly the Republican Senate brain trust, to the extent you can call them that, believe that they could lose Ohio because they`re putting a massive amount of money. And that being said, I very much agree with you. You know, some secretaries of states race, although less though these days, because they`re getting more attention state legislative, judicial races, money disparities can matter a great deal.\par \par \uc2 I think very few candidates for Senate, governor, or major House races these days, much less presidents lose races because of money. They`re all going to have enough. Somebody may have an advantage and that`s where you`d like to be if you`re running a campaign. You can basically do everything, every idea you have, but I think that`s right. I don`t think these races will be decided by money particularly basically at the \uc2 U.S. House\uc2  level, no.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Symone Sanders-Townsend, by the way, congratulations. I haven`t seen you since. David Plouffe, good to have you both. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN on this Thursday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now in this very room just a few feet away from me. Alex, how are you?\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220819ei8i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for August 18, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Cal Perry, Andrew Weissmann, Frank Figliuzzi,  Jacqueline Alemany, Tom Winter, Sahil Kapur, Ben Collins, Eugene Robinson,  Antonia Hylton \par \uc2 8,045 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: New York City. Right now, you are looking at a live picture of the federal courthouse in West Palm Beach, Florida. As we speak, a high-stakes hearing is getting underway that could ultimately reveal crucial details about the search at Mar-a-Lago. A decision on that could come anytime now. The hearing will pit the Justice Department against several news organizations, including this one, that are asking a federal magistrate judge to unseal the affidavit that led to the search.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department says that would be a grave mistake warning the judge "if disclosed, the Affidavit would serve as a roadmap to the government`s ongoing investigation, providing specific details about its direction and likely course, in a manner that is highly likely to compromise future investigative steps." Last night on this network, Connecticut Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jim Himes\uc2  who`s a member of the House Intelligence Committee says that`s not the only potential risk here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM HIMES (D-CT): Affidavit contains all sorts of information, including testimony by informants, witnesses, and others that now that we know that not just \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Republican Party generally will make threats, including threats on the lives of people, including \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents that are involved here. There`s an awful lot of risk.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So it could be unsealed or it could be kept sealed, but there is a third option for the judge and that would be releasing it with redactions. But the DOJ says if they redact everything they need to, the document will essentially be worthless. Bottom line, that affidavit could answer the why behind the search, about exactly what was taken. Investigators are apparently still figuring that out. Two law enforcement officials tell NBC News they`re still going through the material just as they have been for the past week and a half, trying to figure out what is and what is not relevant to their investigation.\par \par \uc2 And those aren`t the only documents making headlines today. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  says federal prosecutors looking into last year`s insurrection have issued a grand jury subpoena to the National Archives asking for "all materials in whatever form that the archives have sent to the January 6 committee." The Time says a subpoena shows the DOJ is following the committee`s lead to an extent but also that the department believes evidence of a crime may exist in those documents.\par \par \uc2 So much to get to, I want to bring in NBC`s Cal Perry who`s outside the courthouse in Florida. Jacqueline Alemany is a Congressional investigations reporter for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  and an MSNBC contributor. Andrew Weissmann is a former FBI General Counsel, a former member of the Mueller probe, and an MSNBC legal analyst. And Frank Figliuzzi is a former Assistant Director for counterintelligence at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and an MSNBC national security analyst.\par \par \uc2 So, Cal, let`s start where we are right now. Explain what`s going on inside that courtroom. And do we know when we might get word of a decision?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: So we may get word from the bench, from the judge today, as you said he could postpone it setting out some kind of schedule, or he could rule at a later date. So we don`t really know on that front. What we do know is that journalists have just been let into the courtroom and they were allowed to keep their phones, which is how I can tell you that five attorneys have arrived, three that are representing that group of media organizations that you talked about, two our representing \uc2 Judicial Watch\uc2 . We expect to hear from one of the lawyers that are representing this group of media organizations, including NBC News, and we expect to hear from a lawyer who`s representing \uc2 Judicial Watch\uc2 . We will then hear from the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 Now, as you laid out, the Department of Justice is going to say that releasing this affidavit is going to impede their investigation that not only does it put witnesses in jeopardy, but it could show further witnesses and they really don`t want to give away the game plan there. What you will hear from the media is that the public has an interest to this document because "of the historically significant unprecedented execution of a search warrant with regards to a former president." This was from the filing that the media put forward to the judge. It also says "not since the Nixon administration has the federal government wielded his power to seize records from a former president in such a public fashion."\par \par \uc2 And you talk about this happening in public. We have seen Trump supporters in just the last 30 minutes arriving here at the scene at the courthouse. This roped-off area behind me is the area that the law enforcement personnel have set aside for possible protests or demonstrations. And I should add that the judge, in this case, judge Reinhart, who signed that affidavit that allowed for that search warrant to take place, he has received death threats.\par \par \uc2 Our local affiliate here has done some pretty stunning reporting on this in the last few days. But the Palm Beach Gardens Police Department putting out a very brief statement this morning saying that they are "aware of the threats against judge Reinhart and that they are working with their federal law enforcement partners." It is probably why, Chris, you see sort of a high amount of law enforcement presence here on the ground outside of the federal courthouse.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, without a doubt, Cal. And you talked about the report, it`s coming from within the courthouse including one that we just got that the Trump attorney Christina Bobb, and we`ve mentioned her name before, is inside the courtroom. They will not be making arguments important to point out.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Let`s go back to what the judge is doing right now, Andrew. We talked about the options here, unseal, don`t unseal, or maybe redact. What kinds of things is he going to be looking at and what ultimately are you expecting to see here?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: So, I`m going to go out on a limb and say, there is zero chance that the judge is going to rule that the full affidavit gets unsealed. There are national security interests, there are criminal interests, there are concerns about witnesses, it`s just -- it`s just unheard of. There`s no precedent for that. The only thing that I think the news organizations could hope for is taking up the footnote where, as you noted, Chris, the government said they`ve thought about a redacted version, but they thought it would not be -- it would be so redacted, it wouldn`t be helpful. But that`s all they said.\par \par \uc2 And I could see the court saying, you know, I want the government to give me what they would propose to redact because I need to see myself if that`s really true, that there`s nothing that could be released here. I think that`s the most that could happen. And if that is what the judge rules, it`s going to take some time because then the government will get time to submit that, the court will assess it, and then hear from the government about why it couldn`t go forward and release that. I think that the most that could happen eventually out of this hearing.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: As we look at what he`s weighing, Andrew, obviously, on one side is the idea of protecting the investigation, the other is serving the public interest, there`s a whole lot of levels to that, but at least in part, it`s trying to keep the country from devolving into violence. How do you balance that against the DOJ`s warning? Is it always going to lean toward the DOJ?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: In this case, it will. I mean, the court is going to look to precedent. And so the precedent here is really lopsided. I hear you about the public interest here, but that`s not really going to be paramount. I mean, eventually, if someone is charged, this document will see the light of day, and even then probably in a redacted form because there`s no way that the classified information that is highly likely in this affidavit is going to be released publicly. So I hear you, but those are -- those kinds of interests that the news organizations have are not going to outweigh the significant national security, criminal interests, and witness protection issues.\par \par \uc2 And then one final thing which you mentioned, Chris, which I found interesting is you know the former president has publicly said that he wants the names of witnesses, but sort of unusually, he actually hasn`t filed any papers in court and his lawyers aren`t going to actually be heard. So that really does appear to be more of a precedent than something that -- seriously something that they want the court to consider.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I suppose, Frank, you could also say, look, if there`s going to be violence and you know, we`re hearing from Cal already there are concerns about the judge, it may not matter one way or another. There are people going to be unhappy if they see what`s in it. There are people who are unhappy just because the raid happened, right? We`ve seen the threats that have been made real and verbal against the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , tell me your thoughts on this today.\par \par \uc2 FRANK FIGLIUZZI, MSNBC NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST AND CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I`m -- I agree with Andrew that we`re unlikely to see any kind of helpful release of the affidavit. And here`s why. There`s a real danger here to human life not only damage to what the DOJ is already called an ongoing criminal investigation and references to witnesses past and future. But look, there appear to be cooperators in this case.\par \par \uc2 Don`t forget, we`ve tried to subpoena -- we saw DOJ try a subpoena. It didn`t work. We saw that the Trump attorney filed allegedly a document saying we gave you everything and that was wrong. And then something prompted them to go in with a warrant. And that really smells a lot like cooperating witnesses. It`s quite possible. We also have heard that the security footage outside the storage room really alarms DOJ, all of that is in that affidavit.\par \par \uc2 Remember you -- the affidavit establishes probable cause for the three offenses asserted in the warrant. And so for example, we would learn things like well, how many witnesses you know, and when did you talk to them? That would identify human beings who would be at risk. And then with regard to the charge of falsifying or destroying documents that obstruction like the charge that`s in the warrant, we would learn in that affidavit why they think something`s been falsified.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 Has somebody blocked out classification markings on documents? Did the former president instruct people to destroy documents or gin up a fake declassification document? That would be revealed. So what would a redacted version of that look like? A black magic marker after black magic marker, I don`t think it`d be very helpful.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And one of the things we just talked about, Jackie, is that Christina Bobb is in there. She`s not going to take any participants -- participate in any of the arguments. But there`s -- publicly, Trump`s saying, oh, he`s very happy with his legal team, your reporting is something else. Tell us what you found out.\par \par \uc2 JACQUELINE ALEMANY, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, Chris. Well, I think we should reinforce what Andrew pointed out, which is that despite Christina Bobb being in the courthouse today, Trump`s legal team has not submitted any formal motion to the court declaring a stance to release the affidavit in the interest of transparency as the former president has previously said. But in the lead-up to today`s case, we have reported that Trump`s current legal team was seeking prosecutors, defenders, lawyers with more experience.\par \par \uc2 They had reached out to multiple different lawyers, some quite prominent ones in the Republican Party who all declined to join the case. We`d also been told that this was actually a long-standing search that preceded this spilling into public view. And had multiple voices telling us that in the case of such a serious criminal matter, that it`s important that the former president have legal representation with people that have more experience that are suited to this issue of the former president potentially being charged criminally with mishandling classified information.\par \par \uc2 That being said, there are a few members of his team who do have extensive prosecutorial high-level experience. People like John Trusty, Evan Corcoran, but we`re also hearing is that there is some bubbling concern that people like Evan Corcoran, who might have been involved with some of the previous attestations about the presence or existence of certain documents and boxes, might ultimately embroil him in some legal trouble, so much that he potentially have to recuse himself from representing the former president. These are all things that we`re keeping our fingers on the pulse of, and watching very closely today as this unfolds in Palm Beach.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And you know, Andrew, that what the president -- the former president has said is these aren`t classified documents, I just declassified everything. Everything that I took out of the oval lie immediately marked who said was -- were declassified. So earlier today, he post this memorandum that appears to show that he did declassified documents related to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s crossfire hurricane, which then led to the Mueller probe, which you know, well, but does that speak at all to what we`re talking about today?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: So I actually think that document hurts the president. If I were his counsel, I`m not sure I would have advised to release that. And the reason I say that is one, whether something is classified or declassified is actually irrelevant to the statutes that were listed in the search warrant. And there are other statutes that could be charged as well that where it`s irrelevant that it`s classified or not.\par \par \uc2 Second, it`s not clear that there`s a complete overlap, indeed, it`s highly unlikely that the only documents that were found in Mar-a-Lago with classification marks were the hurricane -- crossfire hurricane documents, to which there`s a clear set of documents that the Department of Justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had. And the third problem is that document that the former president posted shows that the president knows exactly how to declassify documents in the formal correct process.\par \par \uc2 But that is not what Kash Patel and the former president had been saying about the documents in Mar-a-Lago. They said that there was a standing order where documents miraculously got declassified because you move them from one room in the White House to another room in the White House, which, as you can tell from the way I phrase that, I and many other people are very skeptical of that factual proposition. But I think that memo shows that the president knew exactly how to do this in the correct fashion.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And as he was talking about that, Frank, I saw you nodding and smiling so, obviously, you agree about what that might show for the president. But let me ask you about what`s happening with the seized materials because attorney Mark Zaid and who`s worked on cases involving seized materials talked about the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s process of going through them on "MORNING JOE" today. Let me play a little bit of what he said.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK ZAID, ATTORNEY: I have had the \uc2 FBI\uc2  when they`ve seized clients records of mine or you know, my clients` records take months. I think I still have a client who hasn`t gotten back files in six or eight years. But that`s not going to happen in such a high-profile case, obviously, as the former president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But why, Frank, does it take that long? And how long might this take? Obviously, they know that there is a red-hype hot spotlight on what they`re doing right now.\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: Well, when it comes to classified documents or even just government documents, right, we`ve been talking about the lack of relevance to whether they`re classified or not, they belong to the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 . So I -- if we`re -- if the question is how long until Trump gets \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  documents back? The answer is, maybe never. If he ever has a library and museum, the National Archives will decide what goes into that library and museum.\par \par \uc2 But these are our documents, yours and mine. They belong to the \uc2 United States government\uc2 , regardless of classification level. If a filter team looks at some things as allegedly they did with his passports and says, yes, these really -- these can go back, they`re not -- they`re not of relevance, they`re not -- they`re really aren`t necessarily of the government`s, yes, then that`ll go back. But what we`re talking about here are things that belong to the government.\par \par \uc2 And there`s a painstaking long process and doing a damage assessment, Chris, which involves tracking back in history, where those documents came from, whether they were originated in the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , Allied governments that may have provided the Intel to us, and usually a TS/SCI documents, there are specific markings about who touched them, where they came from. There`s a history and a lot kept on TS documents, I`d have to sign at my desk at FBI Headquarters, time, date, name, I touched it, I gave it back to the career, done. All of that has to be tracked.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, a timeline is going to be established with the security cameras outside that storage room, which Trump has provided apparently. And when people went in? Who are their face -- what are their faces? Who are those people? Did a cleaning person go into the storage room? Go interview that person. Who were all the visitors to the residence at Mar-a-Lago? There was --there were pilgrimages you`ll remember after January where everybody and their brother went down to say hello to the former president. All of that has to be cross-referenced to determine who had access, who saw it, and who shouldn`t have.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We could talk about this for the entire hour, fascinating conversation. Frank, Andrew. Jacqueline, thank you very much. Cal, of course, we`ll come back to you outside the courthouse if you get any updates at all, so let us know.\par \par \uc2 There is one other important hearing we`re following for you, \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  CFO Allen Weisselberg facing a judge in New York today with big legal implications for the former president, plus, why Republicans are now targeting the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We are looking at a live shot of the Florida courthouse where at any moment a judge could decide to unseal the Mar-a-Lago search warrant affidavit. We have a team of people on site, and we`ll have that decision for you as soon as we learn it.\par \par \uc2 In another courthouse, 1200 miles away in New York City, \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  CFO Allen Weisselberg admitted that the company had an off-the-books scheme to avoid paying taxes on lavish benefits and other perks for employees. But this is only about the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . Rather than turn on his old boss and testify in a broader investigation, the 75-year-old has agreed to do five months in jail. The plea also requires him to testify truthfully about illicit business practices at the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  during a trial scheduled for this fall, and he must repay the state and city of New York nearly $2 million.\par \par \uc2 For more, we`re joined by NBC`s Tom Winter at the courthouse, with me in studio, Shan Wu, a former federal prosecutor who now represents clients in white-collar criminal matters. So, Tom, tell us what happened in the courtroom this morning.\par \par \uc2 TOM WINTER, MSNBC INVESTIGATIONS CORRESPONDENTS: Right. So for Alan Weiselberger, he really kind of closes a bit of a chapter for him and yet opens another one of going from staunch defendant to somebody who admitted his guilt and will now have to testify against the very Corporation he used to be the Chief Financial Officer for.\par \par \uc2 That off-the-books payment scheme that you mentioned there, Chris, has the idea that he was able to get payments for \uc2 Mercedes Benz\uc2  -- multiple \uc2 Mercedes Benz\uc2 , cars that he had, apartment in New York City, a garage, other cash benefits. It`s not necessarily illegal for him to have received those things from the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . It was illegal, according to prosecutors, and now according to Weisselberg himself, for him not to pay the taxes for that compensation and for the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  not to present the proper filings for it.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s really what jammed him up here. That`s what he`s in trouble for. As you said, yes, a five-month jail sentence on Rikers Island here in New York City for him. That`s what he has ahead of him. But that`ll be sentenced and he`ll go there after a late October trial involving the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  which was indicted at the same time as Weisselberg, part of the same indictment approximately a year ago. So that`s coming up first. Weisselberg has to provide truthful testimony to that. No indication here at all, Chris, that the former CFO plans to turn against the former president personally.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So you, Shan, represent white-collar clients, right? And I`m wondering what you make of this decision by him, OK, I`m going to plead guilty. In this case, that`s about the organization but I am not going to cooperate with this other investigation by the Manhattan DA into Trump.\par \par \uc2 SHAN WU, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: This is a big win for Weisselberg`s defense team and really a loss for the DA. It`s a sweetheart deal for him. It`s very little time. I`m not putting down the fact that a 75-year-old going to jail for any amount of time is not easy. But given what he was facing, 15 years, this is a slap on the wrist for him. It`s another example of really the kind of -- I don`t know what the reason is for it, but the timidity of \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  in going after Trump. I mean, he had prosecutors ready to go on Trump, backed it off. Weisselberg is the CFO of the organization. So unless the theory is he was running rogue doing things, it`s inexplicable that the path doesn`t lead to Trump.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So was this a case do you imagine where they took what they could get, they wanted to make sure that he was punished to some degree.\par \par \uc2 WU: It`s the case honestly, as a former prosecutor, now a defense counsel.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WU: It`s the case where the prosecutors blinked. They had a lot of leverage on him at his age with that kind of time. They could have demanded that they go to trial if it wouldn`t take a harsher plea because of white-collar cases, all about the paper documents. They don`t have a great defense to say, I got this income but I didn`t pay the taxes to it. He`s the CFO, it`s not like he`s a very simplistic minded person unsophisticated so they had a lot of leverage. They blinked, basically, just to check the rocks, and get the case done with.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Tom, I`m also curious. If he`s willing to talk -- to testify about the business, what are the implications for Trump? I mean, it is hard to separate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  personally, from the business professionally, right? It`s intertwined with his personal wealth, his lifestyle, his image.\par \par \uc2 WINTER: The man is the brand and the brand is the man effectively. And so you`re right, Chris, yes, it is difficult to separate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from his business or vice versa. But it is a little bit easier to separate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his wealth from his business. He does have his own -- and we know this from his presidential filings, don`t know his current financial condition, but we know from the filings when he was president that he had his own wealth, his own financial securities, and instruments outside of his business. So it`s not as if that`s the only thing that he has if the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  was shut down today, every golf course closed, every hotel closed, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would still have some wealth granted, not to the degree that he has.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s also important to remember that over the years, Donald Trump`s shifted away from owning the physical properties himself, from managing the physical properties himself to licensing his name and brand. Now, we haven`t seen as many of those deals, since obviously, he became president. And since then -- and since he`s left the White House, and now former president, but still he was able to accumulate cash wealth that way, is a means of driving his own pockets as well as his -- to the benefit of his corporation. So there is a little bit of daylight there that does exist and as far as the wealthy hold personally.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Shan, you mentioned that Weisselberg because of his position likely knew if not everything, most things right that were going on within the organization. In a plea deal like this, where does cooperation end? If there`s some new investigation, for example, would it indemnify -- would it keep him from being charged in something like that? Where it is a cooperation agreement end?\par \par \uc2 WU: In this situation, his lawyers have fashioned a very narrow cooperation agreement. He`s basically agreeing to tell the truth if he gets called to testify, and he`s admitting what he personally did. So if new charges arise, he could easily take the Fifth Amendment to those charges, and basically insulate the organization and insulate Trump from doing that. This is not what we typically think of as a cooperation agreement where they`re coming in and sitting next to the prosecutors, investigators going through the books explaining everything to them. It`s very narrowly construed and that`s why it`s really a big win for him and not a good deal for the prosecution.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Shan Wu, thanks so much for coming in. Appreciate it. Tom Winter, appreciate your reporting as well.\par \par \uc2 One more court appearance of note today, the man accused of stabbing author Salman Rushdie was indicted by a grand jury on one count of second-degree attempted murder and one count of second-degree assault. Hadi Matar appeared in court today for his arraignment after pleading not guilty to charges on Sunday. Matar is accused of stabbing Rushdie 10 times last week at a speaking engagement. Rushdie is still recovering from his injuries in a Pennsylvania Hospital.\par \par \uc2 We are watching the courthouse in West Palm Beach where at any moment a judge could unseal the affidavit over the search of Mar-a-Lago, plus the GOP goes after the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . It`s a pretty shocking letter from a senator about open jobs at the agency that`s causing some controversy. We`ll tell you all about it. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:55]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So while we`re still waiting for word out of the courthouse in West Palm Beach, Florida, where the judge is deciding whether to unseal that affidavit over the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of Mar-a-Lago, we did just get notice that the judge has said he will release some of the procedural filings that currently are under seal now. These are more uninteresting documents related to the warrant.\par \par \uc2 The DOJ had said they did not oppose them being released but we`ll see what information is in those as we wait to see about that other bigger decision that could come down at any moment.\par \par \uc2 And after the heavy criticism of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , Republicans are now escalating their rhetoric against a different three-letter agency, the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . Their target, how $80 billion slated for the agency from the Inflation Reduction Act, will be spent. Take a listen to what a few of them are saying.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Those \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents are designed to come after you. They`re not designed to come after the billionaires and the big corporations. They`re designed to come after small businesses and working families.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA: They`re going to go after the mom and pop, they`re going to go after the small business person, the independent contractor, the \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver. And they are going to focus on basically -- so the country that don`t support what the regime is trying to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And even the House minority leader Kevin McCarthy tweeted that the \uc2 IRS\uc2  will hire an army of new agents to, quote, "spy on Americans." All of these leading to this stunning moment where U.S. Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  urged people seeking jobs not to apply to what he calls the \uc2 IRS\uc2  super police.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC senior Capitol Hill correspondent Sahil Kapur. Also with us Ben Collins who of course covers disinformation, extremism and the internet for NBC News.\par \par \uc2 So, Sahil, let`s do a little fact check here. Are there going to be a group of people who are \uc2 IRS\uc2  super police? And walk us through this letter from \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUT, NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: No, there won`t, Chris. That`s the answer to your first question. And secondly, it`s not every day that a United States senator tells Americans not to apply for a particular job by threatening to eliminate it but that`s exactly what Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  did in this rather extraordinary letter.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 He penned an open letter to American job seekers warning them not to apply for new \uc2 IRS\uc2  jobs under the $80 billion cash infusion under this law, arguing that Republicans hope to take control of Congress this fall and one of first things they will do once that happens is defund those positions.\par \par \uc2 Now there are a couple of problems with this. The first is that even if Republicans do capture control of Congress, not a certainty but certainly plausible, they will not -- you know, President Biden will still have the veto pen. And he will be able to block any attempts to undo his signature legislation. And the second is, the premise of this is unsubstantiated at best. There`s a May 2021 Treasury report that suggests the \uc2 IRS\uc2  could hire about 87,000 employees over a decade.\par \par \uc2 That`s a number that Republicans have seized on, but a lot of those employees will be replacing exists employees, and not all of them would be in enforcement so the depiction that 87,000 new \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents would be auditing the taxes of regular Americans is not backed by the facts. The Biden administration has gone to great lengths to point out that they will only use these resources to audit the taxes of wealthy Americans who are not paying their -- you know, who are using sophisticated means to avoid taxes at this point.\par \par \uc2 And the Republican argument at this point is partly based on the fact that this is their best case to make against this Democrats` only reconciliation bill given that other aspects of it such as the prescription drug pricing empowering Medicare to negotiate prices and higher taxes on corporations are popular. This is one aspect that they think they can make political hay of, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, and look, Ben, it`s not like the \uc2 IRS\uc2  is the most popular ever organization. Having said that, you know, we`ve seen this playbook before, stir up resentment toward a federal agency. In this case, is any of it sticking?\par \par \uc2 BEN COLLINS, NBC NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: It`s sticking everywhere. It`s being conflated on far-right parts of the internet with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of Mar-a-Lago. They`re saying if they`re going after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , they`re going to go after you for the same reason, even though it`s a completely different agency, obviously. It`s being conflated into one thing. It`s sort of the completion of the Infowars-ification of this party.\par \par \uc2 This is something that Alex Jones specifically has been warning against for 10 or 20 years. The idea that the \uc2 IRS\uc2  is, you know, going to -- poses a front for law enforcement, come and take your guns, come and take all of your assets. That`s what you see on Militia TikTok right now which is a ting. Militias are on TikTok trying to recruit people, but saying, you know, their standard rhetoric about come and take it, they`re pointing it toward the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , not towards any sort of law enforcement agency.\par \par \uc2 They thinks this is part of a much larger plan. To take away both your guns and your money. And by the way, it`s different now than 10 years ago, or even seven years ago. You know, seven years ago there was this conspiracy called Jade Helm, where the idea was \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  was going to use the facade of a military exercise in Texas to occupy Texas and never leave power. Ted Cruz back then said he called the Pentagon and there`s nothing to worry about it, don`t worry about it.\par \par \uc2 But now Ted Cruz is all up in this \uc2 IRS\uc2  fearmongering thing. The party has leaned closer and closer to conspirational rhetoric like this because they realize it`s a winner among their base.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ben Collins, Sahil Kapur, both great reporting as usual. Thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 It`s a huge number. $650.5 million, but that`s what a federal judge told \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , CVS and \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  to pay for their role in the deadly opioid crisis in just two counties in Ohio. That combined fine is to be paid out over the next 15 years after the companies were found liable last November for over-supplying pills that ended up on the black market.\par \par \uc2 Thwarting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That is Liz Cheney`s goal now, but what can she try that hasn`t already been tried to shift the GOP away from the former president? We`ll talk about it on CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:43:13]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Didn`t take long. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is already moving on. After her nearly 40-point defeat, the congresswoman from Wyoming shifted the rest of her campaign war chest into a new entity, the Great Task, which, as the AP points out, is a phrase from Lincoln`s Gettysburg address. Now Cheney says her one goal is to keep \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  out of the Oval. But can she battle the former president in a party that he has singlehandedly reshaped in his own image. How does \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  go up against this big cult of personality?\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist Eugene Robinson.\par \par \uc2 Always great to see you, Eugene. So, look, we know Wyoming is very red. I was there for a couple of days, I talked to voters at the Hageman victory party. There is no greater treason for them than opposing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So my question is, what is it that \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  think she can change? Does she have some secret plan nobody else has tried before against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 EUGENE ROBINSON, WASHINGTON POST COLUMNIST: Well, if she is a secret plan, it`s super-secret, and I can`t imagine what it is. I mean, look, we know where the Republican Party is now. If you look at the primaries that we`ve seen so far, he hasn`t won every single race. Trump hasn`t won every single race with his preferred candidates but he has won most of them. And the members of Congress, the Republicans who dared to vote for impeachment or to vote for conviction, with the exception of Senator Murkowski, who did very well in Alaska, having voted to impeach him, a second impeachment. Nonetheless, the others, I think it`s, what, eight out of 10 are gone.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: I mean, and, you know, so, that`s a pretty good record if you`re betting 800. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is getting control of the Republican Party right now. And I don`t see how \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  cracks that.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:06]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Which kind of supports the argument that Gary Abernathy made in your paper which is that folks like Cheney, he writes, need to give up on the delusion that Republicans have just somehow temporarily lost their way. So maybe this is bigger than whether \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  can save the Republican Party. But if you`re talking about, say, the party of Reagan and the Bushes, is it dead already?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: I don`t think the party of Reagan and the Bushes really exists right now. If it does, it`s kind of in exile. Those people are basically independents now, or they`re Republicans without much of a voice in the Republican Party. It`s a much more populist Republican Party now. This is not your father`s Republican Party. It`s not your Republican Party of just a few years ago before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  came in and activated and energized the populist revolution that I suspect was already going on inside the Republican Party. It`s not all Trump. He was a catalyst, but it had to be there before he got there.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Eugene Robinson, it`s always great to talk with us. Thank you for being on the program.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, you`ve probably heard the names \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , Dr. Oz, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , but what about Cheri Beasley? All of them are running for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . NBC went on the trail with Beasley in North Carolina to learn why she thinks she can flip a seat from red to blue.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:24]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Breaking news, and an update on the hearing to unseal the affidavit that led to the search of Mar-a-Lago. Let`s go to NBC`s Cal Perry outside the courthouse in West Palm Beach.\par \par \uc2 What`s new, Cal?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Chris, so this is coming from our producer who is in the courtroom right now, Juliette Arcodia. Now she writes that the DOJ started talking to the judge. It was very conversational to begin with. That`s when we heard that there would be some release of documents, again procedural documents, as you put it sort of not the exciting bit.\par \par \uc2 Then the media took over, that is the group of media organizations represented by their attorney, took over and made their arguments saying that this was in the public interest as we had sort of seen in the previous court filings that the extraordinary nature of what had happened made it necessary for the government to release this affidavit. They said that you can`t see anything in the dark, that they needed to sort of shine a light on this.\par \par \uc2 Now DOJ is making their case. And here`s what they`re saying. They`re calling the document a, quote, "detailed roadmap" of an investigation that is still in its, quote, "early stages." A top counterintelligence and national security official at the DOJ argued that the document needs to be kept under wraps because it contains, as he said, quote, "substantial grand jury information" in a, quote, "unique case" with, quote, "national security overtones."\par \par \uc2 He then said, quote, "This is a volatile situation with respect to the particular search across the political spectrum." That is a mild way, Chris, of saying that this search has set off a lot of opinions, least of all in Trump world, least of all at the DOJ, and least of all with the media. So you have this now back and forth argument. DOJ, we understand, is going to have the last word in these court proceedings.\par \par \uc2 The media lawyers may speak up one more time but again DOJ is going to have the final word. And a reminder, this judge is a former federal prosecutor. So it`s likely he is going to listen to these arguments of national security.\par \par \uc2 And one more time, Chris, we don`t know what we don`t know but the Department of Justice is saying and they`re saying clearly now, and I think this is probably going to be one of the headlines, that this investigation is, quote, "in the early stages," Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Fascinating and important stuff. Cal Perry, thank you. We`ll come back to you as needed.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, there`s new poling in the critically important North Carolina Senate race and it shows as dead heat. Millions of dollars continue to pour into a race you may not have heard much about but that could be make or break to determine which party runs the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Antonia Hylton went to North Carolina to show us what former state chief justice Cheri Beasley is doing to try to become the state`s first black senator.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Former state Supreme Court justice Cheri Beasley has to be a different Democrat to win the Senate seat in North Carolina, one that spends as much time in rural counties as they do in urban centers.\par \par \uc2 CHERI BEASLEY (D), SENATE CANDIDATE IN NORTH CAROLINA: Certainly industries and corporations are coming to North Carolina. They tend to go to Raleigh and Charlotte but they miss out on the talent in our rural community.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Beasley lost her last statewide race by just 401 votes as thousands of registered rural voters color sat out the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): Do politicians come here that often?\par \par \uc2 WHYDON SELLERS, NORTH CAROLINA VOTER: No, no. We usually see them at\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: What do you think is at stake right now?\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL COLE JR., NORTH CAROLINA VOTER: Well, again, being a rural county, just getting people out to vote.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON (voice-over): Beasley is in a competitive race with Republican Congressman \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 , giving her a shot at becoming the first black senator from the Tar Heel State.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Stir up that good trouble up there in Washington.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Her race also seen as one of the Democrats` rare chances to pick up a Senate seat.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): With the frustrations that some voters have with the Biden administration over the economy and inflation, has the president hurt your chances here?\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: You know, I certainly understand why there are people who are frustrated and who are disappointed in what`s happening in Washington. They expect more and they deserve more.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON (voice-over): Beasley`s Republican opponent meantime happy to hammer the president.\par \par \uc2 REP. TED BUDD (R-NC): Every single county is now a border county because of Joe Biden`s policy.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: And critiquing her judicial record when meeting with police groups.\par \par \uc2 BUDD: I`ve got an opponent right now, she may not admit it, she`s going to try to run deceptively so as a moderate.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: At Beasley`s campaign stops in rural counties, it was abortion rights driving the conversation.\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: It is a lot of contradiction to on the one hand protect fundamental freedoms and on the other hand support women who want to have their babies.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:01]\par \par \uc2 HYLTON (on-camera): Has it angered people? Has it changed some voters` minds?\par \par \uc2 ERIKA JOY, NORTH CAROLINA VOTER: I think so because I think people, as far as women, you know, they want to have control and make decisions that`s going to benefit their bodies and not to be told what to do.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON (voice-over): With less than three months until the election, Beasley is betting on rural voters. She needs them and they need someone who won`t forget them once in Washington.\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: It felt good here today, right? It felt good here in Anson County. It felt good in Richmond County, and no one should feel discounted. No one should feel a sense of disengagement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And Antonia is here with us. I was looking into some of the polls and there`s some analysis that some of this shift towards Beasley is about abortion. Is that what you heard on the ground?\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Absolutely. And in fact, at the two stops that we followed her to, the first questions out of voters` mouths were about abortion. And a lot of the voters I just sat with myself, it was top of mind for them. And this was really fascinating for me, Chris, because as part of our "MEET THE PRESS" county-to-county series, I spent a lot of time in rural North Carolina talking to voters who didn`t vote in the 2020 election. And many of them were telling me back in the winter and the spring not sure that anything is going to get me out to vote right now, I don`t trust either party, people have forgotten about us, and now abortion is bringing a lot of these people to events, a lot of them to the table.\par \par \uc2 And Beasley herself told me that she`s seeing that, that even young people, young voters are coming out and saying that now they`re paying more attention to her campaign because of this.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And one of the things we know if people show up, they`re invested, they`re more invested and more likely to vote.\par \par \uc2 Fascinating stuff. Thank you, Antonia. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to joins us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday, 1:00 p.m. Eastern. "KATY TUR REPORTS" will have much more on the Mar-a-Lago search affidavit. We are waiting for the judge`s decision to come down. So don`t go anywhere. Katy will be here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Good to be with you. I`m Katy Tur. It could be a pretty big day and it`s all up to one man.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220819ei8i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for August 17, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Dahlia Lithwick \par \uc2 6,858 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:36]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 And you are off to a fantastic start, as everyone can see. I really could listen to you and Jelani Cobb -- in fact, if you want to use just another ten minutes or so --\par \par \uc2 ALEX AWAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT": I will bring it back in the studio --\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Andy night, any night, Alex, when you need, say, an extra five, ten minutes, just text me and I will stay upstairs.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Friday, Friday.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: OK, all right. Thank you, Alex.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Have a good show, Alex.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 It would be very bad news for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is that \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  has finally, today, learned to talk like a lawyer again. Today, for the first time in years, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  did not sound like a hallucinating mad man when he walked into the Fulton County courthouse in Georgia to testify to a grand jury.\par \par \uc2 He did not sound like someone willing to tell any lie for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He didn`t sound like someone ready to take any legal risks for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Not anymore. Not now that he has been formally declared a target of the grand jury`s criminal investigation of exactly what \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  did for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  you are about to see is truly shocking now. But it is the way \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  often handled press questions when he was a federal prosecutor in Manhattan in the 1980s. Today, with \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  clinging to the wreckage of his legal career with his law license suspended, pending possible permanent disbarment, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  found his lawyerly voice once again.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: When you talked to Georgia lawmakers, did you lie to them?\par \par \uc2 RUDY GIULIANI\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP ATTORNEY: We`ll not talk about this, until the grand jury, and the grand juries, as I recall, are secret.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Do you believe President Trump is the ultimate target of this investigation?\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI: I`m not going to comment on the grand jury investigation, until I know more about it.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: What are you expecting to talk about here today?\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI: Look, they ask the questions and we`ll see.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Will you be cooperative? Your attorney in New York says he can`t promise how responsive you`ll be.\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI: Goodbye.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Arriving at the courthouse at 8:30 a.m., before the doors were even unlocked, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  showed no evidence of the health issues that his lawyer had told the judge made him physically incapable of doing what he did today, walking into that courtroom and testifying. The 78-year-old \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  showed no physical weakness and actually demonstrated more mental strength than we have seen in him in a very long time.\par \par \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  was asked, do you believe president Trump is the ultimate target of the investigation? And he said, I am not going to comment. That should leave \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  terrified tonight. \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  did not launch into a Giuliani rant about the grand jury going off to his hero \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  unfairly. \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  said the kind of thing people say when they are very afraid of what is going to happen to them in the grand jury room.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is very afraid tonight about what actually did happen in that grand jury room today. There is no law that prevents \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  from telling \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  everything that was said in his six hours of testimony to the grand jury.\par \par \uc2 But whatever summary of his testimony \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  might be giving \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tonight -- possibly at this very moment -- \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should not believe it. \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  entered the grand jury room alone, as all witnesses do, to face 23 grand jurors, who already suspect him of criminal conduct. That`s why he is a target of the grand jury`s investigation.\par \par \uc2 Public evidence seems to show that \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  did commit the crime of lying to Georgia government officials. He is doing exactly that with members of the Georgia legislature in this video.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:05:01]\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI: There is overwhelming proof before it, it depends on you. And it depends on your determination of credibility. I don`t have to be a genius to figure out that those votes are not legitimate votes. You don`t put legitimate votes under a table.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No.\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI: Wait until you throw the opposition out and in the middle of the night, count them. You would have to be fools to think that. So, no, no need to push it any further. But there is more than ample evidence to conclude that this election was a sham. In my view, is part of a concerted plan. Certain cities were picked in order to carry this out, where they thought they could get away with it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Those votes are not legitimate votes. All of that, everything he said, is a lie, all of it.\par \par \uc2 Today, he was no doubt asked detailed questions about each sentence, possibly each word of each sentence that you just heard him speak. He was asked under oath today, in the grand jury, about everything he said on that video recording and other statements he made in Georgia and elsewhere. He could not claim attorney client privilege and refused to answer questions about what you just saw, because that does not involve a conversation with his client at the time, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But he could have pleaded the Fifth Amendment and refused to answer questions about what we just heard him say, because his answers could obviously implicate him and in even more criminal conduct. If \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  played it tough the way \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  no doubt wants him to, and refused to answer all questions by citing the Fifth Amendment, the way \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did in a New York deposition last week 440 times, then why was \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  in the grand jury room for six hours?\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  cannot be confident tonight that \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  did not give that grand jury, intentionally or not, some evidence that moved them closer to the biggest possible target of their investigation, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here is what District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  said two weeks ago about the possibility of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  testifying to that grand jury.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: How important is it that the special grand jury hears from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  directly?\par \par \uc2 FANI WILLIS\uc2 , DISTRICT ATTORNEY, FULTON COUNTY, GA: I think that the special purpose grandeur a need to hear as much information from as many people that are willing to come and testify truthfully.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Georgia`s Republican Governor Brian Kemp is now fighting a subpoena from \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  to testify to the grand jury. The governor`s motion today to quash the subpoena reveals, for the first time, that Governor Kemp was subpoenaed to give testimony tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Legal correspondence attached to the filing shows that District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  began negotiation with the governor`s lawyer to obtain his testimony in the most cooperative, possible way, without a subpoena. But she changed her mind about that, as the governor`s lawyer, Brian McEvoy, became uncooperative. District Attorney Willis sent an email to the governor`s lawyer, and negotiations broke down, showing exactly how forceful a prosecutor she can be.\par \par \uc2 Her email to the governor`s lawyer said, in part: The email you have sent is offensive and beneath an officer of the court. You are both wrong and confused.\par \par \uc2 Let`s discuss some of the ways you are wrong. This is not a politically motivated investigation. It is a criminal investigation and often at the end of criminal investigations people are cleared and often they do go to prison.\par \par \uc2 You repeatedly referring to it as a politically motivated investigation does not make it so. In fact, you repeating it so many times only proves you have become comfortable with being dishonest. There has not been one leak of the special purpose grand jury. In fact, much to my dismay, attorneys of witnesses have disclosed witnesses were summoned and spoken publicly.\par \par \uc2 However, I understand they legally can do both. Please be advised I have a great deal of respect for the position Governor Kemp holds and the fondness for his wife, due to her advocacy for human trafficking victims. Due to those facts, I have directed my staff to bend over backwards to accommodate him.\par \par \uc2 Now let`s discuss your confusion. In criminal proceedings of this nature, proffers are not done by my office. We were giving Governor Kemp the courtesy of having his lawyer present for a taped interview to avoid him having to testify live.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:02]\par \par \uc2 In any case, this offer is now off the table. He will be formally summoned to the special purpose grand jury, and you will be welcome to wait for him in my Ruby Bridge Lobby, while he testifies. Judge McBurney is the only one with the authority to outline issues of evidence and privilege. The state will strictly comply to any order Judge McBurney sets for your client`s testimony. I personally will question him before the special purpose grand jury.\par \par \uc2 That is the prosecutor who might be the first prosecutor in history to subpoena a former president of the United States to testify to a grand jury, a grand jury that is investigating him.\par \par \uc2 And you can be sure that if she does subpoena \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to testify to the grand jury, she personally will question him.\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight is Gwen Keyes Fleming, former district attorney of DeKalb County, Georgia, and Harry Litman, former U.S. attorney and former deputy assistant attorney general under President Clinton. He is now a columnist for "The \uc2 Los Angeles Times\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 And Gwen Keyes Fleming, you know how these grand jury`s work in Georgia. You were the D.A. in the neighboring county, friends with \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What do you make -- what can we make, if anything -- of \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  spending six hours with a grand jury today?\par \par \uc2 GWEN KEYES FLEMING, FORMER DISTRICT ATTORNEY, DEKALB COUNTY, GA: I think it demonstrates that they were either a lot of questions or a lot of information to be gathered. And again, the special grand jury has a very big job. And I am (INAUDIBLE) being diligent, as is Fani`s team, in making sure that they get all of the evidence they need to make a very important and difficult decisions that we can expect to see, both from Fani`s office, and the special grand jury possibly later this year.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Harry Litman, we know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spent a similar amount of time in an under oath deposition in New York, with the New York state attorney general in a civil matter. But he took the Fifth Amendment 440 times.\par \par \uc2 Could that be a version of what happened today in Georgia?\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Definitely. So, typically, in federal practice and other criminal trials, when there was a target, they don`t have to show up, they can just say, I am not going to talk, and the prosecutor gives them the pass. Not so in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 I suspect that he didn`t give very much and he gave some combination of the Fifth Amendment -- which he can validly a search to any question -- and possibly attorney-client privilege, though, as you note, he has no legal basis for it. But there is no way to sort of freeze proceedings, go to the judge and get a ruling in the middle of things. They can push it later.\par \par \uc2 So, I think, quite likely, it forced every single question on him, so that the grand jury could hear him answer every single question. But he either invoked the Fifth Amendment or attorney client privilege.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Gwen Keyes Fleming, knowing what you know about how these grand juries work -- what are \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 ` options if \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  comes in there and does take the Fifth Amendment hundreds of times?\par \par \uc2 FLEMING: I think you are going to see her team -- again, we will see from the special grand jury`s report, when it becomes public. She`s going to keep pressing the point. This is a sphere in which there is extensive experience in complicated cases. He knows how to write big concepts and big fact patterns into little, bite sized pieces that either help establish or would controversy any type of case she would be considering bringing.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that she would have taken her time to make sure that the grand jurors got of all of the detail that they would need, to make whatever recommendations we can expect to see later this year.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Harry Litman, going into this, the D.A. team had to anticipate \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  taking the Fifth Amendment for most questions, probably. Would they then have had a strategy ready? Would it be likely they would have had a strategy ready, to perhaps grant immunity to \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , so that they could then obtain testimony from him on the spot today?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Not on the spot, no, I don`t think so. But they would have a strategy ready. And there is a little complication here. Because as Gwen points out, this is a special grand jury that is issuing the report. Will the da call for an interim report, so she could bring charges now against Giuliani? Or will they wait until the end to get one single report from the special grand jury, after which he will make a recommendation?\par \par \uc2 But I think they have probably decided, are we going to charge him?\par \par \uc2 [22:15:03]\par \par \uc2 And they could do that soon. Or are we could going to try to get him to use immunity? It is a little bit of a calculation.\par \par \uc2 One other reason, by the way, that you tell someone they are target, is so that they can come in and try to say, I will cooperate with you before charges are actually levied. Don`t see exactly Giuliani doing that. But as you say, there is something about grand jury proceedings that sober people up and make them think a little bit more clearly than his public persona of the last couple years might indicate.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Gwen Keyes Fleming, I for one have never read anything like some of the material that I read in today`s motion by the governor to quash his subpoena, which included all or most of the legal correspondence between the district attorney`s office and the governor`s lawyers, especially that email that \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  since to the governor`s lawyer, basically calling him a liar, saying his language is ridiculous and calling this stuff political.\par \par \uc2 And right, they`re in writing, taking the offer off of the table of doing this the easy way, where the lawyer would be allowed to be in the room, and treating the governor like anyone else, any other witness coming in there.\par \par \uc2 What was your reaction to what was disclosed in that part of the final?\par \par \uc2 FLEMING: I think it demonstrates that the stakes are high on both sides. Obviously, the governor and his lawyers are trying to beat back the subpoena, and the department on their own terms, and you see Fani not (INAUDIBLE). I think it`s important to note that probably both parties realize these emails would have been made public, either through FOIA or through court proceedings like this.\par \par \uc2 And I think it is important to see what Fani he`s trying to convey, that this is not politically motivated, that she has a job to do, she has taken an oath. And she is pursuing -- and properly investigating this matter consistent with that oath. And she`s not going to be bullied. And she is going to ensure that our grand jury gets all of the testimony that they need.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Harry, what was your reaction to reading some of these exchanges? Here I was in jaw-dropping mode as I was sitting in my desk reading this.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Yeah, they are volatile, especially coming from a prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 By the way I was very impressed with that last sentence. She`s going to question them personally. That is not the usual practice for a DA. They think -- everyone in the Republican Party in Georgia -- may think they have a strategy because she hosted a fund-raiser for her opponent -- of the person she hosted for, was the person that she had subpoenaed. And the judge said, that`s too close.\par \par \uc2 Kemp and everyone in Georgia is going to try to run that same strategy. But Gwen is 100 percent right. Every word here is going to go to the public. And she has decided to play it very aggressively.\par \par \uc2 Who are you calling a politically partisan prosecutor? Not me and she`s going to fight back hard, both in court and, more broadly, in the rhetorical war in country.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Harry Litman and Gwen Keyes Fleming, thank you both very much for starting off our discussion, thank you.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 FLEMING: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up, breaking news tonight on another federal criminal investigation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , involving the same evidence that has been collected by the January 6th committee. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:23:14]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We have breaking news from "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " tonight. Justice Department prosecutors have subpoenaed the National Archives for all of the documents that the archives has provided to the January 6th Select Committee. "The Times" reports, the subpoena issued to the national archives in May made a sweeping demand for all materials in whatever form that the archives had given to the January 6th House Committee.\par \par \uc2 Those materials included records from the files of Mr. Trump`s top aides, his daily schedule and phone logs, and a draft text of the president`s speech that preceded the riot. The grand jury subpoenas suggests that the Justice Department has not only been following the committees lead in pursuing its inquiry, but also that prosecutors believe evidence of a crime may exist in the White House documents the archives turned over to the House panel.\par \par \uc2 This explains, or could explain, a reference that the Justice Department made to other investigations in its filing in federal court in Florida opposing the release of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  affidavit that a federal Florida judge relied upon, to find probable cause, that there could be evidence of Donald Trump`s crimes at Donald Trump`s home -- authorizing the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search warrant at stone Trump`s home.\par \par \uc2 In the Justice Department`s filing in that case, which the judge will consider in a hearing tomorrow, the department says that the affidavit contains highly sensitive information about witnesses, including witnesses interviewed by the government specific investigative techniques, and information acquired by law to be kept under seal pursuant to federal criminal procedure 6E.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:03]\par \par \uc2 Disclosure of the government`s affidavit at this stage would also likely chill future cooperation by witnesses whose assistance may be sought as this investigation progresses -- and here is the key part -- as well as in other high-profile investigations.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Mark Zaid, an attorney who specializes in national security and security clearances.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 This is relevant reporting tonight about the subpoena to the archives for the January 6th committee material because of that reference in the Justice Department filing of Florida, saying, the sources used -- the material used -- in obtaining the search warrant could, if revealed publicly, compromise that investigation and, as they put it, other high-profile investigations. That sounds like this investigation, where they subpoenaed all that material from the Archives, that the January 6th committee has.\par \par \uc2 MARK ZAID, NATIONAL SECURITY ATTORNEY: It could certainly apply to that. Look, we know there are a number of targets -- everyone always talks about President Trump, President Trump, President Trump. But there are a number of other federal officials that work in and around the White House, as well as, frankly, some of his lawyers, who acted on behalf of him during the stop the steal exercises.\par \par \uc2 All of which could be somehow under this umbrella, far-reaching umbrella, that no doubt goes beyond what we`ve publicly known.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What do you expect to happen in the hearing tomorrow about releasing the affidavit that underlies the search warrant?\par \par \uc2 ZAID: I would expect, ultimately, that the judge is going to maintain that it does not have to be disclosed. I represent a lot of media. I had gone after under the Freedom of Information Act, supporting affidavits in some of these types of cases. Every once in a while, actually with the government`s cooperation, we can get access. But it is usually years after a case has finished.\par \par \uc2 And there is no way that the magistrate judge is going to want to jeopardize any ongoing investigations. It may be that the judge reads it in. Well, actually, the judge has already seen it -- might ask the Justice Department to give him a redacted version, just to see what it might look like.\par \par \uc2 But I think, at the end of the day, it is going to be withheld. We will see eventually. And certainly, the Trump people will see, it if anyone is indicted.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What do you think the timetable is for the judge making a decision about this?\par \par \uc2 ZAID: Well, I have learned in 30 years of law practice, never to try and predict when a judge will rule. But this is such a high profile matter -- and it is not really a difficult decision, to be frank. So, I imagine we will have a decision very short term.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Mark Zaid, thank you very much for joining us tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ZAID: Sure, my pleasure.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And joining us now is Democratic Congressman \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  of California. He served as a House impeachment manager in the second impeachment trial of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He is suing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for his role, Donald`s role in inciting the January 6 attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 So, when I see this "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reporting tonight, I am linking it up with that Justice Department filing, where they said the evidence in the affidavit for the search warrant could involve exposure of other high- profile investigations. It sounds like that is what the Justice Department is doing with that subpoena to the archives, to get everything that January 6th Committee has.\par \par \uc2 REP. ERIC SWALWELL (D-CA): Justice is on the move, Lawrence. We are seeing that it is not only at the Department of Justice. It is in Georgia. It is clearly in the state of New York, and now in the Manhattan district attorney`s office, with the former president`s former CFO offering testimony against the former president.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, it`s been frustrating, Lawrence, how slow it moves. As a former prosecutor, justice takes an incredible amount of patients to achieve. But it is a crescendo that we are approaching with all of these cases.\par \par \uc2 And what is clear is that, at best, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was a lousy custodian of classified information. And at worst, he wanted that classified information to do something that would help \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and harm others.\par \par \uc2 And we keep saying classified information, classified information, you mishandled it. I think it is important for your viewers to understand what we are talking about. And this is what I see every day on the intelligence committee.\par \par \uc2 This is information that protects our troops. It is called force protection. This is information about our nuclear capabilities. This is information about our adversaries` nuclear capabilities.\par \par \uc2 [22:29:46]\par \par \uc2 This is information that informs the president on a daily basis about big decisions. And if any of it is in the wrong hands or sloppy hands, troops could die. Our ability to be a strategic leader in the world would be reduced. And so \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should not be in the same zip code as any of this information.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Let`s listen to what \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  said today about the possibility of him testifying to the January 6th Committee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If there was an invitation To participate, I would consider it. It would be unprecedented in history for a vice president to be summoned to testify on Capitol Hill. But as I said, I don`t want to prejudge. So, if there is ever any formal invitation rendered to us, we would give it to due consideration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What was your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: Well, it was pretty darn unprecedented for the former president to incite a mob to violently attack the Capitol, you know, see a police officer and many others later die. One lost his eye, one lost fingers. We barely were able to certify the election. So, you know, that is not really a defense.\par \par \uc2 I also believe, Lawrence, that President Bush and Vice President Cheney cooperated with the September 11th Commission.\par \par \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is a big boy. He was a member of Congress. He was a governor. He has taken a number of different oaths. And of course, as vice president, his duty is to America, not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I think as we go into the midterm elections, frankly, now as we are seeing the defenses of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  via Republicans, it`s clear that if Republicans win Congress, they will fight every day in Congress for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And Democrats have to make it clear that if we win the Congress, we are going to fight every day for you. And that is exemplified in what you just showed of \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes. And on the unprecedented note, vice presidents and presidents have testified to Congress. Gerry ford, as president, testified to Congress about the pardon he gave to \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  when \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  left the presidency, Gerry Ford ascending from the vice presidency. And that was filled with a whiff of scandal.\par \par \uc2 So, the idea of a vice president going up there, to talk about possible criminal conduct by a president -- well, we have seen it before. When a Republican vice president who became president, talking about a Republican president, who did in fact commit crimes, \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2 . And so \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  would be following in that old Republican tradition.\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: That`s right, Lawrence. He is going to learn that you need to put your feet in one lane. He is trying to have one lane distanced from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And his foot in another lane, where he is still with MAGA Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  showed us last night, you know, the honorable lane that a Republican should be in right now if they want to be loyal to their country.\par \par \uc2 And I hope \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , for the sake of somebody who, I think, wants to be honorable and do the right thing, finds the correct lane.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congressman \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2 , thank you very much for joining our discussion tonight.\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, Dahlia Lithwick will join us on the latest cruelty by the forced birth movement in the state of Florida, where a 16-year-old is being forced to have a baby because judges believe she is not mature enough to have an abortion. That is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:33:08]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: In Florida, a teenager described in court documents as, quote, "almost 17 years old and parentless, submitted a handwritten petition seeking a waiver of the state`s parental notification and consent requirements. Under Florida law, an abortion cannot be performed on a minor without the consent of a parent or guardian.\par \par \uc2 According to the teenagers petition, quote, "The minor states that she is sufficiently mature to make the decision, saying she is not ready to have a baby, she doesn`t have a job, she is still in school, and the father is unable to assist her."\par \par \uc2 At the time of the petition, the teenager was ten weeks pregnant. The three judge panel of the Appeals Court claimed the teenager, quote, had not established by clear and convincing evidence that she was sufficiently mature to decide whether to terminate her pregnancy\par \par \uc2 So Florida judges have decided that a 16-year-old is not mature enough to have an abortion, but is mature enough to have a baby.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Dahlia Lithwick, senior editor and legal correspondent for Slate and host of the podcast "Amicus". She is an MSNBC law and politics analyst. And Dahlia, this is the latest example of what the forced birth movement is doing around the country, in this case in Florida.\par \par \uc2 DAHLIA LITHWICK, MSNBC LAW AND POLITICS ANALYST: It is, Lawrence. And I think you have exactly nailed it. There`s something just on its face, preposterous, about a court adjudicating someone too immature to make the decision to terminate the pregnancy, and then by default have them be mature enough to bring a child into the world, and presumably raise it.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:46]\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: And one thing that really strikes me in this case, however, is that I have read the order a couple of times from the appellate court. It feels like the judges were kind of trying to do the right thing. They were trying to conform to the judicial bypass law. They were trying to figure out if this teenager, in fact, was mature enough.\par \par \uc2 And what`s breaks my heart a little bit is that we are living under an old regime, where there were other systems to protect and now we are in this new post-Dobbs regime, where nothing matters, except that this child will be forced to give birth.\par \par \uc2 And in a weird way, it throws open -- and I worry about this so much -- every one of these judges looks like a hack now. Everybody looks like a hack. Every judge. You are seeing North Carolina, South Carolina judges making decisions every day.\par \par \uc2 And this is why we had stare decisis, so that judges didn`t look like hacks. Now it is just kind of a feeding frenzy of choose your own judicial ending. Everybody looks partisan. And that is so destructive for the legitimacy of the court.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, they also look cruel. I mean, hacks for me would be the kind of description of what these judges are doing. And people in the audience should understand that in an Appeals Court, the judges never see witnesses.\par \par \uc2 An Appeals Court never hears this girl`s voice. They never see her face. They do not know anything about her. They are looking at paper. And they are making this decision about her life. And they have decided they -- have decided, looking at paper -- that she is not mature enough to have an abortion and at the same, psychotic moment, it seems, in their brains, they decide that she is mature enough to have a baby. And they have never seen her to make either one of those decisions.\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: It is true. And they are deferring to a trial court judge. And the dissenting judge goes through, meticulously saying, that he thinks the trial court judge kind of gave it her best shot.\par \par \uc2 But you are exactly right. There are so many red flags in this case. The child is in trauma. She says she is in trauma. She has no lawyer. She says her guardian -- she has no parents -- she says her guardian approve the abortion. The whole thing, as you said, is a handwritten petition.\par \par \uc2 And so, again, she is kind of thrown to the wolves here. And now we are in a world in which she has no recourse. And we should note, she is ten weeks pregnant. At 15 weeks, she can`t get an abortion in Florida.\par \par \uc2 So in addition to everything else, the most merciful judge, the dissenting judge, would throw this back to the lower court judge, in which case she starts again and she has five weeks to try to get an abortion before the clock runs out.\par \par \uc2 Every part of this, every part of this feels like it is not centering her. It is centering some value that makes no sense to me.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, the Supreme Court justices who made this possible, there is not a word anywhere in their opinion indicates that they care at all. Not one bit about the existence of girls like this. They don`t even recognize that these kinds of things happen.\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: They don`t. Girls are invisible to them. Women are invisible to them. Pregnant people are invisible to them. They have this completely fatuous claim, in the majority opinion in Dobbs that by throwing the it back to the states, this issue just resolves itself tidily and we can all sleep easier at night.\par \par \uc2 And you know who is not going to sleep easier at night, is this 16-year-old girl, who just as I said, has no recourse right now. Throwing this back to the states solves nothing, Lawrence. All it did was throw the entire country into chaos and confusion and in many, many cases, panic and fear.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Dahlia Lithwick, thank you very much for joining us for this discussion tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is now one of only four -- only four presidents who have been able to do what every Democratic president since Franklin Roosevelt has wanted to do.\par \par \uc2 That is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:08]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: No president tried it until the supremely effective Franklin Delano Roosevelt in the 1930s and he couldn`t do it. His successor, President \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2 , tried and failed.\par \par \uc2 Then no president tried it for another 20 years until Lyndon Johnson became the first to succeed where everyone else had failed. Then \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  tried and failed.\par \par \uc2 Then another 20 years passed before another president tried. That was \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , who tried and failed. And then \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  tried and succeeded in a very-limited way, followed by \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , who tried and succeeded big time, provoking \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to whisper at the bill signing that it was a big effing deal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Ladies and gentlemen, the president of the United States of America, Barack \uc2 Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. President, this is a big effing deal.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was the first and only president so far who tried to take away some of what had been won but he failed. And then \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  tried and succeeded.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:53]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Of the 46 presidents, only four of them have been able to pass major health care legislation. Lyndon Johnson was the first big winner and remains the biggest winner, having passed through congress and signed into law Medicare and Medicaid. \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  passed a prescription drug benefit. Only four people on Medicare.\par \par \uc2 And then \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  did what Franklin Roosevelt, \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2 , \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  could not do. He passed and signed into law the Affordable Care Act extending health care coverage to millions of people who couldn`t afford it, through an expansion of Medicaid and subsidies for private health insurance.\par \par \uc2 And yesterday, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  signed into law legislation that will strengthen Medicare and reduce health care costs through Medicare and the Affordable Care Act, provoking \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  to say on \uc2 TWITTER\uc2 , "This is a BFD."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We`ve given Medicare the power to negotiate those prices now on some drugs. This means seniors are going to pay less for the prescription drug. That means if you`re on Medicare, you`ll never have to pay more than $2,000, no matter how many prescriptions you have, whether it is for cancer or any other disease. No more than $2,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 The Inflation Reduction Act locks in place lower health care premiums for millions of families who get their coverage under the Affordable Care Act.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Democratic congresswoman \uc2 Lauren Underwood\uc2  of Illinois. She`s a registered nurse who served as senior adviser at the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services. Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 I know how important this is to you especially because your legislation was pulled into this bill. You were advocating the affordability -- -- the health care affordability act, as you called it -- what does that do? And how does it work in this legislation?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LAUREN UNDERWOOD\uc2  (D-IL): Since I joined the Congress, I have been hearing from so many constituents about the high cost of health care. My health care affordability act says that no American will pay more than 8.5 percent of their income for silver level marketplace plans. This is for people that purchased their own health care coverage and it extends the popular tax credits, which makes their premium prices more affordable.\par \par \uc2 This is first signed into law as part of the American Rescue Plan, in the spring of 2021. And then we saw 14.5 million Americans enroll in coverage. That American Rescue Plan provision was set to expire at the end of this calendar year.\par \par \uc2 So what we have in the Inflation Reduction Act is a three-year extension of these incredibly powerful, popular tax credits that allow people to find coverage, oftentimes for less than $10 a month.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So I am imagining your days as a nurse and encountering people who have affordability struggles with the health care services that you are part of providing for them.\par \par \uc2 And I assume, in those days, you were thinking, occasionally, as everyone does, gee, if I was in congress, I would -- is this anything like what you imagined it to be, trying to legislate health care improvements, back when you are working as a nurse?\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD; Well, you know, I didn`t anticipate, you know, the twists and turns of the legislative process. But I think it is pretty impressive. And one term in congress, to be able to extend tax credits for five years, and thereby reducing the uninsured rate in the United States to the lowest level in U.S. history. That is what we have done in the last two years.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, I didn`t know that it would take the form of these kinds of tax credits. But I think the impact is something that every nurse in this country can be proud of.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I think you know by now that any freshman member of the House or the Senate, it`s kind of a miracle for them to get anything significant in legislation. So for you to get this much, in this bill, is very impressive.\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: Thank you. I have been really proud to have ten pieces of legislation signed into law under both presidents Trump and president Biden. And this is just the latest in this long series.\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Lauren Underwood\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us again tonight. We always appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Tonight`s LAST WORD is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:16]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Time for tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA), HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: The president bears responsibility for Wednesday`s attack on Congress by mob rioters. He should have immediately denounced the mob when he saw what was unfolding. These facts require immediate action from President Trump, accept his share of responsibility, quell the brewing unrest, and ensure President-Elect Biden is able to successfully begin his term.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:59:54]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Kevin McCarthy then spent a year and a half attacking \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  for saying things like that. That was part of what led to Liz Cheney`s defeat in the Republican primary last night in Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020220818ei8h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle for August 17, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 7,541 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: Kevin McCarthy then spent a year and a half attacking \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  for saying things like that. I was part of what led to Liz Cheney`s defeat the Republican primary last night in Wyoming. That is tonight`s "LAST WORD". THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE: Tonight, the Big Lie investigation got more intense. A federal grand jury wants White House documents related to the January 6 insurrection. Federal prosecutors want to know what did the former guy do as the Capitol was under siege.\par \par \uc2 The pressure also on his former personal attorney, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  sat for six hours in front of a grand jury in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Then, it is the first week of school. Do you know where your governor is? \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Greg Abbott are fighting the right wing culture war. But is it what parents want were students need. Plus, the CDC admits a lot went wrong with the response to the coronavirus pandemic. No surprise there. But with the monkeypox outbreak happening now, the big question is are the changes coming too late? Big Questions as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. There is breaking news tonight from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  concerning the Justice Department`s investigation into the role \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his allies played in the days ahead of the Capitol riot. The Time says prosecutors are focusing on critical Trump White House records and quote have issued a grand jury subpoena to the National Archives for all the documents the agency provided to a parallel House Select Committee inquiry.\par \par \uc2 According to a copy of the subpoena obtained by the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , those materials included records from the files of Mr. Trump`s top aides, his daily schedule and phone logs and a draft text of the President`s speech that preceded the riot.\par \par \uc2 The Time says the subpoena is not related to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of Trump`s Florida home. So that means the Department of Justice is running a separate probe into the Trump White House and what the former guy was doing on January 6, but there`s also news about the probe into the handling of classified documents by the former guy.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow a federal judge will hold a hearing on requests to unseal the affidavit that details the reasons for that very search.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department opposes releasing that document. While \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and some of his allies want it made public. They have also attacked the \uc2 FBI\uc2  over and over for the search. But today, the former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , called for those attacks to come to an end.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: The Republican Party is the party of law and order. Our party stands with the men and women who serve on the thin blue line at the federal and state and local level. And these attacks on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  must stop. Calls to defend the \uc2 FBI\uc2  are just as wrong as calls to defund the police.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: If they are the party of law and order the question is, is \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , the guy who stands with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it doesn`t sound like it. And what about the Congressional investigation into the insurrection itself? Well, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  also made very big headlines with this response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Just a stain on that generally said if they were to call you the committee to come and testify or talk, would you be agreeable?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: If there was an invitation to participate? I would consider it. I would have to reflect on the unique role that I was serving as Vice President.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: If you are confused and we have not made it crystal clear yet, the former guy and his allies are now the focus of several investigations.\par \par \uc2 Today, a Georgia Grand Jury investigating possible 2020 election interference heard from \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 . He is considered a target in that investigation. And he spent about six long hours inside the Fulton County Courthouse.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUDY GIULIANI\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP PERSONAL LAWYER: Grand juries, as I recall, are secret. They ask the questions and we`ll see.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Another key Trump ally is due in a different court tomorrow. Sources tell NBC News Trump Organization former CFO Allen Weisselberg will plead guilty to several criminal charges. He`s expected to be sentenced to five months in jail for his role in a long running tax scheme that the former president`s family business. Sources also say Weisselberg will cooperate against the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . But there`s no indication he will cooperate in any investigation into the former president. But remember, that business that he ran was a very small family business run by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [23:05:02]\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter with a lot of help tonight from our leadoff panel, Greg Bluestein, a political reporter for the Atlanta Journal- Constitution. He`s the author of "Flipped: How Georgia Turned Purple and Broke the Monopoly on Republican Power." Former U.S. Attorney Joyce Vance, who spent 25 years as a federal prosecutor. She`s now a law professor at the University of Alabama. And Andrew Weissmann, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  general counsel and former senior member of the Mueller probe.\par \par \uc2 Counselor Weissmann, we need you to help us out there is a lot of reporting. And I want to start with this new New York Times report. DOJ prosecutors are now asking those -- for those White House documents that the National Archives gave to the January 6 committee. Does that mean that the walls are starting to close in on Trump and all that we`ve heard from the Gen six committee is now making its way to the DOJ who`s taken action?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, FORMER FBI GENERAL COUNSEL: It means some of what the January 6 committee has is making its way in there because they still -- the department justice still doesn`t have everything that the January 6 Committee has. They gave a specific request for things related to false of the fake collector scheme that was provided so that sort of witness testimony, and presumably some documents.\par \par \uc2 But with this grand jury subpoena, which apparently was issued in May, they asked the National Archives, essentially for all of the White House records, that is a really good sign if you`re looking for sort of widespread laws sort of broad DOJ investigation.\par \par \uc2 There is a legitimate question, however, which is DOJ apparently issued this subpoena in May of this year, the House issued its subpoena in March the year before. So one question is sort of historically is just sort of why`d it take DOJ so long, but if you`re looking at it from the perspective of where we are now, it certainly is a good sign in terms of the department having a broad scope in terms of its investigation.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, let`s turn and talk about another investigation. Joyce, you wrote this on your blog about the investigation into the classified documents at Mar-a-Lago quote, DOJ flatly says that it has an ongoing criminal investigation, one that continues even though the execution of the search warrant is complete. Those are a lot of scary words. What do they mean, what are you looking for here?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Well, I think perhaps we`ve blown past this very important point that DOJ reveals in its pleadings, you know, stuff, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  loves to make the point. He`s made it repeatedly now, that DOJ does not talk about its cases in the public that when it speaks, it speaks in court. And it speaks in its pleadings.\par \par \uc2 And here in the pleadings, we have DOJ telling us that this search warrant at Mar-a-Lago was about more than going in and reclaiming classified documents and bringing them back to a secure place. In fact DOJ clarifies there is an ongoing criminal investigation. And although they don`t tell us who the targets are, you know, you have to infer a little bit here. And maybe it isn`t, maybe it isn`t the former president.\par \par \uc2 But the fact that one of the places they explicitly sought and received authorization to search was the office designated as the 45 Office, that would appear to indicate that Trump is a real target and is in real danger from this investigation as well.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Do you expect a judge tomorrow to agree to release the search warrant affidavit? The Department of Justice does not want it to happen. They`re saying it will put their case as well as potential witnesses at risk. And Trump is saying Go for it. Let`s see it.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I`d be very surprised if the judge released this. The normal course of business is that DOJ and other prosecutors are permitted to conduct their investigations in secret. DOJ lays out the reasons for that. You don`t want to give the folks that are under investigation, a roadmap to where the investigation is headed, because that would permit them to interfere with it. It could mean that witnesses would become intimidated and might not be available.\par \par \uc2 And since the government`s whole goal in these investigations is to get to the truth. It`s essential that they have access to those witnesses. That I think is one of the primary reasons, but also this sort of an affidavit contains material obtained through the grand jury that has to be kept secret. That`s a legal requirement. There`s likely a lot of classified material in this search warrant that needs to be protected, and even the rights of people who are under investigation but haven`t been charged their rights or best protected when something like this affidavit remains sealed. So I think it`s awfully unlikely that we`ll see the entire affidavit unsealed tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:00]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We got another one. Andrew, ABC reporting that weeks before the Mar- a-Lago search Trump loyalists, Kash Patel said he would take the classified documents and publish them on his own website. What in the world is he doing? Is he basically putting the crime out there and trying to normalize it because the Department of Justice is paying attention to this.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: So the Kash Patel piece I find fascinating and I`m sure DOJ is all over this to Kash Patel in June of 2022. So, you know, just couple of months ago was designated by Trump as his representative to the National Archives in terms of the presidential records.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know what in the world Kash Patel can be thinking in terms of saying I`m going to spread out in the world classified documents. But when you look at the timing of when Kash Patel is saying this, when he gets appointed by the former president to be the representative to the National Archives, and the search that was done in Mar-a-Lago, I don`t think there`s a coincidence, this is all happening within days of each other. Because you can imagine the department saying, you know, there is top secret material at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 And we have Kash Patel, a designee of the former presidents talking about making this public. So from a national security perspective, you could really see the urgency of the department taking action to protect all of us.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: If Andrew Weissmann actually said on our show coincidence, I think not I would be the happiest person in the world. Joyce, something else happened today that made very big news, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  said he would consider an invitation to speak to the January 6 committee. Here`s what I do not understand why in the world, hasn`t he been subpoenaed yet?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: You know, Congress has run into a little bit of difficulty trying to enforce it subpoenas that has to rely on the Justice Department and except in the most egregious cases, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  was the first one. DOJ hasn`t really backed Congress up in the way that Congress might have hoped that they would, there would be a lot of difficult legal issues that the former vice president could throw up to try to at least delay testimony.\par \par \uc2 So it`s really in the committee`s interests to try to get to a negotiated situation where Pence would agree to come in and testify given the way the clock is running, that could be a better move for them to make. Of course, if DOJ decides that it needs the former vice president`s testimony at some point, those issues don`t really apply. DOJ would be able to issue a subpoena. It would be extraordinarily unusual, it could even be inflammatory.\par \par \uc2 But if Pence`s testimony is essential, and it very likely is to get to the heart of some of the January sixth issues involving whether the former president is culpable or not, then he should be issued a subpoena and it should be his honor to testify truthfully.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: He certainly knows a lot. All right, Mr. Bluestein. I`m coming to you. As we mentioned earlier, there`s also very big news involving \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 . Kristen Welker is following that story for us. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WALKER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Today, Mr. Trump`s former attorney \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  testified before a federal grand jury in Atlanta for six hours. It`s where he led the President`s efforts to overturn the state`s 2020 election results.\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI: It is clear that the count you have right now is false.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Giuliani was told he`s the target of the probe. His attorney saying he would invoke attorney client privilege if asked about conversations with his former boss.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Nobody knows Georgia better than you, Greg, what do you know about what happened behind closed doors?\par \par \uc2 GREG BLUESTEIN, ATLANTA JOURNAL-CONSTITUTION POLITICAL REPORTER: Well, six hours of testimony and we have no indication that he said much of anything at all behind those closed doors. His attorney wouldn`t say whether he answered any of the questions but as you just heard, he was either -- he suggested he would either invoke attorney client-privilege or invoke the Fifth Amendment now that we know that \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  is a potential target of criminal investigation.\par \par \uc2 But what we do know is that investigators are looking at at least two different things they`re scrutinizing Giuliani over his role and peddling election fraud lies at December legislative hearings in the State Capitol in Georgia, and also his role in quarterbacking the fake electoral plot in Georgia, the 16 fake electors of who are also facing potential criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 So the prosecutors, the investigators, they`re looking at him for those two reasons. We know that from witnesses who have also gone before the special grand jury, and were asked direct questions about those two issues.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Joyce, how worried should \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  be does attorney client- privilege fly in this case?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Attorney-client privilege isn`t really much of an issue here because it only applies in a situation where the client is seeking advice from the attorney and they are alone, the presence of any other person in that conversation voids the privilege.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:05]\par \par \uc2 In this situation, we`re really talking about Giuliani running a little exercise down in Georgia, that`s far away from that attorney-client relationship and sort of the places where the privilege is pointed to protect. So the real privilege that Giuliani could use here if he chose to, and one suspects he did would be to assert his Fifth Amendment right to avoid incriminating himself. But six hours is a long time. And it would -- go ahead.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: No, no, no, yes, six hours, six hours of saying nothing.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: It`s a long time. And it could be prosecutors asking all of the questions on their notepad and Giuliani meticulously asserting the Fifth Amendment in response to every question and refusing to answer. But it`s also possible and it wouldn`t be the first time that a witness implied in public that he was not cooperating when in fact, he was.\par \par \uc2 So the real issue here for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is that he doesn`t know. He doesn`t know. And there`s uncertainty. And he has to be worried that his former lawyer will cooperate in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, Greg, let`s commingle prosecutors and politics because DA \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  has subpoenaed Governor Brian Kemp. We all know he was praised for resisting Trump`s pressure to intervene in the election. He did the right thing. But he doesn`t want to testify. So what`s going on? Is he trying to get Trump`s endorsement? Is he trying to get back in his good graces here?\par \par \uc2 BLUESTEIN: That`s what Democrats are saying here in Georgia. And up until today, it looked like he was going to cooperate. There was even a date on the books for a few weeks ago for a videotaped testimony. That did not happen.\par \par \uc2 Now we know in vivid detail how those negotiations fell apart because we even have emails that were part of a court filing to and from Governor Kemp`s attorneys and DA \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  but \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  is laying down the law. She is saying that he cannot make excuses. He cannot move the goalposts and accusing Governor Kemp`s attorney of just acting dishonorably, essentially, and trying to avoid him testifying.\par \par \uc2 The governor`s attorney counters that, you know, we`re getting closer to the midterm election, it would take valuable time out of the campaign to out of fundraising, all the things the governor has to do. We`re getting too close to the November election. So at the very minimum, they`re asking that he testify after November, either early next year, or later this year.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Andrew, let`s now talk Allen Weisselberg, former Trump business CFO, he didn`t just work for the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  for a few years. He worked for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He worked for \uc2 Fred Trump\uc2 . He has been with this family for decades. He is expected to plead guilty tomorrow, even if he does not directly implicate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself. Trump had to have signed every check and approved everything.\par \par \uc2 So ultimately, isn`t \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  going to be responsible if laws were broken? For years, he co-mingled his personal finances and his business finances.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: This, I think is the sleeper investigation. You know, everybody sort of wrote this off when they heard that Mark Pomerantz, and Carey Dunne the lead prosecutors on the matter had resigned, and people thought that this case was going nowhere.\par \par \uc2 This is going to be a really interesting play. Now Weisselberg gets out of this a five-month term. So he caps his exposure. But the government`s here, the Manhattan DA`s office gets quite a lot because as you said he is pleading to actually be indictment, 15 counts, a massive tax scheme. And it wasn`t for just Weisselberg. This was a scheme run by the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  where it`s alleged that the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  profited to the tune of millions of dollars and various executives did. And that Weisselberg is going to be a witness if called at the trial in October of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And it`s one thing for Weisselberg to try and have some PR spin now to say, Well, I`m not cooperating against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But when you`re on the stand, and you`re talking about, as you said, Stephanie, a small family run organization. What does that mean? Organization only works through people. So he`s going to have to identify people and the indictment alleges that these are checks that were signed by the head of the organization, which is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So this is a very bad development for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, that is no sleeper. We will certainly be paying attention come October. Greg Bluestein, Joyce Vance, Andrew Weissmann, thank you all for starting us off tonight.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, it is back to school in some states, but two red state governors are busy fighting, not for their students to fight in their own culture wars.\par \par \uc2 [23:20:00]\par \par \uc2 Is this the kind of leadership we need to make our schools smarter, safer and better? We`ll get into it all. And later, the CDC says it learned its lesson about major outbreaks and then making changes. Some critics say the changes came too late. And they need to handle the monkeypox outbreak now and do it a whole lot better.\par \par \uc2 One of our favorite doctors will be here to help us get some answers. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a very busy Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Little boys and little girls follow in the rules getting ready for the first day of school. Politicians say they make our land safe and free. They`re supposed to stand for you and me. Keep our children safe for you and me. Keep our children safe for you and me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It`s hard to watch, isn`t it? That harrowing ad was produced by the group Mothers Against Greg Abbott. And in case you missed it, that is an elementary school child dressed in body armor for his first day of school. It is meant to remind voters that Texas has not passed any new gun safety laws since the school shooting in Uvalde at the end of this last school year, and it`s not just in Texas, that they`re dealing with back to school tensions.\par \par \uc2 Florida has a teacher shortage so severe its Republican governor is now recruiting former military personnel and police officers to try to fill those gaps. And yesterday \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  slammed the institutions that train a license the teachers they do have.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R) FLORIDA: I think the schools of education and the specific way they go about it, I don`t think is the right way to do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: With us now, Sommer Brugal, Education Reporter for the Miami Herald and Texas State Senator Roland Gutierrez, you have seen him here before, because his district includes Uvalde. Sommer, you are out in Miami Dade today for the first day of school. What are students and parents telling you?\par \par \uc2 SOMMER BRUGAL, THE MIAMI HERALD EDUCATIONAL REPORTER: Yes, thank you for having me. It was really an exciting day to be honest. A lot of students were excited to be back. But we were talking to parents that mentioned concerns about safety. That mentioned concerns about just how their children are going to be safe in a school environment, especially as large as Miami-Dade County.\par \par \uc2 You know, we`re also talking to teachers ahead of, you know, ahead of today. And during the school day today, we had reporters from across the newsroom scattered across the district talking to teachers, and they definitely you know, a couple of them definitely raised concerns about, you know, a couple of new laws that are going to be that are already in effect and are going to be kind of tested out in this new school year.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Roland, your governor in Texas is spending all sorts of money on things that will do nothing to teach or protect children, things like busing immigrants to New York and Washington, nothing on protecting your children from gun violence. As I said, you represent the families in Uvalde. What are parents that are telling you? Are they ready to send their kids back to the classroom to teachers feel comfortable going back?\par \par \uc2 ROLAND GUTIERREZ, TEXAS STATE SENATOR: Well, Stephanie, no, they`re not. And they`ve made it very clear to Governor Abbott that they`re not ready. It was I believe on your show or on your network where MSNBC called him up out for not going to the community. Finally, two weeks ago, he began to start to go to Uvalde and he got an earful. And yet he still has refused to do any of the things that those community those constituents asked him specifically to call a special session raising the age limit to 21 to buy a long gun in Texas.\par \par \uc2 It took \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  23 days after Parkland to do that in Florida. And yet this governor in Texas doesn`t have the fortitude necessary to do the right thing or to listen to his constituents.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Sommer, you`ve reported on Florida`s teacher shortage, yesterday while talking at a teacher recruitment event. Your governor said that Florida is the place where quote, woke goes to die. I need you to get real with me, is wokeness whatever in the world that actually means is that actually a significant problem in Florida schools and I want to remind our audience, our kids are suffering coming out of COVID, we`ve got one to two years of significant learning loss. But wokeness that`s the problem for Florida kids.\par \par \uc2 BRUGAL: You know, I think it could depend on who you ask in which parents you ask. I think there is, you know, a growing group of parents who might agree with that, that there is this you know, so called woke ideology that it`s that`s being infused into our education system.\par \par \uc2 But on the other side, there`s a large number of people who would fully disagree with that. And I think our governor`s, you know, words and things that he said yesterday, I think just kind of underscore how he has been using education, and how he has been using these terms such as wokeness in the classroom and woke ideologies, to really use education as this kind of part of his political identity, political platform especially ahead of, you know, this upcoming election.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:11]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Roland, Texas is also facing a teacher shortage. It`s not a surprise teachers are woefully overworked, underpaid, and now under attack, how do you get more qualified teachers in Texas classrooms? It`s crucial.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: You know, it`s really hard. We just saw a survey in Texas where 70 percent of the teachers, or actually in Texas, were actually thinking that they wouldn`t come back to teaching over the next several years. Because of a lot of the issues that are happening in the legislative process.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes I think that Abbott is DeSantis`s Mini Me, because what Florida does, Texas does. We had a weak watered down CRT bill, this last in a special session because Abbott said we have to do something about CRT, yet he won`t do anything about guns.\par \par \uc2 My suspicion is that that CRT bill is going to come back in the spring. We`re going to see a much stringent CRT bill, let`s face it, these aren`t problems. But this is how things, how bad things begin. And it`s how bad things may have begun historically, it`s how the Nazi, it`s what happened in Nazi Germany, they began to burn books, they began to tell people how to think that is what`s happening with people like Rick DeSantis. People like Greg Abbott. People need to wake up. We`re certainly a woke problem. But it`s the fact that people need to wake up as to what`s happening with these two leaders in this country.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, well, so if Greg Abbott has done nothing on gun violence, you don`t have enough teachers and Texas has an enormous power grid problem. OK. These are three issues that impact people across your state and the governor is doing nothing. If he`s doing nothing on any of those, what is he doing for the people of Texas? And the reason I ask is because this guy may have his eye on the White House.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Yes, no doubt that Greg Abbott has his eye on the White House. And, you know, I got to believe that will, not cannot happen. But we`ve seen it all, I think in these United States. I think that it`s high time that people understand that Greg Abbott has probably had more and I don`t mean this. This is not sarcastic. It`s had more to do with people dying in this state than any governor, any governor in our history. 800 people have died during the winter storm and 2021. 19 kids, five massacres already and this man has done absolutely nothing other than waste our money on political stunts with regard to his Operation Lone Star, and let`s not have people forget that the Operation Lone Star police, those were the guys at Uvalde and those were the guys that failed these kids.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Speaking of money, there are people in Texas still dealing with bills from that horrible freeze and power failure. What was it now, two years ago, still suffering. Sommer Brugal, State Senator Roland Gutierrez, thank you both for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, she is the Michigan mama who went viral with her passionate response to extremists and liars. She`s fighting abortion bans and militias and a threat to democracy in her own state. We`ll ask her about what she is doing to ensure what happens in Michigan doesn`t just stay in Michigan when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:38:07]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Since Roe was overturned states across the country have been scrambling to navigate abortion access. The Detroit Free Press reports that today in Michigan, a judge temporarily extended an order that blocks prosecutions of abortion providers in the state`s biggest counties. That hearing will reconvene tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 For more we`re very happy to welcome Michigan State Senator Mallory McMorrow. Mallory, how critical is this case? What I`m trying to understand is what the Michiganders really want when it comes to abortion rights. It`s a big state, you have a lot of views.\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. MALLORY MCMORROW (D) MICHIGAN: It is a big state. We are a state of 10 million people. But overwhelmingly, people do not want us to go back to our 1931 law, which makes abortion a felony with no exceptions for age rape or incest. And right now our Republican legislators want to extend that felony to a 10 year prison sentence. Nobody wants to go there. And right now, there is one judge standing in the way of that our rights are just hanging by a thread. It`s devastating.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I want to talk about something else in your state. You tweeted about a situation with a Michigan library saying quote, this is canceled culture. The library got stripped of its -- the town got stripped of its library because it contained books that just had LGBTQ people in it. Just just for having those books, they defunded the library. How do you fight that?\par \par \uc2 MCMORROW: It is such a scary time that we`re living in. It`s a few books that often aren`t even checked out that feature characters who are LGBTQ and there was an organization in this town that coordinate an effort to defund the library so they`re going to put another effort on the ballot and this library may disappear in 2023, depriving an entire town of every single book in it.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:07]\par \par \uc2 And my husband also pointed it out, the same library has something like 13 copies of 50 Shades of Grey, and nobody had any issues with that. So it is just -- it`s terrifying right now the idea that you can eliminate all of the books, because there are a handful of books that just acknowledge that gay people exist.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I can`t even handle that you just said that, like, do me a favor. If you want to read that book, just buy it, please don`t check it out, and then return it to any of those 13 copies.\par \par \uc2 MCMORROW: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But then here`s what I don`t understand. OK? If the majority of people in Michigan do not want to go backwards in terms of abortion, if the majority of people in Michigan think it`s crazy, to deprive a town, a county of its library, how come these things might happen?\par \par \uc2 MCMORROW: Right now, this version of the Republican Party is being taken over by far right Trump backed conspiracy theorists, and they`re winning. It is the loudest voices in the room, we`re organizing, and it`s a concerted effort to get the rest of us so worn out and exhausted that we just quit. That is what`s happening right now. We know that an overwhelming majority of us don`t want these things. We want our kids to have access to books. We want the rights to make our own decisions to our bodies.\par \par \uc2 And part of the reason that I`m staying in this, and so is the governor in our Attorney General in a state like Michigan where there are consistently death threats against women legislators, is because we can`t let this version win. We have to send a loud message for Michigan by defeating them this November that this version of the Republican Party will not win. And hopefully we kick it back to normal and we can get back to debating tax policy instead of whether or not people have the right to exist and women are full citizens.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then what`s your playbook for Democrats? Because people often say that Democrats have facts. But Republicans, they know how to win fights. You earlier this year had Republican colleagues making awful false allegations about you. You fought back hard and you won. Right now Democrats have a lot of wins under their belt, how do they fight to win on messaging midterms around the corner?\par \par \uc2 MCMORROW: I mean, the attitude is we have to fight. We have to get in our minds. This is not a normal time. It is not a time where we can just talk about policy and win because right now there is a culture war, but it is manufactured. It is the Republican Party manufacturing issues to get you so angry and hateful and fearful towards somebody else to deflect from the fact that they`re not doing anything to bring down inflation, that they`re not doing anything to bring down health care costs, but Democrats are.\par \par \uc2 So step one is we have to fight the culture wars and win by showing how ridiculous they are that it is just scapegoating and it`s a distraction. And then that opens the door for us to celebrate. Democrats, we`re not really that good at celebrating. We`d love to nitpick each other. We have a lot to celebrate right now.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve got to show people what they`re doing on the other side is absolute garbage. Here`s a lot of what we are doing and what we can do more of in a state like Michigan where the Michigan Senate has been GOP controlled since 1984. If you give us a chance, give us a chance to lead and we will not let you down.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The woke war is made up. You heard it here. Mallory McMorrow, thank you for joining us. Coming up, the CDC says it has learned a big lesson from the COVID pandemic and they`re making changes or those changes working. Dr. Vin Gupta is back on what it means for the agency and what it means for us the American people who need critical information on public health when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:48:30]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Big changes coming at the CDC. Earlier today CDC director \uc2 Rochelle Walensky\uc2  announced a complete overhaul of the agency including how it analyzes and shares data and communicates information to the public. That last part really important.\par \par \uc2 With us now to discuss MSNBC medical contributor Dr. Vin Gupta. He`s also a critical care pulmonologist and a faculty member at the \uc2 Institute for Health Metrics and Evaluation\uc2  at the \uc2 University of Washington\uc2 . I`m so glad you`re here as always, we need your help. \uc2 Rochelle Walensky\uc2  said that the CDC `s COVID response did not reliably meet expectations. They`re making big changes. Do you think these changes are enough? And what`s your take on the whole thing?\par \par \uc2 DR. VIN GUPTA, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Stephanie, good evening. And for all your viewers out there, we absolutely need a credible trusted CDC, Stephanie, that first and foremost is nimble in a crisis is able to effectively engage in rapid response. Part of that is designing executing smart policy quickly with the best available data at that moment, not letting that perfect be the enemy of the good, but part of it also, Stephanie, is crisis communication.\par \par \uc2 Specifically, what is the message? Who`s the messenger and how are we going to communicate it? You do not get how taught as a physician, I`ll say this, how to crisis communicate at the bedside. So while these reforms are welcome, and I applaud Dr. Walensky for accountable leadership, execution here is going to be key and specifically how Do we crisis communicate.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:00]\par \par \uc2 And if I may just point of personal privilege here, Stephanie, and we`ve been at this for two and a half years. I think we have a slide here of lungs with and without COVID that I wanted to show your viewers here to drive home the point. This is what was posted. I posted this early in the rollout of the vaccines to help clarify why do you get vaccinated. Normal lungs on the left, COVID ravage lungs on the right. This is why you get vaccinated to stay out of the hospital, leveraging visuals, some storytelling, clear messaging, didn`t over promise of what the vaccines could do versus what they could not do. This is what we really need to be trying to internalize hopefully moving forward.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Listen, it`s easy to attack the CDC now but are we forgetting during the Trump administration, they were not able to communicate with the public the way they wanted to. The former president was preventing it. Members of his team were changing content on their website. And also COVID was changing while they were communicating with us. I mean, all the different strains. They never experienced a pandemic like this in your or my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: So that`s exactly right. Which is why you`re right, neither administration has been perfect. The last thing we need to be doing is bandwagoning on the criticism of the CDC and Dr. Walensky. I applaud again the move today for accountable leadership, and many mistakes were made in 2020. Some mistakes were made here and 2021.\par \par \uc2 Moving forward, how do we deep politicize the agency? Let`s make the appointees to the directorship and Deputy Director be completely removed from the presidential cycle. They should not be -- the agency heads for these important health agencies need to not be presidential appointees. They should span the terms of different presidential administrations so we can rebuild public trust, Stephanie. But again, storytelling, having visuals and then critically authentic messengers, the public in matters who`s messaging on these issues and a crisis here is we have to identify the right people.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, that`s what we should do in the future. But for now, can we trust them because I want to ask about monkeypox. There CDC is reporting today that there`s 14,000 cases here. Are we doing enough? Should we be concerned I`m about to send my kids back to school.\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: So just to emphasize here I think monkeypox like many other contagious viruses and other diseases, infectious diseases is highly contagious. It`s transmissible through many different routes of transmission. But clearly, let`s be clear here the primary mode, as multiple studies have shown Stephanie, for all your viewers out there, especially parents, it`s through -- it appears to be through sexual contact, close skin to skin contact, oral and anal sex, primarily in demographics, such as men who have sex with men. Let`s be very clear.\par \par \uc2 Is it possible that school children will get exposed to infected? Yes, because anything is possible. There`s different routes of transmission, is it likely? No, it`s not. And I do believe the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and CDC have responded to the early criticism have ramped up the response. We have more JYNNEOS vaccine. \uc2 FDA\uc2  commissioner Califf is pursuing a strategy of basically one dose of the vaccine now can be distributed at five individuals that we can basically met out an intradermal vaccination approach that will still connote some protection, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 So, while imperfect on rollout, I do think that there has been improvement here and it`s really critical to know here who is at risk and who`s not.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Dr. Vin Gupta, you said it best trust and communication are essential when it comes to public health. And we are very lucky to have you here. Thanks for joining us. When we come back, a big night for our good friend Al Franken that featured plenty of jokes, and even some fact checking when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:57:44]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AL FRANKEN, FMR. U.S. SENATOR: It is so great to be here. I`m Al Franken. I`m the only former U.S. senator. I`m the only former U.S. senator to guest host Jimmy Kimmel Live.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight a very special shout out. We wanted to congratulate our friend Al Franken on a pretty unique accomplishment hosting Jimmy Kimmel Live, quite possibly inspired by our own for facts sake. He called out the former guy`s social media posts from a few days ago, yet again, Trump pushing the big lie that he had more votes than Biden, and the former senator would not let that lie stand.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRANKEN: Now, Trump keeps saying that he quote, got more votes by far than any sitting president in the history of our country. I know that sounds good. But it doesn`t mean much when you lose by 7,052,770 vote.\par \par \uc2 Now you`ve heard of Ireland, right? That`s more than the entire population of Ireland. It`s the entire population of Ireland plus, every single person in Nebraska combined, plus, a completely sold out Michigan Stadium and Ann Arbor. And everyone who works at every \uc2 Burger King\uc2  in America plus, every person the average American will meet in their entire life, plus, every major league baseball player and \uc2 NBA\uc2  player, plus a Southwest flight at maximum capacity. Throw in every person today who`s got monkeypox in Indiana. And there`s still be six people left over. That`s the entire Allman Brothers Band. That`s how many people \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost by.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [00:00:05]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, there you have it, a former senator, a comedian, a late night host. And right there now a mathematician. I hope that clears up any confusion about the big lie. And if you`re looking for more of that fact checking stick with us right here at THE 11TH HOUR, we have the facts. And Al Franken right there he happens to be one of our favorite guests.\par \par \uc2 And on that fine note, I wish you all a very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late with us. I`ll see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220818ei8h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for August 17, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,264 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:59:42]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said she fears for our very democracy if Republicans win in November. Do you?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The fate of democracy and the Republican Party in the wake of Liz Cheney`s loss.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): I think we have to make sure that we are fighting against every single election denier. The election deniers right now are Republicans.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Then, what did \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  tell a Fulton County grand jury?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you believe President Trump is the ultimate target of this investigation?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And are we on the verge of seeing a roadmap to the government`s ongoing investigation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Plus, raw politics at the greengrocer in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For those watching in Pennsylvania, you know, how particular many people are about their groceries? What happened with Wegman`s and Wegners? Can you explain that to them?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And a Republican reckoning with extreme abortion laws that needs to be seen to be believed?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That weighs on me. I voted for that bill. These are affecting people.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. We now have of course the results from the Republican primary for Wyoming`s loan at large congressional seat. Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , Vice Chair January 6 Committee was, well, annihilated as we expected. She lost by a resounding 37 points, 28.9 percent to 66.3. Wyoming voters overwhelmingly rebuke Cheney who, of course, stood up to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  after his insurrection, voting to impeach him and then helping to lead the committee investigating his actions.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  has earned a lot of praise, especially in Liberal circles for taking a stand against Trump and his attempt to end America`s constitutional republic. Fellow January 6 Committee member Congressman Jamie Raskin has spoken incredibly fondly about her, recently telling The New Republic that she has acted with great honesty to confront the lies of the Trump machinery. Raskin also said about his unlikely friend, "She will follow \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the gates of Hell if she needs to make sure that justice is done and that truth is known."\par \par \uc2 And there`s no doubt \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  has made genuine career political sacrifices on behalf of American democracy. That was evident last night almost as soon as polls closed. And what she has done is praiseworthy and important. But it`s also the case that, you know, Cheney is a bone-deep reactionary, a conservative to her core, and also, crucially, a partisan Republican. None of that has changed. And as strange as it may seem, she has, I think, taken this course of action in an attempt to save the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 This morning on the Today Show, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  laid out her case.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Look, I think the Republican Party today is in very bad shape. And I think that we have a tremendous amount of work to do. I think it could take several election cycles. But the country has got to have a Republican Party that`s actually based on substance, based on principles, you know, based on a belief in limited government and low taxes and a strong national defense.\par \par \uc2 I believe that the -- that the family has got to be the center of our community and of our lives. And those are the principles I believe in. That`s what the party used to stand for. And we`ve got to get the party back to that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, I will leave aside my own significant quibbles with the definition of what the party used to stand for. You`ll notice just to make one observation, she admitted the torture program, the war crimes that were championed by her father and endorsed by her from that list. But the key point here is that in her own understanding, Congresswoman Cheney is standing up to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not just for the sake of American democracy, but for the sake of the institutional vitality and indeed survival of the Republican Party itself, and I think she is right on this. And I think it`s under-appreciated how right she is by everyone.\par \par \uc2 Because here`s the thing, a Conservative Party in a two-party liberal democracy like ours, which is what the Republican Party is and has been, cannot continue to thrive or even exist if the MAGA authoritarian cult takes over. There is no space for that institution in the world in which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is successful, in which he successfully overturns American democracy, which is to say, to save the Republican Party, you actually must save American democracy.\par \par \uc2 And somehow lose Cheney seems to be one of the only members of her party to grasp this elemental truth, or at least one of the only ones to act on it. The other Republican cowards cannot muster the same courage to do what she has done to save their own hides in the end. Instead, they have dealt with this tension between the need to again, preserve American democracy and their desire to win elections, retain their jobs by basically going along with whatever \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says, winking and nodding and evading sometimes pretending they didn`t hear it.\par \par \uc2 And that`s basically the consensus view. That appears to be the calculation that everyone from Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , to Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has made. Now, the Republican who has been the most forthright about his thinking on this actually is Sen. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina. Graham, of course, not always a fan of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . In fact, he has made a real U-turn since that 2016 campaign.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:22]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Here`s what I think. I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a political car wreck.\par \par \uc2 He`s a race-baiting, xenophobic, religious bigot.\par \par \uc2 He`s a jackass.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not strong. He`s actually weak. He`s a bully. He`s a cartoon character.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is most unelectable Republican I`ve seen in my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 He shouldn`t be commander-in-chief.\par \par \uc2 He`s destroying the party. There to be a generation before we can overcome this, maybe never.\par \par \uc2 You know how you make America great again, tell \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to go to hell.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: No, Graham, I suppose to his credit has been very honest about why he has come around on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . This is what he said at a right-wing pro-Trump summit just last month.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: If you think Trump is bad for the party, I disagree with you. I think President Trump is good for the party. Let me tell you why. President Trump has gotten people who wouldn`t give me or Romney or anybody else the time of day. They believe that he is on their side. I`m trying to move toward a strong America and he`s the vehicle to get us there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, this is really interesting, because actually, if you look at the earlier clips of \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and the latter ones, he`s making the same argument. It`s just about what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does for the Republican Party, right? At first, he thought he was bad for the Republican Party. Now, he`s become convinced he`s good. And the argument is basically, what are you going to do, the base loves him.\par \par \uc2 And in the short term, to win elections for the Republican Party, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is correct. The math just does not work for Republicans in this election or the one two years after to achieve reliable broad victories without \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his supporters. It doesn`t work. But Graham and everyone who toes that line, right, they don`t seem to have thought through what happens next.\par \par \uc2 So, you win some elections. Maybe you get power. What happens if Trump and his people win? These are people who are, by their own words, right, fundamentally opposed to the American system. What happens if you have people who think that Hugo Chavez`s ghost hack voting machines, that the Chinese snuck in bamboo paper to steal an election? What happens when you have them running our elections?\par \par \uc2 We`re close to that. We`re on the precipice of that. Under the \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  strategy, that`s what is going to happen if he`s right. People come out and vote for him. What happens when that succeeds, when Trump succeeds? What happens when Trump succeeds where January 6 barely failed? I`ll tell you what happens. And I think \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  would agree with me.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Party, the thing we know is that, will not be preserved. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  succeeds in his project of destroying American democracy, it will succeed in creating something entirely new, something very ugly, and let me tell you, incredibly unpredictable. And it will not be a world I would be willing to bet in which people like \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  or \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  or a lot of the rest of them thrive.\par \par \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  seems to be just about the only person of the party who recognizes this at a cellular level. Keep in mind, she is not a liberal in any way. She is a solid right winger. She was number three in House leadership. She voted in line with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  even more than \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  did. She was with Trump just about every step of the way, even when he was essentially kidnapping children. She was with him until he tried to topple America`s constitutional republic.\par \par \uc2 But watching him attempt to assassinate American democracy made Liz she realize that you cannot keep the tiger on the leash because he will eat you. She realized there is no future with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , even for her most selfish political, institutional interest the Republican Party to which she is an heir. What would there be left to rule except a kingdom of ashes if Trump is successful in his project?\par \par \uc2 And if enough of her colleagues have realized it too, if they had the fortitude and courage \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  has, they might have been able to do something about it. They had a moment if they had collectively struck when they had the opportunity during the second impeachment trial. If they had voted to convict and bar him from future office, I don`t think we`d be in this situation, but they were cowards. They were all cowards. And so, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is alone and we`re going to see all of us how the \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  strategy plays out.\par \par \uc2 Tim Miller is a writer-at-large for the Bulwark and a former \uc2 RNC\uc2  spokesman. Mona Charen is the policy editor for the bulwark and a former speechwriter for Nancy Reagan. And they both join me now. First, let me just start with your response to that thesis that there -- that it is in the institutional interest of the Republican Party, as a conservative party in a -- in a democracy, for democracy to continue, and that \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  understands this and at some level is doing something that she thinks adheres to the benefit of what we come to think of the Republican Party. Mona?\par \par \uc2 [20:10:28]\par \par \uc2 MONA CHAREN, POLICY EDITOR, THE BULWARK: I have a slightly different perception of \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  from you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Please.\par \par \uc2 CHAREN: I think that she is being far less concerned about Republicans and the fate of the Republican Party and she`s really all about the fate of the country, recognizing that Donald Trump`s ascendance is a threat to our very democratic system, and therefore, she has really shed a lot of her partisan concerns.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you can -- you can hear it in the interviews that, you know, you said she hasn`t become any less partisan. I don`t agree. If you listen to her, she`s really just about unity now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I think -- yes, I think that`s fair. I think she has -- she has become part of the popular front in defense of America`s constitutional governance, basically.\par \par \uc2 CHAREN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: A broad -- which is broad as I like to joke. It goes from Noam Chomsky to Bill Kristol. It`s a -- it`s a big tent. She is -- she is in that, right?\par \par \uc2 CHAREN: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: But I also do think that -- I guess the point that I think she grasps that others don`t, Tim, is like, I don`t think Graham and the rest can conceptualize Trump`s success. This is what I think it is. I don`t think they think he`s capable of pulling it off. And so, I think they think they will still be in the driver`s seat, that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  will get power back, and he`ll sort of run the show, and they`ll get their judges. And no one has thought through, like, what does it actually look like if they really pull it off, right?\par \par \uc2 Like, if the whole American experiment collapses in on itself, what`s life like for \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 ? I think it doesn`t look as good as I think they might understand.\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, WRITER-AT-LARGE, THE BULWARK: Yes, I agree. I think that what`s so refreshing about \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is that her view of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is really not all that different from Mitch McConnell`s view of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , right?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Correct.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: They both think he`s dangerous. They both think that he`s buffoonish. You know, they both wish you would just disappear. But what \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  does is then act on that as if that`s a very serious thing that needs to be acted upon, right? And that has been the refreshing thing about her. And so -- and the frustrating thing for all of us, you know, over the past six years who know that our former colleagues and our former friends see him in the same way that we see him but aren`t acting on it, right?\par \par \uc2 And that I think relates directly to this threat assessment, right? Like she saw January 6 in the same way that we all did as a -- as a potentially an existential threat to the country. She sees all these MAGA Secretary of State`s and gubernatorial candidates that are coming up as an existential threat to the country. And I think that whether \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  has convinced himself that he can nudge \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the right direction, or whether he thinks that this is all too buffoonish and silly to actually come down, or whether he lives in this kind of privilege bubble, you know, that makes him feel like the American system will never be toppled and that`s not something to worry about, or some combination of all those things, they just are not acting on the reality that we all see and she is.\par \par \uc2 CHAREN: But, Chris --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well -- yes, go ahead. Go ahead, Mona.\par \par \uc2 CHAREN: If you -- if you as a Republican -- if you as a Republican can look at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and say, yes, he represents a threat to our way of life. Yes, he represents a threat to this democratic experiment, but I`m going to go ahead and support him anyway because my reelection is at issue, then you really don`t care. If you really understand that when you know what`s at stake, then you really don`t care about the country in the end. That`s what it comes down to.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and there`s also, Mona -- I mean, there`s a -- there`s a kind of tactical question to which I find -- which is so frustrating I think in retrospect, when you look at the 10 Republicans that voted for impeachment, for instance, among whom \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is one of them, you know, that basically, there`s only two remaining candidates for reelection, the others have bowed out, like Cheney had been defeated by Trump-backed challengers, right? Like they have been picked off one by one.\par \par \uc2 But there was a moment after January 6, I remember sitting, you know, in front of this camera when news broke that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  was leaking he didn`t know how he`s going to vote on impeachment, right?\par \par \uc2 CHAREN: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: If people had moved -- the collective action of people -- if people move as a group --\par \par \uc2 CHAREN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: -- it might be different, right? But they all --\par \par \uc2 CHAREN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: -- they ended up going out there and then turning around, and no one is behind them.\par \par \uc2 CHAREN: You know, and it`s funny because you know, they are these sayings on the Hill, you know, when they have like a tough vote, where they say, well, if we all hold hands together, then we can do it, right? Where if like across the aisle, you know, we`ll take some heat from our side, you`ll take it. And so -- but they never applied that principle within the Republican Party dealing with Trump. If they had just all held hands, they could have done it on two occasions. So, they didn`t.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:16]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And they -- and they haven`t now, Tim, which means that they have -- they`ve got the tiger problem. I mean, this is -- this is really it, right? And it`s not -- it`s not a thing, and I think you -- all three of us agree on this. It`s not a thing that can be ignored or compromised or massage or synthesized. It`s a dividing line that can`t be bridged. And, you know, Cheney and Kinzinger and you two and others are -- you know, called that but very few others will.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: You know, I would be in the metaphor to death that the tiger owns them now, right? I mean, they`re totally at the mercy of the tiger. They have no control over this. They might up for a moment, right, but now they`ve completely even given up pretending as if that they do. And I think that the thing that is so frustrating when I listened to your intro, Chris, is not only was it the right thing for morally and for the country for them to all hold hands and jump together during the second impeachment, but it was the right thing for the party in the medium term, right?\par \par \uc2 CHAREN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: They could have very easily just moved on to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , right? It would have been three percent of the party that would have been \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  dead-enders, like the you know, last Japanese that still fighting the war, but most of the party would have gladly moved on to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . The Trump run would have been done. A couple of them would have been sacrificed in the primaries. We would have needed a few more Liz Cheneys. That`s it. And that is the only thing that separates her from them is that she had the courage to say I will sacrifice myself on the pyre, and so she has.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that`s my point. That is my thesis here is that all the other things aside, I don`t think the people that run the Republican Party have correctly adduced their own self-interest in a more medium term than just the next election. And I think, you know, everyone is going to pay for it in the end. Hopefully, not in the worst-case scenario.\par \par \uc2 CHAREN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tim Miller and Mona Charen, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 CHAREN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, the incredible scene outside a Fulton County Courthouse today down the state of Georgia. Donald Trump`s former lawyer \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  testifies for a Georgia grand jury. What did Rudy say and what does it mean for his old boss? Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUDY GIULIANI\uc2 , TRUMP ATTORNEY: There`s overwhelming proof of fraud. It depends on you and it depends on your determination of credibility. I don`t have to be a genius to figure out that those votes are not legitimate votes. You don`t put legitimate votes under a table.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No.\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI: Wait until you throw the opposition out and in the middle of the night count them. We would have to be foolish to think that. So, no need to push it any further, but there`s more than ample evidence to conclude that this election was a sham, and in my view is part of the conservative plan, because certain cities with picked in order to carry this out where they thought they could get away with it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That was Trump lawyer, coup plotter \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  in Georgia back in 2020 with baseless lies and accusations, pretty close to slander actually, that the presidential election was rigged. Well, today, he returned to the state to testify in the ongoing Fulton County criminal investigation into alleged interference in the 2020 election, an investigation that we have since learned he is formally a target of.\par \par \uc2 As you can see, Giuliani was initially locked out of the courthouse, had to wait for a security guard to let him into the building with all the press around him assembled. When asked, Giuliani was non-committal as to whether or not he would cooperate with the investigation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What are you expecting to talk about here today?\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI: Well, they asked the questions and we`ll see.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Will you be cooperative? You attorney in New York says you can`t promise how responsive you`ll be.\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI: Right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And while he had much to say about Georgia after the election, it`s unclear exactly what he said today in his more than six hours before the grand jury. According to the Atlanta Journal-Constitution, "Giuliani`s attorneys would not say whether he pleaded the Fifth Amendment to avoid self-incrimination."\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Roiphe\uc2  is a former Manhattan Assistant District Attorney. She is now a law professor at \uc2 New York Law School\uc2  and she joins me now. Rebecca, first, I want to just talk about the situation Giuliani finds himself in which he has been informed he is a target of the investigation and also subpoenaed for testimony. Again, one plus one sort of seems like it adds up to two if you`re advising him legally. Like, how common is this kind of thing where a grand jury has a subpoena for someone that has also been informed by the prosecutor`s office as their target?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA ROIPHE, FORMER MANHATTAN ASSISTANT DISTRICT ATTORNEY: You know, it happens and different states do it differently, but you know, it certainly happens most of the time under these circumstances. You know, any individual in the right mind would plead the Fifth because obviously, the investigators have told -- the DA has told \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , that they have substantial evidence at this point to link him to criminal activity and that an indictment is possible. And under those conditions, it`s hard to imagine why he would be forthcoming.\par \par \uc2 Now, he had said previously that he was going to invoke the attorney-client privilege, but that would have only covered part of his testimony here because it only covers communications with a client --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: -- confidential communications for the purpose of obtaining legal advice. There`s a lot of other information that they`re looking for that that doesn`t even come close to. So, the question is, does he plead the fifth or not? And, you know, it would be unusual to say the least if he was forthcoming under these circumstances.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:12]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know -- you know, I`ve spoken about this case a number of times, and it`s stuck with me one time when I -- we were talking about this case, and I was talking about that presidential phone call, particularly, Trump`s phone call with Raffensperger. And I said, you know, this just seems criminal on its face. And you said, look at you know, it`s there`s intense here. It`s not -- it`s not a slam dunk case by any means.\par \par \uc2 What is your assessment of the legal threat right now, not just -- not to Trump who obviously is not been identified as a target, but the people like Giuliani who do seem like they`re in the crosshairs?\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: You know, I think since then, there has been a lot of information that may have changed my position somewhat. And most of that comes from the number of times from top advisors. He`s been told that he lost that election. And so, the notion that he honestly believed at this moment that the election was stolen, I thought when I last came on and said that, that was plausible. And I think it`s an increasingly less plausible defense here.\par \par \uc2 Now, in terms of \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  and whether he thought that, again, you know, it`s hard to imagine, especially given the fact that, you know, we`ve heard reporting that he said, you know, I don`t even -- I don`t even know whether there`s evidence, it just you know, seems like there has to be or something like that. So, clearly, you know, that doesn`t seem like a likely defense for him either.\par \par \uc2 And he`s a lawyer, you know. So, at a certain point for a lawyer, you say, look, you`re charged with understanding the difference between fact and fantasy. And you`ve -- if your defense is you bought into a fantasy, that`s a lame defense.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I want to ask a question about another local prosecutor`s case with another Trump-related case, which is your old office in the Manhattan District Attorney`s Office, which is brought criminal charges against Allen Weisselberg who`s the CFO for the Trump Org and criminal charges against the Trump org itself. We`ve got word today that Weisselberg will plead guilty, apparently, tomorrow to a number of felonies, and will not cooperate against Trump personally, but reporting indicating that he will cooperate in a criminal trial of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: You know, that`s big news because it makes it such that it`s hard to imagine what defense the organization has, because an organization obviously has no -- it has no separate existence other than through its employees. And so, you know, if a high managerial agent, someone in a position of power like Weisselberg is committing crimes and says that he was committing crimes on behalf of the organization, that strips the organization of I think almost any defense in this case, which then leads to, you know, a very likely criminal conviction.\par \par \uc2 And that`s a big deal because that -- you know, we used to say in the office, that`s a death sentence for an organization or a corporation. A criminal conviction makes it such that it`s very highly unlikely that the organization can continue. Now, I know that Trump Org. is complex and this does not involve every piece of \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , so with that caveat. You know, I do think it`s, it`s significant for Trump and his business and the future of his business in New York.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, \uc2 Rebecca Roiphe\uc2 , as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, the Justice Department has been tight-lipped about why they searched Trump`s retirement home. But tomorrow, a judge could reveal a lot more. Former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Special Agent Asha Rangappa breaks down what to expect next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Before the \uc2 FBI\uc2  he got a search warrant to go through Donald Trump`s Florida residence last week, agents presented a judge with an affidavit. This is standard operating procedure in which presumably they made the case of why the ex-president`s home needed to be searched, what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  expects to find there, and how agents obtained their information. They presented all that to a judge who then issued the warrant, as we know.\par \par \uc2 Last week, that search warrant was unsealed. It gave us an idea of what was recovered at Mar-a-Lago. The affidavit, however, remains under seal. That`s why there`s a hearing set for tomorrow at 1:00 p.m. A judge will decide if that document should be released or not. The Department of Justice is fighting the release warning that "If disclosed, the Affidavit would serve as a roadmap to government`s ongoing investigation in a manner that is highly likely to compromise future investigative steps. The fact that this investigation implicates highly classified materials further underscores the need to protect the integrity of the investigation."\par \par \uc2 Asha Rangappa is a former FBI Special Agent focused on counterintelligence investigations. She`s an attorney and senior lecturer at \uc2 Yale University\uc2 . And she joins me now. George Conway was on the program last night, Asha, and he said, look, there`s no chance this judge is going to unseal this. That seems to be the consensus. Is that were you are?\par \par \uc2 ASHA RANGAPPA, FORMER FBI SPECIAL AGENT: Yes, that`s where I am. And I think that the government -- I think that`s typical in a case. The time for the government to show -- drop the money, I guess it were, would be at trial, right? If they choose to bring charges, that`s where they have to reveal their witnesses, let Trump confront them, give him all access to the evidence that`s going to be used against him.\par \par \uc2 But what right -- what is happening right now is that the investigation is ongoing. They are still building the case. And there`s a few clues in this government filing. As you mentioned, first, they mentioned the roadmap. The fact that this would disclose the direction and scope of the investigation, investigative steps, witnesses. And Chris, this tells me that this probable cause was based on a number of factors, not just one source. They have a lot of different pieces of information which would be expected if they were asking a judge to approve a search in this case.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:19]\par \par \uc2 The second compelling reason is that they say, revealing these witnesses or sources could put them in danger. And there`s a footnote here that actually cites to the attack on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  Cincinnati`s office. So, this is a way in which Trump and his supporters` rhetoric is actually working against them in this legal argument. And then, finally, as you mentioned, they mentioned -- they say that this implicates highly classified information. So, there`s a national security interest here.\par \par \uc2 And I think this is definitely what tipped the balance in the government`s favor, because there`s a lot of deference given when the government is trying to protect national security, and a compromising of an investigation that is trying to protect national security would be harmful to the entire nation.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let me ask you about the Department of Justice more broadly, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . I mean, you and I, again, we`ve spoken a number of times. I think you`ve been in the camp of -- a little bit in the camp of like, what exactly is going on over there, you guys? Are you guys watching this January 6 hearing? Are you -- are you moving along on this? It does seem like there`s sufficient, you know, predicate for an investigation all that.\par \par \uc2 You know, in the intervening weeks, we`ve now got lots of activity of that grand jury, January 6 grand jury. In DC, we`ve got this search of the Mar- a-Lago home. Have you changed your perspective on what your understanding of where DOJ and \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  are on this?\par \par \uc2 RANGAPPA: So, Chris, I`m not sure if you`re asking about where they are on this in terms of the Mar-a-Lago investigation or January 6 because I think they`re a little bit different.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK.\par \par \uc2 RANGAPPA: I continue to believe that \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is very Hamlet-like. Like, I think he thinks about this and struggles with it. And, you know, I think he understands that this is a historical moment. I think that the normal considerations that we`ve talked about before with the January 6 investigation pursuing Trump, he had to put those aside in many ways because this involves, again, national security concerns.\par \par \uc2 In other words, he had no choice but to recover these documents because they are so sensitive, and because doing so could seriously injure the United States or help one of our adversaries. So, I think in many ways he was forced to take the step. That`s why I think it is still an open question whether he is going to actually pursue charges, because when you have a national security interest, there`s competing goals here. National security interests are a little bit different than the criminal punishment deterrents, all that kind of stuff.\par \par \uc2 They recovered the documents, so there`s a possibility that he could say, look, goal achieved, mischief managed. You know, we now have these. We can assess the damage. We can move on, focus on these other investigations. I think the filing to go back to the affidavit, their argument is that it`s inappropriate to unseal an affidavit before charges are filed, and that this is an ongoing investigation, which again, suggests to me that they believe that the fair part -- the part where Trump`s interests will be vindicated and his rights etcetera will be when charges are filed.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 RANGAPPA: So, I think it might be moving in that direction in this case mainly because I think it would look really bad if he didn`t -- if he wasn`t ready to go all the way. I still am not completely sure about the January 6 investigation partly because some of those criminal charges are trying to fit a square peg into a round hole. So, I think it really depends on the evidence that they accumulate among other factors.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. Speaking of that evidence, we should note the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  just reporting right before we went on air that federal prosecutors have issued a grand jury subpoena to the National Archives for all the documents the agency provided to the January 6 Committee. That seems to be playing a little bit of catch-up, but obviously, that`s also a huge cache of documents. So, that -- you know, we are learning about things. I think this actually subpoena was issued back in May. So, we`re learning about things a bit lately in terms of what that grand jury has been up to, but it has been quite active as well.\par \par \uc2 Asha Rangappa, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 RANGAPPA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, why is Dr. Oz having so much trouble connecting in his very recently adopted home state of Pennsylvania? It might have something to do with his grocery shopping habits. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The Pennsylvania Senate Race, Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , the Lieutenant Governor of that state, has demonstrated from the get go a very clear vision of how to run against his Republican opponent, millionaire, TV doctor Mehmet Oz. Paint him as a rich, out-of-touch buffoon who does not live in nor understand the state he is ostensibly running to represent.\par \par \uc2 Back in April, Oz released a video, sort of standard stuff, attacking Democrats for high prices of the grocery store, and it has resurfaced this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: So, I`m at a grocery shopping. I`m at Wegner`s. And my wife wants some vegetables for today, right? So, here`s a broccoli. That`s two bucks. There`s a ton of broccoli here. There`s some asparagus. That`s $4.00. And carrots, that`s four more dollars. That`s $10 of vegetables there. And then, we need some guacamole. That`s $4.00 more. And she loves salsa, yes, salsa, they`re $6.00. There must be a shortage of salsa.\par \par \uc2 Guys, that`s $20.00 for crudites, and this doesn`t include the tequila. I mean, it`s outrageous. And we got \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to thank for this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:20]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, I`ve watched that like 40 times now. It just keeps paying off. First, there`s no store called Wegner`s which is what he says he`s at. Oz probably meant to say Redner`s, which is a Pennsylvania-based supermarket chain, but he got it confused with Wegmans, which is a fancier grocery chain in Pennsylvania and throughout the Northeast with many locations in New Jersey where Oz, of course, actually lives. And so, Redner`s and 