ay in South Carolina, the Republican-led House Judiciary Committee met to consider a near-total abortion ban with no exceptions for rape or incest, which is where a Republican South Carolina lawmaker gave this moving speech.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEAL COLLINS, REPUBLICAN SOUTH CAROLINA STATE REPRESENTATIVE: A 19-year-old girl appeared at the ER. She was 15 weeks pregnant. Her water broke and the fetus was unviable. The standard of care was to advise her that they could extract or she could go home. The attorneys told the doctors that because of the fetal heartbeat bill, because that 15-week-old had a heartbeat, the doctors could not extract.\par \par \uc2 There`s a 50 percent chance -- greater than 50 percent chance that she`s going to lose her uterus. There`s a 10 percent chance that she will develop sepsis in herself and die. That weighs on me. I voted for that bill. These are affecting people. And we`re having a meeting about this. It took -- that whole week I did not sleep.\par \par \uc2 I followed up with the doctor a week later. She had heard nothing, did not know about the 19-year-old. Thank God, I followed up two weeks later, she did return to the ER. They did extract the now non-beating fetus. What we do matters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:55:04]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That committee voted 13 to seven to advance the bill. Five Republicans including the man you`re just watching didn`t vote.\par \par \uc2 Shefali Luthra is a health reporter of The 19th, an independent news organization which doing great reporting on this where she covers the intersection of gender and health care. And she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Shefali, first, I wonder, because you`re sort of in the weeds on this and reporting on this, and there`s a lot of different stories to track the sort of general view of what`s been happening in the states, particularly Republican-controlled states that didn`t have some automatic law that just, you know, flipped on after the Dobbs decision, what they`ve been doing in those states, which all seems to be moving mostly in one direction towards, you know, outlawing abortion?\par \par \uc2 SHEFALI LUTHRA, HEALTH REPORTER, THE 19TH: This has been really complicated and very difficult for these states that were theoretically prepared to move forward and start passing abortion bans. We saw this in Indiana, right, where exactly what you (AUDIO GAP) divided amongst not Democrats, but Republicans over whether to include rape and incest exceptions really derailed it for a while for the bill that now as passed and will take effect soon. Republicans are really, really torn over this.\par \par \uc2 In West Virginia, the House and Senate passed competing abortion bans, one with rape and incensed exceptions, and the other without, and they haven`t been able to reconcile that. And it is, in fact, such an intense debate that it has, (AUDIO GAP) passing this ban that they were called back into session to make law. And I think what`s really striking is that this has been such an essential issue for Republicans for a long time, right? We`ve been passing abortion bans since Roe was law of the land, right?\par \par \uc2 And we heard so many governors and political leaders talk about calling a special session as soon as Roe is overturned, to pass these new bans and new restrictions, and a lot of them (AUDIO GAP). There was supposed to be this wave of new laws that came about this summer. And right now, what`s instead of happening is that Republicans too are really grappling in some cases for the first time with the personal and pragmatic and also the political consequences of these kinds of laws.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s a really interesting point. I mean, there`s also of course, the politics of this I think as they grapple with that. I mean, this new -- there`s new polling today for the Marquette poll, which was polling the statewide Senate race in Wisconsin, but it also asked people about abortion. Obviously, that`s a very closely divided state. And, you know, do you basically -- do you to favor the \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  decision to overturn Roe v. Wade? Are you on the side of the court here? Only 62 percent of Republicans favor it, 28 percent oppose. That`s a huge number. Among Independents, two- thirds oppose it. Among Democrats, 92. Overall 60 to 33.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen, you know, numbers like that in state after state after Dobbs, which I got to imagine is part of what`s entering the mix as these states are moving in some cases fitfully to criminalize abortion.\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: This backlash has the potential to be really potent, right? It`s what Democrats are counting on to hold on to the Senate, right? You hear Senator Elizabeth Warren talk all the time about Wisconsin, Pennsylvania, in particular, as states where she really sees the backlash to the overturning of Roe v Wade as a chance to pick up seats.\par \par \uc2 We`ve heard this from the Democratic Senate campaign arm, right? They really hope that this is something that voters are going to react to. And the polling is often tricky, right, because polling on abortion is always complicated. And it depends on how you frame the question. But I think you`re absolutely right, that there is a real chance that this sort of breaks something that had been holding the Republican Party together for a long time. That`s really what we saw in Kansas.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s also, of course, the human aspect of this, which is harder to report on obviously, because all of this is shrouded in privacy as it should be. But this latest story out of Louisiana where a woman named Nancy Davis says her baby was diagnosed with Acrania, which is a rare and fatal condition where the baby`s skull fails to form in the womb.\par \par \uc2 And according to health experts, babies with this condition only survive minutes to hours after birth. But because the want -- the mother`s life is not in danger, she does not qualify for an abortion. She was denied one and she now has to carry the baby to term or cross state lines to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Again, these are just one of the stories that we`re hearing, but we know with certainty there are others like this. What effect do you think these kinds of individual stories are having on people`s perception of this?\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: I think they`re tremendous. I think for a long time this has been seen as this really abstract issue, right? It`s something that happened to someone else and not to your loved one or a sister or spouse or child. And now people are reading these stories. They`re seeing themselves, right, because this could happen to anyone. And they are horrified that have no exceptions for the death of the fetus. They`re horrified by these laws that are so unclear that doctors can`t provide health care they expect to be able to give.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Shefali Luthra who has been following this closely at The 19th which has been a great source for this that I`ve been depending on, thank you very much for your reporting.\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Wednesday night. The Alex Wagner -- no, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220818ei8h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for August 17, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Matthew Dowd, Jonathan Allen, Ali Vitali, Blayne  Alexander \par \uc2 8,053 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Hello, I`m Chris Jansing, live in front of the Historic State Capitol in beautiful Cheyenne, Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 And today, a new era begins for this state, as new questions are being raised for the country with Liz Cheney`s landslide defeat exposing the stark choices for Republicans come November. And the massive divide between those willing to follow \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and those who won`t. Cheney spoke about those choices last night. And even in the face of defeat, it was hardly a typical concession.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R-WY): No office in this land is more important than the principles that we are all sworn to protect. And I well understood the potential political consequences of abiding by my duty. Tonight, Harriet Hageman received the most votes in this primary, she won. I called her to concede the race. This primary election is over. But now, the real work begins.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: On the other side, the woman who beat her, Harriet Hageman, spoke in Trumpian terms about what this one means for her state.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIET HAGEMAN, WON WY GOP HOUSE PRIMARY: Wyoming has drawn a line in the sand that if we put you in power, you will be accountable to us, you will answer to us, and you will do what is in our best interests. And if you don`t, we will fire you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  also took a victory lap that he`d been waiting to make for more than a year, claiming he`d help send Cheney to the "depths of political oblivion." But did he? You have to consider that question in the context of what surely is a fateful moment for the former president. Despite federal and state investigations seemingly closing in on every side, he has been able to maintain his hold over the party. Of course, the bigger test of that hold will be during the midterms when his integrity and influence will once again be on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Liz Cheney`s future is yet to be written. This morning, the Politico playbook said "Cheney lost her seat but gained something else. She is now the undisputed leader of the Trump opposition." Last night, she talks about the work ahead, work that includes the January 6 committee and the leadership pack designed to oppose Trump and what he stands for, especially if he decides to run again for president. And then there`s the question of whether she herself could be his opponent.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Are you thinking about running for president?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: It -- that`s a decision that I`m going to make in the -- in the coming months today. And I`m not going to make any announcements here this morning. But it is something that I am thinking about and I`ll make a decision in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Joining us to discuss is Steve Peck, Public Affairs Senior Producer at Wyoming PBS. He`s with me on set. Jonathan Allen has been covering this race. He`s a senior politics producer for \uc2 NBC News Digital\uc2 . Matthew Dowd is the founder of Country Over Party and an MSNBC political analyst. Ali Vitali joins us from Anchorage, Alaska, where she`s been covering the other big primary that took place last night.\par \par \uc2 So, Steve, I`m wondering what you think about what we have heard over the last 12 hours or so from \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , and what do you think her next move is? You`ve been around this for, what 40 years plus.\par \par \uc2 STEVE PECK, SENIOR PRODUCER, WYOMING PBS: That`s true, I have. And welcome to Wyoming, by the way.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PECK: This race reminds me of sort of a person who says I`m going to lick my finger and stick it right into a light socket. It doesn`t make a lot of sense. It doesn`t sound logical. You know what`s going to happen but it`s still a shock when it happened. So for a year or more, there has been one line of consensus really, in this race, which has been that she couldn`t win. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  could not win. The questions being, how big the margin of defeat would be, and whether the crossover vote from the Democrats and the unaffiliated voters in Wyoming, which is a group almost as large as the Democrats, might make the margin closer. Regarding --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It didn`t.\par \par \uc2 PECK: And it didn`t. And I think in terms of your question about the voters, there was some Democratic crossover. But I believe that the analysis of the -- of the results will bear out that those voters were more interested in some down-ballot races at the statewide level rather than at the federal level where the Trump candidate was on the ballot three times, but only one of those. And leading to the conclusion perhaps that it`s still \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , and her record and her rhetoric are what they are, and the perception that she had become a darling of Democrats, at least in this state, I think the lie was put to that last night.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So does that rule out a run for president? Is she teasing us or is she really thinking about it?\par \par \uc2 PECK: She`s doing both. You begin by teasing I think and then you -- then you plunge in. What I would say to that is her Wyoming problem is now behind her. She no longer has to worry about being nominated in Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: She was never going to win over that state.\par \par \uc2 PECK: The smallest state in the Union.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PECK: Even though two years ago, she won by a landslide, her third straight landslide each by a bigger margin than the one that preceded it. But that`s now behind her. And so being the lone member of the 435-member body, from the smallest state of the union, that`s not a particularly impactful position in Congress, honestly. She made it more so because she was able to maneuver to the -- almost to the pinnacle of Republican leadership. But that`s behind her now.\par \par \uc2 To what she has is several more months of the January 6 inquiry. She`s now a national figure. She has at least for the time being a centrist appeal, in some places, perhaps not in Wyoming. But Wyoming is in the rearview mirror now. And this is not the end of the world for her I`m sure if what she sees is a wider path that she wants to start on now.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right, so you`ve given us a lot to unpack. Matthew, we know Cheney wants to commit to fighting Trump and bringing the country back together. Lots of people are already doing that, including you. Can \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  be effective in that fight in a way others have not been? I mean, after all, she did raise a lot of money from all around the country.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEWS DOWD, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think she`s already been incredibly effective in that fight. I mean, she`s obviously adopted a pro- democracy pro-constitution banner, she`s one of we can`t forget -- neglect to say she`s one of the most conservative members of the Republican caucus. She`s one of the most conservative as defined by conservative principles. And so having Republicans who are conservatives stand up and say, listen, I`m with you on all these other issues, I just can`t be with you on destroying our democracy and being -- and lifting up autocracy which would -- which is what it seems that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants. I think that is a very effective tool.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think she`s calculating whether or not she can win Republican primaries in a presidential race. I mean, if I sat down with her and looked at the numbers, I would tell her about the same thing she probably knows. That she can`t win that nomination because she`s out of step, they seem to want election denialism. But I think it`s a very effective platform for her to raise these national issues that`s going on in every state, it`s not just Wyoming, it`s Arizona, it`s Michigan, it`s Pennsylvania, it`s Nevada, it`s all these states. So if she can do that and continue to do that after her career as a congressperson has done, I think it`s incredibly important in this debate and in this fight that is happening in America today.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Jonathan, look, obviously the numbers would not look good for her if Matthew sat down with her. But last night, she went ahead and talked about Abraham Lincoln. And I want to play a part in that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: The great and original champion of our party, Abraham Lincoln, was defeated in elections for the Senate and the House before he won the most important election of all. Lincoln ultimately prevailed. He saved our Union. And he defined our obligation as Americans for all of history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Her message has been the party is broken, and I can fix it. But Republicans, many of them at least don`t seem to believe it`s broken. In fact, they think Trump fix what was wrong with it. So how does she answer that?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN ALLEN, MSNBC SENIOR NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: It`s a very difficult question to answer. And I think we`ll see how she`s answering it over the course of time as she started this new political action committee and as she thinks about running for president. I mean, the big problem for her is, as Matthew suggested, there`s not a path in the Republican Party to a nomination. And if she wants as an independent, it`s not clear whether she would siphon more votes from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s impossible to project that because you`re talking about state-by-state elections with an independent on the ballot, potentially, which swing states which he helped the Democrats defeat Trump, which states which you end up helping Trump defeat the Democrats. And I think she`ll do a lot of research into that. But it`s a difficult question to sort of answer at this moment. We just don`t know what`s going to happen over the next couple of years before that presidential election.\par \par \uc2 One thing I would say, though, is, if you look at the people who are small d Democrats, people who believe in this union, who believe and prioritize the Republic over their party, she is among the most effective voices for that -- for that message at a time when that`s the axis on which our politics are fought. It would be difficult for you to name three or four big D Democrats who are better at messaging than \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is. So I think there will be a role for her to play in the upcoming presidential election and probably as well in the midterm elections, and we`ll just have to see how she determined she can -- she can best be of use.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. Well, we were at the Harriet Hageman headquarters last night that turned out to be a victory party. I talked to some voters there. And I couldn`t resist asking them what they thought about the idea that \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  might run for president. And here`s a sampling of what they told me.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Some people say if she doesn`t win tonight, she might run for president.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It might be worse than this one.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Some people think she might run for president.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Good luck. It won`t happen.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: She`s campaigning everywhere but here, and why? That`s as good a reason as any.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: My impression, and it`s a snapshot in time, it`s not your 40 years of experience was that there was not a single person in that room whose mind was going to be changed because of all the other issues combined about this state, they felt less strongly about than they feel about her going after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PECK: That`s absolutely true. That`s the only way to explain how she could have lost considering who she is, who she`s been, who her father is, and her previous electoral success. But Wyoming doesn`t matter in presidential politics. Wyoming has voted for the Democratic candidate once in seventy years.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But do you think it`s an indication of where the Republican Party is?\par \par \uc2 PECK: It certainly is, in Wyoming -- in this particular race. But in Wyoming, the governor for the past 50 years has been a Democrat more often than a Republican has been. So I think a lot of it is focused at the tip of the funnel related to \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the impeachment on January 6 that created this position for her.\par \par \uc2 One thing I have observed in presidential campaigns, as well as the campaigns, often are shaped at the primary level, largely by the people who don`t win. Think of \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , for example, or -- and others but so she would -- could have a role to play in that by debating on the stage against Trump or someone else, for example, and could have an impact there -- a memorable impact, if that`s what she wants to do. What someone else said is very true that we don`t know what`s going to happen in two years. No one two years ago --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: we don`t know what`s going to happen in two days.\par \par \uc2 PECK: No one two years ago could have predicted we`re here now.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PECK: And if they said they would, that just clicks of untruth, clanks false. I`ve been at -- looking at Wyoming politics for a long time and people say, oh, boy, this is -- these are crazy times, aren`t they? And I said, well, yes, they are. But it`s always crazy times in politics. And two years from now, it will be again, and I -- we just don`t know what`s going to happen. I think we do know that she`s decided she wants to be part of that, and is in a position and has the knowledge and experience, the patience, and the long vision where she can make sure that happens in some capacity for something.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And as -- and as Jonathan pointed out, the messaging skills. But let me try to get it this, Matthew, from another direction. Here`s what was written in the New Republic. Cheney represents the idea that there are Republicans out there who are horrified by what is happening to their party. There`s little evidence that there are many of them, however. Cheney still has supporters in the Republican Party and among the masses, but all of this adds up to pockets of resistance, not a silent majority. A career might be built on these foundations, but it`s not likely to be an electoral one.\par \par \uc2 How much of the Republican Party -- this is the bottom line question I have, Matthew, how much of the party does Cheney really represent?\par \par \uc2 DOWD: Well, if you look at polls after polls after polls over the last year, and I`ve looked at a lot of -- including her favorability among Republicans, and the favorability of people that espouse this sort of pro- democracy side against the big lie, it represents less than 20 percent of the party. And so we`ve seen this in election after election. We saw it in Wyoming yesterday. It wasn`t just \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 . It was the sort of big lie promoting election-denying secretary of state Republican that won the nomination in Wyoming on the same day. And so I think it`s a very small segment.\par \par \uc2 And one thing I would remind \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , of on the Lincoln thing is the old party had to be completely defeated the Whigs and decimated before somebody like Abraham Lincoln could arise in something new. And so in my view, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , if she does is successful at this, has to begin to build something new in this sort of post-Republican Party. I would also speculate, I know \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , I worked with \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  in the 2004 Bush-Cheney campaign, she is a person that stands on principles and doesn`t lose sight of those. She will not do anything that will help elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 So if she considers an independent, and she does the research and finds out that her entrance as an independent would help Joe -- would help \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and hurt \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , she will not do that. So I think her sort of Northstar right now is how to remove the Republicans that are election- deniers from this and defeat a possible \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  candidacy. But she`s only got about 20 percent Republicans behind her.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, beyond Cheney, the other marquee name on the ballot last night was \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2 . Ali, yesterday, you said this could take a while to sort out, so where are we this morning?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Doing exactly that. It`s going to take a little bit of time to sort out if \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  is the person who`s going to be heading to Congress most imminently.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 Though again, we have to remind people, Chris, this is a race that comes back around in November to serve out an actual full term in Congress. Palin made the ballot for that as well last night. But look, last night in Alaska was again, another example of the country and midterm elections trying to answer this question of what kind of party is this and what`s Trump`s role in it, \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  was hoping that the role that Trump would play in it is booing her to victory.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2 , though the incumbent who had her own primary election last night, was hoping that the answer would be quite different. And when I asked her about this larger Republican landscape, and if she even thinks that she fits in it anymore, her answer was really interesting. Listen to what she told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LISA MURKOWSKI\uc2 , (R-AK): Those Republican values and principles that I signed on to when I was 18 years old, those are still what I think ground are still, what I hope ground of the Republican Party. And so I hope that we do not become the party of one person, I hope that we do not become the party of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VITALI: And, Chris, what I countered with to Senator Murkowski was the fact that it does seem like that is exactly what the Republican Party is doing. Murkowski is one of those rare people in Congress where I usually spend my time who will reach across the aisle, who will work in a bipartisan fashion. And I think it`s striking when we see her election come up again in November because she was assured to make the top four that she needed to make to be on the ballot here come November. November is the real Judgment Day for Murkowski.\par \par \uc2 Again, she`s the only senator who voted to convict \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  of impeachment charges, who`s actually facing an election battle in this current cycle. So she`s the real litmus test there for not how it plays in the House, which is, of course, much more closely huge to the way that the public would vote in terms of the fact that it goes district by district as opposed to the state by state. But so Murkowski is one that we`re going to be paying attention to even well after this primary night, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali, thank you so much from beautiful Alaska. Jonathan, Matthew, thank you. Steve, appreciate you coming out and being with us today. And a quick reminder, NBC News`s "PLAN YOUR VOTE" tool is here to help you successfully cast your ballot in the midterm primaries. You can get key information on voting rules where you live, just head to NBCNews.com/PlanYourVote now.\par \par \uc2 Another major political flight that`s heating up here in Wyoming and in fact all over the country, abortion, the fascinating new poll data showing what that could mean in November, and the conservative lawmaker who appears to be having second thoughts, and more legal developments surrounding the former president, important updates on \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . You`re watching "CHRIS JANSING REPORTS" live from Cheyenne, Wyoming, only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: As more and more legal developments rock Trump world, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  takes center stage today. Right now, he is appearing before a Georgia Grand Jury investigating 2020 election interference in that state. As Giuliani arrived at the courthouse this morning, he found himself locked out. A few months later, a guard unlocked the door to let him in. Meantime, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  opening the door to talking to the January 6 committee saying today in New Hampshire, he would consider testifying if invited.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  is reporting the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has interviewed top Trump White House witnesses, including former White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , and his deputy. That is according to three sources familiar with the matter. NBC News has not confirmed that report. And as so many different tentacles of law enforcement get closer and closer to Trump`s inner circle, there is this from the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 . "Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and close aides have spent the eight days since the \uc2 FBI\uc2  searched his Florida home rushing to assemble a team of respected defense lawyers, but the answer they keep hearing is no."\par \par \uc2 To help us break all this down, let`s bring in NBC`s Blayne Alexander in Atlanta, and Harry Litman, former U.S. attorney, and former Deputy Assistant Attorney General. So, Blayne, what`s the latest at the courthouse?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, we know that it`s been almost five hours now since \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  and his counsel walked into this downtown Atlanta courthouse here behind me. So that testimony is still going on. Now, of course, this is all secret proceedings. This is a grand jury testimony so he`s behind closed doors testifying under oath so we don`t know exactly what is happening inside that room, how he`s being questioned, or perhaps more specifically, how he`s answering those questions.\par \par \uc2 But before in the days leading up to this, his attorney did give some sort of indication as to at least the questions that he would not be answering. He said that Giuliani would stay away from any questions that had to do with conversations he had with former President Trump and that he would claim attorney-client privilege. So that`s that.\par \par \uc2 But the other piece of this, the big backdrop, of course, that makes all of this something that we`re watching even more closely is the fact that he`s now been named the target in this investigation. And of course, according to a number of legal experts that I`ve spoken to, they say that they would certainly believe that he would plead the Fifth on a number of these questions.\par \par \uc2 Now, it is important to talk about why he`s here, why the DA wants to hear from him in the first place. When I spoke with \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  in an interview last month, she`s made it clear that she`s conducting a robust investigation. She wants to hear from anybody who had any sort of knowledge about the former president`s mindset, his actions, really anything revolving around the 2020 election here in Georgia, specifically for \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 . That pertains to a number of statements that he made before some Georgia State Legislatures essentially talking about a number of false claims about the state`s election, debunked, and unproven conspiracy theories, really, that we expect him to be questioned on today.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Blayne, thank you so much for that. So, lots to talk about, Harry, but let`s start with \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  because a former aide was on CNN yesterday, and here`s how he describes the former mayor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEN FRYDMAN, RUDY GIULIANI`S FORMER PRESS SECRETARY: He knows he lied to legislators, he knows that he concocted this false electoral scheme. You know, he knows he lied for his client. And he knows we all know. It`s clear. Rudy flew too close to the sun. He got too close to Trump. He got burned.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, like you, Rudy is a former federal prosecutor. He knows how law enforcement works, and how meticulous these investigations can be. What is he potentially facing here? And what do you make of the fact that at least somebody close to him says he`s pretty nervous?\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: That`s some frank New York talk, isn`t it? He`s facing being indicted for a crime. They -- it`s true, they`re interested in Trump. But when they called him a target, Chris, that means they are ready. They`ve crossed the sort of subjective threshold. They are ready to indict him for his own activities in December 2020 when he went down and gave us the sort of greatest hits of fraud. He said, your count is false. He talked about the suitcases, the machines, dead people voting, everything, and it was all malarkey.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And then you`ve got this late-breaking development from former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , who, as we all know, was in an extremely precarious situation during the insurrection. He may be ready to talk to the January 6 committee. What would be your first questions for him?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Well, what did Trump say here there, and over this place? He will not answer them. Even his answers, there were very sort of tiptoe and he`ll think about it. So I personally think it won`t be that valuable, an exchange, maybe a nugget or two when he`s already in putting some arm`s length requirements and conditions on. We`ll see if it happens. And then we`ll also see if it`s anything at all useful.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, let me ask you about those conditions because he`d said he`d considered testifying if there was an invitation to participate. Would there be that in discussions between Pence`s and the committee`s lawyers? And what do those kinds of discussions usually look like?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Polite but increase -- but then they get increasingly tense. What he also said is I`ll have to consider the historic role of the vice president. What does that mean? He`s a witness here with some very valuable testimony to give and I think that he was vice president except as a gives rise to legal privilege claims, really has nothing to do with it. But that`s what it`ll be is it sort of decorous and befitting his role. And he`s a very sort of cautious and even, you know, mealy-mouthed, sometimes politician. So that`s what it would entail but, of course, having him up there saying a few things, that would be informative.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And his old boss, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , is apparently having trouble finding qualified lawyers. Now, look, if you`re an ambitious attorney, I think it would be hard to find a more high-profile case, that`ll get you on TV more. So does it surprise you at all that there are so many lawyers apparently saying thanks, but no, thanks?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Only because it`s not the first time. But yes, what didn`t you think and you know, he deserves good counsel, but they are in droves. And I know what`s happening. Anybody who is prominent in a big firm, the partners are going them and saying get, don`t go anywhere near him because all our other clients and general counsels will scream, it`ll be terrible for the firm. And so anyone with any prominence who has a future practice is steering clear of him.\par \par \uc2 But --and, of course also, it doesn`t help that he never pays his bills and winds up trashing whoever his lawyer was. So he`s probably the worst client in the -- anyone`s ever seen and that that feeds into it but you know, it`s remarkable, right? The former president can`t get a lawyer.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Harry Litman, well said. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Meantime, a legal and political fight heating up here in Wyoming, well, nationwide, the political and personal implications of the new abortion legislation are pushing people to have second thoughts and they`re showing up in polling unexpectedly on the floor of a heavily Republican state legislature as well. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS live from Cheyenne, Wyoming, only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:45]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Welcome back to a special edition of CHRIS JANSING REPORTS live in Cheyenne, Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 And right now the abortion fight is heating up in this state after a judge ruled that abortions can continue while a lawsuit that challenges the ban is decided. But that`s just one of dozens of fights across the country over abortion. One state over, in Idaho, a judge has ruled that a near-total ban can go into effect at the end of the month. The court battles have ignited a very different fight, one for women voters.\par \par \uc2 In a new FOX News poll has some fascinating numbers. Look at the shift away from the Republican Party and toward Democrats. Women up seven points. White women eight. Non-white women 10. Suburban women up 9. And the same poll shows that men are up 4 points. Dads are seeing a 28-point shift that could reflect the power of moms and daughters maybe talking to their fathers and husbands.\par \par \uc2 All of this is likely why we`re seeing the airwaves fill up with Democratic ads that target Republicans for their anti-abortion positions across House, Senate, and governor`s races.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Jasmine Hall, a reporter for the "Wyoming Tribune Eagle" and Politico`s Megan Messerly, who covers health care for this site.\par \par \uc2 This is a big legal question, but it turns into a political one when it impacts the way people think about how they`re going to vote. You`ve been talking to some of the people that this will impact directly. Tell me what they`re telling you.\par \par \uc2 JASMINE HALL, WYOMING TRIBUNE EAGLE REPORTER: Yes. Well, so it`s been an interesting conversation. I`m lucky to be one of the reporters covering it. There`s been a lot of other people involved in this. But after talking with local attorneys as well as health care providers, there`s a great concern for the language that`s included in this law because at the moment the only exceptions would be in cases of sexual assault or incest. And if there`s a dire medical circumstance.\par \par \uc2 But there`s no language that describes that medical circumstance or what might happen between a doctor and a patient when they`re making that decision. There`s a lot of doctors who are nervous that if they give care such as, you know, providing medication to pass a miscarriage or even discussing, you know, treatment for an ectopic pregnancy that they might be at risk of a lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:08]\par \par \uc2 And then in other cases where the baby can`t be carried to term, they might die outside of the womb, doctors have no ability to treat that at the moment. They have to send their patients down to Colorado. But it`s an interesting conversation just because in Wyoming before this law went into effect there was only one clinic that was giving medical abortions to women and other people who were in need.\par \par \uc2 And it was medication. There have been no surgical abortions in this state. And they have continued to send those women down to Colorado, to other states, which puts pressure on Colorado. I know that Governor Polis, he actually made it so that there will be no investigations -- or cooperation with investigations --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That might come out of here.\par \par \uc2 HALL: Yes, for women who might travel. And here in southeastern Wyoming that`s a big conversation. We`re only 45 minutes from Fort Collins. We`re 15 minutes from the border. It`s not hard to get down there. But it does cost women a lot in terms of resources. What kind of money do you have? What kind of travel accommodations can you make? And people are really having to look at that in the future. But at the moment there has been a preliminary injunction granted. And so that choice won`t have to be made at the moment.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: At least for the moment. But that leads me exactly to what`s happening in South Carolina. And there is a Republican state representative, his name is Neil Collins, he actually broke down yesterday while telling the story of a 19-year-old woman. He was told by a doctor that the woman`s water broke at 15 weeks and that her fetus was unviable but had a heartbeat. Because of the state`s new heartbeat bill, attorneys advised doctors they could not extract the fetus. They sent her home.\par \par \uc2 The doctor told him there was a 50 percent chance that teenager could lose her uterus, a 10 percent chance she could get sepsis and die. Let me play what Collins said to his fellow lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEAL COLLINS (R), SOUTH CAROLINA STATE REPRESENTATIVE: That weighs on me. I voted for that bill. These are affecting people. And we`re having a meeting about this. It took -- that whole week I did not sleep. What we do matters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you, Representative Collins.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: One second, Mr. Chairman. I`m almost finished.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Megan, the doctor did tell him that the woman survived. But this Republican lawmaker is from Pickens County, South Carolina, where Trump received the highest percentage of votes in 2020. And I think when you, you know, put that together with what`s happening here, the reddest state in the nation, is the heart of the debate in many ways what we`re seeing now? Where is that line? How specifically do we need to draw it and at what point do you take medical decisions away from a doctor?\par \par \uc2 MEGAN MESSERLY, FORMER LAS VEGAS SUN REPORTER: Exactly. I think that`s really what we`re seeing, especially Republican lawmakers at the state level, grapple with. I mean, before Roe was overturned, not that it was easy to be pro-life, but I`ve had some Republican lawmakers say that -- you know, who describe themselves as pro-life say, you know, it was a pretty easy position to take. You didn`t have to deal with these thorny questions over how many weeks, do you want exceptions or not, what does it mean to protect the life of the pregnant person, to protect the pregnant person`s health, how do you allow abortions in cases of lethal fetal anomaly where there`s no chance the fetus is going to survive?\par \par \uc2 And so I`ve had a lot of Republican lawmakers tell me that being, quote- unquote, pro-life in a post-Roe era is very hard and filled with nuance. You know, I`m talking to these folks. Like that example you mentioned from South Carolina yesterday. You know, there`s a lot of Republican lawmakers in South Carolina that I`ve been talking to who sort of acknowledge and recognize that even though they in general are opposed to abortion and want to limit access to abortion as much as possible that there are these cases where it`s not so black and white and they`re really thinking through, you know, how do we craft the laws in such a way that we`re banning the abortions that we`re trying to ban without banning other procedures that we`re not?\par \par \uc2 And I just think they`re finding it`s a lot more complicated than perhaps any of them had anticipated before Roe was actually overturned.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We saw the political impact of this in Kansas. And I want to go back to those FOX News numbers. The swing toward the Democratic Party. Those dad numbers alone stand out. What are you seeing in terms of Democrats seizing this moment? And maybe the bigger question, can they sustain it until November?\par \par \uc2 MESSERLY: Yes. That`s the million-dollar question. I mean, Democrats are extremely eager to talk about abortion and reproductive health care.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:02]\par \par \uc2 You know, they know obviously like those polling numbers you mentioned, this is a winning issue for them, or they believe it to be a winning issue with voters, especially when they`re dealing with, you know, the economy, inflation. They would much rather be talking about abortion. You know, all this comes back to the question of, you know, are voters willing to cast their ballot on abortion right? Is that going to be the deciding issue?\par \par \uc2 Interestingly enough, you know, talking to some Republicans, they`ve expressed concern to me, Republican state lawmakers, who have said, you know, we`re worried that we`re going to lose some of these, you know, quote-unquote, "Republican suburban women" who are a little bit more moderate, that they`re going to lose those voters to Democrats. The question is of course where this makes a difference.\par \par \uc2 And obviously where we`re looking to is the gubernatorial races. You know, whether you lose those Republican moderate suburban women could make a difference in some of these really close races. You know, I`m thinking Pennsylvania. I`m thinking Nevada. States where the future of reproductive health care really depends on the balance of power in the state between the governor and the state legislature.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Megan Messerly, Jasmine Hall, I know you were out late last night. We were both covering the election. So thank you for coming over to see us.\par \par \uc2 A tidal wave of pandemic fraud. So many cases, so many agencies involved in finding out who took government money illegally that prosecutors are drowning trying to keep up. The author of that stunning report joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:45:37]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In what`s being called a tidal wave of pandemic fraud, widespread investigations are under way that according to the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " have a huge number of people struggling to keep up with the staggering caseload. This investigation involves 500 people and 21 inspectors general. That includes investigators from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the Secret Service, the Postal Inspection Services and the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . 1500 people have already been charged in fraud cases, with 450 convictions so far. And agents are sorting through two million potentially fraudulent aid applications.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, the author of that article, David Fahrenthold, a "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reporter and an NBC News contributor.\par \par \uc2 David, this is mind-boggling. You describe it as one of the largest frauds in American history, with billions of dollars stolen by thousands of people including at least one amateur who boasted of his criminal activity on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 . Tell us more.\par \par \uc2 DAVID FAHRENTHOLD, NEW YORK TIMES REPORTER: Yes. The scale of this is sort of hard to imagine. So during the pandemic there were three really large programs. Expanded Unemployment Aid and two big loan programs from the \uc2 Small Business Administration\uc2  that were supposed to help struggling businesses that basically had a huge loophole, the same loophole in all of them, which is that they were built on the honor system.\par \par \uc2 There were key elements to qualify, people just had to swear they`re eligible. And obviously there`s a lot of people out there who are willing to swear to things that were false. As a result thousands and thousands of people committed -- stole, you know, like we said billions and billions of dollars. And it`s just the scale of that is so big that even for the giant federal law enforcement apparatus it`s almost too much to chase.\par \par \uc2 It`s almost too many leads. Almost too many tips. And they`re even now struggling to catch up to, you know, all the leads that are out there.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, earlier this month President Biden signed bills extending the statute of limitations for some pandemic-related fraud to 10 years from five, maybe for exactly that reason, David. And he had a warning to those committing fraud. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My message to those cheats out there is this. You can`t hide. We`re going to find you. We`re going to make you pay back what you stole and hold you accountable under the law.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I mean, are we looking at potentially a decade-long investigation? And are they kind of prioritizing them, I don`t know, based on how much money they believe a person took?\par \par \uc2 FAHRENTHOLD: Yes. The first part of what Biden said is true. You can`t hide. The investigators I talked to said these are -- the trail of evidence in this case is footsteps left in concrete. You lied on a government document. The government still has that document. It`s really hard to get out of this once they catch you. The problem is, you know, the second part of Biden`s statement which was we`re going to catch you, you know, we`re going to track you down. Even with 10 years, you know, with that incredibly long time horizon, I don`t think that`s going to turn out to be true.\par \par \uc2 In fact, when we talked to some of these investigators they said yes, we`re prioritizing the biggest cases, the ones where there were, you know, ring leaders organizing a bunch of other people, habitual criminals committing these cases. And there are a lot of cases including people -- one very common fraud was people who took advantage of an advance grant program from the \uc2 Small Business Administration\uc2  where they got $10,000 they didn`t have to pay back. If they got that they may never see the feds at their door because it costs so much to investigate those crimes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: David Fahrenthold, it was an eye-opening article. I recommend it to people. Thank you so much for being on the program.\par \par \uc2 New water restrictions on the way for drought-stricken states like Arizona, Nevada. Right here in Wyoming as well. What those restrictions look like and what the newly signed climate and health law will do about it. That`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching a special edition of CHRIS JANSING REPORTS live from Cheyenne.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:53:14]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Wyoming is in a race against the clock to cut water use as seven other surrounding states prepare for cut as well because of an ongoing drought. The drought has been shrinking the Colorado River and its surrounding reservoirs to perilously low levels.\par \par \uc2 Here`s CNBC`s Valerie Castro.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VALERIE CASTRO, CNBC REPORTER: Unprecedented cutbacks to water use from the Colorado River will hit Arizona and Nevada the hardest, as drought continues to scorch the southwest. 40 million people across seven states rely on the water. Millions more rely on power it generates through the Hoover Dam at Lake Mead and the Glen Canyon Dam at Lake Powell. But years of using up the reservoir`s supplies are now reaching a critical point.\par \par \uc2 DR. JACK SCHMIDT, PROFESSOR, \uc2 UTAH STATE UNIVERSITY\uc2 : We just kept spending out of the bank account and saying this is great, we can keep spending, but we never rebuilt the bank account. We never rebuilt the balance. And now there`s no alternative but to call for very large cuts and reductions because there`s no more money in the bank account.\par \par \uc2 CASTRO: Jack Schmidt, director of the Center for Colorado River Studies, says the basin states haven`t come to an agreement on their own while Lake Mead levels have reached a new low, triggering the federally mandated cuts.\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: Some of the states have tries to say we think it`s a really important problem and those other guys need to fix it, and stop spending. And other states are saying well, we were willing to cut more but we`ll be damned if we`re going to cut more if no one else is going to cut at all.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS UDALL, AGRIBUSINESS AND WATER COUNCIL OF ARIZONA: Everybody has to tighten their belts in this situation.\par \par \uc2 CASTRO: The new cuts will force Nevada to reduce usage by 8 percent. Arizona will cut back by 21 percent. The impact hitting the state`s farmers already working with less water.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:03]\par \par \uc2 UDALL: They`re trying to do what they can to reduce, still have production, not fellow lands but stay in operation to meet the consumer demand.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That was CNBC`s Valerie Castro.\par \par \uc2 President Biden`s signing of that massive climate and health care package yesterday is about as timely as you can get because it includes $4 billion in drought relief for the rivers and its reservoir.\par \par \uc2 And before we go, there`s some business we have to tend to, monkey business. It all began with a mysterious 911 call to deputies in San Luis Obispo, California, from an unlikely location, the zoo. Well, the sheriff now thinks this small simian named Route was apparently having an otherwise uneventful Saturday night when she decided to monkey around. They posted on \uc2 