ted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:23]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: Trump confidential.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we`re still sifting through the rubble of a political earthquake. One day after documents related to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search on Donald Trump`s Florida estate were on unsealed by the Justice Department. Among those documents, the search warrant and property receipt of what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was, after, both obtained by NBC and confirming an investigation into potential crimes associated with violations of the Espionage Act.\par \par \uc2 After the agency removed nearly two dozen boxes of material, 11 sets of qualified documents, some of them top secret, removed from Trump`s Mar-a- Lago office six days ago. Two months after Trump lawyer signed a letter saying all classified material had been returned to the government. That`s according to new reporting from "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ," citing four sources familiar with the letter.\par \par \uc2 Regardless, over the last week, the response from Republican politicians has ranged from uncritical solidarity with Trump to an increasingly marked silence, as the depth of the implication becomes too extreme to dance around.\par \par \uc2 Of course, that hasn`t stopped many from trying that some Republican lawmakers continue to demonize Federal law enforcement, even after one January 6 insurrectionist has already died this week by targeting \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents in Cincinnati, and across right-wing social media, the language has gotten even darker as Trump supporters call for violence against the Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , the Justice Department, even the \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents who merely did their jobs this week.\par \par \uc2 Drama has overshadowed what would be an unabashed victory lap for President Biden and Democrats, the Inflation Reduction Act passing yesterday in Congress along strict party lines. But with now less than three months before most of America votes in our midterm election, the obvious political question is whose base will be more energized by any of these developments.\par \par \uc2 A massive POLITICSNATION show the night after a historic news week, so let`s get right to it.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Congressman \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2 , Democrat of Maryland and the House Majority Leader.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Hoyer, thanks for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 We are going to get to the --\par \par \uc2 REP. STENY HOYER (D-MD): Glad to be with you.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Thank you, again. We`re going to get to the Trump news in a minute, but first of all, a big win for Democrats. The climate and tax and healthcare bill passed the House yesterday on a party line vote, appearing to cap a slate of victories for Democrats and, of course, President Biden ahead of the midterms, which we`ll get to shortly.\par \par \uc2 But for the American people that are watching this show, what does yesterday`s vote mean to them?\par \par \uc2 HOYER: It means that we`re going to bring their healthcare costs down, prescription costs down. We`re going to make the Affordable Care Act continue to be affordable for those who are struggling and we`re going to also make sure that people pay their fair share of taxes, so those that the lower end, under $400,000.00 don`t pay more because the people at the top are not paying their fair share.\par \par \uc2 In addition to that, we`re going to make sure that insulin costs for those on Medicare are capped at $35.00 and as I said, we`re going to negotiate on drug pricing through the years, so it was a really very significant step and you say, it was a victory for Democrats and for Biden, what it was a victory for is for America and for Americans because it will, as the previous three legs on what I call the table of opportunity and success, which was the American Rescue Plan, which got money in people`s pockets.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:12]\par \par \uc2 HOYER: So they didn`t fall through the floorboards and got kids in school, which everybody thought was necessary and appropriate and that`s what we got. And we got shots in arms, which is why most people in America now aren`t wearing masks because most people are vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 And then we passed the infrastructure bill, we`re going to create hundreds of thousands of jobs, got a deal with broadband, we are going to deal with water pipes that are not toxic for kids and others, we`re going to deal with roads, bridges, and highways, and airports, and seaports, a big bipartisan victory.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, Reverend, as you know, we passed the Chips Bill, which is going to energize our manufacturing site, that is going to put chips back in cars and every electronic device that we use, and we`re going to make it in America, we`re going to make them in America. We`re not going to rely on the unreliable.\par \par \uc2 So those three bills, plus the bill that we just passed, are an extraordinary accomplishment in a Congress that`s evenly divided in the Senate and we only have a four-vote majority in the House.\par \par \uc2 So I`ve served, as you know, Reverend for about 40-plus years, this is one of the most productive congresses for the people that I`ve served in.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And now, this bill is important. The Chips Bill will certainly provide jobs. One of the things, and you know of my Civil Rights works down through the years, you and I have worked together on many issues, and we are those that have been disproportionately, the ones suffering with healthcare needs, climate needs, where we`ve seen a lot of the waste dumps, putting those communities will be disproportionately helped by this because they are the ones that need the impact.\par \par \uc2 So I think, it is really a major, major piece of legislation.\par \par \uc2 Of course, I always want more, but it`s a lot --\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Reverend, it`s the biggest investment in the environment and in climate challenge in the history of the world and it is going to make a real difference as we deal with what we clearly see as the heating of the globe and the heating of America and the challenges that the environment is causing us though climate change and the heating of the Earth.\par \par \uc2 So, it is a big deal. And it is a very important piece of legislation.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As you know, we, in the activist community, we keep pushing for more, but it is a big deal.\par \par \uc2 Now, let`s get into the week that just was, a week that started with the unprecedented \uc2 FBI\uc2  search on Donald Trump`s Mar-a-Lago estate and ended with the revelation that Trump, a former sitting President had documents removed from that estate as part of an investigation into potential violations of the Espionage Act.\par \par \uc2 Now as House Majority Leader, what can you tell us about the conversations Democratic leadership has been having going into the weekend, Congressman, because this is startling even for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We`re talking about possible espionage here.\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Well, Rev, I`m not so sure it`s startling for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  treads very closely to not following the law in so many different respects, and not honoring what is the law, what`s tradition, and what ought to be done.\par \par \uc2 So I`m not sure that it is out of character for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but having said that, I really don`t know a lot more than you know and the public knows from reading the papers, but I will say this, and you talk about the discussions, this is happening. It`s very important. Nobody is again, above the law.\par \par \uc2 And I am astounded by the hypocrisy of Republican supporters of Trump, who were hell bent to put \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in jail, hell bent to have her investigated and applauded all of the work that Comey did, which I think may have cost her the presidency. And then we say this is unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 Trump is not President now. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was not President. He`s not above the law.\par \par \uc2 As you pointed out earlier in your opening statement, the lawyers had said all the papers were turned over. Clearly all the papers were not turned over and some of those papers were top secret.\par \par \uc2 There may have been a violation of the Espionage Act, which is a very, very serious criminal endeavor. But very frankly, one should not be surprised with the President who invited people to come to Washington, who incited them in front of the White House and then deployed them to an insurrectionist action on the Capitol when we were counting the votes for President.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: No, it is interesting to me, we`re out of time, but these are the conservatives who would say to people like me in the Civil Rights community, don`t question \uc2 FBI\uc2 , don`t question the law, blue lives matter and they`re the ones that are now threatening \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents and questioning the authority of the legal or law enforcement officers that they always preached about.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:27]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: It is absolutely 180-degree turn and I had to bring that up.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for being with us, Majority Leader.\par \par \uc2 HOYER: You are right and it`s additional incitement. That`s the sad thing about it, and people are at risk, but thank you for your speaking out.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Thank you for coming on tonight, Majority Leader of the House of Representatives, \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2 , Congressman from Maryland.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is my political panel. Susan del Percio, Republican strategist and Juanita Tolliver, Democratic strategist both are MSNBC contributors.\par \par \uc2 Susan, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has politicized the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s search of his home and made himself out to be a victim. This mobilizes his millions of supporters who thinks this incident was politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 Today, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " reports a Trump lawyer signed a statement in June, stating that all classified material had been returned to the government. This, according to four people with knowledge of the document.\par \par \uc2 If Trump`s lawyers were not totally forthcoming with investigators, it could explain the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s decision to serve a search warrant this week. Meanwhile, Attorney General Garland and the others at the DOJ have the challenge in their investigation of striking the balance between carrying out their legal obligation and being aware of the political implications.\par \par \uc2 What`s your take on how Attorney General Garland has handled this investigation and the inevitable backlash from Republicans?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DEL PERCIO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: I think \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has done an incredibly good job of balancing the needs of holding people accountable and being aware of the political situation, but not playing into it.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of people who are frustrated that \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  did not take action on other things against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to date, especially what we`ve learned from the January 6 Committee, but this man is thorough, he is precise, and he does not pull the trigger unless he thinks he`s got the goods, and that`s what`s important here.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can hem and haw and talk about all of the nonsense he wants. I hope he fired his lawyer if that`s the case, and this lawyer would now, I think would face disbarment. So there`s that. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will have to explain if in fact, he lied to his attorney.\par \par \uc2 But the point is, is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  simply does not care, Rev. You know, when we talk about these classified documents, it`s really important to remember when we`re talking about sources and methods, these are people`s lives. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does not care about anyone, but himself and he is willing to put the men and women who protect this country`s lives on their line, whether it be overseas or at home, whether it be \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents where he basically gave out their names on the warrant, put them in jeopardy, we`ve seen it play out. He just doesn`t care.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Juanita, the latest Trump news unfortunately overshadowed the great week President Biden has had. On Tuesday, Biden signed a $280 billion bipartisan bill to strengthen domestic manufacturing of semiconductors. The following day, a bill expanding healthcare benefits for veterans exposed to toxic burn pits became law. And on Friday, the House passed the Inflation Reduction Act, which the President plans to sign next week.\par \par \uc2 Now considering the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of Trump`s home, riling up Republicans this week and the legislative wins from Democrats, which party has the most enthusiasm going into the midterms and will it last?\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Rev, you know, I`m going to side with Democrats on this one. Even though their stories and headlines were overshadowed this week, you better believe that the President and the White House will be making big news as those bills make it to his desk and he signs them and they will be enumerating for the American public exactly what that means.\par \par \uc2 And House Majority Leader \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  made it explicitly clear: Historic climate investment, critical changes in tax law to make sure that the wealthiest pay their fair share, and lowering drug prices. I think the other thing that will help make it last is going to be the fact that they paint a very clear contrast with Republicans, point out that yes with the, for example Burn Pit Bill, Republicans are voting against critical healthcare for veterans.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:02]\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: With the Marriage Equality Act and the bill that they put on the floor for a Senate vote for that. Republicans voting against ability to marry whomever you love. With this Inflation Reduction Act, Republicans voting against capping insulin costs for seniors at $35.00.\par \par \uc2 Going point by point, I think is going to be critical for Democrats to keep this energy going because what they have here is energy that`s not only going to be like fully well-received by Democrats, but also Independents. Because when you talk about Republicans trying to paint Trump as a victim, for example, we know the cap on that is only with the people who already support Trump.\par \par \uc2 I think I saw a recent poll that showed that only 33 percent of voters said that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search at Mar-a-Lago was unjustified. Whereas you have the vast majority of the rest of the voting population saying it was justified, including 47 percent of Independents.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Democrats and Independents, overwhelmingly.\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: That`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Said it was justified.\par \par \uc2 Susan, let`s turn now to more legal trouble for the former President. "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reports a Manhattan State Court Judge on Friday declined to throw out the criminal case against Donald J. Trump`s family business and its longtime Chief Financial Officer.\par \par \uc2 Now this comes after Trump invoked the Fifth Amendment right against self- incrimination, Wednesday. He did it nearly 450 times. What`s your reaction to Trump refusing to talk?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: Well, I thought \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said that if you take the Fifth, you must be guilty of something and only monsters take the Fifth. So I`m not sure where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  exactly stands. But you know, the Manhattan criminal case is something really interesting, it was deep in that article.\par \par \uc2 Guess what, Rev? Jury selection for that case starts October 24. So it`s just days before the midterm elections, and I think it`s something that everyone, you know, when you look at the calendar, it really should wake everyone up that that trial is going to start with jury selections in New York against Donald Trump`s organization, that may be a motivating factor in itself to get Democrats out.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, that might be the October surprise or the fact that maybe we`re beyond being surprised with Trump World.\par \par \uc2 Juanita, amid Trump`s other State and Federal investigations, he has hired a prominent Atlanta criminal defense attorney known for defending rappers to represent him to the special grand jury that`s investigating whether he illegally tried to interfere with the 2020 election in Georgia. What do you make of this case? And we are out of time, so respond quickly, please.\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: Look, it just shows that Trump is scared. It shows that he feels the heat, especially when he`s on tape requesting that the Secretary of State finds votes for him. It shows that he is scared especially also when you have Giuliani and \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  also set to testify here. He knows that he is next and so he`s building his team to prepare for that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Juanita Tolliver and Susan del Percio, thank you very much to both of you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, from backing the blue to bashing the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Why the GOP`s attacks on law enforcement this week should come as no surprise.\par \par \uc2 And later, former White House senior official \uc2 Omarosa Manigault Newman\uc2  joins me to share her experiences with how the Trump White House handled sensitive information.\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague Richard Lui with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s \uc2 Defense Intelligence Agency\uc2  warning of new provocations at the Russian occupied nuclear plant in southwestern Ukraine. Ukraine and Russia are blaming each other for the shelling of Europe`s largest nuclear plant. Each side saying there were 10 hits on the facility Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The suspect who stabbed acclaimed novelist Salman Rushdie yesterday was charged with attempted murder and assault. New York State Police say today that Rushdie is 75. He is currently in critical condition.\par \par \uc2 According to his agent, Rushdie is on a ventilator, cannot speak and is likely to lose an eye. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said in the statement released this evening that he and the First Lady are "praying" for his health and recovery.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Los Angeles Police Department\uc2  is no longer investigating the car crash that left actress Anne Heche legally brain dead. Previously police were looking into whether Heche was driving under the influence after drugs were reportedly found in her system. Heche crashed her car into a Los Angeles home a week ago and suffered a brain injury. She was 53 years old.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK) [17:24:01]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: This week, Republicans once again reminded us what they really mean when they say they are the party of law and order.\par \par \uc2 When the \uc2 FBI\uc2  served a search warrant on former President Trump`s Mar-a-Lago club, the GOP didn`t rise up to back to blue and urged the former President to comply respectfully with their request. Instead, many spent the early part of this week insinuating that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was conducting a corrupt operation to try to frame Trump. Here is what a few of them had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA BOBB, DONALD TRUMP LAWYER: No, there is no security that something wasn`t planted. I`m not I`m not saying that that`s