w Republicans obsessed over Hillary Clinton`s handling of e-mails while she was Secretary of State. And this week, former President Trump himself even tried to insinuate former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  somehow mishandled documents, although there is no evidence whatsoever to support the claim.\par \par \uc2 And when it comes to ordinary Americans, no form of justice is too rough. When Trump spoke at CPAC last weekend, he drew thunderous applause when he spoke about swift trials for drug dealers, ending in executions, and rounding up the homeless to be placed in encampments.\par \par \uc2 By rallying around Trump this week before all the facts were revealed, many Republicans this week reminded us yet again, they want a country where justice is far from blind. It is a world some of their most extreme supporters dream of, but it would be a nightmare for the rest of us.\par \par \uc2 I got-cha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:31:56]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 As the fallout over Mar-a-Lago`s search and its revelations continue to grow, what a former Trump White House aides thinking? Are they surprised that the home of their former boss was searched by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  as part of an investigation into possible espionage violation? Or does it remind them of just another day at the office?\par \par \uc2 As always, I`ve got the perfect guest for those questions. Joining me now is \uc2 Omarosa Manigault Newman\uc2 , former senior White House adviser, White House official to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and author of "Unhinged: An Insider`s Account of the Trump White House."\par \par \uc2 So Omarosa, always great to see you and thanks for joining us. And you might be one of the most perfect and certainly timely guests we could have tonight after this past week. You know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  well. You worked around him as a candidate and then in the White House.\par \par \uc2 What is your gut reaction to the last six days from the Mar-a-Lago search to yesterday`s unsealing of the search warrant?\par \par \uc2 OMAROSA MANIGAULT NEWMAN\uc2 , FORMER SENIOR WHITE HOUSE ADVISER: Rev. Thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 I have to tell you that of all of the legal woes that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is facing, this might be the one that is most dangerous for the United States. It`s not just concerning that he had these documents. My big question is what did he do with these documents? Who did he show these documents to? Who has he shared them with? And has he compromised our national security?\par \par \uc2 Certainly \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had no respect of the fact that we have to preserve these records for the Presidential Records Act; throughout my time in the White House and trying to brief him was impossible because he would tear these things up and throw them in the trash, but never did I imagine that he would take documents intended for only to be shown in a SCIF in these secure compartmentalized places and take them to Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 This is really, really dangerous for our country, Rev.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And you know, when you say that, my mind goes back to I remember, not long after he had been sworn into office, we saw photos of him showing some documents on his desk in the Oval Office to Lavrov, one of the heads in the Soviet Union. I mean, it was outrageous.\par \par \uc2 And if he would do that in the Oval Office, you have to wonder why he brought these documents to Mar-a-Lago and who he may have shown them to?\par \par \uc2 NEWMAN: Well, I can tell you that Donald like to keep little whatnots and notes and letters. He would keep a box in fact. Anytime you flew, whether you were on Trump Force or Air Force One.\par \par \uc2 Anytime I flew on Air Force One with him, the seat next to him contained a box of things that he wanted to go through, that he wants to look at, and some of those documents had to be handled very, very carefully, because you can tell a sensitive document, Rev.\par \par \uc2 I`ve worked in the White House twice. I`ve worked for two US Presidents. I`ve worked for the Vice President. I`ve worked for three US agencies. I have never once accidentally taken a classified or sensitive document home.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:09]\par \par \uc2 NEWMAN: And I can tell you that there are very few homes in the United States that have any type of classified document.\par \par \uc2 Donald just really has no respect for the boundaries, for the limitation and for the systems put in place to protect you and I, to protect our country, and to make sure that these documents don`t fall in the wrong hands.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, we know that several months ago, they turned over 15 boxes and we are told now that there was a letter signed by his lawyers in June saying that they had given everything up. Now, we hear all of what`s going on with these new boxes and new pieces of classified and some very serious documents.\par \par \uc2 Why would they withhold them? What would be the reason?\par \par \uc2 You worked in that environment? Would they have just been that careless? Or do you think, given the environment that you can`t absolutely testify, but given the environment you worked in, is this something that only Trump could have instructed to be done? Particularly when a lawyer said, you all have everything we got months ago?\par \par \uc2 NEWMAN: Oh, boy, yes, Rev. You`ve known -- you`ve known \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  probably longer than I and you know that he thinks that he is smarter than everyone in the room. He probably thought he could outsmart the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and he probably even lied to his attorneys, it would not surprise me.\par \par \uc2 But what`s abundantly clear, is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had an opportunity in earlier part of this year to hand over everything that he had at Mar-a-Lago to authorities, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and/or his team made the decision not to hand those documents.\par \par \uc2 The