u know him better than I do.\par \par \uc2 NEWMAN: Well he was fundraising after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid. He sent out a fundraising e-mail, so we kind of know what it is.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Well, Omarosa, thank you for being with us tonight. \uc2 Omarosa Manigault Newman\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 After the break, I`ll talk to a Florida Democrat seeking to take on Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:42:35]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 The midterm elections are just 87 days away and voters around the country are preparing to cast their ballots for November. One of those battleground states is Florida.\par \par \uc2 Incumbent Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is looking to serve a second term and primary elections are set for later this month, August 23rd to be exact.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is one of the Democrats seeking for the nomination for Governor, \uc2 Nikki Fried\uc2  who currently serves as Florida`s Commissioner of Agriculture. Thank you for joining me, Commissioner Fried.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI FRIED\uc2  (D), CANDIDATE FOR GOVERNOR OF FLORIDA: Thank you for having me this morning, Reverend.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me ask you, this week, Governor DeSantis was quick to defend former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as he decried the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s raid of the former President`s Palm Beach home in Mar-a-Lago calling it "weaponization of Federal agencies." What is your reaction?\par \par \uc2 FRIED: You know what, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  did was he escalated the situation by calling it weaponization. We should know that this was a calculated risk that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  did, knowing that if he showed support for this individual, for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that it was only going to gain favoritism to that base.\par \par \uc2 But unfortunately, he is just escalating the situation. And the irony is just the week before, he removed a duly elected State Attorney in Hillsborough County, weaponizing his department, his agency, his overhead, and unfortunately, this is something that`s very dangerous to our democracy.\par \par \uc2 And so the fact of the matter is that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is a great threat to our state, a great threat to our nation, because we know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the greatest threat to our national security. And the fact that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is escalating the situation and showing support, we have been very strong in opposition to that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, let me go to this. On Friday, the House passed the Inflation Reduction Act, a sweeping Democratic bill aimed to combat climate change and extend healthcare coverage, which is now ready for President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to sign off with his signature.\par \par \uc2 Our analysts have called this bill the most ambitious legislation passed on climate in the nation`s history, as it could cut US emissions by 40 percent by the end of the decade.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:04]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Last Tuesday, experts at the \uc2 National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration\uc2  released their forecasts for high tide flooding in the US and stated that Southwest Florida is expected to see daily floods by 2100. Other experts have also previously warned that due to rising sea levels caused by climate change, highly populated Floridian counties such as Miami-Dade could be flooded by three feet by the year 2080.\par \par \uc2 The climate emergency well and already has caused human displacement and damage to infrastructure. How do you plan on addressing these issues in your home state to prepare for these crises that are inevitably in Floridians future?\par \par \uc2 FRIED: It`s already happening here. Florida is ground zero for the climate crisis. It is something that I`ve been working on for three-and-a-half years as Commissioner of Agriculture. We are seeing hotter summers, we are seeing wetter winters and making sure that, you know, we`re about to have the 30-year anniversary of Hurricane Andrew.\par \par \uc2 I was born and raised in Miami, and we all thought that was going to be a once in a lifetime storm. And unfortunately, we`re seeing this happening, you know, these huge storms almost every single year, threatening the coastal United States.\par \par \uc2 So these are important issues that we`re seeing all across our state, that every time that it rains, not even just these massive rainstorms, but every time it rains, you`re seeing flooding. And unfortunately, you`re seeing a lot of this flooding happening in minority communities where they haven`t had the money and the resources.\par \par \uc2 So we have to make sure that we`re working with the State government, with our local government, and of course, the Federal government to be taking these resources and putting them into the communities that are going to need it the most, that we are ready for this influx of is climate change.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve got to make sure that this is a climate rescue, that we`re rescuing our climate with this landmark legislation, landmark dollars, and making sure that we are utilizing these resources to make sure that we`re preparing for the future.\par \par \uc2 The crisis is already here in the State of Florida. And unfortunately, we`ve had three decades of Republican control of our state that haven`t been making this as a priority.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, last Thursday, I have to ask you this, last Thursday, Governor DeSantis suspended the top prosecutor in Tampa, Andrew Warren, accusing him of incompetence and neglect of duty for vowing not to prosecute those who seek or provide abortions.\par \par \uc2 Warren has since vowed to fight his suspension, and also it has accused DeSantis of trying to "overthrow" democracy in Florida by suspending him. Now, this has come after DeSantis signed a new law back in April, which took effect at the start of July that bans abortions in Florida after just 15 weeks of pregnancy, and does not make exceptions for cases of incest, rape, or human trafficking.\par \par \uc2 What is your response to this? And also, how do you plan on protecting abortion rights in Florida?\par \par \uc2 FRIED: First of all, this is a complete overreach of government, and again, the hypocrisy that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , when after the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , less than five days that when he had his own law enforcement and local law enforcement come in and physically remove a duly elected prosecutor that the people elected to protect them in those communities. This is atrocious, and this is one of our greatest threats of democracy is \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And instead of letting the people of that county make that decision, he came in and removed a prosecutor for making a decision which he hasn`t even done yet. And so this isn`t even about the abortion ban here in the State of Florida. This is about democracy. And so what I`ve said on first of all day one, when I am governor of our state, we`re declaring a state of emergency for women`s reproductive healthcare.\par \par \uc2 I`ve already been working with attorneys and local organizations on the ground here in the State of Florida, to make sure that we have that right to privacy for abortions here in the State of Florida, and put it back onto the Constitution. It is already there as a right to privacy. But unfortunately, my guess, if I had to bet that this very conservative Supreme Court of Florida is going to uphold this 15-week abortion ban and take away mine and millions and millions of women`s rights in the State of Florida.\par \par \uc2 So we need to have a new constitutional amendment that I have no doubt that the people here in the State of Florida are going to support, but unfortunately \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  doesn`t care about the people on the ground. He doesn`t care about democracy.\par \par \uc2 All he cares about is supporting an insurrectionist and supporting his bid to become President of the United States in 2024, but we`re going to stop him here in the State of Florida. We`re going to need all hands on deck here in the State, but I have absolute guarantee that we are going to make it happen in November.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: \uc2 Nikki Fried\uc2 , thank you for being with us this evening.\par \par \uc2 After the break, my final thoughts. Plus, this week`s headlines including a story of justice denied despite decades of work.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:54:20]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Lastly tonight, I want to highlight a few more stories that you may have missed this week.\par \par \uc2 More than six decades after the murder of Emmett Till, a Mississippi grand jury has declined to indict a White woman whose false accusations allegedly triggered his lynching.\par \par \uc2 The jury determined that there was insufficient evidence to prosecute Carolyn Bryant Donham on charges of manslaughter and kidnapping, which will make her very unlikely to ever be charged.\par \par \uc2 Three White men have been given additional sentences on federal hate crime charges after the murder of Ahmaud Arbery. Father and son, Gregory and Travis McMichael were given life sentences, while their neighbor William Bryan has received 35 years.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:15]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And finally, I went to Atlanta on Thursday in my capacity as head of the National Action Network to give the eulogy of Brianna Grier, a young Black woman who died from her injuries as a result of falling out of a police car while in custody. They had handcuffed her, brought her to the police cruiser because her parents said she was having a mental health episode. Once you`re in custody, you`re responsible for them.\par \par \uc2 How does she fall out of a moving vehicle? Was she seat-belted? Why wasn`t the door locked? We demand justice.\par \par \uc2 We were joined at the funeral services by the mother of another Breonna -- Breonna Taylor, who sought Federal charges on those officers that led to Breonna Taylor`s death two years ago.\par \par \uc2 We keep fighting and we still will keep fighting until justice is something that we are not just aspiring toward, but is expected.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 p.m. Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 "American Voices" with Alicia Menendez starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [16:00:09]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220814ei8d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for August 10, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 7,595 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: We will follow the facts wherever they lead.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  gets tonight`s "LAST WORD", the love and power with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, the growing legal challenges for the former president, we spent hours taking the Fifth in one investigation. Just days after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search at Mar-a-Lago for another. Then as Republicans rally round Trump, Democrats celebrate some big wins. We`ll fact check some of the fiction coming from the right.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the brutal war on Ukraine now nearly six months old Ukrainians pushed back while fighting intensifies near a nuclear plant. A former four star general is standing by as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. Tonight brand new reporting about what led the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to execute a search warrant Monday at Trump`s Florida home. The Wall Street Journal reporting that the feds may have gotten a tip off from an insider after officials visited Mar-a-Lago back in June to ask about government documents possibly stored there. The Journal reports this, in the following weeks, however, someone familiar with the stored papers told investigators there may still be more classified documents at the private club. After the National Archives Retrieved 15 boxes earlier in the year people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 NBC News has not independently confirmed this reporting. So far, the Justice Department has not commented on the search either. But Trump and his supporters are ramping up their baseless attacks, even suggesting that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  could be planting incriminating evidence.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAND PAUL, (R) KENTUCKY: Do I know that the boxes of material they took from Mar-a-Lago that they won`t put things in those boxes to entrap him? How do we know --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: His lawyer said they brought in backpacks, what was in those backpacks, was -- did they bring those in to fill them up? Or did they have something in there?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m concerned that they may have planted something, you know, at this point who knows?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s just stop there. Because the truth matters, but only if you hear it and millions of people are watching that. And there is absolutely no evidence that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  planted anything.\par \par \uc2 What \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is facing is legal scrutiny like no other president before him from both federal and state officials. And today in a separate investigation, he finally showed up at the offices of New York`s Attorney General Letitia James for a deposition in her investigation into his business practices. A source tells NBC that over the course of four hours, Trump took the Fifth more than 440 times. His own lawyer tells NBC News he did answer one question, his name. He added that Trump also read a statement in which he accused the Attorney General of trying to destroy him. Trump attacked her earlier today on his social media site. And over the weekend during his CPAC speech down in Texas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) FORMER UNITED STATES PRESIDENT: I got a racist Attorney General in New York that`s been after me for years. She campaigned on the fact I will get \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says nothing about me, I will get, terrible people is a terrible people. I built a great company.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Late today Attorney General James issued a statement of our own saying the investigation will continue. With that, let`s continue this hour and get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel, Jackie Alemany, who has been scooping up story after story this week, Congressional Investigations reporter for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  and an MSNBC Contributor, Barbara McQuade joins us, a veteran, federal prosecutor and former U.S. Attorney for the Eastern District of Michigan. She worked with the Department of Justice during the Biden transition, and is now a professor at the University of Michigan School of Law. And \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2  is here, Department of Justice veteran and former Acting Solicitor General during the Obama administration. He has argued dozens of cases before the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Barb, I want to start with you. Here is what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  the former guy has said previously about those who take the Fifth.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Fifth Amendment, Fifth Amendment, Fifth Amendment, horrible. When you have your staff taking the Fifth Amendment, taking the Fifth, so they`re not prosecuted. I think it`s disgraceful. You see the mob takes the Fifth. If you`re innocent, why are you taking the Fifth Amendment?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But today, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wrote this. I once asked if you`re innocent, why are you taking the Fifth Amendment? Now I know the answer to that question. He says, it`s to protect his own interests. Barb, is that how it works?\par \par \uc2 [23:05:04]\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Yeah, you know when you have no shame and you don`t care about hypocrisy it`s very easy to shift your position like this.\par \par \uc2 I think the truth is, that all of us have a Fifth Amendment right not to incriminate ourselves. Our system is an adversarial system, not an inquisitorial system. And we have that absolute right to do that. I think where he was out of bounds was criticizing people who invoke their Fifth Amendment right not to testify.\par \par \uc2 I expect that Leticia James fully expected him to invoke his Fifth Amendment right. Today was really his opportunity to tell his side of the story. If there`s an innocent explanation for inflating your assets on one occasion and deflating those same assets on another, let`s hear it. You got a good reason. Let`s hear it. That was his opportunity today. Instead, he chose to stay silent because he believed that truthful answers would incriminate him.\par \par \uc2 The other interesting thing about a civil case is that unlike a criminal case, a fact finder in the civil case is allowed to know that a person refused to answer questions, and may even draw an adverse inference from that fact.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: To that very point, we heard from former prosecutor Andrew Weissmann, about what Trump said or didn`t say and where the case goes from here. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, FORMER SENIOR MEMBER OF MUELLER PROBE: In a civil case, once the defendant who you`re thinking of charging is taking the Fifth Amendment, you have an adverse inference. So if you`ve got some evidence, you know, all you have to do is prove the case by a preponderance. And once the jury is going to be told that there`s an adverse inference against the other side, your case is pretty well done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Do you agree with that, Neal, the case is done. So now, Tish James can present this. Here`s my evidence, Trump said nothing. Here`s my evidence, Trump said nothing, and if so, that`s a bad look.\par \par \uc2 NEAL KATYAL, FORMER ACTING U.S. SOLICITOR GENERAL: Exactly. Andrew is exactly right. That`s what will happen in the civil case. So Trump is basically thinking his civil case. Now, why is he doing it? Because he`s worried about the criminal allegations here. This is the Fifth Amendment privilege against self-incrimination. That`s about criminal stuff. He`s worried about going to jail for these offenses, and there is no adverse inference there.\par \par \uc2 Now, zooming out, Steph, I think the big issue here, you know, we saw the president`s former -- former president`s home raided on Monday, first president ever in American history to have that. Today, I think we have again, another historic thing. I`m not aware of any other former president who`s ever taken the Fifth Amendment before, our 45th President has now taken it over 420 times today. And you have to ask why. And to me, it`s because he and his lawyers, who are his lawyers, right, basically able to convince him of the herculean task of shutting up for four hours, something \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has, like never done before. And the reason is, because his attorneys and ultimately Trump knew if you answer these questions, you`d have something damaging to say, and yes, it really does seem like right now, Trump is linked to a laundry list of crimes. And that`s what the Republicans are saying that the reason why he`s linked good is because he played fast and loose with the law so much. And so now the law is catching up with them in a really powerful way, both on Mondays events, and then today`s.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That is a good point, knowing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  the way we do, he could not have liked sitting for a total of four hours today.\par \par \uc2 Jackie, let`s turn to Scott Perry, a longtime Republican ally of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We know he got his phone seized yesterday. His lawyer says he`s been told he, Scott Perry is not a target. If he`s not what are they looking for?\par \par \uc2 JACKIE ALEMANY, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, Stephanie. I think everything that we`re hearing from lawyers representing people who are under investigation right now are to be taken with a grain of salt. Scott Perry is, after all, someone who has repeatedly refused to comply with the January 6 Select Committee investigating the insurrection, when there`s been a mountain of evidence, showing his direct involvement in some of the efforts to overturn the election for over two years now. And it was first in the Senate Judiciary report that actually outlined his involvement in efforts specifically at the Department of Justice to overturn the election results. He was the person that made the introduction between \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Jeffrey Clark, who ultimately did the president`s bidding at the Department of Justice. And he really was the sort of connective tissue between members of Congress and the White House and the coordination that occurred in order to try to find some ways to support the former president schemes.\par \par \uc2 But it is quite possible that Perry is ultimately not the target, but regardless, they do feel that -- investigators feel that there is evidence that he could have. We do know he was in frequent touch with Jeffrey Clark. He was in touch as well with people in the West Wing especially around that time period. And we`ve already seen some of his text messages come out as a result of \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  handing over 1000s of his messages at the beginning of the January 6 investigation. So there`s certainly a litany of things that investigators are going to want to check out even if Perry himself is not the actual target.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:23]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , who we talk a lot about, who we hear from very rarely. Neal, there are some reports today, we`ve been asking for days, how did the government know why did they think there was more classified documents that they had not yet seen? And now, there`s reports that someone in Trump`s circle may have tipped them off? Does that seem likely to you?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Yeah, I mean, Stephanie, then about an hour of the news of the search breaking, I think goes on our network, basically saying, I think the government has someone on the inside. And the reason for that is I said, it just didn`t strike me as plausible the government risks this kind of massive search with all of the attention and, you know, certainly all the backlash that Trump and his minions are going to say, unless they knew there was something there. And it does look like there`s a serious violation, not just the stealing of government property, which is itself a crime, but classified information.\par \par \uc2 And it`s important to remember \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does not have a security clearance the way that normally presidents do, because President Biden determined you couldn`t trust \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with the nation`s secrets. So if Trump does have classified information, he`s totally unauthorized and not able to possess it, even if he`s miss -- you know, even if he everyone acknowledges, you know, you`re safe or something like that is not the way to store classified information. But he may not be able to maintain any sort of classified information, even if he put it in the right safes and the like. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the guy who just a few years ago signed into law, a bill that made it a felony to do the kinds of stuff that he`s doing. And so, you know, it`s not surprising to me that the government had someone on the inside and acted accordingly.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Barb, NBC`s reporting that some people within the Department of Justice want \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  to explain the Mar-a-Lago search. Does that surprise you?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Not really. But it`s a difficult call. And I`d be surprised if \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  does that, you know, he has said repeatedly that the Justice Department has certain norms. They`re designed to protect the accused from being disparaged in the press, as \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was by \uc2 James Comey\uc2  several years ago. And that it`s even more important to adhere to those norms during challenging times. And so for that reason, I`d be surprised if he does, but I can see why they might be advocating for it.\par \par \uc2 You know, you can say a lot of things, I think, by way of explaining, without disclosing details about the investigation itself, you know, just for example, how a search warrant works, the authority necessary to get it, the kinds of things that might prompt the seeking of a search warrant, the significance of classified information or the definition, this isn`t just like, you know, a laundry list that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  held on to classified information is defined as information to the disclosure of which would cause exceptionally grave damage to the national security of the United States.\par \par \uc2 And so it may be that there`s room a middle ground for him to, as he`s done before, with updates on the January 6 investigation, to just sort of explain big picture about why the Justice Department does certain things and how it works, just to assure the public that this is on the up and up.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, here`s what I want to explain Jackie, right wing media, and lawmakers are on every possible social outlet, attacking the DOJ and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  for the search. But is anyone defending or explaining why in the world \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had all these documents to begin with?\par \par \uc2 ALEMANY: No, Stephanie, they`re not. And also everything that we have reported so far, even with these lingering questions left, really undercut all of the unsubstantiated false claims being put forward right now by conservative media and people trying to claim that this is the \uc2 FBI\uc2  trying to, you know, weaponize this this search and trying to politically damage the former president.\par \par \uc2 This is a former president who has never adhered to the presidential records acts. Throughout his four years in office, we have documented and reported on that extensively. This is someone who has also already fessed up to taking back inappropriately 15 boxes of documents, that some of which were ultimately classified documents to Mar-a-Lago, which had already been retrieved that was -- and that was even before these new 12 boxes were retrieved by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and, again, I think that in the coming days we`re going to see new information come out that supports why the \uc2 FBI\uc2  ultimately decided to execute this search warrant.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:17]\par \par \uc2 And while the Post hasn`t independently confirmed that there was an aide to the former president who flipped essentially and provided this information, there is something that had to have been aggravating the situation in order for the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , to take these measures and decide, okay, we`re going to make the calculus of going right in there and getting it ourselves. Again, if we even just look at the data points that we already know of, in those 15 boxes, that the National Archives already retrieved. There was 100 page inventory that we got to read out of the unclassified items, and a three page inventory of the classified items. And even in those classified items, if there`s just a sentence or a paragraph or a small portion of top secret information, which we`ve been told, is actually contained in some of these items that requires special handling, that can do grave harm national security at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And note, it is not the overwhelming majority of Republican lawmakers out there defending Trump. It is the right wing regulars, and they`re doing it loudly. Neal, quickly before we go, what is your take? We are seeing from the right wing media, these suggestions over and over, that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  may have planted evidence at Mar-a-Lago on any of our reporting. There`s absolutely no evidence of that. What do you think because it is damaging to tell that to the American people? It`s like the big lie part two.\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: 100%, Stephanie, I think the legal term for that is poppycock. And it`s not just, you know, Republicans in general, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself, went on his social media site and suggested it as well about the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and who put the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director in place, that guy named \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Chris Wray\uc2  is his appointee. He put him there. And the whole evidence planting narrative to me, smacks of desperation. The fact that these Republicans are resorting to these lies about the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , instead of defending Trump, we`re doing exactly what you said, Steph, explaining why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has all these documents in his possession. They can`t do that. So they`re shifting the narrative. And the last thing I`d say is, you know, give me a break.\par \par \uc2 Now, these folks want to say, oh, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , law enforcement plans, evidence, you know, there are 1000s of people prosecuted, you know, day in and day out, and there`s all sorts of legitimate complaints against law enforcement against the poor and powerless. You know, if you`re rich like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , no, I don`t think there`s a chance in the world that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is planting evidence is, you know, Looney Tunes, but it does happen and other low level settings. And, you know, that`s a real thing that Americans should be thinking about.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: They should be thinking about it, but for any news organization, suggesting it or reporting it, there are no facts supporting it. Jackie Alemany, Barb McQuade Neal Katyal, thank you so much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, President Biden signs another important bill into law as the House prepares to vote on a sweeping climate and health care bill. Why all matters for the American people now.\par \par \uc2 And later, historian Jon Meacham is here on what has already been a very busy week of big news involving the former president. And it`s only hump day. His take on the state of the country tonight, THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a busy Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:23:18]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: This is the most significant law our nation has ever passed to help millions of veterans who are exposed to toxic substances during the military services. You know Secretary McDonough can tell you I was going to get this done come hell or high water.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: President Biden signed the PACT Act into law earlier today. It is just the latest in a string of wins for the President, including a recent bipartisan deal on guns and competitiveness with China.\par \par \uc2 Later, this week, the House is expected to pass the Inflation Reduction Act which would represent huge investments in climate and health care. With us tonight to discuss our own Symone Sanders-Townsend, former Communications Director for Vice President Harris and hosts of the show Symone right here on Peacock, and Tim Miller Contributor to the Bulwark and former Communications Director for Jeb Bush. He wrote the new book, "Why We Did It."\par \par \uc2 Symone, long that you worked in this White House this week should be dominated by positive White House headlines because of all of these legislative wins. Is it getting overshadowed by the Trump headlines even though those are bad for Trump? Biden`s not getting the headlines?\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS-TOWNSEND, FORMER SENIOR ADVISOR & CHIEF SPOKESPERSON FOR VP HARRIS: You know, Stephanie, I think that the White House is doing everything they can to push through. The day that the President signed. He also signed the CHIPS Act this week, right? He got a whole big thing outside on the South Lawn. And as it was starting, it wasn`t anywhere on television. None of the network`s covered it MSNBC covered it once the President started speaking but you heard very little mention of it in the lead up to it. The Chips Act is huge, Ok. The semiconductors everything from the cars to the toasters. They all need a chip, the cell phone, the laptops so a very big deal but you`re not hearing about it necessarily on the front pages if you will when you turn on the television because so many things have been happening.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:10]\par \par \uc2 So the White House has to get creative. It`s just not on them, though Democrats across the board. This is something folks are at home in district this week, the Senate, the House, they should be talking about this everywhere they can on the radios on the airwaves, because it is something to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Does this cripple though, Tim, Republicans and their communication strategy right, weeks and weeks ago, it was all about supply chain shortages and blaming the president inflation and blaming the president, and now Republicans across the board have to go to their home districts and say, yes, I did not sign something literally called the Inflation Reduction Act, and they haven`t offered any alternative.\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, FORMER COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR TO JEB BUSH: Yeah, I just feel like the Republicans settled on their midterm message in spring, and then a bunch of new stuff happened in summer, you know, these campaigns are long, you have to be nimble. And I think that they`ve struggled to be honest to settle on a message to put back and Biden in addition to what you just laid out, I think the overarching message was that this White House was feckless. You know that they were not competent, and that you have all these problems and that Biden isn`t capable of solving them.\par \par \uc2 Well, now, Biden and this administration, working with Congress have directly addressed a lot of the key problems that people have, including inflation. So I think that if you watch Fox and saw Republican messaging for about a week, there was a lot of flailing. And I think over the past two days, you`ve seen one rallying to the president, former president`s defense, and I don`t know how much that`s going to help them the midterms. But that`s something that united on. And I do think on the Inflation Reduction Act, they`re going to zero in on these 87,000 new \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents. And that might be, you know, an area where they have a little bit of a message that might work. But I think that the Biden administration and the Democrats in Congress are in much better position having something to talk about going home it through August recess than they were a month ago.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Symone, back to that, you know, weeks ago, when people would say, listen, voting rights are hugely, hugely important. But it`s tough to connect them with the American people. They`re not obscure, but they feel that way. Americans care about kitchen table issues. And if you think about all the things Biden has done over the last few weeks, it`s all about kitchen table issues, shortages, gun control, inflation. These are the things people talk about every single night, the fact that he`s now taking this on, can this change things come the midterms? Which last month, we thought we`re going to be shaking for Democrats?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: I mean, I absolutely think it can, Stephanie. And we are seeing the results in primary elections, right? Wisconsin, and Minnesota just yesterday, on Tuesday, we saw Kansas the special -- the primary were the ballot initiative to keep abortion in the Constitution, passed or did not pass, it was defeated because of activists and organizers. The Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee is out with some new research basically, that basically says they see a fighting chance for themselves in the midterm.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think that this idea between kitchen table issues or social issues, as some people like to say, is a bunch of malarkey. Because the reality is if you`re a woman in this country, and you live in a state right now, where abortion is now illegal, or you can`t get it after six weeks, that is a kitchen table issue for you if the health care you need is an abortion, right? If your child -- if the people in Uvalde, Texas, gun safety measures, people all across this country gun safety measure is a kitchen table issue.\par \par \uc2 So I do think that the President has put his money where his mouth is he has -- you know, I worked on the campaign, he has delivered on a number of pieces of the agenda that he said he was going to do. The question, Stephanie is out of the people sell it. And I think you can expect my Democratic friends to be out in full force come September, because let`s be honest people on vacation, trying to sell this agenda and connect with the folks across the country telling them exactly what this means for them.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Tim, the Roe reversal, that sort of Republicans put their money where their mouth was, they talked about it forever and ever and ever. And now they got it. It hurt them in Kansas, it`s starting to hurt them in polls across the country, is that what you`re seeing?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: I think so I think it remains to be seen about whether it`s going to yield a Kansas like reaction in house districts across the country. Here`s where it`s definitely going to make a difference and where the Democrats really needed presser advantage in these key swing states where the governor`s race is up, or the governorship is up. Wisconsin, Pennsylvania, Arizona, it`s really important for the 2024 election security who the governor is there. And in all three of those states the Republicans have taken radical anti-abortion views like, you know, from the moment of conception, no exceptions, these things that have like only 10% support and either you even Republicans don`t support this as we saw in Kansas.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:02]\par \par \uc2 I think that if Democrats can look at Arizona, Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, in particular and say if these folks become the governor our democracy is at risk, your ability to get an abortion, even at one week is at risk, a rapes, 10-year-old child`s ability to get an abortion is at risk, that is going to resonate in these purple states, and I think is a big opportunity for Democrats. We`ll see if it also I think, potentially could be an opportunity to other way -- in other places, but those governor`s races would I`ve looked at.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You have got some far, far right candidates running and a whole bunch of states. We are paying attention, Symone Sanders, host of Symone right here on MSNBC and Peacock and Tim Miller, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Ukraine on offense. General Barry McCaffrey is here on the significance of Ukraine`s latest attack, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:35:26]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It`s been more than a minute since we`ve had the chance to report on the now 167 day long conflict in Ukraine, and it matters, why? Because there has been no end to the destruction. For example, Ukraine`s military says nine Russian planes were just destroyed and an airbase in Crimea, which of course has been under Russian control since 2014. Russia has denied the damage or that the attack even happened at all.\par \par \uc2 With us tonight to discuss, one of the best experts. Retired four star \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  General Barry McCaffrey, a decorated combat veteran of Vietnam, and a former battlefield commander in the Persian Gulf.\par \par \uc2 General, every time I read that introduction, I`m reminded how lucky we are to have you here. Help us understand this, this escalation how big of a deal is it? Because there`s reporting it was carried out by Ukraine Special Forces?\par \par \uc2 GEN. BARRY MCCAFFREY, \uc2 U.S. ARMY\uc2  (RET.): Yeah, well, we don`t know exactly what the weapon delivery system was. It seems unlikely it was Ukrainian aircraft or cruise missile, you can spot those in the air. It will be on a range of U.S. supplied HIMARS rockets. It was multiple explosions. Even given secondary effects. It looked to me like a series of sabotage initiated events. And with aviation bombs, rockets fuel in the area, probably improperly stored to boot. It was a devastating strike, and it was in Crimea. And that`s not too far from the heartbeat of the Russian Navy in Sebastopol. So I think it was a significant event. Ukrainians are being pretty quiet about it. They`re leaking information, but they`re not doing a victory dance. So they understand the political sensitivity of all of this.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I want to turn to the fighting around Ukraine`s nuclear facilities. What`s the latest? And how dangerous is that situation?\par \par \uc2 MCCAFFREY: Well, it`s a level of irresponsibility on the part of the Russians that, to be honest, surprises me. They did go -- they understand the power of nuclear radiation. And the lingering effects of wind blowing fissile material back into Russia would be devastating to them also. So it`s completely irresponsible. The level of ignorance of Russian tactical units earlier in the war, up north, north of Kyiv on another power plant was simply abysmal. They were digging in and heavily contaminated Chernobyl radiation areas. So I don`t understand what they`re thinking. I think they have bad control of their tactical units. This makes no sense. That would be a strategic disaster. The Europeans would be even more outraged that they put it at risk. They`re fairly confined land area in the south of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: How would you assess where we are in the war right now, right? In the months, we were following it day in, day out, hour by hour, where do things stand now?\par \par \uc2 MCCAFFREY: We all I think, first one, no one is quite sure. We don`t understand the level of Ukrainian casualties and the internal struggles they`re going through, their operational security has been good. That`s a right thing for them to do. They`re not trying to give vulnerabilities to the Russian forces. Having said that, the U.S. finally came out and said the Russians have suffered 80,000 killed and wounded and have lost a massive amount of their equipment. By some counts, more than a third of the modernized weapons they went into Ukraine was they`re in trouble, running their smart munitions. A lot of their military technology depends on foreign imports and chips and other sophisticated equipment. They legally, in theory, can`t send conscripts into the fight in the Ukraine special military operation. He has to declare general mobilization, which Putin is fearful to do, rightfully so.\par \par \uc2 And then finally, there`s some great work coming out of \uc2 Yale University\uc2  and talking about the Russian economy is crumbling. In fact, they`re not able to pay for the war, simply through the sales of energy supplies to Western Europe, and they can`t find new customers in China or elsewhere overnight. So Putin in a disaster as the war grinds on. The question is will \uc2 NATO\uc2  stay with the Ukrainians? So I think they will. We`re right in the verge of having both Sweden and Finland become the 31st and 32nd nations who are part of \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [23:40:13]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But even if Russia is currently losing the war and their economy is suffering, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  isn`t losing his will. So how scary is that desperate times call for desperate measures?\par \par \uc2 MCCAFFREY: Well, I think you make a good point, you know, he`s in a corner, it`s hard to see any way out. And by the way, the notion Zelenskyy put on the table categorically that, that Russia would seize Crimea in 2014. And sees it as a Russian enclave as part of Russian, a mother Russia, Zelenskyy is saying, everything`s on the table, we`re going to regain sovereignty over all of our material, our land area. Putin will never, ever consider leaving the Crimea, among other things, never mind eastern parts of Ukraine, the Donbas and other areas.\par \par \uc2 So I think he`s desperate. There`s no limit to the potential escalation he could try. I don`t think he`s going to use nuclear weapons that would open Pandora`s Box on him. And I think his generals know that. But he`s definitely not going to give up. The question is, will the Russian military start to crumble? And we would hope to see that in the coming months, perhaps by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Russia, give up on Putin. General, thank you for joining us tonight. General Barry McCaffrey.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, it has been a long, long week. It`s only Wednesday. One of our favorite historians Jon Meacham is here to help put some of the major news we`re covering into context, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:46:28]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN F. KENNEDY\uc2 , U.S. FORMER PRESIDENT: For our nation is founded on the principle that observance of the law is the eternal safeguard of liberty and defiance of the law is the surest road to tyranny.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The surest road to tyranny. JFK gave that warning nearly 60 years ago, after Mississippi`s Governor threatened the Feds and defied the law by refusing to allow a black man to attend classes at Ole Miss, six decades later, there are new and disturbing challenges to the rule of law in this country. But this time they are coming from a former president and his party.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Pulitzer Prize winning historian dear friend of this show, Jon Meacham, author of, "And There Was Light: \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  and the American Struggle," which will be out in October, occasionally advises our current president, as well.\par \par \uc2 Jon, you posted a portion of JFK speech on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Why did you do that?\par \par \uc2 JON MEACHAM, PRESIDENTIAL HISTORIAN: I did. Well, I was doing a -- preparing a podcast for next week on the anniversary of James Meredith, the incredibly courageous black man who emigrated Ole Miss, he graduated next week. And so I was putting together an anniversary package of that, and read Kennedy speech, which had been delivered, obviously, a year before, when the, when Oxford, Mississippi was in riots, when see if any of this sounds familiar, when a huge chunk of the country, my chunk of the country largely was being defiant of the will of the federal government, which was the manifestation of the national will, about applying the principles of the declaration of independence through constitutional means to create a more perfect union.\par \par \uc2 Huh, as Mark Twain is reputed to have said history may not repeat itself, but it does rhyme, right? And so what Kennedy`s speech was just this, as great oratory does, as great insights do, it resonated. And he says later, in that same address from the fall of `62, that if the rule of law collapses, then no judge can be sure of his writ, and no neighbor can be sure of his safety.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s the huge point here is that the rule of law is a covenant. It protects you, because you respected in protecting others. That`s the entire fuel mixture of democracy of American democracy. And we`ve been imperfect. Obviously, I come from a region where we refused -- many of us refuse to accept the verdict of the Civil War, the import of the 13th, 14th and 15th amendments for decade upon decade upon decade. But that`s -- that story, though it unfolds in some ways, is largely, I think, should be an example to us. What side do we, you and I, want to be on when people are telling the story of this time? Do you want to be on the side of the people who are breaking the rule of law? Or do you want to be on the side of the people who actually stood up and understood that the Constitution has to be preserved?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, Jon, The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  is reporting that historians like yourself, are actually trying to warn President Biden that democracy is teetering sort of what you were just laying out for us. So what does the President need to understand about where we`ve been historically, and where we are right now?\par \par \uc2 [23:50:14]\par \par \uc2 MEACHAM: Yeah. So that was a private meeting, I was there. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is my friend, I help him when I can. And so take this for all with that context. So without revealing anything that I shouldn`t reveal, President Biden understands this in his bones. He talks about democracy versus autocracy, he sees this global struggle. You`re just talking to General McCaffrey about a hot front, in this in Ukraine. We`re seeing it in this vicious attack on the rule of law at home. The success in Republican primaries of election deniers of people who want to take away your right to vote, your right to choose your own leader.\par \par \uc2 And so this is really an element -- what I think we all have to understand starting with President Biden, but it`s really a stress test for all citizens is, this is a great test. Democracies have not historically, in long endured, if this were easy, everybody would be doing it. America is not easy. And it`s because it requires this sense of I`m going to defer to you today, because there`s a pretty good chance that I`m going to need you to defer to me tomorrow. And the only way I`m going to do -- I`m going to defer to you is if I`m pretty sure if I have a confidence that you`re going to hold up your side of the covenant. And that`s what`s at risk.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But Jon, a growing number of Americans don`t want it to work. You recently posted something about the word, unprecedented. And I hate that word. I used to ban it on the show. If people use it, I`d make them pay me $2. Because for four years, we gasp on TV and say it`s unprecedented. But the thing is, people want to break those norms. The former president laughed at it, he loved it. And so this idea that what`s happening is unprecedented. People don`t care, they actually like it.\par \par \uc2 MEACHAM: They like it, but what the -- but the precedents we have to pay attention to is a rule of law, a love of neighbor, and a mutual respect. Otherwise, it`s not going to matter, because then we`re going to descend into a state of total warfare all the time. I`m not arguing for some sentimental past. I don`t think there`s, you know, when people talk about restoring certain things, hell, it wasn`t all that great then, right? I mean, I`m a boringly heterosexual, white, southern male Episcopalian, you know, things work out for me in this country. But if you -- but for a lot of people it doesn`t.\par \par \uc2 The story of the country is a journey toward a more perfect union, not a perfect one. And so what we have to remind ourselves, and this is hugely important, and it makes what you do so important, and is in the lived experience of a lot of people, the America doesn`t really work, right? It starts with September 11. There`s the bad intelligence with Iraq. There`s the financial crisis. There`s COVID, there`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is this huge force. You know, I teach students at Vanderbilt who were born after the attacks of September 11. So their political -- imagine what you were your political consciousness was shaped and where their political conscious shaped, is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And so therefore - let me just say this, it puts a huge weight, a huge burden, I think, to tell a story, that the rule of law, that having a constitution that can be perfected, is central otherwise, and maybe you disagree. But here`s the question, what comes next? If you tear this up, what comes next? And if you can`t answer that --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I`m reminded of \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  who wanted to tear the system down. And in terms of a perfect union, you`ve got all sorts of Americans that do not want us to be a union. And I leave you with the reminder that the whole premise of Make America Great Again, while there`s millions of Americans who want to do that, for millions of others Americans, it wasn`t great. It wasn`t great before 2022 or 2021, for lots of us.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, we are out of time, Jon, but please, please, please come back always. You know how much I love talking to you. Jon Meacham.\par \par \uc2 MEACHAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a day that was a long time coming and an ovation for the man who helped make it happen, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:59:04]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, Mr. Stewart goes to Washington, Jon Stewart said it would happen and it did. Dedicated veterans and their families did not leave Capitol Hill until the PACT Act was passed last week, and he has not left their side for years. This man has devoted so much of his time and energy to help American veterans and 9/11 first responders. And Jon Stewart clearly gets results. President Biden recorded a video and FaceTime with the protesters on the Capitol steps to show his support. He also called Stewart personally to thank him for his advocacy for veterans.\par \par \uc2 Today, Biden got the chance to thank Stewart in-person when he signed the bill officially into law. So here it is their moment of Zen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And Jon, I want to thank you again. I wanted to come up and hang out in the Capitol steps into the Secret Service said I`d be a pain in the neck would let me do it. So at least we did a little video on there but what you`ve done Jon matters and you know it does. You should know it really, really matters. The refusal that anybody forget, refused to let them forget. And we owe you big man, we owe you big.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We got to love a guy from Jersey. Thank you, Jon Stewart for making us laugh, and even more importantly, for making us care. And on that note, I wish you all a very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late with us. I`ll see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220811ei8a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Chris Jansing Reports for August 10, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Paul Butler, Peter Baker, Ken Dilanian, Tom  Costello, Julia Jester, Robert Gibbs, Kavita Patel, Miguel Almaguer,  Shaquille Brewster \par \uc2 7,876 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Hello, everyone. I`m Chris Jansing live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 With the legal troubles facing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  closing in from two distinct directions at this hour, the former president has now done something he mocked others for doing, he pleaded the Fifth. Take a look. You see him there leaving Trump Tower this morning and route to answer questions from the New York Attorney General who is investigating the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . Trump was set to testify under oath about his business practices, including, we believe, what investigators have said is a pattern of embellishing the worth of his holdings, something with major implications for his taxes and the future of his company.\par \par \uc2 But faced with those questions, he`s decided not to talk. In a statement Trump said, "I once asked if you`re innocent, why are you taking the Fifth Amendment" Now, I know the answer to that question. When your family, your company, and all the people in your orbit have become the targets of an unfounded politically motivated witch hunt supported by lawyers, prosecutors, and the fake news media, you have no choice."\par \par \uc2 Trump`s response there to call it a witch hunt is a familiar practice of him playing the victim card. In response to the federal probe, the second pressure point he`s feeling is there`s another virial playbook being given new life, build a defense for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by undermining the integrity of the government using innuendo and unsubstantiated allegations. Trump`s own lawyers were doing just that yesterday suggesting without any proof that \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents searching Trump`s home, were breaking the law.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALINA HABBA, \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  ATTORNEY: This is a joke. This is a memento. And quite honestly, I`m concerned that they may have planted something, you know. At this point, who knows? I don`t trust the government. And that`s a very frightening thing as an American.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA BOBB, \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  ATTORNEY: But at this point, I don`t necessarily think that they would even go to the extent of trying to plant information. I think they just make stuff up and you know, come up with whatever they want. And I -- that`s the way that they will have to proceed in order to actually try to indict the president because they don`t have anything. There`s just nothing there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, Trump`s lawyers and House Republicans who gathered with him in New Jersey last night cry foul and demand answers from AG Merrick Garland, new details are emerging about that search at Mar-a-Lago. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reports it was actually eight months in the making that despite getting 15 boxes of material in January, officials with the National Archives were "aggressively contracting people in Trump`s orbit throughout the spring trying to get more material they believed hadn`t been turned over." And in a key clue to why this is so important and why officials would make an unprecedented move that they knew would create a political firestorm, well, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reports this investigation is being led by the \uc2 National Security Division\uc2  of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC Chief Justice and intelligence correspondent Ken Dilanian. Peter Baker as chief White House correspondent for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . And Paul Butler is a former federal prosecutor, professor at Georgetown, and an MSNBC analyst.\par \par \uc2 So much to get to but, Paul, let`s start with the Fifth, Trump has the right to plead the Fifth. He can`t assume guilt because of that. But given what we know about Trump, what do you think it might tell us?\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Chris, any good defense attorney would have advised Trump to take the Fifth because the Manhattan district attorney is conducting a parallel investigation of Trump`s business practices. And the DA has said that he`s monitoring what the New York Attorney General is doing. When politicians are under criminal investigation, they sometimes get conflicting advice.\par \par \uc2 But their defense attorneys telling them to take the Fifth and their political advisors saying you can`t do that. Trump is trying to turn his legal problems into a political advantage by claiming that all these investigations he faces are witch hunts and that he`s being persecuted. And, Chris, that may work with his political base but is not a defense in any of these potential civil or criminal cases.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Peter, I mean, there are a lot of times \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  mocked people who took the Fifth. He once said only the mob pleads the Fifth, Even his own kids who testified in this investigation just a few weeks ago didn`t do it. This is -- I don`t know, how would you characterize it, a major but not maybe unexpected about-face?\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, it might not be unexpected. It is pretty unprecedented as far as I can recall. I can`t think of any president who`s ever pleaded the Fifth before. Of course, look, you know, we are told by lawyers we shouldn`t infer anything from that but it does mean that you are saying in effect that your answers to these questions might tend to incriminate you. That is a pretty more amazing thing to hear from a president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 We saw when \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  highlighted \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2  taking the Fifth during their congressional January sixth investigation when he was asked questions like do you believe in the peaceful transfer of power? So you have a situation here where a former president of the United States does not believe that he can testify and not expose himself to legal Jeopardy. Now, his argument is, of course, that the prosecutor is biased and politically out to get him and those are certainly, you know, boards that will resonate as Professor Butler just said, with a lot of his base and a lot of his people. He spent a lot of years discrediting anybody who ever investigated him.\par \par \uc2 But of course, anybody who`s investigated says that they are under a witch hunt. I mean, nobody I -- nobody recall said, yes, I`m being legitimately investigated. So, you know, we take that with a grain of salt, obviously. But it is an extraordinary moment. We have a first-time ever search of a president -- former president`s residence and a first-time ever invocation of the Fifth Amendment by a former president. That`s a big deal. We don`t know where it takes us.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, And obviously, Paul, Peter is absolutely right. You don`t see a lot of defendants or potential defendants going yes, come on, they`re investigating me no problem. Having said that, it is something different, is it not for a lawyer to say a stuff was planted, or even beyond that, there`s somewhere like concocting stuff?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: It`s really revealing that Trump is questioning whether \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents planted evidence. I think it means that he`s concerned that the search warrant uncovered material that will get him in trouble. And it`s also a way for Trump to rehearse at their best in the event that he`s prosecuted. But here`s the thing. Chris, when I prosecuted drug dealers back in the day sometimes they would claim that the cops planted evidence, and juries usually don`t buy that. Reportedly, 30 \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents participated in the Mar- a-Lago raid, and Trump has absolutely no evidence that anything improper happened.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: How would that be documented? How would they be able to -- and I`m sure they anticipated this, that there might be accusations allegations, what generally happens in a situation like this very high profile search, how would they make sure to be able to say there`s no way this happened?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: So for all of its recent roles, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is still probably the world`s best law enforcement agency. So we have at least 30 agents there, all looking around the apartment. Sometimes, but not always, when warrants are executed they`re videotaped. But at the end of the day, it would be the testimony of these public servants against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And I don`t have much doubt about who a jury will believe.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Ken, to the point of a witch hunt. I mean, what goes into even getting a search warrant? It`s worth, I think, revising that question today.\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, MSNBC JUSTICE & INTELLIGENCE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, so there are two -- there were two steps. First of all, the Justice Department had to decide that this was the best way to get the information that somehow a subpoena wasn`t going to work. And we know -- we have all this reporting about the negotiations that were going on, Chris, between senior Justice Department lawyers, some of whom were traveling to Mar-a-Lago and talking to Trump lawyers. But it`s not 100 percent clear what broke down there.\par \par \uc2 Something happened that led the Justice Department to say we have to go in there strong. We have to go get this search warrant. And to get the search warrant, they had to go to a judge and convince that judge that there was probable cause to believe that there was a crime that occurred and that evidence of that crime was in this location, at that time, was in Mar-a- Lago. So it`s not just the executive branch, the Justice Department, as part of this process, it`s the judicial branch, an independent magistrate judge had to sign off on this. And eventually, we will see that affidavit that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  -- that an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent presumably swore out to this judge, demonstrating and explaining all the rationale for this search.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it`s under seal. But just -- it`s really -- to Peter`s point, you know, yes, very often, people who are the subject of -- who are under investigation claim it`s a witch hunt. And even when President Clinton was being investigated by a special counsel, he was -- he criticized the prosecution. But it`s not often that you see an entire major party in the United States, including U.S. senators comparing the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to the Gestapo, and almost every major Republican official just immediately without evidence going to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is in the wrong here. That`s what -- this is a remarkable place we`re in, in this country with the nation`s foremost law enforcement officers.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, we`re -- where we talked about undermining institutions. And, Peter, we`ve also talked about -- we did a deep dive yesterday with my colleague, Ben Collins, about all this online traffic that has been generated by this search, the threats of civil war, a violent response, does that in any way put additional pressure on them to reveal something to justify the search, maybe defuse some of the anger out there? What about the pressure they`re feeling right now, Peter?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes, you know, it`s interesting. You`re right about that. Obviously, it`s such a provocative move. It`s such an extreme move in the history of the Justice Department that it would seem like they would want to explain to the public as best they can. They haven`t done that. They haven`t released the documents that have justified the search.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, neither is President Trump. You know, former President Trump has a copy of this search warrant. They leave it for you when they execute a search like this. He knows what they took, there`s an inventory. Presumably, he could release that search warrant if, in fact, it showed anything that would exonerate him. He hasn`t done that either. So we don`t know enough about this.\par \par \uc2 It is interesting, as we mentioned that our paper reported, my colleagues reported that this has been done under the purview of the National Security section. But we don`t know what that tells us. I mean, that does suggest that there was an urgency here that was over and above a paperwork issue. You know there`s not just oh, gosh, you know, you have a paper that you shouldn`t have, which, you know, we`ve seen in the past.\par \par \uc2 President Nixon fought the federal government for quite a long time over the right to keep his paper. That went to civil court. That wasn`t ever, you know, a criminal issue. They obviously decided here as -- and follow it. And as said, that something rather extraordinary justified such an action. We just don`t know what it is and it would be helpful, obviously, at some point, if somebody could help us out.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. I guess, Paul, that begs the question what would they typically let us know? Are they worried about tipping their hand? I mean, what do you think about the possibility or, in fact, given the tensions that are out there, whether it would be a wise decision to talk about this and why they did what they did when they did it?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: So, Chris, Trump pretends like he doesn`t understand this, but the Justice Department is actually trying to protect him, as it does every subject of a criminal investigation. DOJ doesn`t discuss a case unless charges are brought because it`s not fair to accuse someone unless prosecutors can back it up with the kind of evidence that`s contained in a criminal indictment.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Peter, where does all this lead politically? Well, I showed at the beginning of the show the class photo from Bedminster where Trump met with House Republicans last night. And I want to play what Jim Banks said afterward about the search at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM BANKS, (R-IN): It unifies us in our outrage to fight back. And if anything, it paves the way for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to unify the party once again and run for president. I really believe if he gets out and hits the campaign trail, he`s going to fire up Republicans and non-traditional Republicans to come out to the polls in November to fight back against corruption.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And I want to read what a Republican strategist who was organizing a PAC to support \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  told Politico. He said "we can hang it up. If Trump wants it at this point, I don`t see how it`s not his. It`ll be a coronation at this point, not a primary." I mean, before this week, there were a lot of Republicans who thought Trump would be a drag on the ticket for the midterms. Frankly, they weren`t sure he should run in 2024. That would be bad news for the Republicans overall. Has this changed the equation for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes, Chris, that was so last week. You know, I mean, the truth of the matter is, there`s a whole, you know, shoe store of shoes still the drop here. We don`t know. I mean, you know, if he were to run today for the nomination, yes, I think everybody more or less agrees that the Republicans would nominate him. But we don`t know what this investigation is going to lead to. There could be a lot of developments that they now at a time when this becomes an active political contest, in which we learn a lot more.\par \par \uc2 They may -- it may help President Trump, it may add to his sense of grievance, he is an apostle of grievance and that`s, you know, why he is highlighting this search to make himself a victim and appeal to those supporters of him who, you know, identify with that. But, you know, if they end up showing something in court that changes our understanding of what this week`s actions are all about, we have no idea and we shouldn`t -- we shouldn`t get too far ahead of things.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, but a whole shoe store of shoes yet to drop. That`s my line from Peter Baker today. Peter, thank you. Ken, Paul, appreciate you very much.\par \par \uc2 Big economic news today, as the latest report on inflation reveals that it`s easing slightly in July, what will that mean for the average consumer, and the \uc2 FDA\uc2  now taking action in the fight against monkeypox, what they`re trying to do to stretch the availability of vaccines, plus, another win for another election denier, all the results out of a battleground Wisconsin. You`re watching "CHRIS JANSING REPORTS" only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A key economic number released just this morning showing some small signs of improvement to runaway inflation. The Consumer Price Index pegs July inflation rate at 8.5 percent, still high, but down from the 40- year high of 9.1 percent in June. That change led by lower gas prices, airline tickets, and used car prices. That economic news also coming as President Biden tries to build momentum after a series of legislative victories, including Senate approval of the Inflation Reduction Act and the signing of the chips bill yesterday. Here he is this morning at the signing of the PACT Act.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The economic plan is working, and the second is building an economy that will reward work. Wages are up this month, provide opportunity to help the middle class, and still have work to do but we`re on track.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his legal problems dominating the news, can the president and congressional Democrats break through the noise to reach voters? For more, let`s bring in NBC`s Tom Costello. Robert Gibbs is a former White House press secretary. And NBC`s Julia Jester is in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Tom, give us a sense of what these inflation numbers actually mean for the average American.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, we should make no mistake about it. I mean, it is hot and steamy in Washington. And inflation is still hot, but maybe some of the steam is coming out of it.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 Because that number you showed, the month over month number, is what is so critical, no change in inflation from June into July. In other words, it did not get worse. As a result, we`re starting to see the year-over-year number come down. So, eight and a half percent is still a big bad number, but it`s better than the 9 percent that we saw in June. Where did we see the decrease? It`s where you see it every single time you fill up. Gas prices are dropping, $4 and a penny is now the national average down from just over $5 and a penny back in June. That`s helping so are -- so is a drop in airfares. Air fares have dropped, I think, it`s 20 -- make it seven and a half percent.\par \par \uc2 However, however, they`re still up 28 percent from a year ago, right? Gas prices still up 44 percent from a year ago. Across the board, food is up, home energy is up, home prices are up, all of that is what is driving the inflation picture apparel, for example, bad timing on apparel when kids need back-to-school supplies. So this is still a high priority for the administration trying to tame inflation. Really, it`s up to the Fed to do that, though, right? The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  expected to raise rates again in September.\par \par \uc2 And so the reason you saw the stock market rally today is because maybe the thinking goes, the Fed won`t have to raise rates as aggressively if, in fact, inflation is starting to tame. And so if we get another decent number in August, like the one we just had in July, if there`s evidence that, in fact, inflation is starting to slow down while we still have good jobs, numbers 50-year lows on the unemployment picture, then maybe we can avoid a recession and maybe the Fed won`t have to raise rates as aggressively. It is still a very perplexing economy but we did get, as I said, a little bit of good news today. No inflation change month over month, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Julia, what is the White House saying about these inflation numbers?\par \par \uc2 JULIA JESTER, MSNBC ASSOCIATE PRODUCER: President Biden began his remarks today touting one word, zero, that zero month over month inflation increase. But he also said "people are still hurting," acknowledging the reality of most Americans that while it`s good that gas prices are falling, food and housing, still see increased prices, which is impacting families` budgets. And so he also touted the jobs report last week, as well as the 3.5 percent unemployment rate. And so he acknowledges that they`re not out of the woods.\par \par \uc2 And so he`s also trying to get ahead of future potential headwinds like the war in Europe or pandemic shutdowns in Asia that could also cause supply chain issues in the future, even as there seems to be progress with inflation. And so, all eyes will be on that Inflation Reduction Act as it makes its way through the House and eventually on the President Biden`s desk to sign, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And in the meantime, there`s a lot at stake here as you well know, Robert, midterms are coming. Yesterday at the signing of the chips bill, President Biden said decades from now people are going to look back at this week with all we pass and all we moved on, and say that we met the moment at this inflection point in history. Then The Washington Post`s Matt Viser wrote, that may be true, but they also may not remember it tomorrow. So how do Biden and the Democrats break through this nonstop noise that continues to come from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GIBBS, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, it`s a great question. And I -- and I think the administration just has to keep chugging along. First, it should largely ignore what else is happening in the news cycle, particularly around \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But I think, focus on getting the president, getting the cabinet, getting other members of Congress out there to tout what`s happening. I think there`s no doubt that this economic news today is good news. And the White House now wants to see that inflation number repeated month over month until we get to the -- to Election Day really, and beyond.\par \par \uc2 But they`ve got to go merchandise what`s in the Inflation Reduction Act? They`ve got to go talk about what I think Biden, rightly believes, history books will remember well. And don`t forget this too. We`ve seen a tightening in this generic congressional ballot over the last few weeks, largely driven by the Supreme Court`s abortion decision. So all of this stuff taken together, give the Democrats a bit of momentum and a bit of hope heading into the last 12 and a half, 13 weeks of this election.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Is Trump the biggest noise that needs to be drummed out, is that inflation, obviously, you know, as we just talked about, the numbers are better today, but hardly good? What are they up against?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Well, I think inflation is still the biggest thing that the White House has to combat and the biggest thing that likely keeps them awake at night because even as we see that inflation didn`t grow month over month, we`re still dealing with extraordinarily high prices.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 Really smart for the president to go out there and both tell improvement but understand and live where people live and that is that inflation is too high and they`re still hurting. They need to see that improve while other things atmospherically help them on the way to Election Day.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Robert, Tom, Julia, thank you all so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian forces say they have struck a key Russian airbase in Crimea, destroying nine warplanes there and killing one person in what experts are calling a major escalation. Huge blasts were reported in that territory. That was annexed by Russia. A Ukrainian government official told \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  the attack is the work of Ukrainian Special Forces and comes on the heels of that Russian missile strike in southern Ukraine that killed 13 people and wounded 11.\par \par \uc2 A new warning from abortion rights experts, how they say a case in Nebraska could give us a glimpse of what to expect in a post-rural America. We`re talking felony charges, even a warrant for \uc2 Facebook\uc2  messages. That`s next on CHRIS JANSING REPORTS.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:05]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC HOST: A case that prosecutors say involves \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and abortion and a mother and daughter facing charges has abortion rights experts warning about what a post-Roe digital surveillance in America could look like. Prosecutors say a Nebraska mother gave abortion pills to her daughter, who was 17 at the time.\par \par \uc2 Now, Nebraska law makes abortion illegal at 20 weeks and the police affidavit says the teen had a miscarriage at around 23 weeks, shortly after taking the pills. Investigators use \uc2 Facebook\uc2  messages between the teen and her mom to make their case after \uc2 Facebook\uc2  complied with a warrant. The company says the warrant did not mention abortion.\par \par \uc2 The mother and daughter have pled not guilty. Their lawyer did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Kim Wehle, constitutional law expert and professor at the University of Baltimore School of Law. Kim, it`s good to see you. Let`s look at the legal issue on both sides. Let`s start with prosecutors. They say they got the proof they needed in these online chats. So, did \uc2 Facebook\uc2  do anything wrong here? Or is this not about \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , but more a red light flashing for anyone who thinks still that their online communications can come back to hurt them?\par \par \uc2 KIM WEHLE, UNIV. OF BALTIMORE SCHOOL OF LAW: Well, it`s very complicated legally. Actually, I think what this demonstrates is that the privacy laws didn`t anticipate Dobbs, did not anticipate what is going to happen, I think moving forward, which are more of these prosecutions. It`s not like this didn`t happen before, but it`s was more rare.\par \par \uc2 So, we -- the reason that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  turned this over is because they had a warrant. So they were required as a matter of law to do that. Whether they should be storing this information is another question. That`s something they can decide for themselves. We -- when we go to the doctor`s office, we sign all those forms. We know them as HIPAA forms. That`s a federal law that protects our privacy, but it only applies to providers, it`s not going to apply to \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , et cetera.\par \par \uc2 So, this also leaves providers kind of in this tricky position. Do they turn over this kind of information? Going forward, I think, women and girls need to use, you know, apps that are encrypted. They need to leave their phones at home, have to be careful about \uc2 Google\uc2  searches. It`s kind of a scary moment, Chris. And really the only entity that can fix this probably across the country is the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  by quickly passing a law that would amend HIPAA to close some of these loopholes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, we`ll see what happens there, but we should also say it varies from state to state. But as you give this warning to folks, and you talk about encryption, what are we looking at here? What kinds of penalties? What kinds of charges?\par \par \uc2 WEHLE: Well, that gets to the state-by-state abortion law. So it looks like in this day in Nebraska, the prosecutors are going after what I think even a year ago when I would talk to people about the possibility of tinkering with Roe. Folks, even experts in this area said that will never happen. It will never be that girls and women are actually prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 We`re seeing it now, Chris. We`re seeing -- we`re not -- they`re not going just after prosecutors, aren`t just going after providers, they`re going after women and girls for having abortions under criminal law and claiming that this is a felony. And these women, frankly, in the 17-year-old child really could be paying the ultimate price with her liberty for this political pitch battle around abortion that`s playing out.\par \par \uc2 I think we are in a culture of cruelty and the laws just haven`t caught up. I think it`s -- there`s whiplash in this moment. But their political points to be scored, unfortunately, for being really draconian about children and women seeking health care that recently was protected, at least to the point of viability by the United States Constitution through the Supreme Court for the last 50 years.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Kim Wehle, thank you so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  trying a new approach to fight the monkeypox outbreak. Details of the newly approved plan, what it means for access to shots and will it work as cases continue to spike across the country? A medical expert and former White House policy director joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:38:25]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: As monkeypox continues to spread, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is taking new action. More than 9,000 cases of the highly contagious virus have now been reported in 49 states. That`s according to the CDC with Florida, just the latest state to begin rationing vaccine supplies. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has formally announced a plan that we first talked about last week cutting shots to a fifth of the normal vaccine dose to help stretch the available supply. That also means the shots in arms will be given differently.\par \par \uc2 Joining me to talk about all of this, Dr. \uc2 Kavita Patel\uc2 , an MSNBC Medical Contributor. Hey Dr. Patel, always good to see you. So now we`ll get five doses out of a standard one dose vial. Given the demand, and you`re going from 400,000 vials in the national stockpile to 2 million doses, it sounds significant, is it and how effective is this much smaller dose?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 KAVITA PATEL\uc2 , NBC NEWS MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, Chris. So good news. I`ll start with the last question first. This is believed based on previous trials, not ones that have been done with this monkeypox vaccine kind of in real time, but with previous trials that shows the same effectiveness. And it`s really that switch in the method of administration.\par \par \uc2 So it`s going from underneath the skin layer into the fat originally, to a little bit of an angle and a little different needle into the skin layer. So that`s really the difference we`re talking about here. It`s not that it`s easy, but it`s not something that that`s unfamiliar to people such as myself in primary care.\par \par \uc2 And then, will this be enough? I think that`s the most important question.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:01]\par \par \uc2 Certainly getting 2 million doses is an incredible accomplishment. It buys us time while the United States brings on some domestic manufacturing capacity. Because, remember, all this vaccine is coming out of one manufacturer in the European nation. So we`re not necessarily kind of doing it in house, but we will buy time to be able to spend the next several months getting more domestic kind of finishing to the manufacturing of this very necessary vaccine, as it`s clear that it`s becoming more and more of a community spread in terms of the disease, the threat of the virus.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You talked about the next couple of months. But in the next couple of weeks, an awful lot of folks are going to be going back to school. And we`ve talked about before --\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: -- that this health emergency is not restricted to the LGBTQ community, which was sort of put out there in the beginning. But health officials are concerned that college campuses could become hotspots when students go back this fall. And, in fact, even with so few people on campus in the summer, at least five colleges have confirmed cases of monkeypox.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So are we right to be worried and what can be done about college campuses?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes, I think college campuses, look, I`ll say health care facilities. I`ll say some of the same facilities we talked about, Chris early on in COVID. Even just places where you have a lot of people, you know, together correctional facilities, places where essential workers end up having to do a lot of their work often without gloves, often without masks and protective equipment.\par \par \uc2 So I think it`s an incredibly important question you`re bringing up. Here`s the key word and missing piece data. We need a better handle on the spread and understanding the network of spread. We know enough about this virus. It`s not new, it`s decades old.\par \par \uc2 But the epidemiology is changing, Chris. What we`ve known about it is different because it`s coming up all around the world in a way that we hadn`t seen before. So we have to kind of, you know, design the airplane while we`re also flying it. And I have faith that we can do it but we`re going to need data, we`re going to need state cooperation. That`s why I wish we had done that public health emergency earlier. Better late than never. But it would have helped us weeks ago as we`re heading into a busy fall season.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So even though in the beginning of this, which, you know, we were asking all of these same questions, many very similar to what we asked at the beginning of the pandemic, we were told it`s not airborne.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Are we still confident of that?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: I think what we`re confident of is the mechanism of spread is still close contact that the different than the way we talk about it with COVID. With COVID, we talked about close contact, you know, the 15 minutes being around somebody. We know that this is not just touching somebody that was touching a doorknob, Chris. This is definitely less contagious than the BA.5 variant, for example.\par \par \uc2 But what we`re learning is that what used to be skin to skin contact only, we`re trying to understand, does this get passed on in, you know, other body secretions, vaginal fluids, urine. That`s the type of thing that we`re still trying to unfold. That was not the case, even just months ago.\par \par \uc2 So just having somebody cough on you in close contact over and over? Yes. But it`s not the kind of airborne that we`ve been giving people precautions with COVID. I say all of this to tell you that I think that the risk to most people is still low because we don`t see people end up in the hospital, or unfortunately, or fortunately dying, unless they have other chronic conditions.\par \par \uc2 So this is an illness that can be very painful. It can be very stigmatizing. We need to recognize it and treat it early for that reason. But it`s still less severe than any of what we`ve discussed around COVID, even in the early months when we had all those deaths around us.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So let me ask you some practical advice. Let`s say I live in a dorm and I have four or five suitemates and we`re always hanging around together. Are we back to the point where we`re saying, maybe we wear a mask when we`re indoors or we`re in close quarters? Or we go to a class where there are several 100 people in an auditorium?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes, this is -- so number one, I don`t think that this is a scenario where roommates or even if you`re sending, you know, a young adult off to school for a period of time that you would take different precautions other than, Chris, if you have any sort of new or suspicious illness, a new rash, those are all telltale signs to get checked early. We actually do have the testing available.\par \par \uc2 And then absolutely, Chris, if you are on a campus where there has been a known outbreak, yes, that would be a reason, maybe not the masking, but to think about hand hygiene, very important. Thinking about cleaning of surfaces, something the school should take seriously or institutions. And then third, any sort of vaccine strategy.\par \par \uc2 This is where those 2 million doses can go quickly. An entire campus can go through those vaccines in a heartbeat. So I think those are the kinds of precautions we need to take. So anybody going back to school, no matter what age, think about your surroundings, and then think about your own illness and the people around you take those precautions. And that will be safer than doing anything or getting scared about something that might not be relevant to your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Always so much great advice. Dr \uc2 Kavita Patel\uc2 , thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A pilot`s quick thinking saved the lives of his two passengers when he made a crash landing on a freeway in Southern California Tuesday afternoon. Look at this. It happened after the single engine plane lost power mid flight, and the pilot actually landed it on the 91 freeway before it burst into flames. Here`s more from NBC`s Miguel Almaguer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW CHO, PILOT: It was pretty scary.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Pilot Andrew Cho says he`s lucky to be alive this morning after these terrifying moments Tuesday. His small plane crash landing onto a California freeway, clipping the back of a pickup truck with three people inside before bursting into flames in the middle of traffic about 50 miles southeast of Los Angeles. Miraculously, no one in the plane or on the ground was injured.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Responding to a plane crash on the freeway eastbound 91.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (voice-over): This video released by TMZ appears to show people running from the wreckage.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Airplane landed on the freeway.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (voice-over): Cho says he and a friend were taking a lunchtime leisure flight to Catalina Island in his single engine piper when he began to have engine trouble and had to quickly decide where to land.\par \par \uc2 CHO: In my judgment, we weren`t going to be able to make the airport landing field without possibly hitting a building. And so, I had to make a decision to land on the freeway.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (voice-over): Cho and his friend kicking out a door of the plane just minutes before the plane erupted into a fiery ball of flames. Firefighters putting out the blaze as everyone in the area breathed the sigh of relief.\par \par \uc2 CHO: I`m ecstatic, I`m alive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, no kidding. And here`s my question. So you`re driving your truck and in your rearview mirror, you see a plane coming at you. What do you do? They did not cover that in my drivers training courses. I`m quite sure.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Miguel Almaguer for that.\par \par \uc2 The highway patrol says the fact that no one was injured is absolutely remarkable and the \uc2 FAA\uc2  is now investigating exactly what happened. More misery for those trying to fly, more than 1,000 flights were delayed 200 more canceled just yesterday. Of course, we know airlines are facing staff shortages, bad weather, high demand for travel. And in appearance this morning on Morning Joe, Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  said his department is working to hold airlines accountable.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: Look, I would -- when the right thing to do is to roll up our sleeves and bring the airlines to the table and work with them, I prefer to do that. But we`re also going to use our enforcement powers to make sure passengers have a better experience.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Airlines say they are planning to thin out their schedules ahead of the fall. Secretary Buttigieg said this morning, they expect demand to drop after the summer it usually does before it picks back up for the holidays.\par \par \uc2 Another win for another election denier. A Trump-backed candidate takes the GOP nomination in Wisconsin`s race for governor. We`ll head to that key battleground state looking ahead to what`s expected to be a big showdown in November. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:27]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The stage is now set for what`s expected to be an epic governor`s race in the battleground state of Wisconsin. Trump favorite and election denier, \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2  has won the Republican nomination for governor and now faces the incumbent Democrat \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  in a general election race already focusing on voting access in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 As for the upcoming Senate race, Democratic Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes won as expected. He`s already mounting sharp attacks against Republican Senator Ron Johnson.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now with the very latest NBC News Correspondent Shaq Brewster live from Madison. So Shaq, I want to take these races one by one, both critical for the Democrats. How`s the governor`s race looking right now?\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, the field is now set in this incredibly important battleground state. You have incumbent governor \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 , a governor who has been somewhat of a firewall through his veto pen against conservative legislation passing in the state on things like abortion, guns and voting rights. He will be facing \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2 , a candidate who says he`s an outsider, a businessman and who says the endorsement he received from former President Trump was crucial in him winning his party`s primary.\par \par \uc2 And already in the first couple of hours of this general election, you`re seeing the role, you`re getting a preview of the role that former President Trump will play. Listen to the messages we heard from both of these candidates.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TIM MICHELS (R), WI GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: I`d like to thank President Trump for his support. It was a tremendous validation of our meteoric rise in this campaign. He knows that we need to have new leadership in Madison, and he sees a lot of similarities. He didn`t have to run for president. I don`t have to run for governor.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TONY EVERS (D-WI): Donald Trump`s not running in this race. If Michels let him be his partner, you know, day after day that`s his probably not mine. We are going to be focusing on what the people Wisconsin care about.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Chris, this is a race that will be incredibly important not just for the midterms, but when you think about 2024 in the certification of election results in setting election laws in this state, expect a lot of attention to be focused on this and a lot of money to be spent by both the candidates and outside groups.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, money and attention in the Senate race toO because this is one of only two Republican held seats that Cook Political Report suggests is up for grabs. What are we looking for?\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Another race where there are those national implications. Ron Johnson is the incumbent Republican senator representing the state of Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 If you look at Marquette polls, the gold standard of polls here, he is one of the most vulnerable senators across the country. He is relatively unpopular. He`s someone who said that he was only going to run for two terms, and is now running for a third term.\par \par \uc2 Last night, Democrats nominated their Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes, someone who`s painting himself as a candidate of the middle class, who has popularity. And the one word that you hear with him is that he has a broad coalition of support.\par \par \uc2 Again, Chris, this will be an expensive race. A lot of eyes focus on this race. And again, when you have that 50-50 Senate, every race is important, especially when you`re talking about the Senate. And Ron Johnson is someone who has been behind in all polls before his two previous wins and that`s the case now. We`ll see if he`s able to eke out another win this time around, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Shaq Brewster, always great to see you out there on the road. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And NBC`s Plan Your Vote tool is here to help you successfully cast your ballot in the midterms. Get key information on the voting rules where you live, including registration deadlines, and more. Head to NBC news.com/PlanYourVote now.\par \par \uc2 And file this one under, shouldn`t we have seen this coming? Pizza loving Italy, home to some of the best pizza in the world won`t be home to Domino`s anymore. Seven years after opening its first location in Italy, Domino`s is closing the last of what was once three dozen stores. American style toppings like pineapple and barbecue just never caught on. As the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  summarized it, Domino`s was unable to win over picky palates in the place where pizza was invented. Say that fast three times.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for Chris Jansing Reports every weekday, 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. Katy Tur Reports starts next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220810ei8a0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for August 9, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Vaughn Hillyard, Charlie Sykes, Ali Vitali, Tali  Farhadian Weinstein, Ben Collins, Frank Figliuzzi, Guad Venegas, Shaquille  Brewster \par \uc2 7,879 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Hello, everyone, I`m Chris Jansing live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Today, we are in uncharted territory. Details are still coming in but one thing is clear. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  search at Mar-a-Lago has no parallel in American history. Never before has the Justice Department under one president carried out an unannounced search at the home of his predecessor, who may also be a future political opponent. It raises two questions of historic consequence. Does this put us one step closer to criminal charges against former President Trump, and could he after hinting as recently as this past weekend that he`s going to run for reelection be barred from holding office ever again?\par \par \uc2 These are questions not only facing him but also his most fervent supporters. We saw a bit of their anger last night when a small group of protesters gathered outside Mar-a-Lago after the news broke. And Republican Senator Tim Scott of South Carolina reminded us of this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT, (R-SC): Certainly, is the largest voice in American politics period, Republican or Democrat. And it feels like he`ll continue to be the more focus you put on him, the more likely the supporters will rally around him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Meanwhile, on Capitol Hill, a furious reaction from some other big-name Republicans. Right after the news broke, House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy threatened an investigation in writing against Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  and the Justice Department. And just this morning, NBC News learned that more than a dozen members of the Republican Study Committee are going to Bedminster to have dinner with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tonight. It has apparently been planned for a while but takes on added significance given McCarthy`s threat.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know exactly who`s invited but committee members include some of Trump`s most aggressive defenders, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2 , \uc2 Madison Cawthorn\uc2 , \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2 , \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 . Bottom line, it`s clear, 24 hours after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  first entered Mar-a-Lago, that battle lines are being drawn with far-reaching consequences. The question now, what will this reveal about America`s institutions and how well they survive what`s coming next? We will also get very quickly to the heart of the Republican Party, how many will embrace their traditional law and order roots, and how many others will continue to pledge loyalty to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , whatever the cost?\par \par \uc2 So, we`ve got a lot to discuss. NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard is at Mar-a-Lago. Ali Vitali is on Capitol Hill. Josh Dawsey is a political investigative reporter for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 . And Charlie Sykes is editor-at-large for The Bulwark and an MSNBC contributor. Thank you all for being here on such an important day.\par \par \uc2 Vaughn, we don`t know the details of the search warrant. It would have to be meticulous, a judge would have had to sign off, top officials at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and DOJ would almost certainly have to sign off. But to a lot of these Republicans, none of it seems to matter, does it?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: No. And that`s why you don`t hear Republicans giving a defense of actual allegations of you know, we -- Trump team`s understanding is that this is at least in part related to the potential material that was taken from the White House and brought here to Mar-a-Lago, then which some of these materials may be classified documents. But you don`t hear these Republican elected officials from \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , from \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , from Ted Cruz, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , defending the actual substance of the matter here. Instead, they are taking shots at the Department of Justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You even heard last night \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  on Fox News, talking about when the Republicans were to take the -- back the \uc2 U.S. House\uc2  this next year, that Jim Jordan, that he should work to have appropriations funding cut off to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  here, that essentially this is all part of what we have heard over the last year is the idea that the deep state is out to undermine the Republican Party and undermine \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the so-called America First movement here. You know, \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2  last night was on the airwaves saying that this is -- led him to say publicly for the first time that he wants his dad to run for president again. You know, this has been a sowing of distrust for years now, Chris, here.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: And when you see Republicans, you know, they are -- some of them are suggesting that they want the Department of Justice to come out and lay down the case in front of the American public as to what probable cause they had to ultimately go through with this search warrant.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 At the same time though, you have seen in lockstep the Republican Party. It`s not only political apparatus, but also its elected leaders continue to stay behind the former president and potential 2024 nominee for their party.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Charlie, it seems like we`re old enough to remember this because there was a time not too long ago. Where in a situation like this, members of the party, of a person under investigation would at least lay low but maybe even run the other way. In this case, we`re already seeing them run toward him. What does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE SYKES, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Well, exactly what one just mentioned. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has spent years trying to de-legitimize these law enforcement agencies and of the deep state. In the last 24 hours, we got an indication of how deep his subversion -- his campaign of subversion has gone in the Republican Party. I mean, it`s one thing for the hardcore Magaverse to react with fury. But what you are seeing is across the board, elected Republicans not giving the benefit of the doubt to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , not observing, you know, the studied silence that you would normally do when evidence is being gathered, but attacking the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , undermining the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , feeding to this, this anger out there.\par \par \uc2 And it is ironic, number one, for a political party that claims to back the blue, to be the party of law and order. It`s also worth noting that Donald Trump`s campaign in 2016 was largely built around that chant, lock her up. So all of this outrage about the weaponization of law enforcement does seem to be ironic, but there`s no question about it that you are seeing the degree to which this party is prepared to rally around \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  no matter what the evidence is, no matter what crimes may be committed.\par \par \uc2 And they basically are arguing that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should, in fact, be above the law. And Kevin McCarthy seems to have fully embraced the idea that he will threaten the Department of Justice and apparently, an attempt to intimidate the Attorney General, and will retaliate against the Department of Justice if it goes after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which is extraordinary, by any measure.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, we should note that not everyone is jumping fully feet first to his defense. Trump allies, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  tweeted in fact that suspicion is warranted but he added some caveats like no one is above the law and time will tell. Ali, give us your sense on the Hill as you`ve been talking with your sources. I know folks have gone home for a little break. But how seriously are Republicans taking this and what are you hearing from them?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: They have to take it seriously. Whether or not they buy into the reason that the DOJ or frankly, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , did this search of Mar-a-Lago, this is something that`s happening and captured the attention of the entire country. It certainly puts Trump again at the forefront of the Republican Party and forces them to grapple with that image that Charlie and Vaughn were both just talking about. This idea that they are both the party of law and order, but with an asterisk for what it applies to someone who`s inconvenient for them, in this case, the loudest voice and the leader of their party, the former president.\par \par \uc2 Lindsey Graham`s tweet, I think, is actually really instructive if we`re looking at the different caveats for Republicans here because, in the same tweet thread, he says that no one is above the law, but at the same time that this is problematic because we`re close to an election, which, by the way, Trump is not on the ballot in 2022 nor is he get an active political candidate. Though Charlie, Vaughn, you, I, all of us have long talked about the idea that Trump`s imminent candidacy is in many ways tied to these legal threads because it`s easier for him to explain away and politicize these different threads from DOJ, and now from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , that if he is an active political candidate, and so certainly that`s one of the next things that we`re looking for here.\par \par \uc2 But on the Hill, it tells the story too of what we see the Republican majority if they were to retake the House, what that would look like. We always knew that Kevin McCarthy was likely going to be on the attack against Democrats. But now in that tweet that Vaughn laid out, the fact that he`s telling the DOJ and the Attorney General, preserve documents, if we -- if we retake the House, we`re going to be doing hearings. That`s instructive.\par \par \uc2 And as much as Democrats don`t want this to be the referendum issue of the midterms, you saw from \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , for example, as they were being asked about this late last night and early this morning, they didn`t have much to offer or say. This kind of cuts the momentum that Democrats had after the veterans` PACT Act, after the chips bill, after that reconciliation movement over the weekend. It cuts the momentum that they felt they were building, but at the same time, this is also an instructive moment for voters. Do you want a party that is only going to rally around and defend the former president or do you want something else? It`s a litmus test, even if Democrats don`t want it to be.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, I mean, look, I think, Josh, obviously if you run for Congress, you`re used to and in fact revel in operating in a political sphere, right?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 Not if you`re DOJ, not if you`re \uc2 FBI\uc2 , you want to say, I`m having nothing to do with this, and yet, after we saw members of the Republican Party tweeting about this after we saw what Kevin McCarthy wrote -- there was actually a statement and they quoted this in your paper so let me read it.\par \par \uc2 The president of the FBI Agents Association, Brian O`Hare issued a broad defense of the investigators who carry out court-approved searches without commenting specifically on the Trump case. What is the tight rope that these agencies are walking now? They were in a little bit, but when you go, and you have the \uc2 FBI\uc2  enter the home of a former president, it kicks it up to a whole new level.\par \par \uc2 JOSH DAWSEY, POLITICAL INVESTIGATIVE REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON POST: Right. Well, yesterday, the news cycle was dominated basically by what former President Trump and his supporters said, you know. You had a Florida blogger who broke the news about this on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , he said this happened. And a few minutes, maybe seven minutes or so later, you had the former president put out a statement that characterizes that his way, DOJ has not said anything publicly, since then.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, there`s a -- there`s a protocol of not talking about ongoing investigations, obviously, that has been changed at certain times. They talked about the Hillary Clinton investigation, for example, you remember the \uc2 James Comey\uc2  press conference. They`ve talked about other investigations in the past that occasionally, but the usual practice and protocol is that they do not publicly comment on what they are doing privately behind the scenes.\par \par \uc2 But you`re seeing a lot of folks demand that they do, you`re seeing a lot of even Trump critic Republicans demand that they do. Another is saying that it just will not be able to sustain itself for them not, you know, for them -- for them not to say anything. But you also have folks who are saying, you know, if you start talking about ongoing investigations, you open up Pandora`s box, and is that really -- is that really wise to do? Should the government be doing that?\par \par \uc2 And now we`re seeing some more details come out. Obviously, we know more about what they wanted. It was about the classified you know documents that he took down there. The agents were there for many hours, the former president was not there himself. And we know a little bit about this. But none of that is coming through on official government channels.\par \par \uc2 And you have \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , who was viewed by most people in Washington as a cautious man who is going to be at the center of this -- of this maelstrom. You`re going to see a lot of Republicans demand information. You`re even going to see probably some Democrats demand more information about what`s going on. But here`s when you take such a step, as you know, going in the home of a former president and asking for you know all of these documents in the -- in the eyes of many, you better know -- you better have something good that you`re looking for. You better have something important to take such a step like that.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And you know, Charlie, Dan Scavino, Trump`s former deputy White House Chief of Staff tweeted last night just after the news broke. Do it, exclamation point with a number 45. If there was any question about whether Trump was going to run again, does this vast escalation of legal pressure seal the deal in your mind?\par \par \uc2 SYKES: It`s hard to know what`s going on in Donald Trump`s mind, but I would think that it is certainly moving toward that with one big caveat that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I think was taken by surprise, I think he thought he could bully the Department of Justice into not taking action against him. So he`s got to be asking, you know, what are they looking for? What are they going to do? What charges might I face?\par \par \uc2 But having said that, no, I think you`re seeing in the Republican reaction, how difficult it will be for any other Republican to run against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or to take him on. Because he relishes running as a martyr, he relishes running as a victim of the deep state, he would run a campaign of redemption. I -- you know, it was a great president, and then they stole the election for me, and look, they`re coming after me, which means they`re coming after you. Who is going to be able to push back in Republican circles against that?\par \par \uc2 Now, how that will play with the general electorate -- of the general electorate is a very different matter. But I do think that this makes it more likely that he will run and it makes it more likely that many other Republicans will decide they`re going to take a pass on challenging him because of the environment right now.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Vaughn, Josh, and Ali thank you all. Charlie, you`re going to come back a little later on this hour. And be sure to check out Ali`s new book. Electable: Why America Hasn`t Put A Woman In The White House Yet, which is being released later this month. You can preorder.\par \par \uc2 And we`re going to continue our coverage of the unprecedented search of the former president`s Mar-a-Lago home. Will authorities find highly classified information in those boxes, and what legal ramifications could follow? Plus a potential serial killer on the loose in New Mexico, what we`re now hearing from families and victims in a recent string of murders of Muslim men in Albuquerque. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: More than 24 hours after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  showed up at Mar-a-Lago, we are only beginning to learn about what really happened there. We know from NBC`s own reporting, it`s related to government files potentially, highly classified information taken from the White House to Mar-a-Lago. We know the search resulted in agents spending the majority of the day at the estate and ultimately leaving with several boxes. We don`t know what was in those boxes, but if it was classified. Taking them from the White House is illegal and a potential threat to national security.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a lot to discuss. Jacqueline Alemany is a Congressional investigations reporter for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 . Katie Benner covers the Justice Department for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . And Tali Farhadian Weinstein is a former federal and state prosecutor in New York, as well as an MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 So, Jackie, you actually broke the story of a similar rate happening back in January and I thought about that when this first broke yesterday. And I thought so why are they going back now? Do we have a clue?\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JACQUELINE ALEMANY, CONGRESSIONAL INVESTIGATIONS REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON POST: It`s a really good question, Chris. It`s the question of what exactly the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was looking for when they executed their search warrant yesterday. We know that initially in January -- at the end of January after extensive back and forth between the National Archives and lawyers to former President Trump that 15 boxes were eventually recovered from Mar-a-Lago, a combination of classified items and unclassified items. According to reporting that we published yesterday, that inventory of unclassified items actually, is 100 pages long, detailing the various things that were improperly taken to Mar-a-Lago and not archived in a -- in adherence to the Presidential Records Act. We also know that the separate inventory of classified items is roughly three pages long, according to an estimate from a person who is familiar with this inventory.\par \par \uc2 But after this process was handed over from the National Archives to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and there was a realization that there were classified items in the tranche of documents that were taken to Mar-a-Lago -- from Mar-a-Lago. A lot of questions remained about what else potentially might be missing from there. We`re still ringing our sources, trying to figure out what they were looking for exactly whether it was information that would have been under the Presidential Records Act, or whether it was classified information. But at the end of the day, even a very small item or just a snippet, or a paragraph or a portion of a document that is improperly stored and is might be classified could do some serious grave damage to national security, and to any potential military or covert operations that the federal government has been conducting.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Katie, your beat is justice. Does the fact that DOJ felt a search warrant was necessary that the time had come to do this? Help us understand anything about the motivation for the search. How far along the investigation might be? What does it tell you?\par \par \uc2 KATIE BENNER, JUSTICE DEPARTMENT REPORTER, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Yes, I think it does give us some clues. First of all, to Jackie`s point, when the National Archives reached out to Trump and said please submit the information that you`ve improperly taken from the White House, they also asked Trump to continue to look for other documents that may have been in his possession somewhat indicating that they were not sure that he had actually given them everything that he should have.\par \par \uc2 And then the fact that the Justice Department has issued a search warrant is really interesting. Oftentimes, a subpoena will do the trick. The government reaches out to somebody and says, please furnish us with all the information, all the documents, and they expect the person they`ve spoken to, to respond with some sort of speed and to comply.\par \par \uc2 So the fact that they chose the search warrant route, meaning, they had to show that there was some evidence of possible criminal activity. And it also sort of indicates they don`t think that somebody will comply with a subpoena. That those things were brought before a judge and a judge signed off, allowing them to search the property, I think, tells us a lot about what they thought of Trump and his willingness to furnish them with documents.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Katie, when Jim Jordan goes on TV last night and says, I demand to know what they were looking for at Mar-a-Lago and asking. "Why not talk to President Trump and have him give the information you`re after?" The answer is they did.\par \par \uc2 BENNER: Things they did not feel that he would be willing to give him the information simply with a subpoena. And I think that if Jim Jordan wants to know what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  took and what they were looking for, he could simply reach out to Mr. Trump`s lawyers and find out for himself because, as we all know, when the federal government takes things, when they execute a search warrant, they do leave inventory list saying what it is that they took.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So to that sort of same thread, Tali, in the last hour and a half, former vice president \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  tweeted this. Yesterday`s action undermines public comment in our system of justice and Attorney General Garland must give a full accounting to the American people as to why this action was taken and he must do so immediately. Is that a fair request?\par \par \uc2 TALI FARHADIAN WEINSTEIN: It`s not, Chris. And it`s really backwards, you know. I worked on lots of search warrants, thought, and lots of them. Usually, when people have federal agents come into their homes and to execute a warrant, that remains secret for a long time because you don`t want to broadcast that to the world. And the Justice Department certainly is not in the habit of talking about investigative steps that they take. The only reason we know about this one, of course, is because Mr. Trump himself said that the warrant was executed. But that disclosure that he made was -- it was really partial, right? Because he also would have received, as Katie just said, an inventory of what they took, you would have received a copy of the warrant.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 Usually, there are attachments that detail where they`re allowed to look, what they`re allowed to look for, what they`re allowed to take. And almost always in my experience, the warrant that`s left behind also, at least, delineates, it says the top charges. And so all of that information right now is in the hands of Mr. Trump, and Mr. Pence could ask him to make that disclosure that he just asked the Justice Department, to me.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Tali -- yes, and, Tali, the actual obviously, the stuff that they seized is actually in the hands of DOJ. So as somebody who worked there, help us to understand what are the departments. Who are the people who are looking at this right now? How does this sort of fit into their larger investigation? What are they doing right now with what they got yesterday?\par \par \uc2 WEINSTEIN: Well, I would guess that there is an enormous team at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  that is going through this material in consultation with and in partnership with prosecutors. I`m assuming that they took some electronic information as well. And if they did, that takes some time to go through. But I think this is a really important thing, Chris, you know, there`s been a lot of reporting about what is likely the investigation here, what are likely the charges that are being pursued here and they have to do with the mishandling, the concealment, possibly the destruction of classified or other government information that would be subject to various criminal statutes if you took it in an unauthorized way.\par \par \uc2 But when you execute a warrant and if you`re following the rules of your warrant, and you pick up other evidence of criminality that can feed into investigations for other crimes, that`s fair game. And I -- it is totally normal -- in the normal course of things to look for that, to seize on it, and to expand other inquiries. And I`m sure that all the people who are going through the materials from yesterday have that on their minds as well.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Katie, back to sort of this DOJ thread because \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , I think it was in May issued a memo to his staff urging caution about the appearance of trying to affect an election by the timing of DOJ statements, investigations, and charges. I mean, similar memos were put out by four predecessors so it`s nothing new. But given that warning of caution and given everything that`s out there and the pressure that`s been on, what does it mean that they decided to take this step, frankly, so close to the midterms and in the middle of what are ongoing primaries?\par \par \uc2 BENNER: Sure. I mean, we`re in an unusual situation. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not the president. He`s not even a politician. He has not declared himself a candidate in a race. And yet he is the de facto leader of the Republican Party. He`s still possibly the most popular politician representing the Republican Party. And as we`ve seen from the responses from Republicans after the search warrant was executed, he has them in his thrall, and he has power over them, even the former vice president, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . So he`s extremely important.\par \par \uc2 To your point, the Justice Department going back several administrations under Republicans and Democrats have a strict policy about when to take the kind of investigative step like executing a search warrant that could inevitably become public. You don`t want to do that before an election. And so the window within which the Justice Department could execute this kind of search warrant and not be considered interfering in an election is pretty slim. It`s basically, generally speaking, up until Labor Day, so if we`re going to see more action like this, there are only a couple of more weeks, I believe, during which we would possibly see something as overt as this.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Katie, Tali, Jacqueline, thank you both -- all very much. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Loyal supporters of the former president are outraged online. NBC`s extremism expert, Ben Collins is here. Why he says he hasn`t seen this level of violent remarks since the lead-up to the January 6 attacks? And former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Assistant Director Frank Figliuzzi on how law enforcement needs to be preparing. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:33:02]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Social media is absolutely flooded today with threatening posts in the wake of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  searching Mar-a-Lago, and it raises real questions about what law enforcement could be facing. Those threatening comments started pouring in within minutes of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  revealing the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was at his home. Online supporters writing things like, "Lock and load," "When does the shooting start?" and "They will cry out in authentic pain soon."\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ben Collins tracks disinformation and extremism and says he hasn`t seen violent comments like this since before the January 6th attack on the nation`s capital. Ben, as you can see, is here with us. Also with us Frank Figliuzzi, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  assistant director for counterintelligence.\par \par \uc2 So, Ben, you`ve been reading all this stuff from the moment this news broke all the way through you`re coming out here. What`s your big takeaway?\par \par \uc2 BEN COLLINS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: They are ready to go. And by the way, it`s the same people who organized this stuff around January 6th, some of them are literally the people from January 6th. One of those comments that you just saw right there was a man who was at --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Can we put those up again?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Can we put those comments up again?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes. So the next screen if possible.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Not that. The comments. Yes.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes. So, one of the comments that was posted last night.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There we go.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes. The second one there as you can see on the screen, that was posted by a guy who is awaiting sentencing for entering the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So he`s already pled or been convicted.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: He`s already pled down, but this might complicate some things here because as you can see --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You don`t want to be his lawyer right now.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: That`s correct. As you can see he posted about how this was, you know, the civil war. How are we not in a cold civil war at this point? And he said, by the way, later on, I would say more, but I`m awaiting sentencing. So not particularly bright people, but these are people who are ready to fight. These are people who are ready to go and they`ve proven it in the past. They`ve proven it, you know, just, you know, a year and a half ago.\par \par \uc2 They don`t have their leader sending directions and giving specific dates and getting everybody riled up for one big day anymore. That`s the big difference, but the level of violence has been --\par \par \uc2 [13:035:03]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But do you -- the level of violence, but do you have the volume? In other words, did this serve as a cautionary tale for at least many of them or most of them, January 6th, and the fact that so many of them are convicted?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: There was a time, three or four months after January 6th, where these people calmed down and they realized that the feds were watching their spaces. But now they don`t care. That`s the difference. Now they are ready to fight regardless if they`re being watched by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  or not.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So if they don`t care, Frank, what alarm bells go off for law enforcement when there are comments like this, especially given the experience of January 6th?\par \par \uc2 FRANK FIGLIUZZI, MSNBC NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: One would hope so. One would hope that the lesson has been learned from failures leading up to January 6th because, again, January 6th was not a failure of intelligence, but a failure to act on available intelligence. And let`s hope that people as talented and astute as Ben is -- are replicated throughout the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the intelligence community, DHS and --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, please tell me that you think they are.\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: Yes. I think they`re tuned in. I don`t know if they have the bandwidth, and they`ve already pretty much said look, they can`t be everywhere at once. And they can`t see every posting and every private chatroom. There are laws regarding that that actually, you know, allow me and Ben to look at things like this, but not necessarily allow undercover activity, for example, in some of these private chatrooms before things get violent.\par \par \uc2 And so what concerns me the most is that, you know, this is now on steroids with regard to what we saw in the lead up to January 6th. By that I mean we still have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at the helm. We still have FOX News doing what they do. But now, boy, throw in all of the members of Congress, throw in 24/7 news cycle, anger at this. I`ll give you just one example. And Ben may have seen this already, but one thing circulating around today is a comment from former \uc2 NYPD\uc2  commissioner Bernie Kerik, who apparently told the "Daily Mail" that he expects an assassination.\par \par \uc2 He wouldn`t put, quote, "I wouldn`t put it past the Democrats to try to kill \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if the \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid, so-called raid does not result in criminal charges." So we have someone who used to be the commissioner of \uc2 NYPD\uc2  saying you know what, I think they are going to try and kill Trump.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: That`s very dangerous. I would not be surprised if we see the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  issue some kind of bulletin that we are entering a heightened threat environment.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So back when I was covering the Obama White House, I did a lot of coverage of Islamic extremism. And the big problem then was how you differentiate between the talkers and the people who actually might mobilize, who might actually do something like we saw on January 6th. Is that still the biggest challenge, Frank?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: Yes, I mean, look. There seems in some ways to be more and less organization to this and by that I mean you got, what, nine Oath Keepers, five Proud Boys charged with seditious conspiracy. So the message has been sent there. The leadership understands that they are constrained right now. But Ben just gave you a fine example. Somebody already charged, already speaking out.\par \par \uc2 So if you think 850 people charged is a lot, understand there are thousands upon thousands more that are out there waiting and what are they waiting for? They are waiting for the signal from a very desperate former president, who says it`s time. Right? And the more desperate and cornered he gets with all these various legal challenges, the more likely it is that he`s going to give that call to arms, so to speak, and that we`re going to see violence.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, November is just around the corner with regards to all of the potential challenges and chaos that could exist around the midterms.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ben, is that what they are waiting and watching for?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: They need something to rally around. They need a base to rally around. They need a space to be, specifically. The timing of this is I don`t know if this is intentional, but CPAC was last weekend. These people have already dispersed. They went back to where they were, right. There is no upcoming date that I can tell that they are ready to do something, which presents a dynamic threat.\par \par \uc2 You know, January 6th, all of them were in one place. All of the militias were in one place. This is a lot of people who are ready to fight something, but with nowhere to go. So it`s a different kind of threat than January 6th. On January 6th, it was all in one place. Now we have all these riled up people looking for a place to put that anger.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ben Collins, Frank Figliuzzi, we are going to continue this conversation, I`m sure. But thank you, guys, so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 There is a potential serial killer still on the loose in New Mexico. We`ve got new reaction from the families of victims in that recent string of the killings of Muslim men. We are live from Albuquerque, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:43:59]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In just a few hours, the Islamic Center of New Mexico will hold a memorial to honor the four men killed in a recent string of attacks within their community. All four of them are Muslim. And police say the murders were targeted attacks, maybe the work of a serial killer. They have identified this \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  sedan that they believe was involved in the shootings. And now Albuquerque Crime Stoppers are offering a $20,000 reward for information that may lead to an arrest and conviction.\par \par \uc2 Here with me now NBC`s Guad Venegas in Albuquerque for us.\par \par \uc2 Guad, is there anything new from police and are we hearing from the victim`s families?\par \par \uc2 GUAD VENEGAS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Chris, well, yesterday we reached out to the police to see where the investigation was, and they did tell us that they`ve received new tips this after they shared the photo of the \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  sedan that they`ve been looking for. They`ve now received tips from the public. They didn`t tell us what type of tips these are, but they are working with that. So the community is participating. There`s also a portal on their Web site, on the Albuquerque Police Department`s Web site where people can upload photos and videos.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:06]\par \par \uc2 And then of course there`s that reward for $20,000 from Crime Stoppers, additionally an Islamic organization is offering $10,000 to information that could lead to an arrest and conviction. So that`s where the authorities are at.\par \par \uc2 Now when it comes to the families, you can imagine that they are fearful as this investigation continues because they still don`t know who the killer is or the killers are. Now I spoke to the brother of one of the victims, Aftab, who you can see on the screen. His brother told me, you know, for someone to identify a person on the street the way they killed my brother, and know that he is Muslim is not as simple in a place like Albuquerque.\par \par \uc2 He gave me an example. Sometimes he says when they walk down the street, people might confuse them with someone Hispanic. There`s a lot of people in the city who are Hispanic. So he said, for someone to go down the street and kill my brother or kill other members of the Muslim community, he thinks they knew who they were. They had previously identified and followed them.\par \par \uc2 Now this is the brother of one of the victims telling me what he thinks about the investigation and where things could lead. So there`s a lot of questions with the community. We also spoke with the head of the Islamic center here who told us that he`s trying his best to make people feel safe. This is where they are organizing that memorial, by the way. This is part of the conversation that I had with them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AHMAN ASSED, PRESIDENT, LEGAL COUNCIL FOR THE ISLAMIC CENTER OF NEW MEXICO: These are broken, devastated, and reflective a lot of the community that is disheartened. They feel a sense of helplessness. They`re in fear. Their whole world has been flipped upside down. Their community is, you know, it`s very deflated and eagerly awaiting some response, some answer to why.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS: And Ahman has really been the one helping the community here as the head of this Islamic center. And he also told me they are very grateful for the work that local and state police have done helping the community, but he does think that federal officers could help with more intelligence that could help with this investigation, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Guad Venegas, thank you so much for that update.\par \par \uc2 It sounds like a broken record. Tens of millions of Americans are in the midst of a brutal heatwave with intense heat and excessive humidity. But the danger remains real with alerts and warnings in effect all across the country. So dangerous places like California and Washington have adopted workplace rules to address heat exposure. But there may be some good news coming. A cool front is on its way and expected to cool down the country starting in the northeast.\par \par \uc2 The fallout from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of Mar-a-Lago. Will the former president run in 2024 any way? And what voters are saying about the impact as they head to the polls today.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:39]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC TRUMP, DONALD TRUMP`S SON: There`s a guy that got a call this morning, and I called my father to let him know that it happened. So I was involved in this all day. And, you know, welcome to politics in, you know, in the 2000s. They don`t want \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to run and win again in 2024. And Sean, that`s what this is about today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That was Donald Trump`s son Eric last night on FOX News, making the argument that this is beginning to take root on the right. That the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of Mar-a-Lago was purely political.\par \par \uc2 That`s not true. The search followed the law. It involved a warrant, law enforcement, and a judge. But we do have some indication it could motivate Trump as he decides whether to run again in 2024. A person close to the former president told NBC News, quote, "If he wasn`t running before, he is now. It ticks him off that they can do this to him. One way to get it to stop is to control the government again."\par \par \uc2 And we literally just got this in over the commercial break. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  in South Carolina saying, "I talked to the president just about an hour ago. The one thing I can tell you, I believe he was going to run before. I am stronger in that belief now."\par \par \uc2 For more, we`re joined by NBC`s Shaquille Brewster who is covering today`s primaries from Waukesha, Wisconsin, and Charlie Sykes, editor-at-large of the "Bulwark" and an MSNBC contributor is back.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, do you think there`s at least the possibility, maybe a likelihood, this becomes an overriding issue that dominates the midterms? Beyond the economy, beyond Democratic legislative achievements, beyond abortion. Do Republicans start to run on the idea of this is federal government out of control, if they can do this to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , are you safe?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE SYKES, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: They may try to do that. And of course a lot depends on whether or not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  announces. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  announces he`s running for president before the midterms, then he is on the ballot. Republicans will try to weaponize this and try to link this creation of 80,000 new \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents. But keep in mind that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is exactly the kind of polarizing figure the Democrats may need to turn out their base as well.\par \par \uc2 So be careful what you wish for. But there`s no question about it that this will mobilize Republican voters. This will mobilize Trump supporters and it does strengthen Trump`s hand in Republican primaries.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Shaq, you`ve been speaking to voters at the polls in Wisconsin. What are you hearing? Are they even aware of the search? Did it motivate them one way or another? What are they telling you?\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, most of them said they are aware of it or at least they saw the headline of it. But nearly everyone that we spoke to said that it is not influencing how they vote once they hit the ballot box. And it`s reinforcing their ideas, especially in this race where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is looming large. He endorsed one of the candidates Tim Michaels over Rebecca Kleefisch.\par \par \uc2 One thing that we`re seeing in the conversations that I`ve been having is among Republican voters they say they`re upset by the search. And I want you to listen to one of these conversations I had with a voter a couple of minutes ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Does it make you feel any better to know that a judge signed off on it before it happened?\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE MULROY, WAUKESHA, WISCONSIN VOTER: No. I`m tired of it.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: What do you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 MULROY: I feel like there`s a lot of things to be investigated besides Trump in our government. And it just doesn`t seem to happen that way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:55:07]\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: But you also heard her tell me later that that was not what she was thinking about as she was voting. She was thinking about the economy, she was very particular and very interested on abortion. So that`s kind of the frustration that you hear from Republican voters as they go in. And think about other issues and vote on other issues in this competitive primary race.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, Charlie, we`ve only got a minute left. But you`ve said it`s likely to mobilize the GOP. But does it stop what seemed to be for the Democrats having wind at their back? Or does it mobilize them as well? Look, we got to make sure that we protect democracy.\par \par \uc2 SYKES: Oh, no, I mean, look, you know, as bad as last year`s August was for Biden and the Democrats, this year it has been a string of successes. And that doesn`t change. But look, a lot depends on the economy. A lot depends on inflation. And again, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may mobilize the Republican voters. But in Wisconsin, one of the keys is, will Democrats in places like Madison and Milwaukee turn out in big numbers.\par \par \uc2 And the one thing that would really boost that would be for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to make it very, very clear that the midterms is about him, as well as other candidates.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Charlie Sykes, Shaquille Brewster, thank you, guys. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday at 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. Keep it right here. "KATY TUR REPORTS" starts next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Good to be with you. This is Katy Tur.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220810ei8900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for August 8, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Jonathan Lemire, Ali Vitali, Matthew Dowd, Guad  Venegas, Ron Allen, Shaquille Brewster, Antonia Hylton, Kathy Park,  Jonathan Lemire \par \uc2 8,411 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good afternoon, everyone, I`m Chris Jansing live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 And today, we`re watching a tale of two parties heading in two different directions. As we speak, Democrats are on the verge of landing that huge and hugely unexpected legislative win, the major climate change and health care bill. It passed the senate Sunday with the vice president breaking a 50-50 tie.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY): At last, we`ve arrived and we are elated. Every member of my caucus is elated about what happened because we`ve -- really, we`ve changed the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Just likely to pass it this week. And that`s on top of a string of some other very big recent wins. Among them, major gun safety legislation, a computer chip bill, and the veterans` burn pit law. For Republicans, meanwhile, the biggest buzz isn`t on Capitol Hill, it`s coming from CPAC in Texas and some of the most extreme voices in the party.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE LINDELL, CEO, MY PILLOW: The Democratic Party just likes that they were picked to this fraud election, that we skipped right over socialism to communism is where we`re at.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, OHIO REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE: The \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  economy has made it hard for families to have hot dogs with hot dog buns and that is an unacceptable thing.\par \par \uc2 STEVE BANNON\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP ADVISER: We`re at war. We are at war. We`re in a political and ideological war.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ, (R-TX): In the Senate every day, I represent 30 million Texans in my job. It`s like the old Roman Colosseum where you slam on a breastplate and you grab a battle ax and you go fight the barbarians.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was also there, delivering the keynote speech Saturday to huge applause. And the Sunday straw poll showed he`s still the clear favorite among conservatives for 2024, 69 percent to Ron DeSantis`s 24 percent. Trump`s also been trying to flex his political muscle on the campaign trail. He`s endorsed candidates that have won in states like Arizona, Maryland, and Michigan. And he`s got another chance tomorrow in Wisconsin with businessman \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2  running in the Republican gubernatorial primary. We`re going to have a little bit later in the show some more on that.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve got a whole lot to discuss. NBC`s Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vitali joins us now. Jonathan Lemire is White House bureau chief for Politico, and host of "WAY TOO EARLY" on MSNBC. Matthew Dowd is the founder of the group Country Over Party and an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 So, Jonathan, you tweeted out the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  "which called it an extraordinarily productive run for a congress better known for its paralysis." What cured the paralysis and is this it now until after the midterms?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN LEMIRE, MSNBC HOST: This is a huge run of success for this White House and the Democratic-controlled Congress. Let`s reflect back to the beginning of the Biden administration when he and his team really embraced the comparisons to Franklin Delano Roosevelt and Lyndon Johnson suggesting that this president was going to have as huge of a consequential term as his. And yet that didn`t happen, at least for a while because there`s a key difference. FDR and LBJ had massive -- at least to start, massive advantages in Congress. This president had a 50-50 Senate and only a couple advantages -- seat advantage in the House.\par \par \uc2 But what we`ve seen here is a remarkable run. Some of it done in secret stunning to many even the closest Washington observers that deal between Schumer and Manchin. But it`s not just reconciliation, which goes to the House on Friday. It`s the Chips Act. It`s the PACT act. It was the gun reform legislation over a few months ago.\par \par \uc2 The Biden White House also can say they claimed -- they killed America`s most wanted man, the leader of al-Qaeda. So this has been a remarkable run for this president. He himself was in COVID isolation for some of it, but White House aides quick to point out that was -- he still was working the phones, he called more than a dozen Democratic senators over the weekend to try to close the deal. And there is hope now. Democrats have a big platform from which to run in November, and that the wind now may be a Joe Biden`s back and looking to turn around those slumping poll numbers.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, to that point, Ali, before members went home or go home to their districts, what are you hearing? What`s the expectation about what all this means for their chances of holding the Senate or even the House this fall?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: That`s the open question. But I got to tell you, Chris, as members were leaving yesterday, I`ll be it in a very sleepy fashion because they worked all night overnight Saturday into Sunday. They were saying that they do feel like they have new momentum, several of them saying to me that they feel like when they look back on their time in Congress, that this is going to be some of the most significant legislation that they`ve had the opportunity to work on. Because of the sweeping pieces of it that touch economics, that touch health care, that touch cost reduction on drugs and capping the price of insulin, all of those caps -- all of those pieces of legislation that Democrats were prioritizing, yes, they will readily admit to you that they didn`t get everything that they wanted here but no sooner did Democrats pass this on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 The new ads from Democratic groups were up on the airwaves in states like Arizona, Georgia, and Nevada, touting the fact that Democrats got this done. And, of course, it wasn`t always a given that they were going to be able to do so. You and I covered how many rounds of negotiations on Build Back Better over the course of the last year that watch people like Joe Manchin and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  continue to whittle down what was actually going to be able to be in this package.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, we see now where they landed, about $430 billion in spending and about 300 billion in deficit reduction. And it allows Democrats to sort of campaign on both fronts, proactively saying that they were accomplishing policy agenda items while also proactively saying that they are speaking to what is arguably, the number one or number two issue for voters, is the economy and inflation. Republicans going to try to leverage that as well, but Democrats at least now can say that they have actively taken steps on that front too.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, I think we just heard that Matthew, you know, you can`t afford a hot dog and you can`t afford a hot dog bun and that`s not acceptable. What they`re saying, a lot of Republicans falsely is that this is a big tax increase for the middle class. They`re talking about things they`re clearly going to take home on the campaign trail, I doubt they`ll take home the fact that they squashed the idea of helping people who need it pay for their insulin, but do they have a message that will work?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW DOWD, MSNBC POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR & ANALYST: Well, I think that`s the 92-day question, which is how many days until Election Day? I mean, if you had looked at this and asked me two months ago, I would have said that Democrats were on the verge of losing the Senate and we`re going to lose badly in the House. Today, when you look at all the trajectory of everything, even before the win this weekend, it already moved to a more likelihood that Democrats would keep the Senate and the House is now becoming -- is becoming competitive in this environment.\par \par \uc2 And so I actually think with everything that was said just before me, it not only provides a positive message for Democrats, it provides a great contrast message to Republicans. They will talk -- Republicans will talk about inflation but as an -- as gas prices come down, and as we see it, we`ll see an announcement on what inflation is. My expectation is the inflation rate is going to come down primarily driven by gas prices in this.\par \par \uc2 Democrats can argue, on one hand, we`re for competency, they`re crazy. And then the other hand said we`re standing for the Constitution, whether that`s freedoms like Roe v. Wade, and that and the other side, the GOP wants to destroy our democracy, you know, wants to corrupt our democracy in this. So I think it provides a great argument for Democrats to run on his last 92 days, and the trajectory was already moving towards them. And so I think they`re going to fill the wind at their back.\par \par \uc2 The great thing for Democrats is they have not only a message that motivates their base, they have a message that persuades independents that they need to win in this election. So that -- Republicans will push back on the economy and inflation, but it`s much less of a resonant issue today than it was two months ago.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, again to that contrast, at the same time, Democrats were celebrating those big legislative wins. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was at CPAC. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: As we gathered tonight, our country is being destroyed more from the inside then out. America is on the edge of an abyss. The streets of our Democrat-run cities are drenched with the blood of innocent victims. People are walking outside and getting shot in the head.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, one thing you`ll say for him, Matthew, he`s the same \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who won the presidency. Do Republicans really believe it can work again or just the folks at CPAC?\par \par \uc2 DOWD: And I`d be curious to see what Jonathan says on this. But I think if you gave Republicans truth serum, they would say I wish \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would go away and not be any part of the next 92 days. Donald Trump`s popularity rating is lower than Joe Biden`s. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is more of an anchor on Republicans today than Joe Biden`s numbers are on Democrats. And every poll proves that out. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , for some reason cannot look anything but in the rearview mirror. He`s looking back at the last election. He`s looking back at everything.\par \par \uc2 And so I think voters want to vote what`s their present, what`s going on in the present, and what is their future look like. So if I were a Republican, I would hope \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which he won`t do, he would just sort of fade away at least for the next 90 days. But he`s not going to. And I think every time \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  shows up to an event like this, it`s a benefit to the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You do see the lingering impact, Jonathan, of what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  brought, the \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  praying over a guy who was a Capitol rioter in a cage, praying a little bit of performance art there. To Matthew`s question to you, what do you make of all of it?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: Well, I`m not much into the prediction business, but I can pretty well bet that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not going to fade away over the next 92 days. But Matthew`s right, there are very few Republicans that want to see him right now play such a visible role. No one outside of the few who collect their paychecks at Mar-a-Lago or Bedminster wants him to declare his candidacy before the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Republicans think that would just be a terrible distraction, they think that Trump would become the story, and will distract from what they still think could be a good November because of inflation and so on, though, GOP strategists who our earlier conversation are far less confident about it now than they were a couple of months ago.\par \par \uc2 But Trump`s not going anywhere. And certainly, his poll numbers have taken a hit this summer, largely in part, we believe because of those January 6 hearings. But he is still the loudest voice in the room. And there aren`t many Republicans who were willing to challenge him publicly. Privately, yes, they don`t -- they don`t want him out there right now. They`d rather he wait until 2023 and see where things are. There are certainly some of the GOP who would like to turn the page and move on to another candidate. Trump seems unlikely to oblige that. The question is when not -- if he jumps in, but when?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali and Matthew, thank you. Jonathan, we`re going to talk with you a little later on in the hour. Joining me now, Democratic Senator Michael Bennet of Colorado. Good to see you. Thank you for being on. Your party named it the Inflation Reduction Act, and arguably the -- against the odds you got it passed. When are people going to start feeling the impact of this bill, Senator?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MICHAEL BENNET, (D-CO): Well, I think they`re feeling it already. I was on the phone this morning with my daughter, Caroline, she`s 22 years old. And the joy in her voice that we had actually finally done something for climate change, I think that`s going to give Caroline`s generation hope that they haven`t had in a very long time that Washington is actually trying to help build a little bit of a better world for them finally. And this bill which, you know, substantially lowers health care caused by -- like by capping drug prices for seniors a $2,000, the biggest climate investment in our history, closing tax loopholes on the wealthiest people in the largest corporations, and reducing the deficit on top of everything else.\par \par \uc2 That is a very different piece of legislation than what Republican majorities bring, which is usually just cutting taxes for the richest people and then spending their time putting right-wing judges on the courts that are now reversing Roe versus -- have now reversed Roe v. Wade. So I think there`s a big difference coming out of here. And I think my own view is that the democracy is at stake this November. And I think we just gave our democracy a huge shot in the arm.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: No surprise to anyone. Republicans see this very differently. Let me play some examples of that, Senator.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL, (R-KY): For yet another -- yet another reckless taxing and spending spree. Democrats have already robbed American families once through inflation, and now their solution is to rob American families. Yes, the second time.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): The American Rescue Plan, remember that one? That was supposed to make us -- make everything better will it became a recession plan. This is going to make everything worse.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You have any concern, Senator, that this could make people`s economic situations worse instead of better?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: I don`t -- I don`t. And I -- and I would say that another piece of good news this week was we had more than 500,000 jobs created in the economy. So I`m not sure what my colleague \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  was talking about. And then with respect to \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , I mean, his position is the most absurd position of all. There`s nobody in American history who`s putting more debt on the balance sheet of this country than \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . But the way he`s done it is by cutting taxes for rich people over and over again, and saying that somehow that was going to trickle down to the American people and pay for itself. All lies. These are all lies.\par \par \uc2 And there are things I would have rather done here that we -- that we weren`t able to do, like, reverse the Trump tax cuts on the wealthiest Americans because 52 percent of that went to the richest people, I would have loved to make permanent, my bill, to extend the Child Tax Credit, which cut childhood poverty in half last year, reduced hunger by 25 percent. We didn`t do that but we close some important loopholes. And unlike the Trump bill that McConnell supported, which added $2 trillion to the deficit, this one`s reducing our deficit by $300 billion. And I think, you know, they can`t stand what the facts really are.\par \par \uc2 And it`s true too with the other stuff. I mean, think about it. It`s not just this bill, it`s the bipartisan infrastructure bill. It`s the -- it`s the chips bill that`s going to bring back manufacturing here to the United States of America. It`s the PACT Act for our veterans also bipartisan, the bipartisan gun bill, and even postal reform, something that`s really important to rural Colorado. That looks triumphant compared to any legislative -- any legislative agenda that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  ever had, which is to say none except cutting taxes for the richest people and blowing up the deficits and then blaming that on Democrats. That`s not going to work in this election.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And those are the arguments that you and your fellow Democrats are going to be able to make on the campaign trail. Your Republican opponent says you are raising taxes on struggling Colorado residents while you`ve been getting rich. He says you`ve increased your wealth by $31 million since you got to the Senate. I think part of his implication is how can you relate to the average person in your state.\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Well, first of all, that -- I wish that were true about my wealth. I don`t know where he got those numbers. And I know when my wife and kids see that they`re going to wonder where I`ve been hiding it because it`s not remotely true. Second, this is a guy who literally posted on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  a picture of him and his wife riding their horses to buy sushi in his fancy neighborhood. So I don`t know, you know, who needs to prove -- who`s in touch with working Coloradans.\par \par \uc2 But I can tell you this, anybody, who thinks that the Trump tax cuts for the wealthy were somehow a tax cut for working Americans is delusional. And anybody who thinks that this bill raises taxes on working people is delusional unless your definition of working people are the richest hedge fund managers in America and the largest corporations, corporations that make more than a billion dollars in income and haven`t been paying any taxes.\par \par \uc2 The American people are sick and tired of these folks, you know, paying one and a half percent, paying zero percent, paying 2 percent. And all this says is you got to pay at least 15 percent. And by the way, that`s less than school teachers are paying in the state of Colorado. That`s a debate I`m happy to have going into this election. And I`m looking forward to it because I think we need to expose the Republican rhetoric for the lies that it is. It`s done so much damage to this country.\par \par \uc2 Think about 50 years of trickle-down economics and what that has wrought. It`s creating an economy that`s worked incredibly well for the top 10 percent, but hasn`t worked for anybody else. What this chips bill is a vote for a better future for America, that`s what the bill is that we voted on this weekend. The Inflation Reduction Act is about creating a renaissance in energy manufacturing here in the United States of America. It looks a lot better than Mitch McConnell`s agenda I think.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Colorado Senator Michael Bennet, a pleasure to have you on the program, sir. Thank you so much. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Thanks for having me, Chris. Thank you. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: On the heels of that bill and what it means for addressing climate change, as Senator Bennet just said, we`re monitoring the president`s trip to Kentucky this hour, President Biden touring the deadly flood devastation in Kentucky. He`s meeting with families and what we can expect to hear from him when he makes remarks there. But first, new warnings of a possible serial killer on the loose in New Mexico as police and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search for a suspect in a recent string of murders of Muslim men. You`re watching "CHRIS JANSING REPORTS" only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Police and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  are searching for a suspect in a string of murders, leaving New Mexico`s Muslim community on edge fearing there`s a serial killer on the loose. Four Muslim men shot and killed in Albuquerque most recently on Friday, when a man was murdered only hours after attending the funeral of two other victims. Police are calling these targeted killings, and now say they have a lead on a vehicle of interest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CECILY BARKER, DEPUTY CHIEF OF POLICE, ALBUQUERQUE, NEW MEXICO: If you see a dark-colored four-door sedan, Volkswagen Passat or Jetta, we encourage you to call the police. We urge people not to take things into your own hands. If you see this vehicle or you know who might possibly be driving it, call police.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: For more on this, we`re joined by NBC`s Guad Venegas in Albuquerque and Zainab Chaudry with the \uc2 Council on American Islamic Relations\uc2 . Guad, beyond what we just saw, which obviously they`re looking for a particular vehicle, what else do we know? What`s the latest there?\par \par \uc2 GUAD VENEGAS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Chris, well, I will add that that vehicle if anyone has information, they can also upload photos or videos that could lead to you know -- could help the \uc2 FBI\uc2  or local authorities. They`ve set up -- Albuquerque police has set up a website where they can upload these videos or photos that could help -- that could help them. Now, if you see in the photo, there`s also markings on the rims. They`re hoping that this can help the public identify the vehicle by pointing out these markings at the rims have.\par \par \uc2 So, other than that, they`ve released the name of the last victim. You`ve shown photos of the victims. We know that three of them were originally from Pakistan. All four of the men that have been killed are members of the Muslim community. And you know, if you look at the people in Albuquerque, you`re talking about roughly half a million people in this city. Less than 1 percent of those are members of the Muslim community. So that`s why this is worrying those members of that community and it`s just horrific to see how these men were shot and killed.\par \par \uc2 We know that, as you mentioned, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is involved, and is working with local authorities. Also, they`ve added more patrol units to schools and to areas frequented by the Muslim community here in Albuquerque to try and help people feel like they are safe because as you can imagine, members of the community are scared with what`s happened. In fact, one of the family members of the last man that was killed told NBC News that at the funeral of the other two men, they told him to be careful. And now he is the latest victim. But as of now, we know that they`re asking for the public`s help with any information that could lead to the arrest of anyone responsible for this, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That`s almost unbelievable. Zainab, the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  spoke to a Muslim man in Albuquerque who said he`s afraid to step outside his home to water his plants or retrieve books from his car or even venture out onto his balcony. And another man who attended the same mosque as all four of the victims says he may never return, citing a fear of becoming bait. How would you describe the feeling in the Muslim community right now?\par \par \uc2 ZAINAB CHAUDREY, MARYLAND DIRECTOR, COUNCIL ON AMERICAN ISLAMIC RELATIONS: You know, obviously, this is a very small tight-knit community, about 5000 Muslims, as mentioned, in Albuquerque. And they are on edge. There is a very real sense of fear and concern for an individual`s safety.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that Keir (PH) wants to mention is that it`s really important to be prepared and be informed and you know, just take precautions, but to not let fear win. When perpetrators of these kinds of crimes do these kinds of actions, horrific acts of violence, that`s their objective, they want our communities to live in fear.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 We as a nation cannot let hate and violence win, we must stand united in solidarity against all forms of hatred and fear cannot win.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In your organization, as I said the \uc2 Council on American Islamic Relations\uc2 , is offering a $10,000 reward for information on the killings. And this weekend, New Mexico`s Governor \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2  announced that she sent additional state police officers to Albuquerque to work with the police and \uc2 FBI\uc2 . From what you`ve been able to observe, do you think enough is being done right now?\par \par \uc2 CHAUDREY: Well, we definitely want to acknowledge the response from local state and federal officials and agencies who have been stepping up to provide support to the local Muslim community, to the local mosques. Obviously, in these kinds of circumstances, it`s very important for them to know that they are supported and they`re not alone in this. And even the interfaith allies who can reach out and offer support to them would be reassuring in this kind of a sense.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, more needs to be done. The perpetrator of this crime is still that large and until they -- he or they are brought to justice, he, she, or they are brought to justice, there`s going to be a lot of unanswered questions. And right now, there are families who are mourning the loss of their loved ones. As you mentioned -- as was mentioned earlier, one of the victims has he literally attended the victim of two -- the funerals of two victims on Friday, and a few short hours later, he himself became the fourth victim. And it`s just horrific to know what the community is going through at the moment.\par \par \uc2 But we do acknowledge the response from the governor. Her reiterating the fact that New Mexico is home to Muslims and that Muslims are welcomed there. And this is actually an opportunity for other elected officials on every level of the government to stand strong and unequivocally lend support to not just the Muslim community, but all communities who historically have been marginalized and who suffer from hate and violence.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Zainab Chaudry, Guad Venegas, I`m sure we`ll continue to get updates from you as well. Thank you both so much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we`ll go live to a courthouse in Georgia where sentencing is underway in the federal case against the men convicted of killing Ahmaud Arbery. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The first of three men convicted of murdering 25-year-old Ahmaud Arbery has been sentenced to another life term plus 10 years on federal hate crime charges. A Georgia judge sentenced Travis McMichael this morning and is now deciding on sentences for his father Gregory and then their neighbor William "Roddie" Bryan.\par \par \uc2 A federal jury back in February convicted the three white men concluding they targeted Arbery out of racial hatred. All three had already been given life sentences after their state trial.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ron Allen is outside the courthouse in Brunswick, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 What`s the latest from inside, Ron? I know you`ve been listening.\par \par \uc2 RON ALLEN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, the hearing for Greg McMichael sentencing is ongoing. And we expect the judge to hand down that sentence at any moment. We`ve been hearing impact statements from Ahmaud Arbery`s family, from his mother and father and aunts and others, and we also heard the defense making a case for Greg McMichael. They are saying that he is a 66-year-old man with health problems. They are arguing for some leniency.\par \par \uc2 They are arguing, as was the case with his Trevor McMichael, the son, for him to serve some of his time in federal detention instead of state detention. They are concerned, his lawyers are, about his safety and worried about whether or not he will even survive being in that kind of an environment because of his health concerns.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, Ahmaud Arbery`s family is arguing for the maximum penalty, life in prison, and for it to be served in state prison. They see the McMichaels` efforts to try and move to federal prison as them trying to pick their own prison, pick their own punishment and the Arbery family strongly, vehemently rejects that. In some of their impact statements they really tore to the fact that he is a father, and said that as a father you should have known better and as a human being you should have done better in the situation.\par \par \uc2 The only other extenuating circumstance perhaps in this sentencing that`s different is that Gregory McMichael, the father, did not pull the trigger. His son did. So there is some difference there. But basically, they still face the same charges and the expectation is that he will be sentenced to life in prison shortly. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ron Allen, thank you so much for that update.\par \par \uc2 One person is dead, 17 firefighters are missing, and more than 120 are injured in Cuba after oil tanks struck by lightning exploded. The fire that broke out on Friday night is still burning. The original fire spread to a second tank of crude oil setting off several explosions. Both Mexico and Venezuela have sent help to fight those flames.\par \par \uc2 Well, the travel misery keeps piling up. Heavy storms cancelling 1500 flights this weekend with at least 1300 more delayed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I just don`t understand how this is OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s a broken system and then people get left having no idea where to go, what to do, no answers, and no compensation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, the nasty weather a major headache on top of crew and pilot staffing shortages and have meant fewer flights just as more and more people are taking to the skies. But there may be some better news on the horizon. The Department of Transportation is proposing new guidelines that would give passengers a refund if an airline changes their departure or arrival time by at least three hours.\par \par \uc2 A major test for the GOP in less than 24 hours. We`ll get the latest on Wisconsin`s high stakes race for governor with the former president and vice president at odds again.\par \par \uc2 And new blowback in Indiana after the state became the first to pass an abortion ban since Roe was overturned. How political leaders, activists, and some of the state`s biggest employers are fighting back.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:38:15]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Another test for the GOP comes in less than 24 hours in Wisconsin. That`s where the Republican race to take on Democratic Governor \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  is the one to watch. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his former VP \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  once again backing different candidates.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Shaquille Brewster is in Waukesha, Wisconsin, and joins me now. What`s the lay of the land there, Shaq?\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Chris. Well, this is a race to take on Democratic Governor \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  here in this incredibly important battleground state of Wisconsin. And it`s a test of the direction that Republican voters here want to take this party. And that`s because you not only have former President Trump endorsing Tim Michaels, someone who`s painting himself as a political outsider. He`s a construction executive, but he`s going up against the former lieutenant governor, \uc2 Rebecca Kleefisch\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kleefisch is backed not just by Vice President Pence, but she`s also backed by the popular, at least among Republicans here in Wisconsin, the popular former governor, Scott Walker. So when you talk to Republican voters, as I have been doing all day, they sound a little bit stressed about their decision because you have these national figures, these big Republican names weighing in on this primary race.\par \par \uc2 I want you to listen to some of those conversations.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m going to go with Trump. That`s just -- that`s my thing.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: You`re going with what Trump suggests.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Over Scott Walker.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don`t really think the endorsements affect my vote. I just think that it would be good to have someone leading this state that has not been connected to politics at all in their life. So I think that`s why I`m making the vote for Michaels.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: So not only is there almost that proxy battle there, but there are national implications to what happens here. The Republican governor, as I mentioned, will go up against Democrat \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 . Evers has been vetoing many of the Republican election-related bills passed by the legislature here in the state of Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:07]\par \par \uc2 That`s something that both candidates say that they`ll do. They`ll pass any bill that the Republican legislature here signs that restricts election laws that makes it harder to vote absentee for example as we`ve been seeing. They say they will sign those pieces of legislation. So when you talk about a state like Wisconsin, a state that President Biden won by about 21,000 votes, that former President Trump won by about 27,000 votes, it`s a state that election laws truly matter in terms of those national implications. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Another referendum on the big lie. Shaq Brewster, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 And today the blowback continues fast and furious after Indiana passed the first state ban on abortion post Roe. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " put it this way. As some of the biggest employers objected to the restrictions, Democratic leaders strategized ways to amend or repeal the law and abortion rights activists made plans to arrange alternative locations for women seeking procedures. Here`s what Indiana lawmaker said ahead of the vote.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. ANN VERMILION (R-IN): Each one of us legislators could be raped. We could have a piece of our soul ripped from us and be violated. But only 23 percent of us can know what it`s like to fear that that horrific moment could create a baby.\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. MAUREEN BAUER (D-IN): We are told by the Republican supermajority that you cannot legislate moral behavior when it comes to issues like gun violence in America. But in a matter of two weeks, we will legislate a way a woman`s God-given free will to make her own decisions.\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. CINDY LEDBETTER (R-IN): When I moved to Indiana 30 years ago, I did not have access to affordable women`s health care and contraceptive services. As a result, I had an unplanned pregnancy. I ask this body not to continue to ignore the women in this body and most importantly the minority women in this body who continue to speak knowledge and truth to this matter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: For more, we`re joined by NBC`s Antonia Hylton and Katie Paris, founder of "Red Wine and Blue," a group that mobilizes suburban women.\par \par \uc2 Good to have both of you here. Antonia, you`ve been following the backlash to this. Both the political and the economic. What are you finding out?\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, on the political front, what we`re going to see now is Democrats take what we`ve seen happen in Indiana and draw a contrast to what we just saw happen in Kansas. A more traditionally conservative state where voters just rejected this kind of restriction on abortion access. They are going to be highlighting that this kind of legislation is often deeply unpopular. And they are going to be attacking their opponents on it.\par \par \uc2 On the economic front, we`re going to see companies like \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2  coming forward and saying that they are now concerned about the economic impacts over all of this and their ability to attract and retain employees, families that would otherwise have been interested in settling and working in Indiana. Take, for example, also Cummins, a major engine manufacturer that just released a very similar statement.\par \par \uc2 So already we`re seeing the economic fallout. But for some, this is now too little, too late. Take a listen to a conversation we had with Dr. Tiffany Green at the \uc2 University of Wisconsin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. TIFFANY GREEN, UNIVERSITY OF WISCONSIN ASSISTANT PROFESSOR: It`s heartening that businesses are coming out and talking about this. To be frank, they`re a little bit too late. Indiana has had really heavy abortion restrictions long before the Dobbs decision dropped. And now this feels like a little bit not too little too late, but it`s just not enough.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Keep in mind, lawmakers and business owners in the state have already been feeling like Indiana has been at the forefront of the national conversation. And that`s coming on the heels of this story of a 10-year-old who was raped in Ohio and had to travel to the state to access abortion care. And it`s really raised the stakes for people for what we may see happen in Indiana going forward.\par \par \uc2 And not only that, but we`re going to see people, you know, on the side of pro-abortion access. They are going to be, you know, trying to figure out their avenues politically and legally had, but even for folks who are antiabortion, they don`t think this legislation went far enough. So they want to fight for more.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Katie, let`s talk about the politics of this. Your group was founded on the frustration, even anger of suburban women against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , right. And for a lot of them, the fight was exhausting. You and I talked about the fact that some of them had to step away for a while because they were so exhausted. Has the abortion issue reenergized that critical voting bloc?\par \par \uc2 KATIE PARIS, FOUNDER, "RED, WHITE AND BLUE": There`s no question about it. This was never just about Trump. This is about extremism and suburban women don`t like it. Whether it`s these tall abortion bans, whether it`s book bans, whether it`s arming teachers. You know, the suburban women I know are not even seeing this as a partisan election anymore. It`s about chaos versus common sense. It`s about extremism versus just being mainstream moms, just being an American.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:01]\par \par \uc2 So I think that we`re going to see the consequences of what is happening all across this country to be huge in November. You know, in Kansas, the people had a chance to speak. In Indiana, the legislature seeing what the people would say if they let the people speak, they said we`re not going to let them. We are going to mandate, enforce this all-out abortion ban on the people. And it`s only making women madder.\par \par \uc2 And it`s so deeply personal. I`m so glad you played the clips of those legislators, those women legislators in Indiana speaking to this issue. It is so deeply personal. This is a visceral issue. It impacts all of us personally. Our daughters, our children, everyone in our lives. And we`re speaking up in personal ways and we`re talking to our friends about it. We`re talking to our networks.\par \par \uc2 And yes, there might have been some exhaustion after Trump, but we know that for the sake of our kids and for our communities, we got to set that aside and you saw that energy come out in Kansas and you`re going to see it come out all across this country as Republicans just become more and more chaotic and more and more extreme.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Katie, we don`t have a lot of time, but I do want to ask you about the second part of this, which is that suburban women have a lot of economic impact as well, right. A lot of them they control a lot of money that gets spent. Are we going to see them use that? Are they paying attention to the corporations that are taking action depending on what`s happening in their individual state legislatures?\par \par \uc2 PARIS: You know, it`s so interesting to me because politicians in Indiana, these Republican politicians, seem to think that they can get away with ignoring the people, women, voters just fine. Corporations know that they can`t. We are their customers. And you`re right about the purchasing power of suburban women. We control the household budget. And that is going hit their bottom line. So they are very aware that we`re making those decisions and acting accordingly.\par \par \uc2 And yes, we`re paying attention. Just like the governor of Indiana tried to sign -- he did sign this abortion ban on the dead of night, in the middle of August, hoping no one would notice. Guess what, we noticed. And corporations know we`re watching, too. So \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2 , let`s see more of it. All across the country. Let`s see companies step up and represent and recognize and show that you`re listening to us and that you don`t want to lose us as customers. Republicans are going to lose us as voters. Let`s see if they make us (INAUDIBLE) decision.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Katie Paris, Antonia Hylton, thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 What we expect to hear from President Biden, oh, in just about 15 minutes or so as he tours the devastation left behind from those deadly floods in Kentucky. What more can the White House do for those affected? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:37]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We have a breaking update on the hate crime sentencing of three white men convicted of murdering 25-year-old Ahmaud Arbery. Just moments ago, a judge sentenced Gregory McMichael to another life sentence plus 10 years on federal hate crimes charges. His son Travis received the same sentence this morning and their neighbor, William "Roddie" Bryan, will be sentenced next.\par \par \uc2 Minutes from now the president and first lady will be meeting with families impacted by the devastating floods in Kentucky that killed at least 37 people. They`ve been surveilling damage in the area where rescue efforts are still underway.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Kathy Park, who is in Lost Creek, Kentucky, for us and bring back Jonathan Lemire, political White House bureau chief and host of "WAY TOO EARLY" here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 Kathy, tell us a little bit about what`s happening there right now.\par \par \uc2 KATHY PARK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Chris. Good afternoon to you. So the president just moments ago had an opportunity to see the damage firsthand. And his initial expression was that of shock. Everywhere you turn you see homes that have been leveled. Some of them completely lifted off their foundation and the floodwaters were so intense it even pulled some of these large vehicles, cars, away from the original location.\par \par \uc2 So it certainly is a long road to recovery. Officials are saying, as you mentioned, the death toll right now is at 37. Two people are unaccounted for but there has been so much damage to the infrastructure in this community, thousands of people are still without water. The power is slowly coming back on so the power outage numbers are a lot lower than what we saw several days ago but tens of thousands of residents are still under a boil water advisory.\par \par \uc2 But, Chris, I have to say, you know, being out here for several hours now, there is a lot of positivity, all things considered. They are still very resilient in the face of this natural disaster. We have seen volunteers coming together, but it is starting to warm up. Things are starting to dry out. It certainly is a muddy mess that has been left behind by the floodwaters. So it is certainly making the clean-up efforts even that much more difficult.\par \par \uc2 And you might have heard forecasters are expecting even more severe weather in the forecast over the next couple of days, which could only complicate the recovery efforts, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s almost cruel, Jonathan, to think about that. And you`ve already got, what, more than three dozen lives lost, the worst flooding in the state`s history. What would you expect to hear from the president in just a little bit?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN LEMIRE, POLITICO WHITE HOUSE BUREAU CHIEF: Well, already the president has vowed to put the full force of the federal government behind the recovery and rebuilding efforts. He said that they would do everything the White House could do under the scope of the law and said, if we need to, we`ll change some laws. That might be little Biden hyperbole but certainly this is something where the federal government and the White House has broad authority to cut through red tape and fund the rebuilding efforts.\par \par \uc2 And also what we`re going to see now is a hat that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has to wear sadly quite a bit, which is comforter-in-chief, consoler-in-chief. And he will meet the families of those impacted, those who lost loved ones or lost their homes, who may still be wondering where their loved ones are, if they`re still in the missing.\par \par \uc2 President Biden speaks to grief like few others we`ve ever had in public life, in part because of the losses he has suffered in his own life.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:05]\par \par \uc2 And we certainly -- I`ve been on a number of these trips with him over the years, to disaster sites or mass shootings and he hits the same note. He`ll tell those people that, though anguish is what they feel now, someday, slowly, things will get better and that the memory of a loved one will bring a smile to their face, rather than a tear to their eye, paraphrasing thereabouts. This is something that the president sadly is good at and as well as he will make sure that the people there in Kentucky know that the federal government has not forgotten about them.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And Jonathan, Biden is with the Democratic governor who was elected by a thin margin. We can`t ignore the fact that often these kind of events become political. You want to focus on the humanity of it, the lives that are lost, the rebuilding that needs to be done but there is a political component to this. In our closing seconds, speak to that.\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: Yes, certainly. And sometimes political figures pay a price. \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  thinks his presidential campaign was derailed when he hugged President \uc2 Obama\uc2  when \uc2 Obama\uc2  traveled to New Jersey in the wake of Superstorm Sandy. Certainly here, you know, Kentuckians, let`s hope that there isn`t a political price to pay for the Democratic governor there who will be with the president of his own party who are trying to focus simply on rebuilding on helping.\par \par \uc2 And the governor has been very quick to praise President Biden and the full force of the federal government. We should also note so has Senate minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , also a Kentucky native. He has also praised the federal government. So hopefully at least for now politics have stayed out of this one.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That rare example. Jonathan Lemire, Kathy Park, thanks to both of you.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every week day at 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. "KATY TUR REPORTS" starts next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Good to be with you. I`m Katy Tur.\par \par \uc2 Historic after a mammoth vote-a-rama weekend in the Senate, Joe Biden`s Inflation Reduction Act is now on the verge of passing.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220809ei8800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for August 7, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Julie Tsirkin, Richard Lui \par \uc2 6,642 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:00:00]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening welcome to POLITICSNATION. Good evening to POLITICSNATION. Tonight`s lead, breaking news lead, fight for the future.\par \par \uc2 Right now, Senate Democrats are basking in a historic win with their landmark spending bill passing just hours ago, 51 to 50. President Biden wasting no time applauding the passage of what among many things may become the largest investment in climate change intervention the nation has ever seen.\par \par \uc2 The timing is almost cinematic as every region of the nation has been baked by extreme heat. This summer, many states terrorized by extreme weather disasters that have caused billions of dollars and hundreds of lives. And coast to coast, we are bracing for more as fire season in the western states has opened with more record blazes while federal scientists warn of an above average and above normal hurricane season still threatening the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 Yet for all the unprecedented climate action promised by the Inflation Reduction Act, it is still just a step towards a sustainable future, the core which is moving the country away from fossil fuels and substantially curving the nation`s emissions over the next eight years.\par \par \uc2 But even with this legislative battle over climate action nearly complete, is America ready for the changes called for? And what about our most vulnerable communities, those already more susceptible to the kind of economic and public health disruptions promised by unrestrained climate change? Big questions about our climate future tonight on POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 In a moment we will talk with the perfect guest, the administrator of the \uc2 EPA\uc2 , \uc2 Michael Regan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, first, let`s go to Capitol Hill where the Senate just passed the Inflation Reduction Act earlier this afternoon. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  broke the tie passing the bill at 51 to 50 vote, the Inflation Reduction Act, where it`s expected to pass there later this week and then be sent to President Joe Biden`s desk for his signature.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get right to NBC`s Julie Tsirkin on Capitol Hill. Now, Julie, the Senate has passed this historic bill where Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  broke the tie. The bill will now go to the House. What can you tell us about what is included in this bill and what it will mean for the average American watching?\par \par \uc2 JULIE TSIRKIN, NBC NEWS REPORTER: Yes, Reverend Al. I asked Vice President Harris that exact question, what will this bill that is reducing the cost of prescription drugs, that is investing over $300 billion in climate and energy in this country and closing some loopholes for those who make the most money in this country, large corporations, what will it mean for Americans? She said this will mean the world for American families, and people in this country who have been longing for legislation to address the problems they face.\par \par \uc2 Now, this bill does a lot for climate. It is the first step, as you mentioned, Democrats, like Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , really unhappy that it doesn`t go far enough. But with a 50/50 Senate, as you know well, they need all 50 Senate Democrats to get on board to pass anything. And that`s why we saw some last minute back and forth today to appease Senator Sinema, changes they needed to make to just get this bill done before they head out of town.\par \par \uc2 It has a methane reduction program, which will help farmers and ranchers being able to move to clean energy, giving them credits for doing so. It has electric vehicle credits in it as well. It also transitions those who have been used to having coal jobs, especially in West Virginia, to more cleaner energy jobs. It also makes historic investments in our drug prescription policies as well, Medicare, for the first time, being able to negotiate those prices. They didn`t get, of course, everything they wanted in the bill, but all in all Democrats happy that this bill does accomplish all of those things and has a couple hundred billion dollars for deficit reduction as well.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Julie, let me ask you this quickly, there is actually a big change to the bill, a $35 a month cap on insulin was killed after all but seven GOP senators voted it down. What are senators saying about this because that`s an important issue for a lot of people?\par \par \uc2 TSIRKIN: Yes, it is huge. The cost of insulin rising over the last couple decades, Democrats seeking to address this. And they were able to do it but only for seniors who are covered under Medicare.\par \par \uc2 Now, they had it for private insurers in their bill as well but Republicans, as you mentioned, blocked that, other than those seven Republicans, including Senator Collins, who was negotiating a bipartisan insulin cap before the bill came to the floor. But, Democrats, like Senator \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2 , taking to the floor, calling out Republicans for blocking this.\par \par \uc2 And this was really their goal. They wanted to have this fight on the floor so that it wasn`t on them when this came out of the bill, it was on the Republicans who ended up blocking this provision. But, still, for over 3 million Americans who rely on insulin to help care for their diabetes who are over the age of 65, this is big.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Thank you, NBC`s Julia Tsirkin on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  Administrator \uc2 Michael Regan\uc2 . Administrator Regan, we thank you first for joining us tonight. And before I get into more specifics about the challenges because of the \uc2 EPA\uc2  that we`re facing because of climate disruption, I have to, of course, ask about what appears to be a breakthrough on climate policy in the Senate, the Inflation Reduction Act passing. The bill, of course, contained the largest federal effort devoted to climate change, nearly $400 billion to curb emissions and move away from fossil fuels. As head of our \uc2 EPA\uc2 , at this point in history, where does the bill do the most good?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL REGAN\uc2 , EPA ADMINISTRATOR: Well, Reverend, thank you for having me and thank you for asking that question. This is historic. This is a big day for our country. This is a big day for the president. Because when he ran for this seat, the presidency, he said he pledged that he would bring labor, the environmental justice committee, the business community together to tackle the climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 This investment that we`re looking at, the potential investment in the IRA it is the largest investment to tackle the climate crisis in the United States` history. This is a big day for the country. This is a big day highlighting the president`s leadership. This is a big day for \uc2 EPA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, the entire country has endured successive waves of extreme heat this summer. I myself learned this week that in the last 30 years, heat deaths have been the single greatest cause of weather related fatalities. I think about that, of course, with black communities but also Native American communities found they have the highest percentage of heat related deaths per group. Can you tell us why that is and what is this White House doing to mitigate the effects?\par \par \uc2 REGAN: Reverend, you`re absolutely right. Let me add a couple of other stats to that. We know that black Americans, 34 percent are more likely to live in communities because of climate change. And if we see a two-degree increase in temperature, 34 percent of black Americans children will see an increase in asthma.\par \par \uc2 We also know that if we see a 2 percent increase in temperature, 40 percent of Latinos, Hispanic-Americans are more likely to see labor loss. Climate change, heat, temperature, all of these things are having a disproportionate impact on communities of color, low income communities and tribal communities.\par \par \uc2 So, we are prepared. We are taking action not only through regulation to mitigate the emissions or the pollution that cause climate change but through adaptation policies, thinking about ways that we can educate our communities, we can protect our communities, we can provide direct resources to our communities so that they can take action to protect not only their public health but also make the economic investments in their communities, like cooling centers and other places, to reduce that exposure to the death.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Because a lot of the toxic waste turns all of that, and we`ve talked about in our communities, Administrator, you spoke at our last National Action Network conference, which I`m president of, earlier this year. You and I have had a cup of tea since then. So, you understand, in my view, this is a civil rights issue. People say to me, you`re going to talk about civil rights stuff, not the environment. Breathing is a civil rights issue if you`re in an area where you don`t have the equal kinds of environment toward breathing without asthma and other infirmities being at a higher rate in your communities.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:10]\par \par \uc2 And when you last joined us on this show, we got into the administration`s equity agenda. It impacts your work on environmental justice. I know you just got back from Alabama, you were in Puerto Rico. Before both trips, part of your journey to justice initiative, in both cases, to address pollution and communities of color. What is the common thread between the two and other sites you visited on your tour?\par \par \uc2 REGAN: Well, the common thread is it`s no coincidence that systemic racism has plagued our communities across a number of facets, and environmental justice is no different. Now, what we have seen is a disproportionate impact to black and brown communities and tribal communities on the frontlines of the threat of pollution.\par \par \uc2 And what I am very proud to say as part of the Inflation Reduction Act, $60 billion, billion with a B, that will be dedicated to environmental justice. These are the types of resources that we need to finally bring to communities, like Puerto Rico, like Lowndes County, Alabama.\par \par \uc2 Rev, I was just back in Lowndes County, Alabama, for the second time. And I took my good friend and Secretary of Agriculture Tom Vilsack and Mitch Landrieu from the White House, because we made a significant announcement, an announcement that provides millions of dollars of technical assistance to these communities so that they can be competitive for the billion dollars that we`re seeing flow from the federal government, whether it be from the bipartisan infrastructure law or the resources that we`ll see from the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 For far too long, these communities have been impacted, but under this administration, under the president`s leadership, we are finally not only seeing the policies, I`m not only beefing up enforcement putting regulations in place to protect the most vulnerable, but we`re actually getting the resources to bring to these committees for once and all.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Administration Regan, I want to go back to one of the goals of the climate portion of this reconciliation bill that passed earlier today on the Hill, which, of course, to reduce the carbon emissions significantly by the end of the decade to a half about 2005 levels.\par \par \uc2 Given your life`s work and current job, I can imagine that you don`t think it can be done. But I do wonder if you think the American people have been prepared in terms of messaging for what that cleaner future means, because it will require both federal and individual action. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 REGAN: I`m very optimistic. And I`m even more optimistic when I get outside of Washington D.C. The partisan politics within the beltway really do constrain what we can do as a country. But as I travel the country, whether it`s the East Coast, the West Coast, the northeast, southeast, people are prepared.\par \par \uc2 Rev, you know this just as well as anybody, communities have had solutions to these problems for decades. It is the federal government, it`s the state government, that have lacked the resources and the leadership and the willingness to acknowledge that people have been unfairly treated in this country.\par \par \uc2 Well, now, thanks to President Biden`s leadership, we have it. We have a government that recognizes people are disproportionately impacted, and we have a Congress and a president who are acting to provide the resources so that we can master these solutions.\par \par \uc2 I am extremely optimistic and I`m going to keep hitting the road, staying outside of Washington, talking with people to be sure they get these much needed resources.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I know we`re out of time but I must ask you this. Lastly, how is the Biden administration working to make sure that black and brown communities are included in the technological progress coming with this push towards clean energy, making sure that we are prepared for the jobs of the future as this nation transforms?\par \par \uc2 REGAN: Well, Rev, as you saw with the bipartisan infrastructure law, 40 percent of those resources will go to the communities that need them to most. Those resources are going in the form of cleaner job training, education, economic development.So, that means the communities will be an integral part of the future.\par \par \uc2 We are also ensuring that, as we work with the private sector, because we`ve got a lot of public-private partnerships, that we are including a diverse workforce, diverse training, to be sure that we create a rising tide not just from a pollution production standpoint but from an economic development jobs and economic vitality standpoint as well.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:20]\par \par \uc2 This is all hands on deck. This administration is laser-focused to ensure that we don`t leave anyone behind.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Thank you, \uc2 EPA\uc2  Administrator \uc2 Michael Regan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And stay here on MSNBC later tonight on AMERICAN VOICES with Alicia Menendez. The guest will be White House National Climate Adviser Gina McCarthy to continue this very important conversation about the Democrats` historic spending deal and how it will affect U.S. environmental policy.\par \par \uc2 And coming up next on POLITICSNATION, Maryland Congresswoman Kweise Mfume joins us to discuss Democrats` effort to hold on to the House in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 But, first, my colleague, Richard Lui, with today`s other news stories. Richard?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC HOST: Rev, good Sunday to you. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is cleared for travel after a second negative COVID test today, that happening on a Sunday. He left the White House for Delaware this morning for family time. He heads to Kentucky tomorrow to see the damage caused by deadly flooding.\par \par \uc2 Four ships carrying Ukrainian grain left the country`s Black Sea port today, according to Reuters. It`s part of a deal with turkey to unblock Ukrainian sea exports as it continues to resist a Russian invasion.\par \par \uc2 And dangerously high temperatures grip the U.S. East Coast. Heat advisories are in effect in major cities, including Boston and New York. Meteorologists say the heat index could reach 104 degrees Monday.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this short break.\par \par \uc2 C (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:20:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back. Joining me now, Congressman Kweise Mfume, Democrat of Maryland. Congressman, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 First, the breaking news, the Senate Democrats climate health care and tax bill having just passed. Your reaction, what does it mean for your Baltimore district?\par \par \uc2 REP. KWEISE MFUME (D-MD): Well, it`s a tremendous piece of legislation. It is not Build Back Better, it`s a smaller version of it, but in many respects, it is groundbreaking because, for once, we are able to say to working Americans were going to actually reduce the cost of prescription drugs, we give Medicare the ability now to negotiate prices on an open market, that has never happened before. The cost of health care under the Obama plan, which was going to end the subsidies that are going to end next month will now be extended well beyond that into 2025 at the very least. There is a $35 cap on insulin for diabetics. And you were right to point out earlier, unfortunately, that does not cover private insurers but, for 3.5 million Americans who are on Medicare, their cost for insulin will be kept at $35.\par \par \uc2 There is, in this bill, significant efforts to deal with the environment, the air that we breathe, the water that we breathe and the way that we are using and disposing of things. And I agree with you again, at the very least, if what we breathe through the air is not a civil rights matter, I don`t know what can be, particularly given the increasing amounts of cancer that we see all over our society as a result of carcinogens because of a carbon footprint that is way out of place in terms of where it should be. So, that`s going to be reduced, I think, by about 40 percent by the end of this decade.\par \par \uc2 As you pointed, out there are a number of things, thankfully, I was really glad to hear the remarks of the \uc2 EPA\uc2  director on the provisions and things that they are undertaking. But this bill also does a number of couple of other things. It takes corporations who make over a billion dollars, and for the first time, puts on them a mandatory tax rate of 15 percent. If every day people have to pay taxes, there is no reason why those corporations should not, which is probably why the other side of the aisle thought it so very, very long.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s groundbreaking in many respects. It`s coming to the House. We`re in session this coming week. We will get that bill to vote on and to finalize it and then to send it to the president`s desk for signature.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman, let me go to another subject. You know I`ve been very vocal from the beginning about the case of Brittney Griner, and you`ve become one of Brittney Griner`s biggest advocates in Congress throughout her detention, and as of last week, conviction for cannabis possession in Russia earlier this year. Griner now is facing nine years in prison.\par \par \uc2 Now, both Russia and the U.S. have signaled openness to prison swap that would bring Griner home as well as probably Paul Whelan. Again, we`ve been back and forth. I`ve tried a clergy visit, you`ve taken a strong stance. Are you optimistic about a swap that could lead to Brittney`s release?\par \par \uc2 MFUME: Well, I`m cautiously optimistic. I would be remiss if I did not thank you for your public support of this. In the early days, when it first broke, a lot of people weren`t saying anything because the word was, the less said, the better things are. But we realized that that`s not true. The more said, the more opportunities there are to get Ms. Griner home.\par \par \uc2 So, I`m cautiously optimistic and let me just explain why. Putin is a great chess player. He realized that this was an issue here in this country. And so instead of a one-for-one, he is looking to find others that he can put into that deal, in my opinion, that will sweeten the deal and then cause the swap to take place.\par \par \uc2 Viktor Bout is a merchant of death, as he is known. He has a gun runner, has been arrested and locked up for some time.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:03]\par \par \uc2 It is clear that Putin wants him back on Russian soil. Ms. Griner, along with Paul Whelan, another American who has been locked up since 2020, are both, and as we know it, in detention. And so the question becomes, is it a one for one a two for one or a two for two swap? And I think Putin would prefer a two for two swap. There`s a lot of talk about a Russian who is in jail in Germany, which has nothing to do with the U.S., about adding that to the situation.\par \par \uc2 So, what we hear coming out of Putin`s mouth and his foreign minister has to be taken with a grain of salt. It is primarily for domestic consumption in Russia. We know that there are backchannels that are open and have been open for over three and a half weeks where discussions are taking place. My assumption is that it will culminate in the release of Ms. Griner and perhaps even Mr. Whelan.\par \par \uc2 But at this particular point in time, part of the problem, I think, the president faces is that people, for the most part, don`t want prisoner swaps. And we`ve had a situation where that`s never been the case. But even foreign secretaries of this country have said over and over again, foreign relations secretaries, that there comes a time when these things are necessary, and this is one of those times.\par \par \uc2 I was listening to \uc2 Bill Richardson\uc2  the other day, who has, for some time, contributed in this area of foreign relations, who, by the way, is someone who does not favor these swaps, has said even he realizes that now this is a time to do it. We`ve got to get Ms. Griner home and we`ve got to be very careful for people traveling abroad, particularly traveling in Russia, because my sense is that they`re going to try to grab more Americans, just like they are doing in Afghanistan, in Venezuela and some of these other places.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And it seems to me it`s just so egregious to me that we don`t even have athletics now above the global politics because Brittney Griner, for seven years, was working on a Russian basketball team when she was off- season here in the United States, and it won the hearts of a lot of Russian people. In fact, some of her teammates and her former coach testified for her in Russian court. But it seems some things we could not bring down the global politics, but it`s sad that that is the case.\par \par \uc2 Let me go to another subject to you. What do you take from all the Trump- backed candidates that prevailed in Republican primaries last week? What party gets helped or hurt in November by this?\par \par \uc2 MFUME: Well, we are dealing with three parties now, the Democratic Party, the old Republican Party, and the Make America Great Again party, which seems to have more and more influence and has really divided Republicans almost straight down the middle.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  endorsed 57 non-incumbents in this last round of elections, 57. And in doing that, he came away with 33 winners and 9 losers. So, his mark is at 78 percent. That is how effective he is in the MAGA people are. The problem though is that these are primary elections.\par \par \uc2 And there is a bigger picture when you go into a general. You just can`t campaign from the far-left or the far-right. You have got a governor and campaign from the middle. That is probably where we are going to see a correction of that number.\par \par \uc2 But having said that, 78 percent of Donald Trump`s candidates being elected over the past couple of months is unbelievable in a country that really has seen \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for who he is and, quite frankly, has seen many Republicans for who they are.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, we have got to work very, very hard here in this upcoming general election, which is about 94, 93 days away. Otherwise, a low voter turnout will doom the Democratic Party. And Republicans are energized, particularly the MAGA Republicans. They sense victory. It`s kind of like the tea party of a decade or so ago. So, they are going to turn out in large numbers. The only way to thwart that is to stay right on the issues, pass their kind of legislation and turn out and vote.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me ask you this lastly, Congressman. Having headed the \uc2 NAACP\uc2 , you`re national president in your former career, now a member of Congress, with less than 100 days to go before midterm election, really, I wanted to ask you this based on what you just said. Do you feel Democrats are doing enough to reengage black voters who may have lost faith in the party and the Biden administration over the last two years?\par \par \uc2 MFUME: I think up to this point, the answer may have been, we could have done more or Democrats could have done more or somebody else could have done more. At this particular point, when I`m seeing from around the country is an effort to not take this for granted and definitely not to take the larger black or Latino communities for granted.\par \par \uc2 [17:30:03]\par \par \uc2 Not that they were, but in these instances, people need you to communicate to them. This is what I`m doing for you. This is what I will do for you. This is what I have done for you. And this is why we are in this together. It`s got to be a clear message that connects with people. And that`s nothing the way it is. Nobody`s going to come out and vote because me, or someone else says do what they have to believe they`re voting for something or against something. And there`s a lot to vote for, particularly when you look at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  looming as the next presidential candidate on the Republican side again.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you Congressman Mfume, always glad to have you.\par \par \uc2 MFUME: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Up next. It`s time to rise up and take the fight for abortion rights to the states. Stay to tune.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:34:57]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Earlier this week, voters in Kansas break the 100 degree August heat to vote against an amendment that would have stripped abortion rights from the state constitution. Nearly 60% of voters stood up in favor of protecting a woman`s right to choose in a state that has gone Republican, every presidential election since 1964. The amendment vote is the first time Americans have had their say on abortion since the Supreme Court overturned Roe vs. Wade in late June, and it gives abortion rights advocates a clear sign, this fight is not over.\par \par \uc2 After all, Kansas is not the only state where abortion access is going to be on the ballot. This November, voters in California, Michigan, Vermont and Kentucky will be asked whether to affirm or reject abortion rights. And according to the statistical analysis performed by the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , four out of every five states in the country will likely vote to protect abortion rights if the people were given the opportunity to vote on them.\par \par \uc2 And so it seems clear that abortion rights activist was now focused much of their energy on the states where public opinion is on their side if we can make sure that the people`s voices will be heard and respected. It will not be easy. Just this week, Republican lawmakers made Indiana the first state to enact severe anti-abortion laws post Roe. But history shows us that local that the state and local politics have always played a critical role in abortion access.\par \par \uc2 Conservatives spent years chipping away at abortion rights at the state level in order to set up the legal confrontation that led to the overturning of Roe. Abortion rights advocates must now rise up and do the same thing in reverse knowing that the will of the American people is behind them. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:40:57]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to Politics Nation. Just over an hour ago, Senate Democrats passed a big climate health care and tax package. What will this mean for voters? Let`s get to my political panel to weigh in. Joining me now is former Democratic Senator \uc2 Doug Jones\uc2  of Alabama, and former Republican Representative David Jolly of Florida. Welcome to both of you.\par \par \uc2 Let me go to you first, David. President Biden has had a long list of legislative wins during his first two years in office, which includes his infrastructure bill COVID Relief Package, and a Supreme Court justice appointment. In spite of these successes, many Americans remain very worried about the economy and inflation. How do you see the passage of this democratic spending bill affecting the narrative surrounding Democrats effectiveness in Washington? Is it enough to change some minds ahead of the midterms in your opinion?\par \par \uc2 FORMER REP. DAVID JOLLY, (R) FLORIDA: Yeah, Rev., that`s great context, because President Biden, Democrats have had a great week, much less a great month, but you will very likely not see the immediate impact on the economy and on voters actual way of life between now and November from what they did. But what they did is actually deliver on the values that Democrats want to contrast with Republicans, right? In the bill that was passed today, you had an enormous investment and climate, you gave the government the ability to negotiate prescription drug prices, and you said no more billionaire, corporations paying zero taxes, those are democratic values.\par \par \uc2 And so I think what it merits is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doing something he doesn`t often do, which is take a public victory lap and get out in front of the American people and say, this is what we stand for as Democrats. Republicans do not. It might not be enough to change the economy by November, it probably is not. But that doesn`t mean you can`t showcase the leadership of values that today`s Democrats are putting in front of the American people.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Doug, a lot of the key components of Biden`s Build Back Better plan is not in the Democrats agenda bill that just passed in the Senate, such as universal pre-K, parental leave, expanded Child Tax Credit, is that a mistake in your judgment?\par \par \uc2 FORMER SEN. \uc2 DOUG JONES\uc2 , (D) ALABAMA: Oh, no, not at all Rev. I think David`s right about this. It is -- it helps the President talk about the agenda. You know, look, we all know democracy is not perfect. Democracy is not going to give you every item that you want for. But what I think is so significant about this bill and what`s been happening over the last several months, you`ve seen the Democrats big tent, all come together and coalesce around progress.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what this is, they are coalescing around things, getting certain things done, understanding that they can`t get it all done. And I think that`s really important going into the midterms, because the contrast, as David said, is important. And one of those contrasts is the fact that Democrats can deliver on things. Republicans have not provided a single item of trying to fight inflation, what they`re going to do on climate, and Democrats are not only now showcasing their values, they`re saying that even as a big tent with people on all sides of the agenda, we can get things done.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what the American people want. They want to know that people are out there working for them and fighting for them.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: David, you along with former New Jersey Governor Kristin Todd Whitman and former Democratic presidential candidate in New York mayoral candidate \uc2 Andrew Yang\uc2 , recently announced you`re launching a new political party called the Forward Party. I`d like to hear your pitch for the new third party but in particular, what you think it will offer voters like black voters who don`t always feel valued by the Democratic Party, but I`m put off by the racist politics of the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:00]\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yeah, Rev., thank you for that. Look it is a response to 40% in the country that today still says I can`t see my politics in either major party. And so we did something very different. We brought together and left, right and middle to form basically a new kind of political party and new type of political coalition.\par \par \uc2 And it`s a coalition that actually celebrates independent thought, celebrates the diversity of our communities and says, you know what, instead of a party that from the top down, says, you must believe these things to be a part of our coalition. What it says is, let`s come together around basic principles of economic opportunity, personal liberty, and defensive democracy. And then you exercise those politics that reflect your community best. Frankly, it`s the notion of pluralism upon which the nation was founded.\par \par \uc2 I`ll say this, Rev. because I know a lot of people freak out when they hear about a third party. Please don`t conflate it with a third run for the presidency in 2024. What Governor Whitman and Andrew and myself, did is tried to lay the framework for a viable, durable third party that can run candidates from Mayor to town supervisor to judge, to clerkships. The law does not permit a party to start today and run a 50 state candidate and 24. That is not the exercise that we`re currently engaged in.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, Doug, let`s turn to former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  dead who spoke for two hours at CPAC last night about homeless encampments and death sentences for drug dealers. And he hinted yet again at another bid for the White House. 69% of attendees chose Trump in our straw poll as their preferred candidate edging out Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  at 24%. Now, we`ve seen many Republican candidates win primaries by continuing to embrace Trump. What influence do you think he is having on the midterms overall?\par \par \uc2 JONES: I think he`s having a tremendous influence on the midterms within the Republican Party. You only have to look and see what`s happened in Pennsylvania and Arizona and other places in the country. Look, CPAC is no longer a mainstream conservative Republic. It is the MAGA faction, the more radical right. So you`re not shouldn`t be surprised. I don`t think we have that. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  got 69% of the vote in the straw poll, who spoke for two hours, but like most every Trump speech, he really didn`t say anything.\par \par \uc2 He just stoke resentment, and fear. That`s his whole mantra. And that`s what CPAC unfortunately has become their own their top speaker was \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2  from Hungary, an on autocrat and racist European races. And that`s unfortunately the sad state of affairs for CPAC these days. So I think, you know, what we`re going to see in the future is, Trump is still going to have impact in that Republican Party until enough people come out and not only push back a little bit, but condemn his actions.\par \par \uc2 Every Republican member of Congress, every Republican candidates ought to be asked the question, did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  engage in conduct that was a supreme dereliction of his duty as president on and before and right after January 6, everyone ought to be put on the spot by that. And let`s get this out in the open, there`s going to be a referendum on Trump. So be it, let it be that. I think Democrats would likely welcome that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: David, your thoughts on the Trump factor then in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: OK, it`s a big question. Will he run or not? I personally think he will. I think what is different where we are right now, Rev., than where we were six months ago, is if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if we were talking about him running, it likely would have been a field of one. I now think that if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  runs, there will be other Republicans in the race.\par \par \uc2 I think \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  runs regardless of what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says. It is not to contest \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he`ll probably say, I support the president and voters choose him, he has my support. I think you`ll see Asa Hutchinson, some others begin to nibble up with getting into the Republican race. I think this could be a crowded Republican race. It is still Donald Trump`s to lose, but he very well might lose it.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, former U.S. Senator \uc2 Doug Jones\uc2  and former Congressman David Jolly. Thank you both. Up next, my final thoughts.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:53:25]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me remind people that yes, it is good that we saw the Senate passed that bill today that will deal with inflation and climate change, and that we are making sure it goes in the areas that needed the most once the House passes. It is good to see that expectations was over done in terms of well, if we achieved more than expectations, let me put it that way. And the jobs that were created last month, when we saw the jobs numbers this week, released on what happened in July, and the economy was stronger than predicted.\par \par \uc2 Yes, it was great to see progress in the Breonna Taylor case in which the federal government came in and indicted four of the officers surrounding her killing, her murder in Louisville, Kentucky, as they`ve come in on Ahmaud Arbery case as they`ve come in, on the case of George Floyd to see a Justice Department finally working towards civil rights.\par \par \uc2 But that does not mean we should relax. We need to keep going. And we need to say wait a minute. These victories only will matter if we sustain them and vote in November and they`re chipping away at voting rights. That`s why I`m staying on the issues. I`m staying going forward. I`m full steam ahead as well. I`ll be in Atlanta this Thursday with the family of Brianna Grier, another Brianna who died in the hands of police.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:03]\par \par \uc2 We can not stop until we have the ultimate victory of stopping those that are trying to return this nation to a nation of states` rights, whether it`s women`s right to choose whether it`s voting, whether it is civil rights for blacks, brown and others, we have got to push all the way back to we have stopped this move forward. Unless we do that, we will have a temporary celebration and end up crying when it`s over. We cannot take our foot off the pedal. We must go forward. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 5 p.m. Eastern. "AMERICAN VOICES" with Alicia Menendez starts right now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220808ei8700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for August 6, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui, Carlos Curbelo \par \uc2 6,187 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:26]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: Vindication.\par \par \uc2 Right now, President Biden and Democrats are poised to cap a week of wins, in the process giving the party some very real weapons to carry into midterm elections, now just barely three months away, starting with Senate Democrats rallying around a major spending package aimed at reducing record consumer inflation, but also a massive investment in healthcare and climate intervention central to the Biden agenda.\par \par \uc2 And now, on the cusp of proceeding, this weekend, after previous holdout, Arizona senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  announced her support for the bill giving Senate Democrats the votes they need to pass it by Monday morning, and another shot in the arm heading into November, that`s on the top of a surprising strong July jobs report that just more than doubled economist expectation.\par \par \uc2 The successful assassination of one of the main architects of the September 11th attacks and a major vote in favor of abortion rights coming from a Deep Red state all in the last week.\par \par \uc2 But is it enough for the Democrats in either chamber to keep the majorities in three months? I`ll ask the Chair of the House Democratic Caucus in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 And we`re following a troubling story out of Georgia. The family of Brianna Grier is seeking answers after her death in police custody three weeks ago. New body camera footage showing what happened before she fell from a moving police car. Attorney \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2  joins me along with the Grier family shortly as that investigation escalates.\par \par \uc2 And at least one chapter of a different tragedy, the one of Breonna Taylor draws to a close -- all of that on POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , Chair of the House Democratic Caucus. Congressman, Senate Democrats may be on the verge of a big win with the Senate today in a rare Saturday session now with the support of Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 , the votes are there to pass this legislation, Inflation Reduction Act, tackling, obviously our record inflation, but also taxes, climate change healthcare.\par \par \uc2 Of course, before they can get the passage, the Chamber has to go through a so-called vote-a-rama, where Senate Republicans can introduce all the amendments they want until they run out.\par \par \uc2 And NBC is reporting that some Senate Democrats have signaled their plans to vote against all the amendments, Republicans demand, what can you tell us about what things are?\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): Well, good afternoon, Rev.\par \par \uc2 Good to be with you. It`s my hope and expectation that Senate Democrats will successfully be able to move the Inflation Reduction Act, send it over to the House of Representatives at some point over the next few days and we`ll be back in session by the end of the week to pass the bill and get it to President Biden`s desk.\par \par \uc2 And as you indicated, President Biden, Democrats in the House and the Senate, we`re in the zone right now in delivering for the American people and putting people above politics. That`s been the value proposition that we have brought to governance over the last several years, and we continue to deliver in that regard.\par \par \uc2 Lowering costs, pass the American Rescue Plan, save the economy, infrastructure, which will create millions of good paying jobs. We are still seeing record job creation. I believe it was more than nine million jobs that have been created on the President Biden`s tenure.\par \par \uc2 We, of course passed gun safety legislation for the first time in 30 years that will save lives or bring back domestic manufacturing jobs to the passage of a the Chips and Science Act and now we`re going to move the Inflation Reduction Act to drive down healthcare costs and the high cost of lifesaving prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:08]\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: It`s been a pretty good run, but we`re going to keep up the work for the people.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You know, earlier today, Congressman, you tweeted that "Job creation is up, gas prices are down and extreme Republicans are running scared." Now, obviously you`re referring to the Jobs Report released this week showing that hiring in July was more than more double what economists were expecting, nearly 530,000 added last month pushing back against the claim that the country is in a recession.\par \par \uc2 If the trend holds, and the Democrats get this Inflation Reduction Act passed and implemented before the midterms, are you confident that the party will benefit at the ballot box in November?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Yes, I believe when it`s all said and done, and the American people have a clear choice, we, as Democrats are continuing to put people over politics, lower costs, good paying jobs, and fighting for safer communities.\par \par \uc2 On the other side of the aisle, extreme MAGA Republicans who are totally out of control. They are extreme on reproductive freedom. They want to criminalize abortion care and impose a nationwide ban.\par \par \uc2 We know they are extreme on Social Security and Medicare, \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  told us so, they want to end Social Security and Medicare in five years, and we know they are also extreme on democracy, apparently they don`t believe in it.\par \par \uc2 And so we think when you compare our track record of getting things done and our vision for the future for everyday Americans, with the non-record of accomplishment, or even vision or agenda for making a difference that exists with my colleagues on the other side of the aisle and their extremism, that we have a real shot, not just to hold the House and the Senate, but to grow our majorities.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me ask you this. What`s your takeaway from the slate of Trump-backed candidates that prevailed in Republican primaries this week? Which party do these victories help or harm in November?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well, it`s not helpful for the American people that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continues to have a vise grip on the Republican Party, which in many instances has really turned into nothing more than a cult of personality where you`re just required to bend the knee to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And if you fail to do so, you pay the consequences in the context of a primary.\par \par \uc2 But again, because some of these candidates are so extreme on so many issues of importance to everyday Americans and can care less that the Supreme Court has actually taken a right that has existed for 50 years, and they want to nationally do the same thing in terms of Federal legislation, so they take control of the House, that the comparison is not going to serve Republicans well at the ballot box in a General Election with all of these Trumpism type of candidates who are emerging from the Republican primaries.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Congressman, briefly, this weekend, Indiana became the first State to ban abortion after the Supreme Court`s reversal of Roe vs. Wade. This after Kansas voters rejected a State Amendment to ban abortion earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 If Democrats are able to hold on to their congressional majorities, can we expect codification of Roe to be the next -- caucus` next legislative rallying cry?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Certainly that will be right at the top of the agenda, and if we can grow our majority in the Senate, by just two or three senators, and we have a real opportunity to do so and hold the House, we are going to be able to codify Roe v. Wade nationally.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you, Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Switching gears now to two Civil Rights stories that got my attention. Joining me now Civil Rights attorney who I call the Attorney General of Black America, \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2 . He is representing the parents of the late Brianna Grier, her mother, Mary Grier, and her father, Marvin Grier.\par \par \uc2 Now, Attorney Crump, let me go to you first. Thanks, as always for joining us and Mr. And Mrs. Grier, first our condolences and prayers for your loss.\par \par \uc2 And for our audience, I need to briefly summarize accounts of what happened on July 15th when the Hancock County Georgia Sheriff`s Department responded to your request for assistance with your daughter, Brianna, who you said was suffering from a mental health episode.\par \par \uc2 She was arrested by Sheriff`s Deputies and put into a cruiser. The police body camera footage released last week showing a tense arrest, then barely a minute after leaving the scene, Brianna fell out of a moving vehicle, falling into a coma and dying six days later.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:21]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You say you were initially told that she had kicked the door open and jumped out by the Sheriff`s Department, which has since requested a State investigation that has determined Brianna was indeed handcuffed, but the passenger`s side rear door was ultimately not secured. Is that right? Am I saying anything in correctly, Mr. and Mrs. Grier?\par \par \uc2 MARVIN GRIER, BRIANNA GRIER`S FATHER: No, you`re not. We were told by the Sheriff that she kicked the door open and jumped out. Obviously, I didn`t see that in no major -- that happening, but we want to find out why and what did happen for her to come out that car.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You know, because the disturbing thing to me about this is that you called the police because she was having an episode, and for a family to call the police and end up with their daughter dead, you are saying she is going through an episode you want her to have help, and how could she be in the back of a car, Attorney Crump, of a law enforcement vehicle and they have not secured the back door knowing she`s going through a mental health episode.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it is everywhere in this country, that when you arrest somebody and handcuff them, you secure them in the back seat of the vehicle or the back part of the vehicle if it`s a van. I mean, this is sounding like Freddie Gray, Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN CRUMP\uc2 , ATTORNEY: It is very similar, Reverend Al in 2020, we were asking what happened to Breonna Taylor in Kentucky, and now in 2022, we`re asking what happened to Brianna Brier in the State of Georgia, because we learned from George Floyd`s trial, the officers have to understand when a person is in your custody, they`re in your care, and especially a mentally ill patient and that`s why her mother called, Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 She was having a mental health crisis. She was in handcuffs in the back of a police car. She was supposed to get help. She was not supposed to end up dead.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why Attorney Eric Hirs (ph) and I are having on Monday, a press conference to reveal the findings of the autopsy on the steps of the Georgia Capitol.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, you, Mr. and Mrs. Grier, from what I understand, a very religious family, just wanted the right thing to be done, and you find yourself in this situation. What is it that you want the State investigators to just tell you? What is it that you are calling for?\par \par \uc2 MARVIN. GRIER: We are looking for accountability and transparency. We want this not happening to no one else`s child. Out daughter was a loving child. She was a loving mother.\par \par \uc2 Right now, and today, my little grandbaby came to me and said, "Papa, where`s mama?" I thought she was talking about her grandmama, but she said "No. I`m talking about Brianna."\par \par \uc2 So this is why we are fighting for Brianna, and we need to know the answer to what happened, so we can explain to these kids. I can`t keep telling them no story or you lie because sooner or later, they need to know what happened to Brianna.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You`re talking about kids? You`re talking about her children. She had children.\par \par \uc2 MARVIN GRIER: Yes, she has two twins. She had a set of twins, two girls, Marie and Moriah.\par \par \uc2 MARY GRIER, BRIANNA GRIER`S MOTHER: Three years old.\par \par \uc2 MARVIN GRIER: And when they hug me, hug me, and hug me, now, I just try to give them as much support that I can you know, until that day comes and so we can give them a fair justice.\par \par \uc2 CRUMP: And Reverend Al, we`re hoping that you could come to Atlanta to eulogize Brianna, so the world will know and can help us answer the questions of her three-year-old twin daughters, what happened to Brianna.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, I certainly, as President of the National Action Network, I`ll certainly work that out to be there.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:10]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Especially when I`m hearing about these daughters. All of us need to stand with Mr. And Mrs. Grier, at this hour to get a complete transparency, and if my presence can help, I`ll be there.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you, Mr. and Mrs. Grier, tell our audience something about your daughter, how would you like her to be remembered?\par \par \uc2 MARY GRIER: As a sweet and kind, loving person, and anything that she could do for others, she would do it.\par \par \uc2 MARVIN GRIER: She was the best daughter that we could have. She had issues with mental illness, but she would do for anybody, she loved her kids as a mother. She loved the little kids, regardless -- unconditional love, and that`s the same way we loved her.\par \par \uc2 But she had a mental issue and we were trying to get help for it, and if we know that, what is going to happen to her that night, we wouldn`t have never called the police.\par \par \uc2 MARY GRIER: Never.\par \par \uc2 MARVIN GRIER: Never.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Wow. And I emphasize you called the police trying to get help.\par \par \uc2 MARVIN GRIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And I don`t care what kind of mental health issue she may have had. How do they explain not securing the back door of a police vehicle when they`ve handcuffed the person under arrest? I mean, this is unbelievable and certainly, I will be there to stand with you.\par \par \uc2 Attorney Crump, while I have you, I have to ask you about another Breonna because you represented the family of Breonna Taylor for more than two years now. This week four current and former police officers that participated in the Louisville Metro Police Department raid that resulted in her death were federally indicted this week for violating Taylor`s Civil Rights, unlawful use of force and other counts.\par \par \uc2 Now you led the legal effort that secured a $12 million settlement and several policing reforms in the City of Louisville. Obviously, you and I have talked about what the message this sends.\par \par \uc2 We worked on other cases where the Feds came in this year, Ahmaud Arbery, even after the State had convicted, we still were able to get them to come in and indict and convict as well as George Floyd.\par \par \uc2 So this Justice Department has been more active than the last one, but for our audience, what is the wider impact of these charges in Breonna Taylor.\par \par \uc2 CRUMP: I think it is profound, Reverend Al. Attorney -- I need to bake up my co-counsel Sam Aguiar, we were studying it. We believe this is the first time in history that police officers have been charged for killing a Black woman in America on the Federal level.\par \par \uc2 So it underscores the reason we went to vote because elections matter, and this Justice Department, and this Biden Justice Department on deck, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , as well as \uc2 Kristen Clarke\uc2 , who you and I both know, was a great Civil Rights attorney before she became the first Black woman to head the - -\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, in New York. She was very effective in New York. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 CRUMP: And they have been so committed to Civil Rights, more so than any other administration I`ve seen in my life. I can`t think of any other Justice Department, save Robert Kennedy`s Justice Department, which was so committed to Civil Rights with \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  and \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And so we have to applaud this Justice Department for getting guilty verdicts and charges for police killing, getting a guilty verdict in the lynch mob that killed Ahmaud Arbery, and now getting these charges for Breonna Taylor, this Black woman, so we need to applaud them and remind people that`s why we vote.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, many thanks, and again, our condolences to Mary and Marvin Grier, and I am on, I`ll be there with you this week and thank you for sharing your story and of always, to our Attorney General of Black America, \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2 . Thank you for joining us again, as well.\par \par \uc2 Coming up \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star, Brittney Griner remains in custody, unjustly imprisoned in Russia after receiving a nine-year sentence this week.\par \par \uc2 We`ll have an update on efforts to bring her home.\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague, Richard Lui, with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, according to his doctor tested negative for COVID-19 this morning. The President experienced a rebound case of COVID this week. He`ll be in strict isolation pending a second negative test.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:10]\par \par \uc2 LUI: Monkeypox cases climbing to nearly 30,000 globally. This includes more than 7,500 in the United States says the CDC. Wyoming is the only place not confirming cases yet in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Thursday, the Biden administration declared monkeypox a national health emergency.\par \par \uc2 And this weekend, tens of thousands of flights were canceled across the United States with thousands more delayed as well. The \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  implemented delay programs at airports from Boston to Atlanta last night due to summer thunderstorms.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after the short break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Breaking news this weekend: Russia now says as it is ready to discuss a prisoner swap for \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star, Brittney Griner after she was sentenced on Wednesday to nine years in Russian prison for marijuana possession.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:15]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Russia`s Foreign Minister says the negotiations must take place via a diplomatic channel he claims was set up by Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and US President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, Griner has been detained in Russia since her arrest in February on a low-level drug offense that wouldn`t even carry a misdemeanor charge in many parts of the US. Her lawyers say the cannabis cartridges she was carrying were prescribed medically in the US to treat chronic pain.\par \par \uc2 After the sentencing, President Biden called for Brittney`s immediate release for what he called a wrongful detainment. I continue to urge the \uc2 United States government\uc2  to do whatever is necessary to release Brittney.\par \par \uc2 And in my capacity as head of the National Action Network, I also ask for the Russian and US governments to permit me and a group of fellow clergy to travel to Russia and visit with Brittney as a clergy visit and to check on her spiritual and mental wellbeing.\par \par \uc2 And I call on all people across the world to pray for the release of Brittney Griner and other US citizens like Paul Whelan who are wrongly detained in Russia.\par \par \uc2 Innocent people should not be used as political pawns.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:30:48]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Friday afternoon, the GOP announced they would hold their 2024 convention in Milwaukee, Wisconsin, a city that in 2020 was Ground Zero for many of President Trump`s false claims of election fraud.\par \par \uc2 The GOP announcement comes just three months before the midterm elections, which many predict could put them back in charge of the House and maybe the Senate.\par \par \uc2 As Republicans plot a possible return to power, who is really in charge of the party?\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in my political panel for their takes.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is former Republican Congressman from Florida, \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , and Dana Milbank, a political columnist for "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " and author of a brand new book out next week called "The Deconstructionists: The Twenty-Five-Year Crackup of the Republican Party."\par \par \uc2 Welcome to both of you.\par \par \uc2 Dana, I want to get to you to weigh in first one former President Donald Trump`s continued hold on the Republican Party. His handpicked candidates continue to win primaries as Trump endorsees this week scored victories in key states, including Arizona and Michigan.\par \par \uc2 But many of those candidates will face a more difficult road in the General Election and even Trump supporters tell pollsters they aren`t sure they want him to run again in `24.\par \par \uc2 Assuming his legal problems don`t catch up with him before that given all of that, is Trump still the standard bearer of the party, Dana?\par \par \uc2 DANA MILBANKS, POLITICAL COLUMNIST, "THE WASHINGTON POST": Well, after this week, it would be impossible to say anybody but Trump is the standard bearer for the party. We sort of see it has a bit of a parlor game in Washington. You know, is he winning with his endorsements? Is he not winning with his endorsements?\par \par \uc2 I think the crucial thing that we need to understand is that regardless of where Trump is, at the moment, Trumpism has a stranglehold over the party.\par \par \uc2 So I think what you`re seeing a little bit is some of the Trump voters even are saying, we`re not so sure we want to return to that chaos and that ugliness, but the party is in the grips of people who are very much practicing that same type of politics. So whereas they are pulling back a little bit from Trump himself, because of the obvious character issues, they are not unfortunately pulling back in the Republican Party from Trumpism.\par \par \uc2 So you see the Trump himself is still dominant in his endorsements, and you see Trumpism entirely dominant within the ideology and the tactics of the party.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Carlos, let`s take a look at one of Trump`s Republicans, his main Republican rival from your home state of Florida, Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He continues to effectively wield his bully pulpit in the Sunshine State to make national headlines. This week, he suspended a State Prosecutor who didn`t want to enforce his strict new abortion law. DeSantis is the only Republican besides \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with double digit support for the 2024 Republican nomination, trailing by just nine percentage points.\par \par \uc2 Do you think DeSantis can wrestle the party away from Trump by offering many of the same policies without the political baggage?\par \par \uc2 CARLOS CURBELO\uc2 , MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Reverend, there`s no question that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is preparing to run for President and that he believes based on polling and based on his own instincts that he can defeat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and I think that`s indicative of what is happening in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 I agree with Dana. I mean, there is no question that Trump continues to be the dominant force in the party, but that dominance is starting to wane and just the fact that there is someone like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is so close to him in the polls and that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not over 50 percent with Republican primary voters, it shows that he is losing influence, that his old routine is getting old and that people are looking for something different.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:08]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Carlos, sticking with you. Well, what happens to the anti- Trump Republicans, including people like Representative Meijer of Michigan, who lost his primary battle this week after voting to impeach Trump after January 6th, or Wyoming Congressperson, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , who enlisted her father, former Vice President \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  to help in her tough primary fight? Take a listen to this ad that came out this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DICK CHENEY\uc2 , FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: In our nation`s 246-year history, there has never been an individual who is a greater threat to our republic than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He tried to steal the last election using lies and violence. They keep himself in power after the voters had rejected him.\par \par \uc2 He is a coward. A real man wouldn`t lie to his supporters. He lost his election and he lost big.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, anti-Trump Republicans make some powerful arguments about the importance of democracy and truthfulness, but how can they stay relevant if they can`t stay in office, Carlos?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Well, Rev, I think that`s a great point. But this primary season, I think we`ve seen a mixed bag of results. In Georgia, the Republicans who rejected Donald Trump`s lie did very well. We saw in Washington State, \uc2 Dan Newhouse\uc2 , one of the Republicans who voted for impeachment survived his primary. Another one, David Valadao in California did as well. Of course, there are others, like Peter Meijer, who did lose.\par \par \uc2 But you`re seeing that in all these races. You look at, you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  squared off in Arizona. Sure, Trump won, but his candidate, Lake won by a couple of points.\par \par \uc2 So I mean, this is not the Republican Party of two years ago. Again, Trumpism is still alive and well, but there is competition now. And with time, I think that those Republicans who want to change the direction of the Party, want to pull it away from this gross dishonesty and lack of integrity, I think they`re going to stay relevant. They`ll remain on the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 Some of these Trump candidates who won primaries will definitely lose Generals, and then these other Republicans will come back two to six years later and say, "Hey, do you want to go this way instead, so we can actually win?" I think that`s what`s starting to play out.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Dana, let`s turn to your new book, which offers its own answer to this question of what`s happening to the Republican Party. In "The Deconstructionists," you say the real story of the GOP has been the gradual decade-long embrace of White nationalist ideology.\par \par \uc2 You take this back to Newt Gingrich and the Republican Revolution of the 1990s, although its roots may even be older than that. In this move to the right for Republicans, the real determining factor in who leads the party, is there any political leader who could bring Republicans back from the brink?\par \par \uc2 MILBANKS: Well, Reverend, the book is out next week and I very much hope you`ll have me back so we can talk in depth about this, because I think it has something I`d really love to get into with you, to a great extent.\par \par \uc2 You know, so yes, it is decades-long history that that has brought us to the point where we are now. So I think, there is -- you know, what I can talk about now is where we are in this moment in time, and I think you are seeing within the Republican Party some pushback. This does not have to be -- the Republican Party does not have to be a party of White nationalism.\par \par \uc2 We saw an alternative given after the Republicans lost in 2012 and they did that autopsy that said you need to do a better job with Black voters, with Latino voters, with Asian-American voters.\par \par \uc2 We saw it in the way that \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  reached out to Latinos. It does not have to be this way. That was Trump -- there was a choice that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made to accentuate the worst instincts of the party. It`s unfortunately what we`re seeing with the likes of Mastriano with this ugly new slate of candidates in Arizona, what we`re seeing quite a bit with DeSantis down there in Florida, but it is not the direction that the party has to go.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Dana Milbank and former Congressman \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, as Russia`s invasion of Ukraine continues, we cannot forget the millions of refugees whose lives have been turned upside down by war.\par \par \uc2 We`ll have an update on what`s happening and to help, that`s coming next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:43:44]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: It has been 163 days since Russia`s invasion of Ukraine, and since February, about one-third of Ukrainians -- one-third of Ukrainians -- have been displaced out of their homeland amounting to the largest refugee immigration crisis in Europe to date since the aftermath of World War Two.\par \par \uc2 And within Ukraine itself, the United Nations has found that another 6.3 million people have been internally displaced as the repercussions of war has forced many of them out of their homes.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the \uc2 International Rescue Committee\uc2 `s Ukraine Country Director, Marysia Zapasnik, thank you first of all for coming on today.\par \par \uc2 In less than six months, more than 12 million people have left their homes in Ukraine to seek protection from war. The United Nations reports that over 6.3 million refugees from Ukraine have sought refuge in Europe alone, many crossing over into bordering countries such as Poland.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:03]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I imagine many of them are in dire need of support and resources. What can you tell us about the status and protection of those who have been able to migrate out of Ukraine away from the war zone?\par \par \uc2 MARYSIA ZAPASNIK, COUNTRY DIRECTOR, INTERNATIONAL RESCUE COMMITTEE - UKRAINE: Thank you very much, Reverend, for having me on your show. And, as you said, especially poignant timing, as we`re now in the sixth month of this war in Ukraine, and the humanitarian situation inside the country and in Ukraine continues to worsen.\par \par \uc2 You`re absolutely right. The conflict has caused the fastest displacement crisis seen in Europe since World War Two and the majority of the displaced people are women and children who are always most at risk of exploitation and abuse during crises.\par \par \uc2 And on top of that, there are six million people displaced here within the borders of Ukraine and this number is not expected to significantly decrease anytime soon as people are unable to return home, do not feel safe to return to their homes, or simply have no homes left to return to.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UN\uc2  estimates, there are 15.7 million people here in Ukraine who are in need of humanitarian assistance right now. And as the war continues, and families coping mechanisms will continue to be stretched, this number is likely to increase, especially in the harsh winter months.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, meanwhile, the United Nations data also indicates that there are more than 10 million across Ukraine`s border since the start of the war. But millions have also returned home largely those from Poland. What is the plan for those who have not been able to seek safety outside of the war zones or have left and then returned to damaged homes and are forced to be internally displaced?\par \par \uc2 ZAPASNIK: So the \uc2 International Rescue Committee\uc2  is here on the ground in Ukraine. And we`re also working in neighboring Poland and Moldova, providing humanitarian assistance to those who need it the most.\par \par \uc2 Some people have returned from neighboring countries in Europe, and they are mostly people who were from the western parts of Ukraine or from the central parts of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the situation is dire. The humanitarian situation is dire and continues to be dire in the eastern and the south of the country and that`s where IRC is focusing its intervention at the moment.\par \par \uc2 So our focus in this stage in Ukraine is to provide this humanitarian assistance to the people who need it the most, in the east and in the south of the country where the fighting is still the heaviest.\par \par \uc2 And together with our local partners and with local civil society, we are providing cash to vulnerable families to enable them to cover their most basic needs. We are providing non-food items to displaced families. Those that you mentioned, those who are unable to return him, who may have returned to Ukraine, but not to their actual homes.\par \par \uc2 For them were distributing non-food items like diapers, soaps, hygiene items, as many of these families fled their homes with only the items they could carry.\par \par \uc2 We`re also supporting local health services that are becoming overwhelmed and providing essential protection services and this includes the protection of women and children who are most often the most vulnerable in conflict.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, let me ask you this, back in the beginning stages of the war when many in Ukraine was scrambling to flee the country to seek safety, Africans who had been living there reported multiple instances of mistreatment, such as being stuck at the crossroads at the border crossings for days at a time, some without food or shelter and were held up by Ukrainian authorities.\par \par \uc2 Now that was back in the early spring. Since then, can you provide any details on the African migrants who before the war saw Ukraine as their home and have since been displaced? Do you have any updates on where their whereabouts, protection and wellbeing is?\par \par \uc2 ZAPASNIK: So as a humanitarian organization, we offer our support, and we help those people who are most in need, regardless of their background, their race, religion, or political affiliation. And we follow humanitarian principles as do other humanitarian organizations that are here on the ground in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I get that, but I`m asking -- I`m asking specifically about the African migrants that have complained that they had been profiled or mistreated on the border crossings. Do you have any data on that at all about? Has that improved? Has that been dealt with?\par \par \uc2 ZAPASNIK: There were some disturbing scenes at the beginning of the response, but I think things have improved now as information has been shared and awareness has been raised as well about the fact that everyone who is seeking refuge from conflict must be given the same rights, must be given the same freedom to find a place of safety.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:13]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: But you don`t have any specific data.\par \par \uc2 Thank you Miss Zapasnik for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, more than 50 years of social and political activism, but my work is not done. I`ll explain in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Before we end our show tonight, look what I found. Fifty years ago in 1972, "Essence` Magazine did a four-page profile on 17-year-old Reverend \uc2 Alfred Sharpton\uc2 . It was my first national magazine profile. I had been a pretty visible activist then for four years.\par \par \uc2 Well, I have, a century later, I am still on the case.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:01]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Today, I am at the 2022 Martha`s Vineyard African-American Film Festival where a new documentary film on my life as an activist is screening. "Loudmouth" directed by Josh Alexander, and produced by John Legend, has been shown earlier today here and a few weeks ago at the Tribeca Film Festival in New York.\par \par \uc2 Media and publicity aside, my daily work with the National Action Network continues. Why? Because we still have social justice issues, we still have the need to change laws that will make this society have equal protection under the law and equal opportunity for everyone.\par \par \uc2 Getting involved in social justice work doesn`t have a starting date or an ending date. There is no retirement plan. It`s retiring injustice and fighting as long as hard and as hard as you can, until you see injustice eradicated, or at least, somehow effectively brought down into where it is no longer impactful.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 We have a special guest, \uc2 Michael Regan\uc2  the administrator of the \uc2 EPA\uc2 , the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 , that is Sunday right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 "AMERICAN VOICES" with Alicia Menendez starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:21]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220807ei8600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell for August 5, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ali Velshi, Zerlina Maxwell, Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 7,566 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: In fact, she called the 82 days Moses had already spent in jail because of the state's mistakes sufficient. Pamela Moses' case is the latest involving Amy Weirich and the Shelby County D.A.'s Office that's drawn accusations of prosecutorial misconduct.\par \par \uc2 And here's the twist I promised you. Yesterday, the people of Shelby County decided they'd had enough. They voted her out in favor of a progressive challenger, a former civil rights lawyer whose campaign promises included creating a conviction review unit.\par \par \uc2 So, as Rachel often says, elections have consequences. But maybe this time it should be consequences have elections. That does it for us tonight. Rachel is back here on Monday. THE LAST WORD with Zerlina Maxwell who's in for Lawrence O'Donnell starts now.\par \par \uc2 ZERLINA MAXWELL, MSNBC HOST: Thanks, Ali. This week may just go down as one of the best weeks of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's presidency to date. Let's start with the economy. Today, we got a truly stunning jobs report for July. U.S. employers added 528,000 jobs, more than doubling projections. And the unemployment rate, well, it fell to 3.5 percent. The last time the unemployment rate was that low was in 1969, 53 whole years ago.\par \par \uc2 The economy has now recovered all of the nearly 22 million jobs lost since the start of the COVID pandemic. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his signature aviator sunglasses took a much deserved victory lap earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: We're almost at 10 million jobs. Almost a 10 million jobs since I took office. That's the fastest job growth in history. Today, there are more people working in America than before the pandemic began. In fact, there are more people working in America at any point in American history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: In a relief for drivers all across America, gas prices have dropped nearly $1 from highs in June. Highs that were driven in large part by supply shock after \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  started a war with Ukraine. The average price of gas is now $4.11 a gallon. And in many places, including in many red states, gas prices are well below $4 a gallon. President Biden highlighted the seven-week decline in gas prices in his speech today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Gas prices are coming down. They're down almost $1 a gallon from where they were just a month ago and we're making progress. We now have more than 50 straight days of falling gas prices in this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: President Biden's comments celebrated in drop in gas prices were a little bit confusing to the folks over at Fox News.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Why is the president bragging today about gas prices?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEANE-PIERRS, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Because it's gone down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: And that's not all President Biden has to brag about. Next week could have another major legislative win for President Biden after the Democrats final hold out, Arizona Senator Kirsten Sinema announced that she was ready to vote for the Democrat's climate tax and health care package inflation reduction act.\par \par \uc2 To win over the senator, Democrats agreed to include $5 billion in drought resilience funding. Democrats also had to remove a provision on closing the carried interest tax loophole, which allows wealthy hedge fund and investment managers to pay lower taxes and restructure the 15 percent minimum tax on corporations, was also a piece that was included in the legislation.\par \par \uc2 But it was replaced, thankfully, with a 1 percent excise tax on stock buybacks, a policy passed by \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's House that would, as \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  put, reign in some of the most self-serving things that corporate America does. Okay.\par \par \uc2 Also, Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is expected to hold the initial procedural vote on the reconciliation package tomorrow, Saturday, after the Senate parliamentarian reviews the bill. So, joining us now to break this all down, all of this economic news, Kristin Myers editor-in-chief of "The Balance" and former Yahoo Finance reporter. Also, William Spriggs, an economics professor at \uc2 Howard University\uc2  and chief economist for the AFL CIO. Kristen, I'll start with you. Break this all down for us. Break down these jobs' numbers. What stands out to you?\par \par \uc2 KIRSTEN MYERS, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, THE BALANCE: Yes. So, essentially, that 528,000 number was more than double actually what a lot of economists had been forecasting. And this is really huge news. Typically, I don't think too many of us would actually be talking about these jobs' numbers every single month. But given the context that we're in right now, we're just -- last week we had the second quarter GDP growth sinking 0.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:00]\par \par \uc2 A lot of people were really, you know, essentially grabbing their hair and saying, okay, this guy is falling, maybe we are -- right now we're in a recession. But to see these absolutely staggering jobs numbers and the unemployment rate falling to 3.5 percent. I think the question right now really is, really, what recession?\par \par \uc2 So clearly, the economy is still going strong with employers adding back these jobs. The more workers that we have that are employed, the more income that we have in their hands and that means more consumers are spending their money. So, the U.S. economy seems to be doing pretty well, at least for right now.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Professor Spriggs, do you agree with that analysis? And what do you think the impact of the IRA could be potentially? How will it help if at all with inflationary pressures on American families who have been dealing with all of these skyrocketing prices on essential items?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM SPRIGGS, CHIEF ECONOMIST, \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 : Yes. So, the Inflation Reduction Act, the IRA, that you call it, is really aimed at the actual cost of price hikes, which is the tremendous shots that we're having right now to our food production system. A lot of that is the war between Russia and Ukraine. The effect it has on fertilizer production, the effect it has on fuel supplies, which then in turn affects the production of food.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of this is climate change. We are having a drought in Italy. We're having a drought in India. We're having a drought in Africa. None of that has anything to do with the war, but it has everything to do with global warming.\par \par \uc2 The Inflation Reduction Act finally gets us on the side of the environment and doing the things that will help to relieve this tremendous (inaudible) that we're having from global warming. It puts a check on health care cost. Many Americans benefited last year because the Rescue Plan gave them assistance to pay for their health insurance under the Affordable Care Act.\par \par \uc2 This will extend and make permanent their access so that they will be able to afford health insurance. We know how important that is having lived through COVID. And for seniors, we have huge (inaudible) on prescription jobs because for once, Medicare will finally be able to negotiate prescription drug prices.\par \par \uc2 So, a lot of the core things are going to be addressed, the things that are really getting at the price movements. My fear is that, you know, as Kristin said, the numbers are bigger than expected. This is going to make people at the Fed where I think who have been analyzing the economy totally wrong, go bunkers. They are going the have the sense that the economy really is overheating in their minds.\par \par \uc2 They, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , are the risk that we could have a recession because they are interpreting the hindrance that we're facing incorrectly, they are real, they are strong, and tipping the scales by being aggressive and raising interest rates misdirected because they cannot affect the sources of price increases.\par \par \uc2 It can only threaten the jobs of autoworkers, threaten the jobs of construction workers, and raise unemployment. If we have a recession that would be one (ph).\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: It's a really, really helpful analysis. And Kristin, you had mentioned the -- all the folks writing the headlines spelling doom and gloom for the economy and the impending inevitable recession. Today, I think people put a pause on that analysis. But do you agree and share the concerns, with the professor about the fed and an impending recession, and potentially the idea that they may overcorrect and lead us in that direction?\par \par \uc2 MYERS: You know, I'm not necessarily the only one. A lot of folks have really been sharing in some of those worries of the professor. I mean, you need to look no further than the stock market today. We really saw markets taking 