ng that can only really be described as January 6th performance art.\par \par \uc2 No I'm serious. A man dressed in an orange prison style jumpsuit and a MAGA hat pretended to be a January 6th rioter locked up in a prison cell the quote, "prisoner in the cell" kept track of time served on a chalkboard and punctuated his performance by fake crying, weeping and wailing as viewers looked on.\par \par \uc2 Now to be fair to the quote-unquote "actor" in that cell, he probably gave a pretty convincing performance in the role of January 6 defendant. You know all about that life and about how it feels to be arrested for January 6 because he actually was. Back in January, he was convicted of disorderly and disruptive conduct for his actions at the Capitol insurrection.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now to give us some context on what the heck we just saw, Laura Jedeed. She's a freelance journalist who shot the video of the January 6 cosplay enthusiast we just saw. She's also an army veteran who served two tours in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 So, what -- what's happening here? Tell us, explain this video and image of fake jail with fake cosplay of insurrection?\par \par \uc2 LAURA JEDEED, FREELANCE JOURNALIST: Yes. So the way this worked was essentially you approach the exhibit, which was by the WalkAway Foundation. And people were ready to give you the silent disco headset, if you've ever seen them, they kind of glow. Usually used for dance parties. In this case, they were used to switch between three tracks of different descriptions of being arrested on January 6. It's all very harrowing stuff.\par \par \uc2 And everyone basically stood around in these silent disco headset to watch the scene being acted out in front of them by Brandon Straka. Definitely performance art is the right word for it.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Ok. It's really weird and I don't just mean that like in a, ok, this is a little strange kind of way. I started by saying like, are they ok? Did you glean what the purpose of this was? Is it just performance art like for -- art for art's sake? Or was there a political message?\par \par \uc2 I mean I mentioned this man really was a defendant. Is he trying to perform out oppression or victimhood? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 JEDEED: I mean definitely a lot of that. The truth is, I mean it's this CPAC and at a lot of CPACs. a big theme is being very afraid of what the Libs will do to you as a conservative. So I mean it's a big -- it's a big topic in most of the speeches as well.\par \par \uc2 This idea that, you know, they are going after Trump now. They're going after the January 6th rioters now but they'll come after you next. They're going to silence you. They're going to cancel you. They're going to get you fired from your job. You could even go to jail.\par \par \uc2 So when these people are listening to this testimony and looking at what to us seems rather absurd, it does come from a place of real fear. I don't think they are ok. And fear is something that is really going to keep the people in line and getting them to accept all sorts of things. So, it's not great.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: I should just reiterate that that person really did participate in the insurrection, but he got probation. So I don't know why he's pretending jail because he didn't actually receive a sentence of getting jail.\par \par \uc2 Laura Jedeed, thank you so much for being here. Please stay safe.\par \par \uc2 So these images of January 6th and the prisoner performance art at CPAC in Dallas stopped me in my tracks today. I have to say, I was confused for several minutes. I mean, look at it again. Just marinate on it. Sit with it. What is that?\par \par \uc2 I think it is further evidence of how untethered from reality some of the folks on the extreme right have truly become. The rewriting of history to recast themselves as the true victims of what happened on January 6th is truly a sight to see.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:45]\par \par \uc2 And yet somehow, despite eight incredibly compelling public hearings, chock full of new incriminating information about everything that led up to that tragic day, they can't help but think that they are the real martyrs acting out their fake oppression in some sort of weird incarceration cosplay.\par \par \uc2 No matter how much performative persecution happens at CPAC or on social media, they are not the victims of the January 6th attack on the Capitol. And they are not being oppressed when they are being held accountable for violently attacking the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 It is really that simple. The real victims are the more than 150 police officers who were injured and the five police officers who were killed in the riot, or died by suicide in the aftermath of the attack. Also the members of Congress, the vice president and their staff that had to run for their literal lives.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Democratic Congressman \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  of California. He was at the Capitol on January 6 and is suing President Trump for his role inciting the January 6 attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 So Congressman, as someone who was in the Capitol during the attack, what is your reaction when you see January 6th rioters who never even got real jail time referred to as political prisoners? Or pretending that they're the real victims? Is the message that these insurrectionists didn't do anything wrong? That they're being victimized for no reason?\par \par \uc2 REP. ERIC SWALWELL (D-CA): Zerlina, MAGA Republicans are trying to completely erase the violence that occurred that day. And the heroism of the \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  and the Metropolitan police. You just showed an image of Metropolitan Police Officer Danny Hodges being crushed by the mob.\par \par \uc2 And I just wanted to be clear because we are starting to see over the last couple of weeks now that everything the Republicans have said over the last decade is just projection, right. These are the law and order supporters. But they voted against community policing and the American Rescue Plan. They vote against congressional gold medals for the heroes of January 6. And if they are ever in the majority, anywhere close to the majority, they're going to make the rioters the ones who receive the congressional gold medals. That's who they are.\par \par \uc2 They're veterans, they stand with the veterans, right. They stand with the flag and they denounce Colin Kaepernick. We saw last week, they are not with the veterans. They voted against the funding that would help those exposed to toxic burn pits.\par \par \uc2 So this is a crowd that knows how to project but when the rubber meets the road, when it comes down to standing up for law enforcement, when it comes down to standing up for our veterans, Republicans are just projecting and pandering the grievances, and Democrats are getting things done.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: As we mentioned at the top, you're suing the president. Earlier this week, the U.S. district court judge refused to grant the former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  immunity in several lawsuits brought against him by four \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  officers. How does this impact your pending civil lawsuit against Trump and are you optimistic about your own case?\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: I'm very optimistic. And I'm seeking accountability for the president assembling, inciting and aiming the mob at the Capitol to stop us from conducting the count of your vote and every one of your viewers' votes for the 2020 election and for the, you know, violence that ensued. And so we have advanced beyond the federal district court as far as the motion to dismiss the president's claim that he had absolute immunity.\par \par \uc2 Judge Meta (ph) put a 93-page opinion out there saying that there are bounds to a president's immunity. It's the first time ever that a sitting president was found not to have immunity while they were president and that's because you can't incite a mob.\par \par \uc2 And so we are moving up the chain of courts to the Circuit Court of Appeals, eventually probably the Supreme Court. But this case is entirely about whether we are a country that has a rule of law or whether we're going to be ruled by a mob.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: So that's the civil side of things, I want you to take a listen to committee vice chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  on the criminal side of things and on what a decision to not prosecute Trump criminally might mean.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): The question for us is are we a nation of laws. Are we a country where no one is above the law? And what do the facts and the evidence show?\par \par \uc2 So, you know, I think that we're going to continue to follow the facts. I think the Department of Justice will do that, but they have to make decisions about prosecution, understanding what it means if the facts and the evidence are there and they decide not to prosecute, how do we then call ourselves a nation of laws?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Congressman, in a week with no hearings, there was a lot of movement in the criminal investigation. Do you think that we're closer getting further along down the path of the criminal prosecution of Trump?\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: I think the investigation is approaching a crescendo. I mean how could they not after what we've learned from the January 6th Committee, after what the impeachment managers put on just weeks after the riot. It's clear.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:58]\par \par \uc2 And this is how I look at it, Zerlina. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , after the election, after the media called the race, after the states elected, you know, their own electors, after \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  himself conceded the election, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had just said, you know what, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's next president, I'm going to begin the transition. If he had done that, would the mob still have violently attacked the Capitol?\par \par \uc2 If the answer to that is yes they would've, then \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is innocent. If the answer is that only \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could've incited and aimed them, then I think he's guilty and I think that he needs to be held accountable. And I hope that reckoning is coming very soon.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Congressman \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  of California, thank you so much for being here tonight. And please stay safe.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we now know exactly how much Alex Jones' Sandy Hook lies are going to cost him. We will talk to the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reporter who has been covering this case all along. That is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:40:50]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 M7: Tonight, a Texas jury decided what conspiracy theorist Alex Jones deserved to pay one of the Sandy Hook families as punishment for repeatedly telling the horrible lie that their child was murdered didn't really exist and his brutal murder really didn't happen.\par \par \uc2 And, now they will get $45 million from the man who told that lie, Alex Jones. Jesse Lewis was only six years old when he was shot to death in his classroom with 19 other first graders who were massacred in school two weeks before Christmas Day in 2012.\par \par \uc2 It might have been more if Jesse hadn't shouted run to warn the other kids when the killers gun jammed. His father told the court about holding his dead child with a bullet hole in his head. His parents told jurors how because of Alex Jones's lies, people have shot at their house and threatened to kill them in addition to the other harassment they endured while grieving for their dead child.\par \par \uc2 Today's judgment was unanimous. And after the verdict, Jesse's mother addressed the other Sandy Hook families saying, I hope I did well for you.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now to discuss, Elizabeth Williamson from the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ". she's the author of "Sandy Hook: An American Tragedy and the Battle for Truth."\par \par \uc2 So Elizabeth, you followed this nightmare inflicted on these families after the nightmare of having their children murdered. How did it feel today in the courtroom to see Alex Jones finally being punished and held accountable for lying for it?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WILLIAMSON, "NEW YORK TIMES": Thanks for having me, Zerlina. I only wish that he was actually physically there for that. This trial has gone forward almost in his total absence.\par \par \uc2 But Scarlett Lewis, who you just spoke about, definitely had her moment. She was able to confront Alex Jones, to speak with him, it was through an accident of scheduling. He was really trying to avoid the families in this trial. But he turned up after lunch and she was still testifying.\par \par \uc2 So that gave her a moment to address him from the witness stand, actually more than a moment, for 90 minutes, she addressed her comments to him saying, why have you done this? You know this is wrong. Do you realize that every day of my life, since my son's murder, you have made life a little bit worse?\par \par \uc2 It was some of the most powerful testimony people in the room had ever seen. And it was really, I think, made a big impact on the jury.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: $45 million, about $50 million is a big -- that's a big number. Put this judgment into context for us. Is he going to be forced to pay for all of it? How much is he actually worth?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMSON: So there was a hearing today before the jury deliberated on punitive damages that shed light on his net worth. And they put that -- there was a financial experts speaking, and he put Alex Jones's net worth at between $130 and $270 million.\par \par \uc2 So this, although large, is a symbolic still judgment especially because the state of Texas caps awards to plaintiffs at $750,000. Now the lawyers for the Sandy Hook families say that they're going to challenge that, and there have been some constitutional questions raised about those award caps. So they think that this might be a really good test case on that front.\par \par \uc2 But regardless, the Sandy Hook families have always said, this is less about the money than about sending a warning to Americans that from Sandy Hook over the decade leading to the January 6th insurrection, Alex Jones has been emblematic of the spread of false narratives and disinformation in society.\par \par \uc2 He's got his fingerprints on every one of the major events that have been called into question. Pizza-gate, coronavirus myths, Charlottesville, and the January 6th insurrection.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: In the last minute here, you know, you did get to see him in court, although you said he wasn't in there for a portion of this trial today. Any sense that he feels any remorse, any hint of remorse you could glean?\par \par \uc2 [22:49:56]\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMSON: No. As one of the lawyers for the Sandy Hook family said in closing arguments today, Alex Jones will never change. And as if to underscore that statement, he was on his show tonight along with Roger Stone making excuses for himself, blaming everyone but himself for what happened and vowing to fight back, spreading more lies about this being a so-called show trial and trying to raise money for his legal defense by claiming falsely that he is bankrupt.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Well, the saga will continue on. Elizabeth Williamson, thank you so much for being here and helping us understand the latest. Please stay safe.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, as cases of monkeypox continue to be reported all across America, what is it actually like to have the virus? Well, someone who can tell us because he has actually had it will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:50:49]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: In late June, our next guest, Kyle Planck started to feel a little bit under the weather. He had a fever, he had a headache, he had some chills.\par \par \uc2 At first, he thought it might be COVID, but in the back of Kyle's mind, he wondered if it was actually something else. And that's because Kyle is a PhD candidate in pharmacology who studies infectious diseases.\par \par \uc2 He was following the news of monkeypox outbreaks happening all across Europe. And pretty soon, after the onset of his first symptoms, his suspicions were realized. Once his fever broke, spots began to appear on his hands and fingers before spreading all over his body.\par \par \uc2 And then came the pain. Pain was so intense, he would spend the entire day in the bathtub just for a little bit of relief. And more relief finally came when Kyle was prescribed the drug TPOXX.\par \par \uc2 But as Kyle's about to share, his path to getting TPOXX was anything but simple. And even as a researcher working in the field of infectious diseases with all of the right connections and resources, he still struggled to get the treatment he needed.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Kyle Planck, a pharmacology PhD candidate, who has, thankfully, recovered from monkeypox. So Kyle, first off, thank you so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 KYLE PLANCK, RECOVERED FROM MONKEYPOX: Thanks for having me, Zerlina, I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Thank you again. Tell us about your experience. Do you know how you got monkeypox? Tell us more about how you think you contracted it.\par \par \uc2 PLANCK: So, you know, I took pride -- I took part in a lot of Pride festivities in New York City at the end of June. Celebrating LGBTQ+ Pride. And there were a lot of, you know, close quarters and parties and things like that, so I'm pretty sure that that's where I picked it up.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Do you think that someone without your background would be able to figure out what was going on? I mean when your symptoms first started, without your background, do you think you would even have thought of monkeypox as a possibility?\par \par \uc2 PLANCK: Honestly not really, because at that point, it wasn't really being covered in the news a ton. It was there a little bit. But I think it was kind of the furthest thing from most people's minds.\par \par \uc2 And so I was really grateful that I was able to reason through that. But I think now it's a little bit more common and it's in the news, people know to think about it. But back then at the end of June it was still pretty rare.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: As we mentioned, you have all the connections you could possibly have in sort of first understanding what is going on but also knowing who to ask to get treatment.\par \par \uc2 But you still had trouble getting the TPOXX. What were the obstacles in your way in order to get the treatment you needed?\par \par \uc2 PLANCK: Yes. So part of it is, you know, with monkeypox, you're supposed to be isolating because you're contagious. You're not supposed to be going out in public.\par \par \uc2 So back at the very beginning of July when I was getting tested for monkeypox, I actually had to go into the hospital in order to get tested. And so that required me to go out in public, and I had to cover myself up, even though it was really hot and things like that.\par \par \uc2 And then once I got there, even though my doctors were able to advocate for me to get tested, they still had to do a ton of paperwork, hours worth of paperwork, to fill out the IND protocol for me to get access to TPOXX because my case qualified for it.\par \par \uc2 So even though I have all the connections and I was really lucky to get the care that I did, I still was in pain for about a week before I was able to start on the drug.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Wow. Last question on our last-minute here is how do you recommend other folks stay vigilant and keep themselves safe, knowing what you know?\par \par \uc2 PLANCK: Yes. So I think there is a lot of really great information out there, including the CDC Web site which has been recently updated. And not only has information about symptoms, but also how to get TPOXX.\par \par \uc2 So if you are experiencing any of the symptoms of monkeypox that you can see on the list, it's good to stay at home and isolate yourself until you're feeling better or until you're able to get into a doctor to get tested. Testing is a lot more widespread now, thankfully.\par \par \uc2 And if you do end up having a suspected case of monkeypox, there are steps that you can go through to get TPOXX. And those are outlined from the CDC.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Kyle Planck, thank you so much for being here and sharing your story. Please stay safe.\par \par \uc2 That does it for -- that's tonight's LAST WORD. I'm Zerlina Maxwell. Lawrence will be back on Monday.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:59]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, just hours after those eye-opening jobs numbers, the first votes on that big inflation fighting package are expected to begin.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020220806ei8500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for August 5, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Mehdi Hasan \par \uc2 7,872 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: The celebration, we`re going to play a little bit of that celebration, after Kansas voters -- and not just Democrats, Democrats, Republicans, and Independents got together to take down this law.\par \par \uc2 Sorry, Alito, you had a bad week because they had a good one. Joe Walsh, David Korn, thank you both very much. That is tonight`s "REIDOUT". Happy weekend. ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEHDI HASAN, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Today, there are more people working in America than before the pandemic began. In fact, there are more people working in America than any point in American history.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: The Biden boom, a jobs report so strong even Fox can`t deny it.\par \par \uc2 MARIA BARTIROMO\uc2 , HOST, \uc2 FOX BUSINESS NETWORK\uc2 : This is a blockbuster report. This is much higher than anybody was expecting.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Tonight, the string of victories for the Biden administration, and the big win possibly still to come.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): We`re actually helping the American people instead of just playing political games.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Then, the political payoff for doing the right thing.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): I`ve been very clear. I think he`s guilty of the most serious dereliction of duty of any president in our nation`s history.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Reports of a quiet Republican effort to try and save Liz Cheney`s seat. Will it work?\par \par \uc2 Plus, the junior podcaster from Texas and his performative outrage.\par \par \uc2 And a $50 million judgment against Alex Jones for his Sandy Hook lies when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN (on camera): Good evening from Washington DC. I`m Mehdi Hasan in for Chris Hayes. So, it`s Friday morning, the first Friday of the month of August, and you are a producer of Fox. The country is awaiting the July jobs report. And even the White House has admitted that the numbers are going to be bad. Of course, this is the story you go big on. The jobs report is good to be lousy. Joe Biden`s economy is in the gutter.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The White House is already lowering expectations for the July jobs report due out later today.\par \par \uc2 DR. KEVIN ROBERTS, PRESIDENT, HERITAGE FOUNDATION: The American economy is in terrible shape. Unfortunately, while you know I understand the political consequences of that for the President, we ought to lament that because as Americans, we really want everyone to flourish. That job report is an indicator that America is really not flourishing.\par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, CONTRIBUTOR, FOX NEWS: There`s some expectation that the jobs report will come in around 150,000 new jobs added which is much less than half a million jobs they`ve been added over the last several months.\par \par \uc2 Brian Kilmeade, Host, Fox News: Jobless claims were up yesterday above predictions which they think maybe the jobs report, adding jobs might not be as high.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: But then, 8:30 a.m. rolls around, and the report comes out. And it is way better than expected. In fact, more than doubled the number of jobs that economists predicted. U.S. employers added 528,000 new jobs in July for the 19th straight month of positive jobs growth. The economy has now regained all of the jobs that were lost during the Trump administration at the start of the pandemic. You can see that dramatic plunge in early 2020 on this graph.\par \par \uc2 And there were other positive signs as well. The unemployment rate fell to 3.5 percent. And Americans have seen a 5.2 percent increase in their average earnings over the past year. All of this is new, strong evidence that the country has not entered a recession. But if you work at Fox, and you just spent the morning telling your audience that the report was going to be terrible, and the economy is in shambles, what do you do? Well, apparently you find a way to spin a really good jobs report into bad news.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARTIROMO: We have the good news and the bad news here. First, the top line number, the headline here, this is a blockbuster report. This is much higher than anybody was expecting.\par \par \uc2 The bad news, this ensures that we are going to see a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  that stays as aggressive as ever. The bad news is that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will continue raising interest rates aggressively. That`s going to make our borrowing much more expensive and holding debt much more expensive.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, HOST, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK: I think it`s probably the more important thing is participation. The unemployment rate went to 3.5 percent from 3.6 but participation actually went down. It`s still somewhat amazing to me that we have not gotten back up to the same level of participation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Fox was so committed to this bit that they even criticize the White House for getting the number wrong. Take a look at that banner on screen. White House misses forecast on lower July jobs growth. I mean, yes, they missed it, but only because the report was so much better than anticipated. They missed it in a good way.\par \par \uc2 Fox of course has also really, really been pushing the idea that we are in a recession. And we can argue over technical definitions, but I`m sorry, it is very hard to say that we are in a recession when companies in America hired more than half a million people in the single month. Sure, the American economy still has some problems, but it`s also doing quite well.\par \par \uc2 Now, Fox cannot handle America doing well because that means that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats are doing well. They are pro-America patriots only when Republicans are in charge of America. Democratic Senator Joe Manchin of West Virginia called them out on this the other day live on air.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:22]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS FAULKNER, HOST, FOX NEWS: You got a president --\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: The election? So, what happens? I am not going to make a statement on that.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: -- who`s approval rating is like as low as Congress`.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, can`t we all do something great? Are you afraid --\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: I mean, no offense, but you know that when you get into the 30s, no one is that popular and that`s this president.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Harris, are scared we`re going to do something good or help our country and someone might take credit for it?\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: I`m, of course, not. My father served. Are you kidding? Service is in the Bible.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, it sounds like you are.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: That`s what we do. We serve our fellow man and woman. Of course.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: That`s exactly --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: My father served. I found myself cheering for Joe Manchin for the first time after he trolled Fox like that. But Manchin is on a roll at the moment. He made this massive $700 billion deal that if it passes would do a lot of good on climate on health care. And Manchin and Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  also now -- also now have their final holdout, Arizona Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  on board.\par \par \uc2 They did have to make some changes to get her approval, including removing a provision that would have limited the preferential tax treatment of income treated -- of income earned by some wealthy hedge fund managers and private equity executives, sometimes called the carried interest loophole. Thank you, Senator Sinema.\par \par \uc2 Still, Democrats replaced that with a new one percent excise tax that companies would have to pay on the amount of stock that they repurchase, which is a good thing that will raise more money and ensure that the package still reduce the federal deficit by as much as $300 billion, the same amount of Democrats aim for with the original deal.\par \par \uc2 And if this deal goes through the House plans to vote on it a week from today, it`ll cap off a string of recent triumphs for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats. Just in the past week, we saw the killing of al Qaeda leader I`m al-Zawahiri, a major legislative achievement with the Chips Bill, investing billions in semiconductor manufacturing and scientific research, a huge win for abortion rights in Kansas, and these great jobs numbers today.\par \par \uc2 So, should the White House, should Democrats dare to believe that they could make it through the November Midterms with a better outcome than expected? Joining me now is \uc2 Stephanie Kelton\uc2 , a former chief economist for the Senate Banking Committee, as well as a former economic adviser to Bernie Sanders` 2016 and 2020 presidential campaigns. And Julian Castro, former mayor of San Antonio, Texas, former Secretary of Housing and Urban Development under President Obama. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, let me start with you. Put these jobs numbers in context, how good are they? How big are they?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE KELTON\uc2 , FORMER CHIEF ECONOMIST, SENATE BANKING COMMITTEE: They`re huge. They blindsided virtually everyone as you said in your opening, Mehdi. We didn`t see this coming. Economists, analysts, Wall Street forecasters, everybody missed this one. This was a blockbuster number 528,000 jobs, and job revisions to the previous two months. So, the reports that we got last month and the month before are being revised upwards.\par \par \uc2 So, we have some very, very good news when it comes to, you know, the labor market. This is broad base. I mean, it`s everything from leisure and hospitality, to manufacturing and construction, and government jobs. It`s business and it is across the whole of our economy. We are seeing extreme robust job creation and resilience in the labor market.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: So, Julian, President Biden spoke today about how hard it is for some Americans to see and feel the good in this jobs report because he conceded there is inflation that`s still being dealt with. Have a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I know people will hear today`s extraordinary jobs report and say they don`t see it, they don`t feel it in their own lives. I know how hard it is. I know it`s hard to feel good about job creation when you already have a job and you`re dealing with rising prices, food, and gas, and so much more. I get it. That`s why I`m doing everything in my power to lower the cost for families.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Julian, in the long run, is inflation going to overshadow these great jobs numbers?\par \par \uc2 JULIAN CASTRO, FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF HOUSING AND URBAN DEVELOPMENT: Well, Mehdi, I mean, there`s no question that people feel that on a daily basis. At the same time, gas prices have been falling for several weeks now. I think something like 50 days in a row. These are fantastic jobs numbers. And so what you saw the President doing there was something that he has to do which is not only to celebrate the progress that we`re making on the economy, and there is a lot of progress, 3.5 percent unemployment rate, the lowest it`s been measured in 50 years, nine and a half million jobs created under this administration, but also recognize the challenge, the difficulty that still exists for American families.\par \par \uc2 And he told, I thought, a story that people could relate to of as a young man sitting at the dinner table with his parents as they talked about how they were going to afford the next month`s rent or food. And a lot of people can relate to that conversation. So, he did both of those things that he needs to do to take credit for the good stuff, the progress, but also to make sure that people who are still struggling out there understand that they have a president that hasn`t forgotten about them, and is going to keep working to make things better for them.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:33]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Stephanie, two quarters of negative growth traditionally means recession, half a million jobs in a month traditionally means not a recession. Are we in a recession?\par \par \uc2 KELTON: Well, Mehdi, they`re very difficult to call in real time. We usually don`t know we are in a recession until months, sometimes even a year or more after we`ve entered one. I have to say you -- you`re going to find it pretty hard for me to come out and tell you that we`re in a recession at the moment given the job numbers that we just saw today.\par \par \uc2 It`s a mixed picture. There`s no question that you can look for data points in this economy and you can spot signs of weakness. No question. But job numbers like the kind that we saw today send, I think, a pretty strong signal that the consumer hasn`t retrenched. This is what you`re watching for. You`re watching for consumers to start closing up their wallets and pocketbooks and spending less and then businesses see customers disappearing. And that`s where you began to see signs of trouble.\par \par \uc2 This is a good thing. When people get jobs, when they find jobs, and they have been come to spend, it`s a good way to support an economic recovery. So, it gets more difficult to buy into the recession narrative despite that sort of conventional rule of thumb about two consecutive quarters.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Julian, some Democrats have been dodging the question of whether \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  should run again. Does this week of success, not just the jobs report, but the Zawahiri killing, the CHIP spilled a PAC Bill. Does this put some of those concerns about his reelection prospects to rest or are there more fundamental issues that he still has to deal with?\par \par \uc2 CASTRO: Well, I mean, I think the fundamental issue is that you have Midterm elections -- if we`re just talking politically, you have midterm elections that are coming up in just a few months. And so, I think everybody`s focus is on those Midterms. And people say, you know, nothing succeeds like success. And this last week or so has been a week of real success for the President and for Democrats whether you`re talking about this news on the economy, or the fact that he`s going to get big legislation done.\par \par \uc2 What happened in Kansas that is showing that the Democratic base out there is motivated on the abortion issue, and he`s going to take that to the polls in November very likely, things are looking a lot better right now than they were even just a couple of weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Julian, quick last question. We`re almost out of time. I got to ask you, if you were managing the Democrats campaign in November, would you be running top most on abortion rights or on Republican extremism or on jobs and the economy?\par \par \uc2 CASTRO: I`d be running on Republican extremism as expressed by abortion, but also January 6, and other things. They`ve shown themselves to be far outside the mainstream.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Julian Castro, \uc2 Stephanie Kelton\uc2 , thank you both for your time. I appreciate your analysis.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the director of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  issues a new warning about far-right extremism. Texas podcaster Ted Cruz throws a tantrum. The dangers of the current Republican rhetoric. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, \uc2 FBI\uc2 : I understand that passions run high especially on an issue like abortion, but they`re just way too many people who seemed to think that it -- that that justifies engaging in violence and destruction of property and threats of violence. You mentioned Molotov cocktails. I feel like every day I`m getting briefed on somebody throwing a Molotov cocktail at someone for some issue. It`s crazy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: That was \uc2 FBI\uc2  director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  warning about a spike that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is seeing and politically motivated violence in this country. But not everyone seemed to be focused on the real danger of rising political violence in this country. Podcaster slash Senator Ted Cruz was allowed in the hearing because he is a United States senator, and he used his time to remove parts of his cowboy costume and pretend to be outraged over the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s focused on violent domestic extremists.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Yesterday, it was reported that Project Veritas had obtained a copy of an \uc2 FBI\uc2  training material which listed various symbols and themes which in the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s estimation, were indicative of, "militia violence extremism." Also included on this as a text and I was particularly struck is the Gonzales battle flag, "come and take it," as indicative of being a violent extremist militia. Well, I will self-report right now. That every day in the Senate I wear my boots that have the Gonzalez battle flag on the back of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Beyond the pole-clutching from all boots, no cattle Republicans like Ted Cruz, you can tell a nerve has been struck. Republicans don`t particularly like being lumped in with every extremist group and domestic terrorist. And hey, welcome to my world. Hey, conservatives, you might actually find some sympathy from this Muslim guy after the last 20-plus years that you put my community through. Vague indications of extremism, accusations of collective guilt, sound familiar, Ted?\par \par \uc2 [20:20:25]\par \par \uc2 But let`s not take this too far. It`s not like there`s a Gitmo for January 6 rioters. Although, wait, there was this booth at CPAC in Texas today where you`re meant to watch a MAGA hat wearing prisoner cry as you listen to a headset playing testimony of the people arrested for rioting on January 6. You know, an example of the very right wing political violence that Director Wray has been trying to warn us about.\par \par \uc2 Asha Rangappa is a former Special Agent in the New York division of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  specializing in counterintelligence investigations, as well as an editor at the Just Security blog. And she joins me now. Asha, thanks for coming on the show. The director of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  called politically motivated violence "almost a 365 day phenomenon." How does that compare to your time in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and how concerned are you should we be?\par \par \uc2 ASHA RANGAPPA, FORMER FBI SPECIAL AGENT: Well, Mehdi, thanks -- thank you for having me on. I`m very concerned. And this is a shift, right? I mean, in my time at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the terrorism threat was international terrorism. It was al-Qaeda, it was ISIS. And that`s where the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s resources were focused. Last year, the National Security Council released a domestic -- a strategy to counter domestic terrorism. And it identified the growing threat of domestic terrorism and specifically two different types of domestic terrorism. One is racial -- racially motivated extremist violence, and the other is militia extremist violence. And both of these are on the rise.\par \par \uc2 And so what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is doing is dovetailing with that national strategy and they`re coordinating with other intelligence partners. And that document which Ted Cruz was pointing to, is really a descriptive document about specifically militia violent extremists and what they have observed with anti-government and anti-law enforcement groups and how they express their shared ideology through these symbols, which groups are going to do, terrorist groups are going to do.\par \par \uc2 Importantly, at the top of that document behind him, it says that they should not independently be used to, you know, assess that someone is engaged in illegal activity.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Well, thank you for putting that in context. I`m the last person who`s going to defend the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s handling of any terrorist threats, given what happened to a lot of Muslims in America over the last 20 years. But I find it ironic that conservatives are kind of gnashing and wailing right now. We have this weird scenario, where every time the \uc2 FBI\uc2  or the DHS tries to warn about the threat from right-wing domestic terrorism, conservatives, Republicans say were being targeted. Well, no, only if you`re admitting to being domestic terrorist. There`s a weird conflation going on by the right.\par \par \uc2 RANGAPPA: Well, unfortunately, Mehdi, this is increasingly their base that they are appealing to. I mean, not all of them are terrorists, but clearly if they`re sympathetic to the people who attack the Capitol and wanted to kill lawmakers and hang the Vice President which they explicitly are -- they feel like it`s a hoax to be, you know, even investigating it -- then they are appealing to that subset of people.\par \par \uc2 And I`ve said since January 6, 2021, that we basically have a domestic terror movement in this country that has representation in Congress. And I think imagine maybe if after 9/11 you had Congress people expressing sympathy for them, listening to, you know, detainees at Guantanamo crying, you know, and kind of creating a narrative around that, that that should be something that you know, should be -- their actions should be celebrated.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: I mean, \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2 , congressman from Arizona wears a wristband with that insurrectionist name on it, but yes, I can`t imagine a 9/11 comparison. Asha there`s an outlandish story that`s been making the rounds in MAGA land that was repeated by a guest on Steve Bannon`s show at CPAC today. It`s about the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , which like many federal agencies, have law enforcement division. Have a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They`re stalking the \uc2 IRS\uc2  with guns and ammo. Why are they doing this? To go after -- well, we`re going to go after corporations in the -- in the Caymans. That`s like six people, right? You got 90,000 people. You know why? They`re going to come after you. They`re going to come after you. They`re going to come after everybody back there. They`re going to weaponize the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , use the government to intimidate every single Trump supporter and MAGA supporter in America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Asha, as a former law enforcement agent, what do you think when you hear people basically stoking untrue paranoia like that? Where does that lead?\par \par \uc2 [20:25:00]\par \par \uc2 RANGAPPA: Well, it leads to political violence. I mean, what he`s setting up there is a dichotomy between -- and he`s explicitly said between Trump supporters and the government. So, the government is going to come after you if you`re a Trump supporter. And this is kind of also in line with what I mentioned before about the militia violent extremists who based their ideology in, you know, as anti-government, anti-law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 It reminds me maybe of a while ago, this is several decades ago, but when the \uc2 NRA\uc2  started adopting this narrative as well, and they had a poster of FBI SWAT agents in crosshairs that actually made \uc2 George HW Bush\uc2  renounced his life membership from the \uc2 NRA\uc2 , but that`s what they`re appealing to is this idea that the government can`t be trusted. Law enforcement can`t be trusted. And it does feed into the narrative that justifies what happened on January 6.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: It really is 90s paranoia on steroids and just talking of right wing extremists. Last quick question, I want to ask you about a verdict that came down a few hours ago for punitive damages in the Alex Jones trial, it was a pretty astonishing result as the judge read it. Have a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYA GUERRA GAMBLE, JUDGE, TEXAS 459TH DISTRICT COURT: Question number one, $4,200,000. Question number two, $20,500,000. Question number three, $20,500,000. All right, this question is addressed to my jury. Is this your verdict, so say, each and all of you?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: That`s $45.2 million awarded against Alex Jones for his shameful lies about Sandy Hook. Your reaction?\par \par \uc2 RANGAPPA: Well, I think that it`s a notable damages award. I mean, what they were asking for there -- so the jury had already awarded what are called compensatory damages. This is for the pain and suffering that was -- that was experienced by the plaintiffs in this case because of his actions. The second part, the $45 million is punitive damages. This is about punishing the defendant for his conduct, you know, making him feel the pinch to create a deterrent for him and future --0 other people in the future, to not engage in the same conduct.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, in that sense, they are sending a message. And I should note that he has a couple of other lawsuits still coming up. So, these kinds of damages might pile up on top of that.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Yes, there are. It`s Connecticut next. Good luck, Alex Jones. Asha Rangappa, thank you for your time tonight. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 RANGAPPA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Still to come, new reports indicate the DOJ could be, might be closing in on \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , but the man keeps on scheming. How much trouble could he really be in? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: The Justice Department is cranking its way through the hundreds of January 6th rioters arrested and charged in the wake of the attack. Today, one writer who wore a jacket with his name, the name of his company and his company phone number on it pleaded guilty to destroying government property. He could face up to eight months in jail.\par \par \uc2 At least 884 people have been charged in connection with January the 6th, and a big one may be on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 USA Today reports that lawyers for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  "have been in contact with Justice Department officials in an apparent attempt to block access to conversations with his former top aides."\par \par \uc2 Trump has also repeatedly -- reportedly, excuse me, ignored advice to cut off contact with his former White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 . After aides to Meadows Cassidy Hutchinson provided stunning testimony to the January 6th committee potentially singling out Meadows as a key witness in the probe against the ex- president.\par \par \uc2 Quinta Jurecic is a senior editor of the Lawfare Blog where she wrote today about the lessons and limits of the January 6 committee. Quinta, thanks for coming on the show. The committee and the DOJ are running almost in parallel. But certainly at times on intersecting tracks. Which investigation do you think will ultimately have the biggest impact?\par \par \uc2 QUINTA JURECIC, SENIOR EDITOR, LAWFARE BLOG: I think it`s a question of understanding that, you know, these are really investigations that are doing different things. Then, January 6 committee, of course, doesn`t have the power to bring an indictment. They don`t bring a criminal trial.\par \par \uc2 What they`re doing really is more of kind of telling the story of what happened on January 6th, building a public sort of moral and political case against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and they`re certainly pushing the Justice Department on the criminal aspect as well.\par \par \uc2 And what the Justice Department is doing is deciding whether to bring those higher level criminal cases. And as you say, pushing forward with these hundreds and hundreds of lower level charges and some significant charges of seditious conspiracy against the people who are actually rioting in the capitol that day.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: And Quinta, you wrote about the committee`s limits, the one-six committee`s limits, particularly that "The January 6, investigators must assume that their work has a hard deadline of January 2023. And because of that, there are surely difficult trade-offs being made around what to prioritize in the limited time that remains to finish this work."\par \par \uc2 Do you think that limited time, the deadline could prevent us from getting a full accounting of the crimes committed around the Capitol riot?\par \par \uc2 [20:35:02]\par \par \uc2 JURECIC: As my colleague and I Molly Reynolds wrote in that piece, the committee has always known about that deadline. There`s always been that possibility very likelihood that Republicans would take control of the House and not choose to renew the committee in the new -- the new Congress.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that they have been working with that in mind. That said, it`s certainly true that, you know, they`ve said a lot about how these hearings have surfaced new evidence, we saw the testimony of Cassidy Hutchinson, the testimony of \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  following Hutchinson`s testimony, the committee has said that new people are coming in and testifying.\par \par \uc2 So, even as we see them kind of gathering momentum, it`s important to keep in mind, they really only have a few months left before they have to put out this public report.\par \par \uc2 And so, as impressive as their work has been, I do think it`s worth keeping in mind that they`re really about to run up against a wall.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: So, what significance do you see in Trump`s lawyers talking directly to the DOJ? Is this a prelude to something? Or are we making a big deal out of a routine procedure?\par \par \uc2 JURECIC: So, I`d certainly caution viewers not to get too excited. We saw reports like these during the Mueller investigation, and of course --\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Yes, we do.\par \par \uc2 JURECIC: -- do not result in any charges against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But I think it is a reflection of how in recent weeks, we have seen a lot of news reports that suggest that the Justice Department investigation is heating up. We saw reports that White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , his deputy Pat Philbin, are testifying before a federal grand jury. There are a lot of indications that the department is at the very least, looking very seriously and closely at Trump`s personal role in ginning up the violence on January 6th and the events ahead of it.\par \par \uc2 So, I think this new reporting reflects that you know, Trump like everyone else, he reads the news, his lawyers probably have heard what`s going on. And as lawyers do, they`re taking steps to try to defend their client.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Last quick question, if we do get more public hearings out of the one-six committee, what would you want to hear from them? What would you want to prioritize in them?\par \par \uc2 JURECIC: So, we`re expecting to hear more from them in September. They have promised that. I think that they have left a great deal open. They`ve done an enormous amount in sketching out the sort of the broad strokes of what happened on January 6th. And Trump`s personal culpability.\par \par \uc2 I personally think that one of the big open questions that we have has to do with whether or not there was any knowledge or coordination between the Trump camp and the White House, and the folks on the ground on January 6th like the Oath Keepers, like the Proud Boys, that strikes me as something that you know, if the committee brings to light any new evidence in that, that that could be potentially very groundbreaking.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Indeed, it could be. Quinta Jurecic, thank you so much for your time and for your reporting. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JURECIC: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Still ahead, one of the most notorious Republicans in modern American politics is doing the right thing? How should we react to that? That debate, coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: In 2020, Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  of Wyoming won the Republican primary there by almost 50 points. Then \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  staged a coup, Cheney became one of his most vocal critics, and now she`s trailing her election denying opponent by double digits in this month`s Republican primary in Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 Unlike most of her Republican colleagues in the House, since January the 6th 2021, Cheney has consistently spoken out against Trump as a unique threat to our democracy.\par \par \uc2 And after she agreed to become the vice chair of the committee investigating January the 6th, she solidified her position as something of a Republican pariah.\par \par \uc2 But it seems as though some of the party establishment may have changed their minds. Axios reporting that a handful of Republican operatives are quietly working to boost Cheney in her long shot primary, signaling that there is in fact an appetite among a few oligarch (PH) Republicans to try and move on from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But this is still an uphill battle for Cheney. So this week, she brought out perhaps her most high profile and loyal supporter to make the case in an ad.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DICK CHENEY\uc2 , FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: In our nation`s 246 year history, there has never been an individual who is a greater threat to our republic than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He tried to steal the last election using lies and violence to keep himself in power after the voters had rejected him.\par \par \uc2 He is a coward. A real man wouldn`t lie to his supporters. He lost his election, and he lost big. I know it, he knows it and deep down I think most Republicans know it.\par \par \uc2 Lynne and I are so proud of Liz for standing up for the truth, doing what`s right, honoring her oath to the Constitution, when so many in our party are too scared to do so.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: So, watching that, I`m reminded of that great headline from the satirical site ClickHole, Heartbreaking: The worst person, you know, just made a great point.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  is certainly in the running for the title of worst person in modern American politics. He is directly or indirectly responsible for some of the most cruel, shameful and lawless episodes in recent American history. And he`s never been held to account for all the deaths and the bloodshed for the Iraq war for torture, for Guantanamo Bay and countless other calamities.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:01]\par \par \uc2 You could also argue that Cheney`s catastrophic reign is possibly the most powerful and consequential vice president ever laid the groundwork for Trump`s takeover of the Republican Party, and his expansive view of unchecked executive power made the office of the president that much more dangerous once Trump occupied it.\par \par \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  isn`t much better either. She might be even more conservative than her father, she voted with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  93 percent of the time, she continues to oppose, among other things, abortion rights, voting rights and gun control.\par \par \uc2 And neither Cheney especially Dick has offered any kind of real public apology for their past embrace of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  both in 2016 and again in 2020.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the thing. Yes, the Cheneys have done considerable damage to America and American politics. And yes, the Cheneys are 100 percent right about the threat Trump currently poses to American democracy.\par \par \uc2 You can hold both of those thoughts in your head at the same time as I do, they`re not mutually exclusive. And consider this, if the man who was once dubbed the prince of darkness and his right wing daughter, if Dick and Liz say the Republican Party has gone off the rails, just think about how extreme that must make the GOP right now.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, I`ll ask a left-wing journalist and a former member of the Bush- Cheney reelection team, what they think about \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and reaching out across the political divide to stop Trump, don`t go away.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Not that long ago, it would have been unthinkable to imagine someone on the left saying something anything nice about Liz or \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2 . I still refuse to say anything nice about \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has for some unusual political bedfellows and the united front against the ex-president and his anti-democratic tendencies require as at least in this one instance, partnering with people you might otherwise find completely noxious, when they for once are willing to stand up for what is right.\par \par \uc2 Stuart Stevens is a senior adviser with the Lincoln project, a former member of the Bush-Cheney presidential campaigns. And the author of it was All a Lie: How the Republican Party Became \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And John Nichols is a National Affairs Correspondent for The Nation magazine, where he recently published a piece titled can trump oust \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  from Congress?\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for joining me. John, let me start with you. It is understandably difficult for many on the left to give \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  a man accused of war crimes credit for anything.\par \par \uc2 But as you tweeted, as someone who wants thought \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  was the greatest threat, I have to agree with him on this one. You have been a pretty vocal critic of the Cheneys in the past. Explain why in effect, you are on their side now?\par \par \uc2 JOHN NICHOLS, NATIONAL AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT, THE NATION: Well, I choke on the notion of the phrase on their side. What I will say is that I believe that \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  should have been impeached and removed from office. So, I think he was an incredibly dangerous figure who did tremendous damage to his country into the world.\par \par \uc2 By the same token, he`s not in power right now. And his daughter, frankly, isn`t in a lot of power. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is very much on the march, he is part of a U.S. and in fact, global effort to move our politics toward an extreme nationalism that is incredibly dangerous, racist phobic.\par \par \uc2 And so, when he says that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the greatest threat to the United States at this point, in fact, I think in many ways to the world, I would have to agree with him, which is the first time I think I`ve ever said that.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: I`m with you, John. I`m with you.\par \par \uc2 Stuart, here`s my issue with \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2 . You are a Never Trump Republican who wrote a very good book about how it was all a lie. You said you got it all wrong. And I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 The Cheneys, though, backed Trump both times round. And they`ve never really apologized for doing that. In fact, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  still supports most of the hard right GOP agenda, including opposing voting rights protection.\par \par \uc2 So, as much as I say, they`re right on this issue, and Liz Cheney`s doing a good job of the one-six committee, they still haven`t done that.\par \par \uc2 STUART STEVENS, SENIOR ADVISER, THE LINCOLN PROJECT: Well, look, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  has said voting for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was a mistake. And the question I would ask is, why isn`t the rest of the Republican Party doing what the Cheneys are doing?\par \par \uc2 All it is, is they`re just saying words, they`re taking a position. They`re not being asked to charge a machine gun nest or take charge of beats the way others have to defend their democracy.\par \par \uc2 And look, I think the whole point of democracy is to have these serious disagreements about policy, about whether or not this person is a good president or a terrible president.\par \par \uc2 But that`s not where we are now. There really is an autocratic movement in America that is led by the Republican Party. And I think we have to agree that the Cheneys are showing decency and courage by calling it out.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Certainly, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  has shown political courage, there`s no doubt about that.\par \par \uc2 John, you also tweeted "ironically, Cheney was such a disastrous vice president, the Republicans recognize he could never be considered as a 2008 presidential prospect. Unfortunately, the party made no serious effort at course correction after the Bush-Cheney debacle and that opened the way for Trump."\par \par \uc2 I do think that`s an element that gets under discussed here, Bush and Cheney, as people like \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  have argued as well laid the groundwork for a Trump presidency, Trump bet big that going after Bush, going off to Iraq would be a winning issue in 2016. And he was right.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:08]\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: That`s right. And in fact, even Republicans came to hate Bush and Cheney. But the fact of the matter is, that when Bush and Cheney were in office, they abused that office horribly. They use that office to do lawless and irresponsible things at home and abroad, and did tremendous damage to the country.\par \par \uc2 They were not punished for that, they weren`t held to account for that via impeachment or any sort of real action as regards Congress. Nor were they held to account for that by the Republican Party. They were allowed by the Cheneys and the Bushes were allowed to become almost elder statesmen within the party.\par \par \uc2 And then, as time went on, as the party moved further and further to the right, even they got bit by it. But I think it`s really important to understand that when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was at his, perhaps at his most dangerous in 2016, at this point where he could have been stopped, right? Republicans like Dick and \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  did not try to stop them. Most Republicans fell and got on board, and in fact they were in the culture of the party as it now exists, which is, it is more interested in winning office and holding power than it is in doing right by the country.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Stuart, what do you make of the fact that per Axios reporting, some Republican operatives are apparently quietly backing Cheney in her primary? And can an ad from \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  change any Republican hearts and minds in this Wyoming primary?\par \par \uc2 STEVENS: Well, let`s just pause for a second how extraordinary it is what we`re talking about. We`re saying that Republican operatives are supporting a Cheney who is a member of Congress trying to get her reelected in Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we`ve really reached a point where that`s worthy of a story. I mean, that`s extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 I have no idea what`s going to happen in this primary. I hope Liz wins. But I really don`t think that she cares that much. I don`t think that`s what she`s focused on.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I get involved in politics, we used to say, if you only stand for reelection, you don`t stand for anything. And I kind of thought we believe that. And I agree with everything John said about what the party should have done in 16. They should have done it in December 15th when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  call for a Muslim ban.\par \par \uc2 And I think the inability to imagine \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has always helped \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Republicans certainly. I was in that camp, couldn`t imagine a guy who talked in public about having sex with his daughter, getting nominated for the Republican nomination, and didn`t think that he could win the presidency.\par \par \uc2 And I think we`re in that same dangerous inability to imagine what will happen if Republicans are given a chance to do what they want to do to our democratic system.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: I mean, it`s interesting you mentioned the Islamophobia of Trump and the Muslim ban. Of course, this is my problem as a Muslim who has a bit of a memory. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  pushed a lot of war on terror Islamophobic tropes in her time in office and didn`t split with Trump after the Muslim ban stuff. She backed him for several more years, and voted to reelect him.\par \par \uc2 But look, I`m glad she`s speaking out now. I`m one of those people who says, I`m glad she`s doing it. It may cost her a job.\par \par \uc2 John Nichols moment of truth for you. You were living in Wyoming, you`re a Democrat, and you could vote in the primary. Would you vote for \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  to save her?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS\uc2 : Very wrestled with this. I don`t know that I would. I think that at the end of the day, voting for Cheney might be a step too far for me. But I don`t live in Wyoming. And when I was there -- I was there, I talked to a lot of sincere Democrats who will cross over and vote for \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , someone who they never ever thought they would vote for because they think it`s that important.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Quick last question to you, Stuart. There`s a lot of talk about \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  mounting a presidential bid, which I find a bit odd, given she`s struggling to save her seat in Congress.\par \par \uc2 I mean, would she run as a third party person? Wouldn`t that just split the vote? I mean, what do you make of all the talk around \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  in 24?\par \par \uc2 STEVENS: I don`t know what to make of it. And I think anybody who says that they know what would happen in a political science sense about whether an independent candidate coming from the conservative wing of the Republican Party is kidding themselves.\par \par \uc2 I can argue this round or flat, I can argue that it would guarantee that either \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or whoever the Republican nominee is will not win, which I think is important.\par \par \uc2 I can also argue that it will make it too easy for those Republicans who voted for Biden to have sort of an off ramp. If they don`t have the vote for the Democrats that they can vote for this independent, that would reelect Donald -- that would elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or Republican.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Well, let`s see if she even survives this congressional race. We`re out of time, Stuart Stevens, John Nichols, thank you both for a fascinating and important discussion I think. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 [21:00:03]\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN for this week, I`ll be back on Sunday at 8:00 p.m. with my show "THE MEHDI HASAN SHOW". "MSNBC PRIME" though starts right now with Ali Velshi. Good evening, Ali.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220806ei8500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for August 4, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Ken Dilanian, Charles Coleman, Molly Hunter, Kelly  O`Donnell, Geoff Bennett, Adrienne Elrod, Brendan Buck, Maria Teresa Kumar,  Al Sharpton, Vaughn Hillyard \par \uc2 8,254 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good afternoon. I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 And we`re following the breaking news. Major developments in two highly emotional, political, and racially charged stories, first, reaction coming in fast following that harsh sentence for \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner, nine years in a Russian prison. President Biden calling for her release as negotiations are ongoing. We`ll have more on that in a moment. And more than two years after 26-year-old Breonna Taylor was shot and killed in her own apartment, four Louisville police officers involved in that deadly raid have been charged with federal civil rights violations.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to discuss this, NBC News justice correspondent Ken Dilanian. Civil rights attorney and MSNBC Legal Analyst Charles Coleman. And Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 , founder of the National Action Network. Rev., you know Breonna`s family. And as you know, she was an aspiring nurse. She was working as an EMT when she was killed. Police were looking for a former boyfriend who wasn`t even there. As someone who has fought for the family to get justice, what`s your take on this decision today by the Attorney General?\par \par \uc2 REV. \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , PRESIDENT, NATIONAL ACTION NETWORK: Well, I think it is an important step in the right direction. Clearly, when you look at the indictments, two of the officers have been indicted because they actively tried to cover up what they did. They conspired to violate Breonna Taylor`s civil rights, and so was the lady. The other went around the house, broke from the pack of policemen, and shot through what they call the curtains, that was set where you couldn`t see through, shot in and ended up with a bullet gun and go into a neighbor`s house. I mean, clearly violating civil rights.\par \par \uc2 I think that what is important here is as we dealt with the Breonna Taylor case, and I was just with a mother at the White House when they signed the George Floyd executive order, and she was still pushing, as with so many activist groups, including the National Action Network Louisville. And I`m glad that we see that this administration has now brought federal cases in terms of the George Floyd killers, the Ahmaud Arbery, and now on Breonna Taylor.\par \par \uc2 So, in three critical cases, we have seen Judge Garland, who is the Attorney General, and his Civil Rights Division where \uc2 Kristen Clarke\uc2  actively do what we did not see in past administrations. Now, there are many others that we would have loved to see being indicted, the shooters and all in Breonna Taylor. But we`ve gotten 100 percent more action out of this Justice Department on some of these cases than we got into the previous administration.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, let me play for you Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  this morning when he was announcing the charges.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: The federal charges announced today allege that members of the place-based Investigations Unit falsified the affidavit used to obtain the search warrant of Ms. Taylor`s home. That this act violated federal civil rights laws, and that those violations resulted in Miss Taylor`s death.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ken, dive a little more deeply into the charges for us if you will.\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, MSNBC JUSTICE & INTELLIGENCE CORRESPONDENT: Sure. As you said at the top, Chris, this is the case that sparked a nationwide outcry. A 26- year-old black woman gunned down by police, who broke into her apartment in the middle of the night, based on wrong information. Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  today announcing these civil rights charges against four current and former Louisville police officers, saying that the warrant wasn`t just a mistake, it was a lie that officers tried to cover up.\par \par \uc2 Garland said the misconduct directly led to Breonna Taylor`s death. Three of the officers are accused of conspiring to falsify the search warrant application, and a fourth as Rev. alluded to, was charged with unlawful use of deadly force. These charges are coming after widespread outrage when no police officer was charged by the state of Kentucky in Taylor`s death.\par \par \uc2 One officer, Brett Hankison, was charged with firing his weapon recklessly in a way that endangered Taylor`s neighbors if you recall. He was acquitted in Kentucky, but he`s now charged again based on the same conduct. And court documents say the lead detective in the case, Joshua Jaynes, wrote in the March 2020 warrant application that he verified with the U.S. Postal Inspector that Taylor`s ex-boyfriend, a man police suspected of dealing drugs, had received packages at her address.\par \par \uc2 But prosecutors are now saying Jaynes knew that information was wrong. They say that he and others conspire to mislead federal, state, and local investigators. Garland said they even met in a garage two months after the shooting to get their false story straight. At least one of these officers has -- is the subject, Chris, of criminal information suggesting he is cooperating and will plead guilty. Some of the others have been arrested and they will be brought to court for their initial appearances, Chris.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Charles, so Rev. touched on this how this is a game-changer in terms of this administration compared to the previous administration. But explain to us, DOJ`s civil rights division`s unique role in the justice system and what you`ll be looking for in this case.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES COLEMAN, CIVIL RIGHTS ATTORNEY: Well, Chris, what you have to pay attention to when you`re talking about this case is the unique way in which the DOJ is able to make out the charges in terms of how these actual actions violated Breonna Taylor`s civil rights. And I think that`s the distinction that viewers need to understand separate what the charges would have been when a state level to a federal level. They make the connection to on a civil rights level, on a level up what your constitutional rights have for you, as a human being. These violations basically -- they violated that. These actions violated that.\par \par \uc2 But there`s an important point, Chris, that I want to make that a lot of people are missing. In this failure of these officers in this -- in this -- in reprehensible action, this helped to shape a narrative that was pushing back against justice. It helped to shape talking points that people literally used about media. And on the alternative side, to try to suggest that what happened to Breonna Taylor was not unjust. And that`s so important because of the impact that it has on the national discourse and the court of public opinion.\par \par \uc2 Breonna Taylor is one example out of many, some of whom we will never hear about, where justice has not been served as of yet, and many people will never receive justice. And were it not for the persistence of \uc2 Kristen Clarke\uc2 , and the \uc2 DOJ Civil Rights Division\uc2 , we wouldn`t be having this conversation about that today. So it`s really important to understand the very far-reaching effects that when police officers and law enforcement are derelict in their duty or go beyond with nefarious intention, how deeply impactful that is because we`re seeing how it played a part in the national discourse on Breonna Taylor.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And let me ask you, Charles, about a part of that discourse or part of that conversation. Breonna Taylor`s case definitely brought awareness to no-knock warrant policies across the U.S. And just a couple of days ago, the Biden administration again, called for restrictions on no- knock warrants. What changes do we need to see at the federal level to at least try to prevent this from happening again?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Chris, that`s a question of police culture and accountability. I think that we have too much of an emphasis on police training, which they already received in copious amounts. I do understand that for legitimate law enforcement, legitimate police officers who are out there doing their job, that in many respects, no-knock warrants are a valuable crime-stopping tool. But the execution of no-knock warrants and the way that no-knock warrants are distributed needs to be re-examined so that they are only issued in cases where there is truly an imminent danger of either the officers being in danger or sporulation of evidence or evidence being destroyed.\par \par \uc2 No-knock warrants are distributed far too easily. And as you see, in this case, the information is oftentimes not verified and not checked, such that when probable cause is in question, you still get that. So we need to revamp and revisit how those things are distributed to note -- no-knock warrants.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Charles, Ken, thank you both very much. Reverend Sharpton, you`re going to stay with me because now we want to get to that disturbing news out of Moscow. \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner was sentenced to nine years in prison for what the Russians called drug smuggling, but what a top U.S. diplomat there describes as nothing more than an oversight. In a statement, President Biden called the sentence unacceptable. Griner arrested for having less than one gram of cannabis in her luggage.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Molly Hunter has been following this story for us. Kelly O`Donnell is at the White House. And, of course, Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  remains with us.\par \par \uc2 Molly, set the stage. Brittney Griner this morning contrite, she apologized, she took responsibility, but in many ways, this was never about what she did or what she said, was it?\par \par \uc2 MOLLY HUNTER, MSNBC FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: This was never about what she did. And certainly anything she said today, I don`t think would have changed that verdict. She did plead not guilty. That was clearly part of the defensive strategy. And she said again, she reiterated this morning it was an honest mistake. She was packing quickly. This was accidental. She never meant to hurt anyone.\par \par \uc2 I do just want to add to that sentence. So the prosecution had asked for nine and a half, Chris. The maximum sentence for the two charges against her were 10 years, they gave her nine. They also levied a 1 million ruble fine, about $16,000. Now, we did hear from that -- her defense team, they were seriously disappointed, of course. Why don`t you take a quick listen to that?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARIA BLAGOVOLONA, BRITTNEY GRINER`S ATTORNEY: You know when we saw Brittney, I think it was Tuesday and we`re so -- we`re told to see on Thursday and she said, see you on doomsday. So looks like she was right. She was very upset. Very upset, very stressed, and she`s -- well, she can hardly talk, honestly, so it`s a difficult time for her.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 HUNTER: She can hardly talk. But, Chris, as she was leaving the courtroom - - and look, Brittney is six-nine, she was in that cage that we saw her during the hearing all day, she left the courtroom in handcuffs, and she said to the camera in English, I love my family. Now, in a statement, her defense team says they will definitely appeal. The judge said they had 10 days to appeal but there`s no expectation that that would overturn this verdict, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Kelly, we know that the U.S. has made what officials call a substantial offer trying to do a deal, a prisoner swap to get her back home. But what we saw today, a bigger sentence allows them a bigger bargaining chip with the U.S. It raises the stakes, right?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, MSNBC SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, and it comes at a time when the U.S. and Russia have probably the poorest relationship we`ve seen since the end of the Cold War. And that, of course, factors into this. Brittney Griner and Paul Whelan are two Americans who have become embroiled in this controversy at a time when the U.S. is also battling Russia over Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And so there are tense geopolitical interests here. So it is more than about the facts of her case. She referenced that in her remarks to the court today saying that she did not want politics to play into her case when she addressed the court made her apology and asked for the court`s consideration.\par \par \uc2 So for the Biden administration, there is a lot of play here. They say that they`ve made this substantial offer to the Russians to offer a Russian arms dealer, Viktor Bout, in exchange for the two Americans. That is a significant offer when you consider the varying degrees of importance in terms of what Viktor Bout means to President Putin, and of course, what are these two Americans mean to the United States. And so is that going to be substantial enough or is this going to be a protracted discussion?\par \par \uc2 There had been some sense that it would not be likely that Russia would engage in a substantive way until its own judicial process concluded. So, today is perhaps an important marker in that -- in that timeline. And so clearly, the president made a very swift statement calling this unacceptable, saying that she needs to be released along with Paul Whelan. And now the next steps will really put more pressure on Russia. Will they respond? Up until this point, there has not been a substantive response. Will they now engage in negotiations? Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Rev., I want to play a part of what Brittney Griner said before her sentencing today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRITTNEY GRINER, \uc2 WNBA\uc2  BASKETBALL PLAYER: I`m never meant to hurt anybody. I never meant to put in jeopardy the rest of the population. I never meant to break any laws here. I know everybody keeps talking about political pawns and politics but I hope that that is far from this courtroom. I made an honest mistake and I hope that in your ruling, that it doesn`t end my life here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Rev., you`ve been there for Brittney`s wife, you`ve asked for her release, and you`ve asked that she not be used as a political pawn. But are you surprised by what we saw today? And are you happy -- well, happy might be the wrong word. Do you think that the Biden administration is doing everything it can in this case?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I`m very much on surprised by the sentence, yet disturbed at the same time. It`s a moral outrage. Let`s not forget that Brittney Griner was in Russia for seven years playing with a Russian basketball team. Some of her colleagues on the team and the former coach testified in a Russian court for her. So she`s right. It is beyond politics. Athletics ought to be something that goes beyond the boundaries of the geography of one nation to another. And this sentencing today is a moral outrage because it says even Athletics will be played into the geopolitics rather than dealing with the facts.\par \par \uc2 If Brittney Griner, who was prescribed for what she had in terms of cannabis, and with a small amount, if that -- it had been in a courtroom anywhere else in the world, it probably would have been a misdemeanor. For her now to be sentenced to nine years is outrageous. So we are absolutely calling on this president to continue to do whatever is necessary to get her and Paul Whelan out. Clearly, they should not be in jail.\par \par \uc2 And we have -- I contacted the State Department today and we have reiterated that I want to go with some -- a delegation of clergy and have a clergy visit with her. When you look at her, the frustration, the lawyers said she could barely talk, we are concerned about her spirituality. We are concerned about her mentality.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 She`s over there in a jail, transported vehicles before that was too small for a six-foot -- freight -- nine-inch frame. And now she doesn`t know what`s going to happen to her. She at least should have a clergy visit since we`re not waiting longer than we expect. Unless the president who I give credit, President Biden immediately spoke to this, Secretary of Blinken, and others have been in touch with the family. We must see her and Paul Whelan immediately released. This is beyond the word unfair. This is atrocious.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, the mental, physical, emotional strain is almost hard to quantify. Rev., thank you. You`re going to continue with us a little later on this hour. Molly and Kelly, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 His obviously a very busy news day and we`re going to stay on top of all of those stories. But we also have major developments in multiple investigations involving the former president, as his children, Ivanka and Don Jr., testify in a New York Civil Case. And we still don`t know who won the Republican primary for governor of Arizona. When should the final numbers come in? You`re watching "CHRIS JANSING REPORTS" only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now, we`re following major developments in multiple investigations involving former President Trump, his children, and former members of his administration. We learned this week that phones belonging to top members of the Defense Department were wiped after January 6, the third government agency to say texts and e-mails from that day are missing. On this network, Former DHS Secretary Jeh Johnson was asked does that pass the smell test.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEH JOHNSON, FORMER DHS SECRETARY: At the senior most levels of those departments, I`d have to say it does smell. It is problematic. If there was some senior-level directive to wipe clean various communications around January 6, that`s a very serious matter. And there shouldn`t be an IG that we trust who can undertake a comprehensive investigation of this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, in fact, the number two Democrat in the Senate, Dick Durbin, is now calling for an inspector general to look into the disappearance of texts from top DOD officials. This all comes on the heels of news about similar missing texts, of course, from the Secret Service and Department of Homeland Security. But it also comes as the Justice Department is ramping up its criminal investigation into Donald Trump`s actions around January 6.\par \par \uc2 Today, NBC News can confirm a federal grand jury has subpoenaed Trump`s former White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 . It`s unclear what he might reveal, but according to the National Review, it could mean the department is on the verge of bringing criminal charges against the former president.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Luke Broadwater, congressional reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , back with me MSNBC Legal Analyst Charles Coleman.\par \par \uc2 So, Charles, let me read exactly what the National Review says about Pat Cipollone`s subpoena because there`s some question about how much he might reveal and how much is covered by executive privilege. But, Andrew McCarthy writes this. While the mere existence of a criminal investigation does not eviscerate executive privilege, the privilege must yield if prosecutors demonstrate that they have a specific need for important evidence and that that evidence is not reasonably available from other sources. Ergo, unless prosecutors had concluded Cipollone possessed information vital to possible criminal charges, they would not have subpoenaed him to appear before the grand jury.\par \par \uc2 So, he`s saying that this subpoena points directly to the possibility of criminal charges being the next step. Do you agree with that argument?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: I do, Chris. This has been one of the most important points in terms of trying to decide what senior White House officials would be able to divulge in this -- in terms of this question of executive privilege. And I think that the way that that has been framed is exactly correct. If the DOJ or a criminal investigative body is looking at specific criminal charges, the executive privilege that in most cases would apply to Cipollone and others who are counsel or White House counsel would not apply.\par \par \uc2 It`s very important to understand the distinction between executive privilege and attorney-client privilege because people have misconstrued the two throughout the course of this conversation. And in this case, given that now Cipollone has been subpoenaed by a grand jury, it certainly does point to the fact or the idea rather, that criminal charges appear to be looming.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Luke, you`ve heard about this new call for an investigation into the missing texts. What does Senator Durbin want to see and what`s the level of hope they`ll actually find anything?\par \par \uc2 LUKE BROADWATER, CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Right. Well, so I think there`s a couple of levels here in the search for these texts. We know that these phones were wiped both at the Department of Defense and the \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2 . And so that means they`re not -- they don`t have text messages available for journalists, for politicians, for nonprofits to file a FOIA request to get them. But is it possible that there`s somewhere -- they exist somewhere on a server that law enforcement could get with a subpoena? And I think that`s the question right now that both the January 6 committee is interested in, and also the senators are interested in.\par \par \uc2 But we do know also, this exposes a serious problem that occurred at the end of the Trump administration, and that is the destruction and deletion of potential evidence. And Senator Durbin wants to get to the bottom of whether this was a cover-up, or what this was mere incompetence, or whether this was merely a bad records-keeping policy and the policy could be changed. So there could -- he`s leaving open the possibility there could be an innocent explanation to this but they`re also very concerned there could be something more serious going on.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It almost feels, Luke, like you need a flowchart to follow all of this. I mean, are there now multiple investigations going on into all of these missing texts even before potentially an IG might get appointed?\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: Yes. Well, we know the Inspector General is investigating the missing Secret Service texts. But now we know there are issues with the Inspector General and that Congress is calling on him to be removed. And \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , the Attorney General, will take over that investigation.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 They say he`s been compromised. They say he hid evidence from them, that he did not investigate the problem for months at a time and did not relay the concerns to Congress in a timely fashion. So yes, there`s -- you`re right. It`s getting very complex, but in the most simple version, we know, the Secret Service, Homeland Security officials, and the Department of Defense officials, all had text messages go missing. And so that is very concerning to the January 6 committee.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. So much has come out of this already, Charles, so I don`t think lately we`ve heard that much about the New York Attorney General`s civil investigation into the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  but now we know Ivanka and Don Jr. testified, although we don`t know what they said. NBC News has confirmed they did not plead the Fifth. Where is that investigation going and what does this new development potentially tell us?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Well, I think that what we`re seeing in that investigation is really just trying to make the connections and discover exactly how far up things are going. One of the sorts of roadblocks that have been the case in many of the investigations involving \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , his entities, and different organizations, has been trying to make the right connections.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s why switching gears very quickly back to the \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  conversation. Pat Cipollone`s testimony is very, very valuable because he`s one of the few people who can actually connect Trump`s actions, his sayings, his words, his thoughts, to his -- to actual criminal activity. And I think with respect to what Tish James is doing here in New York, as Attorney General, looking into the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  on the civil side is really just trying to probe and see how far they can connect the dots all the way up to the top, be it to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or to one of his senior officials. And that`s where this investigation is present.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Charles Coleman, Luke Broadwater, thank you both so much. The self-proclaimed and most influential gathering of conservatives in the world, CPAC getting underway in Dallas. What`s really at stake for the GOP after Kansas voters just dealt the party a major blow?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:17]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Breaking news from the White House. NBC has learned that the Biden administration is planning to declare monkeypox a public health emergency. And we`re expecting to hear from the \uc2 HHS\uc2  Secretary \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  about the plan about an hour from now.\par \par \uc2 Let me go to Geoff Bennett from \uc2 PBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Geoff, we were coming to you for a different topic, but since this broke we know that there have been some conversations, some pressure from the medical community to do this. What would it mean?\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT, \uc2 PBS\uc2  NEWSHOUR CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: And this is significant, Chris, because you remember the White House just days ago announced a national monkeypox coordinator to work alongside the \uc2 Health and Human Services Department\uc2  to drive awareness about this disease, but also this declaration of a public health emergency allows the federal government to unlock new measures that they can roll out across the country that would allow for greater access to vaccines, for greater public health flexibility.\par \par \uc2 So this is a very important step that public health officials have been calling for for weeks now as they`ve seen cases of monkeypox spike in California and New York. Several governors have already declared public health emergencies. And now what you see here is the federal government taking that step as well.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right, Geoff, you`re going to stick with me. We want to talk about CPAC now because that organization calls itself the largest and most influential gathering of conservatives in the world. And today in Dallas CPAC kicks off with an extremely controversial guest. Hungary`s prime minister \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2 , arguably the poster boy for dismantling democracy. This comes after Republicans nominated multiple election deniers in Tuesday`s primaries.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be at CPAC, too, and among the messages we can expect to hear that Republicans believe they might`ve lost the Kansas abortion vote because they didn`t go far enough. In a tweet, CPAC chair \uc2 Matt Schlapp\uc2  suggested that Kansas voters are so strongly pro-life they rejected timid steps.\par \par \uc2 For more we`re joined by Geoff Bennett, chief Washington correspondent for the \uc2 PBS\uc2  NEWS HOUR and an MSNBC political contributor, Adrienne Elrod, Democratic strategists, and Brendan Buck, who served as the chief communications adviser to former House Speaker \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good to see all of you. Geoff, let me go back to you. Coming off of Tuesday`s primaries, what does this mean for Republicans as conservatives at least plot how to navigate the midterms?\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Well, it makes their job a little bit harder in some of these hotly contested races, Chris, because Democrats make the case that Kansas is not their final fight when it comes to preserving reproductive rights as they see it. And so what Democrats aim to do is to put abortion on the ballot in as many places as they possibly can.\par \par \uc2 In some states that`s happening literally, Vermont, Kansas, or rather, Vermont, Kentucky, Michigan, California. They all had Kansas style referendums that will be on the ballot. And, you know, Democrats do -- it is harder to frame races between two individual politicians as being a referendum on reproductive rights. But that is what Republicans are now going to have to fight.\par \par \uc2 There have been this asymmetry, a passion gap in our politics for so long where abortion was a galvanizing issue on the right in a way that it wasn`t on the left. Think back to 2016, even those Republicans who have misgivings about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , they would say, when it comes to issues I care about like abortion, I care the judiciary, I care about judges being confirmed, and so I`ll just pass that vote.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are now seeing -- potentially are seeing a shift there and they`re trying to capitalize on it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, Adrienne, if, and I think a lot of people accept this, the energy gap has shifted, right, it shifted in favor of the Democrats, \uc2 Matt Schlapp\uc2 , if Republicans adopt the we need to go even further approach, what`s your thought?\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE ELROD, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: My thought is \uc2 Matt Schlapp\uc2  is totally off base and is not in touch with where the American people stand. Look, this may be, Chris, an issue, a partisan issue here in Washington, D.C. This is not a partisan issue in America. And Kansas voters proved that. They proved and what all of us are thinking that no matter, if you`re a Democrat, if you`re a Republican or an independent you want to keep choice the law of the land. You want to keep Roe in place.\par \par \uc2 That is what we saw happened in Kansas. So if Republicans at CPAC this weekend are thinking we need to go harder, we need (INAUDIBLE) harder into not allowing women to make her own choices for her own body, that`s their own prerogative. But that is going to only mean more detrimental harm to them at the ballot box in November.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So Matt -- how much does CPAC, how much does \uc2 Matt Schlapp\uc2  represent the Republican Party in 2022?\par \par \uc2 [13:35:08]\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, FORMER CHIEF COMMUNICATIONS ADVISER TO FORMER HOUSE SPEAKER PAUL RYAN: Well, I think that -- CPAC has long been sort of a sideshow, a bit of a freak show sometimes, depending on who`s up there. And something that I think establishment Republicans tend to just try to ignore. Some people would go and speak to get a little play, but I think at this point it`s a big enough constituency in the Republican Party that you can`t ignore it. That this populist nationalist politics that`s starting to represent has a big home in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 Then, look, as a conservative I think this is gross. You know, \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2  is the type of person we used to hold out as an example of the type of -- a society you need to worry about, warn about. You would be held out as a punching bag. But now he is being brought here because I think our party has become, or at least portions of our party had become so obsessed with what I call entertainment politics.\par \par \uc2 You know, the biggest thing that they`re focused on is voting the libs. This is a great way to own the libs. It`s a great way to get people upset. But it is so divorced from any real issues or fundamental principles that Republicans used to stand for. I`m sure he`ll get lots of applause and it will make lots of news. And I think that`s sort of the point, is to get people all riled up. And, you know, I yearn for a party that cares about ideas again. But CPAC has really never been where you found that and it`s especially true today.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, Adrienne, we know what drives Republicans` calculus. I mean, Donald Trump`s primary endorsements have been very successful. 93 percent of his endorsed candidate so far in 2022 have won according to Ballotpedia. I guess the question then becomes what is their midterm general election viability?\par \par \uc2 ELROD: Well, you know, I think that is left to be seen. Of course we know a lot of the MAGA Republicans who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has endorsed are very extreme. A lot of them like \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  who is the Republican nominee in Pennsylvania for governor was a huge insurrectionist, supported what happened at the Capitol on January 6th. You know, you see a lot of people like that across the country who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have endorsed.\par \par \uc2 His endorsements tend to be a little bit more I guess, you know, for lack of a better term, the crazy wing of the Republican Party. You know, I worked at the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee during the 2006 cycle when Democrats took back the House. And moderate candidates on both sides are the ones who won. We were starting to see that take shape this time around.\par \par \uc2 And I think you`re going to see more of the moderate candidates especially those in the Republican Party perhaps have a better shot. But most of those candidates failed to win in their own primaries because of Donald Trump`s endorsements of MAGA candidates. So I think -- look, I don`t know what`s going to happen, Chris, at the ballot box for Democrats in November. Anytime a president this empowered the opposite party tends to do a little bit better in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 But this is different. And a lot of it has to do with Roe being overturned by the Supreme Court. A lot of it also has to do with the fact that the Supreme Court has erected views that are antithetical to where a lot of Americans` values stand. And I think that is certainly going to help Democrats mitigate a lot of their losses that they might normally have going into this election cycle.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, speaking of values, Brendan, let`s talk about Hungary`s \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2 . He`s a prime minister who among other anti-democratic policy, ruthlessly suppresses press freedom, turned the courts into his own political tool, he`s harshly cracked down on immigration. I could go on and on and on. Does it matter that he`s at CPAC? Does it tell us about the direction of the Republican Party or at least the conservative wing of the Republican Party? What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: It tells us there is an audience for that. You know, look, a lot of the things you just said you can probably relate to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as well. There has long been a nationalist anti-immigrant strain of the Republican Party. A popular strain that we sort of just pushed aside. And it came roaring back toward the establishment in 2016 and it`s here to stay.\par \par \uc2 You know, you would think that a forum that would put \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2  up would be somewhat controversial among Republicans. But it`s not. The former president is going to be there. All kinds of other senators and members of Congress want to be there. They want to be on that stage because there is a constituency in the Republican Party for this right now.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s a shame. Again, I think this is the type of person that in not too long past we would`ve been beating up as the exact type of society that we want to avoid. But here we are and I don`t think that`s going to change anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Brendan Buck, Adrienne Elrod, Geoff Bennett, thank you all.\par \par \uc2 Democrats plotting how to harness that major abortion victory heading into the midterms. But in order to pull off that messaging they might need to tread carefully. We`ll explain next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:44:02]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That stunning victory for abortion rights in Kansas is now emboldening Democrats nationally today. As "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " puts it, it is galvanizing Democrats and underscoring for Republicans the risks of overreaching on one of the most emotionally charged matters in American politics. But even as many strategists are calling for Democrats to run more assertively on abortion rights, there is also a caution for them, too, because abortion is both emotionally and religiously charged.\par \par \uc2 This is part of my conversation yesterday with Amanda Renteria, the national political director of Hillary Clinton`s 2016 campaign.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RENTERIA, THE NATIONAL POLITICAL DIRECTOR, HILLARY FOR AMERICA 2016: It`s incredibly important culturally in the Latino community and the black and brown communities on how we figure out how to talk about abortion services culturally. And I think that is going to be the key piece as we moved to the general election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Joined me now Maria Teresa Kumar, the president and CEO of Voto Latino, and my colleague, the Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 , is back with us.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:00]\par \par \uc2 Is she right, Maria Teresa? Talk about the sensitivities on this issue within the Latino community. And I think it`s worth pointing out that it`s 86 percent white in Kansas. So it`s not exactly the same kind of population that you`re going to see in some of these other states.\par \par \uc2 MARIA TERESA KUMAR, VOTO LATINO PRESIDENT AND CEO: So, Chris, I`m glad that you are having this conversation because I think what we have a tendency of thinking when we think of the Latino community is that we`re highly conservative and very Catholic. That is not the same we`re finding when it comes to polls. We just came out with a poll that was in Georgia and North Carolina, Texas, Arizona, and Nevada. And 68 percent of Latinos believe that the idea of choice was a private issue between a woman and her doctor.\par \par \uc2 And I`m talking about men. So I do think that not only is it a possibility of a winning issue, but it does have to do with how do you message it, recognizing oftentimes that that person -- the person at the polls say, you know, abortion may not be right for me, but who am I to make that decision for someone else? And we know that that type of framing really works and it resonates at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And in fact much in the same way, Rev, there was a Gallup poll back I think it was in the fall of 2020 showing the black committee has slowly become more accepting of abortion over the past couple of decades. But there are deeply held religious beliefs playing into this, especially with older black voters who are incredibly reliable and incredibly important to Democrats.\par \par \uc2 So how should Democrats be thinking about this? Because obviously they want and need to hold on to those voters?\par \par \uc2 REV. \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , PRESIDENT, NATIONAL ACTION NETWORK: I think that they must message it in a way that it is about choice. It`s not about saying I`m voting that I support abortion or not. It`s about I support people having the right to choose because we`re not talking particularly in the black community, and I would say large portions of the Latino community, you`re not talking about whether or not people are going to be able to have an abortion. You`re talking about whether they`re going to have a safe abortion.\par \par \uc2 We`ve always had abortions, but we had these back alley, very risky abortion. And we`re saying that rather than have people in those situations, they should be able to choose whether or not they want to do, even if it`s something that I don`t believe in. The bible, if you`re using this as a religious argument, the bible is about choice. You can go to heaven or hell. There`s nowhere in the bible that said you have to go to heaven.\par \par \uc2 So where do we get this theology of forcing something when the reality is that you can even biblically base that? It`s a question of choice. If you are a minister as I am, you can preach to people to convert them. You do not make laws to compel them.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, let me go a little deeper into the messaging, Maria Teresa. Because there was a tweet, and some people pointed this out, President Biden said that Kansans used their voices to protect women`s right to choose and access reproductive healthcare. It`s one of many examples we have seen out of the administration that does not use the word abortion.\par \par \uc2 Is that a part of the framing to broaden it?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Not only to broaden it, but I think it`s also to talk about that on the ballot in November, we`re talking about freedoms. Do I have the agency and the freedom to choose what`s best for myself and for my family? And the more that gets across the more not just women, but men who are the allies in this fight understands it. And that is what we need to talk about because we recognize that what is on the ballot. We`re not just talking about inflation, the economy in the normal traditional sense, is that we see during the midterm.\par \par \uc2 But this is very much about what kind of freedoms am I going to continue having and those of my daughter in the future if we do not stand up today?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, part of it, I think, Rev, is that, obviously, you don`t want to turn people off, but also Democrats want to use it as a motivating factor, a reason to get people to the polls. We always know the midterms are not -- they don`t get as high a turnout as a presidential race. But what we saw in Kansas was off the charts, the number of people who came out to the polls.\par \par \uc2 Is there a way to message to people of color about this and help them to understand that this is an issue that not just impacts them, but they should vote Democratic for?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, one of the ways that I have found doing my daily radio show and the TV show here at MSNBC, the reaction I get, that has resonated and certainly through National Action Nation, is when you talk about the Supreme Court decision look at what the decision said. It didn`t make new law. It says we will let the states decide. And when you frame it that way, we are not only talking about choice, we`re talking going back to state`s rights.\par \par \uc2 Because if they`re now sending back to the states what they will decide a woman`s right to choose right behind it they`re going to send back to the states how you can vote, whether or not your voting rights are one way or another depending on the states, and on and on, affirmative action cases, we`re going back to a state`s rights model and people in in the black and Latino community know we will always undone in the state`s rights model.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:14]\par \par \uc2 That`s what the Confederates against the Union was about. We must have a strong union, we cannot walk down that road of state`s rights. The Supreme Court did not decide on abortion. They said we`ll leave it to the state of Mississippi. Supposed if they do that on affirmative action, supposed if they do that on anyone else. We must protect the union and we should frame this that way.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 , Maria Teresa Kumar, such a great conversation. Thank you both so much.\par \par \uc2 A Trump-endorsed election denier prematurely declaring victory in Arizona as the high stakes gubernatorial primary remains too close to call. How soon until we get that final result? We`re live in Phoenix after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Arizona`s Republican primary for governor is still too close to call but that`s not stopping candidate Kari Lake from declaring victory. Lake, a Trump-endorsed candidate, who made his 2020 election lies a centerpiece of her campaign, leads Karrin Taylor Robson by less than 2 percent of the vote with an estimated 143,000 votes still to be counted.\par \par \uc2 Joining me live from Phoenix, NBC News correspondent Vaughn Hillyard, who has taken up residence there for a bit now.\par \par \uc2 So, Vaughn when can we expect a final result and why is Lake so confident she won this election despite these results?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: We could get a call as late as today or it could even take a couple of days here. NBC News has not called this race, despite Kari Lake being up by about 2 percentage points. We are waiting ballots that were received by county officials in the final 48 hours. These ballots have to go through the signature verification process to ensure they came from the people who sent them in here.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:01]\par \par \uc2 I want to let you hear, though, from Kari Lake because we do expect that lead for her to widen here. These ballots have trended for Kari Lake here in the past, and we continue -- expect that to continue to remain the case, but she was pressed on this idea because before polls closed on Tuesday night she was suggesting that she had seen fraud and irregularities but was not offering specifics about what those claims were. And now that she`s got a lead, she`s declaring victory which reporters yesterday at this press conference tried to hold her to account to. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: You said this election was messed up, you said you have evidence of cheating. Why should voters trust that you won this election fair and square if elections are just such a mess?\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R), ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Well, we have a lot of evidence of irregularities and problems, and we`re going to address those.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Will you release them?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: I`m not going to release it to the fake news but we`ll release it to the authorities.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Why not release it right now if it`s real?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Why would I release it to a bunch of people who deny that there was fraud when there was obviously fraud?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: That was our NBC affiliate, friend and colleague Brian Resnick asking those questions here. Kari Lake clearly has her eyes on the next 95 days ahead, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So let`s look at the next 95 days ahead because Arizona`s secretary of state \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is now the Democratic nominee for governor. Fair to say this has national implications. A lot of people all around the country are going to be watching this one closely.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Right. I mean, we`re looking at Kari Lake being a very viable candidate if she in fact she is here in this general election. Not only did she show that she could surge turnout in the rural parts of this state, the GOP broke its primary turnout here this week but also here in Maricopa County. She`s essentially just got to make up about 2 percentage points that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did not get in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost by just about 10,000 votes. She`s a known entity here. She`s making this about, you know, essentially making this media versus moms. She`s trying to present herself as a moderating voice while still holding the Trump line here in the state. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Vaughn Hillyard, so much great reporting from there. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern right here on MSNBC. Joe Fryer will pick up our coverage next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, MSNBC HOST: Good to be you. I`m Joe Fryer in for Katy Tur.\par \par \uc2 Guilty, that is the official verdict for \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner who has now been sentenced to nine years in Russian prison for drug possession and smuggling.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220805ei8400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL; August 03, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Frank Figliuzzi; Zerlina Maxwell; Joyce Vance; Glenn Kirschner \par \uc2 7,276 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:11]\par \par \uc2 ZERLINA MAXWELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening. Thank you so much, Ali.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, I am hopeful. American democracy is very much still alive. You know, we focus so much on the threats to our democracy and they are very real and very, very serious. But last night, Kansas showed us the antidote.\par \par \uc2 Voting, organizing, knocking on doors and working the pavement to create change. Reporting on the results and calling victory for the pro-choice side in Kansas last night on this very hour, I was thinking about all the people I don`t know personally but who I knew made it happen, organizers, door knockers, people powered politics.\par \par \uc2 My very first job in politics was as a field organizer in Virginia for \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  in 2008. I learn more from politics in that one single job than any other job I`ve ever had. And that`s because in order to be a good organizer, you have to know the policy backwards and forwards, and you have to know the talking points as well as the defenses.\par \par \uc2 And you apply that knowledge to the very most important skill -- talking to people. You have to be able to have a conversation with another human being. Imagine that. Truly a lost art.\par \par \uc2 My first guest tonight will be two young organizers, 19 and 21 years young, who have thrown themselves into this fight. One knocking on doors in Kansas and one knocking down Congressman \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 , while raising millions of dollars for abortion funds and support of reproductive freedom.\par \par \uc2 And after that, I`ll be joined by the old hand millennial organizing veteran, Lauren Groh-Wargo, who`s been working for change every day in Georgia, change that we can all potentially benefit from.\par \par \uc2 Last night, voters in Kansas voted for choice by an 18-point margin, in a state where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  by 15 points.\par \par \uc2 "Politico" notes, if the politics of Roe proved fraught for Republicans in Kansas, it`s going to be even more treacherous for the GOP in swinger, more moderate swath of the country.\par \par \uc2 Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  echoed that exact message earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): A strong pro-choice turnout we saw last night in Kansas will continue well into the fall, and Republicans who side with these extremist MAGA policies that attack women`s rights do so at their own political risk.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Turnout in Kansas reached almost presidential levels. Over 900,000 people voted, nearly two and even three times as high as previous primaries and two thirds of the turnout in the 2020 presidential election. That turnout reveals just how much the Republican Party miscalculated voter support for abortion rights and what can be an ominous sign of things to come this November.\par \par \uc2 But it`s also a victory for organizers on the ground who tuned out all the political punditry and the analysts and mobilizing galvanized voters through the polls by knocking on doors and actually talking to them about what this amendment would`ve meant for real women and people in the state of Kansas. Fans and severe restrictions on abortion exceptions including no exceptions for rape or incest that are being pushed by Republican legislatures all across America are not with the majority of the American people want.\par \par \uc2 And when faced with the reality of what that actually means, abortion rights advocates convinced voters to send a strong and clear message, including Republicans. As "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " notes: Registered Republicans far outnumbered Democrats in the state of Kansas -- and abortion rights activists made explicit appeals to unaffiliated voters and center-right voters.\par \par \uc2 In interviews last week in populist Johnson County, Kansas, a number of voters said they were registered Republicans but oppose the amendment -- a dynamic that almost certainly played out all across the state, given the margin.\par \par \uc2 We knew when the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade that it would be a powerful, motivating issue for Democrats. But what we saw last night play out was in Kansas. New polling today shows further movement away from the Republicans extreme agenda.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:00]\par \par \uc2 A new Monmouth poll finds that 50 percent of Americans want Democrats in control of the Congress compared to 43 percent who want Republicans to be in charge. And that`s 11-point swing just away from them wrap Republicans just since the month of May.\par \par \uc2 Last night, reproductive rights advocates celebrated the victory and the message sent to Republicans who don`t support a woman`s right to bodily autonomy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: What`s the message that Kansans sent to the rest of the country tonight?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA OSTMEYER, KANSAS VOTER & ORGANIZER: Don`t mess with us! Seriously. There is more of us than there are of them who are trying to it attack our reproductive rights and freedom, and I think we sent a message that lies, manipulation, voter suppression, the other side can try and use those tactics to try and process but they don`t work. We know our power and we`re going to keep showing up to defend it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Don`t mess with us. That`s the message that Republicans in the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  and in state legislatures all across the country should here tonight. The vote in Kansas on abortion rights proved that you at home are not alone. You`re in the majority if you support abortion rights and you have the power to create change because you still have the power to vote.\par \par \uc2 In America and in democracy, the people still have the power, and the way to channel that power is to organize it.\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight is Yazmin Bruno-Valdez, a college student from Kansas who knocked on doors advocating for abortion rights, part of yesterday`s referendum. Also with us Olivia Julianna, activists and strategist for Gen-Z for Change, who raised over $2 million for abortion rights in a response to an attack by Republican Congressman \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for joining.\par \par \uc2 Yazmin, I will start with you first. What is your reaction to last night`s result and turn it. Were you surprised at all given what you heard from talking to actual voters in Kansas? What message does a send?\par \par \uc2 YAZMIN BRUNO-VALDEZ, ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE: Well, I think it sends a very clear message that we are here and that we aren`t going anywhere, and we`re going to stand for reproductive rights no matter how long and how much work it takes.\par \par \uc2 As an organizer, I know what I was like to knock on doors and the hot weather and definitely I would do it 1 million times again just to ensure that every person in Kansas would be insured reproductive rights. I was completely amazed and instill so, so excited. I`ve been on cloud nine all day.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Tell us more about the experience of knocking on doors and talking to voters on the ground. You know, as a political analyst, pundits we sit in studios and we do analysis based on polling data and focus groups. But there`s nothing to compare to actually talking to real people and as you mentioned, it was in the summer which is very hot. So, talk about that experience of actually talking to people about this referendum.\par \par \uc2 BRUNO-VALDEZ: Yes. So as you mentioned it was really hot but aside from that that didn`t take us away from canvassing and knocking on doors every day that we could. We texted -- we did all sorts of things to ensure that voters were informed, that they knew exactly what we were rooting for.\par \par \uc2 As you may have known, the amendment was written in a very confusing, very elitist way that if you knew exactly what it meant even if you are super informed and super involved, he still didn`t really know what voting no and what`s voting yes meant. They did that strategically on purpose. They were very well funded campaign. They sent out lots of misinformation and voters were confused.\par \par \uc2 And that`s exactly what our canvassing efforts trying to fight against. We tried to make sure the people were informed and that low propensity voters, first-time voters, the first time register voters, that they all had the information that they needed to get to the polls, for early and really get their choice of voting being made.\par \par \uc2 So, we were so excited that we did not lead voter suppression in canvas with all the gerrymandering, this is a non partisan amendment, we`re able to get all the people from both sides of the aisle, Republicans Democrat and anybody under the sun in Kansas.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: I love all of that because it gives people some color about what that experience was like if they haven`t done at themselves.\par \par \uc2 And, Olivia, you were body shamed by \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 . I actually haven`t asked you before. But what was your first thought? Was it let`s raise some of money for abortion funds?\par \par \uc2 [22:10:02]\par \par \uc2 I mean, why did you use a personal attack against you, a private citizen, a 19 year old, from a congressman to help you fund-raise or the issue to erase care so much about?\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA JULIANNA, GEN-Z FOR CHANGE ACTIVIST AND STRATEGIST: You know, I am a private citizen, I`m a young person, but first and foremost, I`m an activist, I`m a fund-raiser. And when I have an opportunity that`s going to help some people in our community, that`s what I mean to do. So by attacking me, by blasting me on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  gave me a national top form to talk about why bodily autonomy is important why abortion access is something that people across the country care about.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: So, just a quick follow, Olivia, why is bodily autonomy so important? What message do you hope Americans got from this moment where the attention is on you because of the attack?\par \par \uc2 JULIANNA: Abortion is health care, point black, period. We are hearing from people across the country who people can`t get access to abortion services, whose hospitals would not give them access, where they are slowly dying in their beds because hospitals are afraid to provide these procedures to them because they`re getting sued prosecuted by state governments. It`s a health procedure, it`s very difficult procedure for people have to come to make.\par \par \uc2 So by outlawing this procedure, by targeting women across the country Republicans are essentially saying, they do not care about your health. They do not care about your freedom and they do not care about your constitutional rights. This is a president that has been set for decades long before I was born.\par \par \uc2 And so, for them to go after this, not only are attacking your freedom, but they are also ignoring voters and the opinions of people across the country. An overwhelming amount of people and support access to abortion that`s what we saw last night in Kansas.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Yeah, I think it shows that the polling was accurate, a majority of Americans support pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 Olivia, do you think the older generations underestimated the power of Gen Z and affecting change? I think that you get cast like overly sensitive, snowflakes or maybe, you know, angry about climate change. But do you think that`s basically an underestimation of your power potentially?\par \par \uc2 JULIANNA: I think it`s an extreme underestimation. I know that as long as I`ve been conscious of politics, I`ve heard that notion, young people don`t vote, young people I don`t vote and don`t care about voting. We`ve seen that that`s not true, besotted 2020 in the presidential election when young people turn and record to vote out \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We saw it in Kansas, we saw young people registering to vote. We saw first- time voters. This is showing not only that young people have power at the ballot box but they`re avoid making their voices heard. We`re seeing young organizers political campaigns across the country, young organizers and nonprofits across the country.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think that this idea that young people are not politically involved, they are not socially justice really involved is one that is blatantly false, and I think it`s meant to demoralize people across the country. But clearly it is not working.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Yazmin, same question to you. Do you think you are underestimated? And why do you think your generation is so impatient at this moment and you`re just going and ignoring all the analysis in creating the change you want to see?\par \par \uc2 BRUNO-VALDEZ: Yes, so I definitely do think that -- I completely agree with everything you said. I think we are underestimated, unaccounted for. We are not deemed as actual powerhouses in the ballot box when in fact we are. When -- I personally cannot vote, I`m a DACA recipients, so I understand not being able to understand with American democracy but I do everything and mobilize everyone I know to get out and vote.\par \par \uc2 Gen-Z millennials, we were raised under the notion that abortion was a right, that is not something that can be stripped away so went Roe v. Wade was overruled, we knew they were coming after us that we had to do something specifically in Kansas. We knew we were going to be the first state to vote after Roe v. Wade and we knew we had to mobilize as many people as possible.\par \par \uc2 We did not sit there and cry and be scared like the snowflakes do. We set up and fight. We knew we had to do and got it done. I have so many of my friends there were low propensity voters, voters that have never been talked to mobilize to the polls, and whether it was sending them election guides, finding the poll locations anything that I could do to make sure that they were well-informed and excited to vote made me feel like I was right there with them.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: I`ve really enjoyed this conversation. It`s giving me hope and I hope the folks at home are feeling optimistic and the opposite of cynical right now.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:00]\par \par \uc2 Yazmin Bruno-Valdez and Olivia Julianna, thank you so much for being here. Both of you. Please stay safe.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now Lauren Groh-Wargo, the campaign manager for \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 ` gubernatorial campaign in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 So, Lauren, are you feeling hopeful? I am after that conversation.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN GROH-WARGO, CAMPAIGN MANAGER, ABRAMS GUBERNATORIAL CAMPAIGN: Absolutely, and I think that segment was so emblematic of this year where young voters in particular are so fired up and focused and this issue is so crystal cut about our who`s on our side and who`s against our side. Here we have a lot of different examples I`m excited to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Yeah, in the state of Georgia, obviously, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , a campaign you`re running, running against the current Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . Georgia was a surprise for so many folks in 2020 not just because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  but the special election and this is a matchup that everyone`s paying attention to.\par \par \uc2 I want to take a look at a new ad that is out today from your campaign targeting \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  on this exact issue on abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s an attack on the women of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  made abortion a crime before.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: For many women who didn`t know they are pregnant, and he supports a total ban.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Even if I`m raped.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A victim of incest.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Forced pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Criminalize women with jail time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Outlaws birth control.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Under Kemp, I could be investigated, and imprisoned for a miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: For a miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The only way to stop this to the women of Georgia is to stop \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: You know, Lauren, a lot of people say abortion is health care and it`s often put out there as a talking point. But can you talk about how in the last few weeks a lot of Americans including the ones turn and record numbers down in Georgia understand the life and death stakes when women don`t have access to the full slate of reproductive health care including abortion?\par \par \uc2 GROH-WARGO: Absolutely. A couple of hours earlier this evening, Zerlina, Stacey convened some women who had suffered pregnancy loss in Georgia, and we`re joined by 500 women in on Zoom to listen in solidarity. Then we got to work in organizing after the conference. But those women share that miscarriage in a pregnancy loss looks like a medical abortion in many cases, and now we have district attorneys on the record in Georgia saying they may be forced and law enforcement is going to have to look at criminalize women for potentially illegal abortions in those women will have to prove that it was a health risk in that instance. That is terrifying.\par \par \uc2 So the stories that are happening around the country and here in Georgia with the six-week ban. What is so hopeful however to the theme of your show tonight is that Kansas last night was a real life in person electoral example of what we`ve been seeing in our research. Zerlina, I think you`ve mentioned, you and I have been doing this for a couple of years, I`ve been doing this for about 20 years, I have never seen the type of polling qualitative research we`ve both been seeing internally and both in public polls in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 This six-week ban is an absolute loser politically for Kemp. It is overwhelmingly opposed. The six-week ban in fact is opposed by 25 percent of likely voting Republicans and overwhelmingly opposed by independents and Democrats. I don`t have any illusions that those 25 percent of hard-core Republicans are necessarily going to vote for \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , but they are certainly getting educated about why \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  is a danger to them and their daughters and their sisters.\par \par \uc2 Zerlina, the law of the land now is that if you are a rape or incest victim today in Georgia, by the time you even realize you are pregnant, you cannot access abortion care unless you have a police report. American statistics are something like 10 to 15 percent of women file police reports.\par \par \uc2 So this is an economic, moral abomination of a law passed in the first year of the Kemp administration. This is the same guy, Zerlina, who likes to take credit for not committing treason but his palling around with false electors. Do you hear what happened in past month? He appointed a false elector to a water and soil conservation board. A couple of days ago, we lost a huge music festival in Atlanta Music Midtown because of his gun laws and all the gun things happening.\par \par \uc2 So there are economic, political and human consequences to this far right, anti-woman, anti-safety, anti-community agenda and we are going to vote him out and all of his friends here in Georgia and across the country in November because women and those who love them are not going to elect their wives, and sisters and children languish in a hospital bed and get sepsis before they get hospital care.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:10]\par \par \uc2 And we for damn sure are going to run a lot more ad but most importantly we are organizing. So when we do, Zerlina? After that painful conversation about pregnancy loss, our incredible team of organizers got those women on our organizing tools app and sign him up to go canvas on Saturday. We are building a strong campaign and we hope everybody watching will go