change. And the Republican opposition to this bill is just standing in the way of helping people in the planet for purely partisan reading -- reasons.\par \par \uc2 My colleague and fellow opinion columnist at the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , Michelle Goldberg, and \uc2 Tom Perriello\uc2 , a former Democratic congressman from Virginia who now serves as Executive Director for U.S. Programs at the \uc2 Open Society Foundations\uc2 . Both join me now.\par \par \uc2 Michelle, I just want you to weigh in on the merits of this bill. Is it -- you know, is it a good bill?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, it`s absolutely a good bill. Although, Charles, I actually do disagree with you about the idea that it`s a good bill from wherever you stand or sort of whatever your predispositions are, because it`s not a great bill for people who run hedge funds. It`s not a great bill for billionaires who made their money -- who make their money through investments. It`s not a great bill for the drug companies who will now see some, you know, kind of government negotiation of drug prices through Medicare.\par \par \uc2 [20:50:30]\par \par \uc2 And so, I think that there`s two things going on here. On the one hand, there`s Republicans partisan nihilism. You see it obviously in their -- in the people who want to oppose -- who wanted to oppose the Chips Act, which they all supported before this bill. So, there was no sense that there was any kind of an ideological or political opposition to what was in the Chips Act.\par \par \uc2 You know, and so I think they oppose this bill for the same reason and would oppose it no matter what wasn`t it if was seemed to be helping the Democrats. But I also do think you have a lot of Republicans who are genuinely and deeply committed to protecting the interests of billionaires and big corporations.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: I enthusiastically take that correction because I don`t mind drug companies and billionaires are not -- are not happy with this bill.\par \par \uc2 Tom, you were defeated in a Republican-leaning district after voting for ObamaCare. Is the stubborn partisanship that we see on the right based on a fear of angering their own voters by agreeing with Democrats and not owning the libs?\par \par \uc2 TOM PERRIELLO\uc2 , FORMER VIRGINIA DEMOCRATIC CONGRESSMAN: Well, whatever the thinking, I think it`s been a disaster for Republicans. They`ve had months to put forward a proposal addressing inflation and costs. And instead, they`ve really been focused on conspiracy theories and chaos and, and culture war.\par \par \uc2 So, now, the Democratic Party is really owning the kitchen table issues of affordability, of job creation, of bringing back American manufacturing. And you know, we talked about not making the perfect the enemy of the good. This was definitely a situation where the good of American families, the good of country, was put ahead of the perfect bill, the perfect bill for progressives, the perfect bill for \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It was really a compromise which is what people want where there was something were centrist, progressives independents came together and said what does the country the country needs. The country needs a plan on manufacturing. It needs to plan on affordability, on clean energy. And you really saw the problem solvers come to the table across a wide political spectrum to deliver for the American people.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s presenting voters with a choice, not a referendum on the perfect but saying, hey, who`s moving us forward and who is presenting kind of a divisive chaos we can`t afford.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Michelle Goldberg and \uc2 Tom Perriello\uc2 , thank you both.\par \par \uc2 We`re starting to get numbers in from Kansas. I want to go back to Steve Kornacki at the big board with the latest. Steve?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes, we got about 60,000 in now statewide in Kansas. We`re starting to see a bunch of counties report votes. And you could see No on this referendum by about a two to a little bit less than a two to one margin with the early advantage. And again, this really is starting to come into focus here in terms of how this is probably going to play out tonight here.\par \par \uc2 What it`s looking like is consistently here. Every county is reporting out that mail vote, that early vote first. So, that is the strongest, most democratic vote. We know that it`s likely to be the strongest vote in opposition to this constitutional amendment. So, you know, some more of the counties that have come in.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Riley County, for instance. This is where Manhattan, Kansas is, \uc2 Kansas State University\uc2 . Hey, just take a look here. In the 2020 presidential election, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  carry this county by about four and a half points. Right now, No has about a 50-point advantage. That is on the strength of sort of the mail vote in the early vote. So, again, this is -- you can expect in a county like Riley County that number to come down as the night goes on and as the same day vote comes in.\par \par \uc2 But what you are seeing here, very, very strong early numbers in these counties. This is why in that county this is Wyandotte County. This is where Kansas City is. This is one of the most Democratic counties in the state. Again, you`re seeing what the No side wants to see, I would say, in the early vote here. And it`s raising the possibility certainly that this is going to be a very close referendum when all of it gets sorted out.\par \par \uc2 But I think the ride that we`re in for tonight in terms of watching all these results come in is that No is going to get off to a very fast and a very big start as these counties laid up with that early vote and with that mail vote. And then the number is going to come down and the question is going to be, does No land on the north or the south side of 50 percent? This could be a very close night. This could be a very long night.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Steve Kornacki, thank you very much. That`s ALL IN on this Tuesday night. Our election coverage continues on "MSNBC PRIME" with Ali Velshi. That`s coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:55:00]\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST (on camera): Good evening and welcome to "MSNBC PRIME." I`m Ali Velshi. And thanks for joining us on this big night in American politics. Five states are holding primary elections today. The results from each of them will be telling in entirely different ways. And the polls have just closed in Michigan and Kansas. We`re of course going to have Steve Kornacki here in just a moment to take us through the latest numbers as votes are counted.\par \par \uc2 Now, all eyes are on Kansas today where abortion rights are on the ballot for the first time since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220803ei8200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for August 2, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Claire McCaskill, Dasha Burns, Yamiche Alcindor,  Ben Rhodes, Melissa Murray \par \uc2 8,555 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: That`s it for this edition of "ANDREA MITCHELL REPORTS." Remember to follow the show online on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @mitchellreports. "CHRIS JANSING REPORTS" is live from Kansas City, Missouri right now.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good afternoon, I`m Chris Jansing, live in a beautiful good sweltering Kansas City, Missouri where the polls have been open for six hours already on this huge primary day, voters casting ballots literally just a few feet from where I`m sitting, crucial elections unfolding on both sides of the state line.\par \par \uc2 Here in Missouri is the Senate race featuring the attorney general, a disgraced former governor, and one of the most confusing endorsements you`ll ever see, and in Kansas, we`re watching in real-time as abortion rights are on the ballot for the first time since Roe was struck down. But it`s been a grueling battle just to get to today, political divisions creating a level of contentiousness you typically don`t see in a place that`s known for being Midwest Nice.\par \par \uc2 That`s because the range of issues that have so clearly created dividing lines across the U.S. are playing out right here pitting neighbor against neighbor, family member against a family member, friend against friend, and we know, abortion is one of those issues.\par \par \uc2 But in addition to dividing people, will it also motivate them to get to the polls? Will it drive turnout among women? What about young people? And does it help one candidate over the other? Looking forward, will today`s vote be the first battle in a culture war that will change the complexion of the midterms? If so, it looks like Democrats may already be gaining an advantage.\par \par \uc2 And here`s why I say that. Take a look at this. On June 23rd, that`s the day before Roe was overturned. That generic congressional ballot showed Republicans up by almost three points. Today, their advantage has shrunk to less than a point.\par \par \uc2 One final note, the contests in Missouri and Kansas are also unusual because of some last-minute confusion in both states. In Kansas, it`s because of the wording of the abortion amendment that would pave the way for a ban. Essentially, if you`re anti-abortion, you`re pro-amendment. If you`re pro-abortion rights, you want to vote no. A lot of people are confused by that.\par \par \uc2 And in Missouri, the confusion comes courtesy of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who made a deliberately vague "endorsement" on Monday, tweeting that he supports Eric for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . The problem is there are two Erics, Attorney General Eric Schmidt and former governor \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  both now claiming Trump`s support.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Dasha Burns, about 30 miles from me on the other side of the state line at a polling place in Gardner, Kansas, also joining me, former Missouri Democratic Senator Claire McCaskill, who will be adding her hometown insights throughout the hour, and Steven McAllister former U.S. Attorney for Kansas. You know, we have been watching hundreds of campuses, door to door in this heat in Kansas, many of them coming from out of state. We know how big a deal this is but put it into words and what you`re seeing and hearing on the ground, Claire.\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE MCCASKILL, FORMER MISSOURI DEMOCRATIC SENATOR: Well, it`s complex, and it`s a little chaotic. This was going to be on the ballot before Dobbs was decided. And I think frankly, and my friend from Kansas may disagree with me, I think that the Republicans were trying to be strategic by putting it on the primary ballot. Why do I say that? Well, typically, Republicans outnumbered Democrats two to one in Kansas, and frankly, they`re more unaffiliated voters -- independent voters in Kansas than there are democratically aligned voters. So they -- and those last two Democrats and unaffiliated, typically don`t have much to vote for in the primaries. So they`re not accustomed to coming out in big numbers for primary elections in Kansas.\par \par \uc2 Now, this has made it a much different situation. All of a sudden, there is something very big at stake. Kansas is where rape victims in Missouri who have been impregnated by the rapist, that`s where they can go for a safe and legal pregnancy termination right now. If the Kansas legislature succeeds in putting the kinds of restrictions in place, which they could, if there`s something that passes that are in place in Missouri, that just means people -- women in the Midwest, even those most vulnerable and most at risk, are going to have to travel farther, spend more money, it would be more disruptive and frankly, for many of them impossible to get the health care they need.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, let me take it out to Dasha Burns. There is this last-minute controversy, Dasha, over what was already confusing language in the amendment, voting yes names no longer a constitutional right to abortion. But now someone is sending out texts causing even more confusion and frustration for some Kansas voters. What can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Chris, look, the stakes are incredibly high for those voters casting their ballots behind me as the first in the nation to actually vote on this issue since Roe v. Wade was overturned. And the voters we went talking to you today, they say they have had to navigate a slew of complicated information and straight-up misinformation.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Just yesterday, a text message was sent to Kansans that claimed that voting yes, on the value of them both amendments would protect a woman`s right to choose when in fact, voting yes for this amendment would strip the Kansas State Constitution -- would strip the protections for abortion rights from the state constitution, it would then hand that power over to the Republican supermajority in the state legislature, who would then be able to make decisions on this issue, potentially enact further restrictions or as many abortion rights activists fear, a total ban.\par \par \uc2 Now, who is behind these text messages? We don`t know. The group, Kansans for Constitutional Freedom, who is against the amendment, they slammed this text message saying that the value of both campaigns is lying to Kansans but the value that both coalitions denied any involvement in this. Still, voters have had to navigate all of this and they say it has been challenging and confusing. I`ve talked to voters on both sides of this issue here. Take a listen to what they told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVEN LANE, KANSAS VOTER: I found it very complicated to try and figure out. We spent a lot of time doing a little bit of research and trying to understand what the amendment actually meant and how it meant -- what it meant for our family and our family values.\par \par \uc2 CAROL BUTTS, KANSAS VOTER: It was hard. I wish they would use simple language. I understand they have to use language that can be legal, you know, but for me, it was -- it`s hard to understand.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And look, as you said, Chris, it`s not just about how people vote here but how many people actually turn out. That is so critical. And I`ll tell you just at this location, they say -- typically they see about 300 people or so, on a primary election in the midterms, they already exceeded that number and there are still many hours to go on this primary day, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Steve, in the amount of money that`s been poured into this issue in a state the size of Kansas with the population of Kansas is off the charts. I just want to play a couple of those ads. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Radical Left gave us high gas prices, skyrocketing inflation, and supply chain shortages. Now they`re lying about Value Them Both.\par \par \uc2 KATIE POPE, RN, BSN: Because of a radical state court decision, Kansas now allows painful dismemberment abortions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Vote yes on value them both.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, to restore common sense abortion limits.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You worked for \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , you know how the Supreme Court work and you know -- works, and you know what they said. They said this should go to the States. But as this with what they intended, confusing wording for a lot of people, an incredible amount of money, a lot of it not from Kansas being put into ads that further add to the confusion, text messages going out that have people on their way to the polls trying to figure out they thought they knew what they were voting for now, they`re not so sure. Is this what the Supreme Court has in mind?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MCALLISTER\uc2 , FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY FOR KANSAS: Well, I think they anticipated some sort of reasonable debate amongst the people and that is not what happens. As we`re seeing in Kansas, in fact, it`s a very strong agenda-driven, and the -- and the rules are sort of thrown out the window. There`s no fair of play here. This was calculated from the beginning to achieve a particular result.\par \par \uc2 So, the amendment was misleadingly named, confusingly worded. Someone there said, well, maybe they had to use this language for legal reasons, but they did not. This was purposely confusing. Very smart people have continued to ask me, what is this mean, which way do I vote yes or no? What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 And that -- and that shouldn`t be the case. You should be able to read it and understand what about means. So this was all calculated. And as my colleague from Missouri said, the primary election was a deliberate choice to favor the fact there`d be more Republican voters likely voting in that election. So it was all calculated. And I don`t think the Supreme Court`s you know -- it`s unrealistic to think if you just throw this back to the states, you`re going to have some sort of reasonable debate and discussion about what the people really want. It is going to be very partisan and driven by the right-to-life agenda.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Which brings us to the question, we already knew that the right to life supporters as they -- that organization, they`ve been motivating people, not for months, but for years, even decades, right? They were building to this moment in Kansas and in other states. But do you think that this confusion -- do you think that the -- not just confusion but outright misinformation that`s out there is motivating Democrats as well? And what are you going to be looking for tonight to show you that this is a motivating issue on both sides, and maybe not just in Kansas?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Well, it -- first of all, if you have the kind of turnout they`re talking about in a primary election in Kansas, something is going on.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: This is different.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 And I do think that this is -- the phrase keeps being used, but it`s apt I think the car caught -- the dog caught the car. And now I think it`s kind of flipped, the motivation, the anger, the disappointment, especially when states like Missouri do such extreme laws. I mean, this is a tough subject, right?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Reasonable people can disagree about parts of the issue of legal abortions. But most Missourians, most Kansans, most Americans, want rape victims to be able to terminate a pregnancy. They don`t want dogs sniffing women at airports to see if they`re traveling to have an abortion. They don`t want civil posses out, looking to find people who have enabled someone to get a safe and legal abortion and go take after them in court.\par \par \uc2 This is the kind of stuff that Republicans are doing now. It`s not that they`re just taking their victory and saying, let`s be reasonable. They`re going to such an extreme. I think it is really going to help the Democrats, not only today but also in November in terms of holding on to power in Washington, DC. I could be wrong, but when the Catholic Church puts a million dollars -- the Catholic Church put a million dollars in this campaign in Kansas, in a state where there are all kinds of needs for the poor. I think that that causes a lot of head scratching.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So that brings us to the whole question of, you know, advantage, disadvantage, Steven. And it was a stunning statistic that I saw in the Kansas City Star, which was that the day of the Dobbs ruling, voter registration surged 1,000 percent. Now, we don`t know how many of those people are going to come out. We`ll know in a few hours what the turnout was, but who does that help?\par \par \uc2 MCALLISTER: I think probably in the end that helps the Democrats because I do think that`s probably a lot of young voters and it`s also people who were probably going to sit on the sidelines, but now have engaged. When I went to the polling place in Lawrence last week, on a Wednesday afternoon to advance vote, it was unlike anything I`d ever seen. I mean, it was just packed and people were coming and going --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: How it sound so for people who don`t know?\par \par \uc2 MCALLISTER: People were coming and going and I`d never seen it. Usually, you go for advanced voting, and there`s two or three people in there. So I think people are really engaged. And whether the amendment in Kansas passes or not, I think things have changed. And there`s a new level of engagement, certainly in Kansas.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Steven McAllister, thank you. Claire, you`re going to stick with us throughout the hour. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 We`re here live in Missouri also watching a critical primary race for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . But the big and unexpected question on the Republican side of the race is which Eric? That`s next. Plus, the U.S. just took out the head of al-Qaeda, a man involved in planning 9/11, how they tracked him down after 20 years, and a controversial trip, Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  just landing in Taiwan, the harsh new warning from China. You`re watching "CHRIS JANSING REPORTS" live from Kansas City.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Back live in Missouri where the Republican primary to replace retiring Senator \uc2 Roy Blunt\uc2  is coming to a fiery close in a state that Trump won by more than 15 points in 2020. Attorney General Eric Schmidt has made a late move to the front of a very crowded pack. Schmidt now pulling ahead of the one-time front runner as recently as June, disgraced former governor \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2 . But polls while giving us insights aren`t always right, of course, and it`s hard to quantify the impact of the ubiquitous ads like Greitens` controversial rhino hunting ad going after Republicans in name only. He`s been a heavy target too, like in this Super PAC ad, based on an affidavit by his wife who accused him of abuse.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHEENA GREITENS\uc2 , ERIC GREITENS` FORMER WIFE: I became afraid for my safety and that of our children due to Eric`s unstable and coercive behavior, physical violence toward her children, cuffing our then three year old son across the face.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Greitens campaign manager Dylan Johnson has called the allegations politically motivated and outright lies. Adding to the uncertainty, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  giving his long-awaited endorsement to candidate Eric, nobody seems to know which Eric he`s talking about but both are claiming his support. NBC`s Yamiche Alcindor is in St. Louis, former Senator Claire McCaskill back with me, and joining us is the lead political reporter for The Kansas City Star, Jonathan Shorman.\par \par \uc2 So, Yamiche, control of the Senate is up for grabs. And this is like some sort of bad game show will the real Eric please stand up, what is going on with this endorsement?\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Chris, it is a baffling chaotic situation here in Missouri with his Republican Senate primary. You have, of course, as you just laid out, Eric Schmidt was the-- who was the Missouri Attorney General, someone who was a moderate Republican people tell me -- though I shouldn`t say that he was suing to stop mask mandates here in Missouri from going into effect. And then you have, of course, the embattled former governor \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2 . He is someone who is, as you said, facing allegations of sexual abuse, and domestic abuse, he resigned in disgrace just 18 months after being inaugurated.\par \par \uc2 They`re now fighting it out to just figure out which Eric and claiming which Eric was endorsed here. Former President Trump was already looming large in this race, but of course, he leaned in and is really not providing any sort of clarification here. Take a listen to what \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  told me yesterday when I caught up with him at a campaign event. Shortly after this endorsement, a sort of vagueness started happening.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC GREITENS, MISSOURI REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE: I had a conversation with the president and I thanked him for his endorsement. You`ve seen \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 . came out and said we are all getting behind Eric Grietens.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Again, it`s unclear which of the Erics are here being endorsed. The other thing I want to share with you is that voters here say, while former President Trump is very popular here, there are at least some voters who are uncomfortable with how big of an influence he has in this race. Take a listen to what one voter told me, a young Republican who says she wants her party to be more focused on middle-class issues than the former president. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE MILLER, MISSOURI REPUBLICAN VOTER: To me, it`s a little bit alarming because this man is no longer like a former president to a lot of these people. He is like a God. He is something that they look up to. And to me a lot of times, maybe he isn`t the best person to look up to. That doesn`t go to say that he didn`t do a lot of good things, especially for the middle-class community here but I think once again, people are running a little bit too far with his influence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: There, you hear that voter saying that former President Trump should not be "a God in this race." I also have to add one, Chris -- one other thing, Chris, which is there is a third Eric in this race. Well, he`s not a leading candidate but if you can`t -- you can`t script this. There is a third Eric that is possibly maybe at some point thinking he might get endorsed, we`re not sure. The point is that we don`t know which Eric it is. And as you said maybe the real Eric will one day stand up by the end of today.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yamiche on the -- you can`t make this stuff up bait, thank you so much for all your reporting over the last several days. Claire, you know this state and its voters, what`s your assessment of the Republican race right now?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Well, first of all, Trump did this on purpose because all Trump cares about is claiming credit. So he waited until the day before the election, and then he was sure one of these two guys is going to win the primary and then he can take credit. That`s all this is. He purposely did this. And there is a third Eric, I don`t know if he`ll get any votes but here`s what I think.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Do you think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew there was a third Eric?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: I don`t think he probably did. I -- most Missourians did know there was a third Eric. I do think, in this race, the thing that is really interesting to me is \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  was the guy who really made January 6 possible for President Trump. That`s what President Trump wanted to have to happen. He wanted to have a debate. He wanted to have a challenge to the certification of electoral votes. No Republicans in the Senate were willing to do that, except \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 . And he raised his fist in solidarity with President Trump and early endorsed \uc2 Vicky Hartzler\uc2  in this race.\par \par \uc2 There was an assumption that that would carry weight with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . There was an assumption that what \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  had done for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , no other senator was willing to do. Not so fast. \uc2 Vicky Hartzler\uc2  was given the back of his hand by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . She`s going to finish I think a distant third. I think is demoralizing for \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  politically in this state to have this happen. And I think Trump`s endorsement did do one thing to a lot of Republicans that \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  isn`t the bad guy you think he is. And who knows? It may be enough to pull him up where he is competitive with Schmidt tonight. But if I had to bet money, I`d say Schmidt will be the nominee.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The Super PAC ad that we just played, Jonathan, it`s pretty brutal with the voice of you know, reading the affidavit from Greitens` wife. Again, he`s denied it. But this has been a deluge in the state. The last number I saw was $43 million spent already. Maybe it`s up since then. How would you describe the race itself, and where do you see it going tonight?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SHORMAN, LEAD POLITICAL REPORTER, THE KANSAS CITY STAR: Well, I think really, you`ve seen an intensification over the last month, really where you`ve seen these very tough ads. You`ve seen a lot of money come in last minute. And let`s not forget what Trump said about \uc2 Vicky Hartzler\uc2  just two or three weeks ago, the kind of anti-endorsement of Hartzler really opening up the field for the two Eric`s to kind of battle it out here at the finish. But really Greitens had all of these allegations over him since he resigned as governor. So, yes, the affidavit and some of this stuff is very new, very unexpected, but this is certainly a candidate that`s had a lot of scandals, a lot of controversy following him for years.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We talked about how the abortion issue has -- looks to have energized voters in Kansas. But here, what`s your take on voters? Are they energized? Are they exhausted? What are you hearing on the ground?\par \par \uc2 SHORMAN: You know, I think -- well, One Missouri has banned abortion. So that issue is not on the ballot here. But it is -- I think there is more energy because of this very intense -- excuse me primary race on the Republican side. But let`s not forget the Democratic side either, which has also been this intense battle between Lucas Kunce, a former Marine, and Trudy Busch Valentine, one of the Busch family heiresses. And really that`s kind of unusual on the Democratic side because we haven`t really seen a super intense Senate primary battle like this in a number of years.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. What do you think`s going to happen there, Claire?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Well, you know, they both have been tough. You know, Lukas Kunce has changed his position on some pretty important issues, including abortion from the time he ran before, until now, and now he is gone very far-left, and that is appealing to Democratic primary voters. On the other hand, Trudy Busch Valentine has also done a very good job, not very many heiresses, go back and get their Registered Nursing degree and become a nurse. If you never have to work a day in your life because your family is so rich, I`ve not met many women who go back and become an RN. And I think that resonates particularly with women, and there`s a lot of women that vote in the Democratic primary.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 So I can`t tell how it`s going to turn out. But I think that both of them, you know -- the fact that she`s wealthy has been an issue and the fact that he`s changed his position has been an issue, and we`ll see how it turns out tonight.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Jonathan Shorman, thank you so much for coming in on this steamy day. Again, Claire is sticking with us. Make sure you check out our Plan Your Vote tool, it has great information on registration deadlines by state and a lot more. You can find Plan Your Vote at NBCnews.com/PlanYourVote now.\par \par \uc2 A major win for the administration as President Biden confirms the killing of one of the world`s most wanted terrorists. What his death means for U.S. security? And the national security questions after Nancy Pelosi`s touchdown in Taiwan and threats from China. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We have more coming up on the critical primaries here in Missouri and Kansas, but right now, we want to talk about the two major developments on the national security front. House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  arrived in Taiwan this morning in defiance of warnings from China, which claims the self-ruled Island as its own. And 20 years after 9/11, the leader of al- Qaeda is dead. President Biden announcing the killing of Ayman al-Zawahiri last night, one of the architects behind the September 11 terrorist attacks that murdered nearly 3,000 people.\par \par \uc2 And no one is better to speak on these issues than Ben Rhodes, former deputy National Security adviser in the Obama White House and an NBC News political contributor.\par \par \uc2 Ben, I`m sure it is seared in your brain the day that President Biden, who you worked for, announced that they got \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2 . President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . President Biden says he hopes the killing of al-Zawahiri brings a measure of closure to the families of 9/11 victims.\par \par \uc2 So, after 20 years, Ben, what does this mean to them and the veterans who fought in the wars that followed?\par \par \uc2 BEN RHODES, NBC NEWS POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: I think it`s a really important moment, Chris, because if you think about it, Zawahiri is kind of the last of the core al Qaeda who built that organization, who plotted the 9/11 attacks. They`ve been decimated over the years. And he was kind of the last one out there. And with him removed, I think we can say, while there`s still terrorist threats, you know, al Qaeda, the organization that did 9/11, just doesn`t exist anymore.\par \par \uc2 And given the fact that we`ve had such muddled results in the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan, and so much expenditures and lives and resources in these wars, the one thing I think we can say is we got the guys who did 9/11. And that`s frankly where this all began, Chris. That`s what these men and women signed up to do. And so given again all the uncertainty in the world, I think this is something that people can feel like this was accomplished in this war.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And on the "Today" show this morning, Ben, White House National Security adviser Jake Sullivan was asked whether al-Zawahiri was planning attacks against U.S. interests. Here`s what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: We do believe he was playing an active role at a strategic level in directing al Qaeda and in continuing to pose a severe threat against the United States and American citizens everywhere.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So what does this killing mean for al Qaeda and to U.S. national security interests, Ben?\par \par \uc2 RHODES: Well, given the fact that al Qaeda has been so fractured over the years, lost bin Laden, lost a lot of their operational and strategic leadership, there`s nobody really left who can pull this organization together and give it a kind of strategic focus. And so taking him off the battlefield, removing al-Zawahiri from the leadership of al Qaeda, you know, there are still going to be people that self-identify as al Qaeda, there`s so many people plotting against the United States.\par \par \uc2 But there`s nobody left from that original generation of al Qaeda leaders who can serve as kind of the fulcrum and the strategic -- the person providing strategic direction to the organization. I think it does raise questions obviously that he was in Afghanistan. Al Qaeda had a pre-9/11 alliance with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , the \uc2 Haqqani Network\uc2  which supported al Qaeda over much of last 20 years is now a part of the government of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 So for the administration, I think on the one hand, this raises concerns about whether or not you`re going to see more efforts by \uc2 Taliban\uc2 -associated people to bring back members of al Qaeda to Afghanistan. On the other end, I think they can say that look, we would maintain a counterterrorism capability, and if you doubt that, just look at what happened to this terrorist inside of Kabul.\par \par \uc2 So it`s a sign that we have the capability to continue to go after al Qaeda, even though we don`t have those thousands of U.S. troops inside of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And then you have Speaker Pelosi in Taiwan. The \uc2 White House`s National Security Council\uc2  spokesman John Kirby said administration officials are concerned that China could use the visit as an excuse to take provocative retaliatory steps, including military action in Taiwan. What is your take on Pelosi`s visit?\par \par \uc2 RHODES: Well, look, I`m sure that the Chinese will do something in response. And they`ll have to calibrate what that is. Is that military exercises? Is that saber-rattling? I think the reality is we`re seeing China increasingly in recent years really try to intimidate people from engagement with Taiwan or from frankly any even comment on what China perceives as its internal affairs.\par \par \uc2 And so while this is a provocative visit, I think that given how much China tried to send a message that she wasn`t supposed to go there, and wasn`t allowed to go there, if you`d back down in that context, it would have sent a message not just to other members of Congress in the United States, but to other governments around the world, that, you know, like if you`re threatened by China, you should back down. You should not engage Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 And that`s the play that the Chinese government has been running for years. And I think you`re seeing some pushback in recent years not just out of the United States, but by some other governments saying, wait a second, even though we may recognize a One China Policy, even though we may not be trying to upset the status quo and push Taiwan in the direction of independence, that doesn`t mean that we can`t have some kind of working level contact with Taiwan, which is a democracy and which is a country that has a long standing relationship with the United States.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 So it`s going to stir the pot, Chris. It`s going to raise tensions, but I think that`s the new normal we`re in. We have to get accustomed to the reality that for the foreseeable future, there are just going to be heightened tensions around the issue of Taiwan, given how much China has taken a more aggressive posture and frankly given how much the Taiwanese have moved away from negotiation with China because they don`t see any end point to those negotiations other than being swallowed up in the manner that Hong Kong was over the course of the last year.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ben Rhodes, thank you so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Now we want to get you an update on a story that we`ve been following very closely on this program. After widespread pressure to amp up the federal government`s fight against monkeypox, President Biden has appointed a White House monkeypox coordinate. He`s Robert Fenton, who helped lead FEMA`s COVID-19 mass vaccination effort, and Dr. Demetre Daskalakis, the CDC`s director of HIV prevention and LGBTQ community expert will be Fenton`s deputy.\par \par \uc2 Those appointments come just after California declared a state of emergency. It`s the third state to do that after New York and Illinois.\par \par \uc2 And right now in Kansas, abortion is on the ballot for the first time since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. But what about the ongoing battles over abortion rate rights in other states? And what will today`s vote tell us going forward?\par \par \uc2 Plus any minute now, we expect Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  to make an announcement related to reproductive healthcare access. When that begins, we`ll bring it to you live.\par \par \uc2 You`re watch a special edition of CHRIS JANSING REPORTS live from Kansas City.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:55]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`re back live in Kansas City and the abortion fight is the first on the ballot since Roe was overturned. One of literally dozens of battles over abortion raging across the country.\par \par \uc2 After the Supreme Court ruled, abortion bans were triggered in seven states. A ban in Louisiana went into effect over the weekend. Bans in Idaho, elsewhere they went in very quickly. And in fact, in West Virginia now the legislature is fast tracking a bill that would ban abortion in nearly all cases. In South Carolina, lawmakers are working on a ban that would criminalize helping someone get an abortion. You could get a sentence up to 25 years there.\par \par \uc2 In Indiana, the Senate passed a near total ban in a rare weekend session. That of course the state that became kind of the emotional center of this debate after a 10-year-old girl had to go there for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Kaitlin Lange, she covers the Indiana statehouse for the Indianapolis -- let`s go to Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  who is now speaking about the Idaho law, trigger law. Let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , ATTORNEY GENERAL: -- stabilize the patient`s condition serious jeopardy. In some circumstances, the medical treatment necessary to stabilize the patient`s condition is abortion. This may be the case, for example, when a woman is undergoing a miscarriage that threatens septic infection or hemorrhage or is suffering from severe pre-eclampsia.\par \par \uc2 When a hospital determines that an abortion is the medical treatment necessary to stabilize the patient`s emergency medical condition, it is required by federal law to provide that treatment. As detailed in our complaint, Idaho`s law would make it a criminal offense for doctors to provide the emergency medical treatment that federal law requires.\par \par \uc2 Although the Idaho law provides an exception to prevent the death of a pregnant woman, it includes no exception for cases in which the abortion is necessary to prevent serious jeopardy to the woman`s health. Moreover, it would subject doctors to arrest and criminal prosecution, even if they performed an abortion to save a woman`s life, and it would then place the burden on the doctors to prove that they are not criminally liable.\par \par \uc2 The United States therefore seeks a declaratory judgment that Idaho`s law violates the supremacy clause of the United States Constitution and is preempted by federal law to the extent it is in conflict with EMTALA. The United States also seeks an injunction prohibiting Idaho from enforcing its law against healthcare providers who provide the emergency treatment required by EMTALA.\par \par \uc2 In the days since the Dobbs decision, there have been widespread reports of delays and denials of treatment to pregnant women experiencing medical emergencies. Today the Justice Department`s message is clear. It does not matter what state a hospital subject to EMTALA operates in. If a patient comes into the emergency room with a medical emergency jeopardizing the patient`s life or health, the hospital must provide the treatment necessary to stabilize that patient.\par \par \uc2 This includes abortion when that is the necessary treatment. Any state law that prevents a hospital from fulfilling its obligation under EMTALA violates federal law. On the day Roe and Casey were overturned, we promised that the Justice Department would work tirelessly to protect and advance reproductive freedom. That is what we`re doing. And that is what we will continue to do.\par \par \uc2 We will use every tool at our disposal to ensure that pregnant women get the emergency medical treatment to which they are entitled under federal law. And we will closely scrutinize state abortion laws to ensure that they comply with federal law.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:08]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That`s \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  saying that the U.S. is going to file a lawsuit challenging the Idaho abortion law. Claire McCaskill is still with me. This is what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said he was going to do. He said, listen, to the extent that we as a federal government can look at some of these laws we`re going to do it. What do you make of what we just heard?\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE MCCASKILL (D), FORMER MISSOURI SENATOR: Well, I think what \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is get at is something that I have learned in talking to doctors since Dobbs was overturned. Sometimes these laws, people say, oh, no one is going to be prosecuted, no one is going to be put in jail, but what has happened is doctors are now being told by their lawyers, don`t go there. Don`t perform that procedure. Be very careful. You could go to jail.\par \par \uc2 And that is going to have an impact, dramatic impact on women`s health in this country because doctors are going to be afraid. They are caught. They are caught -- they`re used to just have to worry about their patients. Now they have to worry about prison. And this is putting them in the worst situation possible. And what makes it worse is that you have these laws that are going to be so different and as it`s turning out so extreme in so many states across the nation.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`re going to talk about a lot of different laws now because obviously we have focused earlier on what`s happening in Kansas, but I want to bring into the conversation Kaitlin Lange, who covers the Indiana statehouse for the "Indianapolis Star," Melissa Murray is a law professor at \uc2 NYU\uc2  and an NBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 Kaitlin, we were mentioning the fact that Indiana, obviously, became part of the emotional center of this when the Ohio 10-year-old went to Indiana to have an abortion. And I understand the legislature in Indiana is working on a bill right now. Tell us about what`s going on there.\par \par \uc2 KAITLIN LANGE, INDIANAPOLIS STAR: We`re in this very messy complicated system over here. On Saturday the Indiana Senate passed a bill that would ban abortions except for in rape, incest and to protect the life of (INAUDIBLE). So now we`re seeing what the House wants to do with it. And just this morning they announced (INAUDIBLE) add protection for the health of the mother as well. It`s really interesting to see how the 10-year-old discussion might have (INAUDIBLE) in some of these conversations over here and the decision to include exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Kaitlin, thank you for that. Melissa, look, a lot of the language in these bills is similar, but in some cases it`s also vague like Indiana and South Carolina, there are provisions to protect the life and health of the mother. But, you know, Claire was pointing this out, for a lot of lawyers advising doctors, that`s difficult to define. Are the bills written that way on purpose?\par \par \uc2 MELISSA MURRAY, NBC LEGAL ANALYST: I think it`s fair to say, Chris, that a landscape of chaos and confusion can be as effective in limiting access to abortion as an outright ban. And Claire is exactly right. You have these doctors in the untenable position either performing professional malpractice by not providing this care or exposing themselves to criminal liability if they do.\par \par \uc2 And so again, in those circumstances, many physicians won`t take the risk or they`ll weigh the risk and find that criminal liability far outweighs the prospect of a malpractice suit and they won`t perform these procedures, which makes it difficult for those who actually need this kind of care, whether it`s because of miscarriage management for an ectopic pregnancy or simply because she wants an abortion at some period in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 And so again these are difficult conversations. Attorney General Garland has come out of the gate swinging with its federal preemption suit. This is a really interesting development. Many have castigated the administration for a slow start in dealing with this. But this is I think a very forceful step to make clear that to the extent that these state laws are in conflict with existing federal laws, that the federal laws (INAUDIBLE) and carry the field in these circumstances.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And Melissa, I also want to ask you about some language in the South Carolina bill that apparently would make it illegal to provide information to people seeking an abortion. I mean, can you reconcile that with First Amendment protections of free speech? What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, I think there`s very likely to be a plausible challenge on First Amendment grounds. Certainly to limit speech conferring information about abortion might be viewed as a kind of viewpoint discrimination, which is prohibited under the First Amendment. So there`s a very plausible First Amendment challenge here.\par \par \uc2 Again, all of this is going to proceed through litigations. Much of it through the federal courts. These are the same federal courts that over the course of four years \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  managed to stuff with conservatives like rock-ribbed conservatives. Many of whom were vetted expressly for the purpose of having antipathy toward reproductive rights and abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:06]\par \par \uc2 So this will have to go through those gauntlets first, but ultimately, these are very pitched court battles that are likely to make their way not just to the federal court, but perhaps even to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And I think speaking to the chaos that Melissa mentioned, Claire, I want to ask you a little bit about enforcement because we are seeing even in states like Texas and Alabama, there are DAs, there are prosecutors, who are saying I will not prosecute this restrictive abortion ban that the state legislature put in. How do you see that playing out?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Well, that will be another chaotic battle because what you`re going to see, you`ll see those prosecutors challenged in elections by those Republicans that are more extreme than they are. And I`m sure for some of these DAs, they may be in areas like Kansas City that is bluer than the rest of my state, and they are safe in saying we are not going to be taking these cases to court.\par \par \uc2 But the bottom line is, as long as there are so many people in the Republican Party pushing the envelope to go after people who give information to stop women from crossing state lines to, you know, do civil liability for people who give somebody a ride in their car, an \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver. They are going to turn off the majority of this country. And they are also going to make it very difficult for women in the short run to find a safe place to take care of their health.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Claire, you`re going to stick around. Melissa Murray, as always, thank you so much for your legal expertise. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And all eyes are on Arizona`s high-stakes primary today as the power of the Trump endorsement gets put to yet another major test in contest up and down the Republican ballot. Will voters side with the former president and his false claims of a stolen election? We`ve got the latest from Phoenix, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: If you want to summarize the high-takes Republican primary for governor of Arizona it is no less than a referendum on what it means to be a Republican right now. And as with governors races all across the country with more and more power going back to the states, they are the final arbiter, deciding whether or not to sign bills on issues from abortion to voting rights to gun access.\par \par \uc2 Voters in Arizona are deciding whether it will be Kari Lake, whose campaign is built on the big lie and Donald Trump`s endorsement.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R), ARIZONA GOVERNOR PRIMARY CANDIDATE: Anybody who was involved in that corrupt, shady, shoddy election of 2020, lock them up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Or will it be Karrin Taylor Robson who worked under \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  and has the endorsements of \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and the current governor, \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARRIN TAYLOR ROBSON (R), ARIZONA GOVERNOR PRIMARY CANDIDATE: More and more conservative voices lending their names and their time and their energy to me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Those results are going to give us a pretty amazing view from 30,000 feet. And so will the race for secretary of state. A test of power of election lies.\par \par \uc2 Claire McCaskill, we`re hoping to go to Vaughn. I think that he`s there, has rendered him inoperable. But this really is a theme, right, that we`re into now, like what is the Republican Party? Tonight`s election here in Arizona is going to give us some big clues.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: It will, and if in fact these big lie candidates, anyone who`s watched, really watched the January 6th Committee hearings know what a fraud this was to try to convince Americans that our elections aren`t safe and secure.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:06]\par \par \uc2 And there are so many candidates running on that premise. Arizona is exhibit A. This Kari Lake, she used to be, according to her friends, kind of normal. Now she`s gone to crazy town. And it is all overturning elections and doing away with voting machines and all of this stuff that`s totally impractical and silly. The same thing with the secretary of state race there, he`s basically said we`ll never lose another election if I`m elected secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 So if the Republican Party embraces these nominees, these the candidates, and makes them their nominee, I think that`s good news for our Senate candidate out there, Mark Kelly, who is going to need a lot of moderate voters who don`t necessarily call themselves Republicans but have traditionally voted for a lot of Republicans, I think it`s going to move him over to his side of the agenda.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There is really interesting study in Pew that said the decisive question for 2022 is whether Democrats can push the campaign dialogue away from the economics performance of Democrats, right, and toward concerns like election lies, January 6th, where Republicans, you might argue, could be at a disadvantage with those middle-of-the-road voters.\par \par \uc2 The study concludes that the GOP holds the edge on the economy but Democrats have wide advantages on most other key issues including healthcare, race, abortion, climate change, policies affecting LGBTQ people.\par \par \uc2 It`s a long sentence. Is it a complicated message if you`re a Democrat running against one of these folks who embraces the big lie?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Well, I think it`s the economy versus extremism. Whether it`s extreme voices saying that our elections are no good in the country or extreme voices saying that rape victims are not allowed to legally and safely terminate a pregnancy, or extreme voices saying what we need to do is give our children AR-15s in classrooms so they can protect themselves.\par \par \uc2 That stuff is out there and that`s -- I think that`s what Democrats need to talk about. And I will put this in to this conversation because I think it`s important. A lot of coverage on gas prices going up every day after every day. We have a lot of shots from gas stations.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Gas prices have gone down now for 49 days in a row. If that continues, if the stock market stabilizes, if the jobs numbers continue strong, that will continue to weaken their economic case, and frankly I think put even more emphasis on some of their extreme views that have turned off a lot of suburban voters.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So much happening tonight. It`s going to be an interesting one. Look, when we went on the air, the real feel temperature was 100 degrees.\par \par \uc2 Claire McCaskill, you are a trooper, a good friend and amazing to stay out here in this heat.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Listen, I am used to hot weather in August in Missouri. So this is not a big deal. I campaigned in this kind of weather for way more years than I want to admit.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Claire McCaskill, thank you so much. It`s great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 That is going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us again, though. We have another special edition of CHRIS JANSING REPORTS live from Kansas City tomorrow at 1:00 Eastern Time. Lots of results for you and analysis right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 Stay tuned. Yasmin Vossoughian picks up our coverage next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220803ei8200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle for August 1, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 7,388 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: And I`ll see you tomorrow day and night full coverage of tomorrow`s big primaries and all the results and THE 11TH HOUR starts right now.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:27]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC ANCHOR: Tonight, President Biden`s major announcement a drone strike taking out the al Qaeda leader, one of the masterminds of 9/11 what it means for the war on terror or national security tonight.\par \par \uc2 Plus, it is the eve of key primaries across the country with the future of democracy and abortion rights on the ballot. And we are back on Capitol Hill with the push to pass the PACT Act to help sick veterans with another boat eminent as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Monday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. And while this is the eve of yet another crucial set of primaries, with just 99 days to go until the midterm elections, we begin this evening`s broadcast with major news in the still ongoing war on terror. President Biden made it official just a few hours ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: On Saturday, in my direction, the United States successfully concluded an airstrike in Kabul, Afghanistan that killed the Emir of al Qaeda, Ayman al-Zawahiri. After carefully considering a clear and convincing evidence of his location, I authorized the precision strike that would remove him from the battlefield, once and for all.\par \par \uc2 This mission was carefully planned. Rigorously minimize the risk of harm to other civilians. We make it clear again tonight, that no matter how long it takes, no matter where you hide, if you are a threat to our people, the United States we`ll find you and take you out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Al-Zawahiri was an Egyptian born physician who founded the \uc2 Egyptian Islamic Jihad\uc2 . He formed an alliance with \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  back in 1998. He was indicted for the simultaneous bombings of multiple U.S. embassies that year that killed more than 200 people, including 12 Americans. He was closely linked to the bombing of the USS Cole in October of 2000, and was believed to be one of the main architects of the attacks on September 11. He became the leader of al Qaeda after bin Laden was killed back in 2011. And he`s been on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  Most Wanted terrorists list. The State Department was offered a reward of up to 25 million bucks for help and his apprehension.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has updated that entry to deceased.\par \par \uc2 As the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  sums it up, it was al-Zawahiri brains and blood drenched hands that guided the world`s most notorious terrorist movement. Our next guest says this move decapitates the group behind the horror of 9/11. And he would know.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in John Brennan, former CIA director and MSNBC senior national security and intelligence analyst and retired four star \uc2 US Army\uc2  General Barry McCaffrey, a decorated combat veteran of Vietnam, and a former battlefield commander in the Persian Gulf.\par \par \uc2 John, how big of a win is this for the US in terms of the war on terror?\par \par \uc2 JOHN BRENNAN, FORMER CIA DIRECTOR: Well, it`s a very big win for a variety of reasons. One is that it has taken out the leader of al Qaeda. This is an individual, al-Zawahiri, who has been involved in terrorist activities for the past 40 years.\par \par \uc2 And then he boldly relocated from Pakistan to Afghanistan`s capital, Kabul, and it demonstrates the reach of American counterterrorism capabilities and our intelligence collection capabilities, that we were able to find al- Zawahiri in Kabul, and take this action, as President Biden said, with meticulous planning and with careful regard for preventing any type of civilian casualties.\par \par \uc2 So I do think it demonstrates that we`re going to not relent in terms of our efforts to take out al Qaeda leaders as well as the leaders of other terrorist organizations that present a threat to U.S. interests in persons.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s talk about the precision here, general, because a senior administration official says this guy was taken out while he was standing on a balcony and his nearby family members were not hurt. How does this type of drone mission happen?\par \par \uc2 GEN. BARRY MCCAFFREY, \uc2 U.S. ARMY\uc2  (RET.): Well, look at as Director Brennan says, this is really a brilliant operation. It was the sum total of the intelligence community the massive overhead satellite photography we can bring to bear on a problem that signals intelligence. I`m sure they CIA still maintains an agent, network and country.\par \par \uc2 And then finally, these drones, which will put eyes on a target for weeks at a time and they`ll track every car that visits and their license plate, and they`ll put it all together and end up with a target folder that they can then take action on which is what they promptly did.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:10]\par \par \uc2 You know, I think we should remind ourselves though, that when this major terrorist was killed, he was in the home of a senior \uc2 Taliban\uc2  official, it looks as if the \uc2 Haqqani Network\uc2  was also sponsoring them, which may mean total complicity on the part of Pakistan. So we are over the horizon, we have a limited ability to influence actions on the ground. But this is a very encouraging step by a brilliant operation by the CIA.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So John, what does that mean that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and al Qaeda are merging are working together?\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: Well, it`s a very worrisome development that al-Zawahiri did relocate to Kabul, but as general McCaffrey said the \uc2 Haqqani Network\uc2  is, I think, an instrumental part of this story.\par \par \uc2 Sirajuddin Haqqani\uc2 , who`s the head of the \uc2 Haqqani Network\uc2 , which is a criminal paramilitary drug organization on the pack Afghan border. He is the Minister of Interior of the Afghan government. He is closely aligned with a lot of these terrorist organizations. He himself has blood on his hands and carried out a number of operations against U.S. targets in Afghanistan as well as against the Afghan government.\par \par \uc2 So it`s unsurprising in many respects that these long standing ties between the \uc2 Haqqani Network\uc2  and al Qaeda are now resurrecting in the form of Zawahiri, coming into Afghanistan. And this is something that I think we have to get to the bottom of.\par \par \uc2 And as general McCaffrey said, I think the Pakistani role in this also is crucial. Does the lottery move across that border with the either the knowledge or the support of the \uc2 Pakistani ISI\uc2 , which is their intelligence organization, because of their deep rooted ties to the Haqqanis.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But General part of the deal when the United States withdrew from Afghanistan a year ago was a pledge to not allow terrorist groups to operate within their territory. Given that, and what we just learned, how`s the U.S. supposed to respond?\par \par \uc2 MCCAFFREY: Well, I think we need to recognize Afghanistan is an utter disaster, their military and governmental entity came apart as we withdrew from Afghanistan. It`s a big difference between having 4,000 people in the U.S. Embassy in Kabul and having no overt American presence there. Our leverage is limited.\par \par \uc2 Having said that, I think the one of the conclusions we need to draw from this is the poor Afghan people who are now under the control of \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , you know, position of girls and women is now back to near slavery conditions. There, starvation is imminent. And yet, despite this, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  have embraced a terrorist overt threat to the United States and our allies.\par \par \uc2 So we need diplomatic engagement with these people, somehow, we need to moderate their overt hatred of the United States and the danger they pose to our military and our civilian population.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: John, this was carried out, though, by the CIA, not the military, talking about the significance of that to our civilian audience.\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: Well, President Biden said that the U.S. carried out the strike. He didn`t name the organization that did it. But I must say, having worked at CIA for many, many years, including as its director, the CIA, along with the \uc2 National Security Agency\uc2 , the NSA worked very closely on these counterterrorism operations, as well as with the military.\par \par \uc2 And as General McCaffrey said, these platforms, the predators, the drones, that primarily intelligence collection platforms, they collect full motion video, they collect technical signals, and then they`re used to carry out these very precise strikes within inches they`re laser guided.\par \par \uc2 And so over the course of the last 15 years or so, there has been the technology that has been developed. They are on the missiles, the platforms themselves, that really allow these exquisite operations to take place.\par \par \uc2 Despite the fact that we don`t have a presence on the ground Afghanistan, President Biden was true to his word that that we withdraw our troops who were still going to have the capability to detect these threats and to take action when appropriate, and we did this past weekend.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, even if Al Qaeda is decapitated, I can`t imagine they`re just going to take this and move on in, general. What should we be looking out next from them?\par \par \uc2 MCCAFFREY: Well, I think there`s always been a reaction it says look, it`s like mowing the grass and you kill one of them and another one pops up replace them but there`s no question that we put these organizations into terrorist organization into a real panic when we eliminate their key people.\par \par \uc2 Particularly we have an opportunity then to exploit the outcome of it if we have people on the ground and can seize codes and telephones et cetera.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:00]\par \par \uc2 So this is a bloat al Qaeda. Al Qaeda has been severely limited in its capability anyway during the past 20 years, both by the CIA and over tier one military counterterrorism operations will continue to do that. There`s been some spectacular successes by both the military and the agency in Syria in recent months.\par \par \uc2 So I think the Biden administration done an excellent job of staying focused on what we`ve got to do, which is when we see these targets come up, eliminate them or ask our allies to take care of it. So it`s a good day for the National Security United States.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well the President said if you are a threat to our people, the U.S. is going to find you. And we did. John Brennan, General McCaffrey, thank you both for joining us tonight. I appreciate it. And remember, the President did pull off this mission while he is facing COVID. And with Congress on the verge of passing a major tax and climate bill, which gets us to the night`s other big story.\par \par \uc2 The politics behind the next round of primaries just 99 days until the midterms, and a few hours from now voters go to the polls in five states, Arizona, Kansas, Michigan, Missouri and Washington state. One of the most closely watched decisions will be in Kansas, the first state to put abortion rights on the ballot since Roe vs. Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 Voters will decide whether to remove abortion protections from the Kansas State Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow will also be the latest and perhaps the most revealing test or former President Trump`s political influence within the Republican Party. Several races could help determine just how much GOP voters believe his baseless and totally false claims of a stolen election.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, Trump has endorsed a slate of complete election deniers including Kari Lake for governor and \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  for Senate. In Michigan, Trump back conservative commentator Tudor Dixon is running against four other Republicans, including a January 6 rider to take on Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And just tonight, Trump announced his endorsement in Missouri Senate primary in that closely watched contest the candidates include controversial former governor \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  and Attorney General Eric Schmidt.\par \par \uc2 In a statement this evening Trump revealed that he is supporting Eric. So far he has not yet said which one.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel this evening, Yamiche Alcindor, NBC News correspondent and moderator of Washington Week on \uc2 PBS\uc2  and MSNBC political contributor Matthew Dowd. He is a former \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  strategist and founder of Country Over Party.\par \par \uc2 Yamiche, you have been in Missouri for days talking to voters, talking to candidates, including both Eric`s are seeking the GOP nomination. How are they reacting to the statement from Trump? I`m the real Eric.\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Stephanie it`s just a baffling situation here in Missouri. Former President Trump has injected even more chaos in already a heated and tight and crowded GOP primary to all of these candidates are trying to replace Senator \uc2 Roy Blunt\uc2  in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 What you heard and what we saw today was really former President Trump trying to flex his muscles. He started off the day by saying that he was going to endorse someone sometime today. Several hours went by before he`d sent out this message that you just said was really about quote Eric, in all caps. We don`t know which Eric it was.\par \par \uc2 Eric Schmidt earlier in the day. And Eric Schmidt is the Missouri Attorney General. He told me that he was welcoming the idea that former President Trump would be able to endorse me wanted his endorsement. He has now said that endorsement was for him.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2 , who is the embattled former governor, he resigned in disgrace. 18 months after he was inaugurated, he is facing allegations of domestic abuse, sexual abuse. I asked him specifically because all this while all this was happening, and this all this chaos started happening, I was at an \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  event at an airport. Listen to what \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  told me when I asked him, how are you so certain that he`s talking about you? And also are you worried that voters are confused by all of this? Because former President Trump at this hour has not used the last name so you`re not sure which Eric he`s talking about. Take a listen to what \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC GREITENS (R) MISSOURI U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: It`s incredibly clear because he was very clear. He wants a MAGA champion. Everybody here knows I`ve been behind President Trump, stood behind him on election integrity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: So there you have him. He tried to make his case that he is other Eric. I should also say, Stephanie, this wasn`t confusing enough. There is a third Eric in this race. He`s not a leading candidate. But even though we`re likely sure that former President Trump is thinking as the top two leading characters so there`s another Eric that we should just be keeping our eye on.\par \par \uc2 People that I`ve been talking to think that it was going to be \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  because \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 ., emphasis on the junior, the son of the former president, he has been in support of \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2 . Eric Schmidt has been seen as someone who was moderate, who has tried to attack right at the last moments of this race but not someone who a lot of people in Trump world see as a MAGA loyalist.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:09]\par \par \uc2 So it`s a very interesting race to watch. And I`ve just never covered anything like this. I`ve covered Trump for years. But the fact that we still at this hour don`t know who he`s talking about in his endorsement just tells you how much former President Trump has influenced here, but also how much he`s also wielding the power that he has over the GOP.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Influence power or just creating a whole lot of chaos. Matthew, I know you recently talked to voters in New Hampshire. And I want to share a bit of what they told you with our audience.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO LCIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We`re seeing here, but we have something a phenomenon we haven`t seen before, which is a real distrust in the accuracy of elections. That`s never been a problem here in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There are lots of folks in this state who are convinced that the voting machines are you know, rigged.\par \par \uc2 KYRI CLAFLIN, NEW HAMPSHIRE VOTER: I don`t know how the people can voters can come together, when they fundamentally disagree on reality.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW DOWD, MSNBC Political Contributor: what`s your sense about where we are today as a country or where the health of our democracy is?\par \par \uc2 CAROL WARD, NEW HAMPSHIRE VOTER: I think it`s breaking down honestly, for me. I just feel like I`m even more concerned now than I was four years ago.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Is that what people there are worried about? The democracy is at stake?\par \par \uc2 DOWD: Well, first, I want to let your viewers know on election night, they really ought to win and we start getting returns in the political canary in the coal mine of what`s happening across the country is going to start with New Hampshire, there`s two competitive House races. There`s a competitive Senate race, in fact, that Democratic senator only won by 1,000 votes 2016, 0.1 percent. And the House member that`s competitive there, one of the House members, the district he`s in as a Democrat was carried by \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  twice, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  twice, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won the first time and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  when he defeated \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So it`s going to give us a really good indication.\par \par \uc2 What`s interesting is something that I picked up in the last month or six weeks talking to voters is that yes, voters are concerned about inflation. They don`t like inflation. They`re worried about the economy. But there`s this really deep seated unease about the state of our democracy and where we are as a whole on a number of bigger issues, like Roe vs. Wade, like guns, but the January 6th Commission seems to for many of these voters revealed something more important to them, which is, yes, I`d love inflation to be lower. But I`m really worried for the first time in my life, about the state of our democracy.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s the missing piece that some people have not picked up on, is how much people are concerned today, like they`ve never been before in their lives, about what`s going to happen with the fundamentals of our democracy.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And is that energizing people to vote or having people just wash their hands of the system entirely, because there`s all sorts of people who don`t like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but at the same time, don`t necessarily identify with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and they`re not happy with the system?\par \par \uc2 DOWD: Well, seven, you put your finger on, I think the most important group of voters in this election, which is voters who don`t like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and who also are uneasy and don`t like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , it`s about 15 percent of the electorate.\par \par \uc2 And right now, when you look at the polls, that group today leans democratic. Six weeks ago that group leaned Republican, and so that`s going to be the fundamental divide -- his decision maker in this at this time. Voters, yes, it`s a complex series of issues. There bear -- the Democratic voters have become much more motivated in the last six weeks, again, through a combination of issues like Roe, like guns, and like the January 6 Commission.\par \par \uc2 So right now, it didn`t it wasn`t the case, six weeks, eight weeks ago, Democrats night now are more energized than Republicans today. The reverse was true two months ago.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Two months ago, isn`t that extraordinary how things have changed so much. Yamiche, take us to Kansas because there is this major referendum on abortion rights in Kansas tomorrow? How important is this to voters there?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: It`s incredibly important that we have a great -- our great reporter Dasha Burns was there covering this day in, day out. This is a constitutional amendment that will really decide the access that women have in that state to abortion. It`s also in some ways a nap. It`s a (INAUDIBLE) what is going on in the nation, which is that Republicans now having won this big victory from the Supreme Court having Roe overturned. They`re now trying to figure out sort of what they want to do with that victory and how they`re going to take it to the electorate and how access is going to look awful for women all across this country.\par \par \uc2 So I`ve heard not only from women in this state, which I was in Missouri, but from women all over this country that they are really, in some ways very, very concerned about abortion access. So watching Kansas, seeing how that vote go, seeing how that constitutional amendment ends up or how abortion ends up in the Constitution is going to be critically important for all the other states. So I think it`s a very, very interesting thing to watch.\par \par \uc2 [23:20:00]\par \par \uc2 I also -- I should also notice I`m covering the two areas that are fighting over this endorsement. I also have been talking to some Republicans, especially one young Republican Woman I`m thinking about she`s pro-choice. She says she wants to be Republican, she thinks the Republican Party has done some good things. But she`s worried that they`ve just gone too far when it comes to abortion rights, and she thinks that women need to be able to make private medical decisions for their own selves.\par \par \uc2 I also talked to a woman who is sort of pro-life she really didn`t believe that abortion should have to that woman should have to ask that they have now but she says even in her case, she wants to have exceptions for rape, for incest. Those are the things that you`re not seeing in some states.\par \par \uc2 So even the people who are pro-life who are sort of really happy about the Supreme Court decision, they too are saying this has gone a little too far. And we should you shouldn`t go too far as a country of taking away abortion access. So, Kansas is going to be absolutely someplace to watch but also nationally, this is going to be a critical issue in the midterms as Republicans and Democrats fight it out.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Overturning Roe v. Wade in theory, a conservative win on the Supreme Court could be cursing Republicans in the midterms. Yamiche Alcindor, Matthew Dowd, thank you both so much stay here tomorrow night all night long. Steve Kornacki will be tracking the results at the Big Board.\par \par \uc2 But coming up next, the longest sentence yet goes to a rioter convicting of heading up the mob that overwhelmed police at the Capitol. The message the Justice Department is hoping to send.\par \par \uc2 And later, it is the tax loophole that favors the super rich and it could be the one thing holding up Joe Manchin`s climate and tax deal. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a very busy Monday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:26:14]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: An important courtroom decision today could have a major implication for the Justice Department`s January 6 investigation. The first rioter to go on trial in the Department of Justice`s criminal inquiry. Guy Reffitt has been sentenced to more than seven years in prison. That is the longest punishment yet in cases connected to the attack.\par \par \uc2 Reffitt was convicted on five felony charges in March, including transport of a firearm in support of civil disorder and obstruction of an official proceeding. Here`s what his daughter said just minutes after the sentencing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SARA REFFITT, DAUGTHER OF JANUARY 6 RIOTER GUY REFFITT: To mark my dad is this horrible person and then having a prosecute like this when somebody is maybe even able to get elected again. Doesn`t seem right to me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Trump deserves life in prison that my father`s in prison for this one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s welcome former U.S. attorney and University of Alabama law professor Joyce Vance. She spent 25 years as a federal prosecutor. Joyce, prosecutors wanted a 15-year sentence saying his crimes should be treated as a type of terrorism. But the judge said no way. Is seven years long enough to send a message to other defendants?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: So I think the question comes in two parts, Steph. First off, there`s this rather arcane sentencing guidelines calculation question. And the judge isn`t really being asked to pass on whether the act itself was an act of terrorism. I think we can all look at the conduct on January 6 and agree that what Guy Reffitt did was domestic terrorism. He tried to use coercion or intimidation to influence the government. That`s the technical legal standard and the sentencing guidelines. But the judge also had to consider whether applying it here would have been consistent with other sentences that have been imposed in these cases.\par \par \uc2 And since this is the first one, and since other defendants have engaged in very violent acts, I think the judge was really being cagey and trying to make sure that the sentence would stand up on appeal. So all that is to say that, as a technical legal issue, I don`t think that it`s as problematic as some people have suggested.\par \par \uc2 You know, the sentence that`s imposed here is a little bit more than seven years, had the terrorism enhancement been applied, it would have been closer to 10 years. And what the data on criminal sentencing tells us is that the most important components are that it happens promptly in relationship to the crime and that did here and that the imposition of punishment be certain so swift and certain matters a lot more than light.\par \par \uc2 Here`s seven years certainly sends a message to people who might contemplate supporting Trump or others in the future and acts of violence or intimidation against the government. Seven and a quarter years of your life is a big chunk of change to spend in prison.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, over the weekend, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reported or published the audio of \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 , Congressman \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  talking Trump pardons with longtime Trump ally \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 , prompting this observation from campaign veteran Stuart Stevens, former Republican where he wrote what happened to the GOP under Trump isn`t complicated or novel. A gangster family took over an organization and turned it into a criminal enterprise. But instead of this being a waste disposal company in Jersey, it is one of two major American political parties.\par \par \uc2 Well, I might take issue and that waste disposal company being in Jersey. Does he have a point while the DOJ goes after individual riders, there is a much bigger criminal enterprise to take on. And if they don`t you`re just going to keep getting a bunch of low level hit man.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:03]\par \par \uc2 VANCE: You know, I think it`s tough to quibble with the characterization here. If anything, this sounds like Gaetz was trying to imitate an old Sopranos episode, it was just painful to hear this kind of conversation about something as important as the pardon power, which is supposed to be used to do justice by a president. And here they apparently contemplated using it for exactly the opposite reasons.\par \par \uc2 I think that we can be reassured to some extent by developments last week that suggests that DOJ is now finally in this arena. But of course, delay is problematic and whether or not we`ll get to the end and whether those who are the most culpable for prosecuting or for perpetuating the big lie. And for events on January 6, whether they will ultimately be held accountable is obviously something we`re not going to know for certain until it`s over. And that`s a little bit unsettling for all of us as Americans.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, well, here`s what I want to know for certain, Joyce. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his family delayed their testimony in the New York Attorney General`s civil investigation into his business practices to mourn the death of his ex-wife, \uc2 Ivana Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But since that during that period of time, Trump has hosted this major Saudi golf tournament in New Jersey. Trump has spoken at rallies and he`s out there endorsing candidates. When is he going to start answering questions? He appears to be out of the mourning period.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: If you can host a Saudi sponsored golf tournament, then I`d say that you are sufficiently prepared emotionally to go back and to testify. The decision about the date will sit primarily with Tish James, the New York Attorney General. I`ve not heard yet that she set a new date for this to occur. But obviously it`s primed to take place immediately if not sooner.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, then, we`ll be watching. Joyce Vance, thank you for joining us. Hopefully your dog Bella is on your lap. We thought we might see her tonight. Maybe next time. Coming up, we`re going to check back was one of the family members who was spending their nights on Capitol Hill. Why? Because she is demanding health care funding from, excuse me, health care funding for veterans when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:37:02]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART, COMEDIAN AND VETERAN ADVOCATE: These are the people that I owe a debt of gratitude to and we all owe a debt of gratitude to and it`s about time we start paying it off. So my suggestion is this. They`ve been here all night, for many nights. And when the senators start rolling back in here, I don`t know if you know this, but they`re allowed to stay open past five. They`re not a public library. They`re allowed to stay open past five.\par \par \uc2 So my suggestion to this Senate would be when you come back, if all the members aren`t here, keep the lights on. Keep the doors open. And don`t leave here tonight until you do the right thing by these folks. Simple as that. Don`t make this harder than it is. That`s it. We`re not leaving.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We`re not leaving. It is a message Senate Republicans have heard since they blocked a bill last week that would have expanded health care to millions of veterans who are exposed to toxic burn pits. For the fifth night in a row, dozens of veterans and their advocates, their families are camped out on the steps of the Capitol pushing for GOP support.\par \par \uc2 Another vote on the PACT Act could come as early as tomorrow. Let`s welcome back one of those protesters. Susan Zeier, mother-in-law of late Sergeant First Class Heath Robinson. She joins us again from the steps of the Capitol tonight.\par \par \uc2 Susan, thank you for being here. When you and I spoke Thursday night, your goal was to speak to any of those Republicans who voted against this bill and change their mind. Did you get to speak to any?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN ZEIER, MOTHER-IN-LAW OF LATE SERGEANT FIRST CLASS HEATH ROBINSON: I didn`t personally but some of the veterans organizations and Jon Stewart did have meetings today with some of the senators and we`ve gotten some positive feedback.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What is your message? I mean, we could see another vote as early as tomorrow. And what do you want them to know? What do you want them to hear?\par \par \uc2 ZEIER: I want them to know that this bill is crucial for our men and women who served in Iraq and Afghanistan and even in some other countries. It`s going to not only provide health care for them, but it`s also going to provide disability benefits and survivor benefits for the loved ones.\par \par \uc2 There have been other bills proposed that cost less money, but they basically will cover the health insurance and the health care but there`s nothing for the families once their soldier passes away.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What could a bill like this have done for your son-in-law? He was diagnosed with a rare form of lung cancer and as we all know it`s from burn pit exposure.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:07]\par \par \uc2 ZEIER: Yes. Heath was active duty when he was diagnosed. So as far as the healthcare and the, you know, financial part of it, he was good. It was after he was put on -- entered in the VA system after he was medically separated, is when everything kind of went to hell. The VA denied him a prescription medication to mitigate an excruciatingly painful side effect from his cancer treatments, because it was $2,000 a month.\par \par \uc2 So Heath was suffering and he asked my daughter and I to overdose him because the VA denied him that medication. So they were also denied caregiver benefits for my daughter to take an unpaid leave of absence from work and be compensated because he was 100 percent service connected which is important.\par \par \uc2 But he -- the VA said that he did not prove that his lung cancer was connected to burn pits so they denied him. This bill would change that because the presumption would be there. He -- The veterans would stop being denied.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Susan, I am so, so sorry for what you have gone through, for what your daughter has gone through. And here you are fighting for veterans. You have been there since last week. How long you willing to stay?\par \par \uc2 ZEIER: We`re going to stay till it passes. We`re not going anywhere like I think someone shouted when Jon Stewart was speaking we`re determined to show the senators that they need to stay here until this passes because we`re going to stay here until it passes. Every day is critical for these veterans. Cancer can`t wait. Your cancer treatment can`t wait. And every day a veteran will die from one of these toxic exposure illnesses or even suicide because they they`ve lost all hope that help is coming.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Susan Zeier fighting for the lives of American veterans, something she should not have to be doing. She`s doing it bravely. Susan, thank you so much for joining us tonight. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ZEIER: Thank you, Stephanie for having me.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When we come back, former Labor Secretary Robert Wright here on the fight to close a tax loophole that benefits the super, super rich, but Joe Manchin is climate and tax deal could do about it when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:47:40]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: A loophole that Trump, Mnuchin and \uc2 Gary Cohn\uc2  all said was going to close so what is carried interest.\par \par \uc2 Make the argument for me why carried interest should be in there because the White House just said we wanted it out. Congress wants it in?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, I can`t make that argument. I`ve advocated strongly to remove the carried interest --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then who wanted it in?\par \par \uc2 Private equity firms who seem to be keeping that beautiful loophole carried interest that`s not being taken away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: After years of talking about it, Senator Joe Manchin is reconciliation deal might finally start to address the carried interest loophole. But what is carried interest? Carried interest is a share of the profits that private equity or hedge fund managers take as compensation. It`s a performance fee. And under existing law, this money earned by these executives, a tiny group of the most highly compensated business people on earth they get taxed at a capital gains rate of just 20 percent. Why does that matter? Because that is that is about half the typical tax rate of other high income earners, which is why politicians on both sides of the aisle as well as voters who actually know what this thing is, had been demanding it gets changed for years and years.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, like every other night, I have invited any lawmaker or industry executive that supports this provision to come on this show and explain why. But like every other night over the last five years, absolutely no one said yes. But former Labor Secretary Robert Reich did and he joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, walk us through how egregious this loophole is because it`s not even like it benefits Wall Street, not Main Street. This is specifically for the richest business people in private equity, the people who make hundreds of millions of dollars a year. And until now this provision has been privately preserved while no one pays attention.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REICH, FMR. LABOR SECRETARY: That`s right, Stephanie, this is a loophole for the super rich, the richest of the rich, and there is no redeeming social value. There`s no argument, nobody can provide an argument. And that`s why \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  promised to get rid of it and denied because the industry has so much power. And that`s why even \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  promised to get rid of this loophole, but did not. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  promised.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:00]\par \par \uc2 It is there in the tax code because the private equity industry, and everybody who is relying on this carried interest loophole wants it there. It is a huge benefit financially to them.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: People campaign on it. And then once elected, they hope the general population forgets which we do, because it`s obscure, but it could bring our government over the next decade, something like $14 billion. Yet at this point, Joe Manchin, who wants this to be added to this new package is heading for a collision course against \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 . Could this thing ended up tanking the Manchin plan?\par \par \uc2 REICH: Well, I hope not. It would be a scandal if it did. And a pox on everybody involved who did not allow this to go through. I mean, \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  does like and is supportive of this loophole. I don`t know why. Could it be because she has been a big recipient of Wall Street money? Maybe. I don`t want to impugn her integrity.\par \par \uc2 But there`s no question that she has got to give way on this. 15 votes are necessary Democrats, every Democrat in the Senate is going to have to vote for this huge bill, of which a part of it is closing this loophole.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But how did Democrats and Republicans move on from this? How do they campaign in the next election if right out there in the open, there was the opportunity to close it? And \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  wouldn`t do it?\par \par \uc2 REICH: Well, I think what basically the Democrats have to do is put this bill on the Senate floor and have her vote against it, you know, just dare her say, you know, are you really going to hold up this entire bill? Are you really got to show the country that you are there protecting the richest of the rich with a loophole that nobody can justify? Well, maybe that`s what`s necessary. Maybe that kind of heat would be enough to do it.\par \par \uc2 Otherwise, I don`t know where the Democrats go, I suppose. I mean, Democrats could come up with maybe something else to fill this gap. But it is such an egregious, such a purposeless and such an obvious payoff to Wall Street and to the riches of the rich, that it`s going to be very hard to justify.\par \par \uc2 One final point. Both Democrats and Republicans have been the recipients of money from private equity and hedge funds, and from the big money behind them. And so this is a kind of an equal opportunity bribery problem.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, my quick last question, then, should Joe Manchin walk away from it, right? $14 billion is a lot of money. But in the grand scheme of things, this reconciliation bill, this package has a lot more in it, a lot of good. Should he lose this if he can`t get Sinema to budge?\par \par \uc2 REICH: Well, he may have to ultimately. Joe Manchin, though, has been standing up against this loophole for several years. Two years ago, his name, he was one of three senators who put their name on a bill to get rid of this loophole. He said last week, when he advanced this particular compromise, he said, I carried interest has got to be in here. So he`s put a lot of his credibility on the line.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Imagine your boss was getting taxed just 20 percent. Well, you are getting taxed 37 percent. That`s what this is. And nobody can explain why that`s a good idea. But if they`d like to, I`m here every night at 11. Robert, thank you so much for joining us. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 REICH: Thank you very much, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Coming up, the very good news at a Texas for a young survivor of the Uvalde school shooting when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:58:30]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, Mayah Strong. There are still so much we do not know about the Uvalde school shooting and delayed response that led to the deaths of 19 school children and two adults.\par \par \uc2 But tonight, we have some good news to share. After 66 long days in the hospital, the final shooting survivor 10-year-old Mayah Zamora has been discharged. Mayah arrived at University Health San Antonio on May 24th in critical condition. According to her family`s \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  page, she underwent numerous surgeries while at the hospital to help her recover from numerous injuries.\par \par \uc2 University Health announced Mayah`s discharged on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  saying this. Today it was a happy day at University Hospital. Our final patient from the vault a shooting 10-year-old Mayah Zamora was discharged. She passed out roses and left in style thanks to HEB grocery store. She is our hero, and we cannot wait to see all the two accomplishes in the future. Hashtag Mayah Strong. They also posted the following video of her leaving the hospital. Please watch this.\par \par \uc2 [00:00:12]\par \par \uc2 We are wishing Mayah continued success in her recovery and her trip back home. Mayah Strong is right. And on that beautiful note, I wish you all a very, very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late with us. I will see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220802ei8100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for August 1, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Charles Blow, Steve Kornacki \par \uc2 7,397 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST (on camera): Bill Russell and Nichelle Nichols, a giant on and off the court any legend on this planet and beyond. Two trailblazers who changed the landscape for Black Americans. And for that, we thank them both. And that is tonight`s "REIDOUT." ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts right now.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BLOW, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Charles Blow in for Chris Hayes. In the last half hour, President Biden just formally announced that the United States has killed the head of al-Qaeda Ayman Al Zawahiri. The terrorist was killed in a targeted CIA drone strike in Kabul, Afghanistan on Saturday night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This operation is a clear demonstration that we will, we can, and we`ll always make good on the solemn pledge. My administration will continue to visually monitor and address threats from al-Qaeda no matter where they emanate from. As commander in chief, it is my solemn responsibility to make America safe in a dangerous world.\par \par \uc2 The United States did not seek its war against terror, it came to us. We answered with the same principles and resolve that have shaped us for generation upon generation to protect the innocent, defend liberty, and we keep the light of freedom burning a beacon for the rest of the entire world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLOW: This announcement today was rather unexpected, but quite huge in significance. And to understand just how pivotal Al Zawahiri was to al- Qaeda, it is worth looking at some of his history. He was born into a prominent Egyptian family, but in the 1960s, he joined up with the \uc2 Muslim Brotherhood\uc2  and was eventually arrested as part of a plot to overthrow the government in the early 1980s. And it is the torture that he experienced in that prison that fully radicalized the young man into the terrorists leader that we know today.\par \par \uc2 He recounted being beaten, electrocuted, and even terrorized by wild dogs. And he spoke out forcefully back in 1982, about the torture he and his fellow radicals experienced.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AYMAN AL ZAWAHIRI, LEADER, AL-QAEDA: Where IS Dr. Muharib, who died as a result of negligence to his treatment? Who died as a result of three days of severe torture. Where is (INAUDIBLE)? The one who was included in this case but unfortunately, he is not here. Do you know why? Because they delivered his body to his family, because he died under torture.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Al Zawahiri was eventually released from prison and he pressed his followers to embrace more violent and brutal attacks back in Egypt, including and especially horrific massacre at the Luxor ruins, which were left more than 60 people dead including a 5-year-old girl. The young terrorist first met \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  when he was a (INAUDIBLE) fighter waging war against the Soviets in Afghanistan. The two men would quickly become close.\par \par \uc2 If you recognize Al Zawahiri, it is likely as the right-hand man always sitting next to bin Laden in photographs, as if to show the world how close he was to the head of al-Qaeda. He ingratiated himself to bin Laden becoming his closest confidant as well as his personal physician. Back in 2011, when bin Laden was killed in a U.S. raid, Al Zawahiri took over as a de facto leader of al-Qaeda, but he never achieved the notoriety of his predecessor. Al Zawahiri was where was more intellectual and less charismatic than the reviled terrorists who came before him. And he was plagued by ill health for much of his tenure in charge of the terrorist group.\par \par \uc2 For a while, it was unclear if he was still in charge, or even still alive. Nevertheless, Al Zawahiri spent a decade as the United States most wanted terrorists with about $25 million for information leading to his arrest, the largest bounty the U.S. has ever offered for a terrorist which reflects how eager the government was to get him out of power.\par \par \uc2 Helene Cooper is the Pentagon Correspondent for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and Lawrence Wright is a staff writer at The New Yorker who has written about Ayman Al Zawahiri. Both in the magazine and his book Looming Tower. They both join me now. Helene, I`m going to start with you. What do we know about this strike and how they found Al Zawahiri?\par \par \uc2 HELENE COOPER, PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Hi, Charles. Thanks for having me. So, the senior White House official briefed reporters just shortly before or President Biden spoke and told us that several bucks ago, the United States intel sources started developing an intelligence that showed that Zawahiri`s family, his wife, his daughter, and his daughter`s children, so his grandchildren had relocated from Pakistan into his house, a safe house in Kabul that one government official tells me was the house is believed to belong to the member of the \uc2 Haqqani Network\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [20:05:37]\par \par \uc2 The U.S. continued to follow this and they started developing what they call a pattern of life. They did the same thing, as you`ll recall, with \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  when they first started to suspect that he was in that house, in Pakistan. There was some -- there was some surprise that Zawahiri was now in Kabul. He had been believed to be in Pakistan. But few weeks after they found out that the wife and his daughter were there, they became increasingly certain that the al-Qaeda leader was there as well.\par \par \uc2 And they`ve been developing this intelligence since then. They said that they saw him several times on the balcony where he was eventually struck by these two Hellfire missiles. Apparently, Intel officials and senior national security officials talked to President Biden about this. And President Biden made the decision a week ago to go in, that they would attempt to get Zawahiri with -- using these two Hellfire missiles in an unmanned drone strike.\par \par \uc2 The administration says that there were no civilian casualties. That`s something that -- they often say those, so we`ll wait to see if there any - - if anything develops from there. And there`s -- right now, there`s an interesting back and forth that`s going on between the Biden administration and the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  government in Afghanistan, because the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is pretty upset about this strike. They claim that this is a violation of the U.S. terms -- the agreement to withdraw from Afghanistan. But the Biden administration says that that agreement said that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  wouldn`t let Afghanistan become a safe haven for terrorists.\par \par \uc2 And there you have Zawahiri staying right there in the capital city in a house in Kabul. So, it`s going to be something that I think will be litigated for a bit.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Lawrence, I want you to weigh in on that as well, because that`s what I was struck by. What -- how is he -- was he feeling so comfortable he would move back to the capital city that he would go out onto the balconies? Was this -- what does it say about the conditions on the ground or was he just being reckless?\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE WRIGHT, STAFF WRITER, THE NEW YORKER: As it turns out, he was. But I think that there`s a sense of security in al-Qaeda that they`ve got their training grounds back, Afghanistan is open to them, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  are their allies and to believe otherwise, would be foolish, you know, that he would feel comfortable enough to relocate in Kabul downtown. It`s quite a statement on his part, and he paid for it.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: So, Helene, Biden was calling him a leader, Bin Laden`s leader. What was his role in al-Qaeda now?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: He`s al-Qaeda leader. He was the successor to \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2 . He was Bin Laden`s number two. And when Bin Laden was killed in 2011, Zawahiri became the leader of al-Qaeda. He`s -- he was slightly more low profile than Bin Laden was, but that doesn`t mean he wasn`t lethal. And there are many, many analysts who believe that Zawahiri was more dangerous.\par \par \uc2 He was certainly involved in the planning of 9/11 as well, as you heard, President Biden talking about the embassy bombings that he -- that some intel sources have traced back to point back to Zawahiri as well. So, in the past few years, the intel community is believed that he`s been sick, that he is -- he`s 71 years old. He was not of great