a guy like Mastriano in Pennsylvania gets the Republican nomination, which he did, there`s no guarantee he`s losing in November. So, Democrats I think are playing with fire a little bit here.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: There is a dangerous game being played. Steve Kornacki, thank you so much. Steve, will be back in just a -- later -- it`s just tonight actually, breaking down all the key races in a special hour program. Don`t miss decision 2020, Countdown to the Midterms tonight at 10:00 p.m. Eastern only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 But first, new details about those missing Secret Service texts from January 6 and how the department`s watchdog stop investigators from looking further. All the details from the reporter who broke that story is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN (D-CA): I`m also concerned about the actions of the Inspector General. He sat on this stuff for months and months and months as well. And now he has ordered the Department to stop the forensic analysis of the phones which we need. We need that to happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLOW: That was January 6 Committee member \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2  voicing her concerns about the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 `s inspector general after it was revealed that he knew of the deleted Secret Service texts for months before he reported it to Congress. Now, we are learning new revelations in The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  that earlier this year, "staff at the Inspector General`s office plan to contact all DHS agencies offering to have data specialists help retrieve messages from their phones, according to two government whistleblowers who provide reports to Congress.\par \par \uc2 But later that month, the Inspector General`s office decided it was not collecting any or reviewing any agency phones, according to three people briefed on the decision. This has led to top Democrats renewing their calls for the DHS watchdog to step down in a new letter sent today by Congresswoman Carol Maloney, the head of the Oversight Committee, and Congressman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2 , the head of the Homeland Security Committee.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is one of the reporters who broke that story about the DHS Inspector General, Washington Post Investigative Reporter Carol Leonnig. She is one of the foremost experts on the Secret Service and the author of the book Zero Fail: The Rise And Fall Of The Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 So, help us understand this. It gets down into the weeds because I was trying to understand, you know, at what point was, you know, the message to preserve the call to preserve this synth, and at what point was the committee asking for things, and at what point had they already been deleted? And he, you know, he`s sent the message saying, well, we`ll no longer look for this. Because all of that, you know, the relationship between those dates is, I think, incredibly important here. And it`s getting a little muddy in the telling of this. So, explain that to us.\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEONNIG, INVESTIGATIVE REPORTER, WASHINGTON POST: Sure. And Charles, you know, I think it`s really important as a -- as a lawyer, as a prosecutor, as an investigative reporter, everybody should be thinking in terms of timelines, so it`s really smart to focus on that. Every day, we`ve had another story, another revelation. But when you put it all together in one place, it`s really shocking.\par \par \uc2 First off, the first time any congressional committee asks for texts of Secret Service agents and other national security, Homeland Security officials is January 16. And in mid-January, the Secret Service begins basically deleting texts in a reset of a series of agency phones. You know, here, we`re going to update your phones. Turn them in. And when you do that, you can choose to archive your telephone texts that are government business. But unfortunately, many, many Secret Service agents don`t do it, and this stuff starts vanishing being deleted, essentially. Auto -- a robo deletion, I guess is the best way to put it.\par \par \uc2 Then the problem with the government watchdog who`s asking for this information is he learns James Cuffari at the Department of Homeland Security Inspector General`s Office, that individual, James Cuffari and his front office, learn that the Secret Service texts and many other important \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  phone texts for the January 6 time period, right, an important moment in history.\par \par \uc2 The IG learns that that stuff is missing in December. That`s the first time he learned it, but does not mention that to Congress, or the committees that he that he reports to, or even to the Secretary of Homeland Security. He doesn`t alert the public. His staff proposes a management alert which would be, hey, this is serious and it`s something that goes to Congress and is made public. But he decides that he doesn`t want to do that and he kills that effort.\par \par \uc2 And then finally, in February of this year, one Department of Homeland Security Office offers, hey, we can`t get our texts restored from our phones because of this reset. But you know, Inspector General, if you with your cyber forensics team, if you want to go through our phones, we`ll hand them over to you. And the staff at the Inspector General`s office thinks this is great. Let`s get the phones and let`s also ask the Secret Service for their phones.\par \par \uc2 But again, James Cuffari, the Inspector General and his front office halt that plan and tell their staff no, do not take possession of the agency phones and do not ask the Secret Service or anyone else for their phones. We`re not going to try to reconstruct this evidence.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: All right, so the big question is why. I can`t figure out the why here. And also, if they could have done it then, can they not do the same thing now? Can they not take possession of the phones now and run the same forensics?\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: It`s a great question. You know, we don`t have a full answer because we don`t have subpoena power at this moment here at \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 . But I`ll tell you that the most innocent and ideal answer for the inspector general, if this were true, would be that they made a decision they didn`t think they could reconstruct them, although that would be a rather hasty decision.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know the answer of why. We only know that the staff made recommendations, and he killed those recommendations. We know that a unit of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  offered their phones, and he said no, I don`t want their phones even though they`re offering to let us try to reconstruct the texts.\par \par \uc2 I will say that I`m not a techie, but our team at the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  that really specializes in this did a great story this weekend that basically said a lot of people are suspicious who are experts in this world about the claim by the Secret Service that these texts can never be recovered. And yet, if it`s true, as the Secret Service has claimed that they blocked their phones from getting any backup, then tech experts say it is going to be very difficult to reconstruct them now.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: This mystery gets deeper and deeper every day. Carolyn, thank you so much for joining us tonight. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, the death of basketball legend Bill Russell, and the reminder that the work still has to be done next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:40:00]\par \par \uc2 BLOW: From the moment Black athletes were allowed to play in mainstream professional sports leagues in this country, they have been racial targets. From Fritz Pollard, the first black football player to play in the Rose Bowl and to coach an \uc2 NFL\uc2  team, to Jackie Robinson who broke the color barrier in baseball. And many of these courageous athletes became civil rights activists and icons in response to the way that they and other Black people were treated. Legends like Muhammad Ali, 1960s baseball star Curt Flood, and \uc2 NBA\uc2  star Bill Russell who died yesterday at age 88.\par \par \uc2 As Russell recounted it, he was forced to develop courage and tenacity as a young age when he was harassed by a group of older men in his neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL RUSSELL, NBA HALL-OF-FAMER: These five guys run by. One of them slept as he went by. When I was 9 years old where I did 9-year-olds is supposed to do, I told my mother. And she said what? She grabbed the keys to the house and grabbed me. We would all go to projects looking for those five guys. And so, she says these are the guys, I say yes, ma`am. I don`t know what she`s going to do. I thought she`s going to take care of it WITH the way she took care of it was, OK, you`re going to fight everyone of then, all five of them one at a time. Thanks, mom.\par \par \uc2 So, I go into five fights, and I lose three and win. I say, we`re going back to our place and I`m crying. And she says, don`t cry. You did what you`re supposed to do. It doesn`t matter whether you win or loss. What matter is you stood up for yourself.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Russell went on to make a name for himself as a high school basketball star before leaving the \uc2 University of San Francisco\uc2  to two \uc2 NCAA\uc2  championships and winning eight consecutive \uc2 NBA\uc2  championships with the \uc2 Boston Celtics\uc2 . But Russell, who was initially the only Black player on the team faced widespread racism from Boston fans, including the vandalism of his home.\par \par \uc2 As NBC Sports reports, "racial epithets had been spray painted on the walls. Russell`s trophies had all been smashed. The burglar is even defecated on the bed." This harassment caused Russell to withdraw from interacting with Boston fans, including a well-known refusal to sign autographs. And when the Celtics retired his jersey in 1972, Russell insists it`d be done in a private ceremony.\par \par \uc2 But his chill relationship with the New England sports fans did nothing to curb Russell`s drive for excellence. For his final three seasons as an \uc2 NBA\uc2  player, he simultaneously served as the first Black head coach in the association`s history. He remains the only player-coach to win an \uc2 NBA\uc2  title. In fact, he won two. On the night of that second victory in 1969, his final game with the Celtics, Russell let the world know just how much the team meant to him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUSSELL: I told these guys the point of the game, I don`t care what happens. I wouldn`t trade you guys for any guys in the world.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That was fairly obvious. But the leadership has to come from you, Bill. And to bring a team from fourth place during the regular season to the world`s title, the 11th title in 13 years, you got the respect of everybody in the country.\par \par \uc2 RUSSELL: Well, Jack, it`s my guys. It`s just my guys. These guys play ball for me and it`s just great bunch of guys. I just can`t say enough about the guys.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLOW: As that interview put it, Russell had the respect of everybody in the country. He wanted to fight the racial targeting that he faced with the excellence in his field. And this is true of many Black athletes. Right now we have yet another Black athlete being targeted by racial power structures, \uc2 WNBA\uc2  player Brittney Griner. She remains in prison Russia for the possession of marijuana while the U.S. negotiates a deal to secure her freedom.\par \par \uc2 But the twice impeached ex-President Donald Trump`s simply cannot help himself. Over the weekend, Trump appeared on a podcast where he launched racist attacks against Griner. He called her spoiled and overstated the amount of marijuana that she says she brought accidentally into the country. He basically said that the U.S. should not engage in a prisoner swap for her release.\par \par \uc2 Targeting Black athletes is ingrained in our reactionary system and a predictable part of Trump`s personality. These attacks are weaponized against Black athletes if they rise to the political level beyond the entertainment confines of anything other than shut up and dribble as one Fox News host put it.\par \par \uc2 There is a lengthy history of this happening from Muhammad Ali to Colin Kaepernick. And we will discuss that lengthy history and how it applies today next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 BLOW: As the country mourns the loss of \uc2 NBA\uc2  legend Bill Russell, who passed away yesterday at age 88, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is publicly taking swipes at another basketball star, a gay Black woman. On a podcast released over the weekend, the ex-president went after \uc2 WNBA\uc2  player Brittney Griner who currently faces imprisonment in Russia for possession of marijuana, which she says she accidentally brought into the country.\par \par \uc2 Trump sided with Russia in the dispute and says that the U.S. should not consider a prisoner swap for Griner, a decision that would almost certainly lead to her lengthy incarceration in a Russian prison. So, how does Griner fit in with a long line of Black athletes attacked by the political right? And why does she have to take a second job playing overseas in the first place?\par \par \uc2 Kavitha Davidson is a sports writer and co-author of Loving Sports When They Don`t Love You Back. And Etan Thomas is a former \uc2 NBA\uc2  player and author of We Matter: Athletes And Activism. And they both join me now. Kavita, just in a nutshell, just wrap up for us what the legacy of Bill Russell is both as a player, but for this discussion, as an activist.\par \par \uc2 KAVITHA DAVIDSON, CO-AUTHOR, LOVING SPORTS WHEN THEY DON`T LOVE YOU BACK: Well, as a player, you don`t really have to say very much it hasn`t already been said. He completely revolutionized the way defense was played in the \uc2 NBA\uc2 . He likes to say he turned the horizontal game vertical. And he was the best champion and the most winning a player and coach combined that we`ve ever seen. Off the field -- off the court rather, he also revolutionized and helped to be part of a -- of an era of civil rights activists who realized that their part on the court was so much more than what they could offer the game.\par \par \uc2 He was there with Muhammad Ali at the very famous Cleveland Summit. And he also refused to kind of back down. He didn`t play into an ideal of what white Americans thought that he -- that Black athletes at the time should play into. And I think it`s really important that we remember that.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:27]\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Etan, talk to us about this long history of attacks on these Black athletes especially when they rise above entertaining, right? So, as long as you`re just doing the job of entertaining, dribbling, shooting, passing, catching, tackling, you say nothing, you get beat up, you win a game, let us cheer and let us go home even if you were going home into a racial structure that disadvantages people who look like you, were OK with that.\par \par \uc2 But the moment that the person rises as a political figure, they become the subject of attack. Talk to us about that long history of that.\par \par \uc2 ETAN THOMAS, FORMER NBA PLAYER: Well, first and foremost, and honoring the legacy of Bill Russell, we have to be very honest about who supported him and who doesn`t and who didn`t. You know, we don`t want to lose ourselves in histology. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Trump supporters, the conservative right, they wouldn`t have wrapped their arms around Bill Russell in his heyday, just as they wouldn`t have supported Muhammad Ali or John Carlos and Tommie Smith or Kareem Abdul-Jabbar, or Mahmoud Abdul-Rauf, or Craig Hodges, I can keep going and going. These are the same people who don`t support the modern day athlete activists like Kaepernick and LeBron.\par \par \uc2 So, they are exactly who we thought they were. You know, they`ll actually - - you know, they`ll cast Kaepernick as being disrespectful to the veterans or, you know, for taking a knee. But they will applaud the, you know, the Senate Republicans who block a bill to help veterans exposed to toxic burns.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, again, I don`t expect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his cult following to be anything other than exactly who we know them to be.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: But Kavitha, what impact do you think that this will have, if any, on Brittany Griners case in Russia? I mean, Trump is not the person negotiating here. President Biden, is the person negotiating here. It does -- it is not inconsequential, however, that an ex-president says to the Russian government, this is a bad deal, and we shouldn`t be doing it. You know, and also it probably erodes some support that it was -- that is building for Brittany to be released in some sort of way from some corners that support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . What impact do you think this will have on her - - you know, her family`s attempts to get her out of it?\par \par \uc2 DAVIDSON: I don`t honestly think it`s going to have a hugely consequential impact on the actual negotiations going on because as you said, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not in those rooms. Now, he is also a publicity opportunists and something that we have seen with Brittney Griner, with Colin Kaepernick with LeBron James, is that he will take any opportunity, especially when a Black athlete is in the spotlight to denigrate them to score political points with his base.\par \par \uc2 And I think that that`s really what`s happening right now. You see some fans and some pundits on the right who are saying that she should be so happy that she has America behind her as an athlete who has protested police brutality in this country and it`s a very gross, very cynical message to be sending.\par \par \uc2 But Etan is exactly right. The exact people that we see today denigrating these athletes are the people 10 years ago, 20 years ago who are denigrating the athletes that we now rightfully lionize. And we are -- I`m glad that we`re having this conversation in the context of Bill Russell without whitewashing the treatment that he had. But when Henry Aaron died, whenever we celebrate Jackie Robinson, we seem to forget exactly what the contemporaneous treatments of these athletes was. And we`re seeing that today.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Etan, I`ll give my last question to you because, you know, I just want to talk about how unfair it is. I look at it, when I discuss it, I talk about how heroic it is that they became activist and they became icons because of the tough positions they took in the time that they took them. But no one else has to take those positions, right?\par \par \uc2 So there are other athletes that exist and they just get to focus on their careers. Racism consumes so much of our energy as people. Energy that we could be spinning loving our families or neighborhoods or loving our communities or practicing our sports. But these athletes, all heroes for doing it, but also there`s a toll that it takes that they have to do it. Talk to us in the last minute about what that means for Black athletes in particular.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: Well, you know, Bill Russell has pretty much, you know, laid the groundwork for athlete activists to follow. I mean, he could have easily been quiet. His life would have probably been a lot easier if he was quiet. But the fact that Bill Russell wanted to take upon his role, his platform, his position to be able to speak for all Black people, it`s why he`s so special and why we`re celebrating him today.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Kavitha Davidson and Etan Thomas, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 That is all for us tonight. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220802ei8100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for August 1, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Michael Steele, Steve Kornacki, Vaughn Hillyard,  Yamiche Alcindor, Dasha Burns, Paul Butler, Ken Dilanian, George Solis, Ali  Vitali, Vin Gupta, Lindsey Reiser, Trymaine Lee \par \uc2 8,386 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 With just 99 days and counting until the midterms, we are on the verge of a huge primary day. Five states that could provide some previously unexpected opportunities for Democrats. In less than 24 hours, polls will open in Arizona, Kansas, Michigan, Missouri, and Washington. And spread across those states, some contests that seemed to favor the Republicans a few months or maybe even weeks ago, they could now be closer than we thought. Governor`s races are increasingly looking like a bright spot. Among the questions, we hope to answer tomorrow. Will far-right candidates be able to ride Trump`s endorsement to victory or has the former president`s influence begun to fade?\par \par \uc2 For Trump picks like Arizona`s Blake Masters and Kari Lake, as well as Michigan gubernatorial front runner Tudor Dixon, all signs point to primary wins. But that could actually be a good sign for Democrats who believe far- right candidates actually help them in the general election, the midterms. Plus, we`ll be watching Trump`s strength being tested in states like Michigan and Washington where he`s trying to sink three of the House Republicans who voted to impeach him after the Capitol riot.\par \par \uc2 And finally, for the first time since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, voters will be heard on that issue. And we`ll get our first real clue as to whether abortion rights will be the rallying cry Democrats hope it is. The focus is Kansas, where voters will decide on a constitutional amendment that would essentially clear the way for the state legislature to ban abortion outright. So far, early in-person voting is up more than 200 percent from four years ago. But polling shows the vote itself will be close.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got reporters fanned out across several of the key states. Michael Steele is with me, he is a former chairman of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 , and over at the big board, MSNBC national political correspondent, Steve Kornacki. Good to see everybody. Michael, for a lot of folks, tomorrow is an opportunity for Democrats, maybe one they didn`t expect if they can see that. And I think it`s going to tell us a lot about former President Trump`s influence and power among Republicans. What are you going to be watching for?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, FORMER REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: I -- you know I`m going to be looking at those races where, you know, places like Arizona where Trump has been playing a little bit heavy and a little bit hard to see exactly how that shakes out. But I`ll be honest with you, I`m really less concerned about who`s going to be the nominee for the Republicans going into the fall. What`s going to be the real tell is how the Democrats are able to build a strategy off of whomever their Republican opponent is going to be. Because at the end of the day, the ball is in their court, they`ve got a whole service, they`ve got to be able to maintain the House and expand a majority against the odds.\par \par \uc2 So, the question is how do they then begin to pivot their narrative around these candidates, whether they`re Trump-endorsed or not going into the fall election? So I think you`re going to get a very interesting tell about how the Democrats narrate tomorrow`s events to sort of set up what they want to do going into September.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to get a sense of what we`re hearing about some of those key races from voters and we do have correspondents across the country. I want to start with NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard, who is in Phoenix. And, Vaughn, there is a number of those Trump-back candidates there. What is it looking like? Could it be a sweep for the former president?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: It could very well be. I mean, this is going to be the ultimate test here. I mean, this is going to be where voters are ultimately going to decide which of these Republicans they put in as their nominees, look at for not only Attorney General, Secretary of State, for Governor, \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  is the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  candidate who just told me last week, he would have objected the 2020 certification process.\par \par \uc2 But the Secretary of State candidate, Attorney General candidate, Governor candidate, each of these individuals who could very well win tomorrow have called for the decertification of the 2020 election in the -- despite it being 2022 sending off the Trump slate of electors back to Congress instead of Joe Biden`s. Of course, that is not possible here.\par \par \uc2 And I think, though, that it`s important when we`re talking as Michael was just laying out there, about the general election here. Kari Lake, if she were to be the gubernatorial candidate here, she is the Trump acolyte, who has called for the prosecution of 2020 elections officials, the jailing of her likely Democratic gubernatorial opponent, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , she calls the press the right-hand of the devil. All of these things, I think, are important to take into context because she`s a very viable general election candidate.\par \par \uc2 Karrin Taylor Robson, her Republican primary opponent, has utterly spent more than $18 million against her in this Republican primary. And despite that amount of spending, five times what Kari Lake has put into this, Kari Lake may very well still win this year. And that is where you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won, or I should say lost Arizona by just over 10,000 votes in 2020.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 How? Because he was able to spike the rule GOP Trump turnout, but then also win a sizable share of Republican voters here in Maricopa County. And you know, those strategists not only Republican but also Democratic strategists I talked to, they believe that Kari Lake could very well pull off a similar type of turnout here in the general election against the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So many fascinating angles there. Let`s go over to eastern Missouri, Yamiche Alcindor is there. I saw you there over the weekend, Yamiche. The big race, of course, is Roy Blunt`s Senate seat who says there`s lots of drama in that Republican primary is an understatement. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, drama, Chris, is completely a keyword to use in this race. And this race was already interesting and got a little bit more interesting in the last few hours. So we`re talking about two Erics and a Vicky being the leading candidates here, but former President Trump, his endorsement is looming larger. He has not made a decision publicly yet on who he will endorse. And just in the last few hours, he put -- posted online on Truth Social, his social media, say that he is going to make an endorsement in this Senate GOP primary sometime today. We`re trying to work our sources to see who that might be and when that might happen.\par \par \uc2 Breaking down who is actually in the race. You have Eric Schmidt, who is the Missouri Attorney General. He is one waiting for right now. He`s supposed to be in Washington, Missouri for a campaign event. So if he walks up, we`re going to try to hopefully interview him live here with you. He is someone who I`ve been told is a moderate Republican who has tapped right in order to try to get the support of former President Trump. He is someone who also came out in the last few days against \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  as Senate Majority Leader if Republicans were to take back the Senate and that, of course, echoes the language of former President Trump who has been criticizing \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  after January 6.\par \par \uc2 You also have \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2 . He is someone who is an embattled former governor of this state. He resigned in disgrace just 18 months after being inaugurated. He is someone who has the support of \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 ., but he has a number of allegations against him including domestic abuse and sexual abuse. I`m told that he has alienated himself from a lot of the Republican Party in this state.\par \par \uc2 And then you have \uc2 Vicky Hartzler\uc2 , who is -- someone who is serving right now in the \uc2 U.S. House\uc2 . She voted against certifying the 2020 election. She is loyal to former President Trump. She says what Trump has already said, he will not endorse her under any circumstances. He said that she`s just not up for the job, not up for that Senate seat. So, all eyes right now are on former President Trump at his social media accounts to see what might happen in this race.\par \par \uc2 I also want to play some sound of a voter, a Republican Woman. Listen to what you told me about the influence that President Trump is wielding in this race. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE MILLER, MISSOURI REPUBLICAN VOTER: This man is no longer like a former president to a lot of these people. He is like a god. He is something that they look up to. And to me a lot of times, maybe he isn`t the best person to look up to. That doesn`t go to say that he didn`t do a lot of good things, especially for the middle-class community here, but I think once again, people are running a little bit too far with his influence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: So, she is saying that former President Trump is having way too much influence. She said people should be thinking about community issues, not winning his endorsement. But I should also tell you, I talked to a number of Republican voters who say they really liked President Trump, they believe the 2020 election was stolen, and they really hope that he can endorse in this race, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right. I am now going to go to Kansas City or just outside in Overland Park. Dasha, you`re watching that key vote on abortion, which could tell us a lot about whether the Supreme Court decision overturning Roe will motivate people. What`s the feeling there on the ground ahead of tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Oh, Chris, behind me is one of just five abortion clinics in the entire state of Kansas. And recently, it`s become one of the few in the entire region because, since the fall of Roe, many of these surrounding states very quickly enacted all-out bans or significant restrictions to abortion. And that is why the stakes are just so high here in Kansas, as Kansans become the first in the nation to actually vote on this issue since that Supreme Court ruling. And it`s been an all-out get- out-the-vote effort on both sides here. And you`re right. This is something that the entire nation is watching on the ballot is the value than both amendments.\par \par \uc2 Now, Chris, in 2019, the Kansas Supreme Court ruled that abortion rights are protected in the state`s constitution. This amendment if the yes vote passes, if this amendment passes, those constitutional protections for abortion rights would be removed. And that could potentially pave the way to further restrictions or as activists fear potentially an all-out ban. And everyone`s going to be watching this state tomorrow, Chris because what happens here could say a lot about how engaged voters are with this issue come November. And in this conservative state, look, even if it`s very close -- even if the yes vote wins, but it`s close, that`s going to say a lot. And if the no vote wins, that would make quite a statement about the strength of the abortion rights movement ahead of the midterms, Chris.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, in that conservative state. Dasha, Yamiche, Vaughn, thank you all very much.\par \par \uc2 So, Michael, there was this article in the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  over the weekend that said there is concern within Republican circles that the party with some of these candidates is just moving too far out of sync with popular opinion. Do you think tomorrow will give us a clue? Is it a test of that?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Yes. And I think a real -- a real bellwether for that will be Arizona as was just laid out, when you look at these races around the -- around the country, and particularly those that are on tap for tomorrow, that`s going to be a real testament. You see the tension between the Trump acolytes and the party wanting to -- those in the party wanting to move beyond Trump, you see the tension between the established order or trying to position candidates to take advantage of the opportunity to win seats that they may otherwise lose this November because the Trump-back candidate is so extreme in the views of mainstream voters. So it`s going to be a real test of the push and pull within the GOP, the influence that the establishment has over, try to write the ship.\par \par \uc2 When I was national chairman, I didn`t have the same level of struggle against a tea party that was trying to, you know, take control and move the party in a particular direction. So that`s now changed over the last 10 years to one where they`re asserting absolute control, and they`ve got the president behind them really pushing that particular narrative forward.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Steve, that brings us to sort of a broader perspective, all these races, all these issues. Give us a deeper understanding of what you`re going to be looking at tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes. I mean, tomorrow is sort of broadly here looking ahead towards the midterms and the question of the climate, because I think you`re getting get an interesting bit of tension here. That`s presenting itself as we try to analyze the playing field here for November. So I`m going to show you here. This is Joe Biden`s approval rating right now, Biden, on average, under 40 percent, not a popular president at this point in his presidency.\par \par \uc2 And if you just go back here and you look at his recent predecessors at this same point during different midterm election years, I mean, look, even \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  in 2006 here, Biden`s number actually on average is a little bit lower than where Bush`s was at this point in his second midterm. And that second midterm did not work out well for \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . So we know that historically, the White House Party doesn`t tend to do well in midterm elections. We know that that`s particularly true when the president`s approval rating starts to get down to the range where Biden`s is right now.\par \par \uc2 You take a look here. Here are the results here in terms of how seats lost. All of these incumbent presidents losing seats in midterm elections, the only exception here, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , when he was popular in 2002. That`s when Republicans picked up eight seats. So Republicans have the climate -- the political climate nationally, very much going in their favor when you look at a president with an approval rating like this when you look at the economic news that`s out there.\par \par \uc2 And the question that`s emerging is A., are Republicans nominating candidates -- as you`ve been asking here are Republicans nominating candidates in key races, who simply test the limits of voters too much and end up losing races that in a climate like this, the opposition party should be able to win? And also when he`s talking about that question of abortion and the Roe decision, does that end up being something that motivates Democratic voters to get out to the polls who otherwise in the face of numbers like that for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  might not have been that motivated? So it`s a question here of whether there are factors that Democrats can help feed into, that could offset a climate that we know historically, is not good for a White House Party.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Steve, every day until the midterms, our show is going to profile a specific race every day. And today, we want to drill into a matchup in Michigan that actually pits two Democratic incumbents against each other because of redistricting. Tell us about that.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Here we go. Let`s take a trip here to the newly created, the new version of Michigan`s 11th district, \uc2 Andy Levin\uc2 , and Haley Stevens, these are both two two-term incumbent Democrats. Michigan had a pretty dramatic redrawing of its congressional districts. So they`ve been thrown together in this district. It is based here. You can see it in Oakland County.\par \par \uc2 Oakland County, just north of Detroit, a lot of kind of bedroom communities there in Oakland County, it`s an area there that used to be pretty strongly Republican. It has trended pretty dramatically away from the Republicans toward the Democrats, strongly negative reaction to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  during his presidency here. And so you`ve got Levin and you`ve got Stevens squaring off in Oakland County here. Now, Stevens actually has represented more of this district as it`s currently configured. Levin`s had a piece of it as well.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 There was a piece of polling out there that puts -- there was one piece of polling out there that showed Stevens with an advantage over Levin heading into this thing. If you want to look at it ideologically, Levin has tried to stake out the most progressive territory in this campaign. He`s got Elizabeth Warren endorsing him, he`s got \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  endorsing him, and \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  has been campaigning for him.\par \par \uc2 Stevens is somebody who had to run to get this seat back in 2018. She had to run in a -- what was then a very competitive district in Oakland County. She won by about five points against a Republican. So I would put her voting record in her posture. She`s you know pretty liberal voting record, but she`s not staked out as progressive a posture as Levin has. So there is a bit of a difference there and how they present themselves. But this is one of those when you get these in these redistricting years, two incumbents get thrown in the same district. One of these is going to be on their way to beat a former member of Congress tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Steve Kornacki, who I predict will work 46 and a half out of the next 48 hours, and Michael Steele who probably won`t be far behind, thank you both guys.\par \par \uc2 And with so much at stake as Kansas and Missouri voters head to the polls, I`m heading there to host "CHRIS JANSING REPORTS" live in Kansas City tomorrow and Wednesday, at 1 p.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC. And because Steve doesn`t have anything else to do, he returned to the big board tonight to break down all the key House, Senate, and governor`s races this midterm season. There are so many of them that are so good, 10 p.m. Eastern only on MSNBC. And as you decide who or what to vote for in the midterm primaries, our plan -- our Plan Your Vote tool is here to help you successfully cast your ballot, head to NBCNews.com/PlanYourVote now.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, dueling health challenges unfolding across the country. What more needs to be done as COVID and monkeypox cases continue to spike? And it is judgment day for the first January 6 rioter to stand trial, what his sentence could mean for future cases? And the death toll rising in Kentucky in the wake of devastating floods, the latest reporting on the ground is ahead. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The first January 6 rioter to be tried and convicted in the attack on the Capitol is being sentenced right now, Guy Reffitt, is facing by far the longest sentence so far, 15 years after being found guilty in March on all five counts related to the January 6 attack. DOJ arguing this is a case of domestic terrorism and deserves the harsher sentence. The judge today denied that addition and another for possession of a gun.\par \par \uc2 And another big January 6 development, more on the ongoing case of the Missing Secret Service text messages. A blockbuster story from \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  over the weekend revealed that the government watchdog investigating the matter initially planned to have data specialists try to retrieve those messages from their phones. But then the story says DHS Inspector General Joseph Cuffari, a Trump appointee made a different decision.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEONNIG, REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON POST: At the 12-hour, the Inspector General, James Cuffari, and his front office team halted the plan and quashed it and said we`re not taking their phones, we`re not going to try to recover their texts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: From more, let`s bring in NBC`s Ken Dilanian at the federal courthouse where the sentencing is happening, and Paul Butler, a former federal prosecutor, and MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 So, Paul, you`ve got a senior DHS official in the \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  administration, Paul Rosenzweig, telling the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  this. "It`s like we have a 9/11 attack and air traffic control wipes its records." Is that a fair analogy? And what do you make of this revelation that evidence was turned down apparently?\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yes. You know, Chris, experts have said it`s unusual for a government agency to do a migration without having a backup and plan to restore data if something goes wrong. Remember, if text messages or e-mails pertaining to government business, they don`t belong to the employee, they belong to the government, and they`re required to be preserved. Willfully destroying those records violates the Federal Records Act. It`s a crime. And every government employee learns this on day one.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So on Friday, the chair of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2 , asked the Justice Department to investigate those missing Secret Service text messages, as well as those from Trump`s Homeland Security Leadership. How significant could it be to have a DOJ investigation of an inspector general?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: You know this is huge. So I think DOJ is most likely looking at a possible violation of the Federal Records Act and depending on what the evidence reveals theft or concealment of government property or even obstruction of justice. I think the department needs to investigate why the inspector general didn`t do more to try to recover the lost text, and then why he waited months and nuts to report to Congress once they were lost.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Is there a logical explanation you can think of?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: Um, you know, Chris, in my experience as a public corruption prosecutor, you can never count out negligence or ineptitude at a big government agency. But certainly, with all of these new developments, it`s starting to look less like bureaucratic ineptitude and more like a cover- up.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Meantime, Ken, behind you, federal prosecutors have asked for that 15-year sentence for Guy Reffitt, the extremist`s Three Percenter group member. What`s the argument on both sides that they`re making for longer and shorter terms in prison?\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, MSNBC NATIONAL SECURITY & INTELLIGENCE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, they`ve asked for it but it appears they`re not going to get it because the judge has ruled that the guidelines call for a seven to a nine- year sentence in this case. And notably, she ruled against the prosecution`s attempt to add a domestic terrorism enhancement. That was the first time they had tried to do that in any of these January 6 cases. And it`s a significant defeat for the Justice Department that they didn`t get it.\par \par \uc2 They were arguing essentially, that Guy Reffitt is a different breed because he came to the Capitol that day with a handgun, not just to delay the count of electoral votes, prosecutor said, but to actually go in and seize members of Congress. He explicitly said he wanted to drag \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  out of the Capitol by her ankles, and he later was convicted of threatening his children if they informed the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . And one of his teenage sons testified against him.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 He showed no remorse after January 6, prosecutors say, and only now as he`s crying in the courtroom behind me. Well, his daughter testifies is he -- is he showing some regret for his actions? The defense said, wait a second, he didn`t assault any police officers. He didn`t even make it to the Capitol. So he deserves a sense of no more than two years. It appears that the judge is splitting the difference. But this judge has essentially said to the prosecution, we don`t -- I don`t think that this person is as different a case as you seem to be suggesting. She thinks he`s more in line with some of the other violent Capitol -- January 6 defendants, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Ken, we have a sense of why this was the first case brought by the DOJ. Is there some sort of message they were trying to send with this in particular?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: You know, it`s not entirely clear, it`s a complicated issue. There is no domestic terrorism crime. So this is only a sentencing enhancement and it only applies to certain crimes, not, for example, the crime of assaulting a police officer, which many of these January 6 defendants have been convicted of. So there are some complexities and technicalities, but there are some critics who say the Justice Department should have been trying to bring this enhancement more.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, in denying it today, the judge said that you know, because you haven`t done this in the past and you`re -- and you`re doing this now, it could create an unwarranted sentencing disparity. So it seems like Justice Department`s reluctance to use this enhancement is now coming back to bite the department, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ken Dilanian, Paul Butler, thank you, guys. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 California`s newest wildfire is now the state`s largest of the year. The McKinney Fire has burned more than 50,000 acres near California`s northern border and it`s prompted mass evacuations. Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  declared a state of emergency on Saturday with the fire zero percent contained. Officials say two people have already been killed in that Blaze.\par \par \uc2 And the death toll from those devastating Kentucky floods now stands at 30. Officials warned the number is expected to rise even more in the coming days. For more on that, let`s bring in NBC`s George Solis at Lost Creek, Kentucky which is southeast of Lexington. I know we heard from the governor this morning. He said what rescue workers are searching for, potentially hundreds of people who are still missing. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE SOLIS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that`s right, Chris. The search now expands for more people, 100 still presumed missing after these devastating floods. Behind me, you can see the river still gushing with some of that debris from some of the homes in this region. As you mentioned, 30 dead including some children, unfortunately, that number is expected to go up. Behind me, the neighborhoods are starting to ramp up those efforts to clean up in their neighborhoods. People out here all hands on deck, volunteers, members of the National Guard, volunteers from around the states helping people here who have been displaced, hundreds at this point.\par \par \uc2 We also know that people are dealing with rampant outages of utilities, more than 12,000 at this point still without water, some 25 --excuse me, 12,000 out have power, 25,000 out of water, and 44,000 under boil water advisories at this point. Some silver lining in this, is we are starting to get some cell phone service back in the region which, of course, adds to people being able to communicate with one another, which officials hope will at least lead to some reunions, some good news in this devastating tragedy here for many, many families.\par \par \uc2 The governor also announcing today that for people that want to help out people in this region, they don`t need clothes at this point, they need food, they need water, those are some of the resources that are being asked for at this time. And, of course, if you are going to come out here, be safe. Some of these waters, as you can see behind me are still moving very fast, even though they are starting to recede. Some are concerned about some weather moving into the region at this point, but at this point, it looks like the heat is going to be the major factor as the water begins to dry up.\par \par \uc2 So, again, a lot of moving parts out here, and unfortunately that death toll is expected to climb, still hundreds displaced, and even more so too hard to hear that hundreds are still missing in this devastating flood. That is the latest here, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. Let`s hope with the cell phone service getting restored, they`re able to find that some of us folks they thought were missing are actually OK. George Solis, I know you`ll keep us posted. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats closing on a big win in Congress, can Senator Schumer get the $740 billion reconciliation package floor for a vote this week, and will Senator Sinema support it? You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:33:19]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today still just one vote seems to be standing between Senate Democrats and a huge legislative win, and that vote belongs to \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 . The bill is the $740 billion package on climate change and healthcare that Democrats hope to vote on this week. But without Sinema`s support, it`s not clear if they`ll be able to.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ali Vitali joins me now from Capitol Hill. So, Ali, what do we know? I feel like I`ve asked this question before. What do we know about where Senator Sinema stands right now?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, are you having legislative deja vu, Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, I am.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Because I think many of us up here on the Hill are because we`ve frankly been here before, albeit with bill packages that were much larger in size than this negotiated package was between Manchin and Schumer. But nevertheless the outstanding question here is where the senator from Arizona will come down on it. Sinema`s office again reiterated to us last night that they don`t have a comment. Before the weekend I spoke with Senate majority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  asking him if he`d spoken to Sinema and all he would say to me was that he was giving his members the time to read the roughly 700 pages of documents and that he hoped they would be able to pass this.\par \par \uc2 Senator Manchin this morning back after a COVID diagnosis basically said the exact same thing to our reporters outside of his office also adding that he intended to talk to Sinema today, but making mention of the things that are in the bill and not in the bill that they had both voiced concerns over. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOE MANCHIN\uc2  (D-WV): She has a lot in this bill. She`s the one that negotiated basically and no one changed the Medicare negotiations. And they thought that wasn`t, and she got involved and that was great. I support that. She`s been very adamant and clear on no tax increases. I take that very seriously.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:01]\par \par \uc2 I feel the same way. So I`m hoping that when she reviews and sees it, and she`s had time to do that, then we`ll see where she is. But I`m not going to make any predictions on that at all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Probably wise there, too, Chris, because we know that Sinema is someone who likes to be able to make her own announcements when she`s ready to on this type of legislation. And she`s not given us any signals, which is not exactly surprising given she doesn`t always talk to press or rarely does so up here. But Manchin also making note there of the things that she helped to negotiate that are actually in this package.\par \par \uc2 The idea that Medicare can negotiate directly for prescription drug pricing, that`s something that Sinema was key in negotiating several months ago with other Democratic senators as part of a different piece of legislation that has now ended up in this reconciliation package. I think the only red line that we are looking at at this point is when it comes to the carried interest loophole, it`s something that`s closed in this negotiated package.\par \par \uc2 Something Sinema was opposed to, but now Manchin on the other side of it saying he`s not willing to take that out of the bill if it means keeping Sinema on board. So something is going to have to give here and we`re watching this balancing act in real time.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali Vitali, as always, thank you so much, my friend.\par \par \uc2 That controversial golf tournament funded by the Saudis and hosted by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  drew an underwhelming crowd this weekend. "The Wall Street Journal" reported light crowds were spread across much of the vast grounds and at one point scalpers were selling tickets for as little as $1. The tournament teed off amid protests from families of 9/11 victims.\par \par \uc2 Two major health challenges now plaguing the country. Coronavirus cases are up in nearly two dozen states, and long COVID is affecting millions. Plus why is it so hard to get a monkeypox vaccine in this country? Why appointments are gone the moment they`re posted, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:01]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now the U.S. is dealing with dueling health challenges. COVID and monkeypox. It`s nearly impossible to get a monkeypox vaccine right now. Demand is through the roof. DeKalb County, Georgia, hosted a mass vaccine clinic on Friday. And according to the Atlanta FOX affiliate all 200 appointments were booked within four minutes.\par \par \uc2 It`s happening at the same time COVID cases are up in 21 states. Minnesota`s case total is up more than 250 percent, while across the country there are more and more cases of long COVID. And right now President Biden is back in isolation after another positive COVID test. His doctors say it`s a rebound infection from taking Paxlovid.\par \par \uc2 To dig into all of this, NBC`s Lindsey Reiser is live at a monkeypox vaccination site in Manhattan. Also with me, Dr. Vin Gupta, a pulmonologist and MSNBC medical contributor.\par \par \uc2 So, Dr. Gupta, with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  suffering this rebound of COVID, what do we need to know about Paxlovid and is there a contraindication for taking it?\par \par \uc2 DR. VIN GUPTA, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Hi, Chris. Good afternoon. There are several -- your second question first. There are several potential contradictions to taking Paxlovid. The biggest, for your viewers, is what other medications would you be on right now. And so it`s important to have a conversation, if you test positive to identify as medically higher risk because of age or underlying conditions.\par \par \uc2 Make sure the current medications you may be on do not interact in any way with Paxlovid because that`s where we may have to have decisions on whether or not an alternative therapy might be needed and dose adjustment, for example, or if you have severe liver or kidney injury.\par \par \uc2 Chris, let me just take a step back here. The president`s case, I`m glad he`s doing well. It sounds like he`s asymptomatic. But it`s not unexpected that rebound can happen. And frankly, this is shedding a light on an important issue. Should there be variable dosing of Paxlovid based on your underlying conditions or medical risk? Maybe it`s seven days, maybe it`s 10 days, if somebody is older, has serious underlying conditions like cancer or kidney disease?\par \par \uc2 And we do this all the time, Chris, with antibiotics. Some people may need longer courses, some need shorter courses based on the condition. So we need variable dosing of Paxlovid as we move into the winter, should be in the EUA from the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , should be updated.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Meantime, I want to ask you about long COVID. NPR did a really fascinating report, disturbing actually, that millions of Americans have it. \uc2 The Brookings Institution\uc2  surveyed a data found that conservative estimates, conservative estimate, four million full-time equivalent workers are out of work because of long COVID.\par \par \uc2 So do we even know what the threat is? And if I get a mild case, is long COVID still possible? In other words, does the severity of the case I might have impact the chances that COVID symptoms will linger?\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: We`re still -- unfortunately, we`re still learning about this. It`s complicated, Chris, as you know. We`ve talked about this.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: And our data since only increasing. Having said that, I think four million is conservative, to your point. And supporting these patients is a team effort. It`s rehab physicians, it`s specialists across neurology, pulmonology, cardiology. Those specialties are already being stressed as it is before COVID-19. Here we are now wanting them to provide multi-modal care for patients with long COVID.\par \par \uc2 So we don`t have the infrastructure in place, number one. Number two, there`s still some outstanding questions. To your point, this vaccination and treatment maybe with Paxlovid, does that help de-risk or mitigate the risk of developing long COVID symptoms? Does it hasten time to resolution if you get treated with Paxlovid? We`re trying to answer that question as we speak. But those are the open questions here. Can we actually treat this with an antiviral? Those clinical trials are happening as we speak here. But deep, deep imbalance between demand for services and supply of them.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Without a doubt. So let`s move on to monkeypox because, Lindsey, the CDC now says there are monkeypox cases in 47 states. New York state, we know, declared a state of emergency, San Francisco. Vaccine appointments are incredibly difficult to get. And they`re gone in minutes.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:04]\par \par \uc2 So what are you hearing from folks there who have been in line?\par \par \uc2 LINDSEY REISER, MSNBC ANCHOR: They`re frustrated. Some people are just showing up here without appointments trying to see if they can get one. No surprise, they can`t unless something else opens up. But let`s talk about the current numbers because we did get that update today. 1,472 confirmed cases in New York City. By all estimates, it appears to be an undercount. Health officials believe that the case load is actually higher.\par \par \uc2 And this is since it first emerged in June and right now the daily case count just hit about 90 when just a month ago it was less than 30. So we know that right now it`s mostly happening in the borough of Manhattan, among men between the ages of 30 and 39. Anyone can get monkeypox. It is spread through close physical contact with people. But based on the current outbreak, we know that mostly the populations that are being affected are men who have sex with men.\par \par \uc2 We also know that about 800,000 vaccines are going to be released on the federal stockpile. About 10 percent of those are going to come to New York City, but it`s still not fast enough for people who believe that they`re high risk and they want to get one. Let`s go ahead and listen to two gentlemen who I spoke with today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REISER: Were you successful?\par \par \uc2 JOSE SALGADO, BARCELONA RESIDENT: I wasn`t. But they offered me to come in the evening. If there`s any cancellations, they will provide it for me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why it can`t be available like the COVID vaccine? That`s what they should be doing. I shouldn`t have a (INAUDIBLE) to get a vaccination just to stay alive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REISER: So that gentleman was told to call 311 because he doesn`t have a computer. And of course there are no vaccination appointments to go around, but again, the city`s Web site does say you can try if there`s a cancellation or something like that, but just to put a pin in that point that he just said, just to say stay alive, we should note that fatalities are generally rare. The WHO lists the fatality rate at 3 percent to 6 percent, and right now we`ve seen fatalities in Africa, Spain and Brazil, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And I have to say, Dr. Gupta, just in my friend group in New York City, I have heard concerns about the availability of the vaccine. It`s easy to say yes, you can come and see if there`s a cancellation, but not everybody can take off of work and spend a couple of hours hanging out seeing if they could possibly get in.\par \par \uc2 The former \uc2 FDA\uc2  commissioner \uc2 Scott Gottlieb\uc2  wrote an op-ed predicting that monkeypox could become the next public health failure. So my question for you is, are we failing? What needs to be done right now?\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: Well, Chris, there is crisis response happening now as we speak. Was there a delay? There was a delay. And I think Doctor (INAUDIBLE) articulated that. But in terms of what we can do now moving forward, more testing options here, Chris. What we do know from initial data is how you can swab your -- if you`ve been exposed, maybe even infected and pre- symptomatic, Chris, swabbing your mouth, oral saliva actually has the same level of virus as those skin lesions.\par \par \uc2 Frankly testing those skin lesions can be painful. So thinking about different sample collection types, making it easier to test is going to be really critical. Better provider awareness as Lindsey pointed out, and then frankly the reality here is that we`re not going to have enough vaccines. We`re going to maybe have 800,000 by the fall. Seven million by mid-2023. Treatment needs to become more available. We have two million doses as we speak.\par \par \uc2 Expanded access to be updated is going to make that more easy to obtain, but treatment frankly is going to be the safety net here for a lot of folks who can`t get their hands on the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Boy, Vin Gupta, Doctor, thank you for that. Lindsey Reiser, appreciate you as well.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, what is driving young black voters to the polls? And do college students trust \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as well as the Democratic Party? Trymaine Lee spoke to a group of HBCU students to find out.\par \par \uc2 That`s next on CHRIS JANSING REPORTS.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:35]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: As the midterms approach, we want to know what matters to young voters who have always been the catalyst for social change.\par \par \uc2 This fall, MSNBC`s Trymaine Lee will be hitting the road for a tour of Historically Black Colleges and Universities across the South where he will talk to this generation of young black voters about the issues that matter most to them. And he joins us now for a preview.\par \par \uc2 I`ve really been looking forward to this, Trymaine, because we hear every couple of years, oh, the youth vote, it`s so important, they`re going to vote this year. So I`m very curious what you`ve learned so far about the issues that might drive these young black voters to the polls.\par \par \uc2 TRYMAINE LEE, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Chris, I`ll tell you what, we hear a lot about this perceived disconnect between young people and the political process, even though they`re the super coveted group. But this group of young people that I talked with are laser focused on issues that affect them, like access to reproductive health care, the assault on voting rights, but also, you know, the state of partisan politics in America itself.\par \par \uc2 These are all issues that will be driving these young people to the polls or keeping some back. Let`s take a listen to some of my conversations with these guys.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEE: We`re 100 days away from the midterms. When y`all walk into that voting booth, what issues are you voting on?\par \par \uc2 MONIQUE VAZ, SPELMAN COLLEGE: Definitely housing insecurity, who is taking gun control seriously and immigration issues.\par \par \uc2 AURIEL GOODALL, CLARK ATLANTA UNIVERSITY: Reproductive issues as well as mass incarceration and food insecurity.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM MORRIS, MOREHOUSE COLLEGE: Healthcare is one of the issues I`m actually voting on this election cycle. How is healthcare going to be subsidized, kind of what is the program going forward, what`s the plan with American healthcare going forward? Are people going to be state sponsored healthcare? Are we going to be third party? If I`m going to have to look to my employer, what benefit can I get from my employer? All of these different things like repealing the Affordable Care Act.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Have the Democrats in Congress especially and President Biden done enough to address your concerns around these issues?\par \par \uc2 GOODALL: It`s hard to say that, yes, Democrats are doing everything that they can when you`re not seeing that change firsthand.\par \par \uc2 VAZ: And I think that they ran on these platforms so they need to really be acting on them. If not they will see what happens in November.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Where do you all think the party should be going, to that middle space or further to the left?\par \par \uc2 GOODALL: The middle is comfortable. It`s like your comfort spot. Like you stay there, like this is my comfort buddy, I`m going to stay here because I like it but it`s not helping. It`s not. It`s been the same for too long.\par \par \uc2 VAZ: So we need to be pushing left, trying different solutions, trying to figure out how to best advance this country for a country that`s changing every day.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:07]\par \par \uc2 MORRIS: If the party goes further left, you will have more support and more push but you might lose some of the middle voters. Then maybe those weren`t voters you needed the whole time. If people are willing to leave your voting base because you want to give people free health care, that may not -- they may not have always been as loyal as you thought.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Do you feel better about America two years into a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  administration compared to where we were, you know, four years ago?\par \par \uc2 MORRIS: I would say, I feel better. Am I over the moon? Absolutely not. But I think we are making progress.\par \par \uc2 VAZ: I think that, you know, a lot of times we disregard all the amazing things that President Biden has done because we think that it`s, you know, almost common sense like --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 VAZ: But I am looking forward to what will happen in the next two years and how like this country can continue to progress because a lot of Joe Biden`s term has just been reversing what the past president did.\par \par \uc2 MORRIS: It`s not all on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to fix everything because we have to, you know, vote at every step. So I think I definitely feel a lot better but it`s definitely all is not well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEE: Chris, these young people believe in the political process. And they also want aggressive, substantive change. Now we`ll see if at least the Democratic Party are listening to this group of very little votes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s nice to hear they at least believe in the process. Trymaine Lee, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And make sure to check out Trymaine`s podcast "Into America" wherever you got your podcast. And a quick reminder before we go, I`m headed to Kansas City to cover the upcoming vote on abortion in Kansas and the Senate primary in Missouri.\par \par \uc2 Be sure to tune in to CHRIS JANSING REPORTS tomorrow and Wednesday, 1:00 p.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for me this hour. Yasmin Vossoughian will pick up our coverage next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220802ei8100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION with \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  for July 31, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Cori Coffin, Joy Reid, Jonathan Lemire \par \uc2 7,726 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:00:00]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION. Tonight`s lead, 100 days to election. A lot is on the line. Who will take control?\par \par \uc2 Right now, America is barreling towards a milestone election, the culmination of a midterm cycle defying uncertainty, upheaval and anger, uncertainty over the economic shocks still really roaring our nation and over the Supreme`s Court decision overturning Roe versus Wade, and the political upheaval still playing out over a year after the January 6th insurrection.\par \par \uc2 The struggle is real for Democrats trying to hold on to their majorities in the Senate and in Congress, both of which hanging by a thread while the, Biden administration has to fight off back-foot three months out from midterms, trying to promote its and the parties considerable accomplishments despite the president`s dismal approval ratings, the enduring influence of President Trump and his big lie on the Republican side, hanging in the balance, the future of the congressional January 6th investigation, Republicans having vowed to dissolve it along with the Biden legacy to this point.\par \par \uc2 Of course, I care about both the big picture and details among Democrats. They must give details of, because black voters expect to see and hear the details of how their voting right will not just be protected but enhanced. How civil rights will be defended from a conservative majority Supreme Court that has signaled nothing is safe.\par \par \uc2 And as Democrats prepare to give millions of dollars to police departments through reconciliation, will communities of color finally see federal policing reform that we were promised just two years ago? A lot at stake in just 100 days will impact all of it tonight on POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get started. Joining me now, Congressman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , Democrat of New York, chair of the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee. Congressman, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 100 days, 100 days, three months and a week out from what is shaping up to be a definitively midterm election. There are primaries dog in five states this Tuesday that will go a long way in setting up the rest of the field for November. We will have complete coverage of all of the results here on MSNBC. But ahead of that, what is the DCCC`s case for why voters should help Democrats keep, if not, grow their congressional majority?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2  (D-NY): Well, thank you for that question. That is the question. And particularly for folks watching the show, they should know that Democrats aren`t perfect. But if you remember what Amanda Gorman said at the inauguration, that beautiful poem, she said, we are not broken. We are just unfinished. And we have some work to do. But we have done real work on the infrastructure bill that will rebuild our communities and repair communities that were divided by racism, removing every lead service line in America. That is a huge impact for communities of color.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about the work we did just this week in the House and that we could do next week to get a prescription drug costs down at the cap insulin at $35. What we find is that when people know the good work that we have been doing, they support it.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:03]\par \par \uc2 And they need to know what the other side has been doing as well. What the other side has done has taken away 50 years of reproductive freedom so that women won`t be able to make those choices for themselves, some politicians that the governments going to make it. That`s the MAGA Republican choice. That is terrible for our country.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, they`re doing nothing about the gun violence that has ripped apart communities, like we saw in Buffalo with the tragic shooting at the Tops Supermarket. MAGA Republicans will do nothing about that. In the House, we just passed an assault weapons bans on new sales, did that just this week. There are clear differences. We saw the other president, Trump, trying to take away peoples voting rights, trying to storm the Capitol with his supporters. We are the only ones holding people accountable for that.\par \par \uc2 So, Democrats aren`t perfect and we have got work to do on voting rights, you better believe it, on the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Voting Rights Act, and on other issues of real importance. But we are not broken. We are just unfinished.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: With such a crucial election looming and a documented decline in the president`s approval ratings among black voters, what is the DCCC doing to bring those voters back to the fall for turnout? You have to turn people on to turn them out.\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: I hear you say that and I love that expression. It`s true. I also think you`ve got to put your money where your mouth is, because when people are serious, they put it in their budget. And so what we did a year ago is we said we`re going to take $30 million, never been done before, $30 million, and we`re going to invest in our most reliable voters. And you know those are African-American voters, Hispanic voters, AAPI voters. Particularly, African-American voters, African-American women. And were going to do direct engagement in those communities with culturally competent folks from the community, and that`s what we`re doing.\par \par \uc2 And you see it as recently as this week, where we`re rolling out a new ad defining the issue for folks on choice where the MAGA Republican stand to take away your reproductive freedoms, where we stand, going to protect Roe v. Wade, but doing it in a way that is from the community and in the community, doing things like black radio, doing things like having trusted voices in the community, and doing it much, much earlier, so we don`t just to show up that election day.\par \par \uc2 We have \uc2 Nikema Williams\uc2 , that rising star from Georgia, who is running our voter protection efforts, because we have got to protect people`s vote, we are investing in that to make sure people won`t, with these new voting laws, get turned away at the polls, or if they vote, their vote will count. So, we have to put our money where our mouth is, and that is what we are doing.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, this close to November, how are congressional Democrats preparing for a potential reelection bit by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , one which could reportedly happen any day now? And would that necessarily fade the Democrats trying to keep the House for Republicans from trying to retake it. Who would you think would benefit the most if Trump were to announce before November?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Well, the Republicans are scared to death that he`s going to step all over them and announce his presidential campaign before the election. So, I don`t know who is going to help or hurt, but I know they`re afraid of it.\par \par \uc2 And what they`re really afraid of is being accountable for not just Trump but all the stuff that came with it. So, overturning Roe v. Wade, ignoring the attack on the Capitol, right, the epidemic of gun violence, particularly mass shootings, that the MAGA Republicans will do nothing about, trying to take away peoples voting rights, trying to go after the most vulnerable people in our country. That`s with the MAGA Republican movement representatives.\par \par \uc2 If Trump gets in this race right now, he is going to remind people of that. So, I think it will hurt him but he`s going to do what he thinks is best for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That`s what we know about him. What we are going to do is we`re going to make sure that people understand we are going to protect your rights, we`re going to protect your benefits, we`re going to protect your freedoms, we`re going to get you a good jobs and cheaper health care, bring your costs down, get this economy working again and build a country where everybody counts and there`s some justice and there`s accountability, and we`re going to invest in that. That`s the Democratic alternative.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, we`re out of time, but, quickly, House Democrats passed legislation Friday to ban assault weapons for the first time in 20 years. The vote was strictly along party lines. It`s not expected to pass the Senate due to Republican opposition, and it is unclear also if Senate Democrats have the votes they need at this time, regardless of that after Buffalo and Uvalde and other shootings. I would imagine that Democrats want to keep the gun issue front and center in November. Am I right about that?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Well, what we have seen is tragedy after tragedy, and in every community, right? I mean, it was African-American people just trying to do their shopping in Buffalo. It was Latino kids in Uvalde, Texas, with white kids up the road for me in Newtown, Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 Look, none of us is safe from this gun violence, and, yes, we can do things about it.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:01]\par \par \uc2 We think that future sales of AR-15s, these assault-style semiautomatic rifles are part of the problem. We`re going to do something about it. And we`ve also passed other gun legislation that will help with mental illness, red flag laws, with making sure that kids have to get real background checks and wait longer before they get weapons. We are not powerless to do so. It`s just the big money and the big gun companies that want to keep things the way they are. And the MAGA Republicans won`t do anything about it, but we will.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Thank you, Congressman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 From a federal national to a local race, but with huge implications, joining me now is North Carolina Democratic Senate Candidate Cheri Beasley, former chief justice of the North Carolina Supreme Court. Judge Beasley, very happy to have you with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 And your Republican opponent in North Carolina in this race for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , Congressman \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 , was endorsed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He objected to the certification of the election results in Joe Biden`s favor, even after the January 6th attack. And since then, he has attacked the January 6th committee.\par \par \uc2 Still, it`s been cast as a tight race. And as I just discussed with Congressman Maloney, the Senate`s makeup hangs in the balance, your race possibly determining what that balance will look like after November. Can you tell our audience what is at stake here?\par \par \uc2 CHERI BEASLSEY (D), NORTH CAROLINA SENATE CANDIDATE: Reverend Al, I`m really excited about this race. I`m excited about where we are in this race. I have served as a judge and as chief justice of the Supreme Court of North Carolina, and I`ve been in service to the people of this state for nearly 30 years, working hard to respect the rule of law and uphold the Constitution and to protect people`s rights. And my husband and I are the proud parents of twin sons who are now 21 years old. And I know how Washington has failed families here in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 And I also know that there`s a real opportunity for North Carolinians to have a change, to have someone in the Senate who is going to stand up for a strong economy and to make sure we are bringing good paying jobs to the state, and to make sure we are fighting really hard to lower costs.\par \par \uc2 And to the contrary, my opponent, \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 , is doing the very opposite. He has voted against lowering gas prices. He has voted against capping insulin. He has voted against a strong economy and so much more that is good for the state. And the reality is people are beginning to see the truth. They do want to know that the next senator is going to fight hard for North Carolina, that she will stand strong, that she will stand for what`s right and that she will call out what`s wrong.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The irony here is that we have a Democratic candidate who was the head of Supreme Court as a judge and stands by the law. You`re a law and order candidate. He is supporting insurrection. I mean, the irony is that the Democrat is the one fighting to maintain law.\par \par \uc2 But after a states appeals court`s decision, voting rights were restored to thousands of previously incarcerated felons in your state of North Carolina this week after a lower court decision in March struck down the state and on felon voting, finding it racially balanced. What does that mean in terms of the politics of your state ahead of November?\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: Well, as a judge, I can tell you that it means that the court found that those who are on probation and those who are on parole and post- supervision (ph) can now be eligible to vote. And it is important understanding what`s at stakes in this election that everyone who is eligible to vote understand the magnitude of this election, understands what`s at stake and that they exercise the opportunity to vote. And we know how important that is, and we do encourage everybody, regardless of party affiliation, who`s eligible to vote to do so.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, while we have time, Judge Beasley, yours would certainly be a historic victory should you prevail to become North Carolina`s first black and black woman senator. And right now, more black women are seeing election to Congress than ever before. According to a data from \uc2 Rutgers University\uc2 , 133 black women running for the House, a fourfold increase from 2016, in a joint 21 black women running for a Senate seat right now, yourself included.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the record number of women serving in Congress now, is it possible to point to a cause for this historic surge among black women?\par \par \uc2 [17:15:00]\par \par \uc2 And I might, before you answer, say that you made it clear, you don`t want people to vote for you because you are a black woman. But why the surge, you think, among black women running all around the country?\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: Reverend Al, representation really does matter. And it matters that the people who offer ourselves for service are representative of diversity and the talent and the experience of those who live in North Carolina. And I think as you note the increase of black people running for office, I think you also note the disparity in my race in particular around the dichotomy in this race.\par \par \uc2 And so while it`s experience and talent and diversity that`s important and, yes, I am running for all of North Carolina, it is important to note and to distinguish myself for my opponent who has been against law and order. He is the one who called the mob to storm the Capitol and attacking law enforcement officers patriots. He is the one who is voting against lowering insulin costs. He is the one who is voting against bipartisan legislation on gun safety. He is the one who is voting against the interests of the state. And it`s time for us to have someone in the Senate who`s going to stand up for what is right and stand up to lower costs and stand up for the interest of the people of the state.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Quickly, Judge Beasley, we are at a time, but if you heard my conversation earlier in the show, what would you say is Democrats` most effective message to black voters right now ahead of November?\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: It`s important that we reach out to all voters. African-American voters are certainly important. We are traveling all across the state and we are traveling to communities, diverse communities. It`s important that we talk with African-American voters and we talk with Latino voters and we talk with AAPI voters, and that we help them to see that they really do have a choice in this election.\par \par \uc2 They can choose mediocrity and they can choose someone who is not going to stand up for their values or they can to someone who shares their values, who will stand up from lowering costs in the Senate, who`s going to make sure that everybody has an opportunity to do well in North Carolina, that we are bringing good paying jobs, that we`re standing for a strong economy, that we respect each other and that I`m fighting hard to protect our constitutional rights. That is deeply important and crucial in this election cycle. And people need to know that there is a clear choice here.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Cheri Beasley, thank you for being with us this evening.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, 100 days until the midterms, I will tell you how to Rise Up and make sure your voice is heard. But, first, my colleague, Cori Coffin, with today`s other top news stories. Cori?\par \par \uc2 CORI COFFIN, MSNBC HOST: Thanks, Rev. Here`s the stories we`re watching for you at this hour. President Biden has tested positive for COVID-19 for a second straight day. That`s the latest from the White House physician, who still considers the presidents test results to be a rebound case after his bout with the virus last week.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the doctor says President Biden continues to feel fine and is continuing to observe isolation measures.\par \par \uc2 NBA Legend Bill Russell has died at the age of 88. Russell`s career with the \uc2 Boston Celtics\uc2  began in 1956 and he quickly emerged as one of the first black stars of the \uc2 NBA\uc2 . He has won eight straight titles and 11 overall for the Celtics.\par \par \uc2 We`re also learning late this afternoon about the passing of groundbreaking actress Nichelle Nichols. Nichols rose to fame playing Lieutenant Uhura in the original Star Trek series. She was one of the few black female leads on T.V. at the time. She was 89 years old.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:20:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: There are 100 days left until the midterm elections. And as I travel across the country in my capacity as host of this program, and as head National Action Network, I`m often asked by folks if their individual votes matters, or whether it`s worth taking the time to support candidates who haven`t always lived up to expectations.\par \par \uc2 I often hear these questions from black Americans. A lot of them will call my radio show who know all too well the lofty campaign promises don`t materialize after the election is over. I share many of their frustrations. Over the past two years, I would have liked to see the George Floyd Justice and Policing Act become law, or a voting rights act a bill that truly defends our democracy become law.\par \par \uc2 But let`s consider what we got this past week, a potentially historic deal on a bill that would, among other things, invest $369 billion in fighting climate change, $64 billion in strengthening and extending the Affordable Care Act, 288 billion in reforms to lower the cost of prescription drugs, all while demanding that billion dollar companies pay at least a 15 percent tax.\par \par \uc2 These would be significant achievements that absolutely would not have happened had Americans not turned out to vote in 2020, another reason to vote.\par \par \uc2 The most antidemocratic forces in this country don`t want you to. We have seen throughout the January 6th select committee hearings this summer that Trump and his supporters operated with the complete and total disregard for the record 81 million Americans who voted for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [17:25:07]\par \par \uc2 They knew they had no evidence to overturn the vote. They even admitted to themselves privately any electors they brought forward would be fake. They kept going anyway, even Republicans who didn`t specifically endorse the big lie took advantage of the situation, with 19 states passing 34 laws to restrict voting access in 2020 went, according to the Brennan Center.\par \par \uc2 So, I encourage all Americans, as the calendar counts down to Election Day, rise up and take the time to research the candidates and read up on the election rules in your state, some of them have changed. Make a plan to vote. We need what we can get and we get it if we vote. And if you can, take a friend along. There are many challenges facing this country right now but not voting won`t solve any of them.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:30:20]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: This nation and black America, in particular, lost but not one, but two of its luminaries today, pro-basketball legend Bill Russell, who led not just the integration of the \uc2 NBA\uc2 , but also one of its most dominant franchises in the 1960s, the \uc2 Boston Celtics\uc2  died earlier today at the age of 88. And more living history left us this weekend, we`re learning the son of actress and Nichelle Nichols confirming hours ago that his mother who blazed the pathway to the stars as your horror on the 1960s run of television Star Trek passed away last night, at the age of 89.\par \par \uc2 For black American in the 60s, these people were bigger than the basketball court or the silver screen, especially for black children, like myself at the time. They rest forever in power.\par \par \uc2 And joining me now is Astrophysicist Neil deGrasse Tyson and MSNBC`s own and my little sister \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  of Joy`s ReidOut.\par \par \uc2 Joy, let me go to you first. Bill Russell`s stood for civil and human rights before it was trendy. In fact, when he died it -- when he did do it, it was risky. I remember as a kid growing up in Brooklyn, when Muhammad Ali stood up against the war in Vietnam, Bill Russell was one of the few that would go near him and stand with him. And that was risky, and there was no, you know, and in that you couldn`t get likes it. He was risking his franchise. And he stood for that, he was bigger than a basketball player. Talk about people that take stands that don`t have to and would probably hurt them more than it would help them.\par \par \uc2 You know, President Obama tweeted a few moments ago, saying in part, "perhaps more than anyone else, Bill knew that it took what it took to win and what it took to lead on the court. He was the greatest champion of basketball history." Off it, he was a civil rights Trailblazer, marching with Dr. King and standing with Muhammad Ali, Magic Johnson on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , calling Russell his idol. And the greatest winner the game has ever seen. He says, "He was one of the first athletes on the front line fighting for social justice, equity, equality, and civil rights." Your thoughts, Joy?\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST, "THE REIDOUT": Well, you know, Rev., it`s always great to be with you. I have to say that, you know, as somebody who grew up when I was a little kid in the 70s, and I was both a nerd, and a tomboy. And so my two things were like sci-fi and sports. And so today, it`s hard for me, like this is -- these are two of the greatest ever, right? And Muhammad Ali is my idol. And I would have to agree with President Obama, that other than Muhammad Ali, probably the most formidable force in terms of pushing for the rights of black people in this country from his platform in sports was Bill Russell. You know, and I say this as I was a tremendous admirer of him. I got very lucky to our friend Lawrence O`Donnell introduced me to him. I think I got to meet him back in 2016. It`s quite a while ago. I went to -- I think they were honoring him in Boston. And this guy was such a kind, kind, cool guy, right?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Very gentle.\par \par \uc2 REID: So smooth, right? Just a smooth brother. And, you know, even as a diehard knick fan, you -- me and Spike Lee -- me and your friends, Mike Lee, like we`re like nick fans for life. But I was always a fan of Bill Russell, the man, even if not the Celtics.\par \par \uc2 But he as a human being other than, Ali, he pushed hard at a time when the economic risk was far higher. He wasn`t making LeBron James money, not even at scale.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 REID: This is a guy who risked his career, risked his career in an era in basketball in the 1960s. When it could have been over for him. You think about what Olympic athletes went through just sticking their hand up, putting their hand up at the Olympics. He risked all of that to say, no, I`m standing with Muhammad Ali. I`m standing for the rights of black Americans to be equal. And also standing for the rights of black athletes to be vocal. You think about what Colin Kaepernick was going through?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 REID: Think about that, doing that in the 1960s. That`s how big Bill Russell was.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And doing it in Boston, where we were still fighting cases of segregated schools. And he still did it. And it`s ironic, you know, I became, as you know, pretty friendly with Muhammad Ali, from when I was in teenager in the 70s on and Ali`s talk about Bill Russell all the time. And last time, I saw Russell ironically, he was going in the White House, going to through an affair with President Obama.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:07]\par \par \uc2 Let me let me go to you, Neil, about Nichelle because she was more than just the style of a science fiction TV show, she helped advance space exploration in real life. Back in February 2021, we talked on this show about a documentary called woman in motion that told the story of how Nichols led \uc2 NASA\uc2  recruitment efforts to bring more women and people of color in the 1970s, the dark, featured you, Neil deGrasse Tyson, and among others, tell us about her impact on that field.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON, ASTROPHYSICIST, \uc2 AMERICAN MUSEUM OF NATURAL HISTORY\uc2 : Well, just we have to back up just a little bit and realize that she wanted to be a Broadway Hofer singing and dancing as part of her professional acting career. But this opportunity came up in California to do this space, opera, you know, soap opera thing, and she didn`t know what it was. And she goes to do it. And she ends up on the deck of the Starship Enterprise, which was a hugely diverse collection of people, the likes of which you never saw on television at the time. And not only that, she had the rank of Lieutenant. And little known fact it she`s in the succession rank to be captain of the ship. Although that never happened in those episodes. The fact is to see a woman of such rank on TV at the time, she did one season and said, OK, that`s enough. Let me try to go back to do what I really want.\par \par \uc2 She was at a party in Los Angeles, and met \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 . And he says, oh, what are you doing? I`m acting and did it. And I`m going to leave. And he said, no -- he said, you can`t do that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TYSON\uc2 : And she said, why not? I`m going to follow my dream. No, we all gather around the TV to watch you, as a representative of a community of disenfranchised people out here. And it is the finest moment of our week, you cannot leave for the sake of the nation and the fate of black people. And so she stayed on the show. And she became a spokesperson for our future in space. Just as you describe, as detailed in the documentary, she helped \uc2 NASA\uc2  transition from that, oh, I have to be white with a crew cut. And I have to be male, to the rainbow of people that \uc2 NASA\uc2  ultimately put into space. She led that effort.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Actively led to have it as that dock and I did an interview on that dock as well, as that dock pointed out.\par \par \uc2 Joy, let`s get your take on Nichols` most famous role as your horror on Star Trek. Not only was she one of the first black female leads on television, but up performance was iconic. In 1968, she shared an on camera kiss with William Shatner, which is sometimes referred to as the first interracial kiss on television. Although in truth, it wasn`t even the first such kiss on the show. What was it about this character that Nichols first played over 50 years ago that resonated all the way to the present day, Joy?\par \par \uc2 REID: Well, it`s the first one I ever saw. I mean, I think, Guess Who`s Coming to Dinner might be might be in that conversation, too. But I have to say, you know, I talked about growing up as a kid in the 70s. You know, I said, I love sports. And I love like comics. I love sci-fi. And if you like -- let`s say you wanted to pretend to be like one of Charlie`s Angels, and you`re a little kid, you want to play. There`s no one for you to be like who you`re going to be if you`re a black girl, you know, living in Denver, Colorado. But if you wanted to play Star Trek, if you as a kid wanted to, like play Star Trek, there was some of you to be. Nichelle Nichols, Lieutenant Uhura was someone I could be. She was somebody who said, I could be on that Star Trek Enterprise. OK, Captain Kirk`s running around with the Green Lady and all that. But he might actually dig someone like me. I actually am somebody that is important. That is attractive. That is intelligent. That is the leader of the communications on the Starship Enterprise. I`m important.\par \par \uc2 And I -- she looked like me. This is so profound. I think a lot of people get mad about the quote, "wokeness" of Hollywood like they`re trying to put everybody, why does it have to be a span of character and a gay character? And why does it have to be every kind of care? Because when a little kid watches a show, like Star Trek, and I`m an OG Star Trek fan, like I love all the new Star Trek. Good OG Star Trek is my Star Trek. There`s someone for each of us to be. George Takei provided someone to be Nichelle Nichols provided someone to be.\par \par \uc2 This show, Gene Roddenberry had a genius. That was a genius that I only saw equally echoed in a show like Sesame Street. That said the key to making every kid love a TV show and love a concept like space and science is to give every kid someone who reflects them. Someone who lets them aspire because that someone looks at feels and sounds like them. She was one of the most important people in my life growing up as a kid loved TV and loved Star Trek.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:08]\par \par \uc2 I tried -- I went to Comic-Con in 2016, open, I would see or run into, I dressed as her, I dressed in my Uhura outfit hoping that she would be there. I absolutely love this woman. And I`m so sorry, I never got a chance to meet her. I`m jealous that you met Ali, though Rev., by the way, and -- because he`s my other idol. But I love these people. And so I`m so sad today, thought. I`m so glad they existed.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Neil, you never made a secret of the fact that you are a bit of a Trekkie. Talk about your earliest memories of the show. And what character Nichols or Lieutenant Uhura meant to you?\par \par \uc2 TYSON: Well, when you see someone like that over many seasons, echoing some of Joy`s sentiment here. It normalizes what`s possible. You know, it`s no longer -- you know, the first season. Wow. Oh, my gosh, and that`s it, yeah, of course. That`s yeah, yeah, that can happen. And when you normalize what is possible, then dreams become real, rather than these unreachable stars that people tell you to have. But you know, deep down, they`ll never happen.\par \par \uc2 And my favorite moment with Nichelle, I had her on my star talk podcast. I tweeted a link to it, if anybody is interested, I tweet at Neil Tyson. So given the interview with her, and she`s telling me about \uc2 NASA\uc2 , and she told me about \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , and we did talk about that First Interracial Kiss on television. There was some in the movies, of course. The first one to tell was, I`m pretty sure it was. And because she kissed Captain Kirk, I went, by the way, there was some spell that like forced them to kiss, OK. And --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: It had to be on the spell at that time, yeah.\par \par \uc2 TYSON\uc2 : That`s a real spell. She told us about the mail that the studio got, she got mail about that. There was some southern guy who says, I don`t like the racist getting together. But if I had to kiss a black woman, it would be you.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Wow, wow.\par \par \uc2 TYSON\uc2 : That`s crazy.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Joy, let me --\par \par \uc2 TYSON: On my show, I`ll got to tell you, on my show. She said, have you ever had a racial kiss? I said, what are you talking about? And she kissed me on the show.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: On the show? Wow.\par \par \uc2 TYSON: In the show. It was audio so you hear a little smack. But that`s my cherished moment with Nichelle Nichols.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That`s one to cherish. Joy, one of my producers pointed out that while we as news hosts and anchors are naturally going to look at the wider history of Bill Russell, and how he impacted the country, it was Russell`s impact on the game of basketball that gave him that platform, like Muhammad Ali. He was the greatest and no small part because he was the baddest. So for a kid watching this and thinking of LeBron or Stephen Curry, or any of the \uc2 NBA\uc2  stars, where does Russell fit in that evolution? What made him special, Joy, as a -- as one that grew up loving sports?\par \par \uc2 REID: Well, I mean, this is the thing is that, you know, the impact that black folks had on basketball because remember, you know, when black people were excluded, it was just the Harlem Globetrotters who would open for the all-white teams in basketball. What the Harlem Globetrotters said is that they had the theatrics, they had the swag. And so people would actually come for the pregame show, maybe not even stay for the full game, when the \uc2 NBA\uc2  finally realized, oh, we need to put these black folks on the court. They -- it raise the level of basketball. You know, Bill Russell was a tactician in terms of his athletics, but he was also an artist, he brought an artistry to the \uc2 Boston Celtics\uc2 , he had a way of going to, you know, going into paint that was just new for what people were used to seeing in 1960s era basketball, he was so dominant. And he was just so interesting to watch. And he was so theatrical, but he was also so smooth. You know, you have to put him in his era in that LeBron category in that Michael Jordan category.\par \par \uc2 You know, I`m a big Elgin Baylor fan as well. He`s another one. There with these men at that time in that era, who you have to remember, it`s not like there were a bunch of different LeBrons, there were a bunch of there had been already Michael Jordans. They were the ones who were in that era, the ones who completely changed the way that the game of basketball look, the way the \uc2 NBA\uc2  looked and felt and the way it was experience. And this was coming into the television era, they completely changed the game. Bill Russell not just changed the world in terms of his Civil Rights Advocates - - advocacy, he changed the game.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: He changed the game, which is --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TYSON\uc2 : To put in there, I just want to echo a point you`ve made. Over -- I`m going to remind people just as you did, over that time, the race tension in Boston was ready to explode, that famous Pulitzer Prize photo of this father with the American flag ready to stab parents and children that are getting off a bus from the busing rules to try to integrate the schools, that took place in Boston over that time.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:10]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And he stood up anyway a great risk. And so did Nichelle they both deserve all have our gratitude and wishing them to rest in peace and power. Thank you \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  and Neil deGrasse Tyson for being with us.\par \par \uc2 REID: Live long and prosper, Neil.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: On short notice but I wanted people that are of noteworthy to really put in perspective the historic icons that we lost today. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the origins of the big lie, how Donald Trump`s deceptions got started long before the 2020 election and how to keep them out of our politics in years to come.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: We are 100 days away from the midterm election. And while we have been talking a lot about various candidates, we know many voters are also thinking about political leaders who aren`t on the ballot. Every midterm is in part a referendum on the current president. This year, the previous president also looms large. Last week from a President Trump visited Washington D.C. for the first time since his administration ended to give a speech about his own peculiar brand of law and order. He called for drug dealers to be executed and homeless people to be rounded up and placed in encampments. The event took place less than two miles from the U.S. Capitol. 18 months ago Trump supporters fought violently would Police and nearly upended our democratic order, driven by Trump`s claim the elections have been stolen from him.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:04]\par \par \uc2 My next guest is author of a new book titled, "The Big Lie" that examines how Trump`s lifetime habit of deception is still affecting our politics.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Jonathan Lemire, White House Bureau Chief for Politico and host of MSNBC`s Way Too Early. Jonathan, thank you for being with us. As you know, you have my -- every morning I work out watching you, I make sure I`m in the gym and five in the morning and you bring me through. But your book, and I`ve read a lot of articles and hand up analysis of The Big Lie and going back into Trump background, but your book is a must read. It really is -- and I`m not saying this because we`re colleagues. It is really in a world of his own, a category of his own and really breaking down \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The book, The Big Lie, and they think of January 6 as conspiracy theories about the 2020 election. But you point out in the book that Trump has been telling almost the exact same lies throughout his time in politics. Tell us more about that.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN LEMIRE, MSNBC HOST, "WAY TOO EARLY": Well, Rev., great to see you. Thanks for the kind words about the book, I do hope our 5 a.m. program inspires you on the treadmill or wherever else you`re doing. And I`ll also note as both a Celtics fan as a Star Trek fan, certainly thinking about the two legends who passed today.\par \par \uc2 But you are right about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , first of all, his whole lifetime. He`s even as pre politics career based on lies, certainly remember his lies he told about the Central Park Five, we remember his -- how his political career was born on the back of a racist lie, birtherism in which he claimed that \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  was not more the United States and therefore not eligible to be president. And it was indeed people think of the big lies connection to 2020 and January 6, but as the book traces it started well before that. In fact, it was a rally in August of 2016, rather unremarkable one otherwise, in Columbus, Ohio, I was there. And then candidate Trump said, for the first, time that he thought the general election would be rigged, it wouldn`t be conducted fairly. And he started planting those seeds, and rallies that followed. It was amplified by some of his aides, including \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 , and I discovered an old an overlooked interview from with Stone back last that fall of 2016, in which he suggests that Trump should just claim victory, even if he loses. And then if he was denied a victory, there would be violence, really foreshadowing what came four years later.\par \par \uc2 And the book really traces how Trump, though, of course, he won in 2016, lies didn`t stop. And throughout his time in office, he told lies both big and small. And he hijacked both the Republican Party and the conservative media to go along with them, and to amplify them, and therefore his supporters were ready to act when he finally got to the big lie in 2020, when he claimed that largely do mail-in ballots that that election was rigged. And, of course, those words inspired violence carried out in his name on January 6.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yeah. Now, one of the things I`m struck by is the influence Trump has had on this current crop of Republican candidates, or one of your colleagues at Politico put out a story recently about figures such as Eric Greitans, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , and \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , who have not only embraced Trump`s ideas, but his knack for playing the victim against a supposedly corrupt system whenever a scandal arises. Is this going to be a permanent feature of American politics now? And what will that mean for the country?\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: I fear that it could be, at a minimum it will be this cycle and through 2024. And the book points out too, of course, the big lie didn`t end on January 6, it didn`t end when Trump finally left office. We`re it has shaped our politics of today. As you well know, Reverend Sharpton, Republican controlled state legislatures and nearly two dozen states across the country, use the big lie as an excuse as cover to tighten voting laws to restrict access to the ballot, largely that will disproportionately impact those people, the young, the elderly, the poor, minorities who tend to vote democratic. We know that an effort to preserve to strengthen the Federal Voting rights protections failed, we know this has challenged the Biden White House, and we know that there are a number of Republican candidates for whom the big lies a litmus test and they want -- if they want Trump`s endorsement, they back his lies and suggest that this year`s elections may not be conducted fairly.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, Jonathan Lemire, thank you again. The book is called, The Big Lie. You guys must read. Let`s go and explore what is impacted the politics as we go into the midterm.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:05]\par \par \uc2 Up next, my final thoughts, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: When I heard today of the passing of Nichelle Nichols and Bill Russell, I thought about how growing up and my preteen years in the 60s and later in the 70s to join the civil rights movement. How significant that was for stars like them in respective fields to be breakthrough people. They were the ones that helped to pioneer in basketball and in dealing with television in space, but at the same time identify the cause of fighting racism and inequality, long before it became trendy.\par \par \uc2 When we as people look up to people today, some of whom are making crazy money, and sometimes may tweet something irrelevant. They are the fruits, but the fruits would not be there if it weren`t for the roots. And the roots of doing that are people like Nichelle Nichols and Bill Russell`s? Don`t discard the roots and just enjoy the fruits. Had there been no roots, there would have been no branches. And there would have been no fruits for us to enjoy today.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching. And we will be back with "AMERICAN VOICES" with Alicia Menendez right after this.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220801ei7v00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word with Lawrence O`Donnell for July 29, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ayman Mohyeldin, Charles Blow \par \uc2 7,426 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:00]\par \par \uc2 AYMAN MOHYELDIN, MSNBC HOST: It`s been a momentous week for the effort to bring about accountability for the January 6th capitol riot, and let me walk you through it, and then we`re going to dive into today`s developments and where we might expect the investigation to go next.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, we learned that Marc Short and Greg Jacob, two of former Vice President Mike Pence`s top aides appeared before a federal grand jury last week. The very next day, "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " broke the story of the summer when they reported that the Justice Department is investigating Donald Trump`s actions in a criminal probe.\par \par \uc2 According to "The Post`s" reporting, investigators are honing in on Trump`s "pressure campaign on \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  to overturn the election and what instructions Trump gave his lawyers and advisers about fake electors and sending electors back to the states."\par \par \uc2 Now, on Wednesday, Cassidy Hutchinson, the January 6th committee`s super star witness was the next to name -- whose name was to come out as having cooperated with the DOJ investigators. Then late last night. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " dropping another bombshell. Text messages during the lead up to January 6th from Donald Trump`s Acting Homeland Security Secretary \uc2 Chad Wolf\uc2  and Acting Deputy Secretary Ken Cuccinelli are missing and unlikely to bet recovered.\par \par \uc2 Now, the missing texts were reported to the DHS inspector general all the way back in February. But according to "The Post" the Office of Inspector General Joseph Cuffari did not press the department leadership at the time to explain why they did not preserve those records nor seek ways to recover the lost data, according to the four people briefed on the watchdog`s actions.\par \par \uc2 Cuffari also failed to alert Congress to the potential destruction of government records. Now, Inspector General Cuffari had already faced calls to step aside from the criminal investigation into the missing Secret Service text messages. And now there are growing calls for Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  and the Justice Department to launch an investigation into that situation.\par \par \uc2 Here is what my first guest, Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 , a member of the January 6th Committee said today about Wolf and Cuccinelli`s texts going missing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): I just don`t know why everybody`s texts and e- mails were suddenly disappearing all over the place. So, I assume it`s not just a technological problem, but we`ll get to the bottom of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Today, House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy became the latest member of team Trump to discredit former White House aide Cassidy Hutchinson`s sworn testimony to the January 6th committee. Here`s what she told the committee about a phone call she received from McCarthy on January the 6th after Trump finished his speech at the ellipse.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER AIDE TO WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF \uc2 MARK MEADOWS\uc2 : He then explained the president just said he`s marching to the capitol. You told me this whole week you weren`t coming up here. Why would you lie to me? I, said I`m not lying. I wasn`t lying to you sir. I -- we`re not going to the capitol. And he, said, well he just said it onstage, Cassidy. Figure it out. Don`t come up here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Now, a full month after Hutchinson`s under oath testimony, McCarthy says he doesn`t remember any of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): If I talked to her, I don`t remember it. If it was coming up here, I don`t think I wanted a lot of people coming up to the capital, but I don`t remember the conversation.\par \par \uc2 MANU RAJU, CNN CHIEF CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Why were you concerned specifically about Trump coming to the capital?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: I don`t remember that.\par \par \uc2 RAJU: You don`t remember being concerned about his comments?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: No. No. But because I didn`t watch it.\par \par \uc2 RAJU: She said under oath that you told her throughout the course of the week or she told, reassured you through the course of the week that he was not going to come to the capital. So apparently --\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: I don`t -- I don`t remember having any conversations with her about coming to the cap -- the president coming to the capitol. I just, I don`t recall any of that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Joining us now is Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 , a Democrat from Maryland. He is of course a member of the House Select Committee investigating the January 6th attack on the capitol and served as the lead impeachment manager in the second impeachment of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congressman Raskin, thank you so much for joining us. I appreciate you staying up late for us this evening. Let me start with what we just heard there if I can, sir. What do you make of Leader McCarthy saying that he doesn`t remember having a phone conversation with Cassidy Hutchinson? If he wants to refute what she told you, shouldn`t he do it under oath?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, the first thing I want to say is I trust Cassidy Hutchinson who has proven to be right about just everything, who has no incentive to lie, no penchant for lying, no record of lying and no motive to lie.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:00]\par \par \uc2 And what she told us conforms to lots of other details of what we`ve learned. To me it defies the belief that Kevin McCarthy doesn`t remember anything about his feelings about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not coming to capitol. He said, well, he didn`t want a lot of people coming so in other words he was grouping \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in with everybody else, with a big group of people that he didn`t want come to the capitol. So, I think you have to suspend disbelief a little bit to fall for that.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Speaking of suspending belief, we learned last night that text messages from Trump`s acting Homeland Security Secretary \uc2 Chad Wolf\uc2  and Acting Deputy Secretary Ken Cuccinelli are now missing. Wolf and Cuccinelli both say that DHS deleted their text messages after they turned in their phones when they left the department.\par \par \uc2 Has the committee spoken to either Wolf or Cuccinelli? And what can you tell us about efforts to recover these whole host of DHS texts that have been missing in addition to the Secret Service messages that are also missing?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, we`re determined to retrieve all missing texts and to assemble all of the evidence that is technologically possible to access and we are planning to do that. Anybody who has tried to defy the legal authority of the committee by concealing evidence has been overcome by other people coming forward to help us get that evidence.\par \par \uc2 So, I continue to have confidence that we will, indeed, be able to find it. What`s troubling to me is that the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  is supposed to be keeping a close eye on domestic violent extremist groups and domestic terrorist plots.\par \par \uc2 And so, my first thought when I heard that these texts at the top of the department went missing was that we were going to lose a lot of evidence that we would otherwise find about what the department was gathering in terms of information about the domestic violent extremist groups and their attempts to storm the capital, smash our windows, assault our officers, drive the House and the Senate out, go after the vice president, hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , and so on.\par \par \uc2 But of course, it`s also possible that other things could surface on those texts. I mean, if anybody in the department knew about various plots in advance, that is something that also could be missing today on those texts.\par \par \uc2 So, we really need to get to the bottom of that because we want to make sure that the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  and its leaders were doing their job.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Let me see if I can play for you and our viewers what the former acting chief of staff to President Trump, \uc2 Mick Mulvaney\uc2 , said. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICK MULVANEY\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP WHITE HOUSE ACTING CHIEF OF STAFF: The one thing I guess that was missing, John, was that if you go to a deposition, there`s going to be another side and there was no other side. They were all questions by the committee designed to try and find out stuff that Mike made President Trump look bad. No one there asking the other side of the questions that might make President Trump look good, but that`s fine. So, it just reaffirms in my mind that the committee is politically biased.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: What can you tell us about the committee interview with \uc2 Mick Mulvaney\uc2 ? I mean, what kind of questions do you ask about somebody who incited a riot and tried to overthrow our government to stay in power to make him look good?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, let me first preface it by saying I`m not going to response any detail because I wasn`t even there, I was in the other committee meetings at the time that that interview took place. Nobody is trying to make anybody look bad or look good. We are just trying to get to the truth of what happened.\par \par \uc2 And I have to assume that that`s some kind of preemptive pandering to the former president. But, in any event, we are a bipartisan committee that does not have a political objective at all. Our mandate is to discover the facts of what took place on January the 6th and the causes behind it and what we need to do to prevent coups and insurrections against our democracy going forward.\par \par \uc2 And of course, the vast majority of our witnesses have been Republicans, and the people that we`ve put up there for America to see have been Republicans like Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , a lifelong Republican and big Trump donor, who told the truth about what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did in trying to get him just to find 11,780 remaining votes.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:03]\par \par \uc2 And the same with Arizona Republican House Speaker \uc2 Rusty Bowers\uc2  and Cassidy Hutchinson who is a Republican who served as the special assistant to Donald Trump`s own chief of staff. It`s amazing to me the way that they turn on their own people and try to, you know, expel them from the party and discredit their Republican bona fides. It`s just amazing to me to see that happen.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Congressman Raskin, please stay with me for a moment. I want to ask you and get your thoughts on a few other important things. One of the main ways \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his allies try to overturn the 2020 election was by exploiting the Electoral College with their so-called fake elector scheme.\par \par \uc2 Those are not my words for it, by the way. E-mails uncovered by "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " this week show that even Trump`s team referred to the electors as fake and acknowledged that that plan wasn`t legal. Next week, the Senate is expected to hold a hearing on a bipartisan bill to reform the 135-year- old Electoral Count Act, and that would make it harder to overturn a presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Joining our conversation now is Democratic Congressman \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2  of Texas. He is a former voting rights attorney. Representative Allred, it`s great to see you again. Thanks for joining us. I`m glad we have you back on this because when you and I spoke on Monday, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " had not yet released their reporting on Trump`s legal team who is trying to implement the fake elector scheme.\par \par \uc2 In an e-mail obtained by "The Times," one of the attorneys actually wrote, quote, "basically that all of us, Georgia, Wisconsin, Arizona, Pennsylvania, et cetera, have our electors send in their votes even though the votes aren`t illegal under federal law because they`re not signed by the governor. So that members of Congress can fight about whether they should -- can fight about, rather, excuse me -- whether they should be counted on January 6th."\par \par \uc2 Representative Allred, does that e-mail just go to the brazenness of Trump and his team and why reforming the ECA is so important at this point?\par \par \uc2 REP. COLI ALLRED (D-TX): Well, thanks for having me on, Ayman. I`m so glad to be here with my dear friend, \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 . And, you know, of course, the plan here was to create chaos and to try and use whatever method they could to muddy the waters enough to try and pull off what they knew was against the law.\par \par \uc2 And of course, the Electoral Count Act has been much discussed, does need to be worked on. And the drafting of it, the original drafting is far too vague. But there certainly some things as we`re going through that process, you know, looking what the Senate is doing, that we have to make sure that we get right.\par \par \uc2 Because every step we take to counter attempts to overthrow legitimate electors, will also make it harder for us in some cases to counter when we may get, for example, a false slate from a state, which Jamie and I have discussed before. We know we have to be prepared for seeing in 2024.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Congressman Raskin, you called the bipartisan senate bill to update the ECA, quote, "fine and necessary, but not remotely sufficient." Picking up on what Congressman Allred was saying there, what specific areas would you like to see improve in the bipartisan plan?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, look, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his team did not set out to over throw the electoral count act, they set out to overthrow the election at the local level, the state level, the federal level, and they set out to seize the presidency.\par \par \uc2 So, if we tweaked the Electoral Count Act to clarify, for example, that the vice president does not have unilateral power to reject Electoral College votes from the states, that will be very minimal progress indeed given that nobody ever thought the vice president have that authority, and nobody thinks that Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  would proclaim such authority.\par \par \uc2 So, we don`t want to get stuck fighting the last battle when really what we know is that the entire institutions of electoral democracy are under attack. And we see that with voters` suppression. And we see that with the gerrymandering of our districts. We see that with attempts to intimidate election officials.\par \par \uc2 So, we need a much broader analysis of Donald Trump`s entire plot and then also other weaknesses and vulnerabilities that strategic bad faith actors will try to exploit.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Representative Allred, let me read for you this critique of the proposed reforms to the ECA from "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist Jason Willick.\par \par \uc2 He writes in part, "The reform bill would cut elected politicians out of the process, transferring most of the power to courts, and especially the Supreme Court. That`s right. To protect democracy, ECA reform would shrink the authority of the democratic branches of government and expand the authority of the least democratic branch. The bill deserves to pass. But the fact that it`s necessary shows that America self-governing capacities have diminished and there will be more trouble ahead."\par \par \uc2 [22:15:04]\par \par \uc2 Your thoughts on that characterization, sir.\par \par \uc2 ALLRED: Yes, I think when we last spoke about this, I mentioned I had some concerns with the judicial review provisions that are being put forward, both from the three-judge panel that this is calling for. Just so you know, Ayman, now currently, it would go to a single district court judge and then would be appealed to the normal appeals process.\par \par \uc2 The ECA reform that`s being proposed would call for a three-judge panel to be empaneled and then if appealed, the Supreme Court to hear that appeal on its merits as a mandatory thing instead of being a discretionary thing from the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 So, if you can imagine what happened in the last election, when the Trump legal team brought dozens of, you know, sham lawsuits, all of those would have to have been heard by their merits by the Supreme Court if they appealed. And of course, that would slow things down. But also, it is true, it would take more of the discretion away from the legislative branch and put it into the judiciary.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s definitely a concern. I think we have to really think about very seriously how we`re going to do the judicial review portion of this because this reform is putting a lot of power in the hands of governors and then a lot of power in the hands of the courts in the review process and very little hands -- power in the hands of Congress.\par \par \uc2 And I`m sure that is by design because there is some concern over what Congress would do. But every action you take has a reaction, as Jaime and I have discussed. And when you gain this out, it`s very difficult to craft legislation to stop, basically, one party from trying to overthrow an election.\par \par \uc2 This is why you should -- we don`t have -- we`ve never faced this in this country before where we`ve had to try and plan out, you know, legislation for how are we going to try and deal with an organized and broad attempt to overthrow an election.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes, and sadly, I feel like there will be another attempt so long as the Republican Party continues on its ways from what they have shown us so far. Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  and \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2 , thank you gentlemen, both, for joining us tonight. I greatly appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the people of Kansas will be the first to have their say on whether there should be a right to abortion services in Kansas in the wake of the Supreme Court decision overturning Roe versus Wade. Kansas Democrat \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2  joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:20:00]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Kansas is set to vote Tuesday on a constitutional amendment that will impact abortion access in that state. Currently, the Kansas constitution protects the right to bodily autonomy, which includes the right to an abortion. If these new amendment passes it would strip abortion rights from the state constitution and open the door for the state legislature, currently under Republican control, to further restrict or even ban abortion outright.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday`s vote is the first time since the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade that voters anywhere in the country will cast ballots on abortion. The ballot measure was planned before the Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe. In fact, groups on both sides have poured millions of dollars into advertising.\par \par \uc2 NBC News reports that before the Supreme Court decision, outside groups spent a combined $1.6 million on ads. Since then, spending has more than quadrupled with groups shelling out nearly $7.8 million on ads through August 2nd. Here is one ad from the Kansans for Constitutional Freedom Pact.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Abortion is already highly regulated in Kansas. Here are the facts. Taxpayer funding for abortion, outlawed. Abortion after viability, banned. Parental consent, required. But you should also know this. This confusing constitutional mandate amendment could lead to a full ban of any abortion in Kansas, with no exceptions for rape, incest or a mother`s life. That`s extreme and goes too far. Vote no. No unchanging the constitution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Alright, joining us now is Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2  from Kansas. Congresswoman, thank you so much for joining us this evening. So, you know, midwestern states have been especially impacted by the Supreme Court decision overturning Roe. Kansas is literally, and we have a map there on our screen for our viewers -- is literally the middle of the country.\par \par \uc2 And as a result, it`s going to have an impact on neighboring states not having any access to abortion. How will abortion access in Kansas and in the region be impacted if this constitutional amendment passes?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SHARICE DAVIDS\uc2  (D-KS): Well, it`s good to be here to talk to you about this because this is such an important topic and issue, especially given the Dobbs decision and the level of fear and anxiety and anger that folks are feeling right now.\par \par \uc2 If these amendments were to pass in Kansas, just like you said, right now, in the state of Kansas, we have a constitutional right under our state constitution to access the full range of reproductive health care services, including an abortion. And the -- right now we are seeing -- we`re seeing one of these extreme attempts to change our constitution.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve watched for months as neighboring states have enacted or attempted to enact total bans.\par \par \uc2 [22:24:57]\par \par \uc2 And you know, Kansans don`t want politicians making their personal health care decisions for them. And I think that that`s what -- that`s what we`re seeing right now, is a lot of folks showing up to make their voices heard on an issue of great importance.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: There was a recent poll, congresswoman, by a Kansas City-based polling firm that found 47 percent of people said they plan to vote yes to remove the constitutional protection to abortion, 43 percent said they plan to vote no. There were 10 percent that were undecided. Does that, you know, seem to reflect the mood of the Kansans you speak to and what would you say to the 10 percent who are not sure how they will vote?\par \par \uc2 DAVIDS: Well, I think that`s -- of course, polling is something that folks look to as a way to try to figure out what people are feeling, boots on the ground, what`s happening. And I can tell you in my conversations with folks at home, first of all, there are a lot of people who are -- I just, I have to keep coming back to this because so many people are, truly, their lives have been upended by this.\par \par \uc2 In fact, I was talking to a doctor who is an emergency room doctor who lives in Kansas but works in Kansas City, Missouri. And Missouri is one of those states that Kansas has watched past a very extreme set of restrictions and essentially a total ban. And they are not able or aren`t sure if they can prescribe the regular course of treatment when someone comes into the emergency room who has been assaulted.\par \par \uc2 And I think that those are exactly the extreme types of situations, and frankly, dangerous situations that we do not want to see that in Kansas. We don`t want to see politicians deciding when doctors can and can`t treat a patient. Those decisions need to be made between a patient and their physician.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes. It is going to be a close one and one that the country will be watching very closely come August 2nd. Congresswoman \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2 , thank you so much for your insights tonight. We`ll be checking back in back with you later in the week.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, a Texas jury is deciding how much Alex Jones should pay for repeatedly lying about the children who were murdered at Sandy Hook Elementary School back in 2014. Elizabeth Williamson who documented the horrors those lies wrought on the families of those children has been in the court room. She will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:32:15]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right. So the trial of right wing conspiracy peddler Alex Jones began this week in a case that will decide how much he must pay the family of a child killed in the Sandy Hook massacre.\par \par \uc2 For years, Alex Jones used his show "Infowars" to attack grieving parents by telling his audience that the Sandy Hook massacre was a hoax. Mark Bankston, the lawyer for the parents of six-year-old Jesse Lewis one of the 20 children and six adults killed in the Sandy Hook massacre asked the jury to award their clients $150 million. The "Austin American Statesman" reports that`s $75 million for damaging the parents` reputation and another $75 million for emotional distress.\par \par \uc2 Bankston said the 475 million figure represents $1 for every American who was duped by Jones` lies, according to a poll that found 24 percent of Americans believe the Sandy Hook shooting was staged or might have been staged.\par \par \uc2 It`s one of the three jury trials Alex Jones will face to determine how much he owes the families of the Sandy Hook victims in damages, and the first witness to testify was the lead investigator on the Sandy Hook shooting, retired Connecticut state police detective Daniel Jewiss.\par \par \uc2 He told the jury that law enforcement put the Sandy Hook deniers into three categories -- the mentally ill, those who believed false reports, and a third group.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANIEL JEWISS, LEAD INVESTIGATOR OF SANDY HOOK SHOOTING: The third group we believe were ones that absolutely knew that it was true, however, they were still going to use that opportunity to spin it for their own benefit, whether it was power or money. They were the most dangerous. And that`s where we put Alex Jones.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Joining us now is Elizabeth Williamson, a feature writer for the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ". She`s the author of "Sandy Hook: An American Tragedy and the Battle for Truth". It`s great to have you with us, Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 Alex Jones wasn`t in the courtroom when retired Connecticut state police detective Daniel Jewiss took the stand. But he was there when the lawyers for the parents of Jesse Lewis told the jury they were seeking $150 million in damages.\par \par \uc2 What was his reaction to that number? And can you put that number in context for us.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WILLIAMSON, FEATURE WRITER, "NEW YORK TIMES": Absolutely. So, he was really shaken by that number. You can see him sort of visibly blanche and he kind of shrugged. I was looking at the back of his head, I saw his neck turn red. And then he sort of charged out of the courtroom at the first break and started holding court in the hallway complaining that this was a kangaroo court, and that this was a show trial which is something he has been saying for years.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:56]\par \par \uc2 But the context that you asked me for is this. This is -- this represents, even if the jury were to award the full judgment, it would represent three years of his revenues of $50 million a year. And actually, that`s conservative, because it was much more than that some years.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Tell us about the harm that Alex Jones` lies have brought upon these families who have already suffered the worst fates imaginable for anybody to experience in this country.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMSON: Sure, so what happened was, and there was testimony today as to Alex Jones` reach and influence. He is by far the most influential amplifier of these conspiracy claims. And he has been that way since hours after the shooting.\par \par \uc2 So what has happened is that people who believed him, and it doesn`t take that many because he has an audience of tens of millions of people. So those who really believed that the Sandy Hook shooting was a hoax, a pretext for gun control, and that the families were complicit in this so- called government plot, people came up to them.\par \par \uc2 They dug through their trash. They looked in their windows. they confronted them on the street. They harassed them on line. They defaced and stole memorials to their lost loved ones.\par \par \uc2 So they made their lives a living hell. And so finally by 2018, the families had had enough and the families of ten Sandy Hook victims sued Alex Jones, in four separate lawsuits now combined into three. He lost all of those.\par \par \uc2 Whether or not he defamed them has already been decided. He did. He lost these cases by default, and now there are three. This is the first of three trials, exclusively for damages, in which juries will decide how much Alex Jones must pay these families for what happened to them after he spread these lies.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Elizabeth, we are out of time, but do you expect Alex Jones will take the stand before the trial ends?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMSON: Unclear right now.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right. Elizabeth Williamson, thank you so much for your coverage and for joining us tonight. Greatly appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Charles Blow, columnist from the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " and an MSNBC political analyst. Charles, it`s great to have you with us this evening.\par \par \uc2 So this cast of characters from Trump world, you know, they believe that they can act with impunity. We`ve seen that time and time again, no matter what destruction they cause.\par \par \uc2 Alex Jones is finally facing accountability. Do you see this trickling down to the rest of Trump world?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BLOW, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: I should hope so. I mean what is happening with Jones and some other actors is that they see an opportunity for a tremendous amount of cash to be made by peddling these lies. You have to understand the psychology around why that works, which is that when you`re confronted -- well some people are confronted with a set of facts that they do not believe, believe in or don`t want to believe in, that make them uncomfortable, that make them afraid, the country is moving away from, there are criminals coming and you need guns -- whatever the set of facts is, it makes them open to an alternative set of facts because they want to believe in something that is counter to the set of fact that they have in front of them.\par \par \uc2 And so these people become open to this idea of, you know, conspiracy theories and lies. And there are some people, maleficent people who are smart enough to know that they can make a lot of money selling those lies to those people who desperately want to believe something other than a set of facts which is primarily about a country moving away from them in power, prestige and culture.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Charles, Alex Jones calls him and a performance artist. The truth is, he speaks to millions of people who hear his message. And Sebastian Murdoch of HuffPost actually caught up with Alex Jones outside the courthouse yesterday.\par \par \uc2 I want to play you this and for our viewers. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEX JONES, INFOWARS: The corporate media is on trial and so is the rigged judiciary here.\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MURDOCH, HUFFPOST: But you, literally today, are on trial. Just to clarify.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You`re on trial.\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: I`m not.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You`re on trial.\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: Ok.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Talk about the damage that Alex Jones` brand of disinformation can do to the American people, and the people who hear him. I mean as we just played in that clip right there, he`s in denial.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:42]\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Right. Well, you know, I want to take the liberty here of expanding a little bit beyond Alex Jones to say that much of the right -- Alex Jones maybe an extreme of it -- but much of the right has become a movement about, you know, collateral damage, right. That they accept tremendous collateral damage as the cost of doing business. Alex Jones may accept that collateral damage in harming these families tremendously in order to make a buck.\par \par \uc2 But there are other ways that the Republican Party has become the party of collateral damage. They will say we will take away all abortion rights, and the little girl who struggles to get -- to deal with a rape, a child -- I mean, a birth -- a baby that has been conceived by a rape -- that is just collateral damage. We will see mass shootings like Sandy Hook, but also shootings elsewhere, but they will say in order for us to protect gun ownership and the proliferation of guns, we will accept that as collateral damage.\par \par \uc2 And on and on and on. And so they kind of build in all of this pain and all of the suffering as collateral damage. This is just going to be the cost of doing business in our movement.\par \par \uc2 And that is what, to me, is most dangerous, about what Alex Jones and the rest of the Republican Party is doing.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: So how important is it that, you know, people like him face responsibility for this kind of rhetoric? I mean what do you make of those that say this is just simply free speech argument his supporters used to give him a blank check to say whatever he wants to say.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: I think that this is really important because I think we have to stop people from using free speech to excuse being held accountable for lying, right. And so every time one of these liars gets caught in their lies, and then gets held accountable for the lies, they say oh no, this is an attack on free speech. Right.\par \par \uc2 Trump, says I should be able to say whatever I want, and if they try to moderate it, that`s an attack on free speech. Alex Jones says I can say whatever I want to say about the Sandy Hook families and their dead children, and if you say anything about that, that`s an attack on free speech.\par \par \uc2 No it`s not. You are free to say whatever you want in this world, but some of that is defamatory and that is against the law. And if you defame someone, you have to pay for that.\par \par \uc2 And I that think drawing the line here and saying this is not just a back and forth volley in the media where one person says one thing and there`s another side of it. No there are actual laws where some things you say you have to be held accountable for those things.\par \par \uc2 If you defame someone, you must be held accountable for that. And if the justice system is about to do that to Alex Jones, more power to it.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Charles Blow, greatly appreciate your time this evening. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Coming up, some Pennsylvania Republicans are striking back against the anti-democratic actions and anti-Semitic allies of \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 . One of those Republicans who is actively working to elect Democrat \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:42:57]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: This week, the Associated Press reports that some Republicans are warming up to supporting Republican nominees for governor of Pennsylvania state senator \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  writing, quote, "As the general election season intensifies, the GOP machinery is cranking up to back Mastriano`s campaign."\par \par \uc2 That`s despite the heartburn that \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  has caused Pennsylvania Republicans because of his extremist views. His embrace of right wing fringe groups, his election lies.\par \par \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  was at the Capitol on January the 6th. He has consistently push Donald Trump`s election lies, and has worked to overturn Pennsylvania`s own election results back in 2020. He was also the point person designated by Donald Trump`s lawyers to lead the fake electors scheme in Pennsylvania, that they knew was illegal.\par \par \uc2 As the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " detailed, quote, Mr. Mastriano needed assurance to go along with a plan other Republicans were telling him was illegal," according to a December 12th email, that also referred to Mr. Giuliani, the former mayor of New York City. Mastriano needs a call from the mayor. This needs to be done. Talk to him about legalities of what they are doing. Electors want to be reassured that the process is legal, essential for greater strategy."\par \par \uc2 Our next guest, Craig Snyder, joins a group of Republican though supporting \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , the Democratic nominee for governor. Craig Snyder who served as chief of staff to Senator Arlen Specter and a group of centrist Republicans have formed the Republicans for Shapiro PAC in opposition to \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If election denier Mastriano is elected governor of Pennsylvania, he could appoint a secretary of state who will overturn the election outcome in Pennsylvania if a Democrat wins the presidential election there.\par \par \uc2 And the Republican-controlled Congress could accept the fake electors sent by an election criminal operating as Pennsylvania`s governor. That is what is at stake in that state.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Craig Snyder, political director of Republicans for Shapiro. As I mentioned earlier, he served as chief of staff to former Republican Senator Arlen Specter.\par \par \uc2 It`s great to have you with us, Craig. What is at stake here with \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  on the ballot in Pennsylvania? Why is his platform so dangerous in your opinion?\par \par \uc2 [22:49:52]\par \par \uc2 CRAIG SNYDER, POLITICAL DIRECTOR, REPUBLICANS FOR SHAPIRO: Thanks, Ayman. Well, you know, Lawrence, on this show has been absolutely right. It`s not hyperbole to say that this is the most important governors` race in the country this year and maybe ever.\par \par \uc2 The question on the ballot, as I see it, is whether the American Republican form of government created in Pennsylvania in 1787 is going to be mortally wounded here in Pennsylvania in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Mastriano is not a traditional Pennsylvania Republican, like my old boss, Arlen Specter, and some of our past governors-- Scranton, Thornburgh, Ridge, et cetera. He`s a radical. As you have described, he is practically Trump`s vice president for the big 