 out that he was directing the Department of Justice to ensure all lawbreakers are prosecuted to the full extent of the law, we must send a clear message not with mercy but with Justice, legal consequences must be swept in firm. Do you know why he wanted that crossed out?\par \par \uc2 JARED KUSHNER\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE ADVISOR: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 PAT CIPOLLONE, FORMER WHITE HOUSE COUNSEL: And that needed to be stated forcefully. (INAUDIBLE) did not represent the -- Jim or the -- his political views in any form or fashion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He also was crossed out, I want to be very clear, you do not represent me, you do not represent our movement. Do you remember -- do you know why he crossed that language out of the statement?\par \par \uc2 KUSHNER\uc2 : I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Add to that, the suggestion that the committee could be on the verge of subpoenaing Ginni Thomas, wife of sitting Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . Here`s committee co-chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R-WY): The committee is engaged with her counsel. We certainly hope that she will agree to come in voluntarily, but the committee is fully prepared to contemplate a subpoena if she does not. I hope it doesn`t get to that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The committee wants to talk to Thomas about her communications with people like \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and John Eastman, as well as her attempts to convince Arizona lawmakers to help overturn the election. But Thomas isn`t the only one of the committee`s cites. According to Congresswoman Cheney, their list of potential witnesses includes members of the Secret Service, Trump campaign officials, and even Cabinet members who discussed invoking the 25th Amendment. So we`ve got a lot to discuss.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in NBC`s Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vital, with me here in studio NBC News correspondent Vaughn Hillyard, also joining me, Pulitzer Prize-winning staff writer for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , Carol Leonnig, and Joyce Vance is a former U.S. attorney and professor at the University of Alabama law school. So let`s start with this new video, Ali, just released by Congresswoman Luria. Explain what that`s all about and why maybe it`s being put out now.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, look, this is a moment where the committee is going into a several-week pause before they pick up with hearings again in September. This may be sort of a reminder that there are still things that we have not yet seen that the committee has its hands on, including videos like this one, where they`re not just establishing that they have the remarks and the draft of the former president for those remarks he gave on January 7.\par \par \uc2 But also a little bit into the thought process of how they came together, the things that he was striking out and unwilling to say. For example, condemning the rioters as not indicative or representative of his movement or crossing out the fact that he wanted the DOJ to leverage their full criminal prosecutorial power against these insurrectionists. That gives a little bit more insight into the mindset.\par \par \uc2 And the fact that you have people like \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  and \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2 , not only them, but then \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , the top White House lawyer talking about what those edits mean, gives a little bit more light into what the former president`s mindset might have been around that, and also gives a little bit more meat on the bone to where they left us on January 6 in their committee hearings, which brought us up in the timeline to the day of the insurrection, and then gave us a little bit more of a sense of remarks that Trump gave on the seventh. All of this is meant to bolster the fact that they still have things on the cutting room floor that maybe we haven`t seen yet, but that we could end up seeing when they move forward in September with what our reporting is at least two more hearings.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: This committee knows how to craft a tease. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Joyce, I also mentioned Ginni Thomas. Originally, she said she couldn`t wait to testify, and then we heard from her attorney who said they hadn`t shown up enough of a reason for her to testify because he says she was just expressing her "personal views in her capacity as a private citizen." Is there any question in your mind that would be justified in bringing her in or issuing a subpoena for that matter?\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Typically, we leave up to the body that`s engaging in investigations, whether it`s Congress or a prosecutor`s office, the decision-making about what information and what witnesses have relevant information for purposes of their investigation. So oftentimes, defendants will try to avoid testifying by saying that they don`t know anything. In that case, typically they can go into a grand jury or other investigative venue and answer questions, and if they really don`t know anything that typically comes clean in the interview.\par \par \uc2 But here, the committee is well within its rights to pursue this testimony. Thomas had conversations with key thinking -- key figures, she appears to be a witness who could shed some further light on what was taking place at this important point in time.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Vaughn, you just got back from Arizona, which plays into all this. How important do you think she could be?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: You know, our friends at the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  have reported that she reached out to 29 Arizona lawmakers before \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was certified as the winner of 2020, urging them to do the right thing and notably saying that he was in the power of the legislature to overturn the election results and send the slate of electors that they deemed appropriate to Washington, DC to be counted.\par \par \uc2 And Ali and I, over our years on the trail, Ali would know this. Ginny Thomas, perhaps she`s a private citizen, but Ginni Thomas was very present at -- and super back fundraisers for Trump and Pence at the Trump hotel lobby. She is not just the wife of a Supreme Court justice, she was a conservative influencer and player who knew the likes of everybody around \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , his key allies, and also U.S. senators, members of the House. Ginni Thomas in -- is her own independent woman and is her own independent figure in this investigation.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There is a lot of controversy surrounding all of that. The Wall Street Journal doing an editorial about this, and here`s part of what they wrote yesterday, Vaughn. "I don`t think this is about the wife of Justice Thomas, this is an attempt to damage the Justice himself." This is going to be if it happens, if there were to be a subpoena, it would be fireworks on the right.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: And it plays into the hands of what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is arguing across the country. And all of these speeches he`s giving, he`s making it himself, as well as the United States democratic system, the martyr here. And he believes or is contending to voters that they are trying to take down the -- takedown him, take down the Supreme Court, take down the American democracy as we know it. That`s how he`s playing this.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali, have you heard any concerns from the committee about the way that could play out politically?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: I mean, look, politically, it does put them in thorn -- in a thorny position. It`s why we`re hearing them say that they would rather have her come forward voluntarily. And it`s also why we`ve seen them over the course of the last few weeks have those ongoing conversations before they`re willing to escalate to a subpoena. And again, it`s not entirely clear that they`re ready or willing to escalate to a subpoena, but that it`s still on the table speaks to the manner in which and how badly they want to speak with Ginni Thomas about whatever her conversations were with John Eastman, with those elector slates in Arizona, as Vaughn was talking about with \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , the former chief of staff.\par \par \uc2 I would also note though, that that Wall Street Journal framing that politically, yes, I`ve heard this idea that this was -- this is all meant to hurt the Justice himself. But as Vaughn mentioned, Ginni Thomas is her own independent woman with her own ties, and motivations in these conservative circles. The idea that she`s doing that and her actions are meant to harm her husband is a pretty misogynist in its frame. She undertook the actions that she took as her own individual person. And that`s why the committee wants to talk to her because she was the one who was sending those text messages to Meadows who was having those communications with John Eastman and others.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, we`ll see, won`t we, Carol, whether or not Ginni Thomas kept what seemed like a promise but now her lawyer says maybe not because the committee also wants the Secret Service to keep its promise? They said these guys were going to testify. And in fact, director James Murray says they have been cooperating with the committee. \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  earlier today wouldn`t speak to that just said that they haven`t come in yet. Do you have any insight into what`s happening behind the scenes?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEONNIG, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: There was a lot of recoiling, Chris, and a lot of anger inside the Secret Service. And Adam Kinzinger`s claim over the weekend that the Secret Service agents who were in that car with the president on January 6, or witnesses to what happened immediately after, the idea that he claimed that they weren`t cooperating anymore because that`s not what they believe to be happening. Now, they`ve retained some independent counsel -- some of them. This is now a criminal investigation, according to the inspector general of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 , whether or not some of these important pieces of evidence and texts among Secret Service agents were deleted intentionally or deleted in a willfully blind way, right, that would violate the law.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 And so that puts everybody on high alert and probably is part of the reason that some of these people have sought independent lawyers. But they`re really arguing that there are ongoing discussions about -- between the committee and the Secret Service agents and their counsel and that ultimately, a date just hasn`t been set yet. I have to give you one little piece of reporting context, though. There are good reasons for both the committee and for the Secret Service agents in this instance, to both kind of want to negotiate and tell they never set a date for them not -- never to come forward.\par \par \uc2 For the committee, the problem is that these two individuals with which you are the sort of the most prime witness to what happened in the car, Tony Ornato, the former White House Deputy Chief of Staff, a secret service executive at the time, that he served as a political adviser to President Trump, and Bobby Engel, the head of his secret service detail, both of those individuals, I`m told will deny that there was any real altercation or also suggest that they don`t recall such an altercation.\par \par \uc2 That`s a problem for the committee if that`s what they offer. And on the committee -- on the Secret Service agent side, they don`t know all the questions they`ll be asked. What else will they be pressed to reveal about a president that from all the witnesses I`ve spoken to these two individuals heavily supported politically and personally?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You know, there`s a number of things that are going on here. But when I think about the Secret Service at large, and you can never look at anything, you know, as sort of they`re all one big monolithic thing, right? But you have the revelation that`s new for some people about the political leanings of some of these agents, right? The allegiance to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the deleted texts, a number of things that obviously don`t make the Secret Service look great. What are you hearing about agents? Have you got from agents -- have you gotten any sense that there is ongoing concern at large within the agency about how they`re coming across here?\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: It`s an excellent question. And I`ll tell you the answer in two parts. First off, the Secret Service is a law enforcement agency. And most law enforcement agencies, local, federal state, they lean conservative, they lean red. And so it is not a shock to me that many people inside this agency have told me yes, I had a MAGA hat on my desk. What about it? Even though this is a politically objective, independent agency that`s supposed to be full of civil servants that check their politics at the door.\par \par \uc2 Deep within the agency, and by this, I mean, mid-level and low level, there`s a lot of concern about credibility, a lot of worry about how they look in the wake of Donald Trump`s presidency. Because he did use them, manipulate them, and ultimately co-opt them into a lot of political shenanigans that are disturbing to those mid and low-level agents who believe that this is a -- is a political no-fly zone. And that all we do here, according to the agents, their favorite saying, the people elect them, we just protect them. Unfortunately, during the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  presidency, they crossed that line again and again and again. And it is a problem that is not going to be resolved unless a president takes this bull by the horns, forgive the terrible metaphors, but somebody has got to do that.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Joyce, on the other side of this, we heard from multiple members of the committee over the last 36 hours talking about what the Justice Department should do about all of it. And I want to just remind folks. We`ll play some of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ELAINE LURIA, (D-VA): I sure as hell hope they have a criminal investigation at this point into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2 , (R-IL): I think it is essential for DOJ to at least run all the way up all the information they can, make the determination if they can, and should indict.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: The violation of his oath of office, the violation of the Constitution that he engaged in is the most serious misconduct of any president in the history of our nation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Joyce, I find this pressure campaign fascinating. Is it helpful? Could it be impactful? What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, the conversation that you`ve just had with Carol demonstrates how dangerous it is for a law enforcement agency to take on any sort of political trappings. And this was what we see from \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . He`s an Attorney General who is intent on restoring the tarnished image of his agency with the public. He wants to make it clear he does not have political masters, even to the -- to the extent that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who very early went on record with some comments about the importance of holding Trump accountable and the Justice Department`s response has been very steadfast, we`ll look our own cases, we`ll investigate the facts, we`ll follow them wherever they lead, we won`t hesitate to prosecute people, but we won`t be subject to political influence.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 That`s what we hear from the Justice Department. I think that`s what`s going on inside of the building. And I`ve heard a little bit of a walking back this morning of the pressure campaign with people in Congress being careful to say this is my opinion or I`m not a lawyer. I think it`s very easy for us to all get carried away because of the strength of the evidence that`s coming out of the committee. In many ways, it`s overwhelming, and it`s forced us to relive the trauma of January 6. But DOJ has a very distinctive separate job, which is to bring charges if the evidence supports violations of law. That`s really different from the court of public opinion where the January 6 committee has been very successful.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Vaughn, this is so much just what`s happened over the last 24, 48 hours, right? And I`m curious because you travel so much. What you`re seeing on the ground, what you`re hearing on the ground, are you -- is there any indication that this is having an impact on the average voter?\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Right, I think this is where I think two things can be true at once. I was talking to an Arizona Republican voter in Phoenix here, more of a traditional fiscal conservative as he`d call himself. And he said what he saw on January sixth was vile. He said he would never have gotten into the Capitol premises. He thinks that it was a protest that got out of hand. He condemns in the strongest words. At the same time, he said that he would still back \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2024 here.\par \par \uc2 And so on one hand, somebody can look at the damning evidence here and believe that there was a great amount of wrongdoing, but then, on the other hand, people can still look at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and view him as a future leader of their party yet again. And that`s where this political part is so important because literally tomorrow, he`s giving a speech in Washington, DC. His first return to Washington, DC since leaving on Inauguration Day, and one could argue --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And you got primaries coming up, right?\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: And you got primaries coming up, and voters have been picking his candidates now, there`s a couple of big ones ahead here. And we`ll see if this -- if these hearings have truly had an impact on Arizona voters, Wyoming voters, Missouri voters, Wisconsin, and Michigan, I think the next three weeks are going to be really telling about Donald Trump`s political future here ahead.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And you and I are both going to be on the campaign trail, looking forward to seeing you. Thank you so much, Vaughn, Ali, Carol, and Joyce, appreciate all of you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden`s health has improved significantly since last week`s positive COVID test. We`re told in an update just about 90 minutes ago that his symptoms are almost completely gone. And today, he`s once again working virtually from the White House holding those meetings with Black Law Enforcement Executives and labor leaders. NBC`s Mike Memoli is at the White House. What`s the latest from there, Mike?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, in the latest memo we`ve received from Dr. Kevin O`Connor, the White House physician, it`s as you indicated, Biden`s symptoms have largely resolved other than just a little bit of hoarseness at this point, his -- that`s the main symptom that remains. His blood pressure, his pulse, his respiratory rate, and his temperature are normal. And he`s experiencing no shortness of breath at this point, which obviously, given how we know this virus works is the biggest concern.\par \par \uc2 Now, as for his voice, when we heard from the President on Friday, he said that he felt better than he sounded. And we`re going to get a test of that twice today, first, in the form of some pre-recorded remarks that the president`s delivering to a Black Law Enforcement group. And then later as he speaks to the main issue at the moment, of course, which is the economy. He`s going to be talking about an important piece of legislation to aim at boosting the semiconductor industry here in the U.S. with some CEOs and some labor leaders.\par \par \uc2 Now, it is also worth noting, Chris, that the president, according to some reporting from Kristen Welker, a colleague at the White House, is going to use his remarks to the law enforcement groups who make some tough remarks about his predecessor, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The president is going to be making the argument that if you claim to be on the side of law enforcement, you need to show that in support for law enforcement in all forms. Obviously, a reference it would seem to what we`re learning as the January 6 Committee continues its work. Some other notes, Chris, about what -- where the White House is, at this moment, we`ve seen the vice president in public today. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , she of course, was listed as close contact, tested negative as has the first lady. But one impact is the communications director at the White House, \uc2 Kate Bedingfield\uc2  extended her tenure. She was due to leave her position on Friday, but at this sensitive moment has agreed to stay on for at least a few extra days.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Interesting. Mike Memoli, thank you for all of that. Between the January 6 hearings, gun violence, and the abortion fight, our political winds shifting in favor of the Democrats. And does federal work have a real chance at defeating Governor Abbott in Texas? But first, thousands of confirmed monkeypox cases in the U.S. Are we prepared to keep this virus under control? You`re watching "CHRIS JANSING REPORTS" only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: New concerns about the growing number of cases of monkeypox in the U.S., nearly 3000 reported including now two children. The \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  is warning simultaneously that while cases have so far been concentrated primarily within gay and bisexual communities, there`s no guarantee it won`t spread beyond those groups. Meanwhile, the Biden administration is facing mounting pressure with top ranking members of the House and Senate demanding answers on how it plans to handle the outbreak. NBC`s Blayne Alexander has more for us from Atlanta. Blayne?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  has now declared monkeypox a global health emergency. This is a rare step. And it`s the highest alert that the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  can give essentially sending a message to global leaders that it`s time to start coordinating a global response. Now, the Biden administration is promising that more vaccines are on the way.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 But when you look at the number of cases, they have been sharply rising here in the U.S. Less than two weeks ago, for instance, the CDC had about 1400 confirmed cases. Now, that number has nearly doubled, we`re just shy of 3000. Now, it`s important to certainly note that this is not spread like COVID-19. It`s not a respiratory virus or an airborne virus, but rather its spread through direct contact, physical contact. And experts say that right now while it is presenting the majority of the cases are presenting in men in the LGBTQ community, officials are making it very clear this is a virus that has and is spreading to any population. In fact, just over the weekend, two children were diagnosed with confirmed cases of monkeypox, one in California, and another in Washington, DC.\par \par \uc2 Now, when it comes to vaccines, the Biden administration says that in the coming weeks, there will be more made available right now. The supply has been low. Experts say that if you have been exposed or you`re at higher risk of exposure, you should make it a priority to get the monkeypox vaccine. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Blayne, thank you so much for that. Joining me now is a senior scholar with the Johns Hopkins Center for Health Security, Dr. \uc2 Amesh Adalja\uc2 . So, look, this is scary for a lot of people who say, OK, now it`s growing, there are more cases, now there are a couple of kids, could this be worse than we were led to believe?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 AMESH ADALJA\uc2 , SENIOR SCHOLAR, JOHN HOPKINS CENTER FOR HEALTH SECURITY: It`s definitely worse than it needed to be because there was a lot of reactive stance, not a proactive stance. When we started to have difficulty with testing, when we had difficulty getting the vaccine, all of that was going to clearly make the outbreak bigger than it needed to be. But it`s important, there`s a tendency to look at everything through the lens of COVID-19. Not every infectious disease is COVID-19, there`s different biological characteristics, and this is just not that contagious. And we have a tried and true mechanism to stop it as the vaccine is just getting that vaccine competently delivered to high-risk populations. And that`s what we`ll end this.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Just like that, we just need to get the vaccine out. And that`s essentially what members of Congress are saying, do we have enough?\par \par \uc2 ADALJA: We don`t have enough. We`re using a second-generation smallpox vaccine, which has less reactogenicity, it`s safer. And because of that, there`s only a small supply of it and it`s been you know a long time since they`ve actually ordered new vaccines. And now they`re starting to finally increase the orders, get those mobilized, there was a vaccine sitting in Denmark that hadn`t been approved because the \uc2 FDA\uc2  hadn`t inspected the plant. All of that it slowed down how quickly vaccine was available to the U.S. That`s starting to get better, but it is still going to be some time before we get all our high-risk populations vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 And we have to start being innovative, thinking about prioritizing first doses like they`re doing here in New York City instead of doing the two- dose regimen, and really get making sure that people are aware that they`re testing, that we have all of the resources and using the antiviral. There`s a lot of things that need to be done to manage this better, but it is something for which we have control measures that have worked in the past.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Because of the Coronavirus, many people now are familiar with a phrase from the \uc2 WHO\uc2 , which is a global health emergency. But it`s rare. It`s not something they do very often. So why do you think in this case was it a preemptive declaration to wake people up, or are we already further along?\par \par \uc2 ADALJA: It`s probably -- the best way to think about this is probably to think about it as galvanizing the world to actually take actions. Actions that we knew needed to be taken. Take focuses the world`s attention that this is something that needs to be contained. This is spreading outside of endemic countries and we don`t want it to become endemic in more countries than it originally was. And to do that, we need people to actually mobilize public health resources, vaccines, testing, contact tracing, all of that needs to start happening. And I think that`s what the public health emergency declaration does. It just focuses everyone`s attention\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So much different disinformation, as you well know. And we`ve talked about -- got out at the beginning and actually through the coronavirus pandemic, the height of the coronavirus pandemic, it made a lot of people skeptical, doubting what the CDC had to say, making it clear to them that there`s not a lot in science that they feel that they can trust. Could that come back now to hurt in the fight against something like monkeypox?\par \par \uc2 ADALJA: It`s definitely going to hurt. That breach that occurred between public health authorities, infectious disease physicians, and the public is going to be the sort of the long tail of the COVID-19 pandemic. Because if people don`t trust experts to be giving them the correct information, if they think it`s all political or something that is to be filtered through identity politics, they`re not going to listen on either side when we think something is not something to panic about or when there is something to panic about. And this is one of the lessons, I think, from COVID-19 that we all have to learn that if we don`t trust science if we don`t -- if we get politics mixed up in it, it`s only going to come back and hurt us. And this is going to be something we pay for long into the future.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Dr. \uc2 Amesh Adalja\uc2 , it is so good to have you here in New York. Thank you for coming out. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ADALJA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And still to come, the new political movement that appears to be underway in Texas. How the Uvalde shooting power grid failures, abortion access, and much more may be contributing to Beto O`Rourke`s success in the governor`s race? You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:33:50]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Some new evidence today that the predictions of a Democratic wipeout in November`s election may have been premature. Seismic events from abortion bans to fallout from the January 6th revelations could very well be shifting some political head winds.\par \par \uc2 A growing number of analysts now believe the Senate, at least, could stay in Democratic hands. And pointedly, in Texas, a recent poll shows \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  has cut Governor Greg Abbott`s once formidable lead to just five points. Financial filings also show that O`Rourke has set a state fundraising record bringing in over $27 million from February to June. That actually smashes previous records.\par \par \uc2 For more, I want to bring in Patrick Svitek, a political reporter for the "Texas Tribune," Aaron Blake is senior political reporter for "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 So, Patrick, I think the core question is whether that slew of events, that might seem to work against Governor Abbott, can in fact outweigh a widespread discontent with the economy. So how do you read the race and where it is right now?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK SVITEK, THE TEXAS TRIBUNE POLITICAL REPORTER: You know, as you pointed out, there is some evidence in the fundraising and polling that this race is getting more competitive. I think that these events that you pointed out, especially as it relates to abortion and guns, have really energized Democrats.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:08]\par \par \uc2 The question is whether it`s changing any minds or it`s just energizing people who are already inclined to vote one way or another in November. I know that Governor Abbott`s campaign, you know, has said that these events have, you know, that they`re worried that these events have led to, you know, more fundraising for candidates like \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2 . They said that this latest fundraising reports came out and prove them true.\par \par \uc2 As you pointed out, he set a new fundraising record in Texas and outraised Abbott for the period, which is very remarkable given that Greg Abbott is probably the strongest fundraiser the state has seen in modern times.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, again, your gut check, how much of this movement toward Beto, which we see in the shifting poll numbers, you think is pinned specifically to those Texas issues, including the Uvalde school shooting and some of the other problems with the state`s power grid, for example? How much of it do you think might be related to something else, including maybe that Beto is a different candidate than he was the last time around?\par \par \uc2 SVITEK: I think those events are definitely, you know, driving some people to \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2 . If you look at the public opinion polling, the way that Texas Republican leadership has handled those two issues in recent weeks, guns and abortion, it largely unpopular. Public opinion polling shows that Texas voters continue to want stricter gun laws, which Texas Republican leaders have resisted so far.\par \par \uc2 And it also shows that having the trigger law that Texas has in place banning abortion at the state level of Roe v. Wade is overturned automatically, that that is also unpopular. And so those two issues that he`s been campaigning very hard on, you know, he`s on the right side of public opinion as far as the polls that I have seen in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And he has been consistent, as you say in that messaging.\par \par \uc2 Aaron, though, we`ve been here before, haven`t we? I mean, in 2018 Beto came close to defeating Senator Ted Cruz in the closest contest in the state in four years, but he did not win. He raised a lot of money back then, too. So let me ask you the question I just asked Patrick.\par \par \uc2 Do you think he`s a different candidate now or maybe that he`s viewed a little differently than he was then?\par \par \uc2 AARON BLAKE, THE WASHINGTON POST SENIOR POLITICAL REPORTER: You know, it`s a good question. I do think that there are some environmental things here. Obviously, Texas is a state that is trending towards purple. Perhaps not as fast as Democrats might have liked in recent years. We had the very close O`Rourke-Cruz race in 2018. We also had the closest presidential election in Texas since 1996 two years ago.\par \par \uc2 So this is a state that is there to be competitive in the relatively near future. I do think that there is a question when you`re talking about a state like Texas, is it a state that`s just going to creep closer and maybe Democrats can`t get over the top quite yet? I think that the -- certainly the environmental head winds that Democrats are facing right now are significant. At the same time, we are seeing in a number of states, we`re seeing some of these Republican nominees who are underperforming in the environment.\par \par \uc2 We`re talking about the Pennsylvania Senate race with Mehmet Oz. We`re talking about the Georgia Senate race with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . Ohio Senate race with J.D. Vance. They are not running as strongly as you would expect Republicans to be running in those kinds of states given where the national environment is right now. I think Texas is maybe a little bit of a different case. Governors races can be a little bit more, you know, up and down.\par \par \uc2 They`re not necessarily people voting for a legislator, one of 100, in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . So it can be more defined by those local issues than some of these Senate races can be. At the same time, as you get closer to an election, you tend to see people go towards their existing camps. And so I think it is very difficult for Democrats to pull through in Texas this year.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, and I think one of the other interesting questions always, and I`m going to avoid the cliche of let`s see where turnout goes, but one thing we do know about turnout is that it`s lower in a midterm, right, than it is in a presidential. And so on the margins, things can have a bigger impact. I`m thinking for example of the fact that you see these huge numbers of young people who have been motivated by the Supreme Court`s abortion ruling.\par \par \uc2 Will they come out to vote? There have been many moves, and we actually saw this in Patrick`s paper, the number of gay Republicans in Texas who have fought for acceptance within the party and are finding quite the opposite. So there is an interesting sort of, if you look down into the polling, sort of question about what of these might actually impact turnout, right?\par \par \uc2 BLAKE: Yes. In fact, Texas is a state which, if you do have some kind of unusual turnout, that can cause bigger shifts. Midterm elections as you said do lead to somewhat unusual turnout. It`s not like a presidential election where most everybody is more likely to vote.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:01]\par \par \uc2 That`s why we have more wave elections in midterm elections. And so it`s certainly a state worth watching. I think the question for the Democratic Party is how much this is a race they want to focus on, how much they want to have their donors send this money to this kind of race given that some of these other states are generally more competitive and could actually decide the balance of power in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Patrick Svitek, Aaron Blake, thank you both so much.\par \par \uc2 And still to come, new warnings from General \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2 . Why he`s called China`s military significantly more aggressive and dangerous and what this might mean as House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  weighs a risky trip to Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Joint Chiefs chairman \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2  says China has become much more aggressive and dangerous in recent years just military officials here are warning of the possibility that China could invade Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:02]\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile China is issuing a warning of its own, that it would take, quote, "forceful measures" if Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  visits Taiwan. President Biden has also advised against that trip. So whether she`ll make it now is an open question.\par \par \uc2 Joining net now retired four-star general, Barry McCaffrey, who is an NBC News military analyst.\par \par \uc2 It`s always great to see you, General. Is General Milley right? Should we be worried?\par \par \uc2 GEN. BARRY MCCAFFREY, NBC NEWS MILITARY ANALYST: Yes, I think so. We`ve watched steadily over the last five years in particular an increase in extremely unsafe and aggressive actions both in the air and at sea of intersections by the PLA. It`s not just U.S., it`s also Canada, the Philippines, Vietnam, Japan, Australia. We`ve seen very aggressive actions in the South China Sea with the PLA putting armed, artificially constructed islands, and these rhetoric about Taiwan has gotten completely aggressive.\par \par \uc2 A lot of intelligence officials now assess that by 2027, the PLA will try and seize Taiwan by armed force. So Milley, Austin, Blinken and the Biden administration are trying to create deterrence before we get into a shooting war.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. We know that \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  was in that region recently. The Biden administration overall has been taking these steps to try to expand its military and security relationships with the Indo-Pacific nations to try to counter, right, China`s influence.\par \par \uc2 How does that fit into an overall plan, though, when you look at the threat that China poses?\par \par \uc2 MCCAFFREY: Well, it`s tough. Look, with their economic interdependence between U.S. and China, China and the West World was enormous. And clearly no one in their right mind wants to see another two-generation confrontation such as the Warsaw Pact that \uc2 NATO\uc2  had. But we have to be sensible. We have to be pragmatic. China in almost every arena now is taking steps to become a global military power. They are increasing their nuclear weapons, which astonished me. They used to have around 300 nuclear weapons. Enough to just create a deterring effect.\par \par \uc2 Now they`re going for a war-fighting effect. Xi internally has totally seized control of China`s political system. He`s suppressing internal human rights. It`s a surveillance society. And so his latitude to make bad decisions has increased. I think it`s a tough situation. President Biden and his team are facing up to it I think in a very measured, thoughtful way. The allies are concerned.\par \par \uc2 You know, I was in Vietnam and they wanted to talk about the Chinese threat. Not talk about the Vietnam War. So we`ve got some challenges ahead of us.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And how fraught does all this make the potential Pelosi trip to Taiwan, General?\par \par \uc2 MCCAFFREY: Well, I think most people say you shouldn`t go. It`s a thoughtless measure at this point to have a politician there. We can get away with having, you know, civilian clothed military visit to Taiwan. That kind of thing. We clearly don`t want to provoke the Chinese. So I`m not sure what positive can come out of it. And there will be some signal, some measure by the Chinese in response to a Pelosi visit. It`s not a good idea.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: General Barry McCaffrey, always good to talk to you. Thank you so much, sir. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And Pope Francis makes the first papal visit to Canada in two decades. Details on the historic apology for the Catholic missionaries` role in the abuse of thousands of indigenous children.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:43]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let`s talk about the gun violence that`s plaguing this country. The latest, a shooting in the San Pedro neighborhood of Los Angeles where two people were killed, five were injured when shots rang out during a car show and a baseball game. The latest victims of gun violence. Something the Stevens family knows all too well. Last May as Lance Stevens was doing yard work and his mother was dropping off his sons from a sleepover, a random shooting shattered their quiet lives. Lance and his mother were seriously injured, two young children left traumatized.\par \par \uc2 Trymaine Lee traveled to Indianapolis to learn more about their story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LANCE STEVENS, VICTIM OF GUN VIOLENCE: Nice shot, man.\par \par \uc2 TRYMAINE LEE, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Indianapolis couple Lance Stevens and his wife, Sophia, tried to build their lives around avoiding violence, carefully selecting a home in a neighborhood where they thought they could raise their young sons in relative safety. But last May a stranger with a gun found them anyway, piercing the family`s peaceful suburban life. In a blink, 39-year-old Lance and his mother Kim Tillman joined the estimated 100,000 other Americans who survived gun violence each year.\par \par \uc2 KIM TILLMAN, VICTIM OF GUN VIOLENCE: He pulled the gun on my son`s head.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Lance was shot in the head and leg. Kim, who`s dropping off her grandsons after a sleepover, caught a wave of bullets in her arms, chest and face.\par \par \uc2 TILLMAN: I don`t even know why he came over and why he shot us.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Lance and Kim are among the nearly 750 people who were shot in Indianapolis last year who survived.\par \par \uc2 TILLMAN: This is dead right here. My implant is here. But down here this is all dead.\par \par \uc2 LEE (on-camera): Your arm, your chest and under your jaw.\par \par \uc2 TILLMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEE (voice-over): Kim has permanent scarring from bullets that punctured her face, shattering her jawbone and several teeth.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): At what point did you realize just how badly you`ve been injured?\par \par \uc2 TILLMAN: I kind of still don`t realize it now.\par \par \uc2 LEE (voice-over): Wounds you can see but also wounds that you can`t.\par \par \uc2 L. STEVENS: I had terrible nightmares just waking up, thinking people want to shoot me.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Lance and Sophia`s 3- and 6-year-old sons were there that day.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 Neither boy was harmed physically, but both are processing trauma. The oldest boy, LJ, saw his father stumble into the house.\par \par \uc2 L. STEVENS: He`s already seeing me, my head bleeding. My son is like, I don`t want to be without you. He`s saying it like I don`t want you to die.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Their younger son was asleep in the back seat of his grandmother`s car, bullets whizzing around him. The family assumed the 3-year-old saw nothing. In therapy they learned he was stuffing it down.\par \par \uc2 SOPHIA STEVENS, LANCE`S WIFE: He just unloaded and we were amazed.\par \par \uc2 LEE: There are other costs, too. Repairs to the Stevens` bullet-ridden home, the loss of a vehicle and the family says $35,000 in medical bills combined.\par \par \uc2 As the family tries to move on and make sense of what happened, I asked what they`d say to lawmakers who are reluctant to make gun reforms a priority.\par \par \uc2 TILLMAN: Shame on you. Just shame on them. Unless it actually hits home maybe to their kids, their grandmother, you know, maybe if it hits home to them and they can actually see it.\par \par \uc2 LEE\uc2 : Through it all, they still find hope and light, and they`re standing up where bullets almost took them down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That`s Trymaine Lee with that report. Thank you, Trymaine, for that.\par \par \uc2 And still ahead, growing evidence that GOP voters and conservative media are starting to turn away from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as two \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2 -owned papers slammed the former president for January 6th. Is the party really looking elsewhere?\par \par \uc2 And I`ll be in the chair for Katy Tur when our coverage picks up right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220726ei7p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for July 20, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Glenn Kirschner, Neal Katyal, Claire  McCaskill, Jill Wine-Banks \par \uc2 6,896 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Ayman.\par \par \uc2 And I was in Massachusetts this morning, not exactly in the neighborhood, but I was thinking about swinging by, but didn`t have the time, had to get out here and do this tonight. So here I am.\par \par \uc2 AYMAN MOHYELDIN, MSNBC HOST, "AYMAN": Maybe next time.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Maybe next time. Thanks, Ayman.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Two words. There are two words that I really hope you remember from the next hour of what you are about to hear. These two words should be included in every story about the shocking developments this week at the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 Now these are two words that I didn`t know last week, at the beginning of last week, but these two words don`t appear in almost all of the coverage of what is happening at the Secret Service. The two words are, James Murray.\par \par \uc2 James Murray is the person who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appointed director of the Secret Service, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew he was going to need the Secret Service for a lot more then protecting him from death threats. When \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made James Murray head of the Secret Service, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew he was going to need the Secret Service to help him hold on to the presidency.\par \par \uc2 We will be talking about other things in this hour. You will hear from \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , when she joins us, about the ban on abortion in Georgia, which went into effect today.\par \par \uc2 You will hear what happened in the \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  trial today, when federal prosecutors confidently rested their case after presenting just to technical witnesses.\par \par \uc2 And you will see and hear, the mother of the mass murderer in Uvalde, Texas, who was confronted by other mothers, today, mothers of the murdered children. Telemundo captured that confrontation on video and we will present it later in the hour.\par \par \uc2 But we begin tonight with James Murray, the director of the Secret Service who has remained in that position during the Biden administration, even though he was appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The first director of the Secret Service appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was actually chosen by \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  when Kelly was the secretary of homeland security. The Secret Service is under the jurisdiction of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 , and Kelly believed that his friend, a retired Marine Corps general, \uc2 Randolph Alles\uc2 , was the kind of outsider that the Secret Service needed to take a fresh look at the management of the Secret Service, which does much more than just protect the president with its $3 billion budget.\par \par \uc2 The Secret Service duties have always included policing counterfeit money, and now include highly sophisticated investigations up by the financial crimes involving electronic communications. Which makes it all the more ironic to put it mildly, that the Secret Service has lost the most important electronic communications in the history of the Secret Service. It is simply not at all believable, not even slightly believable, that the Secret Service got rid of the most important text messages in their history, because no one there knew that those text messages were important.\par \par \uc2 The breaking news tonight is that the Secret Service employees received not one, not to, but three separate notifications instructing them to preserve communications before an agency-wide technology replacement program went into effect in the weeks after the January 6th attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 NBC News is reporting, quote, the first email about preserving records came on December 9th, 2020, from the Secret Service`s office estate planning, and they sack it was in January 2021, from the agency`s chief information officer, through a though a Secret Service official source didn`t provide an exact date. The senior official said, employees received a third email on February 4th, 2021, instructing them to preserve all communications specific to January 6.\par \par \uc2 The emails, quote, included reminders that federal employees have the responsibility to preserve their records and included instructions on how to do so, the senior Secret Service official said. It was James Murray`s responsibility to make sure all Secret Service texts sent and received on January 6 were preserved and he did not do that.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  never liked John Kelly`s choice for director of Secret Service, \uc2 Randall Alles\uc2 . Director Alles struggled with something no Secret Service there were there before him ever faced, how to provide full-time Secret Service protection to a huge high rise building, in Midtown Manhattan, where the president of the United States claim that he still lives, but in fact, almost never visit. The Secret Service budget for protecting that building was higher than the cost of protecting any other home of a previous president and at the same time, the Secret Service budget was skyrocketing to protect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on his constant golfing outings, locally, in Washington, and in New Jersey, and in Florida.\par \par \uc2 Add to that, the 18 members in the Trump family who were given Secret Service protection, and the protective budget of the Secret Service was quickly wiped out.\par \par \uc2 When "USA Today" run a story with the headlines, "Secret Service is going broke protecting Trump," \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  blamed the Secret Service director. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  fired him in April of 2019.\par \par \uc2 In her book about the Secret Service, "Zero Fail: The Rise of the Secret Service" -- "The Rise and Fall of the Secret Service", Carol Leonnig, tells us that James Murray was not Donald Trump`s top choice for his next Secret Service director. Trump wants it to promote Tony Ornato, the leader of his personal security detail to director of the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 Quote: Trump wanted to make Ornato director, but Ornato said he had other plans and suggested to the president that he hire his good friend James Murray, a 23-year member of the service. Trump hired Murray after an interview at lasting roughly ten minutes. The president soon after promoted his loyal detail leader Ornato to a political role that was unprecedented for the nonpartisan Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 At the president`s urging, Ornato took on the job of presidential political adviser as the deputy chief of staff in the Trump White House.\par \par \uc2 A ten-minute interview for his new Secret Service director. That interview may well have included the questions, who did you vote for president? Who were you going to vote for president? And do I have your complete and total loyalty at all times, for anything I might want to do?\par \par \uc2 This was the third year of the Trump presidency. Remember, in the first weeks of his presidency, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  brought \uc2 James Comey\uc2  into the White House for a one-on-one dinner, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was stunningly blatant about loyalty, in a discussion with Comey, who he didn`t even know, and had no reason to trust.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made it very clear, \uc2 James Comey\uc2 , what would be necessary for Comey to continue as \uc2 FBI\uc2  director. \uc2 James Comey\uc2  tells us that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said, I need loyalty. I expect loyalty.\par \par \uc2 Tony Ornato had no doubt certified James Murray`s loyalty to Trump before Trump`s ten-minute interview with him. But we know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was not going to get that job to anybody who did not clearly pledge loyalty to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So we know that James Murray is a Trump guy in every sense important to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would not have promoted him to director of the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 In April of 2019, all of the Democratic candidate for president had announced their candidacies and polling showed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  running far behind \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  at 51, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at 42. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew just like 2016, there was absolutely no way he was going to win more votes than the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew he was going to come in second with the voters, and his only hope was the Electoral College, and this time, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  didn`t want to take his chances with the Electoral College. If it came to it, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was obviously willing to try to change the outcome of the election Electoral College, and it is exactly what he did, and that is what he was still trying to do on January 6.\par \par \uc2 Cassidy Hutchinson testified that Tony Ornato told her, in the White House, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tried to go to the Capitol, on January 6, to join the attackers of the capitol. Cassidy Hutchinson testified under oath, that Tony Ornato told her that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  physically struggling the Secret Service agents in the car when he was demanding that the car take him to the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 The Secret Service deleted every text message about that incident in the car, and everything else that happens on January 6, including possible text messages about Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [22:10:01]\par \par \uc2 Did Tony Ornato send a text message to Mike Pence`s Secret Service agents at the Capitol, telling them to take the vice president away from the Capitol? Did James Murray sent a text message saying that to the agents of the Capitol? To take the vice president away? Were Secret Service agents trying to remove the vice president from the Capitol for his safety, or were they trying to remove the vice president from the Capitol so that the vice president could not certify the Electoral College vote?\par \par \uc2 There could be answers to all of those questions in the text messages, deleted by the Secret Service, which could not have happened without James Murray`s permission. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew that the Secret Service director, that he chose in April of 2019, was going to be the Secret Service director on election day, I was going to be the Secret Service director and the next inauguration day, and that the Secret Service director might be asked to do things that no other Secret Service director in history ever had to do.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Someday, and that day may never, I call up on you to do a service for me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Whatever the specific words were in Donald Trump`s ten minute interview with James Murray before making director of the Secret Service, the subtext of it was, that line from "The Godfather". Someday, and that day may never come, I will call upon you to do a service for me.\par \par \uc2 Did James Murray do a service for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by overseeing the deletion of all the Secret Service text messages on January 6? The January 6 committee can answer all of these questions by issuing a specific personal subpoena to James Murray for his under oath testimony, in a separate subpoena to James Murray for all of his Secret Service text messages, on January 6?\par \par \uc2 Today, the Secret Service told the committee that they had found exactly one text, that is relevant, to the committee`s investigation, out of the thousands more texts that the Secret Service now says we`re deleted.\par \par \uc2 Deleting those texts is a violation of law. James Murray knew that when he allowed them to be deleted. The Secret Service is one of the most sophisticated cyber operations in the federal government. The Secret Service specializes in investigating financial cybercrimes. The Secret Service knows what`s legal obligations are in keeping electronic records, and the Secret Service violated the law.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  said today that the Justice Department investigation of the attack on the Capitol, and they attempt to overturn the election, will not hesitate in bringing criminal charges against anyone who they can prove violated the law. The attorney general stress no person is above the law. He didn`t say Donald Trump`s name, but that is what everyone understood that he meant, and that also means that no Secret Service director is above the law.\par \par \uc2 When James Murray is put under oath by the January 6 committee, or possibly by federal grand jury, he will be asked a long range of questions about the Secret Service electronic records, protocols, he will be asked many questions about what happened to the Secret Service text messages on January 6.\par \par \uc2 And he should also be asked very direct personal questions. How many text messages did you, James Murray send and receive, on your Secret Service phone, on January 6? Who sent you text messages? And who did you send text messages to on January 6? What did those text messages say?\par \par \uc2 The Secret Service sent out a directive to everyone in the Secret Service to preserve all relevant text messages on their phones, did James Murray follow his own directive, or did James Murray personally allow all of the text messages on his Secret Service phones, on January 6, to be deleted, or did James Murray do that himself?\par \par \uc2 Did he delete the text messages from his own Secret Service phone? Did the director of the Secret Service personally do that? Is that the service that he did for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 James Murray was hoping to slip out of town quietly at the end of the month and start his new career in the high-paid world of corporate security. He is scheduled to begin his new job as the head of security for \uc2 Snapchat\uc2  in August.\par \par \uc2 Whoever is handling the preservation of electronic records at \uc2 Snapchat\uc2  now, is doing a better job of it than James Murray will be able to do.\par \par \uc2 Here is someone who James Murray used to work with.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAN BONGINO, FOX NEWS HOST: This is one thing and one thing only. This is an attempt to silence conservatives like you and I, from communicating, before the 2022 election, that`s all that this is.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:05]\par \par \uc2 And it`s a message being sent to anyone who supports \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  either now, or in the future. That is open season on you. It`s haunting season for your private communications. Don`t you dare talk about your intentions, or coordinate, or do anything to get Republican elected again, because \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  will make sure, that they subpoena you and make your life really miserable. That`s all this was.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That guy was a Secret Service agent.\par \par \uc2 Dan Bongino was a New York City police officer who then joined the Secret Service, where he worked for 11 years while James Murray and Tony Ornato were also working in the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 Where they friends with Dan Bongino? Are they friendly with him now? `Is Dan Bongino the public voice of what members of the Secret Service, like James Murray actually think?\par \par \uc2 There is a very serious problem at the Secret Service now. This is the worst crisis facing the Secret Service since the assassination of President Kennedy on November 22nd, 1963, and the director of the Secret Service has not said one word about it, not one word. There has been much justifiable outrage that the chief of the Uvalde school`s police force, went silent after the mass murder at Robb Elementary School in Uvalde, Texas.\par \par \uc2 This is the same thing. The director of the Secret Service facing the worst suspicions that have ever been focused on the Secret Service, in its history, and the director of the Secret Service, James Murray, says absolutely nothing about it? Not one word of explanation? Not one word of defense? Not one word of a promised to find out what`s has been happening at the Secret Service?\par \par \uc2 We have never seen a problem like this at the Secret Service. It`s a very serious problem at the Secret Service, and James Murray is part of that problem, or, James Murray is the problem.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Neal Katyal, former acting U.S. solicitor general and an MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 Neal, the Secret Service story gets worse with every passing day. I was part of makes it worse is the silence of the director of the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 NEAL KATYAL, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: A hundred percent, Lawrence, and I`m so grateful for that very long monologue you did because it was absolutely appropriate. This is frightening. It`s scary, and the idea that somehow the Secret Service accidentally purged all of these text messages strikes me as highly implausible to use your phrase earlier tonight, not believable.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we are talking about text messages with significant data trails. It`s not like a Secret Service all of a sudden started communicating via \uc2 Snapchat\uc2 . And by the way, how on brand does it for the Secret Service Director James Murray, to go and work for \uc2 Snapchat\uc2 , which the company is known for disappearing messages. It seems like he is unfortunately pretty good at that.\par \par \uc2 I do you think we have to consider the timing of the request, and when the texts were deleted. We obviously don`t know what happened here, but the fact that all of these text messages were deleted after they were requested, it raises a bunch of eyebrows. I`m growing a third eyebrow just reading these stories.\par \par \uc2 I think the most important point is that we are not talking about some random guy off of the street, your grandpa being bad at technology. The Secret Service agents are highly trained professionals in electronic communications. The idea that multiple agents all simultaneously failed to back up their data after receiving multiple warning emails, and knowing that the inverse information is being requested by the inspector general, I mean, that seems really farfetched. So, I agree with everything you said.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I mean, you know, what happened to all the data in ongoing criminal investigations that they were conducting which have nothing to do with the presidency? They have so much jurisdiction that has nothing to do with the protection of the presidency, and one of that data was lost. No explanations about any of that.\par \par \uc2 And even if there were no requests at all for the data on January 6th, wouldn`t you, as director of the Secret Service on January 7th at the latest, put out a directive saying, everybody preserve everything that happened yesterday.\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: A hundred percent. The preservation order would be the most standard thing in the world to do. The fact that it was not done is already suspicious.\par \par \uc2 As you say, Lawrence, if they could do it here about January 6th, when obviously everybody knows that the Secret Service were the folks on the ground, guarding Pence, guarding the president, you know, that`s obviously relevant information.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:02]\par \par \uc2 If they could delete that information, heaven knows what they can`t delete you. I mean, you know, this like -- if they do this for the most significant investigation ever, what are they doing for the rest?\par \par \uc2 I am very upset at the Republicans right now, because these are the people that lost their minds over Hillary Clinton`s server, and saying, oh, you know, possibly, data was deleted, or something like that. Here we have an armed invasion of the Capitol, some of the most important evidence in the investigation has now been deleted, and there are crickets. We`re not hearing words of them.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Neal Katyal, thank you for starting off our discussion tonight.\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And joining us now, Claire McCaskill, former Democratic senator from Missouri, an MSNBC political analyst. Also with us, Jill Wine-Banks, who served as an assistant Watergate prosecutor. She`s an MSNBC contributor.\par \par \uc2 And, Senator McCaskill, we also learned today that there were four -- count them -- four House committees who requested this same data before January 6th.\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE MCCASKILL, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: So, there are so many things here, so many layers. I was glad Neal mentioned Hillary`s emails, because what is the irony here? Where is the leadership of the Secret Service? Why are they not having a press conference to address this crisis in the agency?\par \par \uc2 Frankly, why hasn`t James Murray been fired? I mean, if you do not understand -- these were government phones, these are people that are sophisticated federal agencies when it comes to cybersecurity.\par \par \uc2 There is no excuse for the stuff being deleted. None. And so, the assumption that is going to be made is that there was really bad stuff on those phones.\par \par \uc2 Now, can it be recovered? Some of it might be able to be recovered. But this story is going to have a long tale. This is not over by any stretch.\par \par \uc2 There are some people that are going to be in big trouble over this.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Jill Wine-Banks, your reaction to this. And the way -- it gets worse every day, which is just a stunning thing. You think it`s bad as it can be, and then tomorrow, there`s more information about it.\par \par \uc2 JILL WINE-BANKS, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Lawrence, you laid out a really good explanation of what is going on. It is getting worse with each passing day that silence remains.\par \par \uc2 First of all, all government records have to be observed. You can`t just randomly decide that I will get rid of them. And the fact that the Secret Service, who has the technical expertise, could possibly have accidentally lost these records is totally incredible. It`s so incredible -- you know, I look at the days before the 18-1/2-minute gap in Watergate.\par \par \uc2 And this was a two-day gap, and is 144 times longer. But it`s also obviously deliberate. There`s no way, I think as Claire said, you can`t believe this just happened by chance. It had to be not deliberately, and if it can`t be recovered, it was done by real professionals who could get into the cloud, and delete all other copies of it, the "to" and the "from".\par \par \uc2 The fact that it was just on those two days is really suspicious. We know from Watergate, and from the chief of staff Haldeman`s notes that the missing 18 minutes was exactly the time that the conversation turned to Watergate. We know from the timing that January 5th and 6th were days when there would`ve been a lot of messages about protecting or not protecting the president and the vice president.\par \par \uc2 And I think not enough attention is being paid to what was going on with the vice president. The fact that it was possible that the Secret Service was going to whisk him away, at the direction of James Murray, and two words that I won`t forget, I promise you. That they`re going to whisk him away, never to lead him to return to fulfill his constitutional responsibilities to oversee the counting of the ballots.\par \par \uc2 That`s something that we need to know. Who made that order to get out of there? Was it James Murray? Was that the president to James Murray? Was it the president directly to Ornato? We just don`t know.\par \par \uc2 And the good news is, agents can be called to testify. There are witnesses to every single one of these communications. The best evidence, of course, is the text messages.\par \par \uc2 And the quickest and easiest way to get the information is from a compilation of text messages. But with a lot of time and diligence, both the committee and the Department of Justice should be investigating this. It should not be just the committee. These are crimes.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice has an obligation to act on this, as does the inspector general of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [22:25:01]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Claire, there is a line that is attributed to Keith Kellogg in the White House on January 6, when there is a discussion of moving \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , and moving him from the capitol. Kellogg is quoted as saying about the Secret Service, you know, if they take him out of there, they`ll take him to Alaska, or something like that.\par \par \uc2 It is a line that has much more resonance than it has in the past. Why would someone in the Trump White House, in that position, believe that the Secret Service was in Donald Trump`s pocket and would be part of disrupting the actual certification of the Electoral College vote?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: And why was Pence so adamant about not going with them, Lawrence?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Think about that.\par \par \uc2 I think about what is going on in Pence`s mind because if Pence believed this was just about his safety, I think that he might have gone temporarily to another location. But you have to think, maybe Pence was thinking what we`re thinking, that there is an effort here to get me out of the building and keep me away from the building so that I cannot certify these votes, because that`s obviously the goal of the day for team Trump.\par \par \uc2 And you know, the sad thing about this is that all of the institutions that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has degraded during his time -- you know, I remember thinking of the Secret Service, the times that I`ve been around, them and I`ve been around many times in the my career. How reassuring it was that they were not political.\par \par \uc2 This is real, serious, permanent, corrosive damage that has been done to this institution. There needs to be a cleanup here. There needs to be a new set of management. There needs to be a new set of ethics, and there needs to be a re-commitment to this agency not being political.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Claire McCaskill and Jill Wine-Banks, thank you both very much for joining our discussion tonight.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: You bet.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a few hours ago, a federal court put charges six-week abortion ban into effect immediately today, making Georgia another state where abortion is effectively illegal. \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , candidate for governor, will join us, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:32:00]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Today, a federal appeals court ordered immediate compliance in Georgia, with the Supreme Court decision overturning Roe versus Wade. That allows a 2019 abortion law passed by the Georgia legislature and signed by Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  to go into effect immediately.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , Democratic nominee for governor of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 What happens to women in Georgia today?\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D), GEORGIA GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE: Today, women in Georgia lost the right to choose their futures. We are now being governed by one of the most extreme abortion bans in the nation. The six-week abortion ban, which means that women lose their right to choose before they know they are pregnant. And this is happening in the state where we have 82 counties without an OB/GYN. That is more than half the state.\par \par \uc2 We have 18 counties that have no general practicing doctor that does family medicine. Nine counties that do not have a doctor at all. We are in a state of crisis in our health care. And \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and Republicans have made it dangerous, in fact, made it lethal for women to live in the state of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What happens going forward? Of course, the one solution, longer term is electing you as governor. Electing Democratic legislators in Georgia to change this state law.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, what can you tell the women of Georgia that they can do?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Sadly, the only thing they can do is travel to another state. And the closest state is North Carolina. It`s 203 miles to leave the state of Georgia and reach a state where the right to an abortion, the right to full reproductive care is still available.\par \par \uc2 And so sadly, we are going to watch women risk their lives. We`ve seen it happen in Texas, where a woman was investigated for a miscarriage. We`ve seen what happened in Ohio. We`ve seen what happened across this country. Women being forced into ectopic ruptures because doctors are afraid to treat ectopic pregnancies. Women are in danger.\par \par \uc2 And what is so disturbing to me is that because he did not commit treason, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  is being treated as either anti-Trump moderate or a fiscal conservative. He is neither.\par \par \uc2 This is a hard right, he is a hard right, religious extremist who has decided to force women either into pregnancy or into jail. Those are their choices now. And that is wrong in the state of Georgia. It`s wrong for America. And we need a governor who is committed to doing what is right.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why I encourage everyone to go to StaceyAbrams.com to learn more about my plan to protect the women of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Is there -- Planned Parenthood and organizations who are supportive of abortion services -- are they developing a strategy for helping women get out of the state?\par \par \uc2 [22:34:50]\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: They are. The organizations on the ground have been doing their best. But Lawrence, the challenges we are surrounded by these crises -- Mississippi, Alabama, Texas, Tennessee, Florida, South Carolina -- we are in a landlocked situation where the right for a woman to control her body no longer exists.\par \par \uc2 What the Supreme Court did, and what the 11th circuit suborned today was essentially saying that women in Georgia are now second-class citizens. The citizenship has been diminished, simply because we live in the state.\par \par \uc2 And a constitutional right, a fundamental right should not depend on your state lines and your geography. We have seen this before. When states rights become determinant of your human rights, that is simply the calling card that says the state has the right to deny you your humanity, to deny you your citizenship. And that`s what happened today in the state of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, Texas State Senator Roland Gutierrez will join us next on a day when the mother of the mass murderer in Uvalde, Texas, was confronted by mothers of the children he murdered. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:36:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The mother of the mass murderer in Uvalde, Texas was confronted today by mothers and other relatives of the children her son murdered. It was a chance encounter by the side of the road in Uvalde and was caught on camera by a Telemundo crew who was there.\par \par \uc2 Here is some of what the mother of the murderer said toward the end of the confrontation before local police protectively escorted her away.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA MARTINEZ, MOTHER OF UVALDE SCHOOL SCHOOTER: I know my son was a coward. You don`t think I don`t know that? I know. You don`t think I`m carrying all that with me? You don`t think I don`t know. I know. And I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: One of the relatives of one of the children who was killed told Telemundo the mother of the murderer is not innocent.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO CLIP OF DANA MENDIOLA, GRANDMOTHER OF UVALDE VICTIM AMERIE JO GARZA TALKING TO TELEMUNDO)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Texas State Senator Roland Gutierrez. He represents Texas`s 19th district which includes Uvalde.\par \par \uc2 Senator, what were your feelings when you saw that confrontation on video today?\par \par \uc2 ROLAND GUTIERREZ (D-TX), STATE SENATOR: It was just sad. Sad, Lawrence. I mean we know that killers aren`t just born. They develop over time, and sadly and unfortunately this young man had a very, very difficult upbringing because of his mother`s choices. Others around him, that just did not do right by him. And unfortunately, caused him to become the person that he is.\par \par \uc2 But you know, we have got to get -- we are past that moment right now. And we`ve got to look at trying to get answers for these families. So that`s what I`m looking forward to. Trying to find answers and accountability.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And in wrap (ph) you sent a letter today to the lieutenant governor, raising the very important question. How can Texas state police investigate themselves? Which is the current plan for evaluating how the state police forces operated on that day in the school?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: You know, Lawrence, you`ve been doing this a long time. You have been covering these issues for a long time. When police have an officer involved shooting, internal affairs steps in. They begin investigating themselves almost immediately.\par \par \uc2 Here we are 55 days out and they finally decide, ok, we`re going to now investigate ourselves to see if we did all of the policies that we were supposed to do to see if we broke any law. At the end of the day we know that they failed.\par \par \uc2 Steve McGraw called this an abject failure of policing. It`s an abject failure of the Department of Public Safety as well. He wasn`t very adamant about that. He was very good at pointing the fingers at others.\par \par \uc2 But the fact is state troopers stood there in that hallway and did nothing. I want to know who they were talking to, who their superiors were on those phones that they were talking to when they did absolutely nothing.\par \par \uc2 They failed these children. Operation Lone Star, a plused-up immigration task force failed these children. Greg Abbott`s task force failed these children.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And there were, I believe, 91 state police officers at the school, and they want you to believe that they were deferential to the five person police force run by Chief Pete Arredondo of the school police force.\par \par \uc2 Why would a giant state police force come in there, and defer to an obviously incapable, tiny police force?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: And that narrative, Lawrence, was created on day two. On day two that narrative was created because he knew then that his force was an abject failure. He knew that his guys failed. He knew that those guys didn`t get in.\par \par \uc2 Like yes, he created false narrative after false narrative. Blamed Arredondo, blamed the police -- local police, blamed the teacher for putting a stone on a door, which we know she didn`t do.\par \par \uc2 [22:45:00]\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: They (INAUDIBLE) -- they leaked information to the media in different ways just to cover their own behinds. And at the end of the day, the one thing that that report tells us is that each and every one of those police departments, including and especially those that were better-armed and better-equipped, better training -- this is a paramilitary force on our border. 1,800 additional units, 150 in this region, 91 in that school, all from Operation Lone Star. And they failed.\par \par \uc2 The abject failure was indeed the Department of Public Safety as well. And there`s no two ways about it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: State Senator Roland Gutierrez, thank you very much for joining us once again tonight.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, the prosecution made its case against \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  with just two witnesses. Who will testify for the defense? Glenn Kirschner was in the courtroom today. He will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:45:57]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Today, prosecutors in Steve Bannon`s trial completed their case after calling just two witnesses, indicating that the case is as simple as it has always appeared to be. \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  refused to comply with a subpoena to testify to the January 6th committee and that means that all the prosecution has to prove is that it was a legal subpoena and \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  did not show up.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Glenn Kirschner, former prosecutor and an MSNBC legal analyst. He was in the courtroom at Steve Bannon`s trial today.\par \par \uc2 And Glenn, two witnesses -- one a counsel to the January 6 committee saying yes this was our subpoena. Here is the date that it says on the subpoena, he was supposed to show up. And then and \uc2 FBI\uc2  witness -- what did the \uc2 FBI\uc2  witness accomplish for the prosecution?\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Well, you know, he introduced to the jury some of Steve Bannon`s posts on a platform called GETTR, where he was bragging and boasting about willfully defying the congressional subpoenas. So that was a little bit of icing on the cake.\par \par \uc2 Nobody can accuse these prosecutors of over trying the case, I actually think they tried it brilliantly, proving Steve Bannon`s guilt beyond a reasonable doubt, with just two witnesses.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And so the defense case tomorrow, the question in all trials, will the defendant testify?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: You know, Lawrence, I think there is one thing that perhaps all Americans can agree on. \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  loves to run his mouth. Not just on his podcast, but he has been putting on an absurd little dog-and-pony show every day after trial, on the courthouse steps where he is railing against and trolling everybody from \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  to Dr. Fauci to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and others. I mean he is the most aggrieved man on the planet.\par \par \uc2 So it`s going to be really interesting because tomorrow is his big day. He gets to take the witness stand, he gets to tell his story, and he gets to rock the mic, as the kids say.\par \par \uc2 It will be really interesting to see if -- and I`m going to use the word brave -- if he is brave enough to take the stand, and to try to field cross-examination questions, from a really strong, adept prosecutor named Amanda Vaughn (ph), she is they lead counsel in the case. She has performed brilliantly.\par \par \uc2 And I don`t think \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  can withstand cross-examination from AUSA Vaughn because there are a lot of areas that are very ripe for cross- examination.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Are there any other potential defense witnesses?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: The defense did not tip its hand in opening statement. They did not promise the jury that they would present any witness by name. So I think that their case is actually going to sort of peter out. I don`t think they`re going to have much by the way of an affirmative defense.\par \par \uc2 So I really do think it all boils down to whether Bannon wants to take the stand, put on a show, try to make a circus of the proceedings. If I had to bet -- and I`m not a betting man, $1 is my limit -- I would probably bet a buck that \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  does not take the stand tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So if he doesn`t take the stand, and there are no other witnesses, it is conceivable that the defense could just rest and just go to closing arguments? What would be available to them in a closing argument if they did that?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: What they`ll do is they will have to urge the jurors to just disbelieve some of the testimony they heard from two really professional, accomplished, credible witnesses. I mean these two witnesses were unimpeachable.\par \par \uc2 But I will say, Lawrence, is because the government has the burden of proving guilt beyond a reasonable doubt and because that burden never shifts to the defense. And the defense never has to present any evidence, it`s not that unusual for a defense team to rest without presenting evidence and then argue, you know what ladies and gentlemen? We didn`t have to present any evidence because the government failed to meet their burden of proof and here is how.\par \par \uc2 The problem is, the here is how, they have nothing available.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes. I mean we have always said it`s kind of an open and shut case, and that`s the way it played out in court.\par \par \uc2 [22:55:00]\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Yes, he willfully defied the subpoenas. He bragged about it. He did it. Why? To cover up for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which would be some really ripe cross-examination questions if he does testify. But this -- you know, nothing is a sure thing, but this is the proverbial open and shut case.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Glenn Kirschner, thank you for being in the courtroom for us today. Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:00:00]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Tonight`s LAST WORD is a name. James Murray, the director of the Secret Service who presided over the illegal deletion of all of the Secret Service`s text messages on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020220721ei7k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for July 19, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 7,442 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  got tonight`s "LAST WORD". That is THE 11TH HOUR - THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, message send failure. The Secret Service can`t we`ll cover those January 6 text messages ahead of Thursday`s primetime hearings set to focus solely on Trump.\par \par \uc2 Then, as confusion grows across the country about abortion laws, we`ll take an inside look into how one organization plans to fight the restrictions in key battleground states.\par \par \uc2 Then, the bipartisan vote in the House to protect same-sex marriage. How will it play out in the Senate? Why do they even need to do it? As THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle live at MSNBC headquarters here in New York City. As the January 6 committee gathers more evidence ahead of Thursday`s primetime hearing, the Secret Service is coming under some major scrutiny tonight over missing text messages from January 5th and 6th.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the committee issued a subpoena for those messages after an inspector general notified them that they had been deleted. Today, NBC News learned that the agency now has said they have no new tax related to the attack to give House members, meaning they can`t find any of them. The Secret Service says they lost the text as part of a technology upgrade.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 STEPHANIE MURPHY\uc2 , (D) JANUARY 6 SELECT COMMITTEE MEMBER: They received four requests from congressional committees in -- on January 16, to preserve records. And they had this planned migration for the 25th, I believe of January. Nobody along the way stopped and thought, well, maybe we shouldn`t do the migration of data and of the devices. Until we are able to fulfill these four requests. Their process, as explained to us was simply to leave it to the agents to determine whether or not there was anything on their phones worth saving that was necessary to save for federal records. And as a result today, they have -- they did not receive any texts from their agents when they made that transition that was flagged for preservation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The National Archives has asked the Secret Service to find out how in the world this has happened. Tonight, Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas, whose department runs the Secret Service was asked about the missing tax from our own Trymaine Lee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRYMAINE LEE, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Secretary, there`ll be a lot of people who say, sounds really convenient, is merging with information and all these important, possibly important technologies just disappear. Do you believe that this was just a good faith accident?\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, DEPARTMENT OF HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: So the migration was planned well before January 2021. I think the facts will be disclosed. And we will address the facts as they are learned or continued to be learned. And we`ll learn from it and do what we need to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I`m glad we have an expert panel here because his answer right there, I don`t understand a single word of it.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, January 6 Committee Chairman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  has tested positive for COVID. The panel says Thursday`s hearing will still go ahead. Two Witnesses expected to testify. Former Deputy National Security Adviser \uc2 Matt Pottinger\uc2  and Deputy Press Secretary Sarah Matthews, who both resigned from the Trump administration shortly after the insurrection. That hearing will be led by Democrat \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2  and Republican \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2 . Both military veterans.\par \par \uc2 Today they heard closed door testimony from a former White House aide Garrett Ziegler, who work closely with Trump ally and former Trade Adviser \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The latest January 6 hearing will happen just about one mile for this guy \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  is on trial in federal court. He`s charged with contempt of Congress for refusing to comply with committee subpoenas for testimony and documents.\par \par \uc2 And as we learned today, the Georgia Grand Jury investigating Trump`s possible election interference sent subpoenas to nearly a dozen of Georgia`s fake electors. Those fake electors signed a document saying Trump won the election, even though of course, he lost in the state of Georgia. They are now fighting to block those requests.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel, a pair of Pulitzer Prize Winners from the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , Carol Leonnig, Investigative Reporter who has covered the Secret Service for years. In fact, she wrote a book on them. And Philip Rucker, Deputy National Editor, they are co-authors of \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  bestseller, "I Alone Can Fix It." And Andrew Weissmann joins us, former FBI General Counsel, Former Senior Member of the Mueller probe, he was known as the Pitbull of the Mueller team. And he`s also a beloved dog dad and a law Professor at \uc2 NYU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [23:05:28]\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, I have lots of questions for you. But there`s no way either of you are going first, because Carol. Carol Leonnig was the first to break this story today that the Secret Service, despite having deleted those text messages because of a system upgrade, they just can`t seem to get them back. Can you please explain this to us, Carol?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEONNIG, THE WASHINGTON POST INVESTIGATIVE REPORTER: Well, it`s a bit of a mess. As I think I`ve said a couple times to you privately, the Secret Service decided months before and that is true what Secretary Mayorkas said earlier, decided months before January 2021, that they were going to do an upgrade of their cell phones. But in the midst of perhaps the most traumatic and chaotic historic month in any presidency, they decided to proceed with a reset of all of the agents and officers phones in the agency, which essentially allowed individual agents to decide whether or not to upload their cell phone texts were their tax government business, should they upload them, it was up to them.\par \par \uc2 And what happened was, almost all of them concluded they weren`t going to upload their texts. And therefore, really, Steph, critical pieces of evidence are now in the wind. And now they`re -- the Secret Service has told Congress, they can`t provide them, they`re vanished.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, but Carol, they`re gone forever. Think about your own iPhone, right? When you delete something, it`s like you delete it, and then it`s in the junk file. And then it`s in the deleted file. Like there`s no way for this stuff to get retrieved. Come on?\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: So you and I have a friend in common who will remain nameless for this moment who has sent out basically a request for information from every techie in the world to answer the question, can any of this be gotten back? And I`m not going to go down the road of what`s possible? I`ll tell you one thing that a reasonable and very prominent techie told me today, which is that any agent who had a communication on a text, which had iCloud backup, any communication with that person is recoverable. And that includes other people who wiped intentionally, super wiped, deleted, had their phone reset. So there is some portion that is conceivably recoverable.\par \par \uc2 The Secret Service`s mistake here was allowing individual agents to decide and also deciding to proceed at a time when they had questions from the January 6 committee, and they were in the middle of, you know, something that no one had ever envisioned, a historic attack on the Capitol and insurrection that minutes after it began, everyone knew would be the subject of 10 investigations. Don`t forget White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  said we are going to be charged with every imaginable crime if we let this proceed.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And the whole Secret Service involved just decided to go mass delete. Andrew, do you think a lot of these agents are going to get subpoenaed?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, FORMER \uc2 FBI\uc2  GENERAL COUNSEL: Well, you know, I was thinking back to my time as FBI General Counsel, I should say, there has to be a massive inspector general investigation and heads need to roll in just even if you assume that this was not sort of intentionally trying to keep documents from this committee, the sheer incompetence of it. I mean, these -- the idea that, as Carol said, you would leave this to individual agents, when you had a pending request. And as I understand it, actually several requests from Congress, and you didn`t have a litigation hold is unbelievable. That you allowed that to go on? And so yes, I do think that there will be a lot of people who get deposed to talk about what they had on their phones, what they didn`t have. And there are a whole slew of questions here, not the least of which is why it takes the Inspector General for this to be revealed. Why didn`t the Secret Service reveal it themselves as which they should have done if it was responsible agency, as we just heard, the first response from the Secret Service was to deny that this even happened, which not a good look, and to not take responsibility for what happened and then there`s a whole issue about what actually has been heard and turned over what -- and if any agents actually preserved anything? I mean, the huge number of questions.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:06]\par \par \uc2 And finally, the ultimate question, can you get anything including metadata, which would just at least give you who is communicating with who, and potentially for how long, even if you don`t have the substance, so lots of -- lots to be continued. But this is one of the, you know, unfortunate scandals. And they said, the only really bad part of this is, you know, obstruction can be wonderful evidence in a case when you`re actually looking at obstruction by the defendant. But here, you know, unfortunately, I think that the White House and, you know, arguably, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will benefit if these documents are never retrieved.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Phil, I promise I`m going to get to you. But when Andrew brought up heads rolling, Carol, I have to ask you about James Murray. This is the guy who`s the Director of the Secret Service, who Trump put in this job, and he didn`t put him in the job in the beginning of his administration, it was year three, when Trump had learned how do I put my loyalists, my boys in these key positions. And lo and behold, James Murray, beginning of this month, resigned from the Secret Service, and he`s headed to \uc2 Snapchat\uc2  at the end of the month, the one place that specializes in deleted messages. What can you tell -- or messages that disappear, excuse me. What can you tell us about him?\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: Well, I`m glad you mentioned the point about the choice of Jim Murray as director in year three, because as Phil and I documented over and over again, by that year, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had figured out how to move the levers. And what he had actually done was asked Tony Ornato to be his Director of Secret Service. And \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  also had a soft spot for Ornato. They felt that he was very, shall we say, helpful. He was very willing to accede to their demands. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wanted Tony to be his director. But Tony Ornato said, no, no, no, no, I`ll do another job for you. And please have my buddy Jim Murray, be the Director.\par \par \uc2 Many people who work in the Secret Service have said to me, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  figured out how do I get what I want from the Secret Service time and time again? How do I get campaigns during COVID spikes that are lethal? How do I get the clearing of the peaceful demonstrators for a photo-op after Black Lives Matter protests outside the White House? How do I get all this done? That is not so in any Secret Service Security book. I install the people who say yes to me all the time. And Tony Ornato was indeed one of the President`s greatest yes, man. And now he`s an illustration, Steph, of how politicized the Secret Service became during Trump`s administration, something Phil and I found in agency after agency.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, Trump got exactly what he wanted. And now this massive swath of deleted messages, maybe the greatest gift ever.\par \par \uc2 Phil, I know, the National Archives is now dealing with these text. Now, they have interest in them. Explain this to us and beyond them, demanding them. Are there any consequences here? I mean, how scary are the National Archives when they come after it?\par \par \uc2 PHILIP RUCKER, THE WASHINGTON POST WHITE HOUSE BUREAU CHIEF: I don`t know how scary they really are, Steph. I mean, certainly, there are consequences to the reputation of the Secret Service, which has suffered years of reputational damage, as Carol, first among every other reporter has been documenting with security lapses and other failures by the Secret Service. But this just kind of reinforces for people. This -- the belief among many that the Secret Service in the Trump years at least summoned a secret service. And certainly the leadership of the Secret Service was politically tainted that these were people working for Trump and on his behalf instead of for the institution. And on behalf of the country in this just shows further doubt about that. And I think that is a long term damage for the Secret Service as an agency in terms of what sort of legal consequences there could be. I`m not certain perhaps others on the panel know what those could possibly be. But you know, if the texts are not recoverable, there`s not much that the archives is going to be able to do to get them.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Andrew, as long as we`re talking reputational damage, let`s talk about the Department of Justice. Every January 6 hearing is bombshell after bombshell, is this an absolute embarrassment for the Department of Justice? Shouldn`t they have revealed a whole lot of these things for the last 18 months and done something about it?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Well, I`m not sure they should have revealed it but they certainly should have done something about it. I personally think that it is a bit of an embarrassment as terrific as the department has been in prosecuting the sort of lower level people who attacked the Capitol and also making a case against the Proud Boys and Oath Keepers. Those are very complicated, difficult cases. That`s the plus side.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:25]\par \par \uc2 But in terms of taking on, you know, the politically fraught issue of going after people at the White House, you know, unfortunately, I think we`ve seen scarce evidence that that has been undertaken. And we`ve seen a lot more activity, both in Congress and in state, prosecution, that`s made headlines including today. Normally, that is the kind of thing that the Department of Justice leads on and doesn`t follow.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Phil, we`ve got two big witnesses we know we`re going to hear from on Thursday night. And for everyone who says these hearings are a partisan sham. These witnesses are former members of the Trump administration, right, former Deputy National Security Adviser \uc2 Matt Pottinger\uc2 . The committee has already heard from him privately, the fact that he`s going to testify live, what does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 RUCKER: Well, Steph, it`s pretty important because \uc2 Matt Pottinger\uc2  was not some junior aide at the White House. He was somebody at the president side, through many of the historic moments of the presidency. And in the run up to January 6, he`s a former journalist actually worked in Asia for The Wall Street Journal. But it`s a real national security wonk and rose quickly through the ranks in the Trump years to really take on a big portfolio on foreign policy there. And he became, according to our reporting, deeply concerned with how the President was reacting in the aftermath of the 2020 election, with the fraud claims that were just not true, worried about what it meant to stay working there and actually resigned the day after January 6. He was part of that resignation. He`s obviously out of the Trump fold now. And I think his willingness to testify live in the big primetime hearing, kind of tells you something about what you might hear from him. I would very much expect him to be not favorable to Trump in the way he answers some of these questions.\par \par \uc2 The other person speaking by the way, Thursday night is Sarah Matthews, who was a Deputy Press Secretary. She worked right under \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 . She`s not a name that a lot of viewers would necessarily know. She was more of a behind the scenes figure. But she actually worked very hard on January 6 that afternoon to try to get the President to issue a call to his supporters to stand down, to go home. She was truly shaken by what she was seeing on television at the insurrection and the attack unfolded. And when the President refused to promptly take action and lead, she decided to resign. She went down to her desk, packed up her high heels and her other personal items and walked out the door never to return.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Carol, I got to ask you this, despite everything that we have learned not just over the last 18 months, but specifically throughout these hearings. Just today, we learned that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  urged the Wisconsin Assembly Speaker just this month to overturn Biden`s win in 2020. Is this a preview of a 2024 campaign? The fact -- is Trump still on this, I won. I mean, give me a break.\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: Steph, I think I`m going to quote an anonymous source I recently interviewed if I may, who`s described, you know, what they experienced in 2021, the walk up to the attack, the insurrection they felt was building and what their warned me was, blood will be in the streets in 2024 if Trump runs and loses, blood will be in the streets if Trump runs in 2024 and wins. There will be this incitement to violence in many people`s view, because this is a person who`s going to challenge the -- every election is going to challenge the accuracy of things that are accurate. We`ve already seen that happened. And what is that going to stoke? Anger and fear and resentment among the people who believe what he is saying?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, then maybe this is time for that anonymous source with that ominous prediction to reveal him or herself and put democracy first. Hope they`re watching.\par \par \uc2 Carol Leonnig, Phil Rucker, Andrew Weissmann and of course, your dog, Innis (ph) who joined us this evening sleeping just -- I mean, Andrew has a huge, beautiful apartment. We`ve seen it many nights. I just love the fact that when the cameras roll, Innis is like, yo yo yo, I got to get in there. I think it`s fantastic. All right thank you all.\par \par \uc2 [23:20:09]\par \par \uc2 Coming up, as House Democrats fight to protect abortion rights, one group is willing to build a firewall for abortion on the local level. What does that even look like? We`re going to ask the person building it.\par \par \uc2 And later more questions and anger in Uvalde after the blistering report on law enforcement failed response to the shooting. Texas State Senator Roland Gutierrez is here on what he is hearing from the families tonight. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a very busy Thursday -- Tuesday, I don`t even know what day it is.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:25:17]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PROFESSOR LEAH LITMAN, UNIVERSITY OF MICHIGAN LAW SCHOOL: The abortion bans and the fervor of the anti-abortion movement have a broad chilling effect on reproductive healthcare. What we are seeing is that just because a law does not explicitly prohibit a procedure, that does not ensure that the procedure will be available when it is actually needed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Roe versus Wade being overturned has disrupted health care across this nation. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reporting on a story of a Wisconsin woman who was turned away from a hospital while she was experiencing a miscarriage. By the time she found a doctor who gave her the appropriate medications. The woman had been bleeding intermittently for days putting her increased risk of hemorrhage or infection, not to mention the emotional distress.\par \par \uc2 In Washington, 17 House Democrats were arrested today and an abortion rights rally outside the Supreme Court this afternoon. But the immediate battles are happening in the States, which is why the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  is working to create a firewall to protect reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Kary Moss, she`s Acting National Political Director for the \uc2 ACLU\uc2 . Kary, thank you for joining us. I want to start with explain this to us, what would a firewall look like and what states you`re targeting?\par \par \uc2 KARY MOSS, ACTING NATIONAL POLITICAL DIRECTOR FOR ACLU: Well, first of all, let me say thank you so much for having me today. And let`s be clear, we are fighting right now for the political future of our country. And we are fighting for the personal safety, the privacy, the freedom to travel, the liberty of women in this nation.\par \par \uc2 We can`t look to the federal courts now. We have to look to the states and we are well positioned to do that with affiliates in every single state. A firewall strategy is basically going to be four big things. First of all, we want to protect the right to reproductive freedom in Michigan last week, we turned in over 750,000 signatures to put an initiative on the ballot in November that would enshrine the right to reproductive freedom to prenatal care, to reproductive health care for all Michigan women. We`re doing the same in Vermont, we want to enshrine a ballot initiative there to protect reproductive liberty. That`s our first firewall.\par \par \uc2 Our second is just defending the right in places like Kansas where the state Supreme Court has found a right to reproductive freedom in this state constitution. There is now an initiative on the ballot that would try to strip women of that right. We are fighting back.\par \par \uc2 Third, our firewall. We want to hold politicians accountable. We want to hold officials 