 Just moments ago, the panel subpoenaed the Secret Service over those missing text messages from January 6, and the day before.\par \par \uc2 And today, all nine January 6 committee members met with the DHS Inspector General, their focus, trying to retrieve that missing information.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2 , (D) MARYLAND HOUSE SELECT COMMITTEE ON JANUARY 6: It`s obviously an alarming thing to learn that there were Secret Service text messages from January 6 itself and also the day before that were deleted as part of a device replacement program. We don`t know what the facts are, and we`re going to get to the facts about why that happened. And we`re going to do whatever we can to retrieve the substance of those texts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And earlier today, the panel made it official Thursday 8 p.m. Eastern will be their next hearing. That is when they will reveal what Trump was or wasn`t doing when his supporters were rioting at the Capitol. The Committee is keeping the possibility of future hearings still open. And one panel member says this thing is far from over.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF, (D) CALIFORNIA JAN. 6 SELECT COMMITTEE: We are far from wrapping up our work. The investigation goes on. And we might have had some anticipation that we would be closer to the end of the investigation by now. But the reality is witnesses continue to come forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The Committee Chairman weighed in today on recent reporting that there are talks about pushing for interviews with former VP \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BENNIE THOMPSON\uc2 , (D) MISSISSIPPI JAN.6 SELECT COMMITTEE CHAIR: It would add to the body of work for the Committee. If the Vice President, for that matter, the president, two would come and tell us what they know about January 6, and everything else, so.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So are you guys thinking of subpoenaing?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: We`ve had a discussion, but we`ll come to a definitive answer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Also tonight, the Justice Department is backing the committee`s subpoena for former Trump White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 . It came as part of a court filing that said former advisors to presidents who have left office are not absolutely immune from congressional subpoenas.\par \par \uc2 Last month, the DOJ decided not to pursue criminal charges against Meadows for defying his January 6 subpoena.\par \par \uc2 Today, the panel did get a chance to question the former Overstock.com CEO for over seven hours. \uc2 Patrick Byrne\uc2  was in a December 2020 meeting. You remember that December 18th night, when Trump and election deniers pushed to cease voting machines that wild night at the White House.\par \par \uc2 We`re also following other major stories tonight. Today, the House passed two separate bills to restore protections for abortion rights. But here`s the thing, neither Bill has the votes to advance in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden is on his first presidential visit to Saudi Arabia. The President was criticized for a fist bump with Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , ahead of their meeting today. During that meeting, Biden said he brought up the murder of \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  journalist Jamal Khashoggi.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: With respect to the murder of Khashoggi, I raised it at the top of the meeting, making it clear what I thought of it at the time, and what I think of it now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What was the Crown Prince`s response to your comments about Khashoggi?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: He basically said that he was not personally responsible for it. I indicated that he probably was.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: President Biden comes home this weekend.\par \par \uc2 With that, we`ve got a lot to dig into tonight. So let`s get smarter with the help of our lead off panel. Former U.S. Attorney General \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2  joins us. He will be with us for the entire hour. He is the author of "Our Unfinished March: The Violent Past and Imperiled Future of the Vote-A History, a Crisis, a Plan." Leigh Ann Caldwell joins us tonight, a live anchor with \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  where she is leading the coverage on the January 6 hearings and gun talks. She also co-authors the papers early 202 Morning Newsletter. And Luke Broadwater is here, Pulitzer Prize Winning Congressional Reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [23:05:05]\par \par \uc2 Luke, the January 6 committee is now subpoenaing the Secret Service records. But here`s the thing, if the Secret Service already deleted these messages, how do they get them back?\par \par \uc2 LUKE BROADWATER, THE NEW YORK TIMES CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER: Yeah, it`s a great -- it`s a great question. This has consumed the January 6 committee`s discussions over the last two days, there is some belief on the committee that there are perhaps some high tech ways to recover these messages that no message is ever truly deleted or lost.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know. You know, I`m not a tech genius. But apparently there is -- there are discussions about how they could get them back. So what this subpoena is doing is it`s letting the Secret Service know very clearly, we want to do everything possible to recover these text messages. The Secret Service says this is sort of an innocent mistake that this was not a nefarious plot to delete these messages on purpose. It happened almost by accident as they were getting new phones and shifting data.\par \par \uc2 But that said, the Committee still wants these text messages. And so they are letting the Secret Service know you`re going to do everything possible to get them. And we want to know exactly why they were lost, why they were deleted, and anything you`ve done to address these problems with your data. And that`s why they`re requesting those after action reports as well.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Eric, do you think it was an innocent mistake?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER, FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL: I think it`s a little early to tell whether or not it was a mistake. I don`t know whether there`s any kind of criminal liability here. But it was certainly an administrative blunder, given the fact that you had phones that apparently had information from January the fifth, January the sixth. And if there was this migration that was regularly scheduled, somebody should have said, well, maybe we want to hold off with regard to the phones that have information before January, the sixth, maybe a little after January the sixth. Migration, I`m sure is something that could have waited.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, nobody said hold off. That happened then and here we are now. How unusual is it, Eric, for this committee, part of Congress to subpoena a federal agency like this?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: You know, it`s -- I guess it`s somewhat unusual. But it also seems to me that`s a logical and appropriate thing to do here to find out exactly what were the circumstances under which these this material was potentially lost, and then to try to access that material to the extent that it can be recreated.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are depends on the state of the phones themselves, where they are, and a whole variety of other factors. But there are mechanisms, there are ways in which those things might be retrieved or in some ways recreated. So the subpoena makes a lot of sense.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE Leigh Ann, according to committee members, there`s no sign that this investigation is winding down. We could get hearings, maybe even into the fall. No doubt there`s tons of information. But if we do, don`t we need to start getting conclusions and consequences before the midterms come?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, THE WASHINGTON POST LIVE ANCHOR: Well, yeah, and that`s what the committee`s goal is, or at least was. But these committees have shown -- or these hearings have shown that the committee keeps learning more and more information as they hold more and more hearings. So as these committee investigation has gone public, more people have come forward, who would have thought a week or two ago that the Secret Service was going to be part of this investigation? You know that bombshell testimony from Cassidy Hutchinson, directly or indirectly led to Pat Cipollone, coming in to talk to the committee.\par \par \uc2 And so what these hearings have done, which was an attempt to bring the public into the committee`s investigation has also actually advanced the committee`s investigation as well. And yeah, they have not ruled out additional hearings. They also want to do a massive 911 style report on January 6 in the lead up into it, and so they still have a lot of work to do, but they don`t have a lot of time if they really are going to wrap up around November.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Eric, we`ve mentioned your old department, the Department of Justice is essentially backing the January 6 committee`s subpoena for \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 . But here`s what I don`t get, they`re backing that subpoena, but they declined to charge Meadows for defying the subpoena a month ago. Can you explain what`s going on?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Well, it`s hard to tell exactly what the Justice Department is doing with regard to any particular witness. The filing today, though, is really a significant one. I`m not sure I`ve ever seen the Justice Department do that before, in essence, say that what the committee is trying to do is appropriate that Meadows has only qualified immunity, and that the House has met its necessary burden to get access to the information it seeks from him.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:00]\par \par \uc2 With regard to the decision not to prosecute him on the contempt itself I`ve always thought that that meant that there was something else that the Justice Department wanted to do with Meadows, that there was another angle that they wanted to pursue with him. And I may be wrong there, but my guess is that in list of people close to the president, who potentially have some criminal liability, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  is near the top of the list.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Luke, the Overstock.com former CEO or as Pat Cipollone likes to call him the Overstock person. He talked to the committee today about that December 18, meeting that wild meeting at the White House. And here`s what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PATRICK BYRNE\uc2 , FORMER CEO, OVERSTOCK: I was the one who pulled it together, frankly. So another reason that any charges should be made. I surprised Trump outside the Oval Office, and I didn`t really fully think inform Sydney and Mike, they knew that I might have an important meeting, but I`m not sure they fully knew what I was doing until we got there. So I just showed a bit of a jedi mind trick. I hope nobody got in trouble for that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, this guy`s talking about Jedi Mind Tricks. This is the same guy that the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  said that December 18 meeting was housing Swedish meatballs all night. And there you have it, flanked by Mike Lindell of my pillow and \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2 . What do they think they`re going to get from this guy?\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: Well, this was truly one of the most unhinged meetings and probably the history of any White House ever. But \uc2 Patrick Byrne\uc2 , not only was there for this crazy meeting, where we`ve had all these different accounts of, but he was actually financing a lot of the legal challenges to the 2020 election, a lot of these sorts of sham audits and sort of crazy legal theories that you saw in the in the build up to January 6, he was the money man behind a lot of them.\par \par \uc2 So I do think he does have potentially relevant information for the committee, even though he was involved in some truly crazy stuff. I mean, the idea that they came into the Oval Office and pitch the President, on seizing voting machines, using the military to seize voting machines, is the kind of stuff that`s not normally talked about in America. And so, you know, he`s been actually quite open about his role in all of this, you know, he`s sad for documentaries, and given lots of interviews. And so it is interesting, the choice to bring him in now, in the middle of the hearings, when we are hearing a lot about that December 18 meeting. I`m not exactly sure how it fits in the total puzzle, other than as one more player who was pushing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to do extreme things to stay in power.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Eric, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , the Attorney General is under huge pressure to act, especially as these hearings unfold, you once had his job, what position is he in right now? Should he act?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Well, I think that, you know, I -- it`s my view that the Justice Department is in the process of acting that there is an ongoing investigation, and we see, you know, bits and pieces of it becoming public. I think, to the extent that, you know, I could offer a little advice to the folks at the Justice Department. I would say that maybe you need to find a way in which you can appropriately reassure the American people that the Justice Department is on the case.\par \par \uc2 You know, I was prosecutor started my career in the public integrity section, where we looked at and prosecuted official corruption cases, political corruption cases, and you get a feel for these things, after a while. And I got to tell you that on the basis of what I`ve heard, what I have read about this whole January 6 matter, I think we`re going to see indictments here. Now, I don`t know if they`re going to involve ultimately, the president. But I think those near to him have criminal liability, I think people outside the White House are going to be potentially indicted as well.\par \par \uc2 And as I said, that that feel that you get as a public corruption prosecutor makes me think that DOJ is going to ultimately return indictments in connection with the attempt to stop the transfer of power back in January.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then, do you think these hearings that are revealing so, so much are actually helping the Department of Justice in that by the time when and if they act? More and more people don`t think that this is hyper partisan. They think this is simply a huge problem, and our democracy is at risk. This is far from saying this is hyper politicized. I mean, almost all of the witnesses have either been Trump appointees or employees and it feels like every passing day you hear another establishment Republican say enough is enough. They need to act.\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Yeah, I think that actually helps the Justice Department. It lays out for the American people, what the nature of the plot was, who was involved. And the fact that you have as the primary sources of this information people who either worked for the president or people who are simply Republicans, belies the notion that somehow some way that what this Justice Department is doing has a partisan motivation behind it. So I think it helps in that way.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:23]\par \par \uc2 Now, what they do can complicate prosecutions and what the Justice Department might want to do going forward. But I think in terms of educating the American people, and reassuring them, that what the Justice Department ultimately decides to do, is based only on the facts and the law. I think the January 6 committee is doing the Justice Department a great service.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Leigh Ann, the truth matters, but only if you hear it, we say it all the time. And I want our audience to understand this week, many, many people heard this awful, awful story in Ohio, a 10 year old girl was raped, and she had to flee to another state to seek abortion services. Why did she do that? Well, we all know because Roe vs. Wade was overturned. But how did the right respond? How did they talk about the story? How do they cover it? Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Primetime decided to investigate this alleged child rape. No one reported this child rape to law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Not a whisper anywhere.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We don`t know if this story is even true.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We have this abortion activist, acting as a doctor with a history of failing to report.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We`re going to fight this to the end, including looking at our licensure.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN, (R) OHIO: We learned that this illegal aliens did this heinous crime.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Don`t you think his immigration status is irrelevant? I do. He shouldn`t have been in the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I don`t even understand this. They`re denying the story. They`re weaponizing this story, this girl had to flee the state because Roe vs. Wade was overturned. That`s what the right wanted to happen. So what exactly are they doing here?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yeah, that`s an excellent question Steph. I don`t know what they`re doing. But I do know is that they thought, first, that this was -- they were blaming the media as telling lies, as being fake news, when it was actually the truth. And then when they found out it was the truth, and then they transitioned their argument to saying blaming or saying the victim -- or the perpetrator, saying that he should have been in the country because he is illegally here.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, the problem is, though, is that this is exactly what conservatives wanted. And they don`t really have an answer for it. But what they do have is, there`s a huge debate happening right now in Congress and in states around the country and on the campaign trail. What to do in instances of rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 And Republicans more and more likely to say that rape and incest unfortunate not to be an exception. And so this is just, you know, Democrats are continuing to highlight that hypocrisy and that extremism, of course, but also, you know, it raises real life questions for women and girls around the country.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It`s just extraordinary. You can have a discussion about the potential attacker and why he was in the country. And that has absolutely nothing to do with the fact that a 10 year old in the state of Ohio was unable to get safe abortion services. Those are two different and very important topics.\par \par \uc2 Leigh Ann Caldwell, Luke Broadwater, thank you both so much. \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2 , we`re not letting you go. Eric will be here for the entire hour, because we are going to focus on the threat to our democracy. As the January 6 panel highlights that danger, we`re going to look at attacks on voting rights around the country.\par \par \uc2 Plus, redistricting and what it means for your vote. We`re going to ask our experts if you are moving closer to minority rule in this country and break down the impact it`s having on our elections. A very special 11TH HOUR just getting underway on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:23:16]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: January 6, and the lies that led to insurrection have put two and a half centuries of constitutional democracy at risk. The world is watching what we do here.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R) WYOMING VICE CHAIR, JANUARY 6 COMMITTEE: There`s a reason why people serving in our government take an oath to the Constitution, as our founding fathers recognized democracy is fragile.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The January 6 committee highlighting how fragile our democracy is and how close we came to losing it. The panel points out the former president`s big lie was designed to make people doubt their legitimacy of our elections. Here`s how his own attorney general described it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL BARR, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: They obviously were influencing a lot of people of members of the public that there was this systemic corruption in the system and that their votes didn`t count and that these machines controlled by somebody else were actually determining it, which was complete nonsense.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So for the remainder of this hour, we`re going to discuss the ongoing threats to democracy from the attacks on voting rights to revelations from the Jan. 6 committee, and I want to bring in our panel of experts. Top election lawyer Marc Elias, he Founded Democracy Docket, a Democratic Party voting advocacy group, and Sophia Lin Lakin, interim Co- Director for the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  Voting Rights Project where she helps strategize voting rights litigation across the nation, and former Attorney General \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2  still with us.\par \par \uc2 Eric, we are going to talk about how our democracy is working or isn`t. How people vote and how our votes are counted. But how is all of that connected to the storming of the Capitol?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Well, I think it`s all part of a piece in the sense that our democracy is under attack we have to understand how fragile our democracy is.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:09]\par \par \uc2 I think that we are at a real dangerous point here. And I`m not being alarmist. I don`t think I`m being hyperbolic. But I`m real concerned about the state of our democracy. You saw in January this -- on January the sixth, an attempt to keep in place somebody who lost an election by 7 million votes, we have seen instances of voter suppression around the country, we have seen partisan and racial gerrymandering, we have seen attacks and the desire to have an impact on the infrastructure of our electoral system, at the lowest levels where the Republicans now are talking about, infesting the vote counters with partisan actors, something that we have never really seen before. All of this indicates that our democracy is at risk. Our democracy is under attack, and there have to be strong responses to it. If we don`t defend our democracy, we can lose it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What does that look like, when you say there have to be strong responses, like what?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Well, I certainly think that we had the possibility of, one, with regard to the legislation that didn`t make its way out of Congress in the last session. So I would hope that we if we have favorable election results in November, that would be something that Congress might go back to. But I think also the things that Marc Elias has done by bringing lawsuits in our state courts to attack the problem of gerrymandering, after the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  said you couldn`t bring those cases in federal court, we made the determination that, all right, we`ll bring them in state courts and have had remarkable success, attacking the issues there. Also, bringing lawsuits against partisan and racial gerrymandering.\par \par \uc2 And also infusing in people the desire to become more civically engaged, because the reality is that a focused, committed and determined American people, that`s the ultimate bulwark against these anti-democratic tendencies that we now see in the Republican Party. As I say, in my book, it`s kind of a line. It`s not a throwaway line. But I think it`s really true. The Republicans right now just ain`t that much into democracy. You know, that is a sad reality. It pains me to say that, but I think that, in fact, is what this iteration of the Republican Party is all about. They are comfortable with the notion of minority rule, as long as they are the ones that have power.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then Sophia, what happens to a democracy, when more and more people just start to believe that their vote won`t count, they`re not trusting the system, what happens?\par \par \uc2 SOPHIA LIN LAKIN, INTERIM CO-DIRECTOR \uc2 ACLU\uc2  VOTING RIGHTS PROJECT: I can`t think of a better way to discourage people from turning out to vote than making them believe that their vote doesn`t matter, that their voices don`t count. And that`s one of the biggest threats to our democracy. And it`s part and parcel of a larger strategy to make it more difficult for certain voters in this country to be able to access the right to vote. And there is absolutely a through line that we`ve seen, that exists between the big lie that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  actually won the 2020 re-election, the election sabotage scheme behind the January 6 insurrection, and ongoing attacks on the voting and the democratic process.\par \par \uc2 The narrative animating all of this, and fueling efforts to undermine voting rights, interfere with the electoral process, and also is fueling these new attacks and intimidation on election administrators that Eric just mentioned. So this is all rooted in racially charged allegations, suppose voter fraud, that ballot irregularities and carrots conspiracies that fueled what led to the January 6 insurrection. And will continue to fight back and we`ve done this for four centuries and effect will continue. It`s going to take a lot of effort. But I hope that people aren`t discouraged. But it is -- the rollback is quite large, and the attacks are ugly as they`ve been throughout history.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But it`s easy to get discouraged. Marc, when you are fighting these voter suppression laws. How often do you hear the justification for trying to pass them is the big lie? The big lie that Bill Barr himself, Trump`s own Attorney General has said very clearly, it`s a bunch of bullshit.\par \par \uc2 MARC ELIAS, PARTNER, ELIAS LAW GROUP: Yeah, look, we know that the big lie motivated a lot of the litigation that we saw in the immediate aftermath of the of the November election. My team and I were involved in litigating 65 lawsuits, we won 64 of them. And we thought that that would have put to rest, the notion that somehow \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did not lose fair and square. But instead what we`ve seen is the Republican Party take the big lie and transform it into law. So we are seeing state after state after state engaged in enacting new laws and new practices that make it harder for people to vote and easier to subvert the election results.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:11]\par \par \uc2 So it`s not just that we are seeing voter suppression, but we`re actually seeing the undermining of vote counting and votes certification. Right now in Pennsylvania, there are three counties that are refusing to count ballots as part of the Republican primary for Senate that were ordered to be counted as a result of a federal court decision in the Third Circuit, a state court decision. And this is the prelude to what we are going to see in 2022 and 2024, I fear.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And they`re allowed, those three counties, they`re allowed to say no, just not counting.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: No, they`re not allowed to. And they`re being sued now by the state of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So then, why they`re doing it?\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: They`re doing it, I think, because it is the way in which if you are a Republican today, you show fealty to a failed one term president. And the big lie is not viewed as an act of desperation, or an act of failure, but rather one of loyalty. And until the fever of The Big Lie breaks in the Republican Party, we will be fighting this fight in courtroom after courtroom and at ballot box after ballot box.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We`re out of time. It`s just -- it`s so mind blowing to me over and over here. Well, they`re not allowed to do that but they are. In our own lives, when we do something we`re not allowed to do, we get fired. We get arrested. We get in trouble yet over and over we here in our political system in our government right now. No, they`re not allowed to do it. They just keep doing it.\par \par \uc2 We got more to cover. You are all sticking around. When we come back, we`re going to continue this conversation and why it is so important for you to go out and vote, when the 11 Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:36:33]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WENDY DAVIS, (D) FORMER TX STATE SENATOR: We are being ruled by, literally, the tyranny of the minority. In states like Texas, where we have had radical gerrymandering, so that only primaries matter, and where we`ve been chopped into a majority of Republican seats. And those Republican candidates and officeholders believe that they only need to be held accountable to a vast minority of voters who are voting in primaries and deciding the outcomes of their elections. That`s how we wind up where we are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Republicans have never made this approach a secret. Strategist Karl Rove wrote about the GOP plan to take over state legislatures for The Wall Street Journal. Back in 2010, saying this, "He who controls redistricting can control Congress." So let`s discuss, \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2 , Marc Elias, and Sophia Lin Lakin still with us.\par \par \uc2 Eric, we mentioned it before the impact of gerrymandering, help us really understand the consequences, what they`ve been?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Well, with gerrymandering, as Wendy Davis said, you are concerned - - you`re in a safe seat. And so you`re concerned not about a general election, you`re concerned about a primary challenge, which pushes you further and further to the right, and which helps explain why you see through states now, after the Dobbs decision, Republicans taking more and more extreme positions with regard to reproductive rights, it`s all to fend off a challenger who might come in in a primary session and say that you`re not being sufficiently supportive of the Supreme Court and the anti- abortion decision. That`s one practical consequence.\par \par \uc2 And so one of the realities is, if you`re concerned about that primary election, not the general election, cooperating with people across the aisle is seen as a sign of weakness. And that leads to gridlock, which leads to a lack of action by our legislatures, which leads to cynicism in the American people. And so, you know, things that the American people are generally agreed upon background checks, for instance, with regard to gun safety, even with regard to not overturning Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 Again, generally, people around the country did not want to see that happen. You can see Republican legislatures do those kinds of things inconsistent with the desires of their constituents, and not suffer any political consequence. All of that is a function of -- and a result of partisan gerrymandering.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But Sophia, in theory, both parties can try to create districts to benefit them, is this fair to lay it out that way?\par \par \uc2 LIN LAKIN: It`s true that whoever`s in power can control the reins of the redistricting process. And that`s ultimately part of the issue. At stake, you have the fox guarding the hen house, so to speak, politicians choosing their voters instead of the way it should be where voters are choosing their politicians and representatives, people that will ultimately represent their communities. And we`re seeing pretty dramatic impacts on that on both sides of the aisle, when it comes to how it impacts voters of color, or the most marginalized voters in our communities. For the most part, gerrymandering hurts them across the board.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Marc, you`ve been suing over gerrymandered maps for months and months, what`s the latest?\par \par \uc2 [23:40:00]\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: Yeah so, I`ve been searching for years and years. The latest is that we have two cases going before the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  cases that \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2  and I and our teams have been working on. And both of them are really going to be important in the next term. The first is a case out of Alabama where the Supreme Court is reviewing a victory that we had challenging the lack of a second black opportunity district. Alabama drew one seat that black voters could elect their candidate of choice. We believed that it should be two and a three judge panel comprised of two Trump appointees in a regular point, he agreed with us. That case is now before the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . And that`s a very important case that they will decide regarding the scope of section two of the Voting Rights Act.\par \par \uc2 The other case is the big case that you hear a lot of people talking about and this fringe theory that Republicans are promoting, called the independent state legislature theory. This is out of a case out of North Carolina, where we were successful in striking down the map that the Republican legislature had drawn, which was an extreme partisan gerrymander. The state court there said that the state constitution prohibited partisan gerrymandering, and the Republicans have now brought this case to the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  on the theory that the state courts are not allowed to apply their state constitutions when looking at maps.\par \par \uc2 If this was adopted, this could have dramatic impacts not just in the area of gerrymandering, but in voting rights generally. There is no theory more closely aligned with the big lie and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  than the independent state legislature theory. It is the theory that John Eastman was peddling in the days leading up to January 6.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You are scaring me. All three of you scaring us, this is really, really important information that we need to understand more, and we need to care about more. You`re all staying with us. We`re going to be back in just a few.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, state Republicans around this country are pushing voting restrictions. The fight to protect the voting rights in Washington, though, it`s continuing there, what is at stake and why it matters on a local level? When THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:46:54]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: The so-called leaders right here in this state and in other places in the country making it more difficult for people to vote. And we know what we need to do. One of the things we need to do is pass the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Voting Rights Act and the Freedom to Vote Act. We need to elect people who will defend these rights up and down the ballot from district attorneys to state attorneys general to local sheriffs, to governors.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  making it crystal clear that the administration`s fight to protect voting rights is far from over. Since the beginning of 2021, 34 redistrict voting laws have already been passed in 18 states, 34 more are moving through 11 state legislatures right now. \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2 , Marc Elias and Sophia Lin Lakin still with us to discuss.\par \par \uc2 Sophia, I know you`ve called Protecting Voting Rights, the fight of our lives. We just heard Vice President Harris talk about the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Act and another but the problem is, they`re not going to pass in the Senate. If they don`t get something through Congress, what can this administration do? Because let`s be honest, it ain`t looking good?\par \par \uc2 LIN LAKIN: Well, you`re absolutely right, that we need comprehensive federal legislation to resolve the democratic crisis that we really are in. These waves of voter suppression, these constant tax on our electoral system. There`s we need more than what we`re doing right now. At the end of the day, you know, there are the court battles that we are fighting in courts, but the administration can certainly play a part with additional rules that they are putting in place at the administrative level, in addition to joining us on the ground in the fights and battles in the courtroom as well. We are starting to see a lot more efforts in that regard, and hope to continue to see them during the fight.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Eric, one of the reasons Congress doesn`t move is because voters don`t demand it, even though voting rights is massively important. Unfortunately, it`s an abstract for lots of people. It`s not what they think of in terms of kitchen table issues. How do you get them to understand this and care more and demand more? Because unless voters are demanding it, Congress won`t do much.\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Yeah, I think that`s a good observation. When we started the Democratic -- National Democratic Redistricting Committee back in January of 2017, and I was talking about the importance of gerrymandering. You can see people`s eyes glaze over and figuring out, what is this all about. But when you make people understand that gerrymandering, voter suppression has an impact on their day to day lives, and now we`re seeing it in very tangible ways. A woman`s right to choose is a function of what these gerrymandered legislatures, the statutes that these gerrymandered legislatures have put in place that has been attacked. Gun safety laws that have been ruled out, overruled by the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  passed originally in states that do things inconsistent again with the desires of the American people.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:09]\par \par \uc2 And so you`ve got to make people understand that this isn`t something that`s ethereal, that this is something that affects their lives on a day to day basis. Everything from the right to privacy, to the safety of their communities, criminal justice, reform, climate, all of these things are directly connected. And I think if you do that, you can get the American people involved. But this has to be a multifaceted effort. We have to have the American people engaged, focus, we also have to do things legislatively. And then we have to use great lawyers like Marc to bring cases in both federal and state courts, we`ve got to win in a whole variety of ways, in order to save this democracy of ours.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Marc, it`s almost midnight. So how about carrying on a tradition for us and scare the hell out of our audience, when it comes to actually voting and counting the votes, a position that is getting a lot more attention and it should, is a Secretary of State. Why? Because they are usually the ones charged with running state elections. And now you`ve got a whole lot of states where candidates who want that job are people who support the big lie, who would have pushed it, who would have done what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wanted secretaries of State in places like Georgia and other places to do that. How worried should we be with these people who could possibly get these jobs?\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: We should be incredibly worried. You know, before I was known for doing voting rights, I was known as a recount lawyer. And I`ve done more recounts and post-election litigation than probably any other lawyer alive today. And it is not just about the ability to vote. It`s not just the ability to have the vote counted, but it is the ability to have an accurate certification of the election. And we can`t lose sight of that last piece of the puzzle. Because if at the end of the day after 2022, a Democrat wins a Senate race in a state but the certificate of election is not signed by the governor and countersigned by the Secretary of State, that Senator doesn`t get seated in the house. That`s also true in the -- I`m sorry, in the Senate. That`s also true in the House. And if the governor and the Secretary of State don`t sign and countersign their certificate of ascertainment than the Presidential electors don`t go to Congress to be counted.\par \par \uc2 So these roles have that people have not paid a lot of attention to about how votes are counted. And then the certification process leading up to the Secretary of State is where the action is. A lot of people in your audience are hearing a lot of things about the Electoral Count Act. Before we get to how ballots get -- the electors get certified in Congress, let`s worry about how they get certified by the Secretaries of State.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s not just worry about it. Let`s do something about it. And we`re going to pay a whole lot of attention to it, right here on THE 11TH HOUR as often as we possibly can. People don`t understand it, they don`t care. We`re going to hopefully get them to care.\par \par \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2 , Marc Elias, Sophia Lin Lakin and I cannot thank you all enough. It was such an important night. Each of you made us smarter, and I sincerely appreciate it. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Coming up, roasted by a reality star Jersey style, one candidates unconventional strategy to get a bump, bump, I love whoever wrote that is going to get a kiss from me after this show. A bump in the polls when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:57:46]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN, PENNSYLVANIA LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR: This is \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . It`s a fundamental choice that we have here. I only got involved in politics to make my town then Pennsylvania better, safer place. He just moved here to run for office.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Oz moved in with his mother-in-law to qualify for the race.\par \par \uc2 Do you want someone that`s all about North Jersey? Look, he`s not one of us. He says he`ll fight for working people. OK, hey doc Hollywood. Save your money. Pennsylvania is not for sale. I`m \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . And I approved this message.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You had to know I was going to cover this story. The last thing before we go tonight, trolling Dr. Oz. If you`re willing to move in with your mother-in-law, you must really want to win. Pennsylvania`s Lieutenant Governor, you saw him right there, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . He`s been unconventional from the start. OK. A very unconventional candidate for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . At six foot eight this western PA native. He is known as a straight shooter. He`d rather wear hoodies and shorts. I`m going to guess jorts over the traditional suit and tie.\par \par \uc2 Fetterman and his team also know very, very well how to use \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to their advantage. They like to enlist the help of memes and some pretty clever clap backs. Most of them are usually aimed at his Republican opponent TV personality Dr. Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 Well, The Daily Beast recently asked Fetterman if he controlled his way to the Senate. And he responded. I mean, we sure are trying, and he certainly is. One of his frequent attacks against us is for living in Jersey before crossing lines to run for Senate as he said right, they`re moving in with his in laws. He is now selling Dr. Oz for New Jersey bumper stickers on his website. And he even had a plane fly this banner over the Jersey Shore last weekend that said, "Hey Dr. Oz Welcome home to New Jersey, Love, John."\par \par \uc2 The Jersey Shore theme continued this week when Fetterman enlisted one of my home State`s most famous reality stars. You need to watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE "SNOOKI" POLIZZI, TV PERSONALITY: Hey Mehmet. This is Nicole "Snooki" and I`m from Jersey Shore. I don`t know if you`ve seen a bit before. But I`m a hot mess on a reality show basically, and I enjoy life. But I heard that you move from New Jersey to Pennsylvania to look for a new job. And personally I don`t know why anyone would want to leave Jersey because it`s like the best place ever. And we`re all hot messes. But I want to say best of luck to you. I know you`re away from home and you`re in a new place, but Jersey will not forget you. I just want to let you know I will not forget you. And don`t worry, because you`ll be back home in Jersey soon. This is only temporary. So good luck. You got this and Jersey loves you. Mwah.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Back to the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and just about everybody else picked up "Snooki" cameo, Fetterman tweeted, we like to have fun here.\par \par \uc2 And on this Friday night, we like to have fun here too. So "Snooki" may be signing off from Seaside or wherever it is that she`s on the Jersey Shore. And here I am at home on the Jersey Shore. And I`m wishing all of you a very good and very safe night. From our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, I want to thank you for staying up late with us. I`ll see all of you next week.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220716ei7f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for July 15, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Ali Vitali, Frank Figliuzzi, Lisa Rubin, Matthew  Dowd, Carol Lee, Jake Sherman, Jonathan Lemire, Trymaine Lee \par \uc2 7,629 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good afternoon. I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Today, January 6 investigators want to know if critical information from the cellphones of Secret Service agents is gone forever. NBC News has learned that the Secret Service notified the committee that text messages from January 5th and 6th were erased and that it happened after those records were requested by the DHS Inspector General. Here`s committee member Jamie Raskin on Morning Joe.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2 , (D-MD): We don`t know what the facts are -- and we`re going to get to the facts about why that happened and we`re going to do whatever we can to retrieve the substance of those texts. And, you know, fortunately, the chair of our committee, \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2 , is also the chair of the Homeland Security Committee so he`s very well versed in this kind of thing, and we`ll get to the bottom of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The Secret Service claims the texts were deleted as part of a "device replacement program, not due to malicious intent." That said, those text messages could shed light on key incidents, the first the one recounted by Cassidy Hutchinson when former President Trump allegedly tried to grab the wheel of his car to drive to the Capitol. The second incident involves former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  escorted out of the Capitol by the Secret Service on the 6 and then told to get into his armored limousine.\par \par \uc2 In the book, I Alone Can Fix It, Pence is quoted as telling agents, if I get in that vehicle, you guys are taking off. I`m not getting in the car. Something Raskin once called the six-most chilling words of this entire thing I`ve seen so far. That all brings us to the development that`s still unfolding the question of where the former vice president fits into this investigation.\par \par \uc2 Last night, \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  dropped an enticing possibility when talking with the Wall Street Journal, that the committee is still considering either a written interview with Pence or possibly issuing a subpoena to compel him to testify. In fact, the committee met last night to discuss that very question, although, Chairman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  said afterward, no decision has been made yet.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC Capitol Hill Correspondent Ali Vitali. Frank Figliuzzi served as assistant director for counterintelligence at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and is an MSNBC national security analyst. Lisa Rubin is a legal analyst. And Matthew Dowd is an MSNBC political analyst and founder of Country Over Party.\par \par \uc2 Ali, do we know anything about the debate within the committee about what to do about getting information from \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Look, that`s sort of been one of the open questions this entire investigation, Chris, whether or not they should be going for the biggest fish of all, which is the former vice president or even the former president himself? These are questions that we`ve been asking over the course of the last few weeks. And, of course, Congressman Kinzinger spurred a lot of people`s attention when he said that that was one of the things that was under active discussion.\par \par \uc2 Our team was just with Chairman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2 . He declined to comment, ignoring a question on this very issue. But it`s clearly something that they are still debating as they try to start wrapping up the threads here as they head into what we think is the final at least in the summer series of hearings that comes in just a week on Thursday in primetime is what our reporting is.\par \par \uc2 But there`s also new information in regards to those Secret Service deleted text messages. We can now report that the January 6 Committee this morning met with the Inspector General, and he briefed them on this question of those deleted Secret Service texts. That`s two sources familiar confirming to us just in the last few minutes, Chris. And what it says, again, is that this committee is embarking on a still active investigation, even as they are actively laying out what new they`re learning from the American -- from their investigation and telling it to the American public.\par \par \uc2 And even as I`m waiting out here, I keep tracking the steps behind me because the House is actively voting. And this is the time that we start getting information in from those lawmakers as they are in and out of the House floor. So as you and I keep talking, we`ll keep reporting and see if there`s anything new that we can bring to you too.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, absolutely. Come back to us if you get anything, of course.\par \par \uc2 So, Frank, let`s talk about these text messages. The Washington Post`s Carol Leonnig, who might be a better source than any other reporter when it comes to the Secret Service, wrote this. "The Secret Service has had a history of important records disappearing under cover of night and agency staff members refusing to cooperate when investigators came calling seeking information."\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 If we don`t have the text messages here, what`s the likelihood the committee could get the straight story say from the agents themselves?\par \par \uc2 FRANK FIGLIUZZI, FORMER \uc2 FBI\uc2  ASSISTANT DIRECTOR FOR COUNTERINTELLIGENCE: This is quite a mess to use a technical term. And look, the Secret Service has been just rife with scandal and issues and public perceptions. They`ve put -- it`s important to say, Chris, they`re pushing back hard on the allegation from the inspector general, even not agreeing on the timing of his request.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the bottom line. Even if you take everything they say at face value, these texts disappeared. And if they disappeared because of a pre- planned routine transition to a new phone system plug, well, OK, but you still, in terms of leadership, should have known that this was happening.\par \par \uc2 In fact, they can see we knew we were losing tax in this phone transition. They should have known -- this agency under a microscope that we just had an attempted coup. They`re going to be very much in the middle of an investigation, yet apparently, leadership didn`t say, timeout on the transmission, timeout. Everybody save their communications related to in any way to January five or six. Well, that didn`t happen, and so it`s a -- it`s a leadership issue.\par \par \uc2 Can these texts be recovered forensically? Yes, there`s a possibility that that could happen. The January 6 committee is going to have to get forensic experts in on this. And this isn`t the first time we`ve heard about an agency losing important texts or e-mails. It happened at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , you`ll recall in that they struck situation with the Russia investigation, DOJ found there was a phone problem and texts and e-mails were lost, some of them were recovered, some of them not.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Lisa, I see you nodding. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 LISA RUBIN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: You know, this is as Frank said, just one big mess. And one of the things I`m really interested in is whether \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is going to be a part of carrying that mess, Chris, you raised earlier in the hour, whether or not \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is going to come in. And my response to that is \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is going to come in if \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  wants to come in.\par \par \uc2 This isn`t the first time we`ve heard the committee float the possibility of \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  as a witness. So you have to ask yourself, why are we having this discussion again, particularly given that any litigation over whether Pence will be a witness before this committee would take well longer than the process that they seem to have laid out and the schedule that they have?\par \par \uc2 There`s a possibility that the former vice president sees the tide turning against former President Trump, particularly among likely Republican primary voters, and he has information that he wants to share. And his lawyers have signaled that through back channels to the committee. I`m really interested in seeing whether as the days unfold, Vice President Pence, like former White House Counsel, \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , will be what looks to be a reluctant and belated witness, but a witness nonetheless, in front of this committee.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to remind people, Matthew, just how important this moment is, when \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  gets pulled out of the Capitol proper, goes down into the garage, and then is told by Secret Service to get in the car and refuses to get in -- one congressional official told Intercept. "People need to understand that if Pence had listened to the Secret Service and fled the Capitol, this could have turned out a whole lot worse. It could have been a successful coup, not just an attempted one." That could have been the critical moment of the entire day, Matthew?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW DOWD, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, it`s one of a series of critical moments, obviously in this.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, it`s true.\par \par \uc2 DOWD: And add to that, Chris -- add to that, Chris, the idea that there were already threats and there was worry about violence to \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  to begin with, which it seems from what we`ve heard from testimony, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was found encouraging. And then when he found out that people wanted to hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  in the midst of this, the president -- the former president of the United States was basically happy with that, like he under -- like that he was like, I understand that I`m mad at \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  in the course of this. And so I would love the former vice president to testify. I think it`s probably politically a mistake to get into a weeks` or months` long wrangling over what kind of testimony if he`s going to get written testimony or public testimony, or all of that.\par \par \uc2 To me, we`re at the point of we`ve made a two-layer cake, and then we`ve added a third layer to the cake and now we got the fourth layer to the cake and a fifth layer and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  would be important but he`s going to be the seventh or eighth layer. I think, at present, the evidence they`ve already been presented is compelling enough, as Frank and I have had the conversation before to not only open an investigation but indict the former president in relation to this. Again, I would love it. But as earlier, somebody said, it`s going to be totally up to \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . Getting months` long debate on this, I think is probably a mistake for the committee.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, Frank, you and Matthew have talked on this program before about these very things. And I guess part of it comes down to because we know and we heard just again this morning, that they`re continuing -- the committee is continuing to get in new information, continuing to look at other possible people who would come in and testify. But at what point do they have to say both politically, logistically, and realistically knowing that there is an election coming up, that Democrats could lose control of the House? Does all of this really have to land in the DOJ`s lap and they have to pick up the ball here?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: Yes, ultimately. Of course, that`s where the tough calls are going to be made at DOJ. But look, I think because of the timetable that the committee is facing, because of midterms and the fact that they might be -- might go away at some point, they`ve got to do as fulsome investigation as possible, and they`ve done one already, as Matthew said. But I don`t see how you do a fulsome investigation without at least asking the vice president to show up.\par \par \uc2 He`s a key player. It would -- he -- you know, it`s conspicuous by his absence, if he -- if he did not get even asked to show up. So we should see that happen just for the -- for the benefit of understanding that they gave it a try.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So last night, they had the meeting and, Matthew, also today the committee is meeting with former Overstock CEO Patrick Byrne, as we`ve heard from \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  earlier this week, he was in that infamous chaotic six-hour long meeting with then-President Trump on December 18, a reminder.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAT CIPOLLONE, FORMER TRUMP WHITE HOUSE COUNSEL: First of all, the Overstock person I`ve never knew -- I never knew who this guy was. Actually, the first thing I did, I walked in, I looked at him, I said, who are you? And he told me, I don`t think -- I don`t think any of these people will provide the president with good advice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I would imagine, Matthew, that this isn`t just about hearing about that meeting per se, but why he was in that meeting in the first place? I mean, obviously, it speaks, among other things, to the kind of people that the former president was listening to at that point.\par \par \uc2 DOWD: Yes. I mean, Chris, that`s actually exactly what I was talking about. It shows the fact that he was ignoring the counsel at the White House -- his counsel at the White House, he was ignoring the attorney general in the United States. He was ignoring three or four other counsels at the White House with vast experience. He was going to ignore all of them and their advice, but I`m going to go to the Overstock guy.\par \par \uc2 And the reason he was going to the Overstock guy and that litany of other nut jobs that he was had in that meeting was because they were telling him what he wanted to hear. He was searching for somebody, and he landed on Overstock CEO, which is amazing that the Overstock CEO is giving him constitutional or legal advice in the midst of this related to voter crimes. That just shows the desperation upon which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wanted to find anybody who would go along with his crazy ideas.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We don`t know for sure, Lisa, exactly who might be giving the president advice. Now we have some speculation. We know people he`s talked to that have gone to Mar-a-Lago to, as they say, kiss the ring. But Politico has the headline today that says announcing his 2024 campaign could be Trump`s best legal defense. It would obviously change the optics, but would it really change the calculation in the minds of federal or state prosecutors about whether to bring an indictment?\par \par \uc2 RUBIN: It definitely could, Chris. And here is why. The Department of Justice has a long-standing policy that persists from administration to administration that in federal election years, they are particularly careful with taking overt investigative or prosecutorial steps with respect to people who are candidates for federal office. In practice, that`s meant that the Department of Justice hasn`t brought new charges within 60 to 90 days of a primary or a general election.\par \par \uc2 Now, if the former president declares that he`s a candidate for president in 2024, does he bring himself within that protection? Does he count as a candidate for federal office within that DOJ policy? That`s unclear, but he seems to be banking on the fact that it would make it all the more difficult for the Department of Justice that is aware of what they call election year sensitivities to either take further overt investigative steps against him or to indict him. That`s certainly part of the calculus as well as the political considerations of just clearing the fields and making it easier for him to raise money.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So with all the moving parts, Ali, what are the chances -- what are you hearing that next week`s hearing, which is supposed to be the big wrap-up, right, might not be the big wrap-up?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes, you`re talking to no more interested party, and the answer to that question than me and our colleagues up here on the Hill. But that`s the question we continue to answer. And look, the committee has done --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And may I add? Your exhausted colleagues on the Hill.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Our very -- our very indefatigable colleagues up here on the Hill.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, better.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: But look, the question that we`ve continued to ask, Chris, is really that this is a committee that has shown a willingness to continue to include what it`s finding in real time and that requires a flexibility. They laid out a schedule for seven or eight hearings at the outset of this and that`s exactly where we`ve ended up. But the information hasn`t stopped flowing and it depends on if they continue to get more cooperation.\par \par \uc2 The people that I`m thinking, for example, is Chairman Thompson has said he wants to get documents from \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  before they even entertain doing some kind of deposition with \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 . Those documents could be illuminating but they`re certainly not on the timeline of having to come in in the next four or five days so that they can make that Thursday in primetime hearing.\par \par \uc2 We also know and what the chairman told me the other day, is that when they released their final report sometime in the early fall, that they want to do a hearing around that release so that we at least know that we`re getting one more hearing potentially in the early fall when that release comes out. And so they`re all leaving the door open to this, making us wonder at what point they finally put the pen down here and stop fact- finding and just say, OK, this is what we found out, this is what we`re presenting.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Congress may have an August recess, but we will continue to be tireless in our pursuit of this story. Thank you so much for that. Ali Vitali, Frank Figliuzzi, Lisa Rubin, Matthew Dowd, always great to see all of you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 This morning, it`s happening. The highly anticipated and controversial meeting between President Biden and Saudi Arabia`s Crown Prince, the high- stakes politically and maybe for the price you pay at the pump. Plus, back home, how West Virginia Senator Joe Manchin has once again thrown a wrench into the Democrats` agenda. So now what for the Democrats? You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now, President Biden is in the most consequential and controversial meeting of his Middle East trip with Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  of Saudi Arabia. This is the video they call it, the pool spray, the pictures that were taken the video from that meeting that started a little more than an hour ago with a fist bump. A diplomatically fraught moment since MBS, as he is widely known, is believed by U.S. intelligence to have ordered the murder of \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  journalist Jamal Khashoggi. It was a brief, unsmiling moment. And now the question is whether Biden will come away from the meeting with the promise of stepped-up oil production or more.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Carol Lee is traveling with the president. She joins me now from Jeddah, Saudi Arabia. So, Carol, I think even more than on previous diplomatic trips, body language has been carefully scrutinized. For the White House, though the question will ultimately be was taking this controversial trip worth it? What have we seen so far?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEE, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that`s absolutely right, Chris. You hit the nail on the head. Is this going to be worth it? The president has come under so much criticism from members of his own party, human rights activists, the family of Jamal Khashoggi, and others and saying that he should never have made this visit. So, the president saying take -- saying that there are big security issues at stake that are a lot of U.S. interests at stake and that`s why he`s doing this. So what he comes back with will be critical, and we don`t know what he may have gotten out of that meeting just yet.\par \par \uc2 But going into that meeting, we saw that fist bump, the body language, the choreography of this, everything was so scrutinized on the front end, and then by the media, obviously, while it was happening. And when the White House press pool was brought into that meeting, it was very limited coverage. There was no audio. We couldn`t hear what either of them were saying. And our own Peter Alexander shouted a question at \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  asking if he would apologize to the family of Khashoggi. Let`s take a listen to that moment and the \uc2 Crown\uc2  Prince`s response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Jamal Khashoggi, will you apologize to his family, sir?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: President Biden, is said Saudi Arabia is still a pariah?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you, guys. Just go. Let`s just walk.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: President Biden?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Saudi Arabia is a pariah.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEE: So, you can see the Crown Prince sort of seems to smirk upon hearing that question. Peter Alexander says that a Saudi aid tightly grabbed his arm after he asked that question. And another U.S. reporter, Chris, asked President Biden if Saudi Arabia was still a pariah, so that in reference to the president -- now President Biden calling Saudi Arabia or saying that he would make them a pariah state if he were to win office, now sitting across the table from Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As for the rest of the agenda, obviously, Human Rights was front and center. The president was asked going into this meeting if he was going to raise Khashoggi`s murder directly with the Crown Prince, he`s declined to commit to doing that, other issues that are on the table are Iran and obviously, oil, Chris, is President Biden going to come back with any commitments on oil to help lower those gas prices back at home.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Carol Lee, thank you. And just to give it a little context, Carol has been in these meetings before, and I`ve been in these meetings before. It`s not unusual, including after an uncomfortable question has been asked for folks to be ushered out -- the press to be ushered out for somebody to grab your arm is unusual. I`m sure we`ll hear more about that. Carol, thank you.\par \par \uc2 This morning, another devastating photo, a stark reminder of the cost of Russia`s war on Ukraine. According to President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , among the dead in this latest missile strike, 4-year-old Liza. Here in this photo, you see her lifeless body, her shoes covered in blood. Her mother had posted a video to social media from near the strike site just moments before the attack.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 A local charity says they were on their way to a speech therapy class.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Speaking a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Liza`s mother was badly wounded. Zelenskyy says Russia is deliberately targeting civilians.\par \par \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittany Griner is back in court today for the fourth hearing in her trial. And now, the case is adjourned for 11 days after her defense team requested more time to prepare. Griner`s team submitted 20 character references today after a teammate and executive from her Russian team testified on her behalf yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers also introduced medical records indicating a history of injuries and severe chronic pain. Documents from Arizona`s Department of Health show she was OK to use cannabis for medical purposes, and also show a series of negative doping test results. Calls for Griner`s release have increased in recent weeks. Her guilty plea last week may expedite efforts to get her released.\par \par \uc2 And today, the new push for restricting abortion, legislation that would have forced a 10-year-old rape victim to have the baby. What a national right to life official is now saying? The reporter behind this story joins me next. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now on Capitol Hill House Democrats are voting on two bills that would restore and guarantee abortion access nationwide. So far one bill which would codify abortion right protections has passed. They`re taking a public stance, though the proposals are doomed in the 50-50 Senate.\par \par \uc2 The ongoing fight escalated by the case of a 10-year-old girl who had to go to Indiana for an abortion because of restrictive laws in Ohio. Here`s a lawyer at the court proceeding for her accused rapist.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s my understanding she in fact just turned 10 years old.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Just turned 10 years old. Meantime, the Republican Indiana attorney general is considering prosecuting the OB-GYN who performed that abortion, and the top lawyer with the organization Right to Life tells "Politico" that he thinks the 10-year-old girl should have had the baby.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Meghan Messerly, a health care reporter for "Politico" who spoke to that official. Also joining me Dr. Christine Brandy, an OB-GYN and board chair of the group Physicians for Reproductive Health. She just testified on Capitol Hill about abortion access.\par \par \uc2 So, Megan, give us some context about that official, Jim Bob. What else did he have to tell you?\par \par \uc2 MEGAN MESSERLY, POLITICO REPORTER: Yes, so my colleague Adam Wren and I reported on Jim Bob`s remarks yesterday. Jim Bob has authored a piece of model legislation that now multiple state legislatures may be considering using as the basis for future abortion related restrictions, and what he told us is that under his model legislation that 10-year-old girl would have had to carry that pregnancy to term because the bill proposes no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Now it is worth noting that if you read the text of the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2  model legislation it does note that it may be necessary in some states to include rape and incest exceptions, sort of foreseeing that there may be political obstacles there and recommends language in that case, but as Jim Bob told us that is not their ideal position on the matter.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And in fact, Doctor, many of these new abortion laws and proposals have no exceptions for rape and incest, although they may to save the life of the mother. A definition that can be legally tricky even as an adult, right. There`s a lot of questions about how that gets defined state to state if the mother`s life is in danger. But this is a child. That`s not in dispute. And an average 10-year-old girl is 70 pounds and about 4.5 feet tall.\par \par \uc2 What would pregnancy even look like and be like for a 10-year-old?\par \par \uc2 DR. KRISTYN BRANDI, PHYSICIAN FOR REPRODUCTIVE HEALTH BOARD CHAIR: Sure, and thank you for having me. I tell all of my medical trainees on day one of OB-GYN training that pregnancy is always more dangerous than not being pregnant. And particularly for young people they have a much higher risk of complications, things like preterm labor, have retention in pregnancy, and having babies with low birth weight.\par \par \uc2 But I do want to say that no one, no matter what their age, should be forced to undergo pregnancy. It`s a medically complicated condition for everybody but particularly for young people.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Doctor, we see the Indiana attorney general considering charging the OB-GYN who gave the 10-year-old girl an abortion. Tell me about the conversations you and your fellow doctors and women in other states that are dealing with the fallout of the Supreme Court overturning Roe v. Wade are having? What are those conversations like?\par \par \uc2 BRANDI: Sure. We were worried about this from the minute that the Dobbs decision came out. First and foremost, worrying about our patients, would they be able to access essential health care services like abortion or would they have to travel hundreds and thousands of miles to get to that care. But there`s also a lot of concerns amongst doctors. One, do they even have a job right now? Are they able to provide care?\par \par \uc2 But for those of us in protective states, can we take care of people? Will we be prosecuted for doing our jobs for taking care of the people that need this care and are traveling all this way to get to us?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BRANDI: It`s really scary time (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And Megan, Senate Republicans blocked a Democratic bill Thursday that would protect the rights of women to travel to other states to access abortion care legally. This is what Oklahoma Republican Senator Lankford said on the Senate floor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JAMES LANKFORD\uc2  (R-OK): This seems to be just trying to inflame, to raise the what ifs. Millions of other Americans just asked a simple question. Does that child in the womb have the right to travel in their future? Do they get to live?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:35:08]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So I know you`ve been having these conversations in your reporting. What are they showing you, telling you about where the policy fights over abortion might be having?\par \par \uc2 MESSERLY: Yes, I think there`s a couple of different areas. One, those out- of-state concerns are not just hypothetical. We saw some legislation in the form of an amendment from a Missouri lawmaker be proposed last session to actually penalize folks who assist someone in traveling out of state to receive an abortion.\par \par \uc2 I know talking to abortion providers and abortion funds, they`ve told me that they`re concerned that they could be penalized even under existing state law for conspiracy to commit crimes. There`s concerns I know they`ve expressed to me about some of the wording in this model legislation from the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2  about aiding and abetting, so it is a real concern that a lot of folks have.\par \par \uc2 Moving forward the biggest fights are going to be in Indiana where the legislature is set to meet on July 25th in a special session to talk about restricting abortion which is currently legal up to 22 weeks of pregnancy. They`re expected to consider some sort of bill based off of that model legislation. There is expected to be conversations, though, about those rape and incest exceptions. Of the 13 states that had triggered bans when the Dobbs decision came down only five had exceptions for rape and incest and so there`s expected to be more political debate around this in Indiana and in South Carolina which is also meeting in special session likely later this year.\par \par \uc2 The lawmaker who`s heading an ad hoc committee there told me that he is not sure how the state will handle the issue of rape and incest exceptions. He personally opposes the exceptions but he doesn`t know if the votes are there among his Republican colleagues.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Meghan Messerly, Dr. Kristyn Brandi, thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 Joe Manchin is back as a roadblock to the president`s agenda, now saying no to items he once supported. What will he support? We`ll talk about it.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:23]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today Senate Democrats once again feeling the force of West Virginia Democrat Joe Manchin who once again has hit the brakes on some key parts of the president`s agenda. Months of talks with Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  seems to be going well, now Manchin says he will only support a couple of proposals.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Jake Sherman, co-founder of "Punchbowl News," and Jonathan Lemire, White House bureau chief for "Politico" and host of "WAY TOO EARLY."\par \par \uc2 Jake, I mean it sometimes feels a little bit like Lucy and the football on the table long ago maybe now, everything from universal pre-K, paid family leave, free community college, taxes on the wealthy. They were all possibilities.\par \par \uc2 Where are we now and is there any wiggle room left from Joe Manchin?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SHERMAN, PUNCHBOWL NEWS CO-FOUNDER: No, not really, and it`s funny you mentioned that because a White House official, very senior White House official said to me just today that they felt like it was Lucy and the football, so that`s an apt comparison.\par \par \uc2 So here`s where we are. Manchin said this morning on a Hoppy Kercheval`s radio show, a very popular West Virginia radio call-in show which he`s a frequent guest on, that he wants to see the next round of inflation numbers before he makes a decision on a large scale package. But if \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  wants to do this now, if he wants this reconciliation bill to pass now, that they should pass a provision to allow Medicare to negotiate with drug companies, number one, and number two, extend those ACA subsidies, those subsidies that were extended during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 They gave people a little bit more wiggle room when it came to their health care premium payments, and so he said if Schumer wants to do it now then this is the path he should go on. Now it`s a little bit of a false choice, Chris, because they need to do it now or else they`re not going to be able to do it at all because Congress goes on recess in August and they`re not going to want to stay around during August to wait for those CPI numbers which come out around August 10th.\par \par \uc2 And the chances are that they could end up with nothing or they could end up with a smaller bore package. That`s the choice that Manchin has put in front of them. And you have to remember, Chris, I know I`m stating the obvious, in a 50-50 Senate Manchin has a lot of sway. It`s just the way it is, and Manchin likes to say, if you don`t want me to have this much sway you select more progressive Democrats in the Senate, and that`s the way he feels and we`ll see how this all ends up. But I would guess a bird in hand is what the Democrats will take and they`re try to pass this package right away.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Is that what you`re hearing from the White House, Jonathan? Better something than nothing?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN LEMIRE, POLITICO WHITE HOUSE BUREAU CHIEF: That has been the approach but let`s be clear. There`s a lot of frustration at Senator Manchin. First of all just on the timing that this news would come out while the president is overseas and at an extremely sensitive diplomatic mission in Saudi Arabia, one in which he`s already taken a lot of criticism from his own party for meeting with Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  in part because United States is reliant on fossil fuels and what`s falling out of this bill because of Joe Manchin, climate change provisions.\par \par \uc2 So there`s some real frustration about that and reminiscent White House aides point out to me about how Joe Manchin scuttled the last time he said no to Build Back Better. He did so on FOX News. So there`s definitely a lot of insult to injury in the \uc2 White\uc2  House`s side and a real frustration with Senator Manchin, but to Jake`s point they`re also keenly aware of the power dynamic here. It`s a 50-50 Senate. Manchin has a lot of sway and there is indeed discussions as we speak but likely a move to take whatever they can get.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:03]\par \par \uc2 But there`s anger at the White House -- from the White House and even more so from progressives in the Democratic Party who feel like they really went out on a limb for the moderates last year, going ahead and passing that bipartisan infrastructure bill through the House believing that Manchin and Senator Sinema and others would then follow through and do the same for the larger social services program. That of course has not come to pass and now Manchin has thwarted them again.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, to Manchin`s point, Jake, which is elect more Democrats to the Senate, right. There are a number of Democrats who are either in tough reelection fights or hoping to unseat a Republican. For those folks that you`re talking to and folks who are trying to get more Democrats elected to the Senate, how much frustration is there there? Obviously they would love to be able to go and say look at all the stuff we`ve gotten done?\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: I mean, let`s not minimize what this is. I mean this is actually, if this were to pass, this Medicare and ACA subsidy, slimmed down Build Back Better, it would be a pretty big victory for the president, for congressional Democrats. Now it wouldn`t be Build Back Better but both sides, and I hate to say this, but progressives last year gave away huge amounts of leverage when they passed this, when they agreed to pass this infrastructure bill which happened last year without some guarantees on the Build Back Better plan.\par \par \uc2 They trusted Joe Manchin. So there are to blame as well but if you are a Democrat and you were able to go on the campaign trail this fall and say my party, Democrats, passed a piece of legislation that is going to bring down prescription drug costs for people on Medicare that`s a pretty big victory. I mean it`s not everything but you have to work with the government you have not the government you want. And the government that Biden has is a government with a 50-50 Senate and a four seat majority in the House.\par \par \uc2 That`s what it is.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Jake Sherman, Jonathan Lemire. Jonathan Lemire, nobody watches more faithfully "WAY TOO EARLY" but next time I see you in this building you`ve got to explain to me your Wordle obsession.\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: I`m happy to do so. I`m pretty good at it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You and about 20 million other people in the world. Thank you so much guys, good to see you, have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 If a picture is worth 1,000 words take a look at this. Bags piled up at London`s \uc2 Heathrow Airport\uc2 . It shows you what a nightmare air travel has been this summer, so bad there Delta flew an entire plane back to the U. S. with only luggage on board, no passengers. Delta and Southwest say the hiring struggles are getting better but they`re not over yet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s not about hiring people. It`s a question of getting our staffing trained through the pilot training bubble that we`re going through, flight attendants and mechanics as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: According to the travel app Hopper airlines have delayed 50,000 flights so far this month. They`ve canceled 4,000 more.\par \par \uc2 And it felt like one of the hottest summers you can ever remember you`re not wrong. The \uc2 National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration\uc2  reports this June with earth`s sixth warmest on record. In fact the 10 hottest Junes ever have all happened since 2010. Right now South Texas is saying it`s hottest summer on record and next week might just be worse. Some areas of Texas, Oklahoma and Kansas could see their highest temperatures yet between 102 and 170 degrees.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, a community rebuilds. The Buffalo supermarket where 10 people were shot and killed two months ago reopened today. The mixed emotions in Buffalo, next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:40]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The House Judiciary Committee has just announced it will consider legislation next week that would ban certain semiautomatic weapons. What they`re going to do is called a mark-up. It basically means they work on details of the bill and if it does come to the floor for a vote, it would be the first vote on an assault weapons ban in nearly three decades.\par \par \uc2 You can see the rally in support of that ban this past Wednesday. It`s on your screen now after a spate of mass killings, including Buffalo, Uvalde and Highland Park. The last assault weapons ban was signed into law in 1994 but was allowed to expire in 2004.\par \par \uc2 And in Buffalo today, an emotional step for the community forever changed by that mass shooting. The Tops Supermarket where a gunman opened fire is now open again for the first time since the shooting two months ago. The suspected shooter, a white teenager, killed 10 black people prosecutors say specifically targeting a place where blacks shopped. Thursday he was indicted on 27 federal hate crimes and firearms charges.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC`s Trymaine Lee joins me now from the newly reopened Tops Supermarket, and Trymaine, I can only imagine there must be mixed emotions there today.\par \par \uc2 TRYMAINE LEE, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Chris, you got that right. Folks who are streaming into the supermarket today will find stocked shelves, completely renovated, and on the wall a waterfall with a poem in it, memorializing the dead here. But outside there were folks protesting earlier this morning because as you said there are some mixed emotions.\par \par \uc2 You and I know well that communities who are suffering grief and trauma take a while to process that grief and trauma. And just two months after 10 people were killed here they`re opening those doors. I`ve been talking to folks like Pastor Tim Newkirk who says, you know, folks here, it`s going to take some time, right, to understand the gravity of this moment. Let`s take a listen to what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TIM NEWKIRK, LOCAL PASTOR: You know, the mixed emotions that is going through this day because this day specifically is letting the community and the city of Buffalo on 14208 that the wheels have turned. So I`m processing everything and I`m asking myself how is this going to benefit and why is this going to benefit, and who would it benefit the most, and who is going to benefit from having it reopened?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 LEE: A lot of folks question the timing of this opening and say that not enough community voices were raised in deciding when this shop would open. But I spoke to one woman, a cousin of one of the victims who stood outside by that memorial behind me and said my cousin would have wanted Buffalo to stand up, stand strong and move forward and open that store, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Trymaine Lee, as always, thank you so much for your reporting. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 I want to let folks know that this week on "INTO AMERICA," Trymaine will talk to two gun violence victims in Indianapolis and they tell him about the physical, the mental and emotional cost of surviving a shooting. You can listen to that now wherever you get your podcast.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to do it for me this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday, 1:00 p.m. Eastern Time right now here on MSNBC. But up next, "KATY TUR REPORTS." Have a great weekend, everybody.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Good to