 at witness intimidation emerge.\par \par \uc2 And as more disturbing details surfaced from the Jan. 6th Committee, how much longer can Republicans remain silent.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Biden`s first visit to the Middle East as president is underway. What can we expect from his difficult meeting tomorrow as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. There are major developments in the January 6 investigation tonight. According to a letter obtained by NBC News, the Secret Service erased. I`m going to say it again erased text messages from January 6, 2021. And from the previous day.\par \par \uc2 The letter to two House committees from the Homeland Security watchdog says those messages were deleted after being requested as part of an investigation into the Capitol riot. The inspector general says he was told many of the messages had been erased as quote, part of the device replacement program.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the Secret Service issued a statement saying this. The insinuation that the Secret Service maliciously deleted text messages following a request is false. In January 2021 before any inspection was opened, the Secret Service began to reset its mobile phones to factory settings as part of a pre-planned three-month system migration. In that process, data on some phones was lost.\par \par \uc2 There`s also new reporting that efforts to get testimony from the two most important figures on January 6, are now under serious consideration.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal says the House Committee is now weighing whether to push for interviews with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . Member \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  told the paper the committee could decide to request a written interview with Mr. Pence. It could also discuss whether to issue a subpoena to the former vice president.\par \par \uc2 Kinzinger also said he expects the committee will likely decide to make a criminal referral to the Justice Department about Trump`s own actions on the 6th. That will be the focus on the next hearing exactly one week from tonight.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, there are more questions about the witness \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  allegedly tried to contact that witness was reported to be a member of the White House support staff. Today, the committee chairman added this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BENNIE THOMPSON\uc2  (D-MA): It was a White House employee. I don`t know how you classify support staff.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It comes as Trump ally longtime Trump ally \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  prepares to go on trial for refusing to talk to the committee. Today a judge denied his second request to delay that trial, and it is set to begin on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, former President Trump`s first wife \uc2 Ivana Trump\uc2  has died in her home in Manhattan. They were married from 1977 until 1992 and had three children, Don Jr, Ivanka, and Eric. Her cause of death is unknown. But a New York City official told NBC News there was no indication of any foul play. \uc2 Ivana Trump\uc2  was 73.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter this evening with the help of our lead off panel. Jackie Alemany joins us, congressional investigations reporter for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  and an MSNBC contributor, Daniel Goldman, former Assistant U.S. Attorney for the Southern District of New York. He also served as general counsel for the House Intelligence Committee during Trump`s first impeachment. He`s now running for Congress in New York`s 10th congressional district. And my girl, former U.S. Attorney Joyce Vance, who spent 25 years as a federal prosecutor. She`s also a law professor at the University of Alabama.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Goldman, let`s talk about these text messages.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL GOLDMAN, FMR. ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY: My guy, right. Joyce is your girl and my --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, my boy, my boy, walk me through this. Why would any organization the Secret Service or otherwise think it`s OK to go through a routine system upgrade that might compromise data, data from January 6 when there was a riot on the U.S. Capitol? Are we really supposed to believe this?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: So many questions, Stephanie. The first of which is is it only January 5th and 6th that was happens to have been deleted? I may -- but I think clearly the most damning part of that letter is the allegation that these text messages were deleted after the request to preserve them was given.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:13]\par \par \uc2 When that happens when there`s a request for text messages, there`s something generally called a litigation hold. But whenever anyone makes a request, you hold on to things and you don`t destroy them. So it is highly suspicious, it`s conceivable that they were doing some sort of a routine phone migration. But it is unusual that they would do that after a request has been made to preserve the text messages or to turn over the text messages. So it is highly suspicious as you point out.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But Joyce, they had to know that we would find out that the messages were deleted, given that they were comfortable with that come getting out. What does that tell you about what could have been on those messages, that they`re willing to accept the wrath of everyone knowing that this mass deletion happened? What does that tell you? What must have been on there?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, FMR. U.S. ATTORNEY: So I think, as Dan is saying, it`s tough to know exactly what went on here. And the one certainty that we have at this point is the Secret Service will now have to engage in a full accounting of how this happened, because Stephanie, we had issues with data migrations in the law enforcement community, when cellphone usage was new in federal law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 And so it`s tough to believe that this happened accidentally in 2021. But that said, it`s always possible. They`re always these technical glitches, as Dan says, would all look for the most clearly is whether the time covered is only the 5th and the 6th, or whether it`s other dates, because if it`s just those two dates, then you have to be deeply concerned about the kind of communications between agents that could have been involved in those deleted messages and whether they might for instance, corroborate some of Cassidy Hutchinson`s testimony.\par \par \uc2 But until we know the full length of, you know, the full scope of these deletions of messages, and whether it`s benign, like the Secret Service says or whether it`s something more devious like the IG implies, we won`t really be able to assess this.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Cassidy Hutchinson`s testimony. And you remember over a week ago, we were told that Trump`s Secret Service person Anthony Ornato, he was going to refute what Ornato, what Hutchinson said yet we haven`t heard from him.\par \par \uc2 And our friend Michael Beschloss pointed this out tonight, that in December 2019, Trump tweeted that Anthony Ornato, that Secret Service guy would become his deputy chief of staff. For \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to name a secret staff member to a job like that, especially on the eve of a reelection campaign, that is abnormal should not have raised a serious red flag. Daniel?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Is it to me? I`m sorry. I think, you know, I think the relationship that Tony Ornato had with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and with the White House is incredibly unusual. The Secret Service, and those who work for the Secret Service are supposed to be nonpartisan. They`re supposed to simply either protect the President or some of them are investigative agents.\par \par \uc2 And the notion that Tony Ornato would have become such a diehard Trumper to the extent that while he was protecting Trump, he made that known and so much so that he was hired as the Deputy Chief of Staff is very unusual, and it is somewhat, it is highly suspicious.\par \par \uc2 So, I think given his background, and then ultimately what his job is, if he does testify, I do think that his -- you have to question a little bit of what his motives were and motives are, it is noteworthy that he hasn`t come through. There`s other reporting that indicates that the allegations that or the testimony that Hutchinson made about what Ornato told her about the limousine, The Beast going to the Capitol, and that altercation is corroborated by at least one police officer, not a secret service agent but a police officer who was there.\par \par \uc2 So a lot still remains to be seen as to what exactly happened.\par \par \uc2 I do want to make one point here which and there have been a lot of allegations about hearsay related to Cassidy Hutchinson`s testimony as if that somehow diminishes her testimony. The reason why hearsay is not allowed in court is not necessarily because it`s not reliable.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:00]\par \par \uc2 It`s because you cannot cross examine the person who made the statement. So the notion that just because some evidence is hearsay means it`s not reliable is wrong. It may not be reliable, but it very well may be and it`s no less reliable than any other testimony that might be subject to cross examination. And that`s the real reason why it`s kept out.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, Jackie, let`s say Hutchinson testimony is unreliable or untrue. Why hasn`t Ornato or anyone else in the Secret Service come forward and agreed to testify under oath and refute our statements?\par \par \uc2 JACKIE ALEMANY, THE WASHINGTON POST CONGRESSIONAL INVESTIGATIONS REPORTER: Two things here, Steph. I think that the further we get along here, the more the narrative and the notion that Cassidy Hutchinson is not reliable is being disproven, without corroboration from people like Tony Ornato and Bobby Engel directly, the two people that Cassidy had referenced and who would have witnessed the former president`s behavior during that time period.\par \par \uc2 You saw in that last hearing, actually, that Cassidy and Tony had a pretty seemingly friendly relationship after that seven-minute montage that outlined the tumultuous a quote unquote unhinged meeting on December 18 in the Oval Office with President Trump this Sidney Flynn, Michael Powell, \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , Eric Hirschmann meeting. You -- The committee threw up a text message from Cassidy to Tony Ornato, the two of them going back and forth, with Cassidy urging Tony to get to the White House ASAP.\par \par \uc2 So clearly those two had a relationship, they had some rapport. And I think it just makes the notion that Cassidy would have sort of fooled this anecdote out of thin air. That was harder to believe.\par \par \uc2 That being said, I do want to add a word of caution here. We have reporting in the past few days, actually, from the Secret Service spokesperson, that Tony and Bobby Engel are still available to testify, and that they had heard from an informal channel that there was maybe some potential to get that another private deposition on the books as they had previously promised.\par \par \uc2 But as of at least yesterday, we did not follow up on that today. The committee had not formalized a specific date and given them a final invitation to come and give another deposition before investigators.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Joyce, how about this news that the committee is seriously considering trying to get testimony from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  helped me out here. I am a mere mortal, why wouldn`t they want to sit down with these two, a year and a half ago? If I showed up dead tomorrow, the first people the police would talk to would be my husband and my mother. They wouldn`t say well grieving husband will leave him out. These are the two key players here. What are we waiting for?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Exactly. I think that that`s a fair point, there`s no reason that the committee shouldn`t voluntarily request and then promptly follow up with this subpoena for the testimony of both Pence and Trump because this is not a court of law. This is not a case where we have to worry about who`s a witness who`s a subject who`s a target, who could be a defendant down the road.\par \par \uc2 The committee`s purpose is to get to the truth of the matter and to create a historical record that permits them to address ongoing problems. Both Pence and Trump should be subpoenaed. And at a bare minimum, the public record should reflect their failure, their lack of willingness to testify if that`s the case. Otherwise, they can show up like every other witness in this proceeding, have a closed doors, sit down with committee staff, under oath, answer preliminary questions, and the committee can take it from there, but there is no reason that they should be treated differently from any other fact witness to the big lie on January 6.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Jackie, do you have any reporting on that?\par \par \uc2 ALEMANY: Yes, this is something that`s been a topic of conversation for really months now. And previously, when this issue was raised, we heard from aides to former Vice President Pence, that there was no way he was going to cooperate. And that`s why we heard from people like Greg Jacobs, or Mark Short, who appeared in a videotaped deposition. Previously, people who had been willing to cooperate with this investigation, but not the former vice president himself.\par \par \uc2 We were told at the time that he felt that testifying before the committee himself was sort of beneath the office and that these people who were around him at the time had just as much of a purview to what was taking place during that time period, as he did.\par \par \uc2 That being said, as we`ve learned throughout these seven blockbuster hearings, so far, there`s nothing really more powerful than hearing a firsthand account of something that happened from the person who experienced it themselves regardless of whether or not it`s rehashing a story that we think we already know about.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:06]\par \par \uc2 I think that Joyce is also right, and that the committee potentially needs to get these two people on the record potentially rejecting the opportunity to come forward and say their piece. And at the end of the day, as the committee is obviously sort of implementing a reverse Department of Justice strategy here, I think ethically there -- there`s also some obligation to offer the former president to to at least give some answers here to the allegations under oath.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then why wouldn`t this committee, Daniel, want that to happen? Want these two people to be called to answer questions? And if they refuse, let the country know that, right? There`s a very good chance both \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , and or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would like to run to be the next president, shouldn`t we know if either of these men is unwilling to talk to the January 6 committee about something they were directly involved with?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: I think part of the strategy may be to wait for these public hearings. And just like all of a sudden, \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  decided that he was going to cooperate after a few scathing for several months. There`s a lot more public pressure on someone like \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  to come and testify after we`ve seen these hearings, and everybody is paying such close attention. And they`re so compelling.\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  had been subpoenaed before we knew all of this evidence, and he had said no, I don`t think it would have made much of anyone would have been that surprised. Now, if he doesn`t want to come in and describe his conversations that he had with the Secretary of Defense that he had with other Cabinet officials that his own lawyer and chief of staff did not -- did not listen to, there is information that \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  has that no one else has. And there`s a lot more pressure on him now because of these compelling hearings.\par \par \uc2 So, I do think the strategy of waiting until the hearings so that the American people understand all of the evidence, and then asking Trump and Pence to come testify has a lot of strategic merit to it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, then I have to ask, I know we`re out of time. But when Daniels talking strategy, Joyce, it`s got me wondering, is this the exact strategy that the Department of Justice always wanted? Right. An overwhelming amount of evidence comes out. It`s the January 6 committee over the course of seven soon to be eight hearings, laying it out for the American people with mostly Trump appointees or employees.\par \par \uc2 And over time, you`re hearing more and more Republicans, whether they`re establishment Republicans, the Mick Mulvaneys of the world, conservative judges saying Trump did something seriously wrong here, some even saying he should be charged. Is this what the Department of Justice wants, so when it comes time for them to drop the hammer, it`s going to be very hard to say that this is a partisan hit job?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: DOJ may or may have benefited in a most unusual way from the committee`s proceedings. But typically, if you`re at DOJ, you don`t want any other investigators to get out in front of you. And that`s because of technical reasons, involving evidence that you have to turn over to defendants in the course of a criminal prosecution. For instance, you`re under some obligation to turn over any other statements that your witnesses have made.\par \par \uc2 So as a prosecutor, the last thing I want to see happening is to see Congress interviewing my witnesses under oath for hours and generating written transcripts. That`s just really anathema to prosecutors. So whether this was intentional from the outset, that seems very dubious to me, whether DOJ might benefit from it in the long run is an entirely different question.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, there is nothing usual happening here. Jackie Alemany, Daniel Goldman, Joyce Vance, thank you all so much for being here. If only I had time for Jackie to tell us what is in the cupcake tonight. When we come back, you know what, you know what, Jackie, we`re at a time I`m going to need you to open it, photograph it and tweeted at us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, with so much damaging information coming out about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and January 6, we`re going to go dig deep into what Republicans are saying about it. And here`s a quick spoiler alert. The answer is nothing.\par \par \uc2 And later, Biden said to meet with Saudi Arabian leaders, after once promising to make the country a pariah. Ben Rhodes is here on the President`s high stakes Middle East trip. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a busy Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:24:26]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): President Trump tried to call a witness in our investigation. A witness you have not yet seen in these hearings. That person declined to answer or respond to President Trump`s call and instead alerted their lawyer to the call. Their lawyer alerted us. And this committee has supplied that information to the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The shocking revelations about the former guy keep emerging to radio silence from Republican Party leaders.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:05]\par \par \uc2 But those same leaders are expected to be in attendance when Trump returns to Washington on July 26, to address the America First Policy Institute. So let`s discuss, A.B. Stoddard joins us, veteran Washington journalist and associate editor and columnist for Real Clear Politics. And David Jolly, who had not seen in quite some time, former Republican congressman from Florida. He`s now chairman of the Serve America Movement and an MSNBC political contributor.\par \par \uc2 David, what is with the radio silence from Republicans? We were supposed -- \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  was the one we thought was going to be in charge of sort of the counter attack, the messaging on the other side of these hearings. And it`s as though Republican leadership is all away at summer camp.\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOLLY, SERVE AMERICA MOVEMENT CHAIRMAN: Well, because it is going so disastrously bad for the former president. And Stephanie, I don`t know that going into the hearings we knew the exact strategy this committee would take. But this is now unfolding more like a grand jury indictment. I mean, they are singularly isolating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , as the culpable actor. This is not a thorough examination of the wider network, though, that has touched on every hearing points to one individual, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And so Republicans have very little in the way of defense, and look, many of them are actually in bed with him. This is the caucus that enabled him as his COVID policy led to the death of Americans as he did nothing about school shootings, as his immigration policy separated family and led to the death of children.\par \par \uc2 So the notion that the former president would get in bed with the Proud Boys and risk life and limb and harm to fellow countrymen on January 6th, that`s just par for the course for this Republican Party and the former president.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, well, this guy lost the election, lost before January 6, even happened. So now if you think about how America as a whole feels about him, A.B., we know he or he is reportedly determined to announce his 2024 candidates see sooner rather than later, is that Republicans want or are they watching these hearings? After watching this guy lose an election and saying please don`t do it. Anybody, anybody but you.\par \par \uc2 A.B. STODDARD, REAL CLEAR POLITICS ASSOC. EDITOR AND COLUMIST: Just look to the anonymous quote in that piece on the report that he`s so tempted to not even make it to September to announce his next presidential campaign, from some top prominent Republican strategist talking about how selfish \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has not heard about the Republican Party since 2015 when he talked about this, `16 when he won the nomination throughout his presidency, and beyond. If he has to break the Republican Party to serve his psychological needs, he`s happy to do so. They know this. That`s why there`s some kind of all-point bulletin stuff where no one, even someone liberated, who`s retiring like Senator Blunt, or Senator Burr or Senator Portman can talk about the hearings, everybody has to be mom, because their single focus is regaining control of the House, and hopefully a majority in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And so they`re so afraid of him rolling up their chances in November, that they`ve all been told to be silent. They`re pretending that they`re busy. They haven`t seen it. And that`s why it`s really important. It is doing damage to his base. That`s why you see in the polling, it`s important to note there are people that believe in focus group and polling, it`s time to move on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It`s not that they don`t love him, but they know he`s been damaged by this. That`s why DeSantis is on the rise. That`s a threat to him.\par \par \uc2 And it will make him even more vocal about his new big lie, which is the January 6 committee. He`s going to be, you know, announcing his run for the presidency, anything he can to regain the spotlight and attention and it`s to the horror of Republicans, and, of course, by abiding and abetting him, they all deserve it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then, David, if the Republican plan is to stay silent, should Democrats plan be to get really loud? Because these midterms are not about policy. What are you doing on the economy? How are you facing inflation? Our democracy is in danger. We are hearing throughout the hearings, about how close we were to losing our democracy.\par \par \uc2 And while that`s happening, the Supreme Court is rolling back civil liberties. The GOP taking extreme measures against abortion, blocking a bill today to protect travel for abortion. Are Democrats effectively communicating how dire the situation is? This ain`t no regular election.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yes, it`s not -- I love that question, Stephanie. Because most elections in modern political history, our turnout elections, you get your base riled up, you get them upset, anger, motivated, whatever it might be. This is actually one of the rare elections where there may be a persuadable constituency.\par \par \uc2 To your point if you can speak to the American people in a way where they understand that some of their very rights are now hanging in the balance based on who controls the levers of government in a way that we have not seen for perhaps 50 or 100 years.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:10]\par \par \uc2 Now you have persuadable voters go to the Dobbs case, for instance, this is a perfect example. We know the two sides are going to motivate their base that feels strongly about it. But coming out of Dobbs, there is a -- there are tens of millions of Americans who previously may have never identified themselves as pro-choice. Perhaps it was just kind of an anathema term to their orientation. But they`re realizing they were pro Roe all along.\par \par \uc2 If Democrats can have conversations with those constituencies and say, look, the Republicans are really reaching for fundamental rights, and you are at risk of losing them, then I think Democrats could turn around the way that seemed to be going a few months ago, and might have a more favorable November.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: A.B., yesterday, the House passed a bill that would create an act and alert system for active shooter situations. And 168 Republicans voted against it. Who are they doing that for? I`m thinking of all sorts of suburban Republicans, who six months ago love talking about how woke America was getting, and they needed to start voting for Republicans again, because things were getting too progressive. All of those people, they care about active shooter situations.\par \par \uc2 STODDARD: That`s true. And that`s why Steph it`s not just Roe, which I think, you know, the data is showing is extremely energizing for voters in every age group, who are pro-life, people who believe that abortion should be safe, legal and rare, but do not want it to be illegal, as David said, who realized now they are pro-Roe for pro-choice for soft Republicans, for independent for Democrats who never plan to vote in these midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 I think in addition to that, the findings, the revelations from these hearings on January 6, and the coup that began in November of 2020, or before, and took place all through November and December, and led to the failed coup on January 6, and all these mass shootings and the Republicans response to them collectively, are extremely galvanizing as a collective.\par \par \uc2 And so there are people who really cannot tolerate the Republican response to mass shootings anymore. They are turning on the Republican Party for that reason. And then they of course, you have the shock in response to what they`re hearing about Trump and his abettors on January 6, and then of course, the reaction to Roe I think it`s a basket of things that are making people furious, and that they didn`t expect inflation was so infuriating.\par \par \uc2 I think that they just didn`t expect that they would have these issues that might turn out new voters. The inflation voter (ph), as I`ve said, I think is probably still turning up. But I think it a woke -- it awakened a different set of voters who just aren`t going to tolerate, you know, action or inaction on these issues anymore. And that`s why I think it`s going to be a different makeup of the electorate.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: This is an election about democracy. A.B. Stoddard, David Jolly, thank you both for joining us tonight. I appreciate it. Great to see you both. Coming up. President Biden`s high, high stakes trip to Saudi Arabia. Our dear friend Ben Rhodes is here to break down why he thinks this visit is quote, sadly inevitable, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 23:38:14]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The President`s first visit to the Middle East since taking office has gone off largely without a hitch. But tomorrow, President Biden is set to meet with Saudi leaders, including Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , with us tonight to discuss is Ben Rhodes, former deputy national security adviser for President Obama.\par \par \uc2 Ben, I`m really glad you`re here tonight. This is a complicated trip. During his campaign, Biden promised to make Saudi Arabia a pariah. But you say this meeting was inevitable?\par \par \uc2 BEN RHODES, FMR. OBAMA DEPUTY NATIONAL SECURITY: Well, I`m not happy about it. I wish this meeting wasn`t taking place. And look, I`ve been in the White House. And what happens is, you`re looking at a lot of problems coming at you. And they`re thinking, we have a huge problem from inflation. If we can get the Saudis and \uc2 OPEC\uc2  to do any more oil production, maybe that would help. We have a huge challenge with the war in Ukraine. And we don`t want the Saudis and Emiratis who`ve been kind of sitting on that war to drift in the direction of Russia and China. You`ve got this Abraham accords. That is a Trump initiative in which the Gulf states are drawn closer to Israel. That`s a lot to do with Iran and their opposition to Iran.\par \par \uc2 So they`re all these short term reasons that could compel a President to say it`s easier to just make up with MBS and the Saudis get this over with, rip this band aid off. But the biggest problem, the bigger problem, Stephanie, is that, you know, the two existential challenges that we face as a country that we feel every day, is the collapse of democracy all around us, and climate change.\par \par \uc2 And in the long run, those short term impulses that I think are compelling this trip, you know, they end up winning the day over the things that we really have to do in the long run. At some point I really think that has to change.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:05]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I hear you, Ben. But here`s the problem. We live in a world of short termism and those long term solutions that we desperately need, President Biden or other Democrats aren`t even going to get a chance to address those things if they get wiped out come November.\par \par \uc2 So once this war took place in Ukraine, isn`t it sort of all bets are off, and if Biden doesn`t address the things surrounding inflation, then all the other things he`s going to look to get done are going to get a whole heck of a lot harder? He`s certainly not saying, oh, MBS He`s my boy. I`m all in. But he`s got a problem he`s got to solve.\par \par \uc2 RHODES: Yes, I think that`s definitely what tipped it for them. I think the war in Ukraine, and the shortages with Russian oil coming off the market to the U.S. and Europe, is probably the thing that kind of tipped over a policy decision that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  probably didn`t want to make and going to MBS.\par \par \uc2 I also think, Stephanie, that if you really look at this, you know, most analysis is, there`s not that much increased production capacity that can come out of Saudi Arabia and the UAE, our refineries are clicking in 95 percent as it is, there`s just broader shocks and global energy markets. And I think, you know, an increasing reluctance from the Saudis to kind of bail us out.\par \par \uc2 I think on this trip you`ll probably hear some good talk out of Saudi Arabia. But, you know, I don`t think MBS is a reliable actor here. And the question is, is he going to follow through on that top, and there`s somebody that`s invested $2 billion, and \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  is, you know, investment knowledge is that person, really someone who wants to help \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , along the lines that you`re saying, you know, make it through the midterms and make it into 2024? I just don`t trust the guy.\par \par \uc2 And I do think at the end of the day, when you`re framing this, rightly, is a question of democracy versus autocracy in this country and around the world. You know, MBS is on the wrong side of that debate.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So that right there, do you think this isn`t -- this is a bigger risk, you go to Saudi Arabia, you tried to play ball, didn`t want to play ball with us? I mean, truly, MBS wrote a massive check to \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 . \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  during stopped the steal. That`s where he was he was overseas locking in his next career. So is this administration foolish to think they could ever get anything done with somebody like MBs, especially given who he is so the Trump family?\par \par \uc2 RHODES: I think that they have to have and probably do have pretty low expectations about just how much they can get out of MBS here. You know, they`ll get a little bit more on the normalization process with Israel, which frankly, was already in trained because the Saudis have back these Abraham accords from behind the scenes. They might get some positive statements on energy markets that help in the immediate term.\par \par \uc2 But in the long run, you still have that image of a president of United States going to MBS, after the murder of Jamal Khashoggi, and, frankly, after a lot of other problematic behavior of multiyear war in Yemen, that was a mistake for the Obama administration to support, it`s been a mistake to continue us support ever since then. You`ve had Saudi support for withdrawing from the Iran deal, and now you have Iran on the doorstep of having enough material for nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 So again, I just don`t think that in the long run the cost of those images with MBS, and how that`s going to complete the kind of laundering of his reputation. He`s been trying everything from a golf lead to a Justin Bieber conference. He`s been trying everything to open back those floodgates of the businesses and the investments are coming back into Saudi Arabia. That`s what this -- that`s what`s in it for MBS. And that`s a lot here.\par \par \uc2 And I think in the long run, he`s not a reliable partner and friend of the United States, certainly not of a Democratic president. But you know, as I said, I don`t put this all on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I think most people in the White House will be doing what he`s doing for all the reasons that you`ve cited. I just think at some point, we have to address this gap between what we say the story we tell about who we are in the world and democracy and climate change and what the world sees us doing.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Ben Rhodes, every time you were here, you make us smarter. I`m really grateful you joined us tonight.\par \par \uc2 RHODES: Thanks, Stephanie. Coming up, a cloud of controversy hangs over police for their delayed response in Uvalde. Texas. State senator Roland Gutierrez is here to talk about all of it on THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:48:55]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What grade did you just finish?\par \par \uc2 JAYDIEN CANIZALES, UVALDE, TEXAS SURVIVOR: Fourth.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So tell me about what happened at the end of your school year? Why are you here today?\par \par \uc2 CANIZALES: So we can ban weapons now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And why is that so important?\par \par \uc2 CANIZALES: So no one has to go through what I gone through at school.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That`s a 10-year-old survivor of the massacre and Uvalde, Texas. The community now demanding answers after disturbing video from inside Robb Elementary School was edited and released by the Austin American Statesman in KVUE. Here`s how Governor Greg Abbott responded when he was asked about the video.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R) TEXAS: Obviously is disgusting. To see what happened is clear that what was shown on the video was the exact opposite of the information that I was given on the day that I went out and explained what happened during the event. None of the information that was in that video was shared with me on that day. And so it was shocking.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:02]\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Shocking. With us tonight to discuss, Texas State Senator Roland Gutierrez. His district includes Uvalde. Thank you so much for being here. I know what a brutal last two months you have had. Help me understand this. The governor is saying he is shocked. He`s disgusted by what he saw in that video. It`s the opposite of what he was told that day. He`s the governor of Texas. Why didn`t he see this video anytime over the last seven weeks? I would think he or any other official in the state should have seen that day too.\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. ROLAND GUTIERREZ (D) TEXAS 19th DISTICT: No, that`s right, Stephanie. I saw snippets of it. When I wanted to see all of it I was asked to sign an NDA. And I refused. My constituents wanted to see the truth and wanted to know the truth. And they wanted to do it in the right way. And so I ended up filing a lawsuit against the Department of Public Safety.\par \par \uc2 Greg Abbott is ignorant because he wants to be. He doesn`t want to know the truth, actually does know it. He hasn`t been back to Uvalde since day three. Never went to one single funeral. I don`t think that he cares enough about the kids of Texas because he has failed to call a special session to bring us back. And in put an end to having 18-year olds have access to these types of weapons. He refuses to do that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I need to interrupt you. You wanted to see a video. You`re an elected official. Uvalde is in your district, you wanted to see a video of what happened inside that school. And they asked you to sign an NDA? What was the rationale?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: That`s the state that we`re living in here in Texas. It`s -- This is the kind of stuff that happens in Venezuela, happens in Russia. And unfortunately, it happens here in Texas. The facts are the reason I saw three minutes of a video is because I barged into a DPS trailer and an argument with one of their PIOs. As I walked out, I saw about three minutes of a different video.\par \par \uc2 The video I saw was closer to the room that this man had sheltered himself in. And as he fired out to the police, you could see construction material sheet rock flying in over their heads. They have not shown you that video, because they don`t want people to see the awesome power, the fearful, horrible power of these AR-15s.\par \par \uc2 We saw all sorts of props in these investigations and in these hearings in the Senate and the House of Representatives, but we never saw an AR-15 because they don`t want to be having that discussion, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So what information are these families getting? If you an elected official can`t get information, what are the mothers and fathers? What are they being told?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Half-truths, innuendo, finger pointing. This is the worst response in law enforcement history in our modern times in the state of Texas. Absolutely. The fact is there were 12 DPS troopers, even in the video that made for TV video, we don`t see those 12 troopers. The only reason that came out was because I cross examined \uc2 Steve McCraw\uc2  do that, during his testimony in the Senate. This is where we`re at. This is the kind of government we`re dealing with in Texas.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So where are we going to go from here? Are they going to -- are police going to face any consequences? Is this current Texas government going to face any consequences, or not if they keep getting reelected?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: No, that`s the unfortunate thing. It`s my hope that Stephanie that people finally get tired of this. They finally get tired of the neglect. And as I`ve done my own investigations, you`ll start to see very soon that in Texas, there are many things that led up to this massacre, including the fact that Greg Abbott knew about the failed radios.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that was important about that video is that everybody was on their cell phones. They were on their cell phones because the radio systems didn`t work. The governor has known that for seven years, and he`s been asked for money to fix those radio systems for seven counties. And he has refused. This is a story of what`s left.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Those kids just went to school that day, with two days left in the year. Senator, thank you for joining us tonight. I really appreciate it. Roland Gutierrez. I appreciate it. But I wish I wasn`t seeing it. That is actually how I feel.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Coming up. How Buffalo grocery store targeted in a racist attack has now become a symbol of triumph when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:58:54]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, love beats hate. It has been two months since a gunman opened fire at a top supermarket in Buffalo New York, killing 10 people and leaving a tight knit community in mourning.\par \par \uc2 Today the Justice Department announced that a federal grand jury charged the 19-year-old gunman of 27 counts of hate crimes and firearm violations. He also faces 10 counts of first degree murder and 10 counts of second degree murder in the state of New York.\par \par \uc2 A moment of silence and prayer was held today outside the Tops friendly market store to honor the victims and employees impacted by the shooting.\par \par \uc2 Now fully remodeled, the store will reopen to the public for the first time tomorrow morning. Shoppers will be greeted by this water Wall Memorial inscribed to the poem by Julian Handsworth, a poet laureate from Buffalo. It reads in part, let the hopeful healing waters flow, cleansing all pain and fear all hurt and regret. Let the water heal our people. Buffalo`s Mayor Byron Brown spoke about the importance of the stores reopening at the prayer service today.\par \par \uc2 [00:00:05]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BYRON BROWN, BUFFALO MAYOR: How everyone has come together for the opening of our tops on Jefferson Avenue is proof positive that love beats hate. We will take this place of tragedy, and in the days, the weeks, the months, the years to come, it will be a national and worldwide example of a place of triumph.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Amen to that. Love beats hate in East Buffalo, New York tonight. May that be a lesson to us all.\par \par \uc2 And on that note, I wish you all a very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late with us. I will see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220715ei7e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for July 14, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Ali Vitali, Clint Watts, Vaughn Hillyard, Gabe  Gutierrez, Ali Vitali, Antonia Hylton \par \uc2 8,002 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC HOST: Good afternoon, I`m Chris Jansing live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 There is new reporting this hour from New York Magazine that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has decided whether to run again for president, so although, he`s still mulling whether to announce it before or after the midterms. What that decision is? Well, while he suggested, it`s yes, he didn`t say that outright, just as we don`t know whether it`s merely meant to distract from what has been the hottest topic in Washington. Who was former President Trump trying to get in touch with when he reached out to one of the January 6 committee witnesses? What was he planning to tell them? And could that call ultimately amount to evidence of witness tampering by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Here`s what we know. NBC News has confirmed it was a member of the White House support staff, potentially someone who would be in a prime position to confirm testimony from people like Cassidy Hutchinson. But according to CNN, it was not someone routinely in touch with the former president. The Justice Department is now in possession of that information, just one of several threads they`re following. But the pace of the DOJ investigation and the fact that they are coming to the committee for information, has led to a lot of frustration, even from committee members themselves.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): They`ve got potent tools to get information. They can enforce their own subpoenas in a way we can`t. And the idea that a year and a half after these events, they would not have talked to these witnesses, that even the Fulton County district attorney is way ahead of them. Is -- I think a cause for great concern.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And on Wednesday, committee chair \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  said the DOJ had asked for the evidence and witness transcripts related to a plot to create slates of fake pro-Trump electors in battleground states. This is notable because it`s the only specific topic the department has raised with the committee. And then there`s this revelation from \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  officer Aquilino Gonell, the man so badly beaten on January 6, he was forced to retire. This morning, he said lawmakers notably Republicans who he put his life on the line to protect, seem now to want nothing to do with him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AQUILINO GONELL, WOUNDED POLICE OFFICER: Sometimes I run into them in the hallway, and then they pretended not to see me. They avoid -- they avoid me or both to the right or to laugh or pretend to be doing something else. In a year and a half since I was injured that they know my injuries only \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  and \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  have approached me from the other side. Is this hardening or not? I`m not saying that I need that from them, but that shows a lot from the people who claim that they support the police and back the blue, and are pro-law and order.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, lots to talk about. NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard is with me on set. Also joining me, NBC Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vitali, Mary McCord served at the Justice Department as Former Acting Assistant Attorney General for national security, and \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2  worked as a consultant to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  counterterrorism division. He`s now a distinguished research fellow at the Foreign Policy Research Institute and an MSNBC national security analyst. Good to have all of you here.\par \par \uc2 Ali, we`ve got more on the Trump interview coming up but I want to start with this phone call. We don`t know who the president called just that their support staff.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So how broad a category is that? And how much access could a person like that have to have heard or seen something that the committee thinks would be critical to their investigation?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Well, look, when you consider the way that the committee put this information out, it`s right at the end of their penultimate hearing a week ahead of what we expect to be the final hearing in primetime next week on Thursday. That`s a hearing that`s going to be focused on the minute-by- minute accounting of what happened on January 6, itself, the 187 minutes that the Capitol was under attack, and what was happening in the White House. If this is a witness that we haven`t seen or heard from yet, which is what the committee has told us, it`s very likely that this is someone who could shed a lot of light on what was happening inside the White House at that time.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 If they were support staff, they would have had a lot of access to the former president potentially being able to corroborate some of these other moments that we`ve already heard talk about. We know for example, that the former president spent much of January 6 watching the -- what was happening on Capitol Hill from televisions that he had installed in one of the dining areas of the White House. That could be a place that this potential staffer could have been.\par \par \uc2 But again, it narrows the pool for us to think a little bit about this is not someone who was an appointed senior staffer, it lets us sort of get away from that speculation, and instead turn our focus to someone who perhaps like someone like Cassidy Hutchinson, who may not have been someone who was flashy and in these meetings as a central speaker, but instead someone who was more there against the wall and just taking in the information, the details as they were coming at them, someone who can corroborate some of the things that we have already heard.\par \par \uc2 But again, for the committee, it`s both the information that this person could potentially provide, but also the fact that they feel that this is a pattern of potential witness tampering from now, the former president himself, but also from others in his orbit because they`ve put these kinds of allegations forward before, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Mary, if -- as CNN reports, this is not someone who the president was normally in touch with. How important is that piece of information in trying to figure out whether something was amiss here?\par \par \uc2 MARY MCCORD, FORMER ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY, DISTRICT OF COLUMBIA: Well, obviously, it`ll be important as the investigation proceeds to figure out what access did this person actually have to the inner workings of the White House to conversations that Trump may have had with his advisers, or conversations the advisors may have had even outside of President Trump`s presence that would have described may be other interactions with Trump, etcetera.\par \par \uc2 I would say that sometimes people who are not themselves involved in the conversations that are more bicep standards, you know, overhearing things, in some ways may have a better memory about what they`re observing or hearing because they didn`t have a personal stake. They weren`t actually involved in the conversation. They`re just taking it all in.\par \par \uc2 So I do think it is important and we don`t know yet who it is. We also don`t know yet what you know, Trump was attempting to do with this phone call. So I don`t -- I think we can`t get ahead of that -- ahead of ourselves yet in thinking this is going to necessarily support tampering charges.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So let`s talk about something else because the DOJ is looking into as well as this call, the scheme to appoint fake electors. That`s apparently the only specific topic they`ve even broached with the committee. Why do you think that would be?\par \par \uc2 MCCORD: Why it`s the only one they`ve broached or where they`re looking into?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, why it`s the only one they broached.\par \par \uc2 MCCORD: Yes. So I mean, there were some time, you know, a month or so ago that they were also seeking information from the committee. But we also know from a month or more ago that the Deputy Attorney General, Lisa Monaco, made clear that they were looking into this, they were investigating this fraudulent elector scheme.\par \par \uc2 And so I think this just shows that that investigation is continuing and it`s proceeding. I think it`s extremely important because not only did the fraudulent electors themselves, in many cases, violate the law, but anyone orchestrating that or conspiring with them to do that, potentially also is in violation of federal laws and also state laws.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, orchestrating and conspiring, two key words there. So, Clint, look, you know how investigations work, they can be slow. But when you hear people like Adam Schiff criticizing how the DOJ is handling this, we`ve been hearing for a while frustration from a number of people about the pace of it, do you share those concerns?\par \par \uc2 CLINT WATTS\uc2 , FORMER FBI AGENT: Yes. What I find interesting, Chris, is they`ve gone the very bottom-up approach. So, one explanation could be that just the sheer volume of cases that they had to pursue at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and to try at the DOJ after you had that many people enter the Capitol. We`re talking in the hundreds, probably approaching a thousand at this point. That, maybe they`re just overwhelmed and haven`t really moved up that chain.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is, you do see them going and pleading out certain cases. You have to wonder of those cases, are they actually trying to build a bigger broader conspiracy case? And to do that you`d have to flip individuals that were possibly charged, you`d have to get phone records, you`d have to then follow up on that investigation. And while Congressman Schiff is right, it seems slow. In one circumstance, I would tell you that, at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , most cases take many years and even, lead to prosecution. So we`re at the 18-month mark at this point, it isn`t unreasonable that it could take a lot longer.\par \par \uc2 I think the last point is really the investigative style. Most of this has been bottom-up so far. But everything is narrowing down onto a very central cast of characters, almost like a dirty dozen of the case that are the central people that were either coordinating, communicating, and bringing everybody to the Capitol that day, or with the Stop the Steal rally, organizing people in such a way that they would move towards the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 So, with that centralized network, I would think they would start moving in a much more aggressive way if they were going to put charges forward against the president or his intermediaries that were very close to him.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let me ask you too, Clint, about the extremist elements to the January 6 attack because this morning, we heard from Jason Van Tatenhove on Morning Joe and he talked about the connections that still exist between Trump, at least the Trump World, and his former organization, the Oath Keepers. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JASON VAN TATENHOVE, FORMER OATH KEEPERS SPOKESMAN: I think Trump really, you know, kind of gave them permission to continue doing what they`re doing.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN LEMIRE, MSNBC HOST: We know the president -- former president is considering running again, from what you know, people you still talk to in the world with the Oath Keepers, and would they support him again, where he`d run in 2024?\par \par \uc2 VAN TATENHOVE: I think so. I mean, that core base is going to be there. It`s -- there`s still drinking the Kool-Aid so, it -- I think, absolutely he`ll have support from those types of groups.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, the question, Clint, what kind of role could these groups play in 2024?\par \par \uc2 WATTS: Yes, that`s where I`m most scared, not just for 2024 and 2022, Chris. When you look across the electoral landscape in state and local elections, what you`re starting to see is almost two or three different scenarios and polls -- one intimidation of voters or intimidation at polling places that should be a much bigger concern. State and locals just don`t have the ability to do that.\par \par \uc2 Second is the Praetorian Guard sort of situation where candidates are actively courting or working with these election deniers, and they`re sort of militia elements to really try and push their way forward. And then the last part are those that are running -- those are actual extremists that are running for office in many of these locations. They are going to try and push in 2022 and into 2024 to really establish themselves.\par \par \uc2 So, we always talk about international extremism 10 years ago, were based on key events or dates, election years, or key events, and domestic extremism. And this is one that we`re going to get a preview of in 2024. We`ll get a preview of it here in the local and state races in 2022.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And speaking of 2024, Vaughn, I want to ask you about the New York Magazine article where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t exactly come directly out and say I am running. But he says also he is getting advice related or -- the magazine -- the article says that he`s getting advice related to why he might consider running. And let me read from that. A lot of people are saying you`ve got to announce so you`re protected, meaning from the January 6 revelations. It`s a witch hunt. They`re trying to do this to you again.\par \par \uc2 And then later the author writes. "Trump swears that shielding himself from prosecution is not among his reasons for running for president because he is not at risk of being prosecuted." But could the fact that he`s worried about this, or maybe he just wants to distract from all the negative attention that he`s getting or concerns that a lot of Republicans have that he`s losing support be a reason behind him saying -- what he said to Olivia Nuzzi?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Right. We watched in 2020, him, use the investigations, you know, from the Mueller probe to the impeachment trials against him as a political prop in order to boost his own campaign, essentially making him a martyr on the campaign trail. This would allow him to do just that again. And you know, when we`re talking about 2024, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has flirted with this now for months for the last year on the campaign trail. This is the most explicit he has been in this interview with Olivia Nuzzi, saying that he has made his decision. It`s now a matter of whether he announces before or after the midterms.\par \par \uc2 So what is the incentive to announce before? Well, number one, he believes that he`ll be able to go around the country like he is going to do this weekend in Arizona with the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  candidate, with a gubernatorial candidate, a Secretary of State candidate, and be able to essentially force the hand of these Republican nominees in these various states to stand beside him.\par \par \uc2 And if he is a Senate candidate for 2024, when we`re talking about the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  races, he is going to be able to stand by them and say these guys are going to be allies when I win the White House back here. But also when you`re talking about these House races, you know, we last talked when I was in Nevada earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: And I had a conversation with a Nevada GOP chairman, I said, do you really want \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to be the guy that you are standing by in these midterm elections, you have competitive House races? This is a state that has trended blue in recent years. And he said, look, we galvanized over the last four years voters to this Republican Party that we did not have otherwise.\par \par \uc2 And that is exactly what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his allies see as well. They believe that they were successfully able to bring out in 2020 and in the 2018 midterms, millions of Republican voters who otherwise have been there before. So while some would suggest in the Republican Party, we don`t want \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Trump does not have that belief. He believes that he has aided the Republican Party despite the 2020 election -- presidential election.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And you`re never going to convince him otherwise. Vaughn Hillyard, thank you so much. Mary McCord, \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2 , Ali Vitali, we`re going to talk to you again a little later on in this hour.\par \par \uc2 President Biden on defense this morning over his plans to meet with Saudi Arabia`s Crown Prince, the man responsible for the death of journalist Jamal Khashoggi. Senator Chris Coons will join us on that, and the goal also, the president`s Middle East trip.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 Plus, new frustrations for the families of the Uvalde victims, there are still unanswered questions. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: President Biden is setting down some major policy markers in his first presidential trip to the Middle East. In a meeting with Israel`s Prime Minister, he reaffirmed that Iran must never get a nuclear weapon. He said everything must be done to make sure Putin`s war in Ukraine is a failure. And as he prepares to visit Saudi Arabia, he`s also pushing back on criticism for meeting with the Saudi Crown Prince accused of the journalist, Jamal Khashoggi`s death.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I always bring up human rights. I always bring up human rights. But my position on Khashoggi has been so clear, if anyone doesn`t understand it, in Saudi Arabia and their wealth, then they haven`t been around for a while. I`m going to be meeting with nine other heads of state. It`s not just as happy as to be in Saudi Arabia. And so there are so many issues at stake.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in Democratic Senator Chris Coons of Delaware, a member of the Foreign Affairs Committee. Good to see you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You`re someone who has the president here on foreign policy, so we just heard him say his record on human rights is clear.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 What do you say to a voter who argues that meeting with and maybe even making a deal with Jamal Khashoggi`s killer isn`t what they thought when he met when on the campaign trail, he said he makes Saudi Arabia a pariah?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Look, I`m the co-chair, the co-founder of the Senate Human Rights Caucus. I have followed in what was former senator Biden`s long record in the Senate of advocating for human rights as a core interest in American foreign policy and how we conduct ourselves here at home. But one of the things that has changed since the campaign has changed in recent months, is that Russia is committing daily human rights violations, and atrocities in Ukraine against thousands of innocent Ukrainian civilians. And one other thing that`s changed is that the strain, the pressure on our key European allies and partners because of oil and gas prices continues to rise.\par \par \uc2 I respect that the president made a hard decision that he needs to go to the Gulf to meet with, I think, nine different heads of state at this GCC meeting, the Gulf Coordinating Council meeting in Saudi Arabia, and sit down and have a conversation about security, about Iran, about Israel, about the region, and yes about human rights.\par \par \uc2 Jamal Khashoggi`s widow had a meeting at the White House with a senior official, who reassured her that President Biden would raise the issue of her late husband`s brutal murder at the hands of agents of the Saudi Kingdom. And I am confident that President Biden knows fully his responsibilities as the American President to both works to ensure our security and our values.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, to your --\par \par \uc2 COONS: There are so many urgent issues in the Middle East that need to be addressed. I`m glad he`s there. And I`m glad that a president with decades of experience in both advancing human rights and our foreign policy interests is at the table today.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So to your point, Senator, and the president`s point on gas prices, realistically, what could this trip really do to ease not just prices at the pump, but potentially because of its role in inflation overall -- inflation?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, the prices at the pump in my home state of Delaware have come down week after week now, it`s come down almost 50 cents from its high.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But still, I think about --\par \par \uc2 COONS: But that, Chris, can I --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I still think about a -- I still think about a $1.50 more than it was this time last year, just to put it in perspective. But continue, Senator.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Thank you. I do think that there are only three countries that I`m aware of that have significant untapped reserves that could go on to the global market, Iran, Venezuela, and Saudi Arabia. Of those three, I think having a meeting with the Saudis, the Qataris, and the Emiratis, three of the key oil and gas producers in the Middle East makes far more sense than otherwise making some accommodation with the dangerous and aggressive regime in Iran, or with the Maduro regime in Venezuela, which continues to oppress its people and to violate fundamental norms of both democracy and human rights.\par \par \uc2 So I do think that this can make a significant difference for European energy security, which is critical to the path forward in Ukraine. And because it`s an integrated global market for oil and gas, can also help with global prices and inflation.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I don`t need to tell you but all these programs and then many you haven`t mentioned depend on holding control of the Senate, the House in November. And let me talk about the impact of inflation because it was a new 40-year high last month, up to 9.1 percent from last year -- up 9.1 percent from last year. A pound of chicken up 36 cents, a pound of ground beef up 53, milk 59, eggs up $1.07, how do you counter that on the campaign trail? How do Democrats make this sell that they deserve to stay in power in November?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, Chris, one of the key complaints I`ve heard for years from Delawareans about prices is the price of prescription drugs. Prescription drugs aren`t optional. You have to take them. And family after family, from seniors to parents of infants in Delaware have said to me we have to do something on prescription prices. Democrats have a plan. We are poised to pass it.\par \par \uc2 Minority Leader McConnell -- Republican Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is saying if you pass that reconciliation plan to reduce prescription drug prices, I will prevent us from taking up and passing the other key plan that will help reduce prices, the so-called Competes Act.\par \par \uc2 It was passed out of the Senate a year ago by a big bipartisan margin. it would reduce the price of semiconductor chips by increasing their supply. Why don`t we have enough affordable, for example, used cars currently available to buy? Because there aren`t enough semiconductor chips.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 So there`s two things that Democrats are trying to move forward that would reduce the price of things that really matter to people, prescription drugs and vehicles. And we don`t have full cooperation from our colleagues here in the Senate in the Republican minority. There are some working well with us on this Competes Act, and others trying to find a way to block us. I think we have a plan and a path forward. The president has a plan and a path forward. We need Republicans to join us if we`re going to reduce prices at the grocery store, at the pharmacy counter, and at the pump.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Senator Chris Coons, appreciate your time, sir. Thank you so much for coming on the program.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The political fallout after a man is charged with the rape of a 10-year-old girl in Ohio, who was then forced to travel out of state to get an abortion. How that story has forced a deeper look at some of the most restrictive abortion laws in the country. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ohio officials now say that the accused rapist of a 10-year-old girl has not just been arrested but has also confessed. This story putting a sharp focus on the debate over the impact of highly restrictive abortion laws that don`t have exclusions for rape or incest, and it follows intense criticism of President Biden after he said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Just last week it was reported that a 10-year-old girl was a rape victim of high -- 10 years old. Just imagine being a little girl, 10 years old. Does anyone believe that this is the highest majority view, that that should not be able to be dealt with?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Exclusions for rape or incest. And it follows intense criticism of President Biden after he said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Just last week it was reported that a 10-year-old girl was a rape victim. 10 years old. Just imagine being that little girl, 10 years old. Does anyone believe that this is the highest majority view that that should not be able to be dealt with?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Conservatives were quick to doubt the story. With the "Wall Street Journal" editor board headlining it a story too good to confirm, writing, quote, "All kinds of fanciful tales travel far on social media these days, but you don`t expect them to get a hearing at the White House."\par \par \uc2 Joining me now NBC`s Ali Vitali on Capitol Hill, Marty Schladen is a reporter for the "Ohio Capital Journal," and NBC`s Gabe Gutierrez who is closely following this arrest.\par \par \uc2 Gabe, tell us what you can about this suspect and what prosecutors are saying?\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, actually the "Wall Street Journal" just published a new editorial essentially correcting the record, it says, and saying that it appears President Biden was correct. But, yes, this is a case, a lot of fast-moving developments here. The suspect 27-year-old Gershon Fuentes was arraigned yesterday in Ohio, and he`s being held now on $2 million bail.\par \par \uc2 Now the detective went before the courtroom yesterday and talked about how the initial police report for this case came in actually last month. But -- on June 22nd. It was July 6th, though, when the child identified the suspect. And on July 12th, just a few days ago, he was served with a search warrant and his DNA was swabbed. But the child, according to the detective, went to Indiana late last month, June 30th, I believe, to have the abortion.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Because she couldn`t have it anymore in Ohio. She was three days too late.\par \par \uc2 Gabe, I know you`ve got to rush off to another story but thank you for that legal update.\par \par \uc2 So, Ali, let`s talk politics here. This arrest has silenced some on the right, frankly, who were public doubting this case. With abortion shaping up to be a big issue for the midterms, Democrats are already putting out some ads on it. Here`s just one.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If MAGA Republicans get their way, abortion will be banned nationwide with no exceptions. Medicare and Social Security will end in five years with no replacement. Elections will be decided by politicians with no regard for your vote.\par \par \uc2 If MAGA Republicans get back in power, your rights, benefits, and freedoms will be in danger. Democrats will protect your rights. And the only way to stop the MAGA Republicans is to vote for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 FF PAC is responsible for the content of this ad.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Ali, do Democrats see this as a lifeline essentially ahead of the midterms? Especially when you can look at extreme cases like these?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. I mean, look, Democrats have always, and as long as I have been out on the campaign trail, reproductive rights have been sort of a guardrail in Democratic politics but an untested one, Chris, because Democrats for a long time have talked about the potential for Republicans and conservatives to overturn Roe. But most voters never had to grapple with the idea that his could actually happen.\par \par \uc2 Women for the last 49 years have never had to think about whether or not they had a fundamental right to access abortion care. So this is a really untested moment in politics as abortion has always been a galvanizing issue for voters. But now what we`re seeing in polling is the idea that this is rising in the ranks for voters as they consider their top issues going into the midterms which is exactly what Democrats want because they see this as a moment to try to energize an electorate that frankly Democrats badly needed to energize.\par \par \uc2 I would also say that when it comes to this really horrifying story out of Ohio, it does strike me that the knee-jerk reaction when you hear this kind of terror is to doubt the girl at the center of it. We`ve seen this time and again with women and in this case someone who`s not even of woman age yet being doubted in the press.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ten years old. Marty, your reporting suggests that Republicans could have seen this coming. We were bound to see cases like this. In 2021 alone, Ohio saw over 6,000 sexual abuse cases with the victims under 18. That`s according to the Ohio Network of Children`s Advocacy Center.\par \par \uc2 From what you`re seeing, could this be potentially political quicksand for local and state Republicans as well?\par \par \uc2 MARTY SCHLADEN, OHIO CAPITAL JOURNAL REPORTER: I think very possibly. I think that the thing that our state`s leaders don`t want to talk about is at what age should a woman or a girl be required to have her rapist`s baby? That`s a question I`ve asked several times and I`m not getting any answers to.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And this is a very emotional issue, Ali, on the Hill, where you are. There was this highly charged, highly emotional hearing yesterday. Tell us what`s happening on the Hill.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes, two hearings yesterday, the third this week, as we see Democrats coming back to town very focused on this issue and continuing to do a blitz of sorts legislatively.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:01]\par \par \uc2 In the Senate there is an attempt to do legislation that`s not going to end up getting done, on trying to protect women`s ability to move over state line if they need to access abortion care. Exactly what is at issue in this case in Ohio. And then of course on the House side, we`re going to see Democrats -- we`re going to be able to pass this and then run into a brick wall in the Senate as they often do trying to repass the Women`s Health Protection Act, which would codify access to abortion in the way that the Roe versus Wade precedent has done so.\par \par \uc2 But they are also going to try to add protections for women traveling across state lines. And there`s also another push around data privacy. This as Democrats publicly and privately say that they are 