s of Trump.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:06]\par \par \uc2 And I think as he potentially heads into a third presidential race, he`s really nervous about the prospect of having to run against \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And --\par \par \uc2 HOPKINS: Democrats are (INAUDIBLE) for a Trump run.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And CNBC is out with new polling, though, Ian, showing Biden at his lowest approval rating on job performance and the economy ever. Lower than Trump or Obama at their lowest. Are polls like this driving not just Trump`s calculus but frankly anybody looking at the race?\par \par \uc2 IAN CALDERON, MAJORITY ADVISERS PRINCIPAL: Certainly it`s going to have its impact. I mean, I think on the Democratic side, there`s a growing frustration that there is a lack of leadership and this vacuum of a strong message that could push back against a lot of the policies that Republicans are passing across the country.\par \par \uc2 But also at the same time, almost this feeling that Democrats have to own this position that we`re in in terms of the economy, when a lot of the circumstances that led to where we`re at frankly happened during Republican control under Trump administration and we really would like for there to be a more strong message, rather than feeling like we`re on defense all the time. More offense. And I think that that`s why you`re seeing a lot of Democrats really energized by what Governor Newsom is doing, pushing back in a real strong way.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, let me ask you about Governor Newsom because I think he was lieutenant governor when you were majority leader, in any case you know him, you`ve worked beside him. You know his political ambitions. So when you see his travels, his ad buys and his rhetoric, does he look more and more like somebody to you who might run for president?\par \par \uc2 CALDERON: Look, I mean, I think it would be foolish for anybody who could be a contender to not think of it. And so it certainly is a