S: Coming up, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the clear front-runner to be the Republican nominee in 2024. But what happens if he loses the primary? Why Trump running again could be the nightmare scenario for the Republican Party next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: A big poll today that got a lot of attention. And I think people sort of buried the lead on it. So, here`s the lead as I read it. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a uniquely unpopular figure in American life. It`s a fact it`s borne out over and over again. At some point you would think Republicans are going to realize that and really realizing.\par \par \uc2 So, the poll I was referring to is a new one out today from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . You might have seen it. Siena College partner with them. And in a hypothetical presidential rematch between Biden and Trump in 2024, Biden comes in at 44 percent and Trump at 41 percent.\par \par \uc2 Now, keep in mind, this is a poll where the current president, Joe Biden`s current approval rating is a dismal 33 percent, not good. And yet, head to head, he still beats \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And that`s because to state the obvious, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a weak candidate. He is a one-term president. He is a guy who lost the popular vote nationally twice, the last time by like seven million votes.\par \par \uc2 But in a world where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  potentially loses the 2024, Republican primary, right, what does he do? Would he endorse a Republican candidate or does he call the election rigged as he did multiple times in 2020 primary and then in the general election, and lead some kind of exodus with his base? That`s the threat Republicans are all terrified of and explains a lot of the very weird dynamics over in Republican politics right now.\par \par \uc2 NBC News Policy Editor Benjy Sarlin joins me now. Benji, you know, you and I have talked about this for years back and forth. And I was really struck by this polling because this is a huge kind of thing that you could show, even to voters, right? Voters are very motivated, particularly like base voters. They`re very motivated to win, right?\par \par \uc2 They want to get -- like, show this and be like, there`s one guy you can nominate who`s going to have the worst possible chance. Maybe don`t nominate him. And I do wonder whether that message is going to get through at some point?\par \par \uc2 BENJY SARLIN, NBC NEWS POLICY EDITOR: Well, it`s an interesting question. I mean, some of this is that polling has also never felt as relevant as it -- as it does -- did after 2016, right? Because, Trump voters were told that entire time in the lead up that look at all these polls, Trump is going to lose for sure, and then --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s right. It turned out to be totally wrong, right. So, everyone, you know, as a real -- takes it with a real grain of salt. But it is true that there might be a bigger opening for someone like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to make the case. Hey, look, we all love Trump, but there`s just too much baggage here. Why risk it? We all want to get rid of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Let`s take a safer pick.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you see, perhaps with the January 6 hearings, very few base Republicans are going to concede say, wow, this stuff -- this is horrifying. We definitely should abandon Trump. We were wrong ever to support him. But you are hearing some Republicans start to whisper, maybe this does weaken him in the sense that it reminds people that if you were to run again, you would be bringing all this into a general election, whereas right now you have a lot of advantages, just focusing on a president who`s out of 33 present approval in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  poll.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. And you see all these -- I saw a quote from -- I think it was Senator Thune today who basically was trying to wave Trump off from announcing he`s running for reelection before the Midterms. I mean, if they could all make him disappear through some magic deck, like he just doesn`t exist anymore or he went to Mars whatever, they would all do it, McConnell, Thune, the whole Republican political class. Like, they would love nothing more for that, at least for this election if they could.\par \par \uc2 SARLIN: Sure. I mean, if you`ve seen quotes in private at least pretty much to this effect. There were those recordings of Kevin McCarthy after January 6 pretty much making that exact argument. And then within days, he concluded that he needs \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and he was pretty open about the reasons. He said he was walking -- he told the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  he was walking the tightest type rope anyone has ever talked, meaning that Trump could essentially tank the Midterm elections for them if they had an antagonistic relationship to him, which of course, gets to the core problem of this, which is that you can`t just necessarily walk away from Trump without Trump taking his voters potentially with him.\par \par \uc2 He has a completely unusual place in the party in this regard, Chris, and that`s something we`re definitely all worried about. It`s a big dynamic heading into 2024.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:31]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This is the key. And this I think undergirds so much. And you and I have discussed this for a long time. It`s that part of it, right? Play out the game. Like, let`s say there`s a contested primary and Trump starts to lose. We know what it looks like when he lost a general election. Like, what`s it look like -- like, the sort of virulence of Trumpism his sociopathy and his supporters willingness to do things like what they did on January 6, means that like, even running a free and fair election in a primary context is not at all a given to me.\par \par \uc2 SARLIN: No, and it`s important to remember that he was shouting rigged and false, you know, voter fraud the entire time during his primary wins in 2016. Now, obviously, it became irrelevant, because he did end up winning fair and square by a dominant margin. But he started the 2016 campaign threatening to go independent. It was the very first question asked in the Republican debates, will you agree to support the nominee? And Trump said no. And he said it gave him "leverage" by saying no. He was well aware that this was a threat that they cared about.\par \par \uc2 Later, when it looked like there might be some opportunity to stop him at the convention from getting the nomination, he again started talking about going independent. And he said that, "millions of voters would go with him and the party would be doomed, essentially. There`s no way he would not make similar threats, I think, based on his prior behavior if it looked like there was a situation where he was about to lose to another rival. And that rival was suddenly being, you know, held up and conservative media as the new thing, as the thing everyone has to go towards.\par \par \uc2 I think, in addition to it just being a very ugly primary. I mean, we`ve shown how, you know, some of the worst attacks he leveled in 2016. Were against candidates he was friendly with until the last minute.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 SARLIN: Like Ted Cruz. You know, there`s no reason to think he wouldn`t do the same thing to a \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  say, or a Glenn Youngkin, or whoever else run, certainly \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . So, this is just this threat that looms over the entire election for them, Chris. It`s not going to go away.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It is the ordering condition for all calculations of Republican politics. That threat and that threat being made good because it would be so catastrophic. Benjy Sarlin, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 SARLIN: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, a story that`s very important that you may not have heard of. It`s how a ballot initiative in Kansas could be the first real-world a the voting booth test case for abortion rights in post-rural America. That`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The January 6 Committee has changed the time of tomorrow`s public hearing. It`s now going to start at 1:00 p.m. Eastern, had been slated for the morning. It will be led by Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  of Maryland and Congresswoman \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2  of Florida. Now, we`re expecting to hear testimony that connects militia members who stormed the capitol to associates of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself. MSNBC will, as always, carry the hearing live and, as always, our special coverage starts at noon tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And then tomorrow night, tune in for our two-hour recap analyzing everything we learn with Rachel Maddow, \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , Nicolle Wallace, Lawrence O`Donnell, Ari Melber, Stephanie Ruhle, and yours truly, all live from 30 Rock starting at 8:00 p.m. Eastern. Don`t miss it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, I want you to take a look at this map. It`s become a little familiar over the last two weeks after the conservative Supreme Court overturned the constitutionally protected right to an abortion. You may have seen it. We`ve used it on the show before. And it shows you where bans immediately went into effect in state after state.\par \par \uc2 The dark red are the ones that basically banned abortion. There are 16 already in total with two put on hold by the courts. The lighter red are the states in the process of putting those bands in place. Now, there`s a few states that stand out here. One that really does stand out to me is Kansas. Kansas is a very conservative state where abortion is still accessible right now. And that is due to a 2019 state Supreme Court decision which found the state constitution of Kansas secures the right of a woman to "make her own decisions regarding her body, health, family formation, and family life. Decisions that include whether to continue a pregnancy."\par \par \uc2 Not surprisingly, anti-abortion activists and Republicans are now trying to overturn that ruling with a high-stakes ballot organizing effort. And they have tried to stack the deck in their favor by holding the vote to basically change the constitution during the August primary election, which typically draws one far fewer voters in a general election, and also will almost certainly see higher Republican turnout for their contested gubernatorial nomination.\par \par \uc2 But that said, people on the side of abortion rights are also mobilizing. And they`ve launched a public campaign, including this ad which I think is a very clever way of trying to reach voters in a deeply conservative state about the right to privacy that surrounds abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They call it a constitutional amendment. The truth, it`s a strict government mandate designed to interfere with private medical decisions, a slippery slope that could put more of your individual and personal rights at risk. The ballot language is confusing, but one thing is clear. Kansans don`t want another government mandate. So, on August 2nd, send a message. Say no to more government control. Vote no on changing the constitution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Joining me now is the former Democratic governor of Kansas, \uc2 Kathleen Sebelius\uc2 . It`s great to have you on. And I`ve been following this very closely. First, maybe if you can just tell us like how present is this in people`s consciousness there? I`m a little worried that it`s going to go under the radar, although obviously, the Dobbs road news is huge.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:08]\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN SEBELIUS\uc2 , FORMER GOVERNOR, KANSAS: Well, Chris, nice to be with you. The Republican legislature is specifically and very cynically intended for this to go under the radar. This decision was made by the court in 2019. And it actually was a, you know, multi-jurisdiction effort, which started with a protest over a Kansas law in 2015.\par \par \uc2 The legislature passed a restrictive law, doctor sued saying it was overly restrictive, but they sued not under the protection of the federal constitution, but they sued under the Kansas constitution. It made its way through the court and a six-to-one Supreme Court decision. Issued in 2019, the Supreme Court justices said you`re absolutely right. The Kansas constitution actually has broad protection of individual rights. And this is a natural, inalienable right belonging to all of the citizens of Kansas, certainly including the women of Kansas.\par \par \uc2 So, very strong decision. The Republican legislature then crafted a very confusing amendment, intentionally confusing, and chose the lowest possible turnout election, a primary in a Midterm manner, not a presidential year, not anything else. So, it`s a vote in three weeks very intentionally trying under the radar. What they didn`t figure is that the Dobbs decision would come down in this kind of timely manner and really galvanized people in Kansas to take a look at what the situation could be.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it`s interesting, because obviously, you know, you are the governor of that state. There`s currently a Democratic governor in the state of Kansas. It`s a fairly conservative state at the presidential level. It`s a very Republican state and has been very Republican for a very long time going all the way back to the founding of the Republican Party, actually.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I thought the polling -- there`s two things interesting. One, the timing of this vote suggests a little lack of confidence on the part of the people trying to push for it. And I thought this polling from the Kansas Speak survey, you know, which is again, it`s December of last year, but it says, you know, 60 percent agree women are better positioned than politicians to make choices about abortion. 60 percent agree, 14 percent disagree.\par \par \uc2 The Kansas government should not place any regulations on circumstances under which women get abortions. Agree 50, 25. Now, again, all pulling you have to take with a grain of salt, but as someone who was elected statewide with your politics on abortion, where do you think Kansans are on this?\par \par \uc2 SEBELIUS: Well, I think Kansas voters have proven that they are willing to vote for pro-choice elected officials statewide. I was an elected governor, two terms. We have a pro-choice governor right now. We have people who are elected in Kansas. The issue is not what the regulations are in place. And Kansas has pretty, you know, standard abortion regulations about parental consent and waiting periods and a whole variety of things. None of that changes.\par \par \uc2 This is really whether the Kansas legislature should be allowed to totally ban abortion without any exceptions for rape or incest. This would allow the Kansas legislature to really follow some of the other states that have totally eliminated a woman`s opportunity to make her own health choices.\par \par \uc2 And I think Kansans, if they understand what`s at risk, will oppose that kind of sweeping mandate by the government, a force birth mandate if you will. Whatever happens you`re going to carry pregnancy to term, whether you`re healthy or not, whether you`re capable or not, whether you know, it engenders, you know, your health in the future or the other children in your family. I mean, none of that matters. We will force you to carry a birth determine. I don`t think most Kansans feel that that`s what they want the legislature to do.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s -- as you said, this was calendered before the Dobbs decision partly because the anti-abortion politicians are thinking ahead. Because, you know, they want to be able to outlaw abortion as soon as Dobbs comes down. You said it`s been galvanized folks who are organizing the vote no movement. That`s the no vote on the constitutional amendment. Crowds of marchers turned out to rally in Wichita. Are you confident that there`s -- there is a mobilization happening?\par \par \uc2 SEBELIUS: Well, tomorrow is the deadline for people to register to vote. The other issue about a primary election, Chris, is in Kansas, typically, people who are independents don`t vote in primaries, right?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. You see primaries as a partisan choice, and frankly, Democrats don`t have very many primaries. So, primaries are typically Republicans choosing among multiple candidates. Our real challenge, those of us who strongly believed that a no-vote is the appropriate way for Kansas to go forward, is to make sure people know. Make sure people register and vote in August. If we can do that, I think the no votes will prevail.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, \uc2 Kathleen Sebelius\uc2 , thank you so much for your time tonight.\par \par \uc2 SEBELIUS: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Monday night. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220712ei7b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for July 11, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Ali Vitali, Vaughn Hillyard, Cal Perry, Sam Stein,  Ben Collins, Vin Gupta, Ellison Barber \par \uc2 8,104 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good afternoon. I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 We start with breaking news. Two sources telling NBC less than an hour ago that the January 6 Committee is rescheduling Thursday`s primetime hearing. It`s a fascinating development at a time when the committee appeared to be on the verge of wrapping up its case to the American people. Instead, we`re now looking at just one hearing that`s actually on the calendar and it will be starting just 24 hours from now.\par \par \uc2 What happens after that is unclear. We do know there`s been lots of new testimony coming in along with new pictures. This is a video from the new Trump documentary unprecedented released on Sunday. It`s a stark reminder of the dangers the men and women in the Capitol faced that day, the peril that our nation faced as a whole, and the former president`s justification for what happened.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Went to Washington primarily because they were angry with an election that they think was rigged. A very small portion, as you know, went down to the Capitol, and then a very small portion of them went in. But I will tell you, they were angry from the standpoint of what happened in the election because they`re smart and they see -- and they saw what happened.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, among the crowd that day, far-right extremists like the Proud Boys and the Oath Keepers. They will be the focus of tomorrow`s hearing along with Trump`s role in encouraging them to march on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 STEPHANIE MURPHY\uc2 , (D-FL): The president`s tweet on the wee hours of December 19 of, be there, be wild, was a siren call to these folks. And we`ll talk in detail about what that caused them to do, how that caused them to organize, as well as who else was amplifying that message.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The committee member who will lead tomorrow`s hearing, Jamie Raskin, says the key concept they`ll explore is the "convergence of Trump- led efforts to overturn the election and extremists-led efforts to carry out an insurrection at the Capitol." Now, the only witness we know of is Jason Tatenhove, a former spokesperson for the Oath Keepers, pictured here on the left, he`s the one with the cane. He will testify live tomorrow. What we don`t know is whether we`ll hear from the group`s leader, Stewart Rhodes, who`s also offering to testify but only if it`s live, and that`s something that committee has not agreed to.\par \par \uc2 Then there`s that major about-faced by \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , Trump`s former right- hand man and one of the most adamant and vocal opponents of the committee`s work, who`s now saying he would testify as well. According to letters obtained by NBC News, that decision comes after Trump said he would waive his claim of executive privilege amid reports that Trump is frustrated by the incriminating testimony of so many other Republicans and former supporters. Among the key questions that the committee will want to ask what Bannon knew before January 6.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R-WY): On the evening of January 5, the president`s close adviser, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , said this on his podcast.\par \par \uc2 STEVE BANNON\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF STRATEGIST: All hell is going to break loose tomorrow. Just understand this. All hell is going to break loose tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in NBC Capitol Hill Correspondent Ali Vitali, former federal prosecutor Glenn Kirschner, a Republican strategist and former communications director for the \uc2 RNC\uc2  Doug Heye, and NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard, who has been traveling with former President Trump and joins us from Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 So let`s start with that breaking news, Ali, Thursday`s hearing now being rescheduled. Do we know why?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chris, my colleague, Haley Talbot, and I were the first to report that that hearing is being rescheduled. Now, it was never officially noticed by the committee. They typically do that about a week out. But now they are saying that it is postponed -- our sources telling us that it`s postponed to a later date.\par \par \uc2 And look, you have to infer that the reason for that is because they`re getting new cooperation, not least of all, from the former White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 . We saw how people like Vice-Chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and others were pleading with him to come forward and do another deposition with the committee. On Friday, they got that to the tune of nearly eight hours of questions and answered that spoke to in the words of a spokesperson for the committee.\par \par \uc2 Every key point of the committee`s investigation so far, and importantly, that Cipollone`s testimony didn`t contradict any of the testimony that we`ve already heard, including from people like Cassidy Hutchinson, who made many claims during her testimony, including though, that Cipollone said things like if Trump were to go to the Capitol, they would be charged with -- charged with every crime imaginable. And, of course, what Cipollone allegedly told \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  on the day of the 6th as the insurrection was happening, which was that if they didn`t do more, there would be blood on their hands.\par \par \uc2 All of those, are really key points both in Hutchinson`s testimony, and in the committee`s findings, overall, the fact that Cipollone doesn`t contradict any of that is certainly notable and so while the committee may have thought that they were doing two hearings this week, it now looks like they`ll do one, as they try to wrap in all of this new information that they`re getting, and that they could hope to get from possibly people like \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , though there are some hitches in his offer to testify specifically that he wants to do this live and in public, and the committee has effectively said no, not so fast.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, I just want to make sure we`re clear here. We know there is the hearing tomorrow, but another one could possibly be held maybe next week.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes, it will likely pop up. Again, the fact that they are fact- finding in real-time as they are telling this story has always been the balancing act that I`ve been most fascinated by with this committee. That last hearing is one that we`ve been looking ahead to for some time now. It`s supposed to focus on those key 187 minutes while the Capitol was under attack. And Congressman \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  is supposed to be one of the members who lead whatever that final hearing is, and whenever it`s rescheduled, he has said that it`s going to focus not just on what was happening here at the Capitol but heavily juxtaposing it with what was happening at the White House. It`s why this Investigative Committee has wanted to get as close to the former president as they can, as close to being in the room for those key conversations.\par \par \uc2 They`ve managed to successfully do it for things like getting inside the Department of Justice, getting inside the pressure campaign at the state level, but now on this key day of January 6 itself, it makes sense why they would want to not just pull all the pieces together of the outside allies who were working outside the White House to potentially foment this insurrection, but also wanting to get as close as they can to the inner circle of this White House if they can`t get \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , which he doesn`t seem like they`re going to. The fact that they now have \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  is really substantial and could make sense for why they`re wanting to postpone this final hearing or what could be the final hearing for this critical 187 minutes focus that they`re ultimately planning on doing.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. Glenn, the committee has built its case meticulously up to this point. If you were presenting this as say, a closing argument at trial but maybe with witnesses, what would make you stop and reschedule now? What`s your thought about what`s big enough, what`s important enough, or is it maybe just a matter of logistics and being able to get everything done?\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: It could be just a matter of logistics, Chris, but it seems like you know that the presentations have been so seamless up until now that I think it`s less likely this is some kind of a logistical snafu. Remember, not so long ago, it was -- there -- they were imploring \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  to come forward and testify. And then what happened? Cassidy Hutchinson testified about some dramatic communications with \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  and it feels like he then folded his cards, and came in for eight hours` worth of testimony. It seems like they`re collecting information in real-time.\par \par \uc2 I`ve had this happen in my trials when I was litigating a lengthy case, and something new came to our attention. And we would say, Judge, we`re going to need a day or two to assess this, to corroborate it, and then to consider presenting it to the jury, I kind of feel like the J-6 committee is in a similar posture.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Doug, if you`re a member of Trump`s inner circle or a Republican member of Congress, does this make you nervous?\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HEYE, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Absolutely. You`re getting more nervous every day. And you know, Glenn used the word presentation. And I think that`s the exact right term for what the committees put together. These aren`t hearings in the traditional sense, though, there are witnesses and testimony. These are presentations of what the committee knows.\par \par \uc2 And what we know is that the committee knows a lot more than we do. But we`re learning that they don`t know everything. They get caught by surprise, too so they have to process new information before they put that back in front of us. And the information clearly is going to be damaging to members of the previous administration or all the way up to the president himself.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, and I guess if you want to talk about \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , Doug, this does seem like a bit of a last ditch effort by Trump to get somebody out there to defend him. I mean, there are people who gave incriminating testimony who said frankly, they would still vote for him. So it -- does that indicate to you that Trump or people who have his ear are already really nervous about what they`ve seen, let alone what might be coming?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Yes. Obviously, it was a total 180 by \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  and speaks to with what we saw, you know, in the -- in the previous testimony from Cassidy Hutchinson that a lot of Republicans close to Trump are nervous. And clearly, look at \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  and he`s not testifying to do the committee any favors. We -- I think we can all agree on that. So there`ll be more of a circus-like atmosphere. We don`t know exactly what he`s going to say, but clearly, there`s nervousness there over either, what he knew and when he knew it or other people as well.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Glenn, Congressman Swalwell interviewed Bannon during the Russia investigation and he actually was on MSNBC last night and said "I would expect zero absolutely nothing." This guy was like interviewing the Joker. He is insane. What do you think is behind this move?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Chris, this is a ruse, this is a ploy by \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  to try to come up with a half-baked defense. He wants to try to present to the jury in his contempt of Congress trial that well, look now I`m willing to testify because now \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is suggesting he will waive executive privilege, let`s set aside the fact that \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  never had the executive privilege to assert in the first place. But interestingly, just today, the prosecutors in the Bannon case filed what`s called the motion eliminate to prevent Steve Bannon`s defense team from even offering this evidence to the jury that now he`s willing to testify. This is a ruse and it will fail, and \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  really is not somebody who is to be credited.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Vaughn, you`ve been following the former president. What`s he saying about all of this?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chris, the former president is making himself the martyr in this investigation here. He is not running from the existence of it. Instead, he is going to the likes of here in Nevada over the weekend, as well as Alaska, and essentially all but campaigning on the existence of the investigation is a persecution effort against him. And when you`re talking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , there is no veiled reference to the way that he perceives these political opponents of his. He calls them "evil people." Take a listen to him this weekend in Alaska.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The way they go after people that are innocent people, political people, and yet they leave killers on the street to shoot people. Despite great outside dangers, our biggest threat remains the sick, sinister, and evil people from within our country. But no matter how big or powerful these corrupt radicals may be, you must never forget this nation does not belong to them. This nation belongs to you. It belongs to you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Chris, there are no words of remorse or regret from the former president as it pertains to January 6. He is openly talked about in the last month issuing pardons if you were to become president again for those who have been charged, the more than 800 who have been charged by the DOJ for their connection to the events that took place that day. Again, when we`re talking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  making himself the martyr in this situation for political purposes, it`s exactly what we saw him do when it came to the Mueller probe and those impeachments that approached the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, let`s be clear, in that audience, he`s speaking to the choir, right? I mean, it`s not like people are coming out to hear him speak who have to be convinced of what he`s saying.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: And this is also the establishment of the Republican Party today. That Nevada event there where we hear his remarks this weekend, he had the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  candidate -- Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , he had the gubernatorial candidate, the Treasurer candidate, the Secretary of State candidate, the chairman of the GOP party here in Nevada, and essentially, the Republican Party of Nevada was standing right there alongside of him as he is making this case to the broader electorate.\par \par \uc2 I mean, when he comes into stops like this, he`s hitting the local airways and he continues to press the case that he is the victim of a political attack here. And when we`re talking about a sustained effort over the course of the years, the question is, how many more people is he able to bring on board and actually believe that he was, in fact, being a target here by Democrats up on Capitol Hill?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Such important context. OK, Glenn, let`s talk about tomorrow. 24 hours from now this hearing should be underway. I think one of the interesting aspects here is that these groups like the Proud Boys and the Oath Keepers have argued in court, that they were called to Washington on the six that they believe that because of that they were justified in being there. So what are the key connections you want to see established between these groups and the Trump inner circle, if not Trump himself?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Yes, Chris, this is a chain that I contend has three links. We have the Oath Keepers, and we want to see if there`s a link between the Oath Keepers and the war council at the Willard war room because we know that \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 , among others, had some close associations with the Oath Keepers. And then the third link in that chain will be from the Willard war room right into the White House itself. So I think that`s some of what we`re hoping for when we hear from people who are high up in the Oath Keepers organization and that`s some of what we expect to hear tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Doug, we don`t know a lot about Pat Cipollone`s testimony, only that he did not appear to contradict previous testimony in any meaningful way, as we heard from Ali. So where do you think his testimony plays into the hearings, maybe tomorrow, maybe one next week? Where do you think they`re going to focus?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Oh, I`ve seen where this committee is going because who knew what and when did they know it? And Cipollone`s testimony is going to be very key for that not just for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  but those around him. So if you`re a \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , what \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  said is vitally important to your future existence.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Doug Heye, Ali Vitali, Glenn Kirschner, Vaughn Hilliard, thanks to all of you.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 And coming up, should President Biden run for reelection? Well, a new poll suggests that prospects with his own -- within his own party are bleak right now. And rising numbers, high-profile cases lock downs overseas. We`ve seen it all before. But have we let our guard down too much to stop another COVID wave? But first, the latest on desperate efforts to save iconic sequoias of Yosemite Park, our correspondent just got a new vantage point to show you the fire. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: By the time we got down into Yosemite Valley, ash is raining down on us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now, flames burning out of control are threatening a beloved national treasure, the towering ancient sequoia trees at Yosemite National Park. And sprinklers have become a last line of defense to protect them.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 The so-called Washburn fire has grown to over 2000 acres since it began last Thursday. With more hot weather and wind on the way, it`s expected to spread in size and power. Underscoring how climate change is making these fires more explosive than ever. NBC correspondent Cal Perry joins us now from \uc2 Mariposa County\uc2 , California. Cal, I understand you just moved locations. What are you seeing there?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, MSNBC INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes. So, Chris, we wanted to give you a view of what they call a back burn here. And, Carlos, if you want to try to sort of making your way from right to left for the viewer, this is a hill that is just inside one of the busiest entrances to this park. Normally, July and August, you would have a million people coming through this very area. But what`s happening now is firefighters are creating a line and they`re trying to burn out a lot of this brush. They`re trying to basically clear out this entire hill to protect both buildings, but to also direct the fire away from what you`re talking about, these 3000-year-old sequoia trees that draw so many visitors every year.\par \par \uc2 Also worth mentioning that sequoia trees take out so much of the carbon from the air. It is in so many ways a climate story, Chris, as you said these fires burning hotter and longer. It is something that is on the mind of the frontline firefighters who have been doing this now, year-round. Take a listen to what one firefighter told me over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GARRETT DICKMAN, YOSEMITE NATIONAL PARK FOREST BIOLOGIST: Climate is it`s making that fire season longer, it`s making it hotter, and it`s making firefighters exhausted. And you know we`ve been fighting fires for months already and it`s July, we still have many, many more months to go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERRY: The conditions, Chris, are vitally important to how this is going to play out in the coming days and weeks. When you sort of saw earlier in the day we were covered in smoke, that smoke is cleared out which is bad news for the firefighting because you can see the wind is picking up. So the wind is taking this fire and moving it pretty quickly in one direction. More than 2400 acres burned so far, we have a fresh number, also, there`s now 545 firefighters on-scene. A lot of that, of course, is regional support, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, I mean, let me ask you a little bit more about that regional support because I know when I was there in LA -- based in LA and I covered massive fires, you know, you would get reinforcements from Oregon, from Washington, after their fire seasons were over from Alaska, Arizona, and New Mexico. But because as the guy you just interviewed said these fire seasons everywhere lasting longer, how much more difficult is it to get outside help?\par \par \uc2 PERRY: But I mean, you`re hitting it right. It is really hard, especially when there are fires elsewhere. You`ve got some fires raging in the American West, not as bad as actually, we`ve seen last year or the year before, but it`s still early in the season. So when you would normally have crews from the state of Washington, from the state of Oregon, from the state of Utah, for example, well, Utah has two pretty big fires burning right now in the American West, Chris, as you well know is heating faster than