gun law -- excuse me, the biggest advance in 30 years, but it`s under the shadow of the latest mass shooting in Highland Park. And critics, who say he`s still not done enough like Manuel Oliver, a father who lost his son in the Parkland shooting, and who briefly interrupted the president`s speech this morning telling him to do more. There`s also the larger concern, which is the response to the Supreme Court overturning Roe v. Wade. Two weeks after the ruling and about 10 weeks after a leaked opinion gave them time to prepare, Biden is still deciding whether to declare a public health emergency for abortion.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Sam Stein, White House editor for Politico and an MSNBC contributor, Kristal Knight is a Democratic strategist who served as political director for Priorities USA, the largest Democratic super PAC in the 2020 election cycle. She has also worked on the presidential campaigns of \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . Good to see both of you.\par \par \uc2 So, Sam, it was nearly eight weeks between the leak that your publication reported on Roe v. Wade and the opinion itself. The question a lot of folks are asking is why wasn`t the message crafted then so that the minute the decision came, out they would have a response and why the ongoing indecision?\par \par \uc2 SAM STEIN, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: That`s a great question. It`s kind of befuddling for a lot of people, especially after the president himself took a week to come out publicly and say he would endorse scrapping the filibuster if it meant codifying Roe into law. He could have said that the day after the decision, obviously.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:02]\par \par \uc2 We`ve done a bit of reporting on this, my colleague Adam Cancryn today has a story up. Some of this is simply that they petrified of the conservative judiciary, knowing the court that struck down Roe in the first place would end up being an impediment for any action that they took that was aggressive. For instance, you know, opening up federal lands in red states would get a legal challenge swiftly and could be held up in the court because of the court`s conservative tilt.\par \par \uc2 But that`s a policy response. And the messaging response, Chris, it`s a great question because, you`re right, they had eight weeks to prepare for this. And for the past two week, they seemed incredibly reactive, capped off yesterday with a quote from the departing comms director, Kate Beddingfield, which basically admonished a lot of the abortion rights activists within the Democratic Party tent saying that they were living in some sort of unreality.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let me read specifically what that statement said in part. "Joe Biden`s goal in responding to Dobbs is not to satisfy some activists who have been consistently out of step with the mainstream of the Democratic Party."\par \par \uc2 Two things, there are a lot of people would say if the Democrats are aligned on anything, it`s abortion and is calling a key part of your party some activists the best way, Kristal, to keep them on your side?\par \par \uc2 KRISTAL KNIGHT, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: No, that`s not the best way. And actually what it reflects is that this White House is not prepared to meet the moment that they are in. And that statement by the White House communications director, it`s signaling that the progressive issues that the base of this party cares about are not the priority of this administration. And that`s why you see a 64 percent, you know, disapproval rating for this president.\par \par \uc2 That`s why you see these articles and these calls about having a different person being at the top of the ticket for 2024. These are challenging times that the country is facing right now. On top of the economic issues, on top of inflation, and to not have a president who is aggressive, who is on the offense about issues that the base cares about is really unfortunate. And it`s just not meeting the voters where they are right now today.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And there are a lot of people who argue, look, they do have a story to tell. They just need to be better prepared.\par \par \uc2 STEIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Kristal, who or at least what kind of person do you think they need heading the communications office now?\par \par \uc2 KNIGHT: Well, it has to be someone who has been on the ground in the states. Someone who has their ear to what activists are saying. Someone who has the ability to work with all members of the party, really not only on the Democratic side, but also understanding what the other side is saying as well.\par \par \uc2 I think when you have an administration that has not gone out and touted what they`ve actually done, they cannot go back and speak to those same people about their accomplishments. And so whoever is the next person to lead the office needs to understand that coalition building is important. Talking to activists, it is important because these are the people who are actually pushing out your message on behalf of the administration.\par \par \uc2 And so if you don`t have good validators, it`s no wonder that we are in the mess that we`re in right now with the party.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And Sam, sometimes if there`s one controversy, right, especially in this news cycles, we`re in these days that everything moves so fast, you can past it. But there does seem to be this sort of series, right, with abortion, again, knowing what was coming, the president only signed an executive order Friday. He only mentioned the public health crisis idea yesterday and fellow Democrats brought that up weeks ago. The news that he was considering an anti-abortion judge for a federal Kentucky judgeship.\par \par \uc2 Do you think at this point, Sam, would the Democrats just rather see the White House kind of throw in everything but the kitchen sink and fight even if they fail rather than be sort of slow roll a response?\par \par \uc2 STEIN: I think there`s something to that. The idea here that the White House is so reactive to news that they could have been much more aggressive in responding to especially because they had the leaked draft. I mean, remember, the reason -- one of the main reasons \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was elected was this idea that he would bring some sort of normalcy and stability to the country. He wasn`t a big -- he wasn`t the candidate of the party`s base.\par \par \uc2 But he was the candidate of the party`s majority. And that`s why he won the nomination. And that`s largely why he beat Trump, this idea that he brings stability. I think what they`re suffering from right now is a sense that things are careening out of control writ large. It`s not just abortion. You know, the coronavirus, it continues to be a problem for the country. Obviously, the drawdown from Afghanistan started and precipitated, and then of course gasoline prices and inflation.\par \par \uc2 What this White House could really benefit from is a sense that he has brought stability to the country, but there`s not many tools at his disposal to show that or to produce that.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And I want to dig more, if I can, into that "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " poll, Kristal. Only 26 percent of Democrats think the party should renominate him in 2024.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:00]\par \par \uc2 Now given the choice between him and Trump, 92 percent of Democrats said they would stick with Biden, although they`re within the margin of error, just generically on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 I guess the bottom line is, if not Biden, then who, and is it too early for Democrats to be panicking?\par \par \uc2 KNIGHT: Well, I do think it`s a little early for the party to be panicking. There`s still enough time for this administration to turn the curve and go out and show exactly what they have done. You know, what the guest just stated is that the economic situation, it`s not the best right now. Inflation is high. The cost of groceries and everything is high. But what the administration can do is show how they have, you know, stabilized this country under a crisis like the pandemic that we`re still quite frankly living in.\par \par \uc2 But without those messaging tools, without the things, you know, to show specifically what has been done state by state, then it`s no wonder that many Americans don`t understand what this president has done. Miranda rights are under attack, abortion rights are under attack, policing -- excuse me, the George Floyd Police and Justice Act still has not passed. There are a number of things that this administration has not done.\par \par \uc2 But the things that they`ve done, they have not messaged it well. So voters do not know. And if voters don`t know, they will not be excited to go to the polls in November and they definitely won`t be excited to go to the polls in 2024.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Kristal Knight, Sam Stein, what a great conversation. Thank you both so much.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, with COVID restrictions and contact tracing programs rolled back, could the U.S. be caught flat footed by new COVID wave yet again? Vin Gupta on why many epidemiologists are sounding an alarm.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:56]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: As much as we would all like to put COVID and this pandemic behind us, right now it`s hard not to feel a sense of deja vu when you look at some troubling new headlines about the most transmissible and immune- evading variant yet.\par \par \uc2 Senate majority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  now says he has tested positive for COVID. He is boosted. He says he`s fine. But with a Senate heading toward another break, he will have to work remotely.\par \par \uc2 New York City, his home and the one-time epicenter of this pandemic in the U.S., is again recommending mask wearing indoors as this Omicron strain fuels a sixth wave there.\par \par \uc2 And this variant`s impact in China is reverberating through financial markets around the world. As China reimposes curves and Omicron spreads, three cities have already have partial lockdowns.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Dr. Vin Gupta, an MSNBC medical contributor.\par \par \uc2 It`s always great to see you, but it`s always something like this which I`m not happy about because if you gauge by most people`s actions like around New York, almost no one wears a mask anymore, COVID seems to be under control. My question for you is, is it?\par \par \uc2 DR. VIN GUPTA, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Chris, great to see you. Good afternoon. So for your viewers, what`s the situation right now? What`s not going well? We know this newest subvariant of Omicron has a longer contagiousness period. So if somebody watching right now has been infected, they can be contagious for as long as eight days, maybe even longer than that. Prior variants, that window is about five days.\par \par \uc2 We know 75 percent of adults, Chris, of adults are not up-to-date with their vaccines. 30 percent haven`t gotten one shot. And also, I`m a pulmonologist, so looking around the corners to the winter, flu spikes are happening earlier in certain parts of the world like in Oceania, Australia than we would normally see them. So that heralds something potentially concerning in the winter, COVID and flu potentially.\par \par \uc2 What`s going well? The treatments still work. Whether it`s Omicron or it`s Delta, it doesn`t matter. The Omicron still requires the same viral machinery. In the cell, and the human cell requires the same type of machinery to replicate. Those treatments prevent viral replication in the cell. So it doesn`t matter what type of variant it is. Luckily our treatments work.\par \par \uc2 And critically for Senator Schumer here and others, when we use that term immune evasion, yes, antibodies are not that effective, they`re not that present, Chris, in our nose, in our nostrils here, so those antibodies (INAUDIBLE) that numerous so they`re not going to prevent necessarily an infection or positive test when you swab your nose, but our immune defenses are backward deployed in the lungs or T cells. They are still working. So the vaccines still work. The purpose of vaccination is to prevent pneumonia, ending up in the hospital. Unfortunately it`s not to prevent a positive test. That`s not what these vaccines are designed to do.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " has a pretty concerning summation. I`m going to read it for you, Vin. Nearly one-third of the U.S. population lives in counties rated as having high transmission levels by the CDC. Cases are rising especially in the south and west. The death rate from COVID-19 is still much higher than the mortality from influenza or other contagious diseases.\par \par \uc2 Officials have warned of a possible fall or winter wave even as public health measures have fallen by the wayside. And what some epidemiologists say is we cannot measure the risk right now by how many people are dying. So how should we be measuring our own personal risk and how concerned should we be? We don`t want people to die from the flu every year. More people are still dying from COVID.\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: Well, Chris, let me frame it like this. This is what I think we need to do to prepare for the winter because to your point, a lot of people are not as protected today as otherwise could be. They`re not up to date with their vaccines. So what can we do as a country? What could we be doing forward thinking?\par \par \uc2 We need -- you and I have talked about this. We need variable dosing of the therapeutics that work. So if you`re older, if you might have a preexisting condition, five days may not be enough. I know the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is thinking about maybe variable dosing, longer courses for certain people. They may need more of it. We need guidance sooner rather than later because I can tell you it`s anecdotally happening as we speak.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:04]\par \par \uc2 But there`s some confusion there. Pharmacists need full discretion on prescribing authorities similar to me as a doc, Chris. Meaning there was some positive movement from the \uc2 FDA\uc2  earlier -- last week allowing pharmacists to prescribe Paxlovid, other therapeutics. But they needed proof of blood work from the patient within the last 12 months. That`s a barrier. Me as a physician, I don`t necessarily need that proof. I can use my clinic discretion.\par \par \uc2 So I`d like to see more liberalization of pharmacists` ability to prescribe these medications. 50 state telehealth waivers, Chris. Telehealth is critical. It`s a critical access point for people especially in the rural, ex-urban counties to get care. Only 12 states allow out-of-state clinicians to practice telehealth in their state. That`s a problem here.\par \par \uc2 And you know, what I`d like to see, Chris? I`d like to see a driver`s license paradigm for medical licenses, nurses licenses, mental health professional licensing so we can get people the care they need regardless of where these clinicians may reside. Virtual care is now the future. Let`s make it easier.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Common sense. Dr. Vin Gupta, thank you. Always great to see you, my friend. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  seems to be setting up an epic battle over whether he`ll be allowed to walk away from his plan to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Three months after both sides reached a deal, the \uc2 Tesla\uc2  founder filed paperwork on Friday to back out, claiming the company is in breach of multiple provisions of its sales agreement.\par \par \uc2 NBC correspondent Ben Collins joins me now.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been -- first of all, haven`t seen you in several years. Great to see you in person.\par \par \uc2 BEN COLLINS, NBC NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: This is really good. Really good to see you, too.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s good to see you. Look, I want to show you a meme that he sent out, right, because he`s \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: That`s how he operates. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And that`s how it goes. He references bots and spam accounts. What exactly is going on here?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: OK. So let`s back up. Let`s back to how Elon processes \uc2 Twitter\uc2  as a rich person. As the richest person, actually.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. The richest person.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Exactly. He has lots of people impersonating him on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . He says that those people, there are a lot more of those bots, those fake people than there are regular people. That`s not actually true. When \uc2 Twitter\uc2  measures users, they measure daily active users. DAUs. Those bots don`t fall into that category. But in Elon`s brain, it does because he sees lots of people impersonating him. Lots of fake people in his space, right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: So when he bought the company, he was like I`m going to get rid of these things and then he tried to get data after the fact. The problem is, he signed a binding agreement saying I`m going to buy this company for all this money, $54.20 a share. Now he`s trying to back out of that. You can`t really do that. You`re supposed to do due diligence before you buy the thing. Now he`s trying to find excuses to get out of this deal since \uc2 Tesla\uc2  has gone south since then.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So what options does \uc2 Twitter\uc2  have in a situation like this?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: So \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is going to posture as no, we want you to buy this thing at this incredibly marked-up price that you bought this for, for some reason. That meme price that you bought this for, for some reason. So they`re going to keep saying no, buy us. Please buy us, we`re begging you. At the end of the day, this is going to be about a buyout. This is going to be about, you know, people say it`s around a billion dollar buyout to get out of this deal.\par \par \uc2 It might be more than that at the end of the day with this sort of thing. In the process, the issue is the people I have talked to at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  are very upset about what happened. They were told that they were saving democracy that this company meant more to the world than just some Web site. And then instantly, once they got sold out, once somebody came along and said, we`ll offer you like double, that company got -- the people at that company got thrown under the bus.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, but let`s look at this as one company that has one of the largest platforms, right, for free speech and how that`s interpreted and how that`s enforced. Even how or if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could be on that platform or others like him who repeatedly post things that are untrue, there are consequences beyond the economics, right, of the stockholders, the people who work at that company, not to dismiss that, but this is a big deal in that sense.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes, absolutely. Elon is going to open this back up. What he wanted to do with this company, if you look at his filings about how to make money, he wanted to verify every single person on the platform so he could turn it into a payment platform. He wanted it to be like \uc2 WeChat\uc2 , like these much larger almost nationalized platforms. That`s a fundamentally different service that what \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is right now where you can be anonymous.\par \par \uc2 If you`re, say, in a country where you`re not allowed to talk about gay rights, for example. You`re allowed to be anonymous in that space. What he is offering is a very different idea. He might still make that thing, but it`s not what \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is. And he figured that out in the last few months.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right. Well, Ben, it is again great to see you, and thank you for all of that. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, a stunning rescue in eastern Ukraine. This man pulled from the rubble of an apartment building that was destroyed by a Russian missile. Look at them getting him out of there. We`ve got details on that attack next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:53:51]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Rescuers are racing against the clock to find survivors of a Russian missile strike on an apartment complex in eastern Ukraine over the weekend. New video shows rescuers working tirelessly with pick axes and heavy machinery, searching through what remains of the five-story building. At least 30 people are dead, more than a dozen more believed still to be trapped in the rubble. Russia denies targeting any civilians.\par \par \uc2 NBC correspondent Ellison Barber is live in Dnipro, Ukraine with more.\par \par \uc2 Ellison, Russia always denies that they`re targeting any civilians. But let`s talk about those who are trapped and what the latest is on the recovery effort.\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, you said it, at least 30 people are dead after that five-story apartment building collapsed amid heavy shelling. Rescuers, they have actually pulled about nine people out from the rubble alive but their work is still ongoing. Families have been visiting this scene in the days, hours after this building initially collapsed, looking for answers, praying for some sort of miracle.\par \par \uc2 Listen to what one man had to say. He was there searching for his sister and young nephew. Listen.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:03]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through text translation): We don`t have good expectations but I am avoiding such thoughts. He is a small boy. Who could he hurt?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: Russian forces have increased their bombardment in northern Donbas. They are heavily bombarding that area. Despite that, the U.K.`s Ministry of Defense says that Russian forces have probably not made any major advancements but we see in city after city it is wreaking havoc on civilians. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, and the uncle asking the obvious question. A little, who could he hurt?\par \par \uc2 Ellison Barber, thank you so much for your ongoing reporting there.\par \par \uc2 Center court at last night`s \uc2 WNBA\uc2  All-Star Game a show of solidarity for Brittney Griner who remains detained in a Russian jail. Every player came out to start the second half wearing jerseys with her name and her number 42. Griner of course pleaded guilty Thursday to drug charges and will be back in court this week. Her attorneys hoped the plea gets her closer to release.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to do it for us this hour. I`ll see you tomorrow at 11:00 a.m. Eastern for a special preview of tomorrow`s January 6th Committee hearing. "KATY TUR REPORTS" starts next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Good to be with you. I`m Katy Tur.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220712ei7b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for July 10, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui \par \uc2 6,723 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:04]\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, MSNBC HOST: We`ll bring you the full hearings live at 10:00 a.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 But right now, it`s time for "POLITICSNATION" with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Thank you, Michael.\par \par \uc2 Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION. Tonight`s lead, system under siege.\par \par \uc2 Right now, another net is threatening to close around our rights, just as last month`s Supreme Court decision on undoing federal abortion rights was the result of the 50-year movement, a monstrous campaign of Republican- engineered voter suppression is now online just in time for our crucial midterm election. And though the blueprints have also been there for years, this monster`s growth has been accelerated by Republican state lawmakers in the aftermath of Donald Trump`s 2020 defeat.\par \par \uc2 The first signs of its havoc is now visible in the battleground state of Wisconsin with Democrats and voters of color are on the defensive after the state supreme court ruling this weekend that most ballot drop boxes are now illegal in that state ahead of the midterm elections. Of course, on cue, came the inevitable response, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  immediately seizing on the court ruling to pedal that his 2020 loss in Wisconsin must be overturned, leaning on state officials again to undermine democracy for his benefit.\par \par \uc2 As Congress prepares for another bombshell hearing, we have new late breaking information tonight about a prominent member of the white nationalist militia group, the Oath Keepers, who will appear on Capitol Hill this week. It`s all connected and it`s all tonight on POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Also, the son, daughter-in-law and grandchild of Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2 . will join me in a moment to talk elections, gun control and, of course, civil rights in 2022. So, a big show tonight, and we begin in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Congresswoman Gwen Moore, Democrat of Wisconsin and a member of the House Ways and Means Committee. Congresswoman, we appreciate you joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Voter disability -- voter and disability advocates, I should put them in their categories, and they all work together too, but both groups in Wisconsin are on alert this weekend, to put it lightly, this after your Supreme Court ruling Friday that most ballot drop boxes are now illegal in Wisconsin. And with that, voters cannot give absentee ballots to second parties to return in person.\par \par \uc2 The result comes less than a month out from primary elections in your state, midterms, of course, less than four months away. What is the impact here on voters of color and the disabled, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 REP. GWEN MOORE (D-WI): Reverend Al, I mean, you said it all. This will have such a real disparate impact on people with disabilities, elders, people in rural communities, and, quite frankly, all of us experienced this pandemic. And I personally voted by drop box because the pandemic raised some serious threats to my health at the time that they were implemented when there were no therapeutics and there were no vaccinations at that time.\par \par \uc2 About 70 percent of our voters who voted absentee in the city of Milwaukee voted using these drop boxes. And in the entire state of Wisconsin, over half of our voters voted absentee ballot. But when you look at the rigmarole that went on with the pulling up the blue boxes from the post office, you know, this really will impair the ability of people who are not ambulatory to be able to vote. And it`s a shame.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: 70 percent, you said. Let me just make sure our viewers hear you. 70 percent of the people in Milwaukee voted with drop boxes.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:00]\par \par \uc2 50 percent of the state voted absentee.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Those who voted absentee.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Right.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That`s a huge number of people that won`t be able to do that now. And, of course, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did not waste any time jumping on Friday`s state court ruling to push the big lie on his social media platform, basically shaming Wisconsin state GOP lawmakers to, quote, reclaim the electors and turn over the election to the actual winner. That is a quote, by which, of course, he means himself. And your state assembly leader praised the court decision but has not signaled that he will throw out the 2020 results in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Still, what does it say about -- what does it say to you that a bill in your state assembly to overturn a two-year-old election in favor of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , some expecting him to announce his next run for president within weeks, what does this say to you, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, I`d like to say that it`s unbelievable. But given all the rigmarole, we had a Special Counsel Gableman do an Arizona state like audit of our state, coming up with absolutely nothing at great expense to people in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Of course, just like all the other states, no fraud was found. And \uc2 Robin Vos\uc2  is a very clever politician, so I`m hoping that he is just giving lip service to wanting to do something further, because this already will hemorrhage votes. And we are going to have to work really, really hard, do some of the old stuff we used to do and give people rides to the polls since polling sites have changed, and numbers of other things.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And you know how to do it. I remember your first race. I was out there with you. We went from every barbershop everywhere.\par \par \uc2 But I am out of time. But I have to ask you about the president`s executive order to protect abortion rights. It will not restore most abortion access in Wisconsin. So, where the procedure is now almost completely illegal under a nearly 200-year old state law that was revived after last month`s Supreme Court ruling, overturning Roe, with no claims for (INAUDIBLE) abortion in Wisconsin, have you seen the makings of a public health crisis in your state?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Reverend Al, we are hoping to stem the tide of adverse consequences. Already, our state attorney general has said that he will not prosecute any of these cases. We have seen local district attorneys across the country say that they won`t prosecute under this law. We see Illinois, the state right next to us, overbooked for providing abortion care. And we have already started looking at ways to stockpile the abortion medication so that they will be available for people who need this scare.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you, Reverend Al, I`m a 70 year old woman, but I have lived a full life and abortion care is health care if you are female.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Thank you, Congresswoman Gwen Moore, for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow morning, the White House is set to host a celebration to commemorate the historic achievement of the passing of the most sweeping gun reform bipartisan bill in nearly three decades. My next guest will attend the event and are already in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, joining me is civil rights leader, and we worked closely together. Martin Luther King III, Arndrea King and the daughter, Yolanda Renee King, who has been very active herself in leading the fight. We`re welcoming all three of you to the show today.\par \par \uc2 And let me start with you, Martin. The gun reform bill that the president signed into law a few weeks ago provides grants to states for, quote, red flag laws, enhancing background checks to include juvenile records and closes the boyfriend loophole by keeping guns away from unmarried dating partners convicted of abuse. It will also require enhanced background checks for people aged 18 to 21 and funding for mental health services.\par \par \uc2 Now, this was a bipartisan compromise that seemed unimaginable until the recent mass shootings from the crimes in Buffalo to Uvalde, and it horrified the nation.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:02]\par \par \uc2 But it didn`t get all of the things that you and Arndrea and certainly Yolanda have fought for. How do you anticipate this gun reform to change the gun violence epidemic that America is currently facing and do you believe this will be enough? Do we have to keep pushing?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN LUTHER KING JR\uc2 . III, ELDEST SON OF DR. \uc2 MARTIN LUTHER KING JR\uc2 .: First of all, Rev, thank you for the question. And I would start by saying that this is, of course, a marathon and not a sprint. It is a good first step. But when we talk about resolving an issue, until we get to the point where we are willing to say that no one needs to have an assault weapon and that the Republicans who are willing to vote for this legislation, tragically, these incidents that we have seen are going to keep happening.\par \par \uc2 It is wonderful that that is a bipartisan effort that has begun. But we still have a long, long way to go. And we have to deal with this holistically. For example, if we are looking at schools, we have all kinds of technology. We have the technology that keeps dogs from going out. Why don`t we make schools safe and look at creating the technology so that guns will not work in a school zone that is designed as a safe zone, or an area where malls, where people are. Only police, say, would have the ability, maybe.\par \par \uc2 But the fact of the matter is, we have the technology to do much further than we have done. We are just not doing what we need to do. But, yes, we are going to continue to fight to go further because this is just the beginning and first steps. And I must say, in long-term battles, you have to always acknowledge, this is a good first step.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Yolanda, your father mentioned schools and you are just a teenager, still in school. And we know we talked to you alone just a few weeks ago. We know a big part of the gun violence epidemic is also the school shooting epidemic. Schools have been spending billions on safety measures to stop mass shootings but it is not clear any of it will work. Do you think this gun control legislation will be effective in school shooting prevention? What are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 YOLANDA RENEE KING, YOUTH ACTIVIST: I think that, yes, it is a step forward and it is a small victory. My mom always says that you have to acknowledge and celebrate the small victories. So, I think it is going to be a small victory. It is not our final goal, but you kind of -- this fight is a marathon, and so we just finished a small segment of this marathon. And so you have to keep running this marathon.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, in some ways, I think it is going to make the situation a tiny bit better and it is great that we are having legislation passed, but it is not where we want to be. It is not where we want to be at the end. And I think that that is where a lot of people are getting confused. They think, since we are passing this, this is great. This is amazing. This fight is over. But, no, you have to keep on running.\par \par \uc2 And the civil rights movement, they didn`t -- it took a while to pass larger pieces of legislation and, first, they had to pass smaller pieces of legislation before you really saw the big impact, before you saw the big action.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, I`m really happy that this is happening and I`m really happy that we are moving forward, but now we need to continue to move forward. And so I think we need to balance. We need to celebrate this victory but also be aware that this isn`t the end. This is just the beginning.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Well, Arndrea, not only are you the daughter-in-law of Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , Coretta King and Martin`s wife and Yolanda`s mother, you were trained by C.T. Vivian, one of the giants in the movement. So, you know movements and you know battles. And this gun reform bill came after a rare bipartisan agreement on a controversial subject.\par \par \uc2 Switching gears for a moment, how can Democratic leaders reach the same progress on issues, like voting rights and protecting abortion access? You, as a seasoned yet young activist, well trained, that is why I want people to know your background, how do we use this model to start making steps in voting rights as well as women`s rights to choose?\par \par \uc2 ARNDREA WATERS KING, PRESIDENT, DRUM MAJOR INSTITUTE: I think, first and foremost, we have to look at forming vast coalitions. Looking at the intersectionality of this court, the same court, the same states, that are repressing and suppressing the right to vote are also suppressing and repressing woman`s rights.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:05]\par \par \uc2 So, we really need to look at how we can work together across networks.\par \par \uc2 We also need to look at what we can do, state by state by state. We know that this is now a state battle. That is why we are out there. Drum Major Institute, NAN, SCIU, a march on last august for doing the march on to voting rights, and we were in 100 cities all on one day in 41 states. Because we knew even then that this has to be a battle that is looked at, state by state. At the same time, we still have to push for national, federal protections and legislations.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And as you say that, and I want you to finish your thought, but I have been saying a lot the last few days, we are really fighting a states` rights movement. I mean, the civil rights movement that C.T. trained in, and, certainly, Martin, your father, was to really fight states` rights. States` rights is why we had Juneteenth. Texas wouldn`t tell people they were no longer slaves.\par \par \uc2 And now if you go back to states saying, we will decide who gets an abortion in the state, we decide what the voting laws are, we are back to states rights. I remember growing up. I was born in Brooklyn. My mother -- both of you came to my mother`s funeral. She was born and raised in Alabama. She had they had to in a map to say, in these states, we can stay in a hotel, where now, in these states, we know where we can get an abortion. We are back to states` rights.\par \par \uc2 A. KING: Exactly. And, again, that is why we were on the ground in 41 states last year. Also, historically, you have to understand that even the notion of states` rights actually was born from the civil war, right? Because it was states that wanted to continue slavery versus the federal government. So, states` rights always have not been in the favor of black, brown, women or oppressed people.\par \par \uc2 And I think, most importantly, what we have to keep in mind is that \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2 .`s last sermon that he gave here in Washington, D.C., was entitled, sleeping through a revolution. And it`s so interesting to me that now being woke is a lot of times being used as an insult, when during that last sermon that Martin Luther King Hr. gave on this earth, he told us to stay awake, to not sleep through a revolution.\par \par \uc2 And so now, more than ever, we are in the midst of a revolution. And it is up to each one of us to stay awake, to stay vigilant, and to literally get out, vote, and make our voices heard.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. And that last speech there in Washington, at the Washington Cathedral where Martin III has spoke and now Yolanda has spoke, three generations of Kings mounted their pulpit. And the last two, I think, Arndrea helped write the speeches, but I don`t tell family secrets.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to the King family, Martin, Arndrea and Yolanda Renee, thank you for joining me.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, more on this afternoon`s big breaking news, a former Oath Keeper will testify publicly when the January 6th committee hearings pick back up again on Tuesday. We will break down what he might say and what we might hear with my political panel.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  pays tribute to Brittney Griner at the all-star game today as she remains a prisoner in Russia. How can we rise up to help make sure she is home for next year`s game? That all after the break.\par \par \uc2 But, first, my colleague, Richard Lui, with today`s other top news stories. Richard?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC HOST: Rev, a very good afternoon to you. Some of the stories that we`re watching right now, South Korea military officials say North Korea appears to have conducted a series of artillery firing drills. This comes days after the United States deployed F-35 stealth fighter jets to South Korea for joint training.\par \par \uc2 The political party of slain former Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  won a supermajority in today`s parliamentary elections. Abe was shot and killed two days ago. The Liberal Democratic Party and its coalition partners won a total of 87 seats there.\par \par \uc2 And firefighters in California are struggling to preserve a grove of century and thousand-year-old sequoia trees and Yosemite National Park. The massive trees only grow wild in the western slopes of the Sierra Nevada. The Washburn fire is 0 percent contained and more than doubled in size in just one day.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:20:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: For this week`s Rise Up, I want us to continue to lift the name of basketball player Brittney Griner. Earlier today, the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  kicked off its all-star game without one of its biggest stars. The league paid tribute to the Phoenix Mercury center and honorary all-star as she fights for her freedom from a Russian jail.\par \par \uc2 All players from both teams wore Griner`s number 42 in the second half. This week, the 31-year-old pled guilty to drug smuggling charges. She now faces up to ten years in prison. It has already been nearly five months since her arrest, and during this time, she hasn`t been able to speak to her wife or her parents. The only contact she has had is through her attorneys.\par \par \uc2 She is Housed in a jail where 98 percent of the people cannot speak English. Brittney wrote a letter to President Biden saying, quote, I am terrified I might be here forever.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:03]\par \par \uc2 Her wife, Cherelle, has described the difficult conditions her wife has been living in while being transported to court hearings, which are hours away from the jail, including hours spent riding in a small vehicle not able to handle her 6`9 frame.\par \par \uc2 As president of National Action Network, I joined Cherelle in Chicago for a press conference on Friday. This is what she had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHERELLE GRINER, WIFE OF BRITTNEY GRINER: I want to make it very clear that our next move as supporters for B.G. is to make sure that the administration understands that they have our full support in doing any and everything necessary to be able to bring B.G. home, as well as every other wrongfully detained American.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: President Biden has made a commitment to do whatever is necessary in this case. And I will continue to push the administration until Brittney is returned home.\par \par \uc2 I got involved through NAN, because after I mentioned this case here on POLITICSNATION a couple of months ago, there was radio silence. Now, there is momentum and I plan to do all I can to help. I have even reached out to the Russian Orthodox Church and the White House to organize a clergy visit to go and pray with or for Brittney in Russia.\par \par \uc2 I hope this case will help people understand that sports and faith go beyond borders. They transcend politics and even war. Let`s have some areas where we put our differences aside and come together.\par \par \uc2 Brittney traveled to Russia to play basketball in part because female athletes still aren`t being paid what they deserve here in the United States. But she also went to share our culture and serve as an ambassador for American values, as our greatest athletes have always done from Jesse Owens to Muhammad Ali. That is the reason she is being held unjustly and it is also the reason we must rise up and do everything in our power to bring her home.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:31:18]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. I want to bring in my political panel to get their take on some hot topics. Joining me now is Kurt Bardella, an Adviser to the DNC and the DCCC and Rick Wilson, who is a Republican Strategist, and a co-founder of the Lincoln Project. He also is the author of " Running Against the Devil: A Plot to Save America from Trump--and Democrats from Themselves."\par \par \uc2 Rick, let`s start with some breaking news as the January 6 committee gears up for its next hearing on Tuesday, we learned just hours ago that a former spokesperson for the White Nationalist militia group, the Oath Keepers, will appear as a witness at this next session, according to a source familiar with the hearing plans. What do you think we might hear from this witness? And what do you make of the other news this weekend that former White House Adviser \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  might also soon appear before the committee?\par \par \uc2 RICK WILSON, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Well, thanks for having me on Rev. I will say this, I think that the -- this hearing is going to be one of the wrap up points for the committee. They`ve shown this arc of a conspiracy, a set of actions. And now they`re going to show how the shock troops that Trump expected to store in the capital were organized and how what role they played in the violence of that day.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s important to realize that, you know, the Oath Keepers, and the Proud Boys and all the other miscellaneous, what I call the mutant parade of these, you know, outright militia groups, these people came armed. Not all of them had their weapons with them, but they were ready to do violence that day on behalf of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And I think we`re going to see the committee start to really dig into that. We saw what they did with just bear spray and flagpoles and stolen, you know, furniture and clubs and bats that day, God forbid what we would see if they had actually brought weapons to the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Kurt, this coming Tuesday, the panel is expected to focus heavily on extremist groups who played a role in the January 6 attack and their connection to the Trump White House. How much do you think these proceedings are moving the needle for Democrats as they head into the midterms?\par \par \uc2 KURT BARDELLA, DNC & DCCC ADVISOR: Well, I think it just tells the American people, Rev, that the stakes for this November are as high as you can imagine that this could very well be the last free and fair election that we have. Because what the committee has shown us is it wasn`t just about January 6, that the effort to try to throw out democracy is still ongoing, that these extremist groups these, you know, as Rick point out, the Shock Troopers of the Republican Party now, they`re still activated, they`re still trying to take things away from us. They`re still trying to take away our rights. They are hell bent on doing what by force, what they can`t do at the polls. And we need to all tune in and pay attention to that. Millions of Americans have tuned in, Rev, to watch these hearings.\par \par \uc2 The last hearing that they had was one the most watched hearing so far. There is an appetite and an interest in American people in getting the full truth, the full story of what happened on January 6, I think what we`re going to see on Tuesday, is the coordinated effort of the White House and these extremist groups and how that came into play. This wasn`t a spontaneous gathering, Rev. This was planned. This was orchestrated, this was organized and it was organized at the very highest levels of the Trump apparatus.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Kurt, sticking with you, earlier today President Biden gave remarks on abortion rights and the protests calling for the White House to do more. Let`s take a listen to what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: Because -- keep making your point. It`s critically important.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:02]\par \par \uc2 We can do a lot of things to accommodate the rights of women in the meantime. But, fundamentally, the only thing that`s going to change this is if we have a national law that reinstates Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, this comes as a Pennsylvania Senate and House passed a bill that would ask voters to amend the state constitution, to declare there is no right to abortion in Pennsylvania. President Biden has signed Friday, an executive order in an attempt to safeguard access to abortion, medication and emergency contraception. What do you make of the President`s efforts, Kurt?\par \par \uc2 BARDELLA: Listen, Rev, I think that the President is 100% right when he says the surefire way to protect women`s rights and women`s health in America and women`s autonomy is to passed laws. And to do that, we need a pro-choice Senate, we need a pro-choice House representatives. We need to preserve the majorities we have and grow the very, very narrow majority we have right now in the Senate. We need a full pro-choice Senate majority that will be willing to act and codify women`s rights in America. The only way that can happen is if people show up and vote, understand the Republican plan. Everything about what Republicans are doing is predicated on the idea that our side won`t show up and vote that our side is to demoralize, distracted and divided to participate in the democratic process. The single most important and effective thing that we can do is show up in mass and vote and put our money where our votes are, that`s really what it comes down to.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Rick, let me get your take on the abortion issue as well. We`ve seen Republicans in many states who quickly to institute abortion bans after the overturn of Roe versus Wade. And there`s even talk of some form of federal ban if the GOP takes back Congress. Do they risk overplaying their hand?\par \par \uc2 WILSON: You know, Rev, I`ve always made this argument that in these very hot social issues, including abortion, the side that out kicks, its coverage is the side that starts to lose. And I think a lot of Republicans who are not the Trumpy crazies, but the practical Republicans in the political space, some of them are starting to realize that they are now the dog that caught the car. And there are a lot of suburban women, we`ve seen polling that has shown a major switch in Independence, a break off of Republican women who are not, you know, these may not be radical, far left progressive pro-choice women, but they are also deeply uncomfortable with the idea that this very, you know, this white evangelical faction of the Republican Party is going to criminalize, you know, abortion, even in some states in cases of rape, incest, the life of the mother, that they have way overshot where the American people are on this issue.\par \par \uc2 And the polling has certainly shown that we`ve seen Republican voting intention drop. We`ve seen Democrats pick up in certain key races in swing states. I think this has had a political impact that they got what they wanted in their heart of hearts. But, you know, it`s like, I can eat a steak three times a day, it`s not going to be good for me.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Kurt, gas prices have gone down this week after record high in June. AAA says the national average per gallon of gas is just under $4.70, which is a drop from the high price of $5.16 in June, with an 11% drop this week alone, should Democrats be touting this as a success? Or is it too soon to tell if these lower prices will stick around?\par \par \uc2 BARDELLA: Well, I think that Democrats and the President, the administration need to continue doing everything they can to keep pressure on oil companies for one, so that when the price of gas, price of oil drops, so there`s the price of gas we`ve seen for too long, executives and oil companies make profits at the expense of the working Americans who are just trying to make ends meet and have to pay more at the pump. There`s no reason why that should happen.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has been very aggressive of pointing out that as the gas prices in this country has steadily rose, the profits for oil companies they had to, and that`s not the way it should be. So I think should always be cautious. I think we should always manage expectations. It`s obviously good news that gas prices are dropping. It`s good news that job creation numbers continue to be the strongest that we have ever seen in history that this President, this administration has presided over the most robust job creation that we have ever had. And those are things that keep our economy strong. We`re going to have difficult times. We need to be honest about this, American people and the President has, you know, there are challenging times ahead. Not everything is going to be easy or simple. But the bigger things that are on the ballot right now. Things like freedom, things like choice, things like liberty, things like women`s autonomy and health. Those things are permanent situations that we need to address right now. The price of gas will go down, the economy will continue to get better but at the end of the day if people don`t show up and vote things are just going to get bad for everybody in this country.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:07]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, Rick Wilson and Kurt Bardella, thank you for being on.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, I will have a conversation with Jonathan Jackson, son of Reverend \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2  and heir to the seat of Congressman Bobby Rush. Talk about his recent primary win in Illinois, and what he`s looking to contribute to the Congress would really bring that he feels they need to have at this point in history. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Midterms are now less than four months away with completed primaries in most states, setting the stage for November. Late last month, the crowded Democratic primary in Illinois` first congressional district saw nearly 20 contenders fighting to succeed 15th term Congressman Bobby Rush, merging from the PAC to win the nomination was businessman Professor Jonathan Jackson, son of civil rights leader and one of my mentors \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2 , and now cast as likely to win again in November. And heavily Democratic Chicago district is now going to be decided in November. Joining me now is the democratic candidate for Illinois First Congressional District Jonathan Luther Jackson.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:06]\par \par \uc2 Candidate Jackson, great to see you as always. Congratulations, of course, on your primary win. A smart then, we are hoping at some point to also have your Republican opponent here on the show. But let me ask you, what prompted you to run for this seat held by our longtime Congressman Bobby Rush? And what do you hope to do policy wise, you`re a professor, you`re on the smart as academic types I know. What policy wise, do you have in mind that will separate you from the PAC in Washington after you clearly separated yourself from nearly 20 Democrats that you just defeated for the nomination?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN JACKSON, (D-IL) CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: Well, one of the things I like to say, congratulations to all the opponents. I think, in large part, we agreed on most of the same issues that are facing the first district, the first district in Illinois and paying very much like so many other districts that have heavy African American populations, we`ve got to fight to raise wages and all the other good stuff.\par \par \uc2 One of the --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: We`re losing the sound.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: -- the state of Illinois, is we`re not -- is that we are in a blue state. And so we are secure in some ways, but the red states all around us, and the attacks are real, the on women`s reproductive rights. And I think that number one, we should fight to expand that definition. I think the Roe versus Wade decision is being too narrowly defined, strictly for women, that`s for families, and for health, all of us that have wives, that have daughters, that have children that we care about, there are now women that cannot get an abortion, if you will, that have to have cancer treatment in Ohio, that have to go to neighboring states.\par \par \uc2 There`s 10-year-old girl that I read about, that had been molested and had other vicious things happen to her that has to carry this child to term. I would like to be on the forefront of let`s balance this equation. If the perpetrators that did this to this child are seeking to force this child to have, carried his child to term, are we`re going to ask for vasectomies on men. Are we going to ask for castration on men that do violence against women?\par \par \uc2 So I think there`s other things that we can do to bring forward. What makes us a rational discussion right now, it`s irrational. And as far as legislative what I like to do, want to make sure that we are part of the economic agenda, the African American community has been left out, put aside and has not had a strong advocate, and my estimation have been great Congress, people have done great things. I want to join those numbers that are fighting for economic inclusion, the diversity, the equity, the inclusion, I want to be a part of that number right now.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, talking with you right now, candidate Jackson, I can`t help but think about your mother and father, your father, as you know, is a major influence in my life has and you know, this audience wants to know, what influence does he add Bobby Rush, both of them were idols of mine growing up in the movement. When I was a kid, what influence do they have on your thinking as you go to likely go to Washington? And your brother who was a brilliant Congressman.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yeah, I`m going to call you at this moment, Uncle Al. We`ve demonstrated so much, and you`ve been on the front line, as an activist, when the cameras weren`t there, when people wouldn`t show up for those that were dispossessed, those that were down hard, and those have been had suffered against injustice with the police department, that activism is in my heart. So I consider you, not at this moment, Reverend Sharpton, but Uncle Al, you`re going to Washington with me in my heart, my mother and father, and their activism will always be with me. I`m so honored that President Emil Jones, our former past Senate President in the state of Illinois, who led the way on a free South Africa by making the state of Illinois divest of their assets and pensions in South Africa, which in turn, the state of California and \uc2 CalPERS\uc2  and \uc2 CalSTRS\uc2  followed suit and it became a movement across the nation to divest from South Africa.\par \par \uc2 So that`s sort of thought leadership to President Emil Jones, my mother, my father, yourself. So I would like to say I`m going to be a mixture of the legislators and activists that are looking forward to being a part of a new freshman class in the Congress.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, candidate Jonathan Luther Jackson, Luther for \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , who was -- came and prayed for you when you born in, the Godfather, a lot of people don`t know that. He had to be in their family --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: And if I can say one thing, in all fairness, I`ve got an enormous amount of respect, appreciation for Congressman Rush. I joined the race second to last 30 days after he had already made an endorsement. So he`s always been an idol and a hero of mine. I love Uncle Bobby. And so I`ve got much appreciation, so I guess I`ll take a little bit of Bobby in the Panther with me too.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:09]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, Jonathan Luther Jackson, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my final thoughts, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: If there ever was a time for you to be alert, now`s the time. We are seeing before our very eyes a movement to move this country back from a strong national union federal government to states` rights. They`re starting it with women`s rights to choose, one state will do one thing, another can do another. They`re going into voting. They`re going into other areas.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:17]\par \par \uc2 That is why the intersection of all people that have suffered with states rights, blacks, immigrants, LGBTQ, women, all of them must intersect now and wake up and fight back. These stories about, do you like President Biden, and do you like the Congress? It is Do you like yourself, where you will have to go back in the days as I said earlier, where people have to choose states to go to get basic rights. This is what`s at stake in this election this November, or Senate, whatever party you choose, any Congress, whatever party you choose, that will make sure that we have a firm Federal National government that protects us from being in many ways dictated to are those that can get the estate to turn around and bring us back to where we thought you would never go again. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:00:01]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 5 p.m. Eastern. We continue our coverage of all the day`s top stories, including the breaking news that a former member of the Oath Keepers militia group will testify before the January 6 Committee. "American Voices" with my friend and colleague Alicia Menendez starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220711ei7a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for July 9, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Ali Arouzi, Richard Lui \par \uc2 6,607 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, MSNBC HOST: Thanks for watching "Symone."\par \par \uc2 I`m Michael Steele. I`ll be back here tomorrow at 4:00 p.m. Eastern, and mark your calendars for Tuesday when a special coverage of the next January 6th Hearings begin at 6:00 a.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 You can watch the full live hearing at 10:00 a.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 But right now, it`s time for "POLITICSNATION" with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [17:00:24]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Thank you, Michael.\par \par \uc2 Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: A different set of rules.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the inequity of American life and death continues to reveal itself, even through our tragedies, and while the nation mourns the seven people now dead after Monday`s mass shootings of dozens of parade goers in Highland Park, Illinois, their shooter in that case, though in custody and looking at life in prison, is otherwise unharmed, even after an intense police man hunt following the massacre.\par \par \uc2 Two states over in Akron, Ohio, protests have stretched into the second week spurred by the fatal police shooting of Jayland Walker last month.\par \par \uc2 Driven by the grim police body camera footage showing the moment Akron Police wounding Walker more than 60 times after car and foot chase last month. Two tragedies of gun violence, two glaring different outcomes and I`ll talk to Akron`s Congresswoman in just a moment about what her constituents have to say about that difference, what they want from the state`s investigations into Walker`s death.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks after the reversal of Roe versus Wade, how is the escalating abortion rights battle impacting her district and others like it?\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, President Biden issued an Executive Order protecting abortion access as best as he could, conceding that his reach is limited without congressional action to protect abortion, that he needs voters to keep Democrats in the majority to make that happen. And with now less than four months until midterm elections, that window may be closing.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the family of Brittney Griner is exhausting every option to secure her release from Russian custody. Last night, I joined Griner`s wife in Chicago as President of National Action Network, calling on the Russian government to free the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star as the Biden administration faces growing pressure to bring her home. More on that later.\par \par \uc2 But tonight, we start in Akron.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now Congresswoman Shontel Brown, Democrat of Ohio, whose district includes Akron.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 After another tough week for your Akron district, three days after his shooting death by Akron Police, you called on the Department of Justice to monitor the investigation of Jayland Walker`s killing. Given them how graphic the Akron police body camera footage is in its depiction of Walker`s last moments, and the fact that at least eight officers are currently on administrative leave for the role in the shooting, why do you feel the Justice Department`s involvement is necessary here? And what can you tell us about its response thus far?\par \par \uc2 REP. SHONTEL BROWN (D-OH): Well, first of all, thank you for having me, Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 I want to say that I join the family of Jayland Walker and the Akron community in mourning this tragic loss. I wrote the letter to the DOJ to oversee the investigation because the family should be assured that a full and fair investigation will be conducted that accurately portrays the facts and makes the findings consistent with those facts, Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 I think you can agree with me that for too long, Black communities have been punished by investigations that leave us with more unanswered questions than facts.\par \par \uc2 So, I was pleased when the US Attorney`s Office stated that they responded to our letter and advised us that the \uc2 Justice Department`s Civil Rights Division\uc2  and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  field office in Akron are closely monitoring and reviewing the circumstances around Mr. Walker`s death.\par \par \uc2 It`s also important to me that our communities can began to say with some confidence or complete confidence eventually that they believe that law enforcement agencies have performed a fair and just investigation in difficult times like this, so that we can start to rebuild the public trust.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:09]\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Now, I also acknowledge that this is an ongoing investigation, and I welcome the city`s commitment to a transparent investigation and review and we will continue to support the fair and accurate gathering of all the evidence in this case, Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 So, as we let this investigation move forward, I join the Walker family in asking everyone to remain peaceful.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, I am sure that people of Akron are sympathetic to the people of Highland Park, Illinois, and I`m sure you are and I certainly am also, very sympathetic to them.\par \par \uc2 But as they mourn the seven lives lost and the dozens injured in that city`s tragic mass shooting earlier this week, I have to ask, as I promised at the top of the show, if your black constituents have criticized the difference in outcomes between the shooter in Highland Park, who was taken into custody without injury at the manhunt following his rampage, and then of course, there`s what happened to Jayland Walker fired on nearly 100 times, wounded more than 60 times while they claim he was fleeing on foot unarmed at the time.\par \par \uc2 I mean, do your voters see a difference here, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: I think you would have to be blind, Reverend Al not to see the difference. What happened in Highland Park was egregious: Seven innocent lives were taken away from that community on a day when Americans celebrate the country`s independence, so it is beyond heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 And I don`t think that suspects in those cases should be acknowledged, but I understand the concerns, let me say this. Mr. Walker was shot and killed by eight officers for a moving violation, while the Highland Park shooter was taken into custody after firing more than 70 bullets into a crowd without incident.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And I might add, and pardon me for interrupting you, 60 bullets in Walker, 70 rounds by this mass shooter. There was almost as many bullets fired in to Walker as the mass shooter shot, period, at the parade. I`m not trying to minimize either. What I`m trying to show is how egregious it looks to people like me, then you`d shoot somebody like it was almost a firing squad over a traffic violation.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: That`s right. Lives were lost in both, right? But the shooters who were white were peacefully -- peacefully taken into custody. And these situations implicate some type of bias on the treatment of suspects, Reverend Al, and this goes back to why I called on the DOJ to monitor and review this investigation.\par \par \uc2 Too many police involved shootings, especially when they are unarmed Black women and men are left dead and not getting the attention or provoking the change that we need to see to build the trust between Black communities.\par \par \uc2 And let me say this, here in Ohio, I am licensed to carry and our license are attached to our license plate of the cars that we drive. Because of that, I fear my significant other, my brother, my father driving their car, because I don`t want a routine police stop to turn into a death sentence for my family and that should never be the case. And so yes, there is a lot of work to be done here, Reverend Al and the differences are egregious and very apparent.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congresswoman, I want to get your reaction to President Biden`s Executive Order signed yesterday shoring up with Federal protections for abortion he can from the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 In his speech at the signing, he related the story of a 10-year-old rape victim from your state forced to cross state lines into neighboring Indiana to obtain an abortion, because of the Supreme Court decision that had overturned Roe just a few days before, which also triggered your state`s ban on abortion after six weeks, which this child had only missed by days.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, I wonder if you`ve heard stories like this, from the women and girls in your district, Black women and girls, especially since the reversal of Roe.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, the Supreme Court`s decision to reverse Roe was a deliberate attack on individual family`s fundamental rights to decide about their body and their healthcare, Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 For the first time in our history, girls growing up today will not have the same rights as their mothers or grandmothers and the Court`s disgraceful decision has already taken the greatest toll on poor women, women of color, and people who already face systemic barriers to accessing healthcare.\par \par \uc2 Our State is not the only one, many other States have trigger laws in place and some of the highest mortality -- maternal mortality rates in the country, especially among Black women.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, there was a 10-year-old rape victim as you pointed out, 10-year- old rape victim who had to leave the State, so that child and her family were forced to cross state lines because she was six weeks and three days pregnant.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:10]\par \par \uc2 BROWN: A 10-year-old rape victim that is a child. So I hear that fear of women all around me and I share the same concerns. This was a smack in the face of five decades of precedent to turn back the clock on decades of progress of human rights.\par \par \uc2 But I think it is also important to point this back out, Reverend Al, it was 2016, when we talk about elections have consequences. 2016, when we had one of the most qualified candidates in our nation`s history running against one of the most unqualified candidates, it was Democrats who did not fall in line to fully support that candidate, and because of that, we lost the opportunity to appoint other Supreme Court Justices that are making these decisions that are having implications not only right now, but decades, and quite possibly generations to come.\par \par \uc2 So when we talk about showing up at the polls, it is so critical that we fall in line as Democrats and recognize how critically important our vote is. People don`t try to take things away from you that do not have value and when we dismiss it, or treat it cavalier, and say that, you know, oh, this person is likely to win, so I`m not going to show up and vote.\par \par \uc2 We are feeling the consequences of 2016 here today, because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appointed three far-right-wing radical Republicans that are turning back the hands of time, and it is an unprecedented measure that they are setting that is going to have long, long lasting implications, Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, I`m out of time, but I must ask you this. You were at President Biden`s rally this week in Cleveland, where he touted his economic policies to union workers two days before the positive June Jobs Report showed unemployment holding at less than four percent, about 372,000 jobs added last month.\par \par \uc2 Still record inflation has eaten into the President`s poll numbers, inflation that is disproportionately hurting Black voters less than four months out of the way from the midterm elections in your State and others.\par \par \uc2 What must Democrats do differently to make the case that despite inflation, their policies are working?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, as you mentioned, this has been the fastest and strongest jobs recovery in American history, and it would not have been possible without the Biden administration and my Democratic colleagues in Congress.\par \par \uc2 We have more Americans working in the private sector today than any day during the previous administration, more people working today than any time in our history, so no country is better positioned than America to bring down inflation without giving up all the economic gains we have made over the last 18 months now.\par \par \uc2 I will address that we hear your concerns in Congress and we are working to pass legislation at that lowest cost for families, through prescription drug prices, utilities, while reducing the Federal budget deficit and passing the bipartisan Infrastructure and Innovation Act -- excuse me.\par \par \uc2 Last month, the house has the Lower Food and Fuel Costs Act, a package of bills aimed at lowering food and gas prices. These Acts reduce our dependency on foreign and foreign fertilizers and driving up the cost for farmers and strengthens competition in meat and poultry and expands access to cheaper, cleaner, unleaded ethanol blends that will reduce gas by 40 cents per gallon, Reverend Al. So we are doing a lot.\par \par \uc2 What we`re waiting for is the Senate to act. We`re waiting for the Senate to act. So please know, the House of Representatives is taking care of business. And it`s up to our Republican colleagues in the Senate to help us finish the work that we have already started.\par \par \uc2 So we know inflation is on the minds of Americans, but we are doing everything we can to help put money back in their pocket, Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And it`s up to voters to deal with those that are doing the right thing and those that are not.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Congresswoman Shontel Brown for being with us again.\par \par \uc2 Now, let`s go to get an update on the war in Ukraine. Joining me from Kyiv is NBC`s Ali Arouzi. Ali, what`s the latest there?\par \par \uc2 ALI AROUZI, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Reverend.\par \par \uc2 Well, it continues to be a slow, but steady grind for the Russians in the Donetsk area, but their stated aim was to take the whole Donbas. They took the Luhansk region recently. That bought them one close step closer to taking the entire region. And now, they are focusing on three main cities in Donetsk. In Slovyansk, Bakhmut, and Kramatorsk and the shelling there has been intense over the past days and weeks as they try and hit civilian posts, which is a Russian military doctrine of, you know, annihilating the civilian infrastructure to gain battlefield momentum.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:00]\par \par \uc2 AROUZI: But the Ukrainians have also been hitting back.\par \par \uc2 Over the last week, they`ve launched about 19 artillery and missile strikes on Russian weapons depot and Russian barracks. The Ukrainians say that they`ve killed dozens of Russian troops over this last period, but it`s very tough for the Ukrainian soldiers as well, Reverend.\par \par \uc2 I had the opportunity to speak to one soldier who was fighting in the Luhansk. Now, he`s fighting in the Donetsk and he paints a difficult picture of what`s going on, on the ground there. Let`s take a listen to what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The situation was very, very complicated. We were forced to withdraw from Lysychansk due to very much force perspective to be encircled. We pulled all of our forces as much as it was even possible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AROUZI: And Reverend, you know, he was really the personification of the Ukrainian fighting spirit. He vowed to fight for every inch of Ukrainian land, even if it costs them his life. But he reiterated what we`ve been hearing from Zelenskyy all the way down to the Commanders on the ground that they need more and more Western weapons and without those weapons, they`ll be fighting a losing battle -- Reverend.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Ali Arouzi, thank you for your reporting from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Just ahead in this week`s "Got-cha," the GOP`s hypocrisy when it comes to law and order.\par \par \uc2 And another former Trump White House insider testifies in front of the January 6 Committee, but what impact will the hearings have on the midterms? 