 had to cross state line, because she was six weeks and three days pregnant from a rape. Right? And now, like who has those funds?\par \par \uc2 We need to be able to access those funds and we need to help women because every single day every single hour counts.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Sarah, "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reported this week that former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 James Comey\uc2  and his Deputy, \uc2 Andrew McCabe\uc2  were the subjects of an intensive and extremely rare tax audit during the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 The probability of both these high-profile figures being selected randomly is roughly one out of 30,000 according to "The Times."\par \par \uc2 Of course, we know both Comey and McCabe were frequently targeted by President Trump during the Mueller investigation and the Commissioner of the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , at the time was appointed by Trump and remains in that position to this day. What do you make of this story?\par \par \uc2 LONGWELL: Well, this would be the kind of thing that causes people to lose faith in our institutions.\par \par \uc2 Look, it`s very, very fishy. You know, we cannot say for sure that they were being targeted, but look, what do we know about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? We know that he was absolutely not above using the Federal government to target his political enemies and you know, when the institutions of governments are targeted individuals -- targeted at individuals who are you know critical of the President, that is the kind of affront to the rule of law that we saw throughout the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:12]\par \par \uc2 And so while you know, this story came out, it`s not very, very clear whether anybody is willing to say this is a definite case of them being targeted, but it is very unlikely that two people would get audited in the same way -- that`s something that happens very seldom -- who were both were very high-profile critics of the Trump and his administration.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Danielle, \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star, Brittney Griner this week pleaded guilty to drug smuggling charges in Russia. She now faces up to 10 years in a Russian prison.\par \par \uc2 Now her supporters, including me as head of National Action Network, say the charges and the trial were politically motivated as tension heightened between Moscow and Washington. And yesterday, in my capacity as President of NAN, I along with Brittney`s wife, Cherelle Griner held a news conference demanding for her release.\par \par \uc2 I`m also pushing for the White House to organize a trip for me to visit Brittney, hopefully that won`t be necessary, but if she`s not immediately released, I`m still pushing for that.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  named Griner as an honorary All-Star. The All-Star game will tip off tomorrow afternoon and include a tribute to her. It marks the eighth All-Star selection for the 31-year-old.\par \par \uc2 Now, we can hope that after Brittney`s previous week, the Russian Court will handle a case more leniently, but do you think there is more the \uc2 US government\uc2  should be doing to bring her home?\par \par \uc2 MOODIE: I mean, I think that we are in precarious times. We`re at war, right? Like the world is at war. And Putin is using everything that he can as a political tool. And so you know, we have no idea to be honest, what lengths the White House and the Biden administration is going to, but we know that they are working on this.\par \par \uc2 But we also know that what leniency will Russians do?\par \par \uc2 They are a country that lies. They are a country that disappears people. They are a country that targets Americans and uses them as political pawns. And so, you know, I hope that there are negotiations that are happening because I think that the way that she was picked up, I think that all that is happening is politically motivated because of what America has done to push back against Putin being in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Sarah, Arizona`s Republican Governor \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2  signed a bill into law on Wednesday, which criminalizes anyone who records police activity at close range after officers have issued a verbal warning.\par \par \uc2 This, as an incident first caught on camera led to a conviction of former Minneapolis Police Officer, Derek Chauvin, who was sentenced this week in Federal Court to more than 20 years in prison for violating George Floyd`s Civil Rights.\par \par \uc2 Do you see this type of legislation being adopted in other states? And what could be the impact?\par \par \uc2 LONGWELL: I don`t know if other states are going to pick this up. But it is a -- it would be a terrible precedent if they did.\par \par \uc2 It has become one of the main mechanisms for accountability when police officers are behaving in ways that they shouldn`t be. And, you know, I think that you know, if that young woman hadn`t been standing there, when the knee went down on George Floyd`s neck, we wouldn`t have known what happened.\par \par \uc2 And so it`s extremely disheartening that \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2  would put forward legislation like this because, look, this isn`t about defunding the police. This isn`t about being critical of the police. This is just about being able to have all the information and for things to be transparent and for there to be accountability.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And one would have to ask, what is the motive of even having this kind of legislation?\par \par \uc2 Sarah Longwell and Danielle Moodie, thank you both for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up: The rising rates of hate crimes in America. What`s behind the increase and how to stop the rise?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:43:07]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Hate crimes are on the rise. That`s the finding of a new study set to be released this upcoming Tuesday. Researchers at California State University found hate crimes in major American cities jumped over 30 percent in 2021 and increased over 20 percent in one dozen states.\par \par \uc2 Overall, the research found that hate crime cases have gone up every year since 2019. Joining me now is Brian Levin, Director of California State University Center for the Study of Hate and Extremism. Mr. Levin, talk to me more about the rise of hate crimes and what`s been driving it and anything else you can share about this coming report.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN LEVIN, DIRECTOR OF CALIFORNIA STATE UNIVERSITY CENTER FOR THE STUDY OF HATE AND EXTREMISM: Sure, and thank you so much for having us to discuss this very important data and to my friends around the world of the Muslim faith, Eid Mubarak, dear friends.\par \par \uc2 One of the things I think is so interesting, we saw this this real curve up a little bit in 2019 and then 13 percent according to \uc2 FBI\uc2  figures for 2020. Then when we took, as you said so articulately, two data sets, one relating to major American cities, the other relating to many states, and they include California, Texas, New York, Indiana, et cetera, and it was so interesting.\par \par \uc2 Out of those 12, nearly all of them were up, and many of them were hitting records, Texas hit a record for instance; Indiana, Nevada, et cetera. And here is what happened.\par \par \uc2 In 2020 what we had was the worst month in June 2020 for anti-Black hate crime since records began in 1991 nationally.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:02]\par \par \uc2 LEVIN: And we saw this -- bear with me -- this corresponding -- this correlation between these social justice protests even on a day by day tick and anti-Black hate crimes. And yes, anti-White hate crimes went up. But what I thought was so interesting about 2020. It was the fertile ground started first with anti-Asian, which we can discuss a little bit.\par \par \uc2 But then with the social justice protests, we saw the N-word take off online, for instance. We also saw an increase in hate crimes correlated to the social justice protests and a rise in militia-type organizing during the same time.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: So of this rising at the same time of 2020, George Floyd and many of the race-related cases we saw some of which, most of which I was involved in.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you something. This Tuesday in Portland, Oregon, police charged a man with two counts of bias crime, after they say he assaulted a five year old girl and a father of Japanese descent after making comments about their race.\par \par \uc2 Now, research conducted by your institution shows that hate crimes targeting the Asian-American community has reached alarming levels increasing by 339 percent in the last year, compared to the year before.\par \par \uc2 As you analyze these findings, can you tell us why there has been such a dramatic spike in hate crimes targeting the Asian-American community? I`ve gotten to some rallies here in New York and other places. There was a big national march last week. What`s going on with this anti-Asian hate that is really spiking?\par \par \uc2 LEVIN: Yes, and thank you so much. And it started earlier in 2020 for instance, when we saw at that point, the worst month for anti-Asian hate crime -- March and April, corresponded to increasing COVID hospitalizations, but we also saw invective, much of which was transmitted by high folks, President Trump on March 16th, a week before by Secretary of State Pompeo and Congressperson Gosar with stigmatizing language about Asians, and it got interwound in this flotsam swamp, this tributary that would go to conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, he called it the Chinese flu?\par \par \uc2 LEVIN: Yes, absolutely. And we saw this becoming part of discourse in everything from gaming to various encrypted social media platforms. So this stereotyping and conspiracy, got people who are already in fear and online -- we know this from the research, they have this generalized stress, and then it was directed and packaged towards Asians.\par \par \uc2 Later on, it was African-Americans. In 2020, a 45 percent increase. And then we saw in 2021, in the cities, we looked at a 16 percent increase.\par \par \uc2 One last thing really interesting, because this is important. The political polarization and the broadcast media of some other channels, along with social media has this cascaded strata of where the stereotyping and conspiracy theory is repeated and repeated. It started with Asians and it was correlated to the catalyst.\par \par \uc2 But what we saw and this is what is really scary, anti-Asian hatred by the way, with our final result was 224 percent increase across 21 cities, but in those 21 cities, there were more hate crime reported just there than by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  nationally for any year since they`ve been doing it.\par \par \uc2 We have a record year coming up, 2021 for anti-Asian, it`s off those highs. But here`s the thing, the escalation of both anti-Black hate crime, which was of historic proportions, and anti-Asian hate crimes, it was more sticky and it was more elongated. So the hate online stuck around longer, and then we had those downstream effects, which did not come down right away, but were up for months and months and months.\par \par \uc2 One last quick point. Here`s how long it was. After 9/11, hate crimes started declining after one month generally, we saw a multi-month progression where each month was worse than any of the months in the prior decade.\par \par \uc2 It was sticky, higher, and more elongated and more directed.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And one of the things that caught my attention about this report is we`re talking about crimes. We`re not just talking about attitude. We`re talking about actual crimes affecting people that are the victims of it.\par \par \uc2 Briefly talk to us about the connection between hate crimes and terror groups, foreign and domestic. I mean, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and British Intelligence Agencies are warning many would be killers with racist and neo-Nazi motivations are egging each other online.\par \par \uc2 What role as hate and online radicalization is playing in the recent rash of mass shootings? Briefly please, we`re out of time.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:06]\par \par \uc2 LEVIN: Yes, what has happened is replacement doctrine that Whites are being replaced has been used transnationally with different groups, Muslims in Europe, African-Americans here in the United States and others, Latinos. And what has happened is in this elastic pool, many sharks are swimming. Some will just have a more nihilistic, less generalized and directed anger, and others will construct an idiosyncratic set of hatred from above the fray.\par \par \uc2 But what is happening is this access to weaponry, many of them are now being egged on, online, it started 30 years ago worldwide. Now, it`s made possible by these transnational groups, including one that targeted us.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, Brian Levin, will be looking for the full report to drop Tuesday. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the WNBA`s tribute to jailed star player, Brittney Griner and my conversation with her wife, Cherelle as All-Star Weekend gets underway.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: When Brittney was arrested and held in Russia several months ago, nearly five months ago, I spoke about it on this show where I called the State Department and was very concerned.\par \par \uc2 But then there was a lot of silence and many of us were hoping -- I was hoping that silence said there was some negotiations going on.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:08]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: But then when Cherelle Griner, the wife of Brittney Griner came on my radio show and talked about they`d heard nothing, she couldn`t even get the phone call she was promised with Brittney and they were concerned. That is why I went in as President of the National Action Network, raising what we could in our voice asking to be 