it from coal to fight emissions now says it`s needed. The Economy Minister said in a statement that is painful but it is a sheer necessity in this situation to reduce gas consumption.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today, Germany`s Parliament formally voted to reactivate shuttered coal plants. Coal, one of the worst pollutants on Earth.\par \par \uc2 I mean, yes, nuclear energy is not the ideal fuel of the future. Sure. But the decision to start burning more coal than you were before is objectively a nightmare from a climate scenario.\par \par \uc2 At the very least, Germany and other European countries profess to care about carbon emissions.\par \par \uc2 Here at home, the right-wing is now fully masked off about attempting to use government policy to take a crowbar to kneecap the little clean energy infrastructure we do have.\par \par \uc2 Get this, in North Carolina, Republicans recently introduced a bill to supply $50,000 in government money to destroy free public electric vehicle charging stations. I`m not making this up.\par \par \uc2 The bill says public charging stations cannot exist unless the state also includes free gas for combustion engines, which obviously is not going to happen, because there`s a clear difference between the infrastructure required for electric cars and gasoline powered cars.\par \par \uc2 Putting in a gas pump is an enormous and costly piece of infrastructure. Installing an electric charging station is not, that`s one of the big advantages.\par \par \uc2 Oh, but it doesn`t stop there. The proposed legislation would also require private businesses with electrical vehicle charging stations like restaurants to include line items on the bill telling customers how much their money is going to the cost of operating the charger, which would be a preposterous logistical and accounting nightmare.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans love insane burdensome regulations when they`re designed to hurt people and ideas they do not like.\par \par \uc2 And hearing these two stories together are pretty depressing. I get that. But here`s what I would say in some ways, the glass is half full in this respect.\par \par \uc2 We know the easiest thing we could do. The easiest way to start fighting climate change is to not actively institute policies that would make things worse.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:53:24]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Some of the states don`t allow for exceptions for rape or incest. This isn`t some imagined horror. It`s already happening.\par \par \uc2 Just last week it was reported that a 10-year-old girl was a rape victim. 10 years old. Just imagine being a little girl, 10 years old.\par \par \uc2 Does anyone believe that this Ohio`s majority view that that should not be able to be dealt with? Or in any other state in the nation?\par \par \uc2 A 10-year-old girl should be forced to give birth to a rapist`s child. I can tell you what I know. I can`t think of anything as much more extreme.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Two weeks after the Supreme Court took away the constitutional right to an abortion, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  signed an executive order to protect access to reproductive care while acknowledging that only Congress can restore the rights of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 The executive order ensures emergency medical care for people experiencing pregnancy loss and seeks to protect access to contraception and crucially medication for abortion for people living in those states that want to ban abortion or already have.\par \par \uc2 Biden can do that because the federal government has supremacy under the Constitution. Federal law takes precedence over state law. And the federal government in our system is the final arbiter of declaring which drugs are safe and effective for Americans to use.\par \par \uc2 The fact that drugs used to induce medical abortion are