any of us have been calling for, is going to be an important time period for us to watch and advocate for.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, what was the -- there was reporting today that they considered a public health emergency and then, -- and dropped it. I don`t even -- what would that do if that were to be declared?\par \par \uc2 TIMMARAJU: So, a public health emergency, we`ve seen it with COVID. But there`s a couple of different things that could happen.\par \par \uc2 So one, I`ve definitely seen the reporting that it was dismissed. However, we heard in the White House briefing that it`s not off the table, and we are continuing to push for that to be an option.\par \par \uc2 So, when the Secretary of \uc2 HHS\uc2  does his 30-day review and puts out his report, they can in fact decide they want to have a public health emergency declared. And that could in the course of that declaration, and that review, they could, for example, say unfettered access to medication abortion is critical to public health.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s an example of what they could do. And then it would allow, as you said, the setup for the fight about preemption, etcetera. But it would be an important step to clarify what the \uc2 FDA\uc2  role is in this work.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, that`s the big question to me right now, because I don`t actually understand what the terrain is, right?\par \par \uc2 So, we don`t have individual -- you know, the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , we have a federal system that we declare drug -- we say drugs are safe and effective. And in the day that the DOD`s decision overturning roe came out, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  issued a statement basically saying, like, you know, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is the authority here on medical abortion, but I don`t know what the status is if you`re in Louisiana, like, can you get a Telehealth, you know, appointment and get the drugs mailed to you? In Mississippi or North or South Dakota, like or Ohio, what is the status? And who will resolve that ultimately?\par \par \uc2 TIMMARAJU: Well, I think you just nailed it. There is no clear status. So, states are banning abortion, states are banning access, there is an assumption that also covers medication abortion.\par \par \uc2 And until there`s clarification from the federal government, and in this case, the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , we will not -- this will be an unsettled question that will create a lot of chaos and access.\par \par \uc2 So, right now, you know, clinics and Telehealth, telemedicine facilities are advising patients to go to the border or go right across the border and get your telemedicine legally in another state.\par \par \uc2 Organizations are doing creative ways around state bans and state laws. But a lot of this is not the enforcement mechanism for how states are going to prevent folks from having access to things like telemedicine and medication, abortion by mail.\par \par \uc2 It`s really murky. It`s really unclear. And this is why what the president did today asking \uc2 HHS\uc2  to dig into this and what advocates are asking for with a public health emergency could help clear a lot of this stuff.\par \par \uc2 As you said, it will likely end up in the courts.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, all of this is going to be intensely litigated. Obviously, we are -- we`re in for that.\par \par \uc2 What do you think about the president`s message today about basically electing two more Democratic senators, and we will codify Roe. That was basically the pitch.\par \par \uc2 TIMMARAJU: You know, we`re in agreement that voting in this election is going to be critical. I would like to see this House of this Senate try to give it another shot at the Women`s Health Protection Act.\par \par \uc2 You know, the House passed it last year, the Senate came up 49 votes, one vote short. But since the DOD`s decision, we`ve heard Senators Collins, Murkowski and even Joe Manchin indicate an interest in codifying Roe.\par \par \uc2 [21:00:03]\par \par \uc2 TIMMARAJU: The challenge what I have is none of them -- none of them are talking about whether they would eliminate the filibuster. So, I think we have to fight and push hard. If they won`t, then yes, we have to let two more senators.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Mini Timmaraju, thank you very much for your time tonight.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN on this Friday night. "MSNBC PRIME" starts right now with Ali Velshi. Good evening, Ali.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220709ei7800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for July 8, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Carol Lee, Matthew Dowd, Kaori Enjoji, Charles  Coleman, Ebony Hilton, Jonathan Capehart, Cornell Belcher \par \uc2 7,758 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC HOST: Good afternoon. I`m Chris Jansing. Live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City. And today, we`re focused on the limits of presidential power and the difficulties facing President Biden as he tries to find his footing on critical issues, including two that are front and center this hour, the economy and abortion rights. Just about 40 minutes ago, the president signed a new executive order meant to protect women`s access to contraception and abortion.\par \par \uc2 But on his own, there`s little he can do to counteract the Supreme Court`s reversal of Roe. That said, the president`s biggest challenge and perhaps his administration`s biggest source of frustration remains the economy. Take today`s June jobs report showing another 372,000 jobs added. A sign that that part of the economy remains strong.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Today`s economic news confirms the fact that my economic plan is moving this country in a better direction. I`m not suggesting there`s a lot more work to do. But I am suggesting we`re making significant progress.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In fact, the Biden presidency has seen extraordinary job growth. Averaging more than a half million jobs added every month. The president said today that all the jobs lost in the pandemic have now been recovered. And then some, but that`s not what people are feeling. They`re feeling this spike in gas prices and the sky high inflation numbers that for many Americans have made things like summer vacations now simply unaffordable.\par \par \uc2 Which is why the latest A.P. poll found that the number of people who think inflation and gas prices should be the government`s top priority has roughly tripled in the last six months. One very interesting note, those two issues rank one and two among independent voters. And those numbers aren`t just hurting the president. Democrats in general know the clock is ticking for members on the ballot in November.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Carol Lee covering the White House. Farnoosh Torabi is editor at large at CNET money. Peter Baker is Chief White House correspondent for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . And Matthew Dowd is founder of Country Over Party and an MSNBC political contributor. You know, Peter, is the real issue for Democrats that the economy and abortion are not things that the president necessarily has significant control over, at least not now.\par \par \uc2 So, short of some major messaging breakthrough, which frankly, has been a problem for this White House, November is going to be incredibly challenging.\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, THE NEW YORK TIMES CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Whether that was going to be incredibly challenging. History tells us that presidents in their first midterms tend to lose seats in the House especially, they`re feeling a little bit better about the senate because of individual matches. But you`re right. Obviously, this is not the economy that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wanted to go into this midterm with.\par \par \uc2 The numbers today do suggest that regrowth continues to be robust. That`s obviously good news for the administration, good news for the country. But it also presents a challenge the Fed was trying to rein in some of the growth to some extent, and therefore, began to decrease inflation. So, that`s kind of this awkward push and pull right now where in order to beat inflation, you may need it to tamp down in the economy though why it`s growing pretty strongly.\par \par \uc2 But you`re right, the president is frustrated that he has limited amounts of options in terms of that, in terms of abortion. But the problem is, even though he may be substantively correct that within his, you know, toolkit, there`s very little he can do. It`s not very satisfying members of his own party or even members of the independents who supported in the past. So, you see this frustration in the poll numbers, not because they dislike or disapprove of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  necessarily, but they just don`t think he`s doing enough of what he promised to do.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. Frustration in the poll numbers, Farnoosh, but also frustration on the part of the White House who thinks they don`t get enough credit for things like what we saw today, which are these job numbers, which are undeniably good. But of course, the worry now isn`t just inflation, but the possibility of a recession. Very, sort of non-economic question. Can you have a recession when job growth is this strong?\par \par \uc2 FARNOOSH TORABI, CNET MONEY, EDITOR-AT-LARGE: That is the million dollar question, Chris. I mean, I think that what this jobs report today informs the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is that they have the capacity and now, the room to really raise interest rates again, 75 basis points at their next meeting is the expectation. And until these jobs numbers really start to show significant decline, meaningful decline, think the Fed is going to continue its rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 And at that point, sort of a chart, like at what point you`re going to reach where interest rates do get to the point where it does put pressure on commerce, it does put pressure on business owners, you do start to see more layoffs. And so we are now technically perhaps in a recession with the unemployment numbers that would align with that historically.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And again, another non-economist question, how does today`s jobs report that impact the way the Fed tries to deal with inflation or does it?\par \par \uc2 [13:05:04]\par \par \uc2 TORABI: It absolutely does. I mean, I think the Fed is looking very closely at this jobs report as they do other economic in indicators, but the jobs employment picture, while it continues to be the one rosy part of the economy, it`s good news, bad news. It`s good for employees and employees and, you know, that they can go find jobs. And it`s also good news, I guess, for the Fed to feel confident that it can continue to increase interest rates.\par \par \uc2 I think what the Fed is trying to essentially accomplish is this sort of soft landing where they feel like they do have that wiggle room to raise interest rates enough where it doesn`t crash the economy, and they`ve already done this aggressively at the last meeting, so far things seem to be -- inflation is still high. So, they`re going to continue to address that by raising interest rates. The fact that they`re looking at this jobs report and saying, hey, you know, we still have a lot of hiring going on.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment is essentially flat month over month unemployment rate. So we can go in and do this surgery that we need to do and hopefully come out without it really being a sort of a hurricane or wreck for the economy. They`re hoping to do this, I think and this -- today`s report gives them the confidence to proceed with their -- with their rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And Carol, beyond the economic challenges right now, the president is finding out there`s also limited power that he has when it comes to big issues like abortion and guns. So, let`s talk about what he can and is doing on those issues. And is the White House expectation that those things that he`s doing, did just today can resonate with voters?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEE, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, that`s the hope, Chris. The Democrats would like -- including the White House would like the election to focus on, particularly for Democrats. Issues like gun violence and abortion. And that`s why we saw the president signed these executive actions today under pressure from Democrats who are saying that he`s not doing enough, he`s not being vocal enough about this issue that they`re very passionate about.\par \par \uc2 So we heard from the president that he`s taking these steps because it`s what he feels that he can do. Although conceding that it`s pretty limited. And his main message, though, Chris was vote. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The challenge from the court to the American women and men. This is a nation. The challenge is go out and vote. Well, for God`s sake, there`s an election on November. Vote, vote, vote, vote. Consider the challenge accepted court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEE: So you can see the president is setting this up as a political fight, Chris. And so he`s saying -- he went on to say that Republicans are in the extreme that he hopes that women in particular will turn out in November in record numbers to support Democrats. He said that all -- he set the expectation, saying that all that is needed for Democrats to codify Roe are two additional senators in the Senate who support abortion rights and a Democratic majority in the House that supports abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 So, setting this expectation that just with those votes, that they would be able to codify Roe and the president hoping that this is something that will energize his own party to support more Democrats in the fall.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But Matthew, this is exactly what we`ve talked about. You and I, some other folks on the Democratic side who have said they need to figure out better messaging. Give people something specific, not just say vote, but say two more seats on the Senate, right? Anyway, let me play a little more of what the president had to say today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This decision affects everyone unrelated to choice, beyond choice. We cannot allow an out-of-control Supreme Court, working in conjunction with extremist elements of the Republican Party to take away freedoms and our personal autonomy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: An out-of-control Supreme Court. What do you make is that? Is that enough to convince people he`s meeting the moment, Matthew?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW DOWD, NBC NEWS POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR AND ANALYST: Well, if I were him, I would do what he did today and do it every day in the next 120 days. I mean, I think they have to let go of this idea of improving their poll numbers or getting people to believe that they`ve done the right thing on this plethora of other issues, and basically concentrate on democracy and freedom which is guns, which is choice, which is the corruption that has happened to our own sort of institutions.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what he ought to spend his time on in the course of this. I will remind many people that in 2004 when I was chief strategist for \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , the number one issue was the economy. We talked about national security 95 percent of the time and won the election. And so, I think Democrats have to let go of the idea. They have to give people permission to dislike \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for whatever reason and then basically say the threat to the Republic is so strong if these Republicans win.\par \par \uc2 That you have to -- even though you dislike \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , you have to vote for the Democrats. And that`s what the president`s message is ought to be. You got to say it`s fine not to like me but you can`t turn the levers of power over to the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:06]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. I think without that -- it`s fine not to like me, Peter, that is kind of what we heard from the president today. He`s tried to take the focus away from a referendum on his administration and instead warned about the risk of a Supreme Court appointed by Republicans, of Republicans being in charge. But here`s the problem, even on a generic ballot, and there was recent polling on this from 538.\par \par \uc2 It shows Republicans are generally more popular than Democrats. But everything that`s happened at lightning speed over the course of the last couple of weeks, is there still time for a message to change that perception?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Well, you know, we`re obviously a little late in the season. People`s opinions are fairly hardened by now. But I think Matt is right on what the White House will tell you they want to do. That they understand that not going to improve Biden`s numbers. They understand they`re not going to make him popular between now and November. And that the real trick for them, real challenge for them is to explain to the public that the other side is more extreme and unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 And they`re going to wrap all these things together. Guns, voting rights, abortion, Supreme Court, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you know, it`s nothing that this White House would like to see more than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to go ahead and prematurely or preemptively announce he`s going to run for reelection now before the midterm, which would be a violation of the normal traditions.\par \par \uc2 Because it would set the stage for them to make that a rematch and effective two years ago, which is a rematch, they think that they can do well on. And we certainly in some senate races, if not necessarily at least, minimize the losses in the House. But, you know, it`s a -- it`s a message that hasn`t gotten out yet. We`ll see if they`re able to translate that in the weeks and months ahead.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And is that a message, Matthew, that can go up and down the ballot because a lot of people for the entire time they may have voted, maybe decades haven`t necessarily thought a lot about who they`re going to elect for their state legislature, who they`re going to elect for secretary of state probably could name their secretary of state. We`re seeing a change in that, there`s been a lot of reporting on that. Does that message have to be consistent top to bottom?\par \par \uc2 DOWD: It absolutely has to be consistent top to bottom. One of the most important criteria of campaigns winning is first you have to have the right message and second, you have to been disciplined about it up and down the ballot to do this. I would counsel everybody on generic ballot. A couple of things to realize on generic ballot. First, Republicans are not positioned like they were in 2010 and 2014.\par \par \uc2 At this moment of time, right now, it`s barely -- it`s basically barely even right now. So, Republicans aren`t positioned like they were the last time. And second, generic ballot is a lagging indicator of where the country is going and what issues affect. The leading indicators are those senate races. And right now those senate races are telling us Republicans and the Republican candidates they have are in trouble.\par \par \uc2 I think this race -- this -- the election is going to be exceedingly close. I think the House is going to be close. I think the senate is going to be close. So there`s still plenty of time. 120 days to frame this and be disciplined in a way that Democrats can win, but it`s up to them.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Matthew Dowd, Peter, Farnoosh, Carol, such an interesting conversation. Thank you all.\par \par \uc2 Coming up. The man that January 6 Committee has made very clear they want in the witness chair is there now in a closed door meeting. So how will they use former White House Counsel Pat Cipollone`s testimony?\par \par \uc2 Plus, the longest serving prime minister in Japanese history assassinated. How the suspect reportedly got a gun in a nation with just 10 shootings in the entire last year.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a new COVID variant taking over U.S. cases. It`s more contagious. Our current vaccine is less effective against it. The new tool will have to fight the virus come fall.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:17:55]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Stunned, outraged and deeply saddened. Those words from President Biden in response to the assassination of Japan`s longest serving Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 . The former P.M. was shot and killed at a campaign event near Kyoto. The chaotic minutes caught on video.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING (voice over): He will cut the 67-year-old remained after his retirement very popular and was a powerful political force. He actually was stumping for a Member of Parliament when he was killed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s left his country in shock and mourning to say that gun violence is extremely rare in Japan would be a major understatement.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, CNBC`s former Tokyo bureau chief Kaori Enjoji. Also with me, \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  associate editor and host of MSNBC`s "THE SUNDAY SHOW" \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2 . Kaori, what are the details are we learning about exactly what happened and the person who`s been apprehended?\par \par \uc2 KAORI ENJOJI, CNBC FORMER TOKYO BUREAU CHIEF: Well, we`re no -- we know now that the suspect is a 41-year-old male who is in police custody. And we know that the firearm used to honor prime and former Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  was homemade. Made of metal and wood. And during a subsequent search in his home in the nearby town of Nada in western Japan, they found other explosives as well.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know what the motive was. We all -- we have heard from the police that he had held a grudge against a particular organization which he believed had some kind of affiliation with \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 . But as you pointed out, the nation is in disbelief that their prime -- former prime minister could be shot in broad daylight at what was a routine speech rally ahead of elections on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Airlifted and despite hours of trying to revive him, through blood transfusions according to his doctors, he passed away at about 5:00 p.m. local time.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:03]\par \par \uc2 And since this kind of violence, let alone gun violence is extremely rare in Japan, I fear that Japan that will be waking up tomorrow will really no longer be the same.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What sort of reaction have you been hearing there? And what about world leaders? I know that the sympathy has been pouring in from literally around the world.\par \par \uc2 ENJOJI: That`s right. Because he was Japan`s longest serving prime minister, his reach and influence has gone well beyond Asia`s borders. And we`ve heard from presidents and prime ministers around the world. Let me just point out the comments made by \uc2 Fumio Kishida\uc2 , the current prime minister who calls Mr. Abe his mentor. According to him, he said, this is a barbaric act, an unspeakable act.\par \par \uc2 And I think he echoes the sentiment that`s ringing across Japan today. He may have retired as prime minister about two years ago, but he was an influential and towering figure to this day. He`s from a political dynasty, he grew up on the knee of his grandfather, who was a prime minister, his father was a foreign minister as well. And so, his influence across the region has been extremely dark -- widespread.\par \par \uc2 And -- I mean, when you talk about his legacy, Mr. Abe himself would have liked his legacy to be a person who changed the Japanese constitution which is pacifist and give a military -- the military in Japan a bigger role, but that was not to beat. But during his nearly eight years as prime minister, I think he will also be remembered for -- be remembered for trying to revive Japan`s economy with a so-called Abenomics strategy with mixed results.\par \par \uc2 And also really to try and incorporate India, Australia and other Asian countries in the fold in the face of a rise in -- what he called a rising China. So, he was a divisive figure, for some, he was known as being on the right end of the spectrum of the party that swings very boldly from left to right. But still very much a sense of sadness and shock and awe really, that this could happen in a -- in a place like Japan.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Kaori, thank you so much. We appreciate you staying up for us. So, you know, Jonathan, the rarity, absolute rarity of access to and violence from guns in Japan is such a sharp contrast, of course, to what we see in the U.S. And I noticed coincidentally, a new analysis by your newspaper today that shows a staggering 43 million guns were purchased in the U.S. just in the past two years alone while the rate of gun deaths in those years hit the highest level since 1995.\par \par \uc2 More than 45,000 fatalities each year, which begs the question how you fix a problem that is clearly, so deeply ingrained in our culture.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 , THE WASHINGTON POST ASSOCIATE EDITOR: Right. It`s deeply ingrained in our -- in our culture. But also, I don`t -- I don`t think there`s an accident, Chris, between the increase in gun sales and the increase in gun fatalities. It is the access to guns that make them so dangerous in our country. In the fact that we have more guns or we have a gun for every American.\par \par \uc2 That`s more than three -- more than 300 million guns that are out there obviously.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Almost 400 million I think right now.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Right. Almost 400 million. Right. And so, you`ve got that many guns in a country with sort of Swiss cheese, like from a federal system, the 50 states, all having different rules and regulations. No Federal Assault Weapons Ban anymore. And what you end up happen -- having happen is a proliferation of guns, a rise in gun fatalities. And let`s also keep something else in mind, Chris. The popular imagination is fixated on -- understandably so mass shootings.\par \par \uc2 Buffalo, Uvalde, Highland Park. But the number one fatality with guns is suicide. And so, we also see increases in the rate of suicide when there is -- when there is -- generally speaking, open access to guns.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: According to -- you talk about the mishmash, I guess you could call it of regulations for guns, federal state by state. There was a new \uc2 FBI\uc2  report, it came out last month that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  could not complete more than a million background checks in 2020 and 2021. In the three days they`re supposed to take, which meant potentially a million people had the opportunity to buy a gun before their background check could be completed.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, in 11,564 cases, the ATF had to actually go and take guns back that shouldn`t have been sold in the first place. I mean, this new gun law that Congress just passed should help. But as you say, 400 million guns in America, the complexity of it is mind boggling, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Right. And to your point about the background check in the three days.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:04]\par \par \uc2 What`s also mind boggling is if the background check isn`t completed within three days, the gun seller can sell you the gun. And that`s how Dylann Roof was able to buy his gun even though on his background check. He said that he had -- he had some involvement with the police in --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The shooting Charleston, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: When people were having a prayer service.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Right. In 2015 at Mother Emanuel. He was able to buy the gun because the gun background check had not been completed in the -- in the three days. And that`s what`s now being called the Charleston loophole. But, you know, the other thing though is Dylann Roof was actually honest in filling out his background check form. And we have a situation with Highland Park where the father helped his son by the guns, even though we now know of the many interactions between that family and police with the - - with the shooter and an attempted suicide which also involved the police had that background check paid for paperwork been filled out accurately.\par \par \uc2 Honestly, with the guns have been sold, with the gun -- with the -- with the shooter have gotten the -- gotten the weapons that he then use to unleash terror on Highland Park. It said we don`t know because we`re living with it -- we are living with the reality of all those people killed. All those people wounded in a mass shooting that, sadly, is just one of many that happened in this country on a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2 , thank you. And don`t go anywhere because we`re going to talk a little bit more later on in the program. We`ll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 Coming up. A critical witness. Former White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  behind closed doors with the January 6 Committee right now. What are the chances we get to see the videotape of that interview as soon as Tuesday?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:31:05]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We can almost guarantee there are some critically important questions being asked right now behind closed doors. The witness, \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , the former White House counsel to President Trump, testifying and being videotaped before the House January 6th Committee.\par \par \uc2 There you see our cameras catching him arriving this morning.\par \par \uc2 Cipollone previously met with the committee back in April but that was an informal interview with House investigators. The urgency behind his appearance today intensified of course after former White House aide Cassidy Hutchinson testified publicly last week, specifically about a conversation she had with Cipollone on the morning of January 6th.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER AIDE TO WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF \uc2 MARK MEADOWS\uc2 : I saw Mr. Cipollone right before I walked out onto West Exec that morning and Mr. Cipollone said something to the effect of, please make sure we don`t go up to the Capitol, Cassidy, keep in touch with me. We`re going to get charged with every crime imaginable if we make that movement happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Joining me now, Charles Coleman, a former prosecutor and an MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 Great to see you. So if you`re in that room, what`s your question for \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  that he probably could answer and not cite executive or attorney- client privilege?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES COLEMAN, CIVIL RIGHTS ATTORNEY: Well, Chris, I think that when you`re talking about the importance of this witness, one of the things that you want to do is just try to get them to buttress and corroborate, everything that you heard from Miss Hutchinson. Those are things that aren`t necessarily going to run afoul of executive privilege or attorney- client privilege. But what I do expect that is going to happen is that Mr. Cipollone is likely going to more often than none invoke his Fifth Amendment right against self-incrimination.\par \par \uc2 So even the quote from Miss Hutchinson about let`s not go up to the Capitol because we`re going to get charged with every crime imaginable, I don`t expect him to elaborate on a quote like that. I do expect investigators to ask him about that. The committee is going to likely try to hammer that home to try to get more information as to what he meant by that statement but he does have a right against self-incrimination, so in terms of his response, you will likely going to see him plead the Fifth, if we see this videotape at all.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But that statement in particular, how is that criminally incriminating? In other words, if you`re trying to say something or do something to stop something that you think is criminal, is that incriminating? That`s a very bizarre question but I think you follow me.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: I do. The statement itself may not necessarily be incriminating but I think that delving into details around what the statement actually means and what he meant by the statement and why he would have said something by the statement or why he would have said that statement in particular. All of those things begin to speak to the criminal elements that are necessary to prove certain crimes, particularly intent.\par \par \uc2 And I think that Mr. Cipollone is smart enough where he knows that he wants to stay away from that. So those are difficult questions to answer when you`re talking about, well, what did you mean, Mr. Cipollone, when you said, that we`re going to get charged with every crime imaginable. There is only but so much that he can say with response to that question before he begins to actually implicate himself on a criminal basis.\par \par \uc2 And so he does have some leeway where he can actually answer what`s being asked of him but it`s not much and I think he`s aware of that line and he`s going to use his Fifth Amendment against self-incrimination to protect against that boundary.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So let me give you a real world example. Let`s say Cipollone is asked specifically about Cassidy Hutchinson`s testimony that he was pushing for Trump to make a statement to help end the violence, but he was telling that to \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , not the president, let me play that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Pat said something to the effect of, and very clearly, had said this to Mark, something to the effect of, Mark, something needs to be done or people are going to die and the blood is going to be on your effing hands. This is getting out of control. I`m going down there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Any reason he could claim either privilege or he doesn`t want to answer the question because he might incriminate himself?\par \par \uc2 [13:35:05]\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Well, that one is likely, you know, something that`s going to come in. I think that that -- what you`ve chosen in that particular clip, their conversation, that Hutchinson recounted with \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , it`s not only valuable but it`s also important that if Cipollone does recall that and it recalls it accurately, that he actually testified to it, and the reason I say that is, Hutchinson`s testimony, or her account of that conversation, i.e., I observed a conversation between two people where Cipollone said to Meadows, X, Y, Z, that`s never going to hold up, because you`re talking about double hearsay.\par \par \uc2 And so that`s not going to come in if this were to ever be prosecuted. And so that`s why Cipollone testifying to that same conversation as the speaker in that moment is going to be so much more important, and so it`s more valuable. Not only in corroborating what Hutchinson has said and giving her greater credibility as a witness overall but also in terms of long term being able to get that sort of testimonies in.\par \par \uc2 And so he would be able to answer that question. He would be able to speak to that without running afoul of privilege or his Fifth Amendment right against self-incrimination.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I feel now like I could take that one little section of the bar and pass it so I feel good about that. But we just got a little bit of breaking news that`s interesting and let me ask you your reaction to it.\par \par \uc2 This is about Stewart Rhodes, of course you know he`s the founder of a far- right wing militia group the Oath Keepers. His attorney is telling NBC he wants to testify before the committee but only if he can testify in person, not virtually, not from his jail cell, and he wants it broadcast live.\par \par \uc2 What would you make of those terms? Would you take those terms?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Well, I would need a proffer of evidence before I made a decision on that. I think that one of the things that the committee cannot do is get into a game of grand standing with witnesses, particularly civilian witnesses of this nature who may see this as an opportunity to sort of make a name for themselves, and so in order for me to take a deal like that, I would need more information as to what he intends to testify to, what he intends to say on the stand that would be valuable to us, and whether it`s worth that type of risk.\par \par \uc2 Because even though he may make a proffer of evidence, unless it`s severely or explosive or something that`s going to really buttress what we`re already trying to do in terms of implicating criminal activity at the highest levels here, you don`t necessarily know that you want to put a witness on the stand that you haven`t interviewed before. So I`d want a proffer of evidence before that.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Charles Coleman, thank you so much. Have a great weekend. Appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 More contagious and evading vaccines. That`s how experts are describing two COVID subvariants, one of them now the most dominant in the U.S. Up next, how soon could we see updated vaccines for them?\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS, only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:57]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now public health experts are warning the dominant strain of coronavirus is more contagious than our current vaccines appear to be less effective against it. It`s called subvariant BA.5 and it now makes up more than 50 percent of new cases. Those new cases are up in at least 20 states. The subvariant considered by some experts to be the worst version of Omicron yet.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in Dr. Ebony Hilton, associate professor of anesthesiology and critical care medicine at the \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2  and an MSNBC medical contributor.\par \par \uc2 I think there are a lot of folks, it`s hard, Doctor, to keep track of just how many subvariants we`ve had at this point and it does seem like each one becomes more contagious. Is that what we should expect to keep happening?\par \par \uc2 DR. EBONY HILTON, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Right. It should be what we expect at this point because it`s proven to be true in the past, right, and we can learn from our history. What we do know is that in nature, there is a game of survival of the fittest and viruses believe that just as we do as humans, and that it has to evade our immune system in order to create their new generation in their linage, right.\par \par \uc2 And so what we should anticipate is that as a virus passes from one person to the next person to the next person, just like in a telephone game, it will slightly change in message that it sends to your body so that eventually you won`t be able to kill it in the same way. That`s what it`s trying to do. And so when we`re talking about how do we get past this pandemic, we will not get past this pandemic if we don`t stop the spread of the virus in the first place because the virus will, like we`re seeing, always be able to being one step ahead of us.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and \uc2 BioNTech\uc2 , to that point, just announced that they are starting trials on a universal COVID vaccine later this year that would offer broad protection as the virus continues to mutate. In general, how long might something like that take? How long is the testing? Could it potentially replace the vaccines many of us have already gotten?\par \par \uc2 HILTON: Right. I think we can anticipate that it will, we will have another vaccine that will replace what we currently have. But I don`t think it`s necessarily the technology that we have to have an uphill battle against. I think it`s actually crowd participation. I mean at this point, only 67 percent of Americans have the first vaccines that we were rolling out. In fact, only 34 percent have received their first booster.\par \par \uc2 People are not running to get vaccinated, and that is a problem. And it`s not only a problem within the United States of America. We have seen it globally, and at this point, only 20 percent of those persons who live in the poorest of countries have received one dose of the vaccine. It is not available to them.\par \par \uc2 And again, that telephone game does not just apply within the United States of America. We have seen how this pandemic has shown us just how small of a community we truly are when we think about the global community. We are related to each other.\par \par \uc2 So how to stop the spread? We have to get the vacs that we have available currently out into the arms of persons not only within the United States but across the globe.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, especially since we keep hearing about how they`re throwing away literally hundreds of thousands of doses in developed world where, as you say, third world countries are in desperate need of them.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:04]\par \par \uc2 Dr. Ebony Hilton, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, firefighters in California are working to contain a wildfire that broke out in an iconic park of Yosemite National Park. The fire burning near the lower part of the Mariposa Grove which is home to about 500 of those giant sequoia trees. Those are some of the largest living things on earth. If you`ve never been there, it`s astonishing. But right now, that fire is about five to seven acres big, and crews are working to put it out. Until they do, that park is closed to the public.\par \par \uc2 Up next, it took his own party to finally force \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  out as prime minister. So are there lessons for Republicans standing by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS, only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today, there seems to be a lot of new analysis about the tolerance for lying. In the U.K., as "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " headlines so succinctly put it, Boris Johnson`s lies worked for years. Until they didn`t. And when they didn`t, he couldn`t hold on to power.\par \par \uc2 American voters also fed up said no thanks to a second term for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but what`s different here from the Brits is that Johnson`s own party abandoned him.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:08]\par \par \uc2 A stunning contrast to how Trump, even amid growing evidence of his role in the January 6th insurrection hold so many Republicans and the party leadership in his thrall.\par \par \uc2 Back with me to discuss it, \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2 , "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " associate editor and host of the Sunday show here on MSNBC. Also joining us Cornell Belcher, a pollster and political strategist and an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 So, Jonathan, I want to read to you a comparison in the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ." Quote, "In contrast to former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , another politician with an improvisational and often distant relationship to the truth, Mr. Johnson`s approach has rarely been to double down on his lies or delude himself for consistency`s sake into acting as if they were true. Rather he recasts them to fit new information that comes to life, as if the truth were a fungible concept, no more solid than quicksand."\par \par \uc2 Well, I mean, Jonathan, ultimately the quicksand took \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  under. But is there a tipping point especially as we look at the January 6th Committee hearings coming up, is there a tipping point for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that you see?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 , THE WASHINGTON POST ASSOCIATE EDITOR: I mean, look, we`ve seen so many tipping points that, I mean, they`re countless. The difference, though, is there aren`t any Republican leaders who are willing to say this is the tipping point and then saying to other folks come with me and say that we have got to be done with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We`re not getting it from Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , we`re not getting -- certainly not getting it from House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy or any of those elected leaders. And the ones who do speak out, Congressman \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  and especially Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , the vice chair of the January 6th Committee, they`re run out of the party, they`re run out of leadership. So we`ve seen many tipping points, Chris. It`s just Republican leaders don`t seem to care.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So why is it so different here, Cornell? I mean, obviously the systems of government operate differently but it took away the Cabinet resignations and Johnson`s party finally saying we`ve had enough to get him to resign. And look, we did see some of Trump`s Cabinet step down after January 6th but they also didn`t have much time left in office. Why such a big difference?\par \par \uc2 CORNELL BELCHER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think you`ve got to look at two things. One, it is structurally, the primaries, sort of the -- how they pick, you know, candidates in primaries in the U.K. is different, right, and the Lib Dems, Tories and Labour they`re sort of centralized the way that they control who actually gets on -- who actually becomes sort of a party standard bearer.\par \par \uc2 And so structurally I think it is very different in the U.K. than it is in the U.S. where, you know, we have these close primaries that I think we`ve talked about before where you get -- where some of the most polarized, you know, parts of the electorate in fact get to choose who is the standard bearer. So I think from a power of standpoint it`s a little different.\par \par \uc2 But I`m also struck by this, Chris. I`m struck by you have a majority of -- 54 percent majority of voters who voted for the Conservative Party in 2019 also thinks he should resign. So part of it is also that the public there, even the conservative voters there in the U.K. are different from the conservative voters here in this country where you`d never get a majority of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  voters saying that he should be impeached or that he should resign. So structurally as well as attitude they`re different.\par \par \uc2 And one last really quick point, Chris, is it`s the writings on the wall. If you also look at the polling, Labour was opening up a double-digit lead generically over the Tories. The writing was on the wall that their best interests was not with him.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, will there be some things then, Cornell, that will give us guidance about our tolerance for this in November? Let`s say \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , who`s embroiled in a series of scandals any one of which might have taken down a politician in the past, can he still win? I mean, the "Atlantic Journal Constitution" quotes a former GOP executive director saying never has there been a candidate who was more emblematic of how politics has become a team sport than Herschel.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Yes, and that`s part and partial of the problem is no matter how, you know, they disqualify themselves as a candidate you would think for the mainstream, you will still get a majority of the Republicans -- a vast majority of the Republicans are going to back \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and no matter how many affairs or how many children that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  comes out that he has, that race in Georgia is going to be razor thin because Republicans just aren`t going to abandon him. And that`s what we see not only with Walker but lots of other Republican candidates.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In our last 30 seconds, Jonathan, is the only real chance Trump loses his grip if lots of Republicans start losing?\par \par \uc2 [13:55:05]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Because that`s your column, right? "Dang It. It`s Your One Superpower Vote."\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Right. Vote. Exactly. If Democrats come out to vote in the midterm elections especially that Democratic candidates will win and so Democrats have to get out of their own way, even if you have to hold your nose, close your eyes. As long as you`re doing both in a voting booth and clicking for the Democrat, they`ll win and that will send a signal to the Republican Party that they`ve got to walk away from Trump.\par \par \uc2 But the one last thing, Chris, if the Republican Party, even without Trump, Trumpism is still alive. So that`s the biggest danger.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2 , Cornell Belcher, what a great conversation. Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 Be sure to check out Jonathan on the Sunday show every Sunday, 10:00 a.m. Eastern only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern right here on MSNBC. But keep it tuned in because "KATY TUR REPORTS" starts next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Good to be with you. I`m Katy Tur. The Japanese people will wake up in just a few hours to a world changed. A former leader shot and killed in a country where gun violence is virtually nonexistent.\par \par \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 , Japan`s former and longest serving prime minister, was assassinated during a lunchtime campaign rally in the city of Nara near Osaka. He was 67 years old.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220709ei7800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for July 7, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 7,500 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Presidential Medal of Freedom honorees Sister Simone Campbell gets tonight`s "LAST WORD". THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, as the political power of one controversial leader suddenly disappears, efforts to challenge the legitimacy of another kicks into high gear. Then two weeks after the reversal of Roe v. Wade, the ability to obtain an abortion is simply going away in some states. We`ll ask one state lawmaker about what happens now.\par \par \uc2 Plus, with the next mass shooting lately, just days or even hours away, we talked with a big city police chief about how the bitter politics over the Second Amendment is playing out among law enforcement. As THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. The investigation into how we came this close to not having a peaceful transfer of power after the 2020 election is about to enter a critical phase. Tomorrow, the committee carrying out that inquiry, sits down with a key witness Trump`s former White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Testimony from last week`s hearing revealed that he raised red flags about the efforts to try and keep Trump in office were dangerous. Also, there are reports that a week from tonight, the committee will hold what is expected to be the final hearing in primetime TV, but so far, the committee members are not officially confirming this.\par \par \uc2 And hearing scheduled for Tuesday, lawmakers will focus on far-right groups that attacked the Capitol on January 6.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN, (D) CALIFORNIA JANUARY 6TH COMMITTEE MEMBER: We will be connecting the dots as people know and as Mr. Raskin I think is indicated publicly, we`re looking at the connections between the various extremist groups. You know, this wasn`t just an event that unfolded. It was planned. Who did the planning and who were they connected with? How did it unfold? And I think we will be connecting dots, there`ll be new information that has not yet been learned, I think it`ll be worth watching.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And as the January 6 panel digs for more evidence pointing to Trump`s direct role in the interaction, the former guy will be campaigning for candidates and raising cash.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow while lawmakers are grilling his former White House Counsel, guess where he`ll be, the state of Nevada at a rally for GOP candidates for Senate and governor.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  now reporting that Trump has found a new line of work. He`s now giving speeches to supporters who pay to see him. The Post says this new venture allows Trump to raise money that doesn`t have to go to his political action committee. What does that mean? It means he gets to pocket the cash for himself.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, we are tracking the fallout from the dramatic announcement from British Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  that he is stepping down as conservative party leader. Johnson resigned under pressure from his own party after a series of scandals.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 , BRITISH PRIME MINISTER: It is clearly not of the will of the parliamentary Conservative Party, that there should be a new leader. The herd instinct is powerful when the herd moves, it moves. How sad I am to be giving up the best job in the world. But them`s the breaks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Them are the breaks indeed. We`ll have more on \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  and what this means for the war in Ukraine just ahead.\par \par \uc2 While back at home, it was a day of celebration at the White House, President Biden honoring 17 Americans with the Medal of Freedom, the nation`s highest civilian honor for their service, and for their sacrifice.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: The Fourth of July week reminds us what brought us together long ago and still binds us, binds us at our best. We strive for what we strive for. We, the people do what we can to ensure the idea of America, a cause of freedom shines like the sun to light up the future of the world. That`s the soul of our nation. That`s who we are as Americans. And that`s when we see an extraordinary, extraordinary group of Americans up here on this stage that I have the honor to recognize today with the Presidential Medal of Freedom, our nation`s highest civilian award.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: With that, let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel this evening. Jeff Mason joins us, White House Correspondent for Reuters. Before that he worked across Europe covering the E.U. And look who is back with us, Andrew Weissmann, former FBI General Counsel, and former Senior Member of the Mueller probe. He is now a professor at NYU Law School. And tonight marks his official return to MSNBC as a legal contributor.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:11]\par \par \uc2 Let`s also welcome former Florida Republican Congressman \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , who was a member of the Republican Congress conference for two years during the Trump presidency.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Weissmann, it is your first night back, I want to welcome you. And I can tell you, many, many viewers have been writing in, calling and wondering where are you? When are you coming back? So I want to officially welcome you, you have been missed.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, FORMER FBI GENERAL COUNSEL: Thanks so much, Stephanie. Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Now let`s get to work. Let`s say you were in that investigation room with January 6 committee. What are the top questions you would ask \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: I think the main thing to remember, and obviously this is, you know, a field day for anyone whose is in that room is that this is not just about what happened on January 6, and obviously there are a lot of questions there about following up on what Ms. Hutchinson said. You want to know what was going on about the crowd, about danger, about them being armed about whether there was danger to \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . And what President`s view was about going to the capitol and about whether he really was indifferent to danger to the Vice President to the United States. That`s one topic.\par \par \uc2 But the other thing you really have to keep your eye on is all the other prongs of the conspiracy that the committee has laid out, which is what was going on at the state level calls to Georgia and other states, they slate of fake electors, how that was put together. And what was going on at the Department of Justice, where what the President wanted to do was to get rid of the acting Attorney General to put in a flunky, who was going to say that there was an open investigation into fraud. So you want to ask about all of that and find out what Pat`s view was and what he articulated to the President. What did he say about whether those things were legal or illegal?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What does that matter, what he articulating?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: So if you were -- so if you are at the Department of Justice, and you are trying to figure out what the President`s intent was, if you have your counsel saying this is illegal, do not do it. And so remember, Ms. Hutchinson said, make sure he does not go to the Capitol, because if he goes, we`re going to face all sorts of criminality. You want to find out exactly what \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  said, because it would really help the Department of Justice in deciding whether there is a criminal case out there to be brought against the former president.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And remember, Trump might want to say that Cassidy Hutchinson is low level, he didn`t know or she wasn`t connected. Can`t make that claim about \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jeff, so next week, we are expecting one, possibly two hearings, how might what the committee learns from Cipollone tomorrow, determine what happens next week?\par \par \uc2 JEFF MASON, REUTERS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Steph, I think it`s it starts with what he -- what he may or may not confirm from what Cassidy Hutchinson said. And that will lead to the next round of questions that they use for the hearings next week. One person who may be testifying next week is a former Deputy Press Secretary for President Trump, who has been very critical and resigned on January 6, after the erection. So it would -- we`ll see what she has to say and what he -- what they end up getting from Cipollone whether or not he chooses to confirm what she said in her testimony whether or not he goes further, or whether he leaves the fit as others have done, or tries to be very careful in his testimony.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, and that`s the thing, Andrew. And Carlos, I promise I`m going to get to you, are we putting too much weight on what we`re going to hear from Cipollone everybody in their brother connected to Trump loves to claim executive privilege or loves to plead the fifth isn`t Cipollone, the one guy who actually has a case to make around executive privilege, he was the actual lawyer?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: So I think he has a possible argument about attorney-client privilege. But that has previously been rejected when that was raised in the connection with the \uc2 Ken Starr\uc2  investigation involving \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . And so I don`t think that`s going to go very far. And with respect to executive privilege, that`s a privilege that the current president needs to assert, and the current president has not asserted that. So those are all things that could be litigated by \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 . But I don`t think that are going to really go very far in this instance. And I presume that there`s already been an agreement, at least in connection with the interview tomorrow, as to what topics are fair game and what are not, if \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  is served with a federal grand jury subpoena. Those issues could come back and need to be litigated in federal court.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:00]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Carlos, I want to ask you about Cipollone`s own words