 \par \uc2 Now, it`s striking that the support for Ukraine is felt pretty much across the board amongst the British political leadership. And if someone like \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 , who`s named as a potential replacement for \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  were to come in, she is potentially even more outspoken in his support for Ukraine than \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the fact you`ve got this kind of turmoil is something that, you know, it`s not just a case of Britain could do without right now, frankly, \uc2 NATO\uc2  and the rest of the \uc2 European Union\uc2  could do without right now, because there are really serious geopolitical issues and economic issues, right across the region. And having this limbo in the U.K. is not helpful in any way whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, Johnson didn`t say when his resignation would take effect. Do you think he could stay on until the next PM is established? Or is he so ticked off, so embarrassed, so fed up? Is he going to say you wanted me out, boom, I`m gone?\par \par \uc2 TETT: Well, I`ve known \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  for, you know, a quarter of a century since we were journalists together in Brussels, and the one thing I`ve learned is to never underestimate him, and to never know what on earth he`s going to do next. And so the reality is that we simply don`t know. I would imagine that he probably wants to hang in there for a while particularly because there is going to be probably quite a protracted process of trying to find the next leader.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:10]\par \par \uc2 But there`s probably also people who are saying right now to embrace strongly, you know, let`s play a straight back to use a wonderful cricketing term from England and just get on with it, you know, leave the pitch, you know, you have been bowled out again to be using cricketing terms.\par \par \uc2 Dominic Raab\uc2  would be the person who would step in if \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  was to go. He`s a very steady pair of hands, who has 