istrict, or they moved all the polling places around out of her neighborhood because it`s largely black and brown.\par \par \uc2 So voting, Stephanie, is fundamental. And it`s not just about oh, we`re in an election cycle. It`s 24/7 all year, you got to stand on that wall to protect it.\par \par \uc2 FRANKEN: Can`t agree more.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then why isn`t that a bigger -- why isn`t that a bigger issue for voters then, Al? They`re not thinking about it, or they won`t until these rights aren`t protected, and they`re gone?\par \par \uc2 FRANKEN: Well, Michael`s completely right. This is a huge threat. And you`ll saw that the Supreme Court is taking this North Carolina case, which is a complete threat to our democracy. It`s basically this theory that the state legislature is empowered by the Constitution to control all elections, without state judges, weighing in the Secretary of State. This is extremely dangerous. This is an extremely dangerous court.\par \par \uc2 And we saw that and this that needs to be an issue in this campaign, not just abortion. But guns need to be an issue in this campaign. There`s a whole host of stuff that this Supreme Court is dealing with to threaten our democracy forever and to fix the 2024 election, essentially, by allowing state legislatures in Pennsylvania and Michigan and Wisconsin to overturn the results as Trump wanted to do, as Eastman wanted to do. This is an existential threat to our democracy. And I`m glad Michael is saying that that is, should be the number one issue.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So we`re going to keep talking about it right here. Gentlemen, thank you for joining us tonight. Always great to see you both Al Franken and Michael Steele.\par \par \uc2 FRANKEN: Thank you, Stephanie. Thank you, Michael.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, coming up next. It is another underreported aspect of the American economy. Something that`s overshadowed by rising prices, and recession fears. We`re going to bring you a little bit of good news before we sign off tonight.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:58:03]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You all remember what the economy was like when I was elected a country in a pandemic, with no real plans how to get out of it. Millions of people out of their jobs. Families and cars remember backed up for literally miles wait for a box of food to be put in their trunk. Just a box of food to be put in their trunk reason to have up to eat. Previous administration lost more jobs in his watch than any administration since Herbert Hoover.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, jobs, jobs, jobs. We already set it. High prices at the grocery store and the gas pump have Americans upset about the direction of the economy. But the President today was talking up what has been a very bright spot in recent months, the job market.\par \par \uc2 Today, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  reported that even after creating almost 400,000 new jobs in May, employers they wanted to hire even more people. The government says there were close to 11.3 million job openings that month, almost two jobs for every unemployed person. With so many open positions, over 4 million Americans quit their jobs in May. So the great resignation or realignment or reassessment, whatever you want to call it, it is so going on. People feel confident enough about their financial position or their potential new job that they can walk away from what they`re currently doing.\par \par \uc2 And employers they know it. So they are doing whatever they can to hold on to their workers. Layoffs, for example, they are now at record -- almost record lows. Let`s say you own a restaurant and you finally have all the staff you need for the summer. You`re probably going to do everything you can to keep those employees happy and working.\par \par \uc2 So what about those warnings that a recession is on the way with inflation as high as it is with the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raising interest rates, it is definitely possibility but as one economist told us what we`re experiencing in the job market this is not what a recession looks like\par \par \uc2 [00:00:07]\par \par \uc2 We`re getting -- get an even better look at how employment is doing this Friday morning, when the Labor Department releases the much anticipated June jobs report. But until then, I want to leave you with a reminder that there`s actually good economic news out there, and we wanted to be sure you knew it.\par \par \uc2 And on that very good note, I want to wish you a very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late with us. I will see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220707ei7600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for July 6, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Shaquille Brewster, Tom Winter, Maggie Vespa, Mike  Memoli, Ali Vitali, Ben Collins, Ashley Parker, Tim Miller, Blayne  Alexander \par \uc2 8,137 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day. I`m Chris Jansing live in Highland Park, Illinois where just about an hour ago, the authorities said 21-year- old Robert Crimo has confessed to opening fire from a rooftop with an AR-15 style rifle firing more than 80 times and killing seven people during Monday`s holiday parade. Police also made a startling even shocking announcement that after the shooting here, Crimo then drove to Madison, Wisconsin, where he contemplated a second mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER COVELLI, DEPUTY CHIEF, LAKE COUNTY MAJOR CRIME TASK FORCE: It appears when he drove to Madison, he was driving around. However, he did see a celebration that was occurring in Madison, and he seriously contemplated using the firearm in his vehicle to commit another shooting in Madison.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You -- how much ammunition he had at that point?\par \par \uc2 COVELLI: Approximately 60 rounds.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So after the hearing, Crimo is being held without bail facing seven counts of first-degree murder, but he could face many more charges. Here`s Lake County State`s Attorney Eric Rinehart.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC RINEHART, LAKE COUNTY STATE ATTORNEY: There are many different charges that we are reviewing with respect to the other individuals who have sadly been injured by this, frankly, who were present on the scene they were shot at. That may also constitute an attempt murder charge. Every time he fires a bullet at an individual, he is committing aggravated discharge of a weapon whether he hits someone or not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The question now, what can be done to stop the next mass shooting? Well, the editorial board of the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  is calling for what a lot of folks here have been telling me, they want to ban high-powered rifles. The editorial board writing "how many more families and communities have to be needlessly ripped apart before something is finally done about the weapons that negative sanely easy to kill the most people in the shortest period of time?" Bottom line, Crimo was known to law enforcement. He had threatened to kill people before, tried to kill himself. The police determined there was no probable cause for an arrest.\par \par \uc2 Back then in 2019, he was labeled a clear and present danger. But he had not applied back then to get a gun so there was nothing authorities could do. When he finally did apply, his father sponsored him. And once again, the authorities determined there was nothing they could do.\par \par \uc2 In all, Crimo passed four background checks, despite the threats and an extraordinary volume of online evidence, much of it violent content. And so there was nothing stopping this 21-year-old from legally buying multiple high-powered rifles, one of which he allegedly used to commit mass murder just down the street from where I`m standing. Here`s what the mayor of Highland Park had to say about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NANCY ROTERING, MAYOR OF HIGHLAND PARK, ILLINOIS: If the law is there to support the safety of the community, then we need to change those laws because it`s not doing its job and lawfully acquired assault weapons being used. We have to leave the capital after week your mass shootings tells me that our legal system needs to address this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I`m going to bring in Shaquille Brewster here with me in Highland Park, NBC investigative correspondent Tom Winter is digging deeper into the gunman`s past, and Harry Litman is a former U.S. attorney and served as Deputy Assistant Attorney General in the Clinton administration. So, Shaq, here we are, day two. I mean, first of all, confession.\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Maybe another shooting could have happened. This was a stunning press conference a short time ago.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Definitely. And it started before the press conference when we saw the suspect in court for the very first time. And that`s when we learned some really disturbing details about how this could have been worse, which is hard to say when we see so many families still suffering, so many people impacted by this. I think there were two key moments that we learned led to this suspect`s arrest. Number one, he did not intend to leave that gun behind.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors say that after he fired about 83 shots -- 85 shots from the rooftop along the parade route, he initially ran with the gun in a bag. It apparently fell out of the bag. And that`s when he decided to leave behind and go off and take advantage of the chaos that he started. We knew he had already went to his parent`s house. He borrowed his car. But then we learned that detail that you mentioned that he drove to Madison, Wisconsin with a rifle in his car and about 60 rounds at his disposal.\par \par \uc2 Chris, if we were to get in the car right now and drive to Madison, I don`t know if the traffic conditions on July 4, but it would take us about two and a half hours to get there. We didn`t know the vehicle that the suspect had, we didn`t know his name or had his picture until well into the evening about 5 p.m. that day, yet plenty of time to do something else and that`s what we learned not only in that press conference but as we saw the suspect in court for the first time.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, we know from the time that he got off of that roof and we captured that picture --\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That most of us have seen when he was dressed as a woman. It was eight hours until he was arrested. So, you`re right, there could have been a lot more damage than he could have done to that rifle found inside that car. Shaquille Brewster, thank you so much. So, Tom, reporters asked a lot of questions about whether potential charges for other people could be filed. The state`s attorney said he had never charged someone for signing off on an application for a gun license like the father of the suspect did. He did though point to a similar case in Michigan, which suggests to me, Tom, that at least that was something they were looking into.\par \par \uc2 TOM WINTER, MSNBC INVESTIGATIVE REPORTER: It may be, Chris. And obviously, they would want to look at what was going on at the home, what the father knew and when, and what type of concerns had been relayed to them. But, you know, a lot of charging somebody with some sort of a criminal offense, and Harry can certainly speak to this better than I can is establishing some form of intent -- excuse me. So I think there would have to -- they have to establish that the father probably knew that he was going to get this gun license in order to commit some sort of -- sort of form of mass shooting and I think it might have to go beyond some internet videos, in statements.\par \par \uc2 But look, that wasn`t the only tool that was at play here, the Red Flag Law, which went into effect on 2019, January 1, 2019, in Illinois. If after he purchased the guns, somebody had thought that he had a credible threat to himself or others, we`re talking about Crimo, somebody would have been able to go to the court directly. So if people think that law enforcement has to do this, not necessarily.\par \par \uc2 Illinois law allows in a lot of so-called Red Flag laws, allow for a spouse or family member and intimate partner to make a direct petition to the court without that person`s knowledge in order to ask the court and ask the judge to sign a search warrant and go get the guns from somebody that they think might be an imminent threat. Also, they can go the law enforcement route and law enforcement can make a similar type of -- similar type of petition to the court.\par \par \uc2 What`s important here is that if you looked at the totality of his social media posts, some of the things that he had put out there, the idea that he had weapons, and somebody knew him well, really, was there an opportunity missed. I think it`s just too soon to say, Chris, based on the information that we have so far. And I can tell you that I`m sure law enforcement is poring over all of their records in regards to this to try to determine who knew what and when, and was there any sort of intent. And I think all three of those things have to line up before we talk about additional people being charged here.\par \par \uc2 And as we know, according to this individual`s own statements in the investigation, and what we`ve heard publicly, what we`ve heard from law enforcement officials that we`ve spoken to, there`s no indication that anybody else was actively involved in helping him plan or carry out this attack, or did any -- certainly did any shooting themselves. So at this point, I would say it`s probably remote that others are going to be charged, but it`s also just probably too soon to say that with any sort of certainty.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Harry, let`s talk about the possible charges against the suspect. The state`s attorney also said they are going to file charges not just for the people who were killed, but people who were shot and for people who were not shot. Is that unusual? How difficult is that proof in terms of building a case? Tell us about those plans for further charges.\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Now, that he`s confessed, it`s neither unusual nor that difficult. There actually was a little bit of hole in the evidence about fine -- about actually tagging him with the shooting. Now his confession stitches that up. And it`s routine, I would say in such a notorious case that you first, get a solid charge just to lock him up, incarcerate him, and keep him there as they did in this afternoon`s bond hearing, and then working with federal state and local law enforcement, everything that you could to throw the book at him.\par \par \uc2 And that means each shot, as you say, could be some kind of attempted aggravated assault. Perhaps there are some firearms laws, but you know, the guy had 70 rounds, who knows what`s connected with Madison? It`s enough that even if half of them wind up successful, he`ll never leave jail.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: As we`ve said, Harry, he confessed, but does that necessarily preclude the fact that he could say plead guilty by reason of insanity? I mean, what calculations might be going into that decision? And is the fact that he confessed, this cut and dry as some people might make it?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Certainly not cut and dried. And you can expect there to be litigation about whether the confession is valid. That might well happen notwithstanding this.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 In terms of insanity, the Illinois State courts use the same thing as -- the same test as the federal system. That is, did he have a mental disease or defect that made him unable to appreciate the criminality of what he was doing? A tough test for him to make. So of course, he could plead insanity but I think with the apps and more, just that he`s crazy in the vernacular, is you and I might think about it, that is not going to get him there so you know, it remains a possibility. Now, in terms of the dad, by the way, I -- Tom`s right. I think it`s going to be very hard to make a case. But I`ll tell you this, I think there will be families of victims who will wind up suing civilly and will name him. He really does seem to have been derelict and enabling here, whether he tripped up a specific law or not.\par \par \uc2 And on that specific law, my final point is the new federal legislation was supposed to include a ban on 18 to 21-year-olds having assault rifles like this, after all, they`re not even allowed to have handguns. And that proved a step too far for the Republicans. And then there`ll be a lot of reflection on why didn`t the Red Flag Law that Illinois does have a catch this guy? It looks different and harder, you know, from this side, yet, still, there seemed to be several red flags that it`s at least seems tragic and poignant that they were -- they were not used to keep him from having his hands on the assault weapons.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And I can tell you, Harry, again, with conversations I`ve been having with people who live around here, that`s something they raised as well. Tom, just before we came on the air, I talked to the man who owns the building that had surveillance cameras that caught that image of the gunman that we`ve seen so many times now in women`s clothing. And I want to play for you what he told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAL EMALFARB, WORKS NEAR SHOOTING SCENE: I said, well, Mira, (PH) your camera found the killer at tunnel seven entering the back of the alley, leaving at 10:57 -- at 10:15 the other way. This happen in a matter of seven or eight minutes and he was identified by Mira`s camera and (INAUDIBLE) systems. And with that identification that happens about three o`clock or 3:15, the police somehow use face identification or something and identify who it was or somebody recognized them, within 45 minutes the North police -- North Chicago police patrolman pulled him over and he was caught.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That building, Tom, is right across the street from where I`m standing. And it`s just an example of how police got a ton of information very fast. But you touched on this. We`re in the very early stages still of this investigation, what more do they want to know?\par \par \uc2 WINTER: 1Right. And you know it really was quickly -- it really was fast, how quickly they were able to piece this together and certainly him leaving behind the gun and having that unique serial number. And the fact that he was the person who actually purchased the gun was very key due to coming up with an identification and putting him in handcuffs.\par \par \uc2 I think going forward to help prove the aggravated assault and attempted murder charges, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s ERT, Evidence Response Team, is going to be critically important in determining who was shot and where were all those individuals` rounds, when. I can tell you from having seen their evidence presentations in court before, they`ll even use GPS to actually locate and come up with a complete map of that street to determine where each one of those rounds went.\par \par \uc2 Another detail that strikes me there, according to that camera video and according to that interview that you did there, Chris, he`s up on the roof. The shots were reported at approximately 11:14 Eastern Time and by 11:15 Eastern time he`s off the roof. It just goes to speak how many rounds can be fired so quickly from these types of weapons and how easy it is for somebody who`s relatively untrained to be able to do the type of damage that they were able to do that day.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And you have to subtract from that the time it took him to get up there and get down because that`s when the camera caught him. So you`re right. The speed of it, it boggles the mind. Tom Winter, Harry Litman, thanks to both of you.\par \par \uc2 And right now, in this close-knit community in Highland Park, scores of families are suffering the unimaginable horror of having a loved one ripped away from them, a feeling that has become common in this country. Two-year- old Aiden McCarthy is one of those victims, found crying after the shooting, hidden beneath the body of his 37-year-old father, Kevin, and next to his 35-year-old mother, Irina, both of them murdered.\par \par \uc2 He was rescued by strangers and reunited with his grandfather who told the \uc2 Chicago Tribune\uc2  "when I picked him up, he said, are mommy and daddy coming soon?" He doesn`t understand. Aiden`s parents are among the seven who were killed that day, so our community leaders like Jacqueline Sundheim and proud grandparents, Steven Straus and Nicholas Toledo Zaragoza. We just learned the name of the seventh victim, 69-year-old Eduardo Uvaldo.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 And joining me now in Highland Park is NBC`s, Maggie Vespa. We can`t talk about these victims enough.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We need people to understand their lives, to a loss. Again, literally, right down the street, there was a mother and a daughter watching the parade together. Tell us about them.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Sure. Katie Goldstein, her daughter had this conversation last night with Lester and our entire team watched him and it was absolutely gutting. Telling us that she was at the parade with her mom, who one of the most striking details had just lost her mom, like three days ago. So the family was trying to get out of the house and basically cheer themselves up and come to this parade, have some time together while they were already grieving.\par \par \uc2 And then Cassie, the daughter, telling Lester that she heard the gunshots. Everyone starts running. She`s running alongside her mom and she sees her mom gets shot in the chest and go down. And she says she knew right away that her mom was dead. She bent down and told her I love you but I have to keep going because, of her words, he`s still shooting everyone. So she just recounted the unimaginable of going through that alongside her mother who`s one of the seven victims.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, she also really wanted everyone to know a lot about, you know, how her mom is going to be remembered. So here`s a part of what Cassie told Lester about her mom, Katie Goldstein. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASSIE GOLDSTEIN, MOTHER KILLED IN HIGHLAND PARK: I want to share how she was before she died. She was waving to the floats. Every float that went by, she waved to them. I did everything with her. She was my best friend. And I got to have 22 years the best mom in the world so --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VESPA: 22 years old, and so close to her mom.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: So -- I mean, you can just imagine what that feels like. And again, just kind of saying my mom was the kind of person who waved to every flow. Like we all know, someone like that just saying she was an amazing person, and now that family who was already grieving a grandmother, grieving a mother as well.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Oh my goodness. Maggie Vespa, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Sure.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And thank you for being here. We appreciate it. Coming up. How has the shooting here in Highland Park potentially shaken the views of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and other potential presidential opponents, including Democrats? But first, what do we know about the phone call President Biden just had with the wife of basketball superstar Brittney Griner as she pleads with the president to bring her home from Russia? And in just six days, the January 6 committee will hold its next public hearing on the Hill. What we know about the focus and who might testify? You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS live from Highland Park, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Breaking news we just learned in the last hour. President Biden has now spoken by phone to the wife of \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner. NBC`s Mike Memooli joins me now from the White House with more on the call. What do we know, Mike, about this conversation?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, I think this really speaks to the power of personal diplomacy as it relates to President Biden. It was only yesterday that we learned that Brittney Griner had, in fact, written this letter directly to the president appealing for him to do everything he could, saying that -- as she put it. I know you have a lot to do but please don`t forget about us, referring not just to herself, being wrongfully detained in Russia, but other Americans who are there as well. The White House had originally said that, yes, the president had read the letter. Our own team reported this morning that the president`s response could come as soon as today.\par \par \uc2 And as you say just in the last hour, right before the president departed the White House traveling to Ohio for an event on the economy, the White House releasing this readout of a call not just with President Biden but also Vice President Harris participating as well in which they spoke to Cherelle Griner to talk about what the administration is doing to try to secure her release.\par \par \uc2 President Biden in fact even rent -- reading to Cherelle Griner, a letter that he has written in response to Brittney Griner as well. And I`ll read from part of the White House statement saying that the president offered his support to Cherelle and Britney`s family and he committed to ensuring they are provided with all possible assistance while his administration pursues every avenue to bring Brittney home.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s worth noting that something of a public pressure campaign on the White House will continue as we see a rally tonight in Phoenix hosted by the Phoenix Mercury, that`s the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  team that Brittney Griner should be playing for as the summer months continue, and that was going to be drawing a lot of attention, a lot of other professional basketball players as well in the White House getting ahead of this by making it clear that the president is in touch with the family personally.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Mike, thank you so much for that update. We also have major news out of the January 6 committee`s investigation. Former White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  will appear on Friday for an interview that will be both transcribed and videotaped. He`ll be with the committee under subpoena. That subpoena has been extended to this Friday. Also, we now know the committee`s next hearing will be on Tuesday, July 12, with a big focus on extremism. And the committee, once again, has \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in its sights.\par \par \uc2 In a recent interview with \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  committee member Jamie Raskin declined to provide specific details about communications between political collectors close to Trump and the militia groups. But he said it was clear that no mob would have come to Washington or descended on the Capitol, were it not for Trump`s direction.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ali Vitale is on Capitol Hill for us. Also with me, NBC News Senior Reporter Ben Collins, who covers extremism. So, Ali, Cipollone was subpoenad, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  made a point of singling him out at the end of the last hearing, so what is the committee going to want to learn from him?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Chris. This was one of those key names that we kept hearing about from the committee issuing these public pleas for the former White House counsel to come forward and talk to them. Now, we know that that subpoena has been extended and we expect this transcribed and videotaped deposition to happen on Friday, ultimately allowing the committee to ask a whole myriad of questions that they want answers to. We learned some of this from the Cassidy Hutchinson testimony, for example, the idea that Cipollone was in many of these rooms having conversations with the former president, top department of justice officials, and then, of course, his former Chief of Staff, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 All of these are different pockets that were part of various hearings in the past. And now, what Cipollone`s taped deposition will allow them to do is use this information going forward. The idea has always been anyone who has agreed to go before the committee, even if they`re not appearing as a technical witness in the hearing room, is effectively a witness anyway because they are able to use parts of that video deposition, and they`ve pretty masterfully woven it into all of these different hearings. Now, as we head into another week, next week, where we expect them to do more hearings, you have to wonder if this is not a new video that we`re going to start to see.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And, Ali, this obviously, is the White House counsel who had deep concerns about what was happening in and around January 6. But the next question, I think, is whether people who didn`t have those kinds of concerns about what they were seeing, or at least didn`t speak up about them, will now feel pressure to come forward.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Well, I keep thinking about Chairman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  at the end of the last hearing, effectively saying as much, Chris, saying if you have either new memories of things that we may have asked you about in previous depositions or maybe some newfound courage that you now want to come forward and talk to the committee, that is the hope of what the committee hoped to do with the Cassidy Hutchinson testimony. And as they get more people coming in and talking with them, they`re hoping that it inspires more people closer to the former president to also come in and not just come in, but to be cooperative.\par \par \uc2 Now, in terms of the next hearing, it`s going to start focusing on domestic extremism. We know that this is something that Congressman Jamie Raskin has been focused on. He`ll be a leader for that hearing. And, of course, that`s very much Ben Spieth, but this is a moment where you look at what the committee is doing. And also you have to keep an eye on DOJ because it`s a moment where those two investigations could actually start to dovetail.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, and looking into extremism, Ben, is part of your job description, frankly. We don`t have a witness list yet but we do know, you spend time looking for details in the darkest parts of the internet. And you reported back in February, I remember this so well about how the January 6 committee was looking back at how extremist groups like Proud Boys, Oath Keepers came together that day. What are the clues we have already from militia groups who descended on the Capitol in the context of this hearing, but also what are your outstanding questions?\par \par \uc2 BEN COLLINS, MSNBC SENIOR REPORTER: My big outstanding question is still did these people have some sort of direct contact with the White House? And by direct contact with the White House I don`t mean, did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  call Stewart Rhodes of the Oath Keepers and say go attack the Capitol? I mean, what did they work through an intermediary?\par \par \uc2 Roger Stone, for example, is close to the president, a longtime fixer, or the president. We know he had Oath Keepers as private securities, incredibly close to the Proud Boys. How direct were those connections in the run-up to the 6? How much did the president`s team know about what Roger Stone was doing with the Oath Keepers, specifically?\par \par \uc2 I don`t think you`re going to see, for example, you know, Stewart Rhodes himself giving that much testimony. I know if he did that -- I know that he did testify to the committee. I don`t think he`s going to have some smoking gun, I think we will probably would have heard about that already. But what you might hear is connections between these groups. And what we had -- but they do have is things like cell phone data, what they are looking at that might connect to these groups, that might, you know, shed light on phone calls or things like that. That might be something that we`ll see next week.\par \par \uc2 What we`re looking for is some sort of coordination. What we know is that there`s coordination between the Proud Boys and the Oath Keepers. They met in the parking lot before the 6th. They talked about what was going to happen on the 6th. How far up the chain at the government level did this go? That`s the next question.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ben Collins, Ali Vitali, thanks to both of you. We appreciate it. Growing frustration with President Biden from inside his own party, the call for urgency, and what voter frustration could mean for this November as well as 2024? You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:33:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The shooting here in Highland Park is becoming somewhat of a leadership test for President Biden. New reporting from "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " describes a growing impatience, quote, "In the view of many distraught Democrats the country is facing a full blown crisis on a range of fronts and Biden seems unable or unwilling to respond with appropriate force."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have a lot more work to do to do. We`ve got to get this under control. We`ve got to get this under control.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But other party leaders who could challenge him in to 2024 are presenting a contrasting, more fiery response, including the governors of Illinois and California. At the same time there`s a potentially big challenge for Republicans, playing to their base, but getting further and further away from the majority of people who are exhausted and angered by ongoing mass gun violence.\par \par \uc2 "Washington Post" White House bureau chief Ashley Parker has more on the Democrats` frustration with the president. Also with me, Tim Miller, who worked on former Republican Florida Governor Jeb Bush`s 2016 presidential campaign, and is now a writer-at-large for "The Bulwark." Both are MSNBC contributors.\par \par \uc2 Ashley, I want to read some more of your reporting. You write, "Biden`s response is often a mix of scolding Republicans, urging Americans to vote Democratic and voicing broad optimism about the country. For some Democrats, that risks a dangerous failure to meet the moment."\par \par \uc2 It`s really interesting because of course as you know his empathy and his measured tone, compared to a bombastic \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , those things were critical to his election. What`s changed? And how have the likes of \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  and \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  seemed to fill a void?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY PARKER, THE WASHINGTON POST WHITE HOUSE BUREAU CHIEF: Well, that`s right. It is important to note that Biden`s team believes correctly that some of that calm, measured competence is what helped him defeat former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And so when there is this onslaught of criticism from their fellow Democrats that they`re seeing now, they sort of say, look, our theory of the case in the primary and then the general proved correct.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:06]\par \par \uc2 But what has changed is that in a number of threats currently facing the company, Democrats view them as existential. It`s not a disagreement of policy or politics, but on things like abortion rights, climate change, gun control, threats to American democracy, Democrats feel this incredible urgency, and they don`t want a leader who is not going to fight incredibly aggressively.\par \par \uc2 And one final point is that Biden`s team on a number of these issues says correctly, look, there`s a limit to what the president can do, you know, dealing with a 50-50 Senate and the Congress as it is. But what you`re seeing from Democrats, as they may understand that Biden can`t wave a magic wand and have forced Congress to codify Roe v. Wade, but they want to see him fighting. They want to see him angry.\par \par \uc2 When there`s a shooting in Highland Park where, you know, now I believe seven people were killed, when then there`s that horrible story of the 2- year-old boy wandering around looking for his parents who it turns out had died, they want some of that fire and that fury that they did see from Illinois Governor Pritzker. And that is sort of -- he and Newsom and some others are demonstrating an alternative form of leadership that`s quite appealing to a number of Democrats right now.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You know, Tim, she`s right. It`s hard to get out of your head the raw images and stories we`ve seen from here on Highland Park of a 2-year- old little boy born around the pandemic, now he doesn`t have parents because of this shooting. The images we`ve seen of strollers and chairs abandoned in the street behind him from fleeing paradegoers. They`re just starting to get some of their personal property back today.\par \par \uc2 So where does this moment leave Republicans, though?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, WRITER-AT-LARGE, THE BULWARK: Yes. Chris, look, I understand Democrats` concerns and as I`m angry, everyone -- not nearly everyone is angry, but most of Americans are angry, and you want that rage, that anger to be validated.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the thing, though, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ran -- just a couple of months I was saying well, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , you know, ran to unite the country and he hasn`t done that. Well, you know, the shooting in Uvalde happened and he signed a bipartisan gun reform bill, he signed a bipartisan infrastructure bill. So Biden has done what he`s promised to do. I think that if you`re the Democrats I would be looking to these other Democrats, Pritzker and Newsom, but even more importantly, the Capitol Hill Democrats, to try to make the Republicans pay for their radical views.\par \par \uc2 I mean, here`s the thing with the Republicans. The Republicans have a very extreme views right now on gun issues in particular, and they`re not suffering any political consequences for it. And so I think if there`s any criticism of Democrats, it should be of the broader party apparatus. You know, why aren`t Republicans being forced to vote up or down on a new law that would make -- that would raise the age for gun purchasing, from 18 to 21.\par \par \uc2 That`s an 80 percent issue. Half of Republicans are for that issue. \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  signed that bill in Florida, right. There are all of these radical extreme positions that Republicans have on a variety of issues, guns is just one of them, and then the Democratic Party needs to do a better job of making them pay for that politically. They`re not doing that right now and I think that is some of the frustration that voters have, and other Democrats have, that this isn`t resulting in anything.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, I think, Ashley, that Tim is right in that a lot of Democrats see the possibility for an opening, right, for President Biden as the head of the party, as well as for some people who are obviously running this year and will be on the ballot in November. Isn`t it a sense, and I don`t want to oversimplify it, but it`s kind of like read the room, it`s a different room than it was when you were running for president, and yes, they got a lot of applause when First Lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  said when they go low, we go high, but sometimes you have to fight at their level?\par \par \uc2 PARKER\uc2 : Well, that`s what we have been hearing from Democrats. And Tim is right that for frustrated Democrats, it`s not just the president, but he is the leader of the party. He has the biggest bully pulpit, and so a lot of that anger and frustration, fairly or unfairly, gets directed to him before it does, say, the Senate majority leader or a local elected official.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what I was told even from people inside the White House is that there had long been private frustrations and concerns and alarm over, you know, a number of issues where Democrats believe they weren`t fighting hard enough. But Roe v. Wade was just galvanizing in a way, as you said, that the room has changed and the time for fighting and a different tactic has also come upon us, is what Democrats would say.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ashley Parker, Tim Miller, thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:02]\par \par \uc2 And as the election interference probe heats up in Georgia, what we`re now hearing exclusively from the Fulton County district attorney after a grand jury just subpoenaed seven of Donald Trump`s key allies.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today we have a new understanding of just how far reaching the investigation is into top members of Team Trump in the days and weeks following the 2020 election. Seven close allies to the former president, including a sitting senator, have been subpoenaed in a special grand jury investigation into possible election interference in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , and five members of Trump`s legal teams were issued the subpoenas on Tuesday, as the Fulton County D.A.`s office examines if there were any coordinated efforts to alter the outcome of the 2020 election in Georgia. A new statement and attorney for Senator Graham says, quote, "In my conversations with Fulton County investigators, I have been informed that Senator Graham is neither a subject nor target of the investigation, simply a witness.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:11]\par \par \uc2 "Senator Graham plans to go to court to challenge the subpoena, and expects to prevail."\par \par \uc2 Joining me know is NBC News correspondent Blayne Alexander. And I know you just spoke with the Fulton County D.A. What did he tell you?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, this is Fulton County District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  and she told me in this exclusive interview that, one, we should expect to see more subpoenas from people who are either associates of Trump or in his inner circle. And she did not rule out the possibility of issuing a subpoena for the former president himself. In fact I asked her that, and she said, quote, "anything is possible."\par \par \uc2 Now, I want to start where you left off with that statement from Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , of course one of the seven people who`s received the subpoena just out yesterday to come and appear here in Fulton County, Georgia. Now he said in his statement that he would fight the subpoena.\par \par \uc2 One thing that she said when I asked her directly about that statement, she says that she`s used to people not wanting to come to, quote -- what she calls the prosecutor party. She`s used to that. She says her job isn`t to bring people because they want to come, but it`s because evidence mandates that they should come, so she says that she will take it to court, and essentially use the powers that she has to compel him to appear.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a little bit more of our conversation where she dives into what we can expect to see in the coming weeks in this investigation. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Could we expect to possibly see additional subpoenas from people in former President Trump`s inner circle or Trump associates?\par \par \uc2 FANI WILLIS\uc2 , DISTRICT ATTORNEY, FULTON COUNTY, GEORGIA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Are we talking about family members? Are we talking about former White House officials?\par \par \uc2 WILLIS: I mean, we`ll just have to see where the investigation leads us. But I think that people thought that we came into this as some kind of game. This is not a game at all. What I am doing is very serious. It`s very important work. And we`re going toad our due diligence gens in making sure that we look at all aspects of the case. And so all you see is a prosecutor doing their due diligence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Well, Chris, and you heard it right there. She takes this very seriously. This is something that she has said from the very beginning that she plans to take her time. In fact she underscored it with me earlier today. She says she`s not in a rush for this. Remember this special grand jury is able to convene for up to a year. They were seated back in early May. So we`re talking about a long runway here for this investigation to continue.\par \par \uc2 Now when I asked her about a possible timeline, she did say that in order to make it clear that this isn`t -- she`s not trying to interfere with the upcoming midterm election in anyway, she plans to pause this investigation once early voting begins here in the state of Georgia. That`s late -- that`s early October so she says you`re not going to see any indictments, you`re not going to see any subpoenas during that time because she wants to make it clear that she`s not trying to influence the election. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Blayne Alexander, thank you. What a great interview. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And as we continue our coverage of the investigation into the Highland Park shooting, I`ll speak with Democratic Congressman Brad Schneider whose district is in Highland Park and who was at the parade when the gunfire broke out. That`s coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:02]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I`m Chris Jansing back live with our coverage of the Highland Park shooting. It is almost impossible to fathom the depth of the trauma being felt by the folks who attended the Fourth of July parade here, the folks who live here. Our own Dasha Burns spoke with two little girls who were at that parade. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I feel scared of like maybe some louder noises like when there was fireworks, that scared me. But, yes, I`m scared.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m still scared of like big noises, like police sirens and stuff.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m probably not going to go to any more parades.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I just feel scared that now parades, thinking that this would happen again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Lily and Sydney. With me now Democratic Congressman Brad Schneider. He lives here in Highland Park. He was at the parade when the gunfire broke out. It breaks your heart to hear this --\par \par \uc2 REP. BRAD SCHNEIDER (D-IL): Our hearts are all breaking in the whole community.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There was a grandmother today who told me she told her grandchildren you can`t walk around anymore looking at your phone, you always have to be aware of where you are. Tell me how this community is doing.\par \par \uc2 SCHNEIDER: The community is grieving. We`re really suffering. This is -- it`s barely 48 hours since the shooting. Last night there were vigils at synagogues and churches across the community. Vice President Harris was right here at this corner laying a wreath and people are reaching out. We`re reaching out to each other. We`re leaning on each other. But the story of the two girls that just showed, the young women who lost their mother, Katie Goldstein, Aidan McCarthy, 2-year-old boy who lost both parents in this shooting. This community, we will come together but right now we are really suffering.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We did get the news today that the gunman confessed, that he possibly thought about doing this again in another community, Madison, Wisconsin. Tell me what your thoughts were when you heard about those developments.\par \par \uc2 SCHNEIDER: You know, I can`t even get my thoughts around how someone can take a military assault weapon, climb to a rooftop and firing up to 70 shots in a couple seconds, killing neighbors in the community he grew up in. And immediately when I heard about him considering shooting in Wisconsin, I sent a text to my colleague \uc2 Mark Pocan\uc2  and saying I`m so grateful this didn`t happen where you are.\par \par \uc2 But we`ve seen these shootings across the country. Three hundred mass shootings this year alone, more than 300. Highland Park will never be the same. But the community will come back and we will have a Fourth of July celebration again. But today it is grieving for the families that are destroyed and the community that was shattered.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A big part of recovery in mass shootings is knowing that it made a difference. Something could have changed. Is there a consistency to what you`re hearing from your constituents? Is there a way to make this mean something in terms of legislation? I don`t know. You tell me.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:04]\par \par \uc2 SCHNEIDER: We always look for meaning at a time of loss.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCHNEIDER: It`s hard to find any meaning in the idea of someone shooting 70 bullets into a community at the height of a celebration of our country`s independence. I know for me my commitment was already there to do all we can to reduce gun violence in our country. We need to ban these assault weapons, these weapons of war that belong in a battlefield, not in our community.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Do you have any hope of that?\par \par \uc2 SCHNEIDER: I always have hope. I`ve been working at this since I came to Congress more than 10 years ago. We have to believe that we can reach across the aisle, across the Capitol to the Senate, and find people who say enough is enough. We can`t have a situation where in this country, across the country people were celebrating Independence Day, the nation`s freedom, and in our community, it was shattered. But those bullets shattered the idea for everyone in this country. I`m hoping we can get united and come together and do something.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, I was telling you just before we came on the air the kindness that has been shown to us in the media by the members of this community, thanking us for being here, just being very thoughtful about everything. And it`s unspeakably kind in the midst of all of this tragedy. It`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 SCHNEIDER: It`s a very special community. And, you know, the Fourth of July, where we`re standing here, people would be packed five rows deep. Families come together. I talked to someone who`s here with 16 family members, someone else with nine family members, parents, grandparents, grandchildren, celebrating. And that was shattered. But we`re not going to let the bad guy win. We will come back together as a community and celebrate our country for years to come.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Congressman Schneider, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 SCHNEIDER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And we`re so sorry for what has happened here. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going do it for me. I`m Chris Jansing. Hallie Jackson picks up our coverage next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220707ei7600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION WITH AL SHARPTON for July 3, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Maggie Vespa, Cori Coffin, David Jolly \par \uc2 6,693 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:01]\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS, MSNBC HOST: Now, Rev., I know you have an interview with the whip, Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , coming up. I will be watching.\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Well, I thank you for watching and I certainly enjoy watching you. Lady in blue tonight, I see. Thank you, Symone.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, and welcome to POLITICSNATION on the eve of Independence Day. Tonight`s lead, independence for whom.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the country is, in name, celebrating its independence from tyranny and absolute rule. But tomorrow`s July 4th is likely to feel different for millions of Americans who have watched weeks off Supreme Court decisions point the nation down in uncertain path. Among them, millions of women now subject to the court`s decision overturning Roe v. Wade, many having already watched trigger bans on abortion taking effect in their states, while conservative lawmakers dream up cartoonish methods to keep women from fleeing to blue states where abortion is still legal. And President Biden must not only preside over this chaos but use it to drive voters to the polls leading up to a crucial midterm elections in November.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the national abortion fight is far from his only domestic challenge, as this July 4th weekend has been curtailed by the same record inflation and rising gasoline prices that have hampered Democrats` agenda and dented the president`s approval numbers for months now.\par \par \uc2 I would be lying if I said our democracy was not in peril this Independence Day and I won`t patronizing with the easy prescription of vote harder, because marginalized communities will tell you it`s not that simple.\par \par \uc2 And so, as we mark the nation`s 246th birthday, what does fighting back look like? And do Democrats have enough time to do so before it`s too late? That`s the big question on POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, police in Akron, Ohio, just released body cam footage of the fatal arrest of Jayland Walker last week as another police shooting death cast a pale all over the city`s July 4th weekend. We`ll bring you the latest from Akron shortly.\par \par \uc2 But, first, joining me now is Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , Democrat of South Carolina and House majority whip. Congressman Clyburn, we appreciate you joining us as always and, of course, we have a lot to get to tonight.\par \par \uc2 The eve of -- again, thank you for being here. On the eve of a tense July 4th for the nation, it is very tense, but I want to start on a hopeful note. \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , now officially installed as \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  justice is no small feat because you lobbied President Biden for her on the advice of your daughter, Dr. Jennifer Clyburn Reed who lobbied you. You both joined my colleague, Lawrence O`Donnell, hours after Judge Jackson was sworn in last week. Folks at home, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER CLYBURN REED, REP. CLYBURNS DAUGHTER: With the nomination, we knew that representation matter a lot. And the representation just wasn`t there. So, it was an argument that the congressman had no answer for.\par \par \uc2 Anything is possible. And Justice Jackson -- let`s say that again. Justice Jackson is a representation of what is possible for all Americans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, given how close you were personally to this process, Congressman, how are you prosecuting this milestone coming just a few days before the nation`s birthday?\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM CLYBURN (D-SC): Well, thank you very much for having me. You know, watching that a swearing in, reflecting upon the discussions we had back in 2020, when a lot of people were saying that this sounded like a commitment could not be made in public, I thought it was very necessary for people to know the real \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And so I urged him to do it because I thought it would help to change the narrative in his campaign, and it did.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:00]\par \par \uc2 I was so proud of that moment. This justice is going to do herself, her family, her community, this nation real proud. And I am so, so pleased to see it come to pass.\par \par \uc2 And you are right. I have three daughters, and all three of them are involved in those discussions. Jennifer is a little more outspoken than the other. So, she kind of took the lead in the argument. But I listen to my daughters. I talk to them often. Jennifer is here with me now. We`ll spend July 4th together. Angela will be joining us. And we hope that (INAUDIBLE) will show up. She`s down, as you know, in New Orleans. I think she`s seen you down there.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, I`m still here. And I saw that proud fatherly look you had, and I must say your three daughters came from two activists, you and your wife. So, it`s in their blood. And I certainly remember that you were the one that helped to put that president there when you endorsed \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and turned his campaign for the nomination of the Democratic Party around. I will never forget the morning that you did at the endorsement. You had come to the National Action Network South Carolina Minister`s Breakfast, and we gave you the award in the name of your activist wife.\par \par \uc2 But, Congressman, let me not diverge too much. Justice Jackson joins the court as one of three liberals contrasting with a conservative majority that has issued several controversial decisions here in the last few weeks, not the least of which being that reversal of Roe versus Wade. Other than altering dissents to future opinions from the conservative wing, how does Justice Jackson hold the conservative wing in check?\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: Well, I think that, you know, people with an open mind, and most of our justices do have open minds, they may have opinions, conservative versus progressive, but their minds are open. I think they will listen to the argument. And I think the justice can bring to the court 