n, not more. And it`s just going against common sense. And people like you mostly care about, I like to think that I do too, are the people that are going to be most adversely affected by this.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Now, let`s look at Republicans and specifically the top of the party. Former President Trump remains the standard ballot. But it does seem like the accounts of him throwing food and wrestling Secret Service agents put forward by the January 6th committee are getting under his skin. The best evidence for this is that he is now reportedly itching to launch his presidential campaign before the midterms are completed.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , best known for waging war on Mickey Mouse and micromanaging elementary school lesson plans, is raking in millions.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:12]\par \par \uc2 He`s also raking in millions of dollars from Trump donors. My question to you is, are Democrats better off battling the party of DeSantis or the party of Trump?\par \par \uc2 CARVILLE: Well, let`s start -- I`m going to get professional a bit here. I have news for Republicans, not \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2 , the rest of you, repent. The end is near. You understand what I`m saying? Repent. The end is near. This committee is like in the fourth or fifth inning. There`s a lot more to go and there is a lot more that we are going to find out. And that is -- I think that`s going to have a pretty significant change. And we are starting to see that now.\par \par \uc2 And I`m so proud for Congressman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  from Mississippi who is in a state that I`ve spent a lot of time in. I have a place in Bay St. Louis. And the congresswoman from Wyoming was showing what it takes to people from the middle of the country, are showing what it takes to get this thing in the right direction. And I think these people are just getting warmed up. This is the end of nothing. This is the beginning of everything. Repent. The end is near.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Repent, the end is near, wow, from James Carville.\par \par \uc2 Before I let you go, later in the show, I am going to have a special listened to those that are feeling concerned about our country this 4th of July, but I want to get your take. In 2020, I remember you are a voice of calm, telling Democrats that if they stick to the plan, Trump would be defeated and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would be president. What is your take now as we head into these very important midterms?\par \par \uc2 CARVILLE: I am not a voice of calm. I`m petrified. And I will echo what my friend, Congressman Clyburn, said. If this country does not have voter intervention, I am not optimistic about its future. If people and particularly young people, if they care any about this country, and as you know you were a young person one time on the forefront of the civil rights movement, if they don`t get a hold to this and do something, I am mortified. I wholly endorse the Clyburn strategy of voter intervention. But I am not a voice of confidence at all. I am very afraid right now.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, it`s a scary time. And you are right, I`ve been in civil rights movement, I`m still at the forefront but I`m not that young anymore. And you, James Carville, are unified. Very few people know, you are the only man in America that could be roasted at the Kennedy Center and have \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  and \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  as other speakers on the same stage for the same event. I don`t know anyone else that could pull that off. Thank you for being with me tonight.\par \par \uc2 CARVILLE: Well, you had just, it was a big honor to have you there, Reverend Sharpton. Remember, repent, the end is near. And I hope I`m right. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you for being with us. And we want get you up to date on breaking news out of Akron, Ohio now, where the Akron Police Department has just released body camera footage of a deadly shooting involving its officers.\par \par \uc2 Maggie Vespa is there now. Maggie, we know there was a peace march scheduled after the press conference. What are you seeing?\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Sure. Well, Reverend, as you can see, we`re seeing that peace march happening right now. People have been looping around several blocks kind of around city hall and the police department, marching and yelling, what do we want, justice, when do we want it, now, and also saying Jayland Walker`s name. We just heard it over and over. That was the 25-year-old who early Monday morning was shot and killed by Akron police.\par \par \uc2 And the thing that is really infuriating people here are the numbers tied to this story. Police confirming today in the press conference, eight Akron police officers firing roughly 90 rounds, wounding Jayland Walker, excuse, me 60 times. Again, he`s 25 years old, those 60 wounds obviously fatal, the 25-year-old dying of his injuries.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re here in Akron, Ohio, after today police released body camera video of that shooting, including the moments that Jayland Walker was actually shot and killed. And it was just a barrage of bullets, as expected. They also did say they released details and kind of what led up to the shooting, saying they tried to pull Walker over for a traffic violation, saying that they say he shot at them and it appears to show that in the video out of his car widow.\par \par \uc2 But what people here can`t get past is that, again, he was wounded at 60 times. According to initial autopsy reports, 90 rounds fired, and, again, also police pointing out that they believe he was unarmed when he was shot, that he was actually running away from police.\par \par \uc2 So, this is the city of Akron right now. People are furious. They`ve seen the video. They`ve heard what police have to say, and they say they don`t care. They feel like the level of violence against 25-year-old Jayland Walker was disproportionate and we`re told that they are planning to keep protesting throughout Akron throughout the evening.\par \par \uc2 [17:20L05]\par \par \uc2 We are going to stay on the ground and absolutely keep an eye on this. But for now, we`ll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Maggie Vespa, thank you for your reporting.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, why it`s time for the true patriots to stand up this 4th of July. But first, my colleague, Cori Coffin, with today`s other top news stories. Cori?\par \par \uc2 CORI COFFIN, MSNBC HOST: Thanks, Rev. Stories we are watching at this hour for you, Russia has taken control of Lysychansk, the last city in the Luhansk region in Eastern Ukraine, that was still under Ukrainian control. Ukraine`s military said it had withdrawn from the eastern city hours after Russia`s defense minister said his forces had seized control of it.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  told the Australian prime minister today, the city is, quote, still being fought for over the border.\par \par \uc2 Explosions rocked the Russian town of Belgorod, killing four people there as well, according to officials there.\par \par \uc2 4th of July is in full swing, and there are plenty of travel headaches with air travel numbers approaching pre-pandemic levels. Hundreds of flights have already been canceled this weekend and thousands were delayed. That`s according to FlightAware. More than 50 flights are already canceled for tomorrow as well.\par \par \uc2 And Tropical Storm Colin is ruining barbecue plans along the eastern seaboard. The storm has drenched parts of the Carolinas. And although the system is weakening, dangerous rip currents and high waves are still possible.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:25:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Today is Independence Day. There will be flags and fireworks as there