e true patriots. We are the ones living up to the ambitious principles laid out by the founders of the country, even if they didn`t fully live up to them then. And we still struggle to fulfill them now.\par \par \uc2 Let`s celebrate this Independence Day by rising up and continuing to fight to make this country the unique and exceptional nation that it has always aspired to be. Happy July 4th. Let`s do it right.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:30:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. I want to bring in my political panel to weigh in on today`s big topics. Joining me now is former Republican Representative David Jolly of Florida, he is also an MSNBC Political Analyst, and former Democratic Senator \uc2 Doug Jones\uc2  of Alabama.\par \par \uc2 Doug, let`s start with the legal battles the Supreme Court has fought by overturning Roe versus Wade. This week, judges in Florida and Kentucky blocked abortion bans from taking effect. But supporters of abortion rights face an uphill climb with bans and restrictions on their way in at least 22 states. Do you think there are still pathways to protect abortion rights in the courts or should the focus shift to legislation?\par \par \uc2 FMR. SEN. \uc2 DOUG JONES\uc2  (D-AL): I think there are very few -- thanks for having me this Sunday afternoon before the 4th. I think there are very few legal avenues. Some of the states are going to go too far, especially when they`re talking about banning travel, when they get into contraception. Those are going to be the main legal battles that will be fought.\par \par \uc2 There are still a lot of legal battles out there, but the fundamental right is going to be left to the states. And I think that we have got to look at trying to figure out how to do this on a federal legislative level, and otherwise, we are going to have chaos.\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito`s opinion thought that he would settle this issue down and