s direction, it would suggest a state legislature can do whatever it wants with voting administration laws, which we know in red states would ultimately be used to reduce turnout among Democratic (INAUDIBLE) majority and minority communities.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, David, let`s stay with you and talk about the January 6th probe. In surprise testimony last week, a former aide to Trump`s White House chief of staff, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , gave a shocking account of the former presidents actions that day, when the Secret Service officers refused to let him follow protesters down to the U.S. Capitol. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER AIDE FOR \uc2 MARK MEADOWS\uc2 : Mr. Engel grabbed his arm, said, sir, you need to take your hand off the steering wheel. We are going back to the west wing. We are not going to the Capitol. Mr. Trump then used his free hand to lunge towards Bobby Engel. And when Mr. Ornato had recounted the story to me, he had motioned towards his clavicles.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, just as troubling as his own violent behavior was the president`s apparent knowledge of his supporters` violent intent that day, and he knew they were armed. The president was told many in the crowd he had assembled were armed. And yet, according to the testimony, he demanded the removal of screening devices that would have detected those weapons. What should the consequences be for this type of behavior from the commander in chief of the United States?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: I think if you accept Cassidy Hutchinson`s testimony, and granted it has to be tested against other people`s memories of those days, it means the Department of Justice needs to investigate whether or not the former president, while he was sitting in the office, knew that there was an attempt to use violence to prevent the certification of an election and said, I am going to help advance that violence. I am going to reduce the security blanket.\par \par \uc2 One of the most damning things that Cassidy Hutchinson testified that \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , the White House counsel, and \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , the chief of staff, heard \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  agree with the fact that \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  should be hanged. And I think, ultimately, the reason the J6 committee called Hutchinson to testify so quickly is they wanted to set the hook in Cipollone with a subpoena to say, okay, now we believe you were in the room and heard \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  endorsed violence. Is that true or not?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And we do know for a fact, David, that the president himself -- we can dispute her testimony, but the president himself said at that rally, and we`ve seen it over and over again on video, that he was going to the Capitol, which a president cannot go to the chamber of the House and Senate unless he is invited. So, what was he going for? And we do know he knew they were armed.\par \par \uc2 But let me go back to you, Senator Jones, while I have you. I want to mention a significant development in the 1955 lynching murder of Emmett Till. Last week, a team searching a Mississippi basement for evidence about the case found and unserved warrant, charging a white woman in his kidnapping. The relatives of Emmett reportedly want authorities to finally arrest her, nearly 70 years later.\par \par \uc2 Now, you successfully prosecuted two KKK members for their roles in the 1963 Birmingham church bombing nearly four decades after the attack took place. Talk to me about why it is so important that justice be served in these cases, even so many years later.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:02]\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, justice should never be denied. It can be delayed but it should never be denied. And I think the finding of this warrant, Rev, is a really important development. I have for a long time felt like it would be very difficult to prosecute that woman for murder with away it turned out. She had made certain admissions. But the murder case, there was no statute of limitations and I felt it was very tough.\par \par \uc2 But right now with this warrant, what you have got is that authorities felt at the time, in the 1950s, there was enough evidence to charge her with kidnapping. And under Mississippi law, as I understand it, there`s no statute of limitations for kidnapping.\par \par \uc2 And I think that is a very different crime that you could possibly prove given her testimony that she lied to authorities, given the fact that she talked to her husband and the other man who hasn`t admitted that they killed Emmett Till. There are opportunities whether or not a local D.A. or a state attorney general can do that or a grand jury would actually indict with a lady who is in her 80s now.\par \par \uc2 But, frankly, I just think that age should not matter at this point. Justice should still be served no matter how long it takes, is my view.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: \uc2 Doug Jones\uc2  and David Jolly, thank you both much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the Supreme Court has ditched out the Roe versus Wade hearing but apparently does not want to hear from those who disagree with it literally. I will explain, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:45:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. We have been following breaking news all hour. Police in Akron, Ohio have released body cam footage and additional details on the death of Jayland Walker, a 25-year-old black motorist who was shot and killed by police on Monday in Akron, Ohio, after officers sought to pull him over for an unspecified traffic violation. Eight officers have been put on administrative leave as the Ohio Attorney General`s Office leads the investigation into the fatal shooting.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, protesters have been peacefully demonstrating in Akron for days, demanding more transparency and accountability in Walker`s death.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2 , he is an author and is the former CEO of the \uc2 Robin Hood Foundation\uc2 . He is also a Democratic candidate for Maryland`s governor`s race. Thank you for joining me today.\par \par \uc2 Wes, I want to start with this story out of Akron today. As you have work to address urban poverty, you understand all too well the impact of police brutality and the criminalization of black youth. And your home state of Maryland has seen its fair share of these types of cases, including the death of Freddie Gray in 2015. What are your thoughts --\par \par \uc2 WES MOORE, DEMOCRATIC CANDIDATE FOR GOVERNOR OF MARYLAND: Immediately, your thoughts go first to the family of Mr. Walker. And you know that we have to move with the level of urgency about this. We need a thorough and an independent investigation as to what happened. And I talk often about policing with appropriate intensity, appropriate intensity, but absolute integrity in all of this. And we have to be able to get to the bottom of this because the reality, Reverend Al, is we keep on seeing the same things happening in our communities.\par \par \uc2 And for us to truly create a situation where people can feel safe in their own communities, in their own homes and in their own skin, it means that we have to have these thorough, independent investigations as to what happened and make sure that justice, true justice, is followed through on this.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Switching gears here, I want to ask you about another problem that is hitting Americans hard. As the United States` inflation rate has hit record high in recent months, consumers have felt that financial burden, especially in black and Hispanic communities. With your experience of being the CEO of the largest anti-poverty nonprofit, what can be done to help vulnerable communities right now?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes. I mean, the reality, Reverend Al, is that people -- as I`m going all around the state of Maryland, I keep on from hearing people, they say, well, things feel like everything is more expensive. The reality is that because it is more expensive, that too many of us cannot afford, put gas in the car, cannot afford for food on the table, we watch how gas prices have risen over just 75 percent in the past month.\par \par \uc2 And so there are a few things that we can do on the state level to be able to address these issues as you`re watching inflation rise and affordability for all our families fall out of their hands. The first is we`ve got to get people back to work. And the reality of having a broken childcare system continues to bear on all of our societies because not only has that -- not only in the state of Maryland alone we watched 850 childcare centers closed since COVID but that is one of the core reasons why we are not getting people back to work, particularly women.\par \par \uc2 We`ve also got to focus on job rescaling and job retraining, get people back to work in this economy. And we have got to raise wages. Right now, we are talking about a minimum wage that is still not keeping up with inflationary growth. And so to make things more affordable, we`ve got to release this pressure valve off of people, off of the working families who are struggling to survive in this moment, and that`s got to be done with a sense of urgency.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, quickly, I would like your take on another story in your state right now. A Supreme Court marshal has asked the governors of Maryland and Virginia to curtail protest activity outside the homes of Supreme Court justices. What is your take on this request and does it impede on the First Amendment right to protest?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: No. See, I vehemently support the First Amendment and support the right to assembly and the right to free speech. And I also support safety. And I think that when we are talking about the confluence of what this is right now and what we are looking at, I know people are angry, and yet intimidating and threatening judges is not going to be the answer as to what we are going to do to be able to express that anger.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:11]\par \par \uc2 And we`ve seen it in the state of Maryland where we have actually had physical violence against the children of judges. This is the type of thing that, while we have to protect the First Amendment, we also need to support the safety of our judges as well.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2 , former CEO of the \uc2 Robin Hood Foundation\uc2 , currently candidate for governor of Maryland, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my final thoughts, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Tomorrow is the 4th of July, celebrating the Independence Day of this country, and I began every 4th of July reading the speech by Frederick Douglass, what`s to the save is the 4th of July, that he said during a July 4th celebration in the mid 19th century.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:02]\par \par \uc2 He spoke the day after the 4th of July and raised this question. It`s a question that we can ask today. What to blacks is the 4th of July when you see ten blacks killed with automatic and military-style weapon in Buffalo, New York, and this legislative body in the Senate will not even address military-style weapons, or when we see a young man like Mr. Walker shot down and with 60 bullets in his body. They say he had a gun, and they say that he shot at them. There is no evidence of that, but there is clear evidence from the body cameras of the police, they shot and Jayland Walker 60 times.\par \par \uc2 What to the 4th of July is blacks? What to the 4th of July is it to the LGBTQ community when we have a Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  saying, now that we`ve gone after a women`s right to choose and privacy, we can deal with LGBTQ, and we can deal with other rights of privacy? What to women is the 4th of July, when I remember my mother and father telling my how they had to search states during Jim Crow era where they could get public accommodations and sleep in hotels or eat in restaurants? We`ve gone from Jim Crow to Jane Crow, where women have to find states that they can have their reproductive rights honored.\par \par \uc2 It is a day that Frederick Douglass` speech takes new meaning. But the America we want is the America that we will make. What will the 4th mean to us? Ultimately, that will be up to us, to stand up, and make the country live up to what it promised? That`s what Dr. King said, live out the true meaning of your words. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:00:00]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220704ei7300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for July 2, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Cori Coffin, Susan del Percio, Juanita Tolliver \par \uc2 6,858 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS, MSNBC HOST: You can also hit me up on social media where you can find highlights and news about the show on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and the TikTok.\par \par \uc2 But right now, I`d like to hand things over to my friend, Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Rev. I know you have DNC Chair \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2  coming up. I`m going to watch because the people have got questions. I know you`re going to get some answers.\par \par \uc2 [17:00:21]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Well, we certainly have questions. He and I are both here at the Essence Festival in New Orleans. A lot of questions, a lot of people, always good to see you, at least through the TV, my dear friend, Symone.\par \par \uc2 And good evening, and welcome to POLITICSNATION on this July Fourth weekend.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: The Court and the Country.\par \par \uc2 Right now, two weeks of seismic Supreme Court decisions, starting with his reversal of Federal abortion rights have left America`s political faultlines even deeper. On one side of the divide are blue states racing to codify abortion rights and offer a safe haven to women who may now have to cross their state lines for the procedure on the other side.\par \par \uc2 On the other side is a growing list of mostly red states that have already seized upon this moment to ban abortion with some conservative lawmakers in those states contemplating punishments for those that seek the procedure elsewhere, if not just an outright national ban.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Roe`s reversal was just one ruling of the Court`s conservative majority. It has also issued a full slate of decisions on guns, the environment and voting rights that illustrate just how conservative this Supreme Court truly is, and by all accounts, will be for some time.\par \par \uc2 Is America being remade by these rulings? That`s the big question tonight on POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 And another big question: Just how damning was this week`s bombshell hearing in the congressional January 6 investigation? As a former White House aide recounts outrageous behavior by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on January 6th, NBC is reporting that some donors may finally be backing away from Trump as a growing pack of Republican challengers looks to take him on.\par \par \uc2 All of that tonight.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Congressman Troy Carter, Democrat of Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, we appreciate you joining us this holiday weekend. I spend it usually -- it is the first time since the pandemic, I`m back in New Orleans for the Essence Festival.\par \par \uc2 REP. TROY CARTER (D-LA): Welcome back, sir.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let`s get to your state.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Since we`re here and it has national ramifications, let`s get to your State Supreme Court battle over voting maps, but I have to start with our escalating abortion right fight and I want to get into that, but I have to start there.\par \par \uc2 You know, as States continue to wrestle with last month`s Supreme Court overturning Roe versus Wade; this week, a District Court in the person of a Judge in District Court blocked your State, right here in Louisiana, blocked your State`s trigger ban on abortion from taking effect.\par \par \uc2 Now, as abortion providers in State has filed a lawsuit calling the ban unconstitutional. A hearing is set in that case for this Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 So for now, abortion is continuing your State of Louisiana, but how are Democratic lawmakers in your state preparing for what comes after?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Unfortunately, my State happens to have one of the most egregious abortion laws, one that has been triggered, and thankfully we have a District Court Judge in New Orleans that`s halted that at least for the time being.\par \par \uc2 The Legislature is ultra conservative in Louisiana and has passed several going back all the way to 2006, several very egregious, very damaging laws as it relates to reproductive freedoms and the rights of women to choose.\par \par \uc2 We will continue to fight in the \uc2 US Congress\uc2 , but as it relates to the Louisiana Legislature, unfortunately the votes just aren`t there.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:02]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now Congressman, I want your response to another of the Supreme Court`s decisions this week to reinstate your State`s Republican drawn congressional map, previously halted by a Federal Appeals Court decision that found the map to be racially biased in violation of the Voting Rights Act.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court`s decision also blocks the Lower Court`s ruling that a second majority Black district need be drawn in Louisiana. With November midterms elections looming, anything I`m missing here, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: We`re missing basic math. The Supreme Court is missing some basic math. If you look at simple math, African-Americans make up 33 percent of our State. We have six members of Congress, I happen to be the only African-American Democrat by just pure math and reapportioned with numbers. The growth of African-Americans in Louisiana clearly denote that we should have a second seat that an African-American can elect someone of their choosing.\par \par \uc2 Two-thirds of six is going to be to every way you slice it, yet we only have one, and for the Supreme Court to act in this way, as they did with Roe v. Wade, removing the precious right of a woman to choose and now denying African-Americans the right to choose a second member of their choice, clearly the numbers do note that an African-American should have a seat.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: This is the same Court, I just want to emphasize so people understand -- this Court with a six to three conservative now majority, and for all that said voting doesn`t matter, well, this matters because not only are we dealing with Roe versus Wade, we`re dealing with cases like here in Louisiana, where the population would call for at least two Black populated districts, we`re down to one.\par \par \uc2 Now Congressman, I want to get your reaction also to the death of delivery driver, Jayland Walker earlier this week, shot and killed by Akron Police in Akron, Ohio after a car chase and an on-foot pursuit.\par \par \uc2 Autopsy records showing Walker was fired upon nearly 100 times, more than 60 shots hitting his body. The Akron Police Department maintains that Walker posed a threat to its officers, still eight are unidentified officers are suspended. And I want to get you to view this in light of the case that the fact that you have all cases right in Louisiana that you`ve had to deal with police brutality.\par \par \uc2 So as we have dealt with police misconduct, police killings over the last several months, how do you react to now, what we`re hearing in Akron, Ohio?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Horrific. It is horrible to see yet another young Black man shot down like a hunted animal.\par \par \uc2 You know, Louisiana asked for a patterns of practice investigation after the apparent cover-up of the Ronald Greene case. Yet, here we go again, another case in another part of our country where a Black man is shot multiple times like a ferocious animal on the street.\par \par \uc2 Listen, we`re trying to do something in Washington. We`ve passed the act to make sure George Floyd will never be just another name. It now waits on action in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 . We codified Roe v. Wade, to make it a law. It sits and waits in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We have to make sure that people know elections have consequences. And we have to demand that the other side listens. And if they don`t listen, we have to make this November an absolute, an absolute case where everybody goes out and votes and demonstrates that as the Supreme Court seeks to take our rights away, we will renew them with new legislation with new Members of Congress and new Members of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: If that message is not sent, it will continue as business as usual, and I think that if people are angry, they need to vote their anger.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Congressman Troy Carter.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: We`ve got to stay angry long enough to vote.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Absolutely correct.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now -- thank you, Congressman. Joining me now is \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 , Chair of the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 . And Mr. Chairman, you`re here in New Orleans, you`ve addressed the Essence Festival this afternoon, I addressed it after you about voting, Vice President Harris is addressing it this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 And first of all thank you for joining me this evening.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:10]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I think it`s safe to say the recent events have gotten, well, let`s put it this way, it has given Democrats a lot to campaign on and against between the continuing revelations coming out of the January 6 probes, which I`ll get to later, and the past two weeks of controversial Supreme Court rulings on climate, on guns, and of course, the reversal of Roe versus Wade. It is widening in impact.\par \par \uc2 It`s easy to assume that national outrage over the Court`s recent ruling will invariably translate to turn out in November, but how does the DNC and the party seize this moment? Because I`m hearing a lot of people just saying that, "We haven`t seen anything. Nothing is coming to us. What are the Democrats doing? They`re beating up on us. They`re taking away our rights. And where are the Democrats?"\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Well, Rev, I think you said it perfectly. What we are seeing is a full frontal assault on American democracy, but also our freedoms that we enjoy in this country.\par \par \uc2 And so that I know that so many of us are angry. I was angry after the Dobbs decision came out when it was leaked, and then when it came out, it felt like it was a gut punch.\par \par \uc2 But what we`ve got to do, Rev, is tap into that energy and tap into that anger and channel that into real action. And so, you know, it`s very simple, the steps that we have to take in order to codify Roe, in order to codify our voting rights, and so many other things that we all have been fighting for years, because we`re seeing for the first time in the first time in my lifetime, actually taking away a right from millions of women across this country. And the Court with \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  signal that this is just the start.\par \par \uc2 So what do we need to do in order to take the steps to get our rights back, to solidify the rights that we do have? And it`s really five steps. The first thing is we`ve got to organize and that`s what the DNC has been doing for well over a year and a half since I`ve been Chair, putting boots on the ground.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got to register voters. For the first time in 20 years, the DNC is registering voters across this country. We`ve got to mobilize those voters, then get them to vote. And then once they vote, our next step is we now have a larger Senate, if we can get at least two more senators in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , if we keep our House majority, if we pick up Governor`s Mansions on a Federal level and a State level, that January, we can begin to codify Roe, we can get voting rights done and approved in this country and push back against this radical, ultra MAGA right-wing attempt to take away our rights.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got to be just as relentless, Rev in terms of protecting freedom and protecting our rights as they are in terms of taking those rights away from us.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And we`ve got to speak to that on the ground to people so that people will understand that the Democrats are standing for something if they`re going to stand for that.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, the President called on Congress to end the Senate filibuster to pass abortion rights legislation into law, something we wanted them to do around the George Floyd Bill and the John Lewis Bill. He conceded yesterday that Democrats do not have the votes to do so, so, they need to deal with the filibuster law -- the filibuster rule.\par \par \uc2 And that`s in part because you have moderate Democrats in the Senate who continue to support the filibuster, despite the fact it blocks many Democratic priorities.\par \par \uc2 I`ve asked this question before, as we approach midterms and 2024, at what point does the party make the filibuster`s removal part of his platform and a determining factor in what candidates it ultimately tries to send to Washington?\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Well, we know that the leader of the Senate, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  has been pushing and pushing hard. He had a vote up in terms of the filibuster on voting rights. And right now the DSCC has been working hard in terms of the recruitment of candidates running for the Senate, and we`ve got some fantastic candidates, Rev, that I know we`ll do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 People like Cheri Beasley in North Carolina, Val Demings in Florida, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  in Ohio, Fetterman -- Lieutenant Governor Fetterman in Pennsylvania. We have some great candidates who are running hard and we know that when they get elected in those States, and we need to keep Warnock and \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  and Captain Kelly down in Arizona, when they win, we are going to be able to, you know go in January and codify Roe, make sure that the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Voting Rights Bill gets done and that`s why it`s so important.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:07]\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Folks have to understand this, Rev, you know fighting for democracy and protecting democracy is not something static. It`s not something you just do in one election. It took Republicans 50 years to really chip away at Roe and we`ve got to be just as relentless like we cannot give up. We can`t just take it because we voted in one election, then that`s it, I can take a break because the other side is not taking a break, and we can`t either.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: No, it`s a marathon race, and they took some losses in those 50 years, but they never stopped and they never turned on what they wanted.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Chairman, voter advocates are alarmed in Arizona. Let`s go there. They`re alarmed after the Supreme Court ruled this week, in a conservative majority, six to three opinion to uphold a pair of State Election Laws that have some critics have called discriminatory and in violation of the Voting Rights Act, with one law rejecting ballots cast in the wrong precinct, the other restricting anyone but family and caregivers from turning in another person`s mail-in ballots.\par \par \uc2 With the Court set to take up another controversial election case out of North Carolina, what`s your reaction to this ruling, Mr. Chairman, and how are Democrats handling all the new voter restrictions Republicans have put in place for this election cycle.\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Well, Rev, since I`ve been chair, we`ve been fighting hand in glove in the Court system to fight back against this. We even had a case in Arizona, where the DNC sued against the efforts that really hurt Native American communities and the wave in which they vote.\par \par \uc2 And you know, when you`re out on a reservation, you`re not close to the election precincts, and so you need folks to gather the ballots of elders in order to get them to the polls and make sure that their voices are heard, and so we are going to continue that.\par \par \uc2 We have put aside millions of dollars to fight in the Courts. But folks, bottom line is this -- and we have built the largest voter protection team that we have ever had at the DNC, and we started doing that last year, Rev.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got attorneys on the ground in all of our battleground states ready and willing to push back against these Republican efforts to strip away our right to vote.\par \par \uc2 But bottom line is this: The vote is the heart of this effort. And that`s why Republicans are taking these tactics because they want to diminish the vote. They understand that they can`t win on the merits of their argument. They can`t win on the merits of their policies.\par \par \uc2 And so, we know that we`ve got to get people and mobilize them and get them to the polls. That`s why we`ve got to get even more people registered to vote and that`s what we`re focused on right now because we need to make sure that we have the bodies in the Statehouses, in the Governor`s Mansions in the House and the Senate, in Washington, DC to codify the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Voting Rights Act. It is the most important thing that we can do between now and November.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: DNC Chair, \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 , thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 After the break, a war of words in Texas, an outrageous suggestion shows just how far some are willing to go to silence discussion of race in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 And abortion rights advocates fight back. Find out what is being done to mobilize voters after the Supreme Court`s reversal of Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague, Cori Coffin with today`s top news stories -- Cori.\par \par \uc2 CORI COFFIN, MSNBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Thanks, Rev.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the top stories we`re watching at this hour for you.\par \par \uc2 Right now we`re learning that the US has approved a package of more military aid for Ukraine of $820 million. That includes new surface-to-air missile systems and counter artillery radars to respond to Russia`s heavy reliance on long range strikes in the war.\par \par \uc2 Now, this comes as Russia launched dozens of missiles across Ukraine and pinned down Ukrainian forces with fire, sometimes lasting hours at a time.\par \par \uc2 Millions of Americans are set to travel for the Fourth of July holiday even with record high gas and inflation rates. Despite this, AAA predicts that car travel will set a record as an estimated 48 million people are expected to travel over the weekend, nearing pre-pandemic levels.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, flyers across the United States are experiencing hundreds of canceled flights and a few thousand delays on Friday, kicking off the holiday weekend. Now, the \uc2 TSA\uc2  also reporting a record number of airport screenings at almost two and a half million, the highest number since before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And Tropical Storm Colin has blasted through coastal South Carolina and is now headed to North Carolina shores, potentially impacting travel and celebrations this holiday weekend. According to the nation`s Hurricane Center, Colin`s heaviest rains and strongest winds were along the Carolinas Coast on Saturday morning, but those heavy rains could still be expected through the weekend.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:24:10]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in my political panel for their insight on today`s big topics.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined by Susan del Percio, Republican strategist and Juanita Tolliver, a Democratic strategist. They are both MSNBC political analysts.\par \par \uc2 Susan, let`s start with former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It`s hard for me to be shocked by anything about the former guy, but this week`s surprise testimony before the January 6 Committee describing Trump throwing plates in the White House and tussling with the Secret Service officers took things to a whole new level.\par \par \uc2 And some Republican insiders may have finally had enough. NBC News reporting saying "Though many Republicans aren`t watching it and aren`t likely to believe its findings, they say the hearings are still creating unwelcome pressure, leaving some MAGA donors to say albeit anonymously, that they are backing away from Trump."\par \par \uc2 Do you think the January 6 probe is hurting Trump in any way past scandals have not? Do you think this time it`s hurting him?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DEL PERCIO, MSNBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: I do think it`s hurting him not in a direct way, but rather that his supporters, the ones that you mentioned, who were one time supporters, they have had enough.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:42]\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: And there are certain things, yes, the throwing of plates, which is really easy to imagine Trump doing and maybe you know, the story of him trying to reach over and grab a Secret Service agent are really kind of salacious, but the fact that he knew that people were armed and going to the Capitol armed and he did nothing is something that really shakes people to the core.\par \par \uc2 So while I don`t think --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And not only did he not -- not only did he not do nothing, we are clear that he knew they were armed and he told the people to drop the mags. He told them to march over there knowing they were armed. He didn`t do not only do nothing, he told them to march over there and he would meet them there.\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: That`s absolutely right, Rev, and that`s why this testimony, especially on Tuesday for Miss Hutchinson is so important. Because right now, even the Republicans that say they`d vote for Trump, again, they`ve had enough.\par \par \uc2 And not only that, they`ve had enough of Trump talking about it. Every time there`s a hearing, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  fires off something to say, and he put himself back there and having a discussion that people don`t want to have.\par \par \uc2 So what it does is it makes people want to move on. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t want to move on, but his people do, so they will.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: So I think it hurts him in that regard, because he is stuck in 2020 and he isn`t going anywhere and people are looking to 2024.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Juanita, let me go to this issue. Yesterday, Florida`s Don`t Say Gay Bill took effect severely limiting what teachers can say about sexuality and gender to students in kindergarten through third grade. Some teachers even say they`ve even been asked to remove pictures of same sex spouses from their desks.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in Texas, we learned the State Board had pushed back -- had to push back on a proposal to slavery as "involuntary relocation." That`s what they said "involuntary relocation."\par \par \uc2 As educators try to conform to a new law that bans teaching subjects that would supposedly make students feel discomfort. How do Democrats push back on this culture war that is going on in our schools?\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, MSNBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Take it beyond calling it a culture war and name it for the erasure and complete discriminatory harm that it is, right, call out Republicans for this effort to erase LGBTQ people, call out Republicans for trying to erase Black history, American history that happened, right?\par \par \uc2 Like this full utter rejection of truth has become the premise for the GOP and everything that they`re doing and putting forward and so naming it for what it is, and then pushing back on it not only through elections, but organizing around these School Board hearings and meetings so that more and more people can speak up in real time, Rev, because we know that Republicans are going to keep doing this even if they are checked by the Courts, because we saw it with the book bans. We`re seeing it with the anti-LGBTQ legislation.\par \par \uc2 We`re seeing it also in the way these legislative pieces are being deputized or allowing parents and other people to be deputized as the reporting parties. Right?\par \par \uc2 So it`s not even state enforced. It opens it wide open with this Don`t Say Gay Bill for any parent who says, "Oh, my child is uncomfortable." And now they get to file a lawsuit against a teacher, a school or a School District; same as in Texas with SB 8, an anti-abortion legislation.\par \par \uc2 And so I think framing it for the public, as this is the Republican push to erase marginalized communities. This is the Republican push to harm marginalized communities and children and people just trying to live and teach. And also this is the Republican Party rejecting reality, yet again.\par \par \uc2 And so putting it in that package, I think is going to be a central midterm`s message for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And to bring, to say, let`s not talk about slavery. Let`s not talk about LGBTQ. Let`s not talk about women. I mean, we`re headed to let`s only talk about White male landowners, which --\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: Name it.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The country began that way.\par \par \uc2 Susan and Juanita, let me pose this to both of you and I want you to answer quickly if you could.\par \par \uc2 [17:30:05]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The legal battle to ban abortion in many States is underway now that the Supreme Court has overturned Roe versus Wade. This week, Judges in Florida and Kentucky blocked abortion bans from taking effect, but supporters of abortion rights face an uphill climb with bans and restrictions in at least 22 States where they`re going to be.\par \par \uc2 There`s been some debate about how far abortion opponents in the Republican Party will go, and whether they will be content with bans in Deep Red States, or will they push for it nationwide?\par \par \uc2 Susan, first your take, and then we go to you, Juanita.\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: There will be people who will push for nationwide bans should Republicans take over the House and the Senate, but President Biden will veto that. So that`s not going to be the biggest challenge.\par \par \uc2 If I were the Democrats right now, and I was trying to make abortion rights an issue, I would go into those Red States and really go after the legislature`s on rape, incest, and the life of the mother. Try and get a win, similar to the way we got to win on gun safety. You`ve got to start cracking it open a little bit, so you can continue to move forward.\par \par \uc2 If you show that there is an opening, there is more hope. And I think that will rally people together and bring your message that this is such extremism that Juanita was talking about, that not even rape, incest or life of the mother, can these people see fit to let a woman control her body.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Juanita?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: Look, Rev, I agree with you, Susan, to a degree as far as the small wins, but I think Democrats need to fight and go down swinging, if that`s how it`s going to pan out, because sure, I hear Democrats talking about well, we need two more Senate seats, go vote. But there are still actions that can be taken today and conversations that can be taken today. Right?\par \par \uc2 President Biden already talked about a carve out on filibuster, which you know, he doesn`t have the votes for, but making sure that women have protections to cross state lines to get care right now, making sure that the Hyde Amendment is addressed so that Federal funds can be used for that transportation, for that housing, and calling on an expansion of a Supreme Court that has made it explicitly clear that they`re going to go for other protections, and our basic autonomy and our basic privacy.\par \par \uc2 So expanding the Court should absolutely also be something that Democrats are talking about right now that we know they`re steering clear of, Rev. And so they need to stop operating from a place of fear and start talking through action. Action even if they lose, taking that action shows voters that you`re fighting for them and shows that you will keep that same energy when you get those two additional senators.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, and lose, I mean, if they`ve already taken women`s right to choose, they`ve already said out loud, they`re going after LGBTQ rights, voting rights, and other things that go back to a State rights model.\par \par \uc2 What do you have to lose if they`ve taken everything you need to fight to make sure they don`t cement what they`ve taken?\par \par \uc2 Juanita Tolliver, Susan del Percio, I`ll have to leave it there. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Still to come. How the Supreme Court`s reversal on Roe versus Wade threatens much more than just abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:37:32]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe versus Wade, a growing percentage of young women are citing abortion as motivation to vote in the 2022 midterms according to new polling conducted by all in together in Emerson College.\par \par \uc2 As midterm election season heats up, how does this outpouring of emotion for abortion rights translate into real political action?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to discuss that is Lauren Leader, founder and CEO of the nonprofit, All In Together. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Let me go right to it. President Biden said on Thursday that he would support suspending the Senate filibuster to codify Roe versus Wade into law. You recently wrote an op-ed for "Elle" Magazine, titled "Turning Grief and Rage into Power and Progress," where you write that, "We need a long game."\par \par \uc2 In codifying Roe, the kind of long game you are referring to or do you think Democrats also need to be getting more involved in State level fights on this issue?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN LEADER, FOUNDER AND CEO, ALL IN TOGETHER: Absolutely. All of the above.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, part of why we are where we are is because Republicans have taken this fight to every level of the political infrastructure over the course of 50 years. You know, really starting with the rise of \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  and the political power of the Christian right in the 80s, you know, this has been a steady drumbeat.\par \par \uc2 And what Republicans have done very successfully is to look at really every lever of power in our political process, not just the Court system, but also State politics. And at the State level, Democrats are underrepresented in State Legislatures, even in States that are supposedly all red, those States are still not representative.\par \par \uc2 As you and I`ve spoken about before, in States like Florida and Texas, Democrats didn`t even field candidates against a significant number of seats in those State Legislatures. That`s just an abdication of responsibility, and it`s just irresponsible.\par \par \uc2 So ultimately, look, part of what we found in our polls this week is that Democrats are really motivated. The coalition of voters that helped give Democrats the majority in 2018 and then the White House in 2020 is that coalition of Black voters, young voters, especially young women, Democratic women, and Independent women, and that coalition is going to be essential if Democrats are going to fight.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:19]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: But the challenge with that coalition is turnout and research from the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  suggest that Trump significantly improved his standing with women, specifically White women in 2020, as more women voted for him in the last election than they did in 2016. That with that in mind, how do you believe the Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe is going to affect voter mobilization against women for this year`s midterm elections?\par \par \uc2 Does this ruling change anything for the women who sided with Trump at higher numbers in 2020, than they did in 2016?\par \par \uc2 LEADER: I think that`s hard to know at this point. Our polls definitely show that in terms of like core Democratic voters, those reliable Democratic voters and even among Independent women and suburban women who sometimes swing at the margins, that this is an intensely galvanizing issue.\par \par \uc2 We polled voters in September, pre Dobbs and then we polled them again this week. And among young women, it was a 26-point increase in the intensity of the motivation to vote in the midterms because of this issue.\par \par \uc2 You know, ultimately, Democrats do tend to wind up winning on small margins and that incremental increase in turnout, especially among younger women, could brunt what has been the advantage that Trump has had among White women and particularly White Republican women.\par \par \uc2 So that turnout is potentially the game changer here in the midterms, and we`re also seeing this sort of intensity of voting for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Another measure, Rev, in the first 24 hours after the Dobbs decision, Act Blue, which is the main Democratic fundraising site raised $20 million in 24 hours. Meaning, for this question, this is galvanizing.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, Lauren Leader, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Just ahead, the high costs of inflation for Black communities and what can be done to address the issue.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:45:29]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As we continue to cover the Supreme Court`s decision on abortion and the January 6 Committee hearings, I want to remind viewers not to forget about Brittney Griner, the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star who is still incarcerated in Russia after marijuana products were allegedly found in her luggage at an airport in Moscow. Her trial began on Friday.\par \par \uc2 She is facing possible 10 years in prison. The State Department said last month that Griner is being "wrongly detained," and they`re working to get her released.\par \par \uc2 I am one of many Americans who are deeply concerned about the fate of this young woman and the slow pace of efforts to bring her home.\par \par \uc2 I interviewed her wife, Brittney`s wife, Cherelle on my radio show this past Wednesday, and she had this to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHERELLE GRINER, WIFE OF BRITTNEY GRINER: That public pressure, you know, to let them know that, you know, Brittney matters. You know, we`re never going to shut up about this until she is back.\par \par \uc2 We`re never going to allow them to take their precious time because every second that goes by, BG is struggling.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: We cannot allow the case of Brittney Griner, for her to be used as some prop by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . We want to see her released. We want this \uc2 United States government\uc2  to do what it can and there should be no swapping deals that does not include Brittney Griner.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to stay on this. Many of us in the Civil Rights and faith communities will be having a lot more to say in the next few days about freeing Brittney Griner.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:51:26]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. Inflation is at a 40-year high and many Black and Brown Americans are feeling squeezed.\par \par \uc2 A report from the \uc2 New York Fed\uc2  finds these communities are being hardest hit by rising costs for just about everything The study goes on to warn the economic ripple effects could erase recent gains that have been made in reducing the racial employment gap.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Alphonso David, President and CEO of the Global Black Economic Forum, and former Chief of the Civil Rights Bureau of the New York State Office of Attorney General.\par \par \uc2 Alphonso, let`s start with the basics. Why does inflation hit Black and Brown communities so hard? And why would efforts to fix it lead to job losses for some vulnerable communities?\par \par \uc2 ALPHONSO DAVID, PRESIDENT AND CEO, GLOBAL BLACK ECONOMIC FORUM: Well, Rev, first, thank you. Thank you for having me on your show. It`s a pleasure to be here.\par \par \uc2 If you see the background, I`m currently at the Essence Festival of Culture, where I just had the privilege of introducing a segment where the Vice President of the United States is speaking to a large group of people about issues that are affecting Black and Brown communities all across this country.\par \par \uc2 Now, as it relates to inflation, I think you`re absolutely right. Like most economic downturns, including the Great Recession or the pandemic, Black communities are disproportionately impacted. What this means is that Black communities are spending more money on basic necessities, such as food and lodging, and energy, and transportation. And it`s not just about an increase in gas prices. It`s also an increase in rent and an increase in the cost of food.\par \par \uc2 Now, your question is, so why are Black and Brown folks spending more? It`s really because of the wealth gap. The fundamental reason why inflation is disproportionately harmful to Black communities is what we call the wealth gap.\par \par \uc2 There are studies that show that white Households have more than $800,000.00 more in net worth than Black households. And this problem requires really creative solutions.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, POLITICO reports a July Fourth cookout is expected to be 17 percent more expensive than last year according to a recent survey. President Biden this week argued that large price increases in the United States are part of a global problem.\par \par \uc2 Do you agree with the President? And if so, what are the right steps to deal with it if have a group that`s really dealing with this?\par \par \uc2 DAVID: Yes. So I just recently took on as of this weekend, the role of President and CEO of the Global Black Economic Forum and what we`re doing is really looking at systemic problems that are affecting the Black diaspora.\par \par \uc2 So we`re working with business executives, we`re working with policymakers. We`re working with entrepreneurs and activists and consumers globally on thought leadership on corporate and entrepreneurial career development on advancing social and economic justice for marginalized communities.\par \par \uc2 We have a global academy for advancing excellence. We recently acquired the Center for Workforce Excellence, and that is a company that has been in business for more than 20 years, providing consulting, development programs, and executive coaching. It`s a multimillion dollar company and we just acquired it.\par \par \uc2 You can find out more about what we`re doing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.gbef.org"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.gbef.org}}}\uc2  and it is not one solution. There are multiple solutions that we need to implement here because we`re dealing with systemic problems.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:10]\par \par \uc2 DAVID: We`re talking about redlining practices and we`re also talking about barriers to access to capital.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, as we talk about Black communities worldwide, my thoughts turn to our nearby neighbors in Haiti, the island nation that has been devastated by economic problems and political upheaval, causing many to flee, including the young and educated that represent the country`s future.\par \par \uc2 I myself have met with Haitians trying to escape to this country seeking a better life and learned about the harsh treatment in the hands of our Border Patrol. What are your thoughts about this situation since it is global, this group you now head?\par \par \uc2 DAVID: I mean, Rev, the point that you`re making, unfortunately, is something that`s not unique to Haiti. We only have to look at what we recently saw on Ukraine, where we saw Black people that were being treated differently, even though they had lived in the Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And so we`re seeing the same thing with Haiti, where our immigration policies, unfortunately, are at them, discriminatory. And so we have to not only look to see that our policies are on paper, fair, but we also have to make sure that they`re being implemented in a fair way.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Alphonso David, thank you for joining us tonight. We`ll be watching this group as it fights these economic issues.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern. We`ve got a few special guests tomorrow evening, Representative and Majority Whip, \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  joins us, plus, we will welcome Democratic political consultant, James Carville. Again, that`s Sunday at 5:00 PM.\par \par \uc2 "AMERICAN VOICES" with Alicia Menendez will return next weekend, but next is a re-air of this week`s J6 -- January 6 hearing featuring Cassidy Hutchinson in its entirety.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:54]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220703ei7200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for July 1, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Alicia Menendez \par \uc2 7,937 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JASON JOHNSON, MSNBC HOST: And that`s tonight`s "LAST WORD". I`m Jason Johnson. Thanks for watching. Have a good holiday weekend. THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts right now.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, the mob like mentality of a former president, and the alarming new details of potential witness intimidation sent from Trump`s allies. Then the growing confusion over different states abortion laws, what the White House is planning to do to try to protect reproductive rights across the country.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a reflection on the week that changed America. One of our favorite presidential historians, who says we won`t forget where we were on these critical days. As THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, I`m Alicia Menendez in for Stephanie Ruhle. Do the right thing, that reportedly was part of the message sent to Trump White House Aide Cassidy Hutchinson ahead of her questioning by the January 6 committee.\par \par \uc2 NBC News has confirmed she received at least one of the messages shown in Tuesday`s explosive hearing as evidence of potential witness intimidation. Again, here`s a reminder of what it said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R) WYOMING GOP CONFERENCE CHAIR: This is a call received by one of our witnesses. "A person let me know you have your deposition tomorrow. He wants me to let you know he`s thinking about you. He knows you`re loyal. And you`re going to do the right thing when you go in for your deposition."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: According to a source familiar with Hutchinson`s deposition, that person reference there is her former boss, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 . A spokesperson for Trump`s former White House Chief of Staff says no one from Meadows camp himself or otherwise has ever attempted to intimidate or shape Ms. Hutchinson`s testimony to the committee.\par \par \uc2 It is worth remembering Meadows has yet to appear before the panel despite being subpoenaed, and the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  notes, this may all be part of a trend, writing, "Evidence across multiple state federal and congressional investigations points to a similar pattern. Trump and his close allies privately shower potential witnesses with flattery and attention, extending vague assurances that staying loyal to Trump would be better than crossing him."\par \par \uc2 While some on the right are challenging the veracity of Hutchinson`s testimony. There`s more reporting tonight that says Trump did angrily demand to go to the U.S. Capitol on January 6.\par \par \uc2 Two Secret Service Sources tell CNN that he berated his protective detail when he didn`t get his way. Stories about the incident reportedly circulated in the months following the attack and before Hutchinson`s testimony. Those sources say they heard about it from multiple agents, including the driver of the presidential SUV, and say the details were similar to how Hutchinson described it.\par \par \uc2 As for what`s ahead, next Wednesday is the deadline for Trump`s former White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  to testify before the committee. He is seen as a key January 6 witness, one who allegedly and repeatedly resisted Trump`s efforts to overturn the 2020 elections.\par \par \uc2 NBC news reports Cipollone will probably agree to a transcribed interview limited to specific topics to avoid any privilege issues.\par \par \uc2 The same time, the former guy is facing even more legal pressure outside of the January 6 investigation. Trump`s media company has now been subpoenaed by a federal grand jury in connection to a criminal probe. The company was also subpoenaed from the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  in a civil probe.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel. Peter Baker, Chief White House Correspondent for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , Luke Broadwater, Pulitzer Prize Winning Congressional Reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . And Barbara McQuade, a Veteran Federal Prosecutor and former U.S. Attorney for the Eastern District of Michigan. She worked with the DOJ during the Biden transition, and is Professor University of Michigan`s Law School.\par \par \uc2 It is good to see you all. Barbara, I`m going to start with you. Here is how the Select Committee`s former adviser summed up the story now surrounding Cassidy Hutchinson. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FORMER REP. DENVER RIGGLEMAN, FORMER ADVISER TO JAN. 6TH COMMITTEE: Seeing the attacks that are hitting her right now, the fact that she was getting text messages or phone calls to try to persuade her to do the right thing, it just shows you, you know, what that ecosystem looks like, and the kind of pressure they can put to you if they say hey, you know, I said it before, as sort of the godfather type of negotiation, you know, we`re going to, you know, you know, make -- give, what does it give you a deal, you can`t refuse or whatever and -- but if you don`t do that, they`re going to come after you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Barb, putting aside his horrible paraphrasing of that iconic line from the godfather, does this pressure help build a case for witness tampering?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Absolutely, you know, I think that the criminals are often more sophisticated than to come right out and say don`t say bad things about me, don`t tell the truth. They say it in more guarded terms like this, you know, don`t forget whose loyalty you belong to, don`t forget whose team you`re on, those kinds of things.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:18]\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, standing alone, I don`t think it`s enough. But I think if you put it together with other comments, other statements, there`s certainly a motive to try to persuade her. It can be the stuff of a criminal case, and it`s a serious crime. The statute provides for up to 20 years in prison. Sometimes people dismiss these as mere process crimes. But I`ll tell you what, prosecutors and judges consider these very serious crimes. Because lying to juries, lying to fact finders is what works investigators from getting to the truth.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Luke, you reported on Trump`s political organization, his allies helping with legal fees for witnesses. There`s nothing illegal about that, so why is it getting so much attention here?\par \par \uc2 LUKE BROADWATER, THE NEW YORK TIMES CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER: Well, Cassidy Hutchinson has cited this as one reason why she felt she was able to speak more freely before the select committee. When she first was subpoenaed by the Select Committee. She needed a lawyer and so she went and got a recommendation from people in the Trump orbit. And they set her up with an attorney and they -- the Trump`s PAC paid for the legal fees. But through the course of her testimony, she felt that she was constrained and so she`s through a friend \uc2 Alyssa Farah\uc2  got in touch with \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , the Vice Chair of the Committee, and set up a fourth interview. And at this fourth interview, she parted ways with her previous attorney and got an independent attorney.\par \par \uc2 And it was at that interview that she felt not constrained anymore and give a much fuller account of everything that she says she witnessed, and then agreed to testify publicly about it. And so this highlighted an arrangement where more than a dozen witnesses are having their legal fees paid for by the Trump PAC, or a Trump aligned PAC. And so, if Cassidy Hutchinson parted ways, and then talked much more openly about what she witnessed, are there other witnesses in that same situation? And so it raises more questions about this arrangement with the Trump PACs paying for these legal fees.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Barbara, we`ve talked about the PAC. We`ve talked about \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , we`ve talked about other allies, from what we know now, could all of this be linked directly to Trump himself, and add his own legal jeopardy?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Well, it could but, of course, you`d have to show that he had the knowledge that these statements were being made and that he intended for them to be made.\par \par \uc2 One of the reasons it`s often so hard to get to the person who`s at the top, whether it`s a corporate CEO or a mob boss is because the person insulates themselves from the dirty work, they don`t get their hands dirty, they have other people make these statements and do these things. So the question would be whether he himself was involved in issuing these statements and whether he intended to use them to keep people quiet.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Peter, tonight`s reporting from CNN that secret service insiders, they are backing much of what Cassidy Hutchinson testified about this week, about that encounter inside Trump`s SUV that she heard others describe. Does all of that dilute some of the pushback that we have heard from the right about the veracity of her testimony?\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, THE NEW YORK TIMES CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, let`s remember the pushback, first of all, is because they find her to be a credible and powerful witness, right? There`s a reason why they`re attacking her so much. Secondly, I think it`s important remember, she wasn`t pretending or saying to anybody that she witnessed this. She was very careful about what she said. She told us, the country that this is what she was told by Tony Ornato, who`s a deputy chief of staff and a former Secret Service agent for President Trump. She wasn`t pretending to know what actually happened in the vehicle that Tony Ornato told her something that turned out not to be the full version of what happened the vehicle, that doesn`t speak to her credibility.\par \par \uc2 But the -- I think, it`s really interesting to know is what you just said, which is that nobody is questioning the important part and it`d be taken (inaudible). They wouldn`t take him, and then he was angry at them for not faking it to, that tells us something important, which is that he would determine the politically lead, the crowd that he knew, was armed and potentially violent to the Capitol himself. It was not just a throwaway line in speeches, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  tried to say in his book, he himself wanted to go to the Capitol, burst into the to the building where they were counting the votes against them and somehow disrupt that transfer of power. And it was a Secret Service that wouldn`t let them do it, not because they disagree with politics or policy, but because they thought it completely unsafe. But that`s the most important thing that Cassidy Hutchinson testimony told us about that episode.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Luke, Peter just referenced Tony Ornato, you have new reporting about him. I mean, just to remind our audience he left the Secret Service to become a White House Deputy Chief of Staff, a move you do not ordinarily see. What else do we need to know about Ornato?\par \par \uc2 [12:10:03]\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: Well, we`re at -- because privately people close to Tony Ornato have let it be known that he disagrees with Cassidy Hutchinson`s account of this conversation that they had describing what happened in the SUV. We now have sort of two people who are pitted against each other in terms of what they have said and who`s more credible. And what all I`ve heard all week from the select committee is that Tony Ornato`s testimony was seen as less credible to them, was less consistent. There were two different interviews. There were problems they felt with his testimony where they didn`t feel that he was forthcoming or fully forthcoming. And they felt that Cassidy Hutchinson had more corroboration with what she had to say.\par \par \uc2 So I expect we`ll hear a lot more about this. We may even have some more witnesses come forward and testify publicly about these events. But the Select Committee having interviewed all the people involved at different points, did feel that Cassidy Hutchinson, was the most credible and felt very comfortable putting her out there publicly for the whole world to see.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Barbara, there are signs that \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  may agree to a transcribed interview limited to specific topics, can you talk us through the mechanics of a transcribed interview? And how much that could impact then what he has to offer the committee?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Yes, I think because of some genuine privileges, that he may still have. Attorney client privilege, even executive privilege. There are some areas that you probably can`t talk about. But there`s certainly plenty of other areas that he can. Of course, he`s not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the man`s lawyer, he is a lawyer for the President of the United States. And so to the extent that those positions diverged, his legal skills were being representing the presidency. And so I think they will want to carve out, these are the areas we`re going to talk about, these are the areas we`re not. And of course, if any third parties will present at the time of these conversations, then any privilege would be waived. So if \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  is present, or \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2  or anybody else is not subject to the privilege, then that waves as well. So I think they`ll get there. I think they just want to carve out which areas they`re going to talk about, and focus on the ones that matter here. But it seems that you`re talking about things like whether he was going to go to the Capitol and why he believed that they would be charged with every crime imaginable if that happened, I think all of that is fair game.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: So Peter, as all of this is ramping up, your paper reports tonight, the Trump may push up an announcement about running again, in part to blunt damaging revelations from the January 6 investigation, apparently, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , all four and others in the GOP are not, what more do we know?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yeah, I think that`s exactly right. And I do think it says something about their evaluation of the impact of this testimony and the impact of these hearings. The President`s trying to sort of change the topic and effect. And I think that it`s also perhaps a way of, you know, deflecting some of this conversation about whether or not, the former president should be charged to the crime. The dynamics, at least on a political level kind of change a little bit, we`re talking about charging somebody who`s currently running for president of United States, as opposed to simply a former president, which is already unusual. In fact, is unique enough. So that`s, that`s another factor, I think in there.\par \par \uc2 And it`s -- and it`s one way of keeping Trump in charge of the Republican Party at a time when there is some fear among his borders of fatigue factor that people will say, yeah, I`m still for Trump. I like him, I want to do the things he wants to do. But I`m so tired of relitigating the last election, maybe it`s time to look at somebody else. If he gets out in front, because I`m running again, he captures that sort of momentum, he hopes at least, and wants to stay at the top of that party. So I think this is a way of keeping him out in front. But it does complicate the party at a time when he`s trying to win these midterm elections, win his back is probably the front runners for the House elections anyway, in the fall. And then a lot of Republicans are concerned about distracting him up.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Yeah, I mean, Luke, speaking to that fear and that complication, NBC reports, Trump`s fear factor among Republicans may be waning. You have former allies testing the waters for a possible presidential bid, your sense of how vulnerable he is?\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: Well, you know, there`s sort of a game on Capitol Hill where you`re always judging, is this going to be the moment when the Republicans break with Trump? Is that going to be the moment and after outrage, after outrage, the moment never comes? And so maybe it will be this time, but I`ll believe it when I see it.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Yeah, I mean Peter, this is the end of a very busy weekend. So I have two questions for you. One, how big of a deal was it? You had Biden`s comments yesterday supporting a carve out for the filibuster to protect abortion rights? What are you going to be looking for after the July 4 holiday?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yeah, I think it`s a big deal in that sense, it says how sensitive president is to the criticism he`s hearing from the left and his own party he`s not being aggressive enough to counter the decision by the Supreme Court to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:09]\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot of angst inside the party, a lot of consternation, a lot of feeling that the President should be more aggressive. Maybe he should do something even like packing the court with more justices or something like that. That`s not something President Biden is likely to do. So instead, he`s talking about getting rid of the filibuster, which is, you know, a pretty big deal for him because he`s a respecter of the institution and not very easily convinced to try to dispense with things like the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, they don`t have the votes to actually do it. So it`s kind of a theoretical conversation at this point. So in effect, what he`s doing is setting the stage for the fall election saying to voters, Democratic voters, if you really want to do this, if you want to get rid of the filibuster, you`d have to give me a couple of more seats in the Senate, that they have to be two seats. They don`t need to worry so much about Joe Manchin, they could possibly still, you know, overcome the filibuster and then codify Roe v. Wade, of course, you`re going to have to win the House, which is pretty problematic at this point, anyway.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Peter Baker, Luke Broadwater, Barbara McQuade, thank you all so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, one of the governors who talked with the President today about protecting the right to choose. Washington State`s \uc2 Jay Inslee\uc2  joins us next.\par \par \uc2 And later, after recent tumultuous events in the nation`s capital, one noted presidential historian warns, we now live in a different society than we did just 10 days ago. Michael Beschloss will tell us he needs. The 11th Hour just getting underway on Friday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:21:08]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2 , (D) NEW YORK: Mr. President, we`d ask that you consider your ability to use federal facilities. What am I talking about? Veterans, hospitals, military bases, and other places where the federal government controls the jurisdiction in some of the states that are hostile to women`s rights, and make sure that those services can be available to other women. So those are just a few of the ideas that we encourage you to look at.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Exactly one week after the Supreme Court`s reversal of Roe vs. Wade, the President got the chance today to hear the concerns of nine Democratic governors and states where abortion rights are protected. For now, we`re pleased to welcome one of those governors \uc2 Jay Inslee\uc2  of Washington State.\par \par \uc2 Governor, thank you so much for taking the time to be with us. What can you tell us about the meeting today?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 JAY INSLEE\uc2 , (D) WASHINGTON: That was a good meeting. The President`s very engaged in this. He obviously has given deep thought to it. I was really gratified to hear what he had to say obviously stand up to eliminate the filibuster in this consideration. So we can have a statutory protection at some point when we got a couple more votes in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 But more importantly, short term, he was very open to our ideas that we suggested, as you know, we formed a sanctuary in our state and across the west coast and other state, Democratic states as well. And so we talked -- what we talked most about is how can the federal government do the best job to partner with us, to ensure those sanctuaries are effective. So we suggested several ideas to him to make sure that women can be protected around the country to work with our state`s either to come to our states, to have these services provided, or to have pharmaceutical medicine provided to them from our states, so that they can receive this medicine to exercise right of choice without leaving their states.\par \par \uc2 And I actually think that may end up being the most effective solution to this problem. And he`s very open to considering, this has been suggested. The \uc2 FDA\uc2 , making it very clear that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has made these medicines, pharmaceutical products legal, safe and authorized in this out of pre- empting states trying to take away this right of pharmaceutical abortion.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I mean, Governor, to your point, you have already taken an aggressive approach in your own state seeking an abortion rights amendment to the state`s constitution. You`re prohibiting the \uc2 Washington State Patrol\uc2  from aiding abortion investigations. And so my question is, as these vigilante laws continue to take hold our steps like this going to be pro- choice, governor`s best line of defense, what more can you do from your purchase, Governor?\par \par \uc2 INSLEE: Well, one of the things we -- I talked to the President about is, I`m hopeful that he would issue an executive order to prevent the law enforcement, federal law enforcement authorities, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , Homeland Security, ATF, any of their databases or their investigatory systems to be used by governments or these vigilante groups.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, those databases are very powerful. He seemed open to that suggestion. I do think this is an important aspect to not allow the databases which are extensive, as we know the federal government to be used by the prosecutors or investigators of the states that want to take this right away from women. I do think that`s an important thing.\par \par \uc2 We also, I hope, that he will jawbone and talk with the data providers in private enterprise to prevent their databases, which are extensive being -- to being used and accessible to the states. Those are important things as well. And obviously, we`re all going to pass, I believe, measures to prevent to the extent humanly possible, extraditions or civil lawsuits against people come to our states. So there`s quite a number of things we can do. And I feel good after talking to the President that he`s going to be open to as aggressive a position on this as possible.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:02]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Governor, recent polling finds the Roe decision has women more interested in voting this November, especially Democratic women, I wonder what you believe the Democrats at the state level, at the federal level need to be doing in order to earn voters confidence on this issue?\par \par \uc2 INSLEE: I`m not concerned about that. We will do have the voters confidence on this. That`s why I believe we`re going to do better than some people think. Because people know very fundamentally, there`s a basic difference between the Democratic and Republican candidates all across the United States, whether it`s legislative or Congress, or governors, particularly governors that are so important in this matrix right now.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Party is on this obsessive movement to take away this fundamental right of women. I`m telling him people are irate about this. And all we need to do is to encourage them to channel that anger. And by the way, it`s not just women, I think people misunderstand this. We`ve actually had some -- if I can share secrets with some focus groups that show men were angry about this as women, that may surprise people. And so there is a huge amount of anger about one of the first times in American history where we`ve gone backwards, where the Supreme Court has actually stripped Americans of a liberty interest. And this fundamentally, is a liberty interest.\par \par \uc2 So what we can do is just suggest people to don`t get hangry, get active and vote. And I believe people are going to be motivated to vote. And I believe they`re going to be motivated for the only party who stand up for this liberty interest. And not just Democrats. You mentioned Democrats, Republican, people understand that their party has been stolen from them. This is no longer the party of, you know, my predecessor in Congress 50 years ago led us to be the first state in the union to win the right by popular vote the right choice. Those Republicans are extinct. People get this, they`re going to vote on this.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Governor, I have about a minute left. But I do want to ask you, even as you declare Washington, a safe haven for abortion, you have other states in close proximity to you that still have pending abortion bans. When you talk to providers in your state, what is it you were hearing from them about what they need in order to meet what is going to be a forthcoming increased demand?\par \par \uc2 INSLEE: Well, they need the things that we talked about with the President today. They need -- we need access to personnel and financing to accommodate the 1000s of women that will be coming to our state. We`ve just released a million dollars from our emergency fund, we`d already done seven and a half million last biennium.\par \par \uc2 We talked to the President today about potentially increasing the health distribution of federal funds to our states to help with all kinds of reproductive health, including contraception services, and then he can -- the federal government can help on that, even if we -- if there is a Hyde Amendment because it can help our state`s, our health costs. We need to protect women`s right to not lose their privacy. We need to hire more people to actually do this work in the services. We need to enable our telemedicine system which is extremely effective and safe. So that a woman in one state can communicate with a physician and get a prescription for these pharmaceutical measures that we know that are totally authorized by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 . And I believe one of the most important things we`re going to need to do is to make it clear that these pharmaceuticals, the law of the federal government, in allowing them to be used safely preempts these efforts of Republicans to take away this right in the states.\par \par \uc2 And so we believe that there is a good chance that women will be able to access physicians by telemedicine in our state, stay in their states and receive these pharmaceuticals. And I believe that we need to be aggressive in litigation to make that clear. We talked about the president. His team is evaluating that potential prospect right now, and that`s important.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Governor \uc2 Jay Inslee\uc2 , thank you so much for your time tonight.\par \par \uc2 INSLEE: Thank you. Be well.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Coming up, politicians running on or against the big lie, now having to explain themselves to voters. Tim Miller, who knows a bit about Republican campaigns tells us how that is going. When THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:34:08]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIET HAGEMAN, WYOMING REPUBLICAN CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: The threat to our republic really comes from other sources, including the fact that right now we`re seeing that we have two different systems of justice in this country, where we have one system of justice, where you have people like \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  or \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , or even \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who are not accountable or held accountable for the decisions in the bad acts that they undertake.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: The Republican primary voters in Wyoming are witnessing the party split in real time. Several candidates including natural resources attorney Harriet Hageman are running against January 6, the Vice Chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: I will never put party above my duty to the country. I will never put party above my duty to the Constitution. I swore an oath under God and I will abide by that oath. I won`t say something that I know is wrong simply to earn the votes of people to earn political support. That`s what the voters of Wyoming deserve.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [23:35:00]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Let`s bring in Tim Miller, a contributor to the Bulwark and the former Communications Director for Jeb Bush. His new book, Why We Did It: A Travelogue from the Republican Road to Hell, was just released this week.\par \par \uc2 Tim, my friend, congratulations. It`s a big deal to finally have the book out in the world. I got to tell you, listening to Hageman, it sounds a little bit like opposite day or life in the upside down where she started talking about two different systems of justice. And I thought, you know, I think we all -- those of us who are tethered to reality thought we knew where she was headed, and then took that detour. So my question to you is, can Cheney`s principle, reality based stance, save her in Wyoming?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, THE BULWARK CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah, thank you, Alicia. I think that Republican Road in my book subtitle is leading to that Wyoming -- or that Wyoming House debate last night, because boy, I don`t know what is happening that then it is the upside down and the cowboy state.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think that \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  has a realistic path to victory, unfortunately. I`m happy that she`s fighting the good fight. I would love to be surprised. The numbers that I`ve seen, from, you know, both what as public as well, as friends, were working on the race, you know, show her behind quite a bit. And it`s hard to know. Yeah, you know, what she could do that would help turn it around. I mean, the reality is, if you watch that entire debate, which unfortunately, I suffered through, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  sounds like a conventional Republican -- from conservative Republican from 2014 on every issue except for two, whether or not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should have tried a coup. She`s against that, all the other candidates on states are for it. And whether or not we should have been vaccinating people for COVID-19. She was for that, everybody else dabbled in some kind of conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 So what do you do if you`re in that situation of \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 ? You`re only losing because, you know, you didn`t want the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  autocracy, you didn`t want to end our democracy and you will -- and you believe in basic science to save people`s lives. If those are the reasons, she`s going to go down. I mean, at least he`s going down for something good that says really troubling things about the voters who are participating in these primaries.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: And you talk about this exact kind of split in your book, on the very first page, "America never would have gotten into this mess if it weren`t for me and my friends. We were the normal Republicans, the pragmatic and practical. The adults in the room you hear so much about." I mean, do Republican voters even want a so-called adults in the room anymore?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: No, they don`t. And I think the people that I`m talking to in that book are Harriet Hageman, her consultants, as a woman that`s running against \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , are the so called people that were on Team normal. You remember in the January 6 hearing where Bill Stepien said, I was on team normal, it was Rudy, those the crazy ones. This has been happening time and time again. And I go over it in the book for a decade, where people who -- in some cases were well intentioned Republicans, who were just trying to deal with the fact that the voters wanted them to support crazy things and other cases, in most cases, people were not well intentioned, they were just trying to take advantage and trying to win and trying to play the game. But what they did was people who knew better, people who knew that the vaccine jumped with microchips and bodies, people who knew that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost a legitimate election in 2020. They play footsie with the conspiracy nonsense with the extremism stuff, because that`s what they think they need to do to win.\par \par \uc2 And over time, these "normal people," the people who know better have been co-opted by the extremists. And it is very clear that that has happened right now. In 2012, this has been going on for a minimum. I cover 14 years in my book, I`m sure some people have been around longer would say it`s been going on longer than that, but it`s been going on for a decade and a half now. And yet, the vast majority of my former colleagues continue to play the same losing game, you know, where they are complicit in the extremism, in the anti-democratic actions of people like Harriet Hageman, and the former president.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: To your point about extremism, and I want to make sure we get to this because I thought it was some of the most compelling material in the book. You have Florida`s don`t say gay law going into effect today. Here`s what a Florida kindergarten teacher had to say this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CORY BERNAERT, FLORIDA KINDERGARTEN TEACHER: I`m scared for myself. But I also know that I have to stay true to who I am. And I just hope that other LGBTQ plus teachers can do the same, and that they have supportive staff, supportive colleagues, supportive supervisors, that administration and school colleagues that will be behind them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Tim, I watched you shake your head throughout that. You write, and why we did it a travelogue from the Republican Road to how about coming to terms with your own identity as a gay Republican. How does it feel to watch the party continue to push legislation like this?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yeah, to go back to it after we thought we`d won that. You know, look, I reflect on that. I have some regrets about my work in the past for Republican candidates or anti-gay. And you can read the blog, going to see how I grappled with all of that.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:10]\par \par \uc2 But to think that this was something that we felt like we had made progress on and why. And now to just have this vicious backsliding. And what is happening in Florida is less like what was happening in my career and more like 1978. This is like Briggs Initiative, old school, anti-gay, going after gay teachers. And it is just insane. And the bill -- and there was no need, this bill serves no purpose. This new law serves no purpose, except to demonize and to tear down gay teachers and kids in school who come from, you know, LGBT families like mine. And it was written in such a way that any angry MAGA parents can now sue a school and threaten a teacher. And so you have people walking on eggshells around these schools. And so anytime you want to defend it and say, well, you know, this isn`t exactly in the bill, but the way that this bill is written, now, there`s a good reason for teachers, principals, to be scared to acknowledge gay families, kids that are dealing with their own sexuality and gender identity, and it`s going to cause a lot of pain and a lot of harm. And it serves no purpose except for to advance \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , the short term political game.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Again, the new book, Why We Did It: A Travelogue from the Republican Road to Hell. Tim Miller, congratulations and thank you.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Coming up, our next guest says it is the week that literally changed America. Presidential Historian Michael Beschloss is here to weigh in on the landmark Supreme Court rulings and this week`s stunning testimony and the January 6 investigation, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:46:30]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: The Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe vs. Wade was just one of several significant rulings that have changed our country in recent days. For more, let`s welcome celebrated author and Presidential Historian Michael Beschloss. His latest work in a bookshelf full of works is, President of War.\par \par \uc2 Michael, always good to see you. And I want to start with your reaction to --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BESCHLOSS, NBC NEWS PRESIDENTIAL HISTORIAN: Oh, great to see you, Alicia.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: -- all these \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  rulings, this was a huge week, how different is our country today from where we were 10 days ago?\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: Well, 10 days ago, as you will know, there were a lot of people were saying, yes, there`s a conservative new majority on the Supreme Court, but they`ll probably tread cautiously. Anything they do against Roe v. Wade, for instance, will probably be incremental. Same with other things. That is not what`s happened.\par \par \uc2 You know, a lot of people were worried about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  over these last seven years. One of the things that were worried about from the White House was tyranny on behalf of a group of policies that only a minority of Americans support. Well, last week, we`ve seen exactly that same kind of tyranny, now from the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 You know, it`s possible for the Supreme Court to come up with a momentous decision like Brown versus Board of Education, desegregating American society 1954, and say, this is a big social change. Some people may disagree, we`re going to do it in a way that`s kindly and try to bring everyone together. That`s not the tone of the decisions we saw on the last week. Roe v. Wade, and also on gun safety, and also the ruling on restraining the \uc2 EPA\uc2 , that was not a hand on our shoulder in a friendly way. That was a slap across our face. It`s almost as if they`ve arrived in town, almost like bullies and said, you`d better now reckon with us. We`ve got the power, we`re going to do what we want, even though we`re not in touch with a majority of Americans.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: So Michael, let me ask you historically, what has happened after decisions like this? How do Americans typically react?\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: They react in two ways. Number one, it can divide the country almost to the point of civil war. That`s what happened in 1857, the Supreme Court did the Dred Scott decision saying basically black Americans are not people. And if you don`t like slavery, deal with it, it`s going to be here forever. That led directly to a civil war four years later.\par \par \uc2 Mid 1930s, the Supreme Court said to \uc2 Franklin Roosevelt\uc2  in the New Deal, you can`t get so involved in, you know, trying to regulate the economy and other areas of private life and struck down a lot of the reforms that Roosevelt had done. A lot of people were angry. This country was very unsettled. There were demagogues in the late 1930s. I think there`s a very good chance we`re going to see all this again, especially because something they didn`t decide this week, the Supreme Court, but they did ominously announce, and that is, they`re going to take on a case during the next year, that may give the right to decide presidential elections much more strongly to state legislatures.\par \par \uc2 There`s a possibility that -- I hate to say this, Alicia, there`s a possibility that we may have seen our last fair and free presidential election to the United States, I hope not.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Absolutely devastating. I want to play some sound of Cassidy Hutchinson describing the form President`s reaction to Bill Barr`s public statement that there was no election fraud.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:06]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER SPECIAL ASSISTANT TO THE PRESIDENT: There`s catch up dripping down the wall. And there`s a shattered porcelain plate on the floor. The valet had articulated that the President was extremely angry at the Attorney General`s AP interview and had thrown his lunch against the wall.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: As someone who has studied the White House so closely, how does Donald Trump`s behavior compared to other presidents throughout our nation`s history?\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: Every other president is in one category. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in the other. We`re hearing our brave witness Ms. Hutchinson`s testimony saying that this is a president who tried to get his attorney general to lie and say that he really won the election, that Trump did. It`s not his attorney general. It`s our Attorney General. He had no right to do that. Almost every president knows that. But above and beyond that what we heard, even reinforced, expanded from her testimony is, this is the only president in American history who has sought to unleash violence against the Congress on the Capitol, to take down our democracy and the peaceful transfer of power that goes so far beyond what any other president history has ever done. I do hope that we never see it again. I`m not sure that that will happen or will not happen.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Michael, I`ve got about 30 seconds left. But I got to say, I almost lost my breath. When you just said what you said about how we may have seen our last fair and free elections predicated on this, you know, future Supreme Court decision. Do you think Americans understand I mean, I think our viewers do, but do you think Americans at large understand the parallel that American democracy is in?\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: I`m not sure that they do. And they better because of our democracy has taken away and it might be even in the next four or five months, it will be because people stood silent. All I would say to our friends who are watching us is vote, organize, express yourself and peaceful protests would be good too. Remember what Robert Kennedy said, in our society, it is not enough to allow dissent, we must demand it.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: The incomparable Michael Beschloss, Michael, as always, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, our nation`s highest civilian honor, the big list of names to whom President Biden will award the Presidential Medal of Freedom, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:57:15]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA, (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: For the final time as President, I am pleased to award our nation`s highest civilian honor, the Presidential Medal of Freedom.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Last thing before we go tonight, the Presidential Medal of Freedom. January of 2017, President \uc2 Obama\uc2  surprised then Vice President Biden with the nation`s highest civilian honor. Now, as President Biden gets to extend that honor to others, though they and their family are getting more advanced notice than he received. Here`s a look at some of those both living and who are no longer with us who will be recognized this year.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SIMONE BILES, OLYMPIC GOLD MEDALIST: I have to focus on my mental well- being and that`s what I did.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You sure did. That was brave.\par \par \uc2 BILES: Thank you. It was hard, working five years for a dream and just having to give it up. It was not easy at all.\par \par \uc2 STEVE JOBS, CO-FOUNDER OF APPLE: An iPod, a phone and an Internet communicator. These are not three separate devices. This is one 